            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-comedy-03",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Comedy · 03/06",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == comedy}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Comedy. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 3 of 6. }}{{// Source group: recipes-comedy; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPacakbb::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jane Austen / Web Serial\n\nWrite each reply as part of a continuous social occasion, carrying the actual people, their offered courtesies, and their current practical work across installments. Recover earlier wording only when the new reply needs it: a promised bowl that returns, a phrase of welcome repeated under different circumstances, a particular family estimate that now meets a changed arrangement. The comic timing comes from that continuity. A courteous phrase can recur with a stable meaning, be recognized accurately, or gain a supported new application; do not manufacture fresh misunderstanding for a joke. Preserve what a character already knows about the other and about the situation.\n\nGive setup, a present offer and any actual reception the extent their place in the ongoing occasion needs; no reception is owed before the setup can remain at the boundary. A character may accept, defer, answer literally, or continue an unrelated task; thought and private feeling remain available through the active interiority convention. A long interval may contain ordinary labor, silence, or a return to a shared object. End at the active boundary, including within continuing private experience or public activity, without a required changed relationship, new option, or closing joke.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacalaa::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jin Yong / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nGive the request, admission, withholding or offer its actual personal cost. Where shared history, reputation or public obligation bears on that cost, show the particular connection: a person may find it difficult to ask for a skill they are reputed to possess, or to accept help from someone whose goodwill they already trust. Duty and attachment may align; no prior breach, imagined refusal or loyalty test is needed.\n\nArrange comic timing through the actual wording, preparation and reception of the exchange. An elaborate account of what is owed may meet a plain, helpful answer, or a familiar response may arrive exactly when both people expect it. Give the technique or practical terms enough clarity for that relation to be recognizable. The joke need not embarrass anyone, and a serious or unamused response remains possible.\n\nDevelop the permitted person's actual private experience, including direct feeling, extensive reflection or uncertainty that changes no action. Warmth may be easy or awkward, refusal may have any supported reason, and reception may remain pending. Keep the encounter spacious enough for its real activity, explanation and relationships, following the active boundary during speech, thought or action without requiring a natural pause, an answer, reconciliation or an unsettled bond.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacalab::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jin Yong / Chat Roleplay\n\nPresent the shared interval in readable paragraphs with clear location, movement and dialogue attribution. Keep each participant's actual aim legible, whether they seek help, pursue an obligation, enjoy company or attend to an unrelated concern. A known method, remembered promise or public reputation can establish an expectation; show whose expectation it is instead of treating the description as the person's motive or guaranteed capacity.\n\nLet the current use of that method or promise supply comic timing where the sequence supports it. A solemn undertaking may be met by a precise practical question, or familiar competence may produce an exactly anticipated, enjoyable result. Give execution and explanation their needed space. Help, refusal, failure, success and pending response follow actual capacity, willingness and events, without a compulsory rescuer or reaction.\n\nKeep NPCs active in their own lives and preserve the player's ownership under the active contract. The narrator and each speaker retain their supplied voices and knowledge. Full authorized nonspoken experience belongs alongside the public interval in its selected presentation, including thought that never becomes a decision or remark. Stop at the actual response boundary during continuing public or private experience; no natural pause, completed joke, new complication or question to the player is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacalac::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jin Yong / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse vernacular episodic telling to situate an actual encounter within an ongoing sequence of human occasions. Introduce the people and the matter that brings them together in direct, accessible narration. Give travel, work, explanation or social exchange room where present, without reducing a consequential event to a domestic errand or inventing a journey to fill the form.\n\nWhere a prior undertaking, learned method or designation returns, connect its known history to the particular use made of it now. A reputation for precision may become comic when that precision answers a surprisingly exact request; a familiar application may also be enjoyable without surprise. Attribute public estimates and distinguish them from actual ability and private motive. The telling can move from an expansive account to a plain detail, making the comic scale legible without an obligatory loss of dignity.\n\nLink occasions through actual continuing people, commitments and consequences. A recurrence may confirm an existing relation or acquire another use, but every episode need not introduce a reputation, complete a payoff or change the situation. Preserve real competence, error and character mockery where established. Keep the supplied narrator, community and full authorized private experience, and let the active boundary arrive within an ongoing occasion without settlement, relocation, a moral or an imported martial world.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacalad::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jin Yong / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nUse forceful verbs, concrete nouns and compressed transitions to make the actual method and physical circumstances readable. Select the contact, effort or material detail that belongs to this task: how something is held, how a practiced movement is checked, or what a particular operation requires. Preserve the supplied world's real forces and costs without adding wear, injury or equipment failure for texture.\n\nPut a supported reputation or expectation beside the precise work it meets. A method learned for one consequential purpose may serve a modest request, or a task at its full established scale may fit the reputation exactly. Let sentence rhythm emphasize that relation: an accumulating account can meet a direct action clause, while a deliberate physical sequence can make a familiar success funny. Actual failure, restraint or an efficient solution keeps its place; no unnecessary elaboration or guaranteed success is owed.\n\nGive meaningful private thought and feeling their actual extent in the active presentation. Bodily tells can accompany that experience when they occur, but cannot replace it or prove another person's motive. Speakers keep their own diction. Shift rhythm with the character's real attention and events, and follow the active boundary during continuing work or inward experience without requiring completion, recognized mismatch or a loyalty crisis.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacalae::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jin Yong / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCompose the sequence using documents with a clear writer, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Use forms appropriate to the world: letters, reports, inventories, notices, diary entries, or annotations. Each document is an artifact with its own limitations; it reflects what its author knows, chooses to record, or believes the recipient needs to read. Preserve the active narrator and perspective; the document form adds no access, but does not remove access already authorized.\n\nEstablish the comic premise across documents. A reputation, promise, or account of skill appears in one record—perhaps a recommendation, a memorial, or a set of instructions. A later record takes up that premise in a practical way: a recipient asks a modest favor based on the grand description, or a reply answers the question at the wrong scale. The humor lies in the exact relation between the language of the first record and the actions of the second. For instance, a lofty description of a person's ability may be followed by a shopping list, a request for a small repair, or a note of thanks for something embarrassingly simple. Do not require a misunderstanding; the writers may understand each other fully and still produce the comic contrast.\n\nRespect the boundaries of each document. Delivery delays, receipts, editorial notes, or later annotations can become part of the story when they matter. A later hand may correct, underline, or comment on an earlier record, adding another comic layer. But keep knowledge separate: a character's response follows their actual access, including relevant prior knowledge or reports rather than receipt of this particular document alone, and private live thought is not the same as a composed letter. Keep all required nonspoken experience in the active Hidden or Visible presentation, distinct from the document and without inventing a new wrapper. Follow the active response boundary during composition, delivery, reception or continuing private experience. A meaningful correspondence break, new tone, completed exchange or rescue debt is not required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacalaf::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jin Yong / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nCenter the scene on a chosen act of care that fits the immediate situation: a favorite food, a repaired object, a remembered preference, or a comfortable routine. Let the characters' personalities remain intact—one may be fussy, another matter-of-fact; one may enjoy the fuss, another may pretend not to. Show the pleasure of the act itself, not just its outcome: the way something is wrapped, the satisfaction of getting a small detail right.\n\nBuild the comic element from a reputation or solemn undertaking that is set beside this modest care. A character known for great achievements may take unusual pains over a trivial preference, or a formal promise may be fulfilled through an unexpectedly loving small gesture. Comic recognition may arise from an affectionate mismatch or a familiar exact fit: the care is real, the recipient may be surprised or quietly pleased, and no one needs to explain the joke. Do not impose mockery; preserve any actual affectionate teasing or sharper response in its supported context.\n\nKeep skill, willingness, and permission distinct. Someone may offer help that is welcome but not needed, or provide a specific service without taking over. Let recipients contribute, object, or add their own preferences. Preserve privacy and disagreement, with full meaningful nonspoken experience whether or not it changes conduct. Comfort does not mean everyone agrees or that established problems vanish. If there is a moment of warmth, let it be sincere but not obligatory—no required gratitude, confession, or emotional repair. The scene may end with the enjoyment still in progress, the recipient settling in, or a small return of care beginning. No dependence or ceremonial loyalty is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacalag::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jin Yong / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse sustained paragraphs and deliberate transitions to place the actual encounter within the history or social context available to the narrator. Let a reputation, obligation or alliance gather through concrete prior decisions and attributed accounts where relevant. Distinguish what others expect from actual skill, willingness and the person's private attachment; the formal framing need not become a biography or a verdict.\n\nRelate that wider account to a precise present method, preference, remark or authorized private association. The care with which someone fulfills an ordinary request may make a public reputation comically particular, or the current act may remain as consequential as the account suggests. Keep actual scale intact. A gathering sentence and a plain detail can make the relation recognizable without a forced quip, pratfall or explanatory declaration that the scene is funny.\n\nGive the historical movement, current exchange and inward experience their needed development. Direct feeling and ordinary cast speech remain available within the composed narrative distance. Follow the active boundary during any of these movements, including before a return outward; no completed act, recognized implication, resumed context or changed relationship is needed to end the response.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacalah::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jin Yong / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen inside the telling situation: identify the teller, listeners, and what the telling is meant to do in the present. Give the teller a recognizable aim—persuading an ally, excusing a delay, securing help, or justifying a request—and keep the audience's actual interests, including enjoyment of company or the tale, present while the remembered material unfolds.\n\nBuild the told moment around a specific act of restraint, competence, or loyalty. Follow the actual sequence and what skill, care or limitation contributed, whether the act succeeded, failed or remains unfinished. Do not stop the outer scene to moralize; let the telling itself be a move in the ongoing encounter.\n\nWhen the inner account reaches a point that could sound heroic, connect it to a concrete detail the listeners know or actually encounter in the telling: an unfinished task, a borrowed item, a time pressure, a household concern. The comic scale arises when that ordinary matter receives the full apparatus of the remembered deed—sincere planning, technical language, or solemn past obligation—without denying that the deed mattered.\n\nLet reception happen naturally. A listener may interrupt with a question, supply the very item the teller forgot, or agree immediately. Any response that fits the established persons is workable. If a repeated object or phrase appears in both levels, let its return make the relation between deed and current concern recognizable, whether understanding changes or stays familiar, without requiring proof or laughter.\n\nKeep the teller's knowledge separated from later understanding. When the story reaches an incident the teller did not understand at the time, use the present telling to correct or complete it only through knowledge the actual character has since gained by observation, reasoning, reports or another supported means. Otherwise, preserve the original uncertainty.\n\nWhere the sequence returns to the outer aim, listeners may act, refuse, ask a clarifying question or set the matter aside. Follow the active boundary during continuing telling, public activity or private experience without requiring that return, a natural pause, persuasion or completion.\n\nRepresent all allowed private experience for the teller and listeners in the active presentation, including thoughts that remain unspoken during the performance. A listener's inward response to a comic detail may exist without interrupting the teller. Do not replace anyone's interiority with their public words, and do not give the inner account an omniscient explanation the teller could not supply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacalai::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jin Yong / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse social and developmental realism to connect a present method with the conditions that formed it. Where actual training, work, relationships or prior experience enters the encounter, show the particular connection and its known source. A practiced way of explaining a problem may come from long familiarity with a person, or a physical technique from an earlier occupation. Do not invent a formative episode or reduce the history to a required single lesson.\n\nArrange the relation between that formation and current use for comic recognition. A technique learned under solemn circumstances may answer a modest request; it may also fit the present task exactly and be funny through familiar timing or a person's precise habit. Preserve the actual scale and rigor of both occasions. Capability, self-appraisal and public estimate remain different things, and the result follows actual conditions rather than praise or a required humiliation.\n\nLet attachment and obligation enter through the real reasons and relationships involved, including aligned concerns or motives with no conflict. Give reflection on the learned method its practical or personal significance even when it changes no decision, and preserve then-known experience separately from present interpretation. The developmental material may be brief or extended as needed. Keep the current scene and full authorized private experience legible, with no required growth, return to an errand or pause before the active boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacalaj::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jin Yong / Gothic-Punk\n\nPlace the encounter within its actual material surroundings and inherited relations. An office, private room, worn public space or ceremonial object may carry a history of authority; develop the particulars that shape these people's attention, including atmosphere with no immediate practical use. Attribute reputation and public claims instead of treating an imposing place as proof of competence or motive.\n\nLet that standing meet a present preference, request or obligation. A precise use of skill may fulfill an unexpectedly ordinary need, or a consequential task may fit the person's reputation exactly. Arrange the historical weight and actual conduct so their relation can be comic without a required failure, humble errand or flattering display of power. Preserve actual capacity, permission, willingness and the reasons for the choice, whether affectionate, self-interested, reluctant or otherwise supported.\n\nKeep private attachment and public presentation distinct. Trust may allow a change of manner, but a supported voluntary or involuntary change can occur elsewhere, and the presentation may remain intact. Full authorized nonspoken experience can develop beyond any visible sign or practical response. Preserve the supplied world's forces and culture, adding no vampire canon or darkness merely for this style, and follow the active boundary within continuing thought, speech or activity without requiring a pause or a renegotiated authority.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacalak::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jin Yong / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite through social and domestic realism. Show the existing relationships through their actual arrangements of help: who lives nearby, who lends tools or money, who is expected to be present at certain times, who prepares food or handles children. Use these concrete arrangements as the materials of comedy. Do not impose a universal family structure or fixed hierarchy; preserve the specific community and relationships already supplied.\n\nBegin with the present obligation or request. A solemn promise, a remembered loyalty, or a grand declaration may be attached to an immediate practical need: collecting medicine, paying a bill, returning a borrowed item, releasing someone from a shift. Arrange the relation between public language and the errand at its actual scale. Comic recognition can come from a modest application, an exact familiar fit or another supported difference without shrinking the stakes.\n\nLet each character contribute according to their actual ability and willingness. Preserve each person's actual skill, willingness, permission and attachment, including supported limitations or changes. A capable person may do exactly what is asked without hesitation. The humor need not mock anyone; it can come from the precision with which a practiced helper navigates the domestic task, the contrast between public loyalty and private efficiency, or the slight mismatch between what was promised and what was actually needed.\n\nAvoid assigning a permanent rescuer and dependent. Over time, roles may shift; a person helped today may help tomorrow. The writing should preserve each person's independence and reluctance or eagerness as their own. If the scene's authority contract permits, show the private thoughts behind a choice to help, but let the help itself remain outward and concrete.\n\nGround dialogue in individual voices. Not everyone speaks with the same formality. A young person may answer a ceremonial phrase with blunt practical language; an elder may use proverbs; a tradesperson may say almost nothing. These differences may support gentle comedy; any meaning of respect or disrespect follows the actual relationship and usage, not the speaker's age or occupation alone.\n\nUse concrete setting and objects as part of the lived arrangement, including practical use, atmosphere or personally meaningful attention. A crowded kitchen, a shared courtyard, a market stall—show the physical space through actions: who has to step around whom, where the character puts a load down, what they wipe their hands on. Such movements can make domestic realism tangible, while meaningful description or private attention may develop without serving an immediate task.\n\nPreserve full authorized private experience, especially the gap between what a character says in public and what they feel while helping. A person may agree aloud and privately wish they were elsewhere; another may say nothing while actually enjoying the work. Interiority is not required to lead to a visible confession or a change in the relationship. Do not force a conflict between duty and affection; they may coexist without climax.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacalal::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jin Yong / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nAdopt early-modern comic and theatrical telling as your prose method. Treat the scene as a piece of performed business, but write it in prose with actual speech and movement. The comedy comes from the relation between what characters say and what they are physically doing at that moment.\n\nSet occasional concrete physical actions alongside dialogue. A character may deliver a formal apology while stacking boxes, answer a solemn question while eating, or promise aid while backing toward a door. These juxtapositions are not distractions; they are the material of timing. Let the rhythm of the scene rise and fall through such combinations, not through explanatory adverbs.\n\nTrack actual ongoing tasks and conditions. A character may continue work, pause it for conversation or change priorities according to their real attention and circumstances; preserve consequences without requiring constant activity. A famous method or oath may be invoked while the person is holding a mop. Write that coexistence matter-of-factly: the task and promise remain real where established, and the person may address them together, choose an order or leave either pending.\n\nDo not turn every speaker into a performer of grandeur. Keep individual registers distinct. One character may speak in formal public phrases; another may answer with plain words; a third may remain silent. The theatrical quality applies to the telling, not to the whole cast. No character is required to have a sense of stage presence.\n\nLet familiar competence produce comic continuations. An established skill can interrupt a grand declaration with a practical correction; a well-known method can arrive exactly on cue to solve a minor problem; a solemn outing can become a series of small, competent errands. None of this requires a stumble, a physical mishap, or a forced laugh.\n\nKeep the stakes real even when the tone is comedic. A request for help, a debt, an injury, or an old obligation matters to these people. The writing should not dissolve consequences into farce. Comic timing can exist within a scene that also contains fear, loyalty, or anger.\n\nMake dynamic delivery legible: pacing, pause, and the arrangement of speakers. Use paragraph breaks and sentence length to shape how a line lands. A short, plain reply after a long formal address is itself a comic beat. A character beginning to speak, then not speaking, is a beat. The prose should guide the reader's sense of that timing.\n\nRepresent all authorized private experience in the active presentation. A character's inner response to the public business—exasperation, amusement, worry, tenderness—may be rendered in prose without being announced. It remains the character's own mind, separate from what others hear. Do not substitute a public performance for required interiority, and do not grant a character access to another's private thoughts merely through the telling; preserve only access actually supported by the world and active contract.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacalam::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jin Yong / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse polyphonic nonlinear telling around an actual deed, undertaking or encounter. Arrange permitted accounts and times with clear source markers: a participant's experience, a report, a later memory or the active narrator's authorized account. A return may revisit familiar material, confirm another account or supply a different emphasis; novelty is not owed on each shift.\n\nMake the relation between reputation, particular technique and the act's human circumstances visible through juxtaposition. A public account of loyalty may stand beside a participant's precise practical concern, and both may be accurate. Their scale may differ or fit exactly. Comic timing can come from the order in which those supported positions meet without a required liar, hidden motive or diminished deed. Where accounts disagree in fact, interpretation or feeling, keep that disagreement attached to its source while preserving established events.\n\nGive actual shared history, attachment and obligation their place in each account rather than inventing a debt or alliance. Keep original knowledge separate from later interpretation and grant nonspoken access only under the active perspective; no collective mind follows from a chorus of reports. Full private experience may continue within or alongside the account in its selected presentation. End at the active boundary during any segment, without requiring a completed comparison, natural pause or advance in understanding.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacalan::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jin Yong / Light Novel\n\nEnter the actual situation with readable orientation to people, place and the current concern. Make spatial changes clear as they occur, and introduce an unfamiliar method, reputation or prior promise with enough context to understand its use. Explanation can come through speech, narration or authorized recollection in the real causal order, including preparation before action; neither a first-paragraph inventory nor repeated exposition of already familiar facts is required.\n\nUse paragraph rhythm to make a particular expectation and its actual reception easy to follow. A practiced skill may answer a surprisingly exact request, or a familiar method may work just as anticipated. Give execution its real detail without forcing a mishap, a miniature task, a masterful result or universal quips. Characters keep their actual registers, capacities and reasons, and the supplied world retains its existing powers and limits.\n\nLet generous scene movement include activity, explanation, relationships and meaningful private experience. An inward passage or remembered obligation may develop extensively without producing a visible change, and the active narrator keeps the authorized person and access. Paragraph length does not measure fictional elapsed time. Follow the active response boundary during public or private continuation without a fixed setup-response-reaction cycle, a natural pause, a completed laugh or a task that must remain unfinished.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacalao::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jin Yong / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet the scene's rhythm and imagery carry the relation between a known method, promise, or shared history and the exact practical gesture now before the viewpoint. A recurring sound or image may gather weight across the paragraph: the same word that once belonged to a solemn undertaking can return beside a modest errand, and the prose can let that juxtaposition register without announcing it. Build the comic scale through the shape of the sentence, not through a punchline that must follow the image. A plain statement or an ordinary act may meet the shaped expectation and confirm it as readily as qualify it.\n\nKeep the image traceable to the scene or the teller's associations. If the narrator hears an old phrase in the rhythm of someone's current speech, let the association belong to that narrator's memory and permission; another character need not know it. Preserve genuine feeling, actual world facts, and individual cast speech. The lyrical treatment does not make every speaker use the narrator's figurative register, and it does not require the image to fail, the loyalty to be mocked, or the private attachment to become public. Nonspoken experience may continue without a completed comparison or public payoff.\n\nGive the encounter enough scenic movement for the practical situation to remain legible: who is attempting what, what method or obligation is in play, and how the present act relates to the known history. Technique, restraint, alliances, and reputation matter through particular decisions, but the prose may dwell on the feel of a familiar motion or the sound of a name rather than narrating every step. Let the comic disproportion emerge from the exact relation between the shaped expectation and the present act, whether that act is deft, clumsy, affectionate, or merely practical. The scene may end with the business concluded or still underway, leaving the association available without forcing it to resolve into a lesson or a laugh.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacalap::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jin Yong / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the present scene recall a particular earlier occasion through a detail that belongs to the viewpoint: the weight of a familiar tool, the cadence of a phrase, the route once taken under different circumstances. Source the memory to the actual character and represent its retained detail and limitations faithfully, including any supported error without rewriting the original event. The same skill may now serve an unexpectedly modest concern, or a familiar act may remain enjoyably apt, and the comic effect can come from that exact relation between what the undertaking once meant and what it now requires. Do not invent loss, obsolete competence, or a lesson on impermanence to make the callback land.\n\nPreserve what remains available and what has actually changed. A remembered attachment may still be present in private feeling while the outward business proceeds; the reflection may be direct and extensive without changing conduct or ending in a joke. Let the narrator's distance and diction shape the reflection rather than a stock vocabulary of suffering. The melancholy treatment does not require the scene to conclude by announcing that everything is passing or that the old loyalty has decayed. Ordinary pleasure can remain pleasurable.\n\nGive the encounter room for its actual activity, explanation and relationships, including travel where present. The reflective movement may pause the practical action, but the practical action remains consequential: a promise, technique, or public obligation still has its particular demands. Arrange the present act and remembered expectation for comic recognition at their actual scales, allowing a familiar exact fit as well as a difference; private feeling can coexist without a required recognition. The scene may end with the business still underway, the attachment unspoken, or the familiar method once again in use, without forcing a completed emotional statement.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacalaq::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jin Yong / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the scene as a modern memoir: a situated later account by an actual source who remembers the event, with the time of composition kept distinct from the time of the remembered action. Let the memoirist's purpose and position shape what is recalled, emphasized, or left in the background. The active narrator remains the one authorized by the scene; the memoir form does not grant access to other minds or replace live private experience with a retrospective summary.\n\nLet a remembered undertaking gather context before an exact practical detail reveals its comic scale. The memoirist may recall the public reputation, the solemn promise, or the impressive method as it appeared at the time, then record a particular gesture, object or exchange at its actual scale, whether it confirms the account or supplies another comic proportion. Preserve the actual earlier understanding rather than inventing belated wisdom. The memoirist may now see the comedy more clearly than the participants did, but the earlier knowledge must remain what it was.\n\nKeep reputation and private motive able to agree. The account may show that the person was as skilled or loyal as the reputation claimed, and that the comic disproportion lay in the occasion, the expectation, or the exact practical use of that skill. No exile, confession, or moral correction is required. The memoir's retrospective voice may be reflective, satirical, or plain, but it must remain the voice of its actual author. Follow the active response boundary during continuing recollection, composition, public activity or live private experience; attention need not rest and no completed judgment or public payoff is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacalar::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jin Yong / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect a small number of exact details and direct clauses to carry the relation between a known promise, method, or reputation and the particular act now before the viewpoint. The comic disproportion can live in what is placed side by side: the grand phrasing of an old obligation next to the plain object it now requires, or the precise motion of a skilled hand next to the modest result it produces. Omit redundant explanation or praise once the selected particulars make the relation inferable, while preserving actual spoken explanation and meaningful private understanding.\n\nKeep the causes and known history intact. Omission should leave something inferable rather than delete causality. If a character's restraint or technique matters, show the exact detail that carries it: the pause before a familiar motion, the one word that recalls a shared past, the practical choice that fulfils a promise without ceremony. Reputation remains distinct from fact, and skill remains distinct from obligation. A developed inward passage may still be needed for feeling or thought without immediate practical use; do not shorten it merely to meet a quota.\n\nPreserve individual cast voices. Minimalist prose does not make every speaker terse, concealed, or dry. A character may speak at length, joke, or explain, and the narration may still stay economical. Do not impose failure, a deflating last line, or a punchline that must arrive at the end. The scene may conclude with the business concluded or still underway, leaving the exact relation available without a closing judgment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacalas::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jin Yong / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose that moves naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience. Let the shared history enter through its actual practical or personal significance in the present moment: a technique remembered in the hands, a promise recalled by the phrasing of a request, a reputation met by the exact scale of the task now at hand. Make the comic relation legible through that precise use or actual private association. Narrative history may develop at its needed extent, including personal significance that does not explain or change the present encounter; omit only a redundant gloss of the joke.\n\nGive actual technique, reception, and continuing private experience enough clarity for their comic relation to remain legible. A character may perform a familiar method with full competence while the situation around it is modest, inconvenient, or unexpectedly ceremonial; the prose can register both the skill and the scale without requiring a mishap. Competence can work, attachment can align with obligation, and the person can understand the joke already. Preserve independent aims and real consequences: the other characters pursue their own recognizable goals, and the world remains consequential enough for the problem to stay playable.\n\nLet the narrator's voice, the author profile, and the immediate scene set the register and rhythm. Paragraphing and transitions should orient without recap machinery. The scene may end with the business concluded or still underway, with a private thought unfinished, or with a practical act in progress. No duel, loyalty test, changed bond, or punchline is owed at the active boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacalat::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jin Yong / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nChoose scenes and callbacks from the actual relationship premise, shared history, and private language where the supplied material establishes them. Let a known capacity or promise meet an exact preference in a new or familiarly apt way: the same person who once handled a serious obligation may now apply that method to a smaller, more personal request, and the recurrence can be comic without making the skill a joke. Keep skill, willingness, and permission distinct. One person may be capable of helping without being obliged to, and the other may accept, refuse, or contribute in return.\n\nMake the established bond legible through the particular scene rather than through a roster of familiar tropes. Affection, rivalry, comfort, or discovery should have enough space where it belongs, but do not insert jealousy, rescue debt, confession, status change, or new intimacy unless the actual premise supplies them. Mutual contribution and sincere agreement are available; no participant is fixed permanently as rescuer or dependent. The comic shorthand works because the recurrence fulfils the bond's particular promise, not because one person is always competent and the other always in need.\n\nPreserve generous scenic movement and the actual relation between private attachment and public obligation, including alignment without tension. The relationship may be tested by a public demand, a practical errand, or a shared history that others know only partially, while private language and repeated moments retain their actual history and known audience, including anything already shared among or beyond the actual participants. Follow the active response boundary during ongoing public or private experience, with no requirement that the relationship rest, remain intact or complete an emotional payoff.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacalau::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jin Yong / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account a sense of retelling through deliberate openings, compact declarations, and recurring phrasing that belongs to actual deeds. A supported designation may introduce a person or act, then expand into the particular method, restraint, or response that makes its scale comic. The praise can remain true: the character may be as skilled or loyal as the declaration claims, and the comedy lies in the exact practical deed that now answers the grand phrasing. Do not impose mock combat or heroic doom for the cadence; preserve any actual play, conflict or consequence supported by the scene.\n\nDistinguish original motive, public account, and later knowledge. The retold version may compress or elevate what the participants experienced, and the narration can let the gap between the chronicle's cadence and the private moment register without making anyone a liar. Private experience retains its own permissions and extent; being remembered as dependable does not mean that helping was effortless, cheerful, or automatic. Let dialogue interrupt the authority of the chronicle when a speaker objects to a detail or answers an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern.\n\nKeep the particular source tradition coherent rather than mixing every epic convention. Recurrence should come from an established deed or way of speaking, not from an ornamental epithet assigned to every entrance. Follow the active response boundary during a continuing deed, telling or private experience, whether business is concluded or still underway. No next modest act or legendary status needs to be supplied. No communal verdict is owed at the active boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacalav::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jin Yong / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nUse folktale-inflected telling as a definite narrative route: a recognizable situation repeats or returns with enough concrete orientation that the audience can follow the pattern, whether its application varies or remains familiar, while the actual people and world facts remain specific. Establish the pattern through an earlier encounter, a known method, a familiar object, or a promise already made in the story. Let the recurrence carry the comic relation. The same competent act may meet a modest practical use, a different recipient's expectation, or a successful repetition whose scale surprises the people involved. Do not impose a quest structure, a count of three attempts, a magical helper, or a moral lesson; the pattern comes from what the supplied world already contains.\n\nKeep the telling grounded in actual encounters. When a character invokes a reputation, a technique, or an obligation, show the particular physical or social circumstances that give it its present size. A solemn undertaking can be fulfilled by an exact small action without becoming a failure or a humiliation. The comedy arises from timing and proportion: the hearer's actual expectation meets the act at its real scale, and an exact fit or supported gap can carry the relation without a redundant gloss. Let different characters respond according to their own habits. One may treat the modest fulfilment as perfectly natural, another may be disappointed, amused, or relieved, and a third may not notice the pattern at all.\n\nPreserve generous scenic movement and the actual relation between private attachment and public obligation, including alignment without a conflict. Give the recurrence room to travel through real scenes: a promise made in one place may be met in another, and the journey itself can change what the fulfilment means. Keep private experience available in full. A character may feel loyalty, embarrassment, amusement, or reluctance while performing the act, and that inward state does not have to be announced in speech. The narrator's distance and diction should remain consistent with the active authorship contract. Use recurring phrasing sparingly, as a thread the reader can recognize, not as a mandatory refrain at every difficulty. The scene may stop during the work, the reception, or the private thought that follows; no completed moral or public acknowledgment is required at the boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacalaw::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jin Yong / Screenplay\n\nWrite the scene in concise action blocks with clear speaker cues and scene headings only when location or time changes. Establish the expectation through what is actually said or visibly present: a reputation invoked in dialogue, a promise written on a note, a method demonstrated earlier, or an object associated with a known skill. Then show the particular execution in readable physical terms. The comic relation comes from the juxtaposition the screenplay form permits. A cut can place the grand statement in one setting and the modest or exact practical act in another, letting the audience see the scale difference without a character explaining it. A held beat can let a visible reaction or an unchanged object carry the same disproportion. Do not require a mishap, a failed attempt, or a reaction shot to make the point.\n\nUse spatial continuity to keep competent execution legible. When a character performs a skill, show the steps that matter: what they reach for, how they move, what changes in the room. The action block should make the method readable even when the result is small. Make the actual relation between private attachment and public obligation legible through conduct and separately authorized inward experience; speech, action and expectation may align or differ. A cut may connect distinct settings, but it does not grant knowledge across them; each character retains all actually available knowledge, including prior experience and received reports, without gaining information merely because a cut showed it to the audience.\n\nKeep full nonspoken private experience separate from dialogue and voice-over. Required interiority remains clearly attributed nonspoken material in the active presentation, not a line another character can hear and not a voice-over inserted for convenience. The scene can stop during the work, before a response arrives, or after a private realization. No public payoff, completed rescue, or closing reaction is required at the active boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacalax::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jin Yong / Stage Play\n\nUse the shared stage space to make the comic relation playable. A character may invoke a reputation, a promise, or an undertaking in speech while the physical business they perform beside it is exact, modest, or unexpectedly practical. The audience sees both at once: the grand phrase and the plain act. Let the juxtaposition remain legible without an added explanatory announcement, while preserving any actual remark or private recognition. Write clear speakers, entrances, exits, and economical directions for supported blocking, objects, and activity. A pause can hold the disproportion; an object can be set down, handed over, or ignored in a way that changes the scene's scale. Do not require a new prop, a stumble, or theatrical grandeur to produce the effect.\n\nKeep cast voices individual. One character may speak with ceremony, another with literal plainness, and a third may be uninterested in the performance altogether. Their habits of speech and movement should come from who they are, not from a uniform comic register. Gesture and expression remain observable stage business; they do not certify motive, guilt, or hidden feeling. A character's actual private thought stays separate nonspoken material in the active presentation, not an automatic aside delivered to the audience. If an aside is separately authorized, it is performed speech with its own dramatic purpose, not the default destination for interiority.\n\nPreserve the actual relation between private attachment and public obligation through supported public choices and authorized private experience, allowing alignment and open expression as well as tension or withholding. The scene can stop during speech, during work, or during a private continuation. No laugh cue, completed action, or public acknowledgment is required at the active boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacalay::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jin Yong / Stream of Consciousness\n\nBraid present sensation, heard words, memory, private phrasing, and recurring concern through the particular authorized mind. Let the associations move by actual connection: a solemn undertaking may recur alongside an exact practical sensation, an unrelated preference, or the memory of who first taught the method. The comic scale can arise inside the mind when a grand obligation and a small bodily fact sit next to each other without the character forcing them into conflict. Amusement is possible but not compulsory; the character may feel it, miss it, or notice it later. Do not turn every association into a punchline or require the mind to resolve the disproportion.\n\nKeep remembered, imagined, and current events distinct. A memory of a promise is not the same as the present act of fulfilling it, and an imagined reception is not a report of what actually happened. Preserve accurate prior understanding: the character's earlier knowledge remains available and cannot be replaced by a later realization inserted as the earlier motive. The stream may be extensive or unfinished, and it may circle back without completing a thought. Prose length alone does not set elapsed time; actual attention, waiting, and events still have their real duration. The technique grants no additional access. Keep each associative passage situated in its own permitted mind, while preserving separately authorized shifts or broader narrator access with clear attribution and without merging minds.\n\nPreserve the actual relation between private attachment and public obligation, allowing alignment or tension without requiring a choice between them. The character can hold loyalty, reluctance, embarrassment, and practical focus together, and the narration may move among them without announcing a resolution. The scene can stop during the thought, before any outward action changes, or after a private realization that remains unspoken.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacalaz::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jin Yong / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the scene through readable sender-attributed messages. Let the wording someone actually composes and sends establish an expectation: a grand description of a skill, a formal promise, or an urgent request. The next actual reception may confirm it in a modest, exact, or practical way, and the sequence itself creates the comic relation. The reply can be plain, competent, delayed by real activity, or simply smaller than the opening message suggested. Do not invent a misunderstanding, a failed delivery, or a rescue to make the exchange interesting. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to what the characters actually do and know.\n\nPreserve each sender's digital habits and actual knowledge. One person may type formally and send immediately; another may draft, delete, and rewrite; a third may answer with a single practical line. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not the writer's live private thought. Required nonspoken interiority remains separately available in the active presentation and is not transmitted text. A recipient retains all actually available knowledge, including prior experience and reports; the channel does not reveal the sender's unsent considerations merely because narration presents them. Context outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript.\n\nLet the actual relation between private attachment and public obligation inform message choices where relevant: what someone writes, asks for or leaves unstated. Those concerns may align, and nothing must be withheld. The scene can stop before a message is sent, before it is received, or after a reply has been read. No gratitude, response quota, or completed exchange is required at the active boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacalba::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jin Yong / Visual Novel\n\nOrient the scene with concise setting, readable dialogue attribution, and a selected background, object, or expression cue that attracts the authorized focal character's attention. The cue is a point of contact, not a truth indicator. A changed expression can draw suspicion or amusement, but it does not prove what another character feels or intends. Let the known method, reputation, or undertaking meet an actual response at its supplied scale. The focal character may notice the disproportion between the grand claim and the practical act, or may miss it entirely while the reader sees it. Private feeling can be long, serious, amused, or uncertain, and it does not have to be spoken.\n\nDistinguish observation, reputation, and supported inference. The focal character sees a cue; they may recall what they have heard about a person or method; they may draw a fallible conclusion from their actual reasoning and information; presenting the belief does not establish its truth. Another character does not know the focal character's private reaction merely because the narration has shown it. Preserve the active narrator, person, and ownership settings. Do not impose a fixed cue-thought-dialogue rotation or require every illustrated beat to produce a reaction line.\n\nLet the actual relation between private attachment and public obligation enter what the focal character notices, feels or expresses, including aligned concerns without required withholding. The scene can stop during the reception, during the private thought, or before any outward response changes. No images, branching menu, romance route, or closing reaction is supplied by this style.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacalbb::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jin Yong / Web Serial\n\nMaintain the through-line across replies: what is underway, what has been learned, promised, spent, or left pending, and what remains available to continue. When a later installment needs an earlier term, method, or undertaking, recover only the necessary particular briefly. A known skill may find an unexpectedly modest use, or a familiar successful method may repeat in a new context. The comic callback can come from recognition of the earlier term at a new or familiar scale. Do not manufacture lost competence or betrayal for novelty, and preserve any such change that actually occurs. Preserve what was actually established: a repaired object stays repaired, a learned technique stays learned, a promise stays binding unless the story changes it through real events.\n\nLet independent lives continue around the callback. Other characters pursue their own work, relationships, and practical concerns, and the installment can show that activity without making it serve the joke. Keep meaningful private experience available beyond the callback. A character may feel loyalty, embarrassment, amusement, or reluctance about the repeated method, and that inward state does not have to produce an outward decision or a public acknowledgment. Do not require escalating loyalty tests, a new ally, or a cliffhanger to close the installment. The active boundary may fall within ordinary work, a pending request, or a private thought that continues after the visible scene ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacamaa::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jun Maeda / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the difficult admission, request, apology, or return as a real effort that continues to matter while it is being made. Bring in a specific repeated phrase, object, or routine only when the shared history exists. Its familiar use, changed timing, or the other person's actual reaction can create comic recognition without dissolving the need. Do not invent a callback if this is a first encounter, and do not require the moment to end in rejection, reassurance, changed understanding, or a completed response.\n\nLet the cost show in action, speech, and full nonspoken experience. The character can ask directly, stall, speak with forced lightness, or feel exposed; the other person can respond awkwardly, warmly, sharply, or not yet. Comedy comes from the mismatch between the weight of the request and the way a familiar phrase lands—or from the fact that it lands exactly as it always has. Keep private feeling separate from what words can manage, and preserve the original meaning of any callback even as the scene uses it.\n\nThe active response may end in the middle of continuing speech, thought, waiting, or an unfinished conversation. Do not force a punchline, resolution, or emotional turn before stopping. Humor and hurt remain available in the same exchange according to the actual characters.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacamab::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jun Maeda / Chat Roleplay\n\nResolve the submitted material under the active authorship contract, then continue the scene as a readable shared interval with unmistakable dialogue attribution. Let characters pursue their current aims through particular actions and speech. If a repeated phrase, preference, or routine already belongs to the shared history, allow it to meet the present moment naturally; compatible habits or contrasting responses can be funny. Do not force a callback into every reply, invent a player's response, require a relationship change, or complete a joke on schedule. Actual relationship developments follow the supplied people and events.\n\nKeep NPCs actively pursuing their own practical lives. A character may miss the familiar echo, overuse it, react to its absence, or feel it as an unwanted additional pressure. Comedy arises from recognition, misapplication, or the collision of two different readings of the same routine. Preserve each character's actual knowledge and full authorized private experience. A rendered thought does not automatically become another participant's knowledge; preserve actual prior knowledge, communication, permitted mental access, and player ownership.\n\nEnd at a natural continuation point that follows from what has happened. No question, cliffhanger, or forced choice is required. The scene may stop while a character is still considering something, waiting, working, or speaking.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacamac::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jun Maeda / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic telling route. Give the current occasion its own concrete purpose, participants, and sequence. If an earlier shared routine, object, or phrase already exists, connect the present moment to it through the real history of that thing—where it came from, how it was last used, or who remembers it differently. The recurrence can provide comedy through recognition or contrast, but do not turn an ordinary beginning into a planted clue and do not require a new lesson, surprise, or changed bond.\n\nKeep original motives and knowledge fixed at their original time. A character may act now with only what they understood then, even if the narrator can see the connection. Comedy belongs to particular people, not to the episode structure itself. A familiar cadence may be funny when repeated seriously, when one character half-remembers it, or when its practical use has changed.\n\nLet the active response end during ongoing life or thought. No episode is obligated to reach a clean break. The next occasion can begin from whatever remains unresolved, and the shared routine may continue to develop without an announced callback.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacamad::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jun Maeda / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nUse forceful verbs and concrete weight, wear, distance, or cost only where the supplied activity supports them. Put the practical version of a remembered routine beside its current use: an exact familiar effort now looks different on another body, an old object fails at a new task, or the same chore is done with a reversed result. An unchanged familiar effort or precisely expected result can also carry comic recognition; no altered use, pain, failure, or humiliation is required. Do not manufacture either adversity or collapse for the sake of tone.\n\nCompression cuts restatement, not full feeling or thought. A character may hold a long worry, a private pleasure, or a practical calculation inside a short physical paragraph. Roughness of speech belongs to its speaker, not to the whole cast. A gentle character may still describe a heavy object precisely; an abrasive one may soften around a familiar routine.\n\nKeep the repeated object or phrase inside shared life. An observer may notice the wear, but the joke must come from a real relationship to the thing, not from a logistics mismatch alone. The active response can end in the middle of the work, with the task unfinished, the result inconclusive, or the routine interrupted by ordinary life.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacamae::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jun Maeda / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through the actual document form required by the scene, with a clear writer, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Let a selected shared detail recur through recorded wording or a real annotation when that relation matters to the writer or reader. Distinguish the original meaning from any later commentary: the current writer may note the echo, miss it, exploit it, or treat it as newly significant. Accurate agreement and familiar recognition can work as well as real differences.\n\nKeep each writer's register and extent. A hasty note can record the routine without reflecting on it; a longer letter may dwell on the same phrase until it becomes embarrassing or tender. No extra document, new insight, or hidden-kindness discovery is required. The document is an artifact, not the writer's complete live mind. Include full authorized private experience in the nonspoken layer where the contract calls for it, and keep that private experience distinct from what the document says.\n\nConstruct comic timing through the record's actual selection and reception: a difference of emphasis, an ill-timed callback, or a precisely fitting familiar use can carry it without mandatory misinterpretation or wrong timing. The active response can end with the document unfinished, unsent, or already sent, without a later uptake or reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacamaf::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jun Maeda / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nDwell on the preference, accommodation, or little pleasure that makes care specific. Use only knowledge the helper actually has or learns during the scene. A repeated ritual or a spontaneous apt offer can create affectionate comic timing through real reception, including ordinary acceptance, mild refusal, inattention that later warms, or a familiar complaint delivered with affection. Keep affection sincere, but do not require gratitude, confession, recovered mood, improved relationship, or a sense of debt.\n\nIf a phrase or routine from shared history exists, let it return as part of the care: the same wrong tea order now becomes an intentional choice, the old song is played without remark, the careful arrangement of a common object gets a dry acknowledgment. Accuracy and excess can carry the comedy without requiring a hidden design; preserve any actual preparation or intention rather than ruling it out. No one needs to have engineered the kindness in advance.\n\nPreserve private feeling fully. A recipient may privately notice the care, resist it, or be too tired to respond, and none of that must surface. The helper may be clumsy, proud, or unaware of how much it matters. Follow the active response boundary during continuing care, reception, or private experience as well as an actual conclusion. No enjoyment, survival of the moment, emotional repair, or fictional pause is required before ending.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacamag::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jun Maeda / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse sustained paragraphs and deliberate transitions to place a repeated act, phrase, or courtesy within the narrator's permitted social or historical context. Open the frame wide enough to show how this actual exchange belongs to a particular household, workplace, season, or institution. Then let the present use of the familiar thing sit beside that context. The comedy may come from recognition, anticlimax, or a supported reinterpretation of what the routine has meant all along.\n\nKeep the original act's intention unchanged by hindsight. Later knowledge may add weight, but it does not rewrite the earlier purpose. A character can perform the courtesy with full seriousness while the narrator's wider view makes its smallness or unexpectedness visible. Another character may miss the frame entirely. Do not require a completed return, a new understanding, or a moral resolution.\n\nVary the register. Formal narration is not a uniform high style. Direct speech, private feeling, and a dry aside can all enter without breaking the composed paragraph rhythm. Preserve full inward extent for authorized minds, even when the public scene remains a matter of bows, tasks, and familiar words. The active response can end inside an unfinished social gesture, a half-formed thought, or the ordinary noise of a room.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacamah::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jun Maeda / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nBegin with an actual telling occasion: who is recounting, to whom, and what the listeners hope to get from the exchange—perhaps enjoyment, a distraction, an explanation, or simply shared time. Let the teller select a specific habit, phrase, or routine from shared life because it fits that present purpose, and let the audience's familiarity and immediate reactions shape the delivery. A repeated phrase may travel between the frame and the embedded account, but its meaning need not be hidden or suddenly revealed; emphasis, timing, and the gap between teller's knowledge and listener's knowledge can do the comic work.\n\nPreserve each level's real time and access. The outer scene continues while the tale is told: listeners interrupt, agree, lose the thread, or quietly remember their own version. When a familiar detail recurs, render its actual reception within narrator and character access; warmth, impatience, amusement or inattention can belong to particular listeners without requiring a confession or discovery. Preserve separately authorized live private experience beyond the teller's account or visible audience response, without assuming the teller knows another mind. Let the teller's register stay theirs: a person who rarely tells stories may fall back on lists, a habitual talker may circle, and neither must become a polished narrator.\n\nLet the embedded account carry actual lived weight alongside the comedy. A remembered repeated kindness, a recurring argument, or an old shared phrase can be funny because it is still used, not because a hidden meaning is unveiled. After an event, the same object or routine may feel different to the teller, and that changed tone can leak into the telling without requiring the audience to notice it. If the active response ends mid-story or mid-reaction, leave the frame's present attention and the teller's ongoing selection as the natural stopping point, not an imposed cliffhanger or completed lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacamai::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jun Maeda / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nChoose social or developmental realism as the operative method. Establish a specific social expectation in the present encounter—something about help, belonging, duty, or responsibility that this person actually confronts. When an actual earlier occasion exists, use its familiar object, routine, or phrase to connect the expectation to the current encounter. A first occasion needs no fabricated shared history. The comedy comes from the relation between then and now: the person may recognize the repetition, resist it, or simply see the old hope beside the present inconvenience. Do not require a maturation, humiliation, or a changed belief; the recognition itself can be the beat.\n\nLet the developmental line follow real social experience rather than a moral lesson. When a repeated phrase or habit returns, place its actual present reception beside the earlier one. Familiar enjoyment or an unchanged response can carry comic recognition as readily as new impatience, fondness, or a changed reading; no difference is required. Keep original knowledge and later knowledge distinct; a character can remember how they used to feel without telling everyone or reinterpreting the past. Ground comedy in the actual gap between expectation and outcome rather than in mockery of the person's hopes.\n\nPreserve full private confidence or doubt alongside the social encounter. The narrator may register a character's unspoken sense that they are doing this again, without requiring speech or immediate action. If the response ends during an ongoing exchange or private consideration, let the expectation's present weight remain open; no resolution or new insight must arrive before the boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacamaj::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jun Maeda / Gothic-Punk\n\nPlace a real old authority, social mask, or private desire alongside a shared present-life habit—a recurring kindness, a particular phrase, a familiar routine—whose terms matter for this encounter. Let the atmosphere color the experience: the room, the history, the weight of what is unsaid can all be present. But let the familiar detail do the comic work by providing an intimate scale against that gravity. A person may perform an elaborate courtesy while actually caring about a small, routine thing, and neither the mask nor the care need be exposed as false or secretly benevolent.\n\nKeep morally complicated intimacy alive. Show that a gesture can be genuine and still serve the person's own comfort or position. Make the mask's actual ease, cost, or ambivalence available where it belongs to the encounter, without requiring every maintained role to be burdensome. Render full authorized nonspoken desire, feeling, and uncertainty beyond the public mask, and preserve the world's actual mechanisms and knowledge channels. Use urban material detail—the condition of a chair, the timing of a familiar errand, the way a repeated phrase sounds in a particular room—to ground the relation between old authority and everyday life without making the scene a lecture on power.\n\nDon't force a collapse of authority or a hidden-benevolence revelation. The mask may stay, and the intimacy may remain tense. Trust can be freely offered or incautious, and the comedy can arise from the characters' differing awarenesses of what the repeated detail means. Follow the active response boundary within continuing intimacy or an actual conclusion. Pressure and care may coexist, and any real change, unmasking, or understanding follows the supplied encounter rather than a required lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacamak::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jun Maeda / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse situated participant testimony as the route. Convey the actual participant's account through permitted speech, writing, quotation, report, or narration without making the active narrator a participant or memoirist by default. Keep the source's situation and addressee intelligible: why they are telling this, to whom, and what the account is doing in the present. The comedy should arise from the participant's selection and emphasis: a shared phrase, act, or routine from earlier life that they return to because it carries a particular weight or awkwardness now.\n\nKeep the times distinct. Original knowledge, claimed memory, and later interpretation are not the same, and the teller may smooth over or unknowingly alter details. Distinguish how the selected detail was experienced then from its actual present selection or interpretation, preserving any known intent without inventing a strategic desired meaning. Neither confession nor revelation is required. A familiar meaning can remain unchanged, and the humor can be in the teller's role—the person who always tells it this way, the one who never notices what others do, or the one who carefully repeats a phrase that nobody else remembers using.\n\nPreserve full present private experience separately from the testimony. The participant's live thoughts, doubts, or reactions during the telling need not enter the account itself; if the active narrator has authorized interiority, it can sit alongside the composed testimony. Follow the active response boundary within continuing telling, listening, or private experience as well as an actual pause or conclusion. No corroboration, audience reaction, completed interpretation, or in-world pause is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacamal::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jun Maeda / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse a situated diary with reflective notes as the route. Establish the actual recorder—a person who keeps notes for their own reasons—and the occasion and knowledge available at the time of each entry. Place a shared phrase, preference, or everyday event according to what this recorder would actually write down: some details are mentioned in passing, some are listed, some get a single line years before they become significant. The comedy can come from notation itself—the flat record of something readers recognize as loaded, the recurrence of a phrase the recorder treats as ordinary, or the juxtaposition of a trivial preference with a serious event.\n\nDo not invent deliberate foreshadowing in the recorder's original purpose. Any actual planning or anticipation can still be recorded within the person's knowledge. The recorder need not know which shared detail will later matter; its comic continuity comes from actual recurring life, not planted significance. A phrase can appear again and again without explanation, and the reader's recognition is enough. When an event changes the weight of an old object or routine, the entry can register only what the recorder actually knows or chooses to articulate. Keep any later annotation at its own composition time without rewriting original wording, motives, or knowledge.\n\nPreserve separately authorized live feeling outside the record. The diary's omissions or dates do not erase the character's full private experience during the scenes surrounding the entries. Follow the active response boundary inside continuing writing or thought, after a completed entry, or at an actual pause as circumstances support. Neither a paused recorder, an unfinished artifact, nor a completed insight is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacamam::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jun Maeda / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nChoose polyphonic or nonlinear telling as the route. Attribute each remembered occasion, current account, or recorded moment to its actual source and time, and make the attribution part of the reading: a person now, a person then, a note, an overheard remark. Let a recurring detail—a shared phrase, a habit, a routine—appear in different accounts where the real selections become comparable. The comedy comes from the contrast: one remembers the phrase as affectionate, another as irritating; one gives it three lines, another forgets it entirely. Agreement is allowed; actual disagreement is allowed; no one needs to be lying or proved wrong.\n\nKeep the sources' knowledge separate. A later account may know what an earlier one did not, and the reader should be able to trace that difference. Show how each account is shaped by its speaker's position and need, not merely by a lack of information. The recurring detail may stay funny in one voice and tender in another. Preserve those actual emphases without inventing a definitive verdict, while retaining whatever factual certainty, discovery, or agreement the source and narrator genuinely support.\n\nPreserve full authorized minds outside the accounts. The narrator may render private thought, feeling, or explanatory context within actual permissions. Keep accounts attributable rather than replacing them with an invented shared consciousness or mandatory explanatory verdict. If the response ends before the voices converge or before any changed interpretation arrives, leave the polyphony open; the echo itself is the method, not a puzzle to solve.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaman::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jun Maeda / Light Novel\n\nBegin with a clean entry into the present situation: where the characters are, who is present, and what immediate activity occupies them. Give unfamiliar names or mechanisms their necessary context as the scene unfolds—through permitted narration, purposeful explanation, preparation, observation, or actual use, without requiring a fictional pause to supply context. Then place a real recurring phrase, habit, or routine beside its current reception, letting the familiar detail land through the rhythm of the prose. A first occurrence needs no invented history, and a familiar confirmation can be funny without a surprise or a hidden callback.\n\nUse readable paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response to keep the scene's spatial and emotional changes clear. The comedy can come from owned quirks and blind spots as people pursue their actual aims at the supplied scale: someone always answers the same way, always forgets the same step, or treats a small habit with disproportionate seriousness. Let different characters be witty, literal, dry, exuberant, or uninterested in the joke; no one must perform banter.\n\nPreserve the full private experience of authorized characters, including long or conflicted thought when it belongs to them, without making the narrator that character by default. The boundary may stop in the middle of an ongoing exchange, a half-finished action, or an unanswered question. Do not require a delivered callback, a next action, or a closing hook before ending the response; the reply may end during continuing public or private experience without a natural pause inside the fiction.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacamao::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jun Maeda / Lyrical Prose\n\nBegin with a concrete detail from the scene or a memory the viewpoint character can actually access—a sound a machine makes, the way someone says a particular word, a repeated motion of a hand. Develop that detail across the paragraph through syntax and imagery: vary sentence length to mimic its rhythm, return to its sound or shape, and let it collect associative weight from the situation. Do not force a symbolic meaning; the detail can remain a familiar annoyance or comfort.\n\nPlace it beside a plain present action or statement so the contrast creates comic recognition. If the character has said this word for years, the sudden awareness of its exact sound can be funny while still being fond or irritating. Give the emotional response its actual character: tenderness, exasperation, grief, uncertainty, simple pleasure, or a real mixture. Keep the reason for the shared routine intact rather than requiring conflicted feeling. A callback does not require a new understanding or a final change; the image may simply recur with its original meaning now newly loud.\n\nUse the character's usual voice, not a uniform poetic register. A dry literal person notices the sound differently from an exuberant one. Figurative language alters presentation, not the facts of the world or what other people know. End the response at the active boundary, still inside the association if the scene permits.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacamap::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jun Maeda / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nConnect a present particular to a remembered shared occasion. A current object, phrase, or habit can open the recollection without announcing itself as a flashback. Move through the actual recollection with its relation to the present legible; a return may occur when attention reaches it or remain pending at the active boundary. The two times need no moral or completed comparison.\n\nPreserve the exactness that made the routine funny: the way someone mispronounced a word, a rule about who washed which dish, a repeated argument over the same trivial fact. Describe it with enough concrete detail that its humor remains real in memory, even if the person is gone or the relationship has changed. Do not retroactively invent hidden significance. Preserve the act's actual original meaning, whether ordinary or already significant; present longing or recognition does not rewrite what anyone knew or intended then.\n\nAllow present feeling to include affection, absence, or unchanged attachment as the actual scene supplies. No new loss, improved mood, or wiser ending is required. Reflection does not need to pause for outward action; the response can continue inside the character's thought at the active boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacamaq::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jun Maeda / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse modern memoir as the formal route. The narrative may be a participant's authored recollection, but the active narrator need not be the memoirist. Present the recollection through the viewpoint the scene already owns: if the character recalls a past moment, render the past from their earlier limited knowledge, not from later understanding, unless the memoirist explicitly comments at a later time.\n\nSelect a concrete routine, object, or repeated words from the shared past. Describe what it was like at that time: the expectation before the event, the physical detail that made it funny, the other person's actual contribution. Do not backfill a hidden motive or sacrifice. The act had its own reasons then; the comedy can come from recognizing those reasons exactly.\n\nIf the narrative includes later commentary, place it clearly at a separate time. The comic recognition may be familiar or newly felt, but it does not require a changed verdict, confession, or regret. Keep the character's live private experience separate from the memoir's recorded account. Allow the response to end during the recollection or its composition, without an outward return.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacamar::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jun Maeda / Minimalist Prose\n\nPlace the exact shared detail—the phrase, object, or habitual action—next to the context that makes its meaning inferable. Use direct clauses and a small number of concrete particulars. Provide the amount of prior or present context needed for recognition, cutting repeated history and redundant humor gloss. A prior instance or current response may suffice; relevant history, actual explanatory speech, or directly rendered private recognition remains available at its needed extent.\n\nKeep causal and emotional information that matters. A character's private recognition may be long or uncertain; that thought need not prove immediately useful. The comic effect can come from the plain fact that the same person has done the same thing again, without a twist or an ironic deflation. No secret original motive, changed bond, final callback, or unified closing state is required.\n\nUse the character's actual register. Minimalism is not clipped dialogue for everyone. End at the active boundary, with the situation continuing if the scene warrants.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacamas::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jun Maeda / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite in sustained scene-first prose. Begin inside an ongoing moment of action, speech, or perception, and move naturally among these and the character's authorized memories or thoughts. When a repeated phrase, object, or habit matters, place its earlier and present uses close enough for the recognition to arrive through timing: the same words return in a new physical situation, or a familiar object appears during a different task. Make the recurrence readable without redundant gloss, while preserving actual explanatory speech or inward recognition. Narrative explanation alone does not require a pause inside the fiction.\n\nKeep each character's voice distinct. The comedy comes from their particular habits and blind spots, not from universal banter. Real consequences remain possible: the routine may cause friction, cost money, or strain a relationship, but none of those outcomes is compulsory. The scene's ordinary life continues beyond the joke; a character can be funny while washing dishes or waiting for a repair without a dramatic turn.\n\nAllow the response to stop at the active scene boundary, mid-task, mid-conversation, or inside private thought. No completed emotional arc or outward pause is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacamat::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jun Maeda / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nUse the actual relationship premise supplied by the scene. Every repeated moment should belong to that particular bond: a sibling's habitual correction, a coworker's shared lunch rule, a couple's private nickname, a rival's known trigger. Do not import a generic repertoire of comedy tropes. Build the comic situation from actual shared life and present aims, whether aligned or different. A newly shared act needs no invented earlier routine or private history.\n\nPlace a known routine in its actual timing and context, familiar or changed, without erasing its original meaning. Retrieving the same object during an emergency can be funny because it is the familiarly wrong response, not because a hidden motive is revealed. The characters may share knowledge of the joke or be perfectly unaware of it; preserve their actual points of view. Private feeling—affection, irritation, embarrassment, or amusement—remains distinct from what anyone says.\n\nNo confession, mutual misunderstanding, hidden sacrifice, or status change is required. The bond's promise may be fulfilled through accurate recognition as much as through a change. Follow the active response boundary within continuing public or private experience or an actual conclusion; the relationship may continue, change, or end according to the supplied premise and events.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacamau::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jun Maeda / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nOpen with the cadence of retelling. A compact declaration can establish an actual remembered act at its supplied scale: a grand exploit or a particular repeated habit can both acquire comic proportion through precise retelling. Attribute the memory or reputation to its real teller or community rather than inventing shared familiarity. Then expand into the actual particulars of one occasion, so the reputation is shown through lived detail rather than asserted as legend.\n\nUse recurring phrasing so a later occurrence recalls the earlier one. When the same act happens again, the repeated words can carry comic recognition while the act itself remains sincere. The person may take the habit seriously; the joke belongs to the gap between the deed's real value and its retold proportion, not to a forced humiliation. Let the teller's account stay distinct from the focal character's private feeling: being remembered for a habit is not the same as experiencing it.\n\nPreserve the act's actual meaning, including any already understood significance, without requiring it to have been ordinary or secretly important. No oath, quest, tragic aftereffect, or newly assigned reputation is required. The account can end after the deed is told, without resolving what the community will finally make of it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacamav::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jun Maeda / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the domestic realism route of Scandinavian literary tradition: the textured, socially grounded account of ordinary household and community life. Comedy arises from the precise way a familiar possession, routine, or recognized preference meets the present circumstances of its actual use. Show the arrangement of the place—its rooms, schedules, objects, and recurring tasks—so that a shared phrase or habitual way of doing something can carry a new comic weight or the recognizable exactness of an unchanged familiar fit. Let competent coordination be as funny as difference: the parties may manage the same task smoothly in their own accustomed ways, or their separate methods may butt against each other without hidden resentment. Make the reasons behind a preference or routine legible where actual narrator and character knowledge permits, without inventing unavailable motives: a person’s comfort, practicality, affection, or simply long-established habit. Do not manufacture a new chore, family secret, emotional cost, or changed bond around every attempt. Private feeling may be rendered directly and at length; the scene can end while a public or inward action is still under way, without completing the exchange.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacamaw::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jun Maeda / Screenplay\n\nWrite in screenplay form: scene headings to signal changes of place or time, concise action lines, clear character names before dialogue, and a cut only when the supported scene needs it. Let a familiar sound, object, or action reappear in its actual practical context, familiar or changed, where its earlier use still matters. The comedy comes from the visible or audible construction: a character returns to a task with the same careful routine they always use, while the current circumstances make its precise familiar or newly apparent fit legible. Do not push every return toward a mishap or reaction shot. A held competent action can be comic on its own. Keep required private interiority as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation; it is not automatically dialogue or another character's knowledge; retain actual prior knowledge, communication, world channels, and permitted mental access. A voice-over is a distinct authorized device and not a default substitute for thought. The response may end during continuing action or attributed private experience without a callback being delivered or the scene being resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacamax::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jun Maeda / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable stage scene with clear speaker names, entrances, exits, and blocking that actors can perform. Let a repeated phrase, object, or routine reappear in the physical business of the scene so that its old use and its present use stand side by side. The comedy comes from the audience-visible construction: a character enters with the same item or says the same line at a moment when its actual practical weight is recognizable, whether familiar or different. Do not force a new prop, stumble, or confession to make the callback significant. Keep the full private experience of authorized characters as clearly attributed nonspoken material under the active ownership and narrator permissions; asides and monologues need a dramatic purpose within the staging and are not a default means of revealing thought. The response may end during continuing business, speech, or thought, without a completed exchange or a pause that invites a turn.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacamay::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jun Maeda / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite through a single authorized mind as its perceptions, memories, private wording, bodily sensations, and present concerns move together. Let a familiar phrase, object, or routine return where associative attention naturally takes it; show the distinction between the earlier occasion, a possible fantasy, and immediate sensation. The humor can come from the precise recurrence of shared life inside the mind’s traffic: something quotidian keeps surfacing beside bigger worries or sensory input, without requiring a hidden tragic revelation or changed belief; actual discoveries and emotional developments remain possible. Distinguish underlying events from actual fallible recollection, fantasy, and present perception rather than making all scene facts indeterminate. Render the mind's full feeling and uncertainty without requiring comic or practical usefulness. Prose length alone does not determine fictional elapsed time; actual attention, waiting, and events govern the duration of each moment. The response may end during continuing thought, perception, or unfinished private speech, without announcing a conclusion or returning outward.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacamaz::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jun Maeda / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the scene as a sender-attributed text or chat transcript. Use only the messages, timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the current exchange. Let each participant's actual digital habit shape their register: one may write long, another in fragments, another with careful punctuation or none. A shared phrase, earlier message, or known preference can reappear through the ordinary work of composing and sending. The comic effect comes from the actual sending and uptake—the reader sees the known phrase in its actual current context, with familiar or newly apparent weight—without requiring a typo, awkward silence, misreading, or a changed bond. Keep an unsent draft distinct from what is transmitted; keep a private thought distinct from what is sent or received. The response may end during ongoing composing or private feeling without a transmitted answer or a read receipt.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacamba::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jun Maeda / Visual Novel\n\nWrite in visual-novel presentation: concise scene-setting, clear dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response suited to an illustrated scene. A supported object, background, or expression cue can locate a familiar phrase or present action beside an expectation, so that accurate familiar reception is as available as surprise. The cue does not prove hidden motive or transmit thought merely by appearing. Let a repeated routine or shared phrase return within the flexible focal prose: the viewpoint notices it at a moment when its old use and its current situation stand together. Do not force a changed interpretation, a new menu, or a delivered reaction before the response boundary. Keep private feeling extensive when warranted, and let the scene end during continuing speech, action, or thought without a completed exchange.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacambb::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jun Maeda / Web Serial\n\nWrite each reply as a continuing installment in a story where prior details remain available. When an earlier phrase, object, or routine becomes relevant again, recover only the exact wording or particular fact needed to follow the current activity. Carry its actual use, knowledge, and any supported change forward; do not rewrite it as a secret plan or forgotten fact to create a twist. Familiar repetition can carry comic recognition through its precisely expected fit or a genuinely new practical weight; neither changed meaning nor changed use is required. Keep private passages beyond plot utility; do not manufacture forced new meaning or a completed setup-payoff pair. A real change in memory or understanding remains possible if supported by what has already happened. The response may end during continuing public or private experience as well as at a real conclusion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacanaa::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nBuild a comic scene around someone pursuing a recognizable aim that comes with an actual emotional cost, whether it arises now or belongs to established history. Start by making the request's practical terms legible: what the person needs or wants, what conditions would make it possible, and what rules, resources, or habits apply. Use the Kamachi profile's clarity to state those terms without letting them shrink the underlying hurt; the pain can remain real even when a possible answer exists.\n\nWhen a character speaks or acts, let the actual response meet, ignore, or redirect the request according to that person's own concern; literal compliance is one possibility, not a required reply. A literal answer, exact compliance, or a known routine can land as dry comic contrast because it meets a need that has become tangled with difficulty. No hidden misunderstanding, failed sacrifice, or repaired feeling is required for a comic turn; actual outcomes remain those the people and situation support. Render actual private tension and any effects it has, allowing conduct to remain unchanged: someone may accept help but not relax, or make a practical offer without asking for emotional access. Keep actual nonspoken experience distinct from spoken words whether their content aligns or differs; privacy need not be deliberate concealment.\n\nMaintain comic structure through timing rather than comic temperament. Setup, reversal, escalation, or callback can arise from an unused option, an inconvenient rule, or an ordinary aim run against its own internal cost. Make enough of the actual relation legible for comic timing while preserving the narrator's access; reader and character knowledge may coincide or differ. Do not require quip exchanges or forced wit; a character who says less than they feel can be funnier than one who performs. Preserve the active narrator's access to private experience and do not let physical stakes, embarrassment, or a sharp reply become a requirement.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacanab::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the response as a coherent shared interval that continues the current exchange under the active chat contract. Continue the actual scene from the submitted material without an out-of-fiction acknowledgment, then move the scene through clear attributed dialogue and readable paragraphs. Keep the practical setup understandable: if a rule, resource, or condition matters, make it legible where the actual sequence and available knowledge call for it, before, during, or after use, including private consideration or non-use. Do not hide information behind a later thought unless the narrator's access requires it.\n\nUse comic momentum from the interaction itself. A literal answer, a known method successfully applied, or a character's particular habit can create timing without a compulsory mistake. If a speaker is naturally dry, let the silence after a line do work; if a response is exuberant, make the wording specific to that person. Do not make every exchange a punchline or invent a problem just to keep banter going. A task need not be resolved or even concluded in one reply; the active boundary may fall during continuing speech, action, or thought before any beat lands.\n\nFollow the role contract for private experience. If a character's unspoken thought belongs in the raw response per the active settings, write it as ordinary prose with clear attribution and do not transfer it automatically to other characters through reader access; their actual sources determine what they know. If the contract hides some interiority, wrap it in the supplied container exactly. Do not replace private reasoning with a monologue or a typed message. Let each participant's actual knowledge and aims stay distinct, and leave a natural continuation point without forcing a question or cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacanac::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nChoose the social and satirical realism route within Chinese literary traditions. Ground the scene in an actual household, work, or public arrangement, and make its norms legible through ordinary use rather than through an intrusive narrator. Introduce a character's request or need by showing what the arrangement normally allows, what conditions matter, and what resources or rules apply. Let actual social positions inform particular readings, which may differ or agree: one person may treat a rule as binding, another as negotiable, another as beside the point.\n\nBuild comedy from the arrangement's literal operation. A norm can fit perfectly, fail to cover a case, or be applied with unexpected precision; show that relation directly without manufacturing obstruction or bureaucratic excess. When a character complies exactly or takes a rule more seriously than its makers, the humor comes from the gap between their aim and the arrangement's terms. Do not mock anyone for lacking knowledge or for being practical; let their choices make sense from inside their position. Keep relevant interactions of rules, resources, and aims legible where they matter, without reciting the whole arrangement at every step.\n\nLet the narration stay concrete and situated. Use specific household or workplace details, repeated exchanges, and the actual consequences of a decision. Private experience remains available according to the active narrator, but it need not announce a moral. A callback can reuse an earlier phrase or object when its meaning has changed. Preserve character agency and the world's established facts; satire should arise from the scene's own mechanics, not from imported irony.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacanad::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the comic scene with concrete, physically specific sentences and compressed transitions. Establish the aim and the material conditions early: what the character wants, what equipment, space, time, or rule matters, and what has already been tried. Use short active clauses to show the interaction between a resource and a limitation, and let the Kamachi profile's legible mechanism remain visible. Preserve actual failure or success without distortion, using explanation where needed to understand causes and any consequences. An attempted action may remain unfinished without a delivered result.\n\nMake the comic beat land through rhythm and contrast. When a correct use, literal reply, or supported failure occurs, place it clearly in the actual causal sequence, often after a buildup of physical detail. Change paragraph length to adjust pressure: a long sentence can detail a plan's execution, then a short one can deliver the outcome. Let abrasive or rough speech stay grounded in its speaker; do not make every character hard-edged. Humor can come from deadpan matter-of-factness about something small or from the gap between a plan and its actual materials.\n\nRender the person's actual private experience, whether concrete, abstract, or plainly emotional. Objects, conditions, and remembered costs may give reasoning or feeling particulars where present, but no physical marker or practical calculation is required. You may expand a thought without producing an immediate action, without requiring progress or a changed understanding to justify it. Maintain the active narrator's access and avoid adding gratuitous damage or danger. The style's force belongs to the telling, not to a required mood.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacanae::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the comic sequence through actual documents, each with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Choose the document form from the scene's needs: a request, authorization, report, note, or acknowledgment can carry different obligations. Do not rotate through formats; use records that belong to the actual sequence, including parallel or independently prompted documents without requiring the latest record to cause the next. Show the writer's situation in what they include or leave out, and keep each person's voice distinct.\n\nMake the humor from the documents' actual terms. A proposal may be precise, a report literal, an acknowledgment incomplete, or a rule applied with surprising exactness; later or parallel records can reveal the gap between what someone wrote and what someone else understood or did. Do not require every document to be mistaken or every writer to be unreliable. A callback can restate a phrase with changed meaning, but the comedy should come from the juxtaposition of aims, without requiring deliberate miscommunication; actual joking, concealment, or clear communication follows the particular writer's intent.\n\nWhere the active contract permits private experience, render it in the active Visible or Hidden nonspoken presentation, clearly separated from the documents; a memo is not a character's live mind. You may use gaps, revisions, or conflicting accounts to advance the scene, but keep them inferable. Let the document sequence follow actual composition and reception. A record may confirm known terms, remain unread, or leave a response pending without a required knowledge change or obligation. Preserve world facts and character agency; a request may remain unresolved at the end of an entry.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacanaf::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite a scene in which someone attempts a feasible kindness through its actual conditions: timing, permission, resource use, or the specific terms that make it welcome. Make the relevant rule or routine legible where actual context calls for it, allowing explanation before, during, or after the gesture without changing the earlier facts. Use the Kamachi profile's clarity about practical interactions: a gift can be the right object given at the wrong moment, a meal can be prepared according to a familiar habit, or an offer can be literal and correct without being what the recipient needed first.\n\nLet comedy arise from the fittedness of help, not from humiliation. Exact successful help, a literal answer, or a familiar routine can serve as a gentle callback when the recipient's preference is known. The person helped may enjoy the care, ask for something different, or stay preoccupied with their own task; do not require gratitude, corrected care, or emotional repair. Keep different characters' habits distinct so that one person's comfort does not become everyone's comfort.\n\nSpend attention on chosen details: a particular food, a repeated phrase, a shared space, a quiet preference. Let the prose stay warm but specific, with room for ordinary friction and privacy. If private experience is available under the active contract, show a character's unspoken feeling without making them announce it. The scene can end on a small pleasure or an unresolved practical thread; there is no obligation to solve an emotional problem.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacanag::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite the scene in sustained novel prose with deliberate paragraph movement. Open with the social or institutional context that frames the character's aim: show the standing arrangement, its history, or the expectations that make a request consequential. Use narrative distance as the narrator's access permits, but keep the actual practical mechanism legible. State relevant rules, resources, or conditions in precise particulars rather than in abstract summary.\n\nBuild comedy through proportion. After establishing the serious frame, let a plain reply, literal compliance, or continuing condition meet it where the sequence supports contrast. The humor can come from the weight of the formal context pressing on a small exact exchange, without making the exchange meaningless or turning the character into a fool. Keep direct speech natural against the formal narration; different characters may speak with different registers. Do not force every conversation into elaborate or ceremonial language.\n\nMaintain full private experience and ordinary feelings. The narrator may judge or reflect where authorized, but private pain, amusement, or calculation should remain available to the reader as the character's own. Render the actual private experience at its warranted extent rather than substituting a single reported inference. Preserve actual stakes and an unfinished arc: a scene may close on an unresolved obligation or a quiet aftermath. Use callbacks and recurring details to organize the sequence, but do not turn the formal frame into a lecture.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacanah::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nEstablish the telling as an actual social occasion, including ordinary company or entertainment without a required intervention: name the teller, the listeners, and the reason this account is being given now. The tale may report, recall, or openly invent a specific situation involving a rule, resource, permission, or task. Reports and memories follow their actual sources and available knowledge; invented material remains the teller's fiction rather than a fact added to the surrounding world. Preserve the teller's and audience's actual knowledge, which may coincide or differ without a required gap.\n\nBuild the comic sequence through the actual relation between the account's terms and the audience's live reception, including familiar agreement or an unexpected reading. A listener may ask a literal question about timing, access, or sequence; another may recognize a familiar habit; a third may simply keep listening while the teller's emphasis reveals a private aim. Let these responses interrupt, redirect, or extend the tale without requiring the teller to be wrong or the audience to be corrected.\n\nWhen the tale explains a mechanism, show how a character inside it understood or misunderstood the mechanism at that moment. Distinguish what the teller knew then from what the teller knows now, and let questions, statements, or continued listening take their actual place without requiring anyone to expose a difference. An unfinished attempt, a considered non-use, or a successful routine can all carry comic weight; none is mandatory.\n\nUse recurrence to connect the narrative levels. A phrase, object, or question from the outer scene can return inside the tale, or a detail from the tale can change how a listener responds next. The comedy lands when the audience's reception meets the account's actual conditions, not when the teller is humiliated or the tale is dismissed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacanai::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Kamachi Kazuma / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse modernist and absurdist construction through an actual rule, procedure, or condition from the supplied world and follow its ordered logic through a character's practical aim. The procedure may be formal, domestic, technical, or customary; it must be real within the scene, not imported as bureaucratic decoration.\n\nConstruct the comedy by tracking the procedure's terms as they are used, discussed, or considered. Show a character complying exactly, discovering a valid exception, or realizing that the procedure works as promised but at an unexpected scale. The disproportion must be traceable to the actual conditions—the rule's wording, the resource's limit, the character's habit—not to random incompetence or manufactured obstruction.\n\nPreserve what each character actually understands; their knowledge may overlap completely, differ, or remain partial. One may know the rule's history, another its practical cost, another only its current instruction. Their partial knowledge can produce genuine help, actual errors, or unfinished consideration without forcing a resolution. Keep actual dependencies legible, including parallel inputs or independent conditions rather than requiring every step to follow the immediately previous term.\n\nKeep the narrator's distance and syntax consistent with this modernist and absurdist construction. The comedy comes from the procedure's internal logic meeting a human aim, rather than substituting an unsupported label for its operation; an authorized narrator may still comment on what the actual sequence makes clear.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacanaj::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Gothic-Punk\n\nMake the old authority tangible through a supplied room, role, object, privilege, or obligation. Describe its material condition—worn fabric, locked access, inherited procedure—where those conditions matter to reception, without a fixed requirement to describe them before any comic line or act. Do not import vampire mechanics, secret powers, or institutional omnipotence.\n\nExplain the actual condition of access or permission where relevant to the encounter, including consideration, instruction, confirmed terms, or non-use. Show who can enter, what the object does, or what the obligation costs in practical terms. Let a literal response or practical accommodation meet that ceremonial weight: a character may ask for the obvious thing, use the inherited privilege for a small need, or treat the solemn object as a tool.\n\nPreserve the established legitimacy or fraud without forcing an unmasking. A character's private desire can remain private while their public mask stays intact, or the mask can fall away in trusted company. The comedy comes from the collision between the authority's weight and the concrete need, without requiring ridicule or incompetence; preserve any actual flaws, competence, or absurdity the supplied situation establishes.\n\nKeep the emotional texture intimate. Morally complicated intimacy means a character can want something from the person who holds the authority, or resent the obligation while still using it. Render any actual strain and its effects where they occur, allowing conversation and conduct to remain unchanged without requiring a confession, threat, or power contest.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacanak::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse modern urban or popular narration as a craft route through linked encounters in the actual supplied setting, whether urban or otherwise. Each encounter follows the actual people, place, and use of the recurring phrase, permission, or resource, which may remain the same or change. The link is the practical continuity—what remains available, what each person knows, what the history brings back—not a forced journey objective.\n\nTrack the resource or rule across encounters. Show how a character carries it, spends it, misunderstands it, or uses it successfully. Let a familiar successful routine recur without a new obstacle at every stop. Literal compliance can be comic when it meets a different expectation; a callback can land because the reader recognizes the phrase or object from an earlier encounter.\n\nPreserve each person's actual knowledge. One character may know the rule's origin, another its current cost, another only its result. Their actual reception can sustain comedy through shared recognition or differences, without requiring anyone to be foolish or differently informed. The narrator can distinguish what each person knows from what the reader has learned across the linked encounters.\n\nKeep the sequence readable and grounded in the supplied community. The comedy comes from the circulation of a practical condition through different lives, not from exoticizing the setting or manufacturing a moral lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacanal::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse early-modern comic/theatrical telling as a prose method, not a script format. Establish recognizable aims within the narrator's access and let actual words, positions, and objects carry the public sequence. The reader should be able to see who is where and what they are doing without stage directions.\n\nBuild timing through the relation between delivery and physical business. A repeated line can change meaning when the speaker's position, object, or audience changes. A pause can hold the moment while a character notices something concrete. A precise use of a resource—a tool, a permission, a gesture—can create the comic turn without requiring a prop to be used simply because it is present.\n\nPreserve individual speech and stillness. Different characters can be witty, literal, dry, exuberant, or uninterested in the joke. A character may remain silent while the public sequence continues, and their private experience remains available as nonspoken interiority under the active contract. Do not make every private aim publicly spoken.\n\nLet the theatrical method organize selection and emphasis. The narrator can dwell on a physical detail that matters, pass quickly over an entrance, or return to an object that will matter later. The comedy comes from the legible relation between what is said and what is done, not from a forced performance style or a mandatory laugh.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacanam::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse polyphonic and nonlinear telling to present the same practical interaction through different participants' accounts. Attribute each account by person and time, preserving what that person knew and aimed for at that moment. The shared event remains the same; the accounts retain each participant's actual emphasis, priorities, and knowledge, whether these agree or differ.\n\nOrder the accounts for juxtaposition, not chronology. A later account can appear before an earlier one; a minor participant's view can precede the main actor's. The comedy comes from the reader seeing exact agreement, different priorities, or an actual misunderstanding across the accounts. Do not falsify the shared event to create the contrast.\n\nDistinguish narrator synthesis from participant knowledge. The narrator may show what the reader can now infer, but each account must remain limited to its owner's knowledge at that time. A character cannot retroactively know what another account reveals.\n\nLet the comparison remain unfinished across replies. No minimum account count is required; the sequence can stop after a meaningful juxtaposition and continue later. The practical interaction—its rules, resources, and immediate problems—stays legible through the different views, and comic recognition may arise from shared knowledge or a difference in perspective without requiring every participant to miss what the reader sees.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacanan::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly: establish the character's actual aim at its supplied scale, the place, and the other people present. Use clear attribution for dialogue and keep spatial changes easy to follow. Do not assume a young cast, first person, or escalation toward catastrophe.\n\nExplain an unfamiliar mechanism or term where relevant to the actual encounter, including consideration, instruction, or informed non-use. Make relevant conditions concrete enough to follow actual consideration, use, or non-use: what the rule permits, what the resource costs, and what the person thinks it will do. Place explanation where actual knowledge and the scene call for it, without requiring an ensuing action or inventing a delayed reveal.\n\nBuild the comic sequence from actual responses, continuing conditions, and any physical changes that occur. A literal answer can land because the reader understands the mechanism; an effective method can be comic when it meets the aim at an unexpected scale. Follow any actual consequence or continuing state, without inventing breakage, a delivered result, a reaction, or a discovery to complete the interval.\n\nLet inner experience remain long or unfinished. A character may reason through the mechanism, notice a detail, or feel the gap between expectation and result. The comedy comes from the legible interaction between the practical terms and the actual response, not from a fixed reaction slot or a compulsory explanation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacanao::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet the prose extend an image from the scene's actual material: the way a borrowed object is held, the sound of a repeated instruction, the particular light on a room where a practical problem is being worked out. Develop the image through actual attention, syntax, and recurring sound; a real explanation, reply, or continuation may meet it before, during, or after that development. No completed image or reply is owed at the boundary. The contrast does the comic work: the image need not model the mechanism, fail, or resolve the feeling.\n\nWhen a character explains a rule, resource, or condition, keep the explanation legible in its own terms. The narration may linger on an associated image before or after the explanation, but the physical cause and the private experience remain distinct. A character can notice that a remembered phrase sounds grander than the actual errand, or that a familiar object has accumulated private associations beyond its current practical use, without the narration insisting on the joke.\n\nUse the image as a point of return. A phrase or sensory detail introduced during one practical exchange can recur when the same condition appears again, carrying its earlier feeling into the new context. The recurrence may produce a register shift, a dry callback, or a moment of unexpected tenderness; it need not resolve the situation or announce a lesson. Preserve full private experience under the active narrator's permissions, and do not append a sensory coda after every explanation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacanap::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nWhen a present practical situation touches a memory, preserve the earlier aim, the means actually available then, and what the character understood at that time. Do not let the present improve the past. The remembered arrangement may have worked, half-worked, or been left unused; the narration can hold that earlier understanding in place while a concrete particular from the present calls it back.\n\nLet the return happen through a specific object, phrase, or repeated condition rather than a general announcement of loss. A character may remember the exact wording of an old instruction while performing a similar task now, or notice that a resource once treated as permanent has become scarce. The recognition can be dry, rueful, or quietly pleased. The arrangement may still work or may have changed through real events; either can support the callback without requiring a new loss or a retrospective correction.\n\nKeep the practical sequence legible. Explain the current rule, resource, or condition when it matters to the actual encounter, and let the remembered version sit beside it. Full private feeling can persist independently of a practical answer or visible act. The scene may end with the task unfinished, the memory still present, and no emotional repair required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacanaq::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a modern memoir route with the participant and the later teller clearly distinguished under the active narrator's permissions. The participant recounts a relevant rule, resource, or agreement as actually understood and used or left unused at the time. The later teller may confirm or qualify its significance, but the earlier understanding remains the participant's own and is not retroactively improved.\n\nLet the comic timing arise from the relation between the two positions. The participant may have treated a condition as absolute when it was negotiable, or as trivial when it carried lasting consequences. The later teller can note the discrepancy without humiliation or confession: a plain statement that the arrangement still works, or that it never did, can land more dryly than an extended judgment. Required actual private experience for participant or later teller stays in its active nonspoken presentation at either authorized time, distinct from the composed account.\n\nPreserve actual outcomes. The memoir may recount a task completed, abandoned, or continued under changed terms; the later frame does not require a corrected favor, a new understanding, or an emotional payoff. Keep the sequence of events legible through the participant's account, and give the later teller's actual commentary its warranted extent while keeping its relation to the earlier scene legible, without a fixed brevity requirement.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacanar::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect the exact details a reader needs to follow the actual aim and the relevant conditions that permit or limit it: what the character wants, what is available, what rule or habit applies. Use direct clauses and omit redundant explanation rather than actual causes or feeling. A plain reply or a literal use of the available means can create dry timing without every reader inference needing an immediate test.\n\nDo not reduce the mind to useful reasoning. Required private experience keeps its warranted extent: a character may hesitate, resent, hope, or notice an implication that never reaches speech. The narration can state that private content in economical terms without explaining its significance. A setup or question can remain unfinished; the scene need not end with a completed test or a resolved misunderstanding.\n\nLet omission leave something inferable. If a character does not use a resource, the narration may show the resource present and the character's attention moving past it, rather than explaining the non-use. Keep cast voices individual: one speaker may be literal, another evasive, another uninterested in the joke. The comedy comes from what is selected and placed side by side, not from performed fragments or a quota of short sentences.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacanas::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the scene first, moving naturally among activity, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized private experience. When a rule, resource, or condition matters to the actual encounter, explain it in the terms the characters actually use: what it permits, what it costs, and what the person involved understands or misunderstands. The explanation belongs to the scene, not to a detached summary.\n\nLet the people's aims and responses create contrast or recurrence. One character may treat a condition as a fixed limit while another treats it as a starting point; the narration can hold both positions without declaring one correct. A phrase, gesture, or practical arrangement may return later in a changed context, carrying its earlier meaning without requiring a new problem or emotional turn.\n\nCompetence, agreement, and unchanged circumstances remain possible. A task may be completed without a test, a request may be granted without a misunderstanding, and a relationship may continue without a confession. Preserve the active narrator's access and the full private experience of the focal character. The scene can end with business concluded or resumed, leaving the comic recognition available rather than announcing it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacanat::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nWork from the supplied premise, participants, and history. Select a phrase, permission, preference, or shared resource that already belongs to this particular relationship, and let its actual use meet a real response. The response may be fitting help, known terms discussed for reassurance, or a familiar private language used in a new practical context. Do not measure the bond by efficiency, gratitude, a new understanding, or an emotional payoff.\n\nA callback may remain familiar and accurate. When a repeated phrase or arrangement returns, the narration can show that the relevant participants recognize it without requiring a correction or a deepened meaning. The comedy comes from the fit between what they know about each other and what the present situation actually requires. If the premise includes rivalry or difficulty, preserve that friction; the scene need not resolve it into comfort.\n\nKeep private feeling available under the active narrator's permissions. A character may be amused, exasperated, reassured, or uncertain without announcing it. The relationship's promise is fulfilled through scene selection and repeated moments, not through a roster of familiar tropes. The scene can end with the arrangement still in motion and no emotional turn required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacanau::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account a sense of retelling through deliberate openings and compact declarations around an established deed, promise, or attributed memory. Then expand into what people actually knew, used, and did. The weight of the retelling meets the plain means: a grand phrase may introduce an errand, a remembered oath may turn out to have been fulfilled with borrowed tools, and the narration can let that contrast stand without diminishing the act.\n\nLet phrasing recur through actual remembrance or use, preserving the same application or developing another where the scene supports it. A designation earned by an actual deed may return in a different context, with its actual relation to the reputation preserved rather than a required smaller scale. Dialogue can agree with the chronicle, answer an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern, or confirm a familiar designation without changing its application. The comedy comes from the relation between the remembered account and the actual sequence, not from mock combat or heroic doom.\n\nPreserve the act's real scale and any pending effort. The subject of an oft-told incident may still be working, still waiting, or still owed something. Keep shared reputation distinct from a focal character's private account; being remembered as dependable does not mean that helping was effortless or automatic. The account can end with business concluded or resumed, leaving another modest act available to be remembered rather than declaring it legend on the community's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacanav::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nUse essayistic narration from the active narrator's actual position: open with a situated claim the narrator can make from access already granted, not a universal thesis. The claim should concern how a rule, resource, habit, or practical arrangement works in the world. Bring it beside a relevant actual, remembered, or clearly hypothetical exchange whose conditions remain legible: who needs what, what they try, what they receive, and what they know at the time. Let the case confirm, qualify, or refute the claim through the interaction itself rather than through a moral summary.\n\nExplain a mechanism when the case turns on it. If a character exploits or misunderstands a rule, show the relevant actual understanding, attempt, or non-use, with consequences only when they occur. Possibility is not a delivered result, and the case may remain unfinished. The comedy comes from the gap between the claim's scope and the particular case: a method that works only under an unstated condition, a resource used for the wrong scale of problem, or a habit that produces a perfectly reasonable but unintended continuation. Do not require the narrator to be wiser than the participants; the case may leave the claim partly true.\n\nKeep private feeling available as more than evidence. A participant's embarrassment, relief, irritation, or satisfaction can stand beside the practical sequence without being converted into a lesson. The narrator may return to the opening claim with a narrowed or expanded scope, but no completed test, fixed count, or new proposition is owed per reply. Preserve the active narrator's person, knowledge, and output contract.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacanaw::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Screenplay\n\nUse concise action blocks and clear speaker cues to establish the public conditions a practical aim depends on: the object in reach, the rule posted on the wall, the door already open, the person who heard the earlier line. Scene headings mark real changes of location or time, not emotional shifts. Let a literal line, a held interval, or a cut place an expectation beside its actual continuation. The comedy can come from the audience seeing the setup before the character does, or from a response that is perfectly reasonable under the visible conditions but not under the character's plan.\n\nExplain a relevant mechanism through supported visible or audible context, including instruction or discussion before use. A possible action need not be chosen; show an actual attempt, informed non-use, or a resource's relevant condition without requiring a demonstration. Do not invent an accident or forced reaction to produce the turn. A cut can join two occasions and let the second scene reveal what the first prepared, but an unused resource or unobserved motive need not receive a public demonstration.\n\nPreserve required actual private experience, including aims, reasoning, and feeling, in the active nonspoken form, clearly attributed and distinct from voice-over. A character's understanding may remain separate from what another character sees or hears. Keep editing separate from physical causes: a cut does not make an event happen. The scene can stop after a result, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment without a cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacanax::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Stage Play\n\nUse actual speakers, entrances, exits, positions, props, and physical business to make the public exchange legible. Make the public exchange playable through actual positions, actions, and words, while an aim may remain known only through authorized private experience rather than being performed aloud. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the comedy. A plain response, a stillness, or a familiar use of an object can land the beat without a quip.\n\nExplain a condition through supported speech or events where relevant. A character may state a rule they have reason to know, or an offstage fact may be established by an entrance, a carried object, or a line that another character can confirm. Do not turn a prop into a proof merely because it is present. Spatial continuity matters: who can see an action, hear a line, or move between two places should remain clear enough that the scene's practical question has timing.\n\nPreserve required private experience separately from asides and monologues. An aside is performed speech with its own audience and permissions; it does not replace the active nonspoken interiority form. Another character does not know a private aim merely because the reader does. The interval may remain unfinished: the scene can stop after a result, an interruption, or a quiet acknowledgment without a staged demonstration or a completed answer.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacanay::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Stream of Consciousness\n\nBraid perception, remembered words, bodily feeling, imagined outcomes, and recurring concern through the permitted mind's actual attention. Where a practical question actually occupies the mind, let it develop in that person's own order, while other thought or feeling may remain unrelated: a rule half-remembered, a resource noticed then lost to a memory, a plan that loops without reaching a clean conclusion. Keep the mind's model distinct from world conditions and from any action actually taken. The comedy can come from the gap between the inner explanation and what the world then supplies.\n\nShow the mechanism as the mind encounters it, not as a finished lecture. If a character exploits or misunderstands a rule, let the relevant step surface through association, correction, or bodily recognition, distinguishing any actual ensuing action or speech from a possibility that remains unperformed. No outward result is required. A literal response or familiar fact can meet the expectation only as received: the other person's actual words, the object's actual condition, the door's actual state. Thought may remain lengthy, irrelevant, or unfinished without a public payoff.\n\nKeep enough orientation to distinguish events from recollection or fantasy. Perception anchors the present; memory and imagined outcomes may interrupt but should not be mistaken for what happened. Preserve the authorized mind's person, knowledge, and output contract. No completed plan, spoken instruction, or public demonstration is owed per reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacanaz::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nUse sender-attributed messages with only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the exchange. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one writes in fragments, another in complete sentences, one leaves a draft unsent, another replies before reading the whole thread. The comedy can come from the exact transmitted terms meeting the actual receipt: a proposal read as an instruction, an acknowledgment that does not mean agreement, a resource described precisely but understood differently.\n\nDistinguish proposal, transmission, reception, understanding, and acceptance where the difference matters to an aim. Show the message as sent, the receipt as received, and the response as actually made. A literal reply or familiar timing can provide a callback without requiring a typo, delay, or misunderstanding. If a rule or resource is explained in the exchange, keep the explanation in the sender's voice and preserve the recipient's actual reception, prior knowledge, aims, and circumstances when they choose to use, ignore, or reconsider it.\n\nKeep genuine drafts and live thought distinct from transmitted text. An unsent draft is genuinely composed interface text, distinct from the writer's live private thought. Preserve required private experience in the active nonspoken form, clearly separated from the transcript, and preserve visibility according to both actual narrator access and interface capabilities. Context outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the messages. The exchange can stop after a result, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment without a cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacanba::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Visual Novel\n\nUse concise scene-setting and clear dialogue attribution to establish actual conditions and public words. A background or expression cue can orient the moment: the object already on the table, the expression that changes when a rule is mentioned, the pause before a reply. Where a cue draws authorized attention, render the person's full interpretation or feeling rather than reducing it to a fixed reaction portrait. The comedy can come from the focal expectation meeting the actual continuation at its real pace.\n\nExplain a mechanism where it is relevant within the active narrator's knowledge. Keep actual possibility, the character's awareness, and any chosen attempt distinct; the person may notice, miss, consider, use, or decline an option without a required result. Describe available resources within the actual viewpoint. Accurate reading, competent use, and unfinished private response remain possible. A visible cue does not prove its cause or private meaning; another character's expression is not a truth indicator.\n\nFollow the actual continuation after the focal response. The next exchange may support the interpretation, complicate it, or reveal that the speaker was concerned with something else entirely. Do not transfer private thought automatically through reader access; preserve what other characters actually know through established sources or capabilities. The style does not activate branching menus, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands. Preserve whichever authorship contract is active. The scene can stop after a result, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment without a choice screen or a completed demonstration.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacanbb::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across replies: what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover relevant earlier conditions briefly when needed, preserving actual outcomes, available resources, and who knows what. A known rule, resource, or phrase can sustain comic continuity without requiring a new loophole, misunderstanding, or fix at every return. The comedy can come from a familiar method meeting a new situation, or from a known condition producing a different result because the circumstances changed.\n\nExplain a mechanism when the installment turns on it. If a rule or resource is reused, make the relevant conditions legible and preserve whatever attempt, continuing use, or actual result occurs, without requiring a result in this installment. Repetition may confirm an effective method, alter its use, or leave a question pending according to the scene. Do not erase a supported earlier outcome to make a new installment falsely surprising; new evidence can qualify an old understanding without replacing the old event.\n\nLet ordinary life continue around the arrangement. Required private experience may continue without advancing the practical problem, and a character's understanding may remain separate from what another character knows. No escalating problem or finished joke is owed per installment. The installment can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Serial duration should give selected facts room to acquire meaning, not make a fair continuation perpetually recede.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaoaa::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Li Bihua / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nAnchor the scene in a precise physical particular: the rim of a cup someone is afraid to ask for, the exact weight of a kept object in a pocket, the remembered smell of a doorway. Let that particular carry the character's actual need in its spoken or private channel, without requiring a public explanation or a fixed order of thought and speech. Comic scale can arise from the relation between the thing and the asking, including exact, considerate fit or a supported mismatch: someone rehearses a sentence over a trivial favor, or treats a borrowed scarf as evidence of a decision they have not made. Do not require the other person to notice or reject the disproportion; their ordinary reply can land heavily enough.\n\nRender the private cost in the character's own idiom. The narration may follow the body's attention to the thing itself while the mind circles the harder admission: the hand that does not reach, the sentence that becomes a joke at the last moment, the warmth that makes leaving harder. Let the other person's actual kindness remain kind. Care may succeed, fail or remain pending according to the actual people. Its exact reception can carry comic timing without making the character unable to accept it, or requiring emotional relief when they do. Keep the interval inside that experience: the scene may end with the cup still untouched, the sentence still unsaid, or the favor accepted with a feeling that has not caught up to the act. Do not force a confession, a repaired feeling, or a completed joke before the boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaoab::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Li Bihua / Chat Roleplay\n\nOpen by grounding the current submitted action in the shared space: where the characters are, what they are handling, and what each is actually doing or pursuing within the active access, without requiring a visible signal of every aim. Use unmistakable attribution for speech, but let the object carry part of the exchange. A character may answer while turning a cup, smoothing a sleeve, or holding a package too long. Comic timing can come from the actual relation between an object's state and a participant's valuation, including accurate shared importance or a supported gap: one person treats a common item as precious, another handles a precious item carelessly, and neither has to announce the mismatch.\n\nResolve each participant's action within the active ownership contract. The narration may render one character's full private sensation and thought without reader exposure itself communicating that experience to another character; each retains actual prior knowledge, independent sources and permitted access as well as what they currently observe. Do not make the cast share a single desire or a single joke. An NPC may cooperate sincerely, pursue an unrelated aim, or remain indifferent to the object's importance; their purposes stay their own. Keep the interval coherent and readable without requiring a question, a mood shift, or a completed reception at the end. The scene may stop with the object still in someone's hand and the next move still open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaoac::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Li Bihua / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the scene as intimate urban or domestic narration: a particular room, street, stairway, or shared table where the characters' lives press close enough for small preferences to become visible. Let the arrangement of space do narrative work. A prized object placed where everyone must pass it, a remembered corner now used for storage, or a seat kept empty for someone who has not arrived can organize who encounters whom and what they notice. Do not invent a family, city, rival, or failed courtesy to supply this proximity; use the setting and cast actually present.\n\nFollow actual attention to the same sensuous particular, allowing different or shared valuations without opening an excluded mind for symmetry. One person may remember the exact grain of a table or the scent of a particular tea, while another knows only that it is in the way, or that it was expensive, or that it must be cleaned. The comedy comes from these sourced valuations meeting in ordinary handling: the remembered thing is moved, borrowed, wiped down, or mistaken for something else. Preserve whatever accuracy, uncertainty or error each valuation actually has, without requiring a discrepancy or granting truth by narrative juxtaposition. The narration may stay close to one character's private feeling while the other's practical use continues. End at the active boundary, including within continuing private experience or ordinary activity, with no required recognition, reconciliation, or completed joke.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaoad::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Li Bihua / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nUse forceful verbs and exact material particulars where the scene supplies them. Write the actual work of handling: the weight shifting in a bag, the cost of the fare, the stain spreading under a careless thumb, the time it takes to wrap something properly. Do not compress away the character's reasoning or sensation. A long private thought about what the object means can sit inside the hard rhythm; the contrast is the method, not a quota of short sentences.\n\nPlace the attachment beside the handling without letting either cancel the other. A character may carry a fragile keepsake through a crowded station and know exactly what it cost to obtain, while the scene reports the jostling, the heat, and the practical problem of getting it home. Comic scale comes from the object's actual relation to handling: a valued thing may be awkward or unexpectedly easy to carry, a beautiful weight may be managed exactly, or a familiar use may continue beside its remembered importance. No burden or leakage must be invented. Do not require injury, breakage, rough speech, or loss to give the juxtaposition force. The scene may end with the thing intact, the attachment unchanged, and the practical work still unfinished.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaoae::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Li Bihua / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through actual documents, each with a known writer, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Let the comic timing come from what each writer chooses to preserve. A letter may spend three sentences on the texture of a ribbon and one on the news it was meant to carry. A later note may treat the same ribbon as a practical problem: where it was left, whether it can be returned, what it cost to replace. Two accurate records may preserve different aspects of the same gift or place, and precise selection, repetition or a supported difference can give comic timing. No reply, concealed motive, contradictory account or discrepancy is required.\n\nKeep the document's knowledge and selection distinct from the writer's full mind. A composed description of a remembered room is not the same as the live sensation of standing in it; a report of an offer is not the same as the private hesitation that accompanied it. Where the active contract requires nonspoken interiority, render it in the active channel as actual fictional private experience, not as another document. Let the records carry the story's continuity without becoming a substitute for authorized thought or sensation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaoaf::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Li Bihua / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nChoose an actual offering, small pleasure, or remembered preference without inventing familiarity. Make the sensory detail particular enough to be funny through its scale: a whole afternoon spent finding the exact temperature of a broth, a borrowed jacket returned with the right smell still in the lining, a seat saved because someone once mentioned the light there. Let the care be sincere and the detail precise. The comedy is in the proportion, not in the feeling being false.\n\nRender the recipient's actual response, including accurate acceptance. They may be pleased, mildly embarrassed, indifferent, or unable to enjoy the thing for reasons of their own. Do not require gratitude, jealousy, hidden pain, or a bitter ending. Where enjoyment is present, write it directly: the warmth, the taste, the ease of being looked after. Keep personality and privacy intact. The scene may end with the care still in progress, the pleasure partial, or the recipient simply taking the thing as it is.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaoag::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Li Bihua / Formal Literary Novel\n\nBegin with the present encounter in precise sensory terms: the particular glaze on a dish, the exact weight of a door, the smell of a room that has been kept closed. Then widen deliberately through the context the chosen narrator actually possesses: who owned the thing, what it was used for, what public occasion or private history it belonged to, what it cost to keep. Let the widening give the present detail its social scale. The comedy comes from the return: the same dish is now being used for leftovers, the same door is stuck, the same room is being measured for a new tenant. The history does not have to be exposed or debunked; the proportion is enough.\n\nKeep narrator judgment source-owned and ordinary speech individual. A character may speak plainly about the thing while the narration carries the fuller history; or the narrator may judge, but only where the active contract permits. Do not make every speaker share the narrator's register. Full private sensation and thought may remain underway before the return or the comic relation is complete. The scene may end with the present detail still in hand and the widened context still settling around it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaoah::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Li Bihua / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nEstablish the teller, the listeners, and the present reason for the telling before the embedded account begins. The reason may be entertainment, delay, persuasion, company, or a practical request; it does not need to be a life-or-death bargain. Let the outer scene remain active during the telling: a listener may interrupt with a question, handle an object, misunderstand a detail, or continue a task.\n\nInside the embedded story, let the teller select remembered sensory particulars according to their own attachment and emphasis. A person may recall the exact weight of a gift, the smell of a room, the cost of a garment, or the way a companion touched an object. The teller's selection is evidence of their attention, not proof that the event happened as described. A listener may recognize the same object accurately and still care about a different practical detail, such as where it is now or what it cost to keep.\n\nUse the actual relation of narrative emphasis and listener attention to build comic timing, including shared appreciation or a difference of scale. The teller lingers on the sensual importance of a thing or place while the listener pursues an ordinary aim: getting home, settling an account, finding a seat, or confirming a time. The comic pressure comes from the live occasion, not from making the teller foolish or the memory false. The remembered attachment can remain genuinely felt whether listeners share its importance, attend to another scale or have not yet responded. Their reception is not wrong merely because its emphasis differs.\n\nKeep the active narrator separate from the storyteller when the supplied point of view distinguishes them. The narrator may report what the teller performs and what the listeners do, but cannot silently enter the teller's full private mind unless the authorship contract grants that access. Distinguish what the teller remembers now from what the original participants knew then, preserving any actual continuity as well as later learning. Later learning cannot become an earlier motive. Render full authorized private experience on the permitted levels in its selected nonspoken channel; the performed account does not replace it.\n\nA repeated phrase or object may return across the telling when it helps the reader connect the levels. It need not appear at every difficulty. The response may end while the telling, a listener's reaction, or the outer occasion is still underway; no changed audience, completed revelation, or laugh cue is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaoai::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Li Bihua / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse documentary and historical-memory prose as the definite route. When a character recalls a beautiful room, garment, meal, or public place, set that memory beside a source-marked trace from another time: a register entry, a bill, a photograph caption, a repair record, a letter, or the present condition of the object itself. Identify the source and what it can actually establish. The trace is not an automatic correction; it is a second account with its own limits.\n\nBuild the comic scale from the relation between the two. The memory may preserve the feel of silk, the shine of a floor, or the abundance of a table, while the record shows the cost, the seating plan, the date, or the repeated repair. Precise agreement can be as funny as discrepancy: the remembered glamour was real, and the record confirms that it required an unglamorous arrangement. A selective emphasis is not a lie. A real discrepancy may reveal a blind spot without exposing a fraud.\n\nPreserve the remembered attachment as it actually was and follow any present continuity or change without requiring disillusionment or unchanged affection. The character may still love the place or object after seeing the record. The comedy lies in the proportion between private feeling and documented fact, without requiring humiliation or disillusionment. Preserve the character's present aim as it continues, pauses or legitimately changes through the comparison: they may still want to buy, restore, visit, or defend the thing.\n\nDo not make wear inevitable. The object need not be ruined, the place need not have declined, and the later source need not be more truthful than the memory. Later knowledge cannot become an earlier motive. Preserve the actual private experience of the focal character beyond what the document can show; the document is a source, not a window into a mind.\n\nThe response may end while the comparison is still incomplete. The character need not reach a verdict, accept the record, or change their plan before the boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaoaj::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Li Bihua / Gothic-Punk\n\nLet the supplied place carry its actual inherited weight: a maintained surface, a ceremonial object, a familiar threshold, a rule about who may enter or touch. Use the material texture of the place to make that weight present: the temperature of a room, the polish on a rail, the smell of old wood or fresh flowers, the sound of a lock. Do not add supernatural rules or secret powers merely for this atmosphere; preserve the actual world and separately authorized developments.\n\nPlace that attributed gravity beside the character's present ordinary concern. A person may want to borrow a chair, find a bathroom, deliver a package, ask for a signature, or meet someone for lunch. The comedy comes from the actual scale of the arrangements: the old authority demands a particular handling, while the present need is small, urgent, or practical. The character can proceed competently. They need not be awed into silence, corrupted, or seduced.\n\nPreserve the real attachment the character may feel. A beautiful room can still be beautiful while the character worries about the time. A ceremonial object can be genuinely moving while the character tries not to smudge it. The glamorous surface sharpens rather than hides the uncomfortable or funny relationship between the place's demands and the person's aim.\n\nAtmosphere influences experience but does not prove motive. A character's hesitation, reverence or irritation is their own response; the cue alone does not certify guilt or desire. Let any interpretation follow actual context, sources and narrator permissions. Preserve full authorized private experience, while other characters retain their actual prior knowledge and permitted access rather than receiving it merely through reader disclosure.\n\nThe response may end while the practical business is still underway. No completed task, changed plan, or revealed secret is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaoak::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Li Bihua / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse lyric inward narration as the definite route. Follow one authorized mind as an actual texture, scent, contact, or taste draws a remembered person or place into relation with the present. Develop the association through syntax and return: a clause may open from the present sensation into the remembered occasion, then come back to the room, the hand, the waiting task. Let the sentence carry the movement rather than announcing it.\n\nKeep the remembered attachment precise. The focal person may recall the exact way a person held a cup, the particular smell of a doorway, the weight of a bracelet, or the color of a room at a certain hour. The feeling can be warm, divided, jealous, certain, or unresolved. Do not require divided attachment or a confession.\n\nLet a plain present circumstance sit beside the intense private significance. The focal person may be standing in a queue, folding laundry, waiting for a bus, or listening to a relative complain. The comedy comes from the scale: inward significance can meet an exact ordinary circumstance whether the thought is plain or developed, with public reception following actual communication rather than a requirement that no one acknowledge it. The plain circumstance does not refute the feeling; it simply has a different proportion.\n\nPreserve the actual scope of the authorized mind. Preserve the active narrator independently of the focal person. Reader exposure to the person's inwardness does not itself inform another character, whose actual prior knowledge, sources and permitted access remain available. A remembered person is not present merely because they are recalled. Keep earlier knowledge separate from later learning.\n\nThe response may end while the association is still unfolding or the outer task is still incomplete. No changed image, confession, or practical result is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaoal::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Li Bihua / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nChoose early-modern comic/theatrical telling as a portable narrated form, not a script and not a required period setting. Establish a clear narrated occasion: who is present, what business brings them together, and what object or gesture is being presented or handled. Use speaker-attributed lines for public speech, and keep the teller's framing voice distinct from the characters' lines.\n\nLet the physical business carry the comic timing. A character may present a gift with ceremonious care while the recipient tries to read a label, keep a sleeve out of a dish, or remember where to put it. The narration can dwell on the exact handling: the angle of a tray, the pause before a lid is lifted, the sound of paper, the weight of a cup. The comedy comes from the relation between the ceremonious presentation and the exact use or reception, not from making every character witty.\n\nPreserve the teller's knowledge and the active narrator's role separately where the supplied point of view distinguishes them. The teller may frame the scene, but cannot report another character's full private mind unless the contract grants access. Full nonspoken experience remains separate from performed speech; a character's public line may differ from their private attention for a supported reason.\n\nThe occasion can remain unfinished. The response may end before the object is accepted, the business is settled, or anyone laughs. No laugh cue, reversal, or completed exchange is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaoam::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Li Bihua / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nChoose polyphonic nonlinear telling as the definite route. Arrange actual permitted accounts around an object, gift, or remembered occasion, and mark each source and time clearly. A section may move from a letter written years ago to a present conversation, from one person's recollection to another person's practical note, or from a public record to a private memory. The reader should always know whose account is speaking and when it was composed or remembered.\n\nLet sensory particulars connect the accounts. One voice may remember the exact smell of a courtyard, the weight of a ring, or the shine of a table. Another voice, at another time, may remember the same object as too heavy, too bright, or inconvenient. The comedy comes from the juxtaposition of scales: an earlier valuation sits beside a later use, or simultaneous experiences of the same occasion carry different priorities. No voice is automatically mistaken. The agreements can be accurate; the differences can be matters of attention and circumstance.\n\nPreserve the actual scope of each mind. A letter or testimony contains what its author records, not their complete live thought. A document is not a full private channel; render actual authorized nonspoken sensation, feeling and thought at their needed extent beyond what those records select. Do not grant one voice access to another's interiority unless the contract permits it. Keep earlier knowledge separate from later learning.\n\nThe response may end with the arrangement still partial. No completed set of voices, changed judgment, or collective verdict is required in one reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaoan::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Li Bihua / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Orient the people, the space, and the current aim in the opening movement: who is present, what they are trying to do, and what object or custom is about to matter. Introduce an unfamiliar object or custom through supported observation, speech, or narrative context when the reader needs it. Avoid an unrelated glossary inserted merely to explain the style; relevant context or actual discussion may take its needed extent.\n\nKeep spatial changes easy to follow. When the action moves from a doorway to a table, from a shop counter to a back room, or from one speaker to another, make the movement legible with the actual spatial context needed, without a sentence-count quota. Use paragraph breaks around actual speaker changes, action beats, and shifts of private attention. Do not force a fixed reaction cycle after every line.\n\nLet a precise sensory cue carry the comic scale. A character may admire the exact finish of a box while the other person worries about the delivery fee. A dish may be described through its color, heat, and smell while someone tries to eat it without offending the cook. The pleasure or preference can be accurate; the comedy comes from the proportion between the attractive presentation and the actual reception or practical concern. No spill, collision, or startled face is required.\n\nPreserve the supplied narrator person and the actual ages and knowledge of the cast. Full private experience remains available according to the active contract, and what one character feels is not automatically known to another. The setup or reception may continue across the response boundary without a completed joke or resolved exchange.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaoao::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Li Bihua / Lyrical Prose\n\nBegin with a concrete source-owned image from the present scene: an actual surface, smell, taste, texture, or remembered place within the viewpoint character's actual attention or the permitted narrator's own association, with that source clear. Develop that image through rhythm, sound, and recurrence across the paragraph, letting the syntax itself follow the pull of the attraction. If a phrase or sensory note returns, let its weight follow actual attention and context: it may remain steady, recur for timing or take on a supported difference without requiring the scene to move forward.\n\nSet a plain detail, ordinary action, or direct utterance beside the developed image where the sequence supports it. The contrast does the comic work: a person may describe the glaze of a pear in lingering cadence while someone else asks whether it will fit in the bag. The plain element need not puncture the feeling. It can simply occupy a different scale, and the gap between the two scales is the joke.\n\nKeep the narrator's figurative register distinct from cast speech. A character may speak flatly, practically, or with their own kind of eloquence while the narration sustains the lyrical development. Direct explanation remains available where it serves the scene; do not force every comic beat into an image. Preserve actual private feeling at full extent. The attraction, jealousy, divided attachment, or remembered intimacy can remain serious even when the surrounding moment is funny. The response may end during continued attention, speech, or activity without a completed image, deflation, new association, or final valuation owed at the boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaoap::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Li Bihua / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nConnect an actual present particular to a sourced earlier occasion, keeping the distinction between then and now clear. The connection may be a sensory detail, a place, an object, or a repeated gesture. Let the narrator or viewpoint character notice what remains attached to the memory while the present occasion remains recoverable, whether activity continues, pauses or awaits action: the same bowl now holds keys instead of lotus seeds, the same street is crossed for a different errand.\n\nLet current use supply comic proportion without exposing the remembered value as false. A former ceremonial item may now serve an ordinary need, and the comedy lies in the coexistence: the character handles the object with remembered care while someone else treats it as a convenient container. Preserve what the object meant at the remembered time, while allowing its actual present importance to continue, change or remain uncertain rather than requiring either intact attachment or disillusionment. Present pleasure is also available; the scene need not mourn.\n\nPreserve private reflection at its required extent. The character may think about the earlier occasion, the person attached to it, or the small irreversible change that has occurred, without announcing a lesson or resolving the attachment. Do not invent loss, regret, jealousy, or a final insight to justify remembering. A newly introduced recollection is available when active authorship permits it and existing history remains intact; it need not have been mentioned earlier. The response may end during continued attention or activity, with the past and present still held together.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaoaq::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Li Bihua / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the scene as a modern memoir, a deliberate retrospective composition by the actual narrator or memoirist as supplied. Locate the act and purpose of composition: the memoirist is writing now, for a particular reason, and the reader encounters the scene through that written account. Preserve the narrator and memoirist roles exactly as the active authorship contract provides.\n\nA remembered sensory detail can place the earlier value of an object or encounter beside what the present writer chooses to record. The comedy comes from the memoirist's exact selection and current scale of attention: the writer lingers on the scent of jasmine in a courtyard, then records that the courtyard was also where the family argued about a parking space. The glamour of the remembered detail is not false; it simply shares the page with a smaller, more ordinary fact.\n\nMark the layers of time clearly. Distinguish what the memoirist knew then, what they understand later, and what they choose to write now. Candid, accurate self-knowledge is as available as uncertainty; the writer may admit they no longer know why a detail mattered so much, or may record that it still matters. Composition does not replace full private experience. The memoirist's written account is a composed artifact, not automatically their live thought, and required interiority must still be rendered in its active presentation. The memoir may remain unfinished at the boundary without confession, exile, betrayal, or a changed attachment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaoar::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Li Bihua / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect a small number of exact sensory details that keep the object, participants, and present aim legible. A detail should carry weight: the weight of a lacquered box, the particular smell of a closed room, the sound a lid makes. Do not add framing or explanation around a detail that already does its work.\n\nPlace the felt value beside the actual word, use, or reception. Let the juxtaposition carry comic proportion, with relevant explanation or source-owned commentary available where it contributes rather than repeats the relation. A character may handle a silk scarf with visible care while another asks if it can be used to tie up a leaking pipe. The narration records both facts in direct clauses and lets the gap stand. Do not require a damaged surface or concealed motive; the scarf may be perfectly fine and the question perfectly practical.\n\nUse direct clauses and omit duplicated explanation, but do not omit causes or private extent. Meaningful sensation, feeling, and thought may be sustained at whatever length the character's actual experience requires. Speakers remain individual: one may be terse, another effusive, another indifferent. The account may end during continued attention or activity, without an ironic final line or a newly revealed cost.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaoas::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Li Bihua / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the scene in sustained novel prose, moving naturally among action, speech, perception, recollection, and authorized inward experience. Keep the current occasion recoverable at every point: the reader should know where the characters are, what they are doing, and what aim is underway.\n\nLet an attractive object, contact, or place matter through a person's actual attention rather than requiring ownership of the thing. The viewpoint character may notice the particular coolness of a jade bangle on someone else's wrist, the grain of a table they have known for years, or the smell of a shop they pass. Then relate that importance to the real handling or reception in actual order: the bangle is removed to wash dishes, the table is covered with a plastic cloth for a child's homework, the shop turns out to sell plumbing supplies.\n\nDifferent valuations need not become jealousy or conflict. One character may find a thing beautiful while another finds it useful, and the scene can hold both responses without forcing a confrontation. Shared pleasure can be comic through exact timing: two people may admire the same object for entirely different reasons, and the narration records both without comment. Full thought and ongoing activity may continue at the boundary without a new option or changed meaning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaoat::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Li Bihua / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nSelect an occasion from the supplied relationship premise, preserving its actual participants rather than imposing a pair or a roster of familiar tropes. The scene should make the established bond legible through what the characters do, notice, and say to each other in this particular moment.\n\nAn object, scent, or place can recur where someone truly associates it with the bond. The association must follow each person's actual knowledge: one character may recognize the perfume as the one worn on a particular evening, while the other simply thinks it smells nice. The comedy comes from the specific reception: the remembered gesture is offered, received, ignored, or misunderstood according to who each person is. Recognition is not automatic, and a shared memory need not be shared in the same way.\n\nGive affection, rivalry, comfort, or divided feeling their needed extent only where they belong in the supplied premise. Accurate shared pleasure and a direct refusal may both fit; do not impose mandatory jealousy, public acknowledgment, or changed relationship status. The response may end during continued interaction, with the bond still in motion and no payoff forced at the boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaoau::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Li Bihua / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account a sense of retelling through deliberate openings, compact declarations, and recurring phrasing. Establish a person, deed, gift, or exchange in the cadence of something already told: a name, phrase or designation available from actual prior telling or the permitted narrator's current framing, without inventing community consensus or previous repetition. The declaration should come from an actual deed or way of speaking, not an ornamental epithet assigned to every entrance.\n\nSet the remembered estimate beside exact texture, weight, or handling. The comedy lies in the relation between the elevated designation and the sensuous particulars. A gift remembered as magnificent may be described in its actual weight, the way its clasp sticks, the particular smell of the paper it was wrapped in. The deed need not be grand, and the praise need not be false. Their actual relation can carry comic timing through precise fit, sustained emphasis or a supported difference of scale; no discrepancy must be invented.\n\nKeep earlier motive, later account, and actual private experience distinct. What the person meant at the time, what the retelling says, and what the focal character privately feels may all diverge. Return to a phrase where actual emphasis, familiar repetition or a supported new application warrants it, preserving stable meaning without requiring a new use. The response may end during continued activity or telling, with no completed deed or final valuation owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaoav::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Li Bihua / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the scene as social and domestic realism: a particular shared arrangement is kept going by actual people with different habits, and the comedy comes from the exact relation between its valued appearance and the work or preference that sustains it. Organize the route around present shared activity, while actual memory or reflection retains its needed extent rather than being barred to distinguish the form.\n\nMake the arrangement concrete through its material terms. Make actual responsibilities and activity legible through the available context: who sets it out, cleans, carries, repairs, serves or declines it. Do not require a new public demonstration merely to establish the arrangement. Let handling and any authorized private account show the person's actual priority, which may differ or be shared: one may treat the beautiful object as a task to finish, another as a pleasure to prolong, another as an inconvenience to avoid. None of these responses needs to be exposed as false. Competent maintenance, fair cooperation, and sincere enjoyment can carry comic timing as effectively as a mismatch, because the comedy lies in the scale of attention the arrangement actually receives.\n\nUse Li Bihua's sensory precision to make the valued surface present without making it dominate the scene. A glaze, a fabric, a remembered scent, or the weight of a dish can be rendered exactly, then placed beside the practical fact of who is carrying it or what it costs to keep. The glamour should sharpen the ordinary life around it rather than float above it. If a participant feels attachment, jealousy, or divided desire, render that feeling in its authorized private channel, with any actual effect on handling kept contingent rather than required, not as a diagnosis the scene must announce.\n\nKeep each participant's own priorities intact. A person can cooperate fully while privately wanting to leave, or enjoy the arrangement while noticing its expense. Do not impose a shared valuation or force a reversal in which the admirer is proved wrong. The arrangement may remain unchanged at the end of the reply, with its ordinary activity still underway. Stop at the active scene boundary even if someone is mid-task or mid-thought; no completed result, altered bond, or closing joke is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaoaw::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Li Bihua / Screenplay\n\nWrite in concise action blocks with clear speaker cues, and use scene headings only when location or time actually changes. Let the visible or audible sequence support comic construction alongside any authorized private experience: an attractive object is presented, handled, protected, or used in a way that gives its valued surface an exact ordinary scale.\n\nPlace the item's appearance and its actual use in the same scene or in a supported cut. An action line can describe the sheen of a dish, the weight of a box, or the careful way a sleeve is folded, then show the same object being carried through a doorway, set down on a cluttered table, or opened for a practical reason. Let the juxtaposition come from real handling rather than from a reaction shot that tells the audience what to feel. A cut may move from preparation to use, or from one person's care to another's indifference, when the temporal relation remains factual and the selection itself makes the comic proportion legible.\n\nKeep dialogue in the speaker's own register. One character may speak about the object's beauty or history while another answers with a practical question about where it goes or whether it is clean. Neither response needs to be corrected. The humor can come from the accuracy of the two concerns existing side by side, not from one speaker being exposed as shallow or the other as blind.\n\nPreserve required private sensation and thought in the active nonspoken channel rather than converting it to voice-over. A character's memory of a place, their pleasure in a texture, or their jealousy over who else touches the object should be written as full private experience in its proper channel, not performed as narration for the audience. Do not let a close-up or held shot function as proof of that interior state. The scene may end during continuing action, speech, or private thought without a mishap, a completed payoff, or a reaction shot that resolves the moment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaoax::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Li Bihua / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable script with clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can actually be staged. The comedy comes from the physical business around an attractive object: how people set it down, protect it, admire it, pass it, or work around it in a shared space.\n\nLet the object's sensory qualities appear through playable business rather than camera directions. A character can test the weight of a bowl, smooth a cloth, hold a bottle up to the light, or move a chair to keep a display in view. Stage directions should record what an audience can see and what the actors can do, not what a camera would frame. The valued surface becomes comic through its actual handling: one person treats the object as precious, another stacks papers on it, another carries it with practiced efficiency. Accurate coordination is as available as a mishap supported by the actual people and circumstances; the humor lies in the scale of attention the object receives.\n\nKeep dialogue in each speaker's own voice and let it sit beside the business. A line about the object's beauty or its remembered origin can be followed by a practical instruction about where it should go or a pause while someone wipes a mark from it. The relationship between the words and the hands should carry the scene. Do not make every speaker witty or turn the object into a prop that must be dropped or broken to create a laugh.\n\nPreserve required private experience as full nonspoken authoring material in its active presentation. A performed aside or monologue has its own audience and does not replace a character's actual private thought or sensation. If a character feels attachment, jealousy, or divided desire, write that interiority in its proper channel rather than disguising it as a line another character can hear. The scene may end at the active boundary even during speech, handling, or thought, without a completed result or a closing beat that resolves the attachment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaoay::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Li Bihua / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite through one authorized mind, letting perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern braid together by actual association. The comedy comes from the reach of a particular attachment: a surface, scent, or remembered place can touch an earlier occasion or an ordinary present concern at a scale the mind itself does not need to announce as funny.\n\nFollow the syntax where the association actually goes. A character may notice the cold edge of a dish and find themselves back in a kitchen they have not entered for years, then return to the present because someone asks where the cloth is. Let the movement be traceable: the sensory particular can open an association, and attention may return through its own course or an actual sound, word, bodily need or present event; no external interruption or completed return is required. Do not force every association into jealousy or loss. A beautiful surface can lead to a practical worry, a remembered pleasure, or an unspoken comparison without the mind spiraling toward suspicion.\n\nKeep the modes of attention distinct. Mark what is present perception, what is remembered, what is imagined, and what is spoken aloud or only thought. Keep the current physical situation distinct from remembered experience: a person may still hold or handle an object while attending to a recollection. Preserve separately authorized access without merging minds; only the chosen mind's associations structure the passage, and other characters' private experience remains outside it unless separately permitted.\n\nLet thought remain steady or unfinished. The prose length of an association does not itself determine fictional duration; actual attention and events may take time without producing an outward decision, and a brief interruption does not need to change the character's direction. The reply may stop during continuing attention, a half-formed association, or an ordinary present action without a completed realization, altered feeling, or closing joke.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaoaz::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Li Bihua / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the story as sender-attributed messages with only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that actually matter to the exchange. The comedy comes from the relation between what a writer puts into a message and what the recipient actually takes up in reply.\n\nLet each person's digital habits shape their contribution. One character may describe a texture, a color, or a remembered place at length; another may answer the practical question buried in the same message, or recognize the sensory detail exactly, or ask about something else entirely. None of these responses needs to be a misreading. The comic scale lies in the difference between the composed message's weight and the reply's actual focus, not in proving one person shallow or the other excessive.\n\nKeep the status of every text clear. Distinguish a composed message from a sent one, a sent message from a received one, and a received message from what the recipient actually understands. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought. Describe an attachment only as description unless the world actually contains a sent file; do not fabricate an image or receipt to add subtext. Select actual delays, edits or receipts where they contribute to the exchange, its timing or the person's attention; they need not change knowledge or available action.\n\nPreserve full private sensation and thought in the active nonspoken channel, clearly separated from the transcript. A character's jealousy, pleasure, or memory of a place belongs to their private experience and is not transmitted text. The reply may end during an ongoing exchange, an unsent draft, or a wait for a response, without a completed reply, a changed relationship, or a closing joke.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaoba::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Li Bihua / Visual Novel\n\nWrite in concise scene-setting with readable dialogue attribution and focal narration or inner response suited to an illustrated scene. Choose a background, object, or expression reference from the actual scene and use it to anchor the comic relation between an item's felt value and its actual reception.\n\nLet the cue be purposeful rather than decorative. A chipped glaze, a too-bright display, a hand hovering near a dish, or a familiar doorway can orient the moment and give the valued surface an exact ordinary scale. The focal prose should register the cue through the viewpoint's actual attention: what they notice, any actual association, and any action they actually take rather than a next move being owed. Accurate pleasure and continued attention are as available as discrepancy. The cue does not need to expose a hidden feeling or certify desire.\n\nKeep the rhythm flexible. A cue may open the scene, return during dialogue, or stay in the background while the exchange moves forward. Do not impose a fixed cycle of cue, thought, and reply. Let the dialogue remain in each speaker's own voice, with one character's attachment to the object sitting beside another's practical or indifferent response. The comedy comes from that coexistence, not from a reaction portrait that tells the reader what to conclude.\n\nPreserve full private experience in its actual order and in the active nonspoken channel. A character's memory, jealousy, or sensory pleasure belongs to their interiority, not to an expression label or a line another character can hear. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, generated images or executable engine commands merely because of this style; separately supplied authorship and feature permissions remain unchanged. The scene may end during continuing dialogue, focal attention, or private thought without a final reaction or a completed payoff.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaobb::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Li Bihua / Web Serial\n\nWrite the installment as part of a readable through-line: what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. A continuing sensory attachment can give returning objects and places comic timing without resetting their established significance.\n\nRecover actual prior context only when it helps the ongoing occasion. If a valued item or remembered place returns, bring back the specific detail that matters now: the weight of the bowl, the way the light fell in the room, the last person who handled the object. Do not repeat the whole history merely as automatic recap; an actual recollection or account can still take its needed extent, including a complete telling when authorized. Preserve what participants already know; a character does not need to rediscover the object's importance or rehearse its origin for the reader.\n\nLet the return produce a precise practical use, an unchanged affection, or a supported new scale. The comedy can come from the object being treated with the same care in a situation that now makes that care disproportionate, or from the attachment persisting without drama while ordinary life continues around it. Do not require a fresh jealous doubt or a new complication each time the object appears. Setup, handling, and full private experience may span replies, with the current installment carrying only the part that belongs to its scene.\n\nKeep the boundary natural to the active scene. The reply may end during ordinary activity, a partially completed task, or continuing private thought without a completed result, an altered bond, or a closing joke. The through-line remains available to continue because the attachment, the arrangement, and the participants' actual priorities are still present, not because the installment has manufactured a cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacapaa::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Lu Xun / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nBuild the scene around a specific request or withheld need that already matters to the viewpoint character. Give the need its actual context in clean, unsentimental prose: an earlier occasion, a belief about reasonable conduct or the exact present wording may explain its cost where supported, without requiring all three or inventing a prior event. Do not translate the need into a general confession of pain.\n\nWhen another character or the character's own habit invokes what is usual, proper, or considerate, place that accepted explanation beside the actual request. The comic timing can come from a literal answer that satisfies the stated rule while missing the need, from a familiar understanding that arrives too easily, or from a supported mismatch between the character's careful phrasing and what the other person actually hears. Keep any fit or discrepancy particular to this exchange rather than turning every reply into a quip.\n\nDevelop the private feeling in plain language. Direct pain, embarrassment, relief, or resentment may be present in full nonspoken interiority without requiring the character to act differently or the scene to end in repair. A partial or awkward comfort may follow, or the request may remain open. Do not invent shame, cowardice, failed care, or an unresolved bond to justify the tone. Preserve the active narrator, character ownership, and world facts; the emotional cost shapes the comedy but does not force a humiliating answer or a tragic conclusion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacapab::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Lu Xun / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the shared interval in readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution and clear spatial movement. Let each participant pursue a recognizable aim through their own habits. When someone invokes what is usual, proper, or expected, show the actual use that follows in the exchange: an exact cooperation, a familiar repetition, or a practical application of the rule that fits one person's aim better than another's. Use the relation between accepted explanation and actual conduct for comic timing without requiring a revealed hypocrite or a punchline; any real discovery keeps its supported place.\n\nKeep each NPC's aim and understanding independent. One person may accept the usual explanation sincerely while another applies it to a different need; the narrator may notice the fit without every character sharing that knowledge. Preserve the active narrator, player ownership, and the full nonspoken private experience available under the authorship contract. Private thought is actual fictional interiority, not a hidden note to the player.\n\nFollow the active scene boundary within continuing public or private life. The response may end during ongoing speech, action, thought, or an unfinished setup. Do not require a question, cliffhanger, completed comic response, or changed plan to close the reply. A private interval or an unanswered request can remain open without stalling supported action.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacapac::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Lu Xun / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and satirical realism route. Choose a situated arrangement in the supplied community: a rule of fairness, courtesy, ordinary work, or proper conduct that people can state in plain terms. Let the narration observe the particular terms by which that arrangement actually operates. Show who performs which part and what they accept as normal, including what the stated rule covers or actually leaves open. Clean observation and attributable speech make the relation available for comic recognition without requiring an exposure or explanatory lecture.\n\nBuild the comic recognition from the relation between the accepted account and the actual operation. The fit may be exact and still funny, as when a courtesy is performed with perfect form and no practical effect. Or the discrepancy may be real, as when a fair procedure distributes work or blame unevenly while everyone continues to call it fair. Let agreement and disagreement both remain possible. Do not invent exploitation, a public unmasking, or a collective verdict.\n\nPreserve actual help, motives, and costs. A character may cooperate sincerely, benefit from the arrangement, or suffer a small humiliation without becoming a fixed type. Use the language, period, and community already supplied rather than importing regional scenery. Keep the active narrator and full nonspoken interiority available; the social account does not replace private experience.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacapad::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Lu Xun / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nUse forceful diction and compressed transitions to make the actual contact, effort, or condition of the scene concrete. When a character or an accepted account calls something simple, dignified, routine, or well managed, place that claim against the exact operation it describes. The comic force comes from rhythm and scale: a short claim followed by the particular weight, sequence, or resistance of the real task, or a long official phrase meeting one plain material fact.\n\nLet the fit or discrepancy arise from the operation itself. The work may be exactly as routine as claimed and still comic through the precision of the detail. The person may be competent, tired, amused, or indifferent. Do not add wear, failure, or injury for texture, and do not turn every physical detail into a symbol of social damage.\n\nKeep full thought and feeling as actual nonspoken experience. A character's reasoning, resentment, or private recognition may be developed in clean language without becoming a clipped emotional label or a physical tell. Vary the rhythm when a longer thought is needed. Preserve the active narrator, world facts, and character ownership; the compressed style shapes the telling, not the stakes.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacapae::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Lu Xun / Epistolary / Documents\n\nUse the document form the scene actually requires: a letter, report, journal entry, notice, annotation, or other record with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Let each document's knowledge and concealment follow its author's situation. When a document uses an ordinary formulation of what is proper, fair, or expected, place it beside what another record or annotation really takes up. The comedy can come from accurate compliance, an apt literal application, or a courteous form that performs one practical job while another record shows the actual result.\n\nArrange the sequence so the reader can compare records without an omniscient explanation after each one. Gaps, revisions, and conflicting accounts may reveal events, but keep original text and actual prior access distinct. A later annotation does not change what the earlier writer knew. Do not invent a contradictory file, hidden conspiracy, or correspondence endpoint to supply the comic turn.\n\nPreserve the active narrator and full live private experience beyond the artifacts. A document contains what its author records, not the complete mind of the writer. Required nonspoken interiority remains actual fictional private experience in the active presentation, clearly separated from the documents. The response may end during composition, circulation, or an unfinished exchange without requiring a completed correspondence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacapaf::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Lu Xun / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend attention on the exact care and pleasure people choose in the supplied situation. Show the particular preference, the actual attempt to serve it and its reception, including ease or small enjoyment where it occurs; appreciation and success are not guaranteed. Let the narration observe these details in clean, plain language without declaring everyone good or foolish. Affection may be sincere without requiring a confession or emotional repair.\n\nWhen a solemn rule of hospitality, a modest habit, or an accepted social expectation enters the scene, place it beside a person's actual preference. The comic relation can be apt, as when a formal courtesy happens to deliver exactly the comfort someone wanted, or surprising, as when a well-meant custom meets a preference its performer did not anticipate. Let the fit or mismatch remain particular to these people rather than turning the scene into a lesson about the institution.\n\nPreserve disagreement, sincerity, unappreciated effort, and developed private feeling where the actual story permits. Comfort needs no gratitude, repair, or proof that the institution is secretly benevolent. A character may enjoy the care and still hold a private reservation. Keep the active narrator, world facts, and character ownership intact; the warm treatment does not remove established problems or make everyone agreeable.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacapag::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Lu Xun / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse sustained paragraphs and deliberate transitions to frame a situated convention through the narrator's available history and judgment. Establish what the supplied community accepts as proper, fair, or ordinary, and let the narration move between that accepted account and the concrete conduct it describes. Keep claims distinct from facts: what people say the arrangement means, what the narrator knows, and what the scene actually shows remain separable.\n\nRelate the convention to a particular act or authorized private association. The comic scale can come from the distance between a broad social phrase and the exact small operation it names, or from the way a composed account continues smoothly while the conduct beneath it resists the account. Plain wording may carry the formal arc; cast speech need not become ceremonial, and the narrator's judgment need not be announced as a verdict.\n\nAllow historical or inward development to remain unfinished at the active boundary. A character may understand part of the convention, miss another part, or hold a private association the narration does not resolve. Do not force an outward return, a concluding social lesson, or a repaired understanding. Preserve the active narrator, character ownership, and full nonspoken interiority; the formal architecture shapes the telling, not the outcome.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacapah::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Lu Xun / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen by establishing the present telling as an event: who is speaking, to whom, and what practical or social purpose the account serves in the outer scene. Let the teller choose and arrange material from a recognizable aim, not from a need to explain the comedy. The embedded story may secure attention, justify a request, answer a question, or simply fill an expected interval; its selection can suggest what the teller treats as normal, but that inference remains subject to actual access and context, including critical or strategic selection.\n\nCompose the embedded account in the teller's actual register, using clean surrounding narration to make the selection and its ordinary assumptions legible. Let an accepted phrase for duty, respect, economy, or good management meet the particular act it names. The comedy arises from the exact fit or mismatch between the conventional wording and the lived detail: a ceremony described as proper while the actual sequence is cramped, a favor described as generous while the recipient's need is smaller or different, a rule described as fair while its application follows a personal habit. Avoid a redundant announcement of discrepancy while preserving any actual remark or private recognition. Let the audience's response, whether amusement, agreement, confusion or polite inattention, occur according to the outer scene's actual events.\n\nKeep the narrative levels distinct. The teller knows what the teller knew then and may add what the teller has learned since, but a listener's inference is not the teller's memory. If the active contract permits private experience, render the teller's or listener's live thought in the outer scene without converting it into a performed aside for the audience. A repeated phrase or object may return across the telling, but it should connect the outer occasion to the embedded account rather than certify that the account is true.\n\nEnd the response at the active scene boundary. The audience need not interrupt, the tale need not complete, and no one need be persuaded or exposed. The continuation may simply be the next beat of the telling, a shift in the listener's attention, or an unfinished outer action.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacapai::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Lu Xun / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nSelect a modernist and absurdist construction. Begin with an actual rule, instruction, or habitual procedure already present in the scene or world: a way of serving, accounting, greeting, working, or settling a small matter. Follow the procedure through its concrete local steps in plain, legible syntax. Give each step the participants' actual attention and attitude, whether routine, serious, alarmed or otherwise supported; make the sequence itself carry comic weight without prescribing how they must feel about it.\n\nDistinguish what the participants accept from what the narration establishes as fact. A character may call a step necessary, proper, or efficient while the narrated action shows it to be merely customary, or the step may work exactly as intended and still produce a disproportionate result. The comedy lies in the juxtaposition between the procedure's stated purpose and its actual use or reception: a form completed with care to produce a decision already made, a precaution observed to protect an object no one needs, a polite formula delivered at the wrong scale. Do not add an impossible rule, a sudden obstruction, or a bureaucratic setting merely to create absurdity; the procedure must be supported by the supplied world.\n\nKeep syntax and narrative distance steady enough for the sequence to remain legible. A longer sentence may follow a step's full course; a short clause may mark its completion. Let clean observation make the arrangement's operation legible through the ordinary act, allowing actual confirmation or criticism without requiring exposure, an explanatory verdict or a concluding moral. Private experience, where authorized, may show a participant's genuine reasoning or discomfort without turning every character into a critic of the procedure.\n\nThe response may end while the procedure is still underway. No one must refuse, fail, or recognize the absurdity before the boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacapaj::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Lu Xun / Gothic-Punk\n\nGive the actual place and its authority material presence before and during the comic turn. Describe the actual room, object, custom or title through its particular place in people's attention and conduct, without making every detail serve a task. Atmosphere may occupy attention without an immediate task function: a cold corridor, a worn ceremonial chair, a locked cabinet, a name spoken with automatic deference. Keep the inherited arrangement concrete and preserve how people actually accommodate, ignore, resist or benefit from it.\n\nIntroduce the comic situation when an accepted public form meets a precise private preference or practical act. A title may be observed with care by someone who wants only a small favor; a custom may require a lengthy gesture before a mundane errand; an old privilege may apply awkwardly to a present need. The fit can be comic because it is too exact or because it misses by a small, legible margin. Do not force a humiliation or a failed task; competent help and sincere attachment remain possible, and the person with authority may be helpful, indifferent, or unaware.\n\nKeep power, permission, and motive distinct. A character may obey a form without respecting it, or respect it without understanding it. The narrator's clean observation can expose the arrangement through the ordinary act, but no character must announce the exposure. Where the contract permits private experience, render the actual desire, discomfort, or reasoning that accompanies the public performance; a social presentation may change in trusted company or another supported voluntary or involuntary context, or remain intact without becoming a lie.\n\nDo not add vampire physiology, faction rules or supernatural powers merely for the style; retain any forces and culture already supported by the supplied world. The response may end during the encounter, with the form observed, the request made, or the ordinary errand still unfinished.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacapak::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Lu Xun / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse a testimonial or autobiographical account as the organizing form. Attribute the source clearly: who is remembering, for whom, and at what distance from the events. Keep the source's actual speech or narrative register, and use clean presentation of the particular lived details to make the account's social position legible. The active narrator may be the source or may frame the source's account, but the source's knowledge remains limited to what that person experienced, heard, or later learned.\n\nBuild the comedy from the relation between a conventional name and the exact lived practice it labels. A word like help, respect, work, duty, or celebration may be used sincerely while the remembered act is smaller, stranger, more practical, or differently motivated than the name suggests. Let the source recall the practice without insisting on its meaning: the account may fit the conventional term closely, or the fit may be comic precisely because both the name and the act are accurate but disproportionate. Do not impose a confession, newly discovered injustice or imported family structure for this route; preserve any actual disclosure, discovery or relationship the story supports.\n\nDistinguish remembered experience from current telling and later understanding. A source may say what happened then, what it seemed to mean then, and what it means now without erasing the earlier limit. If the active contract permits private experience, render all authorized nonspoken experience in its active presentation, distinguishing live thought or feeling from the remembered account and preserving permitted earlier experience too; a memory is not automatically performed speech for a current audience.\n\nThe response may end with the account unfinished, the source pausing, or the frame returning to the present occasion. No completed moral or changed conclusion is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacapal::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Lu Xun / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nChoose social and psychological realism. Follow one person's actual perception, memory, reasoning, and feeling in causal order as they encounter a familiar explanation, request, or expectation. Follow the actual course of attention and events: thought may precede a chosen action, continue during speech, or arise from earlier knowledge without a new sensory trigger. Do not require any outward/inward sequence or brief recognition before every quick response.\n\nMake the particular reception precise enough for comic timing, whether it is accurate, mistaken or otherwise supported; no error or hidden flaw is required. The person may understand the expectation exactly and find it disproportionate, may accept it sincerely while noticing a small contradiction, or may apply it literally to a situation that does not fit. Correct understanding is as possible as error, and the person need not be self-deceived. Use clean language to make the particular reasoning and any accepted belief legible without a generic social verdict; the belief need not be exposed as false.\n\nPreserve fallible inference and meaningful private experience. A character may reason from incomplete information, revise a first impression, or hold two feelings at once. Where the contract permits, render extended thought before action, including the ordinary work of deciding what to say or do. Do not force quietness, a private-to-public sequence, or a changed conclusion; the person may act on the same understanding they began with.\n\nThe response may end during the reception, before any reply, or after an action that leaves the expectation still in place.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacapam::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Lu Xun / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse polyphonic nonlinear telling. Anchor the response to an actual occasion already established or newly supplied by the scene, and arrange permitted accounts of that occasion across the sequence, using nonlinear returns where they serve the juxtaposition without a minimum number of accounts in each response. Attribute each account to its source and mark its time clearly enough for the reader to place it: before the occasion, during it, after it, or at a later return. The sources may be participants, observers, or people who heard about the occasion, but each account remains that person's own.\n\nLet ordinary wording reveal each source's position without turning that position into omniscient truth. One source may call an arrangement proper, another may call it convenient, and a third may describe only the physical work it required. The comedy arises from the juxtaposition of these compatible or conflicting uses: the same accepted phrase can mean a ceremony to one person and an errand to another, or the same event can be remembered at different scales. Facts and interpretations may agree while the arrangement of accounts still produces the comic effect.\n\nPreserve established events and separate knowledge. A later account may know what an earlier source could not, but the earlier source's ignorance remains real. A familiar return to the occasion need not add new information or refute an earlier version. Where the contract permits private experience, render each permitted source's actual current or remembered private experience in its active presentation, without requiring a live act of telling or merging sources into a collective mind.\n\nDo not add magical facts or a fixed number of voices. The response may end between accounts, during an account, or after a juxtaposition that leaves the occasion still open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacapan::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Lu Xun / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Establish who is present, where they are, and what current concern brings them together, using plain prose and clear spatial cues. Explain unfamiliar names, terms or mechanics where needed for understanding, preparation or personally meaningful context. A supported opening detail or history may orient the scene without an obligatory inventory. Let the active narrator's person and the cast's individual diction remain intact.\n\nUse flexible paragraph rhythm around the real action, speech, and private experience that give an accepted claim its particular application. A paragraph may hold a brief exchange, a single movement, or a longer private response as the character's meaningful reasoning, feeling or association requires. Break where the focus shifts: from the stated rule or expectation to the actual act, from the act to its reception, from public speech to authorized thought. The comedy should be legible in the sequence itself: a familiar social explanation meets a precise practical use, and the fit or mismatch is visible without a narrator's verdict.\n\nLet competent or literal reception be comic without a mandatory mishap. A character may follow an instruction exactly and produce an absurd result, or may understand the expectation perfectly and answer it with a smaller, more practical truth. Clean language makes the accepted belief's actual use legible, with comic recognition possible without an exposure or announcement. Preserve extensive thought where the contract permits; a reaction may be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to its owner.\n\nThe response may end during the action, after a line of dialogue, or inside a private response. No ending reaction, completed task, or cliffhanger is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacapao::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Lu Xun / Lyrical Prose\n\nDevelop the comic situation through a scene-born sound or image that belongs to the actual moment: the repeated click of a latch, the particular way a phrase is pronounced, the shape of an object as it is handled. Let syntax and recurring attention carry that image across the paragraph, but keep the language clean and the image traceable to the scene or the teller's associations. Do not decorate every action with figurative language; a plain statement or exact detail can meet the shaped phrase where the sequence supports the relation.\n\nLet a conventional belief or accepted explanation acquire resonance within the authorized mind's association. A phrase such as a rule about proper conduct, a familiar excuse, or a public estimate of someone's character can be repeated with small variation until its sound becomes part of the scene. Then place it beside what the person actually does or sees. The comic effect can come from exact fit, where the phrase and the act match too neatly, or from a real discrepancy, where the ordinary detail exposes the belief's blind spot. Do not require the image to collapse, reverse, or return outward before the active boundary; the feeling can remain intact while the contact produces comedy.\n\nPreserve direct feeling, actual metaphor or world powers, and individual cast registers. A character who speaks plainly may remain plain while the narrator's shaped attention does the lyrical work; another character may use figurative language as part of their own habit. Keep private experience inside the authorized perspective, and do not let the recurring sound or image grant access to excluded minds. The paragraph can end during continuing attention, speech, action, or thought, with the image still in motion and the social relation still developing.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacapap::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Lu Xun / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nPlace the actual current relation to an established habit beside its remembered occasion with enough temporal orientation to remain clear; no redundant announcement of passing time is needed. The person may perform the habit, vary it, omit it or recall it privately without a new act: an arrangement of supplies, a familiar phrase or a route once taken can retain significance in any of these forms. A particular detail from an earlier occasion may enter through authorized recollection, perception or association in its actual causal order, including before or during the present act. The memory need not be complete or solemn; it can be a fragment of sound, a former pleasure, or an unfinished attachment that still has weight.\n\nAn old explanation or familiar social phrase may remain funny through its actual current use or private recollection; do not require it to be spoken or performed now. It may still fit exactly, or it may acquire another comic proportion because the present circumstances differ from the remembered ones. The comedy does not require the character to recognize the change, to be humiliated by it, or to renounce the phrase. A speaker can repeat a rule about hospitality or proper feeling while the present act shows its different scale, and the reader can hold both the humor and the private weight.\n\nAttribute recollection clearly to its owner and preserve genuine former pleasure as well as what has become unavailable. Do not invent loss, a decayed institution, or a new understanding merely to make time visible. If the world supplies a real change, let it appear through particular details; if the world supplies continuity, let the habit remain continuous. The scene can end during present action or continuing reflection, with the remembered occasion still present and the current moment still underway.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacapaq::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Lu Xun / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse satire and picaresque construction. Follow the actual person through supported encounters in pursuit of their recognizable concern: an errand, a request, a search, a duty, or another aim the scene supplies. Where a familiar claim enters an encounter, make its actual local use concrete without requiring a new claim in every encounter. One person may apply a rule about proper procedure to a practical need it does not fit; another may apply the same phrase to a need it fits exactly. The comedy comes from the relation between the claim and the particular use, not from a trickster identity or a corrupt institution imposed on the scene.\n\nKeep knowledge sourced. What the focal character knows comes from what they have seen, heard, been told, or inferred from available evidence. Another character's private reason remains distinct from the focal character's knowledge; present it only through actual narrator access or a supported means of learning it, without requiring public disclosure when nonspoken access is authorized. Preserve full private experience for the authorized mind: the focal character's reasoning, uncertainty, irritation, or satisfaction can run through the encounters without being announced to the other people present.\n\nLet the sequence remain picaresque in movement, not in required escalation. A helpful procedure or sincere agreement can be comic as readily as an obstruction when timing and scale fit. The character need not travel, win, lose, or complete the pursuit within the response. An encounter can end with the person still moving toward the next practical step, with the familiar claim still in circulation and its local application still producing its particular effect.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacapar::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Lu Xun / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect a small number of exact details and direct clauses. Show the conventional phrase or accepted explanation as it is actually used: who says it, to whom, in what practical situation, and what they do while saying it. Place a relevant concrete particular beside the wording where the actual sequence supports it; the active boundary may precede a completed comparison or continue within private experience. The reader can infer the comic fit or discrepancy from the juxtaposition; do not add a repeated explanatory gloss or a deflating final line to certify the joke.\n\nCut only redundant judgment. Keep the causes that connect the phrase to the act, and keep personally meaningful private experience even when it requires an extended sentence. A character may reason at length about a small obligation, or notice one detail that changes the scale of what they have just accepted as normal. The style is economical in selection, not in thought or in the number of words a particular mind needs to make sense.\n\nAllow actual explanation, praise, and individual verbosity where the character supplies them. A plain speaker can state the accepted rule in full; another can praise a service while performing it incorrectly. The comedy remains inferable because the wording and the concrete particular stand close enough to reveal the relation. No concealment, small outcome, or completed reversal is compulsory. The response can end with the relation established and the situation still in motion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacapas::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Lu Xun / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nMove naturally through action, speech, perception, recollection, and authorized private experience in actual causal order. Let the scene carry a situated assumption and its real application where they become relevant: a character states what is normal or proper, and the next moment shows the actual conduct, the practical need, or the private response that meets it. Literal cooperation can be comic, an apt habit can be comic, and a real discrepancy can be comic. Do not force a reversal or a social-lesson payoff.\n\nGive history and feeling their personal extent. A character may remember the origin of a rule, notice the cost of following it, or continue to believe it while acting against it. Keep private experience inside the authorized perspective, preserving any granted access to multiple positions without inventing a shared belief or explaining an unavailable mind. Use the timing and scale of the meeting between explanation and conduct for comedy without requiring a narrator's verdict; actual supported judgment retains its place.\n\nPreserve real world stakes, active ownership, and independent aims. Other characters continue their practical lives whether or not they share the focal assumption. The response can end at the active boundary during continuing public or private experience: mid-conversation, mid-task, or mid-thought, with the accepted explanation and the ordinary conduct still in relation and the scene still underway.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacapat::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Lu Xun / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nChoose scenes and callbacks from the supplied relationship promise and actual shared history. Let a phrase about care, rivalry, or ordinary obligation meet a particular preference or act in the present scene. The callback can be a repeated private word, a familiar division of labor, a shared joke, or a remembered promise that one person now applies to a different practical need. The comic effect comes from the exact way these people enact or answer the phrase, not from a generic trope inserted into the bond.\n\nPreserve sincere understanding as well as real mismatch. One person may know exactly what the other means and still answer with the wrong practical result; another may apply the familiar phrase with complete accuracy while the other expects something different. Keep each person's capacity, motives, and private experience distinct. Nothing must be hidden or unequal by default; a character can be fully aware of the bond's language and still produce comedy through timing, preference, or ordinary distraction.\n\nDo not impose rescue debt, a jealousy test, confession, or improved intimacy for the callback to count. The relationship can remain exactly as supplied, with its familiar language still in use and its particular promise still legible. The scene can end during continuing interaction, private thought, or unfinished practical business, with the callback still resonating and the bond still operating on its own terms.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacapau::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Lu Xun / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nUse compact declarations, deliberate cadence, and grounded recurrence for an actual act and its known account. Where a supported designation used by a community or particular teller shapes the account, relate it to specific circumstances without requiring a new designation at each opening: who was present, what was actually carried or said, what practical need the act served. The expansion lets praise, accepted custom, or a public estimate gain comic proportion without necessarily proving false. The designation can remain true while its scale becomes particular.\n\nDistinguish original experience from later telling. The focal character's private experience of the act may differ from the public account: the effort, irritation, confusion, or ordinary motive that the retold form compresses. Keep full private access for the authorized mind, and do not let the later telling grant knowledge the character did not have at the time. A recurring phrase can return through a new application or a familiar continuing use, without appearing at every difficulty or changing meaning each time.\n\nDo not require heroic inflation, mock battle, a witness chain, new legendary status, or a completed public verdict in every response. The account can remain modest, and the scene can end with the act still underway, the designation still in circulation, and the relation between what is remembered and what actually happened still developing.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacapav::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Lu Xun / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the scene as a situated essayistic account. Open with a particular proposition about ordinary obligation, stated in the authorized narrator's own terms and tied to the present social situation. The claim should be plain enough that a reader can hold it in mind, but it need not be announced as a thesis.\n\nExamine a concrete case or connected cases through actual narrator access, including observation, reports or broader authorized knowledge, or through an explicitly imagined case marked as hypothetical. The narrator need not have participated. Let the case unfold through specific conduct rather than general commentary. A person can pursue a recognizable aim while treating the proposition as obvious, and the humor comes from the exact fit or misfit between the stated expectation and what the case actually shows. Do not force the case to refute the claim; it may confirm it in an unexpectedly literal way, qualify it through a small exception, or simply sit beside it without resolution.\n\nReturn to the opening proposition only when the case has given it a particular shape. The return may confirm, qualify, or leave the claim open. Do not impose a moral verdict or demand that someone change; actual supported judgment may remain part of the narrator's account. Preserve the actual status of the case: what was said, done, and left pending remains what it was. Keep required private experience available in the active presentation, and allow the response to end before the case or the return is complete.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacapaw::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Lu Xun / Screenplay\n\nUse concise action blocks, clear speaker cues, and scene headings when location or time changes. Establish the available phrase or expectation through actual public material: a line someone says, a posted notice, a repeated instruction, or another visible or audible element the scene has already supplied. Do not invent an offscreen authority merely to state the expectation.\n\nJuxtapose the established phrase with real conduct where the scene supports it. The exact fit between the stated explanation and what a person actually does can carry the humor without a reaction shot, a laugh cue, or a failure. A held beat may simply let the visible action complete beside the earlier line. If a cut connects two places or times, preserve all actual prior knowledge across the cut; do not reset a character's knowledge for a joke, and preserve any actual forgetting or later change that the story supports.\n\nKeep required nonspoken experience separately attributed in the active presentation. It is not dialogue and not automatically available to the other characters. The scene boundary can arrive before reception or during continuing thought; no completed beat, changed plan, or practical result is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacapax::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Lu Xun / Stage Play\n\nWrite the scene with clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can actually be staged. Establish supported positions and objects through readable directions, observation or actual use as the sequence needs: a person sits where the line says they sit, carries the thing the line says they carry, or performs the task the line names.\n\nLet a particular conventional line meet the real task or response in its own timing. The comedy can come from the exact relation between the spoken explanation and the physical business beside it. Do not require a contrast, a new prop, a laugh cue, or a failure. Individual voices remain available; a character may state an actual explanatory remark plainly, and another may answer literally or pursue a different aim without either becoming a fixed comic type.\n\nKeep gesture distinct from proven motive. A pause, a look, or a movement can be observed without certifying what a character feels or intends. Preserve full required nonspoken experience separately from performed asides and monologues; an aside has an audience and a dramatic purpose, while private experience remains the character's own. The active boundary needs no in-world pause or completed beat.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacapay::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Lu Xun / Stream of Consciousness\n\nFollow the authorized mind's actual associations as the structure of the passage. Braid perception, memory, private wording, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern through that particular mind. Let a familiar accepted phrase meet a specific sensation, a remembered use, or an unrelated present concern. The comedy can come from that meeting: the phrase may fit exactly, sit oddly beside the sensation, or be carried along without examination. Amusement, agreement, and plain continued attention all remain possible; the mind need not produce a critical insight or a self-diagnosis.\n\nMark enough source and time cues to distinguish current event, memory, and imagination. A remembered occasion should be recognizable as remembered, and a hypothetical or imagined case should not blur into the present scene. Do not invent a trigger chain to justify every association; the mind may move by sound, habit, or an unstated connection.\n\nAllow meaningful thought to be extensive or unfinished. Prose length alone sets neither elapsed time nor an action requirement. The response may end inside the braid, before the thought resolves or returns to the outer scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacapaz::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Lu Xun / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the exchange with actual sender attribution, recipients, and only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the scene. Let each person's digital habits shape their contribution: one may compose carefully, another may send fragments, and a third may leave a message unread. Do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext.\n\nA familiar claim can become comic through what a sender actually composes and what another person receives or answers. The apt literal reply, the supported difference of scale, or the plain continuation of an unrelated task can all carry the humor. Delays and misunderstandings are not owed; the exchange may proceed smoothly and still be funny through the exact fit between the formulation and its use.\n\nKeep prior knowledge distinct from new channel access. A character does not know a message merely because the reader has seen it, and a draft is not a sent message. Preserve full required Hidden or Visible experience beyond transmission; private thought is not automatically typed, and a composed draft is not automatically the writer's live mind. The response may end during composition or thought before any sending, receipt, or answer.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacapba::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Lu Xun / Visual Novel\n\nOrient the scene through concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and flexible focal narration. Establish a supported object or expression cue through actual use or observation: a thing someone carries, a repeated gesture, a background detail the focal character notices. Do not insert an image cue merely to illustrate an abstract point.\n\nLet the selected cue make a familiar social explanation's actual use noticeable. The focal character can understand the relation, misread it, or miss it entirely. A changed expression can attract attention, but it does not certify motive or truth. Preserve the character's actual inference as fallible: stating a conclusion does not prove it, while any independent evidence or established knowledge retains its actual certainty.\n\nKeep full private experience available whether or not a visible response changes. The focal narration may render thought, sensation, or uncertainty without requiring a dialogue line or an expression change to follow. No fixed cue-thought-line rotation, new image, menu, romance route, or closing reaction is supplied. The response may end during continuing thought or an unfinished setup.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacapbb::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Lu Xun / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across installments: what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover earlier wording or conditions only where re-entry needs them. A brief exact recall of a prior phrase, object or arrangement can be enough; omit redundant reconstruction while preserving any history or private recollection the present experience actually needs.\n\nPreserve what was said, received, done, and left pending. A familiar exact repetition can be comic, and so can a supported new application of the same account. A callback need not reveal a fresh cost, a hypocrite, or a damaged relationship. The recurring account of what is normal may simply meet another ordinary use, and the humor can come from that fit without escalation.\n\nKeep independent lives and meaningful private experience beyond the joke. A character may be concerned with work, food, a relationship, or an unrelated task while the recurring account continues. No reset, escalating exposure, completed miniature chapter, or ending hook is owed at the active boundary. The installment may close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaqaa::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nStage the request, apology, admission, or return through the actual means available to the character: where they are positioned, who can hear them, what words they have prepared or discarded, and what help or obstacle the immediate scene supplies. Make the practical approach specific enough that the reader can see the effort being spent, not only the feeling behind it.\n\nLet the emotional cost remain present inside the attempt. A character may need the other person badly, resent the need, fear the answer, or remember an earlier response that made this approach harder. Render that private state in the active narration without requiring it to shorten, resolve, or visibly alter the outward action. The gap between what the character feels and what they manage to do is itself comic material, but the feeling is not a punchline to be dismissed.\n\nTime the relation between effort and reception for comedy. A carefully prepared approach can meet a completely different concern; an awkward opening can receive a direct, practical answer; a long-delayed admission can land while the other person is occupied with something ordinary. Let the scene's actual participants and their habits determine the reception. Do not force a rejection, a humiliation, or a breakthrough. A successful asking, an unfinished attempt, or a response that changes nothing may all end the interval.\n\nLet actual memories inform the present approach or accompany its feeling and attention without requiring practical usefulness or an altered next act. A remembered response can explain why the character hesitates, chooses a particular wording, or expects a particular outcome, but it does not have to be relived as a flashback or corrected by the present scene. Keep the remembered occasion in the past and let the present encounter develop from what is actually said and done now.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaqab::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Chat Roleplay\n\nOpen by orienting the current aim, the positions of the active participants, and the means already available in the scene. Use readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution so each contribution is clear. Resolve the submitted material under the active authorship contract: the owned character acts, speaks, and thinks within the permissions already established, while other participants remain available for their own owners to play.\n\nLet the comic interval develop from the participants' actual habits, aims, and blind spots. A character may pursue a recognizable goal with a method that fits their history, capacity, or relationship to the others. Cooperation, conflict, and ordinary misunderstanding are all usable; do not force a quip exchange or a random interruption to stand in for comic construction. An actual old encounter may explain present attention or develop feeling beyond immediate action. Render that memory at its warranted extent, briefly or in an extended recollection, without requiring a flashback or limiting it to a contextual note.\n\nKeep NPCs pursuing their own practical aims. They may help, resist, misunderstand, or continue their ordinary business. Their actions and speech are public scene material; their full private experience remains separate and is not automatically known to the player character or other cast members. Likewise, the player character's known motives are limited to what the active contract and actual access permit.\n\nEnd at the natural continuation point of the active scene. Do not require the player to answer a question, make a choice, or respond to a cliffhanger. Do not force a delivered joke, a new obstacle, or a changed relationship before the boundary. The interval may close during continuing action, speech, thought, or an unfinished setup, with the situation left coherent and ready to resume.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaqac::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and satirical realism route. Ground the comic situation in a definite social arrangement: who has access to a resource, role, place, or opportunity, and what that arrangement means to the actual people involved. Keep the supplied language, region, community, and period specific rather than adding a generic family structure, honor system, or national speech pattern.\n\nStage each character's use of the arrangement clearly. Show what they are trying to get, keep, avoid, or return, and what practical means they have for doing so. Comic proportion can come from the precise fit of compatible habits, a real collision, legitimate competing aims, or an actual mistaken assumption about the arrangement, not from random interference. A character may be competent and still funny because their competence is shaped by a particular history and a particular stake.\n\nLet the personal basis of the action remain legible. A character's aim should connect to a relationship, a remembered occasion, or a practical need that matters to them. Let actual memory inform conduct or continuing private feeling without requiring a flashback or lesson; an extended recollection remains available when it belongs to the experience. The emotional significance of the action is real even when the scene is comic.\n\nPreserve full private feeling in the active narration. What a character fears, hopes, or remembers is not automatically visible to the others and does not need to produce a visible effect. Do not require the scene to expose corruption, defeat a hierarchy, deliver a moral, or change the social outcome. The interval may end with the arrangement still in place and the comic recognition complete.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaqad::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nStage the attempt through the physical particulars that actually matter: the grip, reach, weight, cost, fatigue, or material resistance involved in what the character is trying to do. Use forceful verbs and economical transitions. Construct the comic relation through the actual material action, with any narrative commentary retaining its real voice and purpose rather than replacing that construction.\n\nRelate the chosen means to the person. The method should fit the character's real capacity, aim, or relevant remembered experience. An exact competent fit can be as funny as a mismatch when the scene shows why this person would do it this way. Do not invent damage or failure to create stakes; keep supplied pain and risk proportional to what the world and the scene already establish.\n\nVary the rhythm. Compressed movement can give way to a longer private passage when the character's reasoning or feeling needs it. The style is pressure-oriented, not a ban on thought or a quota of short sentences. Let the character's actual state determine the shape of the prose.\n\nKeep the emotional basis present without requiring a visible payoff. A character may act from a remembered wound, a relationship, or a practical need, and the narration may render that private state at length. The comic interval can end with the attempt underway, completed, or interrupted, without a forced setback, triumph, or lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaqae::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through the actual document form the scene requires: a letter, journal entry, report, note, or other record. Establish who wrote it, for what intended recipient or purpose, and when it was composed. Let the document's register belong to its author, not to an omniscient narrator.\n\nMake the relevant action, available means, or stated account legible inside that writer's register. The comedy comes from what the writer chooses to record, emphasize, omit, or misunderstand, and from the practical situation the document reveals. A prior shared phrase or account may meet its present use without requiring a contradiction or a second document to explain it.\n\nDistinguish what the document knows from what the writer privately felt. The record contains what its author chose to put down; full live feeling, reasoning, and unstated motive remain separate and may be rendered in the active narration where the contract permits. Do not treat the document as a transparent window into the writer's complete mind.\n\nLet the document's uptake remain open. A recipient may read it, ignore it, misread it, or act on it according to their own aims. No revelation, useful correction, completed artifact, or changed opinion is required at the boundary. The interval may end with the document composed, sent, received, or still unfinished, and the comic recognition may rest in the gap between the record and the actual situation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaqaf::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nMake the offered care practically understandable and personally specific. Show what the helper actually does, with what capacity, known or expressed preference, and available means. The kindness should be legible as a real action in the world, not a general atmosphere of niceness.\n\nStage the reception clearly enough for affectionate recognition. The recipient may respond with capable familiarity, a spontaneous fit, refusal, or inattention. The comedy can come from the particular fit between the care and the person: a perfectly chosen small thing, a well-meant effort that misses a known preference, or a familiar routine performed with unexpected seriousness. Keep the recipient's personality and privacy intact; comfort does not make everyone agreeable.\n\nLet memories inform the helper without inventing knowledge or hidden design. A remembered preference or a shared occasion may explain why the helper chooses this action, but the memory does not have to be announced, corrected, or relived. The care remains sincere even when it is imperfect.\n\nPreserve full private feeling in the active narration. Affection, amusement, frustration, or weariness may coexist with the offered care and need not be spoken aloud. Do not require gratitude, emotional transformation, sacrifice, or a completed response. The interval may end with the care offered, accepted, refused, or still underway, and the kindness remains real regardless of the outcome.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaqag::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse sustained paragraphs and deliberate transitions to give an actual choice or encounter its permitted social and historical context. Let the narration frame the scene through the surrounding arrangements, customs, or judgments that the chosen narrator can actually know. Do not import an aristocratic setting, archaic vocabulary, or uniformly elaborate speech; the formality is in the architecture of the prose, not the rank of the cast.\n\nMake the practical means and personal stake legible inside that frame. Show what the character is trying to do, with what resources and limits, and why it matters to them. The comic proportion comes from the relation between the composed context and the actual conduct: a small personal act can be treated with full formal attention, or a large expectation can meet a modest practical result. Keep the staging precise rather than inflating it.\n\nAllow a remembered occasion to inform the present without forcing a changed judgment. The narration may supply history or social context that explains why this encounter matters, but the character's present choice remains open. Do not require the scene to conclude with a lesson, a moral correction, or a demonstration of growth.\n\nPreserve individual speech, abstract thought, and direct feeling. The narrator's composed register does not flatten the characters into one voice. Private experience remains available under the active contract and is not automatically visible to others. The interval may close with the act completed, refused, interrupted, or still in motion, with the comic recognition carried by the framing itself.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaqah::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nEstablish the actual telling occasion: who speaks, who is present, and what brings this account before these listeners. Their attention may be willing, reluctant, distracted, or otherwise situated. The teller's real purpose may be instruction, securing cooperation, entertaining a companion, defending a reputation, or simply sharing time; a lesson is not required.\n\nInside the told account, stage the attempt concretely. Give the central figure a clear practical goal, a specific means, and a personal reason for choosing that means. Let the audience's present interest shape what the teller includes: a detail about timing, access, or a companion's habit may be selected because it matters to the listeners, not because it proves a point. Keep the teller's selection and emphasis consistent with their relationship to the people and events described.\n\nBuild comic recognition through the frame rather than through random interruption. A recurring object, phrase, or practical detail can reappear at the outer level and acquire a new proportion because the listeners now know its history. The audience may notice an implication before the teller does, or the teller may knowingly withhold a detail until it becomes useful. Let the listeners remain active: they can ask a concrete question, supply a remembered fact, or respond in a way that changes the telling without resolving it.\n\nPreserve the difference between what the original participants knew and what the teller has learned since. A vivid recollection is not proof of its own accuracy, and a generous account can be sincere while omitting something the teller did not understand. The told act may be funny because its actual positions and means are legible, not because the teller mocks it. Keep the actual nonspoken experience of authorized people available beyond the told account at either narrative level, including a listener's sustained feeling or a teller's private uncertainty without required usefulness. No interruption, useful advice, revealed motive, changed reception, or in-world pause is required before the response ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaqai::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Masashi Kishimoto / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse documentary and historical-memory prose as the route. Introduce an actual record, object, or recollection with its source time and the present character's access to it: a file read years later, a receipt kept for a private reason, a remembered announcement, a note passed through someone else's hands. The document is not a neutral exhibit; it enters the scene through a specific person's attention and purpose.\n\nRelate the record's contents to the present person's understanding or conduct. Place the document beside actual present conduct, attention, attachment, or uncertainty; no external task is required to make its historical presence meaningful. The comedy arises from the gap or fit between what the source records and what the person is doing, wanting, or remembering. A familiar detail may confirm what the character already knew, or it may acquire a new comic proportion because the present situation gives it a different weight. Do not require a discovery, correction, or useful next step to make the juxtaposition meaningful.\n\nKeep the source's knowledge boundaries explicit. The narration may convey what a record says without staging a reading gesture; a character may know its contents from memory, from a summary, or from direct access. The record does not grant unsupported knowledge to anyone, and it does not replace actual private experience. A character can read a document and still feel something the document does not state. Let the record's language remain its own: an official notice, a private note, and a remembered remark should not all sound like the narrator.\n\nWhen actual action occurs, stage its practical and personal terms clearly. A chosen means follows the person's real capacity, history, information, and relationships rather than being invented to force a useful effect from the document. Historical attention may remain meaningful without an act or choice. The record can make a familiar habit legible, expose a blind spot without punishing it, or show that an old arrangement is still active in a comic way. No correction, confrontation, or changed plan is required before the response ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaqaj::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Gothic-Punk\n\nStage the actual access through which old authority meets present life: a permission, a room, an object, a role, a request, or a debt. Make the practical terms concrete before the comedy develops. Who may enter, what must be asked, what is owed, what is inherited, and what is merely assumed should be legible through actual action, dialogue, or permitted narrative context, without a mandatory demonstration or ban on explanatory summary.\n\nPut the personal significance of that access beside real practical conduct. A character may be sincerely courteous, freely offer trust, or feel actual ambivalence while still pursuing a recognizable aim. Let that combination carry comic proportion: the gravity of the setting or role does not make the person's habits, blind spots, or private desires disappear. The comedy comes from the fit between the formal arrangement and the particular person moving through it, without requiring mockery. Any actual satirical judgment or character ridicule remains available within its voice and circumstances.\n\nUse atmosphere to color experience, not to prove motive. A beautiful room, an unsettling ritual, or a damaged object can matter through its actual function and history. A character's unease, comfort, or attraction may accompany their practical reasoning without certifying that someone else is trustworthy or dangerous. Keep private desire available to the narration where the active contract permits, and keep it distinct from what other characters can observe.\n\nLet relationship terms remain actual. A mask may fall away in trusted company, but it does not have to fall. A sincere courtesy can be met with sincerity, a freely offered trust can be accepted, and an ambivalent person can act without resolving the ambivalence. No betrayal, authority collapse, moral correction, or changed outcome is required before the response ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
