            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-comedy-05",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Comedy · 05/06",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == comedy}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Comedy. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 5 of 6. }}{{// Source group: recipes-comedy; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPacavar::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Quentin Tarantino / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose a small number of exact details and direct clauses to carry the scene. Keep the speakers' actual words, the physical acts that matter, and the pauses that change the rhythm of the exchange. Omit redundant narration and summary, but do not cut the participants' interests or their private experience. A sustained comic conversation can run long while the narration around it stays lean.\n\nLet a literal line or a repeated particular carry the timing. A character may say the same thing twice, handle an object the same way, or answer a question literally, and the comedy comes from the exactness of the detail rather than from explanation. The narration does not need to point out that something is funny. A precise sentence can do more work than several performed fragments, and required actual thought and feeling retain their warranted extent without a reasoning or usefulness gate.\n\nPreserve each speaker's individual voice and mind. One may talk at length, another may answer in fragments, and a third may think extensively while saying little. The active boundary may fall before the conversation or setup finishes. The scene can end with a line still hanging, a task still in progress, or a thought still forming, without a required punch line or completed exchange.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacavas::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Quentin Tarantino / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the scene in sustained contemporary-novel prose with natural movement among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience. Give the exchange a subject its speakers actually care about, and let the conversation follow their real attention. A character may offer an example, go off on a tangent, repeat a point, or answer a question literally, and the comedy comes from the shape of that attention rather than from a hidden tactic or a forced quip.\n\nKeep the scene's conditions legible: where the characters are, what they are doing, what they can see and hear, and what each is privately thinking or feeling under the active contract. Aims may be stated openly, left unspoken, or simply be ordinary interest in the topic. The narration may move into a character's mind and back out without announcing the shift, as long as the access matches the authorized perspective. No interruption, status reversal, emotional turn, or punch line is required in every reply.\n\nLet the active boundary fall wherever the reply reaches, including during continuing speech, action, or thought without a fictional pause or ending. The conversation may still be in motion, a task may be half-finished, or a character may be thinking something they have not said. The comic construction does not need to complete itself before the response boundary. Preserve the world's consequences and the characters' agency; the scene may advance, stall, or change direction according to what the participants actually do.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacavat::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Quentin Tarantino / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nChoose a particular exchange that makes the established relationship premise legible. Draw on the actual participants and whatever familiarity the premise supplies: shared interests, a private phrase, a recurring topic, or the way people meeting for the first time attend and respond. Use an established pattern where one exists without inventing a common history. Comedy can come from recognition of an existing pattern or the particular reception of a new exchange, without a required pattern or departure. A callback may be enjoyed exactly as before, ignored, or put to a different use where the scene supports it.\n\nPreserve each person's private response and their right to disagree, change topic, or refuse the familiar rhythm. One character may find the old joke funny, another may be tired of it, and a third may not notice it at all. The narration may show what each is thinking or feeling under the active contract, but that private experience does not need to be confessed or resolved. No proof of chemistry, hidden motive, repaired bond, or relationship milestone is owed.\n\nLet the scene follow the actual conversational interests of the participants. The exchange may run long, digress, repeat, or stop short. The active boundary may fall before the conversation finishes or before anyone acknowledges what the callback meant. The relationship's established habits remain available for future scenes without being completed or transformed in this one.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacavau::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Quentin Tarantino / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the scene the sense of an account being retold. Open with a deliberate declaration that establishes a person or a remembered act, then expand into actual particulars that may substantiate, qualify, or leave the reputation unchanged. Use recurring phrasing around an established deed or attributed memory, but keep the designation tied to what actually happened or what the community actually says. A familiar phrase may return when a later occurrence gives it a new application.\n\nLet individual speech enter according to the actual account, agreeing, qualifying, or addressing another concern without a required interruption or complication. The subject of an oft-told incident may agree with the account, question one detail, or answer an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern. Comic proportion comes from placing public remembrance beside actual conversational particulars, which may fit it or diverge, not from a compulsory puncturing reply. A character may also let the account stand without comment. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective.\n\nPreserve the actual scale of the deed. Do not impose mock combat on household work or heroic doom on familiar inconvenience; an actual speaker's playful comparison can still belong to their account. The account may stop during ongoing business, speech, thought, or telling, or after an actual conclusion, without requiring a resumed task, another act, or a modest scale. Private experience remains distinct from public remembrance; being remembered as dependable does not mean that helping was effortless, cheerful, or automatic.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacavav::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Quentin Tarantino / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nUse essayistic narration to examine a claim that the scene has actually produced. When a character says something general about a person, place, habit, or plan, let the narrator take that statement seriously as a proposition: state its apparent scope, then bring it beside a particular remembered or observed case with exact details. The case may confirm the claim, qualify it, or quietly refute it, but comic movement comes from placing the formulation beside concrete particulars, whether they fit or expose a gap; the speaker need not be confident.\n\nKeep the dialogue itself sustained and specific. Let speakers pursue their actual aims through their own emphases and particulars; argument or a revealed blind spot may occur, but accurate agreement and open motives remain available. The narrator may frame the exchange, but the speakers' words remain their own; do not make every character share the essayist's analytical register. A literal-minded reply, a patient correction, or an ordinary interest defended at length can carry the scene.\n\nWhen private experience is authorized, let it run beside the public conversation without being converted into evidence for the narrator's claim. A character may be thinking about something unrelated, rehearsing a response that never arrives, or feeling a strain that the essayistic frame does not need to resolve. The narration can return to its proposition after the exchange, but it need not deliver a verdict, a completed joke, or a changed relationship. The scene may close while the claim is still being tested, the conversation still underway, or the narrator still turning over a particular that does not fit.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacavaw::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Quentin Tarantino / Screenplay\n\nWrite the scene in concise action blocks with clear speaker cues and scene headings when location or time changes. Let the dialogue run at its natural length: a character can hold a topic, return to a detail, or defend an ordinary preference without being interrupted for pace. The comic timing comes from what the speakers actually choose to say next, not from a mandatory quick cut or reaction shot.\n\nUse cuts to join occasions or shift attention, but keep editing separate from physical causes. A cut does not make a door open, a drink spill, or a person arrive; those events must be written as visible or audible action. When a physical consequence occurs, stage it clearly in the action lines so the reader can see what happened and who could observe it.\n\nPreserve required nonspoken experience independently of voice-over. If the active contract authorizes a focal character's private thought or feeling, write it as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the screenplay presentation, not as dialogue another character can hear and without replacing live private experience with voice-over; separately authorized voice-over has its own performed wording and audience. The scene may end during continuing speech, action, or private attention without a final punch line, reaction, or completed exchange.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacavax::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Quentin Tarantino / Stage Play\n\nUse the shared stage space to make the public interaction legible. Write clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be staged. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the scene: a character may continue talking while adjusting a chair, checking a pocket, or waiting for someone to finish a task. The business does not need to interrupt the dialogue or deliver a punch line.\n\nLet sustained talk develop through actual positions and choices. A pause, repeated phrase, literal answer, or moment of stillness may support comic timing where it occurs, but do not force a prop, entrance, or completed exchange to deliver the beat. The speakers pursue their own recognizable aims and blind spots through what they say and do.\n\nPreserve required private experience separately from asides. An aside is performed speech with its own audience and permissions; it is not the default destination for thought. If the active contract authorizes nonspoken interiority, write it as clearly attributed authoring material in the active presentation rather than disguising it as something another character can hear. Supported offstage events remain available, and the scene may close during continuing talk, action, or thought without a final revelation or resolved exchange.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacavay::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Quentin Tarantino / Stream of Consciousness\n\nBraid actual hearing, private words, memory, bodily sensation, and imagined replies through the authorized mind. Let the character's attention move associatively: a phrase in the conversation may trigger a remembered occasion, a physical discomfort, a rehearsal of something they might say, or a recurring concern that has nothing to do with the topic. The comic movement can come from the gap between what the character is thinking and what they actually say, or from the way an ordinary remark keeps returning in their attention.\n\nDistinguish delivered words from what was received or invented. When another character speaks, render what the focal mind actually hears or notices; when the focal character imagines a reply, mark it as imagined rather than spoken. Inaccessible content remains unknown. The character need not produce a rehearsed comeback, a perfect opening, or a resolved response; a thought may be lengthy, fragmentary, or unrelated to the public exchange.\n\nKeep actual events in their own timing. Track any conversation or action that actually elapses while attention moves; the length of represented thought alone does not force continued speech, a delay, or a response. The scene may close during continuing speech, action, or private attention without a public payoff, a completed joke, or a changed relationship. Required interiority remains available to the reader under the active contract, without granting the cast knowledge merely because the reader has it; preserve each character's actual sources, communication, and established capabilities.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacavaz::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Quentin Tarantino / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nUse readable sender-attributed messages and only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the scene. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one may type in long blocks, another in fragments, another may leave a message unfinished and send it anyway. The comic movement comes from what the senders actually transmit and how the recipients actually receive it, including plain replies, resumed phrases, or familiar patterns.\n\nKeep a sent message distinct from an unsent draft and private thought distinct from transmitted text. If the active contract authorizes nonspoken interiority, write it clearly separated from the transcript so the reader knows it was not sent. A draft is a composed artifact with its own status, distinct from the writer's live private thought. Do not convert a typo, delay, deletion, or read receipt into proof of intent, embarrassment, or a hidden feeling unless the actual story supports that reading.\n\nLet the topic develop through actual reception. A message may be answered directly, ignored, returned to later, or met with a question about something else. No misunderstanding, confirmed interpretation, or final punch line is required. The exchange may close while a message is still being composed, a reply is pending, or the participants have simply stopped typing for now.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacavba::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Quentin Tarantino / Visual Novel\n\nUse concise scene-setting and clear dialogue attribution to follow the actual topic and participants. Let the illustrated rhythm linger on a conversational particular: a phrase, an object, a pause, or a change in someone's attention can receive focal narration without turning it into a truth indicator. The scene moves at the pace of the exchange and the focal character's response to it, not at a mandatory reaction tempo.\n\nWhen a background or expression cue draws authorized attention, render the full interpretation or feeling that follows. The focal character may read the cue accurately, miss it, or connect it to something unrelated. Their expectation remains distinct from what the cue proves; another character's smile, glance, or hesitation does not certify a hidden motive or a lie. Let the actual continuation meet the focal response: the next line may support the interpretation, complicate it, or show the speaker was concerned with something else entirely.\n\nKeep actual private experience distinct from public cues. Required interiority remains available to the reader under the active contract without automatically becoming cast knowledge; preserve actual disclosure, evidence, and established capabilities. The style does not activate branching menus, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands. The scene may close during continuing speech, action, or unfinished thought without a changed social position, a public exposure, or a resolved exchange.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacavbb::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Quentin Tarantino / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across installments by recovering a relevant earlier line, interest, or circumstance briefly when it helps. Preserve the actual speech and knowledge from the earlier scene: a character may return to a topic they were defending, ask about a detail someone mentioned, or resume a familiar argument without the narration rewriting what was said or understood then.\n\nLet the topic resume, drift, settle, or remain untouched according to the people involved. Familiar recognition can be comic without changed meaning: a repeated phrase may simply be a habit, a callback may be affectionate or dry, and a sustained interest may continue across installments without a new objection, revelation, or payoff at every return. The characters pursue their own recognizable aims and blind spots at their own pace.\n\nRequired private experience may continue without advancing the exchange. A focal character may be thinking about the topic, about something unrelated, or about what they did not say; reader access alone does not give the cast that knowledge; each character retains actual sources and established capabilities. The installment may close on completed work, a pending request, a plain acknowledgment, or simply the ongoing life of the conversation. No active interval must complete a joke, resolve a disagreement, or deliver a serial hook.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacawaa::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Salman Rushdie / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nStart from the actual request, admission, or concern in the scene. Let the focal character's private strain remain present while an available memory, circulating saying, or imaginative comparison develops alongside it. The association may exaggerate, deflate, or reframe the difficulty without making the difficulty less real. Use the contrast between the ranging comparison and the immediate emotional cost as the comic engine: the character can recognize the disproportion, refuse it, or miss it entirely.\n\nKeep the association sourced to the character's actual experience or an association within the active narrator's access, with its owner distinguishable. It may arrive as a remembered phrase, a story someone else told, or an invented parallel that the character knows is invented. Do not let the invention become a fact another character could observe. The emotional cost stays in what is admitted, withheld, needed, apologized for, or returned to, and it may remain unresolved.\n\nRender the full actual strain and any effects it has on speech or behavior; it can remain entirely private with outward conduct unchanged. The character may speak before thinking, circle back to a phrase, or stay silent while the association runs. A comic turn can land beside a genuine hurt. Do not force the exchange to end in repair, rupture, or a lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacawab::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Salman Rushdie / Chat Roleplay\n\nAnchor who speaks and what continues in the shared setting before any digression opens. Use unmistakable dialogue attribution and keep the current physical or practical activity locatable: a task, a walk, a meal, an errand, or another ongoing interval. Let a word, object, or name in that shared scene open a narrator-owned or character-owned association. The association may range into recollection, public history, gossip, cultural reference, or imaginative invention, but the shared interval remains the frame the reader can return to.\n\nFollow actual talk, attention, or activity at the boundary of the reply. The digression need not produce a return beat, a question, or a forced choice. It may simply end while someone is still speaking, working, or noticing something. Keep cast voices distinct from a fluent narrator: a character may be literal, dry, exuberant, or uninterested in the joke, and their speech should not inherit the narrator's associative range.\n\nBuild comic situations from the characters' recognizable aims and blind spots. The digression can expose a habit, collide with a misunderstanding, or set up a later callback, while maintaining enough orientation to locate the ongoing scene. Required private experience can extend through the reply without having to return to dialogue or action. Preserve the active authorship contract, including who controls which character and what private experience is available.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacawac::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Salman Rushdie / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse vernacular episodic telling as the definite route. Introduce an actual occasion through a teller's compact orientation: who is present, what has brought them together, and what matter is at hand. Expand the episode through its people and particulars, letting the Rushdie profile's energetic connections among personal recollection, public history, gossip, cultural reference, and imaginative invention move within the episode's frame. The teller may be the narrator or a character with an audience, but the episode must remain a readable unit with a clear occasion.\n\nCarry an attributed name, saying, or anecdote between episodes when relevant. The recurrence can create comic recognition, but it must retain its actual source and status. A story circulating about someone is not the same as a fact about them, and the text should preserve that difference. Do not require the circulating story to be exposed as false; it may be accurate, partial, exaggerated, or misunderstood, and its comic effect can come from how people receive it rather than from a final debunking.\n\nBuild comic situations from people pursuing recognizable aims with their own habits and blind spots. The episode can end at a natural pause in the occasion, leaving the linked saying or name available for a later return. Preserve the supplied language, period, and community rather than importing a generic setting.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacawad::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Salman Rushdie / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nCompress scene transitions around actual objects and bodily activity. Use forceful diction and concrete physical detail: what is worn, handled, carried, spilled, or repaired. Let a sourced public phrase, gossip item, cultural reference, or imaginative comparison meet those exact conditions. The comic effect comes from the collision between the ranging association and the hard particular, not from damage interrupting the digression.\n\nPreserve actual consequences and the full warranted inward passage. Actual thought and feeling may occupy their warranted length without a reasoning or usefulness test, and the thought may range associatively without making the scene less concrete. Material wear and physical stakes remain real: a broken object, a sore body, a failed errand, or a practical obstacle can coexist with comic proportion. Do not turn the genre into survival horror or make every rough surface a threat.\n\nUse useful changes of rhythm. A compressed transition can land beside an extended association, and a short physical beat can punctuate a longer recollection. Ground abrasive or rough speech in its speaker rather than giving every character the same hard edge. Build comic situations from recognizable aims and blind spots, and let the sequence end at its natural boundary without requiring a completed task or a changed plan.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacawae::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Salman Rushdie / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through actual document forms: letters, journals, reports, records, or other authored documents with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Use the document form required by the scene rather than rotating through all available formats. Each document contains what its author actually records for that purpose, distinguishing their knowledge, claims and intentional invention rather than treating written confidence as fact.\n\nTrack a quotation, anecdote, or name as it is recorded and received across entries. The Rushdie profile's energetic connections among personal recollection, public history, gossip, cultural reference, and imaginative invention can appear inside each document, but they must remain the document author's own associations. The travel of the phrase creates comic recognition: a letter may quote it seriously, a report may misattribute it, a journal may recall its source, and a later note may reuse it without knowing where it came from.\n\nRetain known origins and actual reliability. Repetition alone is not corroboration, and a document's confident account does not make it true. Gaps, revisions, conflicting accounts, and material traces can reveal events across entries without an omniscient explanation after every record. Keep live private experience and genuinely composed drafts separate from transmitted records: an unsent draft is a composed artifact, distinct from the writer's live private thought.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacawaf::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Salman Rushdie / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nFollow actual care, ease, or familiar enjoyment at the supplied scale. Spend attention on chosen kindness, practical care, a known preference, or a small pleasure. Let that preference or object carry a remembered anecdote, nickname, or comparison from the Rushdie profile's range: personal recollection, public history, gossip, cultural reference, or imaginative invention. The comic warmth comes from the stories people attach to the kindly act, not from making the care itself a performance.\n\nLet recipients respond as themselves. One character may enjoy the story, another may correct it, and a third may be too absorbed in the practical comfort to notice. Sincere direct affection and private feeling need no gratitude display or resolved problem. Warmth can coexist with pain, embarrassment, or an unfinished difficulty, and comfort does not make everyone agreeable or remove established problems from the world.\n\nKeep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact. The comic situation can arise from a recognizable habit or blind spot: someone over-explains the anecdote, someone misremembers the source, or someone takes the nickname too seriously. No lesson about kindness, confession, emotional repair, or milestone is required; a supported lesson or direct reflection may still belong to the actual speaker or narrator. The interval can end in the middle of the care itself.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacawag::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Salman Rushdie / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse sustained paragraphs and explicit transitions between current particulars and a sourced wider account. The narrator may frame a comic aim through social context, public history, gossip, cultural reference, or imaginative invention, but the movement must remain deliberate: a paragraph can open in the scene, move outward to the wider account, and return with the relation made legible. Do not let the frame replace the scene.\n\nDevelop comic proportion from the actual relation between the particular aim and the wider account. The relation may be fitting recognition, unexpected resemblance, or a gap between public reputation and private habit. Do not force an obligatory mismatch or puncturing of reputation. A character's aim can remain recognizable and consequential even when the narrator places it beside a larger history.\n\nSeparate narrator judgment, quoted public language, and authorized private feeling. The narrator may judge or compare, but a character's spoken register remains their own, and another character's private experience is available only where the active contract permits it. Distinguish narration from ordinary spoken language. Preserve the supplied setting, period, and community rather than importing an aristocratic or archaic frame.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacawah::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Salman Rushdie / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen inside the telling occasion: identify who is speaking, who is listening, and the purpose available within narrator access; an unspoken or inaccessible aim need not be announced. Let a concrete particular from the current situation—an object, a name, a phrase, a remembered place—start the embedded account. Mark the embedded material as claimed, remembered, or invented by its teller, and keep that status stable even when the account grows elaborate.\n\nBuild the comic movement through the teller's habits and the listeners' actual reception. The teller may digress from a recognizable aim into an aside that follows a personal association, a piece of gossip, a public reference, or an imaginative invention. The listeners may interrupt, mishear, demand a detail, become absorbed, or quietly let the teller continue. Let their responses shape the telling: an interruption can force a return to the main aim, a listener's interest can pull the account toward an unexpected particular, and absorbed quiet can let the teller overbuild a claim until its proportion becomes funny. Do not require every digression to be corrected, exposed, or turned into a lesson.\n\nKeep the outer occasion alive around the embedded account. The teller can finish, continue, or be interrupted without a required resolution, question, or cliffhanger at the boundary. When a phrase or object recurs, let it connect the narrative levels through the listeners' recognition or the teller's return to it, not through a proof that the account is true. Distinguish what the teller invents from what the surrounding scene establishes as fact; the comedy can come from the gap between them, but the gap need not be announced or resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacawai::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Salman Rushdie / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse documentary and historical-memory prose as the active route. Bring an actual record or material trace—a notice, ledger, letter, photograph, map, receipt, or institutional file—beside an attributed recollection or circulating account of the same event, place, or person. Establish available provenance: who made the record, when and for what purpose where known, and who remembers or repeats the account from which position. Leave genuinely unavailable origins unknown.\n\nLet the comic recognition arise from the comparison itself rather than from forcing a contradiction. The record may confirm the memory while differing in emphasis, scale, or vocabulary. A person's recollection may attach a public reference, a piece of gossip, or an imaginative association to the document's dry particulars, and the gap between those registers can become funny without making either source false. Follow the energetic connection where it leads: from the record's concrete detail into a personal aside, a cultural reference, or a remembered occasion, then back to the record or onward to the present scene.\n\nKeep the characters' aims and blind spots in play. Someone may consult the record to settle a practical question, defend a reputation, or support a plan; the document's actual content can complicate that aim without requiring the person to change course or expose a lie. Preserve the record's actual reliability and the memory's lived texture. The active boundary may fall during a continuing comparison or after a supported conclusion, without requiring an open state or a lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacawaj::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Salman Rushdie / Gothic-Punk\n\nStart from a supplied privilege, role, or material place that carries inherited authority: an old building, a title, a family obligation, an institutional position, a ritual of access. Let a character with a current practical aim encounter that authority through a known account, reputation, or imaginative analogy. The atmosphere should color their actual experience—the weight of the place, the texture of its objects, any actual social presentation at the threshold—without supplying new supernatural rules.\n\nBuild the comedy from the collision between the story attached to the authority and the present person's habits and blind spots. The character may treat a solemn tradition as a practical inconvenience, misunderstand a coded courtesy, or inflate a minor privilege into a grand association. The authority figure may be literal, weary, theatrical, or uninterested in the joke. Keep the actual power consequential: a refusal, permission, or obligation can still change the character's situation, and the comedy need not dissolve that consequence.\n\nLet the encounter move through the Rushdie profile's associative energy: a personal recollection, a piece of gossip, a cultural reference, or an imaginative invention can open inside the scene and return to the present exchange. Atmosphere and intimacy may shift together—a trusted companion can let a mask fall, a private desire can surface, an urban detail can interrupt the old setting's gravity. Preserve legitimate authority, voluntary or involuntary presentation, and actual powers without requiring exposure, rebellion, or a lesson at the end.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacawak::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Salman Rushdie / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse modern urban and popular narration as a portable method in the supplied setting. Choose an actual topic, name, phrase, or incident that can travel across linked social occasions. Track its movement precisely: who first said or heard it, who repeated it, who knew it independently, and who now uses it, for an existing or new purpose. Keep each encounter concrete in its setting, register, and practical aims—a workplace exchange, a family visit, a market transaction, a phone call, a public gathering—without relocating the cast or importing a generic identity.\n\nLet comic movement come from the phrase's actual social life, including familiar shared meaning as well as change. A person may repeat it with confidence while missing its original meaning, attach a public reference or piece of gossip to it, or use it to impress an audience that already knows its source. The Rushdie profile's associative energy can open a digression from the phrase into personal recollection, cultural reference, or imaginative invention, then return to the present encounter. Make known meaning and source available within the active narrator's access, leaving genuinely inaccessible origins unknown; the comedy can come from the gap between circulation and origin without requiring that gap to be exposed.\n\nDo not force a new misunderstanding, identity lesson, or correction at each return. The phrase may simply arrive in a new context and reveal a different habit or blind spot. Let the sequence reach its active boundary during continuing talk, attention, or circulation, or after an actual completed encounter, without a prescribed ending state.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacawal::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Salman Rushdie / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse diary and reflective miscellany as the active route. Give the recorder an actual occasion for composing: a particular day, place, and purpose that explains why these notes, memories, or lists are being set down now. Let a concrete recorded particular—a note, a remembered phrase, an item in a list, an observation—open into a sourced association. The association may move through personal recollection, public history, gossip, cultural reference, or imaginative invention, then return to the record or continue as composition actually does.\n\nKeep the recorded thought an artifact, not a substitute for live interiority. The diary entry contains what its writer chooses to record; it is not automatically the writer's complete present mind. Where the active authorship contract requires visible or hidden nonspoken interiority, write that private experience separately in its proper presentation, with its own access and attribution. The comic recognition can come from the gap between what the recorder selects and what the surrounding scene or private experience shows, without requiring that gap to be announced.\n\nLet the miscellany's arrangement carry the comedy. A trivial note can sit beside a grand public reference; a list can interrupt a reflection; a remembered occasion can return with a new particular attached. The recorder's habits and blind spots shape the selection—what they include, omit, or inflate—while the actual world remains consequential. Do not force a lesson, correction, or completed insight at the end; the active boundary can fall during continuing composition or after the recorder actually stops, without requiring a fictional pause.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacawam::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Salman Rushdie / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse polyphonic and nonlinear telling as the active route. Choose a concrete phrase or incident as the organizing thread, then present accounts of it in an order that is not simply chronological. Attribute each account clearly to its person and time of composition or telling: a participant's memory, a bystander's report, a public wording, a later imaginative interpretation. Let the reader see the same particular from different vantages while each account retains its own accuracy, emphasis, and purpose.\n\nBuild the comic movement through juxtaposition. Place a public version beside a private recollection, a confident report beside a hesitant memory, or an imaginative interpretation beside a plain fact. The accounts may agree, differ in emphasis, or misunderstand one another without any of them being false. The Rushdie profile's associative energy can open inside a single account—a personal recollection can lead into gossip, a cultural reference, or an invention—then return to the organizing phrase. Do not invent contradiction or retroactive motive to force a reveal; the comedy can come from the reader recognizing a connection the cast does not share.\n\nKeep each voice distinct in register and habit. A speaker may be literal, exuberant, dry, or self-important, and their blind spots shape what they notice and omit. Accounts may remain unfinished at the active boundary, and the sequence need not resolve into a single corrected version.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacawan::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Salman Rushdie / Light Novel\n\nEnter the actual situation cleanly: establish where the characters are, who is present, and what practical aim is underway before opening an associative movement. Track speakers and spatial changes with readable paragraph breaks. When a name, remark, or unfamiliar mechanic appears, give enough sourced context for the reader to follow; explanatory prose alone does not pause fictional time, while an actual pause remains possible.\n\nLet the Rushdie profile's exuberant association operate inside the light novel's fast-readable rhythm. A concrete particular—a name, a phrase, an object, a public reference—can open a digression through personal recollection, gossip, cultural reference, or imaginative invention. Use paragraph breaks to distinguish actual speech, private thought, and event rather than imposing a quick reaction beat after every line. The digression may run for a full paragraph or more when the association needs room, then return to the present exchange.\n\nKeep the cast's voices individual. The narrator may be fluent and associative, but other characters can be literal, dry, exuberant, or uninterested in the joke; do not assign every speaker the narrator's range. Preserve full private passages where the active contract requires them, and keep actual unfamiliarity intact—a character who does not know a reference should not suddenly recognize it for the sake of a callback. Let the scene reach its active boundary during continuing speech, thought or action, or after an actual conclusion, without requiring a completed comic turn or an open situation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacawao::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Salman Rushdie / Lyrical Prose\n\nBuild the comic scene around a particular sound, name, or phrase that the actual situation supplies. Let its syllables, rhythm, or ordinary meaning open an association that the narrator can follow through syntax: a repeated vowel can carry a character's irritation, a misheard word can branch into a remembered public slogan, a familiar name can gather the gossip attached to it. Keep the source traceable to an actual perception, memory or available narrator association; it need not be physically present. Preserve literal facts while figurative or remembered meanings can persist or develop.\n\nLet recurrence develop through sentence shape, whether a character notices the motif or remains unaware of it. A phrase can return in a longer clause when the situation slows, or in a compressed clause when someone interrupts. Set the phrase's plain use beside accumulated associations for comic proportion, whether they fit or diverge. A character may remain entirely unaware of the pattern while the reader hears it.\n\nDistinguish lyric invention from established world facts. A coined comparison or imagined history belongs to the narrator or the focal mind that produces it; it does not alter what actually happened, who said what, or what an object is. When a character speaks, keep their own register. The narrator's figurative reach does not make the cast fluent in it. Let the scene's actual aims, blind spots, and reception continue to organize the comedy; the lyrical movement is how the telling carries them, not a substitute for their consequence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacawap::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Salman Rushdie / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nPlace an actual earlier occasion beside the present through memory available to the active narrator or an authorized character, with ownership clear. Let the memory arrive as a specific phrase, object, or habit that the current scene calls up, with a direct summary of change available where it belongs to the actual voice, rather than required by the tone. The remembered words can be quoted exactly, or their gist can surface in the character's own present idiom. Keep the earlier knowledge and intention distinct from what the character now understands.\n\nLet the comic situation continue to operate through recognizable aims and blind spots. A remembered phrase can make the present attempt funnier because the character is repeating a pattern, or because the present occasion has outgrown the old words. The humor may be dry, affectionate, or self-mocking, but it does not require the character to announce the comparison. The reader can hear the echo while the character simply acts.\n\nPreserve present pleasure and unchanged meaning where the scene warrants. The reflective attention can deepen the current moment without turning it into a farewell. A familiar joke can still be funny; a familiar person can still be loved in the old way. Let strain or loss be present only where the actual scene supplies it. The passage may end with the present occasion still underway, its comic business unfinished, and the remembered phrase still available for a later return.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacawaq::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Salman Rushdie / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse the modern memoir route: present an actual participant's later account, distinguishing what that memoirist knew, said and expected then from what they know now. Preserve the active narrator and person, whether they are the memoirist or present the memoirist's attributed account from their existing position. When the account reaches the earlier comic expectation, render it in the terms available then: the plan, the boast, the misread signal, the confidence that a particular phrase would land. Do not let later knowledge leak into that earlier mind.\n\nLet the later public account, rumor, or interpretation arrive as a separate layer with its own attribution. The narrator may quote what others later said, compare it with what actually happened, or note where the public version has grown. The two layers can agree, differ, or remain unresolved. The comedy can come from the gap between the earlier expectation and the later account, but the narrator need not announce the lesson or resolve the discrepancy.\n\nKeep the scene's actual aims and blind spots operative in both layers. The earlier participant pursued an actual goal with the information then available, including full understanding where established. Later awareness belongs to the memoirist's present position and cannot retroactively change earlier knowledge or motives. Let the account move between recollection and present reflection through readable transitions. The passage may end with the comparison still open, the public version still circulating, and the narrator still holding a private correction or a private amusement.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacawar::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Salman Rushdie / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose a small number of exact details that can carry the comic association. A single name, object, or remembered phrase can stand for a larger history without the narrator explaining that history. Select the particular that makes the connection inferable: the one word that shows the speaker's blind spot, the one object that carries the gossip, the one remembered line that exposes the gap between intention and reception. Keep the causal link between that detail and the scene's action direct.\n\nTrim redundant explanation around the chosen detail. If the character's aim is already visible in what they do, do not restate it. If the association is already carried by the name, do not add a gloss. But keep the connection legible; omission should leave something inferable, not delete the causality that makes the comedy work. A precise extended sentence may serve better than several performed fragments.\n\nPreserve individual voice length and full actual thought. The minimalist treatment applies to selection and explanation, not to the cast's inward life. A character may think at length, feel fully, or speak in their own register. The narrator's economy does not make everyone terse. Let the comic sequence continue through actual reception and consequence. The passage may end with the chosen detail still in play, its association available to the reader without a closing summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacawas::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Salman Rushdie / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the scene first: actual conduct, talk, and perception carry the present moment. When a public phrase, name, gossip item, or imaginative comparison opens a personal association, trace it to its source. Show who said it, who repeated it, or which narrator or focal mind produced it. Keep the source ownership clear so that a character's private association does not become shared knowledge and a public rumor does not become established fact.\n\nLet the association move through readable transitions. A speaker's word can trigger a recollection; a remembered public slogan can return to the present exchange; an imagined comparison can unfold while the character continues to act. The digression need not end in a changed idea or a resumed task. The scene's actual reception shapes the comic sequence: someone may take the reference up, miss it, answer it literally, or let it pass.\n\nPreserve the active narrator's access and the cast's individual registers. The narrator's associative reach does not make every character fluent in it. Let the comic aims and blind spots continue to organize the scene. The passage may end with the present business still underway, the association still private to its owner, and the public phrase still available for a later return.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacawat::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Salman Rushdie / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nSelect the comic exchange from the supplied relationship premise. If the people share a history, use a reference that belongs to that history: a nickname, an anecdote, a private phrase, or a repeated moment that the premise establishes. If they are unfamiliar, let one person offer a new reference and let the other respond from their own position. Do not insert a roster of familiar tropes; the premise itself supplies the material.\n\nPreserve each person's response and full private associations. One character may take up the reference with delight, another may decline the play, a third may answer literally, and a fourth may feel the private weight of the memory without saying so. Shared understanding, direct affection, and comic misalignment are all available. Reader access alone does not give another character that knowledge; preserve actual disclosure, evidence and established capabilities; a familiar reference can mean something different to each person without either announcing it.\n\nLet the particular exchange express the supplied premise through its actual language, reception and private associations, with consequences only where they occur. The reference can deepen an established bond, expose a blind spot, or create a new private language. It need not produce a milestone, confession, or emotional repair. The scene may end with the reference still in play, the relationship's actual state unchanged or only locally shifted, and the comic recognition available to the reader without a closing payoff.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacawau::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Salman Rushdie / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nOpen the account with a deliberate declaration or compact phrase that places the present act in the community's retelling. Attribute the declaration to its source: a public reputation, a remembered deed, a story someone tells, or a phrase that has attached itself to the person. Then expand into the actual speech and activity of the scene. The remembered version can be accurate; the present attempt may simply be another instance of the same habit or aim.\n\nLet a recurring designation gather historical or personal associations as the scene proceeds. When the phrase returns, tie it to actual remembered history or current association; it may retain its meaning without a newly completed event and need not become an epithet at every entrance. A public name can enlarge attention to the comic particulars of the act without falsifying its scale. The person may answer the elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern, and the gap between the retelling and the present attempt can be funny without making the reputation false.\n\nKeep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective. The community's remembered version is not the same as a focal character's private account; being remembered as dependable does not mean the helping was effortless or cheerful. Let the actual aims and blind spots continue to organize the comedy. The passage may end with the present attempt or telling unfinished, without requiring another deed, a modest scale, or a declaration of legend on the community's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacawav::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Salman Rushdie / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nUse essayistic narration to follow an actual narrator-owned or attributed proposition, provisional or definite. State its scope in the appropriate voice, then set it beside a specific observed, remembered, or explicitly imagined case available within narrator access. Return to its vocabulary as the comparison develops, with confirmation and unchanged meaning available as well as qualification.\n\nGive the associative movement a source. A public phrase, song, news item, local saying, or cultural reference can open the comparison, but it must belong to the narrator's actual world or memory. Let the digression run far enough to gather energy, and reconnect it to the concrete case where the actual movement supports a return. Comic proportion can come from fitting recognition or a gap; no return, changed interpretation, or refutation is required within this reply.\n\nKeep the people inside the case pursuing their own recognizable aims with their own habits and blind spots. They do not need to know they are illustrating the narrator's claim. Their dialogue and actions remain their own register; the essayistic voice belongs to the narrator, not to every speaker. A character can be literal, dry, exuberant, or uninterested in the joke while the narrator's comparison continues around them.\n\nLet the claim-and-case movement organize selection and emphasis. When the narrator returns to a term from the opening proposition, it should now carry the weight of the specific incident. The passage may end with the claim confirmed in an unexpected way, qualified, or left productively unsettled. Do not require a refutation, a political lesson, or a new understanding as the fixed outcome.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacawaw::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Salman Rushdie / Screenplay\n\nWrite the comic sequence in concise scene blocks with clear speaker cues and action lines. Let the public action carry the immediate situation: who enters, what they handle, what they can see or hear. Keep each block readable and spatially legible, with a scene heading when location or time changes. The comedy comes from the visible pursuit of recognizable aims, not from camera commentary.\n\nLet dialogue retain each speaker's actual register and length. A sourced allusion, cultural reference, or remembered public phrase may appear in speech when that character would say it. It may also appear in an actual in-world record shown or read aloud, or in separately authorized voice-over. Keep these channels distinct: a voice-over is a performed narration with its own audience and permissions, not the character's private thought, and it does not cause the events it describes.\n\nUse cuts to juxtapose separate occasions and create comic recognition through editing. A cut can place a confident statement beside its later contradiction, or a remembered claim beside the present evidence. The characters do not observe the edit and do not need to know the connection the reader sees. The cut itself does not cause anything in the story; it only arranges what has already happened or is happening.\n\nPreserve required nonspoken experience in the existing private channel. A character's thought, reasoning, or feeling belongs there, clearly attributed without automatically becoming cast knowledge; preserve each character's actual sources and established capabilities. Do not convert it into dialogue, voice-over, or a visible action merely to fit the screenplay form. The scene can end on a completed action, a pending request, or an unfinished setup without a mandatory comic exit or reversal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacawax::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Salman Rushdie / Stage Play\n\nWrite the comic scene in a shared playable space with clear speakers, positions, and real actions. Let a character's ranging account, story, or allusion develop alongside what other people actually do. They may set a table, repair an object, sort papers, wait for something to boil, or simply listen in stillness. The comedy comes from the timing between the spoken material and the physical work, without requiring work to stop for a speech; a person may actually stop and listen or speak when that is their choice.\n\nUse props, pauses, entrances, and exits to frame the reception of a story or allusion where it occurs. A character can set down a cup at the wrong moment, continue folding laundry through a grand claim, or leave the room just before the point lands. These actions are real and staged, not symbolic commentary unless the scene supports that reading. Do not require a mandatory interruption or comic exit at every turn.\n\nDistinguish performed accounts and asides from full nonspoken experience. A monologue or aside is speech with an audience and a dramatic purpose; it is not the default destination for thought. Required private experience stays in the existing private channel, clearly attributed without automatically becoming cast knowledge; actual disclosure and established access remain possible. Offstage activity remains supported by the script's actual conditions: a character who leaves can be heard doing something, or their absence can matter, with any authorized offstage private experience still available to the reader in the separate nonspoken channel.\n\nKeep each speaker's register individual. Give ranging association to the actual teller or permitted narrator where it belongs; other speakers retain their own plain or associative registers without mandatory uniformity. Other characters can be literal, dry, impatient, or absorbed in their own task. The scene can end on a completed action, a pending request, or an unfinished setup without a compulsory comic exit or reversal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaway::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Salman Rushdie / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite the comic passage through one authorized mind's actual associative movement. Braid what the character perceives right now with what they remember, what they have heard said, what their body feels, and what they invent or imagine. Let the syntax follow those collisions: a public phrase can trigger a childhood memory, a bodily sensation can interrupt a grand comparison, and an imagined version of the present can run ahead of what is actually happening.\n\nKeep the levels distinct. Missing speech remains unknown: the character cannot hear what was not said, and the narration must not supply it as if they could. An invented comparison or imagined scene is clearly the mind's own production, not a fact in the shared world. The comedy can come from the gap between the private invention and the actual situation, but the reader must be able to tell which is which.\n\nLet the mind range without forcing time to pass or a comeback to be spoken. The character can rehearse a reply they never deliver, follow an association away from the conversation, or return to a recurring concern without resolving it. Their outward response may be delayed, minimal, or unrelated to the elaborate inward material. Preserve thought that remains unresolved or has no visible consequence; the stream is the character's experience, not a setup that must pay off in action.\n\nDo not make every character share this mind. The stream belongs to the authorized viewpoint only. Other people in the scene remain distinct speakers with their own registers, and their private experience stays inaccessible unless separately authorized. The passage can end inside the ongoing thought, at an interruption, or at a shift of attention without requiring a completed insight or a spoken punchline.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacawaz::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Salman Rushdie / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the comic exchange through attributed sent messages in actual channel order. Each person's digital habits shape their contribution: one may send long associative paragraphs, another short replies, another a forwarded link with no comment. The comedy comes from the timing of what actually arrives and how it lands, not from invented interface events. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the sequence.\n\nTrack forwarded wording and an allusion's known source. When a reference circulates, preserve what it actually says and where it came from. Characters can share an accurate understanding of the same source, or one can misread it for a supported reason. The humor may come from the gap between the original context and the new use, with known original context preserved and inaccessible sources left unknown rather than supplied for a joke.\n\nSeparate genuinely composed drafts, attachments, and live private experience from transmitted text. An unsent draft is a composed artifact with its own wording; it is distinct from live private thought and has not been transmitted. Another person can access the draft only through an actual source such as permitted screen visibility, not merely because the reader sees it. Private thought belongs in the existing private channel, clearly attributed and outside the transcript. Do not invent a typing pause, read receipt, or deletion to create subtext or comic timing.\n\nLet the exchange continue through actual message reception. A reply can arrive late, cross with another message, or fail to land as intended. The active boundary may fall within an ongoing exchange, an unsent draft, or a private thought without requiring a punchline, a cliffhanger, or a forced choice at the end.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacawba::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Salman Rushdie / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the comic scene with concise setting and readable dialogue attribution. Establish the visible space and the people in it, then let the exchange move between speech and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Background or expression cues are purposeful references to what the scene shows; they are not executable engine commands or a mandatory label on every line.\n\nLet a phrase, background feature, or expression open remembered or imaginative material in the focal character's mind. A poster on the wall can trigger a public history, a familiar saying can call up a family story, or another character's expression can prompt an invented comparison. The private association may run far and gather comic energy, with memory, interpretation and deliberate invention distinguished. An association does not by itself establish a world fact, but accurate recollection retains what is actually known.\n\nKeep the public cue only what it actually shows. An expression is an expression, not a truth indicator. The focal character may interpret it correctly, incorrectly, or with comic excess, without giving others that interpretation merely because the reader has it; preserve actual communication, evidence and established capabilities. The comic fit between the private association and the actual reception can remain unknown or become visible through later speech, but do not force a fixed cue-thought rotation or a required changed relationship.\n\nPreserve individual voices. The ranging, associative voice belongs to the focal narrator or the character whose inner response is authorized, not to every speaker. Other characters can be literal, dry, exuberant, or uninterested in the joke. The scene can end on a completed action, a pending request, or an unfinished setup without a branching menu, player decision, or compulsory comic reversal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacawbb::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Salman Rushdie / Web Serial\n\nWrite each installment as part of a readable through-line. Establish what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. When a later installment needs an earlier fact, recover the relevant source and circumstances briefly: the name, the account, the object, or the promise that now matters. Do not retell the whole history that first established it.\n\nPreserve earlier knowledge and actual intent. An actual established change may affect current recall; do not invent forgetting to restart a joke. A statement's original purpose remains its earlier purpose even if the person later adopts another one. New information can qualify an old understanding without replacing the old event or making the character deliberately forget a supported conclusion. The comedy can come from the return of a familiar name or account under new conditions, but the return must be consistent with what was already established.\n\nLet a topic or digression continue, recur, or end through actual events. An associative passage can run across installments, resurface when a related detail appears, or simply stop when the scene moves on. Full private experience can run independently of the public through-line: a character's thought may continue while the visible action pauses, or it may remain unresolved without requiring a payoff in the same installment.\n\nDo not require a new meaning or payoff at every return. A recurring reference can be simply present, accumulating weight without being explained again. The active boundary may fall within ongoing speech, action, thought, or composition. The installment can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known, without an artificial cliffhanger or a complete miniature chapter.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaxaa::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Stephen King / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nLet a character approach an admission, request, apology, or withheld concern through the particular habits and history already established for them. Arrange the relation between any actual preparation and reception for comic timing, allowing an exact familiar fit as well as a gap. A rehearsed speech can meet a practical answer; a familiar phrase can land with exact timing and still cost something to say. Build the humor from that specific mismatch or fit, not from inventing an embarrassing past incident or forcing a humiliating response.\n\nGround the emotional exposure in concrete routine and readable inward movement. Use the actual gesture, recollection, private risk or other particular that belongs before, during or after speaking, without requiring an inventory of all of them. Let the narration move between outward events and inward concern so the reader can feel the weight of the ask in its actual register, including solemnity where it occurs. Direct feeling, real relief, and continued strain are all available outcomes; the joke does not cancel the cost, and the cost does not require a confession or a changed relationship.\n\nWhen a familiar response lands, let its comic effect come from the reader recognizing the person behind it. A character can answer with the exact practical tone they always use, and that can be funnier than any punchline because it is true to them. Preserve the distinction between what the viewpoint character knows and what others can see. A private fear, rehearsed line or relief remains that person's experience; others gain knowledge only through actual supported access, not merely because narration presents it. End where the active scene boundary falls, even if the request is still hanging or the other person has not answered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaxab::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Stephen King / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the shared interval in readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution. Keep place, activity, and speaker clear so the reader can follow who is doing what without losing the collaborative flow. Let a familiar routine, a remembered comment, or a recognizable habit create the comic relation to what actually happens next. The humor should come from the fit or mismatch between a person's established way of doing things and the present response, not from random interruption or forced banter.\n\nKeep NPC lives active around the joke. A character can be pursuing their own practical aim while the comic moment unfolds, and their cooperation or competence remains genuinely available. Sincere help is not a punchline by default. When a memory or recollection enters, let it belong to the specific person and the present occasion; it does not need to appear in every turn or explain the whole scene.\n\nPreserve the active narrator, player ownership, and full private experience. A character's inward concern, hesitation, or recognition remains their own unless the contract permits another character to perceive it. The response can end within continuing public or inward activity: someone may still be speaking, working, waiting, or thinking when the reply stops. Do not force a question, cliffhanger, or completed exchange at the boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaxac::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Stephen King / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse vernacular episodic telling as the definite route. Situate each occasion in direct prose and give actual movement, explanation, or shared activity enough room to unfold. Let a known habit, phrase, or remembered incident connect the present occasion to an earlier one. The comic effect can come from familiar fit as much as from a supported change: a person may do the same thing again, with the familiar recognition or an actual new context making it funny without reducing the character to a fixed joke.\n\nKeep the language and community of the supplied setting distinct. Do not transplant a period, cast identity, or scenery merely because the style is selected. The episodic structure means the current encounter can stand as a legible piece of a longer sequence, with its own concrete particulars and its own relation to what came before. A callback may deepen recognition or sustain a familiar gag; preserve its actual prior use without requiring novelty or changed meaning.\n\nPreserve source knowledge, real stakes, and private experience. A character's recollection or inward concern remains their own, and the narration may move between outward events and private thought as the active contract permits. No response owes a fresh anecdote, a completed episode, a moral, or a transplanted setting. The scene can end within continuing activity, with the next occasion still ahead.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaxad::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Stephen King / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nUse forceful verbs, concrete nouns, and compressed transitions to render real contact, effort, and condition. The comic situation forms in the physical execution of a familiar routine: a practiced habit can work exactly, or it can meet a supported awkward detail, and the rhythm of the prose makes the relation funny without inventing damage or failure. Let the material wear and practical stakes stay actual. A difficult job remains difficult even when the moment is funny.\n\nChange rhythm deliberately. A longer thought can follow a compressed action when the character's reasoning needs the room, but keep the transitions tight enough that the reader feels the weight and speed of the work. Ground abrasive or rough speech in its speaker; not every character talks the same way, and the joke does not require everyone to become hard-edged.\n\nPreserve the world's actual forces and the character's private experience. Developed thought and feeling remain nonspoken interiority rather than bodily tells or public knowledge. A character can be privately amused, worried, or relieved while their hands keep working. No completion, recognition, or changed plan is required at the boundary; the scene can end with the work still underway.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaxae::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Stephen King / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through documents with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Use the specific document form the scene requires—a note, account, instruction, reply, or annotation—rather than rotating through formats. Let a routine piece of writing meet a recipient's real concern through a later reply or annotation where that actually occurs. The comic effect can come from literal competence as much as from mismatch: an instruction followed exactly, a note answered with unexpected thoroughness, or an account that records more than the writer realized.\n\nKeep circulation, earlier knowledge, and later additions distinct. What a document knows or conceals follows its author's situation at the time of writing. A later annotation cannot change what the original author knew. Gaps and revisions can reveal events across entries, but do not add an omniscient explanation after every record.\n\nPreserve the active narrator and full private experience beyond the artifacts. A document is a composed object, not the writer's complete live mind, and required interiority remains available in the active presentation according to the authorship contract. No found secret, contradictory file, or completed exchange is owed. The sequence can stop with a document still in circulation or a reply still unwritten.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaxaf::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Stephen King / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend attention on chosen kindness, familiar preferences, practical care, ease, and small pleasures. Let a remembered preference invite a familiar joke or an unexpectedly exact act of care. The comic effect comes from the particular people involved: one person knows exactly how another takes their tea, or brings the wrong thing for the right reason, and the reception can be warm, indifferent, or otherwise supported by the actual relationship. Affection stays sincere without requiring a confession or emotional repair.\n\nKeep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable, and a private reservation can sit alongside a shared pleasure without spoiling the scene. The humor does not erase real problems from the world or invent a cozy setting where none exists. A character can be genuinely helped and still annoyed, or genuinely amused and still tired.\n\nGround the kindness in concrete routine and practical detail. Show the specific act—the extra blanket, the remembered sandwich, the chair pulled closer—rather than relying on a generic announcement of warmth; actual affectionate words or direct feeling remain available. Preserve full private experience under the active contract: a character's inward relief, amusement or reservation remains their own, with others' knowledge following actual supported access rather than narration alone. No gratitude, improved mood, or completed exchange is required at the boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaxag::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Stephen King / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse sustained paragraphs and deliberate transitions to frame the current occasion through available history, social context, or judgment where the chosen narrator permits it. Let a concrete act, remark, or authorized private association make the relation to that frame recognizable. The comic effect can come from the scale of the meeting: a long local history, a public expectation, or a remembered precedent runs into the exact present routine, and the prose holds both in view without requiring a public verdict.\n\nKeep cast voices individual within the formal narration. Plain or colloquial speech belongs where it belongs to the actual person; the narrator's composed register does not make every character speak the same way. Distinguish narration from ordinary spoken language, but let dialogue carry a person's history rather than a universal narrator voice.\n\nMove between outward events and inward concern with readable transitions. A recollection or private association can open the scene's context without turning into a fixed lesson. Historical or inward development may stay unfinished: the occasion can end with the wider meaning still unsettled, the routine still underway, or the private thought still turning. No return outward, changed meaning, or closing joke is needed before the active boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaxah::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Stephen King / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the scene as a situated act of telling. Establish who is speaking, who is listening, and what the teller wants from this particular audience: to pass time, explain a local arrangement, defend a choice, or make someone understand a familiar person. Let the purpose shape what gets selected, but do not let it turn the anecdote into a polished lesson.\n\nBuild the comic situation from a recognizable routine or remembered incident involving people known through the teller's actual experience or other supported sources, without requiring close acquaintance. Give the routine concrete practical detail: the order in which work is done, the phrase someone always uses, the object that must be moved, the meal that gets prepared the same way. Let the teller establish an expectation through that detail, then follow what actually happened. The humor can come from a perfect fit, a small supported discrepancy, or the audience recognizing the person before the teller names them.\n\nMake the listeners active, not merely present. They can agree, correct a detail, supply the ending, laugh at the wrong moment, or ask a practical question that reveals they were attending to a different part of the story. Their reception is part of the comic event. A listener's accurate anticipation can carry the humor as well as a reversal can.\n\nKeep the teller's outward performance separate from private experience. The anecdote may leave out what the teller felt, did not understand, or only learned later. If the active narrator has access to that interiority, render it in its own register: the teller can be performing ease while privately remembering the actual cost, or can enjoy the retelling while knowing the story has changed in the telling. Do not require a false story, an interrupted anecdote, or a completed return to the outer frame.\n\nUse recurring phrases or objects where they belong to the teller's actual habits, personal meaning or the relation between embedded story and present scene. A familiar line can recur because the teller always says it that way, not because it certifies the story as true. Follow the active response boundary during telling, reception or continuing private experience, without requiring a completed comic relation, anecdote or return to the frame.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaxai::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Stephen King / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse documentary and historical-memory prose as the organizing method. When the scene contains or can legitimately refer to a record—a notice, a log entry, a report, a posted rule, a letter, a receipt—present its exact wording as an authored artifact with a clear purpose and time of composition. Bring it into relation with authorized recollection or present experience of the occasion, following actual attention rather than a required record-before-memory order.\n\nProportion can carry the comic relation without proving the record wrong, while any actual discovery of error remains available. A precise official account and an ordinary remembered detail can both be true. Preserve the record's actual register, completeness and purpose rather than assigning it an impersonal or formal voice. Write the memory in the person's actual voice, with bodily circumstance, private concern or practical routine that may overlap with or extend beyond what the record actually includes. Use the relation between the two for comic scale without requiring a gap. The humor can be gentle recognition or sharper discrepancy, but do not force the record to be exposed as false.\n\nKeep later knowledge distinct from earlier experience. If the narrator or participant later understands something about the recorded event, mark when that understanding was gained, preserving what the participant actually knew then and knows now. Preserve the active narrator's access and diction independently of the record's author or any imagined reader. A character can recall the smell of the room, the waiting, or the exact way a colleague spoke, without turning the memory into a lesson about the record.\n\nDo not require an investigation, a discovered error, or a practical payoff. The scene can simply hold the record and the memory together long enough for the reader to see the difference in scale. Follow the active response boundary during record, recollection or ongoing private experience without requiring legibility of a completed juxtaposition, a correction or a resolved memory.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaxaj::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Stephen King / Gothic-Punk\n\nGive the inherited public meaning of a place or institution its actual material presence. A building has a history, a title carries weight, a custom has survived because someone maintains it. Write the atmosphere through concrete detail: the worn step, the framed photograph, the rule everyone knows but no one can explain. Let atmosphere shape actual attention, including meaningful sensory presence without a task function; people may accommodate, ignore or resist the inherited arrangement as supported.\n\nThen bring a familiar routine or private preference into contact with that inherited meaning. Give the routine its particular owner's actual life and circumstances: the way someone makes coffee, the route they walk, the tool they always use, the personal reason they keep a particular habit. Let the meeting be exact. The humor can come from a perfect fit—the old custom turns out to serve the private preference—or from a supported discrepancy—the public meaning is too large or too small for what the person actually needs.\n\nKeep power, permission, and motive distinct. A person can respect the old authority, ignore it, maintain it, or privately find it ridiculous without being forced into a confrontation. Actual helpfulness remains possible: someone can show another person how the place works, or quietly fix what the old arrangement overlooked. Private desire and social masks can coexist; a character may perform the expected deference while thinking about lunch, or may find that the trusted company in the room makes the performance unnecessary.\n\nDo not import ominous weather, a small American town, a forced unmasking, or any new supernatural rule. Its social and material presence adds no magic by itself; preserve any authority or power the supplied world actually contains. Follow the active boundary during the encounter or ongoing private experience, without requiring a completed comic relation, fallen authority or changed routine.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaxak::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Stephen King / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse social and domestic realism as the organizing method. Follow the actual arrangement of a shared space or household: who does what, when it gets done, which objects belong to whom, and how people negotiate the use of a room, a kitchen, a vehicle, or a stretch of time. Make the arrangement concrete through practical detail—the order of tasks, the waiting, the small adjustments people make for one another.\n\nBuild the comic situation from the exact habits by which particular people give time, care, or work. Supported habits might include checking something twice, leaving a recognizable unfinished task or offering help in a form the recipient cannot use; none is required, and actual helpful competence remains possible. Let the humor come from the familiar or surprising reception of those habits: the person who always anticipates correctly, the person who misunderstands in a supported way, or the moment when the routine works so well that everyone notices.\n\nLet speech carry individual history rather than a generic age or role. A character's way of asking for help, refusing it, or commenting on the work should sound like that person's accumulated experience. Do not assign stereotyped domestic roles or dependence through the style; preserve any actual support needs, capacity and agency. Each person retains capacity, willingness, and permission; care can work, and help can be accepted without hidden resentment.\n\nPreserve full private experience. A character can be patient outwardly while privately counting the minutes, or can enjoy a familiar routine while another person finds it unbearable. The narrator's access and diction remain the active contract's. Do not require a generic family structure, a changed arrangement, or a resolution. Follow the active response boundary during work, conversation or continuing private experience without requiring the comic relation to reach a completed presentation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaxal::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Stephen King / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse diary and reflective miscellany through the miscellany method: compose the scene as a sequence of selected observations rather than a continuous plot report. Each piece of attention belongs to a particular time and owner. A person notices the way a colleague sets down a cup, the exact pause before a familiar reply, the object left in the wrong place, the sound of a routine being done in another room. Give each observation its own weight without forcing it to lead somewhere.\n\nThe comic relation comes from the precise place of a familiar detail in actual attention. A plain noticed thing can be funny because of what it sits next to: the grand plan followed by the small practical obstacle, the formal announcement received while someone is looking for a pen, the remembered phrase arriving in the middle of an unrelated task. Let the juxtaposition be the humor. The detail does not need to trigger a memory, produce an insight, or change an action.\n\nAllow recollection and unfinished association to appear when they belong to the authorized mind. A character can remember a previous occasion without the memory becoming the point of the scene. The narrator can notice something and leave the thought incomplete. Keep the causal order of actual events intact: what happened earlier remains earlier, and later understanding does not rewrite it.\n\nDo not require a written diary artifact, a seasonal inventory, universal quietness, or a completed observation-to-memory-to-action cycle. The narrator need not be a diarist; the miscellany is a method of selection and attention, not a document. Follow the active boundary during any selected observation or continuing private experience, before or after its comic relation becomes apparent, without requiring a summary or return to the opening detail.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaxam::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Stephen King / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse social realism and chronicle as the organizing method. Follow an established public or shared occasion through legible time: a meeting, a celebration, a workday, a civic event, a journey. Make the occasion's actual sequence and purpose legible, including any announced order where present, alongside the ordinary business of particular people; the occasion need not be official or formal.\n\nThe comic relation comes from scale. Preserve any actual public account or ceremonial language in its own register, and relate it to the sequence through practical detail and known local history in the order the account supports: the person who arrives early to secure a seat, the small dispute over a borrowed item, the private concern that keeps surfacing during the formal proceedings. The official event and the private concern may agree or differ without requiring an exposure. The humor can be the simple disproportion between what the occasion is supposed to mean and what it actually costs the people in it.\n\nUse sourced accounts where the narrator has access to them. A character can report what they saw, what they were told, or what they remember from an earlier occasion. Distinguish personal participation from reported events: what the narrator witnessed, what someone else described, and what is public knowledge remain separate. Preserve actual narrator access and full nonspoken experience.\n\nDo not invent a town history, a collective mind, a marvelous event, or a complete chronicle segment. The occasion can remain partial; the scene need not cover its whole span. Follow the active response boundary during the sourced sequence or continuing private experience, without requiring a completed contrast, concluded occasion or resolved concern.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaxan::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Stephen King / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Keep the people, place and current concern legible in the actual course of the scene. Introduce unfamiliar names or practical details with the explanation needed for understanding or preparation, without a fixed opening inventory. Keep spatial changes easy to follow: when someone moves from one room to another, picks up an object, or turns toward a speaker, make the movement legible through concrete prose suited to the actual sequence.\n\nBuild the comic situation from a familiar habit and its actual reception. Establish the habit through recognizable routine: the way a person always starts a task, the phrase they use without thinking, the order in which they check their things. Then stage the reception clearly. Let the apt familiar reply or the supported mismatch land through the sequence of action, dialogue, and response. The humor can come from a perfect anticipation, a small practical collision, or the moment when everyone recognizes the habit except its owner.\n\nUse flexible paragraph rhythm. Break around real action, dialogue, and inward experience so the reader can follow the beat without the prose becoming choppy. A comic line can land at the end of a short paragraph, but a longer thought or a meaningful recollection can occupy its own space when the character's meaningful reasoning, feeling or association needs it. Do not compress the inner life to make the staging faster.\n\nPreserve extensive thought and meaningful history. A character can be funny in public while privately working through something unrelated, or can find the familiar routine comforting while another person finds it unbearable. Let each person's actual diction carry their history. Do not impose a young cast, a short reaction, a first-person switch, a new power, or a closing hook. Follow the active boundary during action, dialogue or continuing private experience, without requiring a completed comic relation, punchline or cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaxao::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Stephen King / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet the comedy arise inside the actual sound and image movement of the scene. Choose a motif that belongs to the present moment: a repeated noise, a familiar object, a phrase someone keeps half-saying, or a physical sensation that returns with a difference. Develop it through rhythm and the viewpoint character's attention, so the recurrence gathers the practical history attached to it. The motif is not a symbol to be solved; it is a shaped detail that can carry recognition, embarrassment, pleasure, or private concern without announcing what it means.\n\nKeep the personal source of the motif legible. If a sound matters, let it be the sound of this particular place or person: a chair, a machine, a voice, a habit. If an image matters, let it come from what the viewpoint actually sees or remembers. The lyrical movement should not replace the ordinary world with decorative language. A plain statement or practical detail can meet the resonant phrase with exact fit or real disproportion, and that meeting is where the comedy lives. The fit may be satisfying, the disproportion may be funny, and neither needs to be explained afterward.\n\nPreserve the cast's individual registers. One person may speak in the narrator's figurative register; another may answer in flat practical terms, and the contrast can be comic without making the plain speaker a punchline. Do not turn every character into a lyricist or force a witty exchange after each beat. Let the viewpoint's private feeling remain direct and particular. A thought can be long enough to follow the association that matters, but it does not need to end in a corrected meaning, a new memory, or an outward action. The scene may close with the image still plain or unfinished, leaving the next moment available without requiring a return to the motif.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaxap::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Stephen King / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nBuild the scene around a known routine, habit, or phrase that the viewpoint character actually performs, varies, omits, or privately recalls. Let the actual present relation to the routine carry the comedy, whether performed, varied, omitted or privately recalled without a new act. If performed, it may work, fail in a supported way or meet someone who knows its history. The reflective weight comes from what the routine has meant before, not from a general announcement that time passes. Keep the earlier occasions concrete. A former pleasure can remain genuinely pleasurable in memory even when the present version is smaller, stranger, or funnier.\n\nLet the viewpoint's attention move between the present action and what is no longer available. The shift can be triggered by a detail, a voice, an absence, or the feel of doing the thing itself. Do not make every recollection sad or every present moment diminished. The comedy may come from the exact difference between then and now, from the persistence of the habit despite changed circumstances, or from another person's different relation to the same routine. The feeling and the humor can share the same beat.\n\nPreserve what the character actually knows and feels. Memory may be clear or partial, and later knowledge must not be inserted into an earlier occasion. Do not invent a loss, a failed care, or a nostalgia the supplied world does not support. The scene does not need to end with a confession, a repaired relationship, a completed lesson about time, or a new act that resolves the feeling. It can stop with the routine resumed, interrupted, or simply remembered, leaving the present and the past both in view.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaxaq::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Stephen King / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse the modern memoir route. The scene is a composed account written later by a particular memoirist, and the writing situation must be legible: who is remembering, for what purpose, and at what distance from the events. Keep the active narrator's person and ownership intact. If the story is being told by a participant, the memoirist's later voice may frame the scene, but the remembered moment itself must retain what that person knew and felt at the time.\n\nLet the comedy come from the accurate recognition of a familiar routine or relationship. The memoirist can see the shape of the old habit, the exact way a person spoke, or the practical detail that made the situation absurd, without granting the earlier self a wisdom it did not have. The later account may notice a pattern the participant missed, but that noticing belongs to the time of composition. Do not let it rewrite the earlier knowledge or turn the past self into a fool for not understanding what only later became clear.\n\nPreserve the ordinary world of the remembered scene: the local relationships, practical work, and personal recollections that give the routine its weight. The memoir form does not require a confession, an exile, an unreliable memory, or a completed retrospective judgment. The scene may end with the memoirist still uncertain, amused, or attached to the old occasion. Any private experience that the active contract authorizes remains available beyond what the memoir itself records; the document is an artifact, not the whole mind of its author.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaxar::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Stephen King / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect the few exact details that make the comic relation inferable. A habit, a remembered phrase, or a practical object can carry the recognition, but only if the chosen particulars show the real relation between routine, expectation, and reception. Use direct clauses to set the familiar fit or the supported difference side by side. The comedy should be visible in what is selected and what is placed next to it, not in a gloss that explains why it is funny.\n\nPreserve the causes and the relevant history. Omission should leave something inferable, not delete the reason a person acts or the past that gives the routine its meaning. If a character's private thought or feeling matters, give it the space it needs. Minimalist prose does not require hidden emotion, small stakes, or a deflating last line. A precise extended sentence may serve the scene better than several performed fragments, and meaningful reasoning, feeling or association may develop at length without an action or comic payoff.\n\nKeep the cast individual. One person's economy of speech is not everyone's; a plain speaker and a talkative one can meet in the same scene without the style flattening their registers. Follow the active response boundary during ongoing public or private experience, before or after the relation becomes clear; neither the situation nor a result must be complete. Do not add a final line merely to close the joke or to make the omission feel deliberate.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaxas::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Stephen King / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the scene in continuous prose that moves naturally through action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized private experience in the actual causal order. Let a routine, a comment, or a memory establish an expectation, then let the actual reception confirm or qualify it. The comedy comes from that relation: the familiar habit used in a new situation, the known phrase answered in an unexpected register, or the private concern running alongside the outward business. Do not pause the scene to explain the joke or to make every beat witty.\n\nGive inward concern the extent it needs. A character may think through a problem, remember a past occasion, or notice a discrepancy while the conversation continues. The thought does not need a fresh trigger or an outward payoff to justify its presence. Let the viewpoint's history and the other characters' independent concerns remain active. One person's blind spot may be comic, but it must be that person's actual habit, not a generic failure inserted to produce a reversal.\n\nPreserve the real stakes and the active narrator. The world remains consequential enough for the problem to stay playable, and the scene does not need to end with a completed result, a joke after every beat, or an ominous turn. Stop at the active boundary with the situation still moving, leaving the next moment available without forcing a cliffhanger or a lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaxat::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Stephen King / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nChoose scenes and callbacks from the actual supplied bond premise, including group relationships and shared history. Let the comedy come from the precise knowledge the participants have of one another: a known preference, a private phrase, or a practiced act that receives an exactly familiar or surprisingly different response. The callback fulfills the premise when it shows the particular people involved, not when it repeats a generic trope. A routine can be funny because one person knows exactly how the other will react, or because the reaction is unexpectedly unlike the established pattern.\n\nKeep each participant's motives and knowledge distinct. One person may find the familiar moment comforting while another finds it irritating, funny, or beside the point. Do not make the relationship a single shared mind or require the characters to reach the same understanding. Private experience remains private; the narration may reveal a focal thought without making it known to the other person. The comedy can come from the gap between what one character intends and what the other actually notices.\n\nDo not insert jealousy, a rescue debt, a confession, unequal care, or compulsory growth to make the callback feel meaningful. The scene may end with the relationship unchanged, the routine still in place, or the participants simply continuing their shared or separate business. The bond promise is fulfilled by the particular scene and its consequences, not by a required emotional payoff.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaxau::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Stephen King / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the scene the cadence of a retold account. Open with a compact declaration that establishes the act or the person, then expand into the concrete circumstances that make the reputation particular. A supported designation should come from an actual deed or way of speaking, not from an ornamental epithet. The comedy can live in the gap between the elevated account and the ordinary particulars: the heroic-sounding errand that was mostly waiting, the famous repair that began with a wrong tool, or the remembered speech that the speaker now denies saying.\n\nLet recurrence carry the story. A phrase or designation may return through a new application or a familiar continuing use, without gaining a new meaning each time. The same act can be remembered differently by different people, and the account can hold those differences without resolving them. Keep public reputation distinct from the focal character's private experience. Being remembered as dependable does not mean the helping was effortless, cheerful, or automatic, and the private thought may be funnier or more tired than the public version.\n\nLet actual dialogue meet the retelling where it occurs: a speaker may agree, qualify the account or address another concern, and the telling may also continue uninterrupted. The subject of an oft-told incident may object to one detail or answer an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern. This need not become parody. Do not impose mock combat or heroic doom on ordinary work; retain any actual play, conflict or consequence the scene supports. Preserve the teller's access and the active narrator. Follow the active boundary during a deed, retelling or ongoing private experience, whether business is concluded or underway; no next modest act or legendary status must be supplied.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaxav::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Stephen King / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the scene as a folktale-inflected telling anchored in the actual world already supplied. Establish one recurring condition, customary phrase, or recognizable situation early enough that the audience can hold it: a particular door that sticks, a neighbor's fixed way of announcing himself, a work task that always begins with the same small argument. Keep the condition grounded in the supplied practical life, relationships and world forces, including established magic without adding it merely for the tale pattern.\n\nLet the recurrence return through actual choices and practical detail. When the familiar condition appears again, show its actual reception and use through permitted observation or private access, without requiring anyone to notice or ignore it for the pattern. A character may fulfill the pattern exactly, vary it without comment, or resist it for a private reason. The comic effect comes from the fit or disproportion between the known pattern and the real response, not from an invented hidden price or a forced reversal.\n\nPreserve the characters' independent histories and current aims. Dialogue carries a person's particular past and present concern rather than a universal narrator voice. A recollection may explain why a routine matters to someone without turning the scene into a lesson. Practical detail such as the sound of a latch, the order of tools, or the time a message usually arrives can carry recognition.\n\nDo not import a quest, triple trial, new magic, changed pattern, or completed return. The recurrence may remain unfinished, be met with plain competence, or produce no public acknowledgment. The scene can end during ongoing work, speech, or private thought at the active boundary without resolving the pattern or delivering a closing comic beat.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaxaw::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Stephen King / Screenplay\n\nUse concise action lines, clear speaker cues, and scene headings when location or time changes. Establish the recognizable habit through public, filmable material: a character arranges tools in a fixed order, takes the same seat, repeats a familiar phrase while opening a door, or performs a routine errand with visible care. Make the public pattern visible without an added redundant explanation, while preserving any separately authorized narration the sequence actually uses.\n\nJuxtapose the habit with its actual use or reception in a later scene or held beat. A cut may connect the preparation in one location with the result in another, or a held beat may let the routine and the response occupy the same frame. The comic relation can be one of exact fit, calm variation, or unremarked disproportion. Do not require a reaction shot, a failure, or a laugh cue to certify the beat.\n\nKeep prior knowledge and real changes intact across cuts. If a character repaired an object or learned a fact earlier, preserve that state and any actual subsequent change. A cut alone erases neither continuity nor a supported conclusion, while real damage or forgetting remains possible when the story supports it.\n\nPresent required private experience as clearly attributed nonspoken material separate from voice-over or dialogue. Another character does not know a thought merely because the reader has seen it. The active boundary may arrive before reception or a completed comic beat; the scene can end during continuing action or speech without a closing reaction.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaxax::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Stephen King / Stage Play\n\nWrite the scene with clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be staged. Make each person's actual position, activity or stillness legible while dialogue proceeds, without assigning a practical task merely because they are present. Keep stage directions economical and playable; do not add film-camera instructions.\n\nLet the comic relation come from the exact physical business carried out beside a familiar line. A character may deliver an ordinary greeting while folding the same cloth, set down a cup at the precise moment another person makes a request, or continue a habitual task through an explanatory remark. The line and the business do not need to contrast or interrupt each other. Timing can come from their exact fit, a small variation, or the audience's recognition of a routine the characters treat as normal.\n\nLet each speaker's actual history inform their diction, preserving explanatory remarks where they occur rather than assigning one to everyone. Dialogue carries a person's past and present concern rather than a universal narrator voice. A character may explain a habit, misjudge another's reason, or say nothing about what the audience can see.\n\nPreserve required private experience separately from performed asides or monologues. An aside has an audience and a dramatic purpose; it is not the default destination for thought. The active boundary may arrive during ongoing speech, work, or thought without requiring an in-world pause or a completed comic beat.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaxay::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Stephen King / Stream of Consciousness\n\nFollow the authorized mind's actual associations through perception, memory, private wording, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern. Anchor each movement clearly enough that the reader can distinguish a present event from a recollection or an imagined possibility. Let syntax and paragraph shape follow the mind's movement while retaining that orientation.\n\nA familiar routine or remembered phrase can enter the braid and meet the present moment with aptness or disproportion. A character may hear a phrase from childhood while performing the same task, notice that a present object is smaller than memory made it, or carry a habitual worry into an ordinary exchange. The comic relation need not be announced or resolved. Plain attention, an unfinished association, or a calm return to the present task remains possible.\n\nPreserve separately authorized shifts without merging minds. If the narration moves to another character's interiority, mark the shift according to the active contract and keep each person's associations distinct. Full thought has its own extent; prose length alone sets no fictional elapsed time.\n\nDo not require a trigger chain, a new insight, a changed act, or a public reply. The scene can end during an ongoing association, a bodily sensation, or an interrupted memory at the active boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaxaz::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Stephen King / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nPresent the exchange through readable sender-attributed messages. Include only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the scene. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one may write in full sentences, another may send fragments, a third may leave a message unsent.\n\nLet wording, a remembered phrase, or a routine request meet a real reply or pending reception. The comic relation can come from exact fit, calm literal understanding, a habitual sign-off, or the ordinary timing of a familiar exchange. Smooth understanding is as available as mismatch. Do not invent a delay, misread receipt, or misunderstanding to create subtext.\n\nPreserve prior knowledge, drafts, and supported access through other means. A sent message is distinct from an unsent draft, and private thought is distinct from transmitted text. No thought is automatically transmitted. If the active contract permits, present full private experience outside the channel in clearly separated prose; it is not something the recipient can read.\n\nThe boundary may arrive before composing, sending, or receiving is complete. The scene can end with a message half-written, a reply pending, or a sender still deciding what to type.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaxba::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Stephen King / Visual Novel\n\nOrient the scene with concise setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Establish the immediate location and who is present without over-describing. Let the focal character's attention select what matters.\n\nA supported object or expression cue may connect a known routine or recollection to its current use. The cue can be a familiar tool, a particular look, or a repeated gesture. Show what the focal character does with that connection: they may understand the relation, misread it, or miss it entirely. The cue anchors attention; it does not certify a motive or force a reaction.\n\nKeep expressions from functioning as truth indicators. A smile or frown may accompany a lie, a truth, or an unrelated concern. A character may form a fallible inference from an expression or other actual evidence; the inference does not prove the motive, and independently established facts keep their certainty.\n\nPreserve established knowledge and full private experience. Another character does not know a focal thought merely because the reader has seen it. Do not activate branching menus, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands. A memory need not follow each observation, and the scene can end during ongoing speech, action, or thought at the active boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaxbb::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Stephen King / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across installments: what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover prior context only where re-entry needs it, and recover the necessary particular rather than retelling the whole earlier sequence. Keep original events, known habits, and later changes distinct.\n\nA routine or remembered incident can return as a comic callback. A familiar successful response may recur without new information or escalation: the same person answers the same request in the same way, a repaired object performs its old function, or a remembered phrase lands again in a new but recognizable situation. The callback earns its effect from continuity, not from a fresh contradiction or a hidden twist.\n\nPreserve independent lives and meaningful private experience beyond the callback. Characters continue their practical work, relationships, and private concerns even when the installment centers on a recurring routine. Actual changed or unfinished concerns remain available to continue.\n\nDo not reset competence, add a fresh ominous clue, or force a miniature chapter ending or cliffhanger at the active boundary. The installment can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacayaa::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Terry Pratchett / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nPlace an actual costly request beside a relevant social account of independence, duty, apology or help. Attribute that account to its real speaker, community context or permitted narrator; the requester need not personally hold a theory about needing or claim to be fine. Keep the account and actual asking close enough for their particular relation to be readable. The comedy comes from the precise practical fit between them—a request that is accurate but badly timed, a declaration of independence followed by a specific small dependence, an apology that names the wrong part of the hurt while still being sincerely meant.\n\nDo not require the claim to be false or the need hidden; preserve any actual falsehood or withholding the person and circumstances support. The character can know exactly what they need and still find the asking costly. Render the inward cost directly: the hesitation before speaking, the rehearsed sentence that comes out altered, the relief or shame after the words are out. Let that private experience develop as it actually does, remaining unresolved or reaching a real understanding without either result being required. The scene may end without a decision, a confession, a repaired bond, or a lighter mood. Warmth and pain can occupy the same exchange.\n\nTime the comic recognition through the other person's response. A precise practical answer can be funny through its exact familiar fit, an unexpected but apt offer, or an actual difference of emphasis. The listener may understand the need and provide the requested help without requiring a wrong focus or unwanted response. Keep each character's relationship to language distinct. One may speak in careful official terms, another in blunt practical ones, another may avoid naming the need at all. The narrator does not have to translate every gap into a joke.\n\nPreserve the world's consequences. The need remains real even when the exchange is funny. A failed or awkward request can change what happens next without being punished by the narrative. Do not require the character to grow, learn, or earn relief before the active boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacayab::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Terry Pratchett / Chat Roleplay\n\nOpen by resolving the submitted material under the active authorship contract and orienting the immediate situation: where the characters are, what they were doing, and what practical aim is underway. Use readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution so each speaker's contribution is clear without stage-direction clutter.\n\nBuild the comic interval from a stated expectation, role, or claim meeting the participants' actual conduct. A character may announce how something is done, what they are owed, or what they would never do, with actual conduct or authorized consideration placing that account in context when it occurs; a complete demonstration need not arrive before the boundary. For example: the competent person doing the task slightly wrong on purpose, the official explanation colliding with the practical reality, the group cooperating accurately while each member understands the plan differently. Accurate cooperation can be as funny as a gap. Do not force every participant to become a witty commentator on the scene.\n\nKeep active NPCs pursuing their own practical lives. They may help, resist, misunderstand, or continue an unrelated task according to their own aims. Their choices do not need to serve the player's interpretation or produce a lesson. Preserve player ownership and distinct voices. Render each viewpoint character's private experience in the active presentation according to the authorship contract, including thoughts that do not surface in speech.\n\nEnd at the active boundary without a manufactured obstacle, correction, witty exchange, response, or changed understanding. The scene may continue with the same unresolved aim, an ongoing task, or a natural pause in the shared interval.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacayac::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Terry Pratchett / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and satirical realism route. Ground the scene in an actual household, workplace, or public arrangement by showing who has time, access, responsibility, or benefit. Let the comedy come from a claim about that arrangement sitting beside its real use: a rule announced for everyone but followed only by the person who wrote it, a shared duty that falls to the one who cannot refuse, a public virtue that turns out to describe a private convenience.\n\nDo not invent an unjust system, representative victim, or forced exposure. The arrangement can be ordinary and the people in it can be competent. Humor may arise from a precise fit—the person best suited to a task is also the one who least wants it—or from personal preferences that make a sensible rule absurd in practice. Give the satire a particular basis in how these people use this arrangement. A broader judgment may remain when the actual narrator or character makes it, without replacing the concrete social behavior with a stock moral.\n\nPreserve individual motives and narrator access. A character may follow a rule for reasons that have nothing to do with its stated purpose, and the narration can show that without making the character a hypocrite. Render full private feeling where the authorship contract permits it, including thoughts that contradict the public account. Keep the supplied community, language, and period distinct; do not relocate the scene or impose a generic family structure.\n\nNo changed hierarchy or moral correction is required. The scene may end with the arrangement still in place, the mismatch still visible, and the participants still living with it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacayad::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Terry Pratchett / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nUse concrete verbs and compressed transitions. Move from one action to the next without explanatory padding, letting the physical facts carry the scene: the weight of a load, the wear on a tool, the distance between where something is and where it needs to be. Where the task supports it, give the material particulars real texture—the resistance of a door, the state of a surface, the effort in a repeated motion.\n\nBuild the comedy from the terms people give an arrangement meeting its practical handling. A character may describe a job as simple, a schedule as reasonable, or a piece of equipment as reliable, and the next concrete detail shows the actual work involved. The fit can be accurate and still funny: the job is simple, but only if done in a particular order that nobody mentioned; the schedule is reasonable, but only for someone who does not also have to carry the materials. Do not invent broken equipment, a failed plan, or suffering to force the joke.\n\nKeep competence and supplied stakes intact. A character can be good at what they do and still find the official description of it absurd. Roughness belongs to its speaker, not to the narration as a universal abrasive voice. When a character's reasoning or feeling needs space, let the longer thought or inward passage stand without requiring it to change the next act. The rhythm can shift between compressed action and extended attention where the material or emotional weight justifies it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacayae::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Terry Pratchett / Epistolary / Documents\n\nUse the scene's actual document form—letter, report, record, note, or other authored artifact—with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Let the document's stated terms sit beside the real context or later uptake that gives it comic proportion. An exact instruction can be funny because the recipient follows it precisely in a way the author did not anticipate. A courteous note can be funny because the courtesy is technically correct and practically useless. A report can be funny because its official language describes an event that everyone involved experienced differently.\n\nAccurate records need no hidden flaw. The document can be truthful, competent, and well-intentioned, and the comedy can come from the gap between what it says and what its situation actually requires. Do not force a secret error, a forged signature, or a concealed motive into every record.\n\nPreserve the difference between what the document knows and what its author knew at the time of composition. A later annotation, marginal note, or appended reply belongs to its own composition moment and actual knowledge, which may remain unchanged; it does not rewrite what the original author knew or wrote. Keep each writer's register distinct: an official report sounds different from a personal letter, and neither should collapse into the narrator's voice.\n\nRender full live private experience beyond the artifact where the authorship contract requires it. The document contains what its author chose to record; the character's actual thought may be more uncertain, more affectionate, more irritated, or more aware than the written words. No second document, corrective note, revelation, or completed correspondence cycle is required before the active boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacayaf::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Terry Pratchett / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend attention on the preference, accommodation, or small pleasure that makes an offered kindness real. Use the helper's actual knowledge: the particular way someone takes their tea, the chair they prefer, the task they hate that someone else quietly does. Let the care be specific enough to belong to these people rather than a generic gesture of comfort.\n\nPosition a relevant account of what help means beside its practical delivery and reception. A character may announce that they are being looked after, that they never accept help, or that a small favor is nothing, while the scene shows the actual shape of the care: the helper doing the wrong thing with complete sincerity, the recipient accepting a kindness they just denied needing, the group's shared understanding of a routine that looks inefficient to an outsider but works perfectly for them.\n\nRecognized competent care and spontaneous offers can be funny without a forgotten worker or hidden cost. The helper can be good at helping and still be funny because their method is particular. The recipient can be genuinely comforted and still be funny because their gratitude takes an unexpected form. Do not discredit the care or the claim to force a joke.\n\nPreserve sincerity, privacy, disagreement, and full private feeling. A character may be grateful and annoyed at the same time, or comforted by something they would never admit to wanting. Keep personality and disagreement intact; comfort does not make everyone agreeable. No thanks, improvement, repayment, or emotional repair must precede the active boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacayag::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Terry Pratchett / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse sustained paragraphs and deliberate transitions to situate the occasion through the social context, history, or judgment the chosen narrator permits. Let the frame establish what the scene is supposed to be: a formal duty, a family custom, a professional role, a public occasion. The frame is not decoration; it supplies the description that the actual behavior will meet.\n\nPlace the relevant description of a role or arrangement beside the person's real conduct. A paragraph may open with the official account of how something is done, then move into the particular way this person does it. The comic proportion comes from the timing of that movement: the qualification can arrive as a precise clause at the end of a long sentence, or the accurate fit can emerge through a sequence of small observed details that confirm, clarify, or genuinely qualify the opening description without requiring a revision. Do not require a false claim, a public exposure, or a final quip.\n\nKeep the narrator and cast voices distinct. The composed frame belongs to the narrator's register; the characters speak in their own. A character may use plain practical language while the narration maintains its formal distance, and the gap between those registers can itself be part of the comedy. Do not make every character speak in the narrator's figurative register.\n\nPreserve serious stakes and full inward extent. The scene can be funny while the underlying situation remains consequential. Render the viewpoint character's private experience in the active presentation according to the authorship contract, including thoughts that do not surface in speech or action. No high-status setting, omniscient expansion, or changed interpretation is required before the active boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacayah::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Terry Pratchett / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nEstablish the outer scene first: who is telling, to whom, where, and what immediate need or pleasure makes this telling happen. The teller is a particular person with a particular register, not a neutral narrator. The listeners are also particular: one may already know the story, one may be waiting for a meal, one may be using the telling to avoid another conversation. Let their attention and interruptions be real social behavior rather than a chorus of reactions.\n\nInside the embedded account, show people pursuing recognizable aims through their own habits and blind spots. When a character in the tale claims a role, explains a rule, or describes how an institution works, place that stated account beside what the tale's people actually do. The comedy comes from the precise fit or gap between the official explanation and practical reality: a procedure described as solemn may be performed while someone is trying to find a lost shoe; a reputation for wisdom may rest on one lucky decision and a great deal of nodding. Make recognition available through the conduct without redundant gloss, while preserving any actual explanatory speech or private recognition in its own voice.\n\nUse the frame to manage knowledge and emphasis. The teller can select, omit, or misunderstand details according to their own position and purpose. A listener can ask about timing, access, or sequence, and the teller's answer may be accurate, evasive, or confidently wrong. Keep the teller's present knowledge separate from what the tale's participants knew at the time. Render full actual nonspoken experience for authorized people at either level, distinct from what the teller performs or listeners visibly show; a long feeling or uncertainty needs no immediate use. A later understanding does not retroactively become an earlier motive. Repeated phrases or objects may connect the narrative levels, but their recurrence should help the reader notice a change or a pattern, not certify that the story is true.\n\nLet the frame respond without forcing a lesson. A listener may already understand the point, disagree with it, or remain distracted by something else entirely. The telling may be interrupted by practical business, continued in a different mood, or simply end where the active scene ends. No corrected belief, useful moral, or natural pause is required before the boundary. The comedy can remain in the unresolved difference between what people say they do and what they are actually doing, at both levels of the story.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacayai::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Terry Pratchett / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse modernist/absurdist construction as the definite route. Begin with a real rule, classification, official claim, or institutional arrangement that the narrator can actually know or observe. State its terms plainly enough that the reader understands what the arrangement says it does. Do not invent a hostile bureaucracy or a trap; build the absurdist relation through the arrangement's stated logic meeting a particular person's circumstances. Its stated logic is not automatically world law; actual outside causes and the supplied mechanisms remain operative.\n\nFollow the terms into their application. Distinguish three levels: what the rule or claim states, what people imagine it implies, and what actually happens when a specific person tries to live with it. The comedy arises from a precise fit or a genuine contradiction among these levels. A form may require a signature from someone who does not exist; a classification may sort a person into a category that changes their access to lunch; a claim of efficiency may produce a queue that exists only to explain the queue. Give any contradiction a concrete local basis while preserving a broader judgment or complaint the actual narrator or character is entitled to make.\n\nKeep the characters' relationships to language and authority distinct. One person may treat the arrangement's vocabulary as natural and complete. Another may use the same words while clearly meaning something else. A third may simply be trying to get home before the rain. Do not make everyone a witty commentator on the absurdity. The humor often works best when one person remains entirely practical while the arrangement's stated terms continue to operate beside them.\n\nPreserve the actual stakes and mechanisms of the supplied world. The absurdist construction does not cancel real consequences, and it does not require the arrangement to fail, the character to correct it, or anyone to learn a lesson. The boundary may fall during continuing consideration or action. Private attention remains available beyond its immediate usefulness: a character can notice the contradiction, feel the inconvenience, or think about something else entirely. Let the scene stop where the active response stops, without a completed test or a changed plan.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacayaj::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Terry Pratchett / Gothic-Punk\n\nLocate a real title, privilege, public image, or private desire in the supplied material relationships. The authority may be old, but it must be actual within the story: a family name, a position, a reputation, a claim to access, or a role that shapes how people address one another. The present-life intimacy may be a friendship, a household, a working relationship, or a private want that does not fit the public image. Do not import vampires, factions, or supernatural powers; use the portable treatment of old authority colliding with present life.\n\nPlace the account given of the authority beside what people actually do or ask for. The comedy comes from a precise familiar fit or gap. A title may demand formality while its holder is trying to unstick a window. A public image of control may coexist with a private habit of losing keys. A privilege may be real and still produce an inconvenient obligation. Build humor through particular conduct without requiring the authority to be fraudulent. Any actual fraud, discovery or satirical judgment retains its supported context. The mask need not fall, and the person behind it need not be exposed as a hypocrite.\n\nUse atmosphere to shape experience rather than motive evidence. Urban material detail, private desire, and social masks can make an encounter feel charged or uneasy without explaining why someone acted. A dim hallway or a locked door matters through its actual function and history, not merely through mood. Keep the difference between what a character feels and what another character can know. A private desire may be fully present to the narrator or focal character while remaining invisible to the person addressed.\n\nPreserve actual freely offered or earned trust, full desire, and established powers. Warmth can coexist with old authority and complicated intimacy. No unmasking, betrayal, concealed kindness, authority collapse, or lesson is required. The comedy can remain in the unresolved tension between the public account and the private particular, and the scene may end during continuing speech, action, thought, or an unfinished setup.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacayak::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Terry Pratchett / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nChoose lyric/philosophical inwardness as the definite route. Follow an authorized person's actual question about fairness, duty, care, or belonging. The question must arise from the supplied situation and the character's own position, not from a general essay topic. Let the narrator or focal character return deliberately to a phrase, a real case, or a hypothetical particular to test the scope of the question. A return is not a refrain imposed on every paragraph; it is a considered return when the thought needs it.\n\nUse the inward movement to place a social claim beside actual or imagined particulars. A rule about duty may be tested against a remembered afternoon, a hypothetical neighbor, or the practical question of who will carry the tea. A precise qualification or unchanged familiar understanding can carry comedy without requiring the thinker's humiliation. Preserve any actual self-recognition, error or judgment rather than excluding it. The character may reach no new insight, or may reach one that is partial and still true. Keep claims and world facts distinct: a remembered event is not the same as a general principle, and an imagined case does not establish what actually happened.\n\nLet the prose sustain the question through syntax and deliberate attention. Lyrical movement can develop an image or a comparison when it changes what the thinker understands or makes the question more present. Plain statements provide contrast. Do not make every character speak in the narrator's figurative register, and do not turn the inwardness into constant solemnity. The character can be practical, irritated, amused, or hungry while thinking.\n\nPreserve full feeling, abstract thought, and uncertainty. No mandatory hypocrisy, superior thinker, bodily cue, new insight, outward result, or mental pause is required at the boundary. The scene may end while the question remains open, while the character is still considering a case, or while ordinary life interrupts the reflection. The comedy and the thought can coexist without either canceling the other.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacayal::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Terry Pratchett / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse a diary with reflective notes as the definite route. The recorder is a particular person in the supplied situation, writing at a particular time for their own purposes. The entry selects what that person would actually record: a phrase someone said, a rule that was announced, a routine that was supposed to happen, or an observed act. The selection is not neutral. It follows the recorder's habits, preoccupations, and sense of what is worth noting.\n\nPlace an account of how things ought to work beside the recorder's actual notation. The comedy comes from the juxtaposition. A rule may be written down in full, followed by a note that nobody followed it. A routine may be described as orderly, followed by a list of what actually happened in the margins. A phrase may be recorded because it sounded official, with a later note about what it turned out to mean. Let the recorder's register carry the recognition. The humor need not come from a failed theory or a corrected belief; it can come from the precise difference between the stated account and the practical note.\n\nKeep original composition, later annotations, and present private experience distinct. The original entry records what the recorder knew and chose to write at that time. A later annotation may add knowledge, correction, or a changed feeling, but it must be marked as later. The present private experience of the character is not automatically the same as the composed entry. An unsent or unfinished draft is a composed artifact, not a live transcript of thought. Preserve the difference between what the diary says and what the recorder actually feels or knows now.\n\nDo not impose a seasonal inventory, quiet temperament, new revelation, hidden original motive, or completed entry before the active response boundary. The entry may stop mid-sentence, break off for a practical reason, or continue into a reflective note that remains unfinished. The comedy can remain in the gap between the official account and the actual notation, without requiring the recorder to resolve it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacayam::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Terry Pratchett / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nChoose polyphonic/nonlinear telling as the definite route. Attribute each account or moment to its source and time. A source may be a character, a document, a remembered scene, or a narrator's return to an earlier moment, but the reader must always know whose account is being presented and when it belongs. Do not merge the sources into one neutral summary. Construct comedy through the juxtaposition of attributed accounts while retaining any explanation or factual judgment available under the narrator's actual authority; it need not replace those situated accounts.\n\nSelect an actual repeated detail or claim that different sources notice differently. The detail may be a phrase, a rule, a routine, an object, or a particular act. One source may treat it as central; another may mention it only because it blocked a doorway; a third may remember it inaccurately while still reporting something true. Place these accounts so that the reader can compare the real emphases. The humor arises from the difference between what each source finds worth recording and what the detail actually does in the scene.\n\nKeep original and later knowledge separate. A source who speaks from memory may know more now than they knew then, and that difference must be visible. A later account does not retroactively change an earlier one. Sources can agree accurately or disagree as supported by their positions and knowledge. Neither equal truthfulness nor an exposed liar is required. The comedy does not need a verdict.\n\nPreserve narrator authority and full private minds. The polyphonic structure does not cancel the active narrator's access or grant access to excluded minds. Each source's private experience remains available only where the active contract permits it. No completed baseline, convergence, verdict, or changed meaning is required at the boundary. The scene may end while one account remains unfinished or while the juxtaposition is still being assembled.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacayan::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Terry Pratchett / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Establish the real location, participants, and activity in the first moments so the reader can follow what is happening and where. Keep spatial changes easy to track: when someone moves, when the scene shifts, or when an object changes hands, make the movement legible without over-describing. Provide unfamiliar terms or mechanisms through permitted context, preparation, observation, or use. A name or rule can be presented when the scene actually needs it, not in a detached explanation.\n\nUse readable paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response. A beat of action can stand on its own. A spoken line can be followed by a reaction that belongs to a particular character. Private response may be serious, uncertain, angry, amused, or practical according to its owner. Do not make every character share a common voice or turn every reply into a quip.\n\nTime a relevant expectation or explanation against actual conduct. A character may state how a procedure is supposed to work, and the next beat may show what actually happens. The comedy comes from the precise relation between the stated account and the practical reception. Familiar agreement can be funny without a correction: two people may both know the rule and still fail to follow it for entirely different reasons. A blind spot may be visible to the reader while the character remains unaware of it.\n\nPreserve actual aims at their supplied scale, character-owned reaction, and full inward extent. The scene need not escalate into a larger crisis or produce a delivered response. No young cast, first person, demonstration, quip, or closing hook is required. The boundary may fall during continuing action, dialogue, thought, or an unfinished setup. The comedy can remain in the unresolved difference between what the situation claims and what the people in it are actually doing.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacayao::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Terry Pratchett / Lyrical Prose\n\nWork from a concrete object, sound, or phrase already present in the scene, and let its recurrence organize the paragraph. Let recurrence develop its felt relation through syntax: a plainly named detail can meet a rhythmic or figurative extension, confirming familiar weight or changing it as the actual attention supports. No fixed first/then pattern or changed meaning is required. The comic turn comes from the gap between social description and actual use. When an official phrase, role, or procedure enters, place it beside the physical thing that gives it its real scope: the official name for a machine next to the noise it makes, the roster of a committee next to who actually carries the chairs. Do not force an ironic correction; the right relation may be accurate, qualified, or simply funny through precise fit.\n\nKeep the narrator's associations attributable and within access. If a metaphor unfolds, it must arise from the perspective of the selected teller, not the author's wit, and must not add world facts. Use syntax to create comic timing: a longer clause building expectation, then a short concrete clause that lands the practical particular; or a short declaration followed by a proliferation of detail that exposes the situation. Maintain cast-voice variation; one character may speak in lyrical cadence, another in plain practical terms. The humor should emerge from the shared world's behavior rather than from every speaker sounding like the narrator.\n\nAllow private interiority and serious feeling to coexist with comedy. A character's anger, anxiety, or tenderness need not be flattened for the joke. The response may end inside the association, without returning to action or announcing a lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacayap::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Terry Pratchett / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nOpen with a present particular that carries a past association: an object, a kind of weather, a phrase someone still uses, a task now done differently. Let the movement into memory follow that thread rather than announcing the past. In the remembered occasion, preserve the original person's actual knowledge, accurate understanding or blind spots rather than inventing either comic blindness or retrofitted wisdom. The comedy often lives in the precision of the old social claim—what someone said the work was for, what the group believed it meant—set against what the present moment can still touch. No new loss is required; the remembered claim may remain perfectly accurate and still be funny because the present has a different practical weight.\n\nThe narrator's recollection is fallible: a detail may be wrong, a date vague, a motive guessed. Let that uncertainty stay visible when it belongs to the teller. Direct reflection may sit alongside an actual sensory present; do not let one erase the other. Serious feeling remains serious: affection, grief, or anger can coexist with the comic recognition, and the humor must not reduce them to a punchline. Let sentence movement and actual attention carry attachment and comic exactness at their warranted extent. Follow the active response boundary during ongoing reflection or another actual phase of the scene, without requiring a pause, moral, return, or improved mood.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacayaq::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Terry Pratchett / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nChoose satire and picaresque construction. Advance the account through encounters linked by the protagonist's recognizable continuing aim, not by arbitrary obstacles. Give relevant local arrangements their actual terms and any explanation the source provides; a gatekeeper, supplier, neighbor or official need not make a speech or have a complete rationale for the encounter. The comedy comes from the precision of that explanation—its internal logic, its practical use, its gap between official language and physical reality—without requiring the protagonist to misunderstand. Any actual misunderstanding or accurate grasp remains part of the person's real reception. The protagonist may be helped, ignored, or delayed, and an encounter may advance, qualify, satisfy or leave the aim unchanged. No per-encounter progress, hostile system, or universal fraud is required.\n\nKeep resources, knowledge, and relationships continuous across the sequence. What the protagonist learned in one encounter can inform the next without demanding a triumph. The narration may shift between the protagonist's private thought and the social scene; full inner experience is available under the active contract, and serious feeling is not sacrificed for satire. When the protagonist's habits or blind spots shape the encounter, make them legible through actual sequence or permitted explanation and private recognition rather than a stock label. The active response boundary can occur inside any encounter or continuation; no journey's end, moral correction, or changed result is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacayar::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Terry Pratchett / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect the stated claim, practical fact, or actual response whose relation makes this comic situation inferable, retaining context already established where sufficient. Use direct clauses without redundant framing; no fixed three-element or three-sentence beat is required. For example, an official phrase appears in one sentence, the tool in someone's hand in the next, and the quiet correction in a third; no commentary on the gap between them is needed. If the claim is accurate, leave it accurate: \"the water rose\" can be followed by \"the bucket was there\" without insisting that anyone was deluded. Particular fit can carry the humor without requiring a lie; actual falsehood or its supported exposure remains available.\n\nCut repeated context only when it is genuinely redundant. Keep any emotion or private recognition at the length its owner needs; minimalism does not demand short thoughts or deadpan feelings. A long thinking sentence is permitted if its content matters now. Use specific nouns and verbs: the worn handle, the borrowed ladder, the rolled sleeve. Omit redundant transition announcements while retaining needed orientation, causality, and actual narrative voice. The response may end inside the final clause—mid-thought, mid-task, mid-exchange—without requiring a summing-up, while preserving any actual explanatory or inward conclusion the scene supports.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacayas::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Terry Pratchett / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite in sustained scene-first prose. Move naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience, using actual attention or permitted narrative context to make transitions intelligible without assigning the narrator's perspective to a character. When a role, procedure, or claim matters, make its actual terms legible through use, preparation, discussion, or permitted consideration, without a mandatory demonstration: the meeting as it runs over time, the borrowed uniform as it fits one person and not another, the announced policy as someone asks a practical question. The comedy lies in that use—its accurate fit, its qualified exception, its silent negotiation—without requiring a narrator's aside about absurdity; any actual aside or explicit judgment may still contribute in its own voice.\n\nKeep character voices individual. The person who knows a rule and the person who works around it keep their actual registers, which may differ or overlap; neither automatically becomes a vehicle for uniform narrator wit. Let practical competence and serious feeling stand without a joke attached. The narrator may know only what the chosen perspective permits, but internal reaction is available under the active contract. Do not ironize every emotion. A response may stop in the middle of an exchange, a task, a thought, or a perception; no conclusive beat, correction, or new development is required at the boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacayat::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Terry Pratchett / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nSelect scenes that make the actual relationship premise legible through real conduct and whatever context or summary the narrator is permitted to provide. If the bond is affection, show the small relevant acts: one remembers the other's order, one reaches for the other's tool. If it is rivalry, follow the actual competing aims and any real shared habits without inventing symmetry or a single point of difference. Use the participants' actual private language, callbacks or history where present; a first encounter needs no invented past and the bond need not be limited to two people. Shared understanding or actual difference can carry humor without requiring one person always right and another always wrong. Preserve whatever real differences in knowledge or competence the story supplies.\n\nPlace what the participants say the relation means beside what they actually do. The declaration may be perfectly accurate and still funny because it lands on a particular moment: \"we always share the work\" said while one person is doing all the carrying. Do not force hypocrisy; capable help, familiar division, and honest affection can be just as comic through precise fit. Keep the bond's serious moments serious. Let a callback have its actual relation to the present, whether familiar enjoyment, a supported new emphasis or a punchline; no changed depth or meaning is required. Render full private experience under the active ownership and narrator permissions, separately from what the participants say or show. Follow the active response boundary during ongoing public or private experience or an actual conclusion. The relationship may continue, change or end according to its supplied premise and events; no status change, confession or forward movement is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacayau::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Terry Pratchett / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nBegin with a deliberate opening that signals retelling: \"They still speak of the day,\" \"It is said that,\" \"The repair that held.\" Let a phrase attached to the deed recur where its actual relation to the telling is meaningful, with familiar or new application. It may connect parts of one remembered act or actual repeated occasions; no new use, ongoing external task, or epithet at every entrance is required. The retold description and the real doing remain distinct: the elevated line from the community account sits beside the practical terms—the waiting, the mending, the borrowed strength, the person who actually held the rope—and the comedy comes from that exact relation. Praise can be entirely true and still funny if the language of legend meets the particular work.\n\nKeep the teller's access consistent. Keep the attributed account's actual source knowledge distinct from the active narrator's permissions. A public account does not automatically know every private motive, but actual communicated knowledge and supported access remain; full authorized nonspoken experience can also be rendered separately from what the account says, without shrinking the deed. The people involved remain people, not statues. The response may end on an unfinished reference or a modest next act; no verdict that the deed will be remembered forever is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacayav::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Terry Pratchett / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the scene through a folktale-inflected telling: establish a real role-description, standing request, or repeated phrase early, then let the actual conduct of particular people sit beside it. The role-description may be a posted notice, a household saying, a job title, a public reputation, or a familiar instruction. Keep it specific to its actual users rather than turning it into a magical law.\n\nPlace the fit or contrast in the movement from the repeated words to the practical behavior around them. A person may fulfill the description exactly while doing something the words did not anticipate, may follow a request so literally that the gap becomes visible, or may continue competent private work while the public description lags behind. Make the actual fit or mismatch legible within narrator access without requiring a discrepancy or turning the person into an emblem. What is funny is a particular mind meeting a general description, not a universal lesson about tradition or modernity.\n\nAllow recognition and routine to remain ordinary. A familiar phrase can recur as a cue, a joke, a complaint, or an honestly applied rule. Do not require an invented role, hidden helper, fixed count, reward, or failed task. Preserve full private experience under the active narrator and ownership contract; another character does not know a focal thought merely because the episode is folktale-shaped. If a real supernatural or magical mechanism belongs to the supplied world, use only that mechanism. The response may end during continuing activity, thought, or an actual conclusion without paying a trial, reward, or correction.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacayaw::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Terry Pratchett / Screenplay\n\nUse concise action blocks, clear speaker cues, and scene headings when location or time changes. Keep each block focused on what is visible or audible to the imagined viewer: movement, position, object use, speech, practical work, and the condition of shared space.\n\nPlace an actual statement, displayed claim, instruction, or established expectation beside what the screen shows during or immediately after it. The comedy can come from exact confirmation, literal execution, a small habitual adjustment, or a real discrepancy between the official wording and the visible work. Do not make the action block announce the joke. Let the audience see the actual conduct beside its description, including fully stated and accurately anticipated work; no omitted condition is required.\n\nUse cuts when the actual sequence needs a spatial or temporal transition, comparison, or change of action emphasis; a cut need not move to another place. A supported cut may move from a claim in one room to the practical situation in another, or from an order to the person carrying it out. Offscreen sound may supply an actual continuing task or presence. Keep full attributed nonspoken experience in its active presentation format, clearly attributed and distinct from dialogue, footage, and voice-over. Presented thought alone grants no automatic character knowledge; preserve actual prior knowledge, communication, world channels, and permitted mental access.\n\nPreserve each speaker's register and each person's actual knowledge channels. Do not require a reaction shot, mishap, exposed hypocrisy, delivered punchline, or fictional pause before ending. The scene may end during ongoing speech, action, or unspoken thought.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacayax::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Terry Pratchett / Stage Play\n\nStage the scene with clear speakers, entrances, exits, positions, objects, and blocking that a company could perform. Keep the practical business grounded in the actual shared space: who is near the table, holding the list, watching the door, or continuing the routine work while a claim or request is being made.\n\nBring a real statement, order, public promise, or familiar explanation into contact with what the characters are already doing during or after it. The comic effect can arrive through literal compliance, competent cooperation, a pause that lets the line settle, or a physical action that qualifies the words without being a hidden irony. One character may say the arrangement is simple while another quietly handles the part the words left out. Give each cast member a distinct relation to language and authority rather than a common witty register.\n\nKeep purposeful asides and monologues actual performed speech with an audience in the imagined production; they are not the default container for private thought. Preserve full required interiority under the active ownership contract, clearly nonspoken and not automatically known to other characters merely through presentation. Preserve actual prior knowledge, communication, world mechanisms and active permissions without requiring an added knowledge event. Objects and stillness should be meaningful, but a prop alone does not prove motive or character.\n\nDo not require a stumble, broken object, public exposure, delivered joke, or completed exchange before the end. The response may stop during continuing action, speech, or thought, with the stage business left underway.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacayay::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Terry Pratchett / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite from inside one authorized mind and follow how perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern actually move into each other. Let transitions follow actual associative movement, including logical links or reasoning when they arise within that traffic rather than reducing the passage to argument alone: a phrase from a notice may surface because a hand is cold, a familiar instruction may return because someone's footstep repeats, or an official explanation may collide with the sensation of standing in the wrong queue.\n\nAllow a social description or familiar claim to recur where attention takes it. It may appear as remembered language, shorthand, a line the character no longer fully believes, or a phrase that fits the present moment with uncomfortable completeness. The comedy comes from the contact between the shared words and the lived particular: the policy, the plaque, the promise, the household saying, the job title, the apology script. Do not assign the mind superior insight merely to carry the joke or force it to recognize a mismatch as funny. Preserve the person's actual knowledge, skill, self-image and understanding, including real expertise or error. The character may feel the whole collision as a small mental bump, a physical shrug, or a serious private question.\n\nDistinguish actual events from fallible memory and imagined cases. A remembered version may be edited by need or habit, an imagined reply may have its own texture, and a real sensory detail may sit beside the wrong explanation. Keep enough orientation for the reader to tell what is happening now. Full feeling, uncertainty, or rumination may remain unchanged; no self-deception, correction, outward act, polished inner wit, or useful conclusion is required. The response boundary may fall within continuing thought, and actual attention, waiting, and events determine their own duration.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacayaz::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Terry Pratchett / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nUse readable sender-attributed messages in each person's actual digital register and extent. One may type in complete sentences, another in fragments, another with careful punctuation, another with none. Keep those habits individual rather than making every sender witty or every message clipped.\n\nPlace a request, instruction, explanation, or standing claim beside the actual uptake that follows in the transcript. The comedy can come from a literal reply, a competent adjustment the original message did not mention, a recipient acting on a different understanding, or the practical gap between an announcement and the next ordinary exchange. Show actual transmitted uptake through the messages; other supported reception or action can appear in clearly separate context under the narrator's real access. Do not invent an explanatory omniscient voice or require a reply to prove uptake. If context outside the exchange is required, present it clearly separated from the transcript and keep knowledge channels intact.\n\nKeep composing, sending, receipt, reading, editing, and delays separate. A delay or short reply is a transmission fact, not a proof of motive, annoyance, indifference, or deception. An unsent draft is a composed artifact with its own status, not automatically the writer's live private thought; preserve full required private experience in its active format and attribution. Use only timestamps, attachments, channel changes, or receipts that carry information for the present exchange.\n\nDo not require a typo, mistaken recipient, correction, sent answer, or changed plan before the active boundary. The response may end after a sent message, during a visible composing state, or within required nonspoken private experience, leaving the exchange inconclusive.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacayba::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Terry Pratchett / Visual Novel\n\nOrient the scene with concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response suited to an illustrated sequence. Let the reader know where the characters are and what particular object, surface, background detail, or expression currently carries attention.\n\nUse an actual cue to place a claim, expectation, or response in its concrete setting. A notice may hang above a counter, a gesture may accompany a promise, a familiar object may sit inside a request's phrasing. Let the cue show the practical terms around the words: the size of the thing, the condition of the room, the distance between the instruction and the task, the face that is genuinely comfortable with a statement another person finds enormous. The comedy can come from an accurate fit as well as a supported contrast; a familiar routine meeting a familiar description may be funny without any new realization.\n\nPresent focal narration or inner response in the selected visible or hidden interiority format. A face or background cue alone proves no motive, and presented thought grants no automatic knowledge to the other characters. Preserve the active narrator, perspective, and character agency. A face may be tired, pleased, indifferent, or concerned; let the actual person behind the cue keep that private extent. Do not impose a fixed cycle of backgrounds, expressions, or reactions.\n\nDo not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, images, or executable commands merely because this style is selected; preserve any separate feature or authorization the active contract actually supplies. The response may end after a line, during focal thought, or within continuing activity without a delivered correction, confession, or new expression label.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaybb::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Terry Pratchett / Web Serial\n\nKeep the current through-line legible without starting each installment by explaining the whole story. Recover only the earlier claim, practical term, relationship fact, or physical condition needed to follow the present activity. A standing arrangement or familiar phrase may be restated briefly because a character would actually restate it, or it may simply show up in the work being done.\n\nCarry real consequences, knowledge, resources, unfinished requests, and ordinary lives forward. A later use of an earlier claim can confirm a familiar pattern or produce a supported new relation: the sign is still misleading, the procedure still requires a particular step, the team still interprets the instruction in two ways. Let recognition build from what the reader has already seen without manufacturing forgotten facts, failed institutions, or new obstacles to restart a joke. The comedy comes from the particular people and their actual arrangements, not from repeating a setup as if the earlier installment did not happen.\n\nGive full private passages their actual extent; they do not need plot utility or an immediate outward consequence. The installment may advance concrete work, knowledge, or relationship, but it may also end during continuing public or inward experience or after an actual conclusion. Do not require a cliffhanger, a corrected misunderstanding, or a new complication at every boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacazaa::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Wang Xiaobo / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nBuild the scene around one person's actual concern: what they are trying to ask, admit, or keep private within the actual relationship or encounter, without inventing a pre-existing cost. Give the concern precise terms, whether privately considered, candidly asked or directly admitted. It can concern an arrangement, promise, debt, habit or expectation at its supplied scale; no small literal answer is required.\n\nWhere the focal character reasons about the concern, follow their actual terms candidly; feeling can also continue without a new deliberation. They may test the exact terms of an expectation, notice where the terms fit and where they fail, or pursue an implication past the point where the other person wants the discussion to end. This reasoning is comic because it is exact and independent, without requiring a witty performance; deliberate humor remains available where it belongs to that person. The other person responds from their own actual interests: they may answer plainly, cooperate, misunderstand, deflect, or refuse the inquiry without becoming a debating partner.\n\nKeep the emotional cost inside the focal character's private experience. The strain of needing an answer, withholding a fuller admission, or hearing a difficult reply can remain wholly inward. It need not produce a visible effect, a disclosure, or a resolved argument. Where the actual person feels both, warmth and hurt may coexist: an accurate answer can still wound, and a kind response can still avoid the question. Do not require the scene to end in repair, rupture, or a changed plan. Stop at the active response boundary during continuing thought, speech or activity, or after a supported conclusion, without requiring the question or feeling to stay unresolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacazab::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Wang Xiaobo / Chat Roleplay\n\nKeep the reply inside one coherent shared interval with clear speakers, readable physical positions, and continuing activity. Take up an actual relevant claim, request, instruction or private question, whether newly voiced or already present. Where someone pursues clarification, follow its concrete terms: what exactly was meant, what the words require, what would satisfy them, or what the present arrangement already provides.\n\nUse unmistakable dialogue attribution and let different participants respond according to their own habits. One may answer literally, one may agree without examining the terms, one may ignore the question and continue working, and one may offer genuine help. The comic movement comes from the focal character's exactness meeting these ordinary responses, not from everyone becoming a debater or scoring points. Allow agreement and accurate fit as readily as mismatch.\n\nKeep private thought available to the focal character during the exchange, without automatically making it audible or known to the cast; preserve actual disclosure, sources and established capabilities. Thought can continue through the reply without forcing a new question, a cliffhanger, or a return to action. End at the natural continuation point of the shared interval: the question may remain open, the activity may continue, and no participant is required to pause, resume, or change direction for the sake of the ending.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacazac::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Wang Xiaobo / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and satirical realism route. Situate the scene in actual work, household, or public arrangements, and let each character's position in those arrangements shape what they can say, ask, or do. Develop the comic sequence from a request or expectation that passes between people whose actual positions may differ or be shared: a rule announced by someone with authority, a service promised to someone who needs it, or a standard invoked by someone who does not have to meet it.\n\nLet a direct question clarify how the norm fits or fails in this particular case. The focal character may ask what the rule actually requires, who it applies to, or how it is being used now. Show real cooperation and self-interest side by side. A person can sincerely uphold a norm while arranging an exception for themselves; another can comply accurately while exposing an absurd consequence. Do not require the norm to be false or a character to be exposed as a hypocrite. The comedy lies in the precise relation between the asserted standard and its actual use, not in unmasking.\n\nKeep the social context concrete: mention the actual work being done, the room, the queue, the ledger, the shared meal, or the household task. Let the inquiry move through these particulars rather than through abstract debate. The scene may end with the question answered, partially answered, or still pending while ordinary life continues.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacazad::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Wang Xiaobo / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nUse forceful exact diction and compressed transitions. Move quickly from a stated expectation to the concrete object, bodily condition, or act that tests it. The expectation may be a claim about how something works, what a person deserves, what a tool can do, or what an arrangement costs. Place it beside actual handling or use: the weight of the thing, the state of the equipment, the time the task takes, the way a body has to move, or the residue left behind.\n\nRender the focal character's actual reasoning and feeling in their causal order: before, during or after material contact, or wholly privately without a new physical act. They may state the mismatch in plain terms, find that the expectation fits after all, or notice a consequence the original claim did not mention. The comedy comes from the exact relation between words and use, not from mockery or manufactured damage. Allow an accurate fit as readily as an absurd one.\n\nKeep full private thought and feeling available whether the outward effect is large, small or entirely absent. A character can be tired, angry, amused, or worried without announcing it. Do not invent hardship, rough speech, or physical stakes the scene does not support. Vary rhythm: a compressed transition can land a comic point, while a longer thought may be needed when the character works out why the arrangement fails. The active boundary can fall during the material comparison, thought, speech or handling itself, or after an actual conclusion; no completed comparison, reversal or practical result is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacazae::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Wang Xiaobo / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through actual documents, each with a clear author, composition occasion, intended recipient or purpose, and time of writing. Use the document form the scene requires: a request, a notice, a reply, a record, a note, or a letter. Do not rotate through formats for their own sake.\n\nLet an exact request, definition, or claim recur across documents. One writer may state a requirement in precise terms; a later document may answer those terms literally, apply them to an unintended case, or record an independent observation that tests the original claim. The comic argument emerges from the relation between the documents' actual wording and their reception, without requiring a corrective verdict; actual narrator comment remains available within the active presentation and access. Keep each writer's knowledge bounded by what they composed, received, or could have known at that time. Genuine agreement and accurate compliance remain possible.\n\nPreserve the difference between what a writer composes and their live private experience. A document may be tactful, evasive, exact, or self-serving without revealing the writer's full mind. When required interiority is active, render it separately from the transmitted text. Gaps, revisions, and conflicting accounts may reveal events across entries, but do not add a corrective verdict after every record. End at a natural point in the document sequence; the question may remain open in the correspondence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacazaf::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Wang Xiaobo / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend attention on actual helpful conduct, familiar preferences, and comfortable participation. Follow an actual occasion of care at its supplied scale: someone making food, sharing a space, fixing something, remembering a preference, or offering practical help. Let the scene enjoy the particular details of that care without requiring a lesson; a supported direct reflection can still belong to the actual voice.\n\nAllow an exact question or dry observation to develop around what people enjoy or provide. The focal character may ask why a routine works, what a preference actually requires, or how much effort a kindness involves. Their candid reasoning can be comic because it is precise and independent, not because it mocks the care. The other person can answer plainly, agree, decline the discussion, or continue the practical task without becoming an opponent.\n\nPreserve direct affection, privacy, and disagreement. Kindness does not require everyone to become agreeable, and comfort does not remove established problems from the world. Do not make affection conditional on winning an argument or require gratitude, confession, or repaired feelings. The scene may end with the care continuing, the question unresolved, or the participants simply enjoying the present moment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacazag::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Wang Xiaobo / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse sustained paragraphs and deliberate transitions. Open with enough narrative distance to state the relevant social expectation: a custom, regulation, reputation, or arrangement that the scene will test. Frame it through the narrator's permitted access to social context, history, or judgment, not through an omniscient moral. Then move into the scene's particulars and let the expectation meet them.\n\nFollow how the claim applies through precise transitions. The focal character may ask what the expectation actually requires, who it covers, or how it is being used in this case. The narrator can supply context, compare the present occasion with a past one, or offer a judgment the narrator is authorized to make. Keep ordinary speech ordinary: the characters do not need to speak in the narrator's formal register, and the comedy does not depend on making them articulate a thesis.\n\nAllow confirmation and undecided cases. The expectation may fit the particulars exactly, fail in a specific way, or remain open to further evidence. Do not require a closing moral, a corrected belief, or a character exposed. Keep the developing relation legible as the scene proceeds. The active boundary may fall during continuing speech, thought or use, with no completed comparison or settled social question required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacazah::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Wang Xiaobo / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nEstablish the teller, audience and telling occasion, with purpose identified within actual narrator access: persuasion, delay, entertainment, warning or explanation. Distinguish a remembered, reported, claimed or invented account from the surrounding scene's established facts. Keep the outer occasion concrete enough that the audience's attention and interruptions remain possible without turning every listener into a corrector.\n\nInside the embedded account, let the teller follow one practical detail or candid question beyond the socially approved answer. Show the exact step where an apparent lesson, rule, or solemn claim meets a particular person's need, habit, or blind spot. The comedy comes from the teller's precise observation of that collision, not from a stock punch line or a universal exposure of fraud.\n\nTrack actual audience reception across the telling. Listeners may agree, listen quietly, ask a clarifying question, or miss the point entirely. A repeated phrase or object may accrue meaning across narrative levels, but its recurrence should connect the outer and inner situations rather than certify that the embedded story is true.\n\nLet selection and emphasis follow the teller's actual attachments, knowledge and blind spots where they matter, without requiring loyalty or a visible omission. A generous account can be sincere while omitting something the teller did not understand. Distinguish what the original participants knew from what the teller has learned since. The active boundary may fall during telling, listening or private attention, or after a supported conclusion; a completed lesson, exposed flaw or audience correction is possible but never owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacazai::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Wang Xiaobo / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nSelect one actual procedure, definition, or formal category relevant to the scene: an application form, a classification rule, a maintenance protocol, a membership criterion, or a similar operative structure. Make its relevant terms readable through use, instruction or consideration; that presentation can itself carry comic recognition without a separate explanatory prelude.\n\nFollow what applying those terms literally permits or requires. Let a character pursue a recognizable aim through the procedure with their own habits and blind spots. Comic recognition can arise from exact wording meeting a familiar fitting case, a real limit, or an unexpected consequence. Competent users may know what will happen and share that understanding.\n\nAllow independent reasoning to confirm the procedure, identify a real limit, or leave it unused. A character may discover that the rule works exactly as written, that its scope is narrower than claimed, or that following it would cost more than the aim is worth. None of these outcomes requires a new obstacle, a victory, or a punctured solemn assumption.\n\nKeep the prose's modernist or absurdist construction in the sequence of operations and the narrator's precise attention to category and consequence. The rule and its users may remain competent throughout. Keep known scope clear while letting the active boundary fall during instruction, consideration, speech or use; no completed scope finding, counterexample or changed belief is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacazaj::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Wang Xiaobo / Gothic-Punk\n\nGround a supplied role or privilege in the material encounter. Show the actual room, object, access point, or inherited arrangement through which the authority operates. Let the atmosphere affect how the encounter is experienced without adding powers, proving fraud, or making the old authority merely decorative.\n\nBring one candid practical question or need into contact with the authority's exact terms. A character may ask what the privilege actually covers, what a ritual costs in time or money, or why a particular exception exists. Comic proportion comes from the precise relation between the inherited claim's presentation and its practical operation, whether they fit or diverge, while the authority may remain legitimate and the questioner may remain respectful.\n\nAllow the encounter to end in ordinary help, private desire, unchanged social presentation, or a quiet clarification. The social mask may fall away in trusted company, or it may stay in place because the situation does not require its removal. No character is obliged to expose a fraud or puncture a solemn assumption.\n\nKeep morally complicated intimacy available: the person holding authority and the person questioning it may share history, affection, resentment, or practical dependence. Render those actual feelings and only the effects on speech or conduct that occur; private feeling may remain undisplayed. The active boundary can fall within the encounter, before scope is clarified, without requiring a lesson, reversal or completed exchange.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacazak::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Wang Xiaobo / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nSettle the account in testimonial or autobiographical form. Identify the actual narrator and, if they present another participant's testimony, keep that participant's voice and knowledge distinct from the narrator's own. Give the account an occasion: why it is being told now, to whom, and what the telling is meant to establish.\n\nReconstruct words and acts at the precision the source supports, distinguishing exact quotation from remembered gist and what was actually known. Keep experience and inference distinct, whether the inference occurred then or later. A candid inference may confirm or qualify a received explanation, but it must remain visibly an inference rather than a remembered fact.\n\nPlace the participant's precise experience beside the received explanation for comic comparison, allowing an accurate fit as well as a gap. The participant may have followed a rule literally, misunderstood a purpose, or noticed a practical detail that everyone else ignored. The comedy belongs to that particular person's habits and blind spots, not to a stock trickster or obstruction.\n\nPreserve full live private experience for each character authorized by the active contract, separately from what testimony selects. Doubt, enjoyment, regret or reinterpretation may remain unspoken; do not invent a participant identity or private mind for an impersonal narrator. The active boundary may fall during the account or private attention, without requiring a precise final claim, exposure, political lesson or correction.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacazal::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Wang Xiaobo / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the social/psychological realism route. Enter one authorized person's actual attention and reasoning about a present exchange. Show what they notice, what they expect, and how the exchange confirms, complicates, or leaves that expectation untouched. Use concrete remembered examples where they occur, but keep the movement anchored in the present scene.\n\nLet the comic expectation arise from the person's particular habits and blind spots. They may expect a rule to be followed, a courtesy to be returned, or a claim to mean what it says. The comedy comes from the precise perception of what actually happens, not from a stock misreading or a required new self-insight.\n\nAllow public conduct and private judgment to agree or differ for real reasons. A character may say less than they think out of politeness, say exactly what they think, or remain uncertain about what they think. Clarification, certainty, and unresolved feeling all remain possible. No character is obliged to correct a wrong reading or announce a lesson.\n\nKeep the relation among scene, perception and interpretation readable without requiring a fixed order or outward response after thought. Actual impulsive speech and later reasoning remain possible. The active boundary can fall within continuing attention, talk or action without a completed exchange, insight or changed belief.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacazam::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Wang Xiaobo / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the social chronicle route. Trace one actual statement or arrangement through dated or otherwise locatable encounters. Show who repeats it, who uses it, who questions it, and with what actual standing in each occasion. Keep each encounter and source distinct rather than merging them into a single generalized public opinion.\n\nLet candid particulars support or puncture the public wording. A character may repeat the claim because it serves their aim, use it literally in a situation it does not fit, or ask what it actually covers. Comic recognition comes from the accumulating relation between public wording and practical application, fitting or discrepant, without requiring a uniform exposure or political lesson.\n\nPreserve each participant's habits and blind spots. The person who repeats the claim may believe it, need it, or merely find it convenient. The person who questions it may do so out of curiosity, self-interest, or genuine confusion. No character is obliged to become a satirical mouthpiece or a corrected fool.\n\nAllow the chronicle to end without a contradiction at each return. The claim may survive some encounters intact, fail in others, or remain simply unused. Keep sourced occasions legible as they accumulate; the active boundary may fall within an encounter or account before the chronicle is complete, without a final verdict or changed belief.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacazan::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Wang Xiaobo / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Establish where the characters are, who is present, and what relevant terms, names, or mechanics the reader needs to follow the exchange. Give unfamiliar elements clear presentation when they first matter, without pausing the scene for a glossary.\n\nLet a plain question or literal answer follow the person's real attention. A character may ask what a rule actually means, take an instruction at face value, or notice a practical detail that the other speaker did not intend to emphasize. The comedy comes from the precise tracking of that attention, not from a rapid alternation of witty replies.\n\nUse paragraph breaks to clarify public response and full private thought. A reaction may be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to its owner. Private reasoning can run longer when the character needs it, and public speech may be shorter or longer than the thought behind it.\n\nAn explanation may confirm known terms or support informed non-use. The character who asked the question may accept the answer, decide the rule does not apply, or remain uncertain without a new obstacle or victory. Keep the developing exchange readable while allowing the active boundary during continuing speech, thought or action; no completed clarification, cliffhanger, punch line or lesson is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacazao::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Wang Xiaobo / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet a concrete comparison develop through actual attention beside the comic question or in later reflection, with its timing and owner clear. When a character pursues an aim or defends a habit, let the narrator or focal mind attach an actual sound, image, or owned analogy that shares the question's shape: a repeated knock, a mislabeled drawer, a route that looks shorter on paper. Extend the comparison through syntax and recurrence as the scene's attention actually returns to it, and let a plain statement interrupt the figure when the practical point needs to stand undecorated. The comparison may change as new particulars arrive; it need not prove the claim wrong or resolve into a lesson.\n\nKeep the comic reasoning direct and nimble. A character may follow a question beyond its socially approved answer by testing the literal terms against a concrete case: what exactly counts as \"on time,\" \"clean,\" \"a favor,\" or \"temporary.\" Develop the inquiry through particular observations and actual responses, with direct summary or judgment available within the active narrator's voice. A real gap can expose absurdity; fitting recognition can also be comic without a compulsory gap or punch line. Help, agreement, and routine success remain available outcomes; the inquiry can find that the expectation fits after all.\n\nPreserve each character's own diction. The lyrical movement belongs to the narrator or the authorized focal mind, not to every speaker. A literal-minded character can answer a figurative passage with an ordinary practical concern, and that contrast can itself carry the comedy. Private feelings need no argumentative use: a character may notice an image, feel its pull, and still act or speak from a different motive. Keep the selected perspective's access consistent, and let the paragraph's rhythm follow actual shifts of attention rather than a fixed pattern of image, rebuttal, and return.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacazap::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Wang Xiaobo / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nPlace a concrete earlier claim, phrase, or habit beside current experience through memory available to the active narrator or an authorized character, with ownership clear. The remembered item should be specific enough to test: a rule someone once stated, a nickname, a plan for how a room or a routine would work. Let the present scene supply the actual fit. The character may notice that the old explanation still holds, that it covered only part of the case, or that it has become irrelevant without anyone announcing the change. Dry recognition is enough; the passage need not conclude that hindsight is superior or that the earlier person was foolish.\n\nLet the comic inquiry proceed through the comparison itself. A solemn assumption can be punctured by the plain difference between what was said then and what is happening now: the promised convenience, the predicted reaction, the definition of a finished task. Follow the question beyond its socially approved answer, but keep the inquiry owned by the particular mind remembering it. The character may feel attachment, irritation, amusement, or unchanged pleasure at full extent, and these feelings can coexist without being resolved into a lesson. No regret, farewell, or announced impermanence is required.\n\nKeep direct reflection available but not mandatory. The narrator may state the comparison plainly, or let a small detail carry the association: a familiar object now used differently, a phrase repeated with a new inflection. Preserve the difference between what the character remembers and what the character knows now, and do not let later knowledge rewrite the earlier motive. The present scene remains the ground; recollection can open beside it and continue through the active boundary without requiring a return to present activity. A remembered explanation can be accurate and still comic, because the world has continued without consulting it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacazaq::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Wang Xiaobo / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse the satire and picaresque route through successive actual encounters in the supplied setting. Carry a person's concrete aim or question between occasions: a request for a permit, a search for a particular item, an attempt to return something, a dispute about what a sign or instruction means. Keep each encounter's participants, terms, knowledge and responses distinct, while an application may stay the same across occasions. Comic recognition can come from a shared understanding or a real difference in reception. No travel, trickster, swindle or obstruction is required; participants may help, comply, refuse or share the focal person's frame.\n\nWhere the focal character takes the inquiry further, follow the actual question beyond an expected answer without requiring a challenge in each encounter. A candid inquiry can test the literal terms against local practice: what counts as proof, who is authorized to decide, why a step exists. Preserve the intellectual independence of the questioner without making every local participant a fool or a villain. The absurdity may lie in the rule's practical life, not in a single person's malice. Routine success and genuine help remain available; the sequence does not need to end in failure or a lesson.\n\nKeep the encounters linked by the carried aim rather than by an omniscient narrator's verdict. Each new scene should be legible on its own terms while the reader recognizes the returning question. Distinguish what the focal character experiences from what is reported or inferred about other participants. Let local speech keep its own diction and concerns; the focal person's directness may contrast with local formality or local shorthand without making either side merely comic decoration. The active boundary may fall during an encounter, private attention or an actual resolution, without requiring a next occasion to open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacazar::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Wang Xiaobo / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect the few exact details that carry the comic qualification and let them stand in direct clauses. When a character tests an expectation against a concrete case, include the claim, the particular that matters, and the actual response. Omit repeated commentary and redundant explanation, but do not delete the causal link between what was said and what happened. A precise qualification can appear in the speaker's own register: a dry correction, a literal answer, a practical question that exposes the gap. Make comedy inferable through selected particulars without requiring an announcement; an actual narrator's direct observation remains available.\n\nLet the inquiry follow its own necessary length. A character may need several sentences to work through a distinction, and full actual thought and feeling may be lengthy without requiring an immediate decision or outward effect. Shortness is not a quota. The minimalist operation is selection: keep exact details that orient, sustain or develop the account, without requiring a changed interpretation. Full actual thought and feeling can run in plain clauses at their warranted extent rather than being gated by argumentative value or fragmented for brevity.\n\nPreserve the full mind that arrives at the qualification. The narrator may show what a character notices, doubts, or privately understands without making that interiority serve an argument. Keep each character's diction individual; a literal-minded speaker can deliver the comic precision while another character misses it or responds from a different concern. The passage need not end with a punch line, a changed plan, or a completed test. The selected particulars can leave the question open and the next move available.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacazas::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Wang Xiaobo / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose that moves naturally among conduct, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience. Let a person test or simply consider the terms of an actual expectation against a concrete case within the scene: a rule announced at the start, a habit someone defends, a promise whose wording now matters. Develop the inquiry through particular observations and actual responses, with relevant summary available within the narrator's voice and access. A character can follow a question beyond its socially approved answer without turning the scene into a debate; the other participants may be eating, working, answering partially, or concerned with something else.\n\nKeep the comic relation in the reception. Any gap between expectation and use must follow actual words and conduct; fitting reception can also carry the scene. Concrete discrepancies, where present, might include the form filled in wrong, the borrowed item returned in a new condition, the definition that shifts when applied. Help, agreement, and candid uncertainty remain available outcomes. A claim may turn out to fit after all, and a solemn assumption may be punctured without anyone declaring victory. Do not require an intellectual win, a humiliation, or a completed test before the scene can end.\n\nPreserve the active perspective's access. The narration may reveal what the focal character privately notices or doubts while other characters remain unaware of it. Let inner experience accompany the scene without forcing it to produce an immediate visible action. Keep each speaker's diction distinct from the narrator's register; the comic precision may belong to one character while another remains literal, impatient, or uninterested. The passage can stop with the question still open, the business still underway, or the next practical step available.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacazat::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Wang Xiaobo / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nSelect an exchange from the supplied relationship premise and the participants' actual familiarity. Let a candid question, a shared literal reading, or a familiar disagreement carry the comic recognition. The comedy should come from the particular way these people question or answer each other: one tests the wording of a promise, the other supplies a practical correction, one follows a rule beyond its intended use while the other already knows where the inquiry leads. Use private language, repeated moments, and the specific history the premise establishes. Unfamiliar participants need no invented backstory; the scene can stay within what the relationship already makes legible.\n\nKeep the inquiry direct and nimble without requiring it to prove chemistry or change the bond. A solemn assumption can be punctured by the participants' own shared terms where established: a nickname used with new precision, a standing joke that suddenly fits a real problem, a familiar argument that reaches a different stopping point. Affection and strain may coexist, and neither needs to resolve into confession, repair, or a new pattern. The relationship can remain exactly what it was while the scene exposes one of its comic workings.\n\nPreserve each person's private experience under the active contract. One character may notice the implication of a line while the other misses it, or both may understand and choose different responses. Private thought need not become dialogue, and dialogue need not summarize private thought. Let selection, actual reception and owned private experience make the supplied premise legible, with consequences only where they occur and no imposed roster of tropes. The passage can end with the exchange still settling, the next practical matter arriving, or the relationship's ordinary rhythm resuming.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacazau::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Wang Xiaobo / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nOpen with a deliberate declaration or recurring phrase attached to an established act or attributed remembrance: the person known for a particular repair, the incident still told in a fixed way, the designation that has outlived its occasion. Then expand into the actual activity and individual speech. Let a precise question clarify what the designation covers: who actually did the work, what the remembered act cost, which part of the story the community keeps and which part the participant recalls differently. Comic proportion comes from placing the compact public account beside exact particulars, whether they fit or diverge. Public memory may be accurate, and private experience may agree with it or include more than it records.\n\nLet actual dialogue enter the account where it belongs, agreeing, qualifying or addressing another matter without a required interruption or puncturing reply. The subject of an oft-told incident may object to one detail, answer an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern, or confirm the story while adding an inconvenient particular. A recurring phrase can return through actual remembered or present associations, retaining its application or gaining a new one rather than appearing as an epithet at every entrance. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective; shared reputation remains distinct from a focal character's private account.\n\nPreserve the present scene's scale. An errand remains an errand, a repair remains a repair, and a remembered deed need not become legend on the community's behalf. The active boundary can fall during continuing business, speech, thought or telling, or after an actual conclusion, without requiring a resumed task, another act or modest scale. No finished deed, humiliation, or corrected reputation is required. The comic inquiry may simply show what the public phrase covers and what it leaves out, and let both stand.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacazav::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Wang Xiaobo / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the scene as essayistic inquiry within the active narrator's access. State an actual narrator-owned or attributed proposition, definite or provisional, in its appropriate terms, then set it beside a concrete observed, remembered or explicitly hypothetical case. Follow what the case actually supports, complicates, or leaves undecided. The inquiry may confirm the claim, narrow its scope, or expose a gap between its stated terms and its practical use. Do not require a counterexample, a changed belief, or a final verdict.\n\nKeep the comic movement in the precision of the comparison. A solemn formulation can meet a small material particular: the rule says everyone must sign, but the pen has been missing since Tuesday. Let the narrator pursue the question beyond its socially approved answer, distinguishing what was actually seen from what was inferred, assumed, or announced. Preserve the full private reasoning that accompanies the inquiry, including irritation, amusement, or genuine uncertainty, without requiring feeling to serve the inquiry or reach a conclusion. Actual feeling may inform a claim about one's own experience, but does not by itself prove external facts or another person's motive.\n\nUse the essayistic form to control information flow. The narrator may open with the proposition, arrive at it through a remembered case, or return to it after a digression. A return can examine or confirm scope, continue a question or reorient the reader, without requiring another test or new understanding. When the narrator recalls an occasion, keep earlier and later knowledge distinct. An imagined example must remain clearly hypothetical rather than becoming a remembered fact. The scene can end with the inquiry still open, with a precise qualification, or with the claim confirmed in a narrower form than its original wording suggested.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacazaw::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Wang Xiaobo / Screenplay\n\nWrite the sequence in concise scene blocks with clear speaker cues, scene headings when location or time changes, and only visible or audible events in the action lines. Let a candid claim meet its actual use or reply in the shared scene. The comic qualification comes from what the audience can see and hear: a speaker announces a rule, then performs an action that gives the rule a different practical meaning; an official term meets an ordinary object; a solemn explanation runs against the physical business already established.\n\nUse cuts to juxtapose separate occasions when the comparison matters. A cut can place a stated expectation beside its later actual result, or show two people applying the same phrase to different situations. The cut connects the occasions for the viewer; it does not make the characters witness the connection or cause the later event. Do not use editing to manufacture a physical outcome that the scene itself has not established.\n\nKeep full required nonspoken private experience in the active presentation's nonspoken form, clearly separated from performed voice-over, with cast knowledge following actual sources and established capabilities rather than reader access alone. A character's private reasoning about a claim is not automatically available to another speaker. Voice-over, when separately authorized, is performed speech with its own audience and purpose, not the default destination for thought. Let the scene end on a completed exchange, a visible result, or an unfinished action without requiring a punch line, a reaction shot, or a new development.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacazax::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Wang Xiaobo / Stage Play\n\nWrite the scene with clear positions, speakers, entrances, exits, and actual physical business that can be staged. Let a plain claim or reply meet what people really do in the shared space. The comic timing comes from the relationship between the spoken proposition and the visible activity: a character explains the correct procedure while another is already doing it differently; a solemn instruction is interrupted by the practical need to move a chair, find a pen, or hold a door. Stillness and accurate cooperation can be as comic as obstruction. Do not require a prop failure, an interruption quota, or a misunderstanding to produce the effect.\n\nKeep the spatial continuity meaningful. What one character can see, hear, reach, or carry should be clear enough that the spoken claim and the physical business can test each other. A line delivered from one side of the stage may not be heard by someone working on the other side; an object moved earlier remains moved. Use the shared space to make the relation between a stated rule and actual use visible, including accurate cooperation as well as a gap, without turning the cast into fixed comic types.\n\nKeep performed argument, monologue, and aside distinct from full required private experience. An aside is spoken for an audience, not the default container for thought. Preserve the active presentation's nonspoken private material separately, and do not let another character know a private conclusion merely because the reader or audience has followed its formation. Supported offstage activity remains available through entrances, exits, and reported action when the scene needs it. The scene can end after a result, a completed exchange, or an unfinished task without an obligatory gathering or final explanation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacazay::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Wang Xiaobo / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite the passage through the authorized mind's actual associative movement. Braid perception, bodily sensation, received words, memory, private wording, and a recurring concern without reducing the sequence to an orderly debate. Let associations interrupt a question, return to it from an unexpected direction, or leave it unresolved. The comic effect can come from the mind's uneven approach to an apparently obvious answer: a solemn phrase keeps resurfacing beside the smell of coffee, a remembered rule collides with the need to find a clean shirt, a private objection is abandoned mid-thought when someone speaks.\n\nPreserve the direct and nimble inquiry of the author profile inside the associative syntax. The mind can state a proposition, test it against a concrete particular, and follow what remains supported or undecided—but the testing need not proceed step by step. A qualification may arrive through a remembered occasion, a physical sensation, or a phrase that returns with a different meaning. Keep imagined cases clearly separate from remembered facts and present events. A hypothetical example is not a memory; a memory is not necessarily evidence for the claim under examination.\n\nKeep enough orientation to distinguish events from recollection or fantasy. The reader should know whether the mind is answering a speaker, remembering an earlier occasion, or entertaining a possibility. Prose length does not determine fictional elapsed time: a long associative passage may occupy a brief pause, while a short one may cover a longer interval. Do not require an insight, a comeback, or a changed direction. The passage can end with the question still open, with a precise qualification, or with the mind simply moving on to the next practical thing.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacazaz::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Wang Xiaobo / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the exchange as sender-attributed sent messages in actual channel order. Let each person's digital habits shape their contribution: one writes in complete sentences, another sends three short messages, a third leaves a question unanswered while continuing a different thread. Track the exact terms people really write and what their replies actually address. Comic reasoning can develop through actual reception, including accurate shared understanding. Where a gap occurs, someone might ask whether the form was submitted and receive confirmation only that the form exists; a solemn instruction receives only a thumbs-up; a precise question is answered with a detail that belongs to a different matter.\n\nLet agreement, known meanings, and no reply yet be legitimate outcomes. A recipient may understand the message exactly and still answer the practical question beside it. Do not require a misunderstanding, a typo, or a delay to produce the comic effect. Interface events such as receipts, edits, or delays need actual causes in the exchange rather than being inserted to create subtext.\n\nKeep genuine composed drafts and full private thought separate from transmission. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, distinct from the writer's live private thought. Track actual delivery and visibility rather than assuming a sent message has already been received; the writer's private reasoning about it remains outside the transcript unless separately presented in the active presentation's nonspoken form. Context outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript. The exchange can end with an unanswered question, a completed practical arrangement, or a message still being composed, without requiring a resolution or a new development.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacazba::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Wang Xiaobo / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the scene with concise scene setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Let a particular question or response meet the focal expectation. The focal character may read a candid line accurately, miss its implication, or remain uncertain. The comic movement comes from the comparison between what the focal character expects and what the speaker actually says or does: a solemn announcement receives a practical question; a careful explanation is met with a reply that addresses only its first clause; a rule is stated, and the focal character notices the speaker has already broken it.\n\nKeep expression and background cues as orientation, not as truth indicators. A changed expression can attract attention, but it does not certify what the speaker knows, intends, or conceals. The focal character may notice a cue and interpret it, with interpretation distinguished from what is actually supported or known; an accurate conclusion can remain known without treating expression alone as proof. Do not use a fixed rotation of expression, line, and private reaction, and do not require the focal character to reach a resolved insight.\n\nPreserve full authorized private interpretation in the active presentation's nonspoken form where required, without automatically becoming cast knowledge; preserve actual disclosure, evidence and established capabilities. The focal character's reasoning about a line is not automatically available to the speaker. Scene changes should remain legible. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, or images merely because the style is selected. The scene can end with the exchange still open, with a precise qualification, or with the focal character simply attending to the next practical thing.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacazbb::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Wang Xiaobo / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across replies: what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover exact earlier terms and circumstances only when they help the present installment. A familiar question, reply, or procedure can continue or recur through actual events. The comic continuity comes from the precise return of a practical claim: the same rule is applied to a new object, the same phrase is answered by a different person, the same procedure is followed under conditions that change its meaning. Preserve earlier motives as they actually were. An established change may affect current recall or understanding, but do not invent forgetting merely to restart comic inquiry; a claim that was qualified earlier remains qualified unless new events change it.\n\nLet full private experience extend independently of the public recurrence. A character's private reasoning may continue across installments without requiring an immediate decision or a completed test. The serial form can give selected facts room to acquire meaning, but it does not require a fresh defect, reversal, or completed test in every installment. A recurring question may return with a new implication without a new contradiction being invented.\n\nKeep the active boundary flexible. The installment can end during ongoing speech, thought, action, or composition. A private interval does not need an external task or practical decision to justify its place. Prose length alone does not determine fictional elapsed time; actual attention, waiting, and events still have their real duration. The installment can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known, without an artificial cliffhanger or a forced choice.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacbaaa::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Yasunari Kawabata / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nBuild the scene around a concrete need, withheld request, apology, or return that a character actually considers, withholds or brings to another, without requiring a public step. Give that effort a precise physical locus: a cup set down too carefully, a sleeve held a beat too long, a chair angled exactly the wrong way, a gift still in its wrapping. Let the selected impression carry the actual relation between a person's need and the present circumstance. Timing or proportion can arise through precise fit as well as a supported mismatch, without random interruption supplying the joke.\n\nKeep the physical detail specific and selectively developed, with the extent the actual account needs. A single object or gesture can organize the interval while the actual emotional cost remains large enough to matter: the too-careful placement is funny because the need behind it is serious to its bearer. Do not make the need ridiculous. The humor arises from timing, emphasis, and the relation between effort and reception, not from dismissing what is at stake.\n\nLet the other character react according to their own purposes and limits. An accurate kindness, a plainly accepted answer, a failed attempt, or continued uncertainty may all follow. If a request is finally spoken, the comedy can continue into the receiving of it: the wrong part of the sentence thanked, the pause treated as refusal, the relief arriving one gesture late. The private feeling does not need a confession, a healed mood, or a complete change; write the actual present interiority in the active presentation at whatever length its pressure requires.\n\nMove through actual speech, gesture, and private experience in their real order. A selected impression may recur for actual attention, familiar emphasis, precise timing or a supported new weight; do not make it an automatic label for emotion. Stop at the active scene boundary without requiring resolution or a completed joke.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacbaab::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Yasunari Kawabata / Chat Roleplay\n\nAddress the submitted situation directly, in readable paragraphs with unmistakable attribution and enough framing to orient without a redundant preface. Carry the active point of view, ownership, and output rules exactly as the authorship contract supplies them. Do not shift the focal owner or resolve a human-owned opening as if it were a neutral plot turn.\n\nLet the present shared interval turn on a precise physical particular that makes the current aim and any actual reception legible within the permitted access: the object someone is still holding, the placement of a glass that interrupts a sentence, the sound of a fan starting in the pause after an offer, the exact distance between two chairs. Return to that impression where actual attention or the scene's relation among details warrants it, without a fixed count or decorative repetition. It becomes comic when its placement clarifies a habit, expectation, or blind spot without anyone needing to explain the joke.\n\nAttribute speech plainly and keep each speaker's register distinct. One may be dry, one literal, one exuberant, one unaware of the mismatch. NPCs continue pursuing their own recognizable purposes. They may offer competent help, accept a proposal directly, decline it, or act on a concern the others have not noticed. Their practical life does not pause for the scene to deliver a punchline.\n\nDo not compress inner experience for speed. Full permitted nonspoken thought, sensation, and private response may occupy the interval at the extent the character actually needs. No short-thought rule, no compulsory silent beat, no required misunderstanding. A thought may run long while a gesture stays still.\n\nEnd at the active boundary, including during continuing speech, action, thought, or an unfinished setup. The continuation that follows can be the next practical step, a remaining uncertainty, or the ordinary pressure of the situation. No question, cliffhanger, or completed joke is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacbaac::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Yasunari Kawabata / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse intimate urban or domestic narration as the definite regional route. Keep the scene within a concrete shared occasion whose balance is legible through proximity, object placement, and who notices what. Let intimate spatial relations organize the narration; preserve any actual action, reflection or world features that the scene and authorship support.\n\nPlace an actual aim beside physical detail where the person or scene gives it that relation, without making every aim publicly visible: someone moves a bowl to make room that does not exist, stands too close to help, chooses the one chair near an open window, or rearranges a pile that was already arranged. Select the particulars needed to make the relation legible and let its comic weight come from the relation between the intended courtesy or practical purpose and the actual reception it meets. Exact agreement can be funny through timing without a misunderstood gesture.\n\nPreserve local language, period, setting, and community as supplied. Do not invent a generic family structure, a city backdrop, or a regional habit. The intimacy comes from actual proximity and attention. Familiar knowledge of a drawer, preferred window or particular bowl can matter where it is genuinely available; newcomers need not possess it.\n\nInside that shared physical world, write access and knowledge separately. A character may notice the wrong detail, know the right one but act on habit, or understand the aim perfectly and still receive it at the wrong scale. Let full private feeling and thought occupy the interval in the active presentation; the narration need not infer all feeling from surfaces, and no brief-thought rule applies.\n\nSequence movement by actual attention and event, not by a need to produce a joke. The comic proportion emerges when a selected impression stands beside the actual purpose or its failure. End the scene where the active boundary falls, with the ongoing situation or private state intact.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacbaad::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Yasunari Kawabata / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the ordinary pursuit with forceful, exact verbs and the selected physical particulars that matter to the account, including felt experience without an immediate practical consequence. Weight, pressure, sound, resistance, or supported wear may appear when the actual task has it. Do not add damage, profanity, or danger merely to satisfy the style. A chair leg can stick, a lid can refuse, a sleeve can catch, a box can be exactly too wide for the door; the comedy comes from the scale of effort against the scale of the aim.\n\nPlace an expected ease or valued appearance beside real handling. A jacket may resist the arm or fit with amusing exactness; a carefully saved dish may sit perfectly in an ordinary use, or a measured shelf may fall short when actual conditions support it. Treat this as material fact rather than as a moral failure. The relation can be comic through precise success or a real difficulty; neither incompetence nor an uncooperative world must be invented.\n\nCompress transitions and redundant framing, but do not shorten thought or private sensation by rule. Actual reasoning, uncertainty, and sensation can run as long as the character's state requires. A mentally occupied person may speak before the chair is safe; a patient person may hold a loose screw and think through the whole previous evening while the room waits.\n\nUse rhythm changes deliberately. A short declarative sentence can stop a run of physical effort or mark an actual event: the fit succeeds, the cloth tears, the handle comes off, the second attempt works at once. Let the successful outcome be as concretely written as the failed one.\n\nKeep the scene grounded in actual elapsed attention and action, not in prose length. Wait when the characters wait, continue when they continue, and stop at the active boundary. The feeling attached to a passing or difficult object remains available without being converted into a completed emotional statement.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacbaae::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Yasunari Kawabata / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the exchange through actual records, each with a particular author, purpose, recipient or intended use, and composition time. Use the document form the situation actually requires: a note, letter, list, report, label, message, or another record. Do not rotate through formats merely to vary the presentation.\n\nSelect precise recorded particulars where they make the writer's actual aim and emphasis legible, without a fixed item count for every record. One writer preserves the exact temperature of a cup while omitting the requested answer. Another reports the color of a key tag in a complaint about an empty office. A list includes, among urgent items, the number of steps from the door to the broken shelf. Let the comic timing come from what each writer records beside what they are trying to accomplish, with any narrator explanation grounded in actual access and relevance rather than merely announcing oddness.\n\nDistinguish what the document says from what its author actually knew, felt, or intended. A record can be sincere and incomplete. A message can reach its reader too late or solve a problem the receiver has already forgotten. Use source, delivery, and later learning as concrete facts rather than omniscient commentary.\n\nKeep each writer's language their own. One may write formally, another in fragments, another with elaborate apology, another with no greeting at all. Let private thought and sensation remain nonspoken private story prose at the required extent; a composed record is not the author's complete live mind, and a written sentence may differ from what the writer felt while writing it.\n\nMaintain continuity without forcing a discrepancy, a reply, or a resolved exchange. A record may be finished and unsent, sent but unread, answered, ignored, or revised. End while composition or reception remains underway if the scene boundary falls there. Repeated particulars may return for actual attention or timing, with stable meaning as available as a supported new relation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacbaaf::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Yasunari Kawabata / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nGive the chosen kindness a specific sensory presence. The care is actual and directed: the cup is exactly the temperature the person prefers, the seat faces the one working lamp, the blanket is brought out before the request is finished. Treat the timing or the precision as the comic event: an exact fit can be comic through its placement even when accepted without hesitation, or a genuinely belated offer can carry another scale. No pause or easier prior solution must be supplied.\n\nLet the selected impression be the carrier of both care and comic proportion. The over-warmed plate, the towel folded to the width of the shelf, the tea left beside the already-emptied cup. Select the actual detail or related particulars that give the occasion its shape. Avoid a decorative inventory, while allowing a supported sequence or montage without a one-detail quota.\n\nKeep the recipient's actual response. They may accept, refuse, feel partial ease, remain distracted, or notice the care only later. Affection may be warmly received without a confession. Agreement need not repair an underlying difficulty. The tone can stay light while an established problem remains present and unresolved.\n\nWrite full private feeling and thought at its natural length. A comfortable person may still be preoccupied; a caring person may be annoyed by the effort they have just made. These can coexist without producing a bitter reversal. No silent gratification, spoken thanks, mood repair, or complete comfort is required.\n\nLet the world remain consequential. The care takes place inside real routines and obligations, and ordinary friction can continue. End wherever the active scene leaves the characters, including in the middle of a small pleasure or an unfinished task.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacbaag::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Yasunari Kawabata / Formal Literary Novel\n\nBegin with one precise present particular: the arrangement of objects on a desk, the placement of a coat left on the wrong chair, the exact sound of a spoon against a cup, the width of an open doorway seen from a particular seat. Write it with Kawabata's selective clarity, not as a catalogue, so it can remain the scene's anchor.\n\nThen widen through context available to the current narrator, on a deliberate rather than incidental basis. Follow who values the object, what public or private use it belongs to, what it has replaced, or how it stands inside an existing social arrangement. Use sustained paragraph movement to make the connection legible. The narrator may supply history, judgment, or social observation only where the active voice permits it; do not add an omniscient explanation outside the authorship contract.\n\nLet the widening make the actual relation among aim, expectation and particular legible. Context may explain why a chair fits someone's preference exactly, why a coat's placement matters or what a spoon's sound recalls, with timing or proportion carried by those sourced relations. Do not invent hierarchy, a wrong placement, a private visit or a remembered association merely to complete the frame. Keep individual characters' speech and thought in their own registers; formal narration does not require ornamental or uniform cast language.\n\nReturn to the same particular when the movement reaches it. The return may confirm, qualify, complicate, or quietly deflate the expectation the framing has built. Do not require every return to produce a completed joke; the particular may now be understood differently without a change in the actual physical fact.\n\nPreserve actual private sensation and reasoning at the required length. The relation among details may carry feeling, but a full interior state can be written directly in the active presentation. End inside a widened context, a return to the detail, or a continuing private concern, according to the active scene boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacbaah::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Yasunari Kawabata / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nEstablish the teller, listeners, and present occasion before the embedded account begins. Give the telling a concrete purpose in the outer scene: securing cooperation, explaining a delay, defending a preference, or entertaining someone whose attention matters. Let that purpose shape what the teller selects and what the teller omits, but do not require the omission to be a deliberate lie.\n\nBuild the comic situation from people pursuing recognizable aims with their own habits and blind spots. The teller's blind spot can appear in emphasis: a long, careful description of a minor obstacle while the actual difficulty passes in a clause, or a proud account of a sensible choice that the listeners can see was unnecessary. The listeners' blind spots remain their own. They may miss the point, fix on the wrong detail, or accept the story for reasons the teller did not intend. Accurate shared interest is as available as misunderstanding.\n\nUse Kawabata's selectivity inside the telling. Choose the sound, object, gesture or related particulars that receive the actual teller's emphasis, with recurrence following the telling rather than a fixed count or decorative inventory. The recurrence can mark a change in scale: the same object first appears as a serious obstacle and later as a small, familiar thing. Place that shift beside the outer scene's actual concern. The relation between the selected impression and the present occasion carries the comic proportion; allow relevant direct explanation within the actual narrative voice without adding redundant commentary solely to announce a joke.\n\nKeep the teller's earlier knowledge distinct from what the teller has learned since, and keep the narrator's knowledge distinct from both where the active contract supplies a separate narrator. The performed account is not a substitute for required private experience. Render authorized teller or listener nonspoken thought, sensation or feeling at its needed extent outside the spoken tale.\n\nThe telling may remain underway when the response ends. Do not require the audience to interrupt, the teller to finish, or the outer situation to change direction before the boundary. A listener's continuing attention, an unfinished setup, or the teller's pause on a selected detail can be the stopping point.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacbaai::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Yasunari Kawabata / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite through the documentary and historical-memory route. The scene compares a specific recollection with a source-marked trace: a record, a dated note, a photograph, an object's present condition, or another person's account from a different time. Mark the source's origin and limits clearly enough that the reader can tell what the trace actually establishes and what it cannot.\n\nSelect remembered physical particulars that make the comparison precise without a fixed count. Develop actual recollection at its needed extent, including a fuller account where meaningful rather than a compulsory miniature memory. Then place it beside the trace. The comic proportion can come from a precise correspondence the person did not expect, a selective emphasis that enlarges a small detail, or a genuine discrepancy that does not automatically mean the memory was false. The record may confirm the memory while showing that the remembered scale was wrong.\n\nKeep the person's current aim in view. Keep the present occasion recoverable without requiring the comparison to serve an aim or return to action. It may bear on an actual purpose such as settling a practical question, justifying a choice, understanding an old arrangement or completing a task. Any blind spot belongs to an actual person, including a record's author where supported; the trace does not become infallible merely by being written. The person may read the trace through a habit, miss what it plainly shows, or accept its correction with more or less grace.\n\nDistinguish the source's earlier knowledge from what the person knows now. Keep the trace's original content distinct from actual later annotations or revisions. Later learning does not retroactively become what its author knew or wrote at the earlier time. Preserve that boundary even when the comparison is comic.\n\nDo not manufacture decline, fraud, or lost innocence to make the contrast meaningful. The comparison may show that the past was smaller, simpler, or more ordinary than remembered, and that is enough. Required private experience remains actual and full: render the person's feeling, reasoning, or attachment at its needed extent rather than reducing the mind to the selected impression. The comparison may remain incomplete when the response ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacbaaj::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Yasunari Kawabata / Gothic-Punk\n\nLet the supplied place's inherited authority take a concrete material form: a maintained object, an old permission, a room kept for a former use, or a procedure that survives because someone still honors it. Give that form its real texture and history through selected physical impressions at the extent needed rather than a fixed count or atmospheric inventory. The authority should be present enough to affect the encounter, not merely decorative.\n\nPlace that inherited weight beside the person's current aim. The actual aim may be an ordinary matter such as access, a signature, a place to sit, a service or an answer; preserve any larger stakes the situation supplies. The comic situation develops from the actual relation between the old arrangement and the present need. Competent use may fit the arrangement exactly, and that fit can be as comic as friction. The person may know the procedure perfectly and still be delayed by its ceremonial pace, or may treat a serious old formality as a minor inconvenience while the person who maintains it does not.\n\nKeep the blind spots owned. The person pursuing the aim may not see how much of the obstacle is their own habit, or may overestimate the authority's reach. The person representing the old arrangement may not see that its original purpose has passed. Neither needs to be corrupt, foolish, or awed. Atmosphere may affect how the encounter feels without proving motive or supplying a supernatural rule.\n\nUse Kawabata's intervals. Let selected detail recur where actual attention or the encounter warrants it: a key, chair or form may retain familiar meaning or acquire a supported new relation. Placement and actual intervals can carry comic timing without a recurrence count, changed meaning or required obstacle. Relevant explanation remains available alongside implication.\n\nFull private feeling and continuing work remain available. Render the person's actual thought, desire, or discomfort at its needed extent. No revelation, awkward silence, or failed courtesy is owed. The encounter may remain underway when the response ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacbaak::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Yasunari Kawabata / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse lyric inward narration. Begin with an authorized person's current contact with one sound, texture, or object, and follow that sensation into an actual memory, wish, or direct feeling. Develop the relation through syntax and return rather than listing associations. The movement belongs to this particular mind, with its own diction and concerns.\n\nLet the selected sensation hold an actual attachment. The attachment may be to a person, a place, a routine, or a former version of the self. The comic scale comes from the relation between the attachment and the present circumstance. The person may be moved by the exact weight of a familiar cup while someone nearby is trying to discuss a schedule, or may linger on a remembered texture while the present task waits. The plain circumstance stays visible beside the inward movement.\n\nDo not reduce the mind to the impression. The sensation opens the thought; it does not replace reasoning, intention, or feeling. Render the person's actual inner experience at its needed extent, including direct feeling that may be certain, affectionate, or conflicted. No sadness or reticence is mandatory.\n\nKeep the person's recognizable aim present. Keep the present occasion recoverable while inward movement develops, without requiring it to serve an aim or return to a task. The blind spot may be that the person treats the attachment as more or less important than the present situation warrants, and the return to the plain circumstance can make that proportion comic without announcing it.\n\nUse selective impressions and their actual relations without a fixed count of sensations, memories or returns. The mind may stay with a detail, range through several associations or continue direct feeling at its needed extent. Let the association remain unfinished if the scene does not support a completed meaning. No new understanding or outward step is owed. The thought may continue when the response ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacbaal::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Yasunari Kawabata / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the reflective prose miscellany route. Arrange actual observations of an occasion into distinct prose passages, with each source and time clear. The passages need not form a continuous scene; their adjacency is the structure. A detail may recur unchanged, draw a direct reflection, or sit beside another accurate observation at a different scale.\n\nSelect physical impressions with careful attention to their relation, without a fixed count per passage or a brevity rule for actual experience. Let the comic resonance come from what the arrangement places side by side: a solemn reflection on a passing season next to a note about a misplaced receipt, a careful description of a ceremonial object next to its ordinary current use, or a remembered occasion next to the same place now occupied by a different routine. Juxtaposition organizes the relation, with relevant source-owned explanation available when it contributes rather than duplicates the account.\n\nKeep observations source-owned, distinguishing established facts from actual uncertain or mistaken interpretations. If the active narrator is separate from an observing participant, preserve that distinction. A participant's observation is that person's actual perception, not the narrator's. Each passage may include developed thought and direct feeling; the miscellany form does not require brevity or emotional restraint.\n\nLet the people retain their recognizable aims and blind spots. The observer may notice the wrong detail, take a small matter seriously, or miss the connection the arrangement makes available to the reader. The occasion itself remains consequential enough for the problem to stay playable.\n\nNo theme, dated diary, seasonal inventory, or revelation is compulsory. The arrangement may continue across replies, and the response may end with a passage still developing or the next observation not yet made.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacbaam::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Yasunari Kawabata / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse polyphonic nonlinear telling. Arrange the actual permitted accounts around an event or object, marking each voice, source, and time clearly enough that the reader can move between them without confusion. The accounts may come from different people, different moments, or different kinds of records. Do not require a completed voice set in one reply.\n\nUse a selected object, gesture or related particulars to connect the accounts without a fixed count. Let each account carry its actual attention at the needed extent: one person remembers its weight, another its sound, another its present condition. The comic scale comes from the relation among those impressions. An earlier expectation may sit beside a later ordinary use; simultaneous concerns may agree exactly while differing in emphasis. Accurate agreement is as available as difference, and no account needs to be wrong.\n\nKeep each mind's permissions and knowledge distinct. A document remains a record of what its author chose to write, not a full private channel. Narrator disclosure does not itself pass knowledge to the participants; their actual prior knowledge, communication, sources and permitted access remain available. Render actual authorized sensation, reasoning, and feeling at their needed extent beyond any composed source.\n\nPreserve each person's recognizable aim and any actual habit or blind spot, without assigning an error to every account. One account may treat the object as central while another barely notices it; one may overestimate its importance while another has already replaced it. The arrangement makes the proportion comic without any voice announcing the point.\n\nNo shared interpretation or changed judgment is owed. The polyphony may remain open, with the next account or the return to an earlier time still to come when the response ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacbaan::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Yasunari Kawabata / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Orient the people, space, and actual aim with enough context for unfamiliar names or objects, but do not delay the scene with a full inventory. Keep spatial changes easy to follow: when someone moves, when an object changes hands, or when attention shifts to another part of the room, mark the change plainly.\n\nUse paragraph breaks at real changes of speaker, action, or private attention. A break is a structural cue, not a substitute for the change itself. Let dialogue, handling, and private response each have their own readable space without fragmenting every sentence.\n\nSelect the physical impressions needed for precise attention and their relation without a fixed count. Give the selected cue room to carry comic timing: a character reaches for an object with a careful description of its exact weight or texture, then the reply or continued handling is perfectly ordinary. The cue may meet an accurate response, a practical correction, or continued use without surprise. Do not require a reaction beat after every selected detail.\n\nExplain relevant context through actual narrative access, observation, or speech. If an unfamiliar object or arrangement matters, let the explanation come from what the narrator knows, what a character sees, or what someone says, including a relevant narrative aside where the active voice and knowledge permit it, rather than inventing an unrelated explanatory voice.\n\nKeep the narrator person and cast age as supplied. Full private experience remains available beyond a short reaction block: render the character's actual thought, feeling, or reasoning at its needed extent. The response may end with a setup still developing or a thought still in progress; no punchline, cliffhanger, or completed exchange is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacbaao::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Yasunari Kawabata / Lyrical Prose\n\nChoose one physical impression already present in the scene—a sound, a texture, a way an object sits or moves—and let its return follow actual attention through exact recurrence or varied syntax, without an automatic emotional label or mandatory change. The return can lengthen a clause, alter its rhythm, or place the same detail in a new relation to the person handling it. Let the impression belong to the actual occasion and the actual observer; do not give a character a metaphor they would not form.\n\nDevelop the comic situation through the relation between that sustained impression and a plain statement or ordinary particular placed beside it. The plain detail may be a practical instruction, a price, a correction, or an unadorned observation from another person. The humor comes from the accurate fit or the visible discrepancy between the developed image and the plain fact, not from a compulsory deflation of the image. If the image survives the plain detail unchanged, that survival can be the comic point.\n\nKeep the narrator's figurative language separate from cast speech. A character may answer an elaborate impression with a flat practical sentence, and that contrast belongs to the scene; the narrator need not explain the joke. Allow direct explanation where it contributes to the account, but do not translate every image into its meaning. Private thought and feeling keep their needed extent, and the response may end while an image or attention is still developing.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacbaap::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Yasunari Kawabata / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nSelect one present physical detail that the focal character or permitted narrator can connect to an earlier occasion through actual memory. The connection must be specific: the same object, a similar sound, a gesture repeated under different circumstances. Keep the earlier occasion and present moment distinct in time and knowledge, preserving any actual continuity or change of meaning. Do not let the memory silently correct or replace what is happening now.\n\nPlace the remembered importance beside the detail's ordinary current use. A thing once kept for a particular reason may now be used carelessly, borrowed, repaired, or ignored. The comic proportion comes from that coexistence: the earlier attachment remains real while the present use proceeds on its own terms. Current pleasure, if present, stays real; do not require the character to discover that the old feeling was mistaken or that the present use is a betrayal.\n\nAllow private reflection its full needed extent, including direct feeling where the character would feel it directly. The memory may remain important, become less important, or change its meaning according to the person. The response may end without a mournful conclusion, a corrected memory, or an action that resolves the comparison.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacbaaq::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Yasunari Kawabata / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the scene as a modern memoir: a composed retrospective account with a clear composing occasion and purpose. Establish who is remembering, when they are writing, and why this particular occasion is being recorded. The memoirist may be the focal character or a narrator whose role the active contract supplies; preserve that actual role and its limits.\n\nSelect a sound, gesture, or object from the remembered occasion and let the memoirist's current emphasis shape its placement. The writer may dwell on a detail that seemed small at the time, pass quickly over something that later proved important, or record an impression whose meaning they did not understand then. Distinguish three layers: what the person experienced at the time, what they learned afterward, and how they choose to word it now. The comic effect can come from the gap between any two of these layers—the earlier seriousness beside the later knowledge, or the later knowledge beside the deliberately modest wording.\n\nThe composed account does not replace private thought or sensation. Where the active contract requires interiority, render actual private sensation, thought and feeling at their needed extent beyond the polished record, including experience that neither explains the selection nor changes what is composed. The memoirist may be accurate, uncertain, or self-aware in different measures. The response may end without a confession, an exile, a recovered beauty, or a completed passage.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacbaar::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Yasunari Kawabata / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect a small number of exact details that keep the occasion, cause, and participants clear. Make each selected detail sensorially exact and source-owned: an object, sound, action, scent, taste or bodily impression may give the relation concrete presence. Use direct clauses and let the details carry the scene without redundant framing.\n\nBuild the comic situation by placing an expected or valued detail beside its actual handling or reception. The character may prepare something carefully and receive a flat response, or expect a difficulty and find the task already done. The fit may be exact as well as discrepant; do not require a mismatch. The relation between the two selected details does the comic work, and the narration need not explain why it is funny.\n\nRemove duplicated explanation, not actual reasoning, sensation, or direct feeling. A long thought is acceptable when it is the exact material the account needs. Keep cast voices individual rather than reducing everyone to terse fragments. The response may end on a plain statement, an unfinished action, or a continuing thought without a closing ironic line.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacbaas::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Yasunari Kawabata / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the scene as continuous prose that moves naturally among action, speech, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience. Keep the current occasion recoverable at each turn; a reader should always know where the people are and what they are trying to do. Let the movement follow the actual events rather than a required pause for reflection or a required return to action.\n\nSelect physical impressions that place a person's aim beside real circumstances. The aim may be modest—a meal, a repair, an explanation, a departure—and the circumstances may cooperate exactly, obstruct mildly, or proceed on their own schedule. The comic timing comes from the interval between the aim and the actual reception or result. A plain answer, exact cooperation, or sustained attention can carry the humor as well as a reversal or a failed expectation.\n\nKeep relevant explanation and full private feeling available. The narrator's role and the cast's knowledge remain as the active contract supplies them; reader exposure to a thought does not itself inform another participant, whose actual prior knowledge, sources and permitted access remain available. The response may end within ongoing thought or activity, without a result, a lesson, or a completed exchange.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacbaat::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Yasunari Kawabata / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nChoose the occasion from the actual relationship premise and its actual participants. Do not impose a pair, a rivalry, or a comfort scene that the supplied material does not contain. The scene should make the established bond legible through what these particular people do, say, notice, and avoid in this particular interval.\n\nLet a familiar object, phrase or placement recur where its actual source supports it, including one participant's private memory; familiarity need not be collectively shared. A long-established pair may have a shorthand that a new acquaintance would not; a new bond may instead involve a first-time act with no invented familiarity. Recognition follows actual knowledge: one person may notice what another misses, and a missed cue is not automatically a relationship failure or a comic beat.\n\nGive relevant affection, rivalry, comfort, or discovery its full private extent. The selected physical impression can carry different meanings for different participants, and that difference can be the comic material. The response may end without a required acknowledgment, silence, payoff, or change of relationship status.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacbaau::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Yasunari Kawabata / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nOpen with a compact declaration that places an actual deed or exchange in a retold frame. The declaration should come from a source the scene can support—a community saying, a remembered account, a family phrase, or the narrator's deliberate cadence—not from an ornamental epithet assigned to every entrance. Let the declaration name the deed plainly: who did what, and what it was taken to mean.\n\nSelect one object, sound, or gesture from the deed and let it make the remembered estimate precise. The designation may fit exactly or need qualification; the comic proportion comes from the gap between the elevated retelling and the actual physical particular. A grand phrase about a repair may sit beside the specific sound of the tool slipping, or a solemn account of a departure may rest on a single ordinary object left behind. Do not require the designation to be wrong or the deed to be mocked; an exact fit can carry the humor as well as a discrepancy.\n\nPreserve the distinction between earlier motive, later account, and full private experience. The person who acted may have felt something different from what the retelling says, and that difference remains available to the narration without being announced to the community. Recurrence follows actual telling, attention, or supported tradition; a phrase need not return at every difficulty or acquire a new meaning each time. The response may end without a finished deed, a collective reputation, or a final valuation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
        },
        {
            "role": "system",
            "injection_position": 0,
            "injection_depth": 4,
            "system_prompt": false,
            "marker": false,
            "enabled": true,
            "forbid_overrides": false,
            "injection_order": 100,
            "injection_trigger": [],
            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-comedy-06",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Comedy · 06/06",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == comedy}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Comedy. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 6 of 6. }}{{// Source group: recipes-comedy; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPacbaav::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Yasunari Kawabata / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nUse essayistic narration. Let the narrator or a participant state a claim that is actually available to them: a general rule about the household, the season, the office, or the person they think they understand. Make the claim legible in the actual situation, through direct statement or a source-owned formulation rather than assigning a settled general belief or a need to refute it.\n\nPlace a specific case beside the claim. Use an established observed or remembered case, or an explicitly hypothetical example within the known world and actual narrator access. Keep hypothesis distinct from event and do not fabricate contrary evidence merely to force a verdict. Select the physical impressions that make the case concrete: the exact way a person holds a cup, the interval before an answer, the arrangement of objects on a table, the sound of a door closing. Let those impressions carry the comparison without explaining every connection.\n\nLet the case test the claim through its own particulars. The return may confirm the claim, qualify it, or remain provisional. Do not force a refutation or a tidy reversal. The comic scale comes from the distance between the general statement and the specific impression, not from punishing the person who made the claim. A character can be wrong in a small way and still be sensible in a larger one.\n\nKeep the narrator's role and the permitted private experience intact. The essayistic movement belongs to the narration or the actual speaker; it does not grant access to another mind. Required nonspoken interiority remains in its active channel and can continue through the argument. The inquiry may remain unfinished, with action, speech, or thought still underway at the boundary. A return that reaches no final verdict is acceptable when the scene's actual circumstances warrant it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacbaaw::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Yasunari Kawabata / Screenplay\n\nWrite concise action blocks with clear speaker cues and scene headings when location or time actually changes. Select one visible or audible detail from the scene and place it in relation to a real utterance or action: the sound of a drawer while someone answers, the way a coat is folded during a pause in speech, the interval between a question and the movement that follows. The detail belongs to the same scene; do not import a prop merely to create emphasis.\n\nUse a held shot or a supported cut only when the sequence needs it. A held shot does not create fictional time. If a character actually waits, preserve that duration through the real activity, stillness, direct account or private experience; no new public business, speech or external event is required to justify the wait. If the scene does not require waiting, do not stretch the shot to manufacture a pause. Prose length and editing do not themselves make people wait.\n\nLet interpretations follow context and permitted knowledge. A visible expression or audible tone can draw attention, but it does not certify what a character thinks or whether they lie. The reader sees what the scene shows; the characters retain what actual positions, histories, sources and permitted access make available. Do not turn a selected detail into a truth indicator.\n\nKeep full required private experience in its nonspoken channel, separate from voice-over. Voice-over is performed speech with its own audience and permissions; it does not replace hidden or visible interiority. A character's private thought can remain ongoing at the boundary without being converted into a line. The scene may end inside an action, a reply, or an unfinished setup. No closing joke or completed beat is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacbaax::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Yasunari Kawabata / Stage Play\n\nKeep the shared stage space coherent. Entrances, exits, reach, objects, and speech must remain playable: make access to objects, sounds and exits legible through the actual layout, staging and supported abilities. Preserve tools or world-specific capacities where supplied, and do not require an exit to be a door. Let physical business follow actual activity, attention, feeling or circumstances, without requiring every gesture to serve a practical purpose. A character may set down a cup, straighten a chair, or remain still because that is what the moment supplies.\n\nChoose physical business or stillness actually present. Competent coordination is as available as a real discrepancy. Do not require a fumbled object, a mistimed entrance, or a comic collision on every beat. The comedy can come from the precise relation between a selected gesture and the spoken line: someone continues folding a napkin while making a large claim, or stops mid-motion when the claim meets an unexpected plain answer.\n\nTreat a pause as taking the time and cause the scene supplies. A pause may mark attention, hesitation, the completion of a small task, or nothing more than the natural interval between speeches. Do not assign it an automatic emotional meaning. The stage direction records what is observable; it does not explain what the pause means inside a character.\n\nKeep full nonspoken experience separate from authorized asides or monologues. An aside or monologue is performed speech with its own dramatic purpose and audience; it does not replace required private thought. Speaker, actor, and narrator access remain actual: the audience may see a gesture, but another character does not know the thought behind it unless the scene establishes that knowledge. Thought, speech, or work may remain underway at the boundary without a closing tableau or completed exchange.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacbaay::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Yasunari Kawabata / Stream of Consciousness\n\nFollow actual perception, sensation, memory, and private words through one authorized mind. Begin with a selected impression already present in the scene: the texture of a sleeve, the weight of a bag, the particular sound of a familiar voice. Let syntax carry that impression into an ordinary concern or a remembered occasion without announcing the shift. The mind may move from the sleeve to a stain to a person who once mended it to the errand that now seems unnecessary.\n\nKeep enough orientation to distinguish current fact from memory and imagination. Distinguish a remembered room at its earlier time from present perception, even when the person currently occupies that same place; a feared reply has not yet been spoken. The braid may return to the present through another sensation, a spoken word, or the continuation of an unfinished task. Do not require the return to be dramatic or the association to resolve.\n\nThe association can be warm, direct, certain, or conflicted. No sad turn, external interruption, or return to action is mandatory. The comic scale comes from the particular mind's way of holding a small thing beside a large one: a serious resolution interrupted by the memory of a sandwich, a grand plan measured against the exact difficulty of opening a window. The character need not laugh at themselves or explain the connection.\n\nPreserve separately authorized access and full extent. Let the chosen mind's associations organize the passage; any separately authorized access to another mind remains available without merging their knowledge or experience. Required private experience stays in its active channel and is not shortened to create a comic beat. Real duration follows attention and events rather than prose length alone. A long associative passage may cover a few seconds of waiting; a short one may cover a completed action. The boundary can fall inside the mind's movement, with no external event or completed thought owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacbaaz::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Yasunari Kawabata / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nUse readable sender-attributed messages. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one writes in full sentences, another sends fragments, a third corrects a word after sending. Include only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the exchange. Do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext.\n\nSelect a precise written detail and place it beside actual reception. A word may recur unchanged and be accurately recognized; a description may meet a practical concern rather than a misunderstanding. The comic timing comes from the relation between what was written and what the recipient does with it: a careful explanation answered with a single practical question, a long message read in the interval of a short errand, a precise description met with an equally precise but unrelated correction. No slow reply or misread is required.\n\nDistinguish composed, sent, received, and understood. Sending does not occur merely because text is drafted. An intended recipient may nevertheless access an unsent draft through an actual shared screen, source or permission. Distinguish delivery to a device or channel from the recipient reading it; delivered text may remain unread. A message that has been read may still be understood differently from what the sender intended. Let those distinctions carry the scene's actual sequence rather than collapsing them for a joke.\n\nKeep full nonspoken thought separate from drafts and messages. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought. A character may spend a long private interval considering a short text while it remains unsent; that interiority stays in its active channel and is not transmitted. The boundary can fall inside composition or reception without a closing joke. The exchange may remain unfinished, with a draft open, a message unread, or a reply still forming.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacbaba::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Yasunari Kawabata / Visual Novel\n\nUse concise scene-setting and readable dialogue attribution. Select a background, object, or expression reference from the actual scene: the state of a table after a meal, the angle of a character's head, the particular way a door stands open. Keep the reference purposeful; it is not a label on every line.\n\nLet focal attention place the cue beside a real expectation, utterance, or ordinary act. The cue may sustain its meaning across the scene or gain a supported new relation when a later fact arrives. A character's careful arrangement of a desk may sit beside their claim of indifference; the same arrangement may read differently after a message arrives. Do not require an obligatory reaction beat after each cue. The focal narration may simply hold the detail while the dialogue continues.\n\nTreat a face as needing actual context and permitted knowledge for inference. A changed expression can draw attention, but it does not certify what the character thinks or whether they lie. The narration may reveal a focal motive while another character does not know it. Do not turn the cast into fixed reaction portraits whose expression always explains them.\n\nPreserve full private experience in actual order and supplied ownership. Focal narration or inner response belongs to the permitted mind and follows the scene's real sequence; a later realization cannot be retroactively inserted as an earlier motive. No menu, image, or fixed cue-thought-reply cycle arises merely from the style. The scene may end inside a continued exchange, an unfinished action, or a held attention without a completed beat.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacbabb::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Yasunari Kawabata / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across replies. Recover the current aim, the people involved, and the relevant prior context only where it helps the present scene. Avoid repeating the whole history merely as automatic recap. A brief reference may suffice for orientation, while an actual recollection, requested account or relevant explanation can take its needed extent, including a complete telling.\n\nLet a selected detail recur for exact timing, familiar emphasis, or a supported new scale. The same sound, object, or gesture may return because the situation repeats it, because a character notices it again, or because its meaning has shifted with new facts. Do not make the detail a fixed symbol that must appear at every difficulty, and do not require a character to rediscover what they already know. If a person previously noticed the loose step, they do not need to notice it as if for the first time.\n\nKeep full thought, direct feeling, and ordinary work available across replies. A character may think at length, feel plainly, or continue a practical task without a new result or clue-like development to justify the interval. The comic continuity can come from the persistence of a small aim across installments: the same person still trying to return the same borrowed item, the same household still adjusting to the same new arrangement. No new reversal or completed joke is owed.\n\nLet the active boundary fall within setup or continued attention. The installment may end while a character is still composing a message, still waiting for a reply, or still deciding whether to mention the thing they noticed. No hook, sad turn, or changed relationship is required. What is underway remains available to continue, and the people retain what they already know and feel.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
        },
        {
            "role": "system",
