            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-crime-02",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Crime · 02/07",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == crime}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Crime. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 2 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-crime; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPadaeav::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Douglas Adams / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nUse psychological and essayistic narration as the definite Scandinavian route. Let the permitted narrator or focal thinker formulate an actual claim, question, or broad explanation about the offense, scheme, or aftermath, then develop that formulation through a real remembered or explicitly imagined case. The case may confirm the claim's full scope, qualify it, or leave the relation uncertain; the passage does not need to end in a correction, a completed investigation, or a practical decision.\n\nMake the Adams contrast operate inside the essayistic movement. When a large account of criminal circumstances meets a small logistical or inward particular, show the particular in its own terms: the exact errand, the remembered room, the unmanageable object, the private hesitation. Let the narrator or thinker follow the consequence of that meeting without turning every encounter into a delivered joke or a tidy reversal. Different people may respond to the same broad claim differently; the essayistic voice belongs to its actual owner, not to a universal comic narrator.\n\nPreserve the supplied community, language, period, and setting rather than importing a national temperament, snow, isolation, or bureaucratic severity. Distinguish the actual participants' knowledge at a remembered occasion, later available context, and the active narrator's own access. The narrator need not have participated in or personally remembered the case. Full feeling, uncertainty, and sustained reflection may continue without an outward act. The response may end during ongoing thought, attention, or waiting.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaeaw::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Douglas Adams / Screenplay\n\nWrite concise readable action blocks with clear speaker cues and scene headings when location or time changes. Place an actual spoken or displayed claim about the offense, scheme, or aftermath beside observable conduct that gives it exact practical terms. A character may describe a plan in broad terms while the action block shows the specific door, package, schedule, or physical task that the plan actually depends on. Use a supported cut or held action when it helps the comparison, but do not require a mishap, omitted condition, demonstration, or delivered gag.\n\nRetain necessary criminal context in the permitted form. If the audience needs to know why an act is criminal, supply the relevant explanation or context in the active contract's permitted form rather than assuming the label is self-evident or inventing a public line, document, or character belief solely to explain it. Keep full private experience separate from footage and authorized voice-over. A character's fear, reasoning, suspicion, or private knowledge remains nonspoken material in the active presentation and is not automatically available to other characters or the camera.\n\nPreserve actual knowledge channels. A cut may show the audience something a character does not know, but it cannot make that character know it. Let different characters respond to the same broad claim according to their own situation and temperament. The scene may end during continuing action, speech, or thought without a completed exchange, a new clue, or a punchline.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaeax::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Douglas Adams / Stage Play\n\nMake the public scene playable through clear speakers, positions, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be staged. Put an actual line or request about the offense, scheme, or aftermath beside the business that gives it particular terms. A character may announce a broad intention while the stage directions show the exact object, movement, or practical obstacle that the intention must accommodate. Keep the relation playable in the shared space while preserving an actual explanatory speech when it belongs to the character and occasion.\n\nRetain necessary criminal context under the active contract. Keep any needed explanation of why an act is criminal in its permitted contextual form; it need not be forced into dialogue, observable business, or a character's private belief. Preserve full attributed nonspoken feeling and actual knowledge. Performed asides and monologues have their own audience and permissions; they do not replace required live private experience. Performed speech has its actual reception, while presenting nonspoken material alone grants no cast knowledge; preserve real prior knowledge and permitted channels.\n\nKeep different characters' responses to the same broad claim distinct. One may treat the practical detail as ordinary, another as impossible, another as irrelevant to the larger plan. The response can end during ongoing action, speech, or thought without a new prop, confession, completed exchange, or theatrical revelation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaeay::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Douglas Adams / Stream of Consciousness\n\nBraid the authorized mind's perception, memory, private wording, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern. Let an explanation or familiar claim about the offense, scheme, or aftermath meet a precise real or imagined particular wherever attention takes it. A broad account of what happened may surface beside the exact texture of a door, a repeated phrase, a remembered errand, or a physical sensation. Follow that association without requiring the mind to reach a useful conclusion, change its belief, or perform an absurd realization.\n\nKeep actual events distinct from fallible recollection and fantasy. The mind may revisit a memory, revise it, or imagine an alternative, but the reader should be able to distinguish what happened from what the character wishes, fears, or misremembers. Preserve the character's actual knowledge and feeling; the technique does not make every character share a mind or turn scene facts indeterminate.\n\nLet logical links, rumination, and emotional pressure continue without an outward act. Prose length alone does not fix fictional elapsed time; actual attention, waiting, and events do. The response boundary may fall within ongoing thought, a half-formed sentence, or an interrupted association.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaeaz::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Douglas Adams / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nUse sender-attributed messages in actual registers and lengths. Select wording and circulation for the offense, scheme, or aftermath: who sends what, to whom, when, and for what actual purpose, including company or expression without an instrumental aim. Place a broad explanation beside actual uptake or clearly separated context. A sender may describe a plan in sweeping terms while the recipient's reply, a delay, an attachment, or a separated scene shows the exact practical term the plan depends on. No error or second message is required to make the contrast visible.\n\nDistinguish composed, sent, delivered, read, and understood states. Delay or shortness alone proves no motive or editing. A message that is read is not automatically understood; a message that is sent is not automatically received. Use timestamps, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes only when they matter to the account. Keep full private experience beyond drafts and transmission. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought, and transmitted text is not a substitute for required nonspoken interiority.\n\nPreserve each person's actual digital habits and knowledge channels. The transcript may show what one sender claims while another does not know it. The response may end before an answer or sent message, during a delay, or after a completed exchange.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaeba::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Douglas Adams / Visual Novel\n\nOrient the scene through clear dialogue and flexible focal prose. Use a real object, background, or expression cue to place an account, expectation, or act in its practical setting. A character may describe an offense in broad terms while the selected cue shows the exact room, object, or physical condition that the account actually depends on. A precise familiar fit or a real contrast can matter without a joke, contradiction, or new discovery.\n\nPreserve the actual narrator, full private extent, and knowledge channels. Focal narration or inner response may render a character's private reasoning, fear, or suspicion, but another character does not know it merely because the reader does. Cues alone prove no motive or guilt. A changed expression can attract attention, but it does not certify a lie, a plan, or a feeling.\n\nLet different characters respond to the same broad claim according to their own situation and temperament. No fixed cue cycle, menu, delivered response, or closed scene is required. The scene may end during ongoing speech, thought, or an unfinished setup.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaebb::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Douglas Adams / Web Serial\n\nRecover only the earlier claim, act, resource, or relationship fact needed for the present account. When a later installment returns to a broad explanation of the offense, scheme, or aftermath, bring back the exact practical term that was previously established: the specific door, the named debt, the particular errand, the known relationship. Let the familiar term support a new relation or confirm an old one without retelling the whole history.\n\nCarry real knowledge, consequences, and unresolved matters forward. A character does not forget a supported conclusion merely because a new reply begins. New information can qualify an old explanation without replacing the old event or resetting competence. Do not manufacture forgotten facts, require a clue, punishment, new threat, or joke per reply. The installment can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known.\n\nLet different characters respond to the same broad claim according to their own situation and temperament. Full private passages need no plot utility. The active boundary can fall during ongoing experience or an actual conclusion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadafaa::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Ernest Hemingway / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nBuild the scene on one plain request made inside the criminal situation: someone asks for silence, for a share of what was taken, for a few nights on a couch, or to be forgiven for the part they played. State the request in direct words, then let the difficulty live in how it is made — asked twice, asked while looking at the table, asked after the other person has already answered.\n\nKeep the objects exact: the key, the envelope, the phone that has not rung. Let the exchange stay practical and economical. Feeling shows in what a person asks for, what they pick up and put down again, how long they wait before speaking — not in polished sentences about their own pain.\n\nGive the withholding a real reason that is not automatically guilt. A character may stay quiet from fear, loyalty, exhaustion, or the belief that naming the thing will change nothing. Where interiority is authorized, let it carry what speech cannot — the want never voiced, the apology rehearsed and dropped — and keep it outside the other characters' knowledge.\n\nUse repetition as cost. When the same request or the same refusal returns, let its emphasis shift: first a test, then a demand, then something smaller. The strain may bend the conversation without breaking it open.\n\nDo not require the exchange to repair the bond, expose the offense, or produce a visible change in behavior. The scene can end with the request unanswered, the apology undelivered, and the silence still in the room.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadafab::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Ernest Hemingway / Chat Roleplay\n\nOrient the shared interval first: where the characters are, who else is present, and what is already in someone's hands — the bag, the keys, the list of names. Keep paragraphs readable and scannable, breaking when the speaker changes or the action moves to a new place.\n\nGive every line of dialogue an unmistakable speaker. Keep practical exchanges direct: 'Give me the keys.' 'Not yet.' 'He said Friday.' Show a character hearing a request and deciding what to do with it, even when the decision is only to keep watching the door.\n\nFollow each request, attempt, or response at its real extent. If someone crosses the yard, lifts the panel, or counts the money in front of the other person, show the movement and its immediate result — including no result yet. Do not make completing the offense, succeeding at the plan, or finding a clue a condition for ending the reply.\n\nKeep the surrounding world live on its own errands: the driver outside still waiting, the bartender still working, someone upstairs moving a chair. These figures act for their own reasons and are not scenery.\n\nRender authorized private thought even when it does not return to action — noticing the lie and saying nothing, wanting to leave and staying seated, feeling dread without showing it. Keep that thought outside the other characters' knowledge unless the active contract grants access.\n\nEnd inside the scene, with a hand still extended, a question still open, or a character still deciding, so the next contribution can resume from the exact position. No cliffhanger or forced choice is needed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadafac::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Ernest Hemingway / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nTell the offense through intimate domestic narration: the crime matters here as it enters a household's routines, not as an official account of it. Choose one actual room and follow its use — a kitchen where money is counted after the shop closes, a stairwell where two neighbors meet at the wrong hour, a table where a debt is named for the first time. Show who belongs in the room, who is tolerated, and who is waiting to be asked to leave. Keep the language, period, and community of the setting as supplied; the route is a method of attention, not a change of address.\n\nLet requests and refusals carry the practical plot. Someone asks for a loan, a place to sleep, an introduction, or a promise that the household will not be drawn in. Keep the dialogue direct and economical; a refusal can be as plain as the request — 'Not here.' 'Ask someone else.' Do not make people narrate their shared history at each other. What is done with a cup, a door, or a bowl offered and declined carries the strain.\n\nHold care and refusal apart from complicity. A woman can feed her brother and refuse to hold what is in his bag. A neighbor can let someone wait inside and still make the call. Care is not proof of innocence, and refusal is not proof of guilt. Ordinary life continues in the room — eating, sleeping, quarreling — while the offense changes only what people are willing to say in it.\n\nLet one familiar object change meaning under the new knowledge: the drawer, the chair where someone used to sit, the window now kept latched. Render whatever private experience the active contract authorizes — what one person thinks while pouring the tea, what is decided and not spoken — outside the others' knowledge. Do not force a threat onto the household; the scene can end with a request unanswered, a guest still present, or a family member still deciding what to tell the rest.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadafad::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Ernest Hemingway / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite with direct nouns and verbs around real handling, distance, expense, and bodily condition: the weight of the bag on one shoulder, the count made twice, the ache in a hand after a night of gripping something, the taxi paid in coins because the notes were marked. Use only material conditions that bear on what the characters can do or what they feel while doing it; do not decorate with damage for its own sake.\n\nCompress transitions but never delete the causal steps. A man can cross the room, take the key from the hook, and be gone in one sentence — provided the reader sees how the key came within reach and why no one stopped him. Move a plan from intention to attempt to result in a few lines, but show the attempt itself. Where the rhythm speeds up, the chain stays legible: who moved, what was touched, what happened next.\n\nKeep speech in each speaker's own register. Compression belongs to the narration and the pressure of the scene, not to a uniform clipped voice. One man answers in two words; another explains at length because the moment needs it; a third says nothing and lets the silence do the work.\n\nLet full private reasoning precede, accompany, or follow the contact. A character can measure the risk before opening the door, feel the fear while passing the money, understand the price only after the other person has gone. The thought does not need to surface in face or voice or produce any result.\n\nUse rhythm deliberately. A long careful sentence before a short violent one; short clauses after a shot or a slammed door. Let the contrast serve the event rather than build a pattern of forced jolts. End while the consequence is still settling or the next move is still undecided.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadafae::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Ernest Hemingway / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the sequence through real artifacts, each with an author, a recipient or purpose, and a moment of composition: a note left under a sugar bowl, an incident report filed to protect the writer, a letter out of a holding cell, a receipt kept because it proved something once. Use the form the scene actually produces — do not rotate through formats for variety — and write each entry as what that author would put down for that reader at that hour, never as a neutral summary of events.\n\nMake the wording do the work. 'I took the money' and 'the money was taken' are different admissions; 'meet me' and 'meet me at the usual place' presume different histories. Keep the language direct and practical. A report can list the facts observed and leave out the suspicion; a letter can ask for money without saying what it is for. When a document avoids something, the avoidance should have a real source: caution, shame, protecting the recipient, or protecting the writer.\n\nSeparate claim from established fact inside the record. A letter can state that a man was seen at the docks; the entry itself does not make it true. Two records repeating one account do not corroborate each other. Let a later document correct an earlier one, repeat its error, or answer a question the first never asked. A missing reply can carry as much as a message sent, and what a document proves is limited by what its author could know and chose to record.\n\nTreat an unsent draft as a composed artifact, not the writer's live mind. Required private interiority is rendered separately under the active presentation and is not replaced by any letter, diary, or report. Keep continuity readable — the reader moves entry to entry on what each author knew, chose to say, and left out — without an omniscient explanation closing every gap.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadafaf::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Ernest Hemingway / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend the scene's attention on one act of practical care inside the milieu or the aftermath, at the scale the situation actually allows: the coffee left on the desk during a long night, the jacket handed over before the cold walk to the car, the plate pushed across the table, the clean blanket shown to someone who cannot go home yet. Write the act itself and its exact object, not a general mood of comfort.\n\nMake the care particular through noticed preference. One person takes sugar; the other adds it without being asked. Someone always sits with their back to the door; the seat is left open. Let the dialogue stay plain — 'Eat first.' 'You look like hell. Sit down.' — without a speech about feelings. The economy is the tenderness.\n\nLet the recipient meet it in any honest way. A character takes the coffee and says nothing. A character refuses the help and stays in the room anyway. A character accepts the meal and remains afraid or angry. Where interiority is authorized, let some of the comfort land in private thought — the specific relief of a warm cup after a bad night — without requiring it to show on the face. Do not require gratitude, relief, or a changed position.\n\nDo not make the help carry sacrifice, absolution, or proof of innocence. A person can be kind and still be guilty. A person can offer comfort and still refuse to lie for anyone. Comfort accepted does not stop anyone from making the call they are going to make. The warmth is sincere within its actual limits; it settles neither the moral question nor the relationship.\n\nKeep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact — comfort does not make the cast agreeable or delete the offense from the world. End with the cup still warm, the offer still open, or the recipient still deciding whether to stay.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadafag::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Ernest Hemingway / Formal Literary Novel\n\nSet a plain criminal act or request inside a frame that makes its meaning social: a man asking for money across a counter, a woman refusing to sign, a courier waiting with a package while the street does its ordinary business. Open on the concrete exchange, then let the narration widen within the narrator's access — to the family's history with this kind of debt, to what the law has done on this street before, to the position that makes refusal expensive. The context must bear on this exchange now, not stand as an essay about crime in general.\n\nMove in sustained paragraphs whose transitions are legible: from the exact amount written on the slip to how such amounts have moved through this neighborhood for two generations; from a refusal to the reputation it will cost by Friday. Place the framing where it changes what the reader understands of the act, and do not break every line of dialogue with commentary.\n\nKeep speech economical and individual. The formality lives in the narration, not in the mouths of the cast: 'I can't do it.' 'Give me until Friday.' The narrator may know the street's history; the characters speak from habit and need, not eloquence.\n\nAnchor the wider frame in exact particulars — the worn edge of the counter, the make of the car idling outside, the name of the street, the figure on the paper — so that judgment and history stay attached to things. Let the narrator judge, foresee, or note what the participants do not yet know, and practice omission at the same scale: name what is absent, and let the reader weigh what the paragraph declines to explain. End with the request unanswered, the consequence still unfolding, or the meaning still unjudged.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadafah::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Ernest Hemingway / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen inside the telling situation. Name the teller, the listener or listeners, and the practical reason this account is being given now — a request, a debt, a warning, a bargain, or the need to make someone understand what happened. Let the reason show in what the teller chooses to include and what a listener asks about, not in an announced thesis.\n\nRender the embedded account of the offense, scheme, or consequence in precise acts and practical exchanges: the objects handled, the route taken, the money counted, the words spoken at the time. Keep the teller's sentences direct and economical. Give each omission a source reason — the teller did not see it, did not think it mattered, or will not say it — and let those reasons differ from one another; an omission can be ignorance, discretion, or protection, and not every gap is a hidden confession.\n\nKeep the outer scene present while the account runs: the room, the hour, the physical state of the speakers, interruptions, silences. The audience receives the telling according to its own interests. One listener may agree quietly, one may ask a practical question about timing or access, one may object to a particular act, one may go silent because the account changes something already believed. The telling can secure cooperation, warn, delay, or persuade; disbelief and confession are only two possible results, and neither is required.\n\nRender what is not spoken as actual private experience under the active narrator access: what the teller feels while saying a particular sentence, what a listener decides not to ask. The spoken account is not the whole mind, and it cannot replace required interiority.\n\nKeep the levels intelligible. The embedded account is the teller's version, not an omniscient reenactment: when the teller reports another person's words or acts, preserve the difference between direct experience, report, and later knowledge. Return to the outer scene through a concrete point of contact — a phrase repeated from the account, an object now on the table, a question a listener asks for the first time — and let the recurrence accrue meaning and connect the levels rather than certify that the story is true.\n\nEnd when the telling has done its actual work in the scene; the boundary can fall mid-account, mid-question, or in the silence after. The larger offense may remain unfinished and no verdict is required. Treat the Arabian Nights reference as a craft source for situated, purposeful telling and delayed meaning, not as scenery: no court, djinn, or bargain enters a setting that does not already own it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadafai::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Ernest Hemingway / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse social and developmental realism as the settled route. Place the crime or its consequence in a particular social situation — work, housing, family obligation, local standing, or the practical demands of an institution — and make it concrete from the first lines: who is present, what is owed, what the place permits and forbids.\n\nStructure the passage around two actual occasions connected by one exact act, phrase, or object tied to the offense: a tool, a sum, a repeated instruction, a route, a sentence someone said once and says again. Write the earlier occasion as it was lived then — what the person knew, wanted, feared, and could do at that time — and the later occasion as its own present moment. Do not let the later self's knowledge leak backward; the earlier scene contains only what was available then.\n\nUse precise objects, direct clauses, and practical exchanges in both occasions, and let the repetition carry the connection. If the person repeats the same act or phrase, the repetition may confirm a habit or expose a refusal to move on. If the later act differs, let the difference show in concrete behavior — what is paid, refused, asked, or no longer asked — rather than in an announcement of growth. Render the wanting and knowing of each occasion as that person's actual experience at its own time, not as retrospective explanation.\n\nPreserve the social conditions around each occasion. What was permitted, expected, or possible may have changed between them; show the change through particular people making particular decisions — an employer, a relative, an official, a creditor — rather than through a general atmosphere of severity, and keep the syntax natural and direct rather than imitating translated word order.\n\nEnd when the connection between the two occasions has become legible through the repeated particular. The later occasion may leave the person in the same difficulty, in a new one, or in a changed relation to the offense. No lesson, remorse, or moral summary is required; let the exact detail carry what remains unsaid.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadafaj::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Ernest Hemingway / Gothic-Punk\n\nGround the inherited authority in concrete space and practical speech before it does any work. Show the room, building, object, route, or routine that gives an old arrangement its actual reach: who holds the key, whose name still opens a door, which favor is still owed, which debt still binds. Atmosphere does not replace mechanism — a heavy door, a locked drawer, or a remembered name matters because of what it permits or prevents, not because it feels ominous.\n\nCenter the scene on a request or exchange belonging to the offense, scheme, or its aftermath, and follow it through what it actually meets: permission, willingness, available means, and the practical limit of each. Write the precise words and acts. When someone agrees, show what the agreement costs or requires; when someone refuses, show what the refusal leaves in place. Legitimate authority and the world's actual powers remain intact — do not require every old arrangement to decay or every social mask to fall.\n\nLet private desire and public presentation follow real circumstances. A character may want something from the old arrangement, fear it, depend on it, or know how to work inside it; render that want as actual private experience under the active narrator access, and let what is said be shaped by who else is present. Trusted company may allow plainer speech, but intimacy here is morally complicated rather than redemptive: it can expose a cost as easily as relieve one, and it need not end in confession.\n\nKeep the scene present with urban material detail: the condition of a stairwell, the sound of a gate, the weight of a key, the timing of a shift change. These particulars belong to the practical world of the offense and to the collision between old authority and present life; they are not a mood inventory. The horror is personal and social — carried by dependence, debt, inheritance, and what people ask of each other — so do not import vampire physiology, faction politics, or supernatural powers to deepen it.\n\nEnd when the exchange has reached its actual limit for this scene. The request may be granted, refused, postponed, or answered only in part; the arrangement may hold, crack, or continue unchanged. Do not force exposure, punishment, or revelation. Let the concrete outcome stand.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadafak::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Ernest Hemingway / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse testimonial and autobiographical accounts as the settled route. Give the account an actual source, audience, and purpose: someone is recording, reporting, or explaining what they experienced or witnessed, for a listener, an official, a page, or themselves. The account fits the active narrator's person and ownership rather than defaulting to a first-person voice, and the narrator's identity stays present in what they know, what they choose to say, and what they cannot claim from direct experience.\n\nRender the offense or its consequence at the precision the narrator actually supports. Distinguish direct experience from inference and later learning at their real times: when the narrator saw an act, write the act; when the narrator heard about it, write the report and name or imply its source; when the narrator concluded something, show the observation that produced the conclusion. Later knowledge does not become earlier knowledge.\n\nUse precise objects and direct language — what was handled, said, paid, taken, or left behind — and let the narrator's selection reveal what mattered to them. An accurate, candid account remains possible, and a known offender can be named plainly without the passage turning into a new puzzle. Keep the language, region, community, and period actually supplied; do not force code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure onto the telling.\n\nKeep live private feeling separate from the articulated testimony. The testimony is what the narrator composes for its purpose; the narrator's present fear, regret, relief, or indifference is actual private experience available in the narration around it under the active narrator access, and a performed confession cannot replace it.\n\nEnd where the testimony has served its actual purpose in the scene — complete, interrupted, or held at a question the narrator cannot answer. The offense may remain unresolved, and the boundary between what the narrator knows and what lies outside that knowledge stays visible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadafal::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Ernest Hemingway / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse diary and reflective miscellany as the settled route. Give the recorder an actual composition occasion: a particular day, hour, place, and reason for writing — habit, obligation, the need to fix a memory, or the pressure of something that cannot be said to anyone else. Let the occasion shape what the entry includes.\n\nArrange the entry around one precise object, act, or remembered phrase tied to the offense or its consequence: something handled or left behind, something done, witnessed, or avoided, something said during the event or repeated afterward. Let that particular carry the entry's movement instead of a general account of the crime. The recorder's language and distance follow their own habits — plain statement, circling attention, dry record — rather than a universal quietness or a mandatory inventory of scenery.\n\nWrite what the recorder actually chooses to record for this purpose. Direct feeling belongs where this recorder would write it: the entry may state fear, anger, relief, or confusion plainly, circle the feeling through concrete detail, or omit what is too dangerous or simply outside the entry's purpose. Do not require the record to be emotionally complete or confessional; selection and omission are the method.\n\nKeep the record distinct from the live mind. The entry is a composed artifact, not the recorder's complete private experience: whatever interiority the active contract requires remains available in the narration around the entry, at the recorder's actual present, and the entry's wording does not stand in for unrecorded thought.\n\nEnd where the recorder would stop writing. The last line may return to the precise object or phrase, note the time, or break off mid-composition; the boundary can fall while the writing is still underway. The offense itself may remain unfinished. Let the selection carry the meaning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadafam::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Ernest Hemingway / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse social chronicle as the settled route. Follow the public life of the offense or its consequence through locatable occasions: where it happened, where it was discussed, where money or goods moved, where someone returned or stayed away. Give each occasion a time, a place, and actual speakers.\n\nTrack one relevant act, account, place, or resource across these occasions. The act may be repeated, imitated, or refused; the account may change as it circulates; the place may reopen, close, or be avoided; the resource may pass through particular hands. Use direct clauses and repeated particulars to show what persists and what changes, and let the order follow the tracked particular rather than strict chronology when circulation itself is the story.\n\nKeep source reliability legible. When a speaker witnessed an event, write what they saw; when a speaker repeats what they heard, mark it as report and, where the narrator can, carry its path. Shared rumor is one circulating source, not independent confirmation. A known culprit can remain known — do not convert the chronicle into a fresh puzzle or force a collective verdict. Preserve the language, community, and history actually supplied, and add no supernatural fact to a world that excludes them.\n\nUse precise objects and practical exchanges in each occasion: what was said, paid, promised, refused, or left behind. Make the social situation concrete through who has access, who is excluded, who benefits, and who must answer, rather than through a list of general conditions.\n\nEnd when the tracked particular has reached a legible point in its public life. The offense may remain unresolved, the rumor still circulating, the resource still missing. No solution, moral, or closing judgment is required. Let the occasions stand as the record.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadafan::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Ernest Hemingway / Light Novel\n\nEnter the actual concern cleanly. Open with the immediate situation — the meeting, the handoff, the discovery, the debt, the injury, or the question someone has come to ask — and establish who is present, where they are, and what practical matter is underway. Do not open with a general description of the city, the era, or the criminal milieu.\n\nKeep spatial changes easy to follow. When someone moves, takes an object, or the arrangement of the scene changes, give the movement its own readable line or short paragraph so the reader always knows where people and things stand. Separate speakers plainly, and let a physical action that changes the situation occupy its own space on the page.\n\nGive unfamiliar names, tools, procedures, or local arrangements clear presentation at the point where the reader would otherwise lose the action: a short, plain explanation tied to what a character is doing or handling, never a lecture. Keep the prose direct and economical — concrete objects, practical exchanges — while the page stays fast to read.\n\nRender private response as the character's actual experience under the active narrator access: what they notice, weigh, fear, or decide not to show. Give inward response its own paragraphing rather than a labeled beat after every line of dialogue. It may be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to its owner, and precise wording can leave implications unspoken so the reader infers what a character will not say.\n\nEnd at a natural continuation point. The exchange may be complete, interrupted, or left with a task underway, and the crime may remain unresolved. Do not require a closing reveal, cliffhanger, or reversal, and do not impose a young cast, a comedy register, or a first-person voice the scene does not already own.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadafao::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Ernest Hemingway / Lyrical Prose\n\nAnchor the crime sequence in exact objects and practical exchanges. Let one sound, object, or authorized association recur because the viewpoint actually notices it: the way a lock turns, the weight of a wet coat, the particular word someone repeats while discussing an arrangement. Do not assign the image a fixed symbolic meaning. Its significance may stay the same, shift as the situation changes, or remain purely sensory while the offense and its consequences proceed through direct speech and concrete action.\n\nDevelop the image through syntax and plain statements rather than ornamental description. A short declarative sentence can place the object in the scene; a longer sentence can follow the attention it holds or the association it opens. Keep the literal facts of the crime legible. The image does not prove guilt, expose a lie, or make every character speak figuratively. It belongs to the narrator's or focal character's actual perception and may remain private.\n\nLet dialogue interrupt or continue the lyrical attention without making the cast poetic. A character may answer an image-laden observation with a practical question, or repeat the same phrase in a different tone. The unsaid meaning stays available through what the person does next, not through an announced interpretation. Full feeling may exist without investigative value. Stop writing at the natural continuation point of the scene, during continuing speech, action, thought, or an unfinished arrangement.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadafap::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Ernest Hemingway / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nUse an exact earlier phrase, object, or habit from the crime's preparation, execution, or aftermath and place it beside the present through authorized recollection. The trigger may be a current particular: the same street, a familiar tool, a repeated question, or the way someone now avoids a word that was once ordinary. Let the memory arrive with its concrete details and direct speech intact. Do not require the character to feel remorse, to lose all pleasure in the remembered occasion, or to reinterpret the past with superior hindsight.\n\nPreserve past motives and later learning separately. What the person knew then remains what they knew then; what they know now may add weight or change the meaning of a detail without rewriting the earlier decision. Let attachment, pleasure, regret, or indifference take their actual extent. The reflective passage may sit beside what remains unsaid in the present exchange, with no completed lesson or return to action required.\n\nKeep the prose concrete and economical. A direct sentence can state what is gone; a longer sentence can hold the present particular and the remembered one together. Let dialogue interrupt reflection or continue alongside it. Stop writing at the natural continuation point of the scene, during a continuing private or public interval, leaving the reflection unfinished rather than resolved into a moral or a plan.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadafaq::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Ernest Hemingway / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the crime sequence through reflective philosophical prose. Attach an actual question of means, cost, willingness, or responsibility to a precise object, offer, or condition in the scene: a sum of money, a door left open, a debt, a promise made under pressure, a tool handed across a table. Let the question arise from the character's concrete situation rather than from a general meditation on wrongdoing.\n\nReturn to the question's actual words as the person considers them. The character may repeat a phrase from an offer, weigh a condition, or test what they are willing to do against what the arrangement requires. Keep the reasoning grounded in exact objects and practical exchanges. The person may reach a judgment, change it, or leave it unresolved. Unchanged judgments and noninstrumental feelings remain possible; reflection does not have to produce confession, sacrifice, or a moral answer.\n\nPreserve factual possibility separately from personal acceptance. What the character believes may be true, false, or uncertain, and the prose should not let reflection blur the difference. Keep direct speech individual and economical; the reflective register belongs to the narrator or focal mind, not to every speaker. Stop writing at the natural continuation point of the scene, during continuing thought, speech, action, or an unfinished arrangement.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadafar::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Ernest Hemingway / Minimalist Prose\n\nUse a small number of exact details and direct clauses to carry the crime sequence. Select the object, act, or line of speech that matters: the amount written on a slip, the sound of a door, the way a person sets down a glass after hearing a proposal. Keep the causal chain legible without restating it. If a character refuses to answer, let the refusal stand in the scene; do not explain it twice or make silence an automatic sign of guilt.\n\nLet an unanswered line or repeated object retain the meaning the scene supports. The same object may appear before and after an offense, and the second occurrence can carry what the first established without a summary. Omit repeated explanation, not the authorized inner life. Full thought, memory, and feeling keep their actual extent independently of usefulness or visible action. A character may think at length about a debt, a risk, or a person while saying very little.\n\nKeep speech individual and practical. A short exchange can establish the terms of a scheme or the pressure of an aftermath. Let the narrator's direct clauses do the work of transition. Stop writing at the natural continuation point of the scene, during continuing speech, action, thought, or an unfinished arrangement. The minimal style selects; it does not require every sentence to be short or every feeling to be withheld.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadafas::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Ernest Hemingway / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the crime sequence as sustained scene-first prose. Move naturally among direct speech, action, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience without stopping to announce each shift. Anchor the criminal situation in exact objects and practical exchanges: the money, the weapon, the key, the phone, the favor asked or refused. Let the viewpoint notice what matters in the moment, remember what bears on it, and act or speak from the reasons the scene supports.\n\nPreserve the reasons a person speaks or does not. A character may withhold a fact because of fear, loyalty, calculation, habit, or uncertainty; the narration can make that reason available without making every silence a confession. Known culpability and genuine help remain available. The prose should not force a reveal, a reversal, or a completed investigation at the end of the reply.\n\nKeep the register contemporary and continuous. Paragraphs may move from a direct exchange to a private thought and back to a practical act without recap machinery. Let the narrator's voice, the author profile, and the immediate scene set the rhythm. Stop writing at the natural continuation point of the scene, during continuing speech, action, thought, or an unfinished arrangement.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadafat::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Ernest Hemingway / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nSelect a particular exchange from the actual supplied relationship premise and the participants' familiarity. It may be a first-time offer, a repeated phrase, an ordinary act that carries private weight, or a direct admission made under the pressure of the crime's preparation or aftermath. Let the scene make the bond legible through what these particular people do and say, not through a roster of familiar tropes.\n\nLet shared phrases, ordinary acts, and direct admissions carry only the meanings their history supports. A word one person uses may mean something specific to the other because of an earlier occasion; the narration can show that weight without explaining it as a loyalty test or hidden motive. Preserve full affection or strain, actual consent, and the consequences that follow from the premise. Do not require betrayal, repair, confession, or a new relationship state by the end of the reply.\n\nKeep the prose concrete and economical. Direct speech and exact objects carry the exchange; private language and repeated moments give the relationship its particular texture. The crime remains present as an offense, scheme, or consequence, not as a backdrop for emotional payoff alone. Stop writing at the natural continuation point of the scene, during continuing speech, action, thought, or an unfinished arrangement.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadafau::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Ernest Hemingway / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nOpen with a compact attributed declaration that places the offense in public memory: who is said to have done what, or what the neighborhood, crew, or family still says about the act. Tie the declaration to a real deed or account, not an ornamental epithet. Then expand into the exact activity and individual speech that the declaration leaves incomplete. The retelling gives the sequence cadence, but the scene itself remains concrete: the practical exchange, the object passed, the words actually said.\n\nUse a recurring phrase tied to the deed or its account. It may return when a later occurrence gives it a new application, or it may simply repeat as the telling does. Public memory may agree with private experience or leave something out. Being remembered as the one who did the thing does not mean the doing was effortless, willing, or fully understood by those who retell it. Keep the focal character's private account distinct from the reputation.\n\nLet dialogue interrupt the authority of the chronicle. A person named in the retelling may object to a detail, answer an elevated introduction with a practical concern, or refuse to speak of it. Humor may arise from the persistence of a local story, but the offense is not mock combat and its consequences are not heroic doom. Stop writing at the natural continuation point of the scene, during continuing speech, action, thought, or an unfinished arrangement. Recurrence needs no new application, countervoice, or completed deed at the reply boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadafav::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Ernest Hemingway / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nLet the case move through the handling of a single report, item, or request. Show its route by concrete transfer: one person hands over the phone, the folder, the envelope; another initials it, sets it aside, or says where it must go next. Use the document itself to carry status. A signature ends one stage. A missing signature returns the matter to the person who brought it. Do not pause to explain the institution behind these acts. The route is the institution.\n\nWrite the exchanges as practical speech. Ask for the item. Ask who signed, who opened, who can receive it. Let the other person answer with the available fact, a correction, or a refusal that is already part of the job. When a request is deferred, show the deferral as a physical consequence: the folder stays on the desk, the door remains closed, the name is entered but no time is given. The offense stays present through the object and what is done with it. Arrest and punishment are not required; the handling itself is the story.\n\nGive the viewpoint room for private attention that yields no immediate act. Waiting in the corridor, the person may notice the cold, an old mark on the wall, the victim's name repeating without a decision following it. Put those movements in the scene as ordinary experience, not as evidence of responsibility. End where the handover, signature, or unanswered request stops. The institution may continue off the page. No verdict has to close the account.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadafaw::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Ernest Hemingway / Screenplay\n\nPut the offense or its handling into present-tense action blocks. Name the object and its movement: he sets the bag on the table, she opens the door from the wrong side, he turns the phone face down. Keep each block to the visible act. Use a scene heading only when place or time changes. Let the cut separate scenes without also deciding cause or motive.\n\nWrite dialogue as direct practical exchange. One person asks for the thing. The other gives it, withholds it, or answers another question that is already in the room. Keep the full spoken exchange even when the lines are short. A pause or a look goes into the action block as a visible pause or a look; it does not translate into guilt or knowledge. Let silence remain a plain silence on the page.\n\nKeep required nonspoken private experience in the active private presentation, not in voice-over or a parenthetical. What the character thinks about the offense, the victim, or the risk belongs to that character only. It is not audible to the cast, and it does not require a reaction shot or a completed event to exist. End on the finished movement, the pending request, or the interrupted reach. A scene may continue the same occasion or cut to a later one. The cut is a change of view, not a verdict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadafax::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Ernest Hemingway / Stage Play\n\nSet the scene through the shared space. Name who can see the door, reach the table, take the envelope, or leave without crossing another character. Move the object in the stage directions: the key from one pocket to another, the drawer opened and closed, the chair turned toward the window. Keep the directions to actor's actions and visible objects. Do not put an emotion in a stage direction.\n\nLet the lines run as practical exchange. A character asks for the thing. Another answers, delays, or changes the subject. Each line belongs to the speaker's purpose and relation. A refusal can be quiet. A question can answer a question. Keep the offense present through what is handled, avoided, or not said, not through a required confession or a final speech.\n\nUse offstage events through concrete reports. A character enters carrying news of a search, a call, or a discovery. The audience hears only the report; let the report name the thing found, the place searched, or the person called. An aside is public performed speech; use it for a dramatic reason. Keep required private experience in the active private presentation, not as an aside. End on the completed exchange, the refusal, the silent standing, or the entrance that changes the scene. The curtain can fall with outcome unsettled.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadafay::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Ernest Hemingway / Stream of Consciousness\n\nBegin inside the authorized mind and let its movement supply the order. Use a received line, a present sensation, or a remembered act as the entry. The word can return without explanation. The hand on the glass, the cold on the window, the coat on the chair, the question someone asked: let them appear not as symbols but as particulars the mind keeps. When an association arrives, show its relation through a shared detail: the same word, the same street, the same sound at a different hour.\n\nKeep the concrete world exact. The paint has a color. The door has a sound. The cup has a weight. Let repetition change emphasis. The same word said twice may deepen attention or show refusal. The same question from another person may reopen a wound without making anyone guilty. Repetition is not evidence by itself.\n\nMark the line between what occurred and what might occur. A memory is established. A fear is imagined. The syntax may move between them, but the direction must stay legible: \"He had seen the car. Now he saw the car turning. He had not seen it turn before.\" Keep private thought going to the permitted boundary, even without confession, outward action, or completed work. End while the mind is still turning. Length follows attention, not a clock.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadafaz::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Ernest Hemingway / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nLet each message be a completed act in the transcript. Attribute the sender. Show what was composed, what was sent, what was received, what was read, and what was answered. Keep the exact wording short or long as the person would write it. One sender uses full sentences. Another sends fragments. A third waits before answering. The pattern is personal, not proof.\n\nType the message you mean to be sent. Send it or stop before sending. An unsent draft is a written artifact; the other person does not receive it. Keep private thought separate from the thread. When the character thinks while typing or after reading a reply, place that thought in the active private presentation. It does not appear as a message unless the active contract separately authorizes display.\n\nUse a timestamp, delay, edit, read receipt, attachment, or channel change only when it makes a difference in the account. Do not invent a deletion or a failed delivery to force a turn. A message may go unanswered. The last visible entry may be the sent message, the read receipt, the unsent draft, or the reply that changes the next required act. The crime stays in the exact transmitted words and in what is never sent. No confession or finished plan is required to end the transcript.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadafba::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Ernest Hemingway / Visual Novel\n\nOpen the scene by placing the place and the object. Name the counter, the door, the bag, the phone, the hand that has not moved. Keep the setting line concrete. Attribute each spoken line to the person speaking. Let the focal narration or inner response follow at the pace of the encounter. The viewpoint notices the moved object, the changed voice, the pause before an answer. The noticing is attention, not a truth indicator.\n\nKeep the focal reading distinct from a known fact. When the character interprets a look or a hesitation, show the interpretation as the character's own. \"It looked like he was waiting.\" \"She thought the bag had been moved.\" The other character does not hear that thought. A repeated cue may hold its meaning or change as later facts arrive. Do not let an expression certify guilt or a background detail solve the crime.\n\nAdvance the scene through an exchange that changes what the characters can do or know. The next line may support the focal reading or show the speaker was attending to something else. A request may be granted, refused, or left open. Do not add branching menus, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands. End on the completed exchange, the new question, or the unfinished movement. No fixed reaction cycle or required relationship change closes the scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadafbb::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Ernest Hemingway / Web Serial\n\nCarry the offense forward through exact words, objects, and acts already established. When a new installment needs a prior fact, bring only that fact into the scene. The door that was locked is used now. The person who said a name says it again under a changed condition. The phone that was on the table is in the drawer. Let the recollection happen inside the action or the exchange. Do not stop to retell the investigation.\n\nPreserve the order in which things happened. A change in the present does not change the earlier fact. If the lock was repaired, the first visit still found it locked. If a witness now remembers another detail, the earlier answer remains true at its own time. Keep later knowledge from becoming an earlier motive. Show what the character knew when they knew it, in the scene where they knew it.\n\nUse repetition to continue meaning, not to restart suspicion. The same question asked again can matter because circumstances have changed. Let the answer reveal the change. Full private experience may stay alive at the response boundary. The installment can end on completed work, a pending request, or a plain recognition of what is now known. Serial time gives selected facts room to acquire weight. No new offense, clue, confession, or completed task is required at every break.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadagaa::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the scene around a specific need, refusal, apology, return, or privately considered disclosure that belongs to the offense, scheme, or its consequences. Make the character's own stake in that need concrete: if he admits, what protection or claim does he lose; if he withholds, what suspicion, separation, or self-accusation becomes more present. Do not state those costs abstractly; render them through the character's live reasoning as he weighs a particular alternative.\n\nWhen the inward argument develops, let it defend, accuse, reverse, or entangle itself in the character's actual terms. He may justify the offense, despise his reliance, insist that silence is the only loyalty, then recognize the fear beneath the insistence. Leave room for direct feeling that precedes or bypasses argument. A plain speaker may respond to this elaborate reasoning with a simple statement or refusal that changes the boundary of possible speech.\n\nLet the emotional cost operate in the physical and temporal progress of the scene. The strain may make a pause, a repeated gesture, an unfinished sentence, or a sudden move toward or away from another person important. Warmth or partial comfort can coexist with that strain, and the other participant may help, refuse, or understand accurately without becoming a moral judge.\n\nThe scene may end without a confession, punishment, repaired bond, or tragedy. Continue the live situation a line or two past the most heated exchange, but do not require a decision, reversal, or new disclosure. The offense and its consequences remain real, while the emotional cost of needing stays the center.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadagab::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Chat Roleplay\n\nTreat the submitted speech or action as an established event and resolve its consequences in the shared interval. Identify what the act changes: access, obligation, risk, suspicion, or a relationship's terms. Do not merely declare those consequences; show them in a concrete object, gesture, or next exchange that belongs to the criminal situation.\n\nWhere the active authorship permits, give the affected character a private moment of reasoning or direct feeling about the contribution. Let the character's own position defend, accuse, reverse, or entangle itself: he may justify the act, resent its necessity, accuse himself of cowardice, then recognize the accusation as pride. Keep this interior movement brief enough for chat scannability, but do not reduce it to a single mood label. Other participants retain their own aims, knowledge, and choices; they may agree, resist, misunderstand, or continue a separate practical task.\n\nAttribute dialogue unmistakably and keep paragraphs readable. The scene may end inside speech, thought, or pending action without a new dilemma, question, cliffhanger, or forced choice. Leave one concrete element in motion—unfinished wording, an object still held, a person still deciding whether to answer—so the next contribution can enter without waiting for the current exchange to complete. The offense and its consequences remain real.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadagac::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and satirical realism route of the Chinese literary traditions. Select one concrete arrangement connected to the offense—a debt, a share of profit, a household duty, a favor owed, or access to scarce goods—and stage an actual moment when someone speaks about that arrangement while someone else's material position remains visible.\n\nThe speech need not be overtly dishonest; it may be an explanation of fairness, necessity, or responsibility that the speaker partly believes. Let the distribution of work, benefit, and consequence be shown through specific physical detail: who waits, who counts, who carries, who goes without. The satire emerges by narrative juxtaposition: the gap between the spoken account and the material terms should be evident from the scene, not from a narrator's ironic gloss.\n\nWhere the active authorship permits, give the focal character a private Dostoevskian turn: let him defend the arrangement, then recognize his own advantage, then accuse himself of weakness, and perhaps decide that the accusation is another form of pride. This interior movement may be incomplete and need not lead to reform or confession. Other characters may have their own fragmentary accounts, and a plain speaker can unsettle an elaborate justification.\n\nPreserve the specific language, period, and community actually supplied for the story. The tradition guides craft; it does not transplant a setting, cast identity, or national temperament. Keep the sequence episodic if that shape fits the scene, with each incident adding to the shared material account without requiring a final collective verdict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadagad::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nSelect exact physical contact, effort, weight, or cost from the actual criminal situation. Use direct verbs and compressed transitions. A hand closes on a cold handle; a debt is counted in paper worn soft; a lock gives with a splintering sound; a payment is carried in the lining of a coat. Material wear and physical stakes remain concrete, but the body is not a punishment.\n\nInterleave hard physical beats with the focal character's private moral movement. One concrete detail can trigger self-justification, then a second detail can reverse it into self-accusation, then a third can show the character noticing his own pride in the accusation. Keep the thought brief enough for compressed prose but let it use full sensation and direct feeling. For example, after setting down a heavy object, the character may think that the weight is the truth of the matter, then recognize that the thought itself is false comfort. Do not make every thought instrumental or practical.\n\nPlace any spoken account in the same compressed register, grounded in its speaker's situation. A plain sentence can contradict an elaborate one. Allow longer reasoning when the character's situation actually needs it, but vary rhythm: short actions, a longer inward turn, then a hard stop or material consequence.\n\nNo injury, disgust, rough speech, involuntary confession, or moral reversal is required to give the process force. Competence and accurate self-understanding may sit alongside pressure. The offense and its consequences remain real, and the scene may end with the matter still in hand.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadagae::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCompose the story through authored documents, each with a clear writer, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Use the specific form the scene requires: letter, journal entry, report, note, ledger, or other record. Do not rotate forms mechanically; choose the form that the character would actually produce under the current pressure.\n\nFor each document, determine what the writer needs to make himself believe or what he needs the recipient to accept. Render that need in the document's rhetoric: a justification that repeats the same excuse in three different phrasings, an omission where the writer changes the subject after a certain date, an error in a name or sum that betrays whose interest the writer is protecting. A writer may state a conviction or record a practical detail without confession, but the document should reveal the character's self-presentation, not just transmit information.\n\nLet the Dostoevskian moral instability appear across documents. One record may accuse another person, then a later note by the same writer may revise the accusation into self-accusation without acknowledging the change. A third document may simply use the disputed information for a different purpose. Do not add a narrator who explains these contradictions; let the reader hold them together.\n\nPreserve readable continuity across entries. Gaps in dates, reappearing physical objects, and repeated phrases can anchor the sequence. Mark uncertain sources or damaged text when that uncertainty matters to the interpretation. Full authorized private experience remains separate from the composed artifact: what a document records is not automatically the writer's complete live mind. Allegation remains distinct from established act unless multiple documents or material traces support it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadagaf::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nChoose one actual kindness or small pleasure that fits the recipient's known preference or immediate need within the criminal situation. Make the care specific: the exact temperature or strength of a drink, the way a coat is folded, the quiet of a room after a report, a task quietly taken over before the person can object. Let the character performing the care be sincere, even if the act is imperfect or briefly awkward.\n\nRender the recipient's reception through concrete detail. He may notice the effort and feel relief, distrust, gratitude, embarrassment, or continued guilt. Where the active authorship permits, give him a brief Dostoevskian interior turn: he may think that he does not deserve the help, then accuse himself of pride in that thought, then accept the warmth without resolving the question. This interior movement should remain light enough for the fluff style: the reader feels the complication without being submerged in argument.\n\nThe offense and its consequences stay part of the world, but they need not dominate the scene. They may appear through a visible object, a postponed task, or the reason the recipient is exhausted. No confession, emotional repair, improved safety, or hidden trap is required. The caring act does not make everyone agreeable or remove established problems.\n\nEnd the scene with the particular comfort still present: the drink half finished, the coat still warm, the room quieter. The next contribution or continuation may move elsewhere, but the moment of care has happened and left its trace.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadagag::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Formal Literary Novel\n\nBuild the scene through deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Before or around the present moment, frame the act, statement, or privately held account about wrongdoing with the social and material conditions that make it legible: the household or workplace arrangement, the debt, the reputation, or the history of the relationship. Let the narrative distance permit judgment where the chosen narrator has that authority, but do not automatically adopt every character's self-description.\n\nWithin that frame, render the character's moral voice in a formal register. Use indirect discourse or narrator-mediated summary when the narrator is not inside the mind, and direct authorized interiority when the point of view permits. The character may defend the offense, accuse himself, reverse the accusation, or remain settled. Do not flatten the reasoning into a single moral slogan; let it unfold in relation to actual work, dependence, or consequence. A plain speaker may appear within the formal frame and unsettle an elaborate argument simply by naming a fact.\n\nUse ceremony, correspondence, or public/private contrast only when the scene's actual social context supplies them. They are structural opportunities, not required decoration. Distinguish narration from ordinary spoken language through syntax and paragraph movement, not through archaic vocabulary or aristocratic trappings.\n\nPreserve individual speech and full private experience. The frame may offer a contextual interpretation that the character's own thought partially contradicts. The argument may remain unfinished: no final verdict, confession, or social reconciliation is required. Formal architecture is the style; the offense and its consequences remain central and real.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadagah::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen with the telling as a present event. Establish the teller, the listeners, and the occasion that compels the account now — a request for help, a debt, a dispute over what happened, an obligation to fill the hours, or the simple pressure of company. Give the listeners a concrete stake in the tale: something they want from it, fear in it, or could use against its teller, and let that stake shape how the telling is pitched. The telling need not produce a confession, a changed verdict, or a rescue.\n\nBuild the embedded account as a performance, not an omniscient replay. The teller selects events, order, and explanation according to what the teller knows, fears, wants, or cannot yet say, and the tale carries the offense itself or its preparation and aftermath as conduct the listeners can picture rather than summary. Keep two clocks separate: what the teller knew at each point in the told past, and what the teller has learned since. Later knowledge may be admitted openly, hinted at, or withheld, but it cannot silently overwrite the earlier state of mind.\n\nRun the Dostoevskian pressure on both levels at once. Inside the tale, a participant may argue with themselves, reverse a decision, or hold two convictions in one breath, and the telling can voice that struggle as the teller remembers or shapes it. In the outer scene, listeners interrupt, object, fall silent, or answer an elaborate justification with one plain sentence that lands harder than a rebuttal; a listener may also be the teller's creditor, victim, or accomplice, so the exchange carries its own interest rather than sitting in judgment. Do not make every interruption a refutation — a listener may understand accurately, misunderstand, agree, or refuse the point and leave it standing.\n\nConnect the levels through recurrence. Let one phrase, object, question, or gesture appear first inside the tale and then in the present room, or first in the present and then in the told past. On its return, let it change what someone notices or can no longer say; repetition should make the relationship between the levels legible, not certify that the tale is true or seal a lesson.\n\nKeep the frame alive at the boundary. The response may end mid-tale, during a listener's answer, or while the teller is still deciding what to say next, and the audience need not reach any decision. A teller's words are chosen for listeners; where the active narrator permits, write the private thought running beside or against them — embarrassment, calculation, a second account the teller will never give — without turning it into a sealed confession or a guarantee of sincerity.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadagai::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Fyodor Dostoevsky / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse developmental realism as the governing route. Establish the present criminal matter first — the offense, scheme, obligation, or consequence that now occupies the focal person — and let the narration reach back to a concrete earlier occasion whenever the present moment calls one up: a teacher, parent, employer, friend, book, job, a sentence served, or a failure once witnessed. The earlier source must be an occasion the person actually lived or learned from, consistent with the supplied material, not an abstract cultural inheritance; and the route is a method of narration, not a décor of bureaucracy, severity, or translated word order.\n\nShow what the person retained, revised, or already understood. An early lesson may have left a rule now obeyed, resisted, or misapplied; a skill that now enables the offense; a distinction that now looks false; a habit of self-justification that runs unrecognized. Do not build a clean origin: someone can have understood a thing correctly and still acted badly, or misunderstood it and acted well, and the narration may let both stand.\n\nLet the Dostoevskian self-argument draw its material from that history. Weighing the present act, the mind may defend, accuse, reverse, or entangle itself in the older terms — a remembered phrase used sincerely, cynically, or turned against its first meaning; a present conviction tested against an earlier one; a dead teacher's voice answering a live excuse. Keep the argument attached to actual names, rooms, wages, and debts rather than to a general philosophical debate.\n\nMove between the two times at the scene's actual pace. The connection between early learning and present use may be explicit, half-recognized, or actively resisted, and the response may end while it is still being worked out — before apology, before a completed moral account, before the person has decided what the old lesson means now. Give both times specific places, relationships, and practical constraints, so a teacher's instruction or a workplace rule reads as part of a lived situation rather than a moral caption. Keep the narration's access distinct from the person's knowledge: it may show what was learned without granting retrospective wisdom or turning every earlier influence into the crime's hidden cause.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadagaj::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Gothic-Punk\n\nGive inherited authority a physical presence the reader can touch. Use the supplied place, permission, obligation, or maintained role to show how an old arrangement is actually lived — a key that still opens one door and no other, a title with duties attached, a schedule nobody questions, a debt paid on someone else's terms, a uniform, a building whose layout enforces an old hierarchy. Let the offense arise from or press against this arrangement, so the material details decide what a person may enter, touch, defer to, or owe.\n\nMake one participant's relation to that authority specific and morally complicated. The person may believe the authority legitimate, resent it, depend on it, protect it, or want something it forbids; they may obey an old rule while despising it, or break it while still believing in it. Do not settle the question by typecasting — not every old authority is corrupt, and not every rebel is sincere.\n\nLet the Dostoevskian pressure work through intimacy and direct feeling. In a trusted or private interval a character may speak with less guard, but the mask need not fall; private thought can run beside the performed role, accuse a loved one, defend the act, or reverse a decision mid-sentence. Preserve the difference between what a person says in company and what that person actually feels — and let a social mask sometimes express a real conviction rather than conceal one. Write the private reasoning at its developed length where the active narrator permits, including its self-accusations and reversals, without requiring awe, hypocrisy, or confession.\n\nGround the collision of old authority and present life in urban material detail. Let the present be worn, renovated, surveilled, or commercially repurposed, and let old and new systems share a single room: a historical privilege enforced by a modern lock, an inherited duty paid through a current account, a family claim argued over a phone. These details carry the scene's practical stakes rather than decorating a general decay.\n\nKeep atmosphere separate from proof. A frightening room, an old ritual, or a damaged object matters through its actual function and history, not through mood; it cannot by itself establish guilt, legitimacy, or danger. Authority may be legitimate or implicated — the story shows its operation and leaves the moral weighing to the actual evidence. Preserve the world's real powers and the narrator's real permissions, and supply no hidden physiology, faction, power, or bond rule beyond what the supplied world already establishes. The response may end inside a private interval, mid-explanation, or during a continuing encounter, without a confession, a loyalty crisis, or a completed unmasking.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadagak::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse philosophical inward narration as the governing route. Begin from something actual: an act the person committed, witnessed, refused, or may soon commit; a request that cannot be set aside; a remembered phrase that reads differently now; a harm already done. The inquiry starts from this material, not from a free-floating meditation on guilt or justice, and it stays attached to the person's own language, relationships, and circumstances as the supplied material establishes them.\n\nLet the authorized mind consider involvement and responsibility in its own terms. The thought may defend the act, accuse someone else, accuse itself, qualify the accusation, reverse a judgment, or hold its position with full certainty. It can move between practical calculation — what can still be done, who must never know — and the moral question underneath, without resolving either. Do not require self-reversal, confession, or the discovery that everyone is guilty; a person may conclude the act was justified, or that the question others would ask is the wrong one.\n\nKeep fact and interpretation visibly distinct. The narration should make plain what happened or is proposed, then show separately how the person takes it: a memory may be accurate while the reading of it is self-serving, or the reading fair while the memory is incomplete. Let the inward voice argue as hard as it needs to, but do not let it quietly revise what occurred.\n\nDevelop the Dostoevskian pressure through the thought's own movement. One sentence answers another; a remembered voice interrupts the present reasoning; a plain fact unsettles an elaborate justification; the person argues with an imagined listener, an absent real one, or an earlier version of themselves. Keep the exchange specific to names, obligations, money, and attachments rather than letting it become an essay.\n\nLet the inquiry remain unfinished if that is where it actually is. The response may stop while the person is still weighing, before any outward decision, before a confession, before the question settles — a real resting point in the thought, not a withheld answer. Write the full private experience at its actual place before, during, or after any speech or action; the inward consideration is the story's present form, not a pause between external events.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadagal::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse social and psychological realism as the governing route. Establish the encounter cleanly: who is present, where they are, what is being asked, reported, refused, or discussed, and how it connects to an offense or its aftermath. Keep the people, place, words, and conduct recoverable — the reader should be able to follow what was actually said and done, not only the focal person's impression of it.\n\nRender the focal person's interpretation and private feeling in the order they occur. The person may grasp a remark at once, misread it, register a gesture before its meaning, recall an earlier scene, or feel something before knowing why. Let the response be specific to this person; no overthinking, hidden shame, or dramatic reversal is required. A plain speaker in the scene may be correct, mistaken, or aligned with the focal person's own view, without becoming the scene's moral referee.\n\nLet the Dostoevskian pressure arise inside the social situation. While listening, the focal person may argue with themselves, answer a spoken justification with an unspoken one, or feel an accusation nobody has made; a single plain sentence from another person may unsettle an elaborate private argument. Keep the conflict attached to the actual relationship, the actual words, and the actual stakes rather than to a general debate about guilt.\n\nPreserve the gap between spoken and private content where one exists, for a reason that belongs to the character — embarrassment, attention, utility, relationship, uncertainty, concealment, or habit — and let speech and thought also align completely when they do. Not every silence hides a confession.\n\nEnd at the encounter's actual boundary. The response may stop mid-exchange, after a private thought, or before the focal person has decided what the encounter means; no changed reading, public admission, or completed moral account is owed. The narration may show everything the focal person knows and feels, but it cannot enter excluded minds or convert the encounter into a verdict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadagam::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse polyphonic nonlinear telling as the governing route. Build the story around an offense or its consequences through several accounts, each clearly marked by its source and its moment: a recollection given years later, a record made at the time, testimony under pressure, a letter, or a narrative position the active contract permits. Every account belongs to a specific person with specific knowledge; do not melt them into an omniscient summary.\n\nLet the same act carry different significance in different accounts — one person reads betrayal, another necessity, another accident, another deserved loss — while the verifiable facts stay stable: what happened, who was present, what was said and done. Interpretations may differ without any being exposed as false, and accurate agreement is also possible; do not force the voices into conflict. Preserve each speaker's knowledge at the time of the account separately from later learning: a person may report what they knew then, what they know now, or both, but the two cannot be silently merged. A later account may correct, deepen, or confirm an earlier one. Treat a letter or record as what its author chose to record, not as that person's whole live mind; where a narrator's access permits, private experience can appear in its own right alongside the composed sources.\n\nRun the Dostoevskian pressure inside individual accounts. A speaker may defend themselves, accuse another, reverse a judgment mid-telling, or argue with an imagined listener; a plain sentence in one account can unsettle an elaborate justification in another without resolving it. Accounts may answer each other across time, but the arrangement is not a debate that must end with the correct moral voice named.\n\nUse the nonlinear order for meaningful juxtaposition rather than confusion. The reader should always know whose account is speaking and when it sits in time; each shift should change what can be seen or compared. The response may end before all accounts are given, before the event is fully explained, or before any voice is authorized as final — no completed roster, refutation, shared guilt, or closing synthesis is owed. Keep the language, community, and history of the accounts as the supplied material establishes them, and add no marvelous or supernatural fact the world does not already permit.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadagan::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Within the first beats, establish who is present, where they are, and what current concern connects them to the offense or its consequences; the viewpoint may belong to an offender, victim, investigator, or bystander. Give just enough spatial and practical information that the reader can picture the room and the stakes without a preamble.\n\nMake any unfamiliar role, rule, or procedure legible through the scene itself: a character notices how the system behaves, explains a rule to someone with reason to hear it, or acts in a way that shows the procedure working. Let the explanation arise from the scene's actual need rather than a tutorial pause, and never force a question or a demonstration just to deliver information.\n\nBreak paragraphs around real shifts — a change of speaker, a movement through space, a turn from outward event to private attention — so the surface stays easy to follow while the content keeps its weight. When the scene changes location, make the transition concrete enough that the reader never loses the physical situation.\n\nLet Dostoevskian moral reasoning and direct feeling run at their actual length inside this container. A character may think a decision through, defend an act, accuse another person, reverse a judgment, or feel something they cannot yet name; the thought may be long, plain, or unfinished. Do not compress it to a single reaction line or a simplified motive — fast readability is a property of the prose, not of the person's inner life. Keep the information flow clear while the moral question stays open: the reader should know what is happening and what the focal person thinks and feels about it, without the story having to decide the question.\n\nEnd where the scene's boundary falls. The response may stop during an action, inside a conversation, in a private thought, or on an unfinished setup; no young cast, comic beat, new threat, or closing cliffhanger is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadagao::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Lyrical Prose\n\nWhen a character perceives an object, sound, or bodily sensation tied to the offense or its aftermath, let the prose register that perception directly. A plain clause may first name it exactly—a knock, a stain, a street noise, a pulse—then a longer sentence can follow what that perception opens: a defended position, an accusation against the self, or a reversal of what the character had just believed. Let syntax, repetition, and shifts of attention carry the image rather than announcing its significance. Keep the image traceable to the scene or the teller's associations, not imported as decoration.\n\nReturn to the image when the character's attention does, but do not require its meaning to change. It may become heavier because the character now knows more, or it may remain the same while the surrounding argument shifts. Allow plain statements to interrupt the rhythmic passage: a direct denial, a practical instruction, or a flat admission can create contrast without being rewritten into the narrator's figurative register. Other characters may speak in their own plainer voices even when the focal prose is shaped.\n\nPreserve the offense's facts and uncertainties. The image develops a person's lived relation to involvement, suspicion, or consequence; it does not prove what happened and does not force guilt, confession, or self-exposure. A character may notice the recurring thing and still refuse to conclude, or may use it to justify a course the narration does not endorse. Keep private experience within the authorized viewpoint, and let the passage end while the act, inquiry, or consequence remains unfinished.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadagap::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet a present detail that belongs to the offense or its aftermath open an earlier occasion the character actually knew. The detail may be a room now entered differently, an object still in use, a phrase repeated in a changed voice, or an ordinary routine made difficult. Trace the association as it occurs: the character notices the detail, then the past returns with its own texture and its own desires. Render the earlier moment in its own terms, including what the person wanted, believed, and felt then, without importing later judgment. Mark later interpretation separately when it arrives—through a shift of tense, an explicit recognition, or a plain reconsideration—so the two times remain distinct.\n\nAllow the reflection to contain more than one moral voice. The character may defend the earlier choice, accuse it, reverse the accusation, or become entangled in a self-argument that changes nothing outside. The remembered occasion may include real pleasure, care, or attachment that remains genuine even beside the crime's consequences. Do not invent an innocent past to sharpen the loss, and do not require the present to be wholly worsened. An ordinary pleasure can remain pleasurable while the character also carries what has changed.\n\nKeep the account within actual access and authorship. A new recollection may surface, but it cannot contradict established history or grant knowledge the character did not have. Let full private reflection take the space it needs even when it produces no visible act, and permit the passage to stop at an unfinished boundary: the person may still be remembering, waiting, or deciding without a completed emotional verdict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadagaq::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nCompose the account as a modern memoir: a retrospective writer selects and arranges an earlier involvement with a crime from the specific location of the composing moment. Make the composing occasion itself part of the prose—where the writer sits, why the account is being written, what it is meant to do—but let it remain a background pressure, not an excuse for summary. The memoirist's 'I' does not automatically replace the story's focal viewpoint, and it does not grant access to other minds.\n\nLet the writer's selection create the Dostoevskian moral pressure. Move among retained knowledge, later learning, and genuine gaps as the writer arranges the account: what was known at the time, what has since been learned, and what remains unknown must stay distinct. The prose may include defended positions, accusations, reversals, and entanglements, but these occur as the memoirist weighs and arranges the material, not as a live unedited outburst. The writer may be candid, evasive, uncertain, or self-justifying according to the person; a confession, conversion, or completed moral resolution is not required.\n\nKeep the composed selection separate from full private experience. The memoir records what its author chooses to record; material the writer omits remains omitted rather than being smuggled in as unspoken interiority. Let omissions and evasions be structurally visible: a passage may stop mid-account, break off before a difficult event, or remain unfinished because the writer cannot yet continue. The crime's facts and uncertainties stay distinct from the memoirist's later arrangement of them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadagar::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Minimalist Prose\n\nName the act exactly, and place a person's account beside it. Give the known facts in direct clauses—who did what, what was said, what was handled, what was left—using a small number of concrete details. Then give the character's meaning in equally plain terms: a defended version, an accusation, a reversal, or a self-entanglement. The gap between the act and the account can remain visible without commentary, and that gap carries the moral pressure rather than an elaborate explanation.\n\nRemove duplicated framing and repeated explanation, but do not remove reasons, feeling, or necessary inward connection. An essential thought may run long enough to make sense; a precise extended sentence can serve the style better than several performed fragments. A known culprit keeps authorized knowledge; do not sacrifice hidden thought or private motive to manufacture a puzzle. Other characters retain their own speech and registers, including accurate cooperation or practical concern.\n\nLet the response end during continuing private or public experience. The passage may stop while a person is still thinking, speaking, waiting, or acting. Do not require a confession, a changed plan, a discovery, or a completed moral result before the boundary. Omission should leave something inferable rather than delete causality, and the remaining details should be enough for the reader to see what is at stake.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadagas::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nOpen in the immediate situation of the offense or its consequences, and move naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inward experience. Use paragraphing and transitions to orient without recap machinery, letting the narrator's voice and the immediate scene set the register. Keep the offense concrete: name what was done, what is known, and what remains uncertain as the scene unfolds.\n\nLet the Dostoevskian pressure arise from situations where self-image and consequence collide. A character may defend an act, accuse it, reverse the accusation, or become entangled in self-argument, triggered by a social or practical context rather than an isolated philosophical set piece. Other participants retain their own practical concerns and registers, including accurate cooperation or indifference, and a plain statement or direct question can unsettle an elaborate argument. Preserve facts beneath interpretations: a character's account may be partial or self-serving without altering the established act.\n\nLet real timing operate independently of prose length. A long paragraph of thought does not stop the scene's clock; actual attention, waiting, and events still have their real duration. The response may end during continuing speech, action, thought, or an unfinished setup. Do not require a new guilt, confession, moral outcome, or reversal before the boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadagat::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nChoose an occasion from the actual supplied relationship premise—romantic, familial, friendly, rivalrous, or a new acquaintance—and let the crime or its consequences create an honest turning point. The scene should make that particular bond legible through concrete requests, favors, refusals, or secrets. Make each callback specific: a private phrase, a repeated gesture, or a known precedent from this relationship's history, grounded in what the premise actually supports rather than a generic trope.\n\nLet the Dostoevskian pressure live inside the relationship: participants may agree on facts but differ in meaning, and each may defend, accuse, reverse, or entangle themselves. The request or remembered account reveals what the bond promises; the response reveals what the bond permits. Keep full private experience and individual knowledge source-owned. Do not require betrayal, unequal knowledge, a rescue debt, public acknowledgment, or a changed relationship status.\n\nGive the emotional consequence enough space where it belongs, but let it remain contingent on the actual scene. Affection may survive a refusal; a shared secret may separate people who still care; a rivalry may produce unexpected cooperation without becoming friendship. The response may end while the relationship's question remains unsettled, with the factual or practical matter still in motion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadagau::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nOpen with a compact declaration already sourced in the story's retelling: who is said to have done what, and what the telling makes of it. The declaration may come from a community's repeated account, a family's version, a witness's phrase, or a participant's own later telling. Let that public reputation become the ground against which private memory and moral experience are placed.\n\nMove from the declaration into the actual particulars: what the person really did, what was known at the time, what was said or refused, and what the private moral experience contained. The focal character may defend the attributed meaning, accuse it, reverse it, or become entangled in self-argument. Dialogue may agree with the retold version, challenge one detail, or stay absent. Accurate praise or condemnation may stand where supported, and uncertainty may remain where the record is partial.\n\nReturn to a phrase when the actual telling or the character's attention does, not on a schedule. The phrase may gain new application or remain unchanged because the telling persists. Keep earlier knowledge, later account, and full private experience distinct: what a person knew then is not replaced by what the chronicle says now, and the private mind is not automatically the public reputation. End while the deed, inquiry, or consequence remains unfinished, without requiring a public confession or a newly earned reputation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadagav::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in essayistic narration using a Scandinavian social realist method. Let the narrator or a participant advance an actual claim about responsibility: a proposition about what a person owes, what an act means, what a bystander should have done, or what punishment can accomplish. The claim should be specific enough to test against particular facts, not a general observation about human weakness.\n\nBring the claim into contact with a situated criminal case. The case may be observed directly, remembered, or presented as a clearly labeled hypothetical, but its concrete conditions must remain distinct from imagined proof. Show the offense, scheme, or consequence through particular people, places, money, work, and relationships. Render those conditions with Scandinavian social realism: attention to municipal institutions, domestic arrangements, seasonal labor, and the practical records or authorities that shape what can be known. Let the case supply its own resistances: a fact that supports the claim, a fact that qualifies it, a person whose behavior does not fit the proposition, or an ordinary detail that makes the argument harder to apply.\n\nLet competing moral voices operate inside the essay. The narrator may defend the claim, accuse someone, reverse an earlier position, or entangle the proposition with self-image and shame. A plain statement from a minor participant can unsettle an elaborate argument. Do not require every person to deliver a philosophical speech; let ideology become a personal problem inside the actual material conditions of the case.\n\nReturn to the same claim when the movement of the case reaches it. The return may confirm the claim unchanged, narrow its application, expose an exception, or leave it provisional. The narrator need not complete a comparison, arrive at guilt, confess, or act before understanding. Preserve full private feeling beyond the argument, and keep the narrator's actual role and knowledge limits. The essayistic form organizes the telling; it does not replace the crime's human aftermath or the Dostoevskian pressure of unresolved conviction.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadagaw::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Screenplay\n\nUse clear speaker cues, concise action lines, and scene headings when location or time changes. Write action blocks that show what a viewer could see or hear: a person entering, handling an object, hesitating, leaving, or speaking under particular conditions. Keep the blocks readable and free of camera commands.\n\nPlace a spoken account beside visible or audible conduct. A character may describe what happened, explain a decision, accuse another person, or defend an action. The scene can then show conduct that supports, complicates, or simply accompanies the account. Do not let appearance automatically prove motive or guilt; let the actual sequence of events, objects, and known wrongdoing carry the inference. A cut or a same-scene sequence may place a claim next to what happened without forcing a confrontation.\n\nLet actual spoken delivery take time. Prose length or editing alone does not create duration; show the pause, the interrupted sentence, the repeated question, or the practical task that continues during speech. A person may state a conviction plainly, reverse a position, or leave a moral argument unfinished. No philosophical speech is required from every speaker.\n\nPreserve authorized private reasoning and feeling separately from performed voice-over. A voice-over is a performed artifact with its own audience and permissions; it does not replace the character's actual nonspoken experience. Keep private thought outside other characters' knowledge. The scene may end during continuing speech, action, thought, or an unfinished setup without a verdict, confession, or completed moral turn.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadagax::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable script with clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be staged. Make the shared space legible: who can see an entrance, hear a line, reach a prop, or move between two places. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene.\n\nPlace a person's actual account of involvement beside the ongoing situation. A character may describe an offense, explain a decision, accuse another person, or cooperate accurately with an inquiry. Let real speech, handling of an object, or stillness show how the account sits next to what is happening. Do not force a contradiction or a confession; an account may be supported by the physical conditions, qualified by them, or left in tension with them.\n\nKeep supported offstage causes and prior knowledge available. A character may know something the audience has not seen, or an event may have occurred offstage, as long as the play's actual world permits it. Let entrances and exits mark real changes in who can hear and act.\n\nPreserve full nonspoken experience as distinct from authorized asides or monologues. An aside or monologue is performed speech with its own dramatic purpose and audience; it does not replace the character's actual private thought. No moral argument must be spoken to count. The scene may end during thought, speech, or activity without a verdict, punishment, or completed moral meaning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadagay::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite through a particular authorized mind. Let perception, bodily feeling, private words, memory, and imagined possibilities move by association rather than by a linear report. Syntax and paragraph shape may follow those associations, while enough orientation remains to distinguish current event, recollection, and fantasy. Mark a memory as memory and an imagined possibility as imagined; do not let memory reenact an act by itself or turn scene facts indeterminate.\n\nLet the mind's relation to wrongdoing emerge through its actual attention. A moral phrase may recur with conviction, doubt, or no new meaning, beside an unrelated ordinary concern. The person may defend an action, accuse someone, reverse a position, or entangle a belief with shame and pride. A plain perception can interrupt an elaborate self-argument. Do not organize the movement as a compulsory confession or a finished moral argument.\n\nPreserve any separately authorized mind shifts and the full private extent of the active mind. Another character's thoughts remain inaccessible unless the active contract permits them. The associative movement may include a person's own past, but it does not grant access to excluded minds.\n\nNo utility, new realization, self-denunciation, or outward return is owed. The response may end during continuing thought, perception, or an unfinished setup. Prose length does not set fictional elapsed time; actual attention, waiting, and events still have their real duration.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadagaz::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite readable sender-attributed messages. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: some write long, some short, some edit, some send quickly, some leave drafts. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the exchange. Do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext.\n\nLet actual message selection and reception carry criminal involvement. A person may state a conviction, give a practical account, accept a plan, ask a question, or leave something unsaid. What a sender chooses to transmit and what a recipient chooses to answer are the public facts of the exchange. A moral argument may be developed or stated plainly, but no philosophical speech is required from every sender.\n\nDistinguish composition, sending, delivery, reading, and understanding. A sent message is not the same as an unsent draft, and private thought is not the same as transmitted text. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought. Context outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript.\n\nPreserve full private experience separately from transmitted text and drafts. A character may think, feel, or reason in ways that never reach the chat. The exchange can remain pending without confession, clarification, or an agreed moral meaning. The response may end during continuing composition, an unread message, or an unfinished exchange without a verdict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadagba::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Visual Novel\n\nWrite concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Orient the actual scene with a relevant object, position, or expression reference. Let background or expression cues be purposeful references, not executable engine code or a mandatory label on every line.\n\nLet permitted focal attention connect the cue to an act, account, or consequence in actual order. The viewpoint character may notice an object tied to the offense, read a person's expression, or recall a prior scene. Do not impose a fixed cue-thought-line cycle. A changed expression can attract suspicion, but a contradiction in an arrangement or account must do the evidentiary work.\n\nKeep observations, interpretations, and actual mind access distinct. A known conviction may be stated where narrator knowledge supports it, but another character does not know a conclusion merely because the reader has followed its formation. Preserve full private reasoning and direct feeling, supplied ownership, and world capacities. A moral voice may defend, accuse, reverse, or entangle itself without every character delivering a philosophical speech.\n\nDo not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected. The scene may end during continuing thought, speech, or an unfinished setup without a forced reaction, confession, or completed moral turn.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadagbb::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Web Serial\n\nWrite installment-oriented prose with a readable through-line across replies. Make clear what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover context briefly only when it helps: a relevant fact, a prior conversation, a pending request, or a person's earlier position. Do not retell the whole investigation that first established the context.\n\nPreserve actual conduct and remembered convictions across replies. What was done, alleged, learned, accepted, or left pending remains true unless the story's actual events change it. A participant need not forget a supported understanding for another reversal. The same conviction may persist, be qualified, or be abandoned according to actual experience. A moral voice may defend, accuse, reverse, or entangle itself without a compulsory new moral turn per installment.\n\nLet independent lives and full private feeling continue beyond an inquiry. A character may work, eat, argue, wait, or think about something unrelated to the offense. Required inward experience remains available when it contributes feeling or thought, even if no immediate decision changes.\n\nUse earned developments and occasional hooks without making every turn a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger. A result may occur when supported, but no arrest, punishment, confession, or finished miniature case is owed per installment. The response may end on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadahaa::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / García Márquez / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nLet the offense or its aftermath enter through a relationship that is already carrying weight. A known phrase, a reported event, or a name spoken in the neighborhood can gather earlier occasions as a character asks for something, withholds an answer, apologizes, or returns to a difficult concern. Show the cost in the actual conversation and in what the person does afterward: a question asked too late, a silence kept out of pride, a favor accepted with resentment, a familiar phrase repeated because it is safer than saying what has changed.\n\nDistinguish public reputation from private motive. What the street says about a crime may be true, false, or beside the point; the character's private experience remains its own fact. Let the narrator's access determine how much of that experience is available, and keep it outside other characters' knowledge unless the active contract permits them to know it. Sincere care and real injury can occupy the same exchange without one canceling the other.\n\nDo not force confession, accusation, betrayal, or moral conversion. A callback can confirm continuing hurt or partial understanding rather than supply a new meaning. The scene may end while the feeling is still unresolved, with the relationship altered only by what was actually said or left unsaid.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadahab::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / García Márquez / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the current interval in readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution, keeping the active people, their practical activity, and the criminal circumstance present from the first lines. When a remembered undertaking, name, or circulating account enters the scene, give it an actual source: a speaker who heard it, a person who was there, a document someone mentions, or a place that carries the association. Then follow what the participants do with it. One may repeat it as fact, another may doubt it, and a third may privately remember the same words meaning something different.\n\nUse a gathering sentence to connect earlier occasions without making rumor into shared memory. The sentence may hold several times together, but each character's knowledge, aim, and private experience remains distinct. Preserve player ownership, NPC activity, and the narrator access supplied by the active authorship contract. The interval may continue through speech, action, thought, or an unfinished setup without a clue, accusation, or forced choice before its natural boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadahac::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / García Márquez / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse vernacular episodic telling. Develop the present encounter through its material circumstances, participants, and interests: where they are, what they handle, who owes whom, and what each one wants from the meeting. Let the offense or its aftermath be visible in those particulars rather than announced as a theme.\n\nWhen a supported designation or account connects this occasion with another, keep each event and source distinct. A repeated phrase may travel from a marketplace dispute to a family quarrel to an official's question, but it arrives each time through a particular speaker or document, and it may preserve an unchanged consequence as readily as reveal another use. Do not impose a communal verdict, an invented chain of tellers, punishment, or a complete episode per reply. Private experience remains available beyond public reputation, and the scene may end within the encounter or its immediate aftermath.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadahad::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / García Márquez / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nUse exact physical verbs, spatial relations, and supported material particulars for the actual circumstances. Show the weight of a door, the position of a weapon, the state of a floor, the condition of hands or clothing. Let transitions compress: move from the act to its immediate consequence, from the consequence to the person left with it, without explaining the connection twice.\n\nA recurring phrase may gather history around an object, place, or act, but do not let its reputation replace the contact or consequence. The name people give a doorway or a debt may be repeated for years while the person who passes through it now still feels the splintered frame or the cold handle. Keep private thought in its actual causal order, before, during, or after action as appropriate, and at its needed extent without requiring it to produce a visible result. Do not manufacture damage, violent escalation, or uniformly abrasive speech to maintain the treatment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadahae::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / García Márquez / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through records that have a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Let each document know or conceal only what its situation allows: a letter written before the arrest cannot mention the arrest, a report filed by an official may omit what the official did not see, a later annotation may correct or misunderstand an earlier entry.\n\nWhen a repeated formulation connects records, keep it attributed. The same phrase may appear in a victim's note, a neighbor's statement, and a ledger entry, but it arrives each time through a particular writer and purpose. It need not contradict or reveal new information; it may simply show how the wording traveled and what each user did with it. Separate original knowledge from later annotation, and composition from handling, delivery, reception, and use as actual events allow. The record is not a complete mind: preserve required nonspoken experience separately. A record may matter without a tactic, legal step, or instrumental result.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadahaf::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / García Márquez / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend attention on chosen help, familiar preferences, and small pleasures within the actual offense or aftermath. Show the particular care: the food someone remembers another person likes, the chair pulled closer, the errand done without being asked, the joke that still works between them. Let the affection be sincere and specific rather than announced.\n\nA gathering sentence or callback can connect the present care with earlier shared occasions, but keep each source and private meaning distinct. The same phrase may have been a joke before the trouble and a reassurance after it, without losing either sense. Preserve genuine affection, refusal, disagreement, and any continuing danger or responsibility. Comfort can remain pleasurable without changing a relationship, exposing a hidden trap, or guaranteeing safety; no confession or redemption is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadahag::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / García Márquez / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse deliberate transitions and composed paragraph movement to relate actual criminal circumstances to known public accounts, family history, or material conditions within narrator access. A paragraph may begin with the offense as the town remembers it, move through the family's version, and return to the particular person now living with the consequence, but keep the source and time of each layer legible.\n\nThe particular consequence may confirm, qualify, or simply coexist with the wider account. The town may be right about the date and wrong about the motive; the family may repeat a version that protects someone without being a lie; the person at the center may remember the same events with an entirely different weight. Preserve individual cast diction, supported judgment, and full private experience. The active boundary may fall within the gathering or reflection before any return or verdict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadahah::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / García Márquez / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the outer scene as an actual occasion: someone is telling another person or group about an offense, scheme, debt, or disputed encounter, and the telling has a present purpose that need not be announced in advance. Preserve the active narrator and viewpoint; if the narrator is not the teller, render the teller's performance through the narrator's attention rather than switching into the teller's private mind.\n\nLet the embedded account carry the crime's preparation, execution, or aftermath through the teller's selection and emphasis. The teller may stress what flatters an ally, omit what they did not understand, or repeat a phrase that has become part of family or neighborhood memory. Show the selection as an act of the teller: a pause, a reordering, a name repeated, an event passed over quickly. Do not require the account to be false or confessional; sincere and strategic motives may operate in the same telling.\n\nGive the listeners concrete interests. One may need the information to act, another may be protecting a reputation, another may simply want the evening to continue. Let their responses interrupt, redirect, or confirm the telling through questions, silences, or a change in posture. A recurring name, object, or phrase should connect the embedded story to the outer scene, but its meaning may shift between levels without becoming a proof of truth or guilt.\n\nKeep time legible. Distinguish what the original participants knew at the moment of the offense from what the teller has learned since, and what the listeners now understand. When a later fact enters the telling, show its later arrival rather than silently correcting the earlier account. The outer scene may end with a listener acting on what they heard, deciding to wait, or simply recognizing a changed understanding; no disclosure, persuasion, or resolution is compulsory.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadahai::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / García Márquez / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse documentary and historical-memory prose. Anchor the scene in an actually available record: a police report, court notice, parish entry, workplace file, newspaper paragraph, or another document the story's world permits. Quote or paraphrase its wording precisely enough that the reader can hold it beside the lived account that follows. Identify the record's source, date, and purpose without turning the prose into an archival inventory.\n\nMove from the document to a particular person's authorized recollection or present experience of the offense. Let the same phrase appear in both the record and the memory, with shared or different significance. When the record says \"the matter was settled\" and the remembered consequence is a closed shop or a sleepless week, do not force the comparison into a discovered error; the difference may be one of scale, time, or purpose.\n\nKeep three channels distinct: received rumor, remembered experience, and current interpretation. Rumor may be attributed to neighbors or officials; memory belongs to the viewpoint character; interpretation may remain inward and unfinished. Preserve what the person knew earlier and what they know now, and do not grant the record authority over private experience merely because it is official.\n\nLet the García Márquez movement between personal life, family memory, public events, and recurring language gather detail across time while remaining anchored in a human consequence. A sentence may hold a public date and a private injury together without explaining which one caused the other. The comparison can end in recognition, unresolved difference, or a decision to act; no investigation, recovered secret, or acted-upon conclusion is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadahaj::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / García Márquez / Gothic-Punk\n\nGround the encounter in actual material surroundings: the condition of a building, the route to a meeting, the way a room has been kept or neglected. Let inherited authority appear as a concrete access condition, obligation, or reputation that bears on the criminal situation. A family name may open a door, an old debt may determine who is permitted to speak, a remembered event may make a present favor possible. Show the source and human significance of that inherited weight rather than treating it as mood alone.\n\nLet a circulating designation or remembered account gather around a present desire, fear, or obligation. One character may repeat what the neighborhood says about a place; another may remember the same event differently. Preserve actual competence, trust, coercion, and voluntary reserve. A social mask may fall away in trusted company, but it may also remain in place; do not prescribe betrayal, unmasking, or corruption.\n\nKeep atmosphere distinct from evidence and world rules. A beautiful room, unsettling ritual, or damaged device can matter through its actual function and history, not merely through its feeling. Do not import Kindred physiology, factions, powers, or blood-bond rules. If the world already permits something uncanny, treat its social reception as ordinary; if it does not, preserve the narrative techniques without inventing literal miracles.\n\nUse the García Márquez movement between personal life, family memory, public events, rumor, and recurring language to let a sentence gather detail across time while remaining anchored in a human consequence. The encounter may end with a changed understanding, a practical decision, or simply the atmosphere settling over an unresolved situation; no new consequence at the boundary is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadahak::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / García Márquez / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse social and domestic realism. Develop the actual terms of work, shared space, money, or care as the medium through which the offense touches the participants. Show who prepares the food, who waits for a payment, who shares a room, who is responsible for a child or an elder, and how those arrangements continue or alter under the pressure of the crime. Keep the setting, language, region, community, and period specific when the story supplies them; do not force English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure onto the scene.\n\nLet an attributed family saying or public account enter the arrangement without becoming a universal cultural code. A phrase repeated by a parent or neighbor may explain a decision, justify a silence, or simply mark a moment. Show who says it, to whom, and with what authority. Private meanings and public explanations may agree, differ, or remain irrelevant to one another.\n\nPreserve actual capacity, willingness, and permission. A character may help, refuse, delay, or act from mixed motives. Do not impose sacrifice, confession, punishment, or an instrumental purpose for every memory. The crime may be present as a missing sum, a changed schedule, a locked door, or a name no one speaks, rather than as a dramatic confrontation.\n\nUse the García Márquez movement between personal life, family memory, public events, rumor, and recurring language to let a sentence gather detail across time while remaining anchored in a human consequence. The scene may end with the arrangement still in place, altered, or newly understood; no resolution or return to action is compulsory.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadahal::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / García Márquez / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse social and psychological realism. Keep the encounter or inward occasion recoverable as a permitted person attends to a familiar phrase, name, or reported event connected to the offense. The person may hear it in a shop, read it in a notice, or recall it while doing something ordinary. Do not force the scene into a dramatic confrontation; the reception itself is the event.\n\nLet the thought gather earlier experience where it genuinely belongs. A remembered room, a previous conversation, or a particular sensation may enter the present attention because the phrase or name calls it up. Show the association as the character's own movement of mind, not as a flashback imposed by the narrator. Preserve what the person knew then and what they know now; a later understanding should not be retroactively inserted as the earlier motive.\n\nKeep the actual event, the public account, and the private memory distinct. The public account may be incomplete, accurate, or mistaken; the private memory may align with it or diverge. Do not require the character to reinterpret the past, confess, or reach a verdict. The thought may remain unfinished at the response boundary.\n\nUse the García Márquez movement between personal life, family memory, public events, rumor, and recurring language to let a sentence gather detail across time while remaining anchored in a human consequence. Do not impose quietness, scenery, guilt, or a return to action. Other participants remain independent people with their own knowledge and interests.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaham::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / García Márquez / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nSelect polyphonic nonlinear telling. Arrange permitted accounts, memories, or records of the offense and its aftermath with clear time and source markers. A section may open with a date, a place, a name, or a phrase that tells the reader whose account this is and when it was given. Move between scales: a public notice, a family memory, a bystander's report, a participant's private recollection. Do not require a fixed number of voices or a final convergence.\n\nLet an offender, witness, victim, or bystander have different or aligned interests. Preserve whichever positions and narrator access the story supplies. One account may confirm another, contradict it, or simply concern a different part of the event. The offender may already be known; uncertainty is not the only source of tension. Facts and interpretations may agree, and agreement can matter without discovering an error.\n\nUse a phrase or concern to connect the accounts where appropriate. A name, a date, a debt, or a recurring rumor may appear in several sections with different weight. Let the García Márquez movement between personal life, family memory, public events, rumor, and recurring language gather detail across time while remaining anchored in a human consequence. A sentence may hold a public date and a private injury together without explaining which one caused the other.\n\nKeep original knowledge and actual events stable. Do not change the event to maintain uncertainty or grant a later account retroactive authority over earlier knowledge. Preserve full private experience under the active contract. The arrangement may end with the accounts still separate, newly legible together, or left for the reader to weigh; no required convergence or resolution is imposed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadahan::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / García Márquez / Light Novel\n\nOrient the relevant people, space, and circumstance with clear paragraph rhythm. Enter the immediate situation cleanly: who is present, where they are, and what practical matter or encounter is underway. Keep spatial changes easy to follow with short, concrete transitions. Break paragraphs around action, dialogue, and private response so the reader can track what is happening and what a character makes of it.\n\nIntroduce a prior account or unfamiliar term when it helps understanding. If a name, debt, or reported event carries the criminal meaning, give its necessary context at the point of entry rather than withholding it as a puzzle clue. An earlier association may precede, interrupt, or follow public action in its actual order. Show whether the detail is remembered, reported, or present knowledge.\n\nGive private feeling its warranted extent. A reaction may be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to its owner. Do not reduce the García Márquez movement between personal life, family memory, public events, rumor, and recurring language to a short beat. A sentence may gather detail across time while remaining anchored in a human consequence, even within the light novel's accessible presentation.\n\nLet a familiar detail confirm continuity without a new meaning. The recurring name or object may simply show that the past is still present, not that a revelation is coming. Do not impose first person, a young cast, compulsory wit, a revelation, or a closing hook. The scene may end with the situation still developing, a private recognition, or a practical next step already underway.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadahao::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / García Márquez / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet a sound, object, or phrase already present in the scene become the recurring carrier of the telling. Return to it by extending syntax rather than restating it: a clause that follows the sound into another room, a relative clause that gathers who first heard it and who repeated it afterward, a second occurrence placed far enough away that the interval itself is felt. Let each return attach a concrete layer to the offense or its aftermath — the act itself, the report that circulated, the household change it left, the private response that outlasts the public account — so a single sentence can span the day of the act and the years after it while staying anchored in one person's consequence.\n\nShift attention through the recurrence rather than around it. A sentence may move from a present perception to a memory, then to what someone else said, then back to the perception changed by that knowledge. Keep the source of each layer clear: what the narrator directly experienced, what a relative or neighbor reported, what is only now being interpreted. When a comparison unfolds, let it alter what the narrator notices or understands; otherwise let it remain a local perception and move on. Set plain declarative sentences against the figurative movement, especially for the mechanical facts — what was taken, who was hurt, what the law calls it — so the lyric does not soften what actually happened.\n\nKeep the recurrence available to the characters in their own registers. A focal character may notice the image privately without ever naming it aloud; another may speak of it in flat practical terms; the circulating public version may repeat it with a meaning the focal character does not share. The narration's lyric register belongs to the telling, not to every speaker. Respect the world's actual limits: where no marvel is authorized, the image stays an image, charged by repetition rather than by supernatural warrant, and its charge comes from the scene, not from a borrowed town. The reply may end while the image is still gathering meaning, with the crime's consequence continuing rather than completed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadahap::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / García Márquez / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nPlace a present particular beside a remembered occasion through a shared detail: the same doorway, the same phrase at the same hour, the same task now done by different hands. Let the present detail arrive first as ordinary perception, then open into a memory the narrator or focal character actually holds — a family occasion, an ordinary errand, a night when the phrase meant something easier. Keep the two layers distinct in time and knowledge: the person in the remembered scene does not yet know what the offense will make of that memory, and the earlier self must not be handed later understanding.\n\nLet recurrence carry the aftermath without demanding a changed meaning. A habit may continue after the crime, a phrase may still be spoken around the table, the public version of what happened may still be told at visits and gatherings, and the reflection can register that continuity as the actual consequence. When a small irreversible change appears, ground it in something specific: who no longer comes, what is no longer kept, which sound is absent from the house. Do not generalize the loss into a lesson about time. Preserve former pleasure as pleasure — the remembered evening was good and remains good, even now that the money is gone or the name is spoken carefully.\n\nKeep the reflective movement inside the authorized perspective. The narrator may think directly about what has changed, or let a detail carry the association without announcing it. Private experience can extend across the interval between present and past without producing a decision, a confession, or a new act; the mind may simply stay with what it misses. Ordinary pleasure in the present — a meal, a joke that still works — can sit alongside what is no longer available. The reply may end during continuing reflection, the remembered occasion and the present aftermath still held together rather than resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadahaq::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / García Márquez / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the account as a situated modern memoir: a later composer, placed by the narration's own access and person, sets down the offense and its aftermath from a stated position — participant, witness, or inheritor of other people's accounts. Establish that relation early and keep it stable. Each recollection must carry its actual source: what the writer saw, what a named relative or neighbor reported, what circulated publicly as version after version, what the writer understands only now, at the desk, years later. The retrospect may confirm a prior meaning as readily as revise it, and must never grant the earlier self knowledge it did not have.\n\nLet a gathering sentence connect the offense to a human consequence across time. A single paragraph may move from the act itself to the household change it left, to the public version that circulated in the market or the family visits, to what that version omitted and the writer's present sense of the omission. Anchor the movement in particular people, places, and names as the story supplies them; when rumor or a public account appears, attribute it — who said it, when, in what room. Use the language, community, and period actually established rather than importing a generic regional temperament, and let the memoir be secular or traditional exactly as far as the supplied world makes it.\n\nCompose the memoir as an artifact without replacing live private experience. The writer's present reflections belong to the memoir, but within the recalled scenes the focal characters still receive their own authorized nonspoken experience rendered then and there — a suspicion felt during a visit, a choice weighed at a door — not merely summarized afterward. Do not impose exile, redemption, confession, or a finished life lesson. The reply may end with the memoir still mid-account, the offense's consequences running on beyond the page.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadahar::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / García Márquez / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect a few exact details that carry the offense's history: a phrase someone said, an object left behind, a time of day, a name now spoken differently than before. Choose details that distinguish what happened from what circulates about it and from the focal character's private response. Use direct clauses and keep only the explanation or inward connection that materially contributes to the present account; omit redundant judgment, never the actual cause or the meaningful feeling. Let the reader infer the relation between the detail and the offense without the narration announcing it.\n\nWhen a phrase or detail returns, let placement do the work of commentary: the second occurrence confirms an accurate account, sustains an unchanged aftermath, or acquires a supported new use from its new setting. One recurrence is enough; do not explain what the juxtaposition already shows. A private thought may run long enough to make sense even when it advances no plan, and a precise extended sentence may gather the years around an act better than a run of short fragments would — shortness is selection here, not a quota.\n\nPreserve the actual source and time of each layer. A sentence may report what happened, then what a relative or a neighbor said about it, then what the focal character now thinks, without a transition that restates the obvious. Keep memory of a directly experienced event distinct from memory of receiving a report about it. Cast voices stay individual: a character may speak briefly because that is how they speak, not because the style clips everyone. Do not impose concealed feelings, a late revelation, or a final verdict. The reply may end with the selected detail still in place and its consequence continuing.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadahas::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / García Márquez / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nKeep the present scene primary. Move naturally among what the characters are doing, saying, perceiving, remembering, and privately thinking, without a transition that announces each shift. When a phrase or concern gathers earlier occasions, let it arise from something in the scene — a name spoken, an object handled, a task resumed — and mark the source and time of the gathered material as it enters. Distinguish memory of a directly experienced event from memory of being told about it, and keep what the focal character knows separate from what others have said.\n\nLet the offense, scheme, milieu, or aftermath stay concrete through the actual situation rather than a mandatory pursuit or investigation. The crime may be present as a changed household routine, a debt being paid off in installments, a person who no longer crosses a certain street, a conversation that keeps returning to the same omission. The communal account can gather around the offense through ordinary talk — at meals, at work, during visits — but each speaker's version remains their own; the narration does not merge them into a single public truth unless the story supplies one.\n\nPreserve authorized inner experience at its natural length. A character may think through a memory, a suspicion, or an unresolved feeling without reaching a decision or changing the visible action, and an interval of attention can hold its place without a practical task to justify it. Use paragraphing and small transitions to orient the reader in time and place without recap machinery. No flashback, new rumor, or changed interpretation is owed anywhere; the scene's own movement — activity, speech, thought, or an unfinished setup — determines where the reply stops.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadahat::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / García Márquez / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nUse the supplied relationship premise to select which shared accounts of the offense return. A familiar name, phrase, place, or incident comes back because it matters to these particular people in this particular bond — a pair, a rivalry, a group with divided roles — not because it is a generic callback. Let the recurrence carry the premise's promise: affection returns to a moment of care, rivalry to a contested account of who did what, comfort to a repeated reassurance, discovery to a detail one participant now understands differently. Keep the criminal circumstances concrete; the bond does not replace the offense or suspend its consequences.\n\nPreserve private knowledge separately from what is shared. One participant may know something about the offense the other does not, may read a familiar phrase differently, or may hold a feeling private while the shared moment continues — and the narration can render that private experience fully without making the other character aware of it. When a callback lands, let its effect be particular: one person is moved, another misses it, a third answers with an ordinary practical concern because their attention is elsewhere.\n\nGive affection, rivalry, comfort, or discovery enough room where they belong. A scene may linger on the private meaning of a repeated phrase, the history inside a shared task, or the gap between what is said and what each person carries; private language between them can hold a history the public account of the offense never had. Ordinary life around the bond continues — work, caution, other people — without every exchange serving the relationship. No scene must complete a bond arc, force a confession, or resolve the offense before the boundary; the reply may end with both the relationship and the aftermath still in motion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadahau::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / García Márquez / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account the cadence of a chronicle being retold. Open with a compact declaration that sets the deed or the person in relation to it, then expand into the particulars that make the remembered version incomplete. Use deliberate openings and recurring phrasing grounded in what the story actually supplies: who did the act, who suffered it, who still speaks of it, what changed in the household or the street because of it. A recurring designation should come from an established deed or way of speaking, never an ornamental epithet pinned to every entrance.\n\nKeep the remembered public shape distinct from the actual circumstances and private cost. The chronicle may record what the deed came to be called and who first called it that, while the narration also shows what happened and what it took from particular people. Preserve three layers separately: what was known at the time, what later tellers added, and the focal character's private account, which may differ from the circulating designation without being framed as the hidden truth that cancels the public version. The public shape and the private consequence can both remain true at once.\n\nLet dialogue interrupt or qualify the chronicle's authority. A subject of the oft-told incident may correct one detail, answer an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern, or leave the telling uninterrupted because they are occupied with something else. This need not become parody. Do not impose heroic doom, punishment, public disgrace, or a completed communal verdict. The reply may end with the deed still being remembered and its consequences still unfolding, another modest act available to be told later rather than declared legend on the community's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadahav::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / García Márquez / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite through a particular authorized mind in a deliberate essayistic movement. Begin from a concrete detail of the offense, its scene, or its aftermath: an object handled, a room entered, a name repeated, a task interrupted. Let that detail open into a situated consideration rather than a report. The narrator moves from the immediate detail to an attributed public account, a remembered occasion, or a qualified generalization about the people involved, then follows the actual thought at its needed extent. Keep each movement anchored in a human consequence: what someone did, heard, avoided, remembered, or continued to believe.\n\nWhen a public account enters, give it a source or a recognizable circulation: the family repeated it, the neighbors accepted it, the office passed it along. Treat the recurring phrase as an object of essayistic attention. The narrator may return to it to weigh what it claims, to recall who first said it, or to notice how it has settled into ordinary speech. The narrator may examine that account sincerely, doubt it, or live beside it without resolving it. Do not turn the offense into a collective judgment spoken by the whole community. Let particular people hold particular views, and let agreement or disagreement arise from actual relationships and interests.\n\nPreserve the distinction between what the narrator knows from direct experience, what others have said, and what remains uncertain. Allow the consideration to be personal rather than instrumental. The narrator need not reach a useful conclusion, identify a culprit, or return to the opening detail. The reflection may remain open at the response boundary, with thought continuing or attention turning to an ordinary present fact. Do not require reserve, bleakness, diagnosis, disillusionment, or a completed explanation. If the world permits the marvelous, let its social reception enter the essayistic movement as one more attributed account the narrator considers; if it does not, keep the narrative techniques without inventing literal miracles.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadahaw::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / García Márquez / Screenplay\n\nPresent the story in concise action blocks with clear speaker cues and scene headings when location or time changes. Let each recurring phrase or account enter through an actual source: a character says it in dialogue, a document is shown, a broadcast is heard, or a visible action demonstrates its effect. When the phrase returns, show the recurrence through staging, actual speech, or a visible reaction to an object or name. A scene may carry an earlier occasion into the present through a repeated line or a familiar object, letting the layered time of the offense become legible in what is seen and heard. A cut may join distinct places or times, but it must not alter chronology or imply that one character knows what another has only thought.\n\nLet the public scene carry what is visible and audible. A character may notice an object, hesitate at a name, or repeat a line they heard, but the action block records the observable behavior, not the private interpretation. Keep required nonspoken experience in its clearly attributed private form, separate from any performed voice-over. Voice-over is spoken performance with its own audience and permissions; it is not the default container for thought.\n\nUse cuts, offscreen sound, and voice-over only when the chosen sequence needs them. Do not fill the page with camera commands. The scene may end on a completed action, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Do not require an evidentiary reveal, reaction shot, chase, or completed scene before the active boundary. Preserve the actual source knowledge of the narrator and cast; a cut cannot erase what a character already knows.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadahax::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / García Márquez / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable scene with clear speakers and economical stage directions for supported positions, objects, and actions. Let a known formulation about the offense enter through an actual speaker: someone repeats what they heard, asks about a name, or refers to an account already in circulation. The line belongs to that speaker and their relationship to the others present. Its delivery can carry the weight of who first said it and how it has circulated, without the audience needing a narrated explanation. It does not become a chorus spoken by the whole cast.\n\nUse entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions to carry the public scene. A character may set down a glass, turn away, or continue a task while the line is spoken. These physical choices are observable conditions, not automatic proof of guilt, agreement, or hidden motive. Distinguish what each character hears and knows from what the audience receives. The audience may understand an implication that another character misses, but the stage directions must not certify a private state.\n\nKeep required private experience in its separately attributed nonspoken form. An aside or monologue is performed speech when separately authorized, not the default destination for thought. Let spoken questions and answers fit the relationship and the immediate physical task. The scene may stop after a result or an interruption without an obligatory gathering, theatrical revelation, or new clue.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadahay::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / García Márquez / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite through one authorized mind as its associations actually move. Braid perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern around the offense. A sound in the present may recall a phrase from the day the news arrived; a name may open into a room remembered years earlier; a repeated public account may surface beside the feel of a door handle or the taste of coffee. Let syntax and paragraph shape follow those associations, while keeping enough orientation to distinguish events from recollection or fantasy.\n\nWhen a received account enters, let it arrive as something heard, read, or repeated by a particular person, not as firsthand experience. The mind's movement between present sensation and past occasion carries the source distinction through the syntax itself: a remembered voice, a half-quoted line, a fragment of what the neighbors said. A recurring expression may gather several occasions or remain attached to one, without requiring a new interpretation or useful plan. The mind may return to the same phrase because it is unfinished, because it belongs to someone else, or because the present moment resembles an earlier one. Do not force every return to yield a discovery.\n\nKeep each associative passage owned by its permitted mind. If separately authorized shifts of mind occur, preserve their attribution. Thought and feeling may continue at the response boundary; prose length alone does not set fictional duration. The passage need not conclude, change direction, or produce a decision. Ordinary reasoning alone is not this stylistic mode, but the associative movement must remain legible rather than dissolving all scene facts into indeterminacy.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadahaz::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / García Márquez / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the story through readable sender-attributed messages. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: some write in fragments, some wait, some forward, some leave a message unsent. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the offense, the participants' interests, or the aftermath. Do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext.\n\nKeep composition, transmission, delivery, and reception distinct. A repeated name or phrase carries its real source into the current exchange: someone forwards what they received, quotes what they remember, or asks about a message they only heard described. The forwarded message carries its earlier source into the current exchange through the actual message sequence, so the transcript shows the difference between what was composed, sent, delivered, and received. The fact that a phrase appears in the transcript does not mean every recipient has read it, understood it, or accepted it.\n\nSelect messages that show a participant forwarding what they received, quoting what they remember, or asking about a message they only heard described. Let the exchange continue around the offense, repeat an accurate understanding, or leave an aftermath unchanged without requiring new information or instrumental effects. Unsent drafts are composed artifacts, not live thought; full required nonspoken experience remains separate and clearly attributed. Context outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadahba::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / García Márquez / Visual Novel\n\nWrite scene-oriented passages with concise setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Orient each scene clearly: where the characters are, what they are doing, and what visible detail matters. Use a meaningful object, background, or expression cue where it matters to the offense, its aftermath, or a character's relation to it.\n\nWhen the focal mind encounters the cue, let the narrative passage follow the actual association as it forms. A familiar doorway may recall the morning the news arrived; a repeated name may bring back a conversation heard years before. The illustrated detail anchors the movement between present scene and earlier occasion, and the passage shows the association without requiring the character to speak about it. Follow the actual private or public reception without requiring a fixed cue-thought-dialogue rotation. The mind may notice the detail and say nothing, or speak about something else entirely. A face is not automatic proof of guilt or a shared interpretation. Preserve any actual knowledge the narrator or cast already has; the illustrated cue does not grant new access to another mind.\n\nGive inward experience its full extent when it contributes feeling or thought, even if no immediate decision changes. Scene changes should be legible, and background or expression cues should be purposeful references, not executable engine code or a mandatory label on every line. The style does not itself activate images, menus, romance routes, or a closing reaction.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadahbb::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / García Márquez / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across installments: what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. When a later installment needs re-entry, recover the specific phrase, name, or fact that the current installment needs, preserving its earlier source and meaning while letting the new context show what has changed or remained. Do not retell the whole investigation or aftermath. Preserve what was said, known, changed, or left pending. A later account may add information, repeat an accurate understanding, or leave an aftermath unchanged. Do not rewrite prior knowledge to prolong suspense or make a character forget a supported conclusion.\n\nKeep events distinct from their retelling. A circulating phrase may return in a new context, but its earlier source and meaning remain part of the history. When a character repeats what they heard, the installment shows the repetition and its present effect without turning the retelling into a new event. The recurring phrase becomes a serial object whose history accumulates across installments: each return adds a new context while the earlier source stays intact. Let ordinary life and private feeling continue around the inquiry or its consequences. A character may work, eat, wait, or remember without a pursuit, discovery, or useful effect. No new rumor, escalating threat, punishment, or cliffhanger is required at each boundary.\n\nUse earned developments and occasional hooks without making every turn a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger. Progress can involve knowledge, relationships, work, or resources. The installment may close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaiaa::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Gu Long / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the difficult emotional exchange as the interval around a precise word or act that makes the need concrete: the exact favor asked, the apology offered, the thing withheld, or the return proposed. Choose that word first, then let the next action or sentence belong to the other person. The emotional cost lives in the distance between what the viewpoint character risks and what actually arrives—not in narrating that distance as a lesson.\n\nUse Gu Long's declarative movement to place the crucial line in a short, plain sentence, then delay its resolution. The other character may answer at once, look away, continue a task, or say something adjacent; the reader feels the strain through that unresolved beat. A distilled image can hold the feeling the dialogue does not state: a glass set down, a door left open, a coat not taken off. Let the image appear once, not as a repeated symbol.\n\nKeep the offense, scheme, or aftermath present as the reason the exchange costs something. A request for money, an alibi, a place to stay, or a promise of silence carries its weight from the specific criminal circumstances already established. Do not explain that weight in general terms; let it shape what the speaker chooses to say first, what gets left out, and what the listener does with their hands or attention while deciding.\n\nAllow private feeling at full depth without requiring a visible result. The viewpoint character may rehearse the request internally, feel the humiliation or relief of saying it, or recognize that the other person's calm is worse than anger. This inner movement is the content, not a setup for a plot beat. No confession, forgiveness, punishment, or changed relationship must occur before the scene boundary. Silence is not proof of guilt, love, or refusal; it is the lived interval that the story is written to render.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaiab::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Gu Long / Chat Roleplay\n\nBegin by resolving the submitted material under the active authorship contract. Orient the shared interval concretely in the first paragraph: who is present, what they are doing, and which criminal circumstance or aftermath bears on the moment. Use readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution; each speaker's action or object can follow or precede their speech without requiring an action marker for every line.\n\nSelect one concrete owned contribution—a question, refusal, piece of information, physical movement, or decision to stay or leave—and place it in a short declarative unit. That unit becomes the scene's readable turning point, not a forced choice. Let the other participants respond to that specific contribution according to their established interests and current knowledge. NPCs may continue an ordinary task, watch a door, check a phone, or choose an action that affects what the player character can see or do, but they need not speak cryptically or reveal a hidden plan.\n\nPreserve player ownership and actual knowledge. Write the player character's private experience to the depth the active contract permits, but do not decide their next major action, emotional conclusion, or relationship commitment beyond the submitted material. NPCs may know less than the player character, but the narration should not hide known information from the reader merely to create suspense.\n\nUse Gu Long's brief declarative movement to keep the exchange crisp. A distilled image—a cigarette ground out, a chair pulled back, a bag set on the table—can orient the reader without slowing the scene. Let a meaningful interval end with the scene still in motion: someone mid-sentence, a car not yet arrived, a decision not yet made. The continuation follows from the scene, not from a compelled question, cliffhanger, or finished reveal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaiac::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Gu Long / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the vernacular episodic mode. Each reply is one actual occasion with a concrete purpose, place, and set of social terms. Open by establishing what this occasion is for—a meeting, a delivery, a meal, a payment, a visit—and who stands to gain or lose from it. Let the criminal circumstance be present as the reason the occasion matters, not as a puzzle to be solved within the reply.\n\nWhen the story already contains earlier occasions, connect them through one real repeated marker: the same teahouse, envelope, person mentioned by two different speakers, or debt counted differently. Choose one marker per scene and let it carry changed significance. When the reply is a first occasion, do not invent a past; let the present event hold its own weight and leave recurrence to emerge later if the story continues.\n\nKeep Gu Long's declarative selection active inside the episodic frame. Use short sentences to mark who acts, what changes hands, and what remains unresolved. A distilled image—a bowl emptied, a receipt folded, a name written once—can anchor the occasion without slowing its social movement. Let an interval hold private feeling or unresolved consequence: a character may sit with what they have just learned, or watch someone leave, without requiring a completed episode, a moral, or a changed allegiance.\n\nDo not force one event per reply. The occasion may begin, continue, or end according to the active scene boundary. The reader should be able to follow the concrete social terms and see how this occasion belongs to the larger criminal sequence. No martial plot, new encounter, or completed episode is required by the style.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaiad::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Gu Long / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nGround the criminal action or aftermath in actual material contact. Choose one telling particular per beat—the heft of a bag, the give of a lock, the scrape of a chair on concrete, the dampness of a coat, the exact amount short—and state it in a direct verb without elaboration. Let the physical fact carry its own implication. 'The envelope was heavier than it looked' does the work that a paragraph about its significance would dilute.\n\nUse Gu Long's declarative movement to deliver the material fact in a short sentence, then leave a gap for its consequence. A distilled image can hold the pressure: a cracked phone screen, a wet footprint, a key worn smooth. Let the image appear once and let the next action or silence respond to it. Vary rhythm deliberately: a longer thought is allowed when the character's reasoning needs it, but the default movement should be economical and hard-edged.\n\nPreserve private feeling at full depth without requiring a decision or result. A character may stand in a cold room and think through what the missing money means, or feel the strain of waiting without any blow landing. The gritty treatment does not add violence, weapons, or a terse cast; it makes the actual stakes physical and immediate. Competence, supplied harm, and individual roughness come from the established characters and world, not from a style-imposed toughness.\n\nDo not require damage, a failed attempt, or a new result before the scene boundary. The pressure can be in the waiting, the watching, the carrying, or the choosing not to act. The reader should feel the material weight of the criminal circumstance, not a checklist of hard-boiled gestures.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaiae::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Gu Long / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through documents chosen for their relation to the offense, the participants' interests, or the continuing aftermath. For each document, establish a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Choose the form the scene actually requires—a ledger page, a note left on a counter, a report filed late, a letter never sent, a transcript—rather than rotating through available formats. One document can carry the entire reply if its selection and wording are exact.\n\nPlace the precise phrase or notation whose context matters. Gu Long's declarative movement works inside documents: a single line in a logbook, a name misspelled, an amount crossed out, a sentence that stops mid-thought. Let the reader notice what the document omits—a report that mentions everyone except one person, a letter whose warmth turns formal at a specific point—without an omniscient explanation after every record. Omission is a property of the artifact, not a puzzle to solve immediately.\n\nKeep the document's knowledge distinct from live private experience. What a writer records is a composed artifact, not the full mind of the person who wrote it. A character may write a careful denial while privately knowing the truth; the document shows the denial, and any interiority belongs to the active narration contract, not to the letter itself. Preserve original composition time versus later annotation time: a margin note added weeks later changes what the document can claim to have known.\n\nDo not require a second artifact, a useful discovery, a contradiction, or completed correspondence before the scene boundary. The sequence should feel like records accumulating around a real offense, not like a puzzle assembling itself on schedule.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaiaf::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Gu Long / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nAttend to the actual preference, accommodation, or pleasure that makes care particular within the offense's circumstances or aftermath. Choose the specific kindness from what the character already likes or needs: the food someone actually likes, the chair saved for them, the door locked without being asked, the phone charger left where they can find it, the cup of tea made the way they take it. Write it as a concrete action or short phrase, not an adjective like 'warm' or 'safe' doing the work alone.\n\nPlace the kind act in a short declarative sentence and then give its reception room to run. The other person may accept it, refuse it, ignore it, or be too tired to notice. Their response need not acknowledge the care for the care to be real. Let an interval hold the moment: someone eating in silence, someone falling asleep, someone saying 'thanks' without looking up. The ease is in the room to do that.\n\nKeep the criminal circumstance present without turning the comfort into a trap or a cure. The danger, debt, or aftermath still exists; the kindness does not immunize anyone from it. A character can be genuinely cared for and still be in trouble. The helper's knowledge and sincerity remain their own; the narration should not reveal a hidden design behind every considerate act. Let the world's practical demands continue around the comfort—a phone ringing, a timetable, an unpaid debt—so the ease remains particular rather than a domestic vignette replacing the genre.\n\nUse Gu Long's distilled images to make the comfort concrete: a blanket folded over a chair, a receipt smoothed flat, a window opened a little. No confession, gratitude, absolution, changed mood, or shortened thought is required. The treatment makes room for particular people to be particular with each other inside a criminal world.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaiag::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Gu Long / Formal Literary Novel\n\nFrame the offense, scheme, or aftermath through sustained social context, material conditions, or permitted judgment. Open with a paragraph that places the immediate scene in a larger situation: what this neighborhood knows, what this family has lost, what this business was before the trouble, what the weather has done to the streets. The frame must be specific to the story's actual world and the narrator's access, not a generic meditation on crime or society.\n\nWithin the composed paragraph, select one precise phrase or fact and place it as a short declarative sentence. It may be the exact amount owed, the name on the warrant, the hour the shop closed, or the promise that was broken. Gu Long's distilled movement works inside the formal architecture: a short sentence after a long one can change the stillness without breaking the paragraph into fragments. Let the following sentence or two extend the implication through attention, memory, or judgment that the active contract permits.\n\nAllow an interval around the declaration to preserve attention or uncertainty. The narrator may hold the moment, describe the room, or move to a related memory. Full private experience remains available: a character's doubt, fear, or resolve can be rendered at depth without requiring a public action or a completed return. Maintain deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement; the prose is composed, not conversational.\n\nKeep the cast's actual register. Formal narration does not make every character speak elaborately or strip them of their ordinary diction. No terse speech, newly revised frame, public verdict, or completed return is required before the scene boundary. The style's work is to hold a precise criminal fact inside a world large enough to explain why it matters.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaiah::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Gu Long / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen the outer scene as a live occasion with a teller, listeners, a place, and a reason for the telling. The reason may be persuasion, payment, warning, entertainment, or simple company. Establish what each listener wants from the account before the telling begins, even if only through a small gesture: a cup held without drinking, a chair turned toward the teller, a hand that stops moving over a ledger.\n\nLet the embedded criminal account move in short declarative units. Select only the details the teller would select: a name, a sum, a broken lock, a wet coat, a promise made in a particular room. Leave intervals between these details. A pause can mark a thing the teller cannot explain, a thing the teller is choosing not to explain, or a thing the teller has never thought to question. Do not fill the pause with explanation.\n\nReturn to the listeners during the telling through small concrete returns to their attention: a cup set down, a glance toward a door, a question withheld, a listener remembering a different version of the same event. Let these returns interrupt the embedded account at the points where the teller's selection or emphasis would actually catch a listener's attention. The interruption does not need to change the teller's course or produce a visible effect.\n\nGive the teller a visible emphasis. A repeated phrase, object, or number should accrue meaning across the telling without proving the account true. When a phrase returns, connect the embedded story to the outer occasion through a concrete detail: the listener recognizes the phrase from their own life, the teller repeats it with a different weight, or the room answers it with an ordinary sound. Let the recurrence revise what the listener understands about the outer scene rather than certify the embedded account.\n\nKeep the two levels distinct in knowledge. The teller may know more than the listeners, less than the listeners, or something different from what the listeners know. Render the teller's private experience and a listener's private experience as actual nonspoken thought and feeling, but one mind does not become transparent to the other. A listener's inference is not the teller's admission.\n\nEnd the response at the natural boundary of the current scene. The telling may continue, pause, or stop without a confession, a useful lesson, or a visible change in the listeners. The account has been given its particular shape, and the outer occasion has received it in some definite way.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaiai::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Gu Long / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse documentary and historical-memory prose. When a record enters the scene, give it a material identity: what it is, when it was made, who made it, who kept it, and how the current viewpoint has access to it. Quote only the wording the viewpoint would actually read or recall. A ledger line, a police notice, a receipt, a letter, or a court transcript may appear in full or in part, but it remains an object with its own time and purpose.\n\nPlace the record beside authorized memory or present attention. Let the viewpoint read the exact wording, then let the mind move to what it remembers or notices: the room where the sum was counted, the voice that gave the address, the smell of the stairwell, the person who was not mentioned in the record. The record may confirm the memory, contradict it, or simply fail to include it. Do not force the discrepancy to resolve.\n\nKeep the record's language distinct from the narrator's language. A bureaucratic phrase should sound like the record; a remembered phrase should sound like the person who spoke it. The gap between these registers is part of the scene. Use Gu Long's short declarative movement to let a single quoted line from the record stand alone before the memory answers it.\n\nPreserve the difference between what the record says and what the viewpoint knows. A record may be accurate about an event and silent about its meaning. A memory may be vivid and mistaken about a date. Render the viewpoint's private experience of reading and remembering as actual nonspoken thought and feeling, without granting access to the minds of the people named in the record.\n\nThe scene may end with the record still open, the memory still unfinished, or the viewpoint still looking at a single line. No new fact, recovered secret, guilt diagnosis, or useful decision is required. The record and the lived recollection have been placed in a definite relation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaiaj::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Gu Long / Gothic-Punk\n\nLocate the actual privilege, access, public image, or private desire in the supplied offense or aftermath. Name the concrete arrangement that carries it: a key held by one family, a building that still bears an old name, a debt recorded in a private ledger, a room where certain people may wait and others may not. Let this arrangement be a fact of the world, not a mood.\n\nChoose one telling particular from present life and place it beside that gravity. A phone charging on a marble sill, a plastic bag left on an antique chair, a modern lock fitted into an old door, a text message arriving during a formal greeting. Render the particular in one or two distilled images. Let the collision between old authority and present life be felt through that object or line rather than explained.\n\nLet atmosphere shape how a specific encounter is experienced. A corridor may feel watched because of who has power there, not because of shadows. A trusted companion may speak more freely in a private room, and the mask may loosen through a small change in posture, address, or willingness to name a fear. Keep the intimacy morally complicated: trust can be real and still carry an unspoken limit.\n\nKeep descriptive intensity separate from evidence. A beautiful room, unsettling silence, or damaged object may matter through its actual function and history. If the narration suggests a connection between an arrangement and the offense, show the concrete link: who could enter, what was recorded, what was left behind. Atmosphere alone does not establish access, motive, or guilt.\n\nPreserve real authority, freely offered trust, coercion, and world mechanisms as supplied. The scene may end with the particular still in view and the gravity still unresolved. No unmasking, sinister room, new power, confession, betrayal, or changed outcome is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaiak::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Gu Long / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse lyric and philosophical inwardness. Follow one authorized person's actual question about the offense or its aftermath. Let the question be precise: whether a debt can be inherited, whether a person who stayed silent shares the blame, whether a home can remain a home after what happened there, whether a particular act was chosen or merely permitted.\n\nGive the question particular scope through a selected phrase, remembered encounter, or explicitly imagined case. The mind may return to a sentence someone spoke, a door left open, a festival that continued after the arrest, a name no one says now. Let the recurrence accrue weight. When the same phrase returns, let it return with a slightly different meaning because of what has been considered in between.\n\nKeep imagined and established events distinct. If the viewpoint imagines what might have happened, mark the imagining as imagining: a conditional sentence, a scene introduced as a possibility, a comparison to something known. Do not let an imagined version quietly become the established event. The question may be pursued through both memory and speculation, but the narration must preserve which is which.\n\nAllow full feeling and abstract consideration to persist together. The viewpoint may reason slowly, feel grief or anger without naming it, notice an ordinary object and find it suddenly unbearable, or return to the question after a long interval of silence. Do not force all thought into terse or cryptic fragments. Gu Long's brevity belongs to the selection of detail, not to a ban on extended inwardness.\n\nThe scene may end with the question still open, the memory still returning, or the viewpoint still looking at the ordinary object. No verdict, confession, moral superiority, or mental pause is required. The question has been given a definite shape, and the mind has moved through it in a particular way.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaial::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Gu Long / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse social and psychological realism. Follow the authorized viewpoint's actual perception, memory, reasoning, and feeling within a specific social encounter related to the offense or its aftermath. Begin with what the viewpoint notices: a particular voice, a changed room, a person who does not meet their eyes, a phrase that arrives a moment too late.\n\nSelect the concrete detail or direct wording that gives the encounter personal weight. One detail is often enough: the way a cup is set down, the exact words of a denial, the number of times a name is repeated. Let the mind move from that detail to what it remembers or infers. The movement should be legible: perception, then recognition, then feeling, then perhaps a decision to speak or stay silent.\n\nKeep private interpretation separate from event facts and other minds. The viewpoint may understand why a person hesitated, but that understanding is an inference, not access to the other mind. The viewpoint may remember an event clearly and still be wrong about what it meant. Render the viewpoint's full inward experience as actual nonspoken thought and feeling without making every interpretation correct.\n\nPreserve the actual narrator distance. If the narration is close, the viewpoint's thoughts may appear directly. If the narration is more distant, the inward experience may be rendered through observed behavior and selected detail. Either way, the viewpoint's private experience remains available to the extent the contract permits.\n\nThe viewpoint may feel before understanding, understand before feeling, or remain uncertain. The scene may end with the encounter still unfinished, the detail still present, or the viewpoint still sitting with what has been perceived. No fixed reaction order, diagnosis, or return to action is required. The reception has been made precise and particular.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaiam::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Gu Long / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse social realism through chronicle. Order the actual events and the attributed later reports so their relation is intelligible within the narrator's access. A chronicle may move from the offense to the first report, from the report to the public reaction, from the public reaction to a later account that changes the story. Keep each step distinct: what happened, what was said to have happened, what was remembered, what was repeated.\n\nConnect the sequence through selected names, words, or material details. A particular street, a shop sign, a surname, a phrase that appears in two reports, a sum of money that changes as the story travels. Let these details recur so the reader can follow the sequence without the narrator explaining every connection. Use Gu Long's short declarative movement to let a single reported line stand alone before the next event answers it.\n\nPreserve any real public knowledge without equating report with firsthand experience. A report may be accurate, exaggerated, incomplete, or deliberately false. The chronicle may record what was publicly said without granting access to the minds of the people who said it. If a report is later shown to be wrong, show the correction as a later event, not as a retrospective change to the earlier record.\n\nKeep full private minds beyond the chronicle's public account. Describe what people did and what they were reported to have said, but do not enter their unspoken thoughts unless the narrator's access permits it. A public reputation is a fact about the community, not a fact about the person's interior life.\n\nThe chronicle may end with the sequence still open, a report still circulating, or a detail still unexplained. No scandal, neutral omniscience, changed verdict, contradiction, or completed history is required. The events and reports have been placed in a definite, intelligible order.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaian::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Gu Long / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. In the first lines, orient the reader to where the viewpoint is, who is present, and what relevant offense or aftermath is in play. If unfamiliar information is needed, give it in the smallest clear unit: a name with a role, a place with a function, a rule with its immediate consequence. Do not pause the scene for a lore block.\n\nKeep spatial changes easy to follow. When the viewpoint moves, name the new location and the significant object or person in it. Use short paragraphs around action, dialogue, and private response. A line of dialogue may stand alone. A physical action may stand alone. A private reaction may follow in its own paragraph, giving the reader a clear beat between what happened and how the viewpoint received it.\n\nSelect one criminal-world detail and place it beside the viewpoint's actual reception. The detail may be a direct line, a practical term, an object, or a small action: a price named too casually, a door that opens without a sound, a phone number written on a receipt. Let the viewpoint notice it, then let the private response follow. The response may be recognition, confusion, fear, anger, or a calm acceptance of something already known. Do not force the response to be dramatic or to produce an immediate action.\n\nPreserve narrator ownership and full private feeling. The viewpoint's thoughts may include practical reasoning, but they are not limited to it. Render sensation, memory, and emotion as the actual person experiences them. If the narrator is first person, the private experience is direct. If the narrator is third person, the private experience remains available to the extent the contract permits.\n\nThe scene may end with the detail still in view, the reception still unfolding, or the viewpoint still deciding what to do. No young cast, first person, delivered clue, or completed response is required. The staging has made the moment readable, and the reception has been given its actual shape.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaiao::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Gu Long / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet one image or sound from the actual scene, object, or authorized association carry the paragraph's emotional development. Recur it with a change in syntax or placement: first as a perception, later as a memory, a consequence, or a thing noticed differently. The recurrence should deepen what the narrator understands about the offense, scheme, or aftermath, not merely decorate it. Keep the image traceable to the scene or the teller's associations; if it is a comparison, let it unfold only when it changes what the narrator sees or knows.\n\nAnchor the lyricism with a direct declarative fact. A short plain sentence can state the practical particular the image belongs to: the money was gone, the door had been forced, the witness would not repeat her answer. This plain fact keeps the crime's status clear without deflating the feeling. The image and the fact may sit side by side, or the fact may arrive after the image has accumulated weight. Do not require an outward cue before the image, a plain-statement return before the boundary, or a revelation that changes the image's meaning.\n\nLet the offense and its consequences remain concrete. Imagery alone proves no guilt, establishes no alibi, and replaces no evidence. A beautiful sentence about a broken lock does not make the break-in metaphorical. When the story needs inference, keep the distinction between what is observed, what is remembered, and what is concluded. The lyrical register may render the observer's attention or feeling, but it must not blur those distinctions into atmosphere.\n\nPreserve the narrator's and characters' actual registers. A focal character may think or speak in plain, practical language even when the narration is lyrical. Do not make every speaker share the narrator's figurative voice. Keep genuine feeling available, but do not require permanent solemnity or a final image that resolves the scene. The response may end inside continuing perception, speech, action, or private experience, with the image still developing or set aside.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaiap::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Gu Long / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nConnect a present particular to an actual or authorized recollection. The present detail may be small: a sound, an object, a phrase, a person's way of standing. Let the memory arrive through that detail, not as a general meditation. Keep the original occasion's knowledge at its own time: what the character knew then, what they noticed, what they expected. If the present interpretation differs, show the difference as a later development, not as a correction inserted into the earlier moment.\n\nUse selective declarative movement to carry the reflection. A short sentence can establish the present fact; a gap can let the memory surface; a second short sentence can return to the present with the remembered occasion still active. The reflection may confirm the present, complicate it, or simply coexist with it. Do not require a new insight, a wise narrator, a moral, or a completed mental resting point before the boundary. Ordinary pleasure can remain pleasurable, and a memory need not become evidence of loss.\n\nKeep the crime's concrete consequences present. The reflection may concern an offense, its preparation, its aftermath, or the relationships it changed, but the remembered detail must remain tied to actual events and actual access. A character may remember a room before it was searched, a promise before it was broken, a voice before it lied. These recollections can deepen the present scene without turning every memory into a clue or a confession.\n\nPreserve full private feeling and the active source access. The narrator may reflect directly or let a detail carry the association. Do not make every character speak in the narrator's reflective register, and do not require constant sadness, compulsory loss, or an announcement of impermanence at the end of every scene. The response may end inside continuing reflection, speech, action, or private experience, with the past and present still held together.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaiaq::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Gu Long / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the account as modern memoir: a participant or close witness selects and shapes an earlier criminal experience from a later composition time. Keep the active narrator and the memoir's author distinct when the story's perspective requires it. The narrator may be the author looking back, or the narrator may present the authored account while the original participant remains a separate figure. Attribute the actual participant source and the composition time through concrete details: what the author knows now, what they can no longer verify, what they choose to include.\n\nSelect a direct phrase, received word, or concrete detail that gives the earlier occasion its real terms. Let that particular carry the scene's weight rather than a general summary. Use brief declarative movement to establish the remembered fact, then expand into the particulars that make the memory incomplete. A gap in the account may be a real gap in knowledge, a deliberate omission, or a thing the author cannot yet say; keep those possibilities distinct without requiring the author to explain every silence.\n\nDistinguish the original experience from the later commentary. When the author reflects, let the reflection arrive at its own time: \"I did not know then,\" \"Later I understood,\" \"I still do not know.\" The later commentary may confirm the earlier experience, revise it, or leave it unresolved. Do not force a redemption, a changed judgment, a source confession, or a completed account before the boundary. The memoir may remain partial, and the author need not claim final authority over what happened.\n\nKeep full live nonspoken interiority separate from the composed account. A memoir records what its author chooses to record; it is not automatically the complete live mind of the participant at the original moment. When the story requires hidden or visible interiority, render the actual private experience under the active contract rather than replacing it with the memoir's retrospective summary. Preserve the actual setting, language, period, and community supplied, without importing antiquity, mysticism, exile, religion, or a single honor code as universal scene requirements.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaiar::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Gu Long / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect the fact, phrase, or actual response that makes the offense, scheme, or human aftermath particular within the narrator's access. One exact detail can stand for a larger condition: the way a person checks a lock, the single question no one answers, the object left on the table. Use direct clauses and a small number of details. Do not add a second detail that merely repeats the first. If a detail is retained, it should contribute to the present account's causality, context, or emotional recognition.\n\nKeep needed causality and context. Omission should leave something inferable rather than delete the connection between events. If a character acts, the reader should be able to see enough of the incentive or pressure to understand the choice. If a consequence follows, the retained detail should make that consequence legible. A gap is not a hidden known thought; it is an absence the reader can fill from what has been shown. Do not withhold a fact the narrator knows merely to create mystery.\n\nLet a long feeling or uncertainty remain long enough to make sense. Minimalist prose does not require every sentence to be short or every emotion to be compressed into a gesture. A precise extended sentence may serve the style better than several performed fragments. When private experience matters, render it directly under the active contract rather than implying it through a single external cue. The thought may be brief, but it must be the character's actual experience, not a placeholder for one.\n\nKeep cast voices individual. A character may speak in longer, practical, or indirect sentences even when the narration is spare. Do not make every speaker clipped, and do not require a deflating final line, a fresh clue, a changed interpretation, or a reveal before the boundary. The response may end inside continuing speech, action, thought, or private experience, with the essential particulars already in place.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaias::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Gu Long / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose that moves naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience. Let the immediate scene set the register and rhythm. Use paragraphing and transitions to orient the reader without recap machinery. When a detail matters, select it precisely: a short declarative sentence can establish the fact that gives the scene its weight, then the prose can expand into the particulars that make the situation concrete.\n\nKeep the offense, scheme, milieu, or aftermath present through actual interests and consequences. The crime need not be the subject of every sentence, but it should remain the pressure or context that shapes what the characters notice, avoid, or pursue. A character may think about something unrelated while the practical consequences of the crime continue around them. Do not turn every interval into a mystery or force a revelation at the end of each scene. Suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did what.\n\nPreserve authorized inner experience as actual private fiction. The narrator may render a focal character's reasoning, attention, sensation, uncertainty, or motive at the length the scene requires. This interiority does not need to prove its value by immediately changing a visible action. Keep access and knowledge separate: the narration may reveal a focal motive while another character does not know it. Do not let the style grant access to excluded minds or cancel access explicitly permitted by the active authorship contract.\n\nKeep individual voices and the actual world's facts. A character may speak in plain, indirect, or practical language even when the narration is more composed. Do not make the cast uniformly witty, quippy, or polished. Preserve the selected setting and period. Let the response end where the continuing scene or private experience naturally pauses or concludes; do not require a clue, reversal, confession, or completed test simply to close the reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaiat::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Gu Long / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nSelect scenes and actual or permitted remembered moments that make the supplied relationship premise legible. The premise may be affection, rivalry, care, jealousy, comfort, discovery, or another bond. Choose the scene because it reveals something particular about how these people matter to each other, not because it advances a plot beat. Give the relationship enough space where it belongs: a conversation may matter more than the crime's next development, and a private moment may carry the scene's emotional weight.\n\nUse private language and repeated moments to fulfill the bond's particular promise. A phrase one character uses, a gesture the other recognizes, an object or place they share, a callback to an earlier scene: these can carry meaning without being explained each time. When a repeated moment returns, let it return with a new application or consequence. Do not insert a roster of familiar tropes. Use only the actual supplied premise and the actual history the story has established.\n\nLet a telling line or detail and its interval carry actual shared or private significance. A short declarative sentence can establish the moment; a gap can let the other person's response land; a later detail can show what the moment meant. Preserve first encounters and original knowledge without invented history. A character may remember something the story actually showed, but do not retrofit a shared past that contradicts the supplied premise.\n\nKeep the crime's consequences present through the relationship. The offense, scheme, or aftermath may create pressure, exposure, divided loyalty, or practical difficulty between the characters. But do not require compulsory secrecy, betrayal, a hidden motive, a loyalty crisis, or a changed relationship status before the boundary. Full inward feeling and actual knowledge channels remain available. Wider participants and the world outside the pair continue to exist. The response may end inside continuing private or shared experience, with the relationship's consequence still unsettled.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaiau::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Gu Long / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account a sense of retelling through deliberate openings, compact declarations, and recurring phrasing. A scene may begin with a short statement that places the act in relation to what will be remembered: who did it, who saw it, who still speaks of it. Then expand into the particulars that make the retold account incomplete. A recurring designation should come from an established deed or way of speaking, not from an ornamental epithet assigned to every entrance. Return to a phrase when a later occurrence gives it a new application.\n\nPlace an actual act beside its source's remembered account. The source may be a witness, a participant, a community member, or the narrator's own memory. Keep the source's access and the narrator's access distinct. A remembered account may be accurate, partial, or mistaken. When the story needs the distinction, show what the source could actually have known and what they could not. Do not invent communal memory or a unanimous public verdict. Different people may remember the same deed differently, and the account may remain contested.\n\nKeep full private experience separate from the public selection. The retold account records what its source chooses to emphasize or what the community repeats. It is not automatically the complete live mind of the person who acted or suffered. When the story requires hidden or visible interiority, render the actual private experience under the active contract rather than replacing it with the saga's public summary. A character remembered as dangerous may have been afraid; a character remembered as loyal may have hesitated. The private experience need not correct the public account, but it must remain available where the contract permits.\n\nKeep the crime's concrete consequences present. The deed may concern an offense, a scheme, a pursuit, or an aftermath. Oaths, kinship, journeys, gifts, feuds, or public deeds can organize the account when the story establishes them, but do not import a Nordic pantheon, heroic doom, or ceremonial dialogue into every speaker. No invented diminishment, punishment, or completed verdict is required before the active boundary. The response may end with the account still being told, a source still disputing a detail, or the consequences still unfolding.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaiav::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Gu Long / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nTake the social and domestic realist route. Write the criminal circumstances through the actual management of a shared life: who opens the premises, prepares the food, minds the child, handles the money, waits for someone, or keeps a routine going after an offense has touched it. Let the crime enter as a concrete alteration in these arrangements — a missing person's chair at the table, a sum no longer available, a task done differently, a visitor received at the wrong hour — rather than as a general atmosphere of suspicion or bleakness.\n\nUse Gu Long's brief declarative movement to state the significant domestic fact plainly and then leave a gap. A character may notice the unchanged breakfast setting, the uncollected delivery, or the familiar coat still on its hook without immediately explaining what it means. The withheld explanation is not a hidden clue to be solved on schedule; it is the interval in which the arrangement continues while its meaning has already shifted. If a revelation comes, let it change the significance of an established particular — the coat belonged to someone who left, the uncollected delivery was ordered before the scheme began — so the earlier stillness acquires a new weight.\n\nKeep the viewpoint character's private experience available in the selected visible or hidden interiority: they may continue a practical routine while privately registering fear, relief, calculation, or the effort of not speaking. Their silence or cooperation does not have to produce a changed arrangement, a confession, or a moral balance. A person can help, refuse, or simply carry on, and the scene can end with the household still functioning under the new knowledge.\n\nUse the setting, language, period, and community actually supplied. If the story is specifically Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish, keep those contexts distinct rather than blending them into a generic Nordic backdrop. The realism lies in the precise allocation of time, space, work, care, and money, not in reserve, snow, isolation, or a national temperament. A criminal aftermath may be quiet and ordinary; it may also be loud, comic, or institutionally managed. Let the chosen offense and the people it touches determine the tone.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaiaw::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Gu Long / Screenplay\n\nWrite concise action blocks that place one significant visible or audible particular in its actual context: the money left on the counter, the door ajar, the phone ringing in an empty room, the sound of a car that does not pass. Use clear speaker cues and scene headings when location or time changes. An event may stay offscreen while its sound carries it. Do not explain the particular in the same block where it appears; let the image stand as a fact whose meaning is not yet available, whether or not anyone on screen registers it.\n\nUse cuts to create Gu Long's delayed revelation. A later scene may return to the same object, sound, or action under different conditions, or a character may refer to it in a way that changes what the earlier scene suggested. The cut is the gap: the audience moves from the image to its consequence without a bridging explanation. If a revelation comes, let it alter the significance of an established visible fact rather than introduce a new one — the door was not left open by accident; the phone was ringing because the caller already knew.\n\nKeep required private experience as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation, separate from the action lines and dialogue. It is not knowledge automatically available to the cast, and it is not automatically voiced. Voice-over, where a sequence genuinely needs it, is performed speech with its own audience; a character may stand at the counter and privately understand what the money means while saying something ordinary to the person beside them. The script can show the pause, the glance, or the unchanged posture without filming the thought itself.\n\nDo not fill the page with camera commands. Do not require a chase, a clue, a reaction shot, or a closed scene at the boundary. A scene may end while a character is still looking at the particular, while a conversation continues, or while an action is unfinished. The crime's consequences stay legible through what is visible and audible, and the withheld explanation belongs to the story's actual uncertainty, not to a mandatory suspense beat.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaiax::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Gu Long / Stage Play\n\nStage the criminal circumstances through actual shared-space business. Give each character a clear position, entrance, exit, or relation to an object: one person sets down a bag they will not open, another stands at the door without entering, a third moves a chair that has been empty. Let the audience see who can reach what, who watches whom, and who leaves something behind. The business is not a gesture that proves guilt; it is the observable condition in which the crime's preparation, pursuit, or aftermath becomes playable.\n\nUse Gu Long's compact exchanges by placing a direct line beside the business that gives it weight. A character may say \"You left it there\" while looking at the bag, or \"He always came through that door\" after an entrance that did not happen. The line does not explain everything; let the pause or the next piece of business carry the withheld meaning. If a revelation comes, let it change the significance of an established stage particular — the bag was not forgotten, the chair was moved by someone never seen — so the earlier stillness acquires a new meaning for the audience.\n\nKeep required private experience as attributed nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation, separate from the performed lines and stage directions. A character may privately understand what the bag contains while saying something ordinary to the person beside them; the stage directions show the pause or the unchanged posture, and the private material carries the actual feeling or reasoning. An aside or monologue, where the drama genuinely needs one, is performed speech with its own audience and purpose — not the default destination for private thought.\n\nDo not add film-camera instructions. Do not require a confession, a tableau, a new prop, or an in-world pause at the boundary. A scene may end while a character is still holding the bag, while an entrance is expected, or while a line remains unanswered. The playable facts and the actual relationships carry the crime's consequences without forcing a theatrical revelation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaiay::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Gu Long / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite through the authorized mind's actual associative movement. Let the criminal circumstance surface through what the character perceives, remembers, feels in the body, repeats to themselves, or keeps returning to after interruption: the smell of the room where the money was counted, the phrase someone used before leaving, the pressure of a door against a shoulder, the image of a hand not raised in greeting. Syntax and paragraph shape may follow these transitions, but keep enough orientation to distinguish what is happening now, what is remembered, and what is imagined or feared.\n\nUse Gu Long's distilled images as recurring particulars. A single phrase or sensation may return at different points, each time carrying a slightly different weight because the mind has moved through other material in between. The recurrence is not a clue to be solved; it is the way the mind keeps touching the same fact without yet explaining it. If a revelation comes, let it arrive as a shift in the association itself — the remembered phrase now connects to a different speaker, the bodily sensation now belongs to a different moment — so the earlier recurrence acquires a new meaning.\n\nKeep the private experience full and available. The character may reason, ruminate, feel fear or relief, or simply continue an ordinary thought while the offense, its execution, or its aftermath moves through the account. No realization, useful conclusion, or changed plan is required. Prose length alone does not determine elapsed time; actual attention, waiting, and events do. The response may end while the mind is still moving, while a memory is still forming, or while an association remains unresolved.\n\nThe technique grants no access it does not already have. It does not make every character share a mind, does not open thoughts the active contract keeps closed, and does not turn scene facts indeterminate. Other characters remain distinct, and the world's actual events stay recoverable even when this mind's account of them is partial or fallible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaiaz::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Gu Long / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the criminal circumstances through actual messages and their transmission states. Choose each message for its real part in the offense, scheme, or aftermath: the instruction sent before the act, the confirmation that arrived too late, the question left unanswered, the ordinary check-in that now reads differently. Use readable sender attribution and only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the story. A delay proves no motive; a read receipt proves no understanding.\n\nUse Gu Long's compact exchanges by letting a precise phrase or a real uptake carry the relevant relation. A sender may write \"It's done\" and receive no reply, or send a question the recipient reads but does not answer. The gap is the actual interval between sent and received, between read and understood, between asked and answered. If a revelation comes, let it change the significance of an established message — the confirmation was sent before the scheme began, the ordinary check-in was the signal — so the earlier exchange acquires a new weight.\n\nDistinguish composed, sent, delivered, read, and understood states. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not the writer's live private thought. Required private experience appears as clearly separated context outside the transcript, attributed to the character who owns it; it is not transmitted text. A character may draft a message and delete it while privately knowing what they will not say. If the story includes a deletion, an edit, or an absence, show the actual interface event or the actual gap; do not fabricate a deletion, surveillance, or confession merely to add subtext.\n\nLet each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution — length, timing, punctuation, how they argue or reassure. The response may end while a message is still undelivered, while a draft remains unsent, or while a conversation continues without a mandatory reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaiba::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Gu Long / Visual Novel\n\nOrient each scene with concise setting and readable dialogue attribution, then let a selected illustrated cue locate the actual act, account, or response. The cue may be an object on the table, a figure in the background, a door not fully closed, or a character's expression at a particular moment. It is a purposeful reference, not an executable command and not a mandatory label on every line. The cue does not prove motive, guilt, or truth; it gives the reader a concrete point of attention while the scene continues.\n\nUse Gu Long's distilled images and meaningful gaps by letting the cue appear without immediate explanation. The focal narration may register the object or expression plainly and then move on, leaving its significance to emerge later. If a revelation comes, let it change the significance of an established cue — the figure in the background was the person who left, the expression belonged to the moment before the lie — so the earlier image acquires a new weight. The shift is in what the cue means, not a puzzle mechanic layered onto the scene.\n\nGive the focal narration and inner response their warranted extent. The viewpoint character may observe the cue, infer something from it, or miss its significance entirely; keep observation and inference distinct, since what the character sees is not automatically what it means. Required private experience remains available in the selected visible or hidden interiority, separate from what other characters know. A character may privately understand what the object means while saying something ordinary to the person beside them.\n\nKeep scene changes legible. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected. The response may end while the cue is still unexplained, while a conversation continues, or while the focal character is still forming an impression.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaibb::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Gu Long / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across installments: what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover only the actual earlier act, phrase, resource, or relationship fact needed to re-enter the current account. If a later scene returns to the money that was counted, the door that was left open, or the message that was sent, briefly restate that particular rather than retelling everything that established it. The recovery is a re-entry point, not a reset.\n\nUse Gu Long's delayed revelation across installments. An earlier particular may remain unexplained for several replies — the sum whose source was never named, the visitor's hour, the name someone stopped using — and then return under conditions that change what it suggested. A person may spend money whose origin was left vague, or mention the meeting they were told never happened. The later use may confirm or qualify the earlier detail, but it cannot erase a known event or make a character forget what was established. New evidence can qualify an old explanation without replacing the old fact.\n\nKeep source claims, known events, and real consequences continuous. If a debt was repaid, a lock changed, or a person released between installments, that change belongs to the history; it cannot be omitted so a later scene falsely contradicts the first account. Full private passages need no pursuit utility: a character may privately register fear, relief, calculation, or the effort of continuing without any immediate decision changing. The installment may close on completed work, a pending request, plain acknowledgment of what is now known, or an actual conclusion.\n\nDo not require a new clue, threat, confession, punishment, or unfinished goal at every boundary. Serial duration should give selected facts room to acquire meaning, not keep established consequences suspended to stretch suspense.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadajaa::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Haruki Murakami / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the crime from inside the quiet aftermath or the strained interval before exposure. For every scene, choose an ordinary practical activity that the narrator can perform slowly and concretely—prepare a meal, wait for a train, sort papers, clean a room, walk a familiar route. Do not use the routine as a break from the emotional line; let each step of the activity become a way of delaying or measuring what the narrator can allow themselves to feel.\n\nWhile the narrator performs the activity, keep the private thought on the cost of a difficult relationship to the crime and to the people involved. The narrator should return to what they owe someone, what they cannot give back, what they have already decided to hide, or what they are afraid to admit. Do not resolve these thoughts quickly. Let the same worry surface again at a later moment, slightly changed by a new fact or a small interaction. Do not use a fixed vocabulary of suffering; keep the diction calm and concrete, so that the hurt appears through what the narrator notices, repeats, or refuses to finish.\n\nLet the physical details of the activity carry the emotional strain without turning into symbolic decoration. For example, if the narrator is making coffee, the amount of water, the delay before drinking, or a cup left untouched can quietly hold the pressure. Choose a recurring detail from the routine and let it acquire new significance through the narrator's repeated attention, but do not insist that every object must be meaningful. Some details remain simply practical.\n\nWhen the narrator speaks, keep the dialogue ordinary and largely below what they privately feel. A practical question can become an accusation without anyone saying so; a pause can matter more than an explanation. Show the strain by changing the rhythm of the exchange—an answer that arrives a moment too late, a question repeated, a sentence that stops and starts again. Do not require a breakdown or a confession. The crime's emotional consequence should remain a long private weather: present in the extended attention to the activity, in the uneven conversation, and in what the narrator continues to withhold.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadajab::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Haruki Murakami / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the scene in clear, scannable paragraphs. Give every spoken line an unmistakable speaker, and keep the action legible: who is present, what they are doing, what they can see. Begin by establishing one ordinary practical activity that belongs to the ongoing moment—making tea, checking a phone, sorting a bag, waiting near a door—and let the crime surface through that activity's surfaces rather than through an explanatory passage. Do not reveal the whole case at once; choose one concrete contradiction, absence, or item that the viewpoint character can notice and hold.\n\nKeep the Murakami profile's calm attention in the shared interval. Describe the practical activity concretely and evenly, and let any strangeness remain quiet: a detail can feel wrong without the prose becoming frantic. During dialogue, the speakers should say ordinary things that are slightly beside the point, while the criminal tension appears through the action around them—a cup set down too carefully, a phone turned over, a bag opened and then closed. If the story permits private thought for the viewpoint character, place that thought clearly in a short, separate beat and do not let it blur into speech; if the scene does not permit private thought, convey the same tension through a visible choice, such as a pause, a repeated question, or a hand that stops moving.\n\nEnd the reply at the actual scene boundary. Stop after a completed exchange, a character's decision to leave, the arrival of one new piece of information, or the viewpoint character's silent recognition of something they have not yet spoken about. Do not add an artificial question, cliffhanger, or forced choice. Leave the world and NPC behavior active so the next turn can continue directly from where the scene paused.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadajac::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Haruki Murakami / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate urban or domestic narrative route. Organize each section around a close observation of one household, street, workplace, or neighborhood space, and move the crime forward through small social surfaces: what is bought, how a room is arranged, who visits, when the lights go on, what is said at a meal. Keep the narrator's focus on the particular people and places supplied by the story; do not import a setting or cast from the style label.\n\nGive each scene one Murakami-style ordinary task—preparing food, waiting for a bus, sorting laundry, walking through a familiar market—and let the task provide the rhythm of the passage. While the narrator performs it, introduce a single social detail that carries private concern: a neighbor who stops visiting, a package no one claims, a family member who changes their schedule, a shop that closes without explanation. Do not explain the significance immediately. Let the detail sit within the routine, and let the narrator return to it later with slightly more knowledge.\n\nTreat conversation as a place where social obligation and private anxiety meet. A discussion about money, reputation, or family duty may reveal the incentives behind the crime, but keep it grounded in specific speech: who asks, who avoids answering, what is left unsaid. Let the narrator know some things and misunderstand others, and let divided loyalty appear through small repeated acts—a door left unlocked, a message not passed on, a debt remembered but not mentioned. Do not turn the passage into a lecture on social context; the commentary should emerge from the narrator's observation of particular routines and relationships.\n\nKeep the crime a real event with real consequences, and let its meaning emerge from the domestic method rather than from a detached procedural account. The ending may leave a question open, but the narrator's final observation should connect the crime to the ordinary life it has disrupted.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadajad::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Haruki Murakami / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite in hard, concrete sentences that name material facts: the weight of a bag, the smell of a stairwell, the scratch on a door, the money folded in a pocket, the bruise on a wrist. Do not smooth over transitions. Move from one action to the next with the shortest possible connecting phrase, letting the reader feel the pressure through what the narrator can see, touch, and carry.\n\nFor each scene, choose one ordinary surface that the narrator notices with Murakami's exactness—the way a cup is placed on a counter, the sound of a train passing, the dust on a windowsill, the time on a clock. Describe that surface in concrete detail, then let the criminal fact enter through it: the door that does not close fully, the cup placed too quietly, the drawer left open. The strangeness remains even; do not make the prose frantic.\n\nManage rhythm deliberately. When the narrator is reasoning, remembering, or trying to understand a sequence, use a longer sentence that holds two or three linked observations. When a decision or physical fact arrives, use a short sentence. Do not make every sentence hard and clipped; the contrast between a long reasoned moment and a short landing creates the pressure. When the narrator is waiting or checking, slow the rhythm by adding one small concrete detail before the next action.\n\nKeep speech hard and specific. A threat, refusal, or crude remark belongs to a particular speaker in a particular situation, not to the narrator's general tone. If the narrator speaks, keep the language practical. Let the crime's physical stakes stay real—money, injury, exposure, loss—and let the aftermath leave marks in the physical world. The story does not need to become survival horror; the pressure comes from the specific material world and the choices made inside it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadajae::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Haruki Murakami / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the story through documents. Each document must state its author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition at the outset. Choose the simplest form the scene actually requires—a single letter, a journal entry, a report, a receipt, a note left in a room, or a late-night message—and do not switch to another form until the information demands a different document author or audience. The sequence should move forward in time and keep the people and places legible.\n\nWithin each document, use the Murakami profile's unhurried practical attention. The writer should describe what they ate, what the weather was like, what they saw on the way home, or what they did with their hands. Do not present these details as filler. Let each concrete detail show what the writer noticed, what they chose to record, and what they could not say directly. A journal entry may mention a broken cup and not the argument that broke it; a report may list times and locations while omitting the writer's fear. Keep the language composed and specific, not hurried or confessional.\n\nMake the crime legible through what the documents know or conceal. When a document consists of ordinary observations, include one small discrepancy that becomes significant only when compared with another document later. Let a later letter answer a question the reader has not seen asked; let a receipt contradict an alibi; let a journal stop for several days and resume with a changed tone; let a report be corrected, amended, or left unfinished. The reader should reconstruct the offense and its consequences by noticing what different writers omit, alter, or mistake. Do not add an omniscient explanation after a record. The crime remains a real event, but its meaning emerges through partial, private, sometimes misleading accounts.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadajaf::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Haruki Murakami / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nKeep the crime real and present in every section. The offense, its consequences, and the people affected by it remain the story's center. Choose a particular person in the aftermath and show a small act of chosen care toward them—preparing a meal they like, brewing tea the way they prefer, walking a dog, folding laundry, buying a small gift, or sitting with them while they rest. Describe this act in unhurried concrete detail, using Murakami's practical attention: the weight of the cup, the order of the task, the sound of the room.\n\nDo not let the care erase the crime. In the same scene, hold one concrete reminder of the trouble: an unanswered message, a locked door, a missed appointment, a name nobody says, a bruise or a debt. The narrator or viewpoint character should notice this reminder during the kind act, but not resolve it. Let the comfort and the danger coexist in the pacing—the warmth may briefly slow the scene, but the crime's pressure remains underneath. The narrator may not speak directly about the offense, but its presence changes what they handle and how they pause.\n\nLet affection be sincere without requiring a confession or emotional repair. A character may show care through action rather than words, remember a familiar preference exactly, or keep someone company in silence. A relationship may remain strained even while comfort is offered. Disagreement, privacy, and fear stay intact; a kind gesture does not make everyone safe or agreeable. The reader should still understand what happened and what is at stake. End the section with the crime's pressure still in motion, even if the immediate act of care is complete.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadajag::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Haruki Murakami / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite the crime in composed novel prose with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Open each scene by establishing a concrete physical or social situation—a workplace task, a family obligation, a neighborhood errand, a scheduled meeting—and let the paragraph develop through the narrator's attention to the ordinary details of that situation. Do not rush from beat to beat; allow the prose to slow so the offense sits inside a particular social world.\n\nWithin each sustained paragraph, keep the Murakami profile's unhurried description of practical activity. The narrator may describe a commute, a meal, a household task, or a familiar street while the criminal facts accumulate. Choose one ordinary object or routine to examine slowly—a ledger entry, a scheduled meeting, a room that has been rearranged, a person who no longer appears at the usual time—and connect it to the narrator's private concern. The strangeness should feel even and psychologically unsettled rather than sensational. Do not turn the detail into a symbol; let it hold its practical function while the narrator's repeated attention gives it weight.\n\nUse narrative distance to frame the crime through social context, history, or judgment where the chosen narrator permits it. The narrator may reflect on how a neighborhood has changed, what a family once expected, what a job required, or what a community chose not to notice. These reflections must arise from the story's actual people and places, not from generic social commentary. Distinguish narration from ordinary spoken language: the prose can be more composed than dialogue, with fuller sentences and a wider view of consequence, but it should not become uniformly elaborate or archaic. End the scene with a shift in understanding or circumstance that keeps the crime's human meaning legible without forcing punishment, moral clarity, or a tidy resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadajah::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Haruki Murakami / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the crime as an account being given to a particular listener at a particular time: someone waiting for a contact, keeping a companion occupied, explaining why money or a person is missing, or trying to make another person act or refrain from acting. Let the telling itself be part of the offense or its aftermath, not a neutral report delivered after the facts are settled.\n\nOpen the outer scene with the teller's ordinary present activity and the listener's reason for staying. Keep the frame's physical details exact and unhurried: the condition of a room, the preparation of a drink, the sound of traffic, the length of a pause. Introduce the embedded account as a deliberate turn in that present situation, and mark the shift clearly so the reader always knows which level is being narrated.\n\nInside the embedded story, follow the crime's concrete sequence from the teller's viewpoint: the arrangement, the errand or meeting, the money or object involved, who was present, what went wrong or was planned to look wrong. Use Murakami's calm, evenly weighted attention for disturbing material; describe a wound, a threat, or a suspicious absence in the same plain register as a meal or a train schedule, and let the unease gather through what the teller notices but does not explain.\n\nMake the teller's selection and emphasis part of the characterization. The account may be sincere but incomplete, accurate about events while misleading about motive, or shaped to flatter, frighten, or reassure the listener. Do not announce the teller's reliability. Instead, let the listener interrupt with a practical question about timing, money, access, or who else knew, and let the teller answer, evade, or return to the story.\n\nUse one or two repeated phrases, objects, or gestures across the frame and the embedded tale: a coin turned over, a phone that does not ring, a phrase about waiting, a door left unlocked. When the repetition returns, let it carry a changed meaning for the listener or the reader without explaining the change. The recurrence should connect the narrative levels, not prove the story true.\n\nAllow the embedded account to affect the outer situation. The listener may decide to leave, make a call, hand over money, or ask a question that changes what the teller says next. The teller may be using the story to delay a departure, test an ally, or prepare a confession. Keep the crime's consequences concrete, but do not require the frame to reach arrest, punishment, or full exposure. The response may end while the telling is still underway, with the listener's attention or suspicion altered and the practical situation unresolved.\n\nPreserve the difference between what the teller knew at the time of the events and what the teller knows now. A later discovery may color the account, but it cannot become the earlier motive. Let the embedded story include the teller's private experience during the crime: the calculation before a choice, the physical sensation of waiting, the moment of recognizing that something had gone wrong. The outer frame may show the teller's present interiority separately, including the decision to begin, pause, or alter the story.\n\nDo not add djinn, magical bargains, or a royal court. Any strangeness remains psychological and situational: an improbable coincidence, a person who behaves as if following a private rule, an object that seems to have arrived from another life. Treat such strangeness as part of the ordinary world, available to the teller's memory and the listener's doubt, never as a supernatural explanation for the crime.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadajai::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Haruki Murakami / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the documentary and historical-memory route. Write the crime as an event that has already left traces: a file, a ledger entry, a photograph, a newspaper notice, a recorded interview, a building that still stands, a person who remembers the day differently. Let the narration move between these traces and the present scene in which someone examines, recalls, or returns to them.\n\nChoose a viewpoint character with a practical reason to reconstruct the event: a relative settling an estate, a former colleague asked to confirm a detail, a journalist or researcher checking an old claim, a neighbor who kept a document. Keep the crime's basic facts stable and recoverable. What remains uncertain is motive, sequence, responsibility, or the meaning of a particular act, not whether the act occurred.\n\nLet documents carry their own limits. A record may be exact about a time or sum and silent about a threat. A photograph may show who was present but not what they understood. A testimony may be vivid and mistaken. When the narrator compares a document with a memory, show the discrepancy concretely: the remembered room is larger, the date in the file cannot be right, the person in the photograph looks less afraid than the narrator expected.\n\nUse Murakami's calm, unhurried attention for the present-day scenes. Describe the physical handling of evidence: the weight of a folder, the smell of old paper, the sound of a cassette or the pause before a recorded voice. Let ordinary activities surround the reconstruction: making coffee, waiting for a bus, walking through a neighborhood that has changed. These routines do not delay the investigation; they give the narrator's private attention a place to work.\n\nInclude the narrator's interiority during the reconstruction. The narrator may notice a connection while doing something unrelated, feel reluctance before opening a particular envelope, or recognize that a familiar story no longer fits the record. Do not turn every memory into a revelation. Some recollections remain doubtful, and the narrator may accept that a question cannot be settled.\n\nLet the crime's human aftermath appear through specific people rather than summary. A victim's family may want the matter closed, an old suspect may have built an ordinary life, an official may defend a decision made under pressure. Show divided loyalty and exposure through what people will say now, what they will sign, and what they ask the narrator not to repeat. The narrator may protect someone, misread a silence, or discover that cooperation has a price.\n\nKeep the documentary method in the prose without making the story read like a case summary. Let records appear as encountered objects and quoted passages, not as headings or bullet points. When the narrator draws an inference, show the step from observation to conclusion: the same address appears twice, the receipt is dated after the witness left town, the key fits a door that was said to be locked. Preserve the difference between what a document establishes and what it suggests.\n\nDo not impose a national temperament, bureaucratic severity, or translated word order. The German-language tradition supplies the documentary and memory route, not a setting. The crime may occur anywhere the supplied story places it. Any strangeness remains psychological and historical: an unexplained gap in the files, a witness who repeats a phrase as if it were a charm, a building that seems to have outlived the people who knew its secret. Treat such strangeness as part of the real world, never as a supernatural solution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadajaj::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Haruki Murakami / Gothic-Punk\n\nWrite the crime as an encounter between an old arrangement and present city life. The old arrangement may be a family obligation, a building with a history, a debt passed down, a business that operates by rules no one now explains, or a person whose authority depends on being remembered as powerful. The present life is concrete and ordinary: rent, work, transit, a relationship, a phone that needs answering.\n\nOpen in a specific urban place and show its material condition exactly. Describe the worn stairway, the security light that stays on, the smell of cooking from another apartment, the view from a window, the way a locked door sounds. Let the place carry the old arrangement without announcing it. The crime grows from someone trying to live, leave, collect, protect, or survive inside that arrangement.\n\nUse Murakami's calm, unhurried attention for disturbing material. A threat may be spoken in a quiet voice while someone pours tea. A body or injury may be described in the same plain register as a broken appliance or a missed train. Keep the narrator's focus on small physical details: the position of a hand, the time on a clock, the weight of a bag, the pause before a lie. Let the unease gather through what is noticed and not remarked upon.\n\nMake intimacy morally complicated rather than purely romantic or purely predatory. Characters may share a bed, a meal, a secret, or a childhood memory while also owing each other money, silence, or access to a crime. Show private desire alongside practical calculation. A character may touch someone gently and still intend to search their coat. A character may confess a fear and still withhold the fact that matters. Do not make every caring act fraudulent; some tenderness is real and still cannot undo the offense.\n\nLet social masks fall away in trusted company, but keep the trust specific. Two people who have known each other for years may speak more plainly in a back room, a parked car, or a kitchen after midnight. The unmasked speech may reveal a plan, a resentment, or a need, but it does not have to produce a confession or a reversal. The mask may also return when a third person enters or a phone rings.\n\nInclude the viewpoint character's interiority during preparation and aftermath. Show the calculation before a choice: what the character expects to gain, what they are willing to risk, what they will not say. Show the physical sensation of waiting and the moment of recognizing that a situation has changed. Keep access limited to the chosen viewpoint; other characters' motives appear through what they do, say, and leave behind.\n\nLet the inherited authority have practical consequences. It may control a building, a job, a debt, a route, or a piece of information. Show how that control works in the present: who has a key, who must be called, whose name still matters, what happens when the old rule is ignored. The crime may be an attempt to escape that control, enforce it, or profit from it. Do not make the authority omnipotent or supernatural; it is powerful because specific people depend on it and specific arrangements still function.\n\nDo not import Kindred physiology, factions, powers, blood bonds, or Humanity numbers. The gothic-punk treatment is atmosphere and social structure, not setting material. Any strangeness remains psychological and situational: a room that feels watched, a person who speaks as if following a private ritual, a building that seems to remember its former use. Treat such strangeness as part of the ordinary world, available to the characters' attention and doubt, never as an explanation for the crime.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadajak::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Haruki Murakami / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and domestic realism route. Write the crime as an act that arises inside a specific household, workplace, neighborhood, or community network, where people know each other's habits and depend on each other's cooperation. Keep the setting's language, region, community, and period specific when the story supplies them, and do not invent a generic Indian family structure or religious obligation.\n\nOpen with an ordinary domestic or work routine and let the crime's pressure enter through small disturbances: a payment that cannot be made, a relative who arrives without notice, a shop that stays open later, a phone call that changes the evening's plan. Describe the routine exactly: the preparation of food, the arrangement of a room, the timing of a bus or train, the shared work of closing a shop or caring for a child. These details are not background; they show what the crime will cost and who will notice.\n\nMake the offense concrete and motivated by the character's actual situation. The viewpoint character may steal from an employer, falsify a record, hide a relative, accept a bribe, break a contract, or help someone escape. Show the incentive as a specific need or loyalty: a debt, a marriage, a medical expense, a promise to a sibling, a fear of exposure. Do not reduce the character to a type; let the choice be deliberate and uneasy, with consequences the character can partly foresee.\n\nUse Murakami's calm, even attention for moments of high tension. When a confrontation occurs, describe the room, the position of the people, the sound of a fan or traffic, the exact words spoken and the pause after them. Let the narrator notice ordinary details during the crisis: a cup left on the floor, a child's voice from another room, the smell of rain. The strangeness of the moment comes from the contrast between the familiar setting and the act taking place inside it.\n\nShow the community as a network of specific relationships rather than a chorus. Neighbors may know part of the truth, a relative may suspect but not ask, an employer may choose to overlook a small theft because of an old obligation. Divided loyalty is practical: someone may help the culprit and still report them, protect a family member and still demand repayment, or remain silent and still change the locks. Let cooperation and refusal both appear.\n\nInclude the viewpoint character's interiority during preparation and aftermath. Show the calculation before the act: what the character tells themselves, what they will not admit, what they hope to avoid. Show the physical sensation of waiting and the moment of recognizing that someone has noticed. Keep the character's private experience distinct from what they say to others; they may reassure a parent while knowing the money is gone, or accept blame calmly while planning to leave.\n\nLet the aftermath unfold through domestic and economic consequences. A household may lose its income, a shop may close, a marriage may continue under a new silence, a relative may move away. The crime need not end in punishment or full exposure. The response may end with the character returning to an ordinary task while the consequences remain unfinished: washing dishes, waiting for a call, locking a door, sitting down to a meal that no one speaks during.\n\nDo not force English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure onto the scene. If the supplied story gives a language or community, let it shape names, food, forms of address, and practical arrangements. Any strangeness remains psychological and social: a person who behaves as if an old promise still binds them, a room that feels different after an argument, a coincidence that seems to confirm a private fear. Treat such strangeness as part of ordinary life, never as a supernatural explanation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadajal::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Haruki Murakami / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and psychological realism route. Write the crime as an act that arises inside a specific workplace, household, or neighborhood, where people know each other's habits and depend on each other's cooperation. Keep the setting's region, season, and community specific when the story supplies them, and do not invent a generic Japanese landscape or a mandatory inventory of seasonal scenery.\n\nOpen with an ordinary routine and let the crime's pressure enter through small disturbances: a shift changed at the last minute, a payment that cannot be made, a colleague who asks an unusual favor, a neighbor who notices too much. Describe the routine exactly: the preparation of food, the arrangement of a room, the timing of a train or bus, the shared work of closing a shop or office. These details are not background; they show what the crime will cost and who will notice.\n\nMake the offense concrete and motivated by the character's actual situation. The viewpoint character may steal from an employer, falsify a record, hide a colleague's mistake, accept a bribe, break a contract, or help someone escape. Show the incentive as a specific need or loyalty: a debt, a family obligation, a medical expense, a promise to a coworker, a fear of exposure. Do not reduce the character to a type; let the choice be deliberate and uneasy, with consequences the character can partly foresee.\n\nUse Murakami's calm, even attention for moments of high tension. When a confrontation occurs, describe the room, the position of the people, the sound of a fan or traffic, the exact words spoken and the pause after them. Let the narrator notice ordinary details during the crisis: a cup left on the counter, a phone vibrating, the smell of rain. The strangeness of the moment comes from the contrast between the familiar setting and the act taking place inside it.\n\nShow the workplace or neighborhood as a network of specific relationships rather than a chorus. Colleagues may know part of the truth, a neighbor may suspect but not ask, a supervisor may choose to overlook a small theft because of an old obligation. Divided loyalty is practical: someone may help the culprit and still report them, protect a coworker and still demand repayment, or remain silent and still change the locks. Let cooperation and refusal both appear.\n\nInclude the viewpoint character's interiority during preparation and aftermath. Show the calculation before the act: what the character tells themselves, what they will not admit, what they hope to avoid. Show the physical sensation of waiting and the moment of recognizing that someone has noticed. Keep the character's private experience distinct from what they say to others; they may reassure a parent while knowing the money is gone, or accept blame calmly while planning to leave.\n\nLet the aftermath unfold through work and domestic consequences. A job may be lost, a shop may close, a marriage may continue under a new silence, a colleague may move away. The crime need not end in punishment or full exposure. The response may end with the character returning to an ordinary task while the consequences remain unfinished: washing dishes, waiting for a call, locking a door, sitting down to a meal that no one speaks during.\n\nDo not force a universal quietness, seasonal inventory, or a generic Japanese temperament onto the scene. If the supplied story gives a region or season, let it shape names, food, forms of address, and practical arrangements. Any strangeness remains psychological and social: a person who behaves as if an old promise still binds them, a room that feels different after an argument, a coincidence that seems to confirm a private fear. Treat such strangeness as part of ordinary life, never as a supernatural explanation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadajam::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Haruki Murakami / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate psychological and urban fiction route. Write the crime as an act that arises inside a specific city, neighborhood, household, or workplace, where people know each other's habits and depend on each other's cooperation. Keep the setting's language, region, community, and period specific when the story supplies them, and do not invent a generic Latin American landscape or a magical-realist default.\n\nOpen with an ordinary routine and let the crime's pressure enter through small disturbances: a rent that cannot be paid, a neighbor who arrives without notice, a shop that stays open later, a phone call that changes the evening's plan. Describe the routine exactly: the preparation of food, the arrangement of a room, the timing of a bus or train, the shared work of closing a shop or office. These details are not background; they show what the crime will cost and who will notice.\n\nMake the offense concrete and motivated by the character's actual situation. The viewpoint character may steal from an employer, falsify a record, hide a relative, accept a bribe, break a contract, or help someone escape. Show the incentive as a specific need or loyalty: a debt, a family obligation, a medical expense, a promise to a sibling, a fear of exposure. Do not reduce the character to a type; let the choice be deliberate and uneasy, with consequences the character can partly foresee.\n\nUse Murakami's calm, even attention for moments of high tension. When a confrontation occurs, describe the room, the position of the people, the sound of a fan or traffic, the exact words spoken and the pause after them. Let the narrator notice ordinary details during the crisis: a cup left on the counter, a child's voice from another room, the smell of rain. The strangeness of the moment comes from the contrast between the familiar setting and the act taking place inside it.\n\nShow the city and neighborhood as a network of specific relationships rather than a chorus. Neighbors may know part of the truth, a relative may suspect but not ask, an employer may choose to overlook a small theft because of an old obligation. Divided loyalty is practical: someone may help the culprit and still report them, protect a family member and still demand repayment, or remain silent and still change the locks. Let cooperation and refusal both appear.\n\nInclude the viewpoint character's interiority during preparation and aftermath. Show the calculation before the act: what the character tells themselves, what they will not admit, what they hope to avoid. Show the physical sensation of waiting and the moment of recognizing that someone has noticed. Keep the character's private experience distinct from what they say to others; they may reassure a parent while knowing the money is gone, or accept blame calmly while planning to leave.\n\nLet the aftermath unfold through urban and domestic consequences. A household may lose its income, a shop may close, a marriage may continue under a new silence, a relative may move away. The crime need not end in punishment or full exposure. The response may end with the character returning to an ordinary task while the consequences remain unfinished: washing dishes, waiting for a call, locking a door, sitting down to a meal that no one speaks during.\n\nDo not force magical realism, code-switching, religion, or a generic Latin American temperament onto the scene. If the supplied story gives a language or community, let it shape names, food, forms of address, and practical arrangements. Any strangeness remains psychological and social: a person who behaves as if an old promise still binds them, a room that feels different after an argument, a coincidence that seems to confirm a private fear. Treat such strangeness as part of ordinary life, never as a supernatural explanation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
        },
        {
            "role": "system",
            "injection_position": 0,
            "injection_depth": 4,
            "system_prompt": false,
            "marker": false,
            "enabled": true,
            "forbid_overrides": false,
            "injection_order": 100,
            "injection_trigger": [],
            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-crime-03",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Crime · 03/07",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == crime}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Crime. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 3 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-crime; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPadajan::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Haruki Murakami / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Open with the viewpoint character in a specific place and time, already involved in the crime's preparation or aftermath: waiting in a car, checking a bag, walking toward a meeting, standing in a room where something has just happened. Give the reader the essential spatial facts in the first few lines: where the character is, what they are doing, and what they are waiting for or trying to avoid.\n\nKeep spatial changes easy to follow. When the character moves from one room to another, from a street to a building, or from a public place to a private one, mark the transition with a concrete detail: a door, a stairway, a change in light or sound. Do not let the reader lose track of where the character is or who else is present.\n\nUse readable paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response. Give each beat its own space: a short paragraph for a physical action, a separate block for speech, and a separate block for the viewpoint character's reaction. Keep the prose fast and clear, but do not reduce every paragraph to a single sentence. Let the breaks create rhythm without fragmenting the scene.\n\nUse Murakami's calm, even attention for exact surfaces. Describe the weight of a bag, the time on a phone, the position of a hand, the sound of a door closing, the smell of rain or coffee. Let these details carry the tension. A threat may be spoken in a quiet voice while someone checks a message. A body or injury may be described in the same plain register as a broken appliance or a missed train.\n\nMake the crime concrete and motivated. The viewpoint character may be preparing to steal, deliver, hide, or destroy something; they may be meeting an accomplice, avoiding a pursuer, or returning to a place where an offense occurred. Show the incentive through specific actions and dialogue: what the character needs, what they are willing to risk, what they will not say. Do not summarize the crime in exposition; let it emerge through the scene.\n\nInclude focused reaction beats after significant actions or dialogue. The viewpoint character may notice a detail, feel a physical sensation, make a calculation, or recognize that a situation has changed. Keep the reaction specific and owned by the character: a tightening in the chest, a decision to leave, a memory that surfaces, a question they decide not to ask. The reaction may be serious, uncertain, angry, or calm according to the character's actual state.\n\nLet the scene end at a natural continuation point without requiring a cliffhanger. The response may stop while the character is still waiting, still walking, still deciding, or still in the middle of a conversation. The crime's consequences may remain unfinished. Do not force a reversal, confession, or completed action at the end of every response.\n\nDo not require first person, comedy, a young cast, or a closing cliffhanger. The light novel container supplies fast-readable staging and focused reactions, not a particular tone or demographic. Any strangeness remains psychological and situational: a person who behaves as if following a private rule, a room that feels different after an argument, a coincidence that seems to confirm a private fear. Treat such strangeness as part of the ordinary world, never as a supernatural explanation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadajao::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Haruki Murakami / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet the rhythm of a paragraph follow the way attention moves through a scene: from an exact practical surface to the association it raises, then back to a consequential detail of the offense or its aftermath. A comparison begins in something actually present—a receipt left on a counter, the smell of cleaning fluid, the way a door closes against its frame—and unfolds only when it changes what the narrator understands or makes a private concern more present. Keep the crime's causal chain legible inside the imagery. If a figure of speech suggests concealment, pressure, or exposure, let a later concrete fact show what that perception means rather than letting the comparison stand in for evidence.\n\nUse recurring sounds and images as an organizing device, not as decoration. A repeated image returns when a later occurrence gives it new significance: the same train announcement heard before a meeting and after a confession, the same color noticed on a coat and then on a document. The repetition deepens the reader's sense of how the viewpoint character is processing events, not signal that the image itself is a clue. Plain statements interrupt the lyrical movement at moments of practical consequence, so that an arrest, a payment, a lie, or a piece of damage remains direct and unmistakable.\n\nPreserve the narrator's conversational distance even when the prose grows associative. The teller may notice that an ordinary object seems to hold more meaning than it should, but the narration does not claim that the object is magical or that the world has become unstable. Keep uncertainty available without making every scene fact ambiguous. A character can feel that a room has changed after a crime without the prose asserting that the room literally changed. Let the account end where the selected scene ends, with the crime's consequences still developing rather than resolved into a lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadajap::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Haruki Murakami / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the crime's aftermath supply the specific irreversible changes the narration reflects on. A character may notice that a familiar routine continues after an offense but no longer means what it did: the same shop is open, the same train arrives, the same person answers the phone, yet the attention now moves toward what has become unavailable. Keep the reflection anchored to the actual consequences of the crime—a missing person, a broken arrangement, a debt, a silence, a place that can no longer be entered without remembering. Do not announce that everything passes or that loss is universal. Let one particular change carry the feeling.\n\nUse remembered occasions as a way to show how the present experience coexists with what is gone. A character may recall a conversation while performing an ordinary task, or recognize that a familiar object now belongs to a different time. The memory should be specific enough to matter: who said what, where the light fell, what was left unfinished. Let ordinary pleasure remain available. A meal can still be good, a walk can still be pleasant, a joke can still land, even while the character carries an unresolved attachment or an unhealed consequence.\n\nPreserve Murakami's conversational distance. The narrator may reflect directly, but the reflection should sound like a person noticing something rather than a voice delivering an elegy. Keep the account unhurried without drifting away from the crime. When a practical matter intervenes—a call, a payment, a question from someone involved—let it interrupt the reflective movement naturally. The scene can end with the character still inside the aftermath, neither healed nor destroyed, attending to the next small thing.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadajaq::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Haruki Murakami / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse framed oral storytelling as the concrete regional route. Establish a situated teller and a listener or audience whose presence shapes the account. The teller may be a bystander, an investigator, a neighbor, or someone who knew the people involved, but the telling remains a composed account delivered in a particular time and place, not an omniscient chronicle. Let the frame return at intervals: the listener asks a question, the teller pauses to make tea, a noise from the street interrupts, or the teller admits uncertainty about a detail. These returns keep the crime's account grounded in the relationship between teller and listener.\n\nPreserve Murakami's unhurried practical attention inside the telling. The teller describes exact surfaces—a doorway, a receipt, a telephone, the way someone folded a coat—and lets ordinary activities carry the narrative forward. The crime emerges through preparation, incentives, relationships, execution, evidence, or aftermath as the teller relates them. Suspense can come from what the teller chooses to delay, repeat, or qualify, even when the listener already knows the outcome. When the teller repeats a phrase or returns to an image, the repetition marks a shift in understanding or emphasis, not ornament.\n\nKeep the regional selection distinct without importing a new setting, religion, honor code, or mystical apparatus. The supplied world and cast remain unchanged. The framed telling may include reflective or philosophical passages, but they arise from the teller's actual situation and the crime's particular consequences. Let the teller distinguish what they saw from what they heard, and what they know from what they suppose. The account can end with the frame still open—the listener preparing to leave, the teller returning to a task—rather than closing with a moral or a completed judgment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadajar::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Haruki Murakami / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect a small number of exact details and let them carry the scene's weight. One detail can stand for a larger condition: a cup left unwashed, a phone number written twice, a door that no longer locks. Choose details that belong to the crime's preparation, execution, or aftermath, and let them remain concrete rather than explained. The narrator's private concern appears through what gets noticed and what gets omitted, not through a paragraph of interpretation. When an inward connection is essential, state it in a direct clause and stop.\n\nKeep clauses short and movement economical, but do not delete causality. The reader should be able to infer why a person acts, what a piece of evidence implies, or how a relationship has changed. If a step in the crime's logic would otherwise disappear, include the necessary fact or action in plain terms. Omission leaves something inferable, not a gap where motivation or consequence should be. A precise extended sentence is acceptable when it serves the account better than several fragmented ones.\n\nPreserve Murakami's even tone and conversational distance without drifting into associative expansion. Strangeness is stated evenly, as part of the scene, not amplified. Let dialogue remain individual and economical. The account can end after a single consequential action or observation, with the next development left unstated but inferable from what has been established.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadajas::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Haruki Murakami / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose in which the crime's movement is carried by action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience. Open a scene in a concrete present moment—a person arriving somewhere, a task being done, a conversation beginning—and let the narrative move naturally among what the viewpoint character sees, says, remembers, and privately thinks. Keep transitions unobtrusive. A recollection can arise from a present detail, and a return to the scene can be marked by a new action or a shift in attention rather than a summary.\n\nPreserve Murakami's unhurried practical activities and conversational distance within the novelistic flow. Let ordinary tasks occupy real narrative space: preparing food, waiting for a train, sorting papers, walking to a meeting. These activities hold the reader's attention while the crime's incentives, relationships, and consequences develop around them. Strangeness is described evenly, as part of the scene's texture, without the narration insisting on its meaning. The viewpoint character may associate an ordinary object with a private concern, but the prose does not stop to explain the association unless the explanation contributes to the present account.\n\nLet paragraphing orient the reader without recap machinery. A new paragraph marks a shift in speaker, a change in location, a movement from perception to thought, or a development in the crime's progress. Do not restate what the reader already knows. The register and rhythm follow the narrator's voice and the immediate scene, not a fixed template. The scene can end at a natural boundary—a conversation concluded, a task interrupted, a decision deferred—without requiring a lesson, a reversal, or a completed development.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadajat::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Haruki Murakami / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship premise the organizing lens for the crime story. Select scenes that show how the offense, scheme, investigation, or aftermath touches the relationship: a shared errand becomes implicated, a private joke resurfaces under pressure, a moment of comfort follows a disclosure, a divided loyalty makes an ordinary conversation difficult. Let the relationship's particular promise shape what gets attention. If the premise is built on trust, show what trust costs under suspicion; if it is built on rivalry, show how the crime changes the terms of competition; if it is built on care, show care continuing even when circumstances grow dangerous. Do not insert a roster of familiar tropes. Use only the actual supplied premise.\n\nGive private language and repeated moments room to work. A phrase the characters use with each other can return at a consequential moment and mean something new. A small ritual—a way of greeting, a shared meal, a particular route walked together—becomes the site where the crime's pressure becomes visible. Let affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery occupy real narrative space rather than being summarized. Preserve Murakami's conversational distance: the emotional payoff emerges through exact surfaces and restrained dialogue, not through characters announcing their feelings in polished speeches. The narrator may observe that something between them has changed, but the change is shown in what they do and say.\n\nKeep the crime's causal chain intact. The relationship is not a substitute for evidence, motive, or consequence. Suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did it. Let the scene end at a natural relationship boundary—a reassurance given, a question left unanswered, a door closed, a hand withdrawn—without requiring a confession, a reconciliation, or a completed emotional arc.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadajau::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Haruki Murakami / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the crime account the sense of a retelling. Open with a deliberate declaration that establishes the offense or its consequence as something people will remember: who did what, who was there, what changed afterward. Then expand into the particulars that make the declaration incomplete. The telling relates preparation, incentives, relationships, execution, evidence, pursuit, and aftermath as deeds whose consequences accumulate over time. A compact declaration can introduce a person or an act, but it is grounded in what actually happened, not inflated into legend.\n\nUse recurring phrasing to mark the account's shape. A phrase returns when a later occurrence gives it a new application: the same description of a door, a debt, a silence, or a promise, repeated after the crime has changed what it means. Let the recurrence deepen the reader's sense of consequence rather than decorate every entrance. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective. The account may relate what people said and did, but it does not claim to know every private mind unless the perspective permits it.\n\nPreserve Murakami's unhurried practical attention and conversational distance. The chronicle remains close to ordinary surfaces: who lent the thing, who repaired it, who waited, who answered the phone. Strangeness is stated evenly. Let dialogue interrupt the authority of the retelling when a participant objects to a detail or answers an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern. The account can end with a deed concluded or resumed, leaving another consequence available to be remembered rather than declaring the crime a completed legend.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadajav::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Haruki Murakami / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the narrative as a social-realist account of an offense and its consequences, anchored in a concrete Scandinavian setting. Choose the specific institution and domestic world the story inhabits: a municipal housing office and its waiting-room routines; a workplace union representative navigating sick-leave rules; a small-town police station handling a reported theft; a parent coordinator at a day-care center; a social worker visiting a family after a complaint. Keep the setting definite, but do not assume snow, isolation, or silence.\n\nUse a single focal viewpoint from the crime's orbit: an investigator, a neighbor, a relative, a victim, or someone implicated. Let that character's practical tasks continue around the offense. Show how they make coffee, wait for a bus, complete a form, lock a door, stack boxes, or prepare a meal. Describe these actions with Murakami's calm exactness, allowing ordinary domestic and bureaucratic detail to carry feeling and implication without commentary.\n\nLet the crime appear through the social fabric: a missing item, a report to the authorities, a changed arrangement, a request for help, a delayed response. Show how the offense alters relationships and obligations, and how institutions respond in concrete ways—an interview, a waiting period, a maintenance notice, a workplace consequence. Do not force a mystery puzzle, a confession, or a punishment. The resolution may remain incomplete; the crime is a disturbance whose human aftermath is the subject.\n\nKeep private concern available in the selected interiority. A character may notice an implication without acting on it, or act from a motive that remains partly obscure. Let psychological strangeness be a feature of realistic attention: a familiar hallway becomes unfamiliar because of what is now known, not because reality has changed. The narration should remain even and unhurried while the crime and its social consequences develop.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadajaw::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Haruki Murakami / Screenplay\n\nWrite in screenplay form: scene headings when location or time changes, concise action lines that describe only visible and audible events, and clear speaker cues. Keep action lines concrete and selective. Describe a person waiting at a station, folding laundry, checking a lock, pouring coffee, or sliding a form across a desk. Use the ordinary activity to hold the scene without explaining emotion. Let small delays and repeated actions carry pressure: a character checks a phone twice, looks at a door, picks up a key and sets it down, or opens a fridge and closes it without taking anything.\n\nCompose dialogue that stays on practical surfaces. Characters discuss bus schedules, a borrowed coat, a broken latch, a work shift, or an errand. Let them speak about one thing while the offense remains present through what they do not say. Do not force every exchange toward revelation. A simple line about a locked door or an unreturned call may be enough. Use pauses and silences as part of the screenplay's action lines or stage directions.\n\nTreat the crime as a disturbance in ordinary life. Show preparation, a visit, a discovery, a delayed reaction, or an institutional consequence through visible behavior and audible sound. A report may be filed offscreen; a conversation may continue after a cut. Suspense can come from what a character does not say, from divided loyalty, or from the gap between what the audience sees and what a character knows. Do not require a culprit to be exposed or punished.\n\nPreserve private concern as clearly attributed nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation. This may be a separate block labeled with the character's name, not dialogue another character can hear. Use it to show the interior judgment, memory, or unease that cannot be conveyed by the visible action. Voice-over is permitted only when the chosen sequence needs it and the active contract authorizes it; do not substitute voice-over for the nonspoken block. Use cuts and offscreen sound selectively to mark changes or extend a scene. End a scene on a completed action, a held look, or an ordinary continuation, never on a forced cliffhanger or a summary of the crime's meaning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadajax::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Haruki Murakami / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable script with clear speaker names, entrances, exits, and economical stage directions. Use the stage's continuous present: a character may be engaged in an ordinary task while another speaks, and the audience can see both the task and the reaction. Stage hands, props, and blocking should carry meaning. For example, a character folds laundry while another asks about a missing key; a third enters from the street and sets down a bag; someone checks a phone during a pause. These activities should be precise, unhurried, and never merely filler.\n\nCompose dialogue that stays on practical surfaces and Murakami's conversational distance. People talk about schedules, errands, a borrowed coat, a work shift, or a broken latch. They may speak around the crime: ask a question that seems small, answer briefly, or let a pause extend. Silences and physical business can do more than explanation. Avoid forcing every scene toward confession or revelation. Suspense may come from who is present, who leaves, or what object changes hands.\n\nLet the crime enter through the domestic and social world. A police visit, a missing item, a changed routine, a request for help, or a piece of news may be reported without being shown. The audience may know more than the characters, or the characters may know something the audience only suspects. Show consequences in altered relationships and obligations rather than in a single explanatory speech.\n\nPreserve required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in its active presentation, clearly separated from spoken lines and stage directions. This material is not a monologue delivered to the audience; it is the character's private thought or feeling that the reader of the script can see, but the other characters cannot. Use monologues and asides only when they have a dramatic purpose and the active contract authorizes them, never as the default container for interiority. End a scene on a completed action, an entrance, a pause, or an ordinary continuation, without requiring closure.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadajay::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Haruki Murakami / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite from a single authorized mind, using the character's associations to shape the telling. Move between present perception, memory, bodily sensation, and private words as attention shifts. A smell, a sound, a familiar object, or a repeated phrase can open a memory connected to the crime without the narration announcing its relevance. Do not organize the piece as a linear report; let syntax and paragraph shape follow the mind's movement while enough orientation remains to distinguish events from recollection or fantasy.\n\nApply Murakami's calm exactness to ordinary things that pass through the mind: the weight of a bag, the sound of a kettle, the feel of a coat, the time on a station clock, the number of steps to a door. Let these details recur and accumulate private meaning. Strangeness should appear evenly as one more noticed thing, not as a sudden shift in the world. The character may be aware of their own distraction or unease, but the narration should not explain it.\n\nKeep the crime present as a concern the mind returns to. Let the character rehearse what happened, imagine a conversation, remember a face, or notice a contradiction without resolving it. Distinguish clearly between what is happening now, what is remembered, and what is imagined. The reader should be able to follow what the character actually did, saw, or learned. When a memory or observation becomes a clue, let it emerge through the association rather than being labeled as a clue.\n\nAllow uncertainty to remain unresolved. The mind may circle a question without answering it. Suspense can come from what the character is trying not to think about, or from a memory that keeps returning under different conditions. The response may end while the mind is still moving. Do not require a confession, a completed investigation, or a changed plan. The crime's aftermath is felt in the mind's disturbance, not in a forced conclusion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadajaz::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Haruki Murakami / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the story through readable sender-attributed messages. Make each person's digital habits concrete: one writes short practical lines and replies immediately; another delays for hours, then sends a brief answer; a third asks a question and then corrects it. Use timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes only when they matter to the situation. The interface itself can be part of the crime's texture: a message left on read, a deleted draft, a forwarded screenshot, a group chat that goes quiet.\n\nKeep the messages unhurried and ordinary in Murakami's manner. People discuss schedules, errands, a borrowed item, a work problem, what to buy for dinner. The crime enters through what is asked, avoided, delayed, or left unsent. A message about a door, a train, or a shopping list may carry more weight than an accusation. Do not push characters to confess or demand answers; let the exchange remain as mundane as possible while the underlying offense changes its meaning.\n\nDistinguish clearly between a sent message, an unsent draft, and private thought. An unsent draft is a composed artifact that the writer chose not to send, not a transparent window into their live mind. Required interiority remains separate from the transcript and clearly attributed in the active presentation, as a narrative block or labeled note. This private content can reveal what a character knows, fears, or intends, while the other characters see only the messages.\n\nLet suspense come from exposure, divided loyalty, or the difference between what someone writes and what they do. A person may answer promptly and say nothing, or delay and say too much. The reader may also see narrative context outside the exchange when required, clearly separated from the transcript; this context can describe what a character is doing or feeling while they type, but should not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext. The response may end on a sent message, a pending reply, or an unsent draft without requiring a resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadajba::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Haruki Murakami / Visual Novel\n\nWrite in concise scene-oriented passages with clear dialogue attribution and focal narration. Set each scene through a few exact details: a kitchen counter, a station platform, an office desk, a hallway light. Let these ordinary surfaces carry Murakami's calm attention. Keep scene changes legible, with a brief background note when the location shifts. The visual-novel format should feel like a sequence of still frames, each with its own quiet focus.\n\nUse dialogue that stays on practical surfaces, with conversational distance. Characters speak about keys, schedules, phone calls, a borrowed book, a work task. Let them imply more than they say. A question may be ordinary but acquire weight because of what the focal character knows or notices. Do not force every exchange to advance the crime. The scene can linger on a small moment: a character pauses at a door, looks at a bulletin board, or sets down a cup.\n\nLet the focal narration or inner response show how the viewpoint character interprets the moment. Use background and expression cues as orientation, not as truth indicators. A changed arrangement—a missing object, a chair out of place, a note removed—or a contradiction in an account must do the evidentiary work. Keep private thought outside other characters' knowledge; the focal character may suspect something but cannot read minds. The crime emerges as a disturbance in ordinary life through visits, conversations, delays, and practical tasks.\n\nDo not add branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands merely because the style is selected. Maintain the active authorship contract and required interiority. Suspense can come from exposure or divided loyalty. The scene may end on a completed action, a held look, or an ordinary continuation. A discovery may open a new conversation rather than force a choice screen, and a relationship consequence may remain unsettled after the factual question is answered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadajbb::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Haruki Murakami / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across installments: what is underway, what has changed locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover context briefly only when it helps; do not retell the whole investigation at the start of each reply. Instead, re-enter through a small concrete detail from a previous installment: a cup left on a counter, a message received, a door that was locked, a task not yet finished.\n\nKeep Murakami's unhurried attention to ordinary life. An installment may spend significant time on a meal, a walk, a work task, a household repair, or a long wait. Let these activities carry feeling and implication without the narration insisting on their importance. Strangeness should appear evenly, as one more noticed thing. The crime remains present in the background, a concern the characters return to between practical matters.\n\nAdvance the crime through earned developments in knowledge, relationships, work, or resources. A conversation may revise what a character thought; a practical task may uncover something; a request may remain unanswered. Do not require one clue, one reversal, one confession, or one completed test per installment. A development may simply be a new piece of information that changes the weight of an earlier fact, or a relationship shift that alters what someone is willing to say.\n\nLet suspense come from exposure, divided loyalty, or the slow accumulation of small facts. An earlier detail may acquire new meaning in a later installment because of what is now known. Preserve required interiority even when no immediate decision changes; show the character's recurring thoughts and quiet realizations. An installment may close on completed work, a pending request, a quiet realization, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Serial duration should give selected facts room to acquire meaning, not make resolution perpetually recede.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadakaa::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jane Austen / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nTreat the crime as a standing injury inside a relationship, and treat every subsequent polite exchange as a fresh contact with that injury. The offense need not be concealed from the reader; the suspense lies in what the focal character can bear to say, admit, or accept while preserving the forms that bind them to the other person. Use Austen's free indirect judgment to render the gap between the performed sentence and the private cost of performing it. The irony belongs to the focal mind: the character may hear their own courtesy as cowardice, their own restraint as complicity, their own kindness as a bribe they cannot afford to stop paying.\n\nBuild each conversation as a sequence of small social obligations that press on the wound. A required greeting, an offered chair, a question about someone's health, a pause that must be filled—each one forces the character to choose between the truthful sentence and the survivable one. Show the choice in the actual speech and in the private reasoning that accompanies it. The character may say the correct thing and feel it as a betrayal of someone else; they may say the honest thing and immediately hear it as a weapon they did not mean to use. Do not let the narrator step outside this pressure to explain it. The reader should feel the character's own awareness of what the performance costs.\n\nLet the emotional turbulence be specific and unreconciled. The focal character may rehearse a confession and then deliver a different sentence when the moment arrives. They may notice the exact instant their composure becomes a form of punishment, either of themselves or of the person listening. They may feel relief and disgust in the same breath, gratitude and resentment toward the same gesture. Allow them to misread the other person's motive and then be corrected by a tone, a hesitation, or a piece of information they cannot ignore. Comfort, when it appears, should arrive through the same social machinery that caused the strain: an apology that also accuses, a kindness that also reminds, a silence that protects and isolates at once. End the scene where the character's performance and their private knowledge are most painfully at odds, not where the plot question is answered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadakab::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jane Austen / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite each reply as a coherent interval in a shared scene about a crime and its social consequences. Use short, scannable paragraphs. Give every line of dialogue a clear speaker through a dialogue tag or the paragraph's established focus. Keep the active narrator's person and perspective as set by the authorship contract; do not force first person or action markers into a narration that does not use them.\n\nOrganize the Austen social interpretation as a pairing operation. When a character speaks, let the focal narration supply the social weight the spoken line does not say: the polite question that is also a test, the offered alibi that reveals too much, the composed reply that privately registers as panic. The free indirect judgment belongs to the viewpoint character, not to an invisible commentator. When the narration slides into the character's own idiom, let it carry that character's particular resentment, hope, or self-deception. The pairing should make the exchange legible to the next writer without explaining the whole relationship in one block.\n\nAdvance the scene through these paired exchanges so that each one changes what someone knows, suspects, or feels. An NPC should act on their own interests: they may volunteer information to protect a reputation, refuse to answer because answering would implicate a friend, or change the subject because the truth is too expensive. Keep the world active without requiring the scene to end on a question, a cliffhanger, or a forced choice. The reply may stop in the middle of a conversation, after a line of dialogue, or at a moment of private realization, as long as the next writer can see what the character would naturally do or say next.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadakac::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jane Austen / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the classical or vernacular episodic telling route. Structure the crime as a sequence of discrete social episodes, each one a conversation, a visit, a letter, or a confrontation that changes the ledger of obligation, reputation, or material dependence. Let each episode open with a clear situation and close with a shift in what someone knows, owes, or fears. The episodes may be separated by time or travel, but the reader should feel the accumulating weight of the offense across them.\n\nTreat each episode as a social transaction whose spoken form and private meaning diverge. A polite offer may be a way of buying silence; a refusal to discuss a missing sum may be a way of protecting a family member; a carefully worded apology may be a way of avoiding a legal consequence. Use free indirect judgment to show the focal character's own accounting of the transaction: what they gained, what they gave away, what they now owe, and what they cannot admit they understood. The narrator's irony should be measured and owned, not a detached moralizing voice.\n\nLet the episodic form serve the crime's aftermath by making each later episode reread an earlier one. An early episode may establish who was owed what; a later episode may show how a small deception about money became a larger one about loyalty. Do not force a moral conclusion. The final episode may leave a character still uncertain whether they were wronged or complicit, still bound to someone they can no longer trust, or still hoping for a reconciliation that the facts make impossible. Preserve the setting, language, and community actually supplied by the story; the episodic route is a craft method, not a command to transplant a particular period or cast.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadakad::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jane Austen / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the crime in hard, concrete sentences. Let the physical facts carry the social meaning: a worn cuff, a locked drawer, a coin placed on a table, a door closed too quietly. Use forceful diction and compressed transitions. Move from one beat to the next without smoothing over the damage. When a character's reasoning needs room, let the thought run longer, but keep it anchored in something they can see, touch, or owe.\n\nRender Austen's social interpretation through the focal character's selective perception of material detail. A polite phrase is a transaction, and the free indirect judgment arrives in what the character notices and what they refuse to name. They see the clean cuff and not the missing ring; they remember the sum and not the lie; they call it a loan because calling it theft would cost too much. The narrator does not step back to explain the social meaning. The concrete detail itself carries the judgment: an offer of tea that delays a demand, a compliment that tests whether a debt will be called in, a refusal to sit that refuses to be managed.\n\nLet the crime's stakes stay physical and practical. Someone needs the money, fears the exposure, or cannot leave the house where the offense happened. Rough speech belongs to a specific speaker under specific pressure; it is not a universal tone. Change rhythm when the scene demands it: a long, careful exchange may collapse into a single hard sentence of action. Do not turn the genre into survival horror. The grit is in the wear, the pressure, and the cost, not in gratuitous violence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadakae::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jane Austen / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the crime through documents, each with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Use the specific document form the scene requires: a letter to a family member, a journal entry, a report to a magistrate, a note left in a book, a record of accounts. Do not rotate through formats mechanically. Each document should exist because its author needed to record, persuade, explain, or conceal something at that moment.\n\nTreat each document as a social performance in writing. The author composes a version of themselves for the recipient or the record, and the free indirect judgment belongs to that author: their self-deception, their careful phrasing, their attempt to make themselves look better than they were. A letter may be full of courtesy and omit the one fact that would change everything. A journal may record a suspicion the writer would never speak aloud. A report may be scrupulously accurate about sums and dates while remaining silent about motive. Do not add an omniscient explanation after every record.\n\nMake the crime's evidence emerge from the difference between the social face a document presents and the material trace or conflicting account that undercuts it. A letter written before the offense may be reread differently after it. A crossed-out line may be more damning than the sentence that replaced it. A document's material condition—a torn edge, a missing page, a seal broken and resealed—can be a trace of what happened. Preserve readable continuity across entries. The sequence should move the reader through the offense, its preparation, its aftermath, and its cost, but the cost should be felt in what the documents' authors cannot bring themselves to write.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadakaf::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jane Austen / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite the crime's aftermath as a space where comfort is real but partial. Spend attention on the small, chosen kindnesses that particular people offer one another: a cup of tea made the way someone likes it, a fire lit before the other person arrives, a chair moved closer, a question not asked because asking would be unkind. Let these gestures be sincere. They do not need to lead to a confession or an emotional repair.\n\nRender each comfort as a social transaction whose meaning the focal character reads through Austen's lens of obligation and reputation. An offer of help may be a way of staying close to someone who is ashamed to ask. A familiar preference remembered at the right moment may be a way of saying what cannot be said aloud. A courteous silence may be a way of protecting someone from a truth they are not ready to hear. Use free indirect judgment to show the focal character's awareness of the gap between the comfort and the damage. They may be grateful and still angry, soothed and still afraid, cared for and still alone. The warmth and the unspoken debt should be held in the same gesture, not separated into a soft moment and a hard reminder.\n\nDo not remove the crime from the world. The offense and its consequences remain real: someone is still missing money, still facing exposure, still bound to a person who hurt them. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable or erase the established problems. It makes the problems bearable for a particular interval, in a particular room, between particular people. Let personality, privacy, and disagreement stay intact. The scene may end in quiet warmth, in a shared meal, or in a moment of ease that does not resolve anything, but that still matters.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadakag::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jane Austen / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite the crime in composed novel prose. Use deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Let the narration move from a particular exchange to the social context that gives it meaning: the family's history, the neighborhood's opinion, the legal or financial arrangements that made the offense possible. Keep the narrative distance appropriate to the chosen narrator. The prose should be distinguishable from ordinary spoken language, but it need not be archaic or uniformly elaborate.\n\nUse the public occasion as the structural unit where private knowledge presses against public manners. A dinner, a hearing, a funeral, or a reading of a will gathers the people the offense has touched. In each such scene, let the focal character's free indirect reasoning supply the private accounting that the public forms cannot say: the courteous visit that is a negotiation over money, reputation, or silence; the composed reply that privately registers as panic; the polite omission that protects a family member at someone else's expense. The narrator may frame the scene with a broader observation about what such occasions mean in this community, but the irony must remain owned. When the narration slides into a character's idiom, let it carry that character's particular self-deception, hope, or resentment.\n\nLet the formal architecture organize the crime's aftermath across these occasions. Correspondence may be read, remembered, or withheld. The contrast between what is said in company and what is thought in private should be a structural principle, not a repeated trick. The novel may end with a judgment that remains socially legible but emotionally unsettled: a reputation restored, a debt paid, a family still divided, a character still uncertain whether they were wronged or complicit.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadakah::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jane Austen / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the crime as an act of telling with something riding on it. Open in an outer frame that fixes a specific audience, an occasion, and a reason this account is being given now: a name to clear, help to secure, a warning to deliver, a decision—about money, a marriage, a dismissal, a prosecution—that the telling is meant to sway. The outer situation should carry live social stakes of its own, so the performance of the tale is itself a move in the offense's aftermath.\n\nLet the teller's character arrive through selection and emphasis rather than editorial accusation. Where the teller recounts a generous act, a courteous refusal, or an awkward silence, let the wording of the account register what the teller believes about that moment while the circumstances as given quietly suggest a debt unmentioned, a threat folded inside politeness, an omission the teller does not notice making. This is free indirect judgment with a clear owner: the irony lives in the teller's phrasing, and the reader measures the gap between the self-portrait and the scene it describes. Keep that gap available rather than closing it with a verdict; the frame is not an omniscient court.\n\nGive the embedded account the shape of a partial view. It can cover preparation, the offense, or the aftermath—a transaction, a journey, a disputed evening—but it should stay bounded by what this teller saw, understood, or is willing to admit. Keep the narrative levels intelligible: the outer listeners can interrupt, press a question of timing or access, exchange a look the teller reads wrongly. Their reactions can revise how the reader understands the tale without the narrator announcing the correction.\n\nUse recurrence to bind the levels. A formula of courtesy, a sum of money, a letter, a key, or a garment can return in another teller's mouth or another episode of the account and mean differently there; let repetition accrue meaning for the reader even where the characters hear only small talk. Preserve the difference between what the teller knew at the time and what the teller has learned since, so retrospective justification stays separable from recollection even though neither level gets the last word.\n\nThe Arabian Nights is a craft source here, not a setting: no court, bargain, or supernatural register is required, and no cliffhanger is owed between nested accounts. End where the scene's own boundary falls—mid-account, mid-interruption, or in the beat after a hard question lands. The human aftermath, what people owe, fear, and refuse to admit, stays visible in how the tale is told.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadakai::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jane Austen / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nTake the documentary and historical-memory route: build the crime out of the distance between what was written down and what people remember. Set a ledger, a letter, a report, a transcript, or a register of names beside remembered conditions of the same events, and keep the two channels distinct on the page. A record states what someone chose to record, in a register suited to whoever would read it; a memory states what a participant experienced or later came to believe. Mark which channel is operating—by syntax, by attribution, by what each can and cannot contain—rather than letting one silently absorb the other.\n\nPut free indirect judgment at the seam. When a document uses a polite formula, a careful omission, or a flattering description, let the narration register what the writer wanted to be seen to believe; when a memory smooths a quarrel into a misunderstanding, let the narration register the cost of the smoothing. The crime becomes legible in the gaps between channels: a debt recorded as a gift, a visit omitted from a letter, a name left off a list, a date that only the record and one remembering person hold in common. The irony needs an owner with interests that shape what gets noticed—a participant looking back, a relative assembling papers, a bystander comparing accounts—never a neutral archivist's voice.\n\nLet comparison, not confession, do the explanatory work. A witness's recollection can contradict an entry; a record can confirm a date a family prefers to forget; a later account can revise an earlier one so the reader sees what changed and can ask why. Suspense can run on exposure and divided loyalty even when the reader already knows who did what; the live question becomes which discrepancies someone is willing to put a name to, and what each maintained version costs the people maintaining it.\n\nTreat the remembered channel as lived experience, not summary: render attention, sensation, and reluctance as the rememberer actually meets them, and let later knowledge sit beside earlier ignorance without retroactively rewriting it. Keep the offense and its aftermath material—money, property, a position someone could not afford to lose—so courtesy, obligation, and family feeling operate as mechanisms of concealment and disclosure rather than decoration.\n\nThis route organizes information and attention; it imports no archive, ministry, national temperament, or translated word order. The records belong to the world actually supplied—a household's accounts, a firm's correspondence, whatever the setting plausibly keeps—and the syntax follows the chosen teller's distance from events.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadakaj::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jane Austen / Gothic-Punk\n\nWrite the crime as the collision of inherited authority with present life. Establish an old claim—a property, a position, a charter, a debt of obligation, a family name—that still commands behavior from people whose needs are current: rent, work, a relationship, a departure someone is preventing or attempting. The offense can be a scheme to preserve that authority, a betrayal inside it, or damage falling unevenly on the people who depend on it. Carry it from preparation through execution to aftermath from whichever viewpoint you choose—culprit, accomplice, victim, investigator, or bystander.\n\nMake the collision material. A building's state of repair, who keeps the lights working, what a room's furniture says about who is expected there: urban detail of this kind grounds the old claim in present maintenance and present neglect. Let atmosphere change how a particular encounter is experienced—the same conversation reads differently in a borrowed office at noon and in a hallway someone's family still owns at two in the morning—and earn its place by attaching to fact, not by substituting for it.\n\nRun free indirect judgment across the masks. A courteous phrase, an offer of help, a refusal, or a silence can carry several meanings; let the narration register what a character believes about their own conduct while the circumstance suggests what that conduct accomplishes. In trusted company a mask can drop and something unguarded surface—but intimacy is also a technique, and where it matters the reader should be able to feel the difference between a genuine unguarded moment and a performance staged to disarm. The private side of the mask is real experience: desire, resentment, fear of what the old claim still makes possible, rendered as the character's own thought wherever the narration has access, not announced to the room.\n\nLet morally complicated attachment drive the aftermath. People may protect someone they love, resent someone they depend on, or stay loyal to an arrangement that has damaged them; divided feeling shows in what they say, what they decline to say, and what they do at hours when no one with authority is watching. Who is willing to lie for whom, and what each lie protects, should make the offense and its consequences legible. Suspense can come from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who acted.\n\nThe inherited authority here is social and material, not supernatural: no hidden powers, factions, or blood mechanics arrive with the atmosphere. Whatever the setting's actual rules permit stands; this treatment adds pressure, texture, and intimacy, not new laws.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadakak::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jane Austen / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the crime through social and domestic realism: the offense lives inside the obligations, material dependencies, and reputational pressures of one particular family and community, and the scene work happens at the scale where those forces operate—visits, thresholds, meals, account books, proposals and refusals, what is said in front of servants or children or in-laws and what is saved for the stair. The crime may center on money, property, a marriage arrangement, a position, or a promise someone could not afford to break; take what the supplied world supports and keep its practical mechanics—documents, witnesses, who must be persuaded—concrete.\n\nGive small exchanges several meanings. An offer can be a claim, a courtesy can establish a debt, a silence can protect or punish; free indirect judgment lets the narration register what a character believes about their own conduct while the circumstance shows what that conduct accomplishes. When someone describes a generous act or a dutiful choice, let the reader see the omission or interest underneath without the narrator prosecuting the case. The judgment needs a particular owner—a focal character whose attention and blind spots shape it—so the irony stays measured rather than becoming general cynicism about the community.\n\nRender the aftermath domestically and concretely. People may protect a relative, resent a dependent, fear scandal, or remain loyal to someone who has harmed them; these divided attachments appear in private thought as well as in conversation, with the private and the spoken differing for character-specific reasons—embarrassment, duty, leverage, habit—or aligning completely when they do. Track who is told what: household information has a circulation, and much of the crime's legibility comes from who first knows, who is kept from knowing, and what it costs to move a fact from one circle to another. Suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did what.\n\nKeep language, region, community, and period to what is actually supplied. Do not impose English or code-switching, a particular religion or caste experience, or a generic family structure; a joint household, a nuclear one, a chosen one, or none of these follows from the world, not from the tradition label. Let syntax, narrative distance, and speech register follow the actual teller and the community's own norms rather than a uniformly polished voice.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadakal::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jane Austen / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the crime through social and psychological realism: the offense, its preparation, and its aftermath unfold within a specific social world of obligations, material dependencies, reputations, and private reservations. Choose a focal carrier—culprit, accomplice, victim, investigator, or bystander—and hold the narration to that person's attention, so the crime is experienced as pressure on a particular life rather than surveyed from above.\n\nWork the gap between performance and private noticing. When a character speaks politely, accepts an obligation, or steers around a subject, free indirect judgment can carry what they believe about their own conduct alongside what they privately observe: the too-prompt agreement, the name not mentioned, the account that improves on repetition. Render that private attention as actual experience—attention drifting to the wrong detail, a suspicion forming faster than it can be justified—while the other characters see only the performance. What the focal character never says aloud can still be fully present to the reader.\n\nUse omission as structure, not only tact. The things characters avoid naming can decide which scenes occur, which questions go unasked, and how long a misunderstanding or an arrangement holds; withholding from other characters is not withholding from the reader when the narration has access to the focal awareness. Direct feeling and plain argument are equally available where the character would use them. No universal quietness is required and no seasonal scenery needs cataloguing; weather or season may matter where this character would actually notice it, and then it should do work—marking time, pressure, or what is ending.\n\nMake the aftermath psychological and concrete at once. People may protect a family member, resent a dependent, fear scandal, or stay loyal to someone who has harmed them; render divided attachment in thought and in conversation, and let the offense be legible through relationships—who will lie for whom, who owes what, what a person does to keep a position or a reputation. Suspense can run on exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader already knows who acted, and neither punishment nor forgiveness is owed by the form.\n\nPreserve the community, period, and speech register actually supplied; this route organizes attention and narrative distance, it does not relocate the cast or dictate a setting.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadakam::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jane Austen / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the crime through social realism, chronicle, and testimony: build the account from attributed tellings—a witness's statement, a neighbor's recollection, a participant's confession, a bystander's report, entries in whatever public or private record the world actually keeps. Keep each account distinct in interest and diction. A testimony says what its teller claims to have seen, known, or done; it is not an omniscient record of the event. Separate personal participation from events a teller only heard about, and let the chronicle's ordering—what is told first, what surfaces late, what a teller saves for last—shape how the offense comes into view.\n\nAttach free indirect judgment to the tellers rather than to the event. Where the narration draws close to a witness describing a generous act, a dutiful choice, or an unavoidable silence, let it register the self-interest or omission beneath the account: a debt described as a gift, a quarrel remembered as a misunderstanding, a visit omitted, a name left off a list. Each measured irony needs its owner—the teller being rendered, or the arranging hand the chronicle acknowledges—so judgment stays particular rather than becoming general cynicism about the community.\n\nLet the crime become legible through comparison of accounts. What one teller emphasizes, another omits; one version stops a minute before the moment another dwells on; the same phrase can recur in two mouths with different interests behind it. Track who says what, and let the reader notice the pressure each telling serves—who is protecting family, who is settling a score, who is keeping a job or a place in the neighborhood. Suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did what; the open question is what the assembled accounts will be made to support and who is willing to put a name to it. Polyphony and a nonlinear order are available—moving between tellers and times—provided each attribution stays clear.\n\nKeep the relevant language, community, and history as supplied, with the material stakes of the offense concrete: money, property, work, a reputation, a border someone crosses or cannot. Do not force a generic family structure, a particular religion, or a single political reading onto the scene, and add no supernatural facts to a world that excludes them; testimony can be unreliable entirely through human interest.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadakan::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jane Austen / Light Novel\n\nWrite the crime in fast-readable beats with clean visual staging. Enter the immediate situation without preamble: fix where the characters are, who is present, and what is at stake in this exchange within the first few paragraphs, and keep every spatial change—an approach, a move to another room, someone leaving the table—easy to follow. Break paragraphs around action, dialogue, and private response so the reader can track, beat by beat, what was said, what was done, and what a character made of it.\n\nMake the reaction beat the load-bearing unit, and put free indirect judgment inside it. After a character speaks—makes an offer, accepts an obligation, declines a subject—the narration that follows can register what that character believes about their own conduct and what the circumstance actually reveals: a courteous phrase functioning as a threat, an offer as a demand, a silence as an admission. That gap between public performance and private attention is where the social meaning of the exchange becomes legible. Keep the judgment owned by a particular perspective; nobody in the cast needs to be uniformly witty or perceptive, and a reaction can be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to its owner.\n\nKeep preparation, incentives, and aftermath visible from the chosen viewpoint—culprit, accomplice, victim, investigator, or bystander—and let money, property, family, and reputation do the mechanical work of hiding or exposing the offense. Staging serves that legibility: who sits where, who watches the door, whose phone or ledger or bag is in whose hands—use whatever objects the world actually supplies—so visual information carries social meaning instead of decorating it. Suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did what; no closing cliffhanger is required, and neither are first person, comedy, or a young cast.\n\nGive unfamiliar names, relationships, or mechanics a clear presentation on first use—a clause of orientation, a placed detail—so a fast reader never has to re-read to know who is speaking or what a term covers. Short paragraphs and quick pacing should not thin the world: the container changes presentation and information flow, not the substance of the crime.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadakao::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jane Austen / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet the offense enter the prose as a change in the value of a familiar thing. Begin with the object or exchange as the focal character first understood it — an unremarkable receipt, a drawer that was always locked, an arrival later than promised, a name spoken once too casually — and then let syntax and image carry the new suspicion into the same thing. A comparison should do work: if the room seems smaller, or the garden farther from the house, that is what the character now perceives, not a general poetic truth asserted on the world's behalf. Keep the figurative attention owned, and let rhythm mark the shift — an even, balanced clause while the thing is innocent, a sentence that runs on or breaks when the knowledge arrives. Set a plain statement beside the figure now and then, so the figure keeps its force.\n\nUse Austen's social interpretation to decide which things carry the change. Manners, money, family, and reputation give a small exchange several meanings: a polite refusal, a timely offer, a silence about a sum. Free indirect judgment can open the gap between the courteous sentence and the focal character's private understanding, provided the judgment has a clear owner and no other mind is entered. Keep the irony measured and specific — a family that speaks of convenience while meaning expense, a neighbor whose concern arrives exactly where it can be observed — and let it sharpen rather than soften the offense. A stolen sum remains stolen; a betrayal remains a betrayal, whatever beauty the prose gives it.\n\nOrganize paragraphs by attention rather than by the chronology of the scheme. One image may call up another through the character's association — the locked drawer to the hand that kept the key — so long as each link is traceable to this scene and this teller. A recurring image, such as a closed door or a folded letter, may return when a later fact gives it a second meaning, and the second meaning should add knowledge rather than repeat the mood. The reply may end inside the unfolding consequence — in the middle of a look, a sentence, or an unfinished thought about the altered thing — without delivering a lesson or closing the investigation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadakap::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jane Austen / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nTreat the offense as the point from which familiar things are seen twice. A room, a phrase, a family occasion keeps the meaning it had in memory while acquiring the meaning it has now, and the prose can hold both at once: the narrator or focal character returns to the earlier occasion through a present perception — the same walk, the same seat at the table, the sentence that was once ordinary — and what has since happened changes the object without erasing what it was. Keep the two moments distinct. If the telling now knows what it did not know then, let that gap be felt rather than flattened; the earlier kindness can remain kind even when the person who offered it is suspected, arrested, or simply gone, and ordinary pleasure may stay pleasurable, only quieter or more complicated.\n\nUse Austen's social attention to make the damage concrete instead of general. A reduced income, a broken engagement, a reputation altered by rumor, a visit that can no longer be paid — these carry consequence better than abstract sorrow, because each one changes what people owe, can offer, and must avoid saying. Conversation can reveal what people believe about themselves and what they cannot admit: a character may call an inconvenience a tragedy, or a serious injury a small misunderstanding, and the narration may hold that difference precisely without mocking every feeling. Keep reflective access consistent with the chosen perspective — the teller may know what the focal character remembers and now understands, but not what another person privately felt on the same occasion.\n\nLet reflection set the pace rather than the investigation. Questions of preparation, evidence, or blame may be present, but the account can pause over a remembered sentence, a changed habit, or the sound of a house after someone has left, and the pause earns its place by showing what the situation now is. Do not require every detail to announce loss, and do not end each scene on impermanence. The reply may stop while the character is still living inside the change — mid-thought, mid-visit, mid-measurement of what can be salvaged — with neither the offense nor the grief required to resolve.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadakaq::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jane Austen / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nTake the social and domestic realist route: the offense and its consequences appear in households, workplaces, shops, and family visits, in the practical arrangements — loans, inheritances, shared rooms, obligations returned and unreturned — that connect people in the supplied community. Keep the setting, language, period, and community exactly as given, including their actual codes of hospitality, discretion, and reputation where the story has established them; do not overlay a generic regional atmosphere, antique mysticism, or a universal honor code. The realism lives in precision: how money is asked for and refused, what may be said openly and what is conveyed through a lowered voice or a message carried by a relative, which visits continue and which quietly stop.\n\nUse Austen's social interpretation to give these exchanges several meanings at once. An offer of help may also be a claim; a polite omission may protect a cousin or conceal a theft; a conversation about a marriage, a job, or a neighbor may expose the pressure that made the offense thinkable. The focal character's understanding develops through what is said, what is avoided, and what the circumstances show, and the measured irony stays owned — a family that speaks of shame when it means exposure, of generosity when it means control. Not every speaker is a suspect; most people are managing their own interests, which is what makes the guilty one hard to hear.\n\nKeep the crime itself concrete. Follow the preparation, incentive, relationship, execution, or aftermath from the chosen viewpoint, and show the offense changing ordinary life: a shop that cannot open, a name left off an invitation, an accusation that redraws which households will receive one another. Write continuous, scene-based prose in which dialogue, perception, and authorized inner experience move naturally, and let private thought differ from spoken courtesy for character-specific reasons. The reply may stop while the social damage is still being measured — no punishment and no completed investigation is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadakar::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jane Austen / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect a few exact details and let each one carry social meaning. Choose things whose placement, absence, or cost would be noticed in this world: a chair moved from its usual place, a name left off an invitation, a purse where it should not be, a pause before an answer. Prefer one right detail to three approximate ones, and do not explain a detail after showing it. Let direct clauses keep the action and the focal character's understanding moving together; when an essential thought is needed, give it the length it needs to make sense, then stop — a second sentence repeating the first is not emphasis but redundancy.\n\nPerform Austen's social interpretation through selection and juxtaposition rather than commentary. A polite sentence placed beside the act that contradicts it does the work: the narration may report that a character called the loss unfortunate and then show him locking the door, without announcing that the word was false. An apology can carry a claim, a small financial arrangement can show who is protected, and the focal character's inference may be stated — but only when it rests on the detail already given. Keep the irony in the gap between what is said and what is known, and keep each mind's access to itself.\n\nLet omission leave something inferable. A refused visit can be understood from the empty afternoon that follows it; a missing object can be noticed without narrating every possible implication. Preserve causality: the connection between an act and its consequence must remain recoverable, even when the narration declines to trace it aloud. The reply may stop at the moment the focal character registers the new fact — mid-exchange, mid-glance — with what comes next left open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadakas::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jane Austen / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the offense as continuous scene rather than case summary. Move naturally among what the focal character does, hears, notices, remembers, and privately thinks, letting paragraph breaks and small transitions — a change of room, an errand begun, the next sentence spoken — orient the reader without recap machinery. Let the scene carry the situation: a conversation about a debt, a visit that reveals an omission, a moment of divided loyalty can advance the offense without pausing to explain its significance, and a recollection can surface inside the scene when something present calls it up.\n\nUse Austen's social interpretation within that flow. Courtesy, offers, omissions, money, family, and reputation give each exchange several meanings at once: a character may offer help in order to learn something; a relative may describe an arrangement as generous while arranging it so that refusal costs more than agreement. Let the focal character's private understanding diverge from what is said, and keep free indirect judgment clearly owned — the narration may render that reading of a sentence, but it does not enter the speaker's mind to confirm it. Not every speech is a polished performance; people also misspeak, deflect, and say what they actually mean.\n\nKeep the crime concrete and let suspense come from the social exposure the viewpoint permits. The reader may know who did it while the focal character does not, or the focal character may know and fear what the knowledge will cost — what can be said, what must be hidden, what a relationship will bear. The reply may end during continuing action, speech, or thought, or inside an unfinished exchange; a discovery need not complete any investigation, and a private realization need not produce an immediate decision.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadakat::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jane Austen / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nLet the offense put pressure on the established relationship rather than replacing it with an investigation. Choose scenes where the relationship's particular promise is at stake: the crime may create a secret between them, expose a divided loyalty, make one protect the other at a cost, or force the conversation they have been avoiding. Use only the actual supplied premise for who these people are to each other; do not bolt on a roster of familiar tropes. Give affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery the space the premise and the present moment support, and no more.\n\nUse Austen's social interpretation to make the bond legible through manners and circumstance. A small courtesy can be an apology, a test, or a claim; an omission can protect or conceal. Money, family, and reputation decide what each can offer the other and what can be admitted aloud, so the obstacles should be ones this world actually enforces. Let private language and repeated moments carry the history — a phrase used only between them, a gesture whose meaning has changed, a room or object attached to an earlier occasion. Establish the callback in the scene or the supplied premise, let its return deliver feeling rather than exposition, and show the focal character's private experience of it without rehearsing the whole history each time.\n\nKeep the crime's consequences inside the relationship. Suspense may come from exposure, divided loyalty, or the fear of losing the other person, even when the reader already knows what happened. A confession need not settle everything; a moment of comfort need not erase the harm; a kindness offered in the middle of a deception is still both things at once. The reply may end with the relationship still unsettled — one of them waiting for an answer, the other deciding what can be said — and nothing is required to resolve first.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadakau::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jane Austen / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the offense as an account that will be repeated. Open with a deliberate statement of what was done or what became known — who took the money, who broke the agreement, who was blamed for it — then expand into the particulars that make the received story incomplete. Compact declarations establish the act; the expansion supplies the material and social conditions behind it: the debt that made the theft thinkable, the alliance the broken agreement embarrassed, the standing the blamed party lost. Keep the present scene's scale. A theft remains a theft even when the chronicle gives it weight.\n\nUse Austen's social interpretation to keep the chronicle's judgments specific. Reputation, family, money, and manners explain why the act mattered and how the community understood it, and a recurring phrase should grow from an established deed or a person's way of speaking rather than from an ornamental epithet attached to every entrance. When a later occurrence gives the phrase a new application, return to it. Keep shared reputation distinct from private experience: being remembered as wronged does not mean the wronged person felt only grief, and being remembered as guilty does not mean the guilty one acted without hesitation or fear — the account may show that difference where the chosen perspective allows.\n\nLet dialogue interrupt the authority of the telling. The subject of the oft-told version may dispute a detail, or answer an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern, and the interruption need not become parody. Keep the consequences concrete: a household altered, a debt unpaid, a name no longer spoken at table, a visit no longer made. The reply may end with the offense still being weighed in the community's memory, another consequence still available to be told, rather than declaring the matter closed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadakav::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jane Austen / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nSet the offense inside a specific community's arrangements of work, housing, family obligation, money, and institutional authority — the Scandinavian social and domestic realist route. Let the crime become legible through ordinary social consequences rather than a puzzle of clues: a theft, a fraud, a breach of trust, or an act of violence alters who may speak freely, whose account is believed, and what each household can afford to acknowledge. Use the community the story actually supplies. If that world names a particular Nordic setting, keep its language, institutions, and neighborly distances distinct from its neighbors' rather than blending a generic North; if it names none, the method still holds. An official inquiry, where one exists, is one limited institution among several — the neighbors' memory, the association's minutes, and the shop's ledger may know more than the investigator's file, and know it differently.\n\nCarry Austen's free indirect judgment through the focal character's private reasoning about those consequences. When the narration slides close to a mind, preserve that character's own vocabulary of duty, embarrassment, advantage, or injury. A courteous refusal can register as a threat; an offer of help can read as an insult; a small omission about money can expose a larger concealment. The reader may see the gap between what a character tells herself and what the surrounding conversation reveals, but the narration should not announce the correct moral reading; keep the judgment owned by the focal mind or by the narrator's measured irony, never by everyone at once.\n\nTreat institutions as real and limited. A workplace inquiry, housing inspection, welfare office, police visit, or family council has its own procedures, delays, and partial sight, and those procedures shape what becomes known and what remains manageable. A character may cooperate for practical reasons, mislead to protect someone, or tell a partial truth because the full one would cost too much. Do not make every official encounter a trap or every private loyalty a betrayal.\n\nMove the aftermath through domestic scenes: a shared meal interrupted by a question, a bill examined twice, a neighbor's visit that changes what can be said at home. Repetition should accumulate social meaning — the same phrase about a missing sum, an absent relative, or a closed door can mean differently once a new fact is known. Do not require a confession, arrest, or punishment to complete the account; the story may end where the social consequences have become clear, even if the legal question remains open.\n\nKeep the prose composed and continuous. Free indirect thought may tighten around a character's anxiety or calculation, but the paragraph should retain enough orientation to distinguish event, memory, and inference. Reserve, where it appears, is a social fact in the scene, not a national temperament.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadakaw::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jane Austen / Screenplay\n\nWrite the public scene in concise action lines and attributed dialogue. Let the camera-facing surface show what an observer in the room could see or hear: who enters, who hesitates, what object changes hands, which question is answered too quickly or not at all. A scene heading marks a real change in location or time, not a shift in conversational mood. Keep the page free of shooting instructions; describe what is visible or audible, not how to photograph it.\n\nUse Austen's social interpretation through the visible management of speech and manner. A character may perform courtesy while refusing information, offer help that imposes an obligation, or answer a question about money with a remark about the weather. Let the dialogue carry the pressure of the offense without every line confessing it. Do not turn a glance, pause, or expression into a reliable sign of guilt; a contradiction between what is said and what is done must do the interpretive work.\n\nKeep required private experience in clearly attributed nonspoken notes, separated from the playable surface. A note may record what a character privately understands, fears, calculates, or misreads while the scene continues. No other character hears or knows this note merely because it appears on the page. Do not convert private thought into voice-over unless the chosen sequence specifically requires that separate device.\n\nLet the crime's consequences move through practical exchanges: a bag searched, a signature requested, a door left open, a message shown to the wrong person. A cut — or a sound heard from offscreen — should connect two moments whose juxtaposition changes what the audience can infer, not manufacture a false revelation. The scene may end on a completed action, a refused answer, or an unfinished exchange. Preserve the active authorship contract's access to minds; the nonspoken note is a presentation wrapper, not a license to enter an excluded character.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadakax::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jane Austen / Stage Play\n\nLet the shared stage space make social claims testable. Who can see an entrance, reach a letter, hear a lowered voice, or leave without being noticed should be clear enough that a character's account of an event can be checked against the physical conditions. Keep the cast occupied with practical business — setting a table, sorting papers, answering a door, returning a borrowed object — so the offense enters through ordinary movement rather than a posed explanatory scene. Keep every direction stageable, with no film-camera instructions.\n\nUse Austen's social interpretation through the relationship between lines and physical business. A character may offer tea while refusing to answer, tidy a room to avoid a question, or hand over money with a remark that makes the gift feel like an accusation. A pause can itself be social business — a hostess letting a question die over the teapot — without becoming a sign of guilt. Courtesy, omission, and reputation carry multiple meanings; the audience may notice a contradiction between what a character says and what the staging permits, but the script should not stop to announce the correct reading.\n\nKeep required private experience in clearly attributed nonspoken authoring material, separated from the playable script. This material may record what a character privately understands, fears, intends, or misreads while the public scene continues. It is not an aside, monologue, or voice-over, and no other character hears or knows it. If an aside or monologue is separately authorized, give it a dramatic purpose and an audience within the scene; do not use it as the default container for thought.\n\nLet the crime's aftermath move through entrances, exits, and objects: a door opened at the wrong moment, a letter left on a table, a chair moved between scenes can alter what the characters may safely say. The scene may end on a completed action, an interruption, or a refusal. Do not require a gathering of suspects, a theatrical revelation, or a new clue at every exit. Preserve the active authorship contract's access to minds; nonspoken material is a presentation wrapper, not permission to enter an excluded character.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadakay::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jane Austen / Stream of Consciousness\n\nLet the telling move through one authorized mind's attention to the offense and its social consequences. Perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, and interruption may braid together: a bill noticed on a table recalls a promise made at dinner; a phrase repeated by a neighbor returns as an accusation the character cannot answer aloud; the feel of a locked door brings back the moment the key was missing. Syntax and paragraph shape may follow these associations, but keep enough orientation to distinguish what is happening now, what is remembered, and what is imagined or feared.\n\nUse Austen's social interpretation as the content of the focal mind's recurring concern. The character may privately rehearse what courtesy required, what an omission revealed, what a sum of money would cost, or what a reputation could not survive. Let the same small exchange return with altered meaning as new facts surface. The mind may justify itself, misread another person, or notice an implication it refuses to finish. Do not make the character universally perceptive or uniformly self-deceiving; the particular mind's habits shape what it returns to and what it avoids.\n\nKeep the crime legible through the mind's movement without turning every scene fact indeterminate. A memory may be partial, but a remembered event remains an event; a fear may be vivid without being true. When a conversation is recalled, distinguish the words that were actually spoken from the reply the character now composes in memory; when an exposure is imagined, mark the shift into fantasy rather than letting it masquerade as scene.\n\nDo not extend the stream to every character or use the technique to enter a mind the active authorship contract excludes; another character's private experience must be separately authorized and clearly attributed. The response may end inside the focal mind's continuing attention, with a question unresolved or a consequence only beginning to register. No external task, decision, or completed realization is required to justify the stop.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadakaz::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jane Austen / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nLet the story move through sender-attributed messages whose choices carry social meaning. A character may reply with perfect courtesy while refusing the actual question, offer help that creates an obligation, delay an answer until the delay itself becomes a statement, or send a message to the wrong person. Let each sender's digital habits shape the exchange: one writes in full sentences, another leaves words out, a third revises repeatedly before sending. Use timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes only when they matter to what the characters can know or claim, and do not manufacture an interface event merely to supply subtext.\n\nTreat the sent message as the public surface and keep private material clearly separated. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought: it may show what a character considered saying, but it has not been transmitted and no other character has received it. Required private experience may appear in clearly attributed nonspoken material outside the transcript, distinguished from both sent messages and unsent drafts. Do not let a character know another's private thought merely because it appears on the page.\n\nUse Austen's social interpretation through omissions and reputation. A missing reply about money, a carefully worded refusal, a group chat that falls silent when one name appears, or a message forwarded without permission can expose the pressure of the offense. Let the same phrase return in a new context with altered meaning. Do not make every delay a confession or every edit a lie; a character may revise for courtesy, anxiety, or ordinary care.\n\nKeep the crime's consequences legible through what is transmitted and what is withheld. A request for help may be answered with an unrelated question; an accusation may be met with a screenshot; a promise may be left on read. The response may end on a sent message, an unsent draft, or a private interval of attention, without requiring a reply, confrontation, or completed discovery first. Preserve the active authorship contract's access to minds; the transcript is a presentation form, not permission to enter an excluded character.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadakba::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jane Austen / Visual Novel\n\nUse concise scene-setting, attributed dialogue, and focal narration in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Mark each change of scene with a clear new setting line or beat so the reader always knows where and when the exchange is happening. A background or expression cue may orient the moment — a door left open, a chair moved, a smile held too long — but do not turn that cue into an automatic truth indicator. A changed expression can attract attention; a contradiction in what is said, shown, or arranged must carry the interpretive weight.\n\nLet the focal narration carry Austen's social interpretation. The viewpoint character may privately explain another person's behavior through courtesy, obligation, money, or reputation, and the next exchange may support that explanation, complicate it, or reveal that the speaker was concerned with something else entirely. Keep private thought outside other characters' knowledge. Do not make the cast into fixed reaction portraits whose faces always reveal whether they lie.\n\nAdvance the crime's consequences through scenes whose information changes what the focal character can safely assume. A revisit may use a familiar visual detail as an anchor: the same envelope on the table, the same chair now empty, the same phrase repeated by a different person. Let the new significance be earned by a later fact rather than announced by the narration, so repetition deepens understanding instead of restarting the same suspicion.\n\nThe style does not activate branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands. Preserve the active authorship contract's access to minds. A discovery may open a new conversation rather than force a choice screen, and a relationship consequence may remain unsettled after the factual question is answered. The scene may end on a completed action, a refused answer, or an unfinished exchange.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadakbb::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jane Austen / Web Serial\n\nLet an exact earlier social detail remain available when a later installment changes what it suggests. A character may repeat a phrase that once seemed polite and now sounds like a threat, ask for money that was previously refused, or mention an absence that earlier passed without notice. Briefly recover the necessary particular rather than retelling the whole exchange that first established it, so the reader can re-enter the installment knowing what is underway and what has newly become legible.\n\nUse Austen's social interpretation cumulatively. A courtesy extended in one installment may read as an obligation in the next; an omission about a sum of money may become significant only after a third party reveals what was left out. Let the focal character's private judgment revise itself without erasing the earlier event. New information can qualify an old explanation, but it cannot make the character retroactively know what was not known or forget a supported conclusion.\n\nLet ordinary life continue around the offense. Meals, visits, work, and family obligations may occupy the foreground while the crime's consequences move underneath, and keep required inward experience available when it contributes feeling or thought, even if no immediate decision changes. An installment's progress can be a changed relationship, a day's work completed, money moved, or a fact newly understood — not only a step in an inquiry. A repeated question may carry a new implication without a fresh contradiction being invented.\n\nThe installment may close on completed work, a pending request, a newly understood remark, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Do not require a clue, reversal, confession, or cliffhanger at every stop. Serial duration should give selected social facts room to acquire meaning, not make the offense perpetually recede. Preserve the active authorship contract's access to minds across installments.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadalaa::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jin Yong / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the crime from inside a viewpoint whose unresolved injury is already active when the scene begins. Do not open by naming the feeling; let it run the narrator's attention. The familiar gesture now performed for someone else, the debt recalled at the exact moment the money is counted, an ally's name pronounced with a half-second too much care — these are the scene's emotional record, and the narrator notices them, avoids them, and returns to them against their own intention.\n\nTreat crime business as relationship business. Give encounters the author profile's room for travel, explanation, and history, and let the pressure live inside that room: a question about who had access becomes a question about who was protecting whom; dividing a take forces the narrator to ask a favor of the person they least want to owe; reporting what happened means describing someone they are not ready to condemn. Preparation, incentive, pursuit, and aftermath stay concrete and legible, but the cost enters the speech and the behavior rather than waiting between plot beats. A witness answers the question and never raises her eyes. An ally's help arrives in a tone that makes it sound like an accusation, and the narrator takes it and resents every minute of it.\n\nPut private attachment and public obligation in conflict through particular decisions. Withholding a suspicion should cost something visible: a sentence swallowed, a report softened by one phrase, a long detour taken for no practical reason. Admitting a need should be awkward on the page — too blunt, too formal, arriving too late. Use the narrator's own diction for the strain, not a borrowed vocabulary of suffering; a hard character's hurt may show as extra courtesy, a faster count, or an uncharacteristic silence.\n\nWhere the active contract allows interiority, let thought and speech diverge for a concrete reason — protecting someone already damaged, fear of what the truth will cost, embarrassment at still caring — and let the reader see both layers. Comfort, when it comes, may be clumsy and incomplete; warmth and pain can share a sentence. Do not steer the offense toward exposure, punishment, or tragedy; the scene may end with the scheme still running, half understood, or quietly betrayed, and the factual answer, if one arrives, does not settle the injury. End where the scene's attention actually ends — mid-errand, mid-conversation, mid-decision — not at a resolution the story has not earned.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadalab::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jin Yong / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the current interval as readable shared fiction: coherent paragraphs, unmistakable attribution on every line of dialogue, and whatever person, action markers, and player-control conventions the active authorship contract supplies. Keep the player character inside that contract — do not act for them, decide for them, or render their private mind beyond what the contract permits. Give every nonplayer figure a practical life that continues around the scene: the fence is late because her regular supplier demands attention, the lookout is covering his brother's shift, the investigator has a report due. Their cooperation, refusal, and silence come from incentive and obligation, not from a need to obstruct.\n\nUse shared history as live material in the exchange. A short reference — the favor from three years back, the job that went wrong, the name nobody uses anymore — should visibly change how the present request lands: cooperation arrives with conditions attached, a refusal comes with an apology, an old debt is repaid at an inconvenient moment. Do not halt the scene to deliver the backstory; let it surface one piece at a time in what people are willing to do, say, and avoid.\n\nKeep the crime concrete inside the shared frame. Whichever side of it the player character occupies — culprit, accomplice, investigator, victim, bystander — the preparation, access, evidence, and pursuit around them show up as things that happen: a meeting arranged, a cut disputed, a question answered too quickly, a car moved to another street. Suspense may come from exposure or divided loyalty even when everyone present knows who did it.\n\nFinish the reply inside the flow of events rather than at a manufactured hook: the visitor still in the doorway, the question just asked, the take half counted, the truck pulling out. No fresh clue, confession, reversal, or forced choice is owed per turn. Let a request stand unanswered or a task span turns when that is what the situation gives, and leave the next exchange something already on the table to continue from.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadalac::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jin Yong / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nTell the crime through the vernacular episodic route: a chain of linked scenes narrated in clear, unhurried, storyteller-inflected prose. Give each episode a definite place, a way in, and a way out — the meeting at the wharf, the visit to the creditor's house, the night watch at the yard — and let one episode's result become the next episode's problem: a bargain made forces a journey, the journey raises a question, the question leads to a confrontation someone cannot refuse. Do not compress the whole affair into a single block of summary; move the reader through the stages, and anchor the chain with recurring names, places, sums, and obligations that gain weight each time they return.\n\nLet the narrator pause where context is needed — who this person is, why the district answers to that family, what the old arrangement was — and supply it in a few direct sentences before returning to the action. The explanation serves the scene; it does not replace it. Keep the telling plain and accessible in whatever language, period, and community the story's own world supplies. The route is a method of scene-building and address, not permission to relocate the cast or restyle its names.\n\nRun the offense through divided obligations. A character's tie to a brother, a patron, or a former partner should bend what they do with what they know: a report delayed by a day, an account softened, a risk accepted because refusing it would cost more than danger. Show technique, restraint, and reputation through what people actually do and how the next episode's cast responds to their names. Keep the competing interests particular and visible — recovery, concealment, protection, profit, peace — and let them collide inside episodes rather than in abstract discussion.\n\nClose each episode where its scene naturally closes: a door shut, a debt entered in the book, a traveler gone in the morning. The linked structure itself promises continuation, so no manufactured cliffhanger is needed and no episode owes the reader a fixed ration of clues or reversals. Whether the offense ends in exposure, settlement, or quiet persistence belongs to the story, not the form.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadalad::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jin Yong / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite hard and concrete. Forceful diction, transitions that cut rather than connect. Let the physical world carry the information: the car with its rewelded quarter panel, the ledger with pages gone soft, the tool put down at last where someone can see it. Objects state what happened — wear, damage, weight, what is missing — and the prose states them once, without interpretation. A cracked phone screen is a cracked phone screen before it is anything else.\n\nCompress the author profile's room for travel, explanation, and relationship into movement instead of cutting it away. A long trip is three details: the road, the wait, what got said in the cab. An explanation arrives in speech that suits the speaker — a foreman's flat arithmetic, a cousin's rambling justification — and runs no longer than the scene needs. Encounters remain encounters, not sequences of blows: who waits, who moves first, who holds back, who covers for whom is where technique, restraint, and reputation become visible, and an old alliance can decide a whole scene without a blow thrown.\n\nVary the rhythm deliberately. Most of the prose moves in quick strokes; slow only when the character has to think — running a timeline against a gate log, hearing the second account contradict the first, weighing whether a partner of eleven years is lying — and keep that longer thought concrete, tied to objects in the room, before the pace returns. Do not pad it with abstract reflection, and do not hard-boil the narrator by default. Rough or abrasive speech belongs to the particular person speaking, grounded in their history, position, and immediate pressure, and different people sound different.\n\nThe crime stays legible under the compression: who planned what, who carried the risk, who is hunting whom, and what it cost the people involved. The aftermath is people, not atmosphere. Stop on a concrete beat — a hand on the latch, the count unfinished, the engine already running — rather than a symbolic image or a summary verdict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadalae::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jin Yong / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the story on paper. Every entry is a document with an author, a recipient or purpose, and a time of composition, and the scene dictates the form: a letter home from someone working the scheme, an incident report written for a supervisor with one name the writer would rather omit, a ledger line that will need explaining later, a journal entry composed the night a meeting went wrong. Do not rotate formats for variety; use what the moment requires and let the same form recur when life does.\n\nLet each document's knowledge and blindness follow its situation. A letter to a former partner keeps the warmth of the old salutation and leaves out the suspicion its writer will not commit to ink. A report states times and access flatly and records nothing of why the writer's hand shook. A journal holds what its author could not say to anyone. Between entries the gaps work: an unanswered letter, a skipped date, a sum that appears twice under different headings, a page removed. The reader assembles the offense — its preparation, its execution, who is pursuing what — from what the papers claim, confirm, and refuse to say, with no omniscient voice arriving afterward to settle the record.\n\nPut the loyalties into the paperwork itself. Documents cross old fault lines: a formal note addressed to someone once called by a first name, a receipt kept eleven years as proof of a debt, an annotation in another hand on a letter its recipient chose not to destroy. What a writer commits to, omits, or burns on another person's behalf is the story's real action, and a recipient's choice to keep, copy, return, or destroy a document is an event in the plot, not decoration.\n\nOrder the documents so the sequence reads forward even when the accounts conflict — a later entry correcting, complicating, or quietly confirming an earlier one. Stop where the paper stops: the last letter posted, the report signed, the journal closed mid-sentence if that is where the writer's night ended. The form carries the story, and its ending is whatever the record leaves standing.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadalaf::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jin Yong / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nGive the scene's attention to care. Inside the offense and its aftermath — the money still missing, the name still in the wrong mouth, the search still running — spend the prose on chosen kindness: tea made the way one particular person drinks it, the lock checked before an exhausted guest is allowed to sleep, food arriving unasked at the room of someone who forgot to eat. Make each act specific to these people and their history rather than generic nurture, and let affection be present without being announced or repaid in confession.\n\nLet loyalty and shared history do practical work. An old ally's reliability is the safest thing in a dangerous week: the offer of a bed, the ride at four in the morning, the piece of information passed along because of who was asking. Familiar preference anchors a scene after a hard one — the brand someone keeps in stock for a friend, the seat saved, the joke only two people in the room understand. Small pleasures matter because of what surrounds them: a quiet meal after a long drive, a stretch of rest before the next task. Warmth is part of the crime's world, not an intermission from it.\n\nKeep everyone particular. Comfort does not smooth disagreement or read minds: a character can accept help while still furious, offer shelter while withholding a truth that would change what the shelter means, and hold a privacy the others have learned to respect. The scheme's dangers, debts, and competing interests remain exactly as real as the situation makes them; a warm scene neither dissolves the pursuit nor turns out to conceal a trap. Do not require a reconciliation, confession, or emotional repair to close the passage.\n\nEase can be the scene's whole subject. Let a reply consist of cooking, mending, low argument about nothing, and the ordinary maintenance of people between two difficult steps, while the world's actual pressure stays present at the edges — a phone that may ring, a story someone is careful not to tell yet.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadalag::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jin Yong / Formal Literary Novel\n\nCompose the prose with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Where the chosen narrator permits, open a scene inside its context — the district where the debt was contracted, the firm that survived two scandals and remembers both, the reputation an old ally carries into a room before he speaks. Let the framing take real paragraph space, then hand the page back to present action; it exists so the reader understands the weight of what follows, not in place of it.\n\nKeep narration distinct from speech. The narrator's sentences may be measured, subordinate, exact, while a character talks rough, fragmentary, or evasive, and the narration frames that roughness — where it comes from, whom it has served — without correcting it into elegance. The formal architecture lives in the telling, not in a cast made uniformly eloquent or a vocabulary dressed older than its world.\n\nGive encounters the author profile's generous movement: a meeting may begin with the journey to it, a confrontation may carry the history that makes it matter, and explanation, restraint, and ceremony can occupy the page alongside action. Let reputation and technique register through particular decisions framed by what they cost — who is trusted with the second key and why, who is not told, which name is kept out of the record.\n\nKeep the offense plainly legible through the distance: who prepared what, on what incentive, who is pursuing whom, and what followed. Use the remove to examine the aftermath — what the neighborhood repeats, what the institution files, what a family agrees not to say at dinner — and set public forms against private conduct where the material offers it: a hearing beside a kitchen conversation, a signed statement beside what its signer actually did. The narrator may judge, but the judgment grows out of the particular situation rather than a settled moral appended to the end. Close the passage where its social and personal attention is spent, not when every question has been answered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadalah::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jin Yong / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the outer scene as an active criminal situation: someone is being asked to account for a job, a debt, a disappearance, or a past offense while listeners hold practical power over what happens next. Make the storyteller's purpose concrete. The account may buy time, recruit help, shift suspicion, warn a listener, or preserve an alliance, and that purpose should shape what the teller includes, softens, or withholds.\n\nUse the Jin Yong profile's sense of shared history to make the embedded tale an intervention rather than a digression. The teller may describe an earlier scheme, journey, betrayal, or act of loyalty that explains a present obligation. Let the account reveal technique and restraint through particular choices: who was trusted, what was risked, what was left undone, and what the teller still considers honorable or unforgivable. The outer listeners should respond to those choices, not only to the facts.\n\nKeep the narrative levels intelligible by returning to the outer scene at moments when the telling changes the present balance. A listener may interrupt to question a sequence, supply a detail the teller omitted, or act on what the account implies. Repeated phrases or objects should accrue meaning across levels: a token, a name, a promise, or a route mentioned in the embedded story can reappear in the outer scene as evidence, leverage, or a test of loyalty.\n\nDistinguish what the original participants knew from what the teller has learned since. The account may be sincere and still be partial, self-serving, or shaped by a later allegiance. Do not require the telling to be false or the teller to confess. Let the outer scene end when the telling has produced its immediate effect on the listeners, whether that effect is cooperation, refusal, exposure, or a new obligation. Preserve the crime's human aftermath through the relationships the telling revises, without importing a court, magical bargain, or compulsory cliffhanger between every nested account.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadalai::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jin Yong / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social or developmental realism route of the German-language tradition. Write the crime as a process embedded in particular institutions, workplaces, neighborhoods, families, or legal arrangements. Show how an offense becomes possible through ordinary conditions: a job that creates temptation, a regulation that rewards concealment, a debt that narrows choices, or a loyalty that outlasts its original purpose. Keep the narration close to the chosen viewpoint so the reader sees how a person comes to act, not merely that an act occurred.\n\nLet the Jin Yong profile's tension between private attachment and public obligation emerge through concrete decisions. A character may protect a colleague, honor a promise, or preserve a reputation while knowing what the law or the institution requires. Do not treat that tension as a simple choice between good and bad. Show the particular costs: what the character risks by speaking, what relationship the silence protects, and what public consequence the loyalty may produce. Give technique and restraint weight by making the character's competence visible in preparation, timing, and the management of other people's expectations.\n\nKeep scenic movement generous. Let encounters develop through travel, work, conversation, and explanation rather than compressing the story into a sequence of decisive blows. Use syntax and narrative distance appropriate to a composed realist narrator: sentences may carry subordinate clauses that connect motive, condition, and consequence, but the prose should remain clear and grounded in observable social detail. Avoid bureaucratic severity, national stereotyping, or translated word order.\n\nEnd the response at a point where the social process has advanced: a decision has been made, a relationship has shifted, or the aftermath has begun to show its shape. The crime need not be solved or punished within the response. Preserve the human aftermath by showing how the offense continues to affect the people who prepared it, suffered it, or covered it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadalaj::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jin Yong / Gothic-Punk\n\nWrite the crime as a collision between inherited authority and present urban life. The offense or scheme should depend on a particular arrangement: an old debt, a family obligation, a former alliance, or a reputation that still controls access to a building, a business, a record, or a person's cooperation. Show the modern mechanisms through which that old authority operates—keys, accounts, schedules, surveillance, employment, housing—so the atmosphere is grounded in practical material detail rather than mood alone.\n\nUse the Jin Yong profile's tension between private attachment and public obligation to make the crime personally costly. A character may act out of loyalty to someone the present world has left behind, or may betray an old alliance to protect a current relationship. Let the social mask fall in trusted company: a private conversation can reveal desire, fear, or resentment that the character would not show in public. Do not make every intimacy corrupt or every caring act fraudulent; the atmosphere should complicate trust, not abolish it.\n\nGive encounters room for travel, explanation, and relationships. Describe the city as a place where old and new systems overlap: a historic building repurposed for modern business, a family name that still opens a door, a debt recorded in a ledger no one officially acknowledges. Let atmosphere change how a particular encounter is experienced—a familiar hallway can feel like a trap when the character knows who else has a key—but keep the source of that feeling concrete.\n\nPreserve the crime's human aftermath through divided loyalty and exposure. The response may end while a character is still deciding whether to honor an old obligation or protect a present relationship. Do not import vampire physiology, factions, powers, or blood-bond rules. The inherited power here is social, economic, or institutional, and its costs remain human.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadalak::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jin Yong / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social/domestic realism route of the Indian literary tradition. Write the crime through the particular texture of family, household, neighborhood, workplace, and community. Show how an offense or scheme grows from ordinary obligations: a relative's need, a business arrangement, a marriage negotiation, a property dispute, or a debt that binds people who share a history. Keep the language, region, community, and period specific when the story supplies them, without forcing English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure onto the scene.\n\nLet the Jin Yong profile's tension between private attachment and public obligation emerge through domestic decisions. A character may conceal a relative's offense, protect a family reputation, or break a promise to someone outside the household. Show the particular cost of each choice: what the household expects, what the community will say, and what the character privately believes is right. Do not reduce these conflicts to a simple opposition between tradition and individual freedom. The obligations themselves may be experienced as love, duty, resentment, or all three at once.\n\nGive encounters room for travel, explanation, and relationships. Let scenes unfold in kitchens, shops, offices, courtyards, or vehicles where people negotiate practical matters while their loyalties remain visible in small gestures: who is served first, who is told the truth, who is asked to wait. Make technique and restraint matter through particular decisions about money, timing, documents, and the management of other people's expectations.\n\nEnd the response at a point where the domestic and social consequences of the crime have become concrete: a relationship has shifted, a secret has been kept or shared, or the aftermath has begun to reshape the household. The crime need not be solved or punished within the response. Preserve the human aftermath by showing how the offense continues to live in the relationships it has strained or protected.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadalal::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jin Yong / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social or psychological realism route of the Japanese literary tradition. Write the crime through the particular pressures of obligation, reputation, and practical survival. Show how an offense or scheme becomes possible through a character's situation: a workplace hierarchy, a family expectation, a debt, a promise, or a fear of exposure. Keep the narration close to the chosen viewpoint so the reader sees the character's reasoning, hesitation, and private cost as the situation develops.\n\nLet the Jin Yong profile's tension between private attachment and public obligation emerge through concrete decisions. A character may protect a colleague, honor a promise, or preserve a reputation while knowing what the law, the employer, or the community expects. Do not treat that tension as a simple choice between good and bad. Show the particular costs: what the character risks by speaking, what relationship the silence protects, and what public consequence the loyalty may produce. Make technique and restraint matter by showing competence in preparation, timing, and the management of other people's expectations.\n\nGive encounters room for travel, explanation, and relationships. Let scenes unfold in offices, homes, stations, or streets where people negotiate practical matters while their loyalties remain visible in small gestures: who is told the truth, who is asked to wait, who is given an excuse. Use personal language and narrative distance appropriate to the actual teller. Seasonal attention, omission, direct feeling, and plain argument are available where the story permits, but do not impose a universal quietness or a mandatory inventory of Japanese scenery.\n\nEnd the response at a point where the social or psychological pressure has advanced: a decision has been made, a relationship has shifted, or the aftermath has begun to show its shape. The crime need not be solved or punished within the response. Preserve the human aftermath by showing how the offense continues to affect the people who prepared it, suffered it, or covered it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadalam::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jin Yong / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social realism/chronicle route of the Latin American literary tradition. Write the crime as a social process embedded in particular institutions, neighborhoods, economies, and histories. Show how an offense or scheme becomes possible through ordinary conditions: a job that creates temptation, a market that rewards smuggling, a police force that cannot protect everyone, a debt that narrows choices, or a loyalty that outlasts its original purpose. Preserve the relevant language, community, and history when the story supplies them, without manufacturing a generic regional temperament or relocating the cast.\n\nLet the Jin Yong profile's tension between private attachment and public obligation emerge through concrete decisions. A character may protect a neighbor, honor a promise, or preserve a reputation while knowing what the law, the employer, or the community expects. Do not treat that tension as a simple choice between good and bad. Show the particular costs: what the character risks by speaking, what relationship the silence protects, and what public consequence the loyalty may produce. Make technique and restraint matter by showing competence in preparation, timing, and the management of other people's expectations.\n\nGive encounters room for travel, explanation, and relationships. Let scenes unfold in markets, offices, homes, buses, or streets where people negotiate practical matters while their loyalties remain visible in small gestures: who is told the truth, who is asked to wait, who is given an excuse. Use the chronicle's attention to social texture without turning the prose into a clinical case summary. Distinguish observation, memory, report, inference, and conclusion as the story requires.\n\nEnd the response at a point where the social process has advanced: a decision has been made, a relationship has shifted, or the aftermath has begun to show its shape. The crime need not be solved or punished within the response. Preserve the human aftermath by showing how the offense continues to affect the people who prepared it, suffered it, or covered it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadalan::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jin Yong / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate criminal situation cleanly. Open with a concrete scene: a meeting, a handoff, a debt, a discovery, or a confrontation that already carries the offense or its consequences. Keep spatial changes easy to follow by naming the location and the movement between locations in plain terms—who enters, who leaves, what changes hands, what is visible. Use short, readable paragraphs around action, dialogue, and private response so the reader can track both the external event and the focal character's reaction without losing the scene.\n\nLet the Jin Yong profile's tension between private attachment and public obligation emerge through particular decisions staged for clarity. A character may protect a partner, honor a promise, or preserve a reputation while knowing what the job, the law, or the group expects. Show the decision through action and dialogue: who is told the truth, who is given an excuse, who is asked to wait. Follow each significant beat with a focused reaction—serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to its owner—that makes the personal cost legible without turning every character into a quip machine.\n\nGive unfamiliar names or mechanics clear presentation when needed. If a scheme depends on a particular arrangement—a route, a code, a schedule, a debt—explain it briefly at the point where the character uses or confronts it. Do not bury the explanation in a glossary paragraph. Let technique and restraint matter by showing competence in preparation, timing, and the management of other people's expectations.\n\nEnd the response at a natural continuation point: after a decision has been made, a relationship has shifted, or the aftermath has begun to show its shape. The crime need not be solved or punished within the response. Preserve the human aftermath through the focal character's reaction and the immediate consequences for the people involved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadalao::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jin Yong / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite the crime as an event whose causes and consequences become present through shaped images, recurring sounds, and the narrator's associative attention. Let a physical detail carry a practical fact: the clean edge of a torn receipt can mark where an account was altered, the repeated sound of a gate can measure who came and went, the weight of a handed object can hold an unspoken obligation. The image must remain traceable to the scene or the teller's memory and must not replace the actual sequence of preparation, decision, and aftermath.\n\nUse the lyric paragraph to move among action, perception, and inward recognition without losing the event's causal order. A comparison may unfold when it changes what the narrator understands about another person's loyalty or about the cost of a choice. For example, a character may see a familiar room as a place already divided by the scheme, the arrangement of chairs and the unwashed cup becoming evidence of who waited there and what they feared. Keep plain statements available for contrast so the prose does not turn every fact into ornament.\n\nPreserve Jin Yong's generous scenic movement by giving encounters room for travel, explanation, and the pressure between private attachment and public obligation. Let technique and restraint matter through particular decisions: a character may choose silence not as concealment alone but as a way of protecting someone whose reputation the crime has already damaged. Reputation and alliance should be tested by what people do, not announced by the narrator. The lyric register belongs to the chosen viewpoint, so other characters may speak plainly while the focal character notices the image that persists after the words are finished.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadalap::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jin Yong / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the offense be understood through small irreversible changes and remembered occasions that reveal what the characters have lost or can no longer rely upon. A character may notice that a door once left open is now locked, that a shared meal has become a careful negotiation, or that a familiar route now passes the place where a promise was broken. The reflection should arise from the actual scene and its consequences, not from a general announcement that everything passes.\n\nUse the reflective interval to examine the tension between private attachment and public obligation. A character may recall an earlier act of loyalty and measure it against what the present crime demands. The memory can change the meaning of a current choice without replacing that choice: remembering who taught a skill may make its criminal use more costly, but the character must still decide whether to continue. Keep the remembered occasion specific and allow present experience to coexist with what is no longer available. Ordinary pleasure can remain pleasurable even while its context has changed.\n\nPreserve Jin Yong's generous scenic movement by giving the aftermath room for travel, explanation, and relationship. Let reputation and alliance be tested through particular decisions rather than summarized as regret. The narrator may reflect directly or let a detail carry the association, but the account must remain attached to the crime's actual sequence. A scene can end with a task resumed or a question left open; it need not conclude by declaring impermanence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadalaq::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jin Yong / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a situated framed telling to carry the crime. The route is urban domestic realism delivered orally: a speaker inside one household or workplace account narrates the offense, its preparation, or its aftermath to listeners who have their own stake in the version being told. Establish who is speaking, to whom, and under what pressure. The frame works as suspense because the teller chooses what to admit, what to delay, and what another listener already knows; the audience's interruptions can expose a competing loyalty or redirect the account toward the part someone does not want examined.\n\nLet the framed story preserve Jin Yong's generous scenic movement and the tension between private attachment and public obligation. Inside the told scenes, give encounters room for travel, explanation, and relationship. A character's decision to protect a relative, a partner, or a trusted ally should emerge through particular actions: handing over keys, lying at a checkpoint, sending someone away before the police arrive, or refusing to repeat a name. Do not announce the obligation as a code. The teller may emphasize a recurring phrase or remembered detail when it helps the listeners understand why an act mattered, but the recurrence must be earned by the events rather than assigned as ornament.\n\nKeep the selected tradition's language and setting distinct. Do not import a universal honor code, religious explanation, or mystical frame unless the supplied world already contains it. The telling may use the material detail of an apartment, market, office, or vehicle and the social pressure of family, work, and neighbors. The story's authority comes from a person who was present, heard the account, or assembled the evidence, and whose own loyalties may shape what the audience is told. A listener's question can force the teller to choose between protecting someone and completing the account.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadalar::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jin Yong / Minimalist Prose\n\nUse a small number of exact details to carry the offense and its consequences. Each detail should do practical work in the crime's sequence: a dropped glove at the doorway locates a moment in the preparation, a locked drawer connects a decision to the execution, a changed phone contact marks the aftermath. Choose the detail that lets the reader infer the next step rather than explaining every movement. Omission should leave something inferable, not delete the reason an act mattered or the connection between evidence and decision.\n\nLet direct clauses carry Jin Yong's tension between private attachment and public obligation. A character's loyalty can be shown by a single action: returning the money, taking the blame, leaving the door open for someone who must escape. Do not explain the loyalty unless the explanation materially changes the present account. Give encounters enough room to matter, but keep the room economical. Travel and explanation may occupy a few precise sentences rather than a broad scenic expansion; what matters is that the encounter changes what the character can do next.\n\nPreserve individual voices. Dialogue may be terse, but different characters should not all speak in the same clipped register. A precise extended sentence may serve the style better than several performed fragments when an essential thought needs to be understood. End the response where the present action or understanding reaches a natural pause; do not add a summary or a lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadalas::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jin Yong / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the crime in sustained scene-first prose, moving naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and the viewpoint character's authorized inner experience. Let the scene itself orient the reader: a conversation about an unpaid debt can be interrupted by the arrival of the person owed, and the interruption can expose a divided loyalty without a narrator's summary. Use paragraphing and transitions to shift location, time, or attention without recap machinery such as \"earlier, she had decided\" unless the shift itself is part of the character's present thought.\n\nPreserve Jin Yong's generous scenic movement by giving encounters room for travel, explanation, and relationship. A journey to deliver a payment or warn an ally can include the practical details of the route and the changing terms of the obligation. Let technique and restraint matter through particular decisions: a character may choose to say less than they know, but the narration should show what that choice costs or protects. Reputation and alliance should be tested by what people do in the scene, not announced by the narrator.\n\nKeep the account attached to the offense's actual sequence of preparation, incentives, execution, evidence, pursuit, and aftermath. Suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did it, because the scene can show what the characters risk by continuing. The narrator's voice, the author profile, and the immediate scene set the register and rhythm. A response can end during continuing speech, action, thought, or an unfinished setup; it need not conclude with a completed revelation or a lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadalat::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jin Yong / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship the organizing lens for the crime. Choose scenes that show how the offense, scheme, or aftermath presses on the particular bond: a shared task becomes a test of trust, a secret becomes a measure of what one character will risk for the other, a reunion after a dangerous errand carries the weight of what could have been lost. Use only the actual supplied premise and characters; do not import a roster of familiar tropes. The relationship's promise should be legible through what these people do and say to each other.\n\nLet private language and repeated moments do the work of continuity. A phrase, gesture, or place that appeared earlier can return when the relationship's stakes have changed, and the callback should reveal the change rather than merely repeat it. Give affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery enough space where the scene genuinely calls for it. The emotional beat must remain connected to the crime's incentives and consequences: comfort may follow a dangerous decision, jealousy may arise from a divided loyalty, discovery may expose what one character concealed to protect the other.\n\nPreserve Jin Yong's tension between private attachment and public obligation by letting the relationship complicate the characters' choices. A character may act against an ally, break a promise, or accept blame because of what the relationship demands, but the decision must be shown as a particular choice with costs. Keep the narration's access consistent with the active viewpoint. The response can end during continuing speech, action, thought, or an unfinished setup; it need not resolve the relationship or the crime before stopping.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadalau::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jin Yong / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the crime as an account of deeds whose consequences will be remembered. Open with a deliberate declaration that establishes the offense or the person involved, then expand into the particulars that make the reputation incomplete. A recurring phrase should come from an established act or way of speaking, not from an ornamental epithet assigned to every entrance. Return to the phrase when a later occurrence gives it a new application, such as when the same promise is kept, broken, or remembered differently by those who were present. Let recurrence mark the progress of the crime's preparation, execution, and aftermath, so that a repeated name or debt becomes part of the evidence of how the offense changed the characters' obligations.\n\nLet the chronicle preserve Jin Yong's generous scenic movement and the tension between private attachment and public obligation. Give encounters room for travel, explanation, and relationship. Oaths, debts, alliances, and public deeds can organize the account when the story establishes them, but do not mix every epic convention or import a Nordic pantheon, heroic doom, or ceremonial dialogue into every speaker. Keep the particular source tradition coherent. Reputation should be tested by what people do, and the teller may note how an act will be remembered without declaring it legend on the community's behalf.\n\nUse recurrence to carry the crime's evidence and aftermath. A place revisited, a name repeated, or a debt recounted can show how the offense changed the characters' obligations. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective. The account can end with business concluded or resumed, leaving another act available to be remembered rather than forcing a completed judgment or a lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadalav::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jin Yong / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the crime through a definite social-realist route: the offense arises from and is investigated within particular institutions, workplaces, families, and neighborhoods. Let the reader see who is obligated to whom, what a job or public role costs privately, and how a scheme becomes possible because people know each other's habits, debts, and histories. The crime need not be a puzzle; suspense can come from exposure, divided loyalty, or the gap between what an institution officially records and what its members actually know.\n\nUse Jin Yong's generous scenic movement by giving encounters room for travel, explanation, and relationship work. A conversation about a missing payment, a disputed route, or a colleague's changed behavior can unfold across a walk, a meal, or a shift rather than compressing into a single exchange of information. Let technique and restraint matter through particular decisions: who chooses to wait, who refuses to sign, who covers for a friend, who reports a superior, and what each choice costs in reputation or standing.\n\nKeep private attachment and public obligation in visible tension. A character may act correctly in public while privately resenting, doubting, or protecting someone. Show the specific institutional pressure behind that tension: a workplace rule, a housing arrangement, a family expectation, a union or professional code, a welfare or legal process. Do not make every institution corrupt or every loyalty a betrayal; some obligations are genuinely held, and some help is real.\n\nGround the aftermath in ordinary social consequence. After the offense is exposed or concealed, show who keeps a job, who loses a home, who must explain to a spouse or parent, who is believed, and who is quietly avoided. Let these consequences unfold in scenes of daily life: a kitchen conversation, a bus ride, a workplace meeting, a neighbor's visit. The style does not require snow, isolation, or bleakness; use the actual setting and community supplied by the story.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadalaw::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jin Yong / Screenplay\n\nWrite the crime in screen-oriented scenes: concise action lines that show what is visible or audible, clear speaker cues, and scene headings when location or time changes. Let dialogue carry relationship history, obligation, and divided loyalty rather than compressing every exchange into plot information. A scene can follow two people through a location while they talk about a debt, a shared past, or a choice that implicates someone they both know.\n\nGive encounters room to move. Use action lines to show travel, waiting, work, or a change of place while the conversation continues. A discussion can begin in a car, continue across a parking lot, and end at a door; the movement is visible, not narrated as interior reflection. Let restraint and technique appear in what characters do with their hands, what they choose not to say, and how they position themselves relative to each other.\n\nKeep private experience as a clearly attributed nonspoken note when the active contract requires it. Such a note is not dialogue and is not available to other characters. It may appear as a brief attributed line separate from the spoken exchange, recording what a character thinks or feels without turning it into voice-over or a camera command. Do not use cuts, offscreen sound, or voice-over unless the chosen sequence specifically needs that device.\n\nLet suspense come from exposure and divided loyalty rather than from withholding the culprit's identity. A scene can show two allies realizing they have protected different versions of the same event. End a scene on a visible result, a spoken decision, or a character's exit; do not require a cliffhanger or a completed revelation in every scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadalax::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jin Yong / Stage Play\n\nWrite the crime as a playable script: clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be staged. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene. A character can check a door, pour a drink, hand over a key, or turn away while speaking; the action is observable and meaningful, not decorative.\n\nUse the shared stage space to test loyalty and obligation. Who can see an entrance, reach an object, hear a line, or move between two places should be clear enough that a character's claim can be checked against what the audience observed. Let technique and restraint appear in what characters do with objects, how they wait, and when they choose to speak or remain silent. A pause can carry as much weight as a line.\n\nKeep required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation. It is not a monologue, an aside, or something another character can hear. If a monologue or aside is used, give it a dramatic purpose within the scene rather than using it to replace every private thought. Do not add film-camera instructions.\n\nLet the crime's aftermath unfold through staged consequence. A character may be asked to leave, refused a seat, handed a letter, or met with silence. Suspense can come from exposure or divided loyalty even when the audience knows who did it. End a scene on an exit, an entrance, a completed action, or a spoken decision; do not require a theatrical revelation or a gathering of suspects in every scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadalay::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jin Yong / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite the crime through the associative mental movement of a particular authorized mind. Braid perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern. A character can notice a smell, remember a promise, hear a phrase from years ago, feel the weight of a phone, and return to a debt or a loyalty that will not settle. Syntax and paragraph shape may follow those associations.\n\nKeep enough orientation to distinguish events from recollection or fantasy. When the mind moves from a present scene to a memory, make the shift legible through a sensory anchor, a repeated phrase, or a clear return to the present. Do not make every scene fact indeterminate; the crime's actual events remain real even when the focal mind circles them.\n\nLet Jin Yong's tension between private attachment and public obligation surface as recurring concern. The mind may return to a person it protected, a promise it broke, a role it performed, or a consequence it cannot undo. These returns are not repetition for its own sake; each return revises or deepens what the earlier association meant. A memory can acquire new significance after a later fact without erasing what actually happened.\n\nUse the chosen viewpoint's access strictly. The style does not make every character share a mind or authorize human-owned thoughts. Another character's private experience remains outside the focal mind's knowledge unless the active contract grants access. Ordinary reasoning alone is not this stylistic mode; the prose must follow associative movement, not a linear summary of the investigation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadalaz::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jin Yong / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the crime through readable sender-attributed messages. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: who writes in full sentences, who sends fragments, who delays, who edits, who leaves a message unread, who types and stops. These habits are character evidence, not decoration. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the story.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought. If context outside the exchange is required, present it clearly separated from the transcript and do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext.\n\nLet Jin Yong's tension between private attachment and public obligation appear in what people choose to send, withhold, or phrase carefully. A character may send a loyal message to one person while delaying a reply to another. A group chat can expose a divided loyalty when someone is included, excluded, or addressed in a tone that shifts. Reputation matters through what is forwarded, screenshotted, or left unanswered.\n\nLet suspense come from exposure and divided loyalty rather than from a hidden culprit. The reader may know who did it while watching others discover the fact through messages. End an exchange on a sent message, a read receipt, a draft, or a silence; do not require a cliffhanger or a completed revelation in every exchange.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadalba::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jin Yong / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the crime in scene-oriented visual novel presentation: concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Let a background or expression cue orient the moment rather than function as an automatic truth indicator. A character's changed expression can attract attention, but what they say, do, or fail to do must carry the weight of suspicion or trust.\n\nGive encounters room for travel, explanation, and relationships. A scene can move from one location to another with a legible scene change while the conversation continues. Let dialogue carry shared history, obligation, and divided loyalty rather than compressing every exchange into plot information. Two allies can talk through what they owe each other while walking, waiting, or working.\n\nUse focal narration or inner response to show how the viewpoint character explains another person's behavior. The next exchange may support that explanation, complicate it, or reveal that the speaker was concerned with something else entirely. Keep private thought outside other characters' knowledge. Do not turn the cast into fixed reaction portraits whose expression always reveals whether they lie.\n\nLet suspense come from exposure and divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did it. A scene can end on a spoken decision, a completed action, or a character's exit. The style does not activate branching menus, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands. Preserve whichever authorship contract is active.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadalbb::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jin Yong / Web Serial\n\nWrite the crime in installment-oriented prose with a readable through-line across replies. Each installment should make clear what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover context briefly only when it helps: a name, a debt, a promise, or a place can be recalled in a phrase rather than retelling the whole history that established it.\n\nLet Jin Yong's generous scenic movement unfold across installments. An encounter can span more than one reply without each installment being a complete miniature chapter. A conversation about a shared past, a divided loyalty, or a scheme's cost can pause at a natural point and resume with the relationship still in motion. Do not force a cliffhanger at every break; an installment can end on a completed action, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known.\n\nLet earned developments accumulate. Knowledge, relationships, work, and resources can change across replies. A character's reputation may shift because of what they did two installments ago, and a loyalty may be tested by a later fact. These developments are earned by the actual events, not inserted as sudden reversals. The style does not automatically create leveling systems, rankings, or progression-fantasy world rules.\n\nLet suspense come from exposure and divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did it. An installment can show one character discovering what the reader already knows, or two allies realizing they have protected different versions of the same event. Preserve required inward experience when it contributes feeling or thought, even if no immediate decision changes.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadamaa::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jun Maeda / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the crime's aftermath through the specific shared life of the viewpoint character and someone implicated in what happened. Choose a repeated object, routine, phrase, or place that belonged to their ordinary attachment before the offense. Return to it at moments when the character needs comfort, must decide whether to speak, or tries to resume a familiar pattern. Let the repeated detail now carry the cost of that choice: the object may be handled differently, the routine may continue with a silence in it, the phrase may be said too carefully or not at all.\n\nMake emotional exposure concrete through behavior and attention rather than through a fixed vocabulary of anguish. Show what the character notices, avoids, starts to say, or does with their hands during a difficult conversation. Let strain alter the actual exchange: a question may be answered with a practical remark, an apology may arrive too late or in the wrong form, and a need may be admitted only after the other person has turned away. Warmth can remain present without resolving the hurt. A shared meal, an old joke, or a remembered kindness may ease one moment and sharpen the next.\n\nKeep the crime's consequences active in the relationship. The character may be protecting someone, hiding their own part, waiting for an arrest, managing money, or living with what an accomplice did. Do not let emotional turbulence replace the factual situation. Instead, let each new development in the case or its aftermath press against the relationship: a phone call interrupts a reconciliation, a piece of evidence makes an earlier reassurance feel false, or a routine errand becomes a test of whether the character can still trust the other person.\n\nAllow comfort to be partial and awkward. A sincere gesture may be accepted without being believed, or refused without ending the attachment. The character can feel relief and resentment in the same scene. Preserve the difference between what the viewpoint character privately experiences and what the other person knows. The narration may reveal a motive, a fear, or a withheld fact while the dialogue continues on a safer subject. End the scene where the emotional situation has genuinely moved, not necessarily where it has been repaired.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadamab::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jun Maeda / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the crime scene as a coherent shared interval with clear speakers and readable paragraph breaks. Give each line of dialogue an unmistakable attribution, and keep action beats close to the speaker so the reader can follow who is doing what. Let the player's character and the NPCs respond to the immediate situation: someone may check a door, answer a message, compare a story, or decide whether to leave. Do not require a question, cliffhanger, or forced choice at the end. Stop at a point where the next move follows naturally from what has just happened.\n\nUse a repeated object, routine, or phrase from the characters' shared life to make the crime's consequences feel personal. The player's character may notice the same takeout container, bus stop, ringtone, or habit that belonged to an earlier, safer time. Let the repetition appear in the scene without stopping the action for a long explanation. A brief recognition, a hesitation, or a change in how someone performs the routine can show that the offense has altered the relationship.\n\nKeep NPCs active in their own practical lives. They may cooperate, refuse, avoid a question, offer help, or pursue their own errand while the conversation continues. Their choices should follow from their situation in the crime rather than from a need to supply the player with a prompt. Let the player's character act and speak according to their selected person and perspective. The narration may reveal what the player's character privately notices or suspects, but do not grant access to NPC thoughts unless the active contract permits it.\n\nAdvance the crime's present moment through concrete exchanges and small physical actions. A piece of information may arrive in dialogue, a character may recognize a lie, or a plan may shift because someone is late. Do not force a reversal or confession into every reply. The scene can end with the characters still deciding, waiting, or moving toward the next step. Preserve the active world and any ongoing pursuit, investigation, or cover-up so the next response can continue from the same situation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadamac::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jun Maeda / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic telling route. Structure the crime narrative as a sequence of connected episodes, each with a clear present situation and a return to something from the characters' shared life. Open an episode by establishing where the viewpoint character is, what practical matter occupies them, and what part of the offense or its aftermath presses on the scene. Close the episode when that situation has moved forward, even if the larger case remains unresolved.\n\nLet a repeated object, routine, phrase, or relationship anchor the episodes. The same market stall, family meal, borrowed coat, account book, or habitual saying can appear in different circumstances. Each return should revise what the detail means: an object that once signaled ordinary care may later mark a debt, a lie, or a loss. Do not explain the whole history at every appearance. Let the reader recognize the recurrence and feel the changed weight through the character's altered response.\n\nKeep the telling direct and concrete. Prefer plain movement, reported speech, and observable behavior over elaborate psychological commentary. Show divided loyalty through what a character does: they may shelter a relative, hide money, repeat a false account, or serve two parties while trying to protect someone. Let suspense come from exposure and the strain of maintaining a position, not only from an unknown culprit. The reader may know who committed the offense while the characters do not, or the characters may know while the law does not.\n\nUse the episode's social setting to make the crime's consequences legible. Neighbors, family members, creditors, officials, or workmates may appear with their own interests and judgments. Their talk can pressure the viewpoint character without becoming an omniscient chorus. Preserve the difference between what the community believes and what the viewpoint character knows. Let humor or tenderness appear where it belongs to the people and the scene, especially in small familiar exchanges that later turn painful. Do not transplant a particular historical period or cast; keep the language, period, and community supplied by the active world.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadamad::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jun Maeda / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the crime in hard, concrete prose. Use forceful verbs and specific physical detail: the weight of a bag, the condition of a lock, the sound of a vehicle outside, the smell of a room where someone has been waiting. Move between beats with compressed transitions. Cut the connective tissue and let the next action or observation carry the reader forward. When a character needs to reason through a choice, allow a longer thought to hold the moment; otherwise keep the prose tight.\n\nAnchor the author profile's repeated details in material objects and routines. Choose something the character handles or passes every day: a phone, a jacket, a doorway, a receipt, a set of keys, a bus route. Return to it at intervals. Let wear and damage show the changed situation. The object may be cracked, missing, cleaned too often, or used differently after the offense. Do not stop the scene to explain its history. A brief callback, a hesitation, or a change in the routine can carry the weight.\n\nMake physical stakes immediate. The character may be short on money, injured, watched, carrying evidence, or trying to keep someone safe. Let the danger appear in practical terms: a door that will not lock, a phone with no charge, a person who should have arrived, a wound that slows them down. Keep abrasive or rough speech grounded in the speaker. Not every character talks hard; let their diction follow their situation and temperament.\n\nVary the rhythm. Short declarative sentences can drive pursuit, confrontation, or a moment of exposure. A longer passage can hold calculation, regret, or the effort of deciding what to do with what the character knows. Do not turn the crime into survival horror or force a tragic outcome. The story remains about the offense and its human aftermath, told with pressure and material precision.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadamae::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jun Maeda / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the crime and its aftermath through documents. Each entry must have a clear author, an intended recipient or purpose, and a time of composition. Use the document form the scene actually requires: a report, a letter, a receipt, a journal page, a message log, a note left on a table, a statement given to someone official. Do not rotate through formats mechanically. Let the form shape what the writer records and what they omit.\n\nUse the author profile's repeated details across entries. Choose an object, routine, phrase, or relationship that several document writers mention in different circumstances. The same borrowed money, shared meal, street corner, nickname, or household habit can appear in a report, a private note, and a later apology. Each recurrence should add or revise meaning. One writer may describe the object as ordinary, another as evidence, another as a reminder of what they did. The reader assembles the fuller picture from the differences.\n\nKeep each document's knowledge and concealment consistent with its author and situation. A person writing to the police may omit what they fear. A person writing to a friend may soften or exaggerate. A journal may record a suspicion the writer would not speak aloud. Do not add an omniscient explanation after every record. Let gaps, revisions, and conflicting accounts reveal events gradually. A date, a crossed-out line, a missing page, or a change in handwriting can be a material trace.\n\nPreserve readable continuity across the sequence. Arrange the documents so the reader can follow the offense, its preparation, its consequences, and the relationships under strain. Suspense can come from exposure and divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did what. A later document may correct an earlier one, but it cannot change what the earlier writer knew at the time. Let tenderness or humor appear in the documents where the writer would naturally include it, especially in small familiar references that later turn painful.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadamaf::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jun Maeda / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite the crime's aftermath with attention to chosen kindness and familiar care. The offense and its consequences remain real: the character may be waiting for news, managing money, avoiding someone, or living with what a relative did. Within that situation, let the story spend time on a small, sincere comfort. Someone may bring food, remember a preference, share a quiet task, or keep a promise that matters only to the two of them. Do not let the warmth erase the crime. The danger, the uncertainty, and the disagreements stay present.\n\nUse the author profile's repeated details to make the comfort specific. Choose an object, routine, phrase, or relationship the characters shared before the offense. Return to it as a source of ease: the same tea, the same route home, the same joke, the same way of folding a blanket or leaving a light on. Let the repetition feel like continuity rather than a forced symbol. The familiar detail may comfort one character and remind another of what has changed. Both responses can be true.\n\nKeep personality and privacy intact. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable. A character may accept care while still angry, offer help while withholding a confession, or enjoy a shared meal and then return to a difficult errand. Affection can be sincere without requiring an emotional repair or a declaration. The viewpoint character may privately notice the kindness and say nothing, or may respond awkwardly because they do not know how to receive it.\n\nLet the scene move through practical care and small pleasures rather than a single dramatic revelation. A quiet interval can include a task completed together, a remembered preference honored, or a moment of ease before the next difficulty. The crime's pressure may return at the end of the scene, but it does not have to cancel the comfort. End where the characters have had a genuine moment of warmth, not where every problem has been solved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadamag::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jun Maeda / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite the crime and its aftermath in composed novel prose. Use deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Let a scene open with enough social context to place the characters: the neighborhood, the household, the workplace, the court, the family's standing, or the practical arrangements that the offense has disturbed. Where the chosen narrator permits, frame the action through history, reputation, or judgment. Distinguish the narration from ordinary spoken language. The sentences may be more measured than the dialogue, but they should not become uniformly archaic or elaborate.\n\nMove from the specific shared life toward a changed understanding of it. Choose a repeated object, routine, phrase, or relationship that belongs to the characters' daily attachment. Return to it at intervals within the longer narrative. A paragraph may describe the object in its ordinary use, and a later paragraph may return to it after an arrest, a confession, a departure, or a death. Let the recurrence carry the shift in understanding without stating the theme directly. The narration can show the character noticing the difference, or can leave the reader to feel it through the altered description.\n\nUse the formal architecture to hold public and private registers in the same passage. A scene may move from what the neighbors say, or what the law requires, to what the viewpoint character privately remembers or fears. The social context should press on the relationship without replacing it. Family obligation, reputation, money, and legal consequence can shape what the characters say and what they postpone. Keep the crime's factual situation active: the evidence, the pursuit, the divided loyalty, or the practical burden of the aftermath.\n\nLet tenderness and humor appear where they belong to the people and the scene, especially in small familiar exchanges that the formal narration can observe with care. Do not force a tragic or miraculous repair. The story may end with the factual question settled but the relationship still altered, or with a partial reconciliation that leaves the larger cost visible. Preserve the viewpoint character's private knowledge and the limits of what others know.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadamah::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jun Maeda / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the crime story as an account being given to a specific listener who shares part of the teller's daily life. The telling must have a live purpose in the outer scene: to persuade the listener to act, to justify an absence or choice, to prepare for a visitor or consequence, or to unburden a memory that has begun to disturb a shared routine. Open with the occasion and the teller's immediate stake; the outer scene should not be a neutral frame but a relationship under pressure.\n\nLet the embedded account follow the actual sequence of the offense and its aftermath, filtered through the teller's present relationship to the listener. The teller selects details, returns to familiar objects, and repeats phrases the listener already knows from their shared life. A cup, a route, a greeting, or a small ritual can appear first in the outer scene and then inside the told events, where it acquires a darker or more complicated meaning. When the listener notices the repetition, show that recognition in the outer frame: a pause, a question, a refusal to interrupt, or a changed way of handling the familiar object. Do not let the same object pass through more than one narrative level without the listener's visible reaction.\n\nDo not deliver the embedded tale as a single confession. Interrupt the telling at least once with the listener's practical concern, memory, or request for clarification, and let the teller resume or revise. The interruption should be prompted by the teller's own emphasis or omission, creating a moment where the listener's knowledge of the shared routine threatens to expose what the teller is not saying. After the interruption, let the teller adjust the account rather than simply continue: they may supply a new detail, skip a part, or frame the offense differently. Keep the levels clearly marked through the repeated objects and the listener's reactions, not through explanatory signposting.\n\nTreat the crime as an event with human causes and consequences, not a puzzle to be solved by the listener. The teller may not understand every motive, and the listener may supply an interpretation from their own knowledge of the teller's habits. When the telling ends, do not force a full reconciliation or a complete explanation. End on the listener's changed understanding of the shared routine and the teller's particular weight from having made that change possible. Use the Arabian Nights craft of purposeful embedded narration without adding a royal court, magical bargain, or compulsory cliffhanger between nested sections.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadamai::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jun Maeda / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the documentary and historical-memory route. Write the crime from a viewpoint that can compare records with remembered conditions: a former neighbor, a clerk, a relative, an investigator returning to a place, or someone assembling an account years after the event. Let the prose move between documents and personal recollection, but give each movement a specific trigger in the narrative, not a general interest in the past.\n\nAnchor the comparison in a repeated object, routine, or phrase from the Maeda profile. Before any document appears, establish this detail as part of the narrator's daily attachment: a workplace habit, a shared meal, a seasonal task, or a familiar saying. Show the narrator engaging with it as ordinary life, so that its later documentary surfacing can change how the narrator understands that attachment.\n\nIntroduce the document through a concrete encounter or discovery, not as an abstract illustration. The recurring detail resurfaces in a ledger entry, a letter, a report, a photograph caption, or a formal statement. From that moment, let the narrator move between the document's version and the remembered version of the same detail. Show how the document preserves something the memory softens, or how the memory explains something the document records without context. The changed understanding should come from this specific gap, not from a general meditation on the past.\n\nKeep the crime concrete: the offense, the relationships around it, and its aftermath. The documentary material may reveal who was present, what was paid, what was said officially, or what was omitted. The remembered material may reveal why a routine mattered, what someone feared, or what the teller did not know at the time. Keep the narrator's attachment to the place and people audible in the selection of remembered details; do not let the documentary voice flatten the prose into a case summary.\n\nWhen the two sources conflict, do not resolve every contradiction. The narrator may conclude that a record was incomplete, that a memory was mistaken, or that both captured different truths about the same day. End with a single re-encounter with the recurring detail, now understood differently through the document comparison, even if legal or practical consequences remain uncertain. Do not end on a general historical meditation or an abstract claim about memory. Keep the route documentary and historical-memory, not Romantic, uncanny, or satirical.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadamaj::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jun Maeda / Gothic-Punk\n\nWrite the crime inside a setting where old authority still touches present life: a family obligation, a building with its own rules, a workplace hierarchy inherited from an earlier era, or a neighborhood arrangement that no longer matches its original purpose. Let the Gothic-Punk atmosphere come from concrete urban material detail and the pressure of that old authority on private choices. The old authority must be presented through its actual mechanisms—who may enter, who must answer, who benefits, who is silenced—not through supernatural powers or imported vampire mechanics.\n\nAnchor the story in one repeated routine from the Maeda profile: a route walked at the same hour, a shared meal, a maintenance task, or a phrase exchanged with a particular person. Show the routine at least twice before the crime as an ordinary part of the characters' attachment to one another. Do not narrate its symbolic meaning; let the reader feel its familiarity through the viewpoint character's ease, small anticipation, or lack of attention.\n\nAfter the offense or its exposure, return to the same routine and make the changed weight concrete rather than stated. The character may notice a particular object in the routine—a key, a table setting, a tool, a doorway—or hear a phrase with new resonance because of what they now know. Show the change through sensory or practical detail: a hesitation in a movement, a different reason for performing the action, a silence where speech used to come easily. Do not let the narrator explain what the routine now means; let the altered action show it.\n\nLet morally complicated intimacy drive the relationships. The character may care for someone who benefits from the old arrangement, fear someone they also depend on, or protect a routine that has become complicit. When a social mask falls in trusted company, make it a specific reveal: a gesture, a tone, or a direct sentence that the private moment has earned. Not every private conversation must be a confession. Some characters will keep their masks even with people they love, and that restraint is itself part of the atmosphere; show at least one such restraint through an unfallen public gesture or a deliberately changed subject.\n\nThe crime should make the inherited arrangement more intelligible without excusing it. Show how the old authority shaped incentives, access, or silence. The aftermath may leave the character inside the same routine but unable to experience it the same way. End with a single return to that routine in the character's changed perception, not with a forced rebellion or a complete escape from the structure.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadamak::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jun Maeda / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and domestic realism route. Write the crime through the daily texture of a specific household, workplace, or neighborhood, keeping the language, region, community, and period as supplied by the story. The domestic setting must be specific: name the rooms, the recurring food or task, the route to work or market, and the times when the household gathers. Do not add generic Indian markers, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a standard family structure that the scene does not already contain.\n\nAnchor the narrative in one repeated routine from the Maeda profile: preparing food, waiting for someone, sharing transport, observing a household task, or exchanging the same words at a particular time. Show the routine in its ordinary form before any crime enters the household, and let the characters' attachment show through small practical acts rather than stated affection.\n\nAfter the offense or its exposure, return to the same routine and let it become a site of changed meaning. Choose at least one character whose behavior changes: someone continues a task because stopping would be more revealing than continuing, someone refuses a familiar task for the first time, someone performs it with different timing or eyes, or someone watches another perform it as if measuring the response. Show the change through physical action and dialogue, not through explanatory narration.\n\nAssign each household member a specific, different piece of knowledge about the crime: they may know the motive, the logistics, the victim's history, the official story, or the family's shared lie. Let the routine reveal those gaps and allegiances. When a character acts differently around the routine, the reader should understand what that character is hiding or testing. Do not force all household members toward the same conclusion, and do not make every disagreement a moral failure. Keep the crime's causes and consequences social and domestic rather than procedural, showing how money, obligation, reputation, or family pressure shaped the decision to act.\n\nEnd with the routine still present but altered: the household outwardly continues, while at least one character experiences the repeated action as a reminder of the crime rather than an ordinary attachment. The ending need not resolve every motive or consequence, but it should leave the specific domestic texture changed in an observable way.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadamal::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jun Maeda / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the diary and reflective miscellany route. Write the crime story as a series of dated or occasion-based entries kept by a culprit, accomplice, victim, investigator, or bystander. Let the entry form shape the information flow: the writer records what seemed worth noting on a particular day, and the reader watches the offense and its aftermath surface through those selections. Each entry should have its own date or clear occasion marker, and its prose should feel personal rather than retrospective.\n\nBuild the Maeda movement through a single recurring daily detail: a meal, a walk, a task, a phrase heard from a particular person, or a small object kept or avoided. Before the crime, let the writer mention this detail at least twice in different entries as an ordinary part of the day. Do not attach symbolic weight to it; the detail should appear as natural selection, not as narrative commentary.\n\nAfter the offense, return to the same detail in later entries. The writer may record it without explanation, omit it where the reader would expect it, or mention it in a changed context. Let the reader infer the altered weight from the entry's shape before the writer explicitly interprets it. When the writer does offer a later reflection that reinterprets an earlier entry, clearly mark the temporal distance: the later entry is a present act of recollection, not a retroactive insertion of knowledge into the earlier day. The writer should show what they now understand, and often what they failed to understand then.\n\nKeep the crime concrete and consequential. The entries may record preparations, encounters, fears, practical problems, or the slow arrival of outside consequences. The writer's attention should remain personal: what they noticed, what they avoided writing, what they could not stop thinking about. An omission can be as meaningful as a recorded detail, but show the omission through the shape of the entries rather than announcing it. Do not turn the diary into a case file.\n\nAllow humor, tenderness, or plain argument where they fit the writer and the day. The diary need not be uniformly somber. End with an entry that revisits the recurring detail and shows the writer's understanding of the shared life changed by the crime. The final entry need not resolve every consequence or explain every motive, but it should demonstrate the changed meaning through the detail itself.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadamam::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jun Maeda / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the testimonial and autobiographical account route. Write the crime story as a personal narration addressed to a specific audience: a younger relative, a community gathering, an interviewer, a court, or a reader the narrator imagines. Let the narrator's purpose shape the telling: to record what happened, to explain a choice, to defend someone, to warn, or to make a shared life legible after the offense. Establish early what the audience already knows or believes, so the narrator's decisions about what to include or omit are visible.\n\nAnchor the testimony in one repeated communal routine from the Maeda profile: a market day, a shared meal, a route walked together, a song, a greeting, or a household task. Before the crime, show the routine as part of the narrator's attachment to a particular place and people. After the offense, return to the routine and show a specific change: it continues in a reduced form, stops, becomes dangerous, or is performed with a new vigilance. Let the audience's recognition of the routine appear in the frame of the testimony, whether the narrator invites it or not.\n\nKeep the crime grounded in the community and history actually supplied by the story. Show the offense's specific causes and consequences: who was involved, what they needed or feared, what the community knew or refused to know, and what the narrator learned later. At least once, have the narrator explicitly distinguish between what they witnessed, what they were told, and what they now believe. This distinction should be prompted by a particular event in the telling, not by a general claim about memory.\n\nLet the testimony's uncertainties be concrete. End in one of these ways: the narrator still lives inside the changed routine, still uncertain about a particular motive and naming the person or fact that remains unresolved; the narrator addresses someone who has not answered, making the absence part of the final occasion; or the narrator holds a new belief about a person or event without fully reconciling it with the community's official account. Use one final return to the familiar routine or phrase to make the altered understanding concrete. Do not end on a general meditation on history or an abstract claim about truth. Do not add generic regional markers, magical events, or a universal political allegory.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaman::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jun Maeda / Light Novel\n\nWrite the crime story with clean scene entry and fast-readable staging. Open each scene by establishing where the viewpoint character is and what they are doing, then move through action, dialogue, and private response in short, clearly separated paragraphs. Keep spatial changes easy to follow: a new location, a shift from inside to outside, or a movement toward another character should be immediately legible.\n\nUse the Maeda profile through one repeated routine that appears in at least three scenes. Establish the routine in an early ordinary scene: a meal, a walk, a message check, a task, or a phrase exchanged with a particular person. Show the routine without symbolic commentary, letting it read as part of the character's daily life.\n\nIn the first scene after the offense or its exposure, return to the same routine. Build the scene around a single physical or sensory detail from that routine—the sound of a kettle, the texture of a bench, the weight of a bag, the light on a window—and let the viewpoint character notice it with changed attention. Give this noticing a focused reaction beat: a hesitation, a memory, a physical sensation, a refusal, or a private thought that reinterprets the familiar action. Do not narrate the symbolic meaning; let the reader infer it from the character's changed behavior and the detail's repeated presence.\n\nKeep the crime concrete and its consequences present in the character's daily life. The viewpoint character may be a culprit, accomplice, victim, investigator, or bystander. Show what they know, what they fear, and what practical problem the crime has introduced. Reaction beats may be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to the character and scene. Do not require a young cast, first-person narration, comedy, or a closing cliffhanger.\n\nEnd at a natural scene boundary. The character may still be inside the repeated routine, now carrying the changed understanding of it. The response should stop when the scene's immediate action, dialogue, or private response reaches a resting point: after the reaction beat has fully executed and the next practical or conversational step has not yet begun. Do not force a completed investigation, a confession, or a reversal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadamao::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jun Maeda / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet the crime enter through the ordinary objects, sounds, and routines that surround it. A key, a receipt, a particular route, or a phrase said before the offense can return with changed weight. When the viewpoint character encounters that detail again, develop the difference through rhythm and image rather than by announcing that everything has changed. A repeated sound or syntactic pattern may hold the memory in place while the scene continues.\n\nBuild comparisons from what the teller can actually see, touch, or remember. A comparison may unfold across a sentence when it changes what the narrator understands: a locked door first noticed as a practical obstacle can become an image of the silence after the crime, but only if the narrator's attention actually moves in that direction. Keep plain statements available as contrast. A lyric passage should not make every object symbolic or every character speak in the narrator's figurative register.\n\nUse recurrence to reframe rather than to repeat. The same phrase can return when a later occurrence gives it a new application: a promise made before the offense may be heard again in the aftermath with a different meaning. The same habit may continue after the crime, now shadowed by what it failed to prevent or what it helped conceal. Let the viewpoint character's private associations remain private. Another character may notice the repeated object without sharing its significance.\n\nKeep the crime's practical weight in view. Preparation, incentives, exposure, divided loyalty, evidence, pursuit, and aftermath can surround the lyric passages without being dissolved into them. Do not require punishment, confession, or miraculous repair. The lyric movement can end with the character still holding the changed understanding, still carrying the routine that has become unfamiliar, or still returning to the place where the offense altered the meaning of an ordinary thing.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadamap::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jun Maeda / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the offense register through small irreversible changes in a shared life. A seat left empty, a habit no longer performed, a phrase no longer answered, or a task that continues without the person who used to do it can carry the aftermath. The narrator may reflect directly on what is gone, or let the detail hold the association while the scene proceeds. Do not force every familiar object to announce loss; some routines continue and may still bring ordinary pleasure.\n\nUse remembered occasions as the material of reframing. A moment before the crime, recalled afterward, can show what the viewpoint character did not yet know or what they now understand differently. The memory need not be dramatic. A meal, a shared errand, or a repeated joke can become significant because the offense changed what it meant. Keep the memory tied to the specific relationship or routine, not to a general meditation on time.\n\nAllow present experience and absence to coexist. The character may notice that the same street looks different, that a familiar sound now arrives without its usual answer, or that an old habit persists even though its original reason is gone. Reflection can pause the scene, but it should not replace the crime's practical consequences. Preparation, exposure, divided loyalty, pursuit, or covering up can continue around the reflective passages.\n\nKeep tenderness and humor available where they belong. A remembered moment may be funny and still matter. A surviving relationship may offer comfort without canceling the loss. Do not require the character to reach a final acceptance or to turn every recollection into a lesson about impermanence. The response can end with the character still living inside the changed routine, still attached to what is no longer available.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadamaq::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jun Maeda / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed oral account as the definite regional route. A situated teller speaks to an audience within the story: a family member, a neighbor, an investigator, a visitor, or another person with a reason to listen. The teller's relationship to the offense shapes what is said first, what is delayed, and what is repeated. The audience's interruptions or questions may redirect the account, but the teller remains the one deciding what to disclose.\n\nLet the frame carry Jun Maeda's movement from shared life toward changed understanding. The teller may return to a familiar object, phrase, or routine from before the crime, and each return can revise what the audience understands. A detail mentioned early as ordinary may be recognized later as preparation, evidence, or a sign of what the teller failed to see. Keep the teller's knowledge consistent: a later understanding cannot be inserted as an earlier motive.\n\nKeep the tradition coherent rather than mixing every regional convention. If the telling is domestic, let the setting, objects, and relationships remain domestic. If it is urban or social, let work, money, and neighborhood obligations organize the account. Do not add mysticism, exile, religion, or an honor code unless the supplied world already includes them. The framed telling is a narrative method, not a requirement that the crime be ancient or ceremonial.\n\nUse the audience to create suspense through exposure or divided loyalty. The listener may know part of the truth, may be implicated, or may be the person from whom the teller is concealing something. The teller's repeated phrase can become a way of avoiding a harder disclosure until the audience's response makes it unavoidable. The response can end with the telling unfinished, interrupted, or brought to a point where the audience now understands the offense differently.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadamar::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jun Maeda / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose a small number of exact details from the shared life around the crime. A key, a cup, a particular phrase, a route, or a repeated task can carry the account. Let one or two of these details return after the offense. Show the change by what the character does with the object or how the phrase is now used, not by explaining that it has acquired new weight.\n\nUse direct clauses and keep only the inward connection that materially contributes to the present account. A thought may be essential even if it is long; do not cut it into performed fragments. Omission should leave the change inferable. If the character no longer performs a routine, the absence can be shown through the one detail that remains. If a relationship has changed, a single exchange or a single avoided phrase can make it legible.\n\nLet recurrence do the reframing. The same object can appear before the crime and again afterward with a different function: the key that opened a shared door may later be evidence, or the phrase said in affection may later be repeated in evasion. Do not add a summary sentence saying what the repetition means. The placement and handling of the detail carry the meaning.\n\nKeep the crime's practical consequences visible through the same selective detail: preparation, a lie, evidence, or pursuit can remain concrete without becoming explanatory. Keep humor and tenderness available in their exact form; a brief joke or a small act of care can remain without commentary. Do not require punishment, confession, or repair. The response can end with the character holding the object, saying the phrase, or continuing the routine, and the reader can infer what has changed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadamas::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jun Maeda / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the crime story as continuous scene-first prose. Move naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience. A scene may begin with a practical task, shift into a memory prompted by an object or phrase, and return to the present exchange without a recap. Use paragraphing and transitions to orient the reader, but do not stop the scene to explain what the reader should remember.\n\nLet repeated objects, routines, and phrases acquire new weight across scenes. A detail established before the offense can return afterward in a different context: the same route walked with a different purpose, the same phrase said by a different person, the same task continued after the relationship that gave it meaning has changed. When the viewpoint character notices the return, let the recognition arise from the scene rather than from a summary passage.\n\nKeep the crime's practical consequences in view. Preparation, incentives, exposure, divided loyalty, evidence, pursuit, and aftermath can unfold through ordinary scenes. A conversation may advance the case while also revealing a changed relationship. A domestic moment may carry tension because the character knows what the other person does not. Do not let reflection replace the offense's material weight.\n\nUse humor and tenderness where they belong to the people and scenes. A joke between accomplices, a small act of care before a difficult task, or a familiar routine can remain warm without canceling the crime's seriousness. Keep the narrator's access consistent with the selected perspective. The response can end with a scene still in motion, a relationship still unsettled, or a routine resumed with its meaning altered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadamat::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jun Maeda / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship the center of the crime story. Select scenes that show the relationship's particular promise: what these people do for each other, what they share, what they avoid saying, and what they have built together. Let the offense enter through that promise. A shared secret, a divided loyalty, a favor asked, a lie told to protect the other, or a discovery that changes what one believes about the other can carry the crime's weight.\n\nUse private language and repeated moments as the material of reframing. A phrase that belonged to affection may return during concealment or exposure. A routine the two shared may continue under strain or stop suddenly. A small object exchanged between them may become evidence, a reminder, or a thing one cannot bear to touch. Let these repetitions acquire new meaning through the relationship's consequences rather than through explanation.\n\nGive the emotional beats enough space. Affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery can occupy a scene without immediately advancing the plot. A moment of care before a dangerous task, an argument that reveals a hidden motive, or a quiet exchange after a difficult event can fulfill the relationship's promise. Do not insert a roster of familiar tropes. Use only the actual supplied premise and the people it establishes.\n\nDo not force every loved one to betray, every confession to collapse, or every comforting room to be a trap. The relationship may survive the crime, change under it, or end because of it, but the outcome must follow from the specific people and the offense. The response can end with a repeated phrase now carrying a different meaning, a private moment still unresolved, or a consequence the two must continue living with.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadamau::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jun Maeda / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the crime as an account of the deeds by which these people will be remembered. A deliberate opening can name the offense or the person whose act set the consequences in motion. Compact declarations can establish a person or a remembered act, then expand into the particulars that make the reputation incomplete. Keep the present scene's scale. A theft, a betrayal, a killing, or a scheme remains what it is; it need not be inflated into legendary doom.\n\nUse recurring phrasing and designations that come from established deeds or ways of speaking. A person may be known by what they did, what they failed to do, or what they always said. Return to a phrase when a later occurrence gives it a new application. Let Jun Maeda's repeated habits and relationships revise the public account: the person remembered as dependable may have helped for reasons the chronicle does not know, or the routine that seemed ordinary may later be recognized as preparation or concealment.\n\nLet dialogue interrupt the authority of the chronicle. The subject of an oft-told incident may object to a detail, answer an elevated introduction with a practical concern, or reveal a private motive that the public account has missed. This need not become parody. Humor may arise from the persistence of a local story, but the crime's harm remains real. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective.\n\nOrganize the account through public deeds, kinship, journeys, gifts, feuds, or oaths only when the supplied world establishes them. Do not import a Nordic pantheon, heroic doom, or ceremonial dialogue into every speaker. The response can end with the offense's consequence still unfolding, another modest act available to be remembered, or the chronicle's version shown to be incomplete without declaring the final meaning on the community's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadamav::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jun Maeda / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the crime story as Scandinavian social-realist domestic narrative: the offense, its preparation, execution, or aftermath is registered through the ordinary shared life of a particular place, its people, and the local institutions that shape their obligations. Keep the setting's Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish context distinct: use its actual housing, work, welfare forms, school or municipal routines, and neighborhood customs rather than a generic northern mood. The crime should alter the meaning of that continuing life—often by disturbing the community's working assumptions—without replacing it with a separate investigation plot.\n\nChoose one or two concrete shared elements—a repeated object, a habitual route, a household task, a phrase, a small ritual—and return to them at intervals. Before the offense, render them as part of daily attachment or tolerated friction. After the offense, let the same element return with changed weight: it may become evidence, a reminder, a site of suspicion, a comfort, or an unbearable ordinary fact. Do not announce the change; let the narrator or focal character register it through attention, memory, or hesitation. The callback should revise understanding, not merely repeat a motif.\n\nMake the crime causally legible through social exposure. Show the incentives, pressures, relationships, or failures that made the offense possible, and show its consequences spreading through the community. Suspense can arise from threatened exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did it: a neighbor's casual question about the missing item, an official form that requires a false answer, a scheduled visit that risks discovery. A character may conceal knowledge during ordinary work or continue a household routine precisely because stopping would reveal too much. Do not force a mystery puzzle, a professional procedural, or a guaranteed punishment. The story may end in unresolved aftermath, partial repair, or plain continuation.\n\nUse inward prose to make the focal character's actual private experience available: what they notice, what they choose not to say, what they remember, and how they explain another person's behavior. Keep that interiority separate from what other characters know. A neighbor's kindness may be genuine, a confession may be incomplete, and a familiar routine may be both comforting and implicated. Do not make every caring act fraudulent or every pause a sign of guilt. Allow humor and tenderness where they belong to the people and scenes, without turning the cast uniformly witty or sentimental.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadamaw::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jun Maeda / Screenplay\n\nWrite the crime story in screen-oriented scenes. Use scene headings when location or time changes, concise action lines for visible and audible events, and clear speaker cues for dialogue. Keep action blocks readable: show who is present, what they handle, where they move, and what they can see or hear. Do not fill the page with camera commands or editing instructions.\n\nBuild the Maeda movement from shared life toward changed understanding through what the audience can see and hear. Establish a specific repeated object, routine, phrase, or relationship in an early scene without editorial emphasis. Return to it after the offense. The callback may appear as a character reaching for the same object but stopping short, performing the same task with a different pace, repeating a line with altered tone, or hesitating at a familiar moment. Let the repetition carry the new weight through performance and context rather than through explanatory dialogue. Match each callback to the actor's available business: a repeated object can be set down too gently; a routine can be started on time but finished late; a familiar line can be delivered as if the speaker is listening for a response that no longer comes.\n\nMake the crime legible through action and speech. Show preparation, incentive, pressure, execution, or aftermath in scenes that reveal who knows what and who is exposed. Suspense can come from a character lying to someone they care about, from a bystander noticing too much, or from divided loyalty. Do not require a detective, a formal investigation, or a punishment. A scene may end on a completed act, a new risk, or an unresolved consequence.\n\nUse clearly attributed nonspoken notes for required private experience that cannot be staged or spoken. A note may record what a character notices, remembers, fears, or decides without another character hearing it. Place the note before or after the relevant action block, clearly marked as nonspoken, and do not treat it as knowledge available to the cast. Do not convert every private thought into voice-over or a monologue; use those only when the chosen sequence needs them. The note shapes performance—why a hand lingers, why a question is repeated—without being uttered.\n\nLet humor and tenderness appear in appropriate scenes through ordinary behavior and warm exchange. Do not make the cast uniformly witty or force a joke into a tense moment. Preserve the active authorship contract for perspective and knowledge. The screenplay form should make the shared life and its changed meaning visible, not reduce the crime to a sequence of plot beats.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadamax::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jun Maeda / Stage Play\n\nWrite the crime story as a playable script. Use clear speaker names, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be staged. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene. Keep stage directions economical: they should establish observable conditions and movement, not describe camera work or unstageable internal states.\n\nUse the shared stage space to make the offense and its aftermath concrete. Show who can see an entrance, reach an object, hear a line, or move between two places. Let the characters occupy themselves with practical tasks—clearing a table, checking a lock, sorting papers, waiting for someone—while the crime's consequences surface in what they do and say. Suspense can come from a character concealing knowledge during ordinary work, from a divided loyalty, or from a bystander realizing what they overlooked.\n\nBuild the Maeda callback through staged repetition. Establish a specific object, routine, or phrase in an early scene. Return to it after the offense. Stage the return as altered business: the same object is picked up and set down again without being used; the same routine is begun but interrupted or completed in the wrong order; the same line is repeated with a different pause or addressee. The audience should feel the new weight through what the actor does, not through a character explaining that things feel different now. If a repeated phrase is used, let its second delivery land differently because of the intervening events; do not gloss it with a line like \"It doesn't mean the same anymore.\"\n\nUse monologues and asides only when they have a dramatic purpose and an audience within the play's convention. Do not use them to replace every private thought. Preserve required private experience as clearly attributed nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation, separate from the spoken lines. This material may record what a character notices, remembers, or withholds, and it may explain an otherwise small physical choice—a pause before touching a drawer, a glance at the door—without being audible to the other characters. Another character does not know a private conclusion merely because the audience or reader has access to it.\n\nAllow humor and tenderness in appropriate scenes through warm exchange and ordinary care. Do not make the cast uniformly witty or force a comic beat into a tense moment. The play can end on a completed action, an unresolved consequence, or a quiet continuation. Do not require a gathering of suspects, a theatrical confession, or a moral verdict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadamay::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jun Maeda / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite the crime story through one authorized mind's associative movement. Braid perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern. Let syntax and paragraph shape follow the mind's movement: a sound may pull up a memory, a familiar object may open a chain of feeling, an unfinished thought may be cut off by a present demand. Keep enough orientation to distinguish what is happening now from what is remembered, imagined, or feared.\n\nUse the Maeda callback as a recurring mental anchor. Choose a specific shared element—a phrase, a routine, an object, a place—and let it return in the focal mind before and after the offense. Before, it may surface with ordinary warmth or irritation. After, give the same element a salience that changes with each return: the mind may linger on it longer than the scene requires, flinch away from it, or find it tangled with a detail of the crime. Use a sensory or verbal trigger to reopen it naturally: the same kettle's whistle now returns the moment the offense was planned; the same hallway smell now returns the sound of a lie. The recurrence itself shows the shift in understanding; do not explain it.\n\nLet the crime enter the stream through the senses and the body. The focal character may register a smell, a sound, a texture, a wrong detail, a familiar face seen differently. The offense's preparation, execution, or aftermath can surface in fragments—a remembered conversation, a pressure in the chest, a repeated image—rather than as a linear case report. Keep the crime's facts recoverable: anchor concrete facts in identifiable memories or present details, and mark uncertain material as wish, dread, or misremembering through the mind's habits (repeating a scene in different versions, refusing to finish a thought, correcting a remembered line). The reader should be able to distinguish what actually happened from what the character imagines.\n\nPreserve the focal mind's limits. Other characters' private thoughts are not available unless the active authorship contract grants access. The stream of consciousness does not make every character share a mind or turn all scene facts indeterminate. Ordinary reasoning alone is not enough; the prose should move by association, interruption, and recurrence, not by a tidy sequence of conclusions.\n\nAllow humor and tenderness to surface in the mind's movement where they belong to the person and the memory. Do not force the cast uniformly witty or make every recollection ominous. The response can end inside the ongoing stream—on a perception, a memory, an unfinished thought, or a present interruption—without requiring the crime to be solved or the feeling resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadamaz::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jun Maeda / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the crime story through readable sender-attributed messages. Use timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes only when they matter to the story. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one may write in fragments, another in full sentences, one may reply immediately, another may leave messages unread. Do not make every character sound like the same writer.\n\nBuild the Maeda callback through digital routine. Establish a repeated phrase, a habitual check-in, a shared channel, or a familiar message rhythm before the offense. After the offense, let the same habit return with changed meaning, and make the change measurable in the transcript's formal features: a reply that used to come in seconds now comes after hours; a phrase that used to end with an exclamation mark now ends with a period; a routine check-in continues with too-smooth wording or stops without acknowledgment. The same phrase may now read as a lie, a plea, a cover, or a painful attempt at normalcy. A delayed reply that was once ordinary may become suspicious. Let the change emerge from the messages themselves; do not have a character announce that everything feels different now.\n\nMake the crime legible through what people send, withhold, edit, or fail to send. Suspense can come from a character lying over text, from a message sent to the wrong person, from a draft composed and deleted, or from divided loyalty expressed in careful wording. The reader may know who did it while the other participants do not. Do not require a detective, a formal investigation, or a punishment.\n\nKeep the boundaries clear. A sent message is transmitted text; an unsent draft is a composed artifact that the recipient has not seen; private thought is not transmitted at all. Show an unsent draft as an explicitly labeled unsent draft, separate from the exchange, and keep it brief. Show private thought only if the active presentation permits a clearly separated context note. Do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext. A read receipt, an edit, or a deleted message should have a story reason.\n\nAllow humor and tenderness in appropriate exchanges through the characters' ordinary digital habits. Do not make the cast uniformly witty or force a joke into a tense thread. Context outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript. The response can end on a sent message, a pending reply, an unsent draft, or a meaningful silence, without requiring the crime to be resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadamba::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jun Maeda / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the crime story in scene-oriented visual-novel presentation. Use concise scene-setting to establish where the characters are and what is visible, readable dialogue attribution for speech, and focal narration or inner response to register the viewpoint character's attention and feeling. Keep scene changes legible. Background or expression cues are purposeful references, not executable engine code or a mandatory label on every line.\n\nBuild the Maeda callback through recurring visual or scene elements. Establish a specific object, location, or expression cue in an early scene without editorial emphasis. Return to it after the offense. Show the return through the focal character's changed attention: the same familiar kitchen now registers as the place where the lie was told; the same object is handled less confidently or not at all; the same background detail that once went unremarked now draws a stare. The reader should feel the new weight through the narrator's selection and comparison, not through a character explaining that things feel different now.\n\nMake the crime legible through scenes whose information changes the available interpretations. Show preparation, incentive, pressure, execution, or aftermath through what the focal character sees, hears, and says. Suspense can come from exposure, divided loyalty, or a bystander noticing too much. The reader may know who did it while the focal character does not. Do not require a detective, a formal investigation, or a punishment.\n\nUse focal narration and inner response to make the viewpoint character's private experience available: what they notice, what they choose not to say, what they remember, and how they explain another person's behavior. Keep that interiority separate from what other characters know. An expression cue may attract suspicion, but it should not function as an automatic truth indicator. A contradiction in an account or arrangement must do the evidentiary work. Let the focal narration distinguish among noticing an odd detail, suspecting a connection, forming a supported inference from a contradiction, and being mistaken.\n\nAllow humor and tenderness in appropriate scenes through warm exchange and ordinary care. Do not make the cast uniformly witty or force a comic beat into a tense moment. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected. The scene can end on a completed action, a new risk, or an unresolved consequence, without requiring a choice screen or a moral verdict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadambb::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Jun Maeda / Web Serial\n\nWrite the crime story as an installment-oriented serial. Maintain a readable through-line across replies: what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover context briefly only when it helps the reader re-enter. Use earned developments and occasional hooks without making every turn a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger. Progress can involve knowledge, relationships, work, or resources, not just plot twists.\n\nBuild the Maeda callback as the serial's re-entry anchor. Establish a specific shared object, routine, phrase, or relationship early. When a later installment returns to it, briefly recover only the necessary particular—the object's earlier state, the routine's previous timing, the phrase's original tone—measured against what the same element means now. The callback can function as a status check for the crime's consequences: the same object is now missing, hidden, or repaired; the same routine is still performed but with a changed participant; the same phrase is repeated under new pressure. Do not unravel the entire callback each time. Let each recurrence deepen the accumulated understanding rather than restart the same suspicion.\n\nLet the crime's consequences accumulate across installments. Show preparation, incentive, pressure, execution, or aftermath through local changes: a relationship shifts, a risk becomes concrete, a piece of knowledge spreads, a practical problem worsens or eases. Suspense can come from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did it. Do not require a detective, a formal investigation, or a punishment. The installment can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known.\n\nUse focal narration to make the viewpoint character's private experience available: what they notice, what they choose not to say, what they remember, and how they explain another person's behavior. Keep that interiority separate from what other characters know. A later realization cannot be retroactively inserted as an earlier motive. New information can qualify an old explanation without replacing the old event or making the character deliberately forget a supported conclusion.\n\nAllow humor and tenderness in appropriate scenes through ordinary behavior and warm exchange. Do not make the cast uniformly witty or force a joke into a tense installment. The serial duration should give selected facts room to acquire meaning, not make the crime's resolution perpetually recede.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadanaa::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Kamachi Kazuma / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nTreat each difficult relationship as a mechanism the viewpoint character is trying to operate while the crime's consequences push against them. When they admit, need, withhold, apologize, or return to someone, make the specific calculation legible: what they expect the other person to do, what resource or obligation is at stake, and what they choose to say or hide. Then show the resulting conversation and behavior. Let the reader see why the strain lands exactly where it does, even when the character's conclusion is wrong.\n\nApply Kamachi's tactical legibility to emotional risk. A favor, debt, shared silence, or promise made before the offense can be handled like a move in a sequence: explain what each person believes it obliges them to do, then have them exploit, misunderstand, or refuse that obligation under pressure. When a character explains their own reasoning, treat the explanation as an action with a cost, not a resolution. An apology can create a new practical problem; a withheld confession can protect someone while making the next move harder.\n\nKeep the emotional exposure intimate and in the viewpoint's actual diction and distance. Let a small physical detail—a pause, a familiar habit, an object that now carries weight—register the cost without a stock vocabulary of suffering. Allow warmth and pain to coexist: comfort may be partial, awkward, or offered by someone who is also implicated. Do not require a breakdown, reconciliation, or tragic ending; the response may end while the hurt and the practical situation remain unresolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadanab::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Kamachi Kazuma / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite each reply as a coherent shared interval in the criminal situation, with unmistakable dialogue attribution and readable paragraphs. Keep the active characters, NPCs, and practical conditions moving within the scene. When a character acts on a scheme, resource, or obligation, make the sequence understandable: what they use, what they expect to happen, and what actually occurs. A rapid reversal should remain physically legible, not a sudden unexplained shift.\n\nWeave tactical explanation into the scene's action and speech. A character can state what they are doing and why in dialogue that fits the active speaker and moment, but the explanation must remain part of the scene—not an aside to the reader. If a mechanism matters, show the concrete step or have the character give only as much as they would naturally say under pressure. Then show the result immediately. Keep other participants' knowledge separate: one character's stated plan is not automatically known to everyone present.\n\nEnd at a natural continuation point that follows from the scene's current practical state. The next reply can pick up an unfinished task, a new arrival, a consequence, or a character's next move. Do not force a question, cliffhanger, or choice screen. Preserve the active narrative person and player control. The crime's pressure can remain active without requiring the scene to pause or resolve before the response ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadanac::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Kamachi Kazuma / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and satirical realism route. Treat the crime as an event that forces a particular community to reveal its interests, obligations, and self-justifications through specific transactions and conversations. Keep the setting, language, period, and community distinct. Show how different people understand the same scheme according to their position: who profits, who must look away, who publicly condemns while privately depending on the arrangement, and who quietly loses.\n\nRender Kamachi's legible systems as social machinery. When a character exploits a rule, resource, or relationship, explain the mechanism in terms of the community's actual practices: a bribe, a contract, a family obligation, a bureaucratic procedure, or a public reputation. Then show the resulting action and its social consequence. Moral instinct appears as a practical problem: a character may know something is wrong and still calculate what they can afford to do about it. Let satire arise from the precise gap between stated principle and actual behavior—shown through who sits where, who speaks first, who is ignored, and who signs the receipt—rather than from mocking caricature.\n\nKeep the narration socially situated. The viewpoint can observe how a room, a transaction, or a conversation reveals rank, dependence, and expectation. Let the prose carry judgment through selection and emphasis rather than omniscient moral commentary. Show the crime's aftermath in how the community absorbs, punishes, or quietly restores its own order, and specify the concrete arrangement that allows this to happen. Preserve the active narrator and world facts; do not transplant a setting or cast identity merely because the route is selected.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadanad::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Kamachi Kazuma / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the crime through hard-edged, concrete prose. Use forceful diction and compressed transitions. Let material details carry the tactical mechanism: a lock scratched, a bag heavier on one side, a phone screen cracked across the last message. Show physical stakes as immediate and specific. When a character takes a risk, render what the risk does to a body, an object, or a place—not what it means in the abstract.\n\nKeep Kamachi's tactical legibility inside the compression. When a character exploits or misunderstands a mechanism, present it as a material operation: what they handle, what they expect, what breaks or holds. Use the shortest concrete sequence that makes the action understandable. Do not pause for explanation. A rapid reversal should land as a physical event: a door that opens the wrong way, a weight that shifts, a sound that arrives too late. Let the reader follow the sequence through objects and movements.\n\nVary the rhythm usefully. Compress routine action into brief hard beats; slow only when a character's reasoning or a physical detail changes the stakes. A longer thought is acceptable when it determines the next move. Ground abrasive or rough speech in its speaker's specific situation, not a generic tough voice. Do not turn the crime into survival horror or omit the human aftermath; the grit is a treatment of the selected genre, not a replacement for it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadanae::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Kamachi Kazuma / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the crime through documents, each chosen for what its author could know, conceal, or misunderstand at a specific time. Write the report, letter, log, receipt, note left behind, statement, or message thread from that author's situation. A lookout records what they saw; a courier records what they carried; an investigator records what they were told. None holds the whole mechanism.\n\nUse each document to make Kamachi's tactical sequence legible across entries. When a character exploits or misunderstands a mechanism, let the document show the part they understood and the part they got wrong. Let a later record correct or complicate an earlier one through a new fact, a revision, or a material trace. Keep the reader able to assemble the physical sequence from these partial accounts: who moved what, when, and why. Do not add an omniscient explanation after every record. Let gaps carry information: a missing page, an unsent draft, a carefully worded denial, or a record that stops at the wrong moment.\n\nPreserve readable continuity by making each document advance the reader's active assembly of the offense, its preparation, execution, or aftermath. Conflicting accounts should be attributable to their authors' positions and purposes, not to random noise. A document is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought; show what its author chose to record and what they left out. Keep the active narrator, world facts, and output contracts intact.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadanaf::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Kamachi Kazuma / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite the crime's world with room for sincere warmth and practical care. Make comfort an operational act: a character remembers someone's preference, provides a useful object, makes a space easier to occupy, or chooses a small kindness that fits the actual situation. Show what the care costs to arrange—time, risk, attention, or a small sacrifice. A hot drink, a clean shirt, a charged phone, a familiar food, or a moment of unhurried attention can matter because of who offers it and what it means in the middle of the pressure.\n\nKeep the crime's established problems active. The scheme, debt, risk, or investigation continues around the warm interval. Warmth can occur inside the pressure: a safe hour, a shared meal before a difficult move, a favor that makes the next step possible. Do not make everyone agreeable or remove disagreement, privacy, or danger. A character can be comforted and still refuse to explain, still lie, still leave. Affection does not require a confession or an emotional repair; it can be present and sincere without resolving anything.\n\nUse Kamachi's legible systems to show how care operates. When a character provides comfort, make the mechanism understandable: what they know about the other person, what resource they devote, and what practical effect it has. A small pleasure can be a deliberate intervention in the other person's state or situation, not a random pause. Let the prose enjoy the particular people and their familiar preferences without turning the crime into a domestic vignette. The genre remains crime; the style is how the warmth is rendered, not a replacement for the offense and its consequences.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadanag::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Kamachi Kazuma / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite the crime in composed novel prose with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Let the narrator frame each scene through social context, history, or judgment where the chosen perspective permits it. Place a transaction within a longer pattern of obligation; understand a pursuit through the neighborhood, institution, or family that shaped it. Distinguish narration from ordinary spoken language: the prose may carry a considered quality without becoming archaic or uniformly elaborate.\n\nKeep Kamachi's tactical legibility inside the formal architecture. When a character exploits or misunderstands a mechanism, explain it clearly and show the resulting action in the next sentence or paragraph. Do not let a social observation replace the physical beat. The reader should always understand who moved what, when, and why. Render a rapid reversal in a deliberate sentence that marks the shift, then follow with a paragraph tracing its consequence through the scene and its social meaning. Use the narrator's distance to show what participants know and what the social situation makes visible, without granting access to excluded minds.\n\nUse ceremony, correspondence, or public/private contrast only when the scene actually calls for it. A formal occasion can organize a meeting; a letter can carry a threat or a promise; a private conversation can be measured against a public account. Give the crime's aftermath the same composed attention as its execution: the changed relationships, the public story, and the private knowledge that remains. Preserve the active narrator, world facts, and output contracts.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadanah::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Kamachi Kazuma / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nMake the embedded account a live criminal operation. Before it begins, establish the teller's immediate purpose—to delay pursuit, recruit help, shift blame, test an ally, or extract information—and the listeners' stake in stopping or disbelieving him. The account is not a neutral report but a move whose success depends on what the listeners need to hear and what they already know.\n\nPresent every operational detail as a rule that the teller uses or misreads. A lock, schedule, transfer, debt, forged document, surveillance gap, or alibi should be stated plainly enough for a listener to act on, then the teller should exploit it, conceal a defect, or realize the listeners know more than he assumed. When the listener interrupts, questions, or offers a correction, let the teller adjust the tale in real time; the reader should see how each change protects a person, covers an error, or tests a relationship.\n\nUse the teller's omissions as the story's pressure. He can honestly describe what he saw while omitting what he thought, or misattribute his own knowledge to a plausible source. The narration may show the teller's private calculation without allowing the listeners access to it. Suspense should come from the gap between what is said, what is remembered, and what the present scene reveals—not from an unreliable narrator who simply lies from the start.\n\nLet recurrence do specific work. A phrase, object, sum, or time can move between levels: a debt first named to justify a visit returns as a bribe; an alibi first offered to one listener is contradicted before another. Recurrence gains meaning by accumulating, but do not treat it as proof. A repeated detail may be a habit of speech, an inherited phrase, or an invented consistency that a listener knows is false.\n\nMaintain the frame-tale levels by attributing knowledge to its actual source. A detail from the original event enters the outer scene only because the teller speaks it; a detail from after the event can be retold but must not be inserted as an earlier motivation. When a later disclosure changes how the listeners reread the account, show what they now recognize about the teller's choices, not what the teller retroactively knew.\n\nEnd when the immediate purpose resolves. The teller may secure cooperation, be caught, postpone danger, or provoke a question that changes the outer situation. The crime itself need not conclude inside the story, and the nested account does not require a cliffhanger. Keep the outer listeners and the teller active until the negotiation breaks, succeeds, or shifts to a new consequence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadanai::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Kamachi Kazuma / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the crime through two layers: the fixed record and the remembering mind. Use documents—ledgers, permits, notices, reports, transfer orders—as mechanisms with stated limits. When a record appears, name what it permits, requires, or proves, then show a character exploiting that formal power, misreading it, or discovering what the document does not cover.\n\nBuild the investigation or aftermath as a comparison between what the record says and what participants recall. The record should be exact and legible: who was permitted, what was transferred, what was forbidden. Memory should supply the conditions the record cannot capture: fear, noise, loyalty, weather, pressure, a face in a doorway. Do not treat either layer as automatically reliable, but make the gap between them the source of practical movement. A forged ledger, an unrecorded threat, or a routine report that accidentally preserves a contradiction can all drive action.\n\nWhen a character consumes a document or a memory, state the underlying rule plainly before the practical choice. Then show the action that follows from that character's reading. For example, a guard who trusts a permit opens a gate; an official who misremembers an order transfers the wrong person; a witness who recognizes a listed name but not its timing withholds testimony. Keep the physical sequence intelligible even when the prose cuts between record, recollection, and present consequence.\n\nUse syntax to carry the source boundary. The record layer moves in short, complete, declarative sentences: entries, entries, requirements. The memory layer can be periodic, unfinished, phrase-heavy, and situated in a specific body and place. The two registers may touch within a single paragraph when a narrator compares them, but the reader should always know which detail comes from a text and which from private remembrance, report, or inference.\n\nPreserve the difference between event-time knowledge and later knowledge. A person may now understand what an old record meant, but that understanding cannot become the motive he had before the offense. Show what each participant knew from the record of that moment and what he later learned; the reconstruction of the crime should separate those layers without stopping for a case-summary voice.\n\nDo not force national scenery, bureaucratic severity, or translated word order. The crime should feel documentable and human, with competing accounts that survive in the material world. End when the available record and memory have changed a person's action or understanding, not when the file is closed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadanaj::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Kamachi Kazuma / Gothic-Punk\n\nLet an old authority operate through a present system. An inherited building, family, title, debt, custom, or memory should have a concrete modern mechanism: a lease, registry, security code, transfer, contract, or zoning rule. State the relation between the old privilege and the present rule as a mechanism a character can exploit, obey, or misunderstand. Then show the action that follows from that character's reading.\n\nDescribe decay and ritual as material evidence, not mood. A cracked floor should matter because it hides a passage or records a weight limit. A formal dinner should matter because it controls who can speak to whom or who holds a key. A private room should matter because of what it contains and who may enter. The narrator should be able to say what a detail permits or prevents, not merely that it feels oppressive.\n\nShow the urban maintenance of inherited power. Name who cleans, guards, pays, repairs, and remembers. Atmosphere arises from cost and labor: the building's coldness is tied to the unpaid heating bill, the locked door tied to a security guard's shift, the dim corridor tied to a broken light no one is allowed to replace. Make every physical detail traceable to a practical choice, so the crime remains a human operation.\n\nLet competing interests divide characters around the same mechanism. One person may use an old rule to protect reputation, another to secure housing, another to meet a modern filing deadline, another to conceal an affair. A character may understand only one part of the arrangement and act on that partial knowledge. Do not make every disagreement corruption or ignorance; a morally complicated intimacy can persist even between people who are manipulating each other.\n\nAllow masks to fall in trusted company without making private desire separate from the offense. A character may briefly reveal a fear, want, or loyalty that explains why he bent a rule. The narration can show that private experience directly, but not every character should infer it. When a fast reversal occurs, connect it to a previously established assumption about the rule, the resource, or the relationship.\n\nNo supernatural rule may close a gap. An old authority, family curse, or eerie building must remain a human arrangement with legal, social, and material consequences. The crime should make the system more intelligible: after a violation, the reader should understand which person paid, which door opened, which claim shifted. End before the whole hierarchy is resolved, but not before the immediate mechanism has produced a consequence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadanak::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Kamachi Kazuma / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nAdopt the testimonial and autobiographical route as an active operational tool. The teller is a participant—culprit, accomplice, victim, investigator, or bystander—who is not merely retelling the offense but using his account to manage blame, protect someone, recruit help, or justify a choice. Establish the present occasion of the telling: to whom he speaks, what he wants from them, and what he would rather they not ask.\n\nWhen the teller explains a rule, schedule, payment, lock, debt, route, or legal exception, state the mechanism plainly enough that a listener could act on it. Then show what the teller did with that rule: he may have obeyed it, misread it, exploited a gap, or realized too late what it implied. Keep the physical sequence of preparation, execution, and aftermath legible even while the account moves between what he witnessed and what he reports.\n\nMark every boundary between participation and report. The teller can own his own fear, calculation, hesitation, or relief as direct experience. When he describes something he did not see, show the source: \"he told me later,\" \"the others said,\" \"I found the receipt after.\" Do not let later knowledge turn into the earlier motive. A realization after the offense may change how the teller now understands what he did, but it must not be narrated as what he thought at the time.\n\nUse the teller's omissions and emphases as the suspense. He may linger on a friend's innocence while skipping his own hour, or describe a fear he felt so vividly that listeners forget to ask what he did next. The reader should see that the telling is a mechanism for exposure, not a transparent confession. The teller's moral instinct can be sincere and still misleading.\n\nLet divided loyalty appear inside the account. A listener may interrupt, question, or supply a fact the teller did not expect. When that happens, show the teller adjusting: he may become more precise, more vague, angrier, gentler, or suddenly helpful. The outer scene can move as quickly as the embedded story, but the reader must always know which level is speaking.\n\nDo not force English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure. Use the language, region, community, and period actually supplied. The crime should emerge as this participant's specific account, with its gaps belonging to him and its explanations obeying actual rules. End when the telling has produced a practical change—a listener leaves, calls someone, believes, refuses, or asks the one question the teller cannot answer—without requiring the whole offense to be resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadanal::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Kamachi Kazuma / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the crime as a social-psychological system. Work, money, housing, family obligation, reputation, and long personal history are rules that constrain action. When one of these rules matters, state what it requires, permits, or costs before the character acts. For example, a company transfer deadline, a lease clause, a workplace hierarchy, or a family expectation should be concrete enough that the reader can see how a person exploits, obeys, or misunderstands it.\n\nLet the chosen viewpoint think through the mechanism in real time. The character may plan using the rule, realize a limit, test another person's knowledge, or make a mistake about what a rule protects. Show the practical reasoning and private response as they happen: fear, relief, calculation, shame, or quiet resolve. Do not let a later realization substitute for the earlier motive. The psychological weight of the offense should come from what the character actually thought and felt while choosing.\n\nUse narrative distance to match the teller or focal character. A person under immediate pressure may think in short, practical bursts; a person looking back may reflect in longer, more formal sentences. Omission, direct feeling, plain argument, and seasonal attention are available when they fit the character and situation. There is no required quietness or obligatory Japanese scenery. When the focal character misses something, the narration may show the missing information as absent without announcing it to everyone.\n\nKeep the physical sequence intelligible through shifts. A decision may lead to an action, a delay, a confrontation, or a changed plan. State the physical movement plainly: who goes where, what is handed over, what door opens, what time passes. Rapid cuts should not erase the actual chain of cause and effect.\n\nLet suspense arise from exposure or divided loyalty, not from every relationship collapsing. A character may protect a family member by breaking a workplace rule, or cover a colleague's mistake while resenting him. The private experience should be accessible to the reader even when other characters infer wrongly. A pause is not automatically guilt; a calm surface is not automatically concealment.\n\nDo not relocate the cast, manufacture national speech patterns, or add a generic regional temperament. The crime should emerge from the specific social world supplied and remain a particular person's practical and psychological response to concrete pressures. End when a consequence changes a relationship, a decision, or a practical situation; the entire aftermath need not resolve.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadanam::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Kamachi Kazuma / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social realism and chronicle route as a rule-bound investigation of a community. The crime should be embedded in specific material conditions: who owns the land, building, or truck; who works where; what a route costs; what a document permits; what a debt obliges; what a market tolerates. State each economic or institutional rule plainly when it becomes part of the offense, then show how a character exploits, obeys, or misreads it.\n\nLet the chronicle's sources follow their actual access. A narrator or focal character may directly observe a meeting, hear about a shooting, read a transaction record, or infer a motive from a remembered threat. Mark these differences in the prose without stopping for a documentary tone. A direct observation can be vivid and immediate; a report enters through a phrase such as \"they said later\" or \"the driver told me\"; a record supplies only what it lists; an inference belongs to the character who made it.\n\nShow the physical chain of cause and effect. A plan, payment, route, gate, package, or signature should move the story forward. When a character acts on a rule, state what he thinks the rule permits and what actually follows. Then show the consequence, even if it comes from a different part of the community than the character expected.\n\nLet competing interests arise from the same concrete arrangement without reducing disagreement to corruption or ignorance. A worker may need access, a merchant credit, an official procedure, a family member reputation. A character may know one portion of a rule and protect an outcome that conflicts with someone who knows another portion. Suspense should come from exposure and divided loyalty, not from a single external force.\n\nUse the chronicle form to compare time layers when necessary. Later knowledge may change how a past record reads, but it cannot become an earlier motive. A narrator may recall that a threat seemed empty until after the offense, or recognize now what a signature meant then. Show the difference between event-time understanding and retrospective recognition by naming the later source of knowledge.\n\nDo not add magical realism or supernatural facts unless the supplied world already contains them. The marvelous is not a default for this route; even if the world is ordinary, the crime must remain human and material. The offense and its aftermath should be causally legible, with specific language, community, and history preserved. End when a particular consequence changes a relationship, a livelihood, or a practical course of action.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadanan::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Kamachi Kazuma / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation with a concrete spatial anchor: a figure moving through a gate, a hand on a ledger, a car stopped at a checkpoint, a voice raised in a closed room. Establish the offense, scheme, or pursuit as an active problem that the focal character is trying to solve right now, not as a backstory revealed through a flashback.\n\nWhen a mechanism matters—a lock, schedule, payment system, security protocol, transport route, or legal exception—state it before or during its use. Explain what it permits, what it costs, and what the character believes about it. Then show the action that follows from that belief. Keep the explanation tight enough to preserve pace, but complete enough that a reader can predict the next tactical step or spot the character's mistake.\n\nUse spatial transitions to maintain a clean causal thread. When a character moves, say where they go and what changes about the situation: a corridor that hides a guard, a vehicle that changes the route, a room where a new person is present. Rapid cuts should not skip the physical connection between actions. Each movement is a chance to show how the rule or resource operates in a new location.\n\nBreak paragraphs around action, dialogue, and private response as distinct beats. After a move or a line of dialogue, give the focal character's reaction room: what she noticed, decided, felt, or failed to see. A reaction can be fear, calculation, anger, uncertainty, dark humor, or simple recognition. Do not make every character witty or every pause conceal guilt. The interiority is part of the story's tactical thread, not decoration.\n\nLet reversals overturn a specific established assumption. A plan that depended on a locked door can be undone when the door is left open; a bribe that assumed a regular shift can fail when the guard is replaced. Before the reversal, the reader should have seen the assumption in action. After the reversal, show what the character now understands about the rule, resource, or relationship.\n\nDo not force first person, comedy, a young cast, or a closing cliffhanger. The point of view can be any participant; the tone may be grim, dry, or light according to its owner. End at a natural scene boundary after a consequence, changed plan, or new question, without requiring the whole crime to resolve. Keep the light-novel presentation serving the story's pace and clarity.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadanao::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Kamachi Kazuma / Lyrical Prose\n\nAnchor each lyrical passage in a working part of the offense: the schedule that leaves a window, the lock whose tolerance one person knows, the arithmetic of a debt, the route timed against a patrol, the message that must not be kept. Build the sentence rhythm out of that element's operation — a long clause that follows a plan unfolding step by step, a short one where a result arrives. Whenever the music gathers, the reader should still be able to say what was done, what it cost, and what it made possible; if they cannot, cut back to the mechanism.\n\nLet an image unfold only as far as it changes what the viewpoint understands or makes present about the crime. A key's worn edge earns its description because that edge is how the copy gets recognized; a returning sound matters because it marks a step that succeeded or failed. Draw comparisons from the scene's own materials or the teller's associations — the trade this person knows, the room they cannot stop seeing — and let a plain sentence land any fact that cannot afford ornament. Dialogue keeps each speaker's own register: a suspect's flat denial stays flat while the narration carries the weight of what that flatness does.\n\nShift attention freely — light, memory, a hand at a drawer — but bring each shift back to the sequence, so the order of actions stays recoverable through every reversal. Render a sudden change in the rhythm itself: the sentence breaking where the plan breaks. End a reply at the turn's living boundary, mid-motion or mid-deliberation or at a settled beat, wherever the scene actually stops, without manufacturing a decorative cadence the action has not earned.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadanap::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Kamachi Kazuma / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nGrow the reflection out of the offense's aftermath rather than a general mood of impermanence. The dividing lines are specific and practical: the route no longer walked because of what happened on it, the phrase that stopped being used after the lie, the drawer that opens differently since the search, the debt that renamed a friendship. When memory returns to the scheme, render the mechanism as it was understood at the time — what the character believed the plan would do, how the rule or resource actually behaved, what could not be undone once it ran. Do not lend later knowledge to the earlier moment; the gap between what the character believed then and what they know now is where the feeling lives.\n\nFavor small irreversible changes over announced sorrow: a routine continuing without the person who made it necessary, a tool put back in the wrong place by someone else's habit, a date that passes unmarked. Ordinary pleasure stays available where the scene permits it — food can still taste good, a joke can still land — and that coexistence is part of the account, not a betrayal of it.\n\nKeep the telling inside the chosen viewpoint's actual knowledge. A character may remember the preparation without knowing its consequence, or live with the consequence while the preparation stays half-remembered; either shape is available, and the prose marks which one is operating. Even when the prose lingers, the sequence stays recoverable: what was done, what was noticed, what is understood differently now. A reply can end inside the reflection itself, on a remembered particular, without an external decision, a task completed, or a closing announcement of loss to justify stopping.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadanaq::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Kamachi Kazuma / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nTell the crime as a situated oral account. Open by establishing the occasion of the telling: who speaks, who listens, and what the moment demands of the story — a witness performing innocence for an officer who lets the story run, an old partner explaining the split before the listener hears a worse version of it. Let that occasion govern pace and shape. An impatient listener pushes the teller toward the mechanism sooner; a question opens a digression that turns out to matter; a need to justify or conceal holds one fact past the point where the listener expected it.\n\nMake explanation part of the telling rather than an interruption beside it. The teller pauses to show how the lock yielded, how the cut was calculated, why this route and not the safer one — because the listener needs to believe it, or because the teller cannot resist proving competence — and then the account returns to what was done with that knowledge and what followed. The listener may misunderstand a rule, challenge the sequence, or set a competing version against it; the frame can revise accordingly, even splitting one incident into two tellings, without making the physical events unreconstructable. Where the teller cannot know, the teller admits it or guesses openly: a bystander reports behavior and marks the motive as inference.\n\nHold one telling tradition coherent rather than stacking framed, mystical, and picaresque conventions together. Repetition serves emphasis, a withheld name is released at the telling's chosen moment, a nested account appears when someone else's story must ride inside this one. Idiom, forms of address, and shared reference come from the community and language the supplied scene actually establishes; do not drape borrowed national texture over a cast the world places elsewhere. End where the telling pauses — a question hanging, the next portion promised, the occasion's business still open — without forcing the frame to yield a confession or a moral.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadanar::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Kamachi Kazuma / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose few details and hold each to a standard of work. A detail earns its place by identifying a resource, marking a limit, breaking a routine, or making a consequence visible — the second phone, the gate open at the wrong hour, the count that comes up short. Anything that only sets a mood the mechanism does not need goes. Trust the reader to carry what is withheld: mention the gloves once and never again, and the reader can bring their absence to the right moment without narration pointing at it.\n\nWrite direct clauses, and compress each explanation to the least language that keeps it usable. Where a rule or resource decides the action — how the alibi is structured, why the cheaper route is the safer one, what the borrowed key actually opens — give exactly enough for the reader to see the choice, then show what is done with it and what follows. Omission leaves inference, not broken causality: what was done, what was noticed, and what it cost must remain reconstructable from what stays on the page. When one extended sentence carries an essential connection better than several fragments, write the longer sentence; shortness is a preference for load-bearing language, not a syllable quota.\n\nKeep voices individual inside the compression. A flat answer can be a decision; a sudden run of extra words can be a calculation; one character's silence does not sound like another's. Inner experience gets the same economy as objects: a thought stays exactly as long as it moves the account. Stop a reply at the edge of the turn's material — an action half-finished, a question asked and not yet answered — rather than appending a closing line that explains what everything meant.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadanas::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Kamachi Kazuma / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose. Enter through a concrete moment of the offense or its aftermath — a handover running long, a search that has stopped being polite, an evening that is also an alibi — and let the narration move among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and inner experience without announcing each transition. Use paragraphs to orient: a break can mark a change of place, speaker, or available information, so the reader never needs a recap to know where they stand.\n\nMake every movement pay one of two ways: it advances the present scene, or it clarifies a rule, resource, incentive, or consequence the scene depends on. When a mechanism matters — how the skim is hidden, why the meeting has to happen before Friday, what the second set of books is for — explain it in the register at hand, as thought while driving, a remembered instruction, one character answering another, or plain narration, then show the action taken on that knowledge, including the action of deciding not to act. Through rapid shifts and reversals, keep the sequence reconstructable: what was done, in what order, with what result known so far.\n\nLet the narrator's voice and the immediate scene set rhythm and register — clipped and quick during execution, slower where someone waits or counts. Authorized inner experience runs long or brief as the character and moment demand; it does not have to change a visible action to belong here. End a reply at the turn's own boundary, which may arrive mid-exchange or mid-motion, and leave the next development available instead of closing a beat the scene has not finished.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadanat::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Kamachi Kazuma / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nLet the established relationship and the scheme run through the same material. Choose scenes where the offense touches what these people already are to each other: the debt one has been hiding, the route that used to be theirs and is now a getaway, the phone one reads because reading it was once allowed, the favor that quietly changes what can be asked. Make the bond legible through private language and repeated moments — the shorthand of an old argument, the habit of leaving food out, the name only one of them uses — and attach each callback to something the mechanism needs, so that when it returns it raises a stake instead of decorating the scene.\n\nGive the feeling real space. Where the scene calls for affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery, let it hold the center while the practical sequence stays visible: what was done, what was understood, what changed between them. One character may explain a rule or resource to the other, misunderstand it, teach it wrong on purpose, or use it to protect, test, or trap; show the action that follows and its double consequence for the scheme and for the bond. Suspense comes from exposure and divided loyalty measured against what the relationship has already promised, not from mandatory collapse — a confession can hold, a kindness can be simply kind, and a betrayal costs what the actual story says it costs.\n\nWork only with the relationship the premise supplies: its history, its private references, its unresolved thing. Keep private thought inside the authorized viewpoint; the other character infers, misses, or misreads, and the miss is scene material in its own right. End a reply where the bond's present moment reaches a genuine pause, even with the scheme mid-step — a question not yet answered, a hand not yet moved, the next consequence left available.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
        },
        {
            "role": "system",
            "injection_position": 0,
