            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-crime-04",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Crime · 04/07",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == crime}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Crime. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 4 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-crime; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPadanau::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Kamachi Kazuma / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the offense as an account of deeds that people will keep telling. Open with a deliberate declaration that fixes an act and its consequence — who broke the agreement, who carried the money across, whose name is now said differently — then expand into the particulars that make the simple version incomplete. Behind every remembered deed, show its working: which rule was exploited, which resource ran out, what the clever route actually cost. The chronicle earns its reputations; the mechanism earns the chronicle.\n\nEstablish persons and acts in compact declarations, and let recurrence do the revising. A designation grows from an established deed or way of speaking — the one who counted twice, the woman who never raised her voice — and a phrase returns when a later event gives it a new application: the same crossing may read as luck before the pursuit and as planning afterward; a debt repaid under pressure may change meaning again once the one who forgave it is gone. Repetition deepens consequence rather than padding the telling. Dialogue can interrupt the chronicle's authority: the subject of an oft-told incident corrects a detail, or answers an elevated introduction with a practical concern about the job still at hand, and the account absorbs the correction without becoming parody. Theft, betrayal, and violence keep their weight here.\n\nHold one coherent telling tradition — its openings, its formulas, its way of marking an oath, a kinship, a gift, a feud — rather than mixing every epic convention, and bring in no pantheon, no heroic doom, and no ceremonial speech the supplied world does not already have. The teller reports public deeds and what was said of them, and marks clearly where motive is guessed. A reply may end with business concluded, resumed, or still running, another act left available to be remembered — no legend declared on anyone's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadanav::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Kamachi Kazuma / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nChoose a small offense whose institutional residue is concrete and unglamorous: a padded expense claim, a faked sick day, a mislabeled work order, a missing portion of a communal fund. Set it in a particular workplace, housing association, church committee, or municipal office, and let its practical aftermath—audit windows, shift rotations, welfare forms, key cards, the order of names on a duty roster—carry the plot. Keep Danish, Norwegian, and Swedish contexts distinct by using the specific names of institutions, streets, and routines actually present in the chosen setting; do not reach for generic snow, silence, or bleakness.\n\nMake one rule or mechanism the visible engine of the story. Define it in plain terms the first time it matters: how the audit window closes, which form must be countersigned, who holds the spare key, what the CCTV covers, why Sunday shifts are unstaffed. Allow a character to exploit or misunderstand this mechanism, then write the resulting action so the reader can trace the physical sequence through each reversal—who was where, what they handled, what they signed, what they saw.\n\nTreat the focal character's misreading as a mechanism in its own right. Show them applying the rule too late, explaining it to the wrong person, or remembering an earlier conversation imperfectly. Their private reasoning may be cautious, embarrassed, loyal, or self-serving, but it must stay available as nonspoken interiority that others cannot hear. Do not conceal an authorized focal conclusion just to preserve a later reveal; a colleague can misinterpret the same fact while the reader knows what the viewpoint actually thought.\n\nLet each domestic or workplace scene advance the consequences rather than solve them: a colleague stops covering a shift, a partner asks about an unexplained phone call, an official sends the form back, a neighbor trades a rumor for a favor. Do not impose confession, punishment, or moral resolution. The response may close on a completed form, a pending question, or plain acknowledgment of what the character now knows, even while the institution continues to move slowly or badly.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadanaw::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Kamachi Kazuma / Screenplay\n\nUse scene headings only when location or time changes; otherwise keep the action within a defined playable space. In each scene, make one mechanism fully legible through what can be shot: a door code punched too slowly, a security pass slid the wrong way, a delivery schedule checked against a wristwatch, a receipt folded and pocketed. When a character can exploit or misunderstand the mechanism, show the relevant step in the action line and let the next beat pay it off—the guard turns the corner, the card reader blinks red, the count is one short.\n\nWrite dialogue that follows or precedes that physical beat. Keep speaker cues clear, and let each exchange carry one practical question or answer: 'Is the back gate locked?' 'It was, this morning.' A character may explain a mechanism only to someone who needs the information to act; otherwise show the action and let a brief line acknowledge its result. Avoid conversation that exists to describe what the viewer has just seen.\n\nPreserve required private experience in an italicized note set off inside an action block, clearly marked as unspoken and visible only to the reader, not as a voice-over or spoken line. For example, after a character closes a drawer, the action block may include: (he knows the spare key should be there; it is not). Another character does not know this conclusion unless it is later spoken or acted. Do not conceal an authorized focal realization; when the viewpoint solves a piece of the scheme, let the note record it, even if the line delivery is cautious or different.\n\nLet each new scene reframe a known mechanism. A cut from a character leaving the office to the same character entering the stairwell can alter the audience's sense of available time or access. A repeated sound—the elevator bell, a metal door—can become a tactical cue. Do not fill the page with camera directions. The scene can close on a failed handoff, a changed alibi, a supervisor's instruction, or a screen showing exactly what the character knows rather than forcing a confession or arrest.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadanax::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Kamachi Kazuma / Stage Play\n\nBuild each scene in a defined playing space and let that space test a claim. An entrance or exit is visible to everyone; if a character says they did not go through a particular door, a later entrance from that door without a scene change can reverse the audience's understanding. Keep blocks and business simple: a coat hung on a chair, a key left on a table, a bag placed behind a desk. When a mechanism matters, make it a piece of physical business—checking a bag, sliding a key across the floor, reading a note aloud because someone else asks what it says.\n\nUse dialogue to establish who can see what in the shared space. A line such as 'I saw you on the second floor' only works if the actor's earlier position made that plausible. Let a question expose a practical contradiction: 'How did you know the side door was left open?' If the answer requires knowledge the speaker could not have gained, pause to let that land. Do not turn characters into oracles; a witness can answer plainly or challenge the premise for a reason that belongs to the relationship.\n\nPreserve required private experience as a printed director's note, not a spoken monologue. Use italics or a parenthetical that the audience cannot hear: (she realizes the spare key is missing, but says nothing yet). Another character does not know this conclusion until they see or hear evidence. Allow an aside only when it serves a theatrical purpose—for instance, a character steps forward to explain a mechanism the audience must understand before the next reversal, then returns to the scene. Do not use asides as a default thought bubble.\n\nLet the physical objects become the engine of reversal. An item placed in Act One may be absent in Act Two; a door opened and left ajar may be found locked; a phone charger plugged in after the supposed departure makes the alibi visibly impossible. Build these reversals through staging, not through dialogue that recounts what the audience already saw. The scene can end on a paused gesture, an unanswered question, or the sound of a door closing offstage, without a forced confession or arrest.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadanay::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Kamachi Kazuma / Stream of Consciousness\n\nOpen one authorized mind — a culprit mid-job, an accomplice waiting on a call, an investigator reading a room — and let the crime live there as recurring concern: the detail that does not fit, the partner gone quiet, the version of events that has to hold until Friday. Tell it by association rather than report: a smell, a half-heard warning, the weight of a key in a pocket pulls attention out of the present room to the planning table, forward to what the money is for, down into the body's own signals — dry mouth, a hand checking the same pocket twice.\n\nWhen a rule matters — how the float gets counted, what makes the alibi hold, which door is someone else's responsibility — surface it as rehearsal instead of lecture: the character walks the steps in their own jargon, catches a misremembered one, re-derives it against what they can see, and the next perception or action shows the corrected result. Let a private line of their own — someone they will not touch, a shortcut they refuse — surface the same way, mid-plan, and complicate the practical chain. Interruptions from outside, whether speech, a door, or a phone, can cut a thought mid-clause and let it resurface changed, which keeps rapid reversals human instead of chaotic.\n\nAnchor every jump so the reader can separate now from remembered from imagined: name the trigger, tense the recollection, mark the return. Keep the physical sequence recoverable through the concrete objects the mind keeps touching, so who was where and what changed hands can always be retraced. What this mind concludes stays inside it; the others know only what leaks into speech or act, and the spoken answer can differ from the running thought when caution, loyalty, or fear of exposure supports it. Stop wherever the flow's boundary falls — mid-task, on a wait, with the worry still circling — owing no confession, arrest, or settled conscience.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadanaz::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Kamachi Kazuma / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nLet the chat itself carry the scheme. Whether the log holds two people running it, a handler and the one being run, or a work group with the plan hiding inside ordinary logistics, everyone writes in sender-attributed messages with habits that identify them before the names do: fragments against full sentences, the voice-note holdout, the one who answers in seconds at strange hours. Put the timing to work — the gap where an answer should have been can be the window a job needed, and a timestamp that contradicts someone's story is exactly the kind of exposure a saved account can prove.\n\nWhen a procedure matters — the drop instructions, the shift swap that buys the half hour, which receipt has to vanish — let it travel as transmission: one side explains or asks, the other confirms, misreads, or answers a different question, and the consequence lands in the next message or in a short, clearly separated context beat stating where the people and the goods actually were. Even when the log jumps between names and channels, the reader can retrace who was where and what changed hands. Include a read receipt, edit, deletion, forward, attachment, or removal from the group only when it changes what someone can prove or do — never as decoration.\n\nAn unsent draft is a thing its writer composed and chose not to send, a decision rather than a window onto the whole live mind; keep genuine private experience in separated context where it contributes feeling or reasoning. Nothing transmits what was never typed: the others know only what the log shows them, and a conclusion the reader has watched form stays unspoken until someone actually says it — or never. Let the exchange end as exchanges do, on a question hanging, work finished, or silence that means waiting; no confession, raid, or reckoning is owed by the form.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadanba::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Kamachi Kazuma / Visual Novel\n\nStage the offense as scenes. Open each with one line of concrete placement — where, when, who is present, the object or document that will matter — then run the exchange in plainly attributed dialogue, and give the focal character a short beat of inner response after the lines that count: a read on the other person, a recalculation, a flicker of what this is costing. Background and expression cues orient the moment rather than decide it; the fixed smile at the loading dock can draw the focal suspicion, but it is a contradiction in an arrangement — a receipt that should not exist, a timeline that will not close, two accounts of the same half hour — that makes exposure real.\n\nWhen a rule matters — how the key is signed out, when the float is counted, what the intake form actually asks — let the focal narration walk it in practical terms while the character uses it: checking the blind spot, counting, timing the route, misreading a line. Show the exploitation or the mistake inside the same action, and let the next scene inherit its consequence. Mark every change of place and time clearly so the physical chain stays followable across rapid shifts; the reader can always retrace who was where and what changed hands.\n\nThe exchange after a focal read may confirm it, complicate it, or reveal the speaker was worried about something else entirely; treat faces as material the focal character interprets, not a lie detector. Private thought stays outside the other characters' knowledge, and a spoken answer can differ from it when caution, loyalty, or fear of exposure supports the difference. Advance through what the mechanism's use cost or earned — an easier plan, a story that now needs maintenance, a supervisor with one more question — and stop at the scene's own boundary. Nothing comes bundled with the format: no branching menu, player decision, romance route, or image, and no owed confession or arrest.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadanbb::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Kamachi Kazuma / Web Serial\n\nRun the crime in installments. Each reply carries the through-line — what is underway, whether that is a handoff, an audit, or a debt being serviced; one local change that actually lands, such as a shift covered, a name asked for, a payment that clears or fails; and an edge the next reply can pick up. Recover an earlier particular only when the current move depends on it — the form that was filed, the hour the camera was down — without retelling the installment that established it.\n\nExplain a rule in plain practical terms the first time a character can exploit or misunderstand it — how the payout clears, how the master key is signed out, what the audit flags — and let a later installment reuse that same rule under new pressure, so the reversal is earned by the established mechanism instead of arriving as information from nowhere. Compress uneventful intervals, but never quietly repair history: if the lock was fixed or the roster changed between replies, that change exists and can matter. Across every jump, keep who was where and what changed hands retraceable.\n\nLet ordinary stakes accumulate alongside the scheme — rent, shifts, who is speaking to whom — and measure progress as knowledge, money, trust, or position, not only as victories. Keep inward experience present when it contributes feeling or thought, even in an installment where nothing gets decided; what one character concludes is not yet known to the others. A hook is occasional and earned — an unanswered call, a name in a ledger — not a debt every ending pays. Close on work done, a request pending, or a risk plainly acknowledged; no confession, arrest, or moral settlement is owed by the form.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaoaa::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Li Bihua / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the crime from the viewpoint of a character who is personally implicated through attachment: they may love, owe, fear, or protect someone involved in the offense, cover-up, or aftermath. Use Li Bihua\\'s sensual precision to attach emotion to concrete objects and places that also carry criminal meaning. Describe a gift that was both affection and payment, a room kept unchanged after a disappearance, a garment bought for someone who is now a suspect, food left uneaten during a conversation that shifts the case. Let the beautiful surface make the cost more legible: the more intimately the viewpoint notices a texture or smell, the more sharply the reader should feel what is at stake.\n\nTreat emotional turbulence as a live force on the scene. When the viewpoint admits need, withholds a question, apologizes, or returns to a familiar place, show how that choice changes the next exchange or the next practical act. Do not let every wound become a confession. Use visible interiority for the viewpoint character\\'s reasoning and sensation: what they notice and refuse to say, how they answer a direct question with a half-truth they believe is protective. Their private experience should be available even when the other character cannot see it, and their internal conflict should affect what they choose to investigate, hide, or reveal.\n\nLet the criminal facts advance through charged details rather than separate exposition. A remembered place can become evidence: the smell of a hallway where a key changed hands, the way a drawer sticks after being searched, a particular dish that links two people to the same time. A shared habit can become a pattern of access. An object can tie affection to the offense. Distinguish what the viewpoint knows from what they hope, and what they suspect from what they can prove. The investigation may move forward, but the emotional consequence does not have to resolve when the factual question does. End the scene where the active exchange, action, or private interval naturally stops, without forcing reconciliation, punishment, or confession. A discovery may leave the relationship more strained, not less, and that strain is part of the crime\\'s aftermath.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaoab::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Li Bihua / Chat Roleplay\n\nIn each reply, present a coherent shared interval in the active crime scene. Use unmistakable dialogue attribution and readable paragraphs so players can follow who speaks, who acts, and what the viewpoint character notices. Keep NPC behavior active: a contact can arrive with information, a message can arrive, a room can be searched, or a conversation can continue. Do not stop at a question or forced choice; instead, leave the scene at a natural continuation point that follows from what just happened, such as an NPC glancing toward a door or a phone vibrating with new evidence.\n\nUse Li Bihua\\'s sensual precision in small, concrete details that belong to the scene and the crime. The viewpoint character may notice the weight of a key, the smell of rain on a coat, how a familiar person touches a glass before lying, or the unusual silence in a room where something has been removed. Choose one or two precise sensory details per paragraph; they orient the moment and reveal the character\\'s divided loyalty or costly attachment without turning every observation into an explanation. Keep the atmosphere intimate but scannable: a single telling detail carries more weight than a paragraph of atmosphere.\n\nPreserve the active authorship contract for narrative person, action markers, and player control. Show the viewpoint character\\'s private reasoning and sensation through the selected narrative person, but keep other characters\\' hidden thoughts outside their knowledge. Let clues and criminal developments emerge through NPC dialogue and sensory evidence: a receipt found in a coat, a lie exposed by a detail the player remembers, a familiar scent linking a suspect to a place. End the reply where the scene naturally continues, leaving the next action, question, or arrival open. The crime may advance through the interval, but the reply does not need to complete an investigation beat or resolve an emotional conflict before stopping.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaoac::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Li Bihua / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic telling route. Structure the crime as a sequence of linked episodes, each one advancing the offense, scheme, or aftermath through a concrete encounter: a meeting that changes an arrangement, a delivery that goes wrong, a return to a place where something was hidden, a conversation that reveals a divided loyalty. Begin each episode in medias res with a specific location and activity drawn from the active story\\'s period and community. End it when the immediate exchange or action concludes, leaving a partial result or unresolved question that the next episode can take up with new information.\n\nLet a recurring object, debt, or relationship connect the episodes forward. Li Bihua\\'s sensual precision should attach to that recurring element: a piece of jewelry that passes between hands and accumulates meaning, a remembered teahouse where a promise was made and later broken, a room that has been emptied but still holds a particular smell. When a place or object reappears, describe it afresh with a small change in sensory detail—a chipped cup, a different light, a worn edge—so the reader feels time and consequence rather than a static recap. The glamorous surface should sharpen the uncomfortable relationship or the criminal consequence, not decorate it.\n\nKeep the viewpoint character\\'s attachment to a person or place active in every episode: what they notice, what they risk, what they omit when they retell an event. Distinguish between what they knew at the time and what they understand later; an earlier episode should not retroactively contain a later realization. Let each episode\\'s dialogue and action carry the vernacular flavor of the supplied language and community without importing a generic historical setting. Do not force a moral summary or punishment at the close of any installment. The episodic form organizes recurrence and consequence, not a fixed lesson; the crime\\'s human aftermath may remain open long after the factual sequence is assembled.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaoad::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Li Bihua / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the crime in hard-edged, concrete prose. Use forceful diction and compressed transitions: move from a knock to a door opening to a hand on a weapon without explaining the space between. Let material wear carry the stakes: a scuffed shoe, a cracked phone screen, a stain that will not come out, a lock that has been forced before. Show physical consequences in the body and the room—a cut that hurts later, a bruise hidden under a sleeve, a chair leg snapped during a struggle—not only in reported emotion.\n\nAdapt Li Bihua\\'s sensual precision to the compressed register by selecting one or two exact sensory details per scene and letting them do the work of atmosphere. A smell of cheap perfume over sweat can mark a betrayal; the weight of a jade bracelet against a bruised wrist can carry both affection and harm; the way a familiar voice changes when it lies can be caught in a single clause. The beauty has a cost, and that cost should appear as a specific surface under pressure rather than as a descriptive pause. A glamorous object can be evidence, a bribe, a keepsake, or a liability—make its role clear through how characters handle or avoid it.\n\nKeep the viewpoint character\\'s divided attachment active through action and practical choice rather than extended reflection. Show what they do for someone they should not help: they pocket a letter instead of reporting it, they lie about a time, they return a key they had resolved to keep. When the character\\'s reasoning genuinely needs space, retain a longer thought, but compress the transitions around it: a short, sharp memory of a shared meal can justify a dangerous decision in one sentence. Vary rhythm so a sudden action lands after a shorter line. End the scene at the natural stop of the active exchange or private interval; do not require a completed investigation beat or a moral turn before ending. The crime\\'s pressure remains in the next unanswered movement, not in a summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaoae::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Li Bihua / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the crime entirely through documents written by people entangled in the offense, scheme, or aftermath. Each document must have a clear author, an intended recipient or purpose, and a time of composition. Choose the specific form the scene requires and use it faithfully: a letter that apologizes without confessing, a police report that records only what was observed, a journal entry that revises the writer\\'s own memory, a pawn ticket that becomes evidence, a text message sent too late. Do not rotate through formats mechanically; let the document type itself reveal something about the writer\\'s situation and what they dare to put in writing.\n\nLet Li Bihua\\'s remembered places and divided attachment shape what each writer records and omits. A letter may describe a room in sensual detail while avoiding the person who was there; a journal may return to a shared meal or a gift and only gradually reveal its connection to the offense. The beauty in the documents must carry a cost: a keepsake described with too much care, a place remembered because it cannot be returned to, a compliment that reads as a threat once the recipient\\'s situation is known. Use the document\\'s own voice to show this—a formal report will flatten the emotional detail that a private diary lingers on, and that difference is information.\n\nAdvance the investigation through gaps, revisions, and conflicting accounts. What one document knows may be contradicted by another written later or by a material trace attached to it: a date that changes between a letter and a receipt, a phrase repeated from a conversation the writer should not have heard, an unsent draft tucked into a file. Distinguish what the writer knew at the time of composition from what they understand now, and do not let a later document retroactively correct an earlier one as if the writer had always known. Maintain readable continuity by arranging the documents in the order they were created or received, but do not add an omniscient explanation after every record. Let the reader assemble the crime from the partial views, and let the emotional consequence remain unresolved even when the factual record becomes clear.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaoaf::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Li Bihua / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite the crime story with room for sincere care and small pleasures that belong to the characters and their situation. The offense, scheme, or aftermath remains active, so the stakes do not disappear when warmth enters. Have the viewpoint character notice and enjoy a familiar preference: a particular tea prepared the way someone likes it, a shared meal after a difficult errand, a clean shirt left out before a meeting, a quiet moment of ease in a place that is otherwise unsafe. Let the comfort be practical and specific, tied to concrete actions and sensory details, rather than a generic promise that everything will be all right.\n\nUse Li Bihua\\'s sensual precision to make the warmth particular. Describe the texture of a blanket, the taste of a favorite food, the sound of a familiar voice when it is not lying, the way a room feels when someone returns. The beauty can carry its cost without canceling the comfort: a gift may be both a kindness and a reminder of what was done; a remembered place may be both a refuge and a scene of the offense. Let affection be sincere without requiring a confession or emotional repair. A character can be cared for and still be difficult, private, or in danger; the viewpoint may accept a kindness while knowing it cannot undo a crime. Do not turn every moment of care into a revelation about the offense, but let the context remain present in small ways—a wary glance at a window, a phone set face-down, a joke that falls flat because of an unspoken worry.\n\nKeep personality, disagreement, and the crime\\'s consequences intact. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable, does not remove the threat, and does not turn the story into a domestic vignette. The characters may disagree about what to do next, withhold information, or make choices that complicate the situation. The warmth is one current in the scene, not a replacement for the crime. Let the investigation or scheme continue through these moments: a quiet conversation may reveal a new detail, a shared task may uncover a clue, a moment of ease may give one character the courage to be more honest—or not. End where the shared interval naturally pauses, without forcing a resolution of the offense or a declaration of love.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaoag::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Li Bihua / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite the crime in composed novel prose with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Let the narration frame the offense, scheme, or aftermath through social context, history, reputation, or judgment where the chosen narrator permits. A transaction can be placed against the history of a family business; a betrayal can be measured against what a community expects; a piece of evidence can carry the weight of a place that has changed. Distinguish the narration from ordinary spoken language: the sentences may be more considered, the observations more composed, but the characters\\' speech remains their own—use dialogue to show individual voices while the narrative voice provides perspective and shading.\n\nUse Li Bihua\\'s sensual precision and costly beauty to deepen the social framing. A remembered place can be described with enough particularity to show why it matters: the light in a room where a promise was made, the smell of a market that has since been demolished, the feel of a fabric chosen for someone who is now a suspect. Let the glamorous surface sharpen the uncomfortable relationship or the criminal consequence. The viewpoint character\\'s divided attachment should be legible in what they notice, what they return to, and what they cannot say, even when the narrative distance keeps their private thought from becoming melodrama. Show interiority through composed reflection: a paragraph that begins with a sensory memory and moves to a social judgment can carry both the character\\'s feeling and the narrator\\'s framing.\n\nPreserve the crime\\'s human aftermath through the formal structure. The narration may move from a public scene to a private memory, from a reported event to its later consequence, or from a social judgment to the character\\'s own reasoning. Keep the difference between what the character knew then and what the narrator can now frame; an earlier scene should not contain later knowledge. Ceremony and correspondence may organize a passage when relevant: a formal dinner where a deal is struck, a letter that arrives too late, a public announcement that changes how a private act is read. The formal architecture organizes the telling; it does not require an aristocratic setting, archaic vocabulary, or uniformly elaborate speech. End the passage where the composed movement naturally rests, without forcing a verdict or a completed investigation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaoah::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Li Bihua / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the crime as an account being given now, to a listener who has a stake in what the teller wants or fears. Establish the occasion and the listener's reason for staying: the teller may be buying time, softening a confession, courting an ally, or making a remembered place too vivid to abandon. Let the outer scene carry the present pressure of the offense while the embedded story carries its preparation, execution, or aftermath.\n\nUse Li Bihua's sensual precision to make the embedded story feel like a kept object: describe the texture of a doorway, the smell of rain on a particular street, the weight of a gift, the exact way a face changed when a promise was made. These details should not decorate the crime; they should show what the teller prized, envied, or could not bear to lose. Let beauty appear beside the wound it cost, so the listener feels the attraction and the damage in the same image.\n\nMake the teller's selection and emphasis an act of concealment or persuasion. The nested tale may linger on a farewell while skipping the moment money changed hands, or return lovingly to a room where a betrayal was planned. Do not announce the omission. Let the listener's questions, interruptions, or silences in the outer frame mark what the account has not yet explained. A repeated phrase or object can accrue meaning across the telling: the same mirror, the same endearment, the same excuse, each recurrence revising what the listener thought it meant.\n\nKeep the narrative levels intelligible by returning to the outer scene whenever the listener's attention or the teller's purpose shifts. The embedded story may be interrupted, resumed, or finished under threat. Its delayed revelation should change how the listener reads an earlier detail, not merely supply a twist. The final turn of the telling should expose the relationship between the teller and the crime: what the teller wanted the listener to believe, and what the listener now understands about the teller's loyalty, guilt, or desire. Do not require a magical frame, a royal audience, or a cliffhanger between every nested passage.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaoai::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Li Bihua / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the Romantic and uncanny narrative route. Let the crime emerge through an atmosphere that makes a familiar place feel estranged after the offense: a house that now seems to remember a struggle, a street where the light falls differently, a routine that continues beside an absence. Do not introduce a ghost or supernatural cause. The uncanny effect comes from the narrator's or focal character's lingering attachment to what the crime has changed.\n\nShape syntax and distance around the chosen teller. A first-person participant may move between precise sensory memory and the pressure of what cannot yet be said. A more distant narrator may hold the scene steadily while the focal character's unease gathers in repeated details. Let Li Bihua's sensual precision attach to objects that carry the offense's cost: a ring left on a sill, a coat still holding a particular scent, a window that will not close properly. Describe these things as if they have become witnesses, not as clues in a case file.\n\nKeep the crime's human sequence concrete. Show the preparation through a small, intimate decision; the execution through its immediate physical and emotional effect; the aftermath through the way a relationship now hesitates, lies, or clings. The atmosphere should sharpen these events rather than replace them. A beautiful or haunting image must connect to an actual act, incentive, or consequence.\n\nAllow the uncanny to operate as memory and guilt made environmental. A character may feel that a room has kept the shape of a final conversation, or that a familiar object has become impossible to touch. The narration should render that experience without confirming that the world itself is haunted. The crime's resolution, if one comes, should explain the human cause and leave the emotional haunting intact. Do not import German national stereotypes, bureaucratic machinery, or translated word order; the route is a narrative treatment, not a setting.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaoaj::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Li Bihua / Gothic-Punk\n\nSet the crime where an old authority presses against present life: a family claim, an institutional privilege, a debt inherited from a previous generation, or a building whose rules outlast its purpose. Make that authority concrete in urban material detail: the worn brass of a door, the smell of a basement archive, the particular silence of a room where decisions are made. The offense should arise from someone trying to live, love, or escape inside that structure.\n\nUse Li Bihua's sensual precision to render the glamour of the old power and its cost in the same gesture. A beautiful room can also be the place where a threat was delivered. An elegant gift can carry an obligation. A lover's attention can be genuine and still entangled with what the lover hopes to gain. Let private desire complicate the crime rather than simply motivate it: the culprit may want freedom and still mourn the authority they betrayed, or the victim may have been complicit in the arrangement that destroyed them.\n\nShow social masks as functional and fragile. In public scenes, characters perform the roles the old authority requires. In trusted company, the mask may fall away enough to reveal fear, longing, or a plan. The narration should distinguish what a character shows from what they privately experience, without making every private thought a confession or every public gesture a lie. Let the crime's preparation, execution, and aftermath move through these shifts in intimacy.\n\nKeep the atmosphere attached to actual events. A decaying building matters because of what happened there, who controls it, or what it hides. A moment of personal horror should follow from a specific threat, loss, or discovery. The crime's consequences should leave the old authority and the present relationship changed, not merely moody. Do not import Kindred physiology, Humanity numbers, factions, powers, or blood-bond rules; those remain outside this style.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaoak::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Li Bihua / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the testimonial and autobiographical route. Write the crime as a first-person account composed after the offense, addressed to a specific reader or remembered audience: a confession, a memoir, a statement given to someone who will judge, or a private record meant to set the record straight. Let the teller's reason for speaking shape what is included and what is softened.\n\nKeep the setting, language, community, and period specific as supplied by the story. Do not force English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure onto the scene. Let the teller's remembered places carry Li Bihua's sensual precision: the exact heat of an afternoon, the smell of a particular kitchen, the way a lane narrowed before a house, the texture of a garment worn on the day of the offense. These details should show what the teller loved, envied, or lost, not merely provide local color.\n\nMake the account's selectivity part of the crime's aftermath. The teller may dwell on a moment of temptation, return repeatedly to a face they failed, or pass quickly over the act itself. The narration should render the teller's divided attachment directly: love and betrayal, loyalty and self-justification, beauty and its cost in the same memory. Let the reader sense what the teller still cannot say plainly.\n\nPreserve the difference between what the teller knew then and what they know now. A later understanding may color the account, but it cannot retroactively become the earlier motive. Show the offense's preparation, execution, and consequences through the teller's actual participation and observation. The account need not end in punishment or full self-knowledge; it should end where the teller's need to speak reaches its limit. Do not require a frame tale, a moral lesson, or a universal family structure.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaoal::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Li Bihua / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the diary and reflective miscellany route. Write the crime through dated entries or occasion-based passages kept by a participant or close observer. Each entry should attend to the immediate moment while also registering what the offense has changed: a meal eaten alone, a street walked differently, a small object now impossible to put away. Let the gaps between entries do some of the work; what the writer does not record can be as revealing as what they return to.\n\nLet Li Bihua's sensual precision live in the diarist's attention. Describe the particular weight of a cup, the color of a sky before an appointment, the exact sound of a door closing, the feel of a sleeve against a wrist. These details should carry the diarist's attachment, jealousy, or dread. Beauty and cost should appear together: a lovely morning remembered as the last ordinary one, a gift described with the knowledge of what it purchased.\n\nKeep the crime's sequence legible through the diarist's partial view. Preparation may appear as a small note about an errand or a hesitation. Execution may be recorded indirectly, or revisited in fragments over several entries. Aftermath should unfold through the diarist's changing relationship to a person, place, or object. The diary form does not require the writer to understand everything; it requires the writer to keep noticing.\n\nPreserve the difference between private thought and written record. The entry is composed, not a live transcript of the mind. The diarist may omit, revise, or perform a feeling for an imagined future reader. Let the narration show what the writer chooses to record and what the language cannot quite contain. Seasonal attention, omission, and direct feeling are available where appropriate, but do not impose a mandatory inventory of Japanese scenery or a universal quietness. The crime's resolution, if it comes, should be legible through the accumulated entries rather than a sudden external explanation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaoam::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Li Bihua / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate psychological and urban fiction route. Write the crime through close third-person attention to one or two focal characters whose desires, fears, and divided loyalties drive the offense and its aftermath. Keep the city specific as supplied: its streets, transit, noise, weather, and the particular rooms where private life happens. The urban setting should press on the characters' choices without becoming a travelogue.\n\nLet Li Bihua's sensual precision render the glamour that entangles the characters. Describe a lover's clothes, the light in a rented room, the smell of a market after rain, the exact way a hand lingers on a table. These details should reveal what the focal character wants and what that wanting costs. A beautiful surface can sharpen an uncomfortable relationship: the same elegant apartment can be the site of a bribe, a seduction, or a threat.\n\nShow the crime's preparation, execution, and aftermath through the focal character's private experience. The narration may render their reasoning, hesitation, sensory attention, and the moment a choice becomes irreversible. Keep access limited to the chosen focal character or characters; other people's motives appear through what the focal character observes, remembers, or infers. Let jealousy, loyalty, and desire complicate the offense rather than reduce it to a single motive.\n\nPreserve the psychological realism of the route. The characters should act from specific incentives and histories, not from a generic fatalism. Doomed attraction may be present, but it must arise from the actual relationship and circumstances. The crime's consequences should leave the focal character changed in a legible way, even if no punishment or full understanding arrives. Do not add magical realism or supernatural facts to a world that excludes them; the intimacy and the city carry the story.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaoan::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Li Bihua / Light Novel\n\nEnter each scene in the immediate situation, with the location and the focal character's position clear in the first lines. Keep spatial changes easy to follow: when a character moves from a street to a room, from a car to a doorway, or from a public space to a private one, mark the change with a concrete detail. Use short, readable paragraphs around action, dialogue, and private response so the crime's sequence stays legible at speed.\n\nCompress Li Bihua's sensual precision into vivid beats. Instead of extended description, give one or two sharp sensory details that carry the scene's emotional weight: the cold metal of a gate under a hand, the smell of perfume mixed with cigarette smoke, the exact color of a bruise beginning to show, the weight of an envelope passed across a table. Let beauty and cost appear in the same image. A lovely room can be the place where a threat was made; a cherished gift can be the thing that binds someone to a crime.\n\nUse focused reaction beats to render private experience. After a significant action or line of dialogue, pause briefly on what the focal character feels, remembers, or decides. The reaction can be serious, uncertain, angry, or afraid according to its owner. It should connect the visible event to the character's attachment, jealousy, or dread. Do not require comedy, a young cast, or first person; the style is the presentation, not the cast.\n\nKeep the crime's preparation, execution, and aftermath concrete. Show the small step that commits a character, the moment the offense happens, and the immediate consequence in a relationship or a place. The fast pace should not erase motive or aftermath. Let the final beat of a scene leave the reader with a clear image or an unresolved question that belongs to the crime, not a compulsory cliffhanger. Preserve the intimacy of remembered places by letting a later scene return to an earlier detail and show how its meaning has changed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaoao::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Li Bihua / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet the crime enter through surfaces the viewpoint character knows too well: the grain of a door, the weight of a gift, the smell of a room after someone has left it. Describe each surface with enough precision that it becomes a trace of the offense or the attachment surrounding it. Beauty must not float free of consequence. A lovely object can be the thing that was stolen, the bribe that was accepted, the keepsake that incriminates, or the reminder of a person the narrator cannot afford to protect.\n\nBuild each paragraph so syntax follows attention rather than a fixed report. Begin with a concrete perception, then let a subordinate clause or a repeated sound carry the narrator toward what the perception costs. Unfold a comparison only when it changes what the narrator understands or makes an absent person present. Keep the figurative register attached to this narrator's actual associations; other characters may speak plainly, and that plainness can stand out without mockery.\n\nUse recurring images to track the case's pressure. The same color, texture, or gesture may return with altered meaning after a new fact is learned. A recurrence should not simply restate an emotion; it should show how the character now reads an earlier moment. When evidence appears, preserve its causal function. An inference must still rest on an arrangement, an account, or a physical fact, not on the mere loveliness or menace of an image.\n\nKeep the chosen viewpoint's access intact. The narration may render one character's private attention richly while others remain opaque. Jealousy, divided loyalty, or doomed attraction may shape what the narrator notices, but they do not grant knowledge of another mind. End the response where the scene's actual attention rests, leaving the crime's consequence still developing rather than announced as a completed moral.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaoap::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Li Bihua / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the offense be felt through what it has made unavailable. A place, a routine, a phrase, or a person can now be reached only through memory, and the narrator's return to it registers the change. Keep the remembered occasion concrete: who was there, what was handled, what was said, what the narrator did not yet know. The crime's consequences give the memory its edge, but the memory remains a real occasion, not a generalized mood of loss.\n\nAllow present experience and recollection to share the same passage. A current detail—a room's temperature, a street corner, the weight of an object—calls up an earlier one, and the narrator notices that the object has outlasted the person or that a familiar place now serves a different purpose. Do not require every reflection to end in a declaration of impermanence. Ordinary pleasure can remain pleasurable even while it carries the knowledge of what followed.\n\nUse the reflective movement to reveal the narrator's stake in the crime. Jealousy, divided attachment, guilt, or the wish to have acted differently surfaces through the comparison of then and now. A present perception can clarify an earlier incentive or risk without pretending the narrator knew it at the time. Regret does not grant access to another character's mind. When the account moves back to present action, let the remembered detail inform what the narrator now notices or decides; it sharpens, rather than replaces, the need for actual evidence and consequence.\n\nEnd the response where the past and present are both still active. The narrator may be waiting, returning, or continuing an ordinary task that the memory has altered. Do not force a completed reconciliation or a final verdict on what the loss means.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaoaq::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Li Bihua / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed oral account as the definite regional route. Establish a situated teller and at least one listener whose presence matters to the telling. The crime reaches the reader through what the teller chooses to relate, in what order, and with what emphasis. Keep the frame active: the listener interrupts, questions, supplies a detail, or is implicated in the events, and the teller's relationship to that listener shapes what is said or withheld.\n\nLet the teller's sensual precision carry Li Bihua's intimacy with remembered places. Describe the room where the offense was prepared, the object that passed between hands, the particular look of a person at the moment a promise was broken. Let beauty have a cost: a glamorous surface may be the thing that drew someone into the scheme or the thing that now makes the memory unbearable. The teller's jealousy, loyalty, guilt, or longing should be legible in the selection and emphasis of details, not announced in commentary.\n\nPreserve the crime's causal structure inside the retelling. The listener may already know the outcome or may be waiting to hear how it happened; in either case, their questions and silences mark what information still needs to be supplied. The teller can withhold information, but that withholding must register in the listener's attention or the teller's hesitation. Preparation, incentive, execution, and consequence remain inferable from the related events. A repeated phrase or a delayed revelation changes the listener's understanding of an earlier detail rather than serving as ornament.\n\nKeep the tradition specific and the world facts supplied. Do not import a generic honor code, mysticism, or ceremonial speech. The teller's access remains limited to what that teller could know or has been told; distinguish reported events from witnessed ones. End the response while the telling is still underway, with the listener's attention or the teller's hesitation marking the next turn rather than closing the case.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaoar::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Li Bihua / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose a small number of exact details and let them carry the crime's pressure. One object, one remembered place, one gesture can stand for a larger attachment or a larger danger, but every chosen detail must be traceable to the scene and the causal situation. Prefer direct clauses. When Li Bihua's sensual precision appears, make it specific and functional: the texture of a coat that was left behind, the temperature of a room after the money was taken, the sound of a door that used to be opened without knocking.\n\nKeep only the inward connection that materially contributes to the present account. A narrator may register jealousy, longing, or the cost of a beautiful thing in a single exact sentence; do not explain the emotion again in the next sentence. If a thought needs more room to make sense, give it that room rather than breaking it into performed fragments. Leave a selected fact open so its meaning can land later, but do not delete the causality it carries. The reader can understand why a character does not answer, or why an object is handled carefully, without the narration announcing the reason.\n\nPreserve the crime's causal spine. Preparation, incentive, execution, evidence, pursuit, and aftermath remain legible through selected facts. A detail may be withheld, but not in a way that erases why something happened. When a new fact changes the meaning of an earlier detail, return to that detail briefly and let the change register without a paragraph of reflection.\n\nKeep voices individual. The narrator's restraint is not a rule for every character; another speaker may be expansive or evasive because the crime has given them reason to be. Let that contrast be visible in dialogue. End the response at the natural limit of the scene's attention, with the next consequence still possible rather than summarized.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaoas::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Li Bihua / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the crime as continuous scene-first prose. Let action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience move into one another without transitional machinery. Begin a paragraph with a practical movement, pass through what the viewpoint character notices, and arrive at a thought that changes the next action. Keep the movement natural to the scene's pressure rather than alternating mechanically among modes.\n\nLet Li Bihua's sensual precision live in the narrator's attention. Describe the particular surface, texture, or remembered place that carries the attachment or the danger. A glamorous detail should sharpen the uncomfortable relationship around it: the expensive room where someone is now afraid, the careful gift that later becomes evidence, the familiar street that has become a place to avoid. Do not let the prose become uniformly ornate. Plain action and direct dialogue provide contrast.\n\nUse the narrator's inner experience to make motives and divided loyalties legible. When a viewpoint character thinks one thing and says another, the difference must belong to that person: fear, attachment, strategy, uncertainty, or the need to protect someone. If the thought and speech align, that is also a choice, not a default. Keep access limited to the authorized viewpoint. Other characters' motives must be inferred from what they do, say, leave behind, or fail to do.\n\nPreserve the crime's causal structure within the flexible attention. A recollection may interrupt the present scene, but it should return the reader to the present with a sharper understanding of an incentive, a risk, or a piece of evidence. Let the response end where the scene's actual attention rests, without requiring a completed discovery, a confession, or a moral summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaoat::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Li Bihua / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nLet the established relationship carry the crime's pressure. Select scenes where the offense changes what the characters can say, expect, or risk with each other. The preparation, execution, or aftermath appears through their private language, their repeated moments, and the consequences that fulfill the particular promise of the supplied premise. Do not insert a roster of familiar tropes; use only the actual relationship and situation given.\n\nUse private language to show history. A phrase, a gesture, or a remembered place means something specific to these two people, and the crime alters its meaning. A repeated moment returns with a new weight: the same meeting place now dangerous, the same reassurance now doubtful, the same gift now incriminating. Give affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery enough space where the premise calls for it, but do not force every exchange into a confession or a quarrel.\n\nUse Li Bihua's sensual precision to make the attachment tangible. Describe the particular way one character touches an object the other has handled, the look of a room they shared, the cost of a beautiful thing that now lies between them. Jealousy or divided attachment should be legible in what a character notices and avoids saying, not only in dramatic declarations. Keep each character's knowledge separate: one may know the truth about the crime while the other suspects, and that gap must generate pressure in what they say or withhold.\n\nPreserve the crime's consequences. The relationship is not a refuge that dissolves the offense. A character may protect, lie to, investigate, or implicate the other. End the response where the relationship's next move is genuinely uncertain because the crime has not been resolved, not with a comforting beat.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaoau::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Li Bihua / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the crime the shape of an account that is being retold. Open with a deliberate statement of who acted and what followed, then expand into the particulars that make the reputation incomplete. Let the offense, the preparation, the pursuit, or the aftermath be related as deeds whose consequences the teller and the community still live with. Keep the present scene's scale: a theft, a betrayal, or a killing may be remembered, but it need not be inflated into cosmic doom.\n\nUse compact declarations to establish a person or an act, then complicate them with Li Bihua's intimate attention. A recurring designation should come from an established deed or way of speaking, not from an ornamental epithet. Return to a phrase when a later occurrence gives it a new application. Let the teller notice the costly beauty of an object, the particular look of a place where loyalty was tested, or the divided attachment that made someone act against their own interest. The glamorous surface sharpens the uncomfortable relationship rather than hiding it.\n\nKeep shared reputation distinct from private experience. The chronicle may record that someone was known as loyal, dangerous, or ruined, but the focal character's actual thought remains private and may contradict the public account. If the selected perspective is inside that character, render the private cost directly; if it is outside, the teller infers it from witnessed acts, speech, and known consequences. The chronicle does not gain access to minds it could not know.\n\nPreserve the crime's causal structure. The retelling may arrange events for effect, but preparation, incentive, execution, and aftermath remain inferable. Let dialogue interrupt the authority of the chronicle when a character corrects a detail that would otherwise mislead the listener or answer an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern. End the response with the account still being shaped, another modest act available to be remembered rather than declared legend.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaoav::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Li Bihua / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the crime through the social and domestic world it moves in. Take the social-domestic realist line of the Scandinavian traditions: households, workplaces, care institutions, municipal offices, the ordinary structures people depend on, as the material the offense, scheme, or aftermath passes through. Let the viewpoint character's place in those structures decide what they notice, what they can ask, and what they let pass. A discovery arrives as a specific social encounter — a neighbor's changed manner on the stairs, a colleague's careful phrasing in a meeting, a relative's refusal at the kitchen table, an official's procedural patience on the phone. Use the setting, language, period, and community the supplied world actually gives; where the story names Denmark, Norway, or Sweden, keep that context specific rather than blending it into a generic North, and add no snow, isolation, or reticence the world has not established.\n\nGive that world Li Bihua's sensual precision. The social surface is physical: the smell of a closed consulting room, the weight of a coat handed back, the light an apartment had during a life that has since changed. Let beauty carry information rather than decorate — the kept table and pressed shirt are what someone maintains while a marriage or an account is failing, and a familiar object takes on charge when the viewpoint learns what it cost. The lushness sharpens the damage; it never anesthetizes it. The point is people continuing to live among attractive, orderly things while something harmful is known or hidden.\n\nLet remembered places do active work. A return to a room, stairwell, or workplace carries an earlier attachment or wound that bears on the present matter, and the return revises what the viewpoint understands about someone involved in it. Keep the memory attributed to the viewpoint and separate from what anyone else knows.\n\nKeep the crime's relationships divided and jealous: someone helps a person they distrust, protects a person they resent, cooperates with an institution they find humiliating. Show the contradiction in action and in whatever private experience the active contract makes available, not in announcement. When the viewpoint reasons about events or motive, ground it in observed behavior, material conditions, and remembered exchanges, keeping what was seen distinct from what is inferred. The story can close on a changed understanding, an unanswered question, or a household continuing under a new unspoken condition; punishment and reconciliation remain available outcomes, not obligations.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaoaw::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Li Bihua / Screenplay\n\nWrite the crime in scene headings, action lines, and attributed dialogue. Open each new location or time with a heading, and keep action lines to what can be seen or heard: a hand stopping over a drawer, a wet coat left across a chair, a ring turned under a lamp, a door closing offscreen. Choose few such details and let them recur or change; Li Bihua's precision works here as selection, not as description imported from prose. Keep camera language out of the action lines.\n\nMake the glamorous surface a visible fact of the offense. A character adjusts a cuff before lying; a beautifully arranged room hosts a conversation about a debt; an elegant meal proceeds while an alibi is rehearsed. Surface and cost are both on screen: the damage shows in what people do with objects and space, never in narration that explains it for them.\n\nLet dialogue carry the divided and jealous attachments. People speak from their relationships — a question that is also an accusation, an offer of help that protects the helper's position, a subject changed to avoid naming what the speaker knows. What is said may differ from what is shown when the character has a reason for the difference; hesitation, interruption, and ordinary phrasing belong. Do not make everyone polished or quick.\n\nWhen the active authorship contract includes private experience, set it apart as a clearly attributed nonspoken note inside the presentation: the character's actual reasoning, memory, or feeling while the visible scene continues. It is not a voice-over unless the chosen sequence specifically uses one, and no other character knows it.\n\nUse cuts, offscreen sound, and voice-over only where the sequence needs them. A cut may move between a remembered place and the present, but anchor the memory in something first seen or heard. Let the crime's developments arrive through observable exchanges and actions, and let a sequence end on a completed action, an unfinished question, or a character alone with what they now understand; confession and arrest are not required destinations.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaoax::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Li Bihua / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable script: clear speaker names, entrances and exits, economical directions for movement and business. Let the relation between lines and physical business carry the scene — a garment folded while answering, a drink poured before a request is refused, a move toward the door while promising to stay.\n\nMake Li Bihua's sensual precision theatrical. Costume, light, and handled objects give the glamour its body: a shawl, a letter, a ring, a dish, a photograph — things offered, withheld, smoothed, or left behind rather than described in the abstract. A character's care for an object or a room stages the surface they maintain; a pause, a changed handling of something familiar, or an answer given too quickly lets the audience feel what that surface costs. The staging's beauty registers the offense's weight rather than softening it: people keep performing ordinary life while something damaging is known or hidden.\n\nUse the shared space to keep the crime's relationships legible. Who can see an entrance, reach the table, or hear a line decides what each person knows, and the blocking should let the audience follow it. Divided attachments surface as dialogue set against business: help offered to someone distrusted, protection extended to someone resented, courtesy paid to an institution the speaker finds humiliating.\n\nGive monologues and asides a dramatic purpose and an audience within the scene, and do not use them as the default destination for thought. When the active contract includes private experience, keep it as nonspoken authoring material set apart from anything the cast can hear — a character's actual reasoning, memory, or feeling while the public scene continues — rather than disguising it as an aside. Do not add film-camera instructions.\n\nLet developments arrive through staged exchanges and actions. Close on an exit, a completed action, a pause, or a character left alone on stage with what they now understand; the script owes no confession scene, arrest, or curtain-line revelation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaoay::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Li Bihua / Stream of Consciousness\n\nLet one authorized mind carry the story, and let the crime surface through that mind's associations rather than through chronological report. Perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and a recurring concern braid together: the smell of rain in a coat opens the night the coat was left behind, which opens the person who wore it, which opens a sentence spoken in another room. Move by sensory and emotional connection; the offense, scheme, or aftermath appears where the mind circles it, including in the places the mind avoids.\n\nGive the associations Li Bihua's sensual precision. The mind lingers on texture, taste, weight, and color — a sleeve, a particular tea, a door's resistance, the exact shade of a bruise — and these details carry the crime's cost together with the character's divided or jealous attachment. A remembered loveliness sharpens present damage: how beautiful the room was is now inseparable from what happened there. The lushness is not escape; it is how the mind keeps touching what it cannot resolve.\n\nKeep the reader oriented. Mark movements between present perception, remembered event, and feared or imagined scene — through tense, a repeated sensory anchor, or an explicit return to the room — so recollection is never confused with what is happening now. The mode stays inside its single mind; other characters' knowledge appears only as the focal mind infers or encounters it, and scene facts remain determinate.\n\nLet the recurring concern be the crime's human aftermath: a face, a missing object, a lie told in a particular voice, the moment a relationship changed. Each return revises or deepens the understanding rather than repeating one image; the mind may reason, doubt, accuse itself, or refuse to go on, and the refusal is itself rendered experience. End mid-association, back in the present room, or on an unfinished thought; the passage owes no resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaoaz::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Li Bihua / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the story in sender-attributed messages. Give each participant distinct digital habits — long composed paragraphs from one, fragments from another, a photograph where a sentence would say too much — and use only the interface events that matter: a delay, an edit, a read receipt, an attachment, a move to another channel. Let artifacts mean something real: hesitation as a long gap before a short answer, a change of intention as an edit, a read receipt with no reply as a fact someone now has to live with. Do not invent an interface event just to add subtext.\n\nLet Li Bihua's precision and divided attachment live inside what is sent. A character can compose a remembered place into a message — the light in a room, the smell of a meal, a coat left over a chair — and that composition is a chosen self, not the whole person. The cost shows in the gap between transmitted words and private experience: what is omitted, what is rewritten before sending, what is typed and deleted. A glamorous message can sit directly above an unsent draft that says the damaging thing plainly.\n\nKeep three layers distinct: sent messages; unsent drafts, which are composed artifacts rather than automatically the writer's live thought; and private experience. When the active contract includes private experience, write it as clearly attributed nonspoken material set apart from the transcript — what the character actually thinks and feels while composing, sending, or reading — visible to no other participant.\n\nLet the crime's relationships and incentives move through the exchange: questions that are also accusations, help that protects the helper, subjects changed to avoid naming what is known. Bring in context outside the exchange only when required, and separate it clearly from the messages. End on whatever the exchange actually produces — an unanswered message, a plain reply, a receipt, the phone set face down — with no obligation to reach confession or resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaoba::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Li Bihua / Visual Novel\n\nPresent the story scene by scene in a rhythm suited to an illustrated frame: a concise setting passage, attributed dialogue, a focal reaction or inner response, then the next exchange. Keep scene changes legible. Use a background or expression cue as a purposeful reference to the visual surface — never a label required on every line, and never executable engine code. Selecting the style does not activate branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, or images beyond what the world itself authorizes.\n\nLet Li Bihua's sensual precision work through what the frame holds and what the focal narration notices: the grain of a tablecloth, the light a remembered apartment had, the weight of a coat returned, the exact color of a bruise. Beauty carries information — a maintained surface is what someone keeps up while a relationship or an arrangement is failing, and a familiar object becomes charged once the viewpoint learns what it cost. Lushness registers the crime's weight; it does not soften it.\n\nLet the focal narration do the interpretive work. Show how the viewpoint explains another person's behavior, then let the next exchange support, complicate, or sidestep that explanation. Keep private thought outside the other characters' knowledge, and let an expression cue orient a moment without certifying what the character feels or whether they lie.\n\nAdvance the crime through scenes whose information changes what can be understood. A later scene may return to a familiar visual detail and let it mean something new because of what has happened in between; repetition deepens understanding rather than restarting the same suspicion. Preserve whichever authorship contract is active. Close a scene on a completed exchange, a changed understanding, or the viewpoint alone with what is now known — with no forced choice screen and no required confession or arrest.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaobb::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Li Bihua / Web Serial\n\nWrite the crime in installments with a readable through-line: at any point the reader can tell what is underway, what has just changed, and what remains open. Recover earlier context briefly and only when it helps re-entry — a clause, not a retold investigation. Let ordinary life continue around the offense, scheme, or aftermath; the serial's unit of progress is a real change in knowledge, relationships, work, or resources, not a completed miniature chapter.\n\nGive each installment physical presence through Li Bihua's precision: the kept room, the coat handed back, the light a remembered apartment had. Beauty carries cost — a surface someone maintains while things fail, an object charged by what the viewpoint has learned it cost. Return to a place, object, or phrase across installments and let repetition accumulate meaning: the coat seen a second time means differently because of what has happened since. The return is earned by story, not restated as summary.\n\nKeep the crime's divided attachments in motion across the duration: help given to someone distrusted, protection of someone resented, cooperation with an institution someone finds humiliating. Show the contradiction in action and in whatever private experience the active contract makes available, even in a reply where no decision changes. Real developments include a question answered, a person reached, a place revisited, a resource gained or lost, a relationship shifted.\n\nClose each installment on what is actually true at that moment — completed work, a pending request, a changed understanding, plain acknowledgment — and use a hook only when one is genuinely available rather than manufacturing a cliffhanger. Significance accrues across duration; the reader is owed continuity, not a perpetually deferred accounting.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadapaa::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Lu Xun / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the emotional cost of the offense through the small, observable ways people manage what they cannot say. Give the strain a concrete source in the crime itself — a debt the act created, a name someone gave or refused to give under questioning, a loyalty that has outlived the deed it was owed to. The difficult relationship may bind accomplices, kin, the person harmed and the person who caused the harm; it need not be romantic to be hard to leave or hard to repair.\n\nLet the strain act on the conversation and on what follows it. A withheld apology becomes an errand done without being asked; the need to return becomes standing in a doorway with a reason that will not hold. Keep the observation plain: when the viewpoint notices another's hands, tone, or the pause before an answer, the detail carries the weight, and the feeling is not translated for the reader. What the focal character admits inwardly may be sharper or more muddled than anything said aloud — render that private experience in its actual shape under the active contract rather than compressing it into a summary.\n\nLet people disagree about what the offense meant without staging a debate. One accepts blame too easily because acceptance is quieter than argument; another refuses comfort that arrives at the wrong moment or from the wrong person. Warmth and injury can share a scene — the meal served anyway, the question not repeated — and a partial reconciliation need not erase anything.\n\nDo not require a confession, a breakdown, or a completed repair before the response ends. It may stop mid-exchange, with the apology unfinished, or while a character sits alone with what they have finally admitted to themselves. The turbulence belongs to these particular people, in the narrator's own diction, not to a stock vocabulary of suffering.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadapab::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Lu Xun / Chat Roleplay\n\nRun the scene as a coherent shared interval in readable paragraphs. Attribute every spoken line unmistakably and keep action markers clear, so the reader always knows who is present, who is talking, and what each of them is doing while the offense, the scheme, or its aftermath is on the table. Take narrative person and the boundaries of player control from the active contract.\n\nLet the social meaning surface through what the participants treat as ordinary. Who is allowed to ask questions, who lowers a name to a murmur, who goes on sweeping or weighing or counting while the matter is discussed — these locate each speaker's position better than any explanation. A character's stake can show in what is not asked, in the errand insisted on first, or in the careful tone kept for the person everyone else avoids. Do not make every silence a confession; let the reader infer from concrete behavior what the scene does not announce.\n\nKeep the world and its people active on their own terms. An NPC continues a practical life around the exchange, answers partially because the whole answer costs something, or turns the talk elsewhere because the subject is unwelcome. Information may be offered, priced, withheld, or contradicted inside the exchange itself, and what changes hands should change what the player can sensibly attempt next.\n\nEnd where the scene's own motion ends. The next turn may open on a reply, a departure, an arrival, or a task half-finished; the close does not need a question, a reveal, or a forced decision to justify itself. Stop inside the ongoing exchange, or in a beat of private attention, and leave the continuation visible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadapac::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Lu Xun / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite this story on the social and satirical realism route. The offense becomes visible through the ordinary acts and unquestioned beliefs of one particular community: who is expected to report it, who is permitted to ignore it, what small humiliation follows a complaint, and what everyone treats as the way things are done. The crime need not be a puzzle to solve; it can be the thing that shows how the place already worked before it happened.\n\nKeep the prose clean and the observation particular. Put the damage into concrete exchanges instead of commentary: the official who asks whether the complainant had been drinking, the neighbor who offers sympathy while steering the conversation away from names, the shopkeeper who apologizes for the inconvenience of having been robbed. The satire lives in the gap between what people say and what they then do, and it cuts deeper when no one in the scene notices it.\n\nHold to the language, period, and community the active story supplies. Do not relocate the cast or dress it in a borrowed setting; the hierarchies, obligations, and habits that make the offense ordinary must be the ones already present in this world. One act of compliance or refusal — a stamp applied, a door shut a moment too soon — exposes the institution more completely than a speech about injustice.\n\nLet the aftermath stay particular to the world's own rules. Punishment may not arrive, or may arrive in a form that changes nothing for the people who were harmed. The weight of the story falls on how the offense and its consequences are absorbed, repeated, or quietly resisted by those who must keep living beside them, not on a verdict imported from outside.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadapad::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Lu Xun / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the offense and its aftermath in hard-edged, concrete prose. Move through preparation, execution, pursuit, or consequence with forceful diction and compressed transitions, letting material wear carry the cost: a door forced and badly rehung, a coat worn out of season to cover what is underneath, a floor scrubbed until the boards pale, a man who moves carefully now and does not say why.\n\nKeep the observation plain and put the social meaning inside physical detail rather than general statement. A witness lowering her voice at a name, the object someone hides without being asked, the errand that has become impossible since the offense — each does more than a paragraph about what the neighborhood has become. Keep rough or abrasive speech attached to its speaker, so a threat, an excuse, or a boast sounds like this particular mouth and motive. Unsentimental irony comes from setting what a person says beside what the material facts already record.\n\nChange rhythm to manage pressure. After a compressed exchange, allow a longer passage when the character's reasoning genuinely needs the room — weighing a risk, working out who can be told what, noticing the detail that alters the next move — but keep that reasoning concrete: it is thought about things, not free-floating dread. Then cut again. The alternation is the engine of the style, and one short flat sentence after a long one can do the work of a paragraph.\n\nDo not convert the story into survival horror or an inventory of wounds. Fear, injury, and anger belong to this crime and these people, and the arrangement the offense disturbed stays legible through objects, money, and work. End where the response boundary falls — mid-action or mid-thought is fine — rather than padding toward a resolution the scene has not earned.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadapae::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Lu Xun / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the offense and its aftermath through documents, each with a clear author, an intended recipient or purpose, and a time of composition. A report written for a superior omits the detail that would embarrass him; a letter of condolence reaches the bereaved without once naming what happened; a ledger enters the missing sum under a heading that makes it ordinary. What each document knows, avoids, and accepts as normal is the story.\n\nLet the damage emerge from what the papers do and do not say. A clerk records a humiliation as procedure, or sets down an act the reader recognizes as part of the crime's machinery, with no sense that anything is wrong. Do not append an omniscient explanation after each entry; the gaps, the revisions, and the quiet contradictions between accounts are where the meaning lives. A later document may revise an earlier one — a figure changed, a name now spelled correctly — and no one needs to remark on it.\n\nUse the document form each moment actually requires — petition, report, private letter, receipt, note left at a door — rather than cycling formats for variety. Keep the sequence readable by letting entries follow the course of events, and let writers be wrong or uninformed: a composer records what was known then, not what the reader can see now.\n\nTreat every document as a composed artifact, not a window into its author's live mind; what someone writes is what they chose to write under that day's fear, fatigue, or habit. Where the active contract gives access to a character's private experience, keep that experience in its own place, distinct from the polished surface of the page, so the reader can measure the distance between them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadapaf::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Lu Xun / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite the comfort inside the crime's aftermath, not as an escape from it. The offense and its consequences remain real — a matter not yet settled, a debt now owed to someone worse, a name the neighbors have stopped using — and the scene can spend its attention on what the people caught in it still give each other: chosen kindness, familiar preferences, practical care, small pleasures that survive.\n\nMake the warmth particular through plain observation rather than declaration. Someone brings the food that is wanted rather than the food that is correct, mends the coat that matters, remembers how the tea is taken, sits through an afternoon without demanding an explanation. The cup set down within reach and the door left unbarred say more than any speech about being there. Affection is sincere and does not have to purchase anything: no confession, no repair, no agreement about what happened.\n\nKeep personality, privacy, and friction intact. The person offering comfort may understand the offense only partly, and the person receiving it may not yet be able to accept it; a small kindness can sit beside unresolved hurt, and a disagreement can survive a warm room. Comfort does not make anyone docile, and it does not lift the established problems from the world — outside this interval the matter still has its teeth, and the characters know it.\n\nLet the scene be worth inhabiting on its own terms. Its meaning is in these particular people and what they can offer each other now — an hour that is genuinely easier, a laugh that surprises the person who laughs — not in a promise that everything has been put right.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadapag::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Lu Xun / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite the offense and its aftermath as composed novel prose: deliberate transitions, sustained paragraph movement, and enough narrative distance to set the act inside its social context, its history, or the judgment the chosen narrator is entitled to make. A passage may open on the ordinary conditions that made the crime practicable — who was owed, who was tired, who had stopped being watched — move through the act or its consequence, and close on what the survivors take up again as normal afterward.\n\nKeep the language clean and the observation particular. The damage shows in an ordinary act rather than an explanation: the official who assumes any complaint is exaggerated, the neighbor who can remember the injured family only by its debts, the bystander who describes the offense the way he describes the weather. Let the social meaning arrive through the framing — what the narration places beside what — rather than through a lecture.\n\nUse the formal architecture to organize the passage rather than decorate it. Sustained paragraphs can carry a street, a household, or a workplace through time; a public exchange can be set against the private interval that follows it; a ceremony, an appointment, or a routine errand can be interrupted by the consequence and then resume its shape. The transitions do the connecting work, moving from context to particular people and back, so the human aftermath stays legible inside the larger arrangement.\n\nDistinguish the narration from ordinary speech without costuming it. The narrator may compare, judge, or place events in a longer history, but the judgment grows from this world and this viewpoint; no aristocracy, archaism, or uniform eloquence comes with the style. Bring the passage to a stop where its own movement ends rather than forcing a formal closure the events have not earned.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadapah::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Lu Xun / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen with the telling rather than the crime. Someone speaks to particular listeners on a particular occasion — a settlement meeting, the bench outside an office, the end of a shift, a household deciding what to do about a name — and the listeners have a practical stake in the account: someone must decide whether to pay, shelter, report, forgive, disbelieve, or show the teller the door. The teller may be culprit, accomplice, victim, or bystander, and is speaking now for a reason: to delay the decision, secure protection, shift blame, warn, or simply be believed. Let that purpose shape the account from its first sentence.\n\nLet the embedded tale do the exposing work. The teller reveals a position through what is treated as normal inside it: the agent recounts a theft and never once says why the tenant was hungry; the clerk calls the bribe 'the usual'; the widow describes the broken seal on her room as though it had been done courteously. Do not explain the institution's damage. Let the listeners' questions snag on what the account leaves out, and let the teller answer around the gap. When the tale quotes a third speaker, mark the shift clearly so the levels stay legible.\n\nMake recurrence earn its weight. A receipt, an empty chair, a phrase like 'he always paid before' can pass from the tale into the outer scene and carry more meaning the second time. Keep what the teller knew then separate from what the teller knows now: a vivid recollection can still be incomplete, and an omission can be sincere ignorance rather than cunning. Where the narration gives access to private experience, render the pressure under the performance — the arithmetic behind a softened detail, the fear beneath a polite formula — instead of leaving the account pure surface.\n\nTreat the telling as an intervention whose success stays contingent. When the outer scene resumes, it may register the tale — a question rephrased, a hand kept on the money, silence where agreement was expected — or the listeners may hold to what they accept as normal and nothing move. End where the response's own boundary falls: mid-tale at a detail that has just become dangerous, mid-question, mid-decision. The tale need not conclude, the crime need not resolve, and the telling need not succeed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadapai::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Lu Xun / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the social and developmental realism route of the German-language traditions. The offense is a transaction inside a functioning institution — a workshop, a housing office, a family firm, a school, a municipal bureau — and the narration follows the institution through the people who keep it running. Trace a position rather than a psychology of evil: an apprentice learning which irregularities are simply the job, a clerk promoted into knowing what the fees are for, an heir growing into the firm's charity and its second ledger at the same time. The crime — a falsified inspection, a pocketed fee, a denounced tenant, a wage held back — grows out of incentives the institution has taught everyone to call normal.\n\nKeep the sentences clean and declarative, with distance calibrated to who is seeing. An insider narrates procedures flatly, the way one narrates weather; the same details, met by a newcomer, a client, or an inspector, register as strange. That double availability — one form meaning routine to one person and injury to another — is where the story's meaning lives. Do not lecture the injustice; a withheld receipt, a signature given without reading, a customary payment everyone calls by its nickname does the saying.\n\nGive preparation, execution, and aftermath as concrete actions whose consequences arrive unevenly: someone loses a place, a sum, a standing; someone else is quietly promoted past the spot. Where the narration carries inward experience, keep it particular and unsentimental — the relief of a divided loyalty settled by habit, the small fear that a stamp will be asked for next — without requiring anyone to name what they have done. The reader may understand the damage before the participants do.\n\nUse the setting, language, and period the story actually supplies. Do not relocate the scene to a German-speaking country, present bureaucratic manner as national character, or imitate translated word order. End at the response's natural boundary — mid-transaction, mid-realization, mid-confrontation — without requiring the offense to be discovered, punished, or completed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadapaj::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Lu Xun / Gothic-Punk\n\nSet the offense where inherited authority still reaches into present urban life: a family that owns what the neighborhood needs, a legacy property with its own rules of entry, a debt or guardianship carried across generations, an old arrangement that newer money and newer law have not dissolved. Let the collision be felt materially — wiring behind a plaster medallion, a locked room the caretaker keeps a key for, the uniform at the door, the ritual of who is let in through which entrance — and tie every atmospheric detail to an actual function and history. A beautiful entrance that opens only for certain names is mood and mechanism at once; use it as both.\n\nLet the crime expose the arrangement through an ordinary act or a small humiliation. A junior heir falsifies an inspection; a tenant breaks a seal that was never lawful; someone sells what was never theirs to a buyer who knew. Characters accept the arrangement as normal until a concrete consequence makes one corner of it visible. Keep the prose clean and unsentimental even when the rooms are close and the light is bad; the irony works by never raising its voice above what people call order.\n\nMake trusted company the place where masks come off. Behind a closed door, in a parked car, in bed, someone admits desire, fear, or divided loyalty that is performed away from that room — and may protect and exploit the same person in a single act of care with conditions attached. Render intimacy concretely: who pours, who checks the lock, which endearment is also an instruction. Where the narration gives inward access, show the actual want or dread beneath the performed role rather than announcing betrayal.\n\nImport nothing from vampire setting material — no clan physiology, Humanity meters, faction names, disciplines, or blood bonds; the story's own world supplies whatever power the old authority actually holds. End at the response's boundary — mid-negotiation, mid-confession, mid-passage down a corridor that has not yet delivered anyone anywhere. The crime need not be solved, the authority need not fall, and the encounter need not resolve into either rescue or ruin.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadapak::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Lu Xun / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the social and domestic realism route of the Indian literary traditions. The offense runs through the obligations of a particular household, workplace, or local community, and the story's first job is to make that world's practical life exact. Let the crime be an ordinary transaction — a loan against a pledge, a dowry demanded or evaded, a property transfer, a job arranged and charged for, a favor called in — and follow its preparation, execution, and aftermath through what people actually do: signatures, refusals, meals served or withheld, names added to or left off lists.\n\nReveal positions through what speakers treat as normal. A parent calls the payment a duty; a clerk calls the bribe the going rate; a creditor describes an interest rate as kindness to someone no bank would touch. Do not announce the social meaning. Let a small humiliation carry it — the chair offered and then withdrawn, the tea not poured, the form that requires a father's name — and let the institution, whether family firm, lender's circuit, cooperative, or school administration, become visible through its own routine rather than through explanation.\n\nKeep language, region, community, and period exactly as the story supplies them. Where the setting is multilingual, let usage follow the actual speakers rather than stapling on English or code-switching as regional decoration; do not attach religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure the scene does not already have. Where the narration gives inward access, render the particular experience beneath the transaction — resentment worn as patience, relief that arrives with shame attached — without demanding a confession or a reckoning.\n\nMake the incentive legible without excusing it: someone falsifies an account, withholds a share, denounces a neighbor, and the story shows what the act buys and what it costs, distributed unevenly. End where the response's own boundary falls — mid-argument over the ledger, mid-wait outside an office, mid-realization — without requiring discovery, punishment, completion, or reconciliation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadapal::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Lu Xun / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the social and psychological realism route of the Japanese literary traditions. The offense moves through ordinary relationships — a workplace, a neighborhood association, a family, a small firm — and the narration gives as much weight to the private reasoning that accompanies the acts as to the acts themselves. Suspense can come from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows from the start who did it; the live question is what approaching discovery does to someone deciding whether to keep lying, keep quiet, or hand another person the blame.\n\nExpose the institution through what people accept as normal. A clerk treats the falsified inspection as routine maintenance; a neighbor describes a denunciation to the ward office as looking after things; a wife books her husband's theft under household economy. Do not explain the social damage. Let it sit in a small humiliation or an unquestioned belief — a form filled in a certain hand, a queue someone is quietly moved out of, the customary greeting a superior no longer returns.\n\nCalibrate the narration's distance to its actual teller, and let feeling be as direct or as oblique as that person is. One character's fear arrives as a flat sentence of procedure; another's resentment runs long interior accounting; both are faithful if they are that person's way of minding. Treat the effort to keep a face as real work with real cost, and use omission deliberately: what a narrator declines to look at can be as legible as what the narrator describes.\n\nDo not impose a universal quietness or an obligatory inventory of scenery; seasonal attention may matter only where the supplied setting makes it mean something. Use the place, period, and social world the story actually has. End at the response's boundary — mid-thought, mid-errand, mid-conversation whose sentence is not finished — without requiring the offense to be discovered, punished, or forgiven.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadapam::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Lu Xun / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the social realism and chronicle route of the Latin American traditions. The offense belongs to the practical life of a specific community — a market block, a cooperative, a parish committee, a neighborhood, a workplace — and the telling keeps a chronicler's grip on names, sums, dates, and what afterward happened to whom. Record the offense plainly: who paid, who signed, who was moved off the list, which house changed hands, who left town and when. The chronicle's authority comes from what it states; resist explaining what the record already shows.\n\nReveal positions through what speakers treat as normal. A vendor calls the protection payment rent; the official calls the land transfer regularization; the committee chair calls the missing fund an accounting practice. Let a small humiliation or an unquestioned belief expose the institution — a signature obtained at a funeral, a queue rearranged, a name omitted from the roster of those compensated. Keep the community's language, composition, and history exactly as supplied, and add no supernatural fact the world does not already permit; where the marvelous genuinely exists in this world, treat it as the locals do, as ordinary weather.\n\nKeep the chronicle's later knowledge separate from what participants knew at the time. The telling can state that no one yet knew the second ledger existed; it should not retroactively install that knowledge in the people it describes. Where a participant's testimony enters the record, mark the difference between what that person did and what that person only heard about. Where the narration carries inward experience, give the actual texture of the wait — the days between a signature and its consequence, what silence about a sum does to a table of people sharing a meal.\n\nMake the incentive legible without excusing it, and let consequences land unevenly and slowly, as they do. End at the response's own boundary — mid-entry, mid-confrontation, mid-wait for a ruling that has not come — without requiring discovery, punishment, or the chronicle's completion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadapan::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Lu Xun / Light Novel\n\nOpen in the middle of the situation and orient fast: who is present, where they stand or sit, and what each one practically wants out of the offense or its aftermath. Keep the staging visual and trackable — the counter, the back room, the doorway, the street outside the office — so every handoff, move, and exit is easy to picture and the reader never has to rebuild the room. Break paragraphs for readability around action, dialogue, and private response, and give any unfamiliar name, role, or house rule one clear line of presentation the first time it matters.\n\nLet the crime be an ordinary transaction that exposes an institution: a falsified inspection, a fee pocketed under its nickname, a seal broken, a neighbor denounced to a committee. Show the act and its consequence in concrete visible detail — the second ledger slid out from under the first, the signature page turned past too quickly — rather than in a stated moral. Characters reveal their positions through what they treat as normal, and the clerk who says everyone does it should say it the way everyone does.\n\nGive focused reaction beats to whoever the moment belongs to. A reaction can be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to its owner: the tenant's stillness, the clerk's brisk unbothered efficiency, the heir's laugh that stops early. Keep the cast from turning uniformly witty or combative, and where the narration allows private response, write it as real thought — the quick arithmetic of what admitting this would cost — not only as visible behavior. Suspense can live in exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did it.\n\nFirst person, comedy, a young cast, and a closing cliffhanger are not requirements of this style; use any of them only if the story already has. End where the response's boundary falls — mid-exchange at the counter, mid-pursuit, mid-thought after the door closes — without requiring the offense to be solved, punished, or revealed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadapao::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Lu Xun / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet the offense enter through the rhythm of ordinary perception. Begin inside an attention the viewpoint is already paying — a sound repeated until it becomes a count, light lying across a room at the hour when something usually happens, an object handled by someone who is not speaking — and let the paragraph's movement carry the pressure of what has been done, ordered, or agreed to. Build each paragraph as a movement from concrete observation toward recognition: the exact way a door has been left open, the silence after a particular question, the small humiliation performed again without protest. Keep every image traceable to the scene or to the teller's own associations, and let a comparison unfold only when it changes what the narrator understands or makes present what everyone present has agreed not to see.\n\nUse the lyric movement to expose what this world treats as normal. The damage of an office, a family, or an arrangement can appear in how a room is arranged, in the tone used to mention a debt, in who is sent out and who is allowed to stay. Repeat an image or a phrase when its second appearance shows the harm has become furniture; recurrence is how you render acceptance without a lecture. Break the figurative movement with plain, short statements when a fact must land at full weight — what was taken, who signed, what was found where. The people around the viewpoint need not speak figuratively; their ordinary speech set against the narrator's shaped attention is itself the exposure.\n\nKeep the account inside what your carrier — culprit, accomplice, victim, investigator, or bystander — can actually see, remember, and feel, including the private thought that accompanies what is said. Suspense may come from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader already knows what happened. The register does not demand permanent solemnity; a joke told at the wrong moment can cut more than lament. Stop where the scene's attention actually rests, even mid-exchange or mid-observation, leaving what has been seen visible rather than converting it into a moral.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadapap::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Lu Xun / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nTreat the offense as a small irreversible change whose full meaning arrives late. Open near the present, then let memory return to the ordinary occasion that came before it — who was sitting where, what was said in passing, the detail that seemed too unimportant to record and is now the only thing remembered. Let a detail carry the association rather than an announcement of loss: the object still in its place, the phrase still in use, the hour that now divides before from after. Keep each memory inside what the viewpoint could actually have noticed, and let the narrator's private experience of it — the wish to have said the other thing, the shame of relief — exist on the page as itself, not only as behavior.\n\nLet reflection do the exposing work. Moving between the present and what is no longer available, the narrator may register that an unquestioned belief made the offense possible, that the humiliation was accepted as normal by the people it worked on, that a room, a routine, or a relationship now means something it did not mean before. Record the acceptance plainly and let the changed meaning emerge from juxtaposition rather than explanation. Not every recollection is a confession; ordinary pleasure can stay pleasurable inside the same paragraph as loss, and a remembered kindness is not required to curdle.\n\nThe account may be carried by a victim, accomplice, investigator, or bystander, and suspense may still run through divided loyalty or delayed exposure even when the reader knows what happened. Keep present experience happening — a task, a conversation, weather — so reflection is anchored to a live moment rather than delivered as a memorial. A reply may end with the present moment and the remembered one standing side by side, their difference clear, without a moral being drawn between them and without anyone announcing that things pass.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadapaq::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Lu Xun / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nBuild the account as a situated act of telling in the framed-oral mode. Before the offense appears, establish the frame in concrete terms: one person telling the story of the crime to a particular listener — a visitor kept past the hour of leaving, a family after a meal, a neighbor at the door, an official who has asked one question too many. Give the frame its own live business — the tea going cold, a child interrupting, the teller pausing to get a name right — and let the listener's questions, corrections, and silences shape what gets told next.\n\nLet the inner tale carry the plain observation. The offense — a debt collected twice, a false accusation repeated until it functioned, a theft absorbed into someone's duty, a punishment administered by an office in its ordinary course — should appear through the exact words people used to justify it and the small humiliations performed as routine. The teller may be a participant, a witness, or someone repeating what the street now says, but keep the tale inside what that teller plausibly knows; where it passes into hearsay, let the listener hear that it has changed hands. The teller's omissions and returns mark where the offense still hurts and where it has been made ordinary: what is repeated with satisfaction, what is hurried past, what the listener corrects and the teller concedes. Where the active contract gives access to a frame participant's private experience, let it live in the telling's present — the thought held back while a sentence is finished — rather than dissolving all inwardness into speech.\n\nDraw the language, forms of address, and community from the world actually supplied, and keep the one tradition coherent rather than blending conventions from several; this method adds no antiquity, mysticism, or honor code the world has not established, and it does not turn the telling into a puzzle box. The social meaning emerges from what teller and listener accept as normal between them. A reply may stop inside the frame — the tale unfinished, a question unanswered, the frame's own business interrupting — with the offense passed into speech but not settled.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadapar::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Lu Xun / Minimalist Prose\n\nLet a few exact details carry the crime. For each scene, decide which particulars will do the work — the cup set down too carefully, the drawer that no longer closes flush, the word the speaker repeats whenever the account reaches a certain name — and keep the rest out. Use direct clauses in the order things were done or noticed. Omission should leave something inferable: the reader can reconstruct why an act matters from what remains present, without the causality being deleted along with the explanation.\n\nLet the spare surface do the exposing. A speaker calls the offense a fee, a rule, an inconvenience, someone else's fault; record the words plainly and let the acceptance show itself without commentary. What the narrator counts and does not count is itself information — noting the exact sum but not the person it was taken from is the social fact. Keep only the inward connection that materially contributes to the present account: one line of private thought where the narrator's own position surfaces is worth more than a performed interior monologue. Let an essential thought run long enough to make sense; a precise extended sentence may carry more than several clipped fragments.\n\nThe viewpoint may belong to a culprit, accomplice, victim, investigator, or bystander, and suspense may arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows what happened. Voices remain individual — the clipped narration does not flatten the cast into one flatness. Stop when the selected details have made the damage visible; the next consequence can stay available without being announced, and business may still be unfinished at the boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadapas::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Lu Xun / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the crime as sustained, scene-first prose. Enter each scene through something concrete already underway — a task half-finished, a conversation in progress, one person watching another perform an ordinary action — and let the offense or its consequence surface from the scene's own movement instead of arriving by announcement. Move naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and the viewpoint's authorized inner experience, and use paragraphing and small transitions to keep the reader oriented without recap machinery. The register follows the narrator's voice and the immediate scene: one passage can hold a payment, a joke, a memory of last winter, and the sentence the viewpoint does not say aloud.\n\nKeep the unsentimental observation working inside that flow. Characters reveal their positions through what they accept as normal — the rule repeated without question, the humiliation treated as scheduling, the arrangement nobody challenges because challenging it has never occurred to anyone. Put the acceptances into behavior and speech and let them accumulate across scenes; the narrator may register one plainly without pausing to explain its social meaning. Inner experience stays particular and authorized: what this person wants, fears, and refuses to look at, including the thought that runs alongside a lie being told — not a running commentary on the crime's significance.\n\nThe account may be carried by a culprit, accomplice, victim, investigator, or bystander, and suspense may arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows what happened. Contemporary presentation requires neither a contemporary setting, understated emotion, nor a closing lesson. Stop at the scene's own boundary — mid-conversation, mid-task, on a held observation — with the next consequence left open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadapat::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Lu Xun / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nLet the established relationship choose the scenes. Whoever these people already are to each other, make the premise legible through selection rather than exposition: the private phrase deployed before a difficult conversation, the gesture that meant safety before the offense and lands differently after it, the place they return to when trust is strained. Work only from the actual supplied premise — its history, its promises, its particular fault lines — without stocking the story with familiar tropes the premise never asked for. Give affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery real room in the scenes where it belongs; here the crime plot and the relationship plot are one plot, because exposure, loyalty, and consequence run through both at once.\n\nGround the emotional weight in plain observation. Each partner reveals a position through what they accept as normal, including from each other: the excuse repeated until it is furniture, the small cruelty answered with another cup of tea, the betrayal discussed as logistics. Record these acceptances without melodrama and let the hurt be carried by the exact words and objects the pair already share. A callback earns its force when the repeated phrase or place has changed meaning, not when it is quoted for its own sake. Let spoken and unspoken diverge for a character-specific reason — protecting the other, protecting the account, habit — and keep authorized private experience available: what the character will not yet say is part of the story, not a gap in it.\n\nSuspense may arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows what happened, and the relationship question may stay unsettled after any factual one is answered. A reply can close on a fulfilled emotional beat — comfort landing, a lie registered and not challenged — without forcing confession, reconciliation, or a next hook, and can equally stop in the middle of the difficult conversation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadapau::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Lu Xun / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the offense as a deed and as what will be remembered of it. Open deliberately, with a sentence that starts the account as an account — the telling already older than the moment it returns to. Proceed in compact declarations: who did what, what was owed, who stood where, who saw. Relate preparation, execution, and aftermath as remembered action, binding each act to the name or phrase it left behind — the crossing the offense gave a story to, the object still called by the wrong owner's name, the promise or excuse the community can now recite without being asked.\n\nLet recurrence do the exposing. A phrase returns when a later occurrence gives it a new application: the excuse given at the time, then given again by someone who was not there; the debt first described as a favor, later as a custom. The return marks how the harm has been absorbed into what people accept as normal, without the teller explaining it — return a phrase when it earns new meaning, not at every difficulty. Speakers may justify the deed as necessity, custom, or another's fault; record the justification plainly and let its acceptance stand as the social fact. Keep the deed particular — a theft, a false accusation, a betrayal, an institutional punishment — with its human cost attached to a person, not to a category.\n\nKeep the teller's access consistent with the chosen viewpoint, whether culprit, accomplice, victim, investigator, or bystander. Shared reputation stays distinct from private experience: being remembered as the one who did it, or the one who never spoke, does not say what the act cost the person it is remembered of, and where the contract permits, that inward cost may surface between declarations. Dialogue may interrupt the chronicle's authority — the subject of the oft-told deed objecting to a detail, answering an elevated line with a practical one — without the telling turning to parody or ceremony. A reply may stop with the deed related and its consequence still open in the telling, another act available to be told, the account never obliged to declare itself legend.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadapav::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Lu Xun / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the crime through the ordinary workings of a specific institution already present in the supplied world: a workplace, housing office, welfare service, municipal department, school, care facility, or another civic structure. Select one definite Scandinavian social-realist route rather than treating the region as scenic weather. Keep the narration close to an individual viewpoint tied to the offense: culprit, accomplice, victim, investigator, or bystander. Show how the offense becomes possible inside accepted procedure.\n\nName the rooms, documents, schedules, and small physical actions that carry the harm. A form is filed without checking, a complaint is routed to the wrong desk, a fee is waived for the wrong person, a safety report is read aloud and then archived, a key is handed to the wrong person, or a family member signs a paper without understanding it. The people involved treat these steps as normal, and their acceptance is part of the damage. Do not explain the institution's cruelty in an editorial passage.\n\nLet the focal character and the other speakers reveal their positions through what they accept as normal. Someone waits too long, signs a paper they do not understand, is told to come back tomorrow, or discovers that the person who should help has already decided the outcome. A speaker can call a fee small, a rule necessary, a delay temporary, or an exception a kindness while the narrative shows the actual consequence. The irony belongs to the gap between the accepted phrase and the damage it permits, not to a narrator who diagnoses the system.\n\nMake the environment social rather than scenic. Weather, landscape, and isolation appear only when they belong to the actual place and season. The meaningful setting is a break room, a bus route, a queue, a shared kitchen, a public meeting, a household budget, an intake desk, or another space where people must wait, ask, or account for themselves. When the supplied world includes a Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish context, keep its civic particulars distinct: forms, offices, titles, transport, payment practices, and institutional names belong to that context rather than being relabeled Scandinavian scenery.\n\nUse memory and repetition as the principal connective tissue. The focal character may return to a phrase from a supervisor, a number that does not match, a waiting room chair, a form that was already submitted, or the sound of a door closing on a request. Recurrence should deepen what the reader knows about the offense or its aftermath, not restart the same suspicion. When a document or claim is checked, preserve what was known at each moment rather than letting later knowledge replace earlier observation.\n\nFollow the offense from preparation or incentive through execution, evidence, pursuit, and aftermath from the chosen viewpoint, leaving the opening and closing moments to the actual scene. Suspense can come from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did what. Crime is not automatically a mystery puzzle or a guarantee of punishment. The story may end on an unanswered request, a completed piece of work, a small change in what someone knows, a filing archived without outcome, or the plain continuation of the institution after the offense.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadapaw::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Lu Xun / Screenplay\n\nWrite the crime in concise action lines and attributed dialogue. Use scene headings when location or time changes, and keep each action block limited to what is visible or audible. Show the offense through ordinary institutional routine: a clerk stamps a form, a supervisor initials a report without reading it, a worker disconnects a safety device because the shift is behind, a family member signs over a document at a kitchen table, a key is handed to the wrong person, or a fee is waived for the wrong applicant.\n\nLet the action lines carry the small physical facts; let the dialogue carry what people accept as normal. Speakers reveal their positions through what they do not question. A character can explain a delay, repeat a rule, call a fee small, or offer reassurance while the scene shows the actual consequence. Do not write dialogue that announces the theme or diagnoses the institution. The irony belongs to the gap between the spoken line and the visible action. A manager can say the matter is being handled while the action shows the file being set aside. A neighbor can say everyone helps each other while the scene shows one person excluded from the shared task.\n\nUse the screenplay tools as carriers of the Lu Xun exposure method, not as decoration. A cut can move from a promise to its administrative result. Offscreen sound can carry a continuing routine: a phone ringing in another office, a machine running after a worker leaves, a queue number announced while a door closes. An offscreen voice can deliver a line that a visible character accepts without reply. A scene heading can itself show the institutional route: from a kitchen table to a housing office corridor, from a care-facility room to a municipal archive, from a supervisor's office to a waiting area where the same rule is repeated. Do not use voice-over as the default destination for private thought.\n\nKeep required nonspoken interiority clearly separated from spoken and visible material. It remains a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation, not dialogue, not voice-over, and unavailable to the other characters unless the scene gives them a reason to know it. The note can record what the character notices before speaking: the weight of a pen, a paused machine, the order of a queue, a sum that does not match, or an instruction accepted because it is easier than asking. It should not become a summary of the scene's argument.\n\nFollow the offense from preparation or incentive through execution, evidence, pursuit, and aftermath from the chosen viewpoint: culprit, accomplice, victim, investigator, or bystander. Suspense can come from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did what. Do not force a procedural victory or a punishment. The scene can end on a completed action, an unanswered question, a pending request, an offscreen routine continuing, or the continuation of the procedure that made the offense possible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadapax::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Lu Xun / Stage Play\n\nWrite the crime as a playable script with clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be staged. Set the offense inside a specific institutional space already present in the supplied world: an office, a waiting room, a staff kitchen, a care facility corridor, a housing office, a municipal meeting room, or a comparable place where people must wait, ask, or account for themselves.\n\nLet the stage directions establish the routine that makes the harm possible. A character moves a file from one tray to another, counts cash into an envelope, initials a form without opening it, locks a door, hands a key to the wrong person, or asks someone to wait outside while the meeting continues. Use spatial ordering as evidence: who sits, who stands, who enters unannounced, who remains behind a counter, who is told to return tomorrow.\n\nLet the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene. A speaker can say the matter is being handled while the stage direction shows the file being placed in a drawer. A character can say everyone is treated the same while another character is told to wait outside. A manager can call a delay temporary while the visible action shows a work order placed at the bottom of a stack. Do not write dialogue that announces the theme or diagnoses the institution. The irony belongs to what the audience sees the characters accept as normal.\n\nUse monologues and asides only when they have a dramatic purpose in the scene. They are performed speech with an audience, not the default destination for private thought. A monologue may be used to repeat a rule the speaker accepts, but it must remain audible speech to the theatre audience and should not be treated as the character's unspoken mind. Required nonspoken interiority remains nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation, clearly separated from the performed script and unavailable to the other characters unless the scene gives them a reason to know it. That material can record what a character notices before speaking: the weight of a stamp, the order of a queue, a signature completed without reading, a door that will not close, or an instruction accepted because challenging it would cost more than obeying it. Keep it out of the dialogue.\n\nDo not add film-camera instructions. Keep the stage directions within what a theatre can show: entrances, exits, furniture, props, light changes that signal time, and actions that actors can perform. Follow the offense from preparation or incentive through execution, evidence, pursuit, and aftermath from the chosen viewpoint: culprit, accomplice, victim, investigator, or bystander. Suspense can come from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did what. Do not force a procedural victory or a punishment. A scene can end on an entrance, an exit, a completed piece of business, an unanswered question, a queue that does not move, or the continuation of the routine that made the offense possible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadapay::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Lu Xun / Stream of Consciousness\n\nBraid the crime through one authorized mind: culprit, accomplice, victim, investigator, or bystander. Let perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern move the telling rather than a linear report. The mind returns to the ordinary details of an institution: a form that must be signed, a queue that does not move, a drawer that will not open, a name called in the wrong order, a sum that does not match, a signature completed without reading.\n\nThese returns are not random; they carry the offense and its aftermath through the character's actual attention. A memory of being told to wait can interrupt a present conversation. A phrase from a supervisor can recur when the character sees a different official document. The smell of a waiting room or the weight of a bag can pull the mind back to an earlier transaction. When a return involves evidence, preserve what was known at each moment; do not let later knowledge replace the earlier perception.\n\nUse the Lu Xun exposure method inside the associative movement. The character accepts a rule, a delay, a small humiliation, or an unquestioned belief as normal, and the narration lets that acceptance surface without an explanatory lecture. A speaker's practical phrase can enter the mind and return in an altered context. A paper signed without reading can appear in memory when another form arrives. The institution becomes visible through what the mind keeps returning to, not through a speech about the institution.\n\nUse syntax and paragraph shape to follow the associations, but keep enough orientation to distinguish events from recollection or fantasy. A present scene may be interrupted by a remembered room, a repeated instruction, or an imagined outcome, and the reader should know which is which. Use concrete sensory anchors for the present: cold hands on a railing, the weight of a bag, the sound of a door closing, the taste of coffee from a machine. Ordinary reasoning alone is not this stylistic mode; the movement must be associative, not merely a tidy internal summary.\n\nFollow the offense from preparation or incentive through execution, evidence, pursuit, and aftermath from the chosen viewpoint. Suspense can come from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did what. Do not force a procedural victory or a punishment. The passage can end on an interruption, a recurring phrase, a bodily sensation, a memory, an unanswered question, or the mind's return to the routine that made the offense possible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadapaz::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Lu Xun / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the crime through readable sender-attributed messages. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one writes in full sentences, another sends fragments, one forwards without comment, one leaves a message unsent. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the offense or its aftermath. Do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext.\n\nLet the Lu Xun exposure method work through what people write and accept as normal. A supervisor sends a reminder that the form was already submitted. A neighbor asks for a small sum and calls it help. A worker forwards a safety warning with the note that everyone already knows. A family member asks why the recipient is making trouble. A housing officer sends a one-line correction and closes the chat. The transmitted text reveals the institution through its ordinary phrases and routines, not through an explanatory message about the system.\n\nUse the interface itself as meaningful evidence. A delay can show hesitation or workload. An edit can change a request into an instruction, a question into a confirmation, or a sum into a smaller number. A read receipt can mark the moment someone knows and does not answer. An attachment can be forwarded without comment, and the absence of an attachment can matter. Each such event should affect the recipient or the reader's understanding; do not add one just to imply tension.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought. A message left in the composer can show what the sender considered writing, but it must not be treated as access to the sender's unspoken mind. Required private experience may appear as clearly separated context outside the transcript, marked as nonspoken and unavailable to the other senders unless the story gives them a reason to know it. That context can record what the focal sender notices before composing or sending: a timestamp, a read receipt, a phrase that is hard to remove, an attachment that was already seen, or a choice to say yes because refusing would require a longer message.\n\nFollow the offense from preparation or incentive through execution, evidence, pursuit, and aftermath from the chosen viewpoint: culprit, accomplice, victim, investigator, or bystander. Suspense can come from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did what. Do not force a procedural victory or a punishment. The exchange can end on a sent message, a read receipt, a pending request, an unsent draft, a forwarded attachment, or the continuation of the digital routine that made the offense possible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadapba::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Lu Xun / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the crime in concise scene-setting, attributed dialogue, and focal narration or inner response. Set each scene in a specific institutional or domestic space already present in the supplied world: an office, a waiting room, a kitchen, a hallway, a shop, a meeting room, or a comparable place where people must wait, ask, or account for themselves. Keep the scene-setting brief and concrete: the position of a desk, the color of a folder, the sound of a queue number, the way a chair has been moved, a cup left on a counter.\n\nLet the focal character notice the ordinary details that belong to the routine, and let that noticing carry the Lu Xun exposure method. The narration can show the character waiting without complaint, signing a paper without reading it, or repeating a rule as if it were natural. A signature, a stamp, a forwarded message, or a door that opens only after a second knock can all appear as accepted parts of the day. Dialogue can carry the same acceptance: a speaker says the matter will be handled, everyone is treated the same, or the form is only a formality. Do not write an explanatory lecture about the institution. The irony belongs to the gap between the accepted phrase and the visible consequence.\n\nUse background and expression cues to orient the moment, not as automatic truth indicators. An expression cue can attract suspicion, but a contradiction in an arrangement or an account must do the evidentiary work. If a character smiles at a delay, the reader learns only that the smile occurs; whether it means ease, habit, or concealment must be supported by other material. Likewise, a room whose order has changed is visible evidence only when the previous arrangement was established earlier. Keep required private experience as focal narration or inner response in the active presentation, outside other characters' knowledge. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected; those require their own authorization or feature.\n\nFollow the offense from preparation or incentive through execution, evidence, pursuit, and aftermath from the chosen viewpoint: culprit, accomplice, victim, investigator, or bystander. Suspense can come from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did what. Do not force a procedural victory or a punishment. A scene can end on a completed action, an unanswered question, a new piece of knowledge, a queue that moves one position, or the continuation of the routine that made the offense possible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadapbb::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Lu Xun / Web Serial\n\nWrite the crime as an installment-oriented serial with a readable through-line across replies. Each installment should make clear what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover context briefly only when it helps: restate the specific form, sum, promise, injury, or routine that the current installment depends on, rather than retelling the whole investigation.\n\nLet the Lu Xun exposure method accumulate through repeated ordinary routines. A character waits again, signs another paper, is told the same rule in a different office, or notices that a familiar phrase now covers a larger harm. The institution becomes visible through recurrence, not through an explanatory passage. A small humiliation from an earlier installment can return with a new consequence. A phrase a supervisor used once can reappear in a different speaker's mouth. A form that was already submitted can be requested again, and the second request can reveal how little the first was read. Do not announce the theme; let the reader see what the characters continue to accept as normal.\n\nPreserve earlier observations when later events qualify them. If a fixed earlier fact is revisited, recover the exact particular: the sum that did not match, the signature that was missing, the room where the form was filed, the message that was never answered. New evidence can qualify an old explanation without replacing the old event or making the focal character deliberately forget a supported conclusion. A rule understood as temporary may turn out to have been permanent, but the first statement of the rule remains part of the story and may need a brief recovery rather than silent erasure.\n\nUse earned developments and occasional hooks without making every turn a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger. Progress can involve knowledge, relationships, work, or resources. A new fact can qualify an old explanation. A relationship can shift because one person finally asks a question, stops answering, or agrees to help for a practical reason. A resource can change because a fee is waived, a document is lost, or a schedule changes. The installment can close on completed work, a pending request, a new piece of knowledge, a familiar routine repeated under a changed condition, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Serial duration should give selected facts room to acquire meaning, not make the offense perpetually recede.\n\nFollow the offense from preparation or incentive through execution, evidence, pursuit, and aftermath from the chosen viewpoint: culprit, accomplice, victim, investigator, or bystander. Suspense can come from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did what. Do not force a procedural victory or a punishment. Preserve required private experience as ordinary narrative prose in the active presentation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaqaa::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Masashi Kishimoto / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the crime and its aftermath through the emotional cost of returning to a difficult relationship. Stage the encounter so the focal character's present action is pushed by a memory of a prior wound, debt, or broken promise tied to the offense. The memory should change what the character is willing to say or do in the current scene, not merely decorate the moment.\n\nLet strain alter the actual conversation and subsequent behavior. When a character admits something, withholds it, apologizes, or asks for help, show the cost in hesitation, deflection, a sharp reply, or a task performed with too much force. Warmth may arrive partially and awkwardly: an offer of help can carry resentment, a sincere apology can be interrupted by a practical reminder of the damage. Do not require the relationship to heal or collapse.\n\nKeep the crime's exposure and divided loyalty as the source of pressure. A suspect, accomplice, victim, or investigator may need something from someone they have hurt or been hurt by. The need is practical and emotional at once: information, access, an alibi, a place to hide, or a promise to keep quiet. The other person's answer should follow from their own remembered grievance and present interest, not from a fixed script of betrayal or forgiveness.\n\nUse clear staging and emotionally significant action. A gesture, a held object, or a sudden movement can carry the weight of the relationship, but the reader should understand why it matters through the focal character's private reasoning. Render that reasoning as the character's particular assessment of the immediate risk: what this old debt makes them willing to risk now, what they suspect the other person might use against them, or what admitting a need would cost before the practical request is spoken. Keep private thought outside other characters' knowledge, and let it remain uncertain or mistaken without turning every exchange into a solved judgment of motive.\n\nLet the scene end with the emotional question still live. The factual matter may advance, but the relationship consequence can remain unsettled after the exchange. A character may accept help without forgiving the helper, or refuse it while still needing it. The crime does not have to resolve into punishment or reconciliation for the style to work.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaqab::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Masashi Kishimoto / Chat Roleplay\n\nPresent the crime story as a coherent shared interval in readable paragraphs. Each paragraph should make clear who is speaking or acting, and dialogue must be unmistakably attributed. Keep the scene moving through the participants' choices rather than through narration that summarizes what happened offscreen.\n\nUse Kishimoto-style clear staging for the criminal situation. Show where the characters are, what they can see or handle, and what practical step they are attempting: meeting a contact, examining a scene, moving money, waiting for someone, or deciding whether to report what they know. Let the emotional weight of the situation come through action and exchange rather than long interior passages.\n\nBuild the scene around relationship-driven conflict. The characters should have a reason to be in the same place that involves both the crime and their history with each other. A memory can change a present encounter: one participant may recall a past betrayal or debt and let it affect how they answer, what they offer, or whether they stay. Keep that memory grounded in the current scene's practical stakes.\n\nResolve the submitted material according to the active authorship contract and leave a continuation that follows from the scene. The next move should be available without requiring a question, cliffhanger, or forced choice at the end. An NPC may continue their practical life, a conversation may pause naturally, or a new piece of information may arrive. Do not manufacture a mandatory decision point.\n\nKeep ongoing world and NPC behavior active. Other characters should respond to the participants' actions with their own interests and agency. Cooperation and refusal are both available. The crime's suspense can come from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did it. Do not turn every pause into a hidden threat or every ally into a potential traitor.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaqac::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Masashi Kishimoto / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nTell the crime through the social and satirical realism route: a vernacular episodic account of a milieu where an offense exposes practical interests, debts, and reputations. Keep the language concrete and the episodes arranged around encounters that reveal who stands to gain, who must answer, and who is quietly complicit.\n\nUse Kishimoto-style clear staging for each episode. Show the characters in a definite place with a definite practical purpose: a shopkeeper settling accounts, a family member asking after missing money, an official receiving a complaint, or a former associate returning to collect a favor. Let the emotional significance of the encounter come through what the characters do and say, not through abstract commentary.\n\nBuild relationship-driven conflict into the social observation. The crime should press on a bond that already has a history: a sibling who covered a debt, a neighbor who witnessed too much, a partner who kept the books. A memory can change the present encounter when a character recalls an earlier kindness, slight, or unpaid obligation and lets it shape their answer. The satire should arise from the gap between what people claim and what their practical choices reveal.\n\nLet the investigation or aftermath move through the episode structure. Each encounter should add a concrete piece of information: who was seen, what was owed, which account changed, or what object is missing. The reader should be able to follow the sequence without an omniscient narrator explaining every connection. Keep the difference between observation, report, and inference clear.\n\nPreserve the relevant language, period, and community as supplied. Do not transplant a particular setting or cast identity from the cultural examples. The route guides craft: social and satirical realism shapes selection and emphasis, but the crime's actual location, customs, and characters come from the active world contract. The account may end with the social damage still visible even when a factual question is settled.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaqad::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Masashi Kishimoto / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the crime in hard-edged, concrete prose. Use forceful diction and compressed transitions: move from one practical action to the next without explanatory padding. Show material wear where it matters: a scuffed floor near a forced door, a wallet with the stitching split, a phone screen cracked during a struggle, a jacket stiff with dried rain.\n\nKeep the physical stakes visible. The offense and its aftermath should involve real risk to bodies, money, shelter, or freedom. A character may be short on time, short on cash, watched, injured, or carrying something that cannot be explained. Ground abrasive or rough speech in its speaker: a threat should sound like the person making it, not like a generic hard-boiled line.\n\nUse Kishimoto-style clear staging for the action. The reader should know where the characters are, what they are trying to do, and what stands in the way. Let emotionally significant action carry the relationship: a shove that stops short, a hand left on a weapon, a door held open too long. A memory can change a present encounter when a character recalls a past debt or betrayal and acts on it in the moment.\n\nBuild relationship-driven conflict through practical pressure. The crime should force people who know each other to choose between loyalty and exposure, help and self-protection, silence and survival. Show the choice in what they do with their hands, their money, their keys, their phone, their silence. Do not explain the emotional meaning after every action; let the concrete detail carry it.\n\nChange rhythm when the character's reasoning needs it. A longer thought is allowed when a decision requires weighing a risk or recalling a specific past event. Otherwise keep the prose tight. The style does not turn the crime story into survival horror: the danger is credible, but the world remains the one supplied by the active contract, and the characters remain particular people rather than pressure-tested archetypes.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaqae::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Masashi Kishimoto / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the crime sequence through documents. Each entry must have a clear author, an intended recipient or purpose, and a time of composition. Use the document form required by the scene: a report, a letter, a ledger entry, a text message, a statement, a receipt, or a note left behind. Do not rotate through formats mechanically.\n\nLet each document know or conceal only what its author would know or choose to record at that moment. A suspect's letter may omit the very detail an investigator's report later exposes. A victim's statement may be precise about one event and vague about another because of fear, loyalty, or incomplete memory. Gaps, revisions, and conflicting accounts should reveal events across entries rather than being explained away.\n\nUse Kishimoto-style relationship-driven conflict to shape the documents. The people writing and receiving these records should have histories that affect what they say. A partner may file a report that protects someone while appearing complete. A witness may write a statement that revises an earlier account after remembering a past kindness or injury. The memory should change what the document includes or leaves out.\n\nBuild suspense from exposure and divided loyalty. The reader may know who committed the offense while still following what each document risks revealing. A receipt can place someone at a scene. A letter can promise silence and then be found. A ledger can show a payment that contradicts a public claim. Let the documents accumulate into a legible sequence without an omniscient narrator connecting every point.\n\nPreserve readable continuity. Arrange the entries so the reader can follow the timeline and the shifting interests. When a document is incomplete or misleading, the next entry should complicate or correct it through its own author's situation. A final entry may settle the factual question while its author withholds something still unresolved, writes to preserve an ongoing purpose, or records a temporary revision that later entries may disturb; no concluding document is required to display emotional repair or permanent damage.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaqaf::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Masashi Kishimoto / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite the crime story's aftermath with room for sincere care and chosen kindness. The offense and its consequences remain real, but the scene can spend attention on what the characters do for each other: a meal brought without being asked, a coat returned, a phone charged, a quiet errand run, a familiar preference remembered.\n\nUse Kishimoto-style relationship-driven bonds to make the comfort meaningful. The characters should have a history that includes the crime or its fallout: a partner who stayed, a sibling who came back, a friend who kept a promise, a neighbor who noticed something and chose to help. A memory can change the present encounter when a character recalls an earlier kindness or shared moment and acts on it now.\n\nKeep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable or remove established problems from the world. A character may accept help while still angry, offer care while still hurt, or sit in comfortable silence without resolving the underlying conflict. Let the warmth be sincere without requiring a confession or emotional repair.\n\nShow practical care through clear staging. The reader should see the small actions that make the comfort concrete: the way someone sets down a cup, folds a blanket, waits without pushing, or leaves a note. These details carry the affection more effectively than declarations. Let the scene have ease and manageable friction rather than high emotional turbulence.\n\nKeep the crime present without letting it dominate the comfort. The characters may be waiting for news, recovering from an injury, hiding from a threat, or deciding what to do next. The danger or consequence can remain in the background while the scene focuses on the particular people and their care for each other. The style does not replace the genre with a domestic vignette; it treats the aftermath through warmth and attention.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaqag::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Masashi Kishimoto / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite the crime as composed novel prose with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Let the narration move from a particular encounter to its social context, history, or judgment where the chosen narrator permits it. Distinguish the narration from ordinary spoken language: the prose may frame a scene through the pressures of a neighborhood, an institution, a family, or a public reputation.\n\nKeep Kishimoto-style clear staging at the center of each scene. Even when the paragraph broadens to social context, the reader should know where the characters are, what they are doing, and what practical or emotional stake drives the encounter. Let emotionally significant action carry the relationship: a gesture, a refusal, a return, or a moment of hesitation should be staged clearly enough to hold the weight the narration gives it.\n\nBuild the crime's competing interests through relationship-driven conflict. The offense should press on bonds that have a history: a family that divided over money, partners who kept different secrets, neighbors whose silence had different motives. A memory can change a present encounter when a character recalls an earlier event and lets it shape what they say or do. The formal narration may reflect on that history without replacing the live scene with summary.\n\nUse the social framing to make the crime's aftermath legible. The narration can show how an arrest, a disappearance, a debt, or a betrayal affects a household, a workplace, or a community. Public and private contrast may organize a passage: what is said at a table, what is written in a record, what is known in the street. Keep the difference between observation, report, and judgment clear.\n\nPreserve the chosen narrator's distance and knowledge. The formal style does not grant access to excluded minds or cancel access permitted by the active authorship contract. The prose may be broader and more deliberate, but the crime's suspense can still arise from exposure or divided loyalty. A scene may end with the criminal question settled and a relationship still in motion, with the factual matter still open and the social damage already discernible, or with both resolved together; the ending follows the scene's actual developments rather than a required state of unresolved consequence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaqah::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Masashi Kishimoto / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nBegin with an outer scene in which one character needs something from another: a debt, a silence, a place to hide, an alibi, a name, or a decision about whether to act. Establish the occasion and the stakes before the telling starts. The storyteller is not a neutral narrator; the account is an intervention aimed at the listener's fear, loyalty, resentment, or sense of obligation.\n\nWhen the teller moves into an embedded account of the offense, keep the transition concrete: what the teller chooses to say first, what is delayed, and what the listener does while hearing it. Use Kishimoto-style clear staging inside the tale. Show the remembered encounter as a scene with readable movement, physical positioning, and a moment of emotionally significant action. The tale should not summarize a plot; it should render a particular incident in which a relationship changed.\n\nMake the memory do work in the present. A remembered wound can explain why the teller is asking for help, why the listener distrusts a promise, or why a third person must not learn what happened. Let the outer listener interrupt, question, or react. The teller may omit what they did not understand, what they are ashamed of, or what they need the listener to believe. Omission is a choice with a visible effect, not a hidden clue to be solved.\n\nKeep the narrative levels distinct but connected. A repeated phrase, object, or gesture can reappear in the outer scene with a changed meaning after the tale. For example, a phrase the teller attributes to an old partner may be repeated by the listener when refusing or accepting the request. Recurrence should accrue meaning through the relationship, not certify that the account is true.\n\nReturn to the outer scene before the response ends. The telling may secure cooperation, provoke a refusal, expose a divided loyalty, or leave the listener weighing an action. The crime's consequences remain open; the storyteller need not confess fully, the listener need not believe everything, and the account need not produce punishment or resolution. End when the present encounter has changed, even if the larger scheme continues.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaqai::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Masashi Kishimoto / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social-developmental realism route. Write the crime as an event produced by a particular social and material situation: work, housing, debt, family obligation, local authority, or a practical opportunity that narrows the characters' choices. The narrator should observe these conditions with steady, composed attention, not as background color but as the ground from which the offense grows.\n\nKeep the viewpoint close enough to render Kishimoto-style relationship pressure. Show what a character wants from another person, what they remember about a past failure or injury, and how that memory enters the present decision. An advance should have both a personal and a practical basis. If a character takes a risk, the reader should see the specific need, loyalty, or resentment behind it and the concrete circumstances that make the risk possible.\n\nStage the key action clearly. Use readable physical detail and spatial clarity: who is present, where they stand, what they handle, what they see. The emotionally significant moment should occur inside or immediately after a concrete action, not as a separate explanation. Let the consequences develop over time. A decision made in one scene may alter a friendship, a workplace, a household, or a debt in the next. The narrator should trace that development without rushing to judgment.\n\nKeep the syntax and narrative distance consistent with the selected realist route. The prose may be direct and composed, with longer sentences where the narrator is tracing cause and consequence, and shorter ones where action or speech interrupts. Do not imitate translated word order, bureaucratic severity, or a generic national temperament. Use the setting, language, community, and period actually supplied.\n\nLet the crime's aftermath remain human and partial. The narrator may show how an offense changes the participants' standing, trust, and practical options without requiring punishment, confession, or moral clarity. End when a particular consequence has become legible, even if the larger situation remains unresolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaqaj::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Masashi Kishimoto / Gothic-Punk\n\nWrite the offense inside a collision between inherited authority and present urban life. An old claim, building, debt, family obligation, or institutional privilege should press against the practical realities of the current city: rent, transport, work, surveillance, noise, and the people who actually live there now. The crime emerges from that pressure rather than from a single villain's whim.\n\nUse urban material detail to make the atmosphere concrete. Show the condition of a room, the light in a stairwell, the sound from the street, the state of a door or lock, the smell of a basement or an office. These details should affect how a particular encounter is experienced: a meeting feels different in a decaying building than in a clean public space, and the difference should matter to what the characters risk or reveal.\n\nKeep Kishimoto-style relationship conflict at the center. The characters should have a history that matters: a debt, a betrayal, a shared failure, a promise, or a wound one of them still carries. Let that memory enter the present encounter and change what a character is willing to do. An emotionally significant action should have a personal and practical basis. Show the moment clearly: who moves, what is said, what is refused, what is taken.\n\nLet social masks fall away in trusted company. A character may speak differently with an old ally than with an employer, a creditor, or a rival. Private desire and morally complicated intimacy can shape the crime: loyalty may pull against self-interest, affection may coexist with exploitation, and a character may protect someone while still harming them. Do not make every intimacy a trap or every caring act fraudulent.\n\nKeep the old authority human and specific. It may be a family, a landlord, a former patron, a religious or civic body, or a person who still commands loyalty. Do not import vampire physiology, factions, powers, or blood-bond rules. The authority's power should be legible through property, obligation, reputation, access, and memory. End when the collision between old claim and present life has produced a concrete consequence, even if the larger scheme continues.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaqak::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Masashi Kishimoto / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and domestic realism route. Place the crime inside a specific household, workplace, neighborhood, or community, and show how the offense grows from ordinary pressures: a family obligation, a property dispute, a debt, a marriage arrangement, a job lost or promised, a reputation at stake. Keep the language, region, community, and period specific when the story supplies them. Do not impose a generic family structure, religion, caste experience, or code-switching.\n\nWrite the Kishimoto-style relationship conflict through domestic scenes. Show the characters in rooms, kitchens, shops, courtyards, offices, or streets, with clear staging and readable movement. Let a memory enter the present encounter: a past failure, a broken promise, a childhood loyalty, or a wound one character still carries. The memory should change what a character is willing to do now, not merely explain their mood.\n\nMake the emotionally significant action concrete. A character may take money, hide a document, lie to a relative, break a promise, or strike someone. Show the action itself and its immediate physical and social consequences. The advance should have both a personal and a practical basis: the reader should see what the character needs and why this particular relationship makes the action possible.\n\nFollow the aftermath through the household and community. The offense should alter obligations, trust, and standing among people who must continue living near one another. Show how different family members or neighbors learn, interpret, or conceal what happened. Do not force a moral resolution; the narrator may observe the consequences without declaring who is wholly right.\n\nKeep the prose direct and socially observant. The narrator may notice how a room is kept, what is served, who speaks first, who is excluded, and how money or work is discussed. These details should carry the story's pressure rather than decorate it. End when a particular domestic or community consequence has become legible, even if the larger situation remains unresolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaqal::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Masashi Kishimoto / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social or psychological realism route. Write the crime as an event with legible social and psychological causes: a character's need, loyalty, resentment, fear, or ambition, shaped by work, family, money, reputation, or a specific relationship. The narrator should observe these causes without turning the story into a case summary or a clinical explanation.\n\nKeep the staging clear in the Kishimoto manner. Show the reader where the characters are, what they are doing, and what changes in the room or street when the offense occurs. Use precise spatial and temporal detail: the position of a door, the time of a meeting, the order in which a character notices something. The emotionally significant action should happen inside or immediately after a concrete physical movement.\n\nLet relationship-driven conflict carry the story. The characters should have a history that matters: a shared job, a family tie, an old rivalry, a debt, or a promise. Let a memory enter the present encounter and change what a character is willing to do. The memory should have a practical effect, not merely a nostalgic or explanatory one.\n\nFollow the aftermath through the chosen viewpoint. Show how the offense alters the character's standing, trust, and practical options. The narrator may render private reasoning, attention, and uncertainty, but should keep access and knowledge separate: the narration may reveal a focal motive while another character does not know it. Do not grant the narrator access to excluded minds.\n\nKeep the prose direct and composed, with personal language and narrative distance following the actual teller. Seasonal attention, omission, and plain argument are available where appropriate, but do not impose a universal quietness or a mandatory inventory of Japanese scenery. End when a particular consequence has become legible, even if the larger situation remains unresolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaqam::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Masashi Kishimoto / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social realism and chronicle route. Write the crime as an event within a specific community, shaped by work, land, money, migration, local authority, or a shared history of pressure and survival. Preserve the relevant language, community, and history when the story supplies them. Do not manufacture a generic regional temperament or relocate the cast.\n\nLet the telling carry a witness's awareness. The narrator may be a participant, a bystander, or a chronicler who knows the neighborhood and its people. The account should distinguish what the narrator saw from what was reported, remembered, or inferred. A vivid recollection is not proof, and a community's shared story may be partial or contested.\n\nKeep the Kishimoto-style relationship conflict concrete. Show the characters in specific places: a street, a market, a workplace, a home, a bus, a courtyard. Stage the emotionally significant action clearly, with readable movement and physical consequence. Let a memory enter the present encounter and change what a character is willing to do. The memory should have a practical basis: an old debt, a broken promise, a past betrayal, or a loyalty that still binds.\n\nFollow the aftermath through the community. Show how the offense alters trust, obligation, and standing among people who must continue living and working together. Different witnesses may interpret the same event differently, and the narrator may record those differences without forcing a single moral verdict.\n\nKeep the prose direct and socially observant. The chronicle may notice how money moves, who controls access, what work costs, and what people say in public versus private. Do not add supernatural facts to a world that excludes them. End when a particular community consequence has become legible, even if the larger situation remains unresolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaqan::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Masashi Kishimoto / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Open with a concrete present moment: a meeting, a message, a job, a threat, a discovery, or a decision already in motion. Establish who is present, where they are, and what they want without a long setup. The crime should be legible as a specific offense, scheme, or consequence from the chosen viewpoint.\n\nKeep spatial changes easy to follow. Use short, readable paragraphs and clear transitions when characters move between rooms, streets, vehicles, or buildings. Stage the Kishimoto-style action visually: show who moves, what they handle, what they see, and what changes in the space. An emotionally significant action should be concrete and immediate, not summarized.\n\nUse focused reaction beats around action and dialogue. After a blow, a threat, a confession, or a refusal, give the viewpoint character a private response: what they notice, what they fear, what they remember, what they decide. The reaction can be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to its owner. Let a memory enter the present encounter and change what the character is willing to do. The memory should have a practical basis: an old debt, a broken promise, a shared failure, or a loyalty that still binds.\n\nKeep relationship-driven conflict at the center. The crime should involve people with a history that matters, and the reader should see how that history shapes the offense, the execution, or the aftermath. Give unfamiliar names or mechanics clear presentation when needed, but do not pause the story for a glossary.\n\nFollow the aftermath through the chosen viewpoint. Show how the offense alters trust, standing, and practical options. End when a particular consequence has become legible or a decision has been made, even if the larger scheme continues. Do not require a cliffhanger, a young cast, first person, or comedy.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaqao::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Masashi Kishimoto / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite the crime account through the narrator's sensory and emotional attention, letting rhythm and imagery carry the weight of an offense, a pursuit, or an aftermath. Select concrete details that earn their figurative expansion by changing how the narrator understands the act, the evidence, or the people involved. A scuffed floor, a repeated name, or a sound through a door can open into a comparison or recollection only when that movement illuminates motive, consequence, or the relationship at stake. Keep images traceable to the scene or the teller's associations, and let plain statements interrupt the lyrical movement at moments of decision, discovery, or physical action so the crime's practical reality remains sharp.\n\nStage action clearly in the Kishimoto manner by sequencing movement, contact, and consequence even while the prose attends to texture and feeling. A confrontation, search, payment, or flight should remain legible, but its beats can also create the paragraph's rhythm: a hand hesitating before touching a weapon, a door closing with the weight of a final decision, a wallet passed with a phrase that carries old loyalty. Let emotionally significant action alter a relationship, and show that change through what the viewpoint character does or says next rather than through an announced feeling. Allow a memory to surface during a present encounter when a concrete trigger—a gesture, object, or phrase—connects the past to the current choice, and let the memory's imagery reshape what the character is willing to risk or admit.\n\nUse the crime's human aftermath to govern the selection and recurrence of images. Evidence can appear as a thing retaining the trace of a hand or habit; a divided loyalty can be rendered as two remembered voices with contrasting rhythms. Suspense may arise from exposure or from what a character notices but cannot yet name, and a recurring image can accumulate meaning across scenes instead of resolving immediately into a clue. The narrator may observe a detail that matters only as grief, fear, or attachment while the factual case advances through separate concrete information. Keep other characters' speech in their own registers; the lyrical voice belongs to the narrator's perception, not to a universal figurative manner imposed on the whole cast.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaqap::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Masashi Kishimoto / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nWrite the crime account with attention to what an offense has made unavailable. Let the narrator notice the small irreversible change—a chair no one sits in, a task done differently, a silence where an argument used to be—and allow that change to carry the weight of the crime without announcing that everything is ruined. Ordinary pleasure can remain pleasurable: a shared meal, a working light, or a finished errand may coexist with grief or regret, and the narration can hold both in the same scene.\n\nAnchor reflection in present action and keep the action legible. Stage encounters clearly so that a memory surfaces during a concrete exchange: someone opens a door, counts money, watches a street, or answers a question. The triggered memory should change what the viewpoint character does or understands in that encounter—revising a plan, exposing a hesitation, or shifting a relationship's terms—rather than simply delaying the scene. Return to the present action to show the consequence of the memory, and let that consequence be visible through movement, speech, or decision. A relationship-driven conflict can turn on the difference between what a person remembers and what the other now needs, and that difference should emerge through action.\n\nLet the crime's aftermath unfold through unfinished attachment rather than a single completed loss. A culprit, victim, investigator, or bystander may remain bound to someone by loyalty, resentment, habit, or hope, and the reflective voice can trace how that attachment persists across repeated occasions. Compare the present moment with what was once possible: the same route walked with a different purpose, the same phrase now carrying another meaning, the same task performed without the person who used to share it. Do not require every scene to end by announcing impermanence. Some things persist and some attachments continue. The melancholy belongs to the particular character's experience of time and consequence, not to a universal mood imposed on the whole account, and it should remain available to the narrator's private access rather than bleeding into other characters' speech or knowledge.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaqaq::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Masashi Kishimoto / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse framed storytelling for the crime account. Establish a situated teller and an occasion for the telling: someone recounts an offense, a scheme, a pursuit, or an aftermath to a particular listener or group, in a particular place and time. The frame remains present throughout. The listener may interrupt, question a detail, or respond to the teller's emphasis, and the teller may adjust, defend, or delay the account because of that response. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective: the teller can relate what they witnessed, did, heard, or inferred, but not what another person privately thought unless the account establishes how that knowledge was obtained.\n\nStage the events within the told account clearly in the Kishimoto manner. A confrontation, a betrayal, a payment, or a flight should remain legible in its sequence even when the teller pauses to explain a motive or a memory. Let emotionally significant action carry the relationship-driven conflict: a bond changes because someone acts, and the teller's memory of that act can alter how the present listener understands the people involved. Use the telling's structure to create suspense: the teller may choose where to begin, what to withhold, when to admit their own role, or how to respond to a challenge, making the listener's growing sense of the telling's purpose part of the scene. Do not let the telling become a lecture; give it a present purpose—a warning, a confession, a request, a defense, a debt—so that the crime account matters to the people now listening and changes what they might do next.\n\nKeep the tradition coherent without importing a generic regional mood. The framed telling may use recurrence, deliberate openings, or a compact declaration to mark a turn. It may include a reflective or philosophical passage when the teller pauses to consider what the events meant, but that reflection should arise from the teller's own stake in the story. Do not require antiquity, mysticism, exile, religion, or a single honor code. Use the setting, language, period, and community actually supplied, and let the frame's occasion shape the telling's register. Allow the wrap-around scene to carry a relationship consequence of its own: the listener may be changed by what they hear, and the teller may revise their own understanding of the crime as they relate it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaqar::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Masashi Kishimoto / Minimalist Prose\n\nWrite the crime account with a small number of exact details that do more than one thing. Choose details that carry both evidence and relationship: a key left on the wrong hook shows a habit and a change in the household; a folded note shows preparation and a personal tie. Use direct clauses and keep the sequence of action clear—who moved, what they touched, what they said, what they did next—so the reader can infer motive and consequence without explicit explanation. If a thought or inward connection materially contributes to the present account, give it enough room to make sense, but do not repeat it in dialogue or narration. Let one precise sentence do the work of several vague ones.\n\nLet omission leave something inferable. A character may not say what they fear; their next action can show it. A memory can surface as a single recalled phrase or gesture that changes what the viewpoint character does in the present encounter. Preserve causality: the reader should be able to see why an action follows from a motive, a relationship, or a piece of evidence, even when the narration does not spell out the reasoning. Keep the cast's voices individual; a short sentence from one character is not the same as a short sentence from another, and their word choice and rhythm should come from who they are. In a Kishimoto-style confrontation or chase, compress the staging into the essential beats: a movement, a reaction, a shift in advantage, a line of dialogue that exposes loyalty or fear.\n\nUse the crime's practical realities as the source of suspense, not withheld emotions. Exposure, divided loyalty, a missed detail, or a lie that cannot be maintained can create pressure through what the narrator notices and what the scene omits. Show the aftermath through what a person does with an object, a room, or a routine. Do not pad the prose to meet a length; an extended sentence may serve better than several fragments when it holds a precise sequence of cause and effect. End when the present action has disclosed enough for the next consequence to be understood, leaving the reader to infer what remains unsaid.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaqas::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Masashi Kishimoto / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the crime account in sustained scene-first prose. Open in a particular moment—a meeting, a discovery, a payment, a departure—and let the scene orient the reader through action, dialogue, and perception. Move naturally among what the viewpoint character does, says, notices, remembers, and privately thinks, according to the authorized access. Use paragraphing and transitions to shift time or location without recap machinery, and let those transitions serve the crime's information flow: a cut can withhold what happened between scenes, a scene break can shift suspicion, and a return can show how an earlier detail has changed in significance. A new scene can begin with a concrete detail that locates the reader rather than a summary.\n\nStage action clearly in the Kishimoto manner. A confrontation, a chase, a search, or a betrayal should remain legible in its sequence even as the prose moves among physical movement, dialogue, and private reasoning. Let emotionally significant action carry relationship-driven conflict: a bond changes because someone acts, and the narration can show the private reasoning or memory that accompanies the act without stepping outside the viewpoint's access. A memory should surface during a present encounter when a concrete trigger—an object, a phrase, a familiar gesture—connects past and present, and it should change what the viewpoint character is willing to do or say next. Keep the style's flexibility from dissolving causality: even when the narration lingers in reflection, the factual chain and the character's choices must remain traceable.\n\nLet the crime's human aftermath govern narrative attention. Suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did it. The narrator may observe a detail that matters only as grief, fear, or attachment while the factual case advances through separate, concrete information. Do not require understated emotion, realism as world physics, or a lesson at the end of every reply. The register and rhythm come from the narrator's voice, the author profile, and the immediate scene, but the crime's practical realities—evidence, timing, access—should remain consistent. End when the present action has reached a natural pause that leaves the next consequence open for the next scene, not when a moral or reversal has been artificially completed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaqat::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Masashi Kishimoto / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship premise the lens through which the crime account is told. Choose scenes that reveal the bond's particular terms—what these people owe each other, what they hide, what they repeat, what they cannot say—and let the offense, scheme, pursuit, or aftermath put direct pressure on those terms. A crime may force a choice between loyalty and safety, a secret may test a promise, or an investigation may expose a private habit, but the choice must be specific to this relationship's history, not a generic moral dilemma. Do not insert a roster of familiar tropes; use only the actual supplied premise and let its promise shape the story's events and emotional beats.\n\nUse private language and repeated moments as both emotional and evidentiary devices. A phrase, a gesture, a place, or a routine can return with new significance as the crime's consequences unfold. A callback should fulfill the relationship's promise rather than merely restate it: the second occurrence may show what has changed—the same phrase said with a different meaning, the same gesture refused, the same place now dangerous—or it may reveal something the crime has altered between the characters. Let emotionally significant action carry the conflict in the Kishimoto manner: a bond changes because someone acts, and the narration should stage that action clearly so the reader sees what the character risks or forfeits. A memory can surface during a present encounter when a concrete trigger connects past and present, and it should alter what the viewpoint character is willing to do or say next.\n\nKeep the crime's practical realities legible, even when the scene's focus is the relationship. Preparation, incentives, evidence, and pursuit should remain concrete: the reader should be able to see why an action is risky, what it costs, and how it advances or obstructs the investigation or scheme. Suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty, and the relationship's payoff may be partial or unsettled; the factual question and the emotional consequence need not resolve together. Keep private thought outside other characters' knowledge unless the access contract permits it, and let the relationship's changes be earned through staged actions and callbacks rather than announced in narration.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaqau::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Masashi Kishimoto / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the crime account as a chronicle of deeds and their remembered consequences. Open with a deliberate statement that establishes who is involved and what act set the events in motion. Use compact declarations to mark a person, a bond, or a turning point, then expand into the particulars that make the declaration incomplete. A recurring designation should come from an established deed or way of speaking, not from an ornamental epithet assigned to every entrance. Return to a phrase when a later occurrence gives it a new application.\n\nOrganize the account around deeds rather than only private feeling. A theft, a betrayal, a killing, a pursuit, or a payment can be related as an act whose consequences persist. Oaths, kinship, journeys, gifts, feuds, or public deeds can organize the telling when the supplied story establishes them. Keep the particular source tradition coherent; do not import a Nordic pantheon, heroic doom, or ceremonial dialogue into every speaker. Stage action clearly in the Kishimoto manner: the chronicle voice may elevate the telling, but the sequence of a confrontation or flight must remain legible, and a decisive action should change a relationship or a legal situation rather than merely change the mood. Let the account's suspense arise from what the reteller knows, withholds, or contests about the crime and from the listener's awareness that the deed's meaning is still being settled.\n\nLet relationship-driven conflict shape the chronicle. A bond changes because someone acts, and the telling can relate that change through what the person did, refused, or returned to. A memory can surface during a present encounter and alter what the viewpoint character does next. Keep shared reputation distinct from a focal character's private account: being remembered as loyal or dangerous does not mean the person experiences the deed that way, and the chronicle may expose that gap when an occasion forces them to speak or act. Let dialogue interrupt the authority of the chronicle; a participant may object to a detail or answer an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern. The account can end with business concluded or resumed, leaving another act available to be remembered rather than declaring it legend on the community's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaqav::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Masashi Kishimoto / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nChoose the social-realist / domestic-realist route from the Scandinavian tradition. Set the offense inside a specific Nordic social world—a housing cooperative, a municipal workplace, a family firm, a small-town association, or a similar community whose ordinary obligations continue under pressure. Make the social context concrete through rent or mortgage concerns, shift schedules, childcare arrangements, public-transport timing, shared laundry rooms, and neighborly expectation rather than through a landscape of snow and isolation. The crime is a disturbance inside relationships that already have histories of obligation, shame, and practical care.\n\nStage each significant action clearly, in Kishimoto's legible manner: who enters, what they handle, where they stand, and what they can observe. Let the action carry feeling without announcing it. A person returns a borrowed key, waits outside a locked office, or walks past a familiar window, and the gesture matters because of what the two people have already been through. An advance in the investigation or scheme must have a personal and practical basis—a known timetable, a favor called in, a record located, a risk taken because of a specific debt—not a victory forced by the scene.\n\nUse memory as a present social force. When a current encounter recalls an earlier wound, benefit, or obligation, show how that memory changes what the viewpoint character notices, says, or decides now. In the Scandinavian social-realist mode, memory often carries institutional or communal shame: a past eviction, a benefit decision, a workplace complaint, a loan repaid too late, or a neighbor's quiet intervention. Tie the memory to the immediate social situation rather than drifting into general reflection, and let it shape the character's next practical choice.\n\nLet the criminal matter proceed through conversations, records, and observed conditions. A neighbor's report, a supervisor's hesitation, a discrepancy in a routine document, or a suddenly locked common room can open the next step. Distinguish clearly what a person reports from what the viewpoint character infers. The Scandinavian institutional route is not a police procedural by default: the observer may be a colleague, relative, or neighbor who has no formal authority. Keep private thought outside other characters' knowledge, even when the narration reveals it.\n\nPreserve required inward experience in the active presentation. A focal character may think about what a question costs, what a silence means, or what they hope to avoid without announcing it. The narration may reveal that motive while another character remains unaware of it. The scene may close on a completed exchange, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known, without forcing a confession, arrest, or moral resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaqaw::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Masashi Kishimoto / Screenplay\n\nWrite the crime story in screenplay form. Use scene headings when location or time changes, and keep action lines concise and readable: who moves, what they handle, what is visible or audible. Do not fill the page with camera commands. Use a cut or offscreen sound only when the chosen sequence needs it, not as a routine transition.\n\nLet the action carry relationship history. A character returns an object, waits in a doorway, or turns away from a familiar person, and the gesture matters because of what the two have already been through. Stage the significant beats so the audience can see the hesitation, the shared look, or the careful distance without a line explaining it. An advance in the scheme or investigation should have a personal and practical basis: the character knows something, has prepared something, or takes a risk for a reason already established.\n\nUse dialogue to expose divided loyalty and exposure. A question can be answered with a practical instruction, a warning, or a change of subject that reveals the speaker's concern. Let characters talk about the work in front of them while the underlying conflict stays visible in what they choose to say and do. Do not make every exchange a polished confrontation.\n\nPreserve required private experience as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation. Place it after the action line or dialogue block it belongs to, set off from the spoken text and physical action, for example as a bracketed note marked with the character's name and \"nonspoken\" or \"interior.\" It is not dialogue and is not automatically knowledge available to the other characters. A focal character may think about what a question costs or what they hope to avoid while the scene continues. Keep the note tied to the immediate action rather than replacing the scene with summary.\n\nThe scene can end on a completed exchange, a pending request, or a plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Do not force a confession, arrest, or reversal at every cut. Suspense can remain in exposure or divided loyalty even when the viewer already knows who did it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaqax::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Masashi Kishimoto / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable script with clear speakers, entrances, exits, and economical stage directions. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene. A character sets down a bag, checks a door, or stands too close to an exit, and the movement matters because of what the audience can see and what the characters know about each other.\n\nUse the shared stage space to make a proposed account testable. Who can see an entrance, reach a prop, hear a line, or move between two places should be clear enough that an attempted reconstruction changes what is plausible. Keep the people occupied with the practical work of checking, not posed around an explanatory speech. A question about where someone stood or when a door opened should be answerable from what has been staged.\n\nLet dialogue expose divided loyalty and exposure. A character can answer plainly, challenge the premise, or change the subject for an actual reason. The underlying conflict stays visible in what they choose to say and do, not in a universal habit of polished confrontation. Use pauses and physical business to let a silence carry weight without making every pause a confession.\n\nPreserve required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation. Place it as a bracketed note attached to the relevant line or business, clearly marked as nonspoken, not as an aside or monologue unless separately authorized. It is not automatically knowledge available to the other characters. A focal character may think about what a question costs or what they hope to avoid while the scene continues. Keep the note tied to the immediate staged action rather than replacing the scene with summary.\n\nDo not add film-camera instructions. The scene can end after a result or an interruption without an obligatory gathering of suspects, theatrical revelation, or new clue. Suspense can remain in exposure or divided loyalty even when the audience already knows who did it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaqay::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Masashi Kishimoto / Stream of Consciousness\n\nTell the crime story through one authorized mind's associative movement. Braid perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern. Syntax and paragraph shape may follow those associations, while enough orientation remains to distinguish events from recollection or fantasy. The reader should know what is happening now, what is remembered, and what is feared or imagined.\n\nLet a recurring sensory or verbal anchor carry the offense through the narrative. A specific doorway, a ring tone, the smell of a stairwell, a phrase someone repeated, or the weight of a borrowed object can return without being fully explained at first. Each return can attach a new implication: the mind now knows who was there, now suspects what the phrase meant, now feels the cost of what it agreed to hide. The anchor keeps the criminal matter present without turning every return into a revealed clue.\n\nLet remembered wounds surface as recurring concerns that change the present encounter. A familiar voice, a smell, a doorway, or a routine question can pull the mind back to an earlier loss, betrayal, or obligation. Show how that return reshapes what the character notices or says now. The memory may be a partial image, a phrase, or a bodily recollection rather than a tidy explanatory flashback, and it may contradict what the character has told others.\n\nKeep the criminal matter legible through the mind's attention. The character may notice a discrepancy in an account, a familiar object out of place, or a person's hesitation, and the thought may circle back to it while other concerns interrupt. Let the investigation proceed through what the mind returns to, what it avoids, and what it finally connects. Distinguish observation, memory, report, inference, and conclusion. Do not let the stream of consciousness dissolve the crime facts into pure impression; the reader must be able to recover what happened and what remains uncertain.\n\nUse relationship-driven conflict as recurring pressure. The mind may argue with a remembered voice, rehearse what it should have said, or feel the weight of a divided loyalty. Private words can appear as the character's own inner speech, but they remain that character's experience, not a transmitted message or a shared mental field. Another character does not know a conclusion merely because the reader has followed its formation.\n\nThe passage can end on a completed thought, a pending question, or a plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Do not force a confession, arrest, or reversal at every pause. Suspense can remain in exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaqaz::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Masashi Kishimoto / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the crime story through readable sender-attributed messages. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter, and treat each one as an act: a screenshot forwarded outside the group, a new person added, someone leaving the channel, a photograph sent instead of a description. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution — one writes in fragments, another answers hours later in full paragraphs, another edits before sending. Do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext.\n\nLet relationship history surface through what people send and withhold. A question about a shift, a debt, or a shared obligation can carry the weight of an older wound, and a message that echoes how someone used to talk can reopen it. A delayed reply, a changed subject, or a thread gone quiet can expose divided loyalty without a character announcing it. The exchange should feel like these particular people using their phones, not like a transcript arranged to confess.\n\nKeep the criminal matter legible through the messages themselves: a discrepancy in an account, a request that arrives too late, a name someone avoids, or shorthand that only the participants share. Distinguish what a person sends from what the recipient infers; the same short reply can read as caution, contempt, or fear depending on who reads it and what they remember. A sent message is transmitted text; an unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought. Mark unsent drafts clearly and keep them separate from the transcript.\n\nWhen private experience is required, set it apart from the transcript as brief context tied to the exchange: what the question costs, what the focal participant hopes to avoid, a memory of what this arrangement cost someone pressing on the fingers at the keys. That thought is not transmitted and is not known to the other side; it can change what gets typed — a truthful reply deleted, a safer one sent — without needing an external task to justify its place. Waiting and rereading take their real duration.\n\nThe exchange can end mid-conversation, on a sent message, a read receipt with no reply, a pending request, or a plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Do not force a confession, arrest, or reversal at every pause. Suspense can remain in exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaqba::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Masashi Kishimoto / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the crime story in concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response, in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene: establish the space in a few purposeful strokes, let a clear speaker act, then give the focal character's reaction its own beat. A background or expression cue — the state of the room, where bodies are positioned, who is watching whom — orients the moment; it is not executable engine code or a mandatory label on every line. Keep scene changes legible.\n\nLet action carry relationship history. A character returns an object, waits in a doorway, takes the seat beside a rival, or turns away from a familiar person, and the gesture matters because of what the two have already been through. Stage the significant beat like a single composed image — the hesitation, the shared look, the careful distance — so the reader can see it without a line explaining it. An advance in the scheme, the pursuit, or the aftermath should have a personal and practical basis: the character knows something, has prepared something, or takes the risk for a reason already established, not because the scene needs a victory.\n\nUse focal narration to show how the viewpoint character explains another person's behavior, and let the next exchange support that explanation, complicate it, or reveal the speaker was concerned with something else entirely. A face can be read as guilt, fear, or loyalty, and the reading can be wrong; what actually shifts the situation is what someone does or reveals, not the expression itself. Keep private thought outside the other characters' knowledge.\n\nLet the criminal matter proceed through scenes whose information changes the stakes — who knows what, who can still be trusted, what has been exposed. A return to a familiar place can use a remembered detail as an anchor: the object still where it was left, the person absent who used to be there, the new significance earned by what has happened since. Repetition should deepen understanding rather than restart the same suspicion. The style does not activate branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because it is selected.\n\nGive inward experience a real place in the scene rhythm: what a question costs, what a silence means, what the focal character hopes to avoid, the memory of an earlier encounter pressing on this one, even when no visible action changes yet. The scene can end on a completed exchange, a pending request, movement still underway, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Do not force a confession, arrest, or reversal at every scene change.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaqbb::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Masashi Kishimoto / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across installments: what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover context briefly only when it helps — a sentence that restores who owes whom, what was taken, or what was promised is enough. Progress can involve knowledge, relationships, work, or resources; no single turn needs to be a complete miniature chapter.\n\nKeep exact earlier facts available and let a later installment change what they suggest. The vehicle everyone believed was clean, the debt someone claimed was settled, the door that was supposed to be watched: when a new installment touches one of these, recover the particular briefly rather than retelling how it was established. New developments can qualify an earlier account without erasing what was already shown — if part of the debt was quietly repaid, the payment belongs to the history and cannot be dropped so the old figure still stands. A character may have lied; the record of what the story showed does not change.\n\nUse relationship-driven conflict as cumulative pressure. A remembered wound can surface when a current encounter recalls it — the same demand from the same person, a place tied to an old failure — and the memory changes what the viewpoint character notices, says, or decides now. Let an advance be earned on both grounds at once: practical, because the preparation or knowledge was already shown, and personal, because of what these people have been through together. The installment does not need to resolve the relationship; it can deepen what the reader understands of it while the situation moves forward.\n\nLet ordinary life continue around the scheme or the pursuit, and keep required inward experience available when it contributes feeling or thought, even when no immediate decision changes. A repeated question can carry a new implication without a fresh contradiction being invented. Waiting, watching, and small preparations take their real duration, and an installment built mostly of attention can still move the story.\n\nClose on completed work, a pending request, an unanswered message, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known, without manufacturing a cliffhanger. Suspense can remain in exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did it. Serial duration should give the established facts, debts, and loyalties room to acquire meaning rather than making resolution perpetually recede.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaraa::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Neil Gaiman / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the crime's aftermath or preparation through one viewpoint whose attention keeps returning to ordinary things that have become charged: a coat left on a chair, a phone that does not ring, a shared route now walked alone, a favor that can no longer be repaid. Let these objects and routines carry the emotional cost instead of announcing it. The telling should remain direct and conversational, as if the narrator is trying to make sense of something too large by describing its small surfaces.\n\nTreat relationships as active sites of strain. A character may need something from someone they have harmed, or owe loyalty to someone whose choices frighten them. Let that strain change what they actually say: they may overexplain, go silent, offer practical help instead of an apology, or ask a question they already know will hurt. Do not require a confession or a clean resolution. Warmth can appear as an awkward meal, a remembered habit, or a door left unlocked, and it can coexist with unresolved anger.\n\nGive names, places, and remembered stories consequence when the scenario supports them. A nickname can become a reminder of what someone used to be. A street or a particular room can carry the weight of an earlier choice. If a character recalls a story from before the offense, let it illuminate their present reasoning rather than serve as a decorative aside. The strange or uncanny should remain a matter of how the viewpoint experiences ordinary life under pressure, not an imported supernatural fact.\n\nKeep the crime's practical stakes legible: what was taken, who is exposed, what obligation remains, who is still looking. Emotional turbulence should complicate these stakes rather than replace them. A character may be too upset to notice a useful detail, or may notice it precisely because it belongs to someone they cannot stop thinking about. Preserve the difference between what the viewpoint knows and what other characters reveal. The ending may leave a relationship unresolved even when a factual matter is settled.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadarab::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Neil Gaiman / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the shared interval in readable, scannable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution. Each speaker's words should be clearly assigned, and narrative action should move the scene forward without long unbroken description. Keep the crime's practical situation active: what the characters are doing, where they are, what they can see or hear, and what they want from each other in this moment.\n\nLet the Gaiman profile's direct telling and layered ordinary detail work through concrete, familiar activity. A character may make tea while discussing a debt, check a lock while avoiding a question, or walk a familiar route while trying to decide whether to trust someone. These actions should feel natural to the scene and reveal something about the character's state without halting the exchange. Do not import gods, ghosts, or supernatural events unless the active world already includes them.\n\nKeep NPCs and the ongoing world active. Other characters should respond, make choices, and continue their practical lives rather than waiting for the player or focal character to act. Agency includes cooperation and genuine help as well as resistance. A contact may offer useful information, refuse to answer, or change the subject because they have their own reasons. Preserve what each character knows and does not know.\n\nEnd at a natural continuation point that follows from the scene. The response may stop during continuing speech, action, thought, composition, or an unfinished setup. Do not require a question, cliffhanger, or forced choice at the end. The next turn should be able to continue the same interval without the scene having been artificially paused or resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadarac::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Neil Gaiman / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic telling route. Structure the crime's sequence as linked episodes: preparation, a decisive encounter, pursuit or exposure, and aftermath. Each episode should have its own concrete scene and immediate stakes while carrying forward a larger consequence. Begin an episode in the middle of a recognizable situation, and let its close open naturally into the next development rather than resolving everything at once.\n\nKeep the telling direct and accessible, with clear action, attributed speech, and enough concrete detail to make the setting and relationships legible. Use the Gaiman profile's sense that an encounter carries another story behind it: a chance meeting may explain an old obligation, a familiar place may recall an earlier bargain, or a name may connect two episodes. These echoes should deepen the present action rather than pause it for exposition.\n\nLet each episode advance the crime's practical stakes. Show who stands to gain or lose, what evidence or obligation changes hands, and how relationships shift under pressure. A character's loyalty may be divided between an old companion and a new demand. Suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who committed the offense.\n\nPreserve the relevant language, period, and community as supplied by the active world. Do not transplant a setting or cast identity merely because the route is selected. The episodic structure and direct telling are the method; the actual place, time, and people come from the story already in progress.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadarad::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Neil Gaiman / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite with forceful diction and compressed transitions. Move from one concrete action or observation to the next without unnecessary connective tissue. Let the prose carry weight through specific material detail: a lock that has been forced, a floor that sticks, a jacket stiff with dried rain, a phone with a cracked screen. Physical stakes should be legible in what characters handle, avoid, or cannot afford to lose.\n\nKeep the telling direct and clear even when the prose is hard-edged. The Gaiman profile's narrative ease means the reader should always know who is acting and why the action matters. A familiar object or place can carry an unstated consequence: a door someone always locked, a route someone no longer walks, a debt recorded on a scrap of paper. Let these details resonate without pausing to explain them.\n\nGround abrasive or rough speech in its speaker. A character may curse because they are cornered, tired, or trying to end a conversation, not because the style requires constant aggression. Let different characters speak differently. Some may be curt, some evasive, some unexpectedly careful. Preserve what each one knows and what they are trying to conceal or protect.\n\nUse changes of rhythm deliberately. A compressed exchange can be followed by a longer passage of reasoning when the character needs to work through a problem: how someone got in, what a missing object means, whether a promise still holds. Do not turn the crime into survival horror or a catalogue of damage. The grit should serve the story's practical stakes and human aftermath.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadarae::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Neil Gaiman / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the crime's sequence through documents with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Use the specific document form required by the scene: a report filed too late, a note left in a kitchen, a statement given to someone who may not believe it, a list kept for reasons the writer does not fully explain. Do not rotate through formats mechanically.\n\nLet each document know and conceal only what its author would know or choose to record. A witness may describe what they saw without understanding its significance. A culprit may write carefully around the moment they cannot admit. A later document may revise an earlier account, and the difference should be legible: a changed detail, a name suddenly omitted, a time adjusted. Gaps between entries can carry as much weight as what is written.\n\nUse the Gaiman profile's direct telling and layered ordinary detail within the documents. A writer may mention a familiar object, a shared route, or a remembered story because it matters to them, and that mention can later acquire consequence. The document's voice should remain natural to its author and purpose, not uniformly literary or confessional.\n\nPreserve readable continuity without adding an omniscient explanation after every record. Let the reader assemble the sequence from what the documents show, their order, and their contradictions. A document may provide a lead that can be checked against another source, but one account is not independent proof of another. Keep the crime's practical stakes legible: what was taken, who is exposed, what obligation remains, and what the writers are trying to protect or escape.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaraf::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Neil Gaiman / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSet the story in the crime's aftermath or in a pause between its pressures, and spend attention on chosen kindness and practical care. A character may bring food to someone who has not eaten, remember how a friend takes their tea, return a borrowed coat, or sit with someone who cannot sleep. These actions should be sincere and specific, not generic reassurances. Let the reader enjoy the particular people and their familiar preferences.\n\nKeep the crime's stakes present without letting them overwhelm the warmth. The offense may still be unresolved, a debt may remain, or someone may still be frightened. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable or remove established problems from the world. A character can be kind and still be angry, careful, or unwilling to forgive. Disagreement and privacy should remain intact.\n\nUse the Gaiman profile's direct telling and layered ordinary significance. A familiar object or routine can carry meaning: a meal shared at the same table, a route walked together, a name spoken gently. These details should feel natural and earned rather than decorative. Do not import supernatural elements unless the active world already includes them.\n\nLet affection be sincere without requiring a confession or emotional repair. A character may show care through action rather than declaration. The ending may leave the factual crime unresolved or the relationship still uncertain, but the scene should offer room to breathe and to enjoy the particular people involved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadarag::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Neil Gaiman / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite in composed novel prose with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Let a scene open into its social context, history, or judgment where the chosen narrator permits it: a neighborhood that remembers an old offense, a family whose reputation has shifted, a workplace where everyone knows what was taken. Distinguish narration from ordinary spoken language, but keep the telling direct and clear.\n\nUse the Gaiman profile's intimate resonance within the formal architecture. A familiar object, place, or remembered story can carry consequence: a house that changed hands after a betrayal, a route that once belonged to two people, a name that now sounds different. Let these details deepen the scene rather than pause it for exposition. The strange near ordinary life should remain a matter of how the viewpoint experiences familiar things under pressure.\n\nKeep the crime's practical stakes legible throughout. Show who stands to gain or lose, what evidence or obligation remains, and how relationships shift under the weight of the offense. Suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did it. The formal style should frame these stakes through social context and judgment, not replace them with abstraction.\n\nPreserve the chosen narrator's distance and knowledge. A narrator may judge, remember, or compare, but should not access minds excluded by the active contract. Ceremony, correspondence, and public/private contrast may organize a passage when relevant, but an aristocratic setting and uniformly elaborate speech are not automatically supplied. The ending may leave a social consequence or relationship unresolved even when a factual matter is settled.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadarah::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Neil Gaiman / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nBuild the scene as a live negotiation where the storyteller uses an embedded crime account to change what the listener is willing to do. Before the tale begins, establish the outer stakes: what the listener is there to collect, decide, forgive, or refuse, and what the teller needs from them. The storyteller may be an accomplice, a debtor, a witness, a fixer, or a person preparing the next move. Let the outer scene stay present through interruptions, questions, and silences, not only at the frame's edges.\n\nMake the listener an active index to the story's gaps. When the teller omits a contact, a decision, a sum, or a moment of violence, let the listener ask about it, misread it, accept it, or use it as leverage. An interruption can redirect the tale, force a changed version, or expose that the teller has prepared only one route through the story. Do not give the listener perfect perceptiveness; they seek what serves their interest, just as the teller withholds what serves theirs.\n\nTell the embedded account in direct, familiar prose, close to ordinary activity. A name, a repeated phrase, a key, a receipt, or a remembered promise should carry a second story behind the first. Use the Gaiman profile's narrative ease so the inner tale stays legible even when its teller is unreliable, and keep criminal stakes concrete: who paid, who was exposed, what remains owed, who can still act.\n\nLet consequence flow back from the tale into the frame. A listener may agree, refuse, threaten, or change the terms, but the change should derive from what the story made them understand or doubt about the teller. End within the frame's live movement: the next question, a shift in the room, or the teller's awareness that the story has already committed them. Do not require a confession, cliffhanger, or resolved debt.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadarai::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Neil Gaiman / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the documentary and historical-memory route. Keep record and memory as two live sources with different authority: a ledger, notice, file, photograph, or official summary states what its maker needed to record, while memory states what mattered to the person who lived it. Do not make one source automatically truer than the other. Move between them so the suspense comes from the distance and the points where they unexpectedly agree.\n\nLet the narrator compare the same moment twice. A familiar detail such as a key, a street name, a room, a receipt, or a repeated phrase can appear in a memory and again in a record, and the difference changes what the narrator understands. In Gaiman's direct telling, that detail should carry a second story behind it: ordinary activity becomes consequential because the narrator returns to it with later knowledge.\n\nMark the narrator's access to time. A first-person participant can distinguish what they knew then from what they learned later; a more distant narrator can quote documents and then show the human circumstances the documents omit. Keep observation, memory, report, and inference separable without turning the prose into a clinical case summary. The narrator's hesitation, loyalty, and feeling should remain part of the account.\n\nLet the crime's aftermath stay concrete and open. The records may show who was charged, transferred, fined, or erased; memory may show who was afraid, who was helped, or what debt was never settled. End with a comparison that revises the narrator's view of a familiar place or person, or leaves the two sources unreconciled. Do not require confession, punishment, or a completed investigation, and do not import one country's bureaucracy or translated word order.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaraj::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Neil Gaiman / Gothic-Punk\n\nHave the offense begin where an old arrangement still presses against present life: a family obligation, a building's history, a long-held debt, a private understanding that outlasted the institution that made it. Do not make a single villain alone carry the crime. Instead, show one character breaking a modern rule to honor an old one, or using an old privilege to escape a present consequence. Keep the incentives, execution, evidence, and aftermath legible.\n\nRender the urban setting through actual use and history. A room, stairwell, door, key, or piece of furniture can be worn, maintained, repurposed, or contested, and the narrator should know who still has access and why that access matters. Use Gaiman's direct telling so these details stay familiar and legible, then let them carry a second story: the place becomes unsettling because the narrator now understands what it cost someone, not because the prose adds generic gloom.\n\nLet intimacy alter the stakes without scripting betrayal. In trusted company a social mask may fall, but what follows depends on the particular relationship. A character may protect someone while lying to them, tell the truth and discover the listener already knew, or ask for help and receive none. Private desire, loyalty, and fear should control who acts and who withholds. Do not make every intimate moment a betrayal or every caring act fraudulent.\n\nKeep the old authority bounded by the active world. It may be a person, a custom, a property claim, or a remembered promise, but it has no supernatural force unless the world supplies one. The response may end with the character understanding the arrangement more clearly, a threat still present, a debt still unpaid, or a bargain newly broken. Do not require a completed pursuit or a moral resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadarak::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Neil Gaiman / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nLet the crime enter through an account that has already been told, retold, or remembered within a community. The narrator may be a participant, a relative, a neighbor, or someone who heard the story from another. Give the telling a present purpose: to explain a reputation, justify a decision, warn a listener, or correct a version that has become accepted. Show who is speaking to whom and what the telling is meant to make the listener do, believe, or forgive.\n\nKeep the narrative levels intelligible. Move between the outer scene and the embedded account without blurring who is speaking. The inner tale should follow the offense, scheme, or betrayal in clear, direct prose, close to familiar activity in Gaiman's manner. Let a name, place, promise, or object recur and change meaning as the listener learns more, so the repetition is an instrument of understanding rather than a chant.\n\nUse community memory to shape the crime's aftermath. What people say about the offense matters as much as what happened, and the tale may have different versions kept alive for different audiences. Let the outer scene test those versions. The narrator may discover that the familiar telling omitted a detail, that two accounts disagree about who acted first, or that the listener has already added the narrator to the story. Suspense can come from the listener's growing awareness of which version the teller needs to be true.\n\nLet the story end within the frame's social movement: the listener's changed answer, a question the community has not settled, or the teller's realization that the tale has already passed beyond their control. Punishment, confession, and completed investigation are not required. Do not force a particular language, religion, caste experience, or family structure onto the scene; use the setting, community, and period actually supplied.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaral::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Neil Gaiman / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the diary and reflective miscellany route. Have the narrator record what they noticed, felt, and feared at the time, then return to those entries with later knowledge. The account may be a literal diary, a set of dated notes, or a personal record composed after the fact. Keep a visible gap between the earlier entry and the later reflection, so a realization arrives at the moment of rereading or rethinking, not as a retroactive insertion into the earlier motive.\n\nMake the ordinary details active. A street, meal, phrase, habit, or small object can appear in an early entry and return later with new consequence. In Gaiman's direct telling, the familiar becomes strange only because the narrator now understands what it meant. Show that change through the narrator's particular attention: what caught their eye then, what they omitted, what they now wish they had written down. Seasonal details may appear where the actual setting and time support them, but do not impose a mandatory landscape or a universal quietness.\n\nLet the crime's suspense come from the narrator's changing grasp. They may have recorded a detail without understanding it, trusted a person they later suspect, or omitted a moment that now seems central. The reader can see the distance between then and now, but keep that distance humane. Let the narrator regret, doubt, or misunderstand rather than become a perfect retrospective analyst.\n\nKeep the aftermath concrete and open. The diary may record who left, who stayed, who was questioned, or what debt remained. The reflective passages can weigh these consequences without resolving them. End with the narrator rereading an old entry, noticing a detail they had forgotten, or deciding what to record next. Do not require confession, punishment, or a completed investigation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaram::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Neil Gaiman / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social realism, chronicle, and testimony route. Let the narrator be a situated witness, participant, or bystander who can distinguish what they personally saw or did from what they were told, overheard, or learned later. When a neighbor's report, a family story, or a public version enters the narrative, make its source clear. The suspense can come from the gap between the narrator's direct knowledge and the versions circulating around them.\n\nWeigh the circulating versions in the scene. Show who repeated a version, to whom, and for what purpose. A report may protect someone, shift blame, exaggerate a sum, or erase a victim's action. The narrator may repeat one version for a time, then test it against their own memory. This social mechanism belongs to the crime's aftermath: who can speak, who is watched, and what the offense costs, without turning the piece into a catalogue of rumors.\n\nKeep the telling direct and grounded in familiar activity. In Gaiman's manner, let a name, place, promise, or repeated object connect a personal memory to a reported event. The narrator's attention, loyalty, and uncertainty should remain part of the account. A detail such as a key, receipt, or phrase can become a touchstone for what the narrator can personally vouch for, while the reported material remains attributed and conditional.\n\nPreserve the specific language, community, and history actually supplied, without manufacturing a generic regional temperament or relocating the cast. The offense should be legible within the social conditions of the scene: who depends on whom, who can speak, who is watched, and what the crime costs. End with the narrator weighing a reported version against their own memory, or with a question they cannot yet answer. Do not require punishment, confession, or a completed investigation, and do not add supernatural facts to a world that excludes them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaran::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Neil Gaiman / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate criminal situation cleanly. Open with the viewpoint character already in the scene that matters: meeting a contact, watching a handoff, discovering a debt, or facing the person they have wronged. Establish who is present, where they are, and what the viewpoint character wants before the first exchange. Keep the prose fast and readable, with short paragraphs around action, dialogue, and private response.\n\nMake spatial changes easy to follow. When a character moves from a street to a room, from a car to a doorway, or from one side of a table to another, state the change plainly. Give unfamiliar names, roles, or mechanics clear presentation when they first appear, without pausing the scene for a long explanation. Use Gaiman's direct telling to make one ordinary object or familiar routine carry a second story: a key, a receipt, a repeated phrase, or a remembered bargain can acquire consequence as the scene develops, giving the quick container a specific focus rather than a stream of unweighted details.\n\nUse focused reaction beats to show the viewpoint character's private response to the crime. A reaction may be serious, uncertain, angry, or quietly calculating, according to its owner. It should arise from the character's actual stake in the offense, not from a compulsory quip or emotional display. Keep the difference between private thought and spoken response character-specific, and let the reaction beat reveal what the character noticed, feared, or decided without slowing the immediate action more than the moment warrants.\n\nKeep the crime's consequences concrete. The scene may end with a threat made, a bargain changed, a debt acknowledged, or a piece of evidence understood. The response can stop during continuing action, speech, thought, or an unfinished setup. Do not require a cliffhanger, a confession, or a completed pursuit. First person, comedy, and a young cast are not requirements of this container.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadarao::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Neil Gaiman / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite the crime through the material things and ordinary movements that carry its weight: a key left on a sill, a receipt folded too many times, the sound of a door being tested after the lock has been changed. Let the narrator notice these objects with lyrical attention, but keep each image traceable to the scene or to the teller's own associations. A comparison may unfold when it changes what the narrator understands about the offense, the culprit, or the person harmed. Do not let the prose drift into decorative atmosphere that replaces the practical sequence of preparation, execution, evidence, or aftermath.\n\nUse rhythm and recurring sounds to bind the account together. A phrase may return when a later event gives it a new application: the same description of a quiet street can mean safety at one moment and exposure at another. Plain statements should interrupt the figurative register so the crime's facts stay legible. The narrator's direct telling remains the spine; the lyricism is how that teller attends to what the crime has touched, not a separate ornamental layer.\n\nLet familiar activity acquire a second story behind it. A person counting change, locking a shop, or wiping a glass may be doing something else as well, and the narrator can sense that additional story without immediately naming it. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective. The account may end with the offense still unfolding, a consequence still settling, or a question the narrator has learned to ask, rather than with a completed explanation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadarap::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Neil Gaiman / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the crime appear through small irreversible changes: a room that no longer smells the same, a routine the victim kept that now belongs to no one, a phrase someone used before the offense and cannot use afterward. The narrator may reflect directly or let one detail carry the association, but the reflection should stay attached to the present account of preparation, pursuit, consequence, or survival. Ordinary pleasure can remain pleasurable; a shared meal or a working radio does not have to become a lesson in loss.\n\nUse remembered occasions to give the crime its human weight without turning the past into a sealed, sorrowful exhibit. A recollection may explain why a particular object matters, why a person hesitates, or why a divided loyalty hurts. Let the present scene interrupt or revise the memory. The narrator can be mid-errand, mid-conversation, or mid-decision when the association arrives, and the account continues from there.\n\nKeep the reflective register from making every scene end by announcing impermanence. Some scenes end with a task resumed, a question asked, or a consequence still developing. The crime's suspense can come from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did it. The narrator's sense of what is no longer available should sharpen attention to what is still being decided, not replace it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaraq::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Neil Gaiman / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed oral-storytelling route for the crime. Establish a situated teller and an occasion for the telling: someone recounts the offense, its preparation, or its aftermath to a listener who has a reason to hear it. The teller may be the culprit, an accomplice, a victim, an investigator, or a bystander. Keep the teller's access and knowledge consistent. The framing occasion can return between sections, and the listener's questions or interruptions may change what the teller chooses to reveal.\n\nLet delayed revelation do real work. The teller may withhold a name, a bargain, or a decisive moment until the listener has learned enough to feel its weight. Recurring phrases and remembered tales can acquire consequence where the scenario supports them, but do not import antiquity, mysticism, exile, religion, or a single honor code as universal scene requirements. The setting, language, period, and community actually supplied govern the telling.\n\nKeep the crime's practical sequence legible inside the oral frame. Evidence, divided loyalty, exposure, and consequence should remain concrete even when the teller's account is partial or self-interested. The listener need not be passive: a question can expose a contradiction, and a later section can show the teller revising an earlier claim. The account may end with the telling interrupted, the listener still uncertain, or the teller's purpose changed, rather than with a completed moral.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadarar::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Neil Gaiman / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect a small number of exact details and let them carry the crime's weight. A single object can stand for a longer preparation, a single phrase for a divided loyalty, a single mark for an aftermath. Use direct clauses and keep the practical sequence of offense, evidence, pursuit, or consequence inferable. Omission should leave something the reader can infer, not delete causality. If a step matters, show it in the same economical register rather than skipping it.\n\nKeep only the inward connection that materially contributes to the present account. A character may think one sentence about why they are doing this, or notice one detail that changes their understanding. Let an essential thought be long enough to make sense; do not replace it with several performed fragments. The narrator's direct telling should still suggest that an encounter carries another story behind it, but that second story remains in the selected details rather than in explanation.\n\nPreserve individual voices. A culprit, accomplice, victim, investigator, or bystander speaks and notices differently, and the minimal style should not flatten them into the same terse register. The account may end with a task unfinished, a consequence still settling, or a question left open, but the ending should feel like a precise stopping point rather than a clipped gesture.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaras::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Neil Gaiman / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the crime in sustained scene-first prose. Move naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience as the immediate scene requires. Let the narrator's voice and the author profile set the register and rhythm rather than imposing a clinical case-summary tone. The offense, its preparation, its incentives, and its aftermath should remain concrete even when the narration pauses to remember or reflect.\n\nUse paragraphing and transitions to orient the reader without recap machinery. A new scene can open in the middle of an errand, a conversation, or a decision, and the reader learns the necessary context through the character's attention rather than through a summary of what has happened so far. Names, bargains, paths, places, and remembered tales may acquire consequence where the scenario supports them, but do not import gods, ghosts, or a preordained outcome.\n\nKeep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective. A culprit, accomplice, victim, investigator, or bystander may carry the account, and suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did it. The scene may end with a task unfinished, a question unanswered, or a consequence still developing. Contemporary presentation does not require a contemporary setting, understated emotion, realism as world physics, or a lesson at the end of every reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadarat::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Neil Gaiman / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship premise legible through the crime's scenes. Choose moments where the offense, its preparation, its incentives, or its aftermath puts pressure on the relationship: a shared errand becomes dangerous, a private joke resurfaces during an interrogation, a familiar comfort is offered or refused after a discovery. Let affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery have enough space where they belong. Use only the actual supplied premise rather than inserting a roster of familiar tropes.\n\nDevelop private language and repeated moments as the relationship's particular promise. A phrase, a gesture, or a recurring place can return when the crime changes its meaning. The payoff should fulfill what the premise established, not merely restate it. Keep the teller's access consistent: one character's private thought does not grant knowledge of another's mind. Suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did it.\n\nLet the crime's practical sequence remain legible inside the relationship focus. Evidence, pursuit, and consequence should still happen; the emotional scenes should not replace the offense or its aftermath. The account may end with the factual question answered but the relationship consequence unsettled, or with a shared moment that does not resolve the larger danger. Do not force every loved one to betray, every confession to collapse, or every comforting room to become a trap.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadarau::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Neil Gaiman / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the crime as an account of deeds by which these people will be remembered. Open deliberately, with a compact declaration that names the offense or the person whose actions set the account in motion, then expand into the particulars that make the reputation incomplete. A bargain, a path, a place, or a remembered tale can acquire consequence where the scenario supports it. Keep the present scene's scale: a theft remains a theft, a betrayal remains a betrayal, and a killing remains a killing even when it belongs to a longer history.\n\nUse recurring phrasing to give the account the feel of retelling. A designation should come from an established deed or way of speaking, not from an ornamental epithet assigned to every entrance. Return to a phrase when a later occurrence gives it a new application: the same description of a locked door can mean caution at one moment and accusation at another. Relate each act to what will be remembered, but do not declare legend on the community's behalf before the consequences have settled.\n\nLet dialogue interrupt the authority of the chronicle. A participant may object to one detail, answer an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern, or remember the same offense differently. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective. The account may end with the offense still unfolding, a feud still open, or a modest act available to be remembered, rather than with a completed judgment or a preordained doom.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadarav::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Neil Gaiman / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the story through the social-realist route of a specific Scandinavian institutional practice: a caseworker’s assessment visit, a police preliminary report, or a workplace follow-up. Choose the practice from the actual setting and let it shape the rhythm of the narrative. Keep the viewpoint close to one participant's practical attention, but allow the report, interview, neighbor's remembered detail, or supervisor's decision to carry pressure equal to an open conflict. The offense should disturb a particular social arrangement, not exist as a free-floating event.\n\nUse Gaiman's direct telling to give that disturbance a second layer. Name the route walked twice, the object left in the hallway, or the small bargain made months before; return to it when the viewpoint encounters it again. If the encounter matters, show the character's changed attention rather than explaining it. Keep the significance within the actual crime scenario; do not add gods, ghosts, or a preordained meaning.\n\nDistinguish what the focal character knows or remembers from what the institutional record contains. The caseworker may notice that a witness's account fits a pattern, while the file records only the address and time. A relative may understand an old debt differently from the investigator. Preserve the active narrator's access; reveal private reasoning where authorized, and do not let another character know it unless the scene provides a reason.\n\nLet the aftermath remain human and incomplete. A charge may be filed, a relationship may continue under strain, or a workplace may silently change its procedures. The offense does not have to end in punishment or a solved puzzle. Its consequence can be a changed routine, a newly noticed detail, or a conversation that will not settle.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaraw::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Neil Gaiman / Screenplay\n\nLet the screenplay's visible continuity create the layered meaning. Choose one specific item, route, or small transaction established on screen and bring it back under a different practical condition: the same door checked twice, the same street passed at two different hours, or the same receipt handled by two characters. The return must be immediately visible or audible, not hidden in a character's unspoken memory. Write the earlier instance in concise, readable action lines so the later recurrence can be recognized without a flashback unless the scene truly needs one.\n\nShape the crime's pressure through characters' routines. A person checks a locked door before the offense matters; later someone else opens the same door without comment. The audience should register the difference through the action line, not through a character explaining it. Use cuts and offscreen sound only when they advance this visual accumulation. Do not fill the page with camera instructions.\n\nKeep required private experience as a clearly labeled nonspoken note in the active presentation. Character: name on its own line, then private thought or feeling in unmistakably non-dialogue text. Another character cannot hear it. Distinguish the audience's knowledge from the on-screen characters' knowledge and never let a character announce a private realization merely to inform the viewer.\n\nLet the crime's aftermath remain visible but incomplete. End a scene on a completed action, an unfinished task, or a familiar routine changed in a small observable way. The offense does not have to conclude with an arrest or a solved puzzle. Its consequence can be an object returned to a different place, a route walked at an unexpected hour, or a door left open without explanation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadarax::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Neil Gaiman / Stage Play\n\nUse the stage's physical business as the carrier of layered significance. Choose one concrete object that can be placed, moved, handled, or ignored: a jacket, a mug, a set of keys. Write its first appearance in the stage directions as ordinary action—a character hangs up the jacket, puts down the mug, takes the keys. Later, let another character perform a slightly different action with the same object: leave it on the wrong hook, turn the mug so the handle faces the wall, hold the keys without using them. The public scene should make the difference observable and meaningful without anyone explaining it.\n\nLet entrances and exits shape suspense. A character may enter just as another exits with the object, or pause before taking it. Use blocking to create misalignment between what is said and what is done. If a character avoids a question, they need not freeze; they can continue tidying the room, but the specific task should relate to the offense or its aftermath.\n\nPlace required private experience as nonspoken authoring material within the active presentation. Use a clearly delimited notation that is not a performed aside, monologue, or audible line, and that another character cannot hear. If an aside or monologue is used, assign it a specific dramatic purpose—confession to the audience, contrast with public speech, or a shift in the scene's reliability—and keep it separate from this hidden material.\n\nEnd scenes on stageable movement rather than a completed explanation. A character can exit with the wrong object, enter with a changed routine, or sit down while a partner keeps talking. The crime does not have to produce a confession, arrest, or theatrical revelation. Its consequence can be a newly noticed physical difference, a silence that changes the next entrance, or a task left deliberately unfinished.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaray::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Neil Gaiman / Stream of Consciousness\n\nAnchor the stream in one recurring house detail the focal mind keeps returning to: the sound a certain door makes, the position of a photograph, or the weight of a familiar key. The first mention should occur in present perception, not as a declaration of importance. Later, when the mind drifts, bring the same detail back through a different sense or remembered condition—the door sound becomes a memory that wakes the character, the key feels heavier after new information. The crime enters not as a summary of evidence but through these changing repetitions.\n\nLet Gaiman's layered significance arise inside that associational return. A small bargain, a route walked twice, or a remembered phrase may surface because the mind anchors it to the recurring detail. Show the character's attention shifting or sharpening on that detail; do not allow an external narrator to explain its meaning. If the world has no supernatural facts, keep the memory of the door, key, or photograph materially caused by the offense's real circumstances.\n\nMaintain the authorized mind's limits. The focal character may mishear the door as a knock, mistake the key for another person's, or remember the photograph from before the crime. A correction may come from another sense, a fresh perception, or a delayed recognition, but another character's private thought cannot appear. When private reasoning permits, let it accompany action or continue without an immediate decision.\n\nLet the response stop inside the mind's movement. The character need not solve the offense, confess, or finish the thought. A recurring detail can become a changed feeling about the house, a burden carried into the next room, or an unanswered question that keeps the dream of the door present. Avoid closing with a completed interpretation unless the story's actual circumstances call for one.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaraz::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Neil Gaiman / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nLet each sender's habitual message patterns carry the layered significance. Establish early that one character always sends a particular question about a shared errand, route, or practical arrangement. Later, the same question recurs with a one-word change, a missing word, a changed timestamp, or an attachment that was not sent before. The recurrence itself is the Gaiman-style meaning; do not explain it in a separate narrative paragraph.\n\nUse interface events sparingly and only when the shift matters. A delayed reply may be work, reluctance, or something else; show it through the pace of messages already exchanged, not by inventing a new reading receipt. If a sender edits a message before sending, make the edit itself relevant to the crime—changing the name of a place, deleting a time, or replacing one instruction with another. Keep both the unsent draft and the sent version distinct in the transcript: a draft is a composed artifact, not the writer's live private thought.\n\nPlace any required private experience in a clearly separated channel outside the exchange, labeled with the character's name. It is not a message, not visible to the other senders, and not automatically readable as a reply. If context is needed to interpret a delay or a terse reply, provide it in the same separated format rather than pretending the transcript's time stamp explains it.\n\nLet the exchange end on a sent message, a pending reply, or an unsent draft that the reader can see but the other participant cannot. The crime's consequence may be a small practical alteration in the message habits—one sender no longer asks the recurring question, or asks it at a different time—rather than a confession or a solved puzzle.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadarba::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Neil Gaiman / Visual Novel\n\nChoose one background object that appears in the scene description from the start: a coat on a chair, a phone on a table, a window reflection. Establish it as an ordinary part of the setting without drawing special attention. In a later scene, return to the same object but have the focal character notice a specific change—the coat is folded now, the phone is turned face down, the window reflection shows a different person behind. Write that change as part of the focal narration, not as an expression cue or a dialogue announcement.\n\nLet scene changes make the layered significance legible. Use a concise scene-setting line that names the place and time, then return to the object through the focal character's attention. A dialogue exchange may occur in the same scene, but the crime's pressure should also be visible in how the character registers the altered object. If a character comments on it, the comment must have a practical, in-world reason rather than serving as explanation for the reader.\n\nKeep private thought outside other characters' knowledge. In the focal narration, write inner response in the active nonspoken form; another character cannot perceive it. A reaction expression or a pause may accompany the line, but no one else knows the thought unless the scene gives them a reason. Distinguish the reader's access to focal narration from the visible knowledge of the other characters.\n\nEnd a scene on a completed task, a pending question, or the character's last glance at the changed object. The crime's aftermath may remain present as that changed object, without a confession or arrest. Do not activate branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, or engine commands. The style remains scene-oriented presentation, not a game interface.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadarbb::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Neil Gaiman / Web Serial\n\nGive the serial a single recurring practical detail that can change its meaning without requiring a new clue each installment. In the first installment, the focal character performs a small ordinary action—locks a door, returns a borrowed key, parks in a particular space. Recover that detail briefly when a later installment returns to the same place or task. Let the new context show why it matters: the door is opened by someone else, the key is left on a different shelf, the parking space is now occupied by the victim's car. The shift is earned by the new information, not by a forced reversal.\n\nUse installments to develop knowledge gradually. A character may complete one piece of work, receive a partial answer, or notice that a familiar routine has changed. Do not require a confession or arrest at the close. If a pending request remains unanswered, let the installment end on the character's next ordinary action—another attempt, a postponed errand, a conversation that clarifies one fact while leaving another open.\n\nKeep required inward experience in the reply and hidden or visible as the active contract dictates. Show the character's changing feeling about the recurring detail: they may hesitate at the door, hold the key longer, or avoid the parking space. Private thought can accompany these actions without producing an immediate decision. The serial's through-line remains the recurring detail plus the crime's consequence, not an escalating schedule of revelations.\n\nUse hooks only when they arise from the installment's actual state. If the installment ends with the character noticing the open door, let that observation be enough; do not add an artificial line to force a return. The crime does not have to conclude with an arrest or a solved puzzle. Its aftermath can remain a changed routine, a noticed detail, or an unfinished conversation that future installments may continue.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadasaa::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite from inside the narrator's sustained attention to what the offense has already cost and what a particular relationship still demands. Let the narrator register the concrete present — who is in the room, what is on the table, what has been left undone — and qualify or correct an earlier impression as the weight becomes harder to hold off. Direct feeling belongs in the account, arriving through the narrator's own diction: a specific memory, a practical worry, a comparison the narrator cannot quite finish.\n\nLet strain alter the actual conversation rather than decorate it. A request may come out sharper than intended; an apology may be offered and then undercut by a defensive explanation; a gesture of warmth may be accepted awkwardly because the narrator is still counting what staying will cost. Keep the spoken reply short of the full private thought when the narrator has a reason to hold back — embarrassment, utility, habit — and let that gap produce the next beat of behavior instead of a summary of suffering.\n\nKeep the criminal work and the emotional weather in one motion. Divided loyalty decides what the narrator is willing to check; a withheld admission narrows what an accomplice can plan; a difficult return changes who can be trusted with a task. Do not stop the scheme for a feelings scene and then resume it unchanged — the cost of needing someone should show in the narrator's choices about evidence, money, access, or silence.\n\nComfort may be partial and awkward, coexisting with the knowledge that it repairs nothing. Preserve the narrator's fallible judgment: a motive misread, a danger overestimated, a reassurance recognized too late as also a warning. Stop where the narrator's present attention actually rests — mid-errand, mid-reckoning, even mid-exchange — without requiring a confession, a reconciliation, or a decisive turn in the case.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
        },
        {
            "role": "system",
            "injection_position": 0,
