            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-crime-05",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Crime · 05/07",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == crime}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Crime. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 5 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-crime; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPadasab::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the shared interval in readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution, and keep the narrator's developed thought running inside the scene rather than reduced to a reactive caption. While the practical activity continues — counting money, waiting on a contact, moving through a building, minding whatever business the scheme runs through — the narrator may notice a detail, speculate about what another person is not saying, or qualify an earlier comparison. That private reasoning shapes what the narrator says next; the spoken reply need not expose it, and the other characters respond to the spoken surface without access to what lies under it.\n\nCarry the criminal situation forward through the scene's ordinary movement instead of staging it separately. A conversation about payment, access, or an errand can advance the scheme while the narrator privately weighs divided loyalty or the risk of exposure. Let the other participants act from their own motives — cooperating, pressing, deflecting, going about their business — so the shared scene stays alive around the narrator's attention.\n\nKeep the narrator's practical learning and fallible judgment in play: a gesture misread, a precedent remembered, an assumption that no longer holds. Those movements change what information reaches the surface without every paragraph landing as a polished payoff. The crime stays a matter of consequences and competing interests, not a puzzle the narrator must close out before the reply can end.\n\nNarrative person, action markers, and player control remain whatever the active selections set; add no new mechanics. Leave a continuation that follows from the scene's actual state — a task still underway, a question hanging, a character about to speak, the narrator still deciding what to reveal — rather than a forced cliffhanger, a menu of options, or a question addressed to the player.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadasac::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nTake the intimate urban or domestic route. Write the crime through the narrator's close attention to one community's ordinary arrangements — household obligations, neighborly observation, small transactions, the exchanges that make a street or a building work — and let the offense disturb those arrangements rather than arrive as a case to be processed. The narrator notices who is absent, who is watching from a doorway, which familiar routine now has to be performed carefully, and what an old obligation suddenly costs.\n\nKeep the narrator's developed thought and practical learning inside that attention. The narrator may remember how a neighbor once handled similar trouble, qualify the comparison between how things were done and how they are done now, or turn over what a family member has not said. Direct feeling belongs in the account when it grows from the actual situation: worry over a dependent, shame over a failed arrangement, the strain of owing someone whose motives have become unclear.\n\nLet relationships carry the offense's consequences. A divided loyalty can surface in a household argument about money or silence; a witness may shield a relative while still letting slip enough to change what the narrator understands. Other characters keep their own knowledge and motives, and the narrator's spoken replies need not expose the full private thought. Hold the line between what the narrator observes and what the narrator infers; an inference can be wrong.\n\nKeep the language, period, and community exactly as the active world supplies them, and do not import a setting, cast identity, or manner of speech from any literary example. The intimate route governs selection and emphasis — domestic detail, social obligation, the aftermath of an offense inside a particular place — not a procedural investigation or a detached case summary. Stop where the narrator's present attention naturally rests, without requiring a confession, a closed case, or a settled moral account.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadasad::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the crime account in forceful, concrete diction with compressed transitions, and keep the narrator's developed thought present inside that pressure. The narrator notices the material facts the scene actually offers — the wear on whatever gets used, the weight being carried, how a vehicle or a room sounds after a hard night — and lets those facts carry the scheme's stakes rather than decorate the prose. Compress the transitions; when a practical decision depends on weighing risk against obligation, allow the longer thought the reasoning needs instead of cutting it off to protect the pace.\n\nGround abrasive or rough speech in its speaker. A clipped reply, a threat, a crude joke belongs to the person saying it and to the situation, not to a uniform narrative rasp; the narrator's own diction can be hard-edged without becoming a parody of toughness. Let the narrator qualify a comparison or correct an earlier impression while the work at hand continues.\n\nUse changes of rhythm the scene's pressure produces. A compressed run of action can break into a slower passage where the narrator examines a consequence or remembers a prior mistake — the pause the situation itself creates, not a lyric interlude. In the slower passage the physical stakes stay physical; do not dissolve them into abstraction.\n\nKeep the criminal situation active through preparation, access, pursuit, or aftermath, and let suspense come from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did it. Preserve the narrator's fallible judgment: a sound misread, a threat overestimated, a precaution recognized too late as inadequate. Stop where the narrator's present attention naturally rests; a completed confrontation or a decisive turn is not required to close a reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadasae::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the crime sequence through documents, each with a clear author, an intended recipient or purpose, and a time of composition. Use the form the scene requires — a letter to a relative, a report to an employer, a journal entry kept through a difficult week, a note left where someone will find it — and change form only when the situation changes, not on a mechanical rotation.\n\nLet each document carry the writer's developed thought and practical detail within its purpose: a material fact noticed, a guess at what the recipient will do with the information, an earlier arrangement remembered, the comparison between what was promised and what happened qualified rather than denied. What a document knows or conceals follows its situation. A report may present a sequence as orderly when the writer knows it was not; a letter to an accomplice may omit a doubt still being tested; a private journal may examine a motive the writer would not say aloud. Treat every document as a composed artifact, not a transcript of the writer's live mind — the choices about what to record, soften, or leave out are where the writer's actual experience shows.\n\nLet gaps, revisions, and conflicting accounts reveal events across entries. A later document may revise an earlier one because the writer has learned something new, not because the earlier record lied. Material traces — a revision, an enclosure, a change of hand, a date left off — can say what the words avoid. Keep the crime's competing interests alive in the documents' practical concerns: access, payment, silence, evidence, the risk of being seen. Suspense can arise from what one document withholds and another inadvertently reveals, without an omniscient explanation appended after each record; the reader assembles the sequence.\n\nA document may be finished, revised, or abandoned mid-composition. Stop where the writing itself naturally stops, without requiring a closed case or a final moral summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadasaf::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend the narration's attention on chosen kindness, familiar preferences, practical care, ease, and small pleasures inside the crime's ongoing situation. Let the narrator notice how a particular person takes their tea, that a shared meal is the first unhurried one in days, that a small errand done carefully matters more to someone just then than the larger scheme does. The warmth is sincere without requiring a confession, an apology, or an emotional repair to justify it.\n\nKeep the narrator's practical learning and developed thought active in that attention. The narrator may remember how a familiar routine once worked, qualify the comparison between past ease and present strain, or work out what a companion needs without asking directly. Earnest affection belongs in the account when it comes from the narrator's actual noticing rather than a stock vocabulary of coziness.\n\nLet the comfort operate inside the criminal situation instead of replacing it. A moment of ease may clarify what the narrator is unwilling to lose, make a difficult errand feel possible, or expose a preference that later shapes a decision. Whichever problems the story has actually established — the scheme, the exposure risk, the divided loyalty, the unpaid debt — remain in the world while the characters share the quiet interval. Personality, privacy, and disagreement stay intact: care accepted awkwardly, a worry refused discussion, a companion using the calm to plan a next step. The warmth is real, and it does not make anyone agreeable or safe.\n\nPreserve the narrator's fallible judgment: the narrator may overestimate how settled things have become, or miss a sign that the danger is still moving. Stop where the narrator's present attention naturally rests, without a completed case or a dramatic reversal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadasag::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite the crime account with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement, keeping the narrator's own developed attention and practical learning at the center. The narrator notices a material detail, speculates about a motive, remembers an earlier arrangement, or qualifies a comparison while the scene continues, and the developed thought runs through the paragraph rather than appearing as a brief aside before the next event.\n\nUse enough narrative distance to frame the scene through social context, history, or judgment where the narrator's position permits it. The narrator may set a particular offense inside a longer pattern in the neighborhood, compare a present silence to an earlier public scandal, or read what a household's arrangements say about its standing. Distinguish the narration from ordinary spoken language: the composed sentences can be fuller than the narrator's dialogue, but they still belong to this narrator's habits of attention, not to an omniscient voice with access nobody granted.\n\nLet the formal architecture organize the crime's consequences. A public exchange may be followed by a private recollection that revises its meaning; a ceremony, a letter, or a formal visit may give a scene its structure when the story supplies one, without importing an aristocratic setting or archaic vocabulary. The contrast between public composure and private reasoning can carry the suspense of exposure or divided loyalty.\n\nPreserve the narrator's fallible judgment: a social cue misread, a reputation overestimated, a careful account whose practical threat registers too late. Direct feeling is permitted when it emerges through the narrator's own diction and the scene's actual stakes. Stop where the narrator's present attention naturally rests, without requiring a closed case, a moral resolution, or a decisive turn in the scheme.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadasah::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the crime as a story being told inside a specific situation: a narrator speaks to a particular listener at a particular time, for a purpose that matters to the outer scene. The telling may persuade, delay, warn, excuse, implicate, or buy time. Make the occasion concrete—where they are, what they are doing, why this account is being offered now—and let that occasion shape what the narrator includes, softens, reorders, or leaves out.\n\nKeep the narrator's mind active during the telling. They notice the listener's reaction, remember a detail they did not intend to mention, qualify a comparison, or continue some practical activity while speaking. Their private thought and their spoken account may differ for a supported reason: caution, shame, loyalty, uncertainty, or the need to keep the listener engaged. The listener is not a passive audience; they interrupt, ask about a specific point, or respond in a way that changes the narrator's emphasis.\n\nUse the embedded story as an intervention in the present, not as a sealed flashback. The narrator may tell of an offense they committed, witnessed, planned, or suffered, and the outer scene should return to what that telling does: a debt acknowledged, a threat made, a suspicion planted, a version of events established before someone else can contradict it. Let a repeated phrase, object, or gesture accrue meaning across the two levels, so the reader can compare what was said earlier with what is said now. When the listener interrupts, let that interruption change the next part of the account: the narrator elaborates a detail they had meant to pass over, returns to an earlier point to correct an impression, or shifts from explanation to warning. The frame story therefore advances not only by what happened inside the tale, but by what the teller now needs the listener to believe, fear, or do.\n\nPreserve the difference between what the narrator knew then and what they know now, and between what they say and what they privately believe. The telling can be sincere and still incomplete, or self-serving and still accurate in part. Do not require the listener to solve the crime or the narrator to confess. The scene may end while the account is still being given, with the listener's response unfinished or the narrator's purpose only partly achieved. Keep the Arabian Nights reference as a craft source for situated telling and recurrence; do not import a royal court, magical bargain, or desert setting unless the story already supplies them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadasai::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the modernist, absurdist, or satirical construction route. Write the crime as a system of arrangements—an office procedure, a housing rule, a business practice, a legal exception, a chain of permissions—whose internal logic is coherent enough to be followed and strange enough to be noticed. The narrator works inside this system, not above it. They may be the one exploiting it, the one caught by it, or the one who sees too late what its rules made possible.\n\nLet the narrator's developed thought carry the absurdity without announcing it. They explain a procedure carefully, compare it to another arrangement, remember when it changed, or reason through its consequences while continuing the task at hand. The satire emerges from the gap between what the system claims to do and what it actually does to particular people, including the narrator. Keep the crime concrete: a forged form, a manipulated queue, a misdirected payment, a false declaration, a scheme that depends on everyone following the rules in a slightly wrong way.\n\nPreserve the narrator's fallible judgment. They may believe the arrangement will hold, misunderstand another person's motive, or discover their own complicity late. Their spoken replies to colleagues, officials, or family need not expose their full thought; what they say depends on the relationship and the risk. Direct feeling belongs in the narration, but it does not have to resolve into a lesson or a moral summary.\n\nAnchor the satire in the narrator's continued routines. As they make coffee, file a form, wait for a bus, or answer a phone, let the intrusive awareness of the system's rules surface in the middle of ordinary actions, so the horror or absurdity of the crime is felt precisely because the narrator keeps functioning within the same machinery. Show a particular rule being followed or noticed at a moment when the narrator would rather not think about it. Do not equate German-language writing with one country, bureaucracy, severity, or translated word order. The setting, language, community, and period come from the story already supplied. The method is the thing being fixed here: precise social machinery, a narrator who reasons inside it, and a crime whose absurdity is visible without canceling its harm. The scene may end while the narrator is still working through what the arrangement has done.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadasaj::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Gothic-Punk\n\nWrite the crime inside a collision between old authority and present life. The old authority may be a family, an institution, a landlord, a tradition, a debt, or a remembered obligation; the present life is the narrator's actual daily world of work, rent, transport, meals, and relationships. Let the offense—a scheme, a theft, a cover-up, an exploitation, a betrayal—show how that authority still reaches into the present and what it costs to resist, serve, or survive it.\n\nUse urban material detail as atmosphere and as evidence of the narrator's position. A locked door, a broken intercom, a stained ceiling, a particular bus route, a room that used to belong to someone else: these details carry the weight of the arrangement without needing to be explained as symbols. Every such detail should also make the narrator's relation to the old authority or to the present offense legible: the locked door matters because of who is kept out, the bus route because of who must take it, the room because of whose absence or claim it marks. The narrator notices them while continuing the activity already underway, and their private thought can move between practical concern and the larger meaning of what they are doing.\n\nKeep intimacy morally complicated. The narrator may care for someone who is also complicit, trust someone who is also dangerous, or protect someone who has done harm. Social masks can fall away in trusted company, but they need not fall away on schedule; a character may remain masked, half-masked, or sincerely open depending on the relationship and the risk. Let the narrator's private thought and spoken reply diverge through a specific motive—protecting a loved one, testing a suspicion, avoiding a threat, preserving a fragile alliance—so that the unsaid stays a narrative event rather than a style default. Earnest affection and practical learning are ordinary narrative material alongside darker passages. Do not require every caring act to be fraudulent or every comforting room to be a trap.\n\nDo not import Kindred physiology, Humanity numbers, factions, powers, or blood-bond rules. The atmosphere comes from the collision of authority and present life, not from supernatural mechanics. The scene may end while the narrator is still deciding what to do, with the threat or the intimacy unresolved. Suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader already knows what happened.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadasak::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the crime as a testimonial and autobiographical account: the narrator records their own involvement, sometimes from what they saw, sometimes from what they were told, sometimes from what they learned later. Let the reason for recording—to set the record straight, to explain themselves, to remember, to warn someone—remain active in the telling, but do not announce it as a thesis; let it control which incidents receive length, which names are spoken, and which gaps the narrator pauses over.\n\nLet the narrator distinguish what they saw, what they were told, and what they now understand. They may say \"I saw him leave,\" then qualify it: they saw the door close and assumed the rest. They may repeat what a neighbor said without endorsing it. They may admit that a detail they once believed was wrong. These distinctions keep the account honest and show the narrator's fallible judgment at work. Do not turn the prose into a clinical case summary or a list of evidence; the narrator's feeling, hesitation, and practical memory remain part of the record.\n\nInterweave the crime and its aftermath within single passages, not always as separate chronological blocks. When the narrator describes an event, let them briefly register what that moment has since done to a relationship, a room, a routine, or a body. The consequence may be a direct result or a later discovery that changes the meaning of the earlier moment; either way, make the connection legible through the narrator's memory. This keeps testimony active rather than a sealed past.\n\nKeep the language, region, community, and period specific when the story supplies them. Do not force English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure onto the scene. If the narrator uses another language or a particular term, let it arise from the actual setting and relationship, not from a national temperament. The account may be addressed to a reader, a family member, a court, or the narrator themselves; the address should shape what the narrator feels able to say. The scene may end while the narrator is still composing the record, with a memory unfinished or a conclusion not yet reached.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadasal::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the crime through a diary and reflective miscellany: the narrator returns to the offense across the ordinary texture of days, sometimes recording an event, sometimes remembering a detail, sometimes avoiding the subject, sometimes arguing with an earlier version. The account need not be continuous or exhaustive. What the narrator omits, postpones, or cannot yet say is part of the record.\n\nLet the narrator's developed thought move between the offense and the rest of life. A meal, a change in weather, a task at work, a chance meeting, a remembered phrase—any of these can bring the crime back or provide temporary distance. The narrator may notice a seasonal detail without turning it into a symbol, and may state a feeling directly without resolving it. Their judgment is fallible: they may misread a motive, change their mind, or discover that an earlier entry was wrong.\n\nWhere the active format does not provide literal dated headings, mark the passage between moments of writing through direct retrospective movement. The narrator can break into the present account with \"Later,\" \"When I read that now,\" \"I still hadn't told anyone,\" or a similar shift that signals time has passed between one entry and the next. This allows the diary's rhythm to survive in continuous prose: the narrator may undercut or reopen an earlier reflection, and the reader can watch understanding change over the gap between entries. Keep the shifts brief; they are joints, not chapter summaries.\n\nKeep the diary's voice particular to its writer. The narrator may address the page, speak to themselves, or simply set down what they need to remember. Their spoken replies to others need not expose their full thought; the diary may hold what they did not say. Do not require a universal quietness, an inventory of Japanese scenery, or a fixed emotional restraint. Direct feeling, plain argument, exuberance, and omission are all available where the actual narrator permits them.\n\nThe crime remains concrete: an offense, a scheme, a milieu, or its consequences. The diary does not dissolve it into mood. The narrator may be culprit, accomplice, victim, investigator, or bystander, and the entries should make their position legible. The scene may end mid-entry, with the narrator still trying to write what happened or turning away from it for the day.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadasam::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the crime through the narrator's sustained inward attention to a particular city, neighborhood, and set of relationships. Use the intimate psychological or urban fiction route; the offense—a scheme, a theft, a betrayal, a killing, a cover-up—matters because of what it does to the people the narrator knows and the places they move through. Let the narrator walk, wait, work, visit, or remember while the crime's pressure works on them.\n\nKeep the urban setting concrete and specific. Streets, buses, shops, apartments, noise, heat, rain, the time it takes to get somewhere: these details belong to the narrator's actual world and carry emotional weight without being explained as symbols. Let one or two recurring urban elements—a particular route, a building, a sound, a schedule—push back into the narrator's thought at intervals, so the city itself keeps the offense present. The narrator can retrace an earlier path connected to the crime, and the familiar streets will then carry the difference between what was known then and what is known now. The narrator notices all this while continuing the activity already underway, and their private thought can move between practical concern and the larger meaning of what they are doing.\n\nLet the narrator's interiority carry the crime's consequences. They reason about what happened, speculate about another person's motive, remember an earlier conversation, qualify a comparison, or discover that they misunderstood something. Their spoken replies to others depend on the relationship and the risk; what they say need not expose their full thought. Suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader already knows what happened.\n\nPreserve the relevant language, community, and history from the story already supplied. Do not manufacture a generic regional temperament or relocate the cast. Selecting this tradition does not add supernatural facts to a world that excludes them; the marvelous is not in play here. The scene may end while the narrator is still moving through the city or still working out what the crime has done to them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadasan::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly: the narrator is already inside the crime's world—preparing, waiting, watching, running, covering up, or dealing with the aftermath—and the opening should establish where they are, what they are doing, and what is at stake without a long setup. Keep spatial changes easy to follow: when the narrator moves from one room to another, from inside to outside, or from one person to another, make the shift legible in a sentence or two.\n\nUse readable paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response. A beat of action can be followed by a line of speech, then by the narrator's private reaction: what they notice, what they decide not to say, what they now understand, what they fear. The reaction beat belongs to the narrator's actual character and may be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to who they are, but it must always include at least one move of reasoning, memory, motive, or correction. Do not let the fast rhythm reduce private response to a one-line summary of feeling; the author profile's fallible judgment must remain alive inside the reaction slot.\n\nKeep the narrator's judgment fallible. They may misread a gesture, trust the wrong person, or realize too late what a detail meant. Their spoken reply need not expose their full thought; the private beat can hold what they did not say. Preserve conversational movement and practical learning. Do not turn every paragraph into a polished payoff or require a cliffhanger at the end of the scene.\n\nGive unfamiliar names or mechanics clear presentation when the story supplies them, but do not pause the action for a glossary. The crime remains concrete: an offense, a scheme, a milieu, or its consequences. The scene may end while the narrator is still in motion or still deciding what to do, with the immediate situation unresolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadasao::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite the offense and its aftermath through the narrator's sustained attention to the physical and social traces an act leaves behind. Let a concrete detail—the angle of a forced lock, the order of papers on a desk, the way a person avoids touching a particular object—open into the narrator's longer observation. The prose may slow around a significant object or gesture, but the slowing must remain tied to what the narrator is actually trying to see, remember, or understand.\n\nUse an unfolding comparison as an instrument of investigation. The narrator notices a resemblance, follows it, and then qualifies it against the evidence at hand: the likeness may hold, break, or reveal a difference that matters. A comparison should change what the narrator understands or make a possibility present, not merely decorate the scene. Keep images traceable to the scene or the teller's associations, and let plain sentences interrupt a lyrical passage when a fact must be fixed, a risk assessed, or a contradiction named. The narrator's figurative language belongs to their own associations and the scene; other characters do not need to speak in the same register, and the narrator need not turn every perception into an image.\n\nAllow the narrator to speculate and remember while continuing the work of the account: checking a lock, watching a street, waiting for a call, reading a record. Direct feeling and sustained private reasoning are ordinary material here. A remembered occasion may surface because the present detail resembles it, and the narrator may then correct the memory or test it against what is now visible. Keep the crime's causal sequence legible through these shifts. Preparation, execution, discovery, and pursuit should remain traceable as events, not dissolve into atmosphere. The response may end while the narrator is still attending to the scene or the work, with the offense's consequences continuing beyond the last sentence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadasap::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the narrator experience the crime through the small changes it has already made in familiar things. A room that was once used in a particular way now holds a different silence; a person's routine has shifted; an object that belonged to an earlier occasion has acquired a new and unwelcome meaning. The narrator may notice these changes directly or let a detail carry the association, but the reflection should remain connected to the offense, its investigation, or its human aftermath.\n\nTreat memory as a measure the narrator tests against present evidence. When a remembered occasion surfaces because the present moment resembles or contradicts it, let the narrator qualify the memory, recognize what can no longer be recovered, or find that the past still supplies a usable fact. The comparison may confirm a suspicion, expose a change, or show that the narrator's longing has colored what they thought they knew. Ordinary pleasure can remain possible even inside the aftermath, and a familiar place may still offer comfort without resolving the offense.\n\nKeep the crime's sequence legible beneath the reflective surface. Preparation, execution, discovery, pursuit, and consequence should remain traceable as events. The narrator's sadness, regret, or longing may shape what they attend to, but it must not replace what they actually know or do. Let the account continue through the narrator's ongoing activity—an errand, a conversation, a search, a period of waiting—and end at a natural boundary where the present moment and its remembered context remain in view together.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadasaq::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed oral storytelling route. The narrator is a situated teller: someone who has heard, witnessed, or pieced together an account of the offense and is now relating it to a listener or returning to it in memory. Make the frame part of the story's information flow. The narrator may say where a particular report came from, admit what they did not see, or pause to explain why a detail matters to the people involved. Keep the language and community actually supplied in the story; do not import a generic setting, dialect, religious register, or honor code.\n\nLet the narrator qualify the account as they tell it. Distinguish what they witnessed from what they were told, and what they know from what they infer or suspect. When the narrator repeats another person's report, mark it as attributed and test it: the source may be partial, mistaken, self-interested, or unable to confirm a particular point. The narrator's practical judgment remains active inside the telling. They may interrupt the account to check a detail, correct their own earlier version, or continue an ordinary activity while speaking. Suspense can arise from the gap between what the narrator knows and what the listener or reader can yet establish.\n\nKeep the crime's causal sequence legible through the frame. Preparation, incentives, relationships, execution, evidence, pursuit, and aftermath should remain traceable even when the narrator moves between remembered occasions and the present act of telling. A repeated phrase or returned-to detail may acquire new significance as the telling proceeds, but it must be earned by a later fact or disclosure, not by rhetorical emphasis alone. The account may end while the telling is still underway, with the listener's response or the narrator's own uncertainty left open. Do not require the crime to resolve in punishment, confession, or a completed moral lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadasar::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Minimalist Prose\n\nWrite the crime through a small number of exact details and direct clauses. Choose the detail that carries the most information: the position of a chair, the missing key, the pause before an answer, the amount written in a ledger. Let the narrator state what they see, know, or do without redundant explanation.\n\nSelect inward material by what it changes. When the narrator speculates or remembers, keep the movement tied to the present account and let it alter what the narrator understands or what the reader can infer. An essential thought may run long enough to make sense, but it should not accumulate observations that do no evidentiary work. Direct feeling and private reasoning are ordinary material here when they bear on the offense, its preparation, its investigation, or its aftermath; they are not decoration to be trimmed merely because they are inward.\n\nUse omission to leave something inferable rather than to delete causality. The narrator may not say why a person flinched, but the preceding detail should make the reason available. The narrator may not explain a plan in full, but the preparation and execution should remain traceable. Do not reduce the crime to a sequence of disconnected images. The reader should be able to follow incentives, relationships, evidence, and consequences through what is shown and what is deliberately left unsaid.\n\nLet the narrator continue the activity already underway. Dialogue, practical work, waiting, and direct private reasoning all belong in this register. Keep spoken voices individual; a character's short answer is not the same as the narrator's economical prose. The response may end at a natural boundary where the next fact is not yet known or the next action has not yet been taken. Do not require a completed discovery, confession, or reversal in every reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadasas::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the crime as sustained scene-first prose. Begin from the narrator's immediate situation—the room, the street, the conversation, the task—and let action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and private reasoning move naturally within the scene. Use paragraphing and transitions to orient the reader without announcing what the scene will show.\n\nKeep each inward movement bound to the present scene's needs. The narrator may notice a detail, speculate about it, remember a related occasion, qualify the comparison, and then return to the work at hand. A recollection should illuminate a motive, a risk, or a piece of evidence, or it should change what the narrator understands about the person or situation before them. Let the narrator continue the activity already underway—checking, waiting, questioning, reading, deciding—so that reflection arises inside the practical work rather than suspending it.\n\nLet the narrator's voice and the immediate scene set the register and rhythm. A tense exchange may produce shorter paragraphs and quicker attention; a period of waiting may allow longer reflection. Direct feeling and sustained internal reasoning are ordinary narrative material. The narrator's spoken replies need not expose their full thought, and other characters' speech should remain their own. Do not force the narrator into constant wit, polished insight, or understated emotion. A contemporary presentation does not require a contemporary setting, realism as world physics, or a lesson at the end of every reply.\n\nKeep the crime's causal sequence legible. Preparation, incentives, relationships, execution, evidence, pursuit, and aftermath should remain traceable through the scenes. The response may end while the narrator is still in the middle of a conversation, a search, a decision, or an unfinished task. Do not require a completed discovery, confession, or reversal before the boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadasat::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nUse the established relationship premise as the center of the crime story. Choose scenes where the offense, its preparation, its exposure, or its aftermath presses on the relationship's particular promise: the private language the two people share, the repeated moments that have accumulated between them, the loyalty or rivalry or comfort or discovery that the premise makes significant. Do not import a roster of familiar tropes. Work only from the actual supplied relationship and let the crime create the pressure that tests, reveals, or deepens what is already there.\n\nLet the narrator's guarded speech and fuller private reasoning create the relationship's suspense. The narrator may say less than they know because of embarrassment, attention, utility, uncertainty, concealment, or the particular terms of the relationship, while the reader can still access what they actually feel and think. A callback to an earlier shared detail should land because the present scene has earned it: the repeated phrase or private joke or familiar gesture must acquire new weight from what is happening now, not merely signal that the relationship exists. Preserve earnest affection and practical learning alongside darker or heightened passages; a relationship scene does not need to become sentimental or ironic to be effective.\n\nGive affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery enough space where it belongs. A quiet moment between the characters may matter as much as a plot development. Keep the crime's causal sequence legible through the relationship. Divided loyalty, exposure, concealment, and consequence should arise from the actual situation rather than from a generic betrayal script. Suspense can come from what one character cannot say to the other, or from what the reader understands about both of them before they understand it themselves. The response may end inside an unfinished exchange or an unresolved emotional state, with the relationship's consequence still developing after the factual question has been answered or left open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadasau::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the crime as an account of deeds and their remembered consequences. Open with a deliberate statement of what was done, who was involved, or what the offense set in motion, then expand into the particulars that make the declaration incomplete. Use compact phrasing to fix a person's part in the affair: who planned it, who carried it, who knew and said nothing, who came after. A recurring designation should come from an established deed or way of speaking, not from an ornamental epithet assigned to every entrance.\n\nLet the narrator's practical judgment qualify the public account. The chronicle may say what a person was known for, but the narrator can also notice what the reputation leaves out: the hesitation, the miscalculation, the private motive that did not enter the public record. The narrator may speculate, remember, or correct a comparison while continuing the work of the telling, and direct feeling is ordinary material when it bears on what the offense has done to the people involved. Keep shared reputation distinct from the narrator's private account; being remembered as dependable does not mean that helping was effortless, cheerful, or automatic.\n\nReturn to a phrase when a later occurrence gives it a new application. The same words may name a promise, a threat, a debt, or a place, and their repetition should mark a change in what the act has come to mean. Let dialogue interrupt the authority of the telling when a participant objects to a detail or answers an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern. This need not become parody; familiar inconvenience is not heroic doom.\n\nKeep the crime's causal sequence legible. Preparation, execution, evidence, pursuit, and aftermath should remain traceable as events, not dissolve into ceremonial phrasing. The account may end with the business concluded or resumed, leaving another act available to be remembered rather than declaring the offense's full meaning settled. Do not require punishment, heroic doom, or a completed moral reckoning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadasav::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social/domestic realism route from the Scandinavian literary traditions. Ground the offense in the routines of specific institutions and households: irregularities in workplace logs, altered schedules, small discrepancies in shared resources, or shifts in who is present at meals and errands. Let the narrator's developed, self-correcting reasoning work through these ordinary details, connecting a late payment to a changed habit or an overheard remark to a familiar procedure.\n\nKeep the narrator's private thought available, moving between observation, explanation, memory, and qualification. Their spoken replies to family, colleagues, or officials need not expose this full train of thought. When the narrator notices a contradiction in an account or an arrangement, let that observation accumulate alongside practical duties rather than demanding immediate resolution. A person may continue working beside someone they now suspect, or alter a household routine to avoid a creditor or investigator.\n\nAnchor the account in the specific social world supplied by the story. Workplaces, housing, transport, meals, weather, and institutional procedures are concrete conditions that shape what people can do and choose to risk. Do not import a generic Nordic landscape or treat reserve, snow, isolation, or bleakness as automatic features. When a Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish context is active, keep its distinct institutions and social expectations; otherwise let the story's own setting carry the social detail.\n\nLet consequences unfold through relationships and practical adjustments rather than through a single explanatory confrontation. The narrator may reach a supported judgment and still live with what remains unsettled, including ordinary care and divided loyalty. The crime's aftermath can include incomplete resolution without a guaranteed punishment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadasaw::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Screenplay\n\nUse concise action blocks and attributed dialogue to carry the public movement of the crime: who goes where, what they handle, what they say, and what visible or audible event changes the situation. Add scene headings when location or time shifts, and keep transitions to a simple cut, offscreen sound, or voice-over only when the sequence actually needs that device. Do not crowd the page with camera commands.\n\nPreserve the narrator's developed private thought as clearly attributed nonspoken notes in the active presentation, using the source module's wrapping convention. These notes hold speculation, memory, a qualified comparison, or a self-correction the character does not speak aloud. They are distinct from dialogue; another character does not know what the note contains, and the note is not a line anyone can hear. A spoken reply may be shorter, more guarded, or differently motivated than the private note that accompanies it.\n\nLet suspense come from exposure or divided loyalty. The narrator can privately recognize that a colleague's explanation conflicts with the receipts, that a friend's offer arrives too conveniently, or that their own earlier assumption was wrong, while still speaking carefully or saying nothing. The audience follows the formation of that judgment through action and dialogue, not through the narrator explaining it on screen.\n\nKeep the crime's practical consequences visible: a workplace procedure followed or skipped, a vehicle checked, a door left open, a phone answered late, a payment made in cash. These observable details support inference without turning every glance or pause into a guilt cue. A scene can end after a completed action, an unanswered question, or a new practical problem, without a mandatory confrontation or reveal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadasax::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Stage Play\n\nBuild the public scene from what can be staged: clear speakers, entrances and exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the crime's immediate pressures. Who can see an entrance, reach a prop, hear a line, or move between two places should be clear enough that an attempted reconstruction changes what is plausible. Do not add film-camera instructions.\n\nPreserve the narrator's developed private thought as nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation, using the source module's wrapping convention. This material holds speculation, memory, a qualified comparison, or a self-correction that the character does not speak aloud. It is not an aside, a monologue, or a line another character can hear. A monologue or aside may appear only when it has a dramatic purpose of its own, not as the default destination for thought.\n\nLet spoken questions and answers fit the relationship and the immediate physical task. A witness can answer plainly, challenge the premise, or change the subject for an actual reason. The narrator's private note may show that they noticed the evasion, misunderstood it, or decided to let it pass for now. Another character does not know a conclusion merely because the audience has followed its formation.\n\nKeep the people occupied with the practical work of checking, not posed around an explanatory speech. A scene can stop after a result, an interruption, or a plain acknowledgment of what is now known, without an obligatory gathering of suspects, theatrical revelation, or new clue. The crime's aftermath can include ordinary care, divided loyalty, and incomplete resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadasay::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Stream of Consciousness\n\nLet the narrator's mind move by association through the crime's material: a smell from the workplace returns with the memory of a locked drawer, a phrase from a conversation repeats while they wash dishes, a bodily sensation interrupts a speculation about who knew what. Syntax and paragraph shape may follow these connections, but keep enough orientation to distinguish what is happening now from what is remembered, imagined, or feared.\n\nPreserve the narrator's developed, self-correcting reasoning inside the associative flow. They may notice a discrepancy, offer an explanation, remember a prior occasion that complicates it, and qualify the comparison without immediately resolving the matter. A recurring concern can return with a changed meaning as new information arrives, but the earlier thought is not erased or retroactively improved. The mind's movement is the method, not a license to make every character share a mind or to dissolve scene facts into uncertainty.\n\nLet the crime's practical details surface through perception and memory rather than through a linear report. A receipt, a timetable, a door's resistance, a colleague's hesitation, a phone number written on the wrong page: these particulars can appear when the mind reaches them, and their significance can accumulate across the telling. The narrator's private words, the ones they almost said or the ones they repeat to themselves, belong to their own mind and are not transmitted to anyone else.\n\nKeep the account particular to one authorized mind. Other characters speak and act in the world the narrator perceives, but their interior lives are not available through this technique unless separately authorized. The narrator's fallible judgment remains visible: they can misunderstand a motive, trust the wrong explanation, or correct themselves later without the story demanding a single explanatory confrontation or a guaranteed punishment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadasaz::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the story through readable sender-attributed messages. Each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one writes in fragments, another waits before replying, a third sends a correction or an attachment. Include timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes only when they matter to the crime's circumstances or to the relationship between the people messaging.\n\nDistinguish carefully between what is sent, what is drafted but not sent, and what the narrator thinks without transmitting. A sent message is available to its recipient and shapes the exchange. An unsent draft is a composed artifact the narrator has written but not delivered; it is not automatically their live private thought, and it is not known to the other person. Private thought is neither sent nor drafted, and it must be clearly separated from the transcript when it appears.\n\nLet the narrator's developed private reasoning appear as context outside the exchange, clearly marked and distinct from the messages. They may notice that a reply took too long, that a friend's wording matches something said earlier under different circumstances, or that their own message said less than they meant. Their transmitted text can be shorter, more guarded, or differently motivated than the private thought that accompanies it. Another person does not know that thought merely because the reader has followed its formation.\n\nLet suspense come from exposure or divided loyalty rather than from fabricated interface events. A delay can be ordinary, an edit can be a typo, a read receipt can mean nothing. When a timestamp, deletion, or channel change matters, make its significance arise from the actual circumstances of the crime, not from an invented subtext. The exchange can stop after a sent message, an unanswered question, or a plain acknowledgment, without a mandatory confession or reveal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadasba::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Visual Novel\n\nUse concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Describe concrete visual details—a colleague's changed smile, a room tidier than usual, someone standing too close to the door—and let the narrator interpret them as part of the crime's practical world. These details are purposeful references, not executable engine code, not mandatory labels on every line, and not automatic truth indicators.\n\nLet the focal narration carry the narrator's developed, self-correcting reasoning. They may notice a discrepancy, offer an explanation, remember a prior occasion that complicates it, and qualify the comparison without immediately resolving the matter. Their spoken reply can be shorter, more guarded, or differently motivated than the private response that accompanies it. Another character does not know a conclusion merely because the reader has followed its formation.\n\nKeep private thought outside other characters' knowledge. The narrator can privately recognize that a friend's explanation does not fit the receipts, that an offer arrives at a convenient moment, or that their own earlier assumption was wrong, while still speaking carefully or saying nothing. A changed expression can attract suspicion, but a contradiction in an arrangement or account must do the evidentiary work.\n\nAdvance the crime through scenes whose information changes the available interpretations. A revisit can use a familiar visual detail as an anchor, with its new significance earned by a later fact. Repetition should deepen understanding rather than restart the same suspicion. The style does not activate branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, or images merely because it is selected. A discovery may open a new conversation rather than force a choice screen, and a relationship consequence may remain unsettled after the factual question is answered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadasbb::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across installments: what is underway in the crime's preparation, pursuit, or aftermath, what changes locally in this reply, and what remains available to continue. Recover context briefly only when it helps the reader re-enter, not by retelling the whole investigation. A particular from an earlier installment can return when a later development changes what it suggests.\n\nLet the narrator's developed, self-correcting account accumulate over time. They may notice a discrepancy, offer an explanation, remember a prior occasion that complicates it, and qualify the comparison without immediately resolving the matter. An earlier observation remains available when a later installment changes its significance. New evidence can qualify an old explanation without replacing the old event or making the narrator deliberately forget a supported conclusion.\n\nKeep the narrator's private thought available when it contributes feeling or reasoning, even if no immediate decision changes. Their spoken replies to family, colleagues, or officials need not expose this full train of thought. Another person does not know a conclusion merely because the reader has followed its formation. Preserve earnest affection and practical learning alongside darker or heightened passages.\n\nLet ordinary life continue around the offense. A person can keep working beside someone they now suspect, alter a household routine to avoid a creditor or an investigator, or answer an official question with partial truth for a reason the narrator can understand without endorsing. Use earned developments and occasional hooks without making every turn a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger. The installment can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Serial duration should give selected facts room to acquire meaning, not make a fair answer perpetually recede.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadataa::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nisio Isin / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nTreat the crime as a relationship under pressure, and let the pressure surface through the way characters handle words. When a speaker defines what happened, objects to another person's version, corrects a term, or revises an earlier claim, make the verbal move also change what they are asking for or admitting to. A refusal to name an act can be as consequential as a confession.\n\nGive the viewpoint character's private experience the same turbulent attention as the dialogue. They may rehearse a sentence before saying it, hear their own wording as wrong, or realize that a correction has exposed more than they intended. Keep that interiority theirs; another character can see the strain or guess at it, but not read it. Let warmth and accusation share the same exchange when the relationship supports it. An apology may be sincere and still leave the practical harm unresolved.\n\nDo not require every conversation to reach a confession or collapse. A character can withdraw from a word they almost said, and the scene can continue with that withheld admission affecting their next action. The speakers may revisit a taken item, witnessed act, arrangement, or concealment, but the emotional stakes come from who is speaking and what the wording costs them. The ending may leave the relationship altered while the factual situation remains open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadatab::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nisio Isin / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the shared interval as a live conversation with unmistakable attribution. Give each speaker a distinct verbal habit tied to their position in the crime: one may define terms carefully, another may object to a word before answering the question, another may revise an earlier statement as if correcting a minor error. Let those differences show their stakes without making every line a puzzle.\n\nUse the language-driven moves as scene actions. A contradiction can shift what the characters are negotiating, and a revised wording can reveal that someone is guarding a particular fact. Keep the wordplay light enough for scannable roleplay: a pointed correction or a loaded definition works better than a long chain of puns. Do not break the scene to explain the verbal move; let the next speaker's reaction show what it changed.\n\nResolve the submitted material according to the active authorship contract and continue the scene from the last established action. The ending should follow from what was just said or done, leaving the other participants free to respond. Do not force a question, cliffhanger, or choice at the end. A speaker may simply notice the implication of the last line, and the scene can stop there with the criminal situation still developing.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadatac::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nisio Isin / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic telling route. Structure the crime as a sequence of linked encounters, each with a clear present moment and a connection to the larger offense or its aftermath. Let an episode turn on a verbal exchange: a witness revises an earlier account, a participant objects to a term in an accusation, or someone defines a relationship in a way that changes what the others thought they were doing.\n\nKeep the language and community actually supplied by the scene. Do not import a generic period setting, honor system, or national temperament. The episodic form should come from how the telling moves: one encounter closes with a changed understanding, and the next opens from that change. A repeated phrase or name can link episodes, but repetition should mark a shifting interpretation rather than certify a fixed truth.\n\nGive each speaker a distinct verbal habit and stake in the crime. An unreliable account may expose itself while its teller tries to explain, but the exposure should be legible through the wording itself, not through an omniscient gloss. Preserve the difference between what a character knew at the time and what they have learned since. The episode may end with the factual question still open and the relationships newly strained.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadatad::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nisio Isin / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the crime in hard-edged, concrete prose. Use forceful diction and compressed transitions. Show material wear and physical stakes: a scuffed floor, a locked door, a weapon's weight, the time it takes to cross a room. When a character reasons through a problem, let the thought run as long as the reasoning needs, but keep the surrounding movement tight.\n\nMake the verbal contests short and pointed. A speaker may correct a word, object to a definition, or revise a claim in one or two lines, and that move should change the practical situation: who is allowed to leave, what is being admitted, which story the others must now answer. Do not let the wordplay slow the scene into commentary. Ground abrasive or rough speech in its speaker and their immediate stake.\n\nUse rhythm changes deliberately. A longer exchange can follow a burst of action, or a single corrected word can cut off a threat. Keep the crime's physical consequences present even when the scene is mostly talk: a bruise, a missing item, a locked exit, a debt. The ending should follow from the last concrete change, not from a required reversal or confession.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadatae::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nisio Isin / Epistolary / Documents\n\nLet the documents carry the crime, each with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Use the specific document form the scene requires: a letter, a report, a journal entry, a record of an interview, a note left at a scene. Do not rotate through formats mechanically.\n\nLet each document carry the verbal action. A writer may define a term to limit what they are admitting, object to a word used in a previous letter, or revise an earlier account as if correcting a minor detail. The correction itself can expose what the writer is trying to control. What a document knows or conceals follows its author's situation and its intended reader, not the full case.\n\nUse gaps, revisions, and conflicting accounts as evidence. A later document may contradict an earlier one, and the contradiction should matter to the crime's reconstruction. Preserve readable continuity without adding an omniscient explanation after every record. Let the reader compare entries and notice the change. A document is not the author's complete live mind; it records what that author chose to set down. The sequence may end with a gap that remains significant.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadataf::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nisio Isin / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nKeep the crime and its consequences present, but spend the scene's attention on chosen kindness, familiar preferences, practical care, and small pleasures. A character may bring food, remember how someone takes their tea, or fix a small practical problem left by the case. Let the affection be sincere without requiring a confession or emotional repair.\n\nUse the verbal habits as affectionate negotiation. A correction can be playful rather than accusatory: one character revises another's word because they know the better term, or objects to a definition as a running joke. A revised wording can be an attempt to be kinder about a difficult fact. Keep personality and disagreement intact; comfort does not make everyone agreeable or erase the crime from the world.\n\nLet the warmth occur during a break in an investigation, after a difficult interview, or in the aftermath of an offense. Let the practical situation remain unresolved. Do not require a confession, a resolution, or a moment of emotional repair. Show two people being good to each other while the case waits.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadatag::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nisio Isin / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite the crime in composed novel prose with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Use narrative distance to frame a scene through social context, history, or judgment where the chosen narrator permits it. Distinguish the narration from ordinary spoken language; the prose should feel written, not transcribed.\n\nLet a definition of an act be weighed against the social meaning the narration has established. An objection or corrected wording can shift what the scene is about, and the narration can make that shift legible without breaking into commentary. An unreliable account may expose itself in the act of explaining, and the reader should see the exposure through the arrangement of the prose.\n\nLet the crime's stakes unfold through the relationship between private thought, spoken exchange, and social context. A speaker may revise a word because of who is listening; the narrator can show what that revision costs. Keep the viewpoint's access and knowledge separate. The scene may close with the social situation altered and the criminal question still open, without a required confession or reversal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadatah::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nisio Isin / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nTreat every oral narrative as a move within an active criminal or post-criminal negotiation, not a retrospective report. The storyteller speaks because the telling will gain, protect, transfer, or destroy something in the present: an alibi, a share, a debt, a legal position, a child’s custody, a vendetta’s future. The outer scene must have real consequences for the tale’s acceptance, so view the frame as the unfinished business of the offense.\n\nBuild each embedded story as a targeted intervention. Before narrating a tale, know what the teller wants the listener to believe, do, or stop doing. The tale may authenticate an object, shift blame, delay a deadline, justify an act, or make an absence seem non-incriminating. The listener’s questions are part of the crime’s pressure: allow interruptions about timing, access, wording, or motive, and make the teller respond with a revision that alters the requested conclusion. If a tale fails to shift the listener, show the failure in the outer scene—a demand for proof, a change in tone, a canceled offer—instead of letting the next tale pretend the objection never happened.\n\nMake the teller’s interest legible through selection and emphasis. A pause before a name, a euphemism for a violent act, a precise figure where vagueness would serve, or a repeated phrase that softens blame is not decoration; it is evidence of the teller’s stake. The narration may let the listener or reader notice these patterns without announcing them. A sincere account can still be strategically shaped, and the teller does not have to be lying to be managing the listener’s acceptance.\n\nUse recurrence as a pressure instrument, not a truth marker. A phrase, object, or gesture can cross between frame and embedded tale, or move from one tale to another, changing its meaning as the listener learns more. When a repeated item reappears, have the teller or listener remark on it, and let that remark reveal what has changed in their practical situation. Do not permit repetition to certify honesty; it can equally be a habit, a threat, a performance tic, or the teller’s attempt to control attention.\n\nPreserve the difference between the teller’s time-of-offense knowledge and present knowledge. An embedded tale may include later learned facts only when the teller has a discoverable reason to reveal or conceal them. Let the listener’s cross-examination probe that gap through concrete details. The crime itself becomes recoverable through the accounts, their contradictions, and the outer scene’s present facts, not through eloquence alone. End the response when the negotiation’s next immediate stake is clear, even if the story remains partially told or the listener has not yet accepted the version.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadatai::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nisio Isin / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the crime through the life of records, choosing the documentary and historical-memory route. A file, ledger, report, testimony, photograph, building register, deportation list, or property map can be the crime’s instrument, its cover, or its unhealed wound. Treat the record as a physical object with a provenance, not a transparent window: who created it, who filed it, who altered it, and who now holds it are never neutral facts.\n\nGive the narrator a definite relation to the archive. If the viewpoint character reads a record, the reader learns what that character notices, remembers, and misunderstands, not an omniscient summary. If the narrator is themselves a filer, a survivor, or a claimant, let their access be partial and their memory of the document’s creation be subject to later correction. In every case, distinguish what a document states, what a witness remembers, what an official concluded, and what the present speaker wants the record to mean.\n\nLet syntax follow the narrator’s position. Documentary passages may use precise, patient sentences that name dates, places, institutional actions, and rubber-stamp phrases. Memory passages may loosen into longer, associative movement, but the shift must be attributable to the narrator’s actual state—reading, remembering, being interrupted—not to a stylistic hover. If the narrator is a clerk, let bureaucratic formulas survive inside their recollection; if a survivor, let the record’s language appear bracketed or contested in their account.\n\nTreat verbal correction as a substantive act on the record. When a character objects to a word in a report, the objection should change what the document can be used to claim: a verb tense may assign responsibility, a noun may classify an act as accident, negligence, or crime, a revision may expose an earlier interest. Let participants argue with different stakes—a survivor may need a word changed, an official may need it kept, a bystander may resist the whole act of naming. If a correction is refused, show the refusal’s practical consequence: a benefit denied, a file reopened, a name left exposed.\n\nKeep the crime’s present consequences concrete. Show how a classification affects housing, employment, custody, inheritance, prosecution, reparations, or the ability to remain in a place. The record is not the only reality; let the characters’ bodies, rooms, and daily tasks resist or confirm the paper version. Competing interests should be practical and specific, not a bureaucratic mood.\n\nPreserve the difference between a record and the event it records. A document can be accurate and still incomplete; a memory can be vivid and still mistaken; a correction can be true and still self-interested; a forgery can be plausible and still unmasked by a date, a signature, or a phrase the narrator recognizes. Let these differences accumulate into a definite account of what happened and why the record became contested.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadataj::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nisio Isin / Gothic-Punk\n\nMake the crime a collision between inherited authority and present life, and make language the instrument of that collision. The offense may be an old claim, title, building, debt, custom, family arrangement, or institutional privilege still exerting force in a modern setting. The verbal contest itself can be the crime: a person is reclassified as heir, tenant, dependent, intruder, or threat, and the act of naming changes what others may do to them. Show the consequence in a door opened, a debt acknowledged, a threat turned into a request, or a person expelled.\n\nUse Gothic-Punk atmosphere to shape particular encounters, not to decorate the whole scene. A dark hallway, a locked archive, a crowded club, a cold apartment, or a private room can intensify what the characters risk at that moment. When a social mask falls away in trusted company, make that moment coincide with a revision of a term: the private self speaks a word the public self would not use. The atmosphere then changes—light, sound, or proximity shifts—and the reader understands that a boundary has been crossed.\n\nLet the speakers’ interests be discoverable through their wording. One may call a person an heir to claim loyalty, another may say tenant to justify rent, a third may use guest to deny the person a permanent place. Have a speaker revise a term during the encounter and show why the correction matters: a past promise is reinterpreted, a debt is retroactively created, a name is restored or erased. Do not let every character be a hyperverbal philosopher; a person who is not articulate can still feel the change in the room and know what it costs them.\n\nKeep the crime’s competing interests specific. The old authority may protect one person and expose another; a scheme may exploit the gap between what a custom once meant and what it can be made to mean now. The aftermath should show who can remain in a place, who must leave, who is believed, and who is owed. Let these stakes emerge through arguments and choices, not commentary.\n\nDo not import vampire physiology, factions, powers, or blood-bond rules. The inherited power in this recipe is social, legal, economic, or familial, and its horror is personal and political. The story should make that power intelligible through the characters’ verbal moves and their material consequences, and end when the next immediate stake is clear, even if the conflict remains unresolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadatak::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nisio Isin / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the crime as a first-person testimonial account given to a definite addressee and under a definite pressure: a statement to a police officer, a deposition before a magistrate, a family reckoning at a death anniversary, a public explanation after a riot, or a private attempt to set the record straight before someone else does. The narrator is a participant in the offense or its aftermath, and the telling is a consequential act—it may clear or incriminate, preserve property, protect a child, or complete a vendetta.\n\nLet the narrator’s self-presentation be unstable through their own wording. When the narrator corrects a word, the correction should change what the listener is being asked to accept. A sentence may begin with a justification and revise into an admission, or begin with blame and soften into shared responsibility. These corrections accumulate so that the reader can see the narrator’s interest without an announcement. The narrator need not be a conscious liar: self-deception, loyalty, fear, and partial memory are enough. What matters is that the wording shifts under the listener’s authority.\n\nMake the listener’s judgment an active part of the account’s pressure. The narrator may anticipate an objection, answer a question not yet asked, repeat a phrase to ward off suspicion, or pause before naming someone. The listener need not be silent; an interruption can force a revision that changes the account’s direction. When the narrator parries the interruption, show what the parry costs: a loss of sympathy, a new suspicion, a need to produce another name. If the listener is an official or elder, let the narrator’s language bend toward legal or communal formulas and then break away.\n\nGround the account in the supplied language, region, community, and period. Use the actual names, places, relationships, and material conditions of the scene. If the setting is specific, let its institutions and obligations shape what the narrator can say, to whom, and in what language. Do not force English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure. If the narrator speaks in a particular dialect or idiom, render its force through the English prose rather than inventing a new verbal system.\n\nPreserve the difference between the narrator’s earlier knowledge and present knowledge. The narrator may now understand something that was unclear at the time, but that later understanding should not be retroactively inserted as the earlier motive. Let the testimony move toward a definite account of what happened, while the reader can see why the telling took this shape. End when the narrator has given the immediate stake its provisional shape, even if the confession remains partial or the listener has not yet ruled.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadatal::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nisio Isin / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the crime’s aftermath as a diary or reflective miscellany kept by someone who participated in or witnessed the offense. Give the record a definite material form: a paper notebook, a phone document, a set of notes later found by someone else, or a collection of dated entries intended for no one or for an imagined reader. The diarist chooses what to record, what to omit, and what to return to on a later day, and the reader should sense the gap between the event and the written account.\n\nLet the diarist revise the meaning of an earlier word across entries. A later entry may correct a term from a previous one: what was called an accident becomes a decision, what was called loyalty becomes fear, what was called a friend becomes an accomplice. But do not make every revision a confession. The correction may be self-justifying, self-deceiving, diagnostic, or an attempt to live with what happened. Show what the revision does for the diarist: it may shift blame, salvage self-respect, confirm a suspicion, or provide a settled narrative where none existed.\n\nMake the record’s omissions legible through habits of attention and avoidance. The diarist may stop before naming someone, write around a date, record a trivial detail instead of a significant one, or break off an entry. The omission is not a failure of completeness but part of the account. When a later entry supplies what was missing, let the reader understand why it was missing and what made it speakable now.\n\nLet personal language and narrative distance follow the actual teller. Age, position, education, and relationship to the crime shape vocabulary, sentence rhythm, and what the diarist considers worth recording. Seasonal attention, direct feeling, plain argument, and omission are available where they fit this narrator. Do not impose a mandatory inventory of Japanese scenery or a universal restrained tone. If the narrator is angry, the prose may be terse and accusatory; if ashamed, it may circle.\n\nPreserve the difference between what the diarist knew when writing an entry and what the diarist knows later. A later entry may interpret an earlier one, but it cannot change what the earlier self knew. The reader may use a later entry to re-read the earlier one, but that later knowledge must be presented as later. Let the accumulated entries produce a definite account of the offense and its aftermath, while the reader can see why the record was kept and what it was used to manage.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadatam::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nisio Isin / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the crime as an event that a community must now account for, using the social realism, chronicle, and testimony route. The narration moves among the people who saw, suffered, committed, investigated, or survived the offense, preserving the relevant language, community, and history. Let the telling feel situated in a particular place and time rather than a generic Latin American setting.\n\nLet each speaker’s wording carry a stake. A witness may call an act by one name to protect a neighbor and by another to implicate an outsider. An official may revise a term to make a death fit a category. A survivor may correct a euphemism and force the others to hear what happened. When a speaker objects to a word or contradicts an earlier account, the change should alter what the group is being asked to accept or do. Show the consequence of the verbal move, not just its cleverness.\n\nMake the chronicler’s selection and position legible. If the narration is presented by a chronicler, journalist, or community scribe, that mediator has their own stake and access. Who gets to speak, whose version is recorded, and whose words are summarized or omitted are acts of power. A contradiction between two testimonies is not automatically a sign of equal truth or equal falsehood; it is a fact about the speakers and their positions. Let the accumulated record move toward a definite account of the offense while preserving the conflicts that shaped the telling. If the chronicler is absent, let the juxtaposition of scenes itself perform the selection, so the reader sees what is shown and what is not.\n\nKeep the crime’s social consequences concrete. Show how the offense affects work, housing, movement, safety, family, reputation, or the ability to speak publicly. The chronicle should make the aftermath legible as a shared condition, not as a series of private moods. Competing interests should be specific and practical: a shop’s opening hours, a bus route’s risk, a name on a list, a child’s school transfer.\n\nPreserve the difference between observation, memory, report, and inference. A witness may describe what they saw, remember what they later learned, repeat what someone else said, or draw a conclusion, and the narration should keep those sources distinct without turning the story into a clinical case summary. The verbal contests should reveal the crime’s human cost and the reasons the record remains contested.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadatan::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nisio Isin / Light Novel\n\nEnter each scene at the moment a verbal contest becomes unavoidable, with the viewpoint character present on a concrete errand: an answer, a signature, a name, a key, a cut, a debt acknowledged, a story accepted. Let the reader learn the stakes from what the characters say and notice in the opening exchanges rather than from preamble. When a nickname, alias, or piece of crew jargon first appears, make its meaning clear from the situation or a reaction without stopping the scene to explain it.\n\nStage the encounter so the eye can follow it: who stands, who sits, who is nearest the door, what object lies between them, what sound or movement marks a shift in pressure. Keep every spatial change legible and break paragraphs readably around action, dialogue, and private response. A reaction beat belongs to its owner — fear, anger, calculation, relief, or amusement follows from what that particular character has just understood or risked.\n\nMake each verbal move change the transaction. A definition, objection, contradiction, or revised wording should alter what the participants are doing to each other: one speaker may recast a favor as a debt, another may correct a name and expose an alias, a third may dispute a verb and reveal whom they are protecting. Land wordplay fast — a double meaning or pointed rephrasing arrives in a single line — and show its cost in the next beat: a hand moving to a pocket, an offer quietly reworded, a chair pushed back. Keep the consequence of every move legible without pausing the scene to comment on it.\n\nGive each speaker a different verbal habit tied to their stake: one negotiates in exact terms, one answers questions with questions, one over-explains when lying. Let self-presentation shift where pressure applies — a character may move from calling himself a partner to an employee to a bystander as the exchange tightens — and let an account that keeps revising itself deepen the threat of exposure on its own. The tension is being seen through, not a puzzle awaiting solution. Do not turn every minor character into a hyperverbal philosopher; a plain-spoken participant can still change the situation with one sentence.\n\nRender the viewpoint character's private experience of the contest while it runs: what they notice, suspect, remember, or decide as the exchange continues. Their private thought need not match their spoken line; when it diverges, anchor the gap in a specific motive — concealment, uncertainty, the need to keep the other person talking — or let them align completely. The narration can carry that character's divided loyalty or hidden role while the others remain unaware, staying within what this viewpoint can actually know.\n\nAdvance the crime through the contest: each exchange should change who knows what, who owes whom, or who is in danger, moving preparation, pursuit, exposure, or aftermath forward rather than displaying cleverness for its own sake. End at the scene's live boundary, even mid-exchange, mid-evasion, or mid-realization, without staging a confession, reversal, or cliffhanger to close the reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadatao::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nisio Isin / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet the focal teller's associative attention generate the lyric register from the crime's concrete after-effects. Begin a passage with a traceable image or sound from the present scene—the surface of a table, a repeating noise from the street, the way a door closes behind someone—and let its syntax already belong to the teller's stake in what is being examined. Do not decorate the scene with a detached metaphor. The image should be chosen by the teller's attention and loaded with a specific suspicion, obligation, or loss.\n\nDevelop the lyric material through a second return, never a static refrain. When an image reappears, place it beside a new fact or a changed account: the same stain now matches a story someone did not tell, the same phrase now arrives from the wrong mouth, the same closed door is no longer proof of the same absence. The return should make the teller's earlier figurative understanding insufficient, and the passage should adjust its rhythm at that point—slowing, repeating a sound, or breaking the sentence where the discrepancy becomes visible. This is how lyric language carries evidentiary movement without becoming a mystery-puzzle ornament.\n\nTreat dialogue as wordplay with different verbal registers. The narrator's own figurative syntax can interpret an exchange, but it must not replace the actual spoken difference. Give each speaker a distinct habit with criminal stakes: one defines a term to delay an account, one objects to a wording because it protects an alibi or a relationship, one revises a sequence and thereby reveals what he is trying not to say. Let the teller sometimes correct her own wording in the narrative itself, stopping mid-comparison to supply the plainer or sharper phrase that changes the claim. The correction can be performed by the prose rhythm, not announced with editorial comment.\n\nKeep speech systems from collapsing into one lyric voice. Some speakers may be sparse, evasive, or practical while the teller's paragraphs remain rhythmically developed. A plain admission can interrupt an elaborate image and still carry the greatest weight because the lyric has prepared a sonic contrast for it. Write the discovery as a shift in attention, not a completed metaphor: sentence length can contract, a repeated consonant can stop, or the image can be abandoned for a direct statement of the discrepancy. End the response where the present perception or exchange has settled, with the image left open rather than forced to pronounce a final verdict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadatap::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nisio Isin / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the reflective voice investigate through remembered wording. Open a movement with a plain, settled recollection such as a phrase someone used before the offense, the way a room looked when an arrangement was still possible, or the sequence of a familiar routine. Let the narrator then correct that memory in the same breath: 'She seemed calm, no, she kept her hands still and that was not calm.' The correction must alter what the remembered event can now support—an alibi, a promise, a motive, or an account of who knew what. This is not a general meditation on loss; it is a slow, verbal revision of the evidence that memory has been holding.\n\nKeep some recollections stable so that a later change has leverage. A reliable detail—where the key was kept, who answered the door first, whose voice is missing from a remembered morning—should remain fixed across the narrative. When a later correction touches it, the reader can feel the difference between the stable past and the revised account. Do not make every memory unreliable; instead make the unreliable memory exert pressure on the stable ones until one of them has to give way.\n\nAllow the present moment to recall the past without canceling its own quality. An ordinary current pleasure, an irritation, or an unexpected kindness can bring back the earlier occasion. When that happens, treat the recall as a competing account: the present detail may show that the earlier meaning was incomplete, that a reported sequence cannot be right, or that a familiar absence has changed its significance. The reflection should therefore be active and consequential, not a sealed mood: a memory can become a piece of newly understood evidence.\n\nLet the melancholy register reside in unfinished attachment and small irreversible changes, not in total sadness. The narrator may still be holding a hope, a resentment, or a question that the crime has not settled. A concluding paragraph may dwell on that unfinished attachment, but only after the reflective correction has moved the account's factual or interpretive ground. End where the revised memory and the present contact have made the current uncertainty specific, without announcing that the past is lost or that the feeling is now resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadataq::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nisio Isin / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nBuild the story as a situated oral telling. Open the frame with a recognizable present setting and a teller who has a reason to tell: a visitor after a journey, a family member after a funeral, a witness after an official silence, or a lingering companion during an ordinary evening. The audience should be concrete enough to question the teller—not 'listeners' in the abstract, but at least one person with an independent stake, a memory, or a contrary account of the same period. Let the frame remain alive between passages: a question, a pause, an objection, or a demand to go back can interrupt the inner story.\n\nSeparate the teller's present speech from reported speech inside the tale. When the teller reports what someone said, use direct quotation the first time so the exact words become evidence. Later, when the same words return, they may be repeated accurately, slightly altered, or replaced by a paraphrase. The alteration itself must be functional: it may reveal that the teller is protecting someone, that a witness's version changed under pressure, that the teller misheard because of her own hope or fear, or that the audience now catches a missing step in a sequence. The teller may also object to her own earlier wording while speaking, stopping to sharpen a word or correct a date; that correction should be audible as part of the performance, not hidden in narrative summary.\n\nGive the audience speech rights inside the frame. A listener may interrupt with an objection grounded in what the framed world permits: 'You said the shop was closed, but the delivery cart passed at that hour,' or 'If the door was locked, who opened the courtyard gate?' The teller can answer, resist, or revise, and that exchange becomes part of the crime's exposure. Keep the audience's knowledge limited to what their position in the frame permits; they are not the reader's proxy with total omniscience. The frame narrator may also supply a brief present-tense note—what the room looks like now, how the teller's hands move—so the oral performance remains situated without becoming a stage direction.\n\nAnchor the disputed wording in concrete social or domestic arrangements. When a word is contested, the teller should be forced to specify a physical or practical detail rather than invoke a general moral code: whose key, which threshold, what time the lamp was lit, who usually answered the door, where a borrowed object was kept. Do not import a generic regional atmosphere, honor code, mysticism, or exile mood. The inner story may travel, but its crime and its verbal disputes must remain tied to specific households, streets, workplaces, or journeys the teller can describe.\n\nLet the telling end on a specific unresolved point. The frame may close with the audience still disputing one word or the teller still refusing one correction, provided the dispute has earned its force by the preceding verbal and physical particularity. Do not force a moral, a punishment, or a neat restoration of the truth. End where the current teller's authority has been genuinely challenged or where a concrete uncertainty has become visible, with the frame allowed to fall silent at that point.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadatar::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nisio Isin / Minimalist Prose\n\nMake the minimal unit a discrepancy that is introduced, corrected, and then acted upon in plain clauses. Open with a direct report: a door, a time, a person's word. Let a second speaker or a present fact supply an immediately different version, often by changing only one word: open becomes unlocked, eight becomes half past, delivered becomes left. Show the change on the page, without a narrator's explanation. The reader sees the discrepancy in the small gap between the two statements.\n\nUse direct speech for every definitional or evasive move. A speaker may define a term to delay, object to a word because it protects someone, or correct a sequence and thereby reveal what he is trying to avoid saying. The correction should be visible in the dialogue itself: 'It was open.' 'It was unlocked.' 'I said I left it, not that I locked it.' Each exchange leaves a specific difference in what the participants can now claim. Let the focal character register that difference in one plain inward sentence—what she now suspects, what she can no longer say, or what she must verify. That sentence is the only explanation allowed; do not restate it in the narrator's voice.\n\nPermit a longer sentence only when it carries the exact causal link between a discrepancy and a new state of knowledge or obligation. If the focal character sees that a familiar phrase was used by the wrong person, or that a story now includes a step it lacked before, the thought may unfold in a single precise extended sentence. Do not use length to add mood, interpretation, or redundant summary. The style's economy is preserved by keeping every optional element out, not by refusing a needed connection.\n\nLet the focal character's self-correction also be minimal. She may say a word, stop, and replace it with another: 'He was calm. No. He was too quick.' The new word should change the account just enough to make a later action necessary or impossible. Keep it in direct narrative syntax rather than analytical commentary. End where the discrepancy has been made concrete and the current claim has shifted, without a recapitulation or a polished conclusion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadatas::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nisio Isin / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite in continuous scene-first prose, but treat scene-level verbal contests as the engine of the crime's movement. A conversation should begin in the practical world: someone has come to return an object, ask about a time, or deny an obligation. Let the exchange then turn on a word. One speaker defines a term so the request cannot proceed; another repeats a phrase with one small change that makes an alibi impossible; a third stops mid-sentence and corrects himself, and the correction reveals what he had agreed to keep silent. Do not pause the scene to announce the significance. Let the contemporary-novel form do the marking: a short denied answer followed by a longer interior realization; a paragraph break after the challenged word; or a shift from quoted speech to the focal character's continuous perception.\n\nDifferentiate the speakers' verbal habits as part of the scene's social texture. One character may speak in exact times and distances, one in evasive questions, one in corrections that always protect another person's reputation. The narrator's own voice may be fluid and capable of extended reflection, but other characters need not share it. Keep the focal character's authorized interiority woven into the scene: half-formed reasoning, a suspicion she does not voice, a delayed recognition that a word was used by someone who should not know it. Her thought can follow the spoken exchange, revise it, or temporarily misread it; it must remain private unless the perspective permits it to be shown.\n\nUse recollection as a live comparison within the scene, not as an imported memory. When a present detail brings back an earlier arrangement, let the recollection alter what the current conversation can claim: a remembered promise makes a present courtesy impossible, a remembered route makes a reported delay suspect, a remembered phrase makes a current denial incomplete. The narrator may correct herself in the same natural prose movement: 'She had said before eight. Before eight meant before the clinic closed. No. She had said before the last delivery.' That self-correction should read as an event of attention in the scene, not as an editorial note.\n\nAllow the scene to end in continuing action or thought. The current exchange may finish with a demand still unanswered, a suspicion still forming, or a practical next step already evident. Do not force a summary or a resolution. What matters is that the last movement—a word, a corrected phrase, a shift in who can ask what next—has changed something available to the scene. If the question now is concrete, you may stop there.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadatat::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nisio Isin / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nCenter the scene on the established relationship premise, but make the pair's private language do evidentiary work. Before the crime's pressure enters, give them at least one shared phrase, nickname, or correction pattern with a legible history inside the supplied relationship. The phrase may be a joke, a reassurance, a way of asking without asking, or a word one of them always changes when the other says it. Use that established material early so its later return has force.\n\nWhen the phrase returns, let it be altered by one of them. One says the old word as usual, the other says it with a small change, withholds it, or corrects it in a way that shows what she now knows or suspects. The alteration should be readable as a relationship event and as a move in the crime's negotiation: preserving an alibi, testing whether the other will notice, admitting a divided loyalty, or protecting someone from a question the pair normally never asks. Do not turn every use into a sentimental call-back. The callback succeeds only when the changed wording reveals a change in what the two can still truthfully say to each other.\n\nLet verbal correction carry the relationship's specific stakes. A character may object to a word the other uses to describe what happened—not only because the word is inaccurate, but because the word places blame, invokes a promise, or assigns a role the pair had not agreed to. The correction itself can be an act of care, jealousy, betrayal, or protection. Keep the speakers' habits distinct: one may use the shared phrase easily, the other may now avoid it. The focal character's inward recognition should be present without turning the scene into an omniscient breakdown. What she thinks the correction means may be right, partly right, or temporarily wrong; the next exchange can complicate it.\n\nUse repeated moments as evidence through small differences. A familiar gesture—reaching for an object, covering a hand, leaving at a certain time—may be performed as usual, withheld, or performed with a difference that the focal character notices. The difference should advance the account by altering what the pair can safely assume about one another's involvement. If the crime remains unresolved, the scene should still end with the relationship's current condition concretely changed: someone has chosen not to correct a phrase; someone has used the old phrase and meant it differently; someone has left the room before a question could be finished. End where that relational movement settles, without forcing a confession, a kiss, or a reconciliation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadatau::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nisio Isin / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nShape the crime as a retold chronicle whose authority is always being tested. Open with a deliberate, compact declaration of the deed and what the teller says it will be remembered for: the breach, the broken oath, the stolen sum, the death. Let the teller's own authority be visible in that opening—a formal beginning, a repeated phrase, a claim about the deed's consequence. Then expand into the particulars that complicate the declaration. The teller may correct a date, supply a missing name, or admit that an earlier version omitted a step. Each correction should alter what the chronicle can now claim.\n\nUse recurring phrasing as the site of dispute, not as decoration. Choose a phrase that can carry legal, kin, or practical weight—her word, the debt, the locked door, who was present at the fire—and let it return at least twice. The first return should affirm the teller's account. The second should be challenged: a listener repeats the phrase with a different word, a participant gives a different sequence, or the teller pauses and realizes the phrase can no longer mean what it said. When a phrase changes, make the change visible in direct narration or quoted speech so the reader can hear the chronicle adjusting itself.\n\nTreat competing claims as reported wordplay. A later speaker may object not by shouting a general denial but by correcting the definition or application of key terms: He did not break his word, he broke the compact; She did not steal the money, she took back what was owed; The door was not locked, it was barred. Let each objection enter the chronicle as a framed account, introduced with who said it and under what circumstances. The teller may resist, concede, or revise. Do not let the narration grant both sides equal verified truth unless that is established; keep the teller's access and the selected perspective consistent.\n\nUse established saga materials only when the supplied world already supports them. If oaths, kinship, gifts, journeys, or public feuds belong to the setting, they can organize the crime and provide the recurrent terms that will be disputed. Do not import a Nordic pantheon, heroic doom, or ceremonial dialogue. A listener's interruption may be a practical correction—the name of the horse, the hour of the market—and may still force a word-level change in the account. Humor can arise from the gap between the teller's elevated opening and an ordinary correction, but it is not required, and household or street-scale events remain their own scale.\n\nEnd where the chronicle's competing versions have become specific. The last section may stop with the teller still insisting on one word, a listener still holding another, or the current audience unwilling to accept the revised phrase. Do not force a final judgment or a moral. The closing should feel like the end of a particular retelling, with the dispute left open because the recurring phrase has changed its meaning and the tale can no longer be returned to its earlier form.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadatav::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nisio Isin / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the crime as social and domestic realism: the offense, its incentives, and its consequences anchored in particular households, workplaces, and local institutions, carried by the practical relationships that hold those places together. Whoever carries the account — culprit, accomplice, victim, investigator, or bystander — is embedded in material conditions and obligations: shifts, debts, care duties, tenancies, the paperwork of whatever institution the crime touches. If the supplied setting names a specific Nordic country or community, keep its particular language and arrangements distinct rather than blending a generic North; if the story stands elsewhere, this route shapes the method without relocating anyone or importing snow, reserve, or isolation as automatic atmosphere.\n\nMake contested language the working surface of the crime. A definition, objection, contradiction, or revised wording should redistribute something social — blame inside a family, standing at a workplace, what a municipality is willing to call the act, whether a neighbor's correction acknowledges the offense or buries it. Institutional speech acts here as a real participant: forms, classifications, minutes, and official terms can reclassify what happened, and a character fighting over a word is often fighting over that reclassification. Tie each speaker's verbal habit to a stake — the one who insists on precise wording may be protecting a reputation; the one who softens a phrase may be negotiating a version of the event the household can live with — and let some people speak plainly or refuse the contested terms altogether.\n\nLet the aftermath move through conversation, recollection, and practical arrangement at least as much as visible action. A household discussion can show the offense being absorbed or denied; a workplace exchange can show it being renamed; a neighbor's small correction can register a consequence no one states outright. Suspense can run on exposure and divided loyalty even when everyone knows who did it: what the community will finally say, and whose revised version grants cover to whom. Keep the focal character's reasoning, uncertainty, and motives available as ordinary story prose — another character does not know that interiority just because the reader does, and a revised spoken account is a social act, not the narrator's settled truth. The crime remains the organizing center, its meaning made and remade in the language this community uses to live with it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadataw::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nisio Isin / Screenplay\n\nPresent the crime's preparation, incentives, relationships, execution, pursuit, or aftermath in screen-shaped scenes: a scene heading whenever location or time changes, concise action lines establishing what is visible or audible and what practically changes, clear speaker cues carrying the dialogue. Reach for cuts, offscreen sound, or voice-over only when this sequence needs them, and keep camera vocabulary off the page.\n\nLet the verbal contest live in the dialogue and land through juxtaposition. A character defines the act, objects to a term, contradicts an earlier account, or revises a wording — and because the screen shows before anyone explains, the cut can do the exposing: the audience has already watched the event, so an account that quietly differs from the shown scene contradicts itself without a narrator marking it. Trust that structure instead of a voice-over explaining the subtext, and give each move a visible consequence in the action lines: the pause after a corrected word, who stops writing, an object set down or pocketed, someone suddenly free to leave. Distinguish speakers by habit and stake — careful legal phrasing held by one, another's flat refusal to name the act, a third repeating a phrase until it stops sounding innocent — while minor figures talk like ordinary people rather than duelists.\n\nKeep required private experience as clearly attributed nonspoken material in the active presentation instead of converting it into dialogue or voice-over; a hesitation is visible, its content is not, and the cast does not hear thought. Suspense can run on exposure and divided loyalty even when the culprit is known — a revised wording can show that someone is now protecting somebody, or that the earlier story has become unusable. Close where the scene's business closes, on a completed exchange, a visible consequence, or an unfinished setup, without owing a reversal, confession, or fresh discovery.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadatax::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nisio Isin / Stage Play\n\nWrite the crime as a playable script: clear speakers, entrances and exits, blocking, handled objects, pauses, and business that can actually be staged. The offense, scheme, or consequence should be legible through what people do and say in the shared space, never through narration the audience could not hear.\n\nMake each verbal move rearrange the room. When a character defines the act, objects to another's term, contradicts a prior account, or revises a wording, the line should change what the characters are physically doing to each other: who keeps the object, who moves to the door, who sits down as though the matter were settled, who turns to face the person they had been sparing. Because the stage keeps bodies present, an explanation can expose itself in front of the people it concerns — a contradiction with something the audience has already watched happen in this same space needs no narrator to underline it. Give each speaker a verbal habit tied to a stake: meticulous wording defending a position, a softer substitution renegotiating the event, a phrase repeated until everyone on stage hears it differently. Minor figures can simply say what they mean.\n\nUse monologue and aside only where a listener or the need to persuade gives them dramatic purpose; they are performed speech, not the default home for thought. Required private experience stays clearly attributed nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation — silence and a held pause are visible, their content is not, and no other character hears it. Suspense can come from exposure or divided loyalty even with the culprit known, as when a revised account collapses in the room where it was first agreed. The scene can close on a completed exchange, an exit, a pause, or an unresolved arrangement, without any gathering of suspects or theatrical revelation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadatay::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nisio Isin / Stream of Consciousness\n\nTell the crime through one authorized mind's associative movement: perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and a recurring concern braided together. The offense, its incentives, and its aftermath surface as what this mind returns to, rehearses, and resists rather than as a linear report. Keep enough orientation — tense shifts, markers like that morning or she had said — that an occurring event stays distinguishable from a remembered one or an imagined one.\n\nMake the language itself the crime's second scene. When the focal mind defines the act, objects to its own earlier word, contradicts what it remembers, or revises a phrasing, the revision changes what the mind is doing to itself: a justification interrupted by the exact memory it is explaining away; a casual word — borrowed, settled, a mistake — returning later as an accusation; a confident definition collapsing when the wrong detail surfaces. Let self-correction happen mid-sentence. The account may expose its own unreliability precisely while explaining — the version offered the third time differs from the second in a way the mind never acknowledges — while the reader still follows what happened, what is recollected, and what is invention.\n\nThe stream belongs to this focal character alone. Other people arrive through perception and recollection: a remembered line may be misremembered or edited, but the narration cannot know what anyone else privately thought. Suspense comes from what the mind notices, avoids, or renames — a bodily sensation breaking into a comfortable version, an object or phrase the recurring concern keeps attaching itself to. The response can end inside continuing thought, on an unfinished setup, or at a plain shift of attention, with no realization, confession, or new information required first.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadataz::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nisio Isin / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the crime in a sender-attributed exchange: readable messages, with only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to this exchange. Let each sender's actual digital habits define their voice — fragments against full sentences, habitual editing after sending, long silences, messages left on read — so that a break in anyone's pattern is itself an event.\n\nMake the interface the arena of the verbal contest. A definition arrives as a message someone insists on; an objection as a quoted line pushed back; a contradiction as what the scrollback already shows — a later message quietly disagreeing with an earlier one from the same sender, visible to everyone still reading. An edit landing after a reply is a revised wording with witnesses; a delay before an answer can change what the answer seems to admit; a read receipt with no response is a refusal with a timestamp. Use these as real moves in the crime's exposure, protection, or aftermath — what someone will not put in writing marks where loyalty divides — and never manufacture an interface event merely to add subtext.\n\nKeep sent messages, unsent drafts, and private thought distinct. An unsent draft is a composed artifact — its author wrote it, and it says what it says — but it is not automatically live interiority; required nonspoken experience stays clearly attributed and clearly separated from the transcript. Context outside the exchange may appear when needed, separated in presentation from the messages themselves. The exchange can still be running at the response boundary — a message unanswered, an edit pending, a setup unfinished — without a confession, a completed pursuit, or new evidence owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadatba::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nisio Isin / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the crime in scene-oriented presentation: concise scene-setting, attributed dialogue, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene, with scene changes kept legible. The offense, scheme, or consequence moves through what the focal character observes, hears said, and privately works out within each scene.\n\nRun the verbal contest in the exchange and in the focal reaction beneath it. A character defines the act, objects to a term, contradicts an earlier account, or revises a wording; the focal character's inner response can rephrase what was just said, test it against what was seen, or catch the one word that no longer fits — private revision running alongside public talk. Let a background or expression cue orient the moment rather than certify anything: a changed expression can draw the focal character's attention or suspicion, but it is a contradiction in an account or arrangement that makes a version unusable, shifts who is exposed, or shows the earlier story no longer holds. Give speakers distinct verbal habits tied to distinct stakes — precise phrasing protecting someone, a softer substitution renegotiating the event, a repeated phrase changing meaning with each return — without turning minor figures into philosophers.\n\nThe focal character's inner response is available to the reader and unknown to the cast; no one else hears it because the scene displays it. The style does not activate branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images — a discovery opens the next conversation rather than a choice screen. Suspense can run on exposure or divided loyalty even when the culprit is known, and a scene can close on a completed exchange, a visible consequence, or an unfinished setup, owing no reversal, confession, or fresh clue.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadatbb::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Nisio Isin / Web Serial\n\nWrite the crime across installments with a readable through-line: each reply makes clear what is underway, what changed locally, and what remains open to continue, recovering an earlier particular briefly and only when it helps. Preparation, incentives, relationships, execution, pursuit, and aftermath can span installments without any one reply closing like a miniature chapter.\n\nLet contested wordings accumulate as the serial's memory. A definition offered casually in an early reply can be quoted back once it has become inconvenient; a phrase repeated across installments can change weight as new facts appear; a revision made under pressure in one installment can become the exposure or the divided loyalty of a later one. When a character is held to what they said before, restate the exact earlier wording — the particular sentence, not a summary of the scene — so the change lands without retelling. Keep each speaker's verbal habits persistent enough to become signature: the careful qualifier, the softening substitution, the repetition. Let some characters stay plain-spoken while the contest runs among the few who fight over words.\n\nDevelop through earned change rather than artificial cliffhangers: progress can be knowledge, a relationship's altered terms, work done, or resources moved. Suspense can come from exposure or divided loyalty even when the culprit is known — the installment where an earlier account becomes unusable is itself a real development, not a reset. Close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known, with no reversal, confession, or fresh discovery owed at any boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadauaa::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / No Named Influence / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the offense, the scheme, or the pursuit through one bond the viewpoint character cannot repair and cannot leave. Crime work runs on people who owe each other: a partner who must be asked for help after the last betrayal, a relative whose silence protects and imprisons at once, an investigator who once trusted the person now in question, a witness who loves the person they are about to implicate. Choose one such relationship and let its particular history, not a general mood, generate the turbulence.\n\nLet feeling change the practical work in the same beat where it appears. A confession can cost the name the listener needed. A refusal can leave a door unlocked or a debt unpaid. An apology can arrive at the exact moment the other person must decide whether to lie for the one apologizing. Afterward, show the residue in behavior rather than diagnosis: the question steered away from, the task done too carefully, the patience that is visibly effortful, the necessary conversation left half-finished. Emotional exposure is a plot event here — it moves money, evidence, access, and cooperation.\n\nKeep the criminal facts legible even while the viewpoint character is distracted by them. Amounts, timings, who had access, what the risk actually is: these stay concrete. Track what the character notices and what they refuse to examine; a lead can be set down unread because of what it would mean about someone loved, and that avoidance is itself a decision the story registers. The case advances, stalls, or bends because of these choices, not around them.\n\nWrite the hurt in the narrator's own diction and at the narrator's own distance. A close first-person voice may circle one wound and skip whole days; a more external narration may show the strain in hesitation, gesture, and what goes unsaid. Do not reach for a standard vocabulary of suffering or force pain into named emotion; register a feeling when the viewpoint character would actually register it. Warmth can surface — briefly, awkwardly, at the wrong moment — without making anything safe. Repair, if it is possible at all, arrives on the relationship's own schedule.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadauab::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / No Named Influence / Chat Roleplay\n\nRun the crime as a shared interval the participants can follow at a glance. Short paragraphs, one main beat each — an action, an observation, or an exchange — always attributable. Establish who is present and what they are doing; use tags and small identifying actions so every line stays owned even when several characters talk across each other.\n\nLet competing interests drive the dialogue rather than plot summary. A suspect wants to leave before something is found; an accomplice wants to hear that it will be fine; an investigator wants one more look around; a bystander wants their name out of it. Put these pressures in what people say and do. When an NPC moves, show the move and its immediate effect on the shared space — a drawer opened, a phone turned face down, a door left ajar, a question answered just too fast — so the next speaker inherits a changed room.\n\nKeep the world and its people active rather than paused around one participant. NPCs can keep searching, arguing, packing, deciding, or quietly leaving while attention is elsewhere; their choices follow their own interests. Do not voice, decide, or think for another player's character; render the viewpoint character's perception, inference, and action, and leave everyone else's interior to the active person and control settings.\n\nStop the reply at the edge the scene actually reaches — mid-exchange, mid-task, mid-wait are all legitimate — not at an engineered cliffhanger or forced choice, and without a summary question to relaunch the room. The next participant picks up from the last clear speaker, gesture, or unresolved movement.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadauac::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / No Named Influence / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nTake the social and satirical realist route. Treat the crime as a probe into one community's web of obligation: who owes whom, who can afford not to see, whose position depends on not asking, who is ruined by the effort to keep up appearances. Build scenes as transactions with a social surface — the bribe offered as a courtesy, the theft committed to cover a family obligation, the silence kept because speaking would cost a place in the hierarchy that cannot be bought back. What each character calculates should be visible in how they perform it: seating and deferral, the careful compliment, the errand that is really a message.\n\nCarry the genre through consequence rather than verdict. The account may follow the offender, the victim, or whoever is asking questions, but keep legible what the offense does to a household, a workshop, a street: the credit that dries up, the marriage quietly renegotiated, the person who stops being invited. Punishment is not guaranteed, and the sharpest damage may be a loss of standing, trust, or livelihood rather than a charge. Satire here is precision about self-justification — the embezzler who experiences himself as the injured party — not a parade of comic types.\n\nMake money and obligation concrete. Amounts, repayment terms, favors owed and called in, the going cost of silence: these are this story's physical facts, its equivalents of wounds and weapons. If the viewpoint is interior, let it weigh obligation in the character's own arithmetic — what a debt costs, whom a favor shames — rather than in commentary. Let reputations be managed in dialogue and small arrangements rather than explained in summary, and let relatives, neighbors, and coworkers operate as the surveillance a small economy runs on.\n\nUse the language, period, and community the story actually supplies. The route is a method of attention — concrete local detail, the mechanics of debt and reputation, a steady eye on how people maintain appearances — not permission to relocate the cast or import a setting and identity from an example.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadauad::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / No Named Influence / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nTell the crime in hard particulars. What the offense costs and what it leaves behind: a scratched lock, a stained jacket, a cracked phone, a payment that comes up short. Let objects carry information another passage would state outright — the condition of a room, a vehicle, a pair of hands can say what happened there without a summary. Write forceful verbs and compressed transitions that do not smooth the gaps: a character enters, registers the broken window, and decides to leave, and the three sentences contain the whole decision.\n\nDeliver reasoning through selection rather than explanation. What the character notices and what they do next is the thought: a glance held on the wrong detail, a question not asked, a route changed mid-street. Trust juxtaposition to make cause legible and let the reader assemble the inference.\n\nKeep the stakes physical and real without making them uniformly lethal: a job lost, a debt called in, a beating threatened, a witness too frightened to stay, a plan failing because someone is too tired or too slow. Ground rough or abrasive speech in its speaker. A threat sounds different from the voice of someone who has been threatened; an officer's impatience is not a debtor's fear; a professional swears out of habit, an amateur out of panic.\n\nBreak the compression when the character's reasoning genuinely needs room. Weighing a risk, recognizing a lie, deciding whether someone can be trusted — these can take a longer passage, and it earns the length by changing the next action. Otherwise the beat stays short; the rhythm follows where the decision lives.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadauae::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / No Named Influence / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the crime in documents: a witness statement, a ledger entry, a letter, an incident report, a text exchange, a note left behind. Give each record an author, a recipient or purpose, and a moment of composition, and let those three facts govern what it contains. An official report flattens a frightened witness into procedure; a suspect's affectionate letter omits exactly the detail the reader needs; a ledger records the payment without the favor it bought; a private note is frank in ways nothing addressed outward could be. A later document can correct, contradict, or quietly reinterpret an earlier one, and the earlier record still stands on the page as written.\n\nLet gaps and material traces do the evidentiary work: the missing page, the message still in drafts, the date that cannot be right, the second signature with no reason to be there, the crossed-out line still legible. Do not step outside the records to explain what these mean. Order the entries so the reader can compare them and notice what their authors did not.\n\nArrange the documents for followable continuity even when the accounts conflict — chronological, or as an investigator, lawyer, or heir assembles them, whichever the situation supplies. A minimal orienting line of who wrote, to whom, and when can head a record; nothing more omniscient. Let relationships and aftermath live inside the forms: the letters that get shorter as the writer gives up, the report whose tone hardens between the first interview and the second, the receipt that puts someone where they swore they never were.\n\nUse the form each scene actually requires rather than rotating the available formats. A reply may end with a record still being composed — an unanswered draft, an unfinalized report, an open ledger — as naturally as with a filed one.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadauaf::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / No Named Influence / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nKeep the offense, its consequences, and its competing interests fully active, and spend close attention on the care that happens inside that situation. Someone brings food to a person in hiding and knows which kind. Someone sits through the night with a frightened witness, remembers what that person needs to hear before they can sleep, takes the chair by the door because their friend watches the door. These are not interludes between the real business; they are how particular people endure a real business.\n\nLet affection appear as specific knowledge. How someone takes their tea, which route they refuse to walk, the joke that works when nothing else does, the meal that tastes like before. A familiar pleasure rendered during a difficult errand shows exactly what a character stands to lose, and it makes a later risk concrete instead of abstract.\n\nKeep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact. Comfort does not make anyone agreeable: a character can accept the food and refuse the question; a caretaker can be warm and still withhold what they know; two people can argue about the plan while one of them quietly repacks the other's bag. Do not require a confession, a reconciliation, or even gratitude; help can be accepted without a word about it. The warmth neither solves the case nor suspends the danger — the debt is still owed, the search continues, the threat does not respect tender moments — and it is not punished for existing either.\n\nAnchor scenes in the genre's actual work: waiting for news, cleaning up after a break-in, hiding someone, packing to leave, keeping an appointment despite fear. The comfort operates inside these tasks, and the small pleasure is real precisely because the reason it is needed is also real.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadauag::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / No Named Influence / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite the crime in composed, continuous novel prose: deliberate transitions, sustained paragraphs, narration distinct from ordinary speech. Open scenes with enough distance to seat the offense in its setting — the district that has agreed not to notice, the family for whom scandal is unaffordable, the institution whose paperwork begins softening the event the moment it is recorded. This framing is where the style does its work: the reader receives the crime already interpreted by a world.\n\nUse the distance the active narrator actually has. A permitted third-person voice may connect a gesture to class, generation, or a particular history; a first-person voice may frame events through memory and judgment, returning to the evening with later understanding. Keep the register above casual dialogue without turning uniformly ornate or archaic; the prose can be exacting while the dialogue stays in each speaker's own mouth.\n\nLet available structures organize passages where the story supplies them: a formal interview, a letter read twice, a meal interrupted by news, a public statement that quietly contradicts a private conversation. Public and private contrast is a natural engine in this treatment — what is said at the door against what is said in the kitchen — and a scene can cross that boundary within one paragraph.\n\nKeep the criminal sequence exact beneath the framing. Preparation, incentives, execution, evidence, pursuit, aftermath: who knew what, and when, remains concrete. A paragraph may travel from private hesitation to public consequence in a few sentences, but the reader finishes it knowing what happened and what changed. The architecture frames the crime; it does not replace it with atmosphere.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadauah::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / No Named Influence / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the crime story as an account being given to a particular audience on a particular occasion, where the telling itself is part of the criminal situation. The narrator may be a culprit, accomplice, victim, investigator, or bystander, but the listeners are not neutral recipients: they have a stake in what they hear, and the storyteller has a reason to shape the account. Establish early why the story is being told now, to these people, and what the teller hopes to gain, prevent, delay, or set in motion.\n\nLet the outer frame carry the present pressure. Between embedded passages, return to the listeners' visible responses, interruptions, questions, or silences. A listener may challenge a detail, demand a name, or react to a sum of money, a broken promise, or a description of someone they know. These returns should not merely pause the tale; they should show the audience weighing the account against their own interests and knowledge.\n\nWhen a nested account appears, make it intervene in the outer situation. A story within the story can persuade a listener to cooperate, warn someone about a danger, justify a past betrayal, explain why a debt went unpaid, or shift blame onto a person who is present. The embedded account should change how at least one listener reads the outer events, even when its facts remain disputed.\n\nUse recurrence to build the reader's ability to compare versions. Repeat a concrete phrase, object, location, or gesture across narrative levels so that its later reappearance revises an earlier impression. For example, a repeated description of a locked door, a shared meal, a phone call, or a particular injury can mean one thing in the teller's first version and something else when another character supplies the surrounding circumstances. The repetition is a device for delayed revelation, not a guarantee that the teller is lying or truthful.\n\nKeep the delayed revelation tied to criminal consequences. What is withheld may be the identity of a participant, the real sequence of an offense, the location of money or evidence, or the reason a witness stayed silent. When the revelation arrives, it should explain a previously puzzling choice or relationship in the outer frame, not merely decorate the tale. The reader may know more than the listeners at any point, but the listeners' changing understanding should remain legible.\n\nPreserve the difference between what a character knew at the time of the offense and what the teller has learned since. A retrospective account can include later knowledge, but it must not quietly turn that later knowledge into an earlier motive. If the teller now understands something that was unclear during the crime, show that understanding as a later acquisition and let it affect the present telling.\n\nDo not import a royal court, magical bargain, djinn, or compulsory cliffhanger between every nested account. The frame is a practical social occasion: a back room, a kitchen, a car, a holding cell, a family gathering, a meeting between former partners. The storytelling tradition supplies the craft of situated narration, recurrence, and delayed revelation, not a fixed setting or supernatural apparatus.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadauai::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / No Named Influence / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social or developmental realism route. Treat the offense not as an isolated incident but as an event with a traceable social history: the conditions, obligations, ambitions, and practical constraints that made a particular transgression possible, and the ways its consequences move through a community, workplace, family, or institution. The narration should make the connection between ordinary life and criminal action concrete rather than symbolic.\n\nChoose a viewpoint that lets the reader see the developmental process. A culprit's account can show how small decisions accumulate into an offense; an accomplice's account can reveal divided incentives; a victim's or bystander's account can show how the aftermath reshapes daily routines; an investigator's account can show how an institution's procedures interact with the lives they touch. Whatever the viewpoint, keep the character's social position specific: their work, housing, debts, family obligations, legal status, or institutional role should affect what they notice, what they can do, and what the crime costs them.\n\nLet syntax and narrative distance follow the chosen teller and their social situation. A narrator close to the events may use longer, accumulative sentences that register the pressure of circumstances; a more distant or retrospective narrator may organize the account into clear stages of before, during, and after. Do not impose a bureaucratic or severe tone. The realism lies in the precision of social observation, not in emotional coldness.\n\nShow preparation and incentive as lived experience. A character may plan an offense because a specific obligation has become unmanageable, or drift into it through a series of smaller compromises. The story should make the causal chain legible without excusing the offense: readers should understand why this person, in this situation, took this step, while still seeing the harm it causes.\n\nTreat the aftermath as part of the crime, not an epilogue. The offense changes relationships, exposes hidden dependencies, or forces other characters to make choices they had avoided. Follow those consequences far enough to show what the crime has set in motion. Suspense can come from exposure, divided loyalty, or the slow recognition of what has been lost, even when the reader knows who committed the offense.\n\nKeep the setting and community specific without turning them into national stereotypes. Use the actual supplied language, region, period, and community. If the story is set in a German-speaking context, let its institutions, housing, labor conditions, or legal arrangements be concrete; if it is set elsewhere, apply the same social realism to the actual setting rather than importing a generic German atmosphere. Do not equate German-language writing with one country, bureaucracy, or translated word order.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadauaj::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / No Named Influence / Gothic-Punk\n\nWrite the crime story as a collision between old authority and present life. The offense, scheme, or investigation should bring an inherited arrangement—an old family claim, a long-standing institution, a building with a history, a debt passed down, a title or reputation that still commands access—into contact with the practical realities of the present. The crime emerges from that collision: someone tries to use the old authority, escape it, exploit it, or finally settle what it owes.\n\nGive the urban setting material specificity. Describe the physical places where the crime is prepared, executed, or investigated: the condition of a stairwell, the light in a back office, the smell of a basement, the way a locked gate has been repaired over decades. These details should not merely create mood; they should show how the environment enables or constrains action, who has access, and what has decayed or been maintained.\n\nLet private desire and morally complicated intimacy drive the characters' choices. A culprit may act out of loyalty, resentment, hunger, or a need to be recognized by someone who holds power over them. An accomplice may cooperate for reasons that mix affection, fear, and self-interest. A victim may be entangled with the offender in ways that make the harm harder to name. Do not reduce these relationships to simple betrayal or corruption; let them remain specific and difficult.\n\nShow social masks and the conditions under which they fall away. Characters may present competence, deference, indifference, or control in public or institutional settings, then reveal a different need or fear in trusted company. The mask is not automatically a lie; it can be a survival strategy, a habit, or a role the character half-believes. When a mask falls, let it happen in a particular encounter and let the revelation affect the criminal situation.\n\nLet atmosphere change how a particular encounter is experienced. The same room can feel like shelter or a trap depending on who enters it and what they know. Use atmospheric description to register a character's perception of danger, intimacy, exposure, or relief, but keep the underlying facts stable. A frightening place is not evidence of a crime, and a comforting one is not proof of safety.\n\nDo not import Kindred physiology, Humanity numbers, factions, powers, or blood-bond rules. The style contributes a narrative treatment of inherited power, modern decay, intimate politics, and social masks, not a supernatural setting. The crime remains a human offense with human causes and consequences, however old the authority it disturbs.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadauak::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / No Named Influence / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the testimonial and autobiographical account route. Write the crime story as a specific participant's retrospective testimony: a culprit, accomplice, victim, investigator, or bystander is telling or recording what happened, why it happened, and what it has done to them and to others. The account should be situated in a particular community, region, language, and period, with the teller's position inside that world made concrete.\n\nEstablish the occasion and purpose of the testimony. The teller may be speaking to an official, writing a statement, answering a family member, or setting down a record for someone who will come later. The occasion affects what the teller includes, what they apologize for, what they accuse others of, and what they still cannot say. The testimony is not a neutral transcript; it is an act with consequences in the present.\n\nKeep the teller's access and knowledge limited and specific. The account should distinguish what the teller saw or did from what they were told, what they assumed, and what they learned afterward. When the teller now understands something they did not understand at the time of the offense, show that later knowledge as a later acquisition. Do not let it quietly become the motive they had all along.\n\nLet the aftermath carry the weight of the crime. The testimony should show how the offense changed the teller's relationships, standing, work, or sense of home. Suspense can come from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did it: the teller may be deciding how much to reveal, protecting someone, or trying to make a listener understand an act that has already happened.\n\nKeep language, region, community, and period specific when supplied. If the story is set in a particular Indian context, let its actual languages, neighborhoods, occupations, legal or social institutions, and family arrangements shape the account. Do not force English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure onto the scene. If the setting is elsewhere, apply the same testimonial precision to that actual community rather than importing an Indian backdrop.\n\nDo not reduce the account to a single lesson or a confession that resolves everything. The teller may remain uncertain, defensive, or divided. The testimony should make the crime and its human aftermath concrete, not turn the teller into a moral authority whose word settles every question.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaual::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / No Named Influence / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social or psychological realism route. Write the crime story by tracing how a particular offense emerges from the social pressures and private reasoning of the people involved. The story should show what each central character wants, fears, owes, or resents, and how those feelings interact with the practical demands of work, family, money, reputation, or obligation.\n\nLet personal language and narrative distance follow the actual teller. A culprit's close narration may register the small rationalizations and hesitations that precede the offense; a bystander's more distant account may observe the aftermath without fully understanding its causes; an investigator's account may move between official procedure and private doubt. Do not impose a uniform quietness or restraint. Direct feeling, plain argument, and even exuberance are available where the character and situation support them.\n\nUse omission as a meaningful narrative choice. A character may leave something out of an account because they are ashamed, because they do not yet understand it, or because they are protecting someone. The omission should be legible to the reader through what surrounds it: a sudden shift in topic, a detail that is described for everything except the crucial moment, or a question the character refuses to answer even in private thought. Omission is not the same as vagueness; the reader should sense what is being avoided and why it matters.\n\nShow preparation and incentive as lived experience rather than as a case summary. A character may plan an offense carefully or drift into it through a series of smaller choices. The story should make the causal chain concrete: a specific debt, a particular humiliation, a threatened relationship, a chance opportunity, or a long-held resentment becomes the pressure that turns thought into action.\n\nTreat the aftermath as part of the crime's meaning. The offense changes how the characters see one another, what they can say, and what they must now do. Follow those changes far enough to show the human cost, but do not force a punishment or a moral resolution. The story may end with uncertainty, exposure, silence, or a relationship permanently altered.\n\nKeep the setting specific without turning it into a scenery inventory. If the story is set in a Japanese context, let its actual workplaces, housing, seasons, or social expectations shape the characters' choices; if it is set elsewhere, apply the same social and psychological realism to that actual setting. Seasonal attention is available where it matters to the characters, not as a mandatory descriptive pause. Do not import a generic Japanese atmosphere or a universal quietness.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadauam::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / No Named Influence / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social realism, chronicle, or testimony route. Write the crime story as a situated social record: an offense and its aftermath are documented through the lives of the people involved, the institutions that respond or fail to respond, and the community that absorbs the consequences. The account should make the crime's social causes and costs concrete rather than treating it as an isolated private act.\n\nChoose a viewpoint that can carry the chronicle. A culprit, accomplice, victim, investigator, or bystander may record what happened, but the telling should register the wider social world: the neighborhood, workplace, family network, legal system, market, or political situation that shapes what people can do and what the crime means. The narrator's own position inside that world should affect what they notice, what they can say, and what they hope the record will accomplish.\n\nPreserve the relevant language, community, and history. If the story is set in a particular Latin American context, let its actual languages, neighborhoods, economic conditions, institutions, and historical pressures shape the account. Do not import a generic Latin American atmosphere or reduce the setting to scenery. If the story is set elsewhere, apply the same social realism to that actual community rather than relocating the cast.\n\nTreat evidence, pursuit, and aftermath as social processes. An investigation may be shaped by who has access to officials, who can pay, who is believed, and who is afraid to speak. The aftermath may include displacement, debt, retaliation, solidarity, or a community's effort to remember or forget. Show these processes through specific actions and relationships, not through abstract commentary.\n\nLet the chronicle distinguish what the narrator saw or did from what they were told, assumed, or learned later. A testimony can be partial without being dishonest. When the narrator now understands something they did not understand at the time, show that later knowledge as a later acquisition and let it affect the present record.\n\nDo not add supernatural facts to a world that excludes them. The marvelous treated as ordinary is a different route and is not selected here. The crime remains a human event with human causes, however strange or unjust its circumstances. Suspense can come from exposure, divided loyalty, or the slow revelation of what the community already knew, even when the reader knows who committed the offense.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadauan::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / No Named Influence / Light Novel\n\nWrite the crime story with fast-readable prose and strong visual staging. Enter each scene cleanly: establish where the characters are, who is present, and what immediate pressure or task is in front of them. Keep spatial changes easy to follow by marking movement between locations with clear transitions, so the reader always knows whether the action is happening in a planning meeting, a stakeout, a back room, a vehicle, or the aftermath of an offense.\n\nUse readable paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response. Give action its own short paragraphs so physical movement stays legible. Let dialogue carry the immediate exchange, then use a focused reaction beat to show what a character privately makes of what was said or done. The reaction should belong to its owner: it can be serious, uncertain, angry, frightened, or funny according to that character's actual situation and temperament. Do not make every character witty or every reaction a punchline.\n\nGive unfamiliar names or mechanics clear presentation when needed. If the crime involves a particular scheme, tool, legal process, or organizational structure, introduce it in a way the reader can follow without a lecture. A character can explain a mechanic to someone who needs to know it, or the narration can register the relevant detail at the moment it matters. Keep the explanation brief and tied to the present action.\n\nPreserve the crime genre's attention to preparation, incentives, relationships, execution, and aftermath. The fast pacing should not erase the reasons people act. Show what a character wants, fears, owes, or resents before or during the offense. A scheme can be laid out in a planning scene; a relationship can be shown through a short exchange that reveals divided loyalty; an aftermath can be staged as a scene of exposure, flight, silence, or consequence.\n\nLet suspense come from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did it. The tension can live in whether a character will be found out, whether an accomplice will stay quiet, or whether a victim will recognize what has happened. Do not require a mystery puzzle or a closing cliffhanger. A scene can end when its immediate pressure is registered, leaving the next turn open.\n\nDo not impose first person, comedy, a young cast, or a cliffhanger. The container supplies readable pacing, visual clarity, and focused reaction beats; the narrator, tone, cast, and ending follow the actual story and its characters.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadauao::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / No Named Influence / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet the crime's material traces become the lyric material of the telling. A stain, a receipt, a locked drawer, a repeated knock, or a street seen twice can gather meaning through syntax and sound rather than through explanation. When a detail returns, change what it makes present: the same object may first be noticed, then remembered, then understood as evidence, then understood as something the teller had already begun to lose. Let the recurrence carry the investigation's movement instead of announcing each development.\n\nShape the paragraph around the teller's attention. A comparison may open from an observed fact and move inward until it alters what the narrator understands; after that, return to the scene with the changed perception still active. Plain sentences can interrupt the rhythm to record a hard fact, a time, a sum, a name, or an act. The contrast between plain statement and figurative passage should feel like the difference between what can be said and what keeps unfolding around it.\n\nKeep suspense in the ordering of attention rather than in withheld facts alone. The reader may know who did it and still feel the weight of a door closing, the pause before a lie, or the way an ordinary object becomes impossible to touch. Do not make every character speak in the narrator's figurative register. Their dialogue may be blunt, evasive, or practical while the narration continues its own associative work. Preserve the selected narrator's access and knowledge. The passage can end while an action, thought, or waiting continues, leaving the image to hold the consequence without resolving it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadauap::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / No Named Influence / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the crime enter as a change the narrator is still measuring. A place, a routine, a phrase, or a relationship may now be understood in two times at once: as it was before the offense and as it has become after it. Do not explain the change immediately. Let the narrator notice a small present detail and then recognize what it used to mean, so that the difference becomes the substance of the scene.\n\nUse remembered occasions as evidence of what has altered. A person may recall an ordinary moment that now seems like the last time something was intact. The recollection need not be dramatic; its force comes from the narrator's awareness that the moment was not recognized as final when it happened. Keep present experience available alongside memory. A meal, a walk, a conversation, or a task can remain genuinely present even while the narrator reflects on what is gone.\n\nLet the investigation or aftermath move through these reflections rather than pause for them. A question asked now may recall an earlier question asked in trust. A name repeated in a report may sound different from the same name spoken in a former intimacy. The reader may know the outcome and still follow the narrator's slow recognition of what the outcome has cost. Do not require every scene to end by announcing impermanence. The passage may close with the narrator still attending to something present, aware of its altered meaning without needing to state the loss completely.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadauaq::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / No Named Influence / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a situated oral or framed account as the definite regional route. The crime reaches the reader through a teller speaking or writing on a particular occasion: a visitor asked to explain what happened, a family member recalling an offense, a neighbor giving an account to someone who was absent, or a written record addressed to a later reader. Let the frame remain active. The teller may pause, correct a detail, admit uncertainty, or withhold something until the listener asks. Delayed revelation should arise from the relationship between teller and audience, not from an arbitrary refusal to state known facts.\n\nGround the account in social, domestic, or urban realism. The offense matters through its effects on a household, a street, a workplace, a debt, a marriage, a migration, or a local obligation. Use exact practical details: who was present, what was owed, which door was left open, what was said in the kitchen or the shop. Let these details carry the incentives and consequences. Do not import a generic regional atmosphere, an automatic honor code, or a mystical frame unless the supplied world already contains them.\n\nKeep the teller's knowledge and access consistent. A framed narrator may report what others said, but reported speech is not the same as direct knowledge. Distinguish what the teller saw, what was heard from another, and what is being inferred now. The crime may remain partly unresolved in the telling, with the listener or reader left to weigh the account. The passage can end while the teller is still speaking, while the audience is still deciding, or while an unfinished consequence remains in the room.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadauar::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / No Named Influence / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose a small number of exact details and let them carry the crime's movement. One object, one repeated phrase, one visible change, or one practical arrangement can stand for a larger sequence without being explained. When a detail returns, its meaning should be inferable from what has happened since. Do not add a second detail that repeats the first one's function.\n\nUse direct clauses to record action, speech, and consequence. Keep the causal chain present: who wanted something, what they did, what it made possible, and what followed. An essential inward connection may be stated plainly and at sufficient length to make sense, but do not explain a motive twice or translate a concrete act into its moral meaning. Let the reader see that a character is afraid, obligated, or relieved from what the character does and notices.\n\nLet dialogue remain individual and economical. A speaker may say less than the narration knows. Omission should leave something available to inference, not remove the reason an event happened. The passage can end after a decisive act, a spoken line, or a visible consequence, without a summary. Do not force fragmentary sentences or a word-count quota. A precise extended sentence may serve the scene better than several short ones.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadauas::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / No Named Influence / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the crime as a continuous scene rather than a case file. Begin inside a particular moment: a preparation, a confrontation, a discovery, a return, or a conversation that changes what someone can do next. Move naturally among what the viewpoint character sees, says, remembers, and privately understands. Let paragraphing and transitions orient the reader without recapping what the scene has already shown.\n\nKeep the criminal material concrete. Incentives should appear through what people want and what they are willing to do for it. Relationships should appear through what they make possible or dangerous. Evidence should appear through what someone notices, handles, avoids, or explains. Pursuit and aftermath should appear through practical choices and their consequences. The reader may know who did it; the scene can still hold attention through exposure, divided loyalty, or the cost of a decision.\n\nUse authorized inner experience to show how the viewpoint character weighs an act or reads another person. The narration may reveal a motive that other characters do not know. Do not grant access to excluded minds, and do not let the style turn every pause into a lesson. The register follows the narrator and the immediate scene: it may be plain, intimate, tense, or dry. The passage can end while an action, conversation, or private realization is still in motion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadauat::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / No Named Influence / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nLet the established relationship premise choose which scenes of the crime matter. If the premise is loyalty, show what loyalty costs when someone learns what the other has done. If it is rivalry, show how the offense changes what each can admit to wanting. If it is comfort, show what comfort can and cannot repair after a criminal act. Do not import a generic set of tropes. Use only the relationship actually supplied by the story.\n\nGive private language and repeated moments room to carry the relationship's history. A phrase, a gesture, a place, or a habit may return with a new meaning because of the crime. The callback should fulfill the relationship's particular promise: it may expose a division, confirm an attachment, or make a previously ordinary moment into something one character now understands differently. Let affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery occupy the space it needs without pausing the crime's consequences.\n\nKeep the offense and its aftermath causally active. The relationship is not a refuge from the plot; it is one of the places where the plot happens. A character may protect, suspect, confront, forgive, or refuse to forgive. The reader may know the culprit and still feel the weight of what the other person now knows or cannot say. Preserve the selected narrator's access. The passage can end on an unfinished exchange, a repeated gesture, or a consequence still settling between them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadauau::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / No Named Influence / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the crime as an account of the deed by which these people will be remembered. Open with a compact declaration that establishes the act or its consequence, then expand into the particulars that make the reputation incomplete. A theft, a betrayal, a killing, a false accusation, or a broken obligation may become the deed around which later accounts gather. Keep the present scene's scale. A small offense remains small even when it enters a longer history.\n\nUse recurring phrasing to mark what is remembered and what is disputed. A name, a place, a promise, or a description of the act may return when a later occurrence gives it a new application. Let shared reputation differ from private knowledge. Being remembered as the one who did the deed does not mean the deed was simple, willing, or fully understood. The teller may relate what was seen, what was said, and what people later claimed, without granting the chronicle access to every mind.\n\nLet dialogue interrupt the authority of the retelling. A participant may correct a detail, deny a motive, or answer an elevated introduction with a practical concern. This need not become parody. The crime's consequences remain concrete: a debt, a feud, a flight, a return, a punishment, or a refusal to punish. Do not import heroic doom or ceremonial speech into every speaker. The account can end with the deed still being weighed, another act available to be remembered, or the consequence still unfolding among those who remain.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadauav::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / No Named Influence / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social-institutional route of the Scandinavian tradition: the offense becomes legible as a disturbance in the practical arrangements of a specific community, and the account follows the people whose ordinary work brings them into contact with it. Choose the viewpoint from the culprit, accomplice, victim, investigator, or bystander, and let the narration stay close to what that person can observe, remember, and infer. Do not import a generic Nordic landscape, climate, or temperament; the setting, language, period, and community already supplied by the story remain the only source of local detail.\n\nLet the investigation proceed through concrete civic and domestic work rather than through explanatory speeches. Show who must be notified, which records or keys or rooms must be checked, what a workplace or household needs to keep running, and which practical question the next step answers. A witness can answer plainly, withhold for a supported reason, or challenge the premise of a question because of an actual relationship or institutional position. The social world does not pause for the crime; people keep appointments, manage households, and maintain ordinary obligations while the consequences of the offense move through their institutions.\n\nUse the inward Scandinavian narrative mode as the place where the crime's human weight accumulates. Let the viewpoint's private reasoning, sensation, memory, and uncertainty appear in the active presentation's authorized interiority, but keep access and knowledge separate. A focal suspicion, motive, or inference belongs to that character alone and does not become public knowledge merely because the reader has followed its formation. Other characters remain distinct through what the viewpoint perceives, recalls, or is told about them.\n\nBuild the causal chain through social detail that can be revisited and revised. Who was present, what they were doing, what they noticed, what they told others, and what records or arrangements changed afterward should remain available when a later fact changes what an earlier observation suggests. Repetition should deepen understanding of the community and its relationships rather than restart the same suspicion. The scene can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known; it does not require a confession, a reversal, or a completed test in every reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadauaw::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / No Named Influence / Screenplay\n\nWrite the crime story as readable screen scenes. Use scene headings when location or time changes, concise action lines for visible and audible events, and clear speaker cues for dialogue. Keep the action blocks concrete: what a person does, handles, enters, leaves, hears, or says. Do not fill the page with camera commands. Use cuts, offscreen sound, or voice-over only when the chosen sequence needs them, and let each choice serve the crime's information flow rather than decorate the scene.\n\nLet the offense, scheme, criminal milieu, or its consequences stay legible through what can be seen and heard. Preparation may appear as a person gathering objects, waiting in a place, or giving instructions. Incentives and relationships may appear through exchanges, arrivals, refusals, or the way someone responds to a request. Execution and aftermath may appear through the visible result, the people who arrive afterward, and the practical work of responding. Evidence belongs to the scene when a character finds, examines, or discusses it, not when the narration asserts it invisibly.\n\nUse the screen's selective visibility to create suspense without cheating. A cut can move from a person's action to its visible consequence, leaving the intervening moment offscreen only when the later scene supplies what the reader needs to understand it. An offscreen sound can announce an event before the characters see it, but it must remain audible in the scene rather than become a hidden fact. A voice-over may carry authorized private reasoning only when separately authorized; otherwise, preserve required private reasoning as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation. That note is not dialogue, voice-over, or knowledge available to the other characters.\n\nLet spoken lines reflect the speaker's actual relationship and position. A witness can answer plainly, withhold for a supported reason, or challenge a question without the scene turning into an explanatory speech. Ordinary life continues around the crime: people keep appointments, manage practical tasks, and respond to interruptions while the consequences of the offense move through their relationships. A scene can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. It does not require a confession, a reversal, or a new clue in every reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadauax::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / No Named Influence / Stage Play\n\nWrite the crime story as a playable script. Use clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be staged. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene. Do not add film-camera instructions. A location change is established by the stage directions and the characters' movement, not by a cut.\n\nMake the shared stage space testable for the crime's preparation, execution, and aftermath. Who can see an entrance, reach an object, hear a line, or move between two places should be clear enough that a proposed account changes what is plausible. Preparation may appear as a character handling an object, waiting, or giving instructions. Execution and aftermath may appear through the visible result, the people who arrive afterward, and the practical work of responding. Evidence belongs to the scene when a character finds, examines, or discusses it.\n\nLet the stage's physical constraints create the crime's suspense. A prop that changes position, an entrance that is blocked or left open, a pause that lets a line land, or a character's movement across the space can make a later account testable. When a scene revisits the same space, use a familiar object or position as an anchor, and let its new significance be earned by a later fact rather than restaged as the same suspicion. Repetition should deepen understanding of the relationships and the physical conditions, not restart the same accusation.\n\nPreserve required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation. Do not disguise it as an aside, monologue, or line another character can hear. A monologue or aside may be used when it has a dramatic purpose, but it is performed speech, not the default destination for thought. Another character does not know a focal suspicion, motive, or inference merely because the reader has followed its formation.\n\nLet spoken questions and answers fit the relationship and the immediate physical task. A witness can answer plainly, withhold for a supported reason, or challenge the premise of a question without the scene turning into an explanatory speech. Ordinary life continues around the crime: people keep appointments, manage practical tasks, and respond to interruptions. The scene can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. It does not require a theatrical revelation, a confession, or a new clue in every reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadauay::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / No Named Influence / Stream of Consciousness\n\nLet the crime story move through the associations of a particular authorized mind. Braid perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern so that the offense, its preparation, its incentives, and its aftermath arrive as the viewpoint actually experiences them. Syntax and paragraph shape may follow those associations, but keep enough orientation to distinguish what is happening from what is remembered, imagined, or feared. Ordinary reasoning alone is not this stylistic mode; the movement should be associative, not merely a tidy summary of thoughts.\n\nKeep the crime's causal chain legible within that movement by anchoring each significant event in a concrete sensory or bodily detail. Preparation may surface as a remembered errand, a bodily alertness, or a recurring object. Incentives and relationships may surface through a phrase that returns, a sensation that accompanies a person, or a memory that interrupts the present. Execution and aftermath may surface through what the viewpoint notices afterward, what the body registers, and what the mind keeps circling back to. Evidence and pursuit remain available as events and inferences, not as indeterminate scene facts. A later realization cannot be retroactively inserted as an earlier motive.\n\nLet the associative movement itself do the work of suspense and exposure. A recurring concern may return under different conditions, a bodily sensation may accompany a place or person, and an interruption may pull the mind from one subject to another for a supported reason. When a memory or sensation returns, let it carry the earlier event forward rather than restart the same suspicion. The reader should be able to follow which details belong to the present, which belong to the past, and which belong to fear or fantasy, even when the syntax blurs the boundary.\n\nDo not make every character share a mind or grant access to excluded minds. The authorized viewpoint's private experience belongs to that character alone. Other people remain distinct through what the viewpoint perceives, recalls, or imagines about them. A focal suspicion or inference may be available to the reader without becoming knowledge available to the other characters. The response can close on a completed thought, a pending question, or plain acknowledgment of what the mind keeps returning to. It does not require a confession, a reversal, or a completed test in every reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadauaz::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / No Named Influence / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the crime story through readable sender-attributed messages. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the offense, its preparation, its incentives, its relationships, its execution, its evidence, its pursuit, or its aftermath. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one may write in fragments, another in full sentences, another may delay replies or edit after sending. Do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext.\n\nLet the transcript's interface events carry the crime's suspense and exposure. A delay may become significant when it contradicts a sender's stated availability, an edit may change what a recipient could have known at a given time, a read receipt may establish that someone saw a message before acting, and an attachment may alter what a recipient can verify. Each such event must remain legible as part of the exchange, not as a hidden fact the narration asserts. When a later message changes what an earlier one suggested, let the reader recover the necessary particular from the transcript itself rather than from a summary.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought. A sent message is what the other characters can actually receive. Private reasoning, suspicion, or motive may appear only when required, clearly separated from the transcript, and in the active presentation's authorized form. It is not transmitted knowledge available to the other senders.\n\nLet the crime's causal chain remain legible through what people actually transmit, delay, receive, or leave unsent. Preparation may appear as a message arranging a meeting, requesting an object, or giving instructions. Incentives and relationships may appear through what someone chooses to say, omit, or postpone. Execution and aftermath may appear through the messages sent afterward, the people who go quiet, and the practical arrangements that follow. Evidence may appear as a message that contradicts an earlier one, a delay that matters, or an attachment that changes what a recipient knows.\n\nContext outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript. It can establish where someone is, what they are doing, or what they can see without turning into a full scene. The response can close on a sent message, a pending reply, an unsent draft, or plain acknowledgment of what the exchange has now established. It does not require a confession, a reversal, or a new clue in every reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadauba::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / No Named Influence / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the crime story in scene-oriented visual-novel presentation. Use concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Keep scene changes legible. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected; those require their own authorization or feature.\n\nLet the offense, scheme, criminal milieu, or its consequences stay legible through the scenes. Preparation may appear as a character arriving somewhere, handling an object, or giving instructions. Incentives and relationships may appear through exchanges, refusals, or the way someone responds to a request. Execution and aftermath may appear through the visible result, the people who arrive afterward, and the practical work of responding. Evidence belongs to the scene when a character finds, examines, or discusses it, not when the narration asserts it invisibly.\n\nUse background or expression cues as purposeful references that orient the moment. A changed expression can attract suspicion, but a contradiction in an arrangement or account must do the evidentiary work. Do not turn the cast into fixed reaction portraits whose expression always reveals whether they lie. Let a familiar background detail serve as an anchor when a scene revisits a place, and let its new significance be earned by a later fact rather than restaged as the same suspicion.\n\nLet the focal narration and inner response carry the crime's private weight. A focal suspicion, motive, or inference belongs to the authorized interiority in the active presentation and does not become public knowledge merely because the reader has followed its formation. The narration may show how the viewpoint explains another person's behavior, and the next exchange may support that explanation, complicate it, or reveal that the speaker was concerned with something else entirely.\n\nLet ordinary life continue around the crime. People keep appointments, manage practical tasks, and respond to interruptions while the consequences of the offense move through their relationships. A scene can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. It does not require a confession, a reversal, or a new clue in every reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaubb::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / No Named Influence / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across replies: what is underway in the offense, scheme, criminal milieu, or its consequences, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover context briefly only when it helps. Do not make every turn a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger. Progress can involve knowledge, relationships, work, or resources. The style does not automatically create leveling systems, rankings, or progression-fantasy world rules.\n\nLet an exact earlier observation remain available when a later installment changes what it suggests. A person uses the entrance previously described, asks for the object another character mentioned, or answers a familiar question under a different condition. Briefly recover the necessary particular rather than retelling the investigation that first established it. New evidence can qualify an old explanation without replacing the old event or making the viewpoint deliberately forget a supported conclusion.\n\nKeep the crime's causal chain legible across installments by letting each reply carry forward a specific piece of the offense's preparation, incentive, relationship, execution, evidence, pursuit, or aftermath. When an installment opens, establish what is underway and what has changed since the last reply. When it closes, leave the reader with a concrete point of continuity: a pending request, an unanswered question, a changed relationship, or a piece of work still to be done. The closure should arise from the actual scene, not from a manufactured hook.\n\nLet the serial form create suspense through accumulation rather than through withholding. A detail that seemed ordinary in an earlier installment may become significant when a later fact changes its meaning. The reader should be able to recover that detail from the text itself, not from a summary. Ordinary life continues around the crime: people keep appointments, manage practical tasks, and respond to interruptions while the consequences of the offense move through their relationships.\n\nPreserve required private reasoning in the active presentation's authorized form. A focal suspicion, motive, or inference may be available to the reader without becoming knowledge available to the other characters. The installment can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. It does not require a confession, a reversal, or a new clue in every reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadavaa::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Quentin Tarantino / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nTreat emotional exposure as a move inside the crime story's exchanges. A confession, an apology, a demand for reassurance, or a sudden admission can be used to buy time, shift blame, test loyalty, soften a witness, or force someone else to show what they need. The other speaker may answer the surface question while dodging the underlying one, and the scene should make clear which level each person is actually playing.\n\nLet the conversation carry the strain. A pause before an answer, a change in address, a too-casual detail, or a shift from banter to accusation can mark where a relationship is fraying. Do not require every exchange to end in a wound. The more turbulent beat can be the one where a character starts to say the true thing, then turns away into a practical task, a question about the job, or a silence the other person decides not to break.\n\nStage the aftermath in behavior, not only in stated feeling. After a hard exchange, the characters may separate into the practical work of the crime—checking a route, cleaning a weapon, waiting for a call, counting money—and their attention keeps snagging on what was said. A small gesture of care can be offered and refused, accepted awkwardly, or ignored because the recipient cannot afford to soften yet. The unresolved hurt should remain visible in how they move around each other afterward.\n\nKeep the interiority character-specific. One person may rehearse the argument and realize what they should have said; another may refuse to think about it and throw themselves into the next task. What a character privately admits does not have to match what they say aloud, but the gap should come from their particular fear, pride, or need, not from a universal rule that everyone hides everything.\n\nThe crime plot continues through these exchanges. A moment of emotional honesty can change what a character is willing to do next—protect someone, betray someone, walk away, or stay—but it need not resolve the relationship. Let the reader feel the cost of the exposure even when the practical plan moves forward unchanged.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadavab::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Quentin Tarantino / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the scene as a live interval in the ongoing crime situation. Open or continue with a concrete activity—waiting for a contact, going over a plan, handling an object, arriving somewhere—so the reader knows where the characters are and what they are trying to accomplish. Keep the viewpoint character's observations and responses in readable paragraphs, not a wall of description.\n\nMake every line of dialogue clearly attributed and purposeful. A speaker may probe for information, test someone's nerve, change the subject, or assert control. Let the exchange shift something: one person learns a detail, loses patience, gains an advantage, or reveals more than they intended. The shift can be small; it does not need to be a plot twist.\n\nUse Tarantino's pressure-dialogue within the roleplay format. A long answer can be interrupted by a short one. A character can answer a question with another question. A mundane topic—a sandwich, a car, a song—can become the surface over which two people negotiate trust or dominance. Keep each speaker's voice distinct without making everyone a wit. The talk should feel like an action: each line moves the negotiation, the threat, or the plan forward, even when the subject is ordinary.\n\nEnd the reply at a natural continuation point. The scene may stop while someone is still talking, while a character is deciding what to do, or while an action is in progress. Do not force a question at the end or manufacture a cliffhanger. The next player should be able to pick up the ongoing action, respond to the last line, or move the scene forward without needing a prompt from you.\n\nPreserve the active authorship contract. If the player controls one character, do not write that character's decisions, speech, or private conclusions beyond what the contract permits. NPCs should keep acting on their own interests within the crime situation, but their behavior must remain responsive to the scene rather than pre-scripted toward a required outcome.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadavac::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Quentin Tarantino / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic telling route. Structure the crime story as a sequence of encounters, each with its own setting, participants, and immediate stake. Build each episode around an exchange—a negotiation, a delivery, a confrontation, a payment, a piece of the aftermath—whose outcome changes the characters' practical position. End the episode at the point where something has shifted: money moved, a promise extracted, a threat made, a relationship damaged. The next episode then has new material to work with.\n\nGround the episodes in the social texture of the chosen Chinese-language community. Characters should care about obligations, reputation, family claims, debts, and practical survival in ways specific to their time and place. Do not import generic honor codes or make every elder wise and every merchant corrupt. Show these pressures through what the dialogue actually negotiates: who owes whom, who can be asked for help, who must be shown respect, who can be refused.\n\nLet Tarantino-style dialogue carry the action. A long negotiation over a small sum can reveal who has leverage. A polite exchange can contain a threat. A joke can test whether someone is still loyal. The speakers' status and interests should shape how they talk: who addresses whom directly, who changes the subject, who offers tea or a cigarette, who waits to be asked. Each line should move the negotiation or the threat forward, not merely decorate the scene.\n\nKeep the telling concrete and scene-based. Describe the room, the weather, the object on the table, the sound from the street, but only as much as the scene needs. Let the physical details carry social meaning—a worn jacket, a borrowed car, a meal shared or refused—without turning every object into a symbol.\n\nThe episodic structure does not require a moral summary at the end of each scene. Let the reader see the consequences accumulate. A character may lose face in one episode and regain leverage in the next, or pay for an earlier choice without anyone announcing the lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadavad::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Quentin Tarantino / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nKeep the prose tight and concrete. Name the thing: the cracked phone screen, the blood on the cuff, the cash folded in a rubber band, the door that sticks. Let physical detail carry the pressure. When a character waits, show what they do with their hands or where they look, not just that they are nervous.\n\nUse Tarantino-style dialogue as an action braced by the material world. A long exchange can happen over a table, in a car, beside a locked door, with the speakers aware of the time, the weapon, the money, or the person who has not arrived yet. Let the talk shift control: someone asks a question that changes who owes whom, someone makes a joke that lands like a warning, someone stops talking and starts doing. The concrete details should give the exchange its edge—the object in someone's hand, the distance to the door, the sound outside—so the words and the physical situation press on each other.\n\nCompress the transitions. Cut from the plan to the execution, from the argument to the aftermath, from the knock on the door to the moment after it opens. The reader should feel the missing time as pressure, not confusion. When a character's reasoning needs more room—working out a double-cross, deciding whether to run—let the thought run long enough to be clear, then cut back to action.\n\nStage physical consequences plainly. A beating, a shot, a fall, a wound: show what happens to the body and the immediate surroundings. Do not linger for its own sake, and do not soften the event into metaphor. The aftermath should stay visible—a limp, a stain, a broken window, a person holding still.\n\nChange rhythm deliberately. A run of short sentences can quicken a confrontation; a slower passage can hold a character's attention on one detail before the next move. Time the variation to the exchange's control shifts: a sudden silence after a threat, a long beat while someone decides whether to answer, a quick cut when the talk turns into action.\n\nKeep the abrasive speech grounded in its speaker. A character may curse, threaten, or mock because that is how they talk under pressure, not because every line must be profane. Let quieter characters stay quiet, and let their silence carry weight.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadavae::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Quentin Tarantino / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the crime story through documents. Each entry must have a clear author, a recipient or intended purpose, and a time of composition. Use the form the scene actually requires—a note left in a car, a police report, a recorded statement, a letter that may never be sent, a list, a receipt, a message thread—rather than rotating through formats mechanically.\n\nTreat each document as a move in the situation. The writer may be stalling, threatening, reassuring, covering a mistake, building an alibi, or trying to get someone to act. What the document says and what it leaves out should follow from who is writing, to whom, and why. A report filed by a frightened witness knows less than the reader may already know from an earlier letter by the culprit.\n\nUse Tarantino's timing within the written form. A short note can land harder than a long explanation. A document can begin with small talk and only gradually approach the real subject. A writer can address the recipient directly, then pull back, then commit. The document's structure—what comes first, what is repeated, what is crossed out or left unfinished—should carry the pressure, just as a spoken exchange would shift control through what is said and what is withheld.\n\nLet the gaps between documents do narrative work. The time between a plan and its aftermath, between a threat and a response, between a confession and its receipt can be as important as the documents themselves. This is where delayed consequence operates: a later document may contradict an earlier one, confirm a suspicion, or reveal that the writer misunderstood what was happening. The reader should feel the missing time as pressure, not as a break in the story.\n\nPreserve readable continuity. The reader should be able to follow the sequence of events across entries without an omniscient narrator explaining what each document means. Let the documents themselves supply the evidence, the relationships, and the consequences. When a document contains a lie, the reader should be able to catch it from the surrounding record, not from an editorial note.\n\nA document contains what its author records. It is not automatically the writer's complete private thought. If the active contract requires interiority, supply it in the permitted form rather than pretending a diary entry always exposes the whole mind.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadavaf::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Quentin Tarantino / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nKeep the crime story active. The characters are still planning, waiting, running, hiding, or cleaning up after something. Within that situation, spend real attention on the moments of care and ease that hold people together: someone makes food, remembers how another person takes their coffee, fixes a torn strap, shares a blanket, plays a song, or simply stays in the room.\n\nLet affection be sincere without demanding a confession. A character can show love by practical action—checking the locks, saving the last cigarette, waiting up, bringing a drink—without announcing what it means. The reader should feel the warmth through the gesture and the response, not through a forced emotional summary.\n\nUse Tarantino-style dialogue as the vehicle for comfort. Two people can talk about nothing much—a movie, a meal, a childhood story, a plan for after—and the exchange itself becomes the care. The conversation can be funny, rambling, or quietly specific. It does not need to turn into a therapy session or a declaration. The talk should feel like an action in its own right: it eases the pressure, re-establishes trust, or gives someone a reason to stay steady, even while the crime's stakes remain in the background.\n\nKeep personality and disagreement intact. A comforting scene can include a small argument, a difference of taste, a boundary, or a person who cannot accept help easily. Warmth does not make everyone agreeable. The crime's dangers and the characters' established problems remain real; the comfort is what they manage to hold onto anyway.\n\nLet the small pleasures be concrete. Name the food, the texture of the blanket, the sound of the rain, the warmth of the car heater, the particular way someone laughs. The details should feel earned by the characters' situation, not pasted on as decoration.\n\nThe comfort does not have to resolve the plot. A scene of care can happen before a dangerous job, after a failure, or in the middle of a long wait. It may change what a character is willing to risk, but it need not make the danger disappear or guarantee a happy ending.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadavag::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Quentin Tarantino / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite in composed novelistic prose. Let paragraphs develop through deliberate transitions rather than jump cuts. A scene may open with the street, the season, the building's history, or the social position of the people involved, then narrow to the room where the exchange takes place. The narration should sound written, not like a transcript of speech.\n\nUse the narrative distance to frame the crime. The narrator may place a deal, a betrayal, or a killing within a longer context: the neighborhood's decline, a family's reputation, the way a particular trade works, the difference between what the law says and what people do. This framing should come from the chosen narrator's actual knowledge and position, not from an omniscient voice that explains everything.\n\nKeep Tarantino's dialogue as the engine of the scene. Even within a formal frame, the talk should shift control, reveal intent, or change what someone is willing to do. The narrator can set the stage, but the exchange itself must carry the pressure. The formal style changes the timing: instead of cutting away after a hard line, the narration can dwell on what the words have done—a silence, a decision, a change in how someone holds themselves. The deliberate transition after the dialogue is the formal counterpart to Tarantino's jump cut.\n\nLet ordinary particulars gain significance through the situation. A meal, a piece of clothing, a route through the city, a song on the radio can matter because of who is present and what is at stake. The formal style gives room to describe these particulars fully, but each detail should earn its place in the scene's social and emotional logic.\n\nDistinguish narration from spoken language. The characters may talk in fragments, curses, or local idiom; the narrator's prose can be more measured and complete. The contrast between the rough talk and the composed narration is part of the effect, not a flaw to smooth away.\n\nPreserve the crime's human aftermath. The formal frame can show how a violent act or a betrayal ripples through a household, a workplace, or a community. The narrator may reflect on what has been lost, but should not moralize every event into a lesson. Let the consequences remain particular to these people and this place.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadavah::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Quentin Tarantino / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nBuild the outer frame around a live criminal dependency that the telling can affect: the teller needs the listener to wait, agree, open a door, make a call, or believe a version of events before someone else finishes a task. The frame's pressure is concrete and timed. The listener has a reason to stay and a reason to doubt: they need something from the teller, but they suspect the story is being used against them.\n\nTreat the embedded tale as a performed move in that negotiation. The teller does not simply narrate a past job; they select each detail for the listener's benefit. A story about a partner's habit can warn the listener about what will happen if they stall. A story about a double-cross can test whether the listener already knows the name before it is spoken. The tale's purpose is visible in its emphasis: the teller lingers on the part that matters to the listener and skims the part that matters to the teller.\n\nMake the listener an active interrogator of the tale. Let them interrupt to ask about a time, a route, a missing person, or an object the teller mentioned in passing. Each interruption changes what the teller reveals. When the listener questions a detail, the teller must decide whether to answer, deflect, or revise the story. The listener's questions should expose the gap between what the teller wants the tale to accomplish and what the teller actually knows.\n\nPlant a recurring detail inside the tale as a test. The teller introduces a specific object, phrase, or name early in the story and watches the listener's face when it appears. The listener's reaction—recognition, confusion, too little surprise, too much calm—tells the teller something about what the listener already knows. The same detail returns in the outer frame with a different meaning: the listener may repeat it back as a threat, ask about it as a check, or avoid it entirely. Repetition connects the levels and changes the balance of control; it does not certify that the tale is true.\n\nKeep the embedded tale in the teller's voice and knowledge. It may be vivid, profane, funny, or self-serving, but it cannot reveal what the teller did not know at the time. When the teller has learned something since, let that later knowledge surface as a shift in emphasis, a bitter aside, or a deliberate omission the listener notices. Do not let the embedded tale become an omniscient flashback.\n\nLet the outer conversation have its own reversals through Tarantino's pressure dialogue. Ordinary particulars gain weight from the situation: the listener asking about a detail the teller glossed over, the teller pausing to check a phone, a change in who controls the room. A listener who seemed passive can reveal what they already knew. A teller who seemed in control can discover that the story has committed them to something they did not intend. The frame is not a container for the inner story; it is the scene where the telling's consequences land.\n\nKeep the crime's consequences concrete in the frame. The telling may delay violence, invite it, or reveal that it has already happened elsewhere. The story's purpose can be as small as buying ten minutes or as large as deciding who leaves the room. End the response at the active boundary of the frame's situation: the teller may pause, the listener may ask a question, or an outer event may interrupt. Do not require the embedded tale to finish, the negotiation to resolve, or the pursuit to pause before the response ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadavai::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Quentin Tarantino / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the documentary and historical-memory route. Assemble the crime's account from attributed recollections, records, and present-day testimony, but never let a source stand alone. Each source enters the scene through a present-day exchange with its own stake: a former accomplice explaining a decision to an interviewer who needs a signature, a witness comparing what they saw with what the file says while someone waits for a confirmation, a relative reconstructing a day from letters and receipts because an investigator has asked them to identify a gap.\n\nGive each present-day exchange Tarantino's conversational pressure. The interview, the reunion, the phone call, or the chance meeting should have its own change of control. Someone wants a signature, an apology, a confirmation, or a way to stop being asked. Ordinary particulars gain weight through the situation. A question about a car's color can become a test of whether the speaker was really there. A pause before naming someone can reveal a loyalty the speaker is still managing. The conversation is not a neutral container for the remembered crime; it is where the source decides what the record will be used for.\n\nAlternate between the attributed account and the present scene where that account is being defended or revised. When the narration summarizes a file, quotes a speaker, or observes the present room, make the shift legible. The reader should know whether a sentence is the source's live speech, the narrator's summary of a document, or the narrator's observation of the speaker's hands, hesitation, or tone. The alternation should expose the gap between what the record says and what the speaker now needs it to mean.\n\nDistinguish the source's present account from the past it describes. Let the narration mark what the speaker knew then and what they know now. A witness may say, \"At the time I thought he was reaching for his wallet,\" and then add what the trial later established. A report may record a time that the survivor now disputes. Do not let later knowledge retroactively become earlier knowledge. The gap between record and memory is part of the crime's aftermath, and the present-day exchange should make that gap matter to someone in the room.\n\nLet syntax and distance follow the documentary route. Prefer precise, plain narration around the attributed material: who is speaking, where the account comes from, what the record contains, what the speaker does while talking. The prose need not imitate bureaucratic German or translated word order. It should make the assembly of sources legible without turning the scene into a clinical case summary.\n\nKeep the crime's human costs concrete. A speaker can be angry, evasive, relieved, or still afraid. The narration can notice the room where the interview happens, the way someone handles a photograph, the moment a prepared story breaks. Suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader already knows who committed the offense. The documentary route does not require a final synthesis or a completed case. The response can end while a speaker hesitates, while a record contradicts a memory, or while the interviewer asks the next question.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadavaj::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Quentin Tarantino / Gothic-Punk\n\nGive the crime a collision between old authority and present life. The offense should be entangled with something inherited or entrenched: a debt owed to a former employer, a family that still controls a building or a route, a promise made before the present circumstances existed, a place whose rules outlast the people who made them. Make that collision concrete in the scene by giving the old authority a material form that the characters can point to, handle, or dispute: a key, a ledger, a name on a lease, a room they are not supposed to enter.\n\nLet the inherited detail be the subject of the negotiation. The characters do not merely stand in an atmospheric room; they argue about what the room means and who controls it. One character claims the old arrangement still binds. Another claims it died with the person who made it. The dialogue should test those claims against the physical evidence in the room: a repaired lock, a clean counter, a photograph turned face-down, a chair no one sits in. The atmosphere changes how the exchange is experienced, but it does not replace the crime's actual stakes.\n\nUse Tarantino's dialogue to expose the social masks through those claims. Characters perform roles for each other: the respectful former employee, the indifferent buyer, the loyal partner, the person who claims not to remember. A mask slips when a character's claim about the inherited detail is tested. The respectful former employee may know too much about the lock. The indifferent buyer may flinch at a name on the lease. The loyal partner may hesitate before touching the ledger. A question repeated with a different tone can force someone to speak as themselves. The masks can also hold: not every performance must collapse. What matters is that the reader sees the effort and the cost of maintaining or dropping the role, and that the cost is tied to the old authority's material presence.\n\nKeep intimacy morally complicated. Private desire, old affection, shared history, or genuine care can coexist with the offense. A character can protect someone and still lie to them. A partner can be tender in one moment and ruthless in the next. Do not make every caring act fraudulent or every trusted companion a traitor. Let the complication be specific: this person wants this outcome and also wants not to lose that relationship. The intimacy should be visible in how the characters handle the inherited objects and obligations, not only in what they say.\n\nDo not import Kindred physiology, Humanity numbers, factions, powers, or blood-bond rules. The old authority in this recipe is human and social: an institution, a family, a landlord, a former boss, a neighborhood's unwritten law. Its power is material and relational, not supernatural.\n\nKeep the crime's preparation, execution, or aftermath present in the scene. The dialogue can be the preparation: two people deciding whether to go through with it while one of them holds the key. It can be the aftermath: someone explaining why it happened or what it cost while standing in the room that started it. Suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows the offense. End at the active boundary of the encounter: a question left hanging, a mask still in place, a decision not yet made, or an outer event that changes the room.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadavak::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Quentin Tarantino / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the testimonial and autobiographical account route. Let a culprit, accomplice, victim, investigator, or bystander carry the crime's story as a first-person participant telling or recalling what happened to someone in the present. The telling has an occasion and a listener with a specific need: a statement to an officer who needs a name, a confession to a relative who needs to know whether to stay, an interview with a journalist who needs a story, a conversation with a lawyer who needs a defense. The listener's need gives the telling its pressure. The speaker is not narrating into a void; they are managing what this particular person will believe, record, or do.\n\nMake the listener's need shape the account. The speaker knows what the listener wants and decides how much of it to give. When the listener asks about a detail, the speaker must choose whether to answer, soften, or deflect. A question about the time a door was locked can test whether the speaker was really there. A question about the name someone used can test whether the speaker is protecting that person. Each interruption changes what the speaker is willing to admit. The account is not a finished confession; it is a live negotiation over what the listener will take away.\n\nGive the telling Tarantino's conversational pressure. Ordinary particulars gain weight through the situation: the time a door was locked, the name someone used, the exact words said before a decision. A change in who controls the conversation can force the speaker to revise their account or double down on it. The listener can reveal what they already know, and the speaker can discover that their story has committed them to something they did not intend. The present-day exchange should have its own reversals, not merely serve as a container for the remembered crime.\n\nKeep the speaker's knowledge and memory distinct. The narration may move between the remembered event and the present telling, but it must preserve what the speaker knew at the time. A later realization cannot become the earlier motive. When the speaker has learned something since, let it surface as a present reflection, a correction, or a hesitation that the listener notices. The reader should be able to tell the difference between the participant's live memory and the teller's retrospective judgment. The retrospective judgment should be tied to the present stake: the speaker is not merely remembering; they are deciding what the listener will do with the memory.\n\nPreserve the language, region, community, and period actually supplied by the story. If the scene is set in a particular city, neighborhood, workplace, or household, keep those specifics concrete: the route someone took, the language they used with different people, the obligations and expectations that shaped their choices. Do not force English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure onto the scene. Do not manufacture national speech patterns or a landscape inventory. The tradition is a formal route, not a costume.\n\nLet the crime's consequences remain present in the telling. The speaker may be trying to clear themselves, protect someone, justify a choice, or simply make the listener understand. Suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows what happened. The account does not have to be reliable, but its unreliability should be character-specific: embarrassment, fear, loyalty, habit, or a supported motive, not a compulsory twist.\n\nEnd at the active boundary of the telling. The speaker may stop before the worst part, the listener may ask the next question, or an outer event may interrupt. Do not require the account to finish, the listener to believe it, or the crime to be resolved before the response ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaval::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Quentin Tarantino / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the early-modern comic/theatrical telling route. Stage the crime scene as a performed present-tense exchange in which entrances, exits, reversals, and spoken roles carry the action. The reader should feel the scene's theatrical shape: someone arrives with a claim, someone performs a role, someone changes the terms, someone leaves or is made to stay. Let the narration mark these movements clearly so the staging is legible. Each entrance or exit should change who controls the room, and each change should be visible in what the characters do with their bodies and the objects around them.\n\nGive the dialogue Tarantino's pressure and timing. Let speakers perform for each other: the calm professional, the offended partner, the helpful stranger, the person pretending not to understand. Ordinary interests and particulars gain significance through the situation. A receipt, a borrowed jacket, a phone number, or a half-finished drink can become the object around which control shifts. The object should be something a speaker has claimed or denied. One character says the receipt is in the jacket. Another says the jacket was never borrowed. The reversal comes when the object is produced, found missing, or shown to be something else. The object's material presence contradicts the performed role.\n\nLet changes of control happen through speech. A question that exposes a lie, a name dropped too casually, a demand repeated with a different tone can force a speaker to revise their role or abandon it. The theatrical shape is not a static set of types; it is a sequence of performances that the dialogue tests. When a role fails, the character must decide whether to improvise a new one or speak as themselves. The cost of that choice should be concrete: a job, a relationship, a chance to leave.\n\nAllow comic excess where the crime genre permits. A speaker can be absurdly confident, elaborately polite, or grotesquely wrong about the situation. The humor should come from the gap between the performed role and the material facts of the crime: the calm professional who cannot remember where the receipt is, the offended partner who is holding the borrowed jacket, the helpful stranger who knows too much about the phone number. A comic beat can sharpen a threat or delay a consequence. When violence or its aftermath occurs, stage it clearly and give it physical consequences. Do not let the theatricality turn harm into weightless performance.\n\nKeep the scene's human costs concrete. The theatrical route does not require every character to be a type. Let a performer's mask slip or hold according to their particular motive. A character can be funny and still afraid. A reversal can be comic and still cost someone something real. Suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who committed the offense.\n\nDo not import a particular Japanese period, costume, or scenery unless the supplied story already includes it. The route is a formal treatment: heightened, speaker-led, sharply staged, and open to comic excess. Personal language and narrative distance should follow the actual teller and the supplied setting. No universal quietness or mandatory inventory of Japanese scenery applies.\n\nEnd at the active boundary of the scene. The response can stop while a speaker hesitates, while a new arrival changes the room, or while a demand hangs unanswered. Do not require the reversal to complete, the pursuit to pause, or the scene to resolve before the response ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadavam::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Quentin Tarantino / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the polyphonic and nonlinear telling route. Let the crime's preparation, execution, and aftermath emerge through attributed accounts and exchanges that the narration moves among across time. A scene from before the offense can sit beside a scene from after it. A conversation between two accomplices can be followed by a conversation between an investigator and a witness who does not yet know what the reader has already seen. The order of scenes should create meaning: a later scene can revise the reader's understanding of an earlier one, or an earlier scene can explain why a later speaker is lying.\n\nBuild the scene order around a shared object, name, or phrase that changes meaning across voices and times. The same car, the same nickname, the same promise, or the same lie can appear in a negotiation before the job, a confrontation after a betrayal, and a casual conversation that later turns out to have been preparation. Each appearance should give the reader a new piece of information about who knew what and when. The juxtaposition is not a puzzle to be solved; it is a way of making the competing interests concrete. A character's confidence in one scene can become legible as ignorance in another. A loyalty shown before the offense can explain a silence after it.\n\nGive each voice Tarantino's pressure and timing. Every attributed exchange should have its own stakes and its own change of control. One scene may be a negotiation before a job, another a confrontation after a betrayal, another a casual conversation that later turns out to have been preparation. Let ordinary particulars gain significance through each situation: the same object, name, or phrase can mean something different in different voices and times. Do not make every speaker share the same profane wit; let each voice have its own register, interests, and way of managing status. The change of control in each scene should be visible: someone gains the upper hand, loses it, or discovers that the terms have shifted.\n\nKeep the attribution and time shifts legible. The reader should always know whose scene this is and when it occurs in relation to the offense. Use concrete markers: a date, a place, a change in who is present, a detail that anchors the moment. Nonlinearity is a formal choice, not a fog. The reader may not yet know how the scenes connect, but each scene should be clear in itself. The shared object, name, or phrase should help the reader track the connections without turning the story into a diagram.\n\nPreserve the relevant language, community, and history actually supplied by the story. If the setting is a particular city, neighborhood, workplace, or household, keep those specifics concrete. Do not manufacture national speech patterns, landscape inventories, or a generic regional temperament. Do not add supernatural facts to a world that excludes them; selecting this route does not make the marvelous available unless the supplied world already permits it.\n\nLet the crime's human costs emerge through the juxtaposition. A character's confidence in one scene can become legible as ignorance in another. A loyalty shown before the offense can explain a silence after it. Suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did it. The polyphony should make the competing interests concrete, not merely multiply perspectives for their own sake.\n\nEnd at the active boundary of whichever scene is current. The response can stop while a speaker hesitates, while a scene shifts to another time, or while a demand hangs unanswered. Do not require all the voices to converge, the crime to be resolved, or the nonlinear structure to complete before the response ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadavan::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Quentin Tarantino / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate crime situation cleanly. Open with who is present, where they are, and what is already at stake: a meeting before a job, a handoff that is going wrong, an interrogation that has stalled, a driver waiting for a signal. Do not begin with a long setup or a summary of the case. Let the reader see the room, the people, and the pressure in the first few lines.\n\nKeep spatial changes easy to follow. When someone stands, moves to the door, takes something from a bag, or turns to face another speaker, mark the movement in the narration. The reader should always know where the characters are in relation to each other and to the objects that matter: a weapon, a phone, a case, a set of keys. Use short, readable paragraphs around action, dialogue, and private response so that each beat is distinct.\n\nAnchor each reversal to a physical object the viewpoint character can see or handle. The object should be something whose position or ownership can change: a receipt that moves from one pocket to another, a borrowed lighter that someone fails to return, a phone face-down on the table that buzzes at the wrong moment. The reversal comes when the object's position or ownership changes, or when a character's claim about the object is contradicted by what the viewpoint character can see. The object is not a symbol; it is a material fact that shifts control of the scene.\n\nLet Tarantino's dialogue carry the action. Sustained exchanges should change the situation: a question that exposes a lie, a demand that shifts control, a name dropped to test a reaction, a pause that makes the other person fill the silence. Ordinary particulars gain significance through the scene. Give each speaker a distinct register; do not make every character share the same profane wit. The dialogue should move the object's position or ownership, or force someone to make a claim about it that the viewpoint character can test.\n\nUse focused reaction beats to make the reversals land. After a significant line or action, give the viewpoint character's private response in a brief, concrete beat: a shift in breathing, a specific worry, a calculation about what to say next, a memory that the line activates. The reaction can be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to its owner. It should belong to the character's actual experience under the chosen viewpoint, not be a generic pause for effect. The reaction beat should drive the next choice: the viewpoint character notices the object's position, calculates what the other person is trying to do, and decides whether to speak, move, or wait. Do not let a reaction beat replace the dialogue's forward movement; use it to make the next spatial or dialogue beat legible.\n\nPresent unfamiliar names or mechanics clearly when they appear. If a character uses a nickname, a code word, a location, or a piece of criminal technique the reader has not seen before, give the necessary context in the narration or in a natural exchange without turning the scene into a glossary. The reader should be able to follow the scheme's incentives and relationships without stopping to decode the text.\n\nKeep the crime's consequences concrete. The scene may be preparation, execution, or aftermath. Violence or comic excess belongs only where the crime genre permits, and physical consequences must be clearly staged when they occur. Suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did it. First person, comedy, a young cast, and a closing cliffhanger are not requirements of this container.\n\nEnd at the active boundary of the scene. The response can stop while a speaker hesitates, while a phone buzzes, or while a demand hangs unanswered. Do not require the reversal to complete, the pursuit to pause, or the scene to resolve before the response ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadavao::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Quentin Tarantino / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet the scene's criminal pressure enter through syntax and sensory attention rather than through immediate explanation of stakes. A sustained exchange can unfold inside a paragraph that follows one image or sound until it changes what the viewpoint character understands. Keep the comparison traceable to the room, the street, the work being done, or the teller's association; do not let figurative language replace the particular object that prompted it.\n\nTreat conversation as an action whose control shifts through timing and withheld intent. Lyrical attention can slow a reply, let a repeated word gather a second meaning, or hold on a gesture while the next line lands. When a speaker takes or loses control, render that change through the rhythm of the exchange: a run of clauses can carry confidence, and a shorter plain sentence can mark the moment someone stops performing. Do not make every character speak in the narrator's figurative register; a plain answer can provide the contrast that makes the shaped passage matter.\n\nStage physical consequences clearly when they occur, but let their meaning arrive through the body's felt particulars and the associations they release. A delayed consequence may surface as a remembered image, a sound that returns, or a detail the viewpoint character notices only after the immediate danger has passed. Keep the account inside the selected perspective and its actual knowledge. End the response where the scene's attention naturally rests, without requiring the exchange to conclude or the scheme to advance.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadavap::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Quentin Tarantino / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the crime's aftermath become legible through small irreversible changes and remembered occasions. A present exchange can carry the pressure of a scheme, an investigation, or a divided loyalty while the viewpoint character notices what has already altered: a routine that no longer holds, a phrase someone used to say, a place that means differently now. Keep the present scene's practical stakes active; reflection should not dissolve the conversation into pure recollection.\n\nUse dialogue as an action whose timing and changes of control reveal what the speakers still want from one another. A withheld intent can become more legible through what a character does not say, but the narrator may supply the private association that explains the omission. Let a remembered occasion return because the present moment calls it up, not as a general meditation on loss. When a memory surfaces, let it alter the character's attention to the present exchange: a familiar phrase may now sound different, a gesture may recall an earlier occasion, or a silence may carry the weight of what is no longer said. Ordinary pleasure can remain pleasurable, and a familiar detail can still offer comfort even while it marks what has changed.\n\nStage physical consequences clearly when they occur, and let their aftermath settle through attention to what remains: an object kept, a debt unpaid, a habit resumed, a silence that now has a different meaning. The response may end with the present scene still underway, leaving the character inside the coexistence of current pressure and remembered life rather than announcing impermanence as a lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadavaq::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Quentin Tarantino / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed oral-storytelling route. Establish a situated teller who is recounting the crime, scheme, or aftermath to a particular listener, and let the telling situation carry its own pressure: the teller may be an accomplice, a bystander, an investigator, or someone trying to control what the listener believes. Keep the frame active without letting it become a neutral wrapper; the listener's interruptions, questions, or silences can change the teller's pace and emphasis.\n\nLet the nested account unfold through sustained exchanges rather than summary. When the teller reports dialogue, render it as speech with its own timing, status, and withheld intent. A change of control inside the remembered conversation can alter the teller's authority in the present frame. When the listener interrupts, let the interruption do work: it may force the teller to defend a detail, revise an emphasis, or reveal something the teller had meant to withhold. Delay a revelation until the listener's need or the teller's purpose makes it land, and let the frame return to show what that revelation has done to the relationship between teller and listener.\n\nKeep the regional route concrete and distinct. Do not import a generic honor code, mysticism, exile, or religious atmosphere. The setting, language, and community actually supplied should shape the telling: the room where the account is given, the reason it is being told now, and the social or domestic particulars that make the story worth retelling. Stage physical consequences clearly when they occur, but let their meaning be filtered through the teller's selection and the listener's response. End where the frame's present moment naturally pauses, not where the nested story must resolve.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadavar::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Quentin Tarantino / Minimalist Prose\n\nUse a small number of exact details and direct clauses. Let the scene's criminal pressure appear through what the viewpoint character notices and what the narration leaves out, but keep the omission inferable rather than deleting causality. A withheld intent can be shown by a pause, a change in address, or the thing someone does not say; the reader should be able to infer the reason from the selected particulars.\n\nTreat dialogue as an action through timing and changes of control. A short exchange can shift status without explanation: one speaker waits, another interrupts, a question goes unanswered. When a physical consequence occurs, stage it in clear, economical sentences. Choose the detail that makes the event legible and let the next direct clause carry its effect. Do not pad the moment with redundant explanation.\n\nKeep inward connection brief but present where it matters. A precise extended sentence may serve the style better than several performed fragments, especially when it links a present detail to a motive or a memory that changes what the character does next. Cast voices remain individual; do not reduce every speaker to the same clipped register. End the response when the selected action or exchange has reached its natural pause, without requiring a completed scheme or a resolved relationship.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadavas::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Quentin Tarantino / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose with natural movement among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience. Let the crime's preparation, incentives, relationships, or aftermath emerge through the viewpoint character's attention rather than through summary. Use paragraphing and transitions to orient the reader without recap machinery; a shift into memory should arise from something in the present scene and return with a changed bearing on it.\n\nTreat conversation as an action whose timing, status, and withheld intent can shift control within the exchange. Let the narrator's voice and the immediate scene set the register and rhythm. A long exchange can sustain pressure through what is not said, while a short interruption can change the room's balance. Do not make every speaker share the same wit or profanity; individual voices should remain distinct.\n\nStage physical consequences clearly when they occur, and let their aftermath move through the character's perception and inner experience. A delayed consequence may return as a recollection, a bodily sensation, or a practical problem that reorders the scene. Keep the account inside the selected perspective and its actual knowledge. The response may end with the scene still underway, leaving the next action available rather than forcing a lesson or a completed beat.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadavat::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Quentin Tarantino / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship premise legible through scene selection, private language, repeated moments, and consequences that fulfill its particular promise. Use only the actual supplied premise rather than inserting a roster of familiar tropes. Let the crime's pressure test the bond: a scheme, an offense, an investigation, or a divided loyalty can expose what the characters want from each other and what they are willing to withhold.\n\nTreat conversation as an action whose timing, status, and changes of control reveal the relationship's current balance. A private phrase can return with new weight when the situation has changed. A callback should deepen the reader's understanding of the bond rather than merely repeat a favorite line. Give affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery enough space where it belongs, but keep the emotional payoff earned by the scene's actual events.\n\nStage physical consequences clearly when they occur, and let them land inside the relationship rather than outside it. A character may protect, confront, comfort, or fail another; the consequence should be legible in what passes between them afterward. Keep the account inside the selected perspective and its actual knowledge. The response may end with the relationship's pressure still unresolved, leaving the next exchange available without forcing a confession or a completed emotional arc.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadavau::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Quentin Tarantino / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account a sense of retelling through deliberate openings, compact declarations, recurring phrasing, and the relation of an act to what will be remembered. Let the crime enter the chronicle as a deed whose consequences outlast the immediate scheme: who was wronged, who kept faith, who broke it, and what later events made the offense matter. Keep the particular source tradition coherent rather than mixing every epic convention.\n\nUse compact declarations to establish a person or remembered act, then expand into the particulars that make the reputation incomplete. A recurring designation should come from an established deed or way of speaking, not from an ornamental epithet assigned to every entrance. Return to a phrase when a later occurrence gives it a new application. Let dialogue interrupt the authority of the chronicle: a participant may object to one detail, answer an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern, or reveal a withheld intent that changes how the deed should be remembered.\n\nStage physical consequences clearly when they occur, and let their meaning be shaped by the account's public dimension. A violent act, a broken oath, or a paid debt can become part of what the community will retell, but keep shared reputation distinct from a focal character's private account. Preserve the selected perspective and its actual knowledge. The response may end with business concluded or resumed, leaving another act available to be remembered rather than declaring it legend on the community's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadavav::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Quentin Tarantino / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the crime as a psychological-institutional aftermath in which the offense is already known, and what remains open is who will speak, what they will admit, and what the available records can and cannot establish. Choose a viewpoint from the participants: an investigator, a family member, a witness, or someone returning to the place where the offense occurred. Let their perceptions of work, home, and official conversation carry the investigation's emotional and social cost.\n\nUse the Scandinavian social-realist route through restrained, precisely observed scenes of ordinary life. The physical world must stay specific and unromantic: a kitchen table, a station office, a bus stop, a workplace break room. Let the pressure come from the situation and the relationships, not from weather or a general national mood. Keep Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish contexts distinct when the story supplies them, but do not manufacture snow, isolation, or bleakness as atmosphere.\n\nBuild Tarantino-style sustained exchanges around practical particulars. People discuss schedules, keys, money, a borrowed car, a phone call, a door left open, a job that started late. These details gain weight because they bear on the crime or its consequences. Let timing and status shape the exchange: a person may delay an answer, answer a different question, correct a small fact, or ask for something before responding. A change of control can occur when someone realizes what a question implies, when a family member stops cooperating, or when a witness offers more than was requested.\n\nKeep speech and interiority distinct. The narration may show what the viewpoint character notices, suspects, remembers, or chooses not to say, but another character does not know that private content. In this psychological-institutional route, let the viewpoint's thoughts be partly occupied by procedure: what has been recorded, what still needs to be verified, and what cannot be said in the room. A silence in dialogue is not proof of guilt, and a calm answer is not proof of innocence. Let the evidence accumulate through what people say, what records show, and what the viewpoint can verify.\n\nGive physical consequences clear staging when they occur, but do not require violence or comic excess. A confrontation may end in a slammed door, a refused statement, a brief scuffle, or a long pause before someone leaves. The scene can stop after a result or an interruption without an obligatory confession, arrest, or reversal. Let the aftermath remain social and procedural: work continues, a household adjusts, an official file stays open, and a relationship changes in a way that cannot be fully settled in one exchange.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadavaw::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Quentin Tarantino / Screenplay\n\nWrite the story as a sequence of readable screenplay scenes. Use scene headings when location or time changes, clear speaker cues, and concise action lines that describe visible or audible events. Do not fill the page with camera commands. A cut, offscreen sound, or voice-over appears only when the chosen sequence needs it.\n\nLet the crime's pressure enter through what characters do while they talk. In an action line, show someone cleaning a weapon, counting money, checking a phone, watching a door, or moving an object that matters to the offense or its aftermath. The dialogue then gains weight because the physical business and the spoken exchange are happening in the same moment. Keep the ordinary particulars specific: a bag, a receipt, a set of keys, a stain, a locked drawer. When a sign of the crime belongs offscreen—vivid enough to deserve a sound, a pause, or an unseen arrival—use a purposeful offscreen cue or scene transition rather than explaining it in dialogue.\n\nBuild sustained exchanges around timing and status. One character may delay an answer, ask a question instead of replying, correct a small detail, or change the subject to something practical. A change of control can occur when someone stands up, takes an object, opens a door, or makes a request that shifts who is waiting on whom. Do not make every speaker share the same profane wit. Let each person's speech fit their position, relationship, and immediate purpose.\n\nKeep required private prose as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation. It is not dialogue and not knowledge available to the cast. A character may think one thing while saying another, or their private attention may simply run ahead of or behind the spoken exchange. Another character does not know that note unless the scene gives them a visible or audible reason to infer it.\n\nStage physical consequences clearly when they occur. A blow, a fall, a broken object, or a sudden stop should be written so the reader can see what happened and what remains afterward. Do not require violence or comic excess. A scene can end after a completed action, a refusal, an unanswered question, or a new piece of information without an obligatory reversal or cliffhanger. Let the next scene heading carry the reader forward when the location or time has changed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadavax::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Quentin Tarantino / Stage Play\n\nWrite the story as a playable script. Use clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be staged. Do not add film-camera instructions. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene.\n\nUse the shared stage space to make proposed accounts testable. Who can see an entrance, reach an object, hear a line, or move between two places should be clear enough that an attempted reconstruction changes what is plausible. A character may claim they were not present, but the audience has seen them enter. A character may say they did not touch something, but the object remains in their hand or on the table before them. Let the audience's knowledge of these observable facts put pressure on the spoken account; do not merely report that a claim is doubtful.\n\nBuild Tarantino-style sustained exchanges around ordinary particulars that gain significance through the situation. Characters discuss a bag, a key, a phone, a drink, a job, a debt, or a time they arrived. Let timing and status shape the exchange. One character may delay an answer, ask for something before responding, correct a small detail, or change the subject to a practical errand. A change of control can occur when someone sits down uninvited, takes an object, blocks an exit, or makes a request that shifts who is waiting on whom.\n\nKeep required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in its active presentation instead of disguising it as something another character can hear. A monologue or aside is performed speech when the character chooses to address the audience or themselves; it is not the default destination for thought. When a character's private attention, suspicion, or memory matters, present it as a clearly attributed nonspoken note. That note may include what the character is working to conceal, what they have not yet put into words, or what they are unaware of, but it remains unavailable to the other characters unless a visible or audible action communicates it.\n\nStage physical consequences clearly when they occur. A shove, a dropped object, a slammed door, a long pause before someone leaves, or a sudden move toward the exit should be written so the actors can perform it and the audience can see what remains afterward. Do not require violence or comic excess. A scene can end after a completed action, a refusal, an unanswered question, or a new piece of information without an obligatory gathering of suspects, theatrical revelation, or confession.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadavay::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Quentin Tarantino / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite the story through one particular authorized mind. Braid perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern. Let syntax and paragraph shape follow those associations, but keep enough orientation to distinguish events from recollection or fantasy. The telling should move associatively rather than as a linear report; ordinary reasoning alone is not this style.\n\nLet the crime enter the mind's flow through a recurring concern tied to the offense or its consequences. The viewpoint may be a culprit, accomplice, victim, investigator, or bystander. Their attention returns to an object, a phrase, a time, a debt, a locked door, a missed call, or a person who has not arrived. This concern does not have to be named each time; it can surface as a sudden weight, a direction the mind will not take, or a wrong word that the character catches after speaking. Do not explain the whole offense at once. Let its pieces surface through what the mind notices, remembers, or tries not to think about.\n\nKeep Tarantino-style exchanges present as events the mind experiences. A conversation may be interrupted by a bodily sensation, a memory, a sound, or a sudden recognition that changes what the spoken words mean. The viewpoint may hear a line and respond while privately following a different association. They may miss part of what was said, answer late, or realize afterward what a question implied. Another character does not know that private content. The dialogue remains actual speech, but the telling follows the mind's movement through it rather than a clean transcript.\n\nPreserve the crime's facts as facts. A memory may be partial, mistaken, or colored by feeling, but the reader should be able to distinguish what happened from what the viewpoint imagines, wishes, or fears. When a later realization changes the meaning of an earlier event, show the realization as a later event. Do not retroactively insert it as the earlier motive.\n\nGive physical consequences clear staging when they occur, but let them arrive through the mind's experience of the moment: the shock of a blow, the sound of a door, the feel of a weapon or an object in the hand, the sudden stillness after something breaks. Do not require violence or comic excess. A passage can end after a completed action, a refusal, an unanswered question, or a new piece of information without an obligatory reversal or confession.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadavaz::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Quentin Tarantino / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the story as a readable sender-attributed message exchange. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter. Do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext. Context outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript.\n\nLet the crime's pressure enter through what each sender chooses to transmit and when. A message may be short, delayed, edited, or sent in place of a different reply. A question may go unanswered while the person is shown typing, or a practical request may arrive instead of an explanation. These timing and selection choices are the action. Keep each person's actual digital habits distinct: one sender writes in fragments, another in complete sentences, another rarely uses punctuation, another sends voice notes or images only when the situation calls for them. Let the transcript itself become the record that a later reader might return to; what is missing—an unsent draft, a time gap, a redirected question—can carry evidentiary weight without being interpreted as proof on its own.\n\nBuild Tarantino-style sustained exchanges around ordinary particulars that gain significance through the situation. People discuss a time, an address, a bag, a payment, a car, a door, a phone that was left somewhere, or a person who has not responded. A change of control can occur when someone stops replying, sends a screenshot, forwards a message, asks for something before answering, or corrects a small detail in a way that shifts who is waiting on whom.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text. Show what was actually sent. An unsent draft is a composed artifact; it can matter to the story when it is discovered, but it is not automatically the writer's live private thought and does not communicate anything to the recipient. Required private experience may appear as clearly separated context outside the transcript, attributed to the person whose mind it belongs to. Another character does not know that private content unless it is transmitted or they have a visible reason to infer it.\n\nThis form does not require a blow or fall to be staged on camera, so do not invent physical action that the transcript cannot display. If a consequence matters, let it arrive through a message that reports it, a delay in response, a changed tone, or a channel switch—or keep it in the separated context. Do not require violence or comic excess. An exchange can end after a completed message, a refusal, an unanswered question, or a new piece of information without an obligatory reversal or cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadavba::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Quentin Tarantino / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the story as scene-oriented visual-novel presentation. Use concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Keep scene changes legible. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected.\n\nLet the crime's pressure enter through what the viewpoint notices and how the next exchange changes that interpretation. A background cue may orient the moment: a door ajar, a bag on the table, a stain near the sink, a phone face-down, a car parked where it should not be. Let the focal narration react to that cue as an observation the character weighs, perhaps comparing it with an earlier memory or questioning its meaning. An expression cue may attract attention, but it does not function as an automatic truth indicator. A changed smile can draw suspicion, while a contradiction in an arrangement or account must do the evidentiary work. Show the viewpoint's evolving explanation: the same cue may become more significant only after a later fact changes it.\n\nBuild Tarantino-style sustained exchanges around ordinary particulars that gain significance through the situation. Characters discuss a time, a key, a payment, a job, a borrowed object, or a person who was supposed to arrive. Let timing and status shape the exchange. One character may delay an answer, ask for something before responding, correct a small detail, or change the subject to a practical errand. A change of control can occur when someone stands, takes an object, asks a question instead of answering, or makes a request that shifts who is waiting on whom.\n\nKeep private thought outside other characters' knowledge. The focal narration may show what the viewpoint character suspects, remembers, or chooses not to say. Another character does not know that inner response unless the scene gives them a visible or audible reason to infer it. Do not turn the cast into fixed reaction portraits whose expression always reveals whether they lie.\n\nGive physical consequences clear staging when they occur. A blow, a fall, a broken object, or a sudden stop should be written so the reader can see what happened and what remains afterward, using the scene-setting rhythm rather than explaining the aftermath in dialogue. Do not require violence or comic excess. A scene can end after a completed action, a refusal, an unanswered question, or a new piece of information without an obligatory reversal or confession. Let the next scene heading or background cue carry the reader forward when the location or time has changed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadavbb::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Quentin Tarantino / Web Serial\n\nWrite the story as installment-oriented prose. Maintain a readable through-line across replies: what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover context briefly only when it helps. Use earned developments and occasional hooks without making every turn a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger.\n\nLet an exact earlier observation remain available when a later installment changes what it suggests. A person uses a door previously said to be locked, asks for an object another character described, repeats a phrase under different circumstances, or returns to a place whose earlier meaning now shifts. Briefly recover the necessary particular rather than retelling the whole offense or investigation that first established it. New information can qualify an old explanation without replacing the old event or making the viewpoint character deliberately forget a supported conclusion. Let the reader's serial memory do its work: the callback is stronger when the installment does not announce what has changed.\n\nBuild Tarantino-style sustained exchanges around ordinary particulars that gain significance through the situation. Characters discuss a time, a key, a payment, a job, a borrowed object, or a person who has not arrived. Let timing and status shape the exchange. One character may delay an answer, ask for something before responding, correct a small detail, or change the subject to a practical errand. A change of control can occur when someone stands, takes an object, asks a question instead of answering, or makes a request that shifts who is waiting on whom. Do not make every speaker share the same profane wit.\n\nKeep required inward experience available when it contributes feeling or thought, even if no immediate decision changes. The narration may show what the viewpoint character suspects, remembers, or chooses not to say. Another character does not know that private content unless the scene gives them a visible or audible reason to infer it. Let ordinary life continue around the inquiry. Work, meals, errands, and relationships may proceed while the crime's consequences remain unresolved.\n\nGive physical consequences clear staging when they occur. A blow, a fall, a broken object, or a sudden stop should be written so the reader can see what happened and what remains afterward. Do not require violence or comic excess. An installment can close on completed work, a pending request, a new piece of information, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. When you use a hook, it should grow from the current installment's actual change, not from manufacturing a threat or a question unrelated to what has just occurred. Serial duration should give selected facts room to acquire meaning, not make a fair answer perpetually recede.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadawaa::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Salman Rushdie / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nTreat the crime as an event the viewpoint revisits through immediate scene, remembered exchanges, public versions of what happened, and private speculation. Each digression returns with new pressure on the present relationship: a rumor about the offense, a half-remembered apology, or a detail of the scene can become the hinge that brings the narrator back to what they still cannot say or ask.\n\nMake emotional strain alter the actual movement of a conversation. Let a character begin a practical question about evidence, money, or loyalty and then swerve into accusation, need, or withheld admission. The narrator may notice the swerve without resolving it. When comfort appears, keep it partial or awkward: someone may offer help while refusing the closeness the other person wants, or may say the right practical thing and still leave the deeper injury untouched. Warmth and pain can occupy the same exchange.\n\nUse the Rushdie profile's associative range to show how a private hurt becomes entangled with public story. A witness's account, a neighborhood version of the offense, or a remembered cultural reference can supply language the characters use to avoid or approach what they feel. Do not let that public material replace the private interiority; let it give the narrator a way to misname, exaggerate, or delay the admission that matters. The turbulent feeling should emerge from the narrator's specific diction and digressive habits, not from a repeated vocabulary of anguish.\n\nKeep the crime's practical stakes legible. The offense still has preparation, consequences, evidence, or pursuit, and the characters still have incentives they may conceal. Emotional exposure does not erase those stakes; it complicates them. A character can withhold a fact because apology feels impossible, or confess a lesser fault to avoid a greater one. Let the reader see the cost of returning to the difficult relationship wherever the scene actually takes it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
