            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-crime-06",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Crime · 06/07",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == crime}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Crime. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 6 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-crime; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPadawab::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Salman Rushdie / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the shared interval in readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution. Keep each speaker's practical interest in the crime clear enough that a reader can follow who wants what: one person may need money, another may fear exposure, another may want the offense kept quiet. Let suspense arise from divided loyalty or the risk of exposure rather than from withholding the speaker's identity.\n\nUse Rushdie's associative energy inside the scene rather than as long narrative detour. A speaker may reach for a remembered story, a piece of gossip, or a cultural reference to explain or avoid the present trouble. Let that reach return to the immediate stakes through the other character's response, whether they take it seriously, misunderstand it, or pull the conversation back to what is actually at risk. Keep the digression brief enough to preserve the roleplay's scannable flow.\n\nGive the narrator or focal character short passages of private thought that connect what is said to what they cannot say. They may notice that a familiar phrase now sounds like a threat, or that an old loyalty has become a burden. Keep that interiority within the active narrator's person and ownership.\n\nEnd at a natural continuation point that follows from what has just happened. The scene may stop during continuing speech, action, thought, or an unfinished setup. Do not require a question, cliffhanger, or forced choice at the end. The next reply should be able to continue the same conversation or practical situation without the recipe imposing a reversal or completed discovery.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadawac::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Salman Rushdie / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nChoose the social and satirical realist route. Treat the crime as an occasion for exposing how a particular community arranges money, reputation, family obligation, and official power. Follow the offense through its social consequences: who profits from silence, who performs outrage, who quietly depends on the scheme, and who is sacrificed to preserve appearances. Keep the satire specific to the supplied setting, period, and community rather than importing a generic cast.\n\nUse Rushdie's associative movement to connect the present offense to remembered scandals, public gossip, and local history. A character may compare the current crime to an older case that everyone recalls differently, or may invoke a cultural reference that lets them criticize without naming the powerful. Let those comparisons reveal the community's habits of excuse, blame, and selective memory. The narrator may digress, coin, and turn, but each return should sharpen the social picture rather than decorate it.\n\nLet the competing interests remain concrete. A family may want the matter settled quietly while an official wants a visible result; a witness may need protection while a merchant needs the supply route kept open. Show how these interests shape what people say in public and what they admit in private. Suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who committed the offense.\n\nKeep the narration socially situated. Use deliberate attention to status, obligation, and public/private contrast where the chosen narrator permits it. Aim the satire at the arrangements that constrain people, and let some characters act from genuine care, fear, or exhaustion so the indictment lands on the system rather than flattening every person into a caricature. Preserve the actual language and community supplied by the story.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadawad::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Salman Rushdie / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite with forceful diction and compressed transitions. Move quickly from one concrete fact to the next: a lock forced, a payment missed, a name scratched off a list, a bruise darkening under a sleeve. Let the crime's material wear show in damaged objects, tired bodies, and places that have been used hard. Keep physical stakes specific: what can be lost, broken, or taken if the next move fails.\n\nUse Rushdie's verbal energy in small, sharp forms. A coined phrase can name a repeated situation, a brief association can link the present offense to an older grievance, and a turn of syntax can make a threat sound like a joke. Do not let those moves expand into long digression. The association should arrive and return to the scene within a sentence or two, carrying new pressure into the action.\n\nVary the rhythm without losing the hard edge. Short sentences can register impact, discovery, or refusal. A longer thought is permitted when the character's reasoning needs it: weighing an alibi, calculating a risk, or deciding whether to trust a partner. Ground abrasive or rough speech in its speaker. A threat should sound like this person's habit, not a generic tough line.\n\nKeep the crime's competing interests legible. Someone wants the scheme to continue, someone wants out, someone wants a debt collected, and someone wants the whole thing buried. Show those interests colliding through concrete actions and exchanges, using the same compressed movement that carries the rest of the scene. Suspense can come from exposure or divided loyalty. End the scene at a natural stopping point without requiring a completed discovery or a change of plan.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadawae::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Salman Rushdie / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the crime through documents with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. A letter to a relative may soften the offense; a report to an official may omit a bribe; a journal entry may confess what the writer could not say aloud. What each document knows or conceals follows its author's situation, not the reader's need for explanation. Use the specific document form required by the scene rather than rotating through every available format.\n\nLet Rushdie's associative range shape the documents from within. A writer may connect the present offense to an old family story, a piece of public gossip, or a cultural reference that lets them justify or condemn what happened. Whatever associative move the document makes, it should expose the writer's selective memory or rhetorical purpose. A witness may embellish to make themselves important; an accomplice may bury a damaging detail inside a long account of someone else's fault.\n\nUse gaps, revisions, and conflicting accounts to advance the crime's evidence path. One document may record a time, another may contradict it, and a third may explain why the first writer lied. Preserve readable continuity without adding an omniscient explanation after every record. Let the reader compare what each writer knew at the time of composition with what later documents reveal.\n\nKeep the crime's competing interests active. A document can be written to secure cooperation, shift blame, preserve a reputation, or create a false record. Show the cost of writing and sending it: a letter may be intercepted, a report may be altered, a confession may be read by the wrong person. End the sequence at a natural point where the documents have changed the available interpretations without requiring a completed case or a final judgment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadawaf::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Salman Rushdie / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nKeep the crime's stakes and aftermath present while spending attention on chosen kindness, familiar preferences, practical care, ease, and small pleasures. A character may bring food to someone waiting for news, remember how a friend takes their tea, or sit quietly with a person who cannot yet speak about what happened. Let affection be sincere without requiring a confession or emotional repair. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable or remove the offense from the world.\n\nUse Rushdie's associative memory to make comfort specific. A shared story, an old joke, a remembered song, or a cultural reference can become a way of saying \"I am still here\" without demanding an answer. Let the narrator digress into a warm recollection and then return to the present room, where the crime's consequences still wait. The digression should feel like a gift of attention, not an escape from the story's actual difficulty.\n\nPreserve personality, privacy, and disagreement. One character may accept care while refusing to discuss the offense; another may offer practical help while keeping their own fear private. Manageable friction can remain: a small irritation about a borrowed coat, a difference over how to handle the next step, a joke that lands badly and is repaired. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable.\n\nShow how warmth operates within the crime's aftermath. The care that matters will follow the particular people and the particular offense: a safe meal, a clean shirt, a place to sleep, or a moment of ordinary laughter can all be ways of giving relief precisely because the danger or loss is real. Let the reader feel the relief without pretending the threat is gone. End at a natural point where the care has been given or received, without requiring a completed investigation or a permanent resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadawag::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Salman Rushdie / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Let a scene open into the social context that surrounds the offense: the neighborhood's history, the family's public reputation, the official record, or the rumors that have already begun to harden into story. Use enough narrative distance to frame the crime through history or judgment where the chosen narrator permits it. Distinguish narration from ordinary spoken language.\n\nUse Rushdie's expansive associative movement inside the formal architecture. A paragraph may move from the immediate scene to a remembered public event, a piece of gossip, a cultural reference, or an imaginative reconstruction of what someone else might have felt. Let each digression return with new weight. The transitions should be deliberate: a repeated phrase, a contrast between public and private, or a shift from the room to the city can carry the reader from one level to the next.\n\nKeep the crime's competing interests socially situated. Show how the offense disturbs arrangements of money, status, family obligation, and official power. A character may perform grief in public while privately calculating an advantage; another may refuse to speak because the truth would damage someone they love. Let the narrator frame these contrasts without granting access to excluded minds. Suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did it.\n\nPreserve the private interiority of the focal character. The formal distance should not erase the cost of admitting, withholding, or returning to a difficult relationship. Keep the narrator's judgment and the character's actual experience distinct. End the scene at the natural boundary the material reaches, where the social and private consequences of the offense have become as legible as the moment permits, without requiring a completed case or a final moral summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadawah::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Salman Rushdie / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nBegin at the moment someone has a reason to tell a crime as a story: a detained suspect buying time, a former accomplice settling an account, a witness persuading a reluctant listener, or a survivor turning an offense into an inheritance. Establish the audience, the occasion, and what the telling is meant to accomplish before the embedded account begins.\n\nLet the teller's selection and emphasis carry the Rushdie energy: digressions into family history, public rumor, remembered insults, and invented embellishment are all true to the teller's purpose even when unreliable as evidence. Keep the outer scene active. A listener may interrupt to ask about a name, a locked door, a missing hour, or a debt; the teller may answer, deflect, or fold the interruption into the next layer of the tale.\n\nUse nested accounts as interventions in the present, not decoration. A story about an earlier theft can explain why a character now trusts no one; a comic tale about a corrupt official can warn a listener against repeating a mistake; a long-delayed confession can be a weapon aimed at the person hearing it. Keep the levels intelligible by giving each embedded tale a concrete link to the outer crime: an object, a place, a phrase, or a person who appears in both.\n\nLet repeated phrases and objects accrue meaning across the telling. A coin, a scar, a proverb, or a nickname may mean one thing when first mentioned and something sharper when it returns in a later layer. Repetition does not certify truth; it shows how the teller builds a version of events and how the audience is asked to accept it.\n\nPreserve the difference between what a character knew at the time of the offense and what the teller has learned since. A retrospective account may explain a motive invisible to the participants without retroactively granting them that knowledge. When the outer scene resumes, let the telling have changed something practical: a listener may agree to help, refuse a request, recognize a lie, or understand why a crime was committed. End when the telling has done its work on the people present, not necessarily with punishment or a solved case.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadawai::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Salman Rushdie / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nTell the crime through documentary and historical memory. Move the narrator between official records, personal recollection, and the stories people tell about an offense, so the crime becomes legible through the gap between what was documented and what was lived. A police file, a property register, a letter, a photograph, or a court transcript can sit beside a remembered conversation or a family story, and the narrator can notice where they diverge.\n\nLet the narrator's attention roam associatively. A bureaucratic phrase in a record can trigger a digression about a neighborhood, a joke, a political slogan, or a childhood memory. These connections must return to the crime, not float away from it. A detail from a document may explain why a witness remembered an event differently, or a remembered insult may clarify why a suspect acted when they did.\n\nKeep the narrator's position specific. The narrator may be a researcher, a journalist, a family member, or a former participant, but the account must make clear what the narrator can know from records, what comes from memory, and what is inference. A vivid recollection does not stand as proof of a documented fact, and an official record does not erase a lived experience the narrator can attest to.\n\nLet syntax and distance follow the documentary route: sentences can be longer and more essayistic when the narrator weighs evidence, then tighten when a scene or a remembered exchange becomes immediate. The crime may remain partially unresolved in the records, but make its human aftermath concrete: who was harmed, who protected whom, what was concealed, and what the narrator now understands differently.\n\nDo not equate the setting with one country, bureaucracy, or national temperament. Use the specific place, language, and period supplied by the story. The documentary method should illuminate the particular social and historical conditions that made the offense possible without importing a generic severity or translated word order.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadawaj::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Salman Rushdie / Gothic-Punk\n\nSet the crime where old authority and present life collide: a family with inherited influence, a building with a history, a neighborhood where a former power still shapes who can act and who must ask permission. Let the offense arise from that collision—a scheme to escape an old obligation, a betrayal inside a trusted circle, or a violent attempt to keep a private desire hidden from the people who would punish it.\n\nUse the Rushdie profile to make the narrator's attention associative and crowded. A character can move from a practical problem to a remembered scandal, a public rumor, a line of poetry, or a joke, and the digression should return with new pressure on the present scene. The urban material detail matters: the condition of a stairwell, the sound of a club, the smell of a basement, the weight of a door. Let those details carry the atmosphere instead of announcing it.\n\nTreat intimacy as morally complicated. A trusted relationship can offer relief and also create exposure; a social mask can fall away in private without making the character safe or good. Show what a character risks by letting someone see a desire, a fear, or a plan. The crime should have consequences for those private bonds, not only for the public order.\n\nInherited authority here is social, economic, familial, or institutional—something the story's world already permits. If an old privilege shapes the crime, make its mechanism concrete: who owns the building, who controls the money, who remembers the old agreement, and who is afraid to break it.\n\nLet the story end where the collision has changed someone's position. A character may lose a mask, gain a debt, accept a dangerous alliance, or understand what the old authority cost them. Punishment is not required, and the crime need not be fully exposed. Leave the reader with the intimate political atmosphere of the offense: the weight of what was inherited, the cost of what was wanted, and the particular damage done in the present.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadawak::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Salman Rushdie / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nTell the crime through the oral, epic, and frame-tale route: more than one voice speaks. A neighbor who saw something, a relative who remembers an older quarrel, a former friend who knows what was owed, a child who overheard a phrase and repeated it wrong. Each teller has a reason to speak, and each version changes how the listener understands the offense.\n\nKeep the Rushdie energy in the movement between tellers. A story can leap from a police complaint to a family legend, from a market rumor to a remembered verse, from a joke to a curse. The digressions should feel crowded and alive, but they must return to the crime. A proverb, a nickname, or a repeated object can travel from one teller to the next and accrue meaning.\n\nMake the telling a social event. The frame may be a gathering, a journey, a waiting room, a funeral, or a late-night conversation, and the audience's reactions must matter. A listener may correct a detail, refuse to believe a version, add what they know, or walk away rather than hear the rest. The crime becomes legible through these exchanges, not through a single authoritative account.\n\nPreserve the difference between what a teller knows firsthand, what they heard, and what they are inventing. A vivid story is not proof, but it can reveal a motive, a fear, or a loyalty that the official account misses. Let the reader track which details recur across tellers and which belong to only one voice.\n\nKeep language, region, community, and period specific when the story supplies them. Do not force English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure onto the scene. End when the telling has changed the listeners' understanding of the crime and its aftermath, not necessarily when the offense is solved or punished.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadawal::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Salman Rushdie / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the narrator's record of the crime and its aftermath in dated entries or short reflective passages, moving between what happened, what was said, what was remembered, and what the narrator now thinks. The record need not be chronological in every entry; a later passage can return to an earlier detail and revise its meaning.\n\nLet the narrator's attention roam within the entries. A practical note about a meeting can lead to a memory of a childhood street, a line from a song, a public scandal, or a joke that suddenly seems less funny. These digressions should return to the offense with new weight. A remembered phrase can explain why a suspect hesitated; a piece of gossip can reveal who was already watching.\n\nKeep the narrator's position specific. The narrator may be a participant, a witness, a relative, or someone who discovers the crime after the fact. The diary form must make clear what the narrator knew at the time of writing and what has become clear only later. A later realization cannot be inserted as an earlier motive.\n\nLet the entries vary in length and pressure. Some days may produce only a line; others may spill into a long reflection on a conversation, a document, or a fear. The crime should remain present even in the quiet entries: a name the narrator avoids writing, an object left in a drawer, a question that returns without an answer.\n\nUse the specific language, place, and period supplied by the story. Seasonal attention, omission, direct feeling, and plain argument are available where appropriate, but no universal quietness or mandatory inventory of Japanese scenery applies. End when the record has brought the narrator to a new understanding of the offense or its aftermath, not necessarily when the crime is solved or punished.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadawam::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Salman Rushdie / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nAssemble the crime through the polyphonic and nonlinear route: attributed accounts that do not arrive in chronological order. A witness speaking years later, a letter written before the offense, a police report filed the same week, a child's memory, a rumor repeated in a market. Each account belongs to a specific voice with its own interests, and the reader must hold them together.\n\nLet the accounts digress and collide. A testimony can leap from the crime to a family history, a political slogan, a song, a joke, or a remembered insult. The nonlinear arrangement must make those connections meaningful: a detail mentioned casually in one account may explain something in an account set earlier or later.\n\nKeep the attribution clear. When the narration moves between voices and times, the reader must know whose account is speaking and when it was produced. A shift in voice can be marked by a name, a document, a place, or a distinct rhythm, but it must remain intelligible. The polyphony must not dissolve into an anonymous chorus.\n\nPreserve the difference between what each voice knows and what the reader can now see across the accounts. A participant may misunderstand an event that another account clarifies; a later voice may know what an earlier one could not. The crime becomes legible through these juxtapositions, not through a single authoritative summary.\n\nPreserve the relevant language, community, and history supplied by the story. Magical realism is one route rather than the whole category; this style cannot add supernatural facts to a world that excludes them. End when the accumulated accounts have changed the reader's understanding of the offense and its aftermath, not necessarily when the crime is solved or punished.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadawan::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Salman Rushdie / Light Novel\n\nEnter each scene at the moment the crime or its consequences become immediate: a hand on a door, a message arriving, a debt called in, a lie repeated to the wrong person. Keep spatial changes easy to follow by naming the place and the movement between places in plain terms. Use short paragraphs around action, dialogue, and private response so the reader can track who is speaking and what is happening.\n\nWhen a character pauses, compress the Rushdie energy into focused reaction beats. Let a memory, a joke, a rumor, or a cultural reference flash through in a sentence or two, then return to the scene. A suspect can think of an old song while deciding whether to run; a witness can remember a family saying while recognizing a face. The digression should add pressure or information, not delay the scene.\n\nGive unfamiliar names or mechanics clear presentation when they appear. If a scheme depends on a particular document, account, route, or relationship, show what it is and why it matters before it becomes decisive. Do not bury a necessary fact in a long aside.\n\nLet the crime remain present in the reaction beats. A character's private response can be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to who they are, but it must connect to the offense: what they fear, what they want, what they are hiding, or what they have just understood. Keep the reader oriented to the stakes even when the scene is quiet.\n\nDo not impose first person, comedy, a young cast, or a closing cliffhanger. End at the natural boundary of the scene or the response, not at a forced reversal. A request can remain unanswered, a pursuit can continue, and a character can be left waiting without the prose inventing a result.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadawao::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Salman Rushdie / Lyrical Prose\n\nTurn the crime's material facts into a system of recurring images whose returns mark changes in knowledge or consequence. A stain, a key, a sound, a repeated route, an object left behind can appear first as ordinary detail, then as evidence, then as something the viewpoint cannot stop noticing. Let the images originate in the teller's own world — family story, neighborhood gossip, a public event the offense brushed against — so the associative movement belongs to this narrator rather than to a general lyric atmosphere. When a comparison unfolds, let it change what the narrator understands about the act or the people involved, and coin a word or turn a phrase only where the coinage catches something the available vocabulary misses.\n\nShape sentences around the rhythm of discovery and aftermath. A long, digressive sentence can gather the circumstances leading to a moment — memory, rumor, public history braided together — and return to the scene before breaking against a short, plain clause that states what was done or what was found. Go back to an image when a later event gives it a new application, so that repetition works as evidence rather than decoration; a pause over the way light falls in a room where a crime occurred must still carry the practical fact that someone searched that room, missed something, or came back to it. Figurative language renders how the mechanics of the offense lodge in perception and memory; it never replaces them.\n\nKeep the human stakes audible inside the heightened register. A culprit's fear, a bystander's divided loyalty, an investigator's exhaustion can move through rhythm and image without making every character speak in the narrator's figurative voice; let dialogue interrupt the lyric flow with ordinary, practical speech, and let that contrast mean something. The reply may end inside a continuing pursuit, a changed understanding, or an unfinished consequence; the crime and the feeling do not have to resolve in the same breath.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadawap::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Salman Rushdie / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nTreat the crime as the point from which time runs in two directions. The present scene carries the practical aftermath — an investigation, a changed household, a person missing, a debt, a silence — and from that present let the narration move into the remembered occasions the crime has altered. A room, a phrase someone used to say, a habit that has stopped, an object out of place can open the association; so can a rumor now circulating, a public event the offense touched, a story the teller cannot keep out of the memory. Let recollection arrive crowded: private memory trailing gossip, history, and half-remembered cultural reference, then returning with something the present needs — a motive clarified, a relationship understood differently, a detail that now reads as warning or evidence.\n\nKeep the movement between present and past legible without announcing it. A sentence can begin in the current moment, turn toward an earlier occasion, and come back with a small, irreversible change made visible. Make absence specific rather than general: the particular chair, the particular errand, the particular way someone answered the door. Let ordinary pleasure stay ordinary where the story permits — a meal, a joke, a moment of comfort need not be undercut. The private register holds what speech does not: longing, self-reproach, the revised meaning of a familiar thing, none of which the narrator has to say aloud.\n\nDo not let melancholy erase the crime's practical stakes. Someone is still hiding something, still owed, still being looked for, and the reflection stays in contact with that pressure. The account can remain unresolved, but the unresolved element must be a real consequence or a real question — who pays, who knows, what happens to the one left — not a refusal to say what happened.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadawaq::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Salman Rushdie / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed oral telling. Establish a situated teller and an occasion for the telling: someone who knows part of the crime recounts it to a listener, a gathering, or a later inquirer. The frame does work: the teller's position shapes what is said, delayed, exaggerated, and left for the listener to infer. A bystander knows the victim's habits but not the culprit's motive; an accomplice may reveal the scheme while concealing a betrayal; an investigator can repeat what witnesses said without yet knowing which account to trust. Draw the frame's conventions from the tradition the world actually supplies, keeping Arabic, Persian, Turkish, Hebrew, Kurdish, and neighboring traditions distinct rather than blending them into one ambient antiquity.\n\nLet the crime emerge through the teller's movement among personal recollection, public rumor, and cultural reference. A proverb, a local story, a remembered song, a piece of gossip can enter when it belongs to the teller's world and bears on the offense — helping the teller explain, accuse, justify, or avoid a subject — never as ornamental wisdom. Give the listener presence: a question, a correction, or an interruption can change the telling's direction or expose a gap in it. Delayed revelation is meaningful when the teller has a reason to delay — shame, fear, loyalty, the need to be believed — and the telling makes that reason legible.\n\nKeep the crime's practical mechanics followable inside the frame: preparation, act, evidence, pursuit, aftermath, and what remains uncertain. Preserve the language, setting, and community as supplied; antiquity, mysticism, exile, religion, or an honor code enter only if the world establishes them. The telling may end before the full truth is reached, with the listener left weighing what has been said.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadawar::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Salman Rushdie / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect a small number of exact details and let them carry the crime's weight: one object out of place, one repeated phrase, one hesitation, one mark on a door, one name said too carefully. Make each detail do more than one job — establish the scene, reveal a relationship, open an inference about the offense. Do not explain what the detail already shows; if a character notices the open drawer, the reader can infer the search without being told the room was searched.\n\nCompress the Rushdie profile's verbal energy instead of extending it: precise, surprising word choices; a single coined or unexpected phrase; an association packed into a few words where another treatment would digress for a paragraph. Keep clauses direct and movement economical; a sentence may turn sharply, but it should not wander. When an inward connection matters, give the thought the length it needs — one exact sentence of private reasoning can carry more than three descriptive ones — and let plain statements carry the hardest facts: who did what, what was taken, who is lying, what a character is afraid to say.\n\nPreserve causality. Omission should leave something inferable, not delete the link between an act and its consequence; if a character decides to hide evidence, show the decision or its result so the next scene is legible. Keep cast voices individual — a terse culprit and a talkative witness should not sound alike. The account can end on a precise, unresolved fact or a continuing risk, but not on vagueness.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadawas::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Salman Rushdie / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the crime in sustained scene-first prose. Begin from a concrete moment — a meeting, a search, a confrontation, a phone call, a return to a place — and let the narration move naturally among what the viewpoint character does, says, perceives, remembers, and privately thinks. Use paragraphing and transitions to orient without recap machinery. When a scene shifts toward recollection or association, let the shift arise from something in the scene — an object, a phrase, a face, a piece of news — and return with a practical yield rather than a detour.\n\nFold the Rushdie profile's energy into the narrator's voice. The prose may coin a phrase, turn a sentence unexpectedly, or connect a private memory to a public event or a rumor moving through the neighborhood, provided the move belongs to this teller and this situation: a character waiting for a call remembers the story that explains why the call matters; an investigator hears a witness's account and recognizes the shape of a story already circulating on the street. Do not let inventiveness replace the crime's concrete mechanics — the reader should still know what was done, what is at stake, and what the viewpoint is trying to learn or avoid.\n\nKeep authorized inner experience available without making every mind transparent: render the focal character's reasoning, uncertainty, and motive while others are known through speech and action, and let suspense arise from exposure, divided loyalty, or the gap between what the viewpoint knows and what others are doing. End at a natural scene boundary — a task begun, a question raised, a risk recognized, a conversation interrupted — without requiring a completed discovery or a lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadawat::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Salman Rushdie / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship the lens through which the crime becomes legible. Choose scenes that show how the offense, investigation, or aftermath presses on this particular bond — a partner who knows too much, a friend who lied, a sibling who needs an alibi, a lover who suspects — and let the relationship's private language and repeated moments carry information. A phrase used between them can return with altered meaning after a discovery; a familiar gesture can become evidence of trust or concealment. Use only the actual supplied premise and its particular promise; do not stock the scene with borrowed tropes.\n\nGive affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery the space they have earned. Reassurance after a frightening event is not filler when it shows what the characters are protecting or cannot say; a quarrel advances the case when it exposes a divided loyalty or a hidden motive, and the emotional payoff should arise from the relationship's established terms rather than from a generic confession or reconciliation. The Rushdie profile's associative range enters as shared reference: the pair's jokes, stories, songs, arguments, the cultural touchstones they carry between them, so that a digression demonstrates intimacy — and its absence tells its own story.\n\nKeep the crime's practical mechanics intact. The reader understands what happened, what is at risk, and what the characters are trying to do about it. Suspense can come from exposure — one character knows what the other does not, or both know what a third party must not discover — and private thought stays available to the authorized viewpoint, so the reader may hold a secret the beloved cannot. End inside a continuing emotional or practical consequence; the relationship and the case need not resolve in the same reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadawau::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Salman Rushdie / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the crime as an account of deeds that will be remembered, argued over, and retold. Open with a deliberate declaration that fixes the act's public weight — who did the thing, who suffered it, what was set in motion — then expand into the particulars the declaration cannot hold: the preparation, the divided loyalties, the pursuit, the aftermath. A compact sentence names the offense and its consequence; the sentences that follow show what the compact version leaves out.\n\nOrganize the chronicle with recurring phrasing and names. A designation should come from an established deed or way of speaking, not from an ornamental epithet attached to every entrance, and a phrase should return when a later occurrence gives it a new application: the act that once meant loyalty later meaning betrayal, a name spoken differently after the crime. Let kinship, oaths, debts, gifts, feuds, or public undertakings organize the account when the supplied world establishes them, and keep the source tradition coherent rather than assembling every epic convention at once; a Nordic pantheon, heroic doom, or ceremonial dialogue has no automatic place here.\n\nLet the Rushdie profile's range supply the chronicle's crowded material: gossip, remembered stories, and public events are what a reputation is made from, and the telling moves among them. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective, distinguishing what was seen, what was reported, and what later became known, rather than granting the chronicler omniscience. The crime's mechanics stay legible — what was done, by whom, with what consequence — and the account may end with a deed still unfolding or a reputation still contested, leaving another act available to be remembered rather than declaring the story closed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadawav::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Salman Rushdie / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the offense through the Scandinavian social-realist crime route: a community whose institutions hold the consequences of what was done. Anchor the story in one concrete social setting — a workplace, a housing cooperative, a municipal office, a union hall, a family flat, a ferry route — and let the crime travel its actual channels: shifts, tenancies, forms, debts, meeting minutes, what neighbors know and pretend not to. Use the setting, language, and community the current story supplies; where the story names a specific country, keep that country's institutions and terms distinct rather than dissolving them into generic Nordic scenery. Snow, silence, and solitude are available facts, not a required atmosphere.\n\nGive the narration Rushdie's associative energy as the investigation's working method. A sentence turns from a witness's statement to a childhood memory of the same courtyard, to a news item, to a disputed account of how a family got its flat, and returns having enlarged what is at stake. Each loop of digression picks up another voice — neighbors, colleagues, officials, relatives, rivals — so the crowd of partial versions becomes both the story's social world and its field of suspicion. Keep every claim attributed and its status visible: what the viewpoint knows firsthand, what was told, what is rumor wearing better clothes.\n\nSuspense lies in exposure, divided loyalty, or what the community will do with what it knows; the reader may know the culprit from the start. Keep the viewpoint's private thought and feeling available in the active interiority — a suspicion can stay unsaid for a supported reason such as fear, loyalty, embarrassment, utility, or uncertainty, and not every silence conceals guilt. Let ordinary life continue inside the harm: meals, repairs, children, shifts, small kindnesses. Stop where the scene and role boundary naturally pause, owing no confession, arrest, or closed case.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadawaw::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Salman Rushdie / Screenplay\n\nWrite in concise screen-oriented scenes: a heading when location or time changes, action lines for what is visible and audible, clear speaker cues for dialogue. Keep action blocks concrete — who enters, what they handle, what the room shows, what carries from offscreen — without filling the page with camera commands.\n\nPut Rushdie's associative energy where the form can hold it: juxtaposition and attributed voice-over. Let a cut do the work a digressive sentence would do — the domestic kitchen against the institutional corridor, a news broadcast playing under a family argument — so the contrast carries the implication instead of narration explaining it. When a sequence needs the disputed past, use voice-over assigned to a specific character: a recalled childhood story, a public event, a relative's version of what happened, then a return to present action. That voice-over is the speaker's performance, not the film's omniscience — it cannot verify what the speaker only believes — and it does not make private thought audible to the cast; keep required interiority as clearly attributed nonspoken material in the active presentation.\n\nLet dialogue carry the competing versions of the offense. Neighbors, colleagues, officials, relatives, and rivals describe the same event according to their interests and partial knowledge, each in a distinct idiom, so the crowd arrives as speech rather than description. Distinguish what a speaker knows from what they say aloud; a withheld suspicion has a supported reason, and a pause or look can carry feeling without certifying guilt. Suspense can be exposure or divided loyalty with the audience already knowing who did it. Close the scene where the boundary naturally pauses — after a completed action, on a pending request, or a plain acknowledgment of what is now known — not on an obligatory confession or reversal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadawax::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Salman Rushdie / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable script: clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and business that can be staged. The stage holds the crowd live, so let Rushdie's verbal energy arrive as contending performance: a neighbor with a story that keeps growing, an official with a procedure, a relative with a forty-year grievance, each in a characteristic idiom that turns, digresses, quotes a public event or a proverb, and is cut off by the next version. The competing accounts of the offense are the scene's music; stage directions stay economical around them.\n\nKeep the physical world doing real work. A document is handed over or withheld, a cup set down too hard, a door left open on purpose, a pause held before an answer. Business and line can disagree — hands doing one thing while speech claims another — and the audience reads the gap without the directions certifying guilt. Objects are the offense's residue: whatever ledgers, keys, letters, or phones the story has already supplied, handled by people who can reach them.\n\nUse storytelling monologue and aside only where the scene earns them: a public account performed to others, testimony, a self-justification addressed past the fourth wall. Performed speech is not the destination for required private experience; keep that as nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation, unheard by the cast. A withheld suspicion needs a supported reason — fear, loyalty, embarrassment, utility, uncertainty — and suspense can be exposure or divided loyalty even with the culprit known to the house. End on business, an entrance or exit, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known; no obligatory confession, no gathering of suspects.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaway::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Salman Rushdie / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite the offense from inside one authorized mind, and let Rushdie's crowd live there as voices. The viewpoint recalls what neighbors, colleagues, officials, relatives, and rivals said, rehearses what they are probably saying now, argues with them, and casts what they would say if they knew. A remembered claim arrives wearing its teller's idiom and stays a claim — vivid, attributable, unverified.\n\nLet association, not chronology, order the material. A knock at the door calls up a childhood street; a smell opens an office from twenty years ago; a phrase someone used at the funeral returns with its weight changed. Braid these with perception, private words, and bodily sensation, and mark the seams so the reader can tell present from memory from rehearsal — a shift of tense or address, a sensory anchor that pulls the mind back, a name repeated. The crime enters only through its consequences here: the debt, the unopened envelope, the face at the window, the call not yet returned. What the mind circles, avoids, or slowly admits is the suspense, whether or not the reader already knows who did it.\n\nKeep the mind whole. Hesitation may carry meals, children, work, weather, small kindnesses — not every turn conceals guilt. Another character does not know a conclusion merely because the reader has watched it form, and an authorized admission is not withheld to protect a reveal. Follow the stream to the scene's natural boundary and stop there — mid-turn, mid-avoidance, or at plain acknowledgment of what is now admitted — owing no confession, arrest, or completed investigation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadawaz::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Salman Rushdie / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the story in sender-attributed messages shaped by each person's actual digital habits: one writes in fired-off fragments, another in careful paragraphs, another sends links or voice notes, another reads and does not reply. Use only the interface events that matter — a timestamp gap, an edit, a receipt, a channel change — and never fabricate one to add subtext.\n\nLet the competing versions of the offense arrive as typed testimony. A neighbor's account in three rapid messages; an official's tidy paragraph; a relative answering a different question with a decades-old grievance; someone pasting a news link that only half fits. Rushdie's associative movement lives in the turns between senders: a question about money becomes a grandfather's story, becomes an accusation, becomes an apology, and returns three exchanges later with its meaning changed. Keep each version attributed and its status legible — firsthand, secondhand, disputed, performative — so a vivid claim is never mistaken for a verified one.\n\nDistinguish sent text from unsent draft from private thought. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, evidence of what its writer chose to shape, not automatically their live inner life; give required private interiority its own clearly separated place outside the transcript — what a character notices in the room before typing, what they delete, and why a suspicion stays unmessageed when fear, loyalty, embarrassment, utility, or uncertainty warrants it. Suspense can be exposure or divided loyalty with the culprit known; a delayed reply may be guilt, a shift, or a sleeping phone. End at a message sent or left unread, a draft abandoned, or a closing beat of separated context — no confession or arrest owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadawba::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Salman Rushdie / Visual Novel\n\nWrite in the visual-novel rhythm: concise scene-setting, attributed dialogue, then a focal inner response, paced for an illustrated moment. Put Rushdie's associative energy in the response beats. A background or expression cue — the cluttered kitchen, the municipal corridor, a face that closes — trips the focal mind into a childhood memory, a public event, a disputed family story, a phrase from years ago, and the next line of dialogue or scene change pulls the narration back to the exchange. The alternation itself keeps the reader oriented between the present scene and the recollected or imagined one.\n\nLet dialogue carry the competing versions of the offense: neighbors, colleagues, officials, relatives, and rivals, each describing the same event out of interest and partial knowledge in a distinct idiom, while the focal narration weighs them — what the viewpoint knows firsthand, what was just told, what is gossip. An expression cue orients the moment and can feed the viewpoint's suspicion or sympathy, but a closing face proves nothing; credibility turns on what the versions actually contain and how they contradict each other, not on any face as a truth indicator. Keep scene changes legible, and add no branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, images, or engine commands; a discovery opens the next conversation rather than a choice screen.\n\nKeep private thought in the focal narration or inner response under the active interiority contract. Another character does not know a conclusion because the reader has watched it forming, and a withheld suspicion has a supported reason. Suspense can be exposure or divided loyalty with the culprit known. End the scene where the boundary naturally pauses — an exchange still open, a pending request, plain acknowledgment of what is now known — without a required confession or reversal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadawbb::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Salman Rushdie / Web Serial\n\nWrite in installments that keep an easy way back in: each reply makes legible what is underway, what changed locally, and what remains open, recovering a name, a debt, or a pending question only when the reader needs it again, never retelling the whole history that established it.\n\nSerialization suits Rushdie structurally: digression becomes planting. An installment may spend apparent distance on a neighbor's story, a public event, a relative's disputed account, a phrase someone used years ago — and a later installment recalls it with new significance once the offense's consequences touch it. The through-line is the criminal present: obligations, exposure, divided loyalties, what the community will do with what it knows. The associative material is the ground that present moves through, not decoration around it. Let the viewpoint character connect the immediate scene to recollection, gossip, and cultural reference, then return; keep each turn attributable and each claim's status visible — known, told, rumored, contested.\n\nLet progress be uneven and earned: knowledge, relationships, work, and resources shift by what the scene supports, while ordinary life — shifts, meals, repairs, children, small kindnesses — continues inside the harm. Keep inward experience available whenever it contributes feeling or thought, even when no decision changes. Use an occasional hook rather than an artificial cliffhanger, and close an installment on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. A withheld suspicion has a supported reason — fear, loyalty, embarrassment, utility, uncertainty — and no installment owes a confession, arrest, or solved case.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaxaa::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Stephen King / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the crime from inside someone whose practical involvement is tangled with a relationship they cannot comfortably name—a partner, an ex, a brother, the friend they keep almost calling. The job, the scheme, the testimony, or the investigation presses this character up against the cost of admitting something, needing something, withholding, apologizing, or going back, and the pressure registers privately first: what they remember about the other person, what they almost said at the kitchen table, what they did instead of saying it.\n\nMake the strain legible through concrete, ordinary detail rather than a stock vocabulary of anguish. The character notices the exact way a familiar room has changed since the other person left, the sound a car makes pulling out at four in the morning, the weight of a phone they keep not answering. Dialogue sounds like shared history: old arguments, half-finished conversations, things the other one used to do. What a speaker says and what they think can differ for a concrete reason—fear, pride, exhaustion, the practical need to keep the job moving—and the private reasoning that fills that gap stays available under the active narrator's permissions.\n\nLet the crime put pressure on the relationship without replacing it. Someone may lie to protect a person, withhold a fact out of old loyalty, or find that pushing the case forward damages someone they still care about; whatever they actually do—delay, mislead, confess, refuse—let it cost something visible in their behavior afterward. The turbulence lives in the movement between the task at hand and the older hurt: a long stretch of thought when the character is genuinely working something through, a short jagged reaction when the pressure leaves no room for it. The offense and its consequences keep moving; the suspense includes whether these people can stand to be honest with each other. Comfort, if it comes, can be partial, awkward, or interrupted by the next practical problem, and the story does not owe anyone a clean apology, a permanent break, or a repaired ending.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaxab::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Stephen King / Chat Roleplay\n\nTake what the player submitted and resolve it into the next coherent interval of the crime story—an interval the player's character can observe, speak into, and act within. Keep narrative person, action markers, and player control exactly as the active authorship contract sets them; this style governs presentation and readability, not those rules. Write scannable paragraphs, and give every line of dialogue an unmistakable speaker.\n\nGive the interval lived-in local memory. The people in the scene have routines, practical worries, and histories that surface in how they talk: a bartender remembers who used to sit in that corner, a mechanic reads what the damage on a fender really says, a neighbor knows which shouting was ordinary and which night was different. That texture gives the player's character concrete things to notice and use—who might know what, who owes whom—and the dialogue carries each speaker's own history and stakes rather than a neutral voice explaining the crime.\n\nKeep the criminal matter genuinely in motion: preparation, a tense meeting, the aftermath of an offense, a search, a conversation that changes what someone is willing to do. NPCs act from what they know and want—helping, refusing, lying, cooperating, or simply going on with their own business—and their choices are not steering mechanics aimed at the player. The reply owes no revelation, reversal, or set piece; supported action advances immediately, and unanswered questions can carry over.\n\nStop where the scene's own attention stops. The character may still be mid-conversation, waiting on an answer, or weighing a move; the world keeps its life around them—a phone can ring, a car can pull up, someone can keep talking—without a required question, cliffhanger, or forced choice. The next move belongs to the player.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaxac::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Stephen King / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nTake the social and satirical realism route. Treat the crime as a crack in a working community: who is owed what, which debts are written down and which are only remembered, whose reputation shields them, and what everyone has agreed not to notice. The offense matters because of the arrangements around it—the credit extended at the counter, the favor quietly returned, the silence one family maintains—so the story explains the community by showing what its rules actually are once they are strained.\n\nUse the local grain to make that network legible: routines that keep the place running, the practical details of who pays, who waits, and who is never asked. A speaker's history of obligations, resentments, and alliances shapes both what they say and what they walk past without saying, and dialogue locates each person inside the web—who defers to whom, who is owed an answer, who cannot be told no. Let the satire live in the gap between what people claim and what they do—the upright figure keeping a second set of books in their head—rather than in caricature.\n\nFollow the consequences through competing interests rather than one culprit's psychology. An inquiry pressures people to choose between loyalty and self-protection; a scheme depends on everyone continuing to play their assigned part; exposure threatens not just the offender but the arrangements others relied on. Suspense can run on divided loyalty and the risk of being seen even when the reader knows who did it. Keep the setting, language, period, and community exactly as the active world supplies them—the route governs how the story is told, not where it happens or how the cast speaks. The account may stop with the social situation still in motion; it does not need to tally every debt or settle every reputation before it ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaxad::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Stephen King / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the crime in hard, concrete prose: forceful diction, compressed transitions, material wear. Cut between preparation, action, and consequence without smoothing over the damage. The physical world is under real pressure—a lock that has been forced before, a jacket stiff from a long night, the drawer where the tool sits between batteries and old receipts—and a mistake costs time, money, blood, or someone's trust.\n\nKeep human attention inside the compression. The viewpoint character notices the specific things about people that matter here: how a partner checks a door twice without thinking, the habit a witness falls into when lying, the small kindness someone still performs in the middle of a bad job. Those observed particulars carry the relationships at scene speed—a character can grasp a betrayal from a half-open drawer without the whole history being narrated—and dialogue is each speaker's own rough or careful speech, grounded in what they have done. A threat lands harder from someone with a past.\n\nWork rhythm deliberately. Short, hard sentences carry a fight, a break-in, a sudden understanding; a longer thought earns its length when the character is genuinely working through a decision or remembering something that changes it. Cut explanation that merely repeats what the detail already showed. The crime stays the engine—preparation, execution, pursuit, aftermath in motion—with suspense running on exposure, divided loyalty, or the question of whether the plan holds. Not every scene is survival horror, and not every caring act is a scam: some people help, some refuse, some damage cannot be undone. The reply can stop between beats or in the middle of one—the pressure may be easing, shifting, or still bearing down.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaxae::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Stephen King / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the crime through documents, each with a definite author, a recipient or purpose, and a moment of composition. Use the form the scene actually calls for—a witness statement, a letter, a logbook page, a text exchange, a report, a note under a wiper blade—rather than a fixed rotation of formats. A document exists because someone needed to record, request, explain, or cover something, and everything in it answers that need.\n\nLet each author's attention run on concrete, practical grain. The statement notes the exact amount missing; the letter gets sidetracked into an old argument before reaching its point; the logbook records times its writer cannot interpret. Recollection appears as the author chooses to include it—what they half-knew then, what they think it means now. What a document knows or conceals follows its author's situation: a report omits what the writer was not supposed to see, a letter softens a fact the recipient would not accept, and a draft left unsent is still a composed artifact shaped by its writer's purpose, not a window onto a whole live mind. If the active contract requires private, unspoken experience, these papers do not supply it by themselves.\n\nBuild the case from the seams between entries. One account can contradict another with neither author lying—they simply knew different things. A later entry revises an earlier one because its writer learned more or changed their mind; a crossed-out line, a missing page, a timestamp that does not match can carry more weight than any narration would. Preserve continuity without appending an omniscient summary after each record; preparation, incentives, execution, evidence, pursuit, and aftermath emerge from the paper trail and its gaps. The sequence may pause with the last document's business unfinished—a letter stopped mid-reasoning, a report filed while the question it raises is still open—rather than closing every account it opens.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaxaf::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Stephen King / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite the crime story with warmth as one of its real materials. The offense, the investigation, or the aftermath stays active, and the same attention that tracks the danger also spends itself on chosen kindness, familiar preference, practical care, ease, and small pleasure: coffee made for someone who has been up all night, the order remembered without asking, the chair everyone knows is hers, the last piece saved back.\n\nKeep the comfort specific through concrete detail. Care shows in action and object—how a person fixes a plate, the old joke that still lands in a bad week, driving someone home and waiting until the door closes, checking a lock that was already checked. Affection is sincere without owing anyone a confession or a repair; a character can be looked after and still be difficult, and a relationship can carry warmth and its old arguments at once. Personality, privacy, and disagreement stay intact: comfort does not make the cast agreeable, and it does not lift the established dangers out of the world.\n\nThe kindness is part of how these people live under the pressure, not a reward for progress or a promise that everything will be fine. It may steady someone before a hard choice, soften an aftermath, or simply run alongside the fear. Keep the scheme, pursuit, or consequence moving on its own track: do not resolve the crime to earn a warm scene, and do not turn a caring gesture into a twist. A reply may end wherever the scene's attention ends—an ease noticed, a ritual kept, care moving in one direction or both—while the larger situation keeps whatever state the story has actually reached.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaxag::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Stephen King / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite the crime in composed novel prose: deliberate transitions, sustained paragraph movement, and enough narrative distance to frame a scene through its social context, history, or judgment wherever the chosen narrator permits that framing. Keep the narration distinct from ordinary speech—fuller sentences, careful sequencing, room for a paragraph to develop an observation—while staying contemporary and true to the supplied world. The formality is architectural, not ornamental: no imported aristocracy, no archaic diction, no uniformly elaborate dialogue.\n\nUse that architecture to give the offense its social dimension. A payoff, a break-in, an arrest can be framed by the arrangements that made it possible: a neighborhood's changed fortunes, a family's guarded name, a workplace's unspoken rules. The narrator can connect a present act to an earlier one and weigh what a choice will cost the people around it, with the frame arriving as lived local fact—the storefront that changed hands twice, the debt everyone still remembers—rather than abstract commentary.\n\nLet the crime's competing interests drive the movement. A pursuit can be narrated with attention to what the pursuer understands and what the pursuit will cost; an investigation can unfold through conversations in which each speaker's history speaks through them and reveals their place in the community. Around the dialogue, the narration may supply the social frame the speakers themselves would not put into words, moving readably between outward event and inward concern within the active narrator's permissions. The style deepens events rather than stalling them. A scene can close on a meaning it has genuinely developed—an act placed in its history, a judgment earned—while the practical situation remains whatever the story has made it: unresolved, half-resolved, or shifted onto new ground.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaxah::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Stephen King / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen in a room where the telling is happening for a reason. Before the embedded account begins, make the teller's present purpose concrete: buying time until someone arrives, shifting blame onto a partner, recruiting the listener, warning them off, justifying something already done, or simply keeping them in the chair. That purpose is where the crime's competing interests live, and it governs what the teller selects, emphasizes, speeds past, and leaves out, so the embedded tale is never a neutral summary of the offense.\n\nMake the embedded account feel lived rather than recited. Fill it with the specific rooms, tools, work routines, weather, and small recollections that belong to the teller's actual past: which door stuck, whose shift it was, what the bag actually weighed. Dialogue inside the tale should carry each speaker's history — a partner's complaining that exposes an old grievance, a victim's politeness that exposes a habit of appeasement. Keep the outer scene alive around the telling: the listener pours a drink, checks a phone, challenges a detail, remembers the same event differently, and the teller can interrupt themself, correct a slip, or refuse a question that touches exactly what the telling is meant to manage.\n\nKeep the two levels cleanly signaled. Anchor the outer scene in one tense and the embedded account in another, and return to the outer room through concrete beats — a question, a noise, a change in the listener's posture. Carry one or two objects, phrases, or gestures across the levels — a key, a receipt, a particular threat, a habit of touching a scar — and let them accrue meaning rather than certify truth: a repeated phrase may sound rehearsed, a repeated object may be the thing the teller cannot stop circling, and the listener may notice the pattern without naming it.\n\nTreat the embedded account as an interested act, not a confession or a case file. The teller can be truthful, partly truthful, or lying, and the reader may know more than the listener does. Suspense comes from what the telling is doing in the present — whether it is working, what it costs the teller to keep going, what the listener will do — not from the listener solving or verifying anything. The frame is a craft device, not a setting import: no court, bargain, cliffhanger between nested accounts, or anything else the story's world does not already permit. A reply can end while the tale is still unfinished, at a refusal, or on the listener staying, leaving, paying, calling someone, or simply understanding what the teller has done; the account never needs to be certified as evidence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaxai::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Stephen King / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social-realist route of the German-language tradition: the offense grows inside a recognizable order of wages, rent, workplace hierarchy, schooling, family obligation, and local reputation. Treat those conditions as the story's mechanism rather than its backdrop. Let one specific material pressure narrow a character's options — the shift that cannot be refused, the loan already refinanced once — and let an institution's ordinary procedure enable or conceal the act: the form nobody double-checks, the inspection scheduled months out, the rule enforced only against some employees. Aftermath lands unevenly: the person with savings walks away; the person without loses the room.\n\nKeep the viewpoint close to one or two ordinary people whose routines and recollections make that world concrete. Let narration move between outward event and inward concern inside the same beat: thinking about a bill while locking a door, remembering a supervisor's tone while bagging what was taken, registering the gap between what the neighborhood knows and what the police will write down. Dialogue carries personal history and social position — a worker's careful explanation of a rule reveals both the rule and the fear of breaking it — without becoming a lecture.\n\nRun the crime as a causal sequence the reader can follow: preparation, incentive, relationship, execution, aftermath, each grounded in ordinary steps and ordinary consequences — a shift swapped, a document altered, a debt called in, a colleague who notices and says nothing. Suspense comes from exposure or divided loyalty inside those social stakes, and the reader may know who did it from early on; this is not a puzzle withheld from view.\n\nWrite composed, continuous prose with room for reflection, grounded in specific rooms, objects, and exchanges. Keep the sentences as plain and direct as the viewpoint character is, and let complexity arise from the situation rather than ornamental syntax; do not translate the tradition into severity, bureaucracy, or a single national temperament. The crime stays legible as a human act inside a particular social order, with no required punishment, moral summary, or closing verdict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaxaj::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Stephen King / Gothic-Punk\n\nBuild the crime as a collision between an inherited authority and present life. Fix that authority concretely — a family, a building, a firm, a debt, a title, a local institution — and show its past still governing who may enter, borrow, work, or speak. Render it through modern material detail rather than gothic decor: a landlord's ledger open in a phone app, a family name on a lease, a locked room in a converted factory, a rule everyone obeys without remembering why.\n\nKeep the viewpoint close to an ordinary person caught in that collision, and let narration move between outward event and inward concern: feeling the weight of a family obligation while counting cash into someone else's hands, hearing a parent's old warning while crossing a threshold, noticing a trusted person's social mask slip and not being able to say so. Dialogue carries personal history and present fear. Add no monster, vampire, secret power, or supernatural rule; the dread is what people are doing to each other inside the arrangement, and the world stays exactly as natural or unnatural as the story has already established.\n\nLet atmosphere alter how a specific encounter is experienced instead of lying over the whole story as fog. A meeting in a back office, a ride through a half-emptied neighborhood, a conversation at a family table, an exchange in a parking garage — each should feel different because of who holds authority in that room and what someone wants there. Work through urban material facts: worn carpet, flickering signage, a chained gate, a phone that keeps buzzing, a coat too thin for the weather. Let private desire and morally complicated intimacy complicate the offense: a character protecting someone they love, desiring someone they should not trust, keeping faith with an institution that has already harmed them.\n\nConstruct the offense through preparation, incentive, relationship, execution, and aftermath. Social masks can fall away in trusted company and go back on; do not make every caring act fraudulent or every pause conceal guilt. Suspense may come from exposure or divided loyalty, and the reader may know who did it from the start. Leave the inherited system more intelligible than you found it while keeping its human costs concrete; no punishment, resolution, or final unmasking is owed, and a reply can stop inside an encounter still underway.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaxak::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Stephen King / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and domestic realism route of Indian literary traditions: the household is the field where the offense is prepared, hidden, discovered, or paid for. Place the crime inside a specific home, neighborhood, and community, keeping language, region, community, and period exactly as specific as the story supplies. Do not force English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure onto the scene; write the particular people and place actually given.\n\nKeep the viewpoint close to one or two ordinary people whose routines and recollections make the domestic world concrete. Move between outward event and inward concern inside single beats: thinking about a marriage arrangement while hiding money in a kitchen tin, remembering a neighbor's remark while answering a question at the door, registering the difference between what the family says at dinner and what it knows. Dialogue carries personal history and social position — a relative's warning, a landlord's visit, a child's question, a shopkeeper's silence can each reveal the social order and the speaker's stake in it — without becoming a lecture.\n\nRun the crime as a causal sequence the reader can follow — preparation, incentive, relationship, execution, aftermath — grounded in domestic steps and consequences: a loan taken, a document altered, a room searched, a job lost, a neighbor who sees but does not speak. Let reputation, obligation, care, shame, and the work of keeping a household running operate as motives and consequences, not decoration. Suspense may come from exposure or divided loyalty, and the reader may know who did it.\n\nWrite composed, continuous prose with room for reflection, grounded in specific rooms, objects, and exchanges; impose no mandatory inventory of spices, festivals, or family roles and no single national temperament. The crime stays legible as a human act inside a particular domestic and social order, with no required punishment, moral summary, or closing verdict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaxal::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Stephen King / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and psychological realism route of Japanese literary traditions: give continuous narrative access to one or two viewpoint characters, and let the crime become legible through their private reasoning. A specific social pressure — workplace obligation, family expectation, neighborhood reputation, financial need, institutional hierarchy — should enter thought itself, shaping what the character notices, avoids, rationalizes, and decides not to say. The social world is not background; it is the material the character thinks with.\n\nKeep the narration close to ordinary people whose routines and recollections make their psychological state concrete. Move between outward event and inward concern inside single beats: hearing a supervisor's tone while deciding whether to log a discrepancy, remembering a parent's warning on the way to a meeting, registering the difference between what is said aloud and what is known. Dialogue carries personal history and social position — a colleague's silence, a neighbor's question, a family member's small kindness can reveal both the order and the speaker's stake in it — without becoming a lecture.\n\nConstruct the crime through preparation, incentive, relationship, execution, and aftermath in a causal sequence the reader can follow, carried by ordinary steps and ordinary consequences: a shift swapped, a form altered, a debt called in, a promise broken, a person who notices and says nothing. Let guilt, fear, obligation, desire, and the effort of keeping a life running work as motives and consequences, not decoration. Suspense may come from exposure or divided loyalty, and the reader may know who did it.\n\nWrite composed, continuous prose with room for reflection, grounded in specific rooms, objects, and exchanges. Seasonal attention, omission, and direct feeling are available where the scene supports them; impose no universal quietness and no mandatory inventory of scenery. The crime stays legible as a human act inside a particular social and psychological order, with no required punishment, moral summary, or closing verdict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaxam::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Stephen King / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social realism and chronicle route of Latin American literary traditions: present the crime as a social event that a particular person records, remembers, or retells because they have a stake in how it gets understood. That stake shapes the account — what is counted, what is skipped, who is named, who is protected. Keep language, community, and history as specific as the story supplies; manufacture no generic regional temperament, landscape inventory, or political allegory, and add no supernatural fact to a world that excludes one.\n\nKeep the viewpoint close to an ordinary participant or witness whose routines and recollections make the chronicle concrete. Move between outward event and inward concern: thinking about a neighbor's warning while describing what was found, remembering a shopkeeper's kindness while listing what was taken, registering the gap between what the street knows and what the official record will say. Dialogue carries personal history and social position — a bystander's account, a relative's silence, an official's question can reveal both the social order and the speaker's stake — without becoming a lecture.\n\nRun the crime through preparation, incentive, relationship, execution, and aftermath in a causal sequence the reader can follow, and let the chronicle stance make remembering itself part of the aftermath: a debt called in, a route changed, a door left open, a witness who speaks, a witness who disappears, a family that keeps silent. Whether the telling looks back from after the fact or accrues while events are still happening, keep the same distinctions: what the teller saw with their own eyes, what they heard secondhand, what they knew then, what they know now. These separations keep the chronicle honest without turning it into a case file. Suspense may come from exposure or divided loyalty, and the reader may know who did it.\n\nWrite composed, continuous prose with room for reflection, grounded in specific streets, rooms, objects, and exchanges. The crime stays legible as a human act inside a particular social and historical order, with no required punishment, moral summary, or closing verdict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaxan::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Stephen King / Light Novel\n\nEnter each scene cleanly: within a few lines, establish where the viewpoint character is, what they are doing, and what immediately presses on them. Keep every spatial change easy to follow — car to building, kitchen to back room, street to stairwell — with a concrete anchor at each threshold: a door, a light, a sound, a change in footing. Use readable paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response so the scene's movement stays legible at speed.\n\nKeep the viewpoint close to an ordinary person whose routines and recollections make the crime concrete, and give private response real room: let the character reason, hesitate, notice, and remember while watching a door or holding a bag, rather than emitting one reaction line per beat. A thought about a debt can run alongside a count of the exits; a parent's warning can surface while handling something that should not be handled. Dialogue carries personal history and social position — a colleague's joke, a landlord's threat, a family member's silence can reveal both the social order and the speaker's stake — without becoming a lecture.\n\nConstruct the crime through preparation, incentive, relationship, execution, and aftermath in a causal sequence the reader can follow, shown in ordinary steps and consequences: a shift swapped, a form altered, a debt called in, a door left open, a witness who notices and says nothing. Let visual staging carry the pressure: a locked room, a crowded street, a ringing phone, a stain on a sleeve, a person standing too close make the stakes immediate without any monster or supernatural rule, and the world stays as the story has established it.\n\nGive unfamiliar names or mechanics a brief, clear presentation when the reader needs it, and skip explanations the reader does not. First person, comedy, a young cast, and a closing cliffhanger belong here only if the story already supports them; none is a requirement. A reply can end on a decision to stay, leave, pay, call someone, or simply on the character understanding what has happened — no twist, confession, or completed case is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaxao::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Stephen King / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet the crime become present through the teller's attention to the objects, routines, and repeated sounds that carry its pressure. A scheme or offense need not be explained all at once; it can emerge through what the viewpoint notices while preparing, waiting, or returning to a place where the act has already changed ordinary life. Keep each image traceable to the scene or to the teller's association. A comparison may extend when it changes what the narrator understands about the risk, the relationship, or the evidence, but it should not replace the concrete fact that makes the crime causally legible.\n\nUse rhythm to manage information flow. Short plain sentences can establish the practical act: who handled the money, which door was left unlocked, what time the call came. Longer sentences can then follow the inward movement that gives the act its weight. Recurring sounds or images may mark a return to the same object under a new meaning: the same receipt, the same stretch of road, the same drawer now opened with a different hand. Do not let recurrence become a symbolic chorus that obscures who did what and what they knew.\n\nKeep the viewpoint's access consistent. A participant may dwell on the feel of a task because the task is part of the offense; an investigator may notice a detail because it connects to an earlier case or a private memory. Dialogue remains individual and practical, carrying a person's history rather than adopting the narrator's figurative register. The account can end with the crime still exerting pressure, a pursuit unresolved, or a consequence only beginning to register, without requiring a completed revelation or a lesson. Let the lyrical attention serve the story's actual causal movement rather than pausing it for ornament.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaxap::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Stephen King / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nTreat the offense as an event that has already altered the texture of a familiar life. The present account may follow an investigation, a cover-up, a return, or an attempt to resume ordinary routine, but the crime should remain causally active rather than becoming only a prompt for sadness. Let the narrator notice small irreversible changes: a habit now performed alone, a room kept ready for someone who will not return, a phrase that used to mean one thing and now means another.\n\nAllow present experience and memory to coexist. A question from an investigator or a practical errand can bring back an earlier occasion without stopping the scene. The remembered occasion should clarify the present attachment, suspicion, or incentive. Do not let every object become a relic or every recollection end by announcing that nothing will be the same. Ordinary pleasure can remain pleasurable, and a character may still enjoy a meal, a joke, or a familiar task even while living with the crime's consequences.\n\nKeep the viewpoint's knowledge and feeling distinct from what other characters know. A reflective narrator may understand that a relationship has changed while the other person still speaks as if it has not. Dialogue should carry each speaker's particular history and present concern. The account may end with an unfinished attachment or a consequence still unfolding. Do not require a confession, a completed punishment, or a final statement of loss. Let the reflection arise from the particular offense and the particular life it has interrupted.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaxaq::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Stephen King / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed oral account: a situated teller relates the offense, its preparation, or its aftermath to a particular listener or gathering. The frame should be concrete and ordinary rather than ornamental. The teller may be a participant, a neighbor, a family member, or someone who has lived with the consequences, and the listener's questions, interruptions, or silence can shape what the teller explains, repeats, or leaves out. Keep the teller's access consistent with what that person could know.\n\nGround the account in social and domestic realism. The crime should emerge from practical pressures and relationships: a debt, a disputed inheritance, a workplace scheme, a family obligation, a hidden arrangement. Describe the routines and local particulars that make the offense legible: who usually opens the shop, how the money is counted, which neighbor watches the street, what the family does after an arrest. Dialogue should carry each speaker's history and present concern rather than a universal narrator voice.\n\nUse the telling itself to manage suspense. The teller may know the outcome but reveal it gradually, or may be trying to understand what happened. A repeated phrase or a return to an earlier detail can gain new meaning as the account proceeds. Do not import mysticism, exile, religion, or an honor code as automatic scene requirements. The frame need not resolve into a moral. The account can end with the listener's response, the teller's unfinished explanation, or a practical consequence still developing, without requiring punishment or complete disclosure.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaxar::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Stephen King / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect a small number of exact details that carry the crime's practical weight. One detail can establish preparation, another can mark the offense, and another can show its consequence: the key left under the mat, the envelope moved to the wrong drawer, the phone call that comes too early. Let each detail do specific work. Do not add a second detail when the first already makes the act legible, and do not explain what the reader can infer from the arrangement of events.\n\nUse direct clauses to keep the causal chain clear. A character may decide, act, notice, or avoid. When inward concern matters, state it plainly: she did not want him to see the receipt; he waited because the neighbor was still on the porch. Keep the essential thought long enough to make sense, but cut the explanation that only restates the visible action. Omission should leave something inferable, not remove the reason a choice was made.\n\nLet dialogue remain individual and practical. A short exchange can reveal a relationship or a divided loyalty without a narrator's gloss. The account may end with a consequence still developing or a question unanswered. Do not force a fragmentary rhythm or a one-sentence paragraph at every beat. A precise extended sentence may serve the scene better than several short ones. The crime should remain causally legible from the selected details and direct movement, not from a summary inserted after the fact.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaxas::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Stephen King / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the crime as a continuous scene-first narrative. Open in a concrete moment of preparation, pursuit, exposure, or aftermath, and let the offense and its consequences become legible through what the viewpoint does, says, notices, and remembers. Move naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience. A practical task can carry the scene forward while the narrator's attention turns briefly to an earlier event or a private worry, then returns to the present exchange.\n\nUse paragraphing and transitions to orient the reader without recap machinery. A new paragraph can mark a shift in speaker, place, or attention. Do not pause to summarize what the reader already knows. Let the viewpoint's access remain consistent: a participant may know the scheme but not what the investigator suspects; an investigator may notice a detail without knowing its full history. Dialogue should carry each speaker's particular relationship and present concern, not a universal narrator voice.\n\nKeep the crime's causal chain clear even when the prose is flexible. Incentives, divided loyalties, and consequences should emerge from the scenes rather than from a narrator's retrospective explanation. The account may end with a question unanswered, a pursuit continuing, or a consequence only beginning to register. Do not require a lesson or a completed resolution at the end of every reply. Let the register and rhythm come from the immediate scene and the chosen viewpoint, with the offense remaining the organizing pressure.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaxat::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Stephen King / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nLet the established relationship premise organize the crime narrative. Choose scenes that show how the offense, scheme, or investigation bears on the particular bond between these characters: a shared secret, a divided loyalty, a promise made before the crime, a discovery that changes what one of them believes about the other. Do not insert a roster of familiar tropes. Use only the actual supplied premise and let its specific promise determine which moments of affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery receive space.\n\nMake private language and repeated moments carry the relationship's history. A phrase one character uses only with the other, a gesture repeated under changed circumstances, or a familiar routine interrupted by the crime can show what the bond costs or protects. When a callback appears, let the later occurrence give it a new application rather than merely restating the earlier scene. Keep the crime's causal facts active: the relationship is under pressure from exposure, divided loyalty, or consequence, not merely providing emotional decoration around an unrelated case.\n\nPreserve each character's access and agency. One may know more about the offense than the other, and that difference should create suspense without making either character a fixed role. Dialogue should carry each speaker's particular history and present concern. The account may end with the relationship changed, threatened, or still holding, but do not require a confession, a reconciliation, or a betrayal at every turn. Let the emotional payoff fulfill the particular promise of the supplied premise while the crime continues to exert its pressure.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaxau::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Stephen King / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the crime as an account of deeds whose consequences become part of what these people remember and retell. A scheme, theft, assault, or betrayal can enter the chronicle because the community has lived with its results. Use deliberate openings to establish the act and its known outcome, then expand into the particulars that make the reputation incomplete. Keep the present scene's scale: a crime remains a crime even when it belongs to a longer local history.\n\nUse compact declarations to establish a person or remembered act, then show the practical details that complicate the public version. A recurring designation should come from an established deed or way of speaking, not from an ornamental epithet assigned to every entrance. Return to a phrase when a later occurrence gives it a new application. Keep shared reputation distinct from a focal character's private account; being remembered as the one who started the trouble does not mean the act was effortless, deliberate, or fully understood.\n\nLet dialogue interrupt the authority of the chronicle. A participant may object to one detail, or answer an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern. This need not become parody. Humor may arise from the persistence of a local story, but a criminal act is not mock combat and its harm is not heroic doom. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective. The account can end with the consequences still unfolding or another modest act available to be remembered, rather than declaring the crime legend on the community's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaxav::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Stephen King / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the crime through the social and domestic realism of a specific Scandinavian community. Let the offense, scheme, or investigation move through ordinary places: a workplace, a housing block, a family gathering, a local office, a commute. Keep Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish context distinct when the story supplies one; do not blur them into a generic Nordic setting or import snow, isolation, or bleakness unless the actual scene contains them.\n\nUse the Stephen King profile's attention to concrete memory and local relationships as the main way a viewpoint character understands the case. A person recalls who usually parks where, which neighbor kept a key, how a colleague behaved the year the workplace changed hands, what a relative said about money. These recollections are not proof. They become material for the institutional side of the story: a police interview, a workplace inquiry, a housing-office record, a journalist's question, a lawyer's preparation. Show the remembered detail being checked, contradicted, confirmed, or given a different meaning by procedure and record. When an institution's summary is partial, misleading, or convenient, let the story follow the human consequence: the witness who is not called back, the report that omits an awkward name, the file that remains open without action, the neighbor who changes what she says after a second visit. Use that institutional friction to complicate trust and obligation without making every official a villain or every record a falsehood.\n\nLet the telling move between outward events and inward concern in readable prose. The viewpoint may notice the smell of a stairwell, the weight of a borrowed coat, the pause before someone answers a question, the effort of speaking politely to a person they have known for years. Keep the private thought available as actual nonspoken interiority, not as a summary of what the character later concluded. Another character does not know what the viewpoint has remembered unless it is spoken, written, or otherwise transmitted.\n\nTreat the crime as an event with human aftermath, not an automatic puzzle or a guarantee of punishment. Suspense can come from exposure, divided loyalty, or the cost of telling what one knows. A witness may protect a family member, a colleague may misremember for an understandable reason, an investigator may care more about one part of the case than another. Let those motives be particular and social rather than making everyone secretly guilty.\n\nDo not impose a grim register. Ordinary warmth, comic friction, practical kindness, and unremarkable routine can appear where the story supports them. The style does not require a monstrous secret, a bleak landscape, or a joke after every beat. End the response where the current scene or investigation naturally stops, without forcing a confession, arrest, or completed case.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaxaw::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Stephen King / Screenplay\n\nWrite the crime story as a screen-oriented sequence of scenes. Use scene headings when location or time changes, concise action lines that show visible and audible events, and dialogue blocks with clear speaker cues. Keep the action readable and concrete: who enters, what they carry, what they touch, what they look at, what they hear. Do not fill the page with camera commands. Use a cut, offscreen sound, or voice-over only when the chosen sequence needs it.\n\nLet the Stephen King profile's attention to ordinary life appear in the observable details of the scene. A character checks the same drawer twice, sets a coffee cup on a stack of bills, recognizes a truck by its engine sound, pauses before answering a question about a neighbor. Dialogue carries a person's history rather than a universal narrator voice. Let people talk about work, family, money, local habits, and the practical weight of the crime without making every line a clue or a confession.\n\nPreserve required private experience as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation, not as dialogue another character can hear. The note may record what the viewpoint remembers, fears, or notices while the scene continues. It is not automatically knowledge available to the cast. Do not disguise it as voice-over unless the selected sequence needs voice-over, and do not use it to replace visible action or spoken exchange.\n\nAdvance the crime through what the scene can show and what the characters can learn from one another. A recollection can be tested by a visible object, a document, a photograph, a location, or another person's account. Let the investigation move through preparation, incentive, relationship, execution, evidence, pursuit, and aftermath from the chosen viewpoint. Suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did it.\n\nKeep the cast ordinary and particular. They may be tired, helpful, evasive, loyal, careless, or precise for reasons the scene supports. Do not make everyone witty or ready with polished banter. A scene can end after a result, an interruption, or a plain acknowledgment of what is now known, without an obligatory cliffhanger or completed case.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaxax::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Stephen King / Stage Play\n\nWrite the crime story as a playable script. Use clear speaker names, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be staged. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene. Do not add film-camera instructions. A monologue or aside needs a dramatic purpose rather than replacing every private thought.\n\nLet the Stephen King profile's ordinary people occupy the stage with recognizable routines and local relationships. They bring in groceries, check a phone, fold a jacket, set down keys, avoid a chair, pour a drink, look at a photograph. Dialogue carries their history: a sibling remembers who answered the door, a coworker knows the schedule, a neighbor recalls the argument, a parent notices what is missing. Keep the speech particular to each person rather than making everyone sound like the same narrator.\n\nPreserve required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in its active presentation, clearly separated from the performed scene. It is not something another character can hear. Do not disguise it as an aside or monologue unless the dramatic purpose is separately established. The audience may know what a character fears or remembers while the other characters do not.\n\nUse the shared stage space to make competing interests visible. Who can see an entrance, reach an object, hear a line, or leave without being noticed should be clear enough that the scene's relationships and pressures remain legible. Let the crime's aftermath appear in practical business: a family dividing tasks after an arrest, a workplace meeting after a theft, a friend returning borrowed money, a witness deciding whether to speak.\n\nDo not force a theatrical revelation or gathering of suspects. The scene can stop after a result or an interruption. Suspense can come from exposure or divided loyalty even when the audience knows who did it. Keep the cast capable of genuine help, refusal, evasion, or plain uncertainty according to the actual story.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaxay::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Stephen King / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite the crime through the associative mental movement of a particular authorized mind. Let perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern shape the telling. The viewpoint may notice a smell and be carried back to a kitchen years earlier, hear a phrase and recall who used to say it, feel a door handle and remember the day the lock was changed. Syntax and paragraph shape may follow those associations, but keep enough orientation for the reader to distinguish what is happening now from what is remembered, feared, or imagined.\n\nUse the Stephen King profile's concrete memory and pressure on ordinary people as the material of the crime's aftermath. The mind returns to a receipt, a phone number, a pair of boots by the door, a neighbor's habit of parking too close, a supervisor's tone, a child's question. These details are not automatically clues. They become meaningful through the way the viewpoint keeps circling them, noticing a change, or connecting one memory to another. Let the crime's preparation, incentive, relationship, execution, evidence, pursuit, and aftermath emerge from that mental movement rather than from a linear report.\n\nKeep the stream of consciousness particular to the authorized mind. The technique does not make every character share a mind, authorize human-owned thoughts, or turn all scene facts indeterminate. Another character does not know what the viewpoint has remembered unless it is spoken, written, or otherwise transmitted. The narration may reveal a focal motive while the other characters remain unaware of it.\n\nLet the prose remain readable and grounded in ordinary life. The viewpoint may be interrupted by a phone call, a knock, a child's voice, a bus arriving, a pain in the back, a sudden need to check the time. These interruptions can break or redirect the mental movement without making the story chaotic. Return to the recurring concern when the mind returns to it, not on a fixed schedule.\n\nDo not impose a monstrous secret, ominous weather, or a joke after every beat. The crime can remain a human event with competing interests and uncertain aftermath. Suspense can arise from what the viewpoint is trying not to think about, what keeps surfacing, or what another person's ordinary words suddenly make harder to ignore. End where the current mental and physical scene naturally stops, without forcing a confession, arrest, or completed case.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaxaz::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Stephen King / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the crime story as a readable exchange of sender-attributed messages. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the scene or the investigation. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one writes in full sentences, another sends fragments, one waits before answering, another edits a message after sending it, one uses voice notes, another never uses punctuation.\n\nLet the Stephen King profile's ordinary people and local relationships appear in what they choose to send. They ask about a shift, check on a parent, mention a car that was parked outside, send a photograph of a receipt, argue about money, arrange to meet, avoid answering a direct question. Dialogue in this form carries a person's history rather than a universal narrator voice. Keep the speech particular to each sender and to the relationship between them.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought. Required private experience may appear as clearly separated context outside the exchange, labeled as nonspoken interiority for the authorized viewpoint. Do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext. A read receipt, typing indicator, or deleted message appears only when it matters to what the characters know or feel.\n\nLet the crime's competing interests emerge from the exchange. A person delays answering because they are at work, not because they are guilty. Another sends too much because they are frightened. A third misreads a short reply as anger. Suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did it. The transcript may include the preparation, incentive, relationship, execution, evidence, pursuit, and aftermath of the offense from the chosen viewpoints.\n\nContext outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript. It can describe where a person is, what they are doing while they type, or what they remember. Do not let that context replace the actual messages or turn every pause into a hidden meaning.\n\nWhen a delay, edit, or channel change does matter, make its significance legible from the surrounding exchange: a question goes unanswered because a shift has ended, an edit replaces a name with a vaguer phrase after a warning, a photo of a receipt is forwarded to someone who should not have it. Do not require every turn to contain a meaningful interface event, and do not make those events carry more proof than they can support. End the response where the exchange naturally stops, without forcing a confession, arrest, or completed case.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaxba::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Stephen King / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the crime story as a scene-oriented visual-novel presentation. Use concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Keep scene changes legible. Background or expression cues are purposeful references, not executable engine code or a mandatory label on every line. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected.\n\nLet the Stephen King profile's ordinary people appear through practical detail and local relationship. A scene may open on a kitchen with unopened mail, a break room with a broken vending machine, a parking lot after a shift, a hallway where a neighbor's door is ajar. The focal narration notices what the viewpoint would notice: the smell of cigarettes, the sound of a specific car, the way a colleague avoids a question, the weight of a phone in a pocket. Dialogue carries each person's history rather than a universal narrator voice.\n\nUse the focal narration or inner response to carry required private experience. The viewpoint may remember who usually sits where, what a supervisor said last week, how a relative reacted to the news. That private thought is not automatically knowledge available to the other characters. Do not turn the cast into fixed reaction portraits whose expression always reveals whether they lie. A changed smile can attract suspicion, but a contradiction in an arrangement or account must do the evidentiary work when the story needs it.\n\nLet the crime's competing interests move through the scenes. A person may help, refuse, evade, or misunderstand for reasons the scene supports. Suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did it. The style does not require a mystery puzzle, a procedural, or a guarantee of punishment. The scene can end after a result, an interruption, or a plain acknowledgment of what is now known.\n\nKeep the presentation readable and free of engine commands. A background or expression cue may orient the moment, but it does not need to appear on every line. End the response where the current scene naturally stops, without forcing a confession, arrest, or completed case.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaxbb::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Stephen King / Web Serial\n\nWrite the crime story as an installment-oriented web serial. Maintain a readable through-line across replies: what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover context briefly only when it helps the reader re-enter the scene. Do not retell the whole investigation at the start of every installment. Use earned developments and occasional hooks without making every turn a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger.\n\nLet the Stephen King profile's ordinary people and local relationships carry the serial progression. A detail established earlier can return with new meaning: the car that was parked outside, the shift someone traded, the receipt left on the counter, the neighbor who usually walks a dog at a certain hour. Briefly recover the necessary particular rather than restating everything. Progress can involve knowledge, relationships, work, or resources, not only new evidence.\n\nWhen an earlier particular reappears, give the reader enough to recognize the original moment and enough to see the difference the return makes. The same car is now missing a hubcap, so the recognition survives while the fact has changed. The traded shift is mentioned by a supervisor who was not present when the trade was made, placing knowledge where it should not be. A receipt that one person compared with a bank statement is now in someone else's hand. The returned detail can support an inference, complicate a relationship, reopen a practical question, or simply show how ordinary time has moved around the investigation. It should not be forced to prove a new clue in every installment.\n\nKeep the crime's human aftermath moving through preparation, incentive, relationship, execution, evidence, pursuit, and aftermath from the chosen viewpoint. Suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did it. A person may delay answering because they are at work, help because they care, or refuse because they are afraid. Let those motives be particular and social rather than making every pause a hidden guilt.\n\nPreserve required inward experience as actual nonspoken interiority in the active presentation. The viewpoint may remember, worry, notice, or reason without an immediate decision changing. Another character does not know what the viewpoint has remembered unless it is spoken, written, or otherwise transmitted. The installment can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known.\n\nDo not add leveling systems, rankings, or progression-fantasy world rules merely because the style is selected. The serial form gives selected facts room to acquire meaning, not a requirement that every reply contain a clue, reversal, confession, or cliffhanger. End where the current scene naturally stops.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadayaa::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Terry Pratchett / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the crime's aftermath inside the gap between official phrasing and private admission. Whenever a character reaches for procedure, policy, or the institution's prepared sentence to postpone saying the thing that hurts, render the moment twice: first as the public sentence, then as the private pressure behind it. A witness may give a factually complete answer about the time of a delivery while the private pressure is about the argument at the door. A colleague may describe the correct evidence-handling form while the pressure is the untouched chair where the missing officer used to sit.\n\nMake strain change the actual machinery of the scene, not just the narration around it. When a speaker begins an admission, let the next clause swerve into a safer practical detail—a case number, a tea order, a request for a signature—and then return to that detail later in the exchange because the first attempt cost too much. Carry that cost forward after the conversation: a witness checks the same drawer twice, a sergeant writes the date of absence in the wrong column, a bar owner polishes a glass already clean. These are not clues to a hidden psyche; they are the visible residue of an avoided sentence.\n\nLet the comedy sit where the institution and the hurt intersect. An officer may quote a regulation about compassionate leave as if it were offering the comfort it withholds. A ledger note may record 'no further action' next to the name of someone still waiting for an apology. A procedural absurdity should reveal who is being protected, who is being blamed, or who is being left alone inside the case. None of this should turn into diagnosis; the narrator should simply keep the gap open and let the reader feel its weight.\n\nWhere the active contract permits interiority, prefer the particular sensation over a summary word: not 'she felt guilty', but the image of her rehearsing the phrase 'I checked the doors' because saying 'I checked on him' was not an option. Not 'he was grieving', but the reflex of starting to call in a warning that the shift roster already proved too late. Keep each person's relationship to language distinct—one character uses policy as a shield, another mocks it while using it, a third barely manages the words at all. Warmth and pain can share the same exchange, but they do not have to resolve. A partial apology may remain explicitly partial; an awkward offer of help may be refused with a phrase that means both 'no' and 'thank you'.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadayab::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Terry Pratchett / Chat Roleplay\n\nStage the interval as a series of clearly attributed exchanges, each one moving a practical piece of the crime while the speakers reveal their relation to authority in the words they choose. When Officer Marlowe says 'the perimeter has been secured', the beat lands because she is holding a roll of tape that nobody has unrolled. When Deacon says 'we should get a statement from the girl', the beat lands because he looks at the girl's locked door, not at his notebook. The humor comes from the precise distance between the official register and what the room actually shows, not from everyone becoming witty.\n\nKeep attribution unmistakable. Dialogue tags may be minimal, but the scene must never blur who is speaking, who is answering, and who is simply present. If a character shifts from crisp procedural language to a halting or blunt phrase, do not gloss over it; let the next speaker respond to that shift. The crime's practical reality should remain in the exchange itself: someone is guarding a door, someone is buying time before a superior arrives, someone is deciding whether the smeared ledger is worth touching. The suspicion can operate through divided loyalty or exposure rather than a clue hidden in every line.\n\nLeave the scene in motion at the end. The last line may be an unfinished order as a radio crackles, a witness rising without being dismissed, or a suspect asking a question that belongs to the next reply. Do not slam a door on a forced choice. The active NPC should be doing something that implies a next action: checking a seal, turning a page, dialing a number halfway and stopping. Preserve the player's separately selected narrative person and control settings. Do not insert first-person private thought for a character whose interiority is not available, and do not narrate the player's reaction as if you controlled it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadayac::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Terry Pratchett / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nTake the social and satirical realist route, and write the offense as a disturbance inside a tightly observed community. Begin each scene by anchoring who owes whom, who can enter which door, and who must answer to whom before the event happens. When a neighbor says the gate was left open, show that the claim is about the night watchman's nephew and the delayed rent. When an office report states the time of discovery, show that the family already knew two hours earlier because a creditor's boy was there. The narration never needs the sentence 'the record is false'; it simply places the official account beside the collectively known sequence and lets the contradiction sit.\n\nLet the comedy exploit the distance between the document and the room. A clerk may spend a paragraph rendering an argument as 'a dispute regarding customary access' while the actual dispute was over a broken scale and a shove. A duty officer may ask solemnly for a list of persons present, and the list will include the name of a man everyone knows was in the next town, written in bold because his father is on the committee. Each comic correction should expose a particular social mechanism: a bribe called 'storage', a beating recorded as 'misadventure', a witness listed as 'unknown' because naming him would cost another household.\n\nDo not collapse the community into one temperament or a single dialect. Let a shopkeeper speak in inventory, an aunt in half-sentences that require the listener to finish them, and a young officer in official formulas that read like a language he is still rehearsing. The crime's ramifications should move through the wider group: a changed testimony affects a loan, a sealed room affects a wedding, a name reported to the registry affects a school place. The viewpoint may be a culprit, accomplice, victim, investigator, or bystander, but the story should remain socially situated. Use the language, period, and community actually supplied by the active world; do not import a shadow government of whimsical paperwork or a generic 'face-saving' emotional rule.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadayad::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Terry Pratchett / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nLoad every transition with a concrete object or physical fact; do not use explanatory padding between them. Open with the smeared threshold, the ledger with the wrong date, the lock plate that has been forced twice, and let each detail ask the next practical question. When the official explanation enters, it must collide with the physical room: the supervisor says the disturbance was 'contained', but two chairs are stacked in front of the rear fire door and no one has moved them. The comedy lives in that collision, not in a wisecrack after it.\n\nTreat material wear as testimony. A coat that is too clean for the weather reveals a man who did not come from the street. A drawer that sticks because it was not closed straight reveals someone in a hurry. A cup that smells of the night before reveals how long the witness waited. Each such detail must connect to the case's practical reality—access, timing, money, a body's position, a missing page—rather than decorate the scene. No detail should be presented as a symbol; it should remain an object someone touched.\n\nUse short sentences for impact and withhold a long one until the character's reasoning actually needs it. A blow, a lock turning, a sudden silence: these are short. A calculation about whether to run, a memory that explains why a name is the wrong one, a plan that changes what happens next: these may run longer. Never let a rough speaker sound like a borrowed line. The threat should be the specific phrasing of the person in the doorway, not a generic 'nice place'. When you write anger, show the hand that knocks over the ink bottle and then rights it. When you write fear, show the officer who checks the same window three times after calling it secure.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadayae::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Terry Pratchett / Epistolary / Documents\n\nLet each document carry the case one step further, but only in the form its writer would actually use and only with the knowledge available at that moment. A station log records 'patrol completed 04:10' in the same handwriting that has underlined the previous day's tea order. A witness letter to the examining magistrate writes 'I am prepared to make a full statement' and then spends three paragraphs explaining that the door may have been open earlier because the wind was strong. The comedy comes from the pressure between the official format and what the writer cannot put into it.\n\nChoose the document by the situation, not by rotation. If a senior officer must be informed, use the memo; if a colleague must be warned without being blamed, use the note left under a ledger; if a suspect must remember something later, use the list written at four in the morning. Do not include private journals unless the story actually requires them and the contract permits their full tenor. Each document must show its author, recipient or purpose, and time; if it does not, show why that information is missing.\n\nLet later documents revise earlier ones without announcement. A report may state 'no further action' and then be followed by a letter asking for the return of a key. A margin note may say 'see attached' over a page that is not attached. A ledger entry for 'cleaning' may precede an invoice for repairs that no one reported. These discrepancies should not be explained by an omniscient voice. The reader assembles the offense from what each document knows, what each one conceals, and where the forms fail to contain the real story. Keep the consecutive entries readable; if a gap is meaningful, let the next document make the reader feel the missing piece, not spell it out.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadayaf::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Terry Pratchett / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nPlace each act of care inside the already-running case, and write the care with the same concrete attention as the evidence. When Maria makes tea for the witness, show the chipped cup she chose on purpose because it holds heat longest, the two spoonfuls from the wrong tin, and the way she wipes the edge before carrying it past the locked door. The warmth is in these particular choices, not in an announcement that she cares. The witness may refuse the tea, may drink it without speaking, or may answer a different question than the one actually asked. None of that cancels the care.\n\nKeep the offense in the same shot as the kindness. The kettle is boiling while the supervisor is still on the phone about the missing file. The clean blanket is folded on the chair where the officer sat until two in the morning. The biscuit is offered to someone who has just been told the body was found. The comfort matters precisely because the bad thing remains in the room; it does not dissolve the danger, and it does not become a trap. Someone is still missing, a debt is still due, a statement still holds the weight of an accusation.\n\nLet the comedy be the social friction inside the kindness, not a punchline aimed at the characters. A desk sergeant may show care by moving a box of files that has been blocking the only comfortable chair and saying it was a fire hazard. A neighbor may express concern by bringing a plate of cold food and then criticizing how the household has not eaten properly. An aunt may fuss so loudly about the door being locked that the family must reassure her before they can discuss the threat. Each voice should remain distinct: one character fusses, another is gruff about helping, another simply sits nearby. The recipe must show comfort operating within the crime genre, not replace it with a peaceful domestic interval that ignores the current danger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadayag::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Terry Pratchett / Formal Literary Novel\n\nFrame each scene with the composed narrator's distance before entering the room. A paragraph may begin with the house's history—who built it, who argued over the lintel, which family stopped being invited—and only then move to the officer standing inside with her evidence case. The transition should be deliberate: a sentence that seals the general condition into the particular morning, such as the lane that had kept its old name while every house on it had changed hands. The narration must not mimic the characters' speech; it should observe their speech from a slightly higher register.\n\nLet the social comedy appear in the narrator's judgment of an institution's language. After a police statement reads 'the deceased was known to the family', the narration may add that half the street knew the deceased and the other half had been owed money. After a report promises 'a full and thorough review', the narration may note that the persons responsible for the review are the same persons who supervised the missing evidence. The humor should expose a specific social order—a committee's euphemism, a contract's silent clause, a neighborhood's quiet decision—rather than produce a general sneer. The comic correction belongs to the narrator's framing, and any character who uses wit should have a specific linguistic habit, not a universal tone.\n\nSeparate private knowledge from public account. When the viewpoint character signs the statement, the narration may show the phrase she left out because saying it would have named her cousin. When the magistrate asks whether the family can vouch for the suspect, the narration may hold the memory of the dinner two years ago that the family has agreed not to mention. The gap must be motivated by a specific need: caution, loyalty, protection, or shame. Keep the crime's concrete stakes present through the formal prose—the unopened account book, the name in the registry, the wet coat hanging by the door. Let the aftermath run through social consequences as much as personal ones: a church pew left empty, a shop that no longer exchanges goods with its neighbor, an association of residents that changes its meeting night.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadayah::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Terry Pratchett / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite from the moment an account of an offense, scheme, or debt has to be delivered to someone who can act on it. The teller speaks for a practical reason: to delay a beating, hold a fence's patience, explain a job gone wrong, keep a witness in the room, or make a partner accept a version of events long enough to move. The listener listens for a practical reason: money owed, fear, loyalty, curiosity, the need to decide. Keep both stakes active in the outer frame, and give the occasion a concrete setting — a table, a counter, a doorway, a cell — so the tale has somewhere to land. No palace, court, or supernatural bargain is needed; a backroom, a debt, and a deadline are frame enough.\n\nMake the embedded tale an argument rather than an ornament. The teller selects and arranges details toward a conclusion about who is reliable, who is to blame, and what should happen next. Whether the tale is true, partly true, or knowingly false, its shape comes from the teller's interests and position: the loyal friend inside the story excuses the teller outside it; the patient creditor inside it flatters the listener; the cautionary courier makes the teller's proposed course sound like common sense. Nest a further tale inside the first only when it does work — buying time, landing a warning, changing who the listener thinks the story is about.\n\nLet the comedy run on the distance between the tale and the room it is told in. A noble motive in the story can sit beside the teller's dirty hands; an official-sounding phrase can be undercut by the actual state of the money or by what the listener knows about how the organization really works. Aim each correction at something particular — a lie, a self-deception, an institutional habit, a relationship being managed — not at cleverness for its own sake. Give the characters different relationships to language: one tells in official polish, one interrupts in blunt shop-talk, one says almost nothing and is understood anyway.\n\nRender the private experience of whichever head the story is told from. A listening viewpoint can weigh the tale against what it knows — the detail that is too exact, the name carefully not used — without saying any of it aloud. A telling viewpoint can watch the listener's face while narrating and privately adjust the next stretch of the story. The spoken performance never replaces this interior life; it is what the interior life is working on.\n\nKeep the levels readable. Mark a return to the outer frame with a concrete change — a question, a refill, a door opening, the listener naming a detail the tale left out. Let a phrase or object travel between levels and gather meaning: the formula describing the loyal friend in the tale returns in the listener's mouth about the teller; the item mentioned in passing sits on the table between them. The offense stays concrete — something taken, an arrangement broken, someone left exposed — and the tale's power is measured in what it makes the listener willing to do, believe, or ask, not in whether it is true. The response can end inside the telling, on the listener's unanswered question, or with both parties still deciding; the frame needs no confession, settlement, or twist to justify stopping.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadayai::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Terry Pratchett / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the documentary and historical-memory route. Treat the offense as an event that has already left paper behind — a ledger entry, a police file, a receipt, a workplace log, a photograph, a letter — and put the telling in the hands of someone working through that paper alongside what people remember: a participant, a bystander, an investigator, a relative sorting the aftermath. Anchor scenes in the artifacts themselves: where a page is creased, what a receipt omits, which name has been typed over.\n\nLet records and memories disagree, and give each its own origin and purpose. A file can record an official reason that everyone involved knew was a pretext; a witness can remember the weather and the smell of the room better than the sequence of events; a ledger can show a payment that never reached the person it was written for. Neither the documents nor the recollections get the last word automatically. When the narrator holds an earlier understanding against a later one, make the difference explicit — what was knowable then, what is known now, what the later knowledge does to the earlier page — and keep the narrator inside what they could actually have seen, done, or been told.\n\nAim the comedy at the space between the record's smooth procedure and the improvised reality it covers. When a report describes an orderly handover, show the panic, the petty obstruction, or the favor quietly called in that the form had no box for. Name the particular person who signed, the particular thing they did not know, and the particular reason the paperwork could not hold it. The joke lands on how this organization actually behaved, not on paperwork in general, and it should sharpen the loss rather than replace it.\n\nGive the narrator a live interior life while reconstructing: the flare of recognizing a name, the effort of not settling an old score in the telling, the flat moment of understanding what a date meant. Let the prose move between present-tense work on the documents and past-tense memory, and mark each shift so the reader always knows which register is speaking. Sentence shape follows the narrator's working attention — longer where the narrator is assembling clauses into one held thought, short where a fact finally sits still.\n\nKeep the cost concrete. Someone lost money, freedom, trust, or a future, and the reconstruction should make both the event and that cost more intelligible. The response can end on a document the narrator cannot square, a memory that stops short, or an explanation that changes nothing for the person who suffered; the divergence between the official version and the lived one is itself a legitimate place to stop. No punishment, full accounting, or verdict is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadayaj::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Terry Pratchett / Gothic-Punk\n\nSet the offense inside a collision between an inherited arrangement and the lives now running through it. The old authority can be a family, a firm, a building, a title, a tradition, or an agreement that has outlived its purpose but still controls access, money, loyalty, or permission. Make it concrete from the first scene: what it owns, who it employs, what it forbids, what defying it costs. Let the crime grow out of that structure — someone in the present needs something the arrangement will not allow, or the arrangement has quietly made a particular person disposable, and someone acts.\n\nGround every scene in material urban detail. The condition of the stairwell, the quality of the light, the smell in the corridor, the weight of the door, the traffic sound through the window: let these shape how an encounter is actually experienced, so the same conversation carries differently in a back office than in a kitchen. Put private desire, fear, loyalty, and exhaustion in the room as the viewpoint character's felt experience, not as something the décor implies.\n\nRun the comedy through the masks people wear in front of the old authority. It speaks in formal, inherited language while the practical matter underneath is unpaid wages, a broken lock, a favor called in; a guardian-of-tradition posture protects a personal interest; a subordinate's respectful phrasing covers an act the authority would never permit. Let each correction expose a particular truth — who is afraid of whom, who is pretending, what each person actually needs from the other — and give different characters genuinely different registers: ceremonial, flat, sly, weary. Not everyone is witty; the funny thing is the fit between the mask and the need.\n\nWhen trusted characters are alone, let the masks come down without turning intimacy into a trap. Two people who have worked the same corridor for years may drop the formal language and say plainly what they will risk; the moment can produce real help, a shared joke, a decision to act together, or an honest refusal. Show what is under the dropped mask — relief, anger, affection, calculation, a mixture — rather than defaulting to menace. A person can be ridiculous in the hall and dangerous in the office; keep both available.\n\nKeep the offense's cost visible throughout: something was taken, an arrangement broken, someone left exposed, and atmosphere does not absorb that fact. The response can end mid-negotiation, mid-argument, or on a decision whose consequences have not arrived. The old authority can be left intact, cracking, or newly irrelevant; do not require its fall, a betrayal, or a reckoning before stopping.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadayak::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Terry Pratchett / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and domestic realism route. Treat the offense as something that grows out of ordinary obligations — a household budget, a marriage arrangement, a job, a loan, a property line, a promise made to a relative, a debt inherited along with a watch. Keep the language, region, community, and period exactly as the story supplies them; do not add English, code-switching, religious practice, caste experience, or a standard family shape the setting has not established. Work with the particular household that exists.\n\nShow the preparation and incentives in domestic terms. The money is for something named — a school fee, a medical bill, a rent payment, a wedding, a journey — and the obligation runs to a particular person: a parent, a sibling, an employer, a neighbor, a spouse. Let the decision to act be traceable to those pressures without being excused by them; the reader should understand why this person, this month, this amount, while still seeing that a choice was made.\n\nPoint the comedy at the difference between the household's public explanations and what everyone in it privately knows. A theft announced as a moment of madness that everyone watched being planned; a quarrel given a pious framing that is actually about water or a shared wall; procedure invoked at the front door while the real decision happened in the kitchen. Each correction should expose the particular relationships and pressures at work — who must be placated, who can be blamed, who is protecting whom — never mock the place or the people in it.\n\nCarry the aftermath in the texture of daily life rather than in verdicts: who eats together now, who pays for what, who is allowed to speak at gatherings, who is blamed in public and excused in private, who has been sent to stay with an aunt. A punishment, if one comes, can itself be a domestic arrangement — silence, an imposed debt, a marriage of repairs — rather than a legal event.\n\nHold the viewpoint steady. A culprit knows exactly what was taken and why; a bystander sees effects and guesses at causes; a victim may understand the loss differently than the person who caused it. Narrate only from the head the story owns, and give that character real nonspoken experience — the calculation before the act, the dread at the dinner table, the shame of being helped — rather than keeping the interior life offstage. The response can end while consequences are still settling; no reconciliation, exposure, or comeuppance is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadayal::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Terry Pratchett / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the early-modern comic and theatrical route. Organize the offense and its aftermath as scenes in which people play parts: they make entrances and exits, deliver speeches they have prepared, answer with lines they have rehearsed, and betray themselves in the gap between the role and the need underneath it. This is a method for the present story, not a costume; do not relocate the cast to a historical stage or import theatrical conventions wholesale — take the staging logic, the timing, and the comic awareness of being watched.\n\nGive each scene a clear spatial and social arrangement: who is present, who is speaking, who must listen, who is positioned by the door, who cannot leave. A confrontation over money can run as a formal negotiation with bows and terms; a confession can be delivered as a performance aimed at a particular response; an accusation can arrive already rehearsed in the mirror. Let the theatricality come from the characters' own awareness of how they appear — who is playing the honest employee, the wronged party, the reasonable authority — not from the narrator forcing a stage metaphor onto every moment.\n\nAim the comedy at the seam between role and person. The honest employee's diction is perfect and the ledger is short; the authority figure's formal phrasing carries a practical threat everyone in the room can price; the culprit performs remorse while choosing the next sentence. Let each correction expose something particular: who is performing for whom, what the performance is meant to achieve, and what it accidentally reveals. Give characters different fluency in their roles, including one who cannot perform at all and is dangerous or disarming because of it.\n\nWhile a character performs, render the private mind doing the performing — the culprit tracking which lie has landed, the witness deciding how much truth the role permits, the boss measuring how long the audience stays frightened. The spoken performance never substitutes for this interior life; it is the surface the interior life is managing. Mark any return from a performed account to the present scene with a concrete change — a question, a movement, a change of speaker — and let a phrase or gesture travel between scenes and pick up new meaning: the formula used to excuse oneself early on returns later in another's mouth, showing what the listener concluded or what the performer forgot to hide.\n\nKeep the cost solid. The offense took money, safety, trust, or a future from someone, and a scene may be very funny while that fact stands in the room; a character can be ridiculous and still dangerous, a situation absurd and still frightening. The response can end mid-performance, on an exit that leaves the arrangement unsettled, or in the quiet after the audience has gone; do not require the performance to succeed, collapse, or be seen through before stopping.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadayam::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Terry Pratchett / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social realism, chronicle, and testimony route. Let the offense be an event that different people can describe from different positions: the one who planned it, the one who carried it out, the one who lost something, the one who watched from a doorway, the one who heard about it afterward and retold it until it was theirs. The account can stay with a single teller or move among voices; either way, attribute every version to a specific person with a specific relationship to what happened, and preserve the language, community, and history the story actually supplies.\n\nKeep the kinds of knowledge separate. A participant describes what they did, saw, felt, and decided; a bystander describes what was visible and what they inferred from it; someone who heard about the crime later reports what they were told and what they now believe. Never let a teller claim to have stood where they did not stand, and when the account shifts between voices, mark the shift — a new speaker, a named source, a change in what the telling can see. Inside the testimony, keep the teller's own stake present as lived experience: the fear held down while speaking, the grievance being fed, the detail enjoyed too much to leave out.\n\nRun the comedy through the distance between the official or public version and the lived one. The public explanation credits an outsider while everyone on the block knows it was a neighbor's boy; the authorities describe a smooth investigation while the participants remember the confusion, the bargaining, and whose cousin got telephoned; a family presents a united front and each member's account quietly dismantles the others. Each correction should expose something particular about the community, the institution, or the relationship — who is protected, who is for sale, who must not be named — not a general satire of officials.\n\nLet consequences land in the neighborhood, the household, the workplace, the market: who is suddenly afraid, who is angry on schedule, who is protecting someone badly, who has decided not to have seen anything, who is trying to turn the event to advantage. People may hold incompatible understandings of the same afternoon, and the chronicle does not owe them a reconciliation. Keep each teller inside their own knowledge — a culprit knows what was taken and why; a victim knows the missing thing and the shape of the violation; neither automatically knows the other's reasons.\n\nThe response can end inside a testimony, between two accounts that do not agree, or on a version the reader can see being shaped for reuse; no single account needs to be declared true, no punishment delivered, and no lesson stated before stopping.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadayan::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Terry Pratchett / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Open inside a concrete moment — someone waiting for money, watching a door, reading a message that changes the plan, standing in a room where something has already gone wrong — and let the offense or scheme emerge through what is visible and what the viewpoint character already knows, without a preamble. When a name, role, or scheme mechanic first appears, present it clearly enough to follow: who this person is to the scene, what the arrangement was supposed to be, what has been taken or broken.\n\nKeep every spatial change easy to track. Moving from the street to an office, from a conversation to a remembered one, from one room to another gets a concrete marker — a door, a change of light, a new speaker, a shift in what the viewpoint character can see — and readable paragraph breaks separate action, dialogue, and private response. Let an action beat land before the next line of dialogue; give a private reaction room to register before events move on. Visual staging does the comic work: put the claim and its contradiction in the same view — everything is under control, says the character, while behind them the reader can see the safe standing open.\n\nRun the comedy on that gap between what is said and what the room shows. An authority figure delivers the official explanation while the practical reality is visible over their shoulder; a culprit performs confidence and their hands disagree; the plan is described as simple by the one person it has already failed. Aim each correction at something particular about the offense or the relationship rather than appending a joke, and give different characters different speech — formal, blunt, evasive, worn out — so the reader can tell who defers to whom and who has stopped bothering.\n\nOwn every reaction beat. A reaction can be serious, uncertain, angry, funny, or numb according to the character having it: the victim's shock or flat exhaustion, the culprit's calculation or fear, the bystander's confusion or reluctant amusement. Give the viewpoint character actual private experience at these moments — the thought that arrives before the words, the noticing that cannot be said aloud — rather than narration that only reports moves. Keep the cost concrete as the pace picks up: someone has lost money, safety, trust, or a future, and speed should not blur that into atmosphere.\n\nThe response can end mid-action, mid-conversation, or on an unanswered question; do not require a cliffhanger, a confession, a completed plan, a produced shift, or a twist before stopping.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadayao::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Terry Pratchett / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite the crime through the way the chosen viewpoint notices what an offense does to ordinary arrangements. Build each passage as a shaped movement: open with a concrete detail from the scene, follow the syntax through the association or implication that detail opens in the viewpoint's attention, then close the movement with a return to the practical world that corrects or complicates the association. A stain on a ledger, a repeated knock, or a door left open gathers meaning because the prose stays with it long enough to show what the observer is learning, not because the image is praised.\n\nUse recurring sounds and images to track consequences across the story. When an image returns, attach it to a changed application: the same wet street that looked like an inconvenience before the offense may look like a record afterward. Keep the comparison tied to what the viewpoint can actually see, remember, or infer. Do not let the narrator's figurative register leak into every character's speech. A clerk may answer a lyrical observation with a flat practical correction, and that correction can be the comic turn that exposes something particular about the case.\n\nMake the humor arise from the gap between elevated attention and institutional reality. Sequence the correction so that it revises the perception rather than merely mocking it: an investigator may notice the light falling on a filing cabinet as if it were a promise, only to find the drawer jammed and the relevant form misfiled. Let the comedy show how the offense is entangled with ordinary procedure. Preserve the active narrator's access and the actual world facts. The response can end while the attention is still developing, leaving the next perception available without forcing a completed insight or a changed plan.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadayap::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Terry Pratchett / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the crime appear through small irreversible changes that the viewpoint can remember or observe: a routine that no longer happens, a phrase someone used to say, a room that has been rearranged because of what occurred. Build each reflective passage around a specific remembered occasion placed beside a present detail. The memory should clarify what the present scene means, not drift into general statements about loss.\n\nAllow present experience and memory to share the same passage. The narrator may notice that the shop is still open, that the same kettle still works, that a particular joke still lands, while also recognizing what is gone. Ordinary pleasure can remain pleasurable. Do not require every scene to end by announcing impermanence or by converting a practical moment into regret.\n\nUse the Pratchett profile's social attention to keep the reflection grounded in how people and organizations actually behave. Let a reflective observation be interrupted by a bureaucratic requirement, a neighbor's practical concern, or a character who remembers the same event differently. Treat that interruption as a comic correction that exposes something particular about the crime's aftermath, not as a failure of the style. Keep different characters' relationships to language and authority distinct. The response can end while a memory is still settling, without forcing a completed judgment or a lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadayaq::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Terry Pratchett / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a social and urban realist route from the Middle Eastern literary traditions. Ground the crime in the particular texture of the supplied setting: the street, the household, the workplace, the market, the office, the stairwell, the queue. Let the offense become legible through how people manage practical life around it. Do not import a generic regional temperament, a single honor code, or an automatic religious or mystical frame. The setting, language, period, and community already supplied determine the concrete details.\n\nWrite the aftermath with reflective prose that moves between present observation and what the viewpoint remembers or infers. Anchor each movement in a specific social transaction: a domestic object that has changed use, a neighbor's comment that revises an earlier account, or a municipal procedure that reveals who actually has authority. Let the reflection stay attached to the particular offense and its consequences rather than becoming a general meditation on fate. Keep the prose socially specific: who waits, who explains, who avoids the official version, and who quietly arranges something else.\n\nLet the Pratchett profile's humor arise from the difference between official explanations and practical reality. A clerk may insist on a procedure that everyone knows does not work; a family may maintain a public account of an event while privately arranging something else. The comedy should expose something particular about the crime's social setting, not mock the community or reduce it to absurdity. Preserve the active narrator's access and the actual world facts. The response can end with the social situation still developing, leaving the next practical adjustment available without forcing a completed moral or a changed plan.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadayar::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Terry Pratchett / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect a small number of exact details that carry the crime's social reality. A single object out of place, a repeated phrase, or a particular bureaucratic form can do more work than a full description. Use direct clauses and let the details stand without redundant explanation. When an inward connection matters, state it plainly and let it be long enough to make sense; do not break it into performed fragments.\n\nLet omission leave something inferable. If a character avoids a question, the avoidance itself can carry the implication. If a procedure fails, the failure can show how the institution actually works. Do not delete causality: the reader should be able to see why a choice or consequence follows from the selected details.\n\nCarry the Pratchett profile's humor through economical contrast. Build the contrast from one detail for the official explanation and one for the practical reality: a sign may promise one thing while the queue outside proves another; a character may give a polished account while a single object contradicts it. Let the space between the two details carry the inference. The comic correction should expose something particular about the offense or its aftermath, not merely sound clever. Keep cast voices individual: some characters speak plainly, some formally, some evasively. The response can end after a precise detail has landed, leaving the next inference available without forcing a completed conclusion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadayas::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Terry Pratchett / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the crime in sustained scene-first prose. Move naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and the authorized inner experience of the viewpoint. Let a scene open in its immediate situation and use paragraphing to shift attention without recap machinery. The narrator's voice, the immediate scene, and the Pratchett profile's social attention set the register: a bureaucratic office may produce a different rhythm than a private conversation or a street encounter.\n\nLet the crime's consequences appear through how people actually behave in institutions and relationships. Show the difference between official explanations and practical reality in the scene's particulars: who speaks with authority, who knows the workaround, who repeats the approved version, and who quietly arranges something else. Characters retain different relationships to language. Do not make everyone a witty commentator; some speakers are plain, some formal, some evasive, and the comedy comes from those differences rather than from a uniform banter.\n\nUse the flexible attention of contemporary-novel prose to let a comic correction expose something particular. Move the scene from an approved version or serious exchange to a practical observation that revises what the viewpoint understood. The correction should illuminate the offense or its aftermath, not merely decorate it. Preserve the active narrator's access and the actual world facts. The response can end at a natural scene boundary, with the situation still developing, without forcing a lesson, a completed discovery, or a changed plan.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadayat::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Terry Pratchett / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nOrganize the crime story around the established relationship premise. Choose scenes that make the relationship legible through what the characters do for or withhold from one another, what they say only to each other, and what they repeat across occasions. Let the offense or its aftermath create consequences that fulfill the particular promise of the relationship: divided loyalty, shared risk, a private joke that turns serious, a comfort that arrives too late or just in time. Use only the actual supplied premise; do not insert a roster of familiar tropes.\n\nGive affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery enough space where it belongs. Let private language and repeated moments carry the emotional payoff. A phrase used once in affection may return under pressure with a different weight. A small ritual may become a way of checking whether the relationship has changed. Keep the emotional attention specific to these characters rather than generic.\n\nLet the Pratchett profile's social comedy operate within the relationship. Build the comic correction from the difference between the relationship's public account and its private practical truth: one character may maintain an approved version for officials or neighbors while the other knows the workaround, the actual cost, or the unspoken arrangement. The humor should expose something particular about their bond or the crime's pressure on it, not mock the relationship or reduce it to banter. Preserve the active narrator's access and the actual world facts. The response can end with the relationship still unsettled, leaving the next emotional consequence available without forcing a confession, a reconciliation, or a betrayal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadayau::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Terry Pratchett / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the crime as an account of the deeds by which these people will be remembered. Use deliberate openings and compact declarations to establish a person or an act, then expand into the particulars that make the reputation incomplete. A recurring designation should come from an established deed or way of speaking, not from an ornamental epithet assigned to every entrance. Return to a phrase when a later occurrence gives it a new application: the same act that once seemed minor may become the thing everyone recalls after the offense.\n\nLet the chronicle relate an act to what will be remembered without declaring every event legendary. A theft, a betrayal, a cover-up, or a patient investigation can earn a place in the account because the community has lived with its consequences. Keep the present scene's scale. A bureaucratic delay remains a bureaucratic delay even when it belongs to a longer history. Preserve the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective.\n\nLet dialogue and institutional reality interrupt the authority of the chronicle. The subject of an oft-told incident may object to one detail, or answer an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern about a form, a fee, or a missing receipt. Let the interruption expose the gap between the remembered account and the practical record of the offense. This need not become parody. The humor should arise from the persistence of a local story against the practical world, exposing something particular about the crime's aftermath rather than mocking the community or turning household work into mock combat. The account can end with business concluded or resumed, leaving another modest act available to be remembered rather than declaring it legend on the community's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadayav::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Terry Pratchett / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the offense as something embedded in ordinary social and domestic arrangements: a workplace scheme, a neighborhood dispute, a municipal shortcut, a family obligation that becomes a crime. Let the investigation move through the practical texture of those arrangements—who shares a kitchen, who signs a form, who covers a shift, who knows a door is left open—rather than through atmospheric isolation or grand corruption.\n\nUse the Pratchett profile's comic correction on institutions and their official explanations. When a character explains how a thing is supposed to work, show the actual practice beside it: the rule posted on the wall, the colleague who ignores it for a particular reason, the manager who enforces it only when someone is watching. Let the humor expose something specific about that institution, not merely produce clever remarks. Different characters should have different relationships to language and authority: one speaks in official phrasing, another in practical shorthand, another carefully avoids committing to either.\n\nKeep the Scandinavian social-realist route concrete. Attend to the ordinary conditions that shape the crime and its aftermath: housing, work schedules, family meals, public transport, local government, weather as a practical fact rather than a mood. If the story is set in a specific Nordic context, preserve its distinct institutions and language habits instead of treating them as interchangeable. Do not import reserve, snow, or bleakness as automatic characteristics; let the actual setting and season supply what is present.\n\nTreat the crime's consequences as social as well as legal. A scheme may protect one person while burdening another; an investigation may disturb a household routine or a workplace alliance. Let characters continue their practical lives during the inquiry, with cooperation and refusal both possible. The comedy can arise from the gap between what people say the arrangement is and what everyone involved knows it actually is, but the offense remains real and its harm remains legible. End a response at the natural boundary of the scene or exchange, not at a required confession, arrest, or moral summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadayaw::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Terry Pratchett / Screenplay\n\nWrite the crime through scenes whose visible and audible events carry the story: a form handed over, a drawer opened, a phone answered, a pause before a signature. Use concise action lines that show what a viewer could see or hear, with clear speaker cues and scene headings when location or time changes. Let the audience follow the offense, its preparation, or its consequences through what people do in rooms and corridors, not through explanatory speeches.\n\nUse the Pratchett profile's institutional comedy by staging the difference between official procedure and practical behavior. One character recites the rule while another demonstrates the workaround; a posted notice is contradicted by the actual queue; a manager demands a report that everyone knows will be filed unread. Let the action lines and dialogue expose these gaps without making every character equally witty. Different people should speak to authority differently: one in official phrasing, one in practical shorthand, one in careful evasion.\n\nKeep required private reasoning in a clearly attributed nonspoken note, such as a focal character's unspoken assessment of what a gesture or omission means. The note is not dialogue, voice-over, or knowledge available to the other characters. Use it when the active contract requires interiority, and let it remain distinct from what the character says aloud. A character may think one thing and say another for a specific reason, or think and speak in alignment.\n\nUse cuts, offscreen sound, or voice-over only when the chosen sequence needs them. A cut can move from a promise made in one room to its practical consequence in another; an offscreen sound can announce an arrival or a discovery without showing it first. Do not fill the page with camera commands. Suspense can come from exposure or divided loyalty even when the viewer already knows who committed the offense. End the scene at its natural boundary: after a completed action, an interruption, or a line that leaves the next move open, without requiring a cliffhanger or a confession.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadayax::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Terry Pratchett / Stage Play\n\nWrite the crime as a playable scene: clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be staged. Let the audience follow the offense or its consequences through what characters do in the shared space—who can see an entrance, reach a drawer, hear a line, or move between two places. Keep stage directions economical and concrete, establishing observable conditions rather than hidden guilt.\n\nUse the Pratchett profile's institutional comedy through the relationship between lines and physical business. A character announces a procedure while another is already doing the practical workaround; a manager demands a form while holding the one that makes it unnecessary; a posted rule is contradicted by the way everyone actually uses the door. Let these gaps be specific to the institution and the people in it. Do not make every character a witty commentator; different people should have different relationships to official language and authority.\n\nKeep required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in its active presentation. A monologue or aside is performed speech with an audience and a dramatic purpose, not the default destination for thought. When a focal character's unspoken reasoning matters, write it as clearly attributed nonspoken material that another character cannot hear. The reader may know what a character suspects while the other people on stage remain unaware.\n\nLet spoken questions and answers fit the relationship and the immediate physical task. A witness can answer plainly, challenge a premise, or change the subject for an actual reason. Suspense can come from exposure or divided loyalty even when the audience already knows who committed the offense. End the scene after a completed action, an interruption, or a line that leaves the next move open, without requiring a theatrical gathering, a confession, or a new clue.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadayay::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Terry Pratchett / Stream of Consciousness\n\nTell the crime through one authorized mind's associative movement. Braid perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern as that particular character experiences the offense, its preparation, or its aftermath. Let syntax and paragraph shape follow the associations, while keeping enough orientation for the reader to distinguish an event from a recollection or a fantasy. Do not make every character share this mind; other people's thoughts remain outside the focal access.\n\nUse the Pratchett profile's comic correction inside that private movement. Let the focal character notice the gap between official language and practical reality: a phrase from a notice repeats while the character remembers who actually ignores it; a supervisor's instruction interrupts a train of thought about the workaround everyone uses; a form's wording returns while the character's hands are already doing the thing the form forbids. The humor should expose something particular about the institution, not merely sound clever. Different characters retain different relationships to language and authority, visible through what the focal mind recalls them saying and how they said it.\n\nLet the crime remain concrete even when the telling is associative. A recurring concern can be a missing object, a debt, a locked door, a person who was not where they said they were. The mind may circle back to it, drop it when interrupted, or return to it with a changed feeling. Suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did it, because the focal character may know, suspect, or misunderstand. Keep the actual sequence of events recoverable beneath the mental movement.\n\nEnd the response at a natural boundary of attention: a thought completed, an interruption that redirects the mind, a memory that settles, or a perception that opens the next association. Do not require a realization, confession, or decision at every stop.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadayaz::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Terry Pratchett / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the crime through readable sender-attributed messages. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one writes in full official sentences, one sends fragments, one corrects a typo, one leaves a message unsent. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the story. A delay can mean hesitation, work, or simply being away from the phone; do not make every interface event a clue.\n\nUse the Pratchett profile's institutional comedy through the contrast between official written language and practical reality. A supervisor sends a policy reminder while a colleague messages the actual workaround; a group chat maintains a formal tone until the manager leaves the channel; an automated notice arrives after everyone has already handled the problem informally. Let these contrasts be specific to the people and the institution. Different characters should have different relationships to language and authority, visible in how they write and what they choose to send.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought; it may be abandoned for a specific reason. Required private experience can appear as clearly separated context outside the transcript, attributed to the focal character, and must not be mistaken for something another sender received. A character may type one thing and send another, or send exactly what they think.\n\nLet the crime remain legible through what people transmit, withhold, or discover in the exchange. Suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader knows who did it. Context outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript. End the response at a natural boundary: a message sent, a reply pending, a draft abandoned, or a channel gone quiet, without requiring a cliffhanger or a confession.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadayba::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Terry Pratchett / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the crime in scene-oriented presentation: concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Keep scene changes legible. Let background or expression cues orient the moment—a familiar office, a changed notice board, a character's brief hesitation—without making them automatic truth indicators. A changed expression can attract suspicion, but the offense and its consequences must be established through what people say, do, and leave behind.\n\nUse the Pratchett profile's institutional comedy through the contrast between what the scene shows and what the focal narration privately notices. The setting displays an official notice while the narration recalls who actually ignores it; a character delivers a policy line while the focal response registers the workaround everyone uses; a tidy desk suggests a procedure that the dialogue quietly contradicts. Let the humor expose something particular about the institution. Different characters should have different relationships to language and authority, visible in how they speak and what they expect the scene to look like.\n\nKeep focal narration or inner response distinct from dialogue. The reader may know what the focal character suspects or privately mocks while the other characters remain unaware. A character may think one thing and say another for a specific reason, or think and speak in alignment. Do not turn the cast into fixed reaction portraits whose expressions always reveal whether they lie.\n\nAdvance the crime through scenes whose information changes the available interpretations. A revisit can use a familiar background detail as an anchor, with its new significance earned by a later fact. Repetition should deepen understanding rather than restart the same suspicion. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected. End the scene at its natural boundary: after a completed action, an interruption, or a line that leaves the next move open, without requiring a choice screen or a confession.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadaybb::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Terry Pratchett / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across replies: what the offense or scheme is, what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover context briefly only when it helps. Let an exact earlier particular remain available when a later installment changes what it suggests: a person uses the entrance previously said to be locked, asks for the object another character described, or answers a familiar question under a different condition. Briefly recover the necessary particular rather than retelling the whole setup.\n\nUse the Pratchett profile's institutional comedy cumulatively. The gap between official explanation and practical reality can recur in different forms: a rule announced in one installment is shown being worked around in the next; a manager's memo is contradicted by the actual queue; a procedure everyone claims to follow turns out to have a local shortcut. Let each recurrence deepen the reader's understanding of the institution rather than restarting the same joke. Different characters should retain different relationships to language and authority across installments.\n\nLet the crime develop through earned changes in knowledge, relationships, work, or resources. Progress can involve a character learning who knew about the scheme, realizing what a delay meant, or discovering that a practical arrangement protected one person while burdening another. Suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader already knows who committed the offense. Do not invent a fresh contradiction every installment to keep the case open; new evidence can qualify an old explanation without replacing the old event.\n\nLet ordinary life continue around the inquiry. A repeated question may have a new implication without a fresh reversal. The installment can close on completed work, a pending request, a changed understanding, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Serial duration should give selected facts room to acquire meaning, not make the outcome perpetually recede.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadazaa::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Wang Xiaobo / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the crime through a narrator who keeps asking what the offense actually cost and who had to pay it. The narrator may be the culprit, an accomplice, a victim, or someone close enough to the act that the question is not academic. Let the inquiry move past the official or socially approved answer: a debt is not settled because a receipt exists, a confession does not restore trust, and a punishment does not make the loss legible.\n\nUse the narrator's direct, nimble reasoning to expose the emotional strain instead of announcing it. When a character admits need, withholds an apology, or returns to a difficult relationship, show the cost in the actual conversation and in what the narrator notices afterward. A sincere admission can make things worse. A withheld apology can be a form of self-protection as well as cruelty. Warmth and pain may occupy the same scene without one canceling the other.\n\nKeep the offense and its consequences concrete. The emotional turbulence should arise from specific incentives, divided loyalties, evidence, or the aftermath of a particular act, not from a general mood of unhappiness. Let the narrator follow a question beyond its convenient stopping point: why someone stayed, why someone lied, why a rule protected the wrong person, why an apology arrived too late to matter.\n\nAllow comfort to be partial or awkward. A character may offer practical help that does not repair the relationship. A conversation may end with the narrator understanding something without feeling better. The turbulence is in the exposure and its unresolved cost, not in a required emotional climax.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadazab::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Wang Xiaobo / Chat Roleplay\n\nPresent the crime scene as a coherent shared interval in readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution. Each speaker's words should be easy to follow without sacrificing the author profile's direct, nimble questioning. Let characters argue about the offense, its incentives, its rules, and its consequences in ways that reveal their practical positions rather than merely exchanging information.\n\nUse dialogue to puncture solemn assumptions. A character may ask why a regulation matters more than the person it was supposed to protect, or why everyone accepts an explanation that does not survive a simple question. Keep the questioning owned by particular voices: the investigator may press for a contradiction, the accomplice may defend a choice, the bystander may notice the absurdity of the official account. Do not make every speaker equally witty or equally cynical.\n\nKeep the ongoing world and NPC behavior active. A suspect may continue a practical task while answering. A witness may refuse to stop eating. An investigator may notice something about the room that changes the next question. The scene should advance through supported action and exchange, not through a forced clue or cliffhanger at the end.\n\nLeave a continuation that follows naturally from the scene. A question may remain unanswered, a relationship may remain strained, or a new fact may open a further line of inquiry. Do not require a dramatic choice or a completed discovery. The continuation point should feel like the next moment in an ongoing situation, not a prompt to the player.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadazac::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Wang Xiaobo / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic telling route. Structure the crime as a sequence of linked episodes, each with its own concrete scene and practical concern, rather than as a single continuous novelistic arc. An episode may cover preparation, a transaction, an interrogation, a discovery, or a confrontation in the aftermath. Let each episode advance the offense and its consequences while remaining readable on its own.\n\nGive the narrator a direct, questioning voice that moves through the episodes. The narrator may be a participant or a close observer who keeps asking what a rule is actually for and who benefits from it. When an episode exposes the absurdity of an official procedure, a debt, or a punishment, show the practical effect on particular people. Do not let the satire float above the crime; ground it in the specific incentives and relationships of the offense.\n\nLet recurring figures, places, or objects connect the episodes. A ledger, a doorway, a debt, or a promise may reappear with a changed meaning as the narrator learns more. Repetition should deepen the reader's understanding of the crime rather than restart the same argument. Keep the narrator's candor and black humor owned by that particular voice, not distributed evenly across the cast.\n\nPreserve the language, period, and community actually supplied. The episodic form and the narrator's rational inquiry are the tradition's craft route; do not transplant a setting, cast identity, or period merely to signal the style. Each episode should end at a natural pause that leaves the larger question open without requiring a cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadazad::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Wang Xiaobo / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the crime in hard-edged, concrete prose. Use forceful diction and compressed transitions to move through preparation, execution, evidence, pursuit, and aftermath. Show material wear: a lock scratched by the wrong key, a floor worn where someone waited, a ledger damp from a pocket, a weapon cleaned too hastily. Let physical details carry the stakes without pausing to explain their meaning.\n\nKeep the narrator's reasoning direct and nimble. When a rule or an official account deserves puncturing, do it through a short, precise observation rather than a long argument. A character may note that the security gate protects the empty office while the cash moves through an unguarded door. The absurdity should be visible in the arrangement itself, not announced as a thesis.\n\nUse useful changes of rhythm. A rapid sequence of action may be followed by a longer thought when the character needs to work through a contradiction or a choice. Do not pad the longer thought with atmosphere; keep it focused on the reasoning that connects the evidence to the next move. Let abrasive or rough speech belong to particular speakers, not to every voice in the scene.\n\nGround the physical stakes in the specific crime. A beating, a debt, a pursuit, or a threat should have a concrete cause and consequence within the offense. Do not turn the story into survival horror or add violence unrelated to the selected crime. The grit is a way of making the crime's costs legible, not a mood applied over the top.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadazae::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Wang Xiaobo / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the crime through documents, each with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Use the document form the scene actually requires: a report filed before the full offense was known, a letter to a superior, a note left for an accomplice, a ledger entry, a witness statement, a receipt, or a private account written after the fact. Do not rotate through formats mechanically.\n\nLet each document's writer follow a question beyond the approved answer. A report may note that the official procedure made the theft easier. A letter may ask why a debt is being collected from the person who was already robbed. A private account may admit that the writer stayed silent for a reason that now looks absurd. Keep the questioning owned by that writer and limited by what the writer knew at the time of composition.\n\nUse gaps, revisions, and conflicting accounts to reveal events across entries. One document may record a version of an encounter that a later document corrects or contradicts. A missing page, a crossed-out line, or an unexplained delay can be as informative as a confession. What each document conceals should follow from its author's situation: fear, loyalty, self-justification, or simple ignorance.\n\nPreserve readable continuity without adding an omniscient explanation after every record. Let the reader assemble the offense, its incentives, and its aftermath from the documents themselves. A document may end with its writer still misunderstanding something, and the next document may show the consequence of that misunderstanding. The crime's suspense can arise from exposure or divided loyalty even when the reader already knows who did it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadazaf::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Wang Xiaobo / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite the crime through the particular kindnesses and small pleasures that persist among people caught in an offense or its aftermath. A character may bring the exact food someone prefers to a meeting that is also about a debt. A witness may remember the way a suspect once fixed a broken latch. An accomplice may keep a shared habit of tea even while discussing a dangerous next step. Let the warmth be specific and sincere, not a signal that everything is safe.\n\nUse the narrator's candor to keep the comfort honest. The narrator may note that the food is good even though the conversation is bad, or that a familiar gesture does not undo a betrayal. Affection can coexist with disagreement, fear, and practical calculation. Do not require a confession, a reconciliation, or an emotional repair. A character may accept care without forgiving the person who offers it.\n\nKeep personality and privacy intact. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable. A kind act may come from a character who is otherwise difficult, and a warm moment may be interrupted by the crime's actual demands. The offense and its consequences remain present in the world; the style spends attention on what is still good without pretending the harm has disappeared.\n\nLet the small pleasures have a place in the crime's sequence. A shared meal may precede a decision. A familiar preference may become a detail in a later account. A moment of ease may make the next pressure more legible. The comfort is a treatment of the selected genre, not a replacement for it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadazag::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Wang Xiaobo / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite the crime in composed novel prose with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Let each scene open into its social context, history, or judgment where the chosen narrator permits it. A theft may be framed by the office's history of unexamined trust. A confession may be framed by the family's long habit of not asking certain questions. The framing should illuminate the offense, its incentives, or its aftermath rather than delay the story.\n\nKeep the narrator's voice direct and nimble even within the formal architecture. The prose may be composed, but it should not become ornate or evasive. Let the narrator follow a question beyond its socially approved answer: why a rule protected the wrong person, why a debt was recorded as a gift, why everyone accepted an explanation that a child could see through. The absurdity should be exposed through the narrator's precise reasoning, not through a general tone of cynicism.\n\nUse public and private contrast to organize passages when relevant. A ceremony, a report, a formal meeting, or a public statement may sit against a private conversation, a memory, or an unspoken motive. Let the contrast show what the crime cost and who was allowed to name that cost. Keep the narrator's candor owned by that particular voice; other characters may be evasive, self-serving, or simply unaware.\n\nPreserve the crime's concrete sequence. The composed paragraphs should still move through preparation, execution, evidence, pursuit, or aftermath. A broader paragraph may contain a longer thought when the narrator needs to work through a contradiction, but the thought should connect to the offense and its consequences. The formal style is the architecture, not a license to replace the crime with social observation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadazah::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Wang Xiaobo / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen inside the outer frame: one person is telling another about a crime, scheme, or transgression because the telling is meant to obtain something specific from the listener—help, payment, silence, alliance, or reputation. Name that live interest in the opening exchange, and keep it visible after every shift from outer scene to embedded account.\n\nThe teller is an interested narrator, not a neutral witness. Choose one distinct distortion that arises from the teller's current motive and carry it through the whole embedded account: the teller simplifies an opponent, enlarges a cleverness, suppresses an embarrassing motive, or standardizes what was actually a mess. Do not pile distortions on top of one another. Let the chosen distortion show in selection and emphasis, not in a self-accusing aside, and let the frame listener come back to exactly the detail that distortion would hide.\n\nAnchor the frame and the embedded tale in one recurring named object. Use the object at least twice: once inside the told version, where the teller presents it as important, and once in the outer scene, where the listener or the present situation shows what the teller was never in a position to see. The second appearance must change the object's meaning without explaining it in a narrative comment. Repeated phrases around that object should help the reader connect the two levels, not certify that the told version is true.\n\nThe embedded account should begin in the middle of the action, speak directly to the present listener, and pause only when the teller wants a visible reaction. At the first pause, have the listener interrupt with at least one precise practical question: a question about money, access, a missing person, a rule that was tested, or the reason someone would believe the account. The teller must then handle that question in character by correcting, passing over, or renaming the difficulty. Do not summarize the listener's response; render the actual spoken or physical interruption.\n\nWhen the repeated object appears in the outer scene, do not force a confession. Instead, show the teller's rational surface as trained by an institution, and let the present conversation make the habit visible: the teller uses an official term for a private arrangement, repeats a rule as if it had been followed, or becomes suddenly precise about a step nobody asked about. End the outer scene when that live interest has changed—the listener now understands something the teller did not intend to reveal, or refuses the request, or offers help on revised terms. It is not necessary to finish the embedded tale or to add a cliffhanger; stop at the moment the relationship has been altered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadazai::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Wang Xiaobo / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the documentary and historical-memory route for the whole scene. Write from a first-person participant in the present inquiry—a relative, clerk, neighbor, colleague, or former suspect—and give that narrator one definite stake in the record's version of the past: they need an inheritance settled, a name cleared, a pension decided, a burial plot released, or an accusation answered. Keep that stake in view whenever the narrator turns back to the documents.\n\nBuild the scene around one original document with an evident administrative purpose: a register, report, memo, photograph, letter, or ledger page. Do not treat the document as a transparent record. Identify in the prose who made it, under what pressure, and with what official or personal interest. Then have the narrator read it carefully, sentence by sentence if the document is short, and state exactly what it claims in its own formal terms before comparing it with another source.\n\nBring in a living person whose knowledge contradicts, complicates, or fills a gap in the document: someone who was present, whose name is missing, or who had a reason to make a different entry. Let the living person speak plainly about at least one concrete fact the record omits—a name placed in the wrong column, a signature witnessed too quickly, a space left blank, a person not counted, or a date made to fit a holiday. The narrator must then reason about why the document says what it says: what rule, convenience, time pressure, or private advantage would have produced exactly that omission or alteration.\n\nKeep the reasoning in Wang Xiaobo's register. Do not moralize at length. Have the narrator ask the practical question the record was designed to prevent: what was this rule for, who did it help, what would have happened if someone had refused. When the answer is absurd, state the absurdity as a precise mismatch between official wording and material circumstances, such as an inspection sheet signed for a day the office was closed or a list of beneficiaries that excludes the person who actually paid. Let any humor come from that mismatch, not from forced aphorism.\n\nUse the narrator's memory, but do not let it certify itself. Each time the narrator remembers a slight, a promise, or a sequence of events, check it against at least one date, one other person's account, or one material object. Distinguish in plain prose what the narrator knew at the time, what they learned later, and what they are now guessing. When memory and record conflict, do not automatically prefer either; show the narrator weighing the interests and access of the person or office that produced each version.\n\nOrganize paragraphs around one document claim, the living person's response to it, and the narrator's reasoning about the difference. You may include actual dialogue, but the dominant texture should be measured prose of recollection and assessment. End the scene when a specific discrepancy or practical consequence becomes clear. The larger question of guilt, repair, or legal outcome may remain open; do not force a resolution or a cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadazaj::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Wang Xiaobo / Gothic-Punk\n\nChoose one specific space where old authority and present maintenance visibly collide: a converted mansion, a decayed factory used as a club, a protected archive with leaked data, or a long-established fund now reduced to recurring fees. Do not move among several settings. Introduce the space through the focal narrator's current practical task in it—repairing a lock, checking a roster, closing an account, delivering a notice—so the setting's unusual details appear as concrete obstacles and permissions.\n\nKeep the narrator a practical person with working access to the mismatch between solemn presentation and ordinary upkeep. Use the Wang Xiaobo register to state plainly what others avoid: the impressive facade depends on a part-time cleaner, the inherited prohibition only makes someone late, the old ritual has no observer but still generates paperwork. When a gate, locked room, window, record, or ceremonial object matters, explain exactly how it actually opens or works in the present: who has a key, what code still functions, which janitor bypasses the alarm, whose list has not been updated. This mechanical detail is the material path that makes the crime possible or visible.\n\nLet the crime unfold as an event with causes and costs rooted in these practical layers: a payment arranged under a false name, a missing token that was never valuable except as proof of standing, a tenant pressured through a building-code exception, or a record altered to protect a legacy. The narrator should not solve the whole matter at once. Let them reason from one observed irregularity to the next, asking who benefits, who would have been present, and what would break if the official story were accurate. When an explanation seems convenient, expose its absurdity by comparing the official description with the material facts: a 'sealed' archive whose door is propped open, a 'ritual' fee collected by direct debit, a 'protected' area reached by a public stairwell.\n\nGive the narrator meaningful nonspoken interiority in at least one private interval before, during, or after an encounter in the space. A trusted person may become more candid when alone with the narrator, and a social mask may fall away. Keep that intimacy tied to actual relationship and stake: the narrator overlooks an irregularity because of a remembered kindness, hesitates because of an old debt, or notices the exact moment a habit of deference turns into concealment. Do not make every private interval a revelation or a betrayal.\n\nEnd the scene after the narrator has seen one live contradiction between what the place or person claimed and what the material facts now make visible. That contradiction should be connected by reasoning to the crime—not merely an oddity—but the full legal or emotional resolution may remain unfinished. Impose no mandated outcome for the narrator's next step.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadazak::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Wang Xiaobo / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite a retrospective testimonial from a first-person narrator who participated in, witnessed, or was made complicit in the crime. Give the narrator one definite reason for telling the story now: to explain, defend, warn, justify a long-past decision, or set down what the official account erased. Name the implied audience that this motive addresses, whether a younger relative, a community meeting, an investigator, or the written record itself. Keep the audience present in the narrator's manner; the memoir may quote or answer them, but it must not become a neutral autobiographical essay.\n\nDo not invent a new regional location. If the active story already supplies language, region, community, or period, use those precise facts. If they are not supplied, do not fill them in with generic Indian signifiers. Let caste, religion, code-switching, or joint-family structure appear only when they are already part of the active world; never import them as atmosphere.\n\nThe narrator should reconstruct the practical reasoning available at the time, not simply remember. Write the self-interested version they once believed as a live belief: a family agreement presented as necessity, a work rule accepted as common sense, or a small violation justified because everyone did it. Then force that version to meet one concrete detail: who paid what, who was excluded from the decision, what the rule actually said, or what happened to the person who had no alternative. Make this a fixed sequence each time the memoir reaches a questionable past choice: state the earlier belief, confront it with the concrete detail, then continue the account with the new implication visible in the narrator's voice. Do not label this as awakening or conversion; let the narrator be impatient, amused, bitter, or wary.\n\nMake the crime's mechanics legible even when the narrator does not know everything. At least once, include the practical documentary or economic fact that makes the scheme work: a property transfer signed by someone who believed it was temporary, a loan guaranteed with a document never read, a report that shifted blame, or a payment whose recipient was never recorded. Distinguish in plain prose what the narrator knew at the time, what they learned later, and what they are now guessing. The later knowledge must not retroactively become the earlier motive.\n\nThe narrator follows Wang Xiaobo's rational inquiry through practical questions: who benefits, what would have happened if someone refused, why does the official term not match the material result. Expose that gap through its concrete effect on the participants, not through sarcasm or a lecture. A community expectation or official procedure may explain the moment of decision, but show how it actually worked on these particular people. Let any humor belong to the narrator's voice, not to every character.\n\nEnd the testimonial at a point where the previously adequate story breaks or bends under the facts now stated. The larger moral or legal outcome may remain open; do not force a resolution or a cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadazal::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Wang Xiaobo / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the social or psychological realism route. Set the story inside one bounded institution or daily arrangement from the active world—a workplace, neighborhood association, family business, school, or rental office—and use the particular local hierarchy, forms, and tasks already present. Do not invent a generic Japanese setting if the story is located elsewhere.\n\nOpen with the institution's ordinary practical work before the scheme becomes visible: a reimbursement form being checked, a delivery recorded, a client's file updated, a report passed along. Let the crime grow from one of these repeated tasks rather than from outside violence. Choose one concrete mechanism and keep it legible: a false reimbursement, a favor sold under another name, a report adjusted to keep a client, or a signature obtained through social pressure. Show the preparation and execution through the focal character's particular duties and attachments, not through an omniscient summary.\n\nKeep the viewpoint attached to one focal participant or a small focused group. When a rule or official explanation is invoked, have the focal character ask the practical next question: who benefits, what would happen if the form were honest, why does everyone pretend not to know. Do not state these questions as essays; render them as live private thought at the moment the explanation is given. The absurdity should appear in the concrete mismatch between purpose and effect: a bank holiday observed by fabricating a date, a safety inspection satisfied by moving items for one afternoon. Let any humor belong only to the focal mind; other characters may be entirely serious.\n\nShow the focal character's inward recognition when a convenient explanation becomes unconvincing. Tie that recognition to a physical object or routine already present: a smudged entry, a file that cannot have been there yesterday, a form whose blank line everyone skips. Let the realization sit for a few moments without confession. It may be followed by a private interval of practical reasoning—whether to participate, oppose, or look away—but do not mandate a decision or a crisis.\n\nUse external sensory detail only when it serves the crime's practical world: sticky tape on a ledger, the smell of a storage room, the time from the office to the station. Seasonal or scenic observation is allowed when it affects the work itself, but do not insert a traditional inventory. Inner life may be substantial, and dialogue should match the particular local manner of the setting without forcing universal reticence or politeness.\n\nThe aftermath should remain concrete. Show who is exposed, who continues as before, what repayment or penalty exists only on paper, and how the people involved revise their account of what happened. End after one such revision is made, or after the focal character understands the scheme well enough to choose a practical position. Do not force a moral resolution or a cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadazam::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Wang Xiaobo / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social realism, chronicle, or testimony route. Begin with the crime as a public or known situation whose interest lies not in a hidden culprit but in the unequal conditions, practical excuses, and remembered versions around it. The offense should be nameable in the opening: a land sale recorded twice, a truckload diverted under a municipal permit, an election fund mixed with private debt, or a disappearance explained by a transfer that never happened.\n\nGive the narrator a definite daily role in the same community or institution where the offense occurs: someone who keeps the ledger, carries the papers, answers the office phone, or sits through the meetings. The narrator may be minor, but their role must give them reason to encounter the relevant records and people. Write the account through direct observation, what the narrator was told, and what they later checked. Keep those three modes distinct in the prose: do not present a later document as if the narrator had held it at the time of the event.\n\nLet the narrator follow Wang Xiaobo's rational inquiry without turning it into a political lecture. When an official explanation, local saying, or respected person makes an act seem inevitable, have the narrator ask the practical next question: whose land or job depended on it, who could have refused, what ledger or procedure actually changed, and why everyone now remembers the decision as unanimous. State the absurdity in the concrete gap between public language and material result, such as a victim described as a participant in a deal they never agreed to or a road that exists only on paper while money was spent elsewhere.\n\nMake the crime mechanically legible. At least once, show the physical and economic reality behind the scheme: distances, waiting times, seasonal work, the weight of a bribe, or the difficulty of getting a record corrected. Each time the narrator reports a witness's account, test it against an official paper or ledger entry, and show the discrepancy: a date that does not fit, a formal term used for a much smaller private arrangement, a person listed as present who could not have been there. Do not let the narrator's memory or resentment serve as sole evidence; state when the narrator is guessing or recalling later information.\n\nThe narrator may be personally affected, but that does not make the testimony automatically reliable. Let the reader see the narrator correcting or qualifying at least one claim: when they learned something later, when a memory is shaped by resentment, or when a public name has been overwritten. Community memory may appear through a street name, market conversation, planned commemoration, or old document, but treat these as historical facts, never supernatural omens.\n\nEnd the scene after the narrator states one version of events strongly enough that its weak seam becomes visible, or after a surviving party refuses to accept the revision. The legal or moral outcome may remain open; do not force a single improved resolution or a cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadazan::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Wang Xiaobo / Light Novel\n\nOpen directly in the immediate situation: the focal character is already in motion or about to act. Name the present location in the first few lines and show what the character sees, hears, or touches there. Do not begin with a long retrospective setup. When the scene moves to a new place, name the new location as soon as the character arrives and give one concrete sensory detail that makes the transition easy to follow.\n\nUse short, clean paragraphs. Start a new paragraph when the character moves, when the action changes, when someone speaks, and when the focal character has a private reaction. Keep dialogue assigned to its speaker without heavy tags, but do not let the prose become a bare screenplay; it should still carry the character's immediate experience.\n\nGive the focal character a direct, practical reasoning voice drawn from Wang Xiaobo. When a rule, superior, customer, or companion gives a solemn or convenient explanation, let the character privately ask the concrete question that exposes its practicality: who actually benefits, what would break if someone followed the form honestly, why is the locked door always left propped open. Put this question in the character's own voice, one or two sentences, immediately after the explanation. The character may say part of it aloud in plain or irreverent terms, and the other person's response should match who they are—angry, tired, awkward, or amused. Do not make every character witty; this particular skeptical turn belongs to the focal mind.\n\nMake the crime causally legible inside the scene. If an offense is in progress, show its moving parts as the character encounters them: the bag handed over, the code entered, the false name used, the document that passes inspection. Provide at least one visual presentation of a clue or complication: a smudged time stamp, a phone left on the desk, a familiar figure in the wrong place. State exactly what the character notices about it and why it matters now. The character may think plainly about the risk without announcing it to the other parties; do not hide the private thought to manufacture surprise. If a name or mechanic is unfamiliar, introduce it through the character's immediate use or a short plain explanation, never a glossary paragraph.\n\nAfter a revelation, interruption, or physical action, let the character have two or three sentences of private response: a physical sensation, an immediate practical worry, or a blunt assessment. Keep these beats short and distinct, one per shift. The story may stop mid-action or mid-thought, but you do not need a cliffhanger. End at any natural boundary where the situation's pressure and the character's practical question remain active; do not invent a false conclusion, a better outcome, a changed plan, or a moral payoff. If a limit or danger has a present effect, render that effect, but leave the next choice open unless the story itself has made it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadazao::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Wang Xiaobo / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite the crime account through the chosen viewpoint — investigator, offender, victim, or bystander — letting a practical question develop in rhythmically shaped prose. Choose one recurring detail from the scene itself and let it return when understanding shifts: a worn desk can first suggest routine, later hold an altered receipt, later still show where someone waited. Tie each recurrence to evidence, motive, or aftermath rather than to unrelated description.\n\nLet a comparison unfold only when it changes what the narrator can use. A suspect's careful pause may become, in the narrator's attention, the sound of a lock tested from the wrong side. That shift marks suspicion; it does not settle proof, and the official or social problem of what can be shown stays open. Keep a plain division of labor: declarative sentences carry the legal and practical stakes — who signed, what was owed, when the deadline fell — while the lyric movement belongs to perception, memory, and reflection. The plain passages give the shaped ones their force.\n\nLet the author profile's directness survive the rhythm. When a rule, a likely consequence, or an official version deserves a question, ask it concretely and personally: what the regulation assumes about people who actually work in that office, why the report's tidy sequence cannot match two independent recollections of the same evening. Irony may puncture solemn procedure, but give it to this narrator's attention rather than to every speaker; private fear, cold interest, and a victim's awkward memory can enter the rhythm without turning the account into lament.\n\nDo not let imagery absorb the offense. The narrator may notice how light falls across a torn envelope; the envelope's date, contents, and chain of keeping still have to be established by what people actually did with it. Use the image to hold two versions of one event together — the explained one and the one the objects support — not to replace the report of what happened.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadazap::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Wang Xiaobo / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the narration return to the scene, papers, routines, or relationships that a past offense touched, in whatever voice and person the active contract has established. Register small irreversible changes rather than grand ruins: the daughter now pauses at a window where she once watched a car leave; a ledger still carries a smudge no one has explained. Ordinary pleasure stays possible — a meal can still be good, a joke can still land — and reflection can sharpen a fond memory instead of only mourning it.\n\nLet candor question the versions on file. When a family, an office, or the narrator's own memory presents an account as closed, notice the surviving detail that does not fit: a receipt kept after the case was closed, a name pronounced carefully in one household and rarely in another. Keep the questioning personal rather than cool. The remembering voice may own guilt, embarrassment, fear, or stubborn affection, and may decide that some questions are better left unasked for now.\n\nAnchor reflection in the crime's practical consequences. A remembered phone call can explain why someone still locks a door twice; an unfinished argument can explain why a friendship was never resumed. Work through concrete links like these rather than through general remarks about time, and when the offense itself is recalled, render it as events with causes and costs, not as a fog the years have produced.\n\nLet passages end on what remains available — a repaired street, a tool back in its place, a person still willing to talk about something else — rather than announcing that nothing can be repaired. Preserve the active perspective and its limits: the narrator remembers what the narrator could know, and other minds stay closed unless the contract opens them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadazaq::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Wang Xiaobo / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nTell the crime as a situated retelling grounded in social and urban realism. A neighbor, relative, former clerk, or collector tells the story to someone on a particular occasion — a meal, a work shift, a condolence visit, an errand — and that occasion shapes what gets told and what gets skipped. Build the material world from the community the story actually supplies: its courtyards or stairwells, its municipal records, its stalls, buses, offices, kitchens. Keep the language and tradition of the actual setting distinct rather than borrowing registers from another literature, and add no honor code, mysticism, or religious moral simply because the style is regional.\n\nLet rational inquiry operate inside the telling. The teller, or a listener with a stake in the matter, can ask what the customary explanation avoids: why the public story conflicts with a receipt, a work schedule, a key that was copied, an argument people still quote verbatim. Irony lives in the gap between formal or neighborly speech and practical action — the polite formula used to hand someone a debt, the blessing that accompanies a refusal.\n\nUse recurrence across tellings to show the crime's continuing pressure. A patched awning, a repeated queue, a familiar dish can return later carrying a weight it did not have before. Let family members and neighbors recall the same event differently — not as a device for manufacturing truth, but to show how loyalty, memory, and self-protection divide people who must keep living together. Distinguish what the teller saw, what the teller was told, and what the teller concludes.\n\nKeep frame and content in proportion. The occasion may interrupt, prompt, or qualify the account, but the crime stays legible as events — preparation, offense, evidence, pursuit, or aftermath, rendered concretely whenever the telling reaches them — and the frame never swallows the story or reduces it to a lesson. The narration holds to the selected perspective; the teller knows what the teller could know.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadazar::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Wang Xiaobo / Minimalist Prose\n\nCarry the crime's practical reality in a small number of exact details. A suspect's account can be checked against a bus time, a torn label, a door that catches, a coworker's lunch break. Let direct clauses render preparation, a decision, a threat, or a mistake without a comment announcing that it matters. When the narrator notices a contradiction, put it in the objects or statements themselves — the office closed at noon; no one who signed the form was there — rather than adding an editorial summary after it.\n\nThis is the author profile's method operating at sentence level: after the approved answer is given, the narrator records the checkable fact beside it and lets the reader do the arithmetic. Keep such observations economical. One tested inconsistency is stronger than three invented incongruities; follow a question only as far as the scene's evidence goes.\n\nEconomy is not fragmentation. An essential thought may run long enough to make its causal relation clear, and a run of clipped sentences that deletes causality has failed the style. When a character hides a phone or keeps a document, show the choice through physical action or through the immediate reasoning the active contract makes available. Where that contract opens a mind, write the thought itself in the same economy; a clipped spoken reply or a significant silence does not substitute for it, and minds it does not open stay closed.\n\nKeep the cast individual. Not everyone is terse, and not every answer is an ironic puncture. Dialogue can serve as evidence — an over-explained alibi, a volunteered detail no one asked for — but people also simply talk. In scene and aftermath, describe what the viewpoint can see, hear, or verify, and preserve the current narrator, role, and access restrictions.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadazas::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Wang Xiaobo / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite scene-first prose that follows the chosen crime viewpoint through the present moment, moving naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and whatever inner experience the active contract authorizes. When a character reads a warning, hears a partial account, or notices someone waiting outside, give enough immediate physical and mental material to make the moment's significance available without stopping for a background essay or recapping earlier events as though the reader had forgotten them.\n\nLet direct inquiry arise inside the scene. A narrator may set a plausible official explanation against the practical world and watch which one bends: the car's mileage against the claimed destination, the office's closing time against the signed date, the way a manager keeps saying \"temporary.\" A character may say a disagreeable thought plainly instead of swallowing it while the same fact is repeated politely. Keep the absurdity concrete and local; do not turn every speaker into a satirist or hand the whole cast one cleverness.\n\nUse paragraphing to manage the tension between evidence and relationship. A conversation that seems domestic can expose that one person has been watching another's schedule for months; a stretch of routine work can reveal which record someone never touches. Keep the source of each detail oriented for the reader — seen now, remembered, read from a document, or inferred — so that present perception and later knowledge do not blur.\n\nKeep outcomes contingent. A scene may end with a phone unanswered, a door left open, or the realization that a friend's help has the shape of surveillance, but no scene owes a twist, a confession, or a loyalty reversal. The crime can advance by a single practical change: someone decides to keep a document; someone else removes a name from a list. Preserve perspective, access, and established world facts.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadazat::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Wang Xiaobo / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship the organizing center of the crime account. Select scenes where private language and repeated habits show what these two people expect from each other: a nickname used only in danger, a window checked before anything true is said, a joke that once meant freedom and now means evasion. Run the crime through that channel — one partner may know that the other removed evidence, borrowed money, or went somewhere forbidden, and the familiar private signal may expose or protect, depending on what the characters actually do with it.\n\nLet their directness with each other carry the profile's questioning. A partner is allowed to say that the official story does not fit the loved person's schedule, fear, or usual lie, and to ask what the arrangement they are living under actually costs. Do not force exchanges into banter or polished wit. Affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery should be particular to these people and the supplied premise; import no roster of familiar tropes.\n\nMake the crime legible through relationship consequences rather than clinical procedure. A promise becomes suspect because of what it asks the partner to ignore; a shared meal exposes whose account has a missing hour. Give the promised beats — exposure recognized, help accepted as genuine, a private phrase suddenly belonging to someone else — enough space where they belong, without requiring a confession or a reunion in every segment.\n\nKeep the criminal stakes concrete. The offense, scheme, or pursuit has a shape and costs someone something; the relationship is not a metaphor for it, and feeling is not a substitute for what actually happened. Private thought belongs to the focal character under the active contract and does not become available to the partner merely because intimacy is central. End where the scene pauses; the next move may be practical trust, exposure, or continued silence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadazau::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Wang Xiaobo / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nOpen as a retelling. A compact declaration names the deed or the person, and the particulars complicate the known version: a respected trader was remembered for keeping every account until someone found the two missing weeks. Let recurring phrases come from practical acts rather than ornament — \"the day the ledger stopped,\" \"the third knock after closing\" — and return to a phrase only when a later occurrence changes what it means. Give no one a ceremonial epithet at every entrance.\n\nTreat the crime as a public matter whose consequences are still recalled, without exceeding what the selected viewpoint can know. The chronicle may report what people said about the theft, the accusation, the payoff; it cannot enter a hidden mind unless the active contract allows it. Keep remembered reputation and remembered scene distinct: a figure recalled as unforgiving may appear in the scene simply trying to buy back a coat, and that discrepancy is the account's real material.\n\nLet the reteller's practical observation interrupt the noble or scandalous version. The lock had been changed the week before; the leader's timetable does not fit; the old quarrel was about a water bill, not an ancient pledge. The puncture does not shrink the crime — it shows communal memory preserving reputation, misattribution, and embarrassment in one breath. Ordinary business may carry recurrence without being staged as battle; oaths, kinship, journeys, and public confrontation appear only where the material establishes them, and no imported pantheon or fated doom arrives with the form.\n\nEnd on a practical consequence still being remembered, leaving another act available for retelling rather than closing the account with a moral.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadazav::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Wang Xiaobo / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the crime as a social-institutional inquiry: the narrator is already inside the practical arrangements that let an offense be recorded, managed, or made to disappear. Treat the offense as a disturbance in ordinary systems—housing, work, aid, transport, municipal record-keeping, a workplace safety file, a benefit decision, a death classified without question. The narration follows the narrator's active examination of how one version of events became official and what that version costs the people who must live inside it.\n\nLet the narrator test records, routines, and spoken assurances against one another. When a form says a visit occurred, compare it with the bus schedule, the locked office, the signature that appears on two different dates, the colleague who was not there. Do not announce the contradiction in general terms; place the two facts close enough that the reader can see the official account strain. A missed entry, an altered date, a rule applied to one person and waived for another, or a meeting that could not have happened as described is enough. The narrator may ask a plain question that the institution has no practical answer for: who had the key, why was the form signed before the inspection, where did the money go after the account was closed.\n\nKeep the narrator's reasoning direct and independent. The narrator can follow a question past the approved answer and state the suppressed implication plainly: if the door was locked, then someone with a key opened it; if the report was written before the visit, then the report is not a record of the visit. Let this candor belong to the narrator's particular attention, not to every speaker. Other characters may defend the arrangement from need, habit, loyalty, or fear, and their reasons should remain legible rather than being flattened into satire. A clerk may repeat the official phrase and still pause at the practical consequence; an official may be evasive because the rule protects them, because they are overworked, or because they have not thought about the contradiction until now.\n\nUse black humor as an owned response to absurdity, tied to a specific perception. When the narrator notices that a missing document has been declared both lost and already reviewed, the observation can be dry and precise without becoming a running joke. The humor should arise from the narrator's recognition of a practical impossibility, not from making every institution automatically ridiculous. A witness can speak in bureaucratic language without being contemptible; expose the cost of the arrangement, not a personality for its own sake.\n\nKeep private thought within the active viewpoint. The narrator may wonder whether a supervisor is lying, whether a file was misfiled, or whether a neighbor stopped answering for a reason connected to the inquiry. A later finding can qualify or confirm that thought, but unspoken reasoning is not available to other characters. When the investigation disturbs ordinary life, show the disturbance as social fact: a shop stays open late, a neighbor stops answering, a fund releases money on a new condition, a worker is moved to a different shift after asking a question. The aftermath matters as a change in people's practical circumstances, not as a lesson pronounced afterward.\n\nDo not impose a moral conclusion or require the inquiry to finish in this installment. The response may end with the sought file located, a meeting postponed, a contradiction named to its source, or the narrator still waiting for a return call. What must continue is the sense that the investigation is using definite methods and that the institutional world remains available for the next step.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadazaw::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Wang Xiaobo / Screenplay\n\nWrite the story in scene units. Each scene heading gives place and time when either changes. Action lines state only what a viewer can see or hear. Dialogue blocks carry attributed speech. Show the inquiry through what enters and leaves the frame: a missing ledger page, an officer turning a badge away from a visitor, an unused delivery bay, a clean keyhole next to a spoken claim about a locked safe. Do not describe the contradiction in prose; place the two facts close enough for the audience to compare them.\n\nUse voice-over only when the sequence needs the narrator's reasoning to bridge visible evidence or to mark that a private thought belongs to one person. The narrator may say, without taking sides, how an official account would require the empty safe to have been opened by someone with no key, while the screen shows the clean keyhole. The voice-over must not explain a motive the audience has not been given, and it must not supply proof that the visible evidence does not yet support. Let the narrator's directness follow a question beyond the approved answer: a line can ask who signed the movement order, why the report changed its date, or how the sealed room was open during lunch. The screen should already have shown the arrangement being punctured before the voice-over names its implication.\n\nKeep the focal character's position visible through what they handle, check, and refuse to accept. When a question is met with solemn assurance, the next scene may show the practical sequel: the sealed room is open during lunch, the report changes its date, the witness's shift now starts an hour later. The dramatic effect comes from the mismatch between speech and staged fact, not from a character announcing \"that's absurd.\" Let one character carry the irreverent directness as their habit; other speakers may be flat, embarrassed, combative, or sincerely confused.\n\nRespect the screenplay's knowledge boundary. A character cannot react to an offscreen meeting unless sound or a later line indicates they could know of it. Written records shown on screen are not automatically read by everyone in the scene; if a full schedule is legible to the audience, another character may still miss it. Private voice-over may reveal the narrator's suspicion, but not what another person felt while alone, and it must not supply proof that the visible evidence does not yet support. Where private experience must be written, clearly attribute it as nonspoken authoring material or the active contracted hidden form, not as dialogue the cast can hear.\n\nEnd on an earned stage rather than a forensic conclusion. A scene can close after a second speaker reads out a line from a recovered document, after a character asks a question that the visible evidence has made unavoidable, or simply with the narrator's quiet statement of what the day made no longer plausible. Do not force a cut to a new suspect or a red herring. Preserve existing authorship and world contracts, including any required interiority.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadazax::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Wang Xiaobo / Stage Play\n\nUse the shared stage as a place of practical demonstration. Stage directions mark entrances, exits, blocking, props, pauses, and business an actor can perform: unlock a cabinet, leaf through a file while speaking, drop a court notice on a table, or stand in a doorway through a whole reply. The audience must be able to hold a simple mental map of who can see which prop, who has moved between two places, and which line contradicts a gesture.\n\nLet one character's direct, nimble interrogation work through concrete stage facts. When a rule protects an official, the question can begin with plain mechanics: how did the page appear in the locked drawer by morning, where was the spare key kept, why does the inventory list an item removed before the visit. The questioner does not need to say \"the rule is absurd\"; instead, another speaker is given a business that shows the arrangement: they close the supposedly locked drawer with an unsecured cage, or they fold the notice away while promising to respond. The spoken assurance and the visible action should be close enough in time for the audience to compare them.\n\nKeep black humor and irreverence owned by particular characters and shaped by their position. The investigator may ask a question that is too concrete to be answered without confessing an inconvenience; a clerk may answer in institutional formulas and still pause at the practical cost. Let silence or a marked pause appear in the stage directions when it changes the audience's reading, not as decoration. Do not make every witness witty or every official self-sabotaging.\n\nStage directions establish observables without certifying hidden motive. An entrance through a fire door is a fact; why the person used it may remain disputed, and another character's remark may offer a false or partial reason. Keep required interiority clearly nonspoken. A monologue or aside can press a doubt further only if its dramatic function is separate from ordinary private thinking; otherwise write the active contracted hidden interiority under its own mark rather than letting another character hear the thought.\n\nThe scene can stop when a practical question has been posed, an exit closes off an avenue of inquiry, or the props themselves have become part of a stated suspicion: a checkbook left on the ledge, a gate key on the wrong ring. Do not force a staged confession or a full tableau of suspects. Preserve the story's current crime focus and the active authorship and world contracts.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadazay::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Wang Xiaobo / Stream of Consciousness\n\nBraid the present perception with the character's live inquiry. Let the movement jump between the weight of a cup, the smell of wet paper, a supervisor's sentence from the morning, and the sentence itself: \"He closed the file and said there was nothing to review.\" The paragraph may continue into remembered corridors or bodily fatigue before returning to the one practical question. Syntax may follow the interruption, but each shift must be attributable to this mind's association, not a general literary haze.\n\nLet the narrator's rational independence surface as the mind's refusal to let a contradiction dissolve. When a version of events would require the locked room to have no key and the inventory to have been made after the box was opened, the thought can recur in different guises: a remembered chat about duplicate labels, a line from the regulations quoted with mild detachment, a physical hesitation in the chest before knocking. Do not settle every recurrence into a polished aphorism. The irreverence can be blunt in one sentence and omitted for several paragraphs while practical observation continues.\n\nKeep the boundaries between event, memory, and hypothetical thought legible. A recalled conversation may be marked by the past tense and one sensory clue: the sound of a door closing, a particular office smell. When the mind imagines a confrontation or reconstructs a schedule, it should be recognizable as imagining, not presented as a scene that has occurred. The reader needs enough orientation to know which items are available as evidence and which are the character's rehearsal, but the prose need not interrupt itself to label every transition. A remembered sentence remains a fixed past event; an imagined reply remains possible, not yet spoken.\n\nDo not grant shared consciousness. The stream belongs to the active viewpoint. Another person's smile, delay, or evasive line may be noticed and interpreted, but not lived through. If the viewpoint's private thought is authorized, render it under the active contract; if another character's interiority is required by the selected role, use the appropriate hidden wrapper rather than folding both minds into one associative current. A sent message, if one appears, is fixed text; the character may repeat it in thought, but the thought is not identical to the transmitted words without reason.\n\nLet the braid follow the character's actual attention and waiting. A night spent awake can include checking a clock, rehearsing a question, recalling a bus ride, and noticing that the same wet smell is now stronger. No external task or new discovery must complete the beat. The response may break after the mind lands on a single grounded observation, or after a sensory detail leads back to a fact that has not yet been spoken. Keep the crime's practical consequences present whenever they return to attention; the style does not erase evidence with introspection.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadazaz::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Wang Xiaobo / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the inquiry through attributed messages. Show sender names, and include a timestamp, delivery receipt, edit marker, attachment, or channel change only when it changes what a reader can support about timing or knowledge. Let each person's actual digital habit sound distinct: one sends short denials with full sentence stops; another types a long explanation, drafts it for minutes, and sends one cautious line; a third never uses the group channel but replies directly the next morning. Those habits are public behavior, not hidden truth.\n\nUse the plain directness of the author profile as a live questioning voice within the transcript. A sender can answer a solemn official assurance with a short, concrete request: send me the entry with the date and the page. or who signed the movement order. The bluntness should fit the speaker; it does not need to appear in every message. Some recipients may answer in institutional phrases, and the transcript should preserve their actual wording without editorial sarcasm. The gap between the formula and the practical request becomes evident when the question remains unanswered or gets an off-topic receipt.\n\nKeep unsent drafts separate from sent text. A draft may read Do not say the key was in the drawer and then be deleted; the recipient sees only the next transmitted message. Use a clearly separated draft label or the active hidden nonspoken convention. An unsent draft is not automatically the sender's live thought made public to anyone in the channel, nor is it knowledge available to another participant. If private reasoning is required by the active contract, place it in its own hidden interiority block around or below the transcript, not as a fake received message or a retrospective addendum.\n\nLet edit histories and delivery behavior contribute only when they can be read by the participants. If a message is edited, show the final form or mark the edit if the platform would. A delay might mean the sender was away, composing, or unwilling to answer; do not narrate its cause unless the sender later states it or another verifiable message shows it. The suspense is earned by a conflict between what the message says and what a previous message says, not by an interface alert invented to reveal an insecurity.\n\nPreserve actual event information outside the transcript when needed, separated by a line or the active presentation: come home late, found the door unlocked, called the office, put the paper in my bag. These notes may establish what the messages refer to, but they are not private thought unless marked as such. Continue the exchange without forcing an outcome. A response can stop after an unanswered question has been left on read, an attachment fails, or one party writes I will check tomorrow, without a confession, a plot twist, or a completed investigation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadazba::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Wang Xiaobo / Visual Novel\n\nPresent each segment as a legible illustrated-scene unit: a short scene-setting line gives place and present condition, attributed dialogue carries the encounter, and the focal narration or inner response follows the active viewpoint. Use background or expression cues as orientation—the desk cleared overnight, the office door standing open, a frown when the shift list appears—but do not let those cues function as truth indicators. A person can look away from guilt or from a headache; the suspicion must rest on a contradiction in the account, schedule, or arrangement.\n\nLet the viewpoint's Wang Xiaobo-style questioning work through plain, nimble attention to concrete details. When a supervisor says it was checked, the focal narration can note the log date before the reply or recall that the same file was still on the cart that morning. The next exchange may support the memory, complicate it, or show that the speaker was worried about a different matter. Keep the narrator's skepticism specific: follow the question beyond the approved answer by asking what the rule cost in practice, not by treating every official phrase as automatically deceitful.\n\nKeep private thought within the viewpoint and outside other characters' knowledge. The focal narration may think he still has not said whether the door was locked or remember the smell of smoke from the stairwell, but no other character knows that until it is spoken or later confirmed by visible evidence. Do not stage a reveal in which a character reacts to an unspoken suspicion. Conversely, do not suppress an authorized focal conclusion just to preserve another character's surprise; the reader can know more than the cast without breaking scene logic.\n\nUse recall and revisit as rhythm, not as proof. A familiar background detail may reappear when a later fact changes its meaning: the spare chair was moved, the weekly cleaning card now shows a different signature. Let the significance be earned by the later fact rather than by a dramatic musical cue or by the character narrowing their eyes. Repetition should deepen the available interpretation without restarting the same suspicion from zero.\n\nKeep the scene-oriented presentation free of branching menus, romance routes, player choices, or images unless those features are separately authorized. The scene can end after a practical discovery, a deferred question, or a quiet acknowledgment of what is now less plausible. A discovery may open a new conversation rather than forcing a decision point, and a relationship consequence may remain unsettled after the factual question is answered. Preserve the active authorship and world contracts, including required hidden interiority where another mind must be represented.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadazbb::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Wang Xiaobo / Web Serial\n\nWrite each installment as part of one ongoing inquiry, not as a miniature chapter with a forced turn. Recover context briefly when it helps: a name, a date, a location, or a previous statement, given in enough concrete detail that a reader who has missed intervening replies can follow the current examination. Let the narrator's practical question carry the through-line—who signed the release, where did the spare key go, why the inventory list disagrees with the log—and allow one installment to close after only part of that question is addressed.\n\nUse earlier details as live material for later developments. A locked room mentioned two installments ago can become significant when a later report says it was opened before the check; a missing tag on a delivery cart can be recalled when a receipt's weight matches the wrong order. Do not rewrite the earlier observation to make the new clue appear; instead, show the narrator checking the old note or simply remembering it with the date and conditions intact. New evidence may qualify the old explanation without replacing the old event.\n\nKeep the author profile's direct, nimble reasoning in the narrator's attention, but keep the black humor owned. The narrator may observe that a timetable has been revised so often it is now a form of ghost, but not every character needs to share that style. A witness can answer plainly, an official can stay with the solemn language, and another person can be angry at the repeated questions. The practical absurdity of a rule should be revealed by its concrete cost or contradiction, not by making everyone exchange quips.\n\nPreserve ordinary life around the investigation. Between visits or while waiting for a response, people still rent rooms, change shifts, buy food, or return a borrowed tool. These details may matter later or may simply keep the world continuous. Do not insert a clue or reversal as an entrance fee. If a week passes with no answer, the installment may include the narrator's waiting, a rescheduled appointment, or a small task completed, without an artificial cliffhanger at the end.\n\nEnd on an earned development: a file is located, a request is sent, a contradiction is stated to its source, or the narrator recognizes that a previously trusted statement is no longer available. A pending request can remain unanswered across the boundary. Do not require a better plan, a changed direction, a recovered result, or a suspect lineup before stopping. Serial duration gives selected facts room to acquire meaning; it must not make a fair outcome perpetually recede or force a new reversal to fill each reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadbaaa::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Yasunari Kawabata / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nTreat the crime as an injury tender to a close relationship: an assumption, withholding, small betrayal, or return that cannot be admitted without exposing more than the speaker can bear. Choose a viewpoint who was involved in or affected by the offense and let the narration dwell on what that person can almost say. Show the strain through brief physical surfaces—a hand withdrawn from a table, a garment set down too carefully, a cup turned so the handle faces the wrong way—rather than through explanations of feeling.\n\nUse intervals and unfinished sentences. When dialogue approaches the wound, let one character look toward a door, a window, a stain, or an ordinary object that has become charged. The observer may think she is about to apologize, but the spoken line can be a practical remark, a question about something else, or a partial version of the truth. The other person's response may create a near-contact or a renewed distance, but it may also be ordinary, evasive, or unexpectedly direct. Keep private turmoil inside the chosen narration; do not require every character to articulate the hurt aloud, and do not assume that anger means wickedness or that silence means guilt.\n\nLet intimacy persist inside the damage. A small act of care can follow an accusation, and tenderness can be awkward, too late, or partly self-protective. The crime need not ruin the relationship completely, but its effects should outlast the discovery. End at the point where the present exchange or private interval reaches its own natural pause: a spoken line that cannot be taken back, an ordinary gesture completed or left unfinished, a decision made in silence, or a moment when the character simply stops trying to explain. Do not force a confession, apology, or repaired bond; leave the emotional cost legible in what remains unspoken, seen, and nearly touched.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadbaab::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Yasunari Kawabata / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the shared interval as a continuing crime conversation in readable paragraphs. Give each speaker a clear attribution and keep actions brief enough to scan: a look at a locked drawer, the click of a door, a pause before taking a coat from the chair. Use Kawabata's selective detail after each exchange—one sensory anchor that the reader can picture and that belongs to this scene's suspicion or strained loyalty. Do not explain what the detail means; leave it in the scene as pressure the next speaker can notice, ignore, or act on.\n\nMaintain active NPC behavior without solving the crime. A witness may become evasive, a partner may continue preparing to leave, or a bystander may return to sorting receipts. The conversation should create pressure from what is not said: an unfinished sentence about the night of the theft, a hand that stops before touching the evidence, a familiar object now in the wrong place. Keep dialogue believable for the active world and character contract; do not force every NPC to speak in epigrams or linger only to foreshadow.\n\nEnd on a natural continuation point, not a forced question or cliffhanger. The final line or action should leave the scene in a state where the other participant can observe, speak, move, or change subject. The crime may remain open, or the present exchange may advance a small piece of it; either way, the moment should be complete enough to return to: someone has just stepped away, closed a window, or set a key on a counter. Use atmosphere as pressure, not as a punishment to be endured. Never include OOC notes, dice rolls, or instructions to the player inside the narrative.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadbaac::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Yasunari Kawabata / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the Chinese intimate urban or domestic narrative route: the story proceeds through closely observed scenes in a household, shop, lodging, or neighborhood where the crime has entered private life. Keep the language and community actually supplied, and do not add architecture, food, festivals, or names from another tradition merely because the style is selected. The Chinese method governs arrangement and emphasis—how daily life presses around an offense, how a domestic surface becomes altered—not the cast's ethnicity or location.\n\nWithin the selected viewpoint, let the crime emerge through Kawabata's selective physical impressions. A narrow focal passage can concentrate the effect without reducing any separately authorized narrator access. Note the tea left too long, the folded newspaper moved, the sound of a stair, the particular way someone avoids a corridor. These details stand at the threshold between familiar comfort and intrusion. Keep the emotional response compressed; instead of stating dread, show a character noticing that the bolt on the back gate is not pushed fully home, then going on with an ordinary task.\n\nLet the consequences of the crime alter small daily relationships. A relative may serve food without speaking, a neighbor may stop coming to the courtyard, or an employer may pay out wages as if the amount were suddenly difficult to count. Do not solve the crime through a moral fable or a communal lesson. The ending should remain proportionate to the domestic frame: a small change in routine, an object out of place, a debt acknowledged or denied, a promise that cannot be spoken, or a return to ordinary work that now feels altered. Let the particular consequence arise from what the scene has actually established. Keep the feeling of transience—of something passing and hard to keep—inside the details themselves, not in a concluding proverb or summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadbaad::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Yasunari Kawabata / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite with hard, short movements and a selective camera. Mention only what a character could notice under pressure and needs to act on: the loose heel of a shoe, the damp mark where a case stood, the scratched latch on the back room door. Skip throat-clearing and implied explanation; if a fact matters, put it in the movement and let the next action show its consequence. A character may check the corner of a receipt, brush a cuff, or listen for the elevator again without being told that he is afraid.\n\nKeep the prose physical but not overloaded. Use gritty diction where the surface is genuinely worn: chipped paint, gray wash water, a bag that has been mended with wrong-colored thread. Ground rough speech in the person speaking—a debt collector may cut a sentence short, but not every character should swear or riddle. Change rhythm deliberately: a very short clause can break a series of longer observations when someone discovers a forgery or hears a key in a lock. Do not inflate violence to prove stakes; danger resides in credible exposure, loss, and the possibility of being found out.\n\nLet Kawabata's transience show through practical endings rather than poetic ones. After a threat or discovery, the prose can close on the object left behind, the heat going off in a stairwell, or a person turning away from a window. Do not use sentiment to cushion the crime, but allow private feeling to shape what is noticed. A longer thought is permitted when the character must reason through a lie or a timetable. End when the immediate pressure shifts; the case may continue, and the scene may close on a hard fact, an unfinished action, or a sudden quiet, whichever the actual moment supplies.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadbaae::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Yasunari Kawabata / Epistolary / Documents\n\nLet letters, notes, receipts, diary entries, or official reports carry the crime in sequence. Every document must have a clear author, a reason for writing, a recipient or intended purpose, and a time. Do not rotate mechanically through formats; use the document that the scene actually requires—an inventory after a break-in, a short note left on a hall table, a report that omits a detail its writer fears. Gaps between documents are part of the story: the missing letter, the page torn from a ledger, the entry dated after the event.\n\nUse Kawabata's selectivity inside each document. Rather than recounting everything, the writer records one or two exact physical impressions: the color of the light on a receipt, the smell of oil on a stair, the fact that a door did not close at its usual angle. These details become evidence because they are noticed under pressure, but they should not be explained as clues. Keep the emotional charge in omission—what the writer does not mention, or mentions only as a change in routine. A mother's note may say the bus was late and end without naming the visitor who did not stay.\n\nPreserve readable continuity without an omniscient narrator. If a fact is only known later, it cannot appear in an earlier document. Let conflicting accounts suggest motive, timing, or concealment rather than prove it. A final report may reconcile some details, but its language should remain compressed and limited to what its author could legitimately know. End the assembled record where the available documents end. The closing document may be a formal report, a note that breaks off, a routine receipt, or a letter that says less than its writer intended; whatever its form, let its omissions and limits remain part of the record. The documents themselves must remain calm in prose even when the events they record are not.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadbaaf::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Yasunari Kawabata / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nKeep the crime in the story, but focus many sentences on small acts of care that persist around it: someone sliding a plate across the counter so another person can sit, a worn blanket folded over a chair, a cup of weak tea brewed without being asked. Use Kawabata's selective eye to make those acts real rather than generic—notice the particular way a sleeve is rolled, the sound of a kettle just before it clicks off, the fact that a favorite biscuit tin is slightly dented. The warmth is sincere, but it does not remove the established danger: a detective may be briefly at ease in a colleague's kitchen, then notice a charge sheet still on the sideboard.\n\nLet affection exist without requiring a confession. A partner can leave food in the oven, a parent can brush lint from a child's coat before a school meeting, an employee can return a found key without saying why it mattered. Comfort may be awkward or partial. Do not make every character smooth or agreeable; let suspicion, annoyance, and practical worry remain, but show how care happens anyway. The crime can be background tension—an unpaid sum, a photograph someone does not open, a lock that needs replacing—while the scene moves through a manageable, particular moment.\n\nDo not turn the story into a domestic vignette that forgets the crime. After a comfortable exchange, return to a small knowledge: the money is still missing, the visitor may come back, the report is due in two days. The reader should feel the shape of the unresolved offense through a single quiet object or sentence. End at whatever natural pause the scene reaches: hands warmed on a mug, a child asleep in the next room, a pair of shoes set straight by the door, or a comfortable moment interrupted by the sound of a car outside. Warmth and danger coexist; neither is used as a plot trick to cancel the other.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadbaag::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Yasunari Kawabata / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite in composed, socially situated paragraphs that treat the crime as a disturbance in a web of obligations, reputations, and unspoken rules. The narrator may move from a private scene to a street, a workplace, or a public room where the offense is discussed, suspected, or politely avoided. Keep the narrative language distinct from casual speech: sentences can be longer than dialogue, transitions deliberate, and judgment implicit rather than sloganized.\n\nUse Kawabata's selective images as pressure points inside the formal architecture. A long paragraph may open with a civic detail—the number of people waiting outside a registry, the color of the smoke from a laundry—then turn to something small that a character sees: a man's unpolished left shoe, a torn edge of photographs spread on a desk, a bird crossing a courtyard at dusk. Do not explain every connection; let the reader feel that the public event and the private wound belong to the same time. The narrative may register what a character does not say by pausing on an object or an interval.\n\nDistinguish public and private norms without making a moral fable. A business partner may continue to bow correctly or shake hands while a debt is being denied; a household may observe a funeral custom while one member knows who stole from the collection box. Let the ceremony, correspondence, or gathered occasion arise only when it fits the scene. The crime's consequences should be social as well as personal: a name removed from a contract, an invitation withheld, a child sent to stay elsewhere, a shop that closes earlier than usual. End by letting the formal frame hold whatever unresolved disturbance the scene has actually produced—a public performance sustained while a private object remains missing, a social form completed while a private harm goes unspoken, or a gathered occasion that continues around one person's knowledge. Society's order and the private harm sit together without reconciliation.}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
        },
        {
            "role": "system",
            "injection_position": 0,
            "injection_depth": 4,
            "system_prompt": false,
            "marker": false,
            "enabled": true,
            "forbid_overrides": false,
            "injection_order": 100,
            "injection_trigger": [],
            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-crime-07",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Crime · 07/07",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == crime}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Crime. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 7 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-crime; payload preserved. }}{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadbaah::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Yasunari Kawabata / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nMake the telling happen because of the crime: an offense, an injury, a shared silence, or a calculation that has brought characters together and now requires an account. Choose a teller whose stake in the offense is not identical to the audience's, and let that difference shape the narrative: someone may speak to delay, to justify, to test, to warn, or to transfer a burden. Do not turn the story into a neutral report.\n\nEmbed each tale in a specific occasion: a room, a vehicle, a corridor, a waiting interval, a meal, a performance. Use Kawabata's selective physical impressions to bind the listening moment to what is being narrated. A lifted glass, a change in the light, a smell of rain or dust, a piece of clothing touched or set aside can carry the emotional shift between outer scene and recounted event. Keep those impressions brief and genuinely noticed by the teller or a listener; do not decorate every paragraph with landscape.\n\nMake recurrence meaningful rather than merely ceremonial. Return to one concrete object, gesture, phrase, or unfinished event at the outer and inner levels, but let it gather different meaning as the crime becomes more legible: an item moved, a name mispronounced, a routine interrupted, a debt acknowledged too late. The audience can remember this recurrence and use it to understand what the teller has not announced.\n\nDelay revelation through the teller's selection and fear, not through arbitrary interruption. A teller may avoid a moment of confession, repeat a sensory detail to postpone it, describe a peripheral person in order not to describe an act. Let the account's stopping places show a character's actual avoidance, loyalty, shock, or guilt. A listener may interrupt, but the interruption should push the teller toward something more dangerous, not merely postpone the story.\n\nCrime enters the telling through responsibility, knowledge, and consequence. What did this teller see or do? What does the audience need from the account? What would be lost if the tale stops? Keep those questions concrete: an amount of money, a missing person, a locked place, a witness who cannot be protected, an arrangement that has already failed. The narrative levels stay intelligible because each shift moves the characters' practical situation, not because each tale is cut at the same interval.\n\nDo not flatten everyone into ornamental misery. A listener may be eager, afraid, skeptical, compassionate, or entirely preoccupied. A teller may speak with hesitation, formal composure, false brightness, or visible strain. Interiority is especially useful at the joins between levels: show a character noticing that the account has changed direction, or recognizing that an old memory now incriminates someone alive in the outer room. Keep the knowledge appropriate to each mind; the audience may understand more than the teller says, but the narrator should not report what a particular character cannot know.\n\nAn ending need not solve the crime or secure punishment. It can leave the outer and inner stories touching at the point where a choice becomes unavoidable: the teller and listener recognize the shared consequence, the unfinished account alters the present relationship, or the final sensory image exposes what speech has been avoiding. Do not append a formal confession unless the scene has earned it, and do not imply that every crime story must end in capture or atonement.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadbaai::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Yasunari Kawabata / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social-realism route of the German-language tradition: write the crime as it is embedded in work, housing, family obligations, local regulation, and gradual personal change. Do not turn the offense into a puzzle separated from daily life. The story may follow an offense, its preparation, a scheme, an investigation, or the long aftermath, but each development should show characters navigating inherited expectations, limited options, and practical relationships.\n\nKeep Kawabata's selective imagery as the engine of emotional legibility. Instead of political commentary or long social explanation, choose one or two precise material details for each scene: a workplace object, a room's changed condition, a hand's movement, the quality of light at a particular hour. Let those details show how an institution touches the characters. Do not pile up documentary visuals or explain the social system in abstract paragraphs; the pressure of the crime should be felt through selected surfaces and omissions.\n\nMake the narrator's distance match the social-realism route. Use a composed third person that can move between exterior observation and a character's interiority, or a first person with a specific place in the community. Avoid the clipped distance of a police report. Interiority should be ordinary prose, not a private wrapper reserved for emotional peaks; show reasoning, worry, attention, and slow realization as they occur.\n\nConnect the crime to the lifespan of a community, not only to a single incident: an offense can be part of a longer pattern of debt, silence, migration, work, or housing. Let that connection emerge through conversations, repeated places, and remembered events rather than through a narrator's lecture. A character may compare a present arrangement to an earlier one, but the comparison should arise from their actual memory and present need.\n\nKeep cause and consequence legible. When an action follows from an incentive or an obligation, show the thought that connects them. The German-language social-realist tendency toward explanation does not mean every motivation must be labeled; use Kawabata's omission to leave the most painful connection unspoken, but make sure the reader can infer it from earlier concrete details. Do not rely on sudden revelations without preparation, and do not treat a character's psychology as a replacement for the material conditions that shaped the choice.\n\nLet recurring imagery function as a subtle social index. A repeated image—an unwashed window, a particular brand of paper, a courtyard children's game—can mark change, pressure, or an unarticulated family memory. It should not become a symbol announced by the narrator; it should simply reappear when the same practical world intrudes again.\n\nAn ending may show the continuation of ordinary life after a crime: someone returns to work, a family rearranges a room, a debt is partially paid, a person leaves. It need not close with punishment, reconciliation, or full disclosure. Whatever the outcome, ground it in the material and social detail already established. If a discovery or confrontation occurs, it should arise from prior actions and observations rather than supplying an unearned moral verdict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadbaaj::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Yasunari Kawabata / Gothic-Punk\n\nWrite the collision between old authority and present life as a crime story rather than a supernatural one. The offense should grow from inherited claims, entrenched institutions, or personal histories that still control property, access, reputation, or desire. Keep the machinery concrete: a family arrangement, an old debt, a building's rules, a person's recorded past, a hierarchy that outlives its original function. Do not invent vampires, blood bonds, or hidden powers.\n\nUse urban material detail to make the past feel physically present in the modern setting. Choose crowded interiors, worn fixtures, behind-schedule maintenance, glinting new fittings against old stone or concrete. Kawabata's selectivity matters: one unsteady fluorescent tube, one locked drawer, one window that will not close completely, one persistent smell can carry the pressure of an older order better than a long description of decay. Let the atmosphere change only how an encounter is experienced; never let mood substitute for the actual fact of a crime.\n\nGive intimacy a complicated moral weight. People may be drawn together by shared history, need, shame, or protection, and those bonds can coexist with coercion and harm. A character's private desire should not automatically corrupt them; a trusting moment may be genuine even if surrounding arrangements are dangerous. Likewise, a protective or loving act can create a criminal debt or a concealment. Show the particular mix in each relationship instead of invoking a general Gothic pessimism.\n\nLet social masks fall away in trusted company, but not only through confession. A character may remove a literal or figurative mask: stop performing formality, admit fatigue, touch an object they usually avoid, speak in a regional or private register. The crime can intensify that unmasking. Use interiority to show the difference between the public face and the private state, but keep access aligned with the chosen viewpoint; one character's vulnerability is not a warrant to expose everyone else's secrets.\n\nMake authority feel inherited without making it omnipotent. Old power may fail, be circumvented, or depend on modern collaborators. A criminal scheme may exploit the gap between old claims and present law; an investigation may discover that a lineage, title, or tradition confers no legitimate control. Preserve the possibility of real harm, refusal, cooperation, and defeat according to the actual characters and world. No character should be immunized by mood, love, or style, and no old family should silently control everything.\n\nUse Kawabata's compressed attention to create dread through what is noticed but not named. A character may fix on a small change—a door left open, a hand's new tremor, the smell of dust after a locked room is entered—and the reader understands the violation before it is spoken. Do not explain the feeling; let it accumulate through selected images and subsequent practical consequences.\n\nAn ending should show how the collision with inherited power has settled or shifted. It may leave the old order damaged but persisting, a person freed, a room reclaimed, a debt restructured, or an intimacy broken by the same history that created it. Avoid automatic redemption or punishment. Close with a concrete detail that binds the crime's aftermath to the material world already established, such as a newly repaired window, a box of papers burned or sealed, or a person's changed way of entering a familiar room.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadbaak::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Yasunari Kawabata / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in a testimonial or autobiographical mode selected from the Indian tradition. Choose a specific speaker who has participated in, witnessed, or suffered the crime, and let that speaker's place in a community, language, region, and period be concrete. The narrator may be a bystander, victim, accomplice, or participant; the key is that the account comes from a real position with limited knowledge and practical stakes, not from an omniscient summary.\n\nDo not impose a generic Indian family, caste situation, religious framework, or an obligatory bilingual texture. Use only the specific names, places, languages, and habits that the story supplies. If the world is multilingual, let that be a concrete property of particular characters and exchanges; do not invent code-switching where it is not grounded or render every sentence in translation-note form.\n\nLet Kawabata's selective physical impressions make the testimony intimate. The speaker notices certain objects, times, and sensory details because they matter to the crime or to memory: a particular bus, a fan's sound, a kitchen shelf wiped clean, a temple or street corner seen daily, a piece of clothing set aside. Recurring images should be anchored in the speaker's actual routines. Avoid a full inventory of scenery or a documentary catalog; selection is the mark of this method.\n\nThe crime should be consequential rather than atmospheric: show the event in terms of work, housing, migration, debt, reputation, or legal exposure. Because the narrator is retrospective, distinguish between what the speaker knew at the time and what they have come to understand later. This distinction can be stated directly where the narrator is candid, or conveyed through a shift from confusion to recognition. The retrospective voice may be remorseful, matter-of-fact, bitter, protective, or still unsure; do not force a confession of perfect understanding.\n\nUse setting-specific social detail to make the crime legible. A police station, a landlord's office, a family gathering, a workplace dispute, a neighbor's persistence, a religious or community obligation can all carry weight, but they should be shown through the speaker's experience rather than explained as social background. The Indian literary tradition supports a strong social conscience; keep it in the concrete pressures and choices of this story, not in a narrator's general commentary.\n\nLet the first person own the narration. The speaker should have ordinary inconsistency: they may remember too much in one place and too little in another, justify an action and then question it, or use precise concrete observations instead of saying how they feel. The reader can infer feeling from the selection of images, exactly in the Kawabata manner; avoid italicized emotional glosses every few paragraphs unless the speaker is unusually self-aware.\n\nEnd the testimony where the speaker's account of the crime changes what can be done or understood. The final thought need not deliver punishment, forgiveness, or full truth. It may leave the speaker still living with an unaddressed debt, a changed neighborhood, a broken routine, or a last image that says what the testimony has not been able to name. Keep that final image concrete and tied to the earlier world so that it functions as the story's true conclusion rather than an appended moral.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadbaal::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Yasunari Kawabata / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite from the social/psychological realism route of the Japanese literary tradition. The story should show the crime inside ordinary social arrangements: a household, a workplace, a neighborhood, a school, a local business, or a long-term personal bond. The offense need not be spectacular; domestic theft, fraud, a concealed injury, a falsified record, or a betrayal of trust is often stronger here than a dramatic robbery.\n\nUse Kawabata's selective sensory attention to carry psychological depth. Pick one or two physical details per scene that the focal character actually notices: the angle of a fan, a smear on a window, the weight of a kettle, the sound of a gate, the condition of a child's sleeve. Let those details mark emotional change instead of stating it. Do not inventory Japanese scenery or force a seasonal image where none is relevant, but do let natural details indicate passing time when they arise naturally: rain on a veranda, heat in a corridor, early darkness in a kitchen.\n\nKeep the narration flexibly interior. Move between careful exterior description and authorized private experience in the selected narrative person. Preserve the actual observer's interpretive limits without changing the chosen viewpoint. The Japanese literary tradition supports direct feeling, but Kawabata's compression means that feeling often appears through selection and omission. Show a character noticing something uncomfortable and then not naming it, or returning to a small object because the mind cannot yet handle the larger fact.\n\nMake the crime's social penalties concrete. How does the offense change who may visit, who must be told, who can keep working, who can continue living in a particular place? Social realism does not mean everyone responds with shame in the same way; a character may be angry, practical, protective, numb, or relieved. Preserve differences by generation, position, and personal history without turning any group into a caricature.\n\nLet interiority reveal reasoning and motive gradually. The reader should understand why a character chooses concealment, confession, flight, or silence through small decisions and remembered details, not through sudden announcements. When a character remembers an event, indicate whether they are recalling what they knew then or have since learned. Keep evidence and its effects concrete: a missing envelope, an altered entry, a second key, a phone message not received.\n\nDo not import ritualized or theatrical Japanese narrative patterns, early-modern comic routines, or fantastic impositions into this route. The story may be quiet, but it must remain a crime story: the offense has consequences, the viewpoint character has a stake, and the narration follows actual human decisions rather than omens or supernatural agents. If the world itself legitimately includes such elements, use them as part of that world; otherwise they do not enter because of the style.\n\nEnd at a point of sustained psychological or social consequence rather than forced closure. A character may decide to say nothing, to return something, to leave, to lie for someone, or to let a relationship change. The final passage should be a specific moment with Kawabata's compressed imagery—something noticed, set down, or left undone—rather than a summary judgment about Japanese society or human nature.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadbaam::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Yasunari Kawabata / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the intimate psychological or urban fiction route of the Latin American literary tradition. Place the crime in a concrete modern community: a neighborhood, a street, a building, a market, a bus, a workplace. Let the narrator or focal character belong to that place, with its own rhythms, habits, and private speech, rather than observing it from a neutral distance. Preserve the particular language, history, and community the story supplies; do not paste on a generic Latin American setting, code-switching, or political allegory.\n\nUse urban sensory detail as Kawabata would: selectively. Choose a few charged surfaces that carry the crime's emotional pressure—a kept window, a stairwell light, the sound of a fan or a motorbike, a café's worn table, the smell of hot paving or food from a nearby stall. These details should be noticed by the particular point-of-view character because they are entangled with memory or danger. Avoid a camera-like tour of the city; let the place exist through its effect on the mind.\n\nThe intimate route foregrounds a single consciousness. Use close attention within the selected narrative person and viewpoint; the recipe does not choose a different narrator. Show ordinary reasoning, hesitation, fear, desire, and delayed recognition. The crime may be a personal betrayal, a theft, an assault, an unpaid obligation, or a long-kept lie; what matters is that the consequences live inside the character's daily movements. Let the reader understand how a past event returns through a present object or encounter. When the character remembers, distinguish between what they knew then and what they know now; do not retrofit the current realization as the earlier motive.\n\nMake the urban world a field of real consequences. Police, neighbors, employers, family members, landlord, and street life have their own practical interests, and the crime changes the character's relation to them. Show that change through small interactions: a gate that is now watched, a greeting withheld, a route altered, a person waiting too long in a doorway. The pressure of the city can feel intimate because the character cannot escape the location of the offense.\n\nDo not import magical realism unless the world itself already authorizes it. The Latin American tradition includes the fantastic, but this route is realism; a ghost, a premonition, or an impossible event would violate the genre contract unless the setting genuinely includes such things. Even then, treat them as part of the world rather than as stylistic embellishment. The psychological charge here comes from material reality: memory, architecture, sound, and the persistence of an unresolved act.\n\nLet Kawabata's omission deepen the intimacy. The narrative may stop short of naming the most painful thing, leaving the reader to infer it from a sequence of selected images. A character can return to a physical detail because the mind refuses the full understanding: a broken tile, a phone that no longer rings, a chair left empty. Do not explain what the image means; let the recurrence do the work.\n\nAn ending may show the character still inside the consequences of the crime. It can be a morning routine resumed, a place revisited, a decision postponed, or a final observed detail that makes the loss or danger unmistakable. Do not force confession, capture, forgiveness, or a symbolic rebirth. End with a precise urban and personal image that belongs only to this character and this place.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadbaan::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Yasunari Kawabata / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly instead of building atmospheric preamble. Open with the viewpoint character already involved: at a meeting, watching a place, receiving a message, making a decision, or noticing something that should not be there. Make the crime's stakes practical from the first beat—money, evidence, an obligation, a risk to someone present—so the reader does not wait for a long setup to learn why the scene matters.\n\nStage space and movement clearly. Use paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response, and orient the reader whenever the scene changes location: a character moves from a street to a stairwell to a back office; a phone changes the frame; a door opens. Do not turn this into screenplay-style slug lines; keep it readable continuous prose, but ensure the reader always knows where the feet are and what is being handled.\n\nUse Kawabata's selective physical images as reaction beats rather than as decorative pauses. A character's attention should fall on one or two concrete details—a key left on a counter, water running over a dish, the loose thread of a cuff, the heat of a wall at noon—and that detail should carry the emotional shift. A serious reaction may be a pause with a single observed image; an uncertain reaction may be a half-formed thought; an angry reaction may be abrupt physical action. Do not insist that every reaction be witty, quippy, or self-deprecating. Match tone to the specific character.\n\nGive unfamiliar names and any special mechanics clear presentation when they first appear. If a weapon, document, vehicle, or local rule matters to the crime, show it in use or in a character's practical knowledge rather than appending a glossary. The light-novel container allows brisk explanation, but keep it in the viewpoint character's attention: why this document cannot be opened, what this street means to someone, why that phone number is dangerous. Do not pause the story for a worldbuilding lecture unless the character themselves is reasoning through it.\n\nPreserve interiority as ordinary narrative, not only as italicized asides. The viewpoint character can reason, notice, hesitate, remember, lie to themselves, or feel a physical response. Show the gap between what they say and what they think only when the character has a concrete reason—fear, strategy, embarrassment, divided loyalty. Otherwise let speech and thought align naturally. Keep access limited to the viewpoint; other characters' motives appear through action, object, and dialogue.\n\nThe light-novel style does not require first person, a young cast, comedy, or a cliffhanger ending. But it does use strong visual staging and quick emotional legibility. Combine that with Kawabata by making the climax small and sharp: a final image the viewpoint character notices after the action—an object not returned, a phone screen lit, a figure no longer in the doorway—rather than a long reflective paragraph. The crime's aftermath can be implied through that image and the character's next movement.\n\nDo not carry the paragraph-by-paragraph progression quota into the fiction. Write the scene as long as the action and reaction require. If the viewpoint character is waiting, show the waiting itself with real duration and selected perceptions; if they are running, let the movement be quick and spatially clear. Stop when the immediate situation reaches a natural boundary or the next action belongs to a new turn. The story does not need to close, confess, or reverse direction each time.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadbaao::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Yasunari Kawabata / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite the crime as a disturbance entering ordinary perception through a small set of recurring sensory images. Choose one or two concrete refrains early—a damp cuff, a lamp left burning, a cracked dish—and let each return at a moment when its meaning changes. The first appearance may be neutral; the second may accompany a discrepancy; the third may carry the consequence. This recurrence, not explanation, makes the offense's lingering intrusion felt.\n\nLet syntax follow the focal character's attention. Use a short declarative clause when a detail interrupts a routine, then allow a longer sentence to unfold the association or dread that follows. Keep the associations traceable to the character's actual experience. If a comparison recurs, let it alter with events: a pale light that first seemed like dawn may later recall a hospital corridor. The comparison should change what the character notices in the present room, not decorate it.\n\nDo not explain the entire mechanism unless the chosen viewpoint would know it. A single sensory detail can stand for what happened—the smell of camphor in a hallway, the weight of a key in a pocket, a smear on a window. The reader may know who acted while the focal character still reads the traces. Let ordinary moments carry the dread without announcing it: a tray set down too carefully, a phone that rings once, a door that does not quite close. The crime's stakes reside in what cannot be kept—a habit, a trust, a particular hour. End within a moment still in progress, when a gesture or unread note holds the aftermath without insisting that the character understands it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadbaap::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Yasunari Kawabata / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the offense enter through the small irreversible differences it has left in familiar things. Choose one concrete object or routine—a chair moved two inches from the wall, a neighbor's window unlit at the usual hour, the absence of a particular sound from a courtyard—and let it carry the aftermath. Do not report the crime as an incident; show its residue in the texture of a room, a street, or a daily habit.\n\nWhen the focal character remembers an earlier scene, place it beside the present object so the gap does the reflective work. A laugh, a shared meal, a harmless habit can return not as nostalgia but as a measure of what is no longer available. The reflection may be direct or carried entirely by the detail: a name now avoided in conversation, a shopping list left behind, a plant that continues to grow in a house under dispute. Ordinary pleasure may remain pleasurable; the style does not require every scene to end with announced sadness. A character can still enjoy tea or a morning walk while knowing something is over.\n\nLet the crime's mechanism be as visible as the chosen viewpoint permits. Instead of withholding how it was done, show what no longer works: a lock that still turns but no longer protects, a telephone number that rings in an empty flat. Use direct reflection sparingly; often a single concrete detail—a folded receipt, a piece of string tied in a particular way—will carry the association. Keep chronological sequence loose, so the present moment becomes the place where past and offense meet.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadbaaq::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Yasunari Kawabata / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse framed oral storytelling with a situated teller. The narrator recounts the offense to a listener after the fact, so the crime enters as a story within a story. Keep the outer frame brief but active: a cup cools, a door opens, a child is sent away. The teller should not be omniscient; only what they saw, heard, or believe can enter the tale. The listener's reactions—a frown, a refilled glass, a sudden interest in the street—shape what the teller chooses to include.\n\nInside the frame, render the crime through a few compressed sensory moments in the Kawabata manner. A particular sound, a piece of cloth, the way a man placed his keys on the table. Use direct, unadorned clauses for the deed. Let the teller pause, correct a detail, or admit they no longer know which version to trust. This uncertainty is not a blanket refusal to explain; it is the teller's actual limited knowledge or changing memory. The aftermath becomes the conflicting accounts, not a restored sequence.\n\nDo not make the frame a moralizing wrapper or multiply tales for their own sake. One embedded telling is enough. The outer frame should return at the end: the teller rises, the visitor leaves, and the reader understands that the tale will be told again with another small alteration. The crime's consequence lives in that retelling—in what is kept, omitted, or changed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadbaar::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Yasunari Kawabata / Minimalist Prose\n\nWrite the crime through a small number of exact details. One detail can be the entire physical evidence: a button on the floor, a receipt with the wrong date, a stain on a sleeve. Do not add secondary clues merely to decorate the scene. Each selected item should either change a character's understanding or confirm a consequence. Use direct clauses, mostly subject-verb-object. Let omission carry the weight: the reader should be able to infer what happened without the narrator stating it.\n\nKeep the causal chain available but unadorned. If a character realizes who acted, show the single fact that triggered the realization. The thought can be long enough to make sense, but avoid restating the realization twice. The same applies to motive: one observed habit, one half-sentence in a remembered conversation may be enough. Do not delete the reason for an action; place it in the same paragraph as the action.\n\nUse short paragraphs and a restrained rhythm, but vary sentence length when the moment changes. A longer sentence after several clipped ones can signal a shift in attention, not a stylistic flourish. The account can end at any moment of change or suspension; the offense need not be fully sorted. What matters is that the reader can see the next likely consequence in the space left open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadbaas::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Yasunari Kawabata / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the offense as a scene among other scenes, not as a case file. Open inside an ordinary action—waiting for a delivery, clearing a table, locking a shop—and let the crime intrude through a concrete detail: a message on a phone, a stranger's question, an object out of place. Use sustained, scene-first prose with natural movement among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized interiority. The narrator's access follows the chosen perspective; do not enter a mind the focal character cannot see.\n\nKeep Kawabata's compression active within the larger structure. Instead of long environmental descriptions, choose one or two sensory details per scene, and let the adjacent paragraphs do the connecting work. A character may notice the smell of rain on a coat just before realizing whose coat it is, but the operation is general: the selected detail should be the thing that triggers or accompanies a shift in understanding. The realization should arise from the detail without an editorial sentence announcing its meaning. Dialogue can remain unglossed when the exchange itself carries the information, but the focal character's private reasoning should be available when it matters.\n\nThe crime's aftermath can span several scenes without being recapped. Use concrete shifts—a changed routine, a locked drawer, a word no longer used—to show consequences. End at any point of active suspension or change; no solution or closure is required. The scene continues beyond the reply, and the reader should know what has newly become possible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadbaat::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Yasunari Kawabata / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship the lens through which the crime is experienced. Let the supplied premise become legible through scene selection: the way they pass a cup, the nickname used only when afraid, the memory that surfaces during a police interview. Use callbacks to earlier private moments; a phrase or object from their past should return when the crime changes them. The callback's new significance does the emotional work, not an editorial explanation of how they have changed.\n\nUse Kawabata's compression for intimacy: one touch, one remembered scent, one detail of a room they once shared. When one partner lies, show the specific reason they lie—protectiveness, fear, habit—not generalized deception. The other may suspect, confront, or remain unaware; the private reasoning must be available under the active point of view. Do not insert a roster of familiar tropes beyond the actual supplied premise.\n\nGive the emotional consequences enough room. A discovery can be followed by a scene of ordinary comfort or rupture. The crime's mechanism need not be fully solved. What matters is how the relationship absorbs it: a promise repeated, a letter left in a pocket, a door closed more softly than before. The response can end while the couple is still speaking or when one of them realizes something they cannot say yet.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadbaau::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Yasunari Kawabata / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the crime as an account that will be remembered, in a voice aware of retelling. Open with a compact declaration that establishes the deed as already part of local memory, then give the particulars plainly. Use concrete local details sparingly, but let each one carry the weight of a deed: the bolt-cutter left on a fence, the coat hung on the wrong hook, the note folded into quarters. The teller may be a participant, a witness, or one who heard it later, but the access must stay consistent.\n\nUse recurring phrasing only when a repeated act or title earns it. A phrase such as \"the black ledger\" or \"the one who waited\" should return at a moment that reframes the act, not as an ornamental epithet at every mention. Let the story move from the offense through its consequences for specific named people. Consequences may include a quarrel, a promise, a journey, a loss of standing. Do not resolve every thread; the account may end with a deed still unreckoned, already being shaped for future telling.\n\nKeep the language restrained, not ceremonial. Kawabata's compression is useful here: a single sensory image—a frost-covered step, a lamp in an unshuttered window—can mark a turning point. The saga tone comes from the relation between act and memory, not from archaic syntax. Ordinary actions, such as sharing a meal or mending a lock, can continue amid the offense; the crime does not suspend daily life. End with the telling continuing, as if another listener has just arrived.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadbaav::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Yasunari Kawabata / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social-realist route of Scandinavian literary tradition: place the offense, scheme, or criminal consequence inside the specific social and institutional world the story supplies, whether that is a workplace, family, neighborhood, or public agency. Do not treat these settings as interchangeable scenery. Let the institution's actual procedures, relationships, and pressures shape how the crime is prepared, discovered, or survived. A housing inspection, a workplace shift, a family meal, or a public office procedure can carry the scene, but the telling should follow the people moving through it rather than explain the bureaucracy.\n\nApply Kawabata's selective attention as the narrative method. Choose a few exact surfaces—a handled object, a window's light, a worn coat, a half-finished task—and let them carry the emotional weight of changed circumstances or accumulating strain. A detail should do its work through placement and recurrence, not through a narrator's explanation of what it means. When a familiar object appears again, let its altered significance be earned by what the story has shown between appearances: the coat is still hanging by the door, but now the person who wore it is gone. Keep the intervals of waiting, routine, and private recognition as part of the crime's human cost, not as padding between plot points.\n\nGive each character distinct inner life under the active contract: a witness's uncertainty, an investigator's tired focus, an accomplice's divided loyalty, a victim's aftermath. Private thought and speech may differ for supported reasons—embarrassment, attention, utility, relationship, uncertainty, concealment, habit—but access and knowledge remain separate. What one person knows cannot be assumed by another simply because the narrative has shown it. Do not make every pause a confession or every familiar object a clue.\n\nKeep Danish, Norwegian, and Swedish contexts distinct when relevant to the supplied setting. Do not turn the setting into a generic Nordic landscape of reserve and snow. The social-realist method supports aftermath and consequence rather than a mandatory courtroom or punishment. The narrative may close on an unresolved custody arrangement, a repaired but altered routine, a formal notice, or a person continuing ordinary life beside a private injury. Lyric compression is a method, not an absence of plot; the offense and its consequences must remain legible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadbaaw::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Yasunari Kawabata / Screenplay\n\nWrite the offense, its preparation, aftermath, or pursuit in concise scene blocks. Use scene headings only when location or time changes; otherwise let action lines and dialogue carry the sequence. Apply Kawabata's selectivity as a structural principle: choose a few exact visual or sonic details—a turned key, a wet coat dripping on linoleum, a drawer left open, a phone ringing in another room—and let them carry the emotional weight that explanation would slow down. A detail earns its recurrence by what has changed between appearances; the same drawer, seen again, may now be closed.\n\nLet intervals and repeated ordinary actions function as scene content, not as padding between plot points. A character waits for a bus, folds laundry, checks a locked door, or watches a light change across a window. These moments are part of the crime's human weight: they show strain, loyalty, or change through what the character does with time. Select the moments that matter rather than listing every step. The script may move from preparation to consequence without a montage or montage-like list.\n\nWhere it fits the established speaker and occasion, dialogue should be economical but real: people speak in partial sentences, avoid a topic, answer a different question, or resume a practical task. Do not use dialogue to state every private motive or turn every pause into a meaningful beat. Subtext may be present without being announced. Let what a character does not say carry as much weight as what they do say.\n\nPrivate thought and feeling belong to the authorized viewpoint character and may be included as clearly attributed nonspoken notes set apart from action and dialogue—for example, a line beginning with the character's name and a colon, bracketed or italicized, to distinguish it from stage direction or speech. Such notes are not heard by other characters and do not become knowledge available to the cast. Voice-over should be used only if the selected sequence genuinely needs it; otherwise the attributed note is the correct container.\n\nDo not insert camera directions such as CLOSE ON, PAN, or ANGLE ON. Actions and visible events should be evident from the text. Offscreen sound may be noted when it matters: a door shutting, a cry, a siren. The script's tension can come from exposure, divided loyalty, or a missed connection shown through behavior rather than through explanatory dialogue.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadbaax::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Yasunari Kawabata / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable scene focused on an offense, its pressures, or its aftermath. Keep the cast occupied with concrete stage business: entering with a bag, hanging a coat, checking a lock, setting a table, sorting papers. Apply Kawabata's selectivity to the props and actions themselves. Choose a few objects that will carry the emotional weight of the scene—a coat that stays on its hook, a lock checked twice, a cup set down without being drunk from—and let them recur with changed significance. The relationship between lines and business exposes loyalty, suspicion, exhaustion, or concealment without a descriptive narrative paragraph.\n\nDialogue should be compressed and sometimes indirect. A character may answer a question with a practical remark, leave a sentence unfinished, or repeat a simple phrase with changed weight. Do not make every line epigrammatic or every silence portentous. The crime's tension can reside in what people avoid saying or in the ordinary tasks they continue.\n\nUse stage directions sparingly but precisely: entrances, exits, an object's placement, a pause. They should be stageable, not cinematic. Avoid camera commands like 'close-up' or 'cut to'. If an offstage sound matters—a knock, a kettle, a door—include it as a sound cue.\n\nPrivate thought and feeling are part of the active script where required, but they must not be converted into public asides or monologues unless the scene's dramatic purpose specifically calls for a character to perform private feeling. Instead, include hidden nonspoken material in the script, clearly attributed to the character and separated from stage directions and dialogue—for example, inside brackets or a clearly marked note. Such material is not heard by other characters and does not become knowledge available to the cast. Use it to show what the character notices or withholds: a familiar smell, a memory stirred by a prop, an unspoken calculation. The audience sees only the visible business; the reader of the script receives the inner life.\n\nLet the scene end on a completed action, an interruption, or a plain unresolved moment. No obligatory confrontation, confession, or moral summary is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadbaay::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Yasunari Kawabata / Stream of Consciousness\n\nAllow a single authorized viewpoint to carry the offense or its consequences through associative movement. The mind moves among immediate perception, memory, bodily sensation, and an unspoken calculation or dread. Apply Kawabata's selectivity as the governing principle: choose which details rise into attention based on what is passing or difficult to keep—a streetlamp's reflection, the texture of a receipt, a child's shoe left by the door. A detail opens a memory or a moment from the crime without the narrative stopping to explain the connection. The association itself carries the weight; the reader feels the link through the movement from one perception to the next.\n\nKeep the stream fluid but not disorienting. Distinguish what is happening now from what is remembered, imagined, or feared by precise sensory anchors: a present action, a repeated phrase, a physical sensation that belongs to the immediate scene. The syntax may fragment under stress but should not make it impossible for the reader to follow who is where and what is real in the scene.\n\nStay inside one character's mind per continuous passage. Do not grant the narrator another character's unspoken thoughts. Other people appear through what the viewpoint notices: a change in their breathing, the way they set down a cup, a hesitation before a question. These observations carry suspicion or pity without becoming omniscient knowledge.\n\nLet recurring images return with altered significance, but do not treat them as proof or automatic symbols. A coat still hanging by the door, an unanswered message's glow, the smell of rain on a borrowed jacket—each recurrence earns its new meaning from what the story has shown between appearances. The selectivity carries the crime's weight without explanatory commentary.\n\nOrdinary reasoning alone is not enough; the prose must actually follow associative movement. However, the crime's facts remain legible, and a later realization cannot be retroactively inserted as an earlier motive. The passage may break off on a perception, a memory, or an unfinished thought without requiring a completed decision or a new piece of evidence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadbaaz::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Yasunari Kawabata / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the offense, its pressure, or its aftermath through the actual digital exchange: messages sent, received, left on read, edited, or not sent. Let each person's habits show in their typing—abbreviations, full stops, long silences, rapid replies, questions deflected into practical arrangements. Do not make everyone sound alike or turn every message into a polished fragment of interiority.\n\nTreat the screen as a surface for Kawabata's selective detail. Choose a few exact physical things that a sender describes—a gate left open, a stain on a sleeve, the light in a hallway—and let them carry the emotional weight that longer explanation would slow down. A detail earns its recurrence by what has changed between appearances: the same gate, mentioned again days later, may now be closed. Omissions matter as much as inclusions, but the reader must not need to guess the basic crime or relationship. The exchange should reveal enough through timing, wording, and what people choose to ask or avoid.\n\nDistinguish clearly among sent messages, unsent drafts, and private thought. If an unsent draft appears, show it as a composed artifact: typed, revised, then deleted or left in the text field; it is not automatically the character's live inner experience. Private thought may be included as clearly separated context or hidden interiority under the active contract, but it is not transmitted text and does not become known to the other participants.\n\nUse timestamps, delivery receipts, typing indicators, or read statuses only when they change the meaning of the exchange: a long delay before a reply, a message seen but unanswered, a file sent after the conversation has moved on. Do not invent interface events merely to create subtext. Background context—location, time, a brief scene outside the screen—may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript by formatting or a line break, but the story's core must remain legible through the messages themselves.\n\nThe tension can come from exposure, divided loyalty, or a request that someone cannot safely answer. A chat may end on a pending question, a read receipt, or a final message that changes what an earlier line meant—without requiring a confession or a cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadbaba::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Yasunari Kawabata / Visual Novel\n\nWrite scene-oriented blocks with concise setting lines, clear speaker attribution, and focal narration or inner response. Apply Kawabata's selectivity to the scene details themselves. Choose a few exact cues—the room's light, an object left from earlier, a sound from the street, a face's particular expression—and let them carry the emotional weight of the moment. A cue sets the scene; it does not authorize a mechanic or become automatic proof of guilt or truth.\n\nLet the focal character's private experience appear in the narrative passages or hidden interiority, clearly separate from spoken dialogue. Other characters do not know what the focal character thinks unless it is shown or said. Do not turn every background cue into a visible tell; a changed expression may draw attention, but the crime's consequences, loyalty, and pressure must be carried by behavior, timing, and what is said or done.\n\nScene changes should be legible: a new heading, a clear break, or a brief location note. When revisiting a place, select a familiar image—a coat still on the hook, a letter moved, a window now open—and let its altered significance be earned by what the story has shown between visits. The coat on the hook means something different after the person who wore it has left. Repetition deepens rather than restarts suspicion.\n\nThe visual novel style does not require branching menus, player choices, romance, or executable engine commands. If a choice-like moment appears, portray it as a character's decision within the prose, not as a prompt. The narrative may show a suspect leaving, a witness refusing to answer, or a victim resuming routine work; it need not resolve every question or deliver a punishment.\n\nDialogue should be natural and economical. People can answer a question with a practical remark, ignore a suggestion, or return to a task. The crime's aftermath can settle into ordinary life with an unresolved injury; the scene may end on a private thought, a completed action, or a pause in the conversation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPadbabb::♢ * [STYLE] Crime / Yasunari Kawabata / Web Serial\n\nWrite each installment as part of an ongoing crime story: an offense in motion, its pressures, or its aftermath. Keep the through-line clear by briefly recovering only what the current scene needs—a name, a relation, a prior action, a location—rather than summarizing everything. The reader should be able to re-enter without a full recap.\n\nUse Kawabata's selective images as continuity devices. Choose a few details from an earlier installment—a borrowed jacket, a chipped cup, the sound of a particular door—and let them recur with changed significance. The connection is felt through placement and recurrence, not explained at length. A detail earns its new meaning from what has happened between appearances: the borrowed jacket means something different after the person who lent it has been questioned. Repeated details acquire meaning gradually; they are not symbolic foreshadowing forced on every appearance.\n\nProgress can come through relationships, work, practical resources, or a shift in what a character is willing to know or say. It does not require a clue, a reversal, or a completed test each installment. Earn developments from what has already been shown: a decision made after days of small events, a person's patience worn down by repeated encounters, a household change that follows the crime's economic pressure.\n\nKeep the installment focused. It may cover one evening, one conversation, or one errand, and end on a completed action, a pending request, or a plain acknowledgment of what has changed. An occasional hook is useful, but do not manufacture a cliffhanger every time. The crime's tension can reside in exposure, divided loyalty, or the slow adjustment of ordinary life.\n\nThe serial style does not require ranking systems, character sheets, or explicit progression mechanics. Do not add web-novel genre rules beyond what the story already contains. Private thought and knowledge remain separate: a character may realize something the reader sees, and that realization may not be shared with others until the next installment, if it is shared at all.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
        },
