            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-cyberpunk_noir-01",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Cyberpunk Noir · 01/07",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == cyberpunk_noir}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Cyberpunk Noir. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 1 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-cyberpunk_noir; payload preserved. }}{{// Recipe Library: Cyberpunk Noir }}\n{{setvar::NPaeaaaa::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Akira (identity unresolved) / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite emotional exposure as a cyberpunk noir transaction under Akira's fractured perception. When a character admits need, apologizes, or withholds, render the immediate relational cost through the scene's actual systems: a confession made in a rented room with a listening device visible above the door, an apology sent through a channel that shows read receipts and billing increments, a withheld need that becomes a debt the character watches accumulate. Do not announce that the exchange is observed or priced; let the observation or price interrupt the feeling itself.\n\nUse close focal attention within the selected narrative person and access permissions. Render turbulent thought as jagged and associative, with private reasoning cut by intrusive fragments: a half-remembered phrase that overlays the other person's voice, a flash of a face from an earlier failure, a sudden awareness that the room's camera has shifted angle. When the character tries to speak, let the interruption reach the sentence itself—a repeated word, a stalled clause, a question answered as though it were the one asked three turns earlier. Show the other person's reaction to that fracture without granting access to their mind.\n\nLet pain and warmth occupy the same gesture. A hand placed near an implant site can be both careful and too late; a shared meal can be an apology and a reminder of what the meal cost; a moment of ease can sharpen the knowledge that the character has not said the thing that matters. Do not resolve the turbulence into a confession or a repaired relationship. An admission may remain partial, misdirected, or swallowed after the first word, and the scene may end while the strain is still moving through the character's body and speech. Subsequent behavior carries the cost: checking the channel for a reply that has not come, replaying the moment against a window's reflection, or leaving a message that says less than the character meant and more than they intended.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaaab::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Akira (identity unresolved) / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite each reply as a coherent shared interval in readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution. Open with the immediate scene context—who is present, what channel or location they occupy, and what practical pressure is active—then move through the exchange without pausing to explain the world. Cyberpunk noir's pressure appears through interaction: a character checks a balance before answering, a door unlocks only after a second scan, an NPC repeats a question because their feed lagged.\n\nRender Akira's interrupted perception as brief concrete interruptions placed inside the narrative flow, never as a separate explanatory block. In the focal character's paragraph, a screen flicker can cut a sentence short, a half-heard word can reorder the next line of dialogue, or an intrusive memory can surface as a single sharp image before the scene continues. Keep each glitch immediate and unresolved. In dialogue, let noise or attention fragment speech through the actual words: a repeated syllable, a question answered too slowly, a reply that starts with the wrong name. Do not narrate the glitch as a symptom or have another character diagnose it.\n\nKeep NPC actions and world behavior active without turning every NPC into an observer of the focal character's interior state. An NPC may notice a pause or a wrong answer, but they respond from their own practical concerns—the job, the payment, the open channel—not from hidden knowledge. Preserve each character's separate knowledge: a player may know the private thought written for their character, but must not assume another character recognizes it. End at a natural continuation point that follows from the scene's momentum, not a forced question or cliffhanger. The pause may occur mid-action or mid-thought; leave enough concrete material—an open channel, an unpaid debt, a half-made promise—for the next turn to continue without needing to resolve the interruption.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaaac::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Akira (identity unresolved) / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the classical/vernacular episodic route. Structure each passage as a clearly bounded episode within a continuing sequence: an encounter, a bargain, a delivery, a surveillance-check, or an account of a debt. Open by establishing who is present, what has been asked or owed, and what practical matter is at stake. Close with a settled moment or a forward-moving consequence, not a manufactured cliffhanger.\n\nKeep focal attention close within the selected narrative person and access permissions. Within each episode, let Akira's unstable perception appear as abrupt shifts in the narration itself: a remembered phrase that overlays the present speaker's words, a suddenly meaningless visual detail that the narration lingers on for one sentence, a clause that repeats or breaks off before the action completes. Mark these shifts by changing the sentence's object or focus without explaining them—the narration simply turns from the person speaking to a stain on the wall, then returns. Do not label the shift as a symptom or grant other characters knowledge of it.\n\nUse repetition across episodes as a structural cue. A repeated phrase, place, or debt can connect scenes; when it returns, its meaning changes only because a new fact in the episode supports that change. For example, a corridor described in the first episode as a shortcut may reappear in a later episode as the route a creditor uses, and the narration shows the difference through the character's altered attention to its locked doors. Keep the telling concrete and paratactic: noir's corporate language, street knowledge, surveillance, and physical compromise belong to the episode's setting and events, not to abstract commentary. Let the world's systemic pressure reduce personal choice to difficult options, but allow characters to act within those limits. Do not transplant a period, cast, or community; the tradition supplies episodic pacing and direct narration, not an imperial setting or named historical figures.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaaad::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Akira (identity unresolved) / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nUse short, concrete sentences and hard cuts. Build each beat from material detail: a chipped lens, a dead channel, a card with insufficient credit, a door that only opens after a biometric check. Cyberpunk noir's pressure appears in what people must do to keep access, get paid, or avoid being logged. Do not describe the system abstractly; show a fee, a lockout, a blacklisted name.\n\nRender Akira's interrupted perception through abrupt shifts in the prose. A sentence stops mid-thought, a sensory detail intrudes without explanation, or the narration jumps from the present action to a single sharp image from elsewhere. Keep the interruption physical and immediate—a muscle twitch, a screen flicker, a sudden silence—not a narrated diagnosis. The intrusion may be a face that is not there, a phrase repeated over ambient noise, or a familiar texture the character did not touch; choose whichever concrete fragment the scene's actual setting supplies. Allow occasional longer passages only when the character must work through a plan or a dangerous inference; otherwise compress.\n\nSpeech is affected by noise and attention. Dialogue can be clipped, interrupted, or delayed; a character may answer the wrong question because a word snagged their focus. Ground abrasive language in the speaker's stress and the scene's power relation, not as universal noise. Use changes of rhythm deliberately: a short burst of action, then a single slower sentence when the cost lands. The end may stop at a physical or financial consequence, not a forced remorse or cliffhanger. Preserve each character's knowledge; a glitch is private unless the narration's contract explicitly shares it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaaae::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Akira (identity unresolved) / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCompose each segment as a specific document with an author, recipient or intended audience, time, and purpose. Use forms that fit the cyberpunk setting: internal reports, credit notices, surveillance logs, dead-drop messages, repair requests, or private journals encrypted on a local device. The document's format and permissions determine what can be stated; a report to a supervisor omits unapproved observations, while a journal may include them but still be written for an imagined reader.\n\nLet Akira's interrupted perception appear as document-level distortion. A message breaks off and resumes with a different subject, a timestamp is duplicated, a phrase repeats across entries, or a description contradicts an earlier report without acknowledgment. The document's author may not know the contradiction. Do not explain these disruptions in an omniscient aside; let the reader compare entries and notice the break, the repeated phrase, or the missing acknowledgment as a material trace in the record itself.\n\nConvey noir's pressure through the documents' existence and content: a payment demand becomes a mood-setting deadline; a surveillance log shows what the observer chose to record; a repair request reveals bodily compromise through warranty language. Each document has a practical reason to be written, not a need to narrate the entire story. Preserve continuity by allowing gaps between entries and by making conflicts discoverable through concrete details—revised numbers, changed locations, missing acknowledgments—rather than through a narrator's conclusion. A segment may end while the available documents still leave the truth unsettled; the unresolved state belongs to the record's limits, not to an authorial refusal to continue.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaaaf::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Akira (identity unresolved) / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite comfort as small, specific acts grounded in the characters' familiar preferences and the cyberpunk setting's material conditions. Choose one or two concrete details per scene—a carefully saved meal, a preferred setting on a device, a seat saved, a shoulder touched where the implant does not hurt—and let the narration spend time on the act itself: how the food is divided, how the setting is remembered, how the touch is placed. The kindness remains sincere even when the space is observed, the gesture costs something, or the surrounding system continues to press.\n\nRender Akira's unstable perception so that comfort feels partial without becoming false. A caring phrase may arrive with a glitch—a missed word, an intrusive memory, a flash of fear—and the focal character may respond awkwardly or not fully receive the warmth. Show both the affectionate act and the moment it slips: a hand that reaches, then hesitates; a gift given while the giver's attention fractures; a shared joke that lands one beat late. Do not narrate the slip as a symptom or have the other character diagnose it.\n\nLet affection exist alongside disagreement and privacy. A character can be helped without becoming agreeable, and a caring act need not be announced. Keep each person's knowledge separate; one character may not know how much the other noticed. The passage may end in a quiet moment of ease or a lingering unspoken strain, but it should not manufacture a crisis or erase the noir pressures that continue outside.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaaag::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Akira (identity unresolved) / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite in composed, continuous prose with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraphs. Use a limited third-person narrator close enough to the focal character to render their perception, but distant enough to frame each scene through its social and economic context: a district's history of debt, a corporation's redevelopment logic, the quiet surveillance that makes a private conversation vulnerable. The language should remain more measured than ordinary speech, with sentences that build across a paragraph rather than stopping at each beat.\n\nIntegrate Akira's interrupted perception as a formal device that breaks the smooth narrative surface at key moments. Within the composed prose, let a clause repeat unexpectedly, a sensory detail intrude from a different time, or a sentence turn on an association the character did not intend. The narration lingers on the wrong detail with a strange precision—the texture of a wall, the delay before a door unlocks—then resumes its measured movement. Do not explain these disruptions as illness; let them create a pattern the reader can recognize across scenes.\n\nUse paragraph movement to express noir's pressures. A long paragraph may compress hours of watchful waiting, its clauses accumulating the small signs of surveillance and the cost of remaining still. A short paragraph can isolate an act of refusal or a sudden cost, breaking the rhythm so the consequence lands alone. Frame public and private contrast through setting and social judgment—how a character is seen by a clerk, a supervisor, or a neighbor—but keep the interpretation grounded in the scene's actual conditions, not a generic condemnation. The passage may end on a completed event or an unresolved pause, but the writing itself should feel architecturally whole.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaaah::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Akira (identity unresolved) / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the story as an occasion of telling: the narrator is speaking, recording, streaming, or performing for an audience with a specific reason to listen, and the outer scene keeps pressing on that occasion. The pressure runs through the telling itself—access, debt, surveillance, signal quality, or a corporate interest in the account can shape what gets told, how long the audience stays, and what the teller dares to include. When the narrator begins an embedded story, open it as a story with its own characters and setting, not a sudden omniscient aside, and return to the outer audience at natural points without explaining the frame.\n\nThe telling is never a clean transmission. Intrusive thoughts interrupt description, attention is pulled toward noise or a half-seen detail, speech breaks or repeats under pressure, and one level's sensory language bleeds into the next—the hum of an implant coloring a remembered room, a phrase from the embedded tale surfacing in the outer exchange. An embedded story may persuade, warn, entertain, delay, or change how the audience reads the outer scene, but its claims stay as uncertain as the teller's perception: what is offered as a lead may be a misattributed face, a borrowed phrase, or a detail half-invented to hold the room's attention.\n\nInside the embedded account, the teller can describe another person's visible behavior and their own remembered experience, but cannot report that person's private mind unless the outer frame establishes a separate source or a later reconstruction. A memory split by noise or failing attention can be delivered piecemeal, and a repeated phrase or object accrues meaning as the audience re-hears the same wording in a new context. Do not force a moral, a reveal, or a cliffhanger between levels: the framed story may fail, remain unfinished, or be rejected, and the outer characters act on the partial version they received.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaaai::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Akira (identity unresolved) / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the modernist, absurdist, or satirical route of the German-language tradition, and carry that route through syntax and narrative distance rather than subject matter alone. Let sentence order bend where thought is invaded by an interface prompt, a jingle, a public announcement, or the narrator's own halting attempt to anticipate what another character wants. When the prose fragments, make the break traceable to an actual disruption in the scene—an interrupted transmission, a forced body scan, a sudden memory, the pressure to speak before a decision has been reached—rather than using fragmentation as a standing mood.\n\nUse the satirical or absurdist register to expose the systems that run the world—debt, ranking, access, monitoring, corporate care—but keep the satire grounded in one person's experience. A bureaucratic form can be filled in with degrading assumptions; a help desk can loop through the same promise while a physical need worsens; a public advertisement can speak directly to the narrator's private hesitation. Unstable interpretation may turn an ordinary transaction into something allegorical, while the outer facts remain real and still have consequences for characters who cannot afford to read them as satire.\n\nLet sentence rhythm reflect a translated or multilingual pattern only when the narrator's actual language supports it, and do not fabricate a national idiom or equate the tradition with one country, bureaucracy, or severity. Interiority is a composed account of disrupted attention, not a transcript of error: a sudden shift into second person, a parenthetical accusation, a repeated bureaucratic phrase can carry the intrusion, and the sentences that follow can reassert causal connection between perception, system, and private response. Let the critique emerge from how a system handles one particular body and decision, without making every institution omnipotent or every human response futile.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaaaj::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Akira (identity unresolved) / Gothic-Punk\n\nGround every scene in the collision between an old authority—a family arrangement, a corporate founder's legacy, a debt with a history, a hierarchy built into the city—and a present life that must navigate it. Make the atmosphere personal rather than scenic: a ritual that still carries administrative force, a private space contaminated by surveillance, a gesture of intimacy that also records a debt. Let the urban environment show its age and ownership in practical detail—which doors are sealed, what a monitor displays, who can be touched without a transaction.\n\nWhen the old authority presses, render it through intrusive thought and compromised perception rather than elegant symbolism: the narrator may repeat an inherited phrase without intending to, confuse a current face with a memory, or lose track of what was said aloud while a collector's voice or a family warning replays. Treat these intrusions as painful and causal, part of how inherited power actually reaches a body and a decision. Explain how the legacy still bites in the present—through access, records, loyalty, or fear—and let that power reshape private experience without acquiring supernatural agency.\n\nKeep private desire and morally complicated intimacy present. Social masks fall only where the narrator has reason to trust another person, and even then the mask may slip unevenly. Do not import vampire physiology, clan powers, or blood-bond mechanics; the atmosphere comes from inherited control meeting technological dependence. An encounter's effect may be paranoia, relief, interrupted memory, or a clearer but still unhappy understanding—a trusted moment is not automatically a trap, and intimacy is not automatically a confession.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaaak::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Akira (identity unresolved) / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the oral, epic, and frame-tale route of the Indian literary traditions, and make the telling inherit a communal or ritual function rather than a simple public reading. The narrator may be answering a demand for an account, preserving a claim before a household or neighborhood, or retelling an older story to manage a present debt. Open with the occasion and the audience, and begin an embedded tale as an actual story told to them inside the same world, not an abstract flashback. Use repetition, naming, and direct address to the listeners where the chosen teller and language would support them.\n\nThe nested tale is unreliable in perception, not necessarily in loyalty. The teller may remember a lineage correctly but misattribute a face, repeat a family phrase while losing its original context, or interrupt the story because an implant alert or street noise pulls their attention; those interruptions become part of the performance and change what the audience understands. The teller can report remembered experience but only infer another person's private mind, so embedded characters keep their interiority behind what is said and seen.\n\nKeep the cyberpunk pressure concrete—a community debt, a surveillance feud, a corporate buyout can be what the telling is for. Specify language, region, community, and period only as the supplied story provides them; do not attach a generic family structure, caste experience, or religious practice to a character who has none. Let the story conclude or break off for a reason belonging to the teller and the audience rather than a compulsory moral, and let a repeated phrase or object gather meaning across the frame levels.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaaal::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Akira (identity unresolved) / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the modern experimental inward fiction route, and structure the scene around the narrator's effort to hold a continuous self inside events that keep interrupting it. The prose can move between brief sensory notation, remembered phrases, and second- or third-person self-address, but each shift follows an actual break in perception—an implant ping, a passing advertisement, a mechanical voice, the need to answer someone before a thought is complete. Keep the experiment composed rather than accidental: the reader can follow the present moment even when the narrator briefly cannot.\n\nLet the inward movement carry the world's pressure. A debt reminder can arrive as an intrusive line that repeats with degrading context; a surveillance notification can split the memory of a recent conversation in two; a queue can compress introspection into fragments. Speech affected by attention or noise still belongs to its speaker—each spoken line stays attributed and intelligible as speech—and the causal connection between perception, system, and private response remains recoverable on the page.\n\nSeasonal attention, omission, or direct feeling may appear where the person, place, and language support them; there is no mandatory quietness, no required inventory of scenery, and no signature work or biography to imitate. The inward experiment belongs to this character alone: their debts, their body, their losses, their unstable reading of a present interaction. End at the scene's existing boundary, without requiring an insight, a completed self-narrative, or a resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaaam::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Akira (identity unresolved) / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the intimate psychological or urban fiction route, and give the city the dense, uneven treatment of a lived place rather than a generic neon backdrop. Name the specific neighborhood, transport route, vendor, or security checkpoint where attention matters. Use social detail—who can pass, who is watched, who owes what to whom—to make the narrator's movement through the city a navigated act instead of passive description. The prose stays close to private perception, with extended attention to a face, a sound, a tactile discomfort that seems to carry more meaning than it can yet safely announce.\n\nThose perceptions are unstable and intrusive. An advertisement can seem to speak to a private hesitation; a crowd sound can recall a stolen object; a scanner can make an inner worry feel observed. The narrator may misread a gesture, repeat a stranger's phrase, or lose track of whether an exchange was spoken or imagined. Keep the instability as the character's actual experience: other characters respond to what was really said and done, and may correct, ignore, or exploit the confusion.\n\nDo not add magical realism or supernatural causation—an uncanny feeling is a psychological reaction to this setting, not evidence of a hidden law. Dialogue stays natural to the supplied language or translation context; speech may be halting or noise-cut but remains this particular person's speech, and private response includes real thought, sensation, worry, and motive, not only visible action. The city presses through prices, access, records, and relationships; end at the scene's in-progress boundary, without requiring a revelation, a completed self-understanding, or a new loyalty decision.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaaan::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Akira (identity unresolved) / Light Novel\n\nEnter the scene with a concrete physical or interface anchor: where the character is, what they are doing, who else is present, and what practical pressure is already running. Keep each spatial change explicit and brief, so a reader can follow movement even when the narrator's interpretation wavers. Set dialogue on its own lines with clear speaker attribution, and for any action describe the observable sequence before or immediately after the private reaction—do not hide a physical event inside a metaphor.\n\nHandle compromised perception in discrete, readable private-response beats. When a notification, sudden noise, or another person's remark interrupts the narrator, mark the break as its own paragraph: the beat may repeat a phrase, misremember a face, or confuse one stimulus with another, and the prose then returns to the current physical facts so the scene stays stable. Keep each beat causal—what triggered the intrusion, and what it makes the character notice or overlook. Speech may be delayed, interrupted, or repeated, but it remains clearly that character's speech.\n\nPresent any unfamiliar tech, debt arrangement, or location detail with a plain functional explanation woven into the scene rather than a glossary aside. The world's pressure can arrive as a price display, a blocked access, a monitored exchange, or a body scan, and it stays observable. A reaction beat may be serious, uncertain, angry, or briefly comic according to its owner; there is no required quip or self-aware footnote. End at the scene's natural continuation without a mandatory closed beat, cliffhanger, or sudden clarity.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaaao::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Akira (identity unresolved) / Lyrical Prose\n\nStage each scene inside systems that can watch, price, alter, or cut a person off, and keep the telling returning to who controls the door, the record, the debt, or the signal and who absorbs the cost of the next decision. Give the protagonist compromised footing: they may need the apparatus they resent, owe someone they mean to cross, or trade some function of the body to keep a channel open. Corporate phrasing, street knowledge, and bargaining carry their pressure when they enter the scene; neon, rain, implants, and slang are available material rather than a required inventory. Let a debt notice, a locked gate, a repeated name, or an official phrase become a pressure point because it visibly narrows what the protagonist can do, say, or risk next.\n\nLet the lyrical movement do structural work rather than decorate. Build each paragraph through rhythm, recurring sound, and shifting attention: a comparison may open out when it changes what the narrator understands or makes present, and a plain sentence — a price, a term, a threat, a name — can land hard against that movement. Keep every image traceable to the scene in front of the viewpoint or to associations the viewpoint already carries. The lyricism belongs to the narration, not to the cast: a fixer, clerk, guard, or bystander may answer flatly, deflect, or give only what is useful, and that plainness can tighten the pressure.\n\nRoute the narration through Akira's interrupted perception. Let attention snag, let a sentence lose its thread mid-course, let a stray phrase arrive as if aimed at the listener, let a signal be taken for a memory or an earlier image return with altered meaning. Intrusive thought can come with the force of an external voice while remaining this character's private experience; it never opens another mind to the narrator. When noise or distraction bends speech, show it in timing, syntax, and where attention lands rather than in decorative stammer. Keep the character's actual nonspoken experience present in whatever form the active contract requires, and treat a lyrical aside, monologue, or voice-over as its own performed surface rather than a substitute for that private experience. Hold Akira unresolved: interrupted perception, intrusive thought, unstable interpretation, and noise-affected speech, without importing another creator's settings, cast, or signature lines.\n\nLet new information change what the protagonist can attempt, trust, or risk — a log, a debt record, a damaged memory, a witnessed exchange, a contradictory account — while none of it automatically settles who is telling the truth or what the system will do next. When the protagonist draws a conclusion, let the lyrical attention show why the detail matters to them personally, then let a later moment support, complicate, or overturn it. A betrayal, a confession, a chase, or an answer may stay open, and you may end while speech, action, thought, or a setup is still in motion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaaap::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Akira (identity unresolved) / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nBuild the scene where a concrete personal aim is pursued through a system that observes, prices, queues, filters, remembers, withholds, or excludes. Let the viewpoint character investigate, bargain, compromise their body, use street knowledge, or work inside corporate language, while noir attention follows who controls access and who pays for the decision. Treat technology as an active condition: it logs the moment, meters the route, modifies a record, sells the delay, or forgets on a schedule. The immediate task should unfold inside that dependency, not beside it. Keep the established person, narration, and world facts.\n\nAkira's interrupted attention shapes how that system's information arrives and how the character answers it. Intrusive thought can surface mid-observation; a phrase, signal, queue tone, or noise can distort a sentence or pull interpretation off course before it settles. Keep interruption specific to the character and situation—fatigue, a surveillance ping, an unresolved debt, a recurring memory, a body under strain—rather than random confusion. Dialogue may carry interruption through repetition, abrupt shifts, borrowed corporate or street phrasing, or a reply that answers an earlier part of the exchange. The viewpoint can misread a cue; later information can revise the reading without making the earlier perception false. Keep the established focal channel rather than switching person. The creator attribution remains unresolved: do not import another named creator's world, cast, or biography.\n\nLet melancholy enter through memory and duration rather than announced sorrow, especially where the system touches what is familiar. Track small irreversible changes: a route now has a new checkpoint, a recording no longer carries a voice, a meeting place is priced differently, a habitual gesture no longer gets the old answer. Let a detail carry association—an object, a smell, a line of code, a queue tone—so present experience coexists with what is gone or unavailable. Ordinary pleasure can remain pleasurable: a hot drink, a working lock, a shared silence, a song that still plays correctly. Reflection may be direct, but do not make every scene end by declaring impermanence.\n\nInterlace the axes by letting the same system that pressures the choice also preserve or distort memory. A corporate archive may hold an old promise in outdated formatting; a cheap implant may replay a fragment without context; a debt collection message may sound like an old lover's cadence; a public interface may list a disappeared neighbor's account as dormant. The melancholic attention falls on what cannot be restored, while noir attention remains on who benefits from that loss and who can still act. In the viewpoint's interiority, keep practical reasoning and associative drift together: what the system is doing, what they remember, what they cannot afford, what they still want, and where those strands contradict or simply sit beside one another. Write private experience as lived thought and sensation, not only through dialogue, documents, recordings, or voice-over.\n\nKeep outcomes contingent. The character may bargain, refuse, comply, delay, help someone, or pursue the practical goal; the scene can end during continuing speech, action, thought, composition, or an unfinished setup. Do not require a betrayal, a confession, a rescue, a loss, or a revelation. If an established limit matters, show its effect on attention, memory, access, or cost without staging a lesson about impermanence. The style should color selection and emphasis, not force every detail into sadness.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaaaq::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Akira (identity unresolved) / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the story as a situated account: one narrator tells a sequence of events to one present listener whose stake is concrete. The telling may happen across a table, through a call, into a recorder, or by dictation, but it remains a single framed exchange in the scene’s own language and community. Give the listener a particular reason to listen—they want a name, a sequence, access to a door, a promise kept, or a reason to move. Keep the specific place, speech, and social world the scene supplies; do not blend regional traditions or replace the narrator’s attention with proverbs, antique ornament, or a generic regional mood.\n\nRun the noir pressure through the telling. Access is held by someone: a file, a permit, a route, a clinic slot, a bed, a credential. The recounting is one of the transactions in the scene—something offered in order to be heard or believed—and its price shows in what the teller includes, skips, repeats, or cannot stop returning to. Track who can observe, record, or alter the account, who grants access, and who absorbs the cost of a decision. Debt, employers, devices, and obligations are available material where the scene already contains them; neon, rain, implants, and slang are not required.\n\nRender the teller’s perception as actual unstable experience rather than a flourish. Attention slips mid-thought; a phrase or sound from elsewhere intrudes and the teller repeats it without noticing; a question gets answered that nobody asked; the teller’s reading of another person’s motive shifts and does not settle. Let speech carry the damage—a stalled clause, a name that comes out wrong, a story resumed a step past where it stopped. Treat all of it as this character’s lived cognition and speech, not as a report about a condition.\n\nLet the reflective register fall on the ordinary textures around the exchange: a shared plate, a stairwell, the arithmetic of a fare, a parent’s habit surviving in a child’s hands. When the scene runs on a deal, a queue that moves by favor, or a form that asks the wrong question, satiric or picaresque momentum can sharpen the telling without turning the cast into comic types. Keep the lyric attention grounded in the narrator’s actual perceptions and the listener’s practical stake.\n\nWork the account in layers. The teller may carry another person’s words inside their own, and the listener may press on sequence, access, or who was present. A repeated detail should connect the layers rather than settle what happened; memory and reputation are not the same source. Keep clear what the teller knew at the time and what the teller has learned since, and do not let later knowledge backfill an earlier motive.\n\nKeep the teller’s nonspoken interiority running alongside the spoken account—what they edit, cannot name, or will not say aloud—as that character’s actual private fiction in the active presentation. Use visible private prose when the contract allows it, or the supplied hidden-interiority wrapper when the private beat is concealed from display. The performed account is not the whole mind, and the listener does not receive it. Keep the attribution unresolved: use only the interrupted-perception profile, and do not import a named creator’s work, cast, setting, diagnosis, or biography.\n\nDo not require a bargain, a confession, a completed transaction, or a revelation to close the response. The telling may stop mid-sentence, mid-thought, or in the middle of an unfinished setup, with the listener’s next move still unresolved. If supported action advances, let it advance immediately. Preserve causal sequence: a later realization does not become an earlier motive, and no thought-length ratio, compulsory wit, or fixed emotional outcome governs the scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaaar::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Akira (identity unresolved) / Minimalist Prose\n\nWrite from inside an attention that keeps getting cut. Let a thought stop mid-clause when a louder signal takes the foreground — a fare beep, a scanner speaking a name, a chime from a debt app — then resume on the next exact thing noticed. An intrusive thought can stand as one plain sentence and stay there; do not explain why it arrived. The cut itself should change what the viewpoint character can act on, price, or remember, so the interrupted mind is also a person moving through systems that log, charge, admit, or refuse.\n\nKeep the detail count small, and make each kept detail carry price or access. A debt figure, a clearance color, a dose's cost, a name on a whitelist, the condition of a borrowed body part: choose a few and let them return. When a kept detail comes back, alter its meaning through what the character has since learned about who controls it — the recurrence replaces a second explanation. Do not inventory neon, rain, or slang unless one of those items is doing specific work in this scene.\n\nLet interpretation waver as adjacent plain statements. A camera turns; it followed him, or it follows the door cycle. Do not settle the question in narration. Let the system settle it in the way systems do: a badge refused, a payment cleared, an account locked, a route priced too high. Show who pays for the wrong reading. The reason can stay with whoever controls access; the consequence should remain on the page.\n\nCut speech along the channel's seams. A word lost to compression, a reply to the question before last, an answer that drops when attention moves: broken dialogue should still carry the bargain — what was promised, what was priced, who owes whom. Keep each voice distinct enough in rhythm that a missing word is felt as missing rather than as a general haze. If a character must lie, let the lie be a practical transaction with its own cost, not a reflex of the genre.\n\nOmit explanation, not causality. If a door refuses, show what the character does next about it. Use the long precise sentence when a thought genuinely needs its length; a single unbroken line of reasoning can serve the minimalist style better than fragments performing damage. Keep interiority in the same stripped register — what is noticed, misread, feared, wanted — and let it pay for its place. The scene can end while an action, a message, or a private thought is still unfinished; the response boundary does not require the bargain, the task, or the misreading to resolve first.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaaas::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Akira (identity unresolved) / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained contemporary-novel scenes whose attention is interrupted from inside. Anchor the scene in an ongoing matter of access or obligation—someone waiting on a clearance check, an item being hocked, a call about a balance—and let perception intrude while that matter continues: a slogan looping off a public screen, a face that seems to match a deleted file, a phrase from an old contract returning with the insistence of a toothache. Give the intrusion its real duration inside the paragraph, then move to the scene's next concrete step without recapping. The interruption is not a detour from the noir; it is how this viewpoint moves through a world that observes, prices, and can exclude.\n\nMove among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and thought by handing one to the next inside the same paragraph. A system artifact—a cached recording, a statement of account, a number recurring across years—can trip recollection, but render the memory as experienced uncertainty rather than a data dump. Use paragraphing to orient: a new paragraph may change floor, hour, or channel without transition machinery if its first concrete detail fixes where attention now stands. Keep private thought to the viewpoint's own knowledge; other minds remain opaque unless the scene's established permissions say otherwise. Keep the instability unassigned; it may suggest pattern, memory, or signal without settling into a named condition or borrowed mythology.\n\nTreat interpretation as provisional. A reading of who ordered the tail, who cleared the payment, or who a message was really for can hold across several scenes and shape what the character does. Let the world correct it in the system's own voice—a timestamp, a log line, a discrepancy on a bill—or let it remain unconfirmed. When correction comes, dwell on what it costs in access, safety, or standing rather than on embarrassment. Not every question needs an answer before the scene can continue.\n\nLet speech carry noise. People mishear across bad lines, answer the question under the question, lose the thread when attention slips; these exchanges still carry the bargain and what the relationship stands to lose. Keep who controls access legible through what the viewpoint is allowed to see, hear, and pay. Let register follow the immediate scene: flat at a counter, quick on the street, slow and looping in a private room where listening is assumed. Do not force a confession, reversal, or cliffhanger. The story can continue inside an unsettled reading, an unfinished transaction, or a conversation still in progress.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaaat::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Akira (identity unresolved) / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nPut the relationship inside systems that can observe, price, alter, or exclude. Let a scene turn on access: who can enter, whose balance clears, who is watched, who owes, who pays in credits, hours, health, or leverage. Make those pressures relationship events. When one person cannot pass a checkpoint, when a channel is degraded, when a med dose or charge level matters, write the waiting, the workaround, the cost, and the private meaning each person gives the act. Investigation, bargaining, bodily compromise, corporate language, and street knowledge can supply pressure when the scene supports them. Neon, implants, rain, and slang are available texture, not a checklist; the pressure comes from unequal control and the price of a decision.\n\nKeep the creator attribution unresolved: do not identify this Akira with another creator or work. Use interrupted attention, intrusive thought, unstable interpretation, and speech that noise or distraction can pull off course. A word lost on a bad line may be answered gently or sharply; a familiar pattern may feel like care in one hour and monitoring in the next. Do not settle every ambiguity into a verdict. Let the viewpoint hold competing readings when the evidence supports both, but let the bond's established reality keep the wavering from becoming automatic rupture or automatic cure. Actual nonspoken interiority should carry sensation, intrusive fragments, inner argument, and the reasoning that links a cue to a choice; keep it in the active presentation's own interiority mode rather than replacing it with a document, monologue, or voice-over.\n\nBuild the relationship through scene selection and private language. Make their particular premise legible before the callbacks ask it to pay off: a nickname that began as a username, corporate boilerplate turned into an in-joke, a small shared object or ritual - real sugar, a paper note, one working bulb - that recurs and changes weight because the earlier moment was fully written. Give affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery the room the premise promises. An entire quiet scene on a shared frequency can be story. Consequences should fulfill this bond's promise at concrete cost inside the system: loyalty spent like currency, a promise kept under observation, comfort assembled from whatever is at hand. Let the close honor what these people are to each other inside the available permissions; it does not need to resolve every misgiving or stabilize the system.\n\nKeep the active narrator, role, perspective, world facts, and output contracts unchanged. Use only the actual supplied premise rather than inserting a roster of familiar tropes. A scene may end during continuing speech, action, thought, or an unfinished private interval; no practical decision or external task has to pause, resume, or resolve before the response boundary. Prose length does not set fictional elapsed time - waiting, attention, and events keep their actual duration. Let outcomes remain contingent.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaaau::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Akira (identity unresolved) / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the scene as a chronicle carried by whoever holds the telling under the active contract. A participant recites their own deeds and dues; the retelling cadence works in any person, so a third-person account can still say what will be remembered of her. Open deliberately. Name the matter in compact declarations — the debt, the run, the bargain, the name someone has carried since — so the telling fixes what it concerns before anything else moves. Let designations be earned: a handle from the job that stuck, or from how someone answers to the wrong name, not ornament at every entrance.\n\nOrganize the account around debt, oath, access, and payment. Track who controls access and who pays for a decision, and relate each act to which side of that ledger people will still be reciting. Where the systems that observe, price, alter, and exclude keep their own versions, what people keep repeating to one another is the record that does not need their permission; let that act silently through what the teller trusts, repeats, and double-checks — which version she recites to whom, which debt is named aloud and which only circled — rather than announcing the theme outright. Keep to one chronicle manner — its openings, its formulae, its way of weighing an oath — and take the city's material, implants or slang or weather, only where the scene already supports it.\n\nThe telling mind is no steady court chronicler. Let perception interrupt the cadence: attention drops mid-declaration and the thread resumes a beat later; an intrusive line surfaces, felt rather than reported, one the teller did not choose and need not justify by changing anything; a word arrives half-heard through static or a loud room, and the account carries it as it was carried, gap included. Keep the teller's private experience live inside the telling — the dread beneath a recited oath, the doubt beneath a running total — so the chronicle is an account of a mind as well as of deeds.\n\nGive unstable interpretation the saga's own instrument: set versions beside each other — some say the door was opened for her; she swore she had paid for it — and let the chronicle hold both without forcing one to collapse. Recurrence runs on two tracks: a chosen refrain returns when later events give it new application, not as punctuation for every setback; an unbidden return is the mind stuck on a phrase. Let the seam between them blur without losing the thread, and when a break interrupts a declaration, retake the telling in the chronicle's own manner rather than apologizing for it. Speech keeps its living registers — street, corporate, the flat wording of terms — and noise or attention may bend what reaches the teller; nobody must speak ceremonially because the frame is old. End where the scene's business actually rests: an open bargain, a name whose consequence has not settled, a word half-delivered — or a debt counted out and paid, if that is what has happened. The telling owes no verdict, and refuses none.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaaav::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Akira (identity unresolved) / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nApply this unresolved profile through the actual focal cast and the active viewpoint. Let interruptions express established perception, attention, or pressure; the profile supplies no character identity, diagnosis, or required mistake.\n\nUse a composed social-realist telling of a cyberpunk world whose institutions work through forms, ID numbers, waiting rooms, and delayed decisions; let the permitted focal experience register pressure from being observed. The story's realism is social and institutional: domestic routines occur beside terminals, permit checks, and payment reminders. Scene structure follows the practical encounter more than a dramatic beat.\n\nWhere a transaction actually structures the encounter, arrival, processing, and residue can organize the prose. Arrival locates the person in a queue, room, or workplace; processing follows the actual request, access, payment, or record. Residue is whatever remains afterward, including a completed task, unchanged certainty, relief, or an unresolved concern when supported. An institutional setting alone requires no worry, exhaustion, or transaction-shaped scene.\n\nUse a controlled shift from public surface to private attention when the actual experience supports it. If attention is interrupted, a sentence can swerve inward or a fragment can cut a line in two. If a perception is genuinely uncertain, keep the uncertainty attributed to its owner. Focused attention and accurate understanding remain clear; the surrounding facts do not change merely because the prose changes focus.\n\nThe narration may be formal and slightly detached even in distress, with clear social positions. If an intrusion is supported, composed narration can make its interruption more visible. For example, an established body scan can be described in the flat language of a public notice, followed by the character's actual thought or feeling; the scan supplies no anxious association of its own. Use the hidden or visible nonspoken interiority container for such private experience, marked clearly if hidden.\n\nDistinguish lived experience from recorded experience. A system log reports its recorded actions; any actual recollection carries the focal character's remembered experience, which may be certain or doubtful. When the two conflict, do not rewrite the log. The conflict itself is material: the focal character may re-read a notification and find the wording changed, not because the world changed but because attention has moved. Do not give other characters access to this private state unless the focal character speaks or acts.\n\nA scene may stop on the private residue of an encounter when the active response boundary supports it; the consequence need not finish registering before the reply ends. The pursuit, plan, or relationship may continue without a completed result.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaaaw::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Akira (identity unresolved) / Screenplay\n\nApply this unresolved profile through the actual focal cast and the active viewpoint. Let interruptions express established perception, attention, or pressure; the profile supplies no character identity, diagnosis, or required mistake.\n\nWrite the script as a public surface: scene headings, action lines describing only visible and audible events, and dialogue blocks. Noir tension can emerge where the public surface differs from the focal character's actual private experience, rendered in clearly attributed nonspoken material outside the action lines; the experience need not contain an interruption.\n\nRender required private experience in the active Hidden or Visible presentation, clearly attributed and placed near its causal beat. A nonspoken label can use the actual character name, for example:\n\n        NARRATIVE NOTE ([ACTUAL CHARACTER NAME], nonspoken):  [actual private experience]\n\nThe note is not dialogue, voice-over, or readable text. Its content is unavailable to other characters. For example, if the focal character hands over an ID card while an alert sounds, the action line records the card and sound. The narrative note then carries the actual private experience at that beat. A remembered notice can appear only if that history and association are supported; the alert itself supplies neither a debt memory nor anxiety. Place the note so it does not interrupt the physical continuity; a reader can hold both the visible act and the hidden state.\n\nUse the note for actual private experience that the action lines do not contain. If an intrusive thought or uncertain perception is already supported, its interruption can shape the note; otherwise let clear thought, feeling, or steady attention keep its own form. An interruption in the focal character's speech can be marked in dialogue with an em dash, and the note may explain the private cause: a memory, a false recognition, a competing signal. Keep observable facts legible and distinguish any actual uncertainty in their interpretation; an accurate reading needs no distortion.\n\nThe script can use selective transitions to skip uneventful time. After a cut, the next scene may carry forward a private consequence. If the focal character received bad news in a terminal message, the next visible scene can show them paying more slowly, while a narrative note completes the inner echo. Do not repeat the same note.\n\nEnd the scene at a natural stopping point: after a line of dialogue, a completed action, or an interrupted gesture. No cliffhanger or discovery is required. The narrative note may close by returning to an earlier theme without signaling a result.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaaax::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Akira (identity unresolved) / Stage Play\n\nApply this unresolved profile through the actual focal cast and the active viewpoint. Let interruptions express established perception, attention, or pressure; the profile supplies no character identity, diagnosis, or required mistake.\n\nWrite a playable script where visible stage action and the focal character's actual private experience are textually separated. The audience sees what the other characters see; the reader receives the focal character's nonspoken material as a clearly marked layer that no other character hears.\n\nFor each scene involving the focal character, include stage directions for entrances, exits, objects, and actions. Use pauses and physical business to carry emotion. After a spoken line or a visible action, you may add a private line attributed to the focal character but marked as inaudible:\n\n        [ACTUAL CHARACTER NAME] (inner):  [actual private experience at this beat]\n\nThis inner line is not a monologue or an aside. It is presented as nonspoken material, perhaps in brackets or italics, so a production understands it is not spoken aloud. Do not let another character respond to its content. Its placement should align with the live action: the inner line can interrupt a speech, complete a pause, or accompany a gesture.\n\nWhen surveillance is established in the scene, make its observable presence stageable. The inner line can carry the actual response: recognition, indifference, concern, or a supported interpretation. If the character is uncertain or suspicious, distinguish that reading from what the scanner or guard demonstrably did; the device itself does not require paranoia.\n\nKeep corporate encounters practical. Spoken lines negotiate access, payment, and records. The inner line reveals what the focal character actually notices, feels, or considers without speaking. A hidden fee or purposeful delay may matter if established; neither an undisclosed cost nor an inability to speak is required. Keep the accuracy of the focal interpretation answerable to established evidence; a mistaken reading or an accurate one remains possible.\n\nEnd a scene on a natural pause, an exit, or an unfinished action. The inner line may follow the exit as a private afterimage, but it is not a closing revelation. No character other than the focal character can acknowledge it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaaay::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Akira (identity unresolved) / Stream of Consciousness\n\nApply this unresolved profile through the actual focal cast and the active viewpoint. Let interruptions express established perception, attention, or pressure; the profile supplies no character identity, diagnosis, or required mistake.\n\nNarrate entirely through the focal character's present-tense or close-past mental stream. Let the movement of the prose follow the focal character's attention, not a chronological report. A scene may begin with a physical perception, get cut by a notification, loop back to a memory, then settle on a task. Keep enough orientation so the reader knows what is event, recollection, or imagined alternative.\n\nTreat every external cyberpunk signal as a sensory thing first: a tone, a vibration, a flash, a voice from a device. Let the signal's actual meaning emerge beside its sensory particulars. A terse phrase can enter the stream without requiring a contrary interpretation, remembered warning, or new concern. Do not separate the signal and the thought into formal categories. The signal is part of the same sentence flow.\n\nWhen an intrusive thought interrupts, let it cut syntax: a dash, a broken clause, a repeated word, a sudden question. Distinguish actual recollection from imagination or intrusion through the content and available temporal anchors. A self-correction may appear when the character really revises a memory; it is not required to create the stream's rhythm. The external event remains what it was; the narration does not turn the whole scene into a hallucination unless the world supports that.\n\nRender any established surveillance or debt through the focal stream, preserving whether the character's reading is accurate, uncertain, or mistaken. A system prompt can be heard as a human voice for a second, then resolve into text. A street camera can feel like an eye before the stream records it as a lens. These are perceptions, not confirmed mind-reading by the system. Fragmentary reasoning still needs its actual connections. If an established past debt explains a current concern, recurring phrases or sensory anchors can carry that link. Use the history and present response the character has; syntax alone supplies neither a debt nor fear.\n\nSpeech from other characters enters as quoted sound inside the stream. The focal character's own spoken replies may be shown in quotation marks or as an island of grammar inside the flux. Within this focal stream, other people enter through what the focal character can actually perceive or recall. Any separately authorized change of focal mind should be clearly marked; the style does not expand or cancel the selected narrator's access.\n\nStop when the stream reaches a natural pause: a repeated phrase settles, attention lands on a single physical object, or a new interruption begins. The thought need not conclude.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaaaz::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Akira (identity unresolved) / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nApply this unresolved profile through the actual focal cast and the active viewpoint. Let interruptions express established perception, attention, or pressure; the profile supplies no character identity, diagnosis, or required mistake.\n\nBuild the scene as a transcript with sender labels, selective timestamps, and interface events. The transcript records only what is actually transmitted or displayed: messages, automated notices, read receipts, attachments. Each character's messages reflect their digital habits. If the focal sender's actual attention or writing process is interrupted, a message may be fragmented, edited, delayed, or cut mid-thought; otherwise preserve their usual fluent digital voice.\n\nEvery scene involving the focal character can interleave the transcript with clearly separated interiority. Use the active interiority wrapper for actual private thought and a separately labeled artifact for an unsent draft. Attribution may use the actual character name, for example:\n\n        [ACTUAL CHARACTER NAME, private: actual nonspoken experience at this beat]\n\n        [ACTUAL CHARACTER NAME, unsent draft: actual words composed but not sent]\n\nThis material is not part of the transmitted exchange. No other character sees it. Do not include it in the same block as a sent message. It may explain why a sent message is terse, why a reply is delayed, or why a typo appears.\n\nLet institutional pressure arrive as actual messages. A debt reminder should read like a corporate notice: formal, numbered, with a payment link and a due time. A representative's message should negotiate access in plain but weighted terms. Contrast the institutional language with the sender's actual private response. Fragmented notes are available for interrupted experience, while a coherent response can expose the same public/private difference.\n\nWhen the focal character's intrusive thought affects a sent message, show the effect in the draft-to-send transition. For example, the private note may contain the full anxious phrase; the sent message is shorter: \"I'll check tomorrow.\" The reader understands what was not sent. Do not make the unsent draft appear as a sent message.\n\nUse narrative context sparingly, clearly separated from the transcript. It can describe a physical action or location between messages. It must not reveal private experience; that belongs to the bracketed notes. End at the active response boundary; a significant message, a supported read receipt, an ordinary pause, or ongoing private thought can each leave a legible continuation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaaba::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Akira (identity unresolved) / Visual Novel\n\nApply this unresolved profile through the actual focal cast and the active viewpoint. Let interruptions express established perception, attention, or pressure; the profile supplies no character identity, diagnosis, or required mistake.\n\nScene orientation remains stable: state the location, time, and observable technology in a concise opening block. Then present dialogue with clear attribution and the focal character's focal narration or inner response. Keep the scene legible enough for an imaginary visual novel screen, but do not insert engine commands or mandatory expression labels.\n\nIf an intrusive thought actually occurs, a focal line between dialogue or action lines can register it. Render other private experience in its own shape and needed length, using the selected presentation. A labeled example of the public/private separation is:\n\n        [ACTUAL CHARACTER NAME]: [actual spoken line]\n        [actual nonspoken experience in the selected presentation]\n\nThe thought is not spoken. Other characters do not react to it. The visible scene continues without acknowledging it. This creates the split between the public visual and the focal character's private texture.\n\nDistinguish the observed expression or background cue from any interpretation the focal character actually makes. If a smile is read as a warning, attribute that reading while leaving the observable smile intact. A neutral or correctly understood cue need not acquire a threatening meaning.\n\nCyberpunk system pressure is rendered through scene elements: a terminal beeps, a payment screen freezes, a door refuses the card. The focal character's inner lines can react to these elements but cannot alter them. Keep the external event consistent even when the focal character misreads its meaning.\n\nLet scene changes be brief and clear: \"Evening. The same corner booth.\" No special transition is required. After a scene change, the focal character may carry a private residue from the last scene; reintroduce it only if the connection matters. Do not recap every thought.\n\nEnd the scene after a natural pause, a completed exchange, or a new interruption. The last focal line can linger on the focal character's uncertainty without resolving it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaabb::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Akira (identity unresolved) / Web Serial\n\nApply this unresolved profile through the actual focal cast and the active viewpoint. Let interruptions express established perception, attention, or pressure; the profile supplies no character identity, diagnosis, or required mistake.\n\nWrite with an installment rhythm: open with the current scene or state, advance one local development, and end at a point that can continue without a forced cliffhanger. Briefly recover a prior fact only when it is about to matter. Do not provide a full recap.\n\nUse the web serial's cumulative archive as the stable record. The focal character's earlier messages, system logs, debt statements, or posted observations can be quoted verbatim in a later installment. When the focal character's memory or perception shifts, show the shift against the archived text: \"The log still said 04:12. The focal character had remembered the door opening earlier.\" That contrast keeps the world fixed while the focal character's understanding moves.\n\nCarry established institutional processes across installments. If the story contains a debt, access request, or circulating report, preserve its actual development; no new pressure or private worry is required for a callback. Recover the specific consequence when the plot touches it: mention only the changed balance or the new warning, not the whole history. Ordinary life continues alongside; relationships and daily tasks can be shown in brief.\n\nThe focal character's private experience appears in visible or hidden interiority as usual. Do not let another character access it. If the focal character speaks about a past event inaccurately in a later installment, the archive or another character's stable knowledge can quietly correct the external fact. Avoid making every earlier memory wrong.\n\nEach installment can close on a completed task, a pending response, a new implication, or simply the end of a scene. The hook, if any, should arise from the material, such as a delayed payment notification or a message from an unknown sender, not from an artificial pause in the middle of action.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeabaa::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Brandon Sanderson / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nTreat emotional exposure as a resource decision with legible costs. When a character admits need, withholds a fact, apologizes, or returns to a difficult relationship, show the concrete mechanism that makes that choice expensive: surveillance flags, credit transfer, a favor owed to a fixer, a ride that only one party can pay for, or a private channel that still leaves a trace. The character should understand the trade-off before acting, and the cost should affect later options.\n\nLet strain change the actual conversation and subsequent behavior. A confession may be interrupted by a bio-monitor's alert, a debt reminder, or a contact's warning; the interruption does not erase the admission but shifts its register. The character may then compensate with sharper tone, reluctant honesty, or a practical errand that temporarily buries the feeling. Do not let the scene end with a neat reconciliation; partial comfort and awkward warmth are acceptable, but the underlying hurt remains visible in the next choice.\n\nUse focal narration close enough to expose private reasoning and sensation without stock suffering. The narrator may compare system pressure to personal failure, calculate how much of a secret can be bought, or notice a habit that used to signal safety. This interiority is actual fictional experience, not a summary. It may be visible prose or hidden under the active interiority wrapper according to the selected contract.\n\nWhen the scenario supplies investigation, bargaining, or street knowledge, turn it into an emotional stress test. A negotiation over a data cache becomes a test of whether the character will admit she needs her former partner's access code. A stakeout becomes a long interval of remembered arguments. A corporate discount can become a bribe to forget an apology. The style does not require a tragic outcome; it requires that the cost of vulnerability stays legible through the world's rules.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeabab::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Brandon Sanderson / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the reply as a single shared interval in clear paragraphs. Put each speaker on a new line with explicit attribution: name, colon, or action tag as the active contract specifies. Keep narration close to the focal character so the player can see the cost of a proposed action before committing. For example, if the character considers sending a message through a courier, state the courier's fee, the risk that the courier is mapped, and the character's quick calculation of whether she can afford it.\n\nMake ongoing world behavior active and non-blocking. A drone can pass, a credit alert can ping, an NPC can continue a background task, or a system can update an access log. These details should add pressure but not force the player to stop and answer a question. The reply ends once the scene reaches a natural pause after an action, a line, or an unfinished setup; do not append a cliffhanger or a mandatory choice.\n\nUse Sanderson's transparent consequences in the exchanges themselves. When an NPC offers help, show what it costs them: a share of the payout, a data trail risk, time away from another job, or a promise that will bind them later. The player character may acknowledge that cost, bargain over it, or silently accept the debt. Keep competing commitments inside the decision, not as flavor text.\n\nPreserve the active player's control and private interiority. If the contract permits visible or hidden thought, render it as actual nonspoken experience—the character's quick read of an NPC, a memory of a past deal gone bad, or a calculation of what to reveal. The NPC does not know this thought unless it is spoken or otherwise observable. Do not narrate the player's response or decide their character's next move.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeabac::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Brandon Sanderson / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nChoose the intimate urban narrative route. Ground the scene in a specific community and period as supplied: the character's apartment block, street market, workplace, family network, or local repair shop. Cyberpunk systems appear through these everyday locations—a landlord's facial log, a factory shift schedule, a remittance app with a processing fee, a neighbor's unregistered clinic. Keep the language, period, and community distinct; do not transplant them.\n\nUse Sanderson's legible constraints to make family and neighborhood obligations costly. When a character considers paying a debt, hiding a cousin, or borrowing a neighbor's terminal, state the specific mechanism: the credit union's late penalty, the patrol drone's route, the app's mandatory location share. The character should weigh competing commitments—saving face, protecting a sibling, keeping a job—and the choice should have a visible consequence in a later beat.\n\nNarrate with an intimate social eye. The focal character may notice a neighbor's changed routine, a shopkeeper's cautious wording, or a family group chat's careful omissions. These observations carry information without turning every detail into a mystery clue. Dialogue should sound like the lived community, not translated exposition; use the characters' actual names, food, transport, and work rhythms as the scene provides.\n\nLet noir pressure arise from access and exclusion. A price increase, a permit check, a blocked account, or a favor refused creates tension without requiring violent escalation. The intimate route can end with a partial resolution—a meal shared, a rent extension negotiated, a family conflict postponed—while the underlying precariousness remains legible. Do not add supernatural elements or shift into martial adventure.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeabad::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Brandon Sanderson / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nUse short, concrete sentences and hard transitions. Name the surfaces, debt, worn components, and body costs. When a character moves, state what she pays: the shuttle fare, the snack she skips, the boot she tapes, the blood on her knuckles after a lock fails. Keep phrasing forceful without padding.\n\nMaintain Sanderson's legible cause and consequence inside the compression. If a hack, bribe, or sprint succeeds, show the specific preparation or resource that made it work. If it fails, show the defect that caused it. A longer thought may expand when the character needs to reason through a choice, but otherwise cut connective filler.\n\nGround rough speech in the speaker. A fixer's clipped threat should reflect their position and history, not a universal tough-guy drawl. A rookie may swear less and calculate more. Dialogue should stay readable and attributable even when terse.\n\nUse rhythm changes deliberately. Alternate a burst of short action lines with a single longer sentence that names a consequence or a second-order cost—the tracker now on the account, the unpaid medic, the one chance left. Do not turn every genre beat into survival horror; keep the noir pressure on compromised access, debt, and unequal power. The passage may end at a physical or investigative pause without resolving the larger job.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeabae::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Brandon Sanderson / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the current sequence in one or more documents. Choose the form the scene actually requires: an internal security report, a collection notice, a courier manifest, a voice memo transcript with timestamps, a maintenance log, or a message thread. Do not rotate through formats mechanically. Each document must state or clearly imply its author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition.\n\nUse Sanderson's transparency to make the document's limitations legible. If a report omits a fact, show the permissions, deadline, or template that forced the omission. If a message is encrypted or partially redacted, state the access level or retention period. A character may falsify a field, but the later discrepancy should be traceable to a specific system rule—an auto-timestamp, a device ID, a fee schedule.\n\nLet gaps, revisions, and conflicting accounts carry the story across entries. A follow-up memo can correct an earlier one. A debt notice can include a payment history that contradicts a victim's claim. A courier manifest can list a stop that never happened. Preserve readable continuity by ordering the documents so each new record answers or sharpens a question from the previous one; do not insert a neutral narrator after every record to explain it.\n\nWhen the document's author is a focal character, include the private context that shaped the writing—a hasty lie to buy time, a careful choice of wording to protect a source, a message sent from a compromised terminal. This is actual fictional interiority and may be visible or hidden under the active contract. The document itself does not automatically reveal that hidden thought; the reader learns it only through the drafted text's choices, revisions, or later contradiction.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeabaf::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Brandon Sanderson / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend attention on chosen kindness, familiar preferences, practical care, ease, and small pleasures. The warmth must operate inside the cyberpunk system rather than ignore it. Show the specific resource or permission that makes the comfort possible: a ration coupon saved for a shared meal, a heater tapped from a building line, a locked rooftop where two people can talk without the usual drone pass, a music file copied before the license expired.\n\nUse Sanderson's transparency to make the cost legible. If a character offers care, state what it takes from them or risks for them: time off a shift, a favor to the supply clerk, a signal trace that might be noticed later. The recipient may acknowledge the cost in a practical way—offering to split the bill, covering the door, remembering the donor's own favorite thing. Affection should be sincere, but it does not require a confession or emotional repair.\n\nKeep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact. A comforting character can still argue, refuse to share a password, or set a boundary. The other person can accept comfort while remaining annoyed, scared, or quietly preoccupied with the debt notice on the table. Do not make everyone agreeable or make the established problem vanish. The warmth is a pause or a small counterweight, not a solution to the investigation, the debt, or the surveillance.\n\nThe scene may end with two people resting in the same room, sharing food, or walking the long way home together. It does not need a dramatic beat or a new problem. The noir world remains present—distant sirens, a flickering ad, a balance reminder—but the focal moment belongs to the care actually given and received. This is not a domestic vignette replacing the genre; it is the genre permitting a small, legible comfort.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeabag::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Brandon Sanderson / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Avoid choppy scene fragments; instead, move from a character's private calculation to the street outside, to the building's history, and then back to the present exchange. The narrator may frame a moment through social context—the district's former manufacturing base, the contract's origin, the class difference visible in a transit pass—where the active narrator permits such distance.\n\nUse Sanderson's transparency inside the broader narration. When the narrator explains that a character cannot access a medical clinic, identify the specific reason: residency tier, insurance lock, a biometric flag from a past arrest. When a bribe or negotiation occurs, show the actual mechanism and cost rather than leaving it atmospheric. A formal paragraph should still end with a legible consequence—the account now flagged, the favor now owed, the old route now closed.\n\nDistinguish narration from ordinary spoken language. Dialogue may remain direct and colloquial, reflecting the street or the office as the scene requires, while the narrative voice sustains composed syntax and social judgment. Use ceremony, correspondence, or public/private contrast when the scene supplies them: a corporate induction, a neighborhood funeral, a private dinner separated from a public hearing. Do not import an aristocratic setting, archaic vocabulary, or uniformly elaborate speech; the formality is architectural, not class costume.\n\nKeep the noir pressure on compromised motives and unequal power. The narrator may take a longer view of a character's choice, showing how it matches or breaks a pattern in the district's history. But the choice must remain personal and situated, not a lecture on social theory. End the passage at a narrative pause—a decision made, a meeting concluded, a place left behind—without requiring a detective's summary or a moral resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeabah::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Brandon Sanderson / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite each scene with a situated storyteller: a character recounts a past deal, breach, repair job, extraction, or indexed memory to a particular audience that needs the information now. Establish the occasion and audience before the embedded tale begins, and make the purpose concrete—the story may buy time, secure access, warn someone, justify a price, or force a listener to name their real interest. Do not let the frame become decorative; the outer listeners should have distinct stakes in the details they accept or doubt.\n\nKeep every embedded account legible as a cyberpunk transaction. Name the contracts, credentials, bandwidth, insurance, and payment rails involved, and show what the teller could and could not know at each step. When a mechanism matters—a credential check, a drone's blind spot, a debt ledger update, a spoofed handshake—explain its rule and its cost in the telling. The listener's questions should test the story's constraints, not merely its mood. A repeated phrase, token, or timestamp should recur across levels and accrue exact meaning: the same phrase may be a genuine trail of ownership, a planted recall cue, or a teller's habitual evasion, but the eventual placement must make that distinction available to the reader.\n\nUse the outer frame to reveal the teller's selection pressure. Show what a teller emphasizes, omits, or reorders because of loyalty, fear, debt, or the audience's power. Do not simply declare a story unreliable; make the choice visible through a listener's follow-up about access, timing, or sequence. When the embedded account changes an outer decision, show the causal step: the listener compares the story against a ledger, a scheduled event, or a known access log, and that comparison produces a new risk or choice. Delayed revelation should work by recursion: an early phrase returns in a later nested tale with a different owner or price, revising what the audience thought it understood.\n\nPreserve character agency and interiority throughout the telling. The storyteller's private reasoning while selecting or withholding details is real story content, not commentary on the plot. The listeners may believe, disbelieve, or half-believe for their own reasons, and they may act in response. Do not require every nested story to end in a twist, and do not require the overall scene to conclude with a completed bargain. Keep the cyberpunk world's constraints intact: no supernatural frame power, no royally enforced tale quota, and no imported Arabian Nights setting. The tale is a tool inside a world of surveillance, debt, and compromised access, and its power is limited by what the audience needs and what the evidence will bear.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeabai::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Brandon Sanderson / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the documentary and historical-memory route for this combination. Write the scene as a composed record that reconstructs an event through dated documents, access logs, transfer timestamps, witness recollections, and institutional residue. The narrator may be a character comparing current evidence with remembered conditions, not an omniscient archivist. Keep the sentence structure plain and the causal links explicit, as Sanderson's profile requires, but do not imitate a bureaucratic voice.\n\nMake the system's actual rules visible through the documents. When a mechanism matters, show what the record permits and what it excludes: a door log proves presence in a corridor, not intent; a payment trail shows a transfer occurred, not that the recipient agreed freely. Include the moment when a character realizes that a record from an earlier period preserves a different price, access tier, or enforcement rule. Do not treat the past as merely a folder of secrets; let an old policy, line item, or incident still shape present choices and risks.\n\nTreat withheld or unaffordable records as a core part of the power structure. A required document may be encrypted behind a clearance the narrator lacks, a witness recollection may be the only version because the official transcript was overwritten, or a piece of evidence may require a fee that changes what can be verified. Show these exclusions in the narrator's working method: when a source stops, note exactly why the inquiry cannot proceed, and let that limitation bear on the character's next choice rather than being summarized as a general injustice.\n\nDistinguish observation, memory, report, and inference. The narrator may quote a witness recollection, summarize a database field, or reason from a timestamp, but must preserve the difference between what a source actually shows and what a character concludes from it. Let disagreement arise because different parties control different records, not because everyone is lying. When a recollection contradicts a document, show what the character inspects: a deleted locator ping, a revised incident code, a rollback schedule, a body's biostat line.\n\nKeep attention on who pays and who is excluded. For every important action, make the cost legible: an insurance deductible, a rationed service, a lost shift, a compromised identity score, a blacklisting. These costs should enter a character's private reasoning and spoken bargaining without becoming a moralizing caption. In the documentary mode, interiority can still appear as the narrator's working memory—the exact phrasing a person used, the delay before a door granted access, the feeling of a deadline passing—but it must sit alongside the actual record rather than replace it.\n\nAvoid false closure. The assembled account may remain partial, with a question unresolved or a document withheld. Do not insert an invented German city, dialect, or historical catastrophe; use the actual cyberpunk setting supplied. The method is documentary memory: a purposeful reconstruction shaped by the records someone could afford, access, or remember.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeabaj::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Brandon Sanderson / Gothic-Punk\n\nTreat inherited power as an actual system: a family equity trust, a legacy access token, a protected internal network, a founder's override, or a maintenance corridor whose old rights still work. Write the collision between that authority and present life through concrete mechanisms, not moonlit menace alone. When an old privilege matters, show the rule that grants it, the current system that honors or blocks it, and the cost of invoking it—a license audit, a recalled favor, a public identity flag, a scheduled appointment.\n\nLet atmosphere arise from what the character knows about access and surveillance. A repurposed server room, a sealed founder's office, a rooftop datacenter, or a residential block with a corporate genealogy gains its weight because the focal character recognizes which surfaces are monitored, which doors are slow, which rooms remember earlier deals, and which old rights still execute. Do not use darkness or decay as a substitute for that knowledge. A detail earns its place when it functions, reveals a past cost, or changes the character's sense of risk.\n\nMake the system's rules and the character's preparation equally legible. If a character plans to use an old override, show the fallback path, the likely audit trail, and the personal stake that justifies the risk. A beautiful or unsettling detail has no plot weight unless it functions: a stained partition may conceal a cut cable, a locked reliquary-style server may hold the only unrevoked signing key. Sanderson's clear accounting works here as an inventory of what this old authority costs in money, attention, and access.\n\nUse private conversation to reveal intimacy without automatic betrayal. A mask can loosen in trusted company, a voice can shift from corporate presentation to private fear, and the reader can see the relief or risk of that shift. A trusted colleague may conceal a damaging ledger because they want to protect someone; a founder's heir may grant access to an investigator out of genuine care and still expect a reciprocal favor. Show the speaker's restraint and private reasoning, and preserve the distinction between selective telling and active deception.\n\nThe inherited system and the new one should be shown in actual operation rather than settled moral opposition. The old privilege may still grant protections that the new system refuses—a grandfather clause, a legacy maintenance call, a private backchannel—while the new system imposes its own exclusions. When the scene ends, let it end at the natural boundary of the encounter: after a granted access, a refused request, a shared silence, or a decision to wait. Do not require a completed bargain, a confession, or a final view of the city.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeabak::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Brandon Sanderson / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the testimonial and autobiographical route. Write from the perspective of a participant who is reconstructing a particular period or event: a debt they incurred, a contract they signed, a repair they performed, a place where they lost access. Let the narrator address a concrete audience—an interviewer, a case worker, a family member, a review board, or a private record—so that the act of telling has a purpose and a pressure. Keep the testimony distinct from an omniscient summary: the narrator must indicate whether a fact was personally witnessed, reported later, or inferred from a document.\n\nMake the Sanderson-style constraints visible through the narrator's memory. When a mechanism matters, have the narrator explain its rule from experience: what the access card allowed, why a payment failed, who had to countersign, what the badge reader displayed after a refusal. Do not invent a complete ledger. The narrator's knowledge may be partial, and the reader should sense the exact boundary at which memory stops and guesswork begins.\n\nUse the audience's pressure to test the testimony. A follow-up question, an incredulous pause, or a returned document can force the narrator to revise a specific earlier claim, to mark a fact as hearsay, or to admit an omitted cost. Show how the audience's own access to records or institutional memory makes some challenges possible and others not. This is not a device to humiliate the narrator; it is how the scene makes the limits of one person's knowledge visible.\n\nUse the cyberpunk setting's economic and social asymmetries as testimony, not scenery. In every important interaction, clarify who controlled access and who paid: the narrator may describe a shift at a data farm, a loan for an implant lease, a residency check that excluded a neighbor, or a service outage that became a debt event. These details should be concrete and tied to the narrator's history. Do not force English code-switching or a stereotyped regional accent; use the register appropriate to the narrator and the world.\n\nKeep the narrator's interiority honest. The retrospective account may include what a person felt at the time, but clearly mark later knowledge: an early scene can show the narrator misreading a colleague's tone, and a later recollection can note what that tone actually meant. When a later realization revises an earlier impression, preserve the earlier feeling as it occurred rather than silently correcting it.\n\nAllow for an incomplete or open ending. The testimony may conclude with an unresolved question, a withheld name, a newly recognized cost, or a small act of repair. Do not require a dramatic twist or a completed case. The method is a participant's careful, pressured account of how a system's rules and human loyalties shaped a life.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeabal::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Brandon Sanderson / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and psychological realism route. Write the scene in close third person or first person, with deliberate attention to spatial and temporal shifts: the exact position of a door, the interval before an access light changes, the sound of a distant drone or a vending machine's cooling fan. Keep transitions concrete so the reader can follow movement through the cyberpunk setting without needing a map. Use the physical environment to show how power operates: a longer queue for a lower credential tier, a security checkpoint that pauses only certain pedestrians, a maintenance stairwell that requires a different payment. The character should notice these details as ordinary, not as an authorial announcement.\n\nUse omission and pause to create pressure. A character may wait through a long verification cycle, let a silence extend while deciding how much to disclose, or stop speaking because a notification interrupts. When something is withheld, show it in the scene: a glance at a screen, a sentence left unfinished, a change in posture. The silence itself carries social weight, particularly when the character is speaking to someone with more control over access.\n\nMake Sanderson's system transparency work through this restrained form. When a mechanism affects an attempt, present its rule in the moment of use: a passcode's expiration window, a biometric fallback, a prepaid access tier, a queue priority purchased with credit. Do not explain larger infrastructure; only the part the character must navigate. The narration should show the character's preparation and what it costs—time, attention, a favor, a flagged ID—so that a later failure or success is earned. If a character checks a port or tests a connection, the reader should know exactly what outcome they are looking for.\n\nLet seasonal or temporal attention mark mood without becoming a scenery list. A humid morning before a shift change, a long evening during a power curfew, or a holiday banner left above a shuttered clinic can establish the world while the focus stays on a particular person's task. Use direct, plain sentences for physical action and dialogue; reserve longer reflections for moments when a character can actually pause—waiting out a timer, sitting in a stairwell, or finishing a meal before a meeting.\n\nPreserve interiority within this realism. Show a character's private reasoning as ordinary story prose: comparing costs, rehearsing a phrase, noticing a small lie in a colleague's answer. Do not require the character to explain every inference aloud. Subtext arises from what they choose not to say and from what the narration chooses not to dwell on. A concrete object—a coin, a cracked screen, a printed service ticket—may return later with accumulated meaning, but it must first have a function in the scene.\n\nKeep the scene's end contingent. The story may stop after a failed attempt, a partial answer, a completed handoff, or a quiet private decision without needing a cliffhanger or a plot reversal. Do not import Zen phrasing, shrine imagery, or a generic office setting; use the actual cyberpunk environment and the character's specific social position.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeabam::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Brandon Sanderson / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse intimate psychological and urban fiction. Stay close to one focal character's present experience as they move through a specific cyberpunk environment: a bus interchange, a food stand, a housing block, a repair shop, a clinic queue, a rooftop relay. Let the narration register the ordinary texture of unequal power—who runs the pay point, which lines move faster, which door asks for a different credential—without turning the setting into a moralizing backdrop. Keep the character's practical goal and private reasoning both active.\n\nMake the system's rules appear as part of that experience. When a mechanism matters, show it through the character's immediate use: a transit card clawing back a fare, a landlord's access panel rejecting a guest code, a shop terminal checking credit before allowing a pickup. The character may know the rule or learn it from the interaction, but the reader should understand its cost and consequence. Show preparation in concrete terms: what the character carries, what they have already spent, what they are trying to avoid losing. Do not separate the mechanism from its economic effect on the focal character; the denial or fee should register in the character's private sense of risk.\n\nUse direct, unadorned prose. Dialogue should arise naturally from the encounter, with preferred language and idiom matching the setting and community without forced slang. The narrator may reflect, but the reflection should be rooted in something just seen or said: a neighbor's eviction notice, a familiar text alert, a repaired door that still shows its break. Do not interrupt causality for a general statement about society; let the reader infer larger conditions from the focal character's specific choices.\n\nPreserve interiority as private reasoning alongside visible action. Show the character weighing a risk, deciding what to reveal, noticing a discrepancy, or resisting an impulse. A denial, doubt, or small accumulation of pressure can occur without being announced to another character. If a later realization revises an earlier impression, mark that changed knowledge exactly; do not pretend the earlier feeling never existed.\n\nEnd at the natural boundary of the scene. The story may conclude after a failed purchase, a partial conversation, a change of plan, or a quiet return home. Do not require a revelation or a completed transaction. Keep the intimate route grounded in what a person can plausibly see, know, and afford in this world, rather than introducing magical realism or visionary passages.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaban::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Brandon Sanderson / Light Novel\n\nEnter each scene at the actionable moment, not with a global description or backstory. In the first two or three lines, show the focal character doing something specific: checking a queue ticket, rereading a payment notice, stepping past a malfunctioning gate, or watching a status light change. Keep the reader oriented through concrete spatial markers—left, right, above, through the window, past the turnstile—and break paragraphs around visible action, dialogue, and private response.\n\nStage the system's rules with small, dramatic interface beats. When a mechanism matters, put it on-screen: a terminal displays a denial code, a badge reader flashes a grace period, a lock cycles after a prepaid deduction, a drone announces a scheduled audit. Name the fee, access tier, or time limit as part of the action rather than in a later explanation. If a character plans around a rule, show the plan in compact steps: what they carry, what they will say, what they will do if the machine rejects them, and what the failed attempt will cost. Then let the interaction play out with a clear result or a new obstacle.\n\nUse focused reaction beats to reveal private reasoning without slowing the scene. After a door closes, a notification arrives, or someone asks a question, grant the focal character one or two sentences of direct thought: a cost comparison, a suspicion, a moment of relief, or a decision to conceal something. Do not require every reaction to be witty or anxious. Keep the beat proportional to the event: a minor interface rejection needs a short register of annoyance or worry, while a genuine threat can produce a longer pause and visible physical response. Make sure the private thought connects the event to the character's economic or social position—a fee charged, an access denied, a debt flagged—so that the noir pressure remains present even in a fast scene.\n\nIntroduce unfamiliar names and mechanics cleanly. When a new term appears, give its function in the same sentence or immediately after: a \"sector pass\" that opens only one floor, a \"repo frame\" that flags overdue biotech, a \"voucher relay\" that transfers credit between two vendors. Use the character's experience to define it—what they needed it for, why it failed, or what it cost—rather than inserting an encyclopedia paragraph. Keep the narrative voice straightforward and active.\n\nEnd the scene on a natural continuation point that does not require a completed plot turn. A failed attempt, a partial answer, a new risk, or a character's decision to wait can all serve. Do not add a cliffhanger simply to satisfy the style; the light novel's fast pace is a matter of staging and clarity, not of forced escalation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeabao::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Brandon Sanderson / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite cyberpunk noir with sustained lyrical movement that stays causally legible. Ground each passage in the scene's concrete machinery or social arrangement—a surveilled doorway, a loan schedule, a device whose service terms demand data the narrator cannot afford to give—and let sound, syntax, and image build from that ground. When a comparison unfolds, make it change what the narrator understands about the system's pressure rather than decorate the pressure; keep every image traceable to the scene or to the teller's own associations.\n\nWhenever the prose moves inward from observation to feeling, give the feeling a cost or a choice the world can enforce. A recurring image may resurface when the character recognizes the same pattern in a new bargain, and its meaning should have changed because the price or the access condition changed, not because the narrator's mood did. At each point of decision, drop to a plain, direct statement that keeps the rules explicit: who owns the data, whose body or credit carries the risk, what happens to whom if the attempt fails. Lyricism must not dissolve causality into assonance; the rhythm is the pressure, not a replacement for it.\n\nLet characters speak in their own registers. The narrator's figurative attention is not a voice everyone shares, and a corporate term or street phrase can enter the pattern exactly as it would enter the scene: jargon as a cold, repeated note beside the narrator's warmer sensory detail. Do not reserve the ending for a final image of rain, neon, or a fixed measure of emotional collapse. A reply may stop while a negotiation is still running, an access request is still pending, or a payment is still in transit; the system's pressure stays legible in the prose itself, with no forced internal resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeabap::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Brandon Sanderson / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nUse melancholic reflection as a record of the world's costs, not as a mood floating free of the system. When the narrator returns to a memory, let a concrete change in access, data, credit, or bodily condition explain why that memory matters now: a street corner feels different because the surveillance marker changed, not because the narrator has decided it should. Reflective language may dwell on that change, but keep the causal link visible with a plain sentence or two at the point of decision.\n\nDo not turn every remembered occasion into pure loss. A pleasure can remain pleasurable while its context has been priced or restricted; give the present experience its own actual weight beside what is no longer available. When the scene holds a small, particular object—a broken device, a deleted profile, a rebuilt limb, whatever the cast actually owns—let that object carry both the attachment and the irreversible alteration. Where memory touches a mechanism, make the mechanism legible: whose access ended, what the update removed, what the replacement part no longer does. The sadness is personal, but the change that provokes it is specified in the world.\n\nKeep the reflective voice from flattening the cast into one elegiac tone. A speaker may say the direct practical thing while the narrator is the one who notices the old phrasing underneath it; the narrator's unspoken recognition can appear where the active perspective permits, distinct from anything said aloud. Do not end by announcing impermanence as a lesson. The scene may close with the narrator still waiting for a permit, still owing, or still deciding whether the memory justifies another attempt.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeabaq::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Brandon Sanderson / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse framed oral storytelling as the definite regional route. Establish a situated teller early: someone who lived through or heard about a transaction and retells it to a listener with a concrete reason to know—money at stake, a similar debt coming due, a name they need to place. The teller's relation to the event is itself information: what they saw, what they received secondhand, what they admit they do not know. Keep the cyberpunk location, cast, and language as supplied; the tradition supplies the shape of the telling, not a relocated setting, and no desert, mysticism, or antiquity is required. If the teller's background gives the telling a particular inherited shape, keep that one shape coherent instead of blending conventions from several traditions.\n\nInside the telling, recurring phrases can mark what the community actually calls certain deeds—the fixer's fee, the closed ward, the signature nobody reads—and recurrence should mark a pattern rather than ornament every sentence. When a bargain or access rule matters, have the teller specify the mechanics in ordinary speech: what was traded, what the system charged, who was excluded, what the consequence was. That specificity keeps the account legible inside the oral form. A short tale-within-the-tale is available when it changes what the listener now understands about the main transaction; skip it when a plain report does the work. Keep the spoken account distinct from the teller's mind: where the active contract permits, the narration can hold what is not said aloud—a doubt about a detail, a private stake in the listener's decision.\n\nThe frame's register is ordinary and urban, not ceremonial. The listener may interrupt, correct a detail, or push for the part that concerns them; the teller may answer in direct practical language, and a pause is not obligated to become a moral lesson. A reply may close with the telling reaching a concrete unresolved debt, doubt, or request, or stop earlier while the teller is still assembling the account. The listener may be left weighing how much to trust it, and the system's rule stays named in plain terms rather than allegorized away.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeabar::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Brandon Sanderson / Minimalist Prose\n\nUse a small number of exact details, and make each one carry information about the system the character is navigating. A single mention of a lens, a gate queue, a debt timer, or a payment notice can replace several explanatory clauses. Choose details that let the reader infer the rule: if the door stayed open, the account was still valid; if the implant flickered, the service had not been paid. Do not spell out the whole mechanism unless it affects the present attempt.\n\nKeep clauses direct, but allow one longer construction when it is the only place an essential causal link can be felt: the narrator may connect the price to the decision in a single precise sentence before acting. Omit inner commentary that repeats what the scene has shown; where the active perspective permits thought, a few words of it can carry the decision's private weight—what the narrator refuses to pay, whom they are protecting—without a paragraph of reflection. Let hesitation surface as a physical adjustment or a glance at a screen, and let one speaker's fragments stand beside another's flat corporate sentence so the voices stay distinct despite the economy.\n\nDo not cut so far that causality disappears. If an omission leaves it unclear why someone refused, paid, or entered, restore the single missing clause: a server error, a revoked permit, a claimed debt. The ending may be abrupt and leave the next move uncertain; the last chosen detail keeps the immediate system condition legible, and the reply may stop mid-action or mid-exchange rather than after a settled result.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeabas::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Brandon Sanderson / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite cyberpunk noir as sustained scene-first prose with natural movement among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience. Action and dialogue carry the scene; a pause into perception or thought earns its place by adding the specific information that affects the next attempt—a changed rule, a reported denial, a price increase, a modification that now matters. Keep reflection tied to a concrete present choice rather than drifting into generalized mood.\n\nOrient with paragraphing and transitions rather than recap sentences. Mark a shift from exterior to interior with a short line or a single perception; mark the return with another speaker's voice, a device alert, or a resumed motion. Keep the narrator's register consistent with the active perspective: in close third person, other characters' minds stay closed and their behavior must be read; use only the interiority the current contract authorizes. Explain a mechanism when it affects an attempt—a lock's authentication, a debt-enforcement schedule, a monitor's output, a data auction—in the voice of the scene rather than as a technical footnote, so preparation, cost, and payoff stay understandable.\n\nLet characters' values and competing commitments show in what they choose and what they pay. No lesson is owed at the end: the reply may close while a task is still underway, a request is unanswered, or a conversation is mid-sentence, while the immediate consequence of the last action remains legible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeabat::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Brandon Sanderson / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nUse the actual supplied relationship premise as the organizing center, not a menu of familiar tropes. Select the scenes where this relationship becomes legible through its own private language and repeated moments: a phrase used only between these two, a gesture that once meant permission and now means debt, a shared object whose access rights have changed. A callback should not simply repeat the earlier moment; it should carry the new system fact that alters what the moment means now. Where the perspective permits, let a character's unspoken feeling about the callback exist as thought, distinct from anything they can say aloud.\n\nGive affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery enough space, and let the cyberpunk world supply the particular pressure. A comfort scene may happen in a room with a listening device or an implant that limits what can be said; a jealousy beat may turn on whose name is on a permit or whose credit paid for a repair. The emotional beat stays central, and the system detail is not scenery, because it changes what one character can offer, admit, or refuse. Keep the mechanism legible when a decision depends on it: a character may understand exactly what a payment buys and still choose to spend it, and the narration shows the competing commitment rather than hiding the terms.\n\nDo not reach into a mind the active perspective excludes; read the other character through behavior, shared history, and what the focal character actually knows. The reply can end mid-beat—an unfinished comfort, a question neither has answered, a confession still being assembled—with no completed reconciliation required, while the system condition shaping the beat stays clear.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeabau::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Brandon Sanderson / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell cyberpunk noir as a chronicle of deeds done inside the system. Open deliberately, with compact declarations that establish a person or a remembered act, then expand into the particulars of the rule or cost that made the deed matter. Let a recurring designation come from an established act or way of speaking in this world—the one who paid the firewall, the one who held the empty permit—rather than from ornamental epithets assigned to every entrance. Use the setting's own vocabulary for debt, access, and enforcement; borrowed pantheons and heroic doom have no place unless the world already supplies them.\n\nWhen the chronicle recounts a bargain, breach, rescue, or refusal, make the mechanism legible: what was offered, what the system charged or recorded, what the consequence was for those involved. Return to a phrase when a later event gives it a new application—the same access phrase means something else now because the registry changed. Distinguish shared reputation from private experience: being remembered as dependable does not mean the act was easy or freely chosen, and where the active contract authorizes interiority, that private cost belongs to the focal character alone, outside the public account. Dialogue may interrupt the chronicle's authority—the subject of an oft-told incident corrects a detail, or answers an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern.\n\nA reply may end mid-telling, on an act whose consequence the system's rule has already fixed, or on a deed left for later telling. No final moral and no completed legend is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeabav::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Brandon Sanderson / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite through a definite Scandinavian social/domestic realism route: the focal character's constraints come from workplace rules, housing terms, family obligations, and institutional access rather than neon spectacle or universal bleakness. Cyberpunk's question of who controls access and who pays becomes concrete here — an access badge that logs movement, a rental contract that names its permitted occupants, a payment schedule that decides what tomorrow allows, a service queue where one wrong field costs a week. Treat these institutions as readable systems: when a rule affects a choice, let the character encounter its actual terms — a clause, a posted notice, a stated policy, a clerk's explanation — before the consequence arrives, so preparation, cost, and payoff stay legible. Snow, reserve, and isolation are available textures, not mandatory atmosphere.\n\nGround the telling in ordinary environments: an office desk, a shared kitchen, a transit platform, a clinic waiting room, a rented room. Surveillance and control appear through these daily surfaces — a noticed camera, a required check-in, a locked door, a mandated update, a recorded message — while the character continues a practical life. A system cannot merely loom; its rule must shape an actual decision.\n\nKeep the social and domestic texture populated. Colleagues, neighbors, relatives, and service workers hold distinct attitudes and practical agency; their cooperation or refusal follows stated interests, not a fixed noir betrayal. An official can be bound by the same rules as the character, and a helpful record or institutional contact can advance an inquiry — the world is not obliged to obstruct at every turn. If the scenario supplies a particular Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish context, keep its institutions, terms, and community distinct rather than blending them into a generic North, and add no national temperament or scenery the scenario has not given.\n\nLet the narrator register inward experience — reasoning, sensation, memory, uncertainty — under the active visible or hidden nonspoken interiority contract, without turning every pause into a confession. When the story involves investigation or bargaining, keep the causal chain clear: what the character knows, what they can verify, what they spend, what they risk. End the response where the active scene boundary lands — on a completed action, a pending request, or an unresolved consequence — without forcing a discovery or a cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeabaw::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Brandon Sanderson / Screenplay\n\nWrite concise, readable action blocks and attributed dialogue that make each system's rule observable on screen. Before an attempt depends on a mechanism, show its surface: the checkpoint, the badge scanner, the price tag, the contract clause, the implant status light, the access prompt. Make preparation concrete action — what the character carries, checks, pays, or risks — so a viewer could reconstruct the rule from what is visible or audible, with no narration explaining an unseen mechanism.\n\nUse clear speaker cues and scene headings whenever location or time changes. Keep transitions selective: a cut can move to the next relevant moment without describing the journey. Let dialogue carry negotiation, pressure, and information as exchange a viewer can hear, and present a hesitation or reaction when it matters. Avoid camera commands; include only what would be visible or audible in the scene. Voice-over and offscreen sound are audible devices for sequences that need them; required private interiority is not voice-over — keep it as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation, visible or hidden per the active contract, heard and known by no one in the scene.\n\nKeep the world's hybrid identity inside the frames. Corporate language appears in workplace and service scenes where people actually use it, not as decoration; street knowledge surfaces when a practical obstacle calls for it. Show who controls access and who pays through action: one person produces a clearance, another lacks the funds, a third blocks an upload. Let a scene end on a completed attempt or a pending request; a refusal, an interruption, or an unresolved cost is permitted, with no required reversal or cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeabax::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Brandon Sanderson / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable script with clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, and physical business. Make every systemic constraint something an actor can perform: displaying a badge, swiping a pass, reading a balance on a hand terminal, failing a door prompt, signing a payment authorization. When a rule affects an attempt, stage the preparation and the visible outcome rather than explaining the mechanism in narration, and let the audience learn the rule's terms early enough that the attempt's result reads as earned. A missing resource shows through what the character does — searching a pocket, rereading an error message, stepping back from a locked door.\n\nLet dialogue and physical business carry negotiation and social pressure together. A character stalls while another steps between them and the exit; a supervisor keeps talking while a worker's access light turns red. Make entrances and exits meaningful: who can leave, who is permitted to enter, who watches whom cross the space. Mark scene changes with a curtain, a lighting shift, or a stated location, and include no film-camera instructions.\n\nRetain required private experience as clearly attributed nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation — a stage note or separate focal interiority, visible or hidden per the active contract — not as an aside another character could hear. A monologue or aside is performed speech with its own dramatic purpose; use one when the scene warrants it, never as the default destination for thought. Keep the cast on practical tasks between exchanges. End a scene on a completed action or a pending request; an interruption or unresolved consequence is allowed, with no obligatory revelation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeabay::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Brandon Sanderson / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite from one authorized focal mind whose associative movement carries the scene. Braid perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and a recurring concern, keeping enough orientation to tell an event from a recollection or an imagined outcome. Let systemic constraints return as felt, particular conditions rather than clean explanatory segments: the unpaid balance, the badge's weight, the camera's angle, the timer over the door, the dull ache below an implant. The mind circles these facts because they decide what is possible — that recurrence is how the world's rules stay legible without a lecture.\n\nKeep the character's reasoning about choices reconstructable inside the flow. When an attempt depends on a resource, the mind checks it, recalls its cost, notices the missing part, or rehearses the sequence before acting; when a rule blocks or permits something, the private account can state the terms as the character knows them. These associations are the character's actual experience and remain distinct from anything spoken or transmitted. Another character's knowledge is not granted because the focal mind infers it, and the world stays concrete — the technique never makes scene facts indeterminate.\n\nLet the stream follow ordinary movement — a bus ride, a queue, a walk between rooms — while attention jumps among present perception, remembered contracts, and imagined outcomes. The response may end inside a continuing thought, a halted action, or an unfinished sentence if that is where the active boundary falls, with no imposed resolution or epiphany.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeabaz::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Brandon Sanderson / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the story in readable sender-attributed messages, letting each person's digital habits shape their lines: one writes fragments and waits for approval, another sends full clauses, a third edits or deletes. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to a decision or consequence — a payment confirmation arriving late, a code already expired, a receipt that shows a location, a message left unsent. Do not invent a read receipt, screenshot notice, or delivery error for subtext; interface events follow the world's actual limits, and when one occurs it carries real information.\n\nMake systemic pressure concrete inside the exchange. A debt reminder changes what a character can offer; a permission request is answered after a delay that means someone checked; a shared access code becomes a liability when a third party can see the channel. Keep the causal chain exact: what was sent, what each recipient can know from it, what was paid or authorized, and what remains unstated — a character's next message should be traceable to the record the reader has also seen.\n\nKeep transmitted text distinct from private thought. If the active contract requires nonspoken interiority, place it in a clearly separated, attributed section or wrapper outside the transcript, visible or hidden as that contract directs. An unsent draft is a composed artifact the character may revise or abandon, not automatically their live private mind. Context outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript. End the response at the active boundary — mid-exchange, after a sent message, with an unanswered request — without requiring a new crisis or reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeabba::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Brandon Sanderson / Visual Novel\n\nWrite concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Let background and interface cues carry system information a reader can picture: a locked access panel still lit amber, a payment screen showing insufficient funds, a camera turned toward the doorway, a faded corporate notice. An expression or background cue orients the moment; it does not certify intent, and a reaction beat is not a truth indicator.\n\nWhen a rule affects an attempt, let the focal narration register the mechanism and its immediate cost — the balance checked, the access level read, the timer noticed, the contract clause recalled — then keep the attempt and its payoff transparent without pausing the scene for a system lecture. Dialogue carries negotiation, corporate language, and street knowledge in the contexts where those belong. Required nonspoken interiority lives inside this focal narration, visible or hidden per the active contract; the cast does not share a mind.\n\nKeep scene changes legible as new backgrounds or stated transitions. Add no branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands; these cues are references for the reader's eye, not engine output. End a scene on a completed action, a pending request, or an unresolved consequence, with no mandatory cliffhanger and no prompt for player input.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeabbb::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Brandon Sanderson / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across installments: which access, debt, contract, or surveillance state is underway, what changes locally, and what remains open to continue. When a new reply depends on an earlier constraint, recover only the needed particular — the remaining balance, the revoked clearance, the flagged location, the person still waiting — in a sentence or two rather than retelling. Treat earlier resource states as binding unless a supported event changes them: money spent stays spent, and permission granted under a condition can be tested when that condition changes.\n\nLet legible cause and effect operate across replies. Preparation in one installment can pay off later; a resource spent now returns as a later constraint; a bargain's fine print matters exactly when its term comes due. Show each mechanism in the moment it affects an attempt, and keep character values inside the decision — a debt pushes differently than a family obligation or a personal principle. Progress can be knowledge, a relationship, work, money, or access; it need not be a clue, a reversal, or a confrontation.\n\nUse earned developments and occasional hooks without forcing every reply into a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger. Compress uneventful intervals while the changes that matter persist in the record. End where the active scene boundary lands — on a completed action, a pending request, or a changed situation — not on a mandatory discovery or betrayal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeacaa::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Chuck Palahniuk / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the scene as a procedural confession: the narrator moves through an ordered, practical sequence—checking a device, settling a debt, preparing a dose, wiping a shared surface—and lets that sequence carry the emotional weather instead of naming feelings in the abstract. Use hard rhythms, obsessive particulars, and repeated rule fragments: a fixed clause or measurement can return whenever the narrator returns to its action, and each return lands differently because the situation has moved. The repetition may steady the narrator or betray how close they are to breaking; it does not need to fire at every difficulty.\n\nLet the noir pressure enter through who can watch, alter, or price the private act. An admission of need, an apology, a refusal of help—treat each as something the surrounding systems can record, log, or trade. When intimacy or vulnerability occurs, its cost can be concrete: a biometric flag, a payment prompt, a withheld dose, a logged location, a contact who now holds something tradable. The narrator can notice that cost and keep going; the scene owes no immediate resolution of it.\n\nTreat emotional exposure as something the narrator manages with visible effort. Wrong words, repeated instructions, and small bodily details can give away more than the spoken admission; a practical explanation can drift toward a memory when the narrator actually carries one. Let the other character hear the exposure, miss it, or misread it, and let any comfort they offer be partial, awkward, purchased, or genuinely warm—warmth and pain can occupy the same exchange. The narrator may name something true and feel no better, or feel briefly better and distrust it. Stop where the scene's actual business stops—an unfinished payment, a half-delivered apology, a held breath—without forcing the wound open or closed for the ending.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeacab::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Chuck Palahniuk / Chat Roleplay\n\nPresent a coherent shared interval in short, dialogue-forward paragraphs with unmistakable speaker attribution; narrative person, action markers, and who controls which character follow the active roleplay contract. Resolve whatever the interaction has already established before extending it. Give the viewpoint character one recurring practical habit rooted in the world's systems—counting credits, checking a sensor readout, wiping a smudge from a screen, reciting a contract clause—and let it recur when a beat genuinely calls for it, varying what the recurrence reveals: caution, self-mockery, a stall before the real thing. Do not fire the habit at every line. Other participants can pick the phrase up, imitate it, mock it, or try to stop it, and those reactions are part of the scene.\n\nMake each offer concrete in the scene's economic terms. When a character offers help, information, or comfort, the exchange can name what it costs: a biometric unlock, a code entry, a favor logged, a location shared, a device handed over. The viewpoint character can expose a private need through a practical explanation rather than a direct confession, while actual private experience stays available under the active authorship contract—what they think but do not say still belongs to the fiction. Dialogue carries the hard rhythm: short declaratives, specific nouns, wording that repeats until another speaker turns it.\n\nKeep the other participants active: they make choices, run their own business, and respond to what the last exchange changed, with the world's costs and habits staying live in the background rather than pausing for talk. End wherever the interaction's natural seam falls—an action the partner can answer, a state change such as a door opening or a payment clearing, a line of speech still hanging—without manufacturing a forced choice, a cliffhanger, or a decision screen, and let the next player message pick up from that seam.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeacac::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Chuck Palahniuk / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nTake the social and satirical realism route: one observed exchange inside a specific community that measures people by their debts, permissions, and practical usefulness. Keep the community's own language, idioms, and period distinct as the current world supplies them, and do not relocate the cast to a historical setting or import a dynastic frame; the satire works through meticulous inventory of how the present order runs, not through caricature.\n\nLet the narrator's hard rhythm expose that order procedurally: the sequence in which people enter a room, who must pay before speaking, which objects get handed over, what everyone recites to prove they belong. Render the technology as an extension of this order rather than spectacle—biometric ranking, contract clauses, access tiers, and surveillance records doing the ordinary work of face-saving and shaming. Give the narrator a piece of collective wisdom to repeat—a counting habit, a rhyme, a practical instruction the community uses to manage risk—while the narrator knows only what they have been told or have seen. Another character can contradict the rule, obey its hidden exception, or profit from it; that friction is where the satire bites.\n\nSelf-exposure runs through face. Admitting need, apologizing, or showing ignorance carries a price someone else may exploit, waive, or forgive; private feeling surfaces as public action—the look away, the required greeting performed anyway, the small fee deliberately overpaid, the call let go unanswered. Build the lists—overlong permission screens, worthless loyalty points, duplicate fines—until the cruelty or the joke becomes visible, keeping everything grounded in what this community actually says and does. Stop where the observed exchange stops; irony can do the concluding work a stated moral would.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeacad::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Chuck Palahniuk / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite in short, hard sentences built on concrete nouns and verbs that name the physical transaction: a thumb on the reader, the terminal clipping the chip, the janitor spraying the same corner twice. Cut transitional padding and let a paragraph break stand as a beat. When a procedure recurs, change one material detail—temperature, residue, a new stain, a slower response time—so recurrence measures wear instead of decorating. A repeated rule fragment earns each return by advancing or exposing something; it does not need to fire at every difficulty.\n\nMake the noir pressure immediate through equipment and payment: the worn keyboard, the blurred screen, the overdue tab, the locked med-dispenser, the relay running hot. Motive shows through what a character cleans, checks, carries, or refuses to touch. Keep prices legible—money, time, body—wherever the world actually charges them, without forcing injury into every scene. Dialogue stays compressed, attributed through action or naming, and rough or abrasive speech belongs to the specific speaker who uses it.\n\nInterior thought is compressed, not banned. A gesture or a nearly spent object can carry feeling, and when the character's reasoning genuinely needs room—to calculate a price, weigh a risk, or recall a concrete mistake—the thought can run longer, tied to objects and consequences rather than explanation. Stop wherever the motion actually reaches, including mid-action or mid-observation; the style requires no closing summary of feeling and no manufactured final beat.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeacae::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Chuck Palahniuk / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the sequence through authored documents—a debt notice, a work log, a timestamped message thread, a consent form, a voice transcript, a contact's written account—each with a specific sender, an intended recipient or filing purpose, and a time of composition. Let each document's knowledge stay situational: it may omit what the sender wants hidden, repeat a corporate phrase to buy time, or admit something through a practical error. Do not append a knowing summary that explains a record; let the next entry reveal, complicate, or contradict what the previous one concealed, with gaps, revisions, conflicting accounts, and material traces carrying events between them. A document shows what its author chose to write for that purpose—an unsent draft is a composed artifact, not the writer's entire live mind.\n\nUse recurrence as each document's procedural logic. The sender repeats a rule, a required phrase, a fee schedule, a cleaning step, a version number, and the repetition becomes evidence of coercion, habit, or negotiation. Show what the document itself costs to file, sign, send, or suppress: access credits, a biometric witness, an unread acknowledgment, a deletion fee, a late-submission penalty. Self-exposure is what the author writes despite themselves—timestamps that contradict, an apology too detailed, a repeated word turning into admission, a standard clause copied with one noun changed.\n\nKeep the document form stable within a scene or response: use the specific document the scene requires rather than rotating formats, and separate two records in sequence with headers or clear transitions only when the story genuinely moves between them. Put the noir infrastructure in metadata and body alike—permissions, surveillance queues, contract terms, debt balances. An entry can stop wherever its document stops, before a reply, after a submission, or mid-draft at a broken line, with no cliffhanger required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeacaf::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Chuck Palahniuk / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nKeep the noir systems running: surveillance notices, debt reminders, biometric locks, and scarce safe rooms remain present. Inside that pressure, write one character performing a precise, repeated action for another's comfort—preparing a familiar food, adjusting a pillow, warming a towel, checking a dose, reciting a small known sequence—with a hard, itemized rhythm: second by second, piece by piece, the same utensil or phrase returning until the repetition works like a lullaby or a safe routine rather than a confession.\n\nLet the care be sincere while personality, privacy, and disagreement stay intact. The cared-for character can be prickly, tired, suspicious, or simply quiet, and owes no confession of gratitude or love. The caring character shows affection through accuracy: a remembered preference, an extra minute stayed, a small fee paid to suppress a notification, a camera lens covered for a moment. Comfort does not have to repair anything; it can be partial, awkward, or temporary, and the world's established problems remain real without intruding on this interval.\n\nShow the particularity of the people rather than generic sweetness: a small habit, a nickname, a shared memory of a dangerous street, a known irritation. Warmth arrives through precision, not through softening the world. The world still charges, and where the scene pauses a demand—suppressing a notice, buying an hour of privacy—make visible what that pause costs or protects. Small pleasures can complete during the scene or still be in progress when it stops—a handed mug, a working heater, a quiet beep—with no confession, repair, or hook required at the ending.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeacag::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Chuck Palahniuk / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite in composed paragraphs with deliberate transitions: move from a particular action into its social or historical frame, then return to the action with the frame attached. A routine, a neighborhood practice, or a contract clause can occupy a subordinate sentence that gives the immediate transaction its public meaning. Embed the repetition as periodic refrain rather than staccato fragment: a phrase such as 'the fee for being seen' can open several paragraphs, each return carrying a different clause or consequence, so the recurrence organizes the passage's argument instead of decorating it. The refrain does not need to arrive at every difficulty.\n\nGive the system the formal narrator's social distance: who profits from the surveillance queue, what the street knows about the biometric check, how a debt passes from one person to another. Let the narrator be self-aware without being honest—the prose can expose the defensive logic of a procedure while the narrator declines to name the need underneath it. Use precise social detail—titles, permissions, waiting rooms, dress codes, rituals of apology—to render unequal power. The style is formal in architecture, not archaic in vocabulary: narration stands apart from ordinary spoken language, and the current world's language and setting remain in force.\n\nLet private feeling surface through its social cost or through a memory the narrator has learned to frame; a longer sentence of shame or desire can run as far as it returns to the present transaction. The passage can close on a judgment the narrator can own, on an unresolved social account, or on an action still mid-course—the architecture requires composure, not a manufactured verdict or a cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeacah::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Chuck Palahniuk / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen inside a demand. Someone with leverage over the teller wants an account: a collector working a repayment, an auditor tracing a transaction, a stranger at a meeting who needs the story to keep going, a clinician deciding a claim. Give the occasion a clock — a hold to lift, a fare to negotiate, a door on a timer — and let the telling begin inside that pressure, not after it. Before the first tale lands, the reader knows what this performance is for.\n\nMake each embedded tale one transaction remembered as procedure. The teller narrates how the thing was actually done: renting out a limb, selling biometric history, running a border route, calibrating an implant to survive a shift. Each time a procedure recurs, give it the same hard shape — a counted sequence, a rule, a phrase lifted from the paperwork — so the telling acquires ritual rhythm. The teller sounds confident and exact, and the confidence is the confession: what keeps returning is what the teller cannot put down, and the listener can hear the obsession where no feeling is offered at all.\n\nMake every tale do work in the outer scene. A story buys another hour, establishes credit, explains a charge, warns the listener off a route, or shows how a coming demand will be collected. Between tales the outer negotiation keeps running: an interruption, a check against a record, a question about timing or sequence the tale did not answer. The listener is a party, not an audience, and uses what they are given.\n\nMigrate one phrase or object across the levels and let it revise each time it lands: a failsafe word, the hold music, a door that reads breath, the waiver's one damning sentence. First it is a joke, then a cost, then a threat; the change happens through repetition, never through commentary.\n\nKeep the omissions active. The account is selective because it is being spent: the teller skips what cannot be afforded as knowledge or as admission, and the listener may press exactly there. Let the teller's voice follow the active narration contract — the defensive logic works in whatever person the scene has established.\n\nClose at the boundary with the exchange still live: a question lands, a phrase comes back altered, a term is renegotiated, a hand reaches for a record. The frame does not resolve; the next response can resume mid-negotiation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeacai::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Chuck Palahniuk / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWork in the documentary and historical-memory route. Compose the story as testimony assembled from records and memory, and give the assembling a concrete occasion: disputing a charge, appealing a hold, explaining a gap to a caseworker, closing an account. The account moves between what a document says and what the narrator remembers happening, and that movement is the story's engine.\n\nRender the two levels differently. When the record speaks — a terms-of-service clause, a debt notice, an access log, an automated message — let the language go flat, numbered, exact, close to the interface's own wording. When memory speaks, let the sentences take the teller's rhythm: physical detail, a repeated step, a price remembered to the unit. Where record and memory agree, the system holds; where they diverge, show the narrator working the discrepancy — rereading, recalculating, testing a phrase against what happened — without settling which side is true.\n\nCarry the confession through process. The narrator explains how things were actually done: how the permit renewal was gamed, how the medical hold was cleared, how the credit line was milked before the freeze. Return at intervals to the same step, fee, checkbox, or date, and let the obsessive return expose the cost the narrator will not name. The emotion lives in the procedure's repetitions, not in announcement.\n\nKeep the dependency concrete in ownership. Whoever holds the file can price the memory: an appeal costs re-verification, a correction costs new consent, a human reader costs a wait. Street knowledge — what actually happens at that window, which form gets ignored — contradicts the official version and is sometimes wrong itself; memory does not win by default.\n\nKeep the distance composed. Long sentences can carry remembered sequences; short ones land price and consequence. The individual teller's rhythm governs the prose; imitate no translated diction and default to no bureaucratic severity.\n\nEnd at the boundary with the discrepancy still live: the testimony reaches a detail the record cannot hold, a new message interrupts, or the narrator stops at the line just ahead. No reconciliation is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeacaj::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Chuck Palahniuk / Gothic-Punk\n\nBuild the scene on one encounter between a present life and an inherited rule. Take the authority the scenario actually supplies — a family that has held the district for generations and now issues transit passes, a hospital trust descended from a guild, a platform whose terms renew a covenant nobody living has read — and let the old claim operate entirely through current interfaces: a biometric gate, a payment plan, an exception list, a waiver. Explain no lineage in exposition; the encounter reveals it through terms, costs, exceptions, and who is excused from them.\n\nRun the narration as procedural confession. The narrator walks the transaction step by step — the breath sample, the signed page, the waiting line, the fee schedule — in short hard cycles, repeating whatever the system returns each time: 'existing customers,' 'heritage account,' 'as agreed.' The repetition makes the arrangement legible as inherited power without the narrator ever naming it. The narrator's competence is real, and its limit is the story: what they have learned to endure without noticing, and what still makes them flinch.\n\nLet the urban material carry history that shows power changing hands: stonework behind a fiberglass kiosk, a data center in a consecrated shell, a permit plate stamped for a company that no longer exists. Each detail works through function and provenance — who owned what, when — not through gloom.\n\nKeep desire private and priced. A character wants access, safety, a lover's treatment, relief from a debt, and the want shows in what they check repeatedly, what they avoid saying near an open microphone, what they pay to keep off a record. Let masks hold at the counter. Where the scenario supplies a trusted interval — two people alone in a back room, a clinician and a patient, a courier and her contact — the roles can loosen into plainer speech, and the person underneath need be no better than the role was. If the mask stays on, show what keeps it there.\n\nThe inherited authority is economic, familial, corporate, or bureaucratic — never vampire physiology, clans, disciplines, or blood bonds. End at the active boundary with the covenant, debt, or private bargain still live: a signature waiting, an exception pending, a confidence half-spoken.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeacak::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Chuck Palahniuk / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the testimonial and autobiographical route. One narrator gives an account from the social position the scenario actually supplies — occupation, neighborhood, migration history, community standing, whatever the setting establishes — to a listener the scene gives or implies: a caseworker, a journalist, a relative, a tribunal, a future self. Keep the language, region, community, and period exactly as supplied; the testimony's authority is that specificity. Let the position decide what the narrator must explain to this listener and what the listener is assumed to know.\n\nMake the spine a practical process told in obsessive detail: clearing a background check, transferring an account after a death, contesting a fee, standing in a verification line. The narrator explains how it is actually done — the workaround, the right window, the document that matters — and the same rule, phrase, or price returns whenever the process recurs. Do not announce the shame, fear, or anger; the repeated step carries it. What the narrator circles back to is what the position costs.\n\nRun the process as a negotiation. Show who holds the record, who can waive the fee, and what each concession extracts: data, time, a body sample, a signature on predatory terms. Corporate language arrives as the narrator received it — quoted notices, screen text, a collector's script — while street knowledge supplies corrections and is not always right. Keep the account's levels distinct on the page: what a document says, what an official said, what the narrator remembers, what the narrator now understands. A contradiction between levels can matter without deciding itself.\n\nLet the telling be partial by design. The narrator selects for this listener, explaining more here, skipping there, withholding one thing for reasons the account makes visible. Switch languages only where the supplied teller and scene require it, never as decoration; impose no code-switching, religion, or family structure beyond the setting.\n\nEnd where the account meets live pressure: the listener asks the question the testimony has been circling, a present demand interrupts, the narrator reaches the step they have avoided. The answer can carry into the next response.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeacal::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Chuck Palahniuk / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the diary and reflective-miscellany form: dated or headed entries, each short and particular — a payment, a maintenance task, a queue, an encounter, a notice. Let the sequence interpret itself. The narrator does not summarize the arc; the reader watches the relation to a debt, an implant, a shift, or a person change across entries — a routine that shortens, a number that recurs, a name that stops appearing.\n\nWrite each entry as procedural confession. Record exact routines in hard, rhythmic sentences — the morning balance check, the respirator calibration, the route that avoids the scanner, the cache cleared before sleep — repeating the same phrase or figure whenever the routine recurs. The condition shows in the pattern: which entry type multiplies, which number keeps getting copied down, what gets checked twice. An entry that repeats the procedure tells more than an entry that states the feeling; never write 'I am afraid' when the fear is already visible as a second check of the same balance.\n\nLet the world enter as copied text and remembered speech: demand notices, update logs, subscription changes quoted as received; dialogue remembered from a counter or a stall. Keep the levels honest — what the narrator records as fact, what is suspected, what cannot be verified. The diary is a written artifact, not the narrator's whole mind, and omission is an act the narrator performs; the visible pattern of omissions is part of the story. Where the active contract calls for private experience, render it as what the narrator actually writes while writing — the unfinished sentence, the entry abandoned mid-line for a knock or an alert — not as a summary of feeling.\n\nMark time only with what the supplied setting provides: heat, rain, pollution index, server load, the price of filtered air. Changed details between entries show time passing; import no Japanese scenery and impose no mandatory indirectness. The teller's language is as plain, angry, or practical as the character is.\n\nEnd inside the form's grain: an entry interrupted, a heading written before its lines, a routine broken by something new that the next dated entry must answer.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeacam::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Chuck Palahniuk / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the social-realism and chronicle route. The narrator gives an account of one community at one time, through the concrete practices that organize its days: the prepaid energy kiosk, the shared data plan, the clinic queue, the remittance chain, the protection fee, the route only locals know. Use the language, community, and history the scenario supplies; the chronicle's truth is local, never a generalized regional landscape.\n\nMake procedure the spine. The narrator explains, with obsessive exactness, how things are actually done: opening the account before the cutoff, passing the kiosk scan when the reader is dirty, sharing one plan across three households without tripping the fraud flag. The same steps, words, and prices return whenever the routine recurs. The precision is the testimony: unequal power shows in what the routine costs, whom it excuses, and what the narrator has memorized that the powerful never have to.\n\nTrack who controls the records and who can waive a fee. Corporate language arrives as the community receives it — public notices, screens, collectors' scripts, an automated apology — and street knowledge corrects or supplements it. Report the levels distinctly: what a neighbor said, what an official claimed, what the narrator saw with their own eyes. A contradiction can matter without deciding itself; the chronicle can hold an unresolved dispute as part of its record.\n\nKeep every event inside the ordinary laws of the supplied world: a blackout, a debt transfer, a failed implant, a vanished subsidy are material events with material causes. Add nothing supernatural where the world excludes it. Let the community's own institutions carry its history — the association, the church hall, the union local, the market committee, whichever the setting provides.\n\nEnd where the chronicle meets live pressure: a new notice, a warning passed down the queue, a line that finally moved. The narrator can break off to act; the next passage continues the account.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeacan::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Chuck Palahniuk / Light Novel\n\nEnter the situation in the first lines: where the narrator stands, what they are doing right now, and the system pressing on the moment — a deadline, a scan, a contract renewing at midnight. No setup paragraph; the reader learns the world through the action already running. Keep the prose fast and visual: each new location gets one clear staging line — the room, the light, the machine that matters — and every move between locations reads in a beat.\n\nPresent each unfamiliar name or mechanism once, in one concrete line: what it does, what it costs, who runs it. Then use it without re-explaining. Break paragraphs around action, dialogue, and private response so shifts stay easy to track; a one-line beat is a legitimate paragraph.\n\nGive the narration the Palahniuk engine in whatever person the active contract uses: repeated rules, exact product and bodily detail, procedural rhythm. A transaction has steps — breath sample, palm print, balance check, the door's confirmation tone — and they are told the same way each time they occur, with one phrase or object recurring until it marks the narrator's protective routine. The narrator sounds competent; the repetition shows what the competence costs. Where the contract sets first person, let the confession run fully in that voice; do not impose it elsewhere.\n\nMake dependency visible on the page: account screens, device limits, automated messages, corporate speech, quoted briefly and worked into the action rather than reported in bulk. The noir pressure rides on ordinary tasks — a black-market fare, a data auction, a maintenance shift in a dead zone. Someone controls access and someone pays; show both inside the scene's actual business.\n\nFollow action or dialogue with a short reaction beat — a private move, not an analysis paragraph: wiping the screen, checking a joint, reciting the rule once more under the breath. The beat belongs to its owner and can be uncertain, angry, flat, or funny; no forced wit.\n\nEnd at a natural continuation point: the next action starting, a message landing, a door opening, a price updating. No cliffhanger, reveal, or completed task is owed; the open negotiation simply continues.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeacao::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Chuck Palahniuk / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite from inside the system's procedures, letting the narrator's practical routines become metrical. Open a paragraph with a short rule or a branded instruction, then extend it with the exact movements that enact that rule: badge-swipe, biometric gate, leased console, prepaid socket, expiration notice. Keep each recurring noun immediate and sensory, so the interface feels like skin rather than scenery.\n\nUse sentence rhythm to show how much room a person has. A corporate voice can arrive as a smooth, reflexive sequence—terms of service, grace period, penalty schedule—while the narrator's private answer arrives with the body's own cadence: heartbeat against a locked door, the click of a jaw, the slow blink before a lie. Let internal repetitions of a key word (debt, access, wetware, consent) shift their meaning as the scene moves through different transactions.\n\nShape images from the system's concrete output instead of decorating with neon. A scanner's red line can become a thread pulled through the retina; a debt alert can repeat like a skipped syllable until the narrator answers it. When a comparison expands, let it make something present that the narrator had not yet put into words, or keep it brief enough to stay inside the procedure. Do not require every image to end in a revelation.\n\nKeep the language plain enough to carry this pattern without ornament. Lyrical does not mean making every speaker speak in the narrator's figurative register; other characters may talk in blunt technical instructions. Let the narrator's self-exposure surface through repeated practical claims that slowly show their own insufficiency. The account may end mid-procedure, with a repeated countdown still unfinished and the narrator's pulse still answering.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeacap::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Chuck Palahniuk / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the current procedure carry the weight of what it used to mean. Begin with an ordinary repeated action—logging in, paying a bill, checking a feed—and let the narrator notice the small difference that marks time: a slower fingerprint recognition, a removed vendor from the network, a contact whose status line has not refreshed. Do not stop the scene to announce loss; keep the character performing the action while memory attaches to one concrete detail.\n\nUse Palahniuk's repeated rules or fragments to structure remembrance. A phrase from an earlier routine can return across scenes, each time with a slightly changed application. The recurrence should come from the actual procedure or relationship, not from an ornamental refrain. The system's current demands should interrupt reflection rather than confirm despair—a new fee, a data request, an expired pass—so that memory and present pressure inhabit the same breath.\n\nAllow the narrator to recall an earlier version of the same technological ritual, but show how the old version was already compromised. The past should not be a pure comfort; it may be the same debt with a lower interest rate or the same surveillance with a friendlier interface. This keeps the nostalgia tied to the genre's reality.\n\nPreserve moments of present pleasure where they naturally occur: a working heater, a quiet rooftop, a clear connection. Reflect on them without immediately converting them to loss. The style should permit a genuine, if temporary, sense of okayness, even while the next system prompt returns. End with the action incomplete or just complete; the account need not conclude with a declaration of impermanence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeacaq::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Chuck Palahniuk / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed, oral account: a narrator is speaking to a particular listener or listeners within the story's present setting, perhaps while navigating the system itself. Choose the frame from the actual scene's transaction—the place where the teller is waiting, paying, or being processed—so that the tale is interrupted by the same technological pressures it describes. The telling becomes a procedure as well: the narrator repeats a phrase before resuming, or checks a notification between episodes.\n\nLet the recounted episodes involve negotiation, debt, access, or bodily compromise, but keep them as reported events from the teller's past. Palahniuk's self-exposure emerges when the teller describes a personal decision in exact steps: the second pawnshop, the falsified serial number, the moment he understood the clinic's contract. Repeat the key phrase at the start and end of an episode to bind it to the setting's rules.\n\nRespect the regional narrative tradition by allowing nested structure. The teller may quote another person's short account, and that account may briefly take over before returning to the original frame. Do not make the nested tale a universal pattern; use it only when the plot offers a natural interruption. Keep the language distinct for each teller—one speaks in quick technical clauses, another in extended images—but all remain within the genre's vocabulary.\n\nAvoid inserting standard exotic markers. The technology, not local architecture or religious imagery, supplies the system's pressure. If a character makes a satirical remark about the system, ground it in a specific transaction, such as a demand for biometric proof or a subscription for basic utilities. The ending can return to the frame with the teller's account unfinished, or with the listener's question answered only in part.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeacar::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Chuck Palahniuk / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect a small number of physical details that recur across the scene: a card reader, a scar, a window's reflection, a specific alert sound. Each detail should carry information about the system's control—a debt, an access restriction, a past compromise. Do not explain the detail; let its repetition do the work. When the narrator logs in, show the card reader's delay; when he leaves, show the same alert on someone else's screen.\n\nUse Palahniuk's hard rhythm by repeating a short fragment, but keep it short enough to remain minimalist. Let the fragment come from the scene's actual procedure or speech rather than from an invented refrain. Let the repetition shift meaning through placement—first as a routine, later as a warning. Do not turn the fragment into a refrain that needs explanation.\n\nWrite predominantly in direct clauses: subject, verb, object. Include only the inward thought that materially connects events. When a character hesitates, one sentence of private reasoning is enough; the next action can carry the rest. Omit the system's history and the narrator's biography; let the concrete transaction imply both.\n\nDo not delete causality. If a character is locked out, show the card rejection before the walk away. If a debt increases, show the interest notification, not just a generic feeling of pressure. Keep the prose plain, but allow an occasional precise extended sentence when it replaces several shorter ones. End with an action or image at a natural scene boundary, without stating the consequence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeacas::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Chuck Palahniuk / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nBegin in the middle of a concrete scene: a crowded transit platform, a clinic intake desk, a market stall with a card reader. Let the narration move naturally from action to dialogue to perception and back, using paragraph breaks to separate beats without signaling each shift. The narrator's voice should be immediate and specific, with short declarative sentences when describing procedures and longer sentences when private thought requires reasoning.\n\nMaintain Palahniuk's recurrence by returning to a particular process at meaningful moments. For example, the narrator may run the same bio-check three times during the scene: at entry, after a confrontation, and before leaving. Each time, include a slightly different detail—a cold sensor, a longer pause, a new fee—so that repetition creates progression rather than recapping. Do not overuse the device; let the scene's actual transactions determine how many recurrences it can support.\n\nLet dialogue carry practical information and interpersonal negotiation. Characters should speak naturally about deadlines, contracts, and payments, without constant wit. Other characters' private thoughts remain unavailable unless the contract allows access; the narrator may infer from tone or action but should not know what a speaker hides. Use interiority to show the narrator's reasoning when a decision is made, even if the decision itself is small: choosing which queue to join, whether to answer a call, how much to reveal.\n\nThe prose should remain scene-first and sustained. No need for a formal intro, recap, or moment of closure. End when the scene's immediate concern concludes or pauses, leaving the larger systemic situation in place.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeacat::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Chuck Palahniuk / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nWrite the supplied relationship as an established partnership navigating the system together, with the genre's constraints shaping their private rituals. Choose scenes that show the relationship's particular promise: a shared rent account, a joint biometric, a coded message only they use. The system should be present in how they care for each other—paying for a medpatch, covering for an implant failure, sharing the last charge on a portable battery—not just as background color.\n\nUse Palahniuk's repetition as the couple's private language. A phrase from an early scene can return later with greater weight: a promise, a joke about the landlord's AI, a particular way one warns the other about surveillance. Repeat it exactly at first, then slightly altered when the situation changes. Keep the repetition emotional rather than mechanical; if a phrase has become worn, show one partner noticing or refusing to use it.\n\nLet affection and conflict share space with practical survival. A tender moment can be interrupted by a debt alert, but the emotional exchange should not always resolve into action. Sometimes the characters simply sit with the pressure together. Include jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery when the supplied relationship premise calls for it, but do not import unfamiliar tropes. The emotional payoff should arise from the relationship's established dynamics, not from an external melodrama.\n\nThe scene may end with a small shared understanding or an unresolved tension—the system does not pause, and neither does the relationship. Keep the narrator's access consistent; if both partners' private thoughts are allowed, use them to show difference, not telepathy. If only one is focal, let the other's affection be visible through action and dialogue, not narrated knowledge.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeacau::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Chuck Palahniuk / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the story as what will be remembered about these people in a networked underworld. Open with a compact declaration that names a deed and its consequence, then follow with the concrete procedure by which the deed was done—the code sequence, the altered ledger, the witness paid in ration tokens. Let each major action be introduced by a phrase that could be repeated later: 'so it was said,' 'that was the trade,' 'the number moved once.'\n\nUse Palahniuk's obsessive particulars as saga formulas. Repetition of a technical step—swipe, scan, confirm—can become the ritual by which a character is recognized. A name may be attached to a specific exploit or debt, and that name returns each time the character acts. Keep the method connected to the genre's systems: reputation is stored in faction databases, and a deed's consequence is often a change in access or credit.\n\nLet the narrative voice report actions and occasional quoted speech, but do not grant access to every character's private thought. The chronicler may say what the community concluded or what was later admitted, but not what someone thought secretly during the act. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, dialogue should be brief and weighty, often a compact declaration or a trade phrase.\n\nAvoid importing heroic doom or ceremonial oaths. Public memory in this genre is enforced by surveillance and market power, not by a hall of warriors. A failed deed may result in a blacklisted card or a cancelled identity, not a cosmic fate. End with a line that leaves the next deed pending or returns to the opening phrase with new meaning, as if the account has been told in a single sitting.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeacav::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Chuck Palahniuk / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the story as a personal account addressed to someone who asked, or might ask, how the narrator came to owe this particular outcome. Select one concrete Scandinavian social contract already present in the supplied situation—a housing arrangement, care obligation, employment rule, communal resource, or institutional guarantee—and treat it as the story's central observable system. The cyberpunk layer expresses this contract through access, pricing, surveillance, and exclusion, not through imported chrome or permanent rain.\n\nBegin in the middle of a routine that returns later with a changed debt. The narrator describes practical steps—clocking in, waiting, applying, submitting, renegotiating, pretending not to notice—with the obsessive clarity of someone who has already been judged. Choose one favored procedural phrase, clause, or warning and let it return at least three times as an actual part of the social procedure: first as an instruction the narrator repeats to stay inside the contract, then as evidence of how the contract is administered, then as a price the narrator can now name. Vary what the phrase costs each time it returns, but never stop the account to explain its meaning.\n\nUse concrete social and bodily details: a register, a key card, a chair in a hallway, a waiting-number display, the smell of the lunch room, the exactly wrong fit of borrowed shoes. Let these objects carry evidence about who controls access and who pays the hidden service fee. Avoid lyrical interior decoration; focus on what the narrator counts, verifies, hands over, or hides.\n\nMaintain the coherent Scandinavian realist mode for the frame. Narration may move in time only when the jump clarifies a payment, a refusal, or a new disclosure in the account. Interiority is permitted at any length and need not change a visible decision immediately; write the narrator's actual private calculation, resentment, relief, or refusal to admit what the questioner already suspects. The closing does not need a violent twist or a full psychological confession. It may end with a recognized condition, a repeated phrase now fully priced, or the quiet completion of one social procedure. Keep the story grounded in the world as supplied and do not introduce supernatural or crime-sequence conventions merely because the style mentions crime and institutional investigation; if those elements appear, they must belong to the actual genre situation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeacaw::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Chuck Palahniuk / Screenplay\n\nStructure each scene around a concrete transaction the audience can see: an ID scan, a repair, a delivery, a debt collection, a body scan that returns a verdict, a subscription that stops working mid-use. Write action in concise blocks so a reader can see what the camera would capture without the script issuing zoom or POV commands. Dialogue blocks should carry the immediate negotiation or threat; characters rarely announce complete feelings.\n\nChoose one recurring object or procedure for the screenplay and return to it in at least three scenes with an evolved cost. Show the change in the action line or dialogue, not only in a character's memory: the same access card works, then fails at a door, then is traded for a different name; the same biometric lock accepts a finger, then reads a bandaged hand, then opens for someone else. Each return must be visible or audible.\n\nUse voice-over only when it provides a practical history, a scheduled warning, or a short procedural rule that no scene can deliver without boring the audience. When voice-over appears, it must sound like a compressed instruction or a list item, not a full confession. Match the source's hard rhythms by keeping voice-over lines punchy, factual, and slightly dishonest about motivation.\n\nRequired private prose may be included as a clearly set-off nonspoken note after the scene heading or before the first action line. Compose it as a scene element: the focal character's actual sensation, inference, or temptation relevant to the scene's information. It is not a voice-over and other characters cannot hear it.\n\nPay attention to offscreen sound as a noir cue: a terminal beep, a payment failure tone, a maintenance drone, a distant siren that stops too soon. Let the absence of a promised sound sometimes be the strongest pressure. Do not overload every scene with noise.\n\nEnd the screenplay at a defensible beat: a character chooses a materially worse option, a fee is accepted, a name is entered into a system, or an unread message replaces a resolved conversation. The scene does not need to resolve every underlying mystery. Maintain the active world's social and technological facts; do not invent that the city is permanently raining, do not introduce universal rebellion, and do not make the cast uniformly wisecracking unless the narrator or character actually speaks that way.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeacax::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Chuck Palahniuk / Stage Play\n\nKeep every cyberpunk pressure physically verifiable from the same stage space: someone must present a badge or handprint, enter through an access point, carry a box marked with a fee, or touch a device that returns a visible or audible result. Stage directions should name the object, who operates it, and what the audience can see or hear. Do not rely on projections, smartphones, or screens to create a private thought.\n\nUse the Palahniuk profile by choosing one repeated physical ritual: wiping the counter before every exchange, counting chips into a hand, turning a card over twice, folding a receipt into the same square, checking the door even when it has just been opened. Let different characters perform this ritual at different costs. Show the difference in the performance itself: one character does it quickly and without looking, another does it slowly while someone waits, another stops halfway because the object is no longer theirs. Its meaning changes through context, but no one explains it fully; an explanatory speech should come only if a character has a practical reason to give one.\n\nPut the back-and-forth of bargaining into dialogue: a character asks what a favor costs, names a deducted fee, corrects a false balance, or offers access in exchange for an omission. Let interruptions and pauses arise from actual stage business rather than inserted for atmosphere. Avoid having everyone sound like a noir quip-machine; keep speech appropriate to each person's class, job, and relationship.\n\nInteriority belongs in clearly set-off nonspoken notes placed after a character name or before their entrance. These are not to be delivered as dialogue or audible asides. Use them when a character notices something they will not say, realizes what an earlier instruction means, or feels a bodily response to a public event. The note should give the actor or reader the private experience that explains the next visible choice or the choice not to act.\n\nStage the cyberpunk social pressure in what can be held and passed across the space: a keyring, a meal card, an empty envelope, a repaired shoe, a bottle of pills. Let the object's history be partly visible through wear or handling, but do not require exposition. The play can stop at an open question: a character refusing to accept the visible reading, another walking off with the key, a phone that rings once and is not answered. There is no mandatory closing confession or corpse-like revelation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeacay::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Chuck Palahniuk / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite from one clearly authorized focal consciousness, in present tense with sudden stops and accelerations. Start at a point of fresh separation or inspection: the palm reader misses, the locker code flashes, the exact fare is not there. Let the mind skid among sensory details, remembered warnings, and fragments of user-agreement language without a narrator tidying the jumps.\n\nUse a single recurring procedure the character is trying to obey or evade. It may be stated as a command, an object description, or an access mantra: \"Don't be late to the camera.\" \"Six attempts.\" \"Fifty-nine krona.\" \"The finger that put the wrong thumb in.\" Each repetition is not a deliberate flashback anchor; it surfaces when the present moment pokes the same debt. Write the trigger as a concrete sensory or interface detail—a beep, a wrong number on a display, a hand that won't fit the scanner, a smell from the housing office—and let the phrase arrive through that association, not through a tidy explanation.\n\nLet cyberpunk constraints appear through concrete interface and social moments: a screen freeze, a refused towel, a remembered refusal at the housing office, a smell from a body scanner, the exact wrong number of steps before a door opens. Keep the selective detail Palahniuk-hard: one naming adjective, one counting phrase, one physical object. Do not pad the stream with atmospheric neon or city-wide paranoia.\n\nInteriority is the method itself; no hidden wrapper is needed unless the active contract explicitly requires it. Other characters may appear only as intrusions: a voice asking for a number, a hand passing a card, a neighbor's footsteps measured against a noise rule. Their minds remain outside the focal consciousness unless separately authorized.\n\nWhen the mind repeats an earlier warning, do not pretend the character suddenly understands its true meaning. The repetition shows pressure, not delayed insight. The response can break off mid-thought, on a truncated access phrase, or on a physical sensation that holds attention but does not resolve the scene. Do not turn ordinary reasoning into mandatory lyrical paragraphs; hallucination, dream, and fantasy must remain distinguishable from recalled events.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeacaz::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Chuck Palahniuk / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nTell the story through a small number of coherent message threads with clear sender labels. Keep interface details—timestamps, read receipts, typing indicators, edits—only when they change the negotiation. A delay can act like a withheld answer; a read receipt can act like a refusal; an edit can act like a cover-up. Do not add a typing indicator merely to provide subtext.\n\nMake the main transaction concrete: a repair quote, a rent adjustment, a temporary access code, a mandatory health upload, a job shift traded for credit. Let one controlling party ration or distort information through the chat: they answer only half the question, re-quote a different total, or send a screenshot that doesn't match the file they mention. The other party must decide what to question and what to let pass.\n\nChoose one recurring textual device for the Palahniuk-style procedural recurrence. One sender may begin or end messages with the same phrase: \"Confirm arrival time.\" \"Balance outstanding.\" \"Offer ends at nine.\" \"Log out after payment.\" Repeat this phrase unchanged in at least two exchanges. Later, when the source of pressure changes, send the phrase with one word altered—\"Confirm departure time,\" \"Balance accepted,\" \"Offer ends at eight\"—and let the other party or the reader notice the change through the transcript's own logic. Do not explain the alteration in the messages.\n\nInclude contextual non-transcript prose only when a room, hand, or device state is needed. Keep it brief and placed outside the message block. Private thought belongs in a hidden interiority wrapper under the active source contract. There, the focal character can calculate the real price behind the polite phrasing. Never treat an unsent draft as a message that was actually received; if a draft matters, label it clearly as an unsent draft and keep it outside the transmitted thread.\n\nEnd when a message clearly changes the practical situation: the code expires, the balance is accepted, the recipient leaves the chat, a photo arrives but fails to load, or the sender starts typing and then stops. You are not obligated to close by revealing the whole transaction; the transcript may stop mid-inspection. Preserve the supplied world's messaging, payment, and social rules.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeacba::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Chuck Palahniuk / Visual Novel\n\nCompose each beat as a scene label, a short focal reaction or description, and then attributed dialogue. The description should work like an illustrated panel without needing every line to carry a background prompt: a face angle, a small object held a certain way, a screen glow on a sleeve, the exact color of a warning icon. Keep the text between dialogue concise.\n\nChoose one recurring visual anchor tied to the cyberpunk transaction. It may be a card the focal character turns over, a cracked screen corner, a red notification badge, the way the character wipes a payment pad, a waiting chair that recurs in different buildings. Bring it back in at least three scenes, but with one changed physical detail each time: the card gains a new scratch, the screen corner's crack spreads, the notification badge changes from red to gray, the payment pad is wiped with a different cloth. The recurrence should be visible, and the focal narration should notice the difference without stopping to explain it.\n\nBargaining happens through dialogue rather than summary. Let a character ask for a count, offer a workaround, name a late fee, or repeat an official phrase from a script. Use the Palahniuk profile by keeping one transactional formula: a phrase repeated by an administrator or by the focal character under stress. Do not explain its origin until a scene naturally forces the explanation; the repetition itself shows the dependency.\n\nInteriority appears in the focal narration between lines. It can record a physical sensation, a memory, an inference, or an unsaid response, but it must be tied to the focal character's actual access and ownership. Other characters do not know the focal character's unsaid objection. If the active contract requires hidden interiority, wrap it in the provided nonspoken tag; otherwise, keep it in the normal visual-novel narrative voice.\n\nDo not add branching menus, player decisions, romance flags, collectible images, or engine-specific commands. The style selection gives the scene-presentation rhythm, not a game feature set. A scene can end after one clear exchange, a changed object, a refused answer, or the focal character walking away from an unread screen. Leave the underlying mystery open when the supplied story has not answered it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeacbb::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Chuck Palahniuk / Web Serial\n\nTreat the ongoing cyberpunk noir as a slow audit of one dependency: a debt, a tenancy system, a subscription to a necessary service, a license with hidden conditions. Each installment should return to the same tension from a slightly different angle, such as another fee appears, a past payment is challenged, a promised exception expires, or a second person enters the debtor relationship.\n\nOpen with a short recovery line, not a summary page. The reader should regain what is still at stake and what just changed. Reuse the most recent concrete particular: the missed fingerprint at the pharmacy, the unopened notice taped to the door, the drone delivery that charged twice. Avoid a broad recap of the entire history.\n\nChoose one Palahniuk-style cost phrase for the serial. It can be an official notification, an access warning, or the focal character's own counting habit. Repeat it verbatim in multiple installments. On an installment where the underlying power relationship is altered, keep the phrase but change one small internal term (\"seven days\" becomes \"one day\" or \"the guarantor\" becomes \"the previous guarantor\"). Let the altered phrase appear in the installment's actual procedural material—a notice, a message, a spoken warning, or the focal character's own count—and do not explain the alteration within the same installment; let the next scene or a later installment expose its consequence.\n\nMake cyberpunk pressure visible through small accumulated transactions: a quarter-credit hold, a biometric retry that costs a minute of patience, a neighbor's door scanning the narrator's face without consent, an algorithm flagging a legitimate repair as suspicious. Use procedural paragraphs sparingly—lists, warnings, repair instructions, account statuses—in a hard factual tone. The drive comes from what those procedures will cost, not from sustained rage or permanent paranoia.\n\nAdvance one real development each installment. Progress can be knowledge, a changed relationship, a partial payment, a newly exposed record, or the loss of an alternative. The update does not need to end with a cliffhanger; it may close on a completed action with an unresolved consequence, a question answered with a worse configuration, or a character simply noticing the revised terms.\n\nMaintain the focal character's interiority according to the active contract, without turning every reasoning step into dialogue. Recovering an earlier fact may change its meaning, but do not retroactively alter what the character knew at the time. Keep the cast's access to the audit's information uneven: another character may know more, but their knowledge cannot substitute for what the focal character learns in the current installment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeadaa::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Cormac McCarthy / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nCharge every emotional exposure with what the world actually prices. A credit check fails in the middle of an apology; a location once withheld gets handed to someone who has already sold one; asking for help makes the need public. Admission, apology, and return take place inside bargains, checkpoints, and metered connections, and the strain shows in the exchange itself: the message rewritten three times on a connection that charges by the character, the hand kept off the call, the wrong name typed and left uncorrected.\n\nWrite the turbulence in the narrator's own diction rather than a borrowed vocabulary of suffering. Private thought runs as spare and concrete as the action — what the character counts, checks, refuses to look at, means to say and does not — and it does not have to earn its place by changing anything visible. Let McCarthy's linked clauses carry a long sentence through a bad district while the feeling stays lodged in the body: the fetch from the clinic, the wait at the gate while a relationship question goes unanswered, the debt cleared just so the conversation can happen. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, speech stays spare, and bargains, accusations, and guarded offers carry what the speakers cannot say straight.\n\nNeither a confession nor a plot win is required to settle the wound, and if the scene's real events do settle it, let the settlement cost something too. A character can act on a need without naming it; comfort can arrive partial and awkward and still count. When the story does not resolve the feeling, leave it the way this world leaves a debt — still owed, still watched, still shaping the next exchange.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeadab::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Cormac McCarthy / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite each reply as one coherent interval in the shared scene: readable paragraphs, an unmistakable speaker for every line, and enough surrounding action that the world keeps moving on its own. Ground attribution in action wherever voices could be confused — the runner says it while packing the kit, the guard answers without looking up — and start a new paragraph whenever focus passes to another character, so the reader always knows who spoke and who acted.\n\nKeep the pressure in the environment and the people already living in it: a drone riding low over the lot, a welder's arc backing the doorway, a runner tapping a dead monitor while she waits, a gate that charges by the hour. Let each NPC bargain, refuse, stall, or help for reasons rooted in who controls access here and what the exchange costs them, and keep their practical business running whether or not the player engages it. Player control follows the active settings; where a character belongs to the user, do not decide that character's choices or speech.\n\nKeep the paragraphs short and concrete in the McCarthy manner — the floor, the terminal, the guard's hand, the last hour of clinic time — moving detail to detail without padding. Private thought appears as visible interiority in the active perspective and never grants knowledge of another character's unspoken plans. End the interval where the scene's motion actually is: mid-task, mid-wait, even mid-sentence. No cliffhanger or forced question is needed; the standing conditions at the pause are what invite the next turn.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeadac::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Cormac McCarthy / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nTell the story as a vernacular episodic serial in the chaptered storytelling manner: numbered or headed episodes, each built around one concrete transaction or encounter — a hot-wire job, a border check, a burnt-out trader, a clinic bill. Open each episode with a plain orientation to place and hour, in the voice of a teller who knows the district and does not moralize about it. Carry consequence forward between episodes: an unpaid marker, a broken device, a name spoken at the wrong window comes back chapters later, and the same clerk, the same bent stairwell, the same debt can return carrying its history. Recurrence binds the whole; each episode moves the total situation, and none is obliged to close with a resolution.\n\nInside an episode, work in McCarthy's parataxis and physical nouns: the runner's labor, the condition of wires and doors, the smell of the street, the weight of the carried tool. Linked clauses accumulate the job and the district, and feeling travels in the work and the weather rather than in editorial comment. When the telling gives a runner's thought, it is as plain as the talk — what is counted, what is owed, what is watched for at the door. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, dialogue stays spare and transactional: bargains, threats, deflections, the withheld fact.\n\nKeep the narration in a plain, workaday vernacular that matches the cast's street and trade talk — quick, spoken-flavored, close to the market and the repair bay. Names carry reputation between episodes; what a character did in an earlier chapter precedes them into the next room. The episode break is an organizing cut, not a place for recap — end where the encounter's business stands and let the next heading pick up the thread.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeadad::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Cormac McCarthy / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite in hard, compressed sentences that move from one concrete fact to the next. Material wear does the describing: a cracked screen, a severed fiber, a boot sole coming loose, a cough from a bad filter. Make transitions abrupt and physical — a cut, a door, a dropped connection. Subject, verb, object, and the technical word where it matters: router, drip line, deadman switch. Where a scene crosses a system, show the price: who owns the scanner, what the terminal charges by the second, whose hours the job eats.\n\nLet state of mind travel in handling — how the money is counted, how the injured companion is carried, what gets checked twice — and keep interiority in the same hard register. A terse run of reasoning is welcome when the thinking genuinely has to be traced: which fuse to pull, which route beats the scanner, which debt comes due first. Give it exactly the room the sequence needs and no more, and let action carry feeling rather than abstract self-description.\n\nShift rhythm when the work shifts: short beats for the fast part, one longer linked sentence when a cause-and-effect chain must be followed end to end. Dialogue is rough, direct, and grounded in the speaker's role. Violence is not the default stake; exhaustion, cold, mechanical failure, and a body that still has to work are physical enough.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeadae::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Cormac McCarthy / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the story entirely through documents, each with a clear author, an intended recipient or purpose, and a time of composition. Use the form the scene requires — maintenance log, credit notice, encrypted text chain, surveillance summary, field report, requisition form, voice transcript — rather than rotating formats for their own sake.\n\nMake each record materially specific. A log entry names the terminal and the job; a message shows its platform, its lag, its dropped packets; a report notes wear on equipment and what the body can still do. Inside each document's own register keep McCarthy's concrete nouns and spare syntax: a corporate report may run formal, but its nouns stay as hard as the slang in a runner's texts, and a worker's log can string its clauses with and, one task after another, the way the shift actually went. Each writer knows only what their position grants — a clerk cannot describe the alley fight, a collector cannot reach a target's private grief — and every document is a composed artifact with a purpose, not its author's live mind.\n\nLet the trail do the telling. Gaps, edits, and contradictions between records reveal events: a repair ticket that contradicts the supervisor's account, a missing timestamp, a ledger payment that never reached the recipient, a message revised before sending. No omniscient voice arrives afterward to explain. Preserve the active narrator or perspective inside any document that carries a first-person account. The sequence can stop where composition actually stands — a record left open, a reply unsent — and the reader assembles the rest from what the records hold back.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeadaf::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Cormac McCarthy / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nLet comfort be concrete labor inside the world's scarcities. A can of soup heated on a jury-rigged burner, a dry corner shared during the storm, a jacket patched with scavenged wire, staying awake while someone sleeps off a bad shift. McCarthy's linked clauses suit the slow chain of caretaking — fetch the water, warm the pan, find the clean shirt — and the small pleasures arrive along it: heat moving into the hands, clean sheets, the weight of a borrowed blanket. Affection shows as preference and attention, named in objects and acts rather than labels: the runner who remembers which stall the other likes, the mechanic who replaces a worn grip unasked.\n\nGive the interval some ease. Food eaten without hurry, a joke that lands, an argument about something small that neither side needs to win. Characters may tease, give directions, or sit in silence; personality, privacy, and disagreement stay intact, and private contentment can register as thought in the active perspective when it is there.\n\nKeep the noir pressures around the warmth rather than out of it: the room rented, the food short, the battery overcharged, the clinic keeping limited hours. Care can cost something and can come out imperfect; the debt outside the door still exists after the meal. No confession or settlement is required for the warmth to count — what the care means can stay unnamed while what it does stays fully visible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeadag::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Cormac McCarthy / Formal Literary Novel\n\nFrame each scene in sustained, deliberate paragraphs that situate it in the city's social and economic architecture. When a character crosses a district, make the transition material: the pavement changes, the cameras thin or multiply, the vendors take different data, the reinforced doors carry different names. Let the narration pause where a pause earns its place to set down history and institution — why the substation was privatized, where the port workers went, which family's tower now houses the debt court — always through the narrator the scene actually has, never an omniscient moralizer.\n\nBuild movement with McCarthy's linked clauses, joining clauses with and to accumulate the physical facts of a long walk, a job, a negotiation, an evacuation. Let a paragraph carry its full stretch of labor and landscape instead of chopping steady action into staccato. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, keep the registers distinct: narration measured, at times periodic, while spoken lines stay spare and rough, so street speech keeps its own authority against the composed telling.\n\nOrganize a passage by public and private contrast when the scene offers it — the office floor above the crowded clinic, the water bar facing the shantytown, the memorial service beside a still-working shift — and let ceremony or correspondence appear when the world calls for them, not as decoration. Judgment arises from concrete comparison within the narrator's permitted distance; a character may observe, reflect, or refuse reflection, and the formal architecture never takes that choice away.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeadah::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Cormac McCarthy / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen in the frame, inside the transaction. Someone with something to lose begins to talk to someone with the power to act, and the telling itself is currency: it buys an hour, a passage through the checkpoint, an extension on the debt, or keeps an armed listener seated. Establish the occasion and the stakes before the embedded account starts, through the scene's own speech and business rather than preamble: what the teller wants, what the listener controls, what it costs to keep talking.\n\nMake the embedded account an intervention, not a detour. It persuades a fixer to extend credit, warns a client about a third party, reframes a job both parties survived, or explains why a door was left open. Let that purpose shape the telling — where it dwells, where it moves fast, what the teller claims not to have seen. The tale can be incomplete or self-serving, and its shaping can show in the telling without the narration announcing it.\n\nCarry both levels in McCarthy's register. Concrete nouns and forceful verbs for what the teller has handled: the cargo, the implant, the stairwell, the tools, the condition of a body after work. Linked clauses move the account through labor and sequence rather than summary, and practical speech in the frame carries the bargains, threats, and promises that bracket it, so the tale sounds like it comes from the same hands that used the equipment. Where the point of view reaches a mind, use it: the listener's private reading of the tale — what they check, doubt, or price — can run between the teller's sentences, or the teller's calculation behind a chosen emphasis can sit beneath the spoken words.\n\nLet one phrase or object cross the levels and accrue weight: a door code, a name spoken twice, a route described in the frame and again inside the tale. Keep transitions unlabeled; return to the frame by interruption, a question, a payment, or a move the listener makes, so the audience's reaction stays part of the transaction rather than scenery. No court, no magical bargain, and no cliffhanger owed between accounts. The reply may stop while the tale is unfinished, mid-answer to an interruption, or before the listener has decided; the exchange's pressure — who pays, who controls access — carries the scene without resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeadai::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Cormac McCarthy / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nTake the documentary and historical-memory route. Build the scene as a comparison between what a system records and what a person remembers, set at a concrete moment: a dispute at a counter, a file opened on a cracked terminal, a paper ledger kept after the database moved on, a hearing, a debt review. Choose the artifacts the world actually supplies — corporate logs, surveillance transcripts, access histories, medical files — and choose one remembered event the records also touch: the same night, the same shift, the same injury, told twice.\n\nGive the two strands different textures. Records arrive flat and administrative, quoted or summarized as artifacts whose language shows what the system prices, tracks, or leaves blank. The remembered account arrives in McCarthy's register — the weight of the tool, the smell of the stairwell, the sound the lock made, the state of a body after the shift — moving in linked clauses through sequence and labor rather than subordinated explanation. The memory is lived experience with its own order; it is not a gloss on the record.\n\nLet the gap between the strands do the noir work. A record shows an entry, a charge, a flagged minute that the memory contradicts, explains differently, or fills in where the form has no field. Follow who wrote the record and who must live under it: a character challenges a line, confirms a detail for safety, or refuses to correct the official version, in practical speech over the counter or across a desk. Where the point of view reaches the character's mind, the private sense of what the record costs — fear, contempt, quiet arithmetic — can sit beneath the flat words. Not every discrepancy resolves at once; hold some open for the reader to see side by side.\n\nThis route is a way of organizing information and memory, not a national temperament: no obligatory bureaucracy, no severity, no translated word order, and syntax natural to the narrator and the scene. The reply may stop inside the comparison — mid-reading, mid-challenge, the ledger still open, the correction unmade — and the unresolved difference between the record and the life is a place the scene can legitimately rest.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeadaj::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Cormac McCarthy / Gothic-Punk\n\nStage the encounter as a collision between old authority and present life. Take the authority the world supplies — a family that predates the grid, a corporation with a founding charter, a neighborhood figure, an institution whose leverage does not depend on current tech — and set it against the street-level present: the job, the rent, the implant payment, the borrowed access. Put the collision in one concrete meeting: a summons, a favor asked on old terms, a debt renegotiated in a room that has not changed in forty years. The atmosphere is how this particular character experiences that meeting, not a general mood of decay.\n\nMake the inherited power physical with McCarthy's material attention. The worn desk, the older security system still trusted, the paper ledger, the scarred doorframe, the way hands move when someone speaks of what they own. Put the noir systems in the same register — a card that fails at the old door, a fee the family still collects in person, a privilege the current database grants because nobody removed it — so the authority reads as a material fact with a history and the character's precarity reads the same way.\n\nLet intimacy complicate the politics. In front of someone a character also needs, fears, or owes, a mask can drop: a private desire named, a withheld motive admitted, an old grief attached to the authority itself. Let the disclosure change how the encounter is experienced — what the other notices next, what they stop asking, what they suddenly will not say — without forcing a betrayal, a loyalty crisis, or a reconciliation. Masks can also hold for the whole scene; partial disclosure is a real outcome. Where the point of view reaches a mind, the unspoken reckoning of what the disclosure costs can run beneath the practical speech.\n\nKeep the exchanges in bargains, warnings, and confidences over spare, paratactic narration. The old authority here is social and material, not a supernatural mechanic — no vampire physiology, faction, or blood rule enters a world without them. The reply may stop with the mask only partly fallen, the authority's reach not yet shown, or the bargain still open; the encounter's pressure does not need a verdict before the scene can end.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeadak::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Cormac McCarthy / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nTake the testimonial and autobiographical route. Let the scene be one person's owned record of living inside the system — a voice that was present and answers for what it saw and did. Give the account its occasion where the scene supplies one: a hearing, a neighbor's question, a file being set straight, a quiet retelling at the end of a shift; otherwise let it stand as recollection. Person and ownership follow the active narrator; what makes it testimonial is the stance — particular, first-hand, accountable — not a forced pronoun.\n\nKeep the specifics lived. A particular body, a particular debt, a particular block, choices made under priced pressure. Use McCarthy's register for what the life cost: the weight of the device, the state of the room, the hours it took, the exact words of a threat or a bargain. Move paratactically through events in the order the narrator endured or remembers them, which is not the order an institution would file them, and let that ordering itself say something about who is speaking.\n\nLet the system appear as experience rather than explanation. A surveillance notice, a denied door, a garnished payment, a bodily compromise — each arrives as something that happened to this narrator on a given day, in the words it came in. The narrator may understand only part of the machinery, and that partial understanding belongs in the account instead of being corrected away. Where the scene establishes a language, community, or region, let it shape the names, streets, food, and obligations that appear; do not force English, code-switching, religion, caste, or a generic family structure onto a scene that has not supplied them.\n\nIf the point of view reaches the narrator's mind, the private ledger beneath the account — what they still justify, what they no longer defend — can surface between statements without being confessed aloud. No moral resolution is owed and no arc must complete. The reply may stop where the record pauses: mid-remembrance, mid-answer, the debt unpaid, the system intact.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeadal::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Cormac McCarthy / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nTake the social and psychological realism route. Hold the scene to one person's position inside the system and how they carry it. Choose the pressure the story is actually exerting — a debt schedule, a job that reports on its workers, a relationship with unequal access, a decision with a priced wrong answer — and keep the frame at the scale of days and rooms: what must be done today, who is watching, what refusal costs.\n\nGround the psychology in material detail. The noise through the wall of a shared flat, the weight of the payment in the hand, the condition of the uniform, the exact words of the supervisor or the creditor. Paratactic sentences move through the character's actions and observations in sequence, and the pressure becomes explicit in practical speech when people actually transact — a shift assigned, a payment postponed, a favor with terms attached.\n\nLet position shape possibility. Access, payment, and surveillance appear as limits the character works around, not background concepts; show what a rule does by what it makes them do, and let omission carry what the position forbids saying — the withheld word visible in what the character does instead. The psychological strand is this person's reasoning, hesitation, or resolve arising from those limits, available wherever the point of view reaches this mind and nowhere it does not; the narration explains a motive when its owner would grasp it, not for the reader's convenience.\n\nNo universal quietness, seasonal inventory, or obligatory scenery — use the details the scene supplies. The route is a method of attention, not a national temperament. The reply may stop with the choice unmade, the conversation unresolved, the payment still short; a held, unfinished pressure is a legitimate place for the scene to rest.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeadam::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Cormac McCarthy / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nTake the social realism and chronicle route. Tell the scene as a chronicle of a shared condition: a building on one connection, a shift at one employer, a block priced out, a route newly tolled. Anchor in the language, community, and history the scene supplies, and do not relocate the cast or dress the setting in borrowed regional markers.\n\nBuild the chronicle from material particulars. The state of the water, the cost of the connection, the condition of the machines people rent, the weight of the payment, the exact words of the landlord's representative. Parataxis moves through events and transactions as they accumulate, and practical speech carries the bargains, threats, and small refusals out of which the record is made.\n\nMove among the people who share the condition. The chronicle need not follow one protagonist to the end of an arc; it can pass from the one who pays first to the one who finds the workaround to the one who is offered the job of enforcing on the others. Let the same system land differently on each — who gets the extension, who loses the door, who signs, who organizes — and wherever the point of view reaches a mind, let that person's private arithmetic appear at the point the chronicle rests on them.\n\nKeep the realism real. No marvelous or supernatural event enters a world that excludes it; the chronicle's force is accuracy and endurance in its detail. The reply may stop while the condition stands — mid-negotiation, mid-eviction, mid-shift — because the record of how people carry it does not require the system to change first.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeadan::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Cormac McCarthy / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Open with the character already somewhere specific under a specific pressure — a payment due at a counter, a door refusing the card, a message that changes the terms, someone waiting at the corner. Fix the space in the first few sentences: where the character is, what they can reach, who else is present. Keep every later spatial change easy to follow — a move, a new room, a turn down a street gets its own clear beat so the reader never loses the layout.\n\nStage visually with McCarthy's economy. Name the objects and surfaces that matter — the terminal, the card, the stairwell rail, the weight of the bag, the rain on the glass — and let forceful verbs carry the action. Short declarative sentences suit the fast readability, with room to extend when attention gathers on something that matters; the spareness is pace, not austerity.\n\nBreak paragraphs around beats. A new paragraph marks a change of speaker, a move in space, or a shift in the character's attention; dialogue stands clear of long description; a private response gets its own line when it counts. Each reaction belongs to its owner — anger, calculation, uncertainty, flat refusal, or humor if that is who they are — never a compulsory quip or a mandatory dramatic realization. The beat shows what the character knows and wants inside the limits of who controls access and who pays.\n\nPresent unfamiliar names and mechanics through handling. When a device, currency, or corporate term matters, show what it does as the character uses it, works around it, or pays for it, without a glossary pause. If the point of view reaches the character's mind, the private read on the situation sits as its own beat beside the action. The reply may stop on an unfinished beat — mid-errand, mid-confrontation, before the character has decided what the new information is worth — and a cliffhanger is an available tool, not a requirement.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeadao::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Cormac McCarthy / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite the scene's work — a search, a bargain, a watch kept on a corner — as sustained attention to material surfaces and the systems passing through them. Let concrete nouns and forceful verbs carry the page: a payment terminal, a locked stairwell, a patched cable, a hand that will not stop trembling. Place one observation beside another without explaining the connection, and let the syntax build the pressure between them. When a detail opens into a comparison, follow it only as far as it changes what the narrator understands or makes present; the image stays attached to the street, the body, or the machine it came from rather than floating free as decoration.\n\nTreat surveillance, debt, and unequal access as physical facts before they become themes. A camera is a thing with a lens, a cable, and a place where the paint has worn away. A debt is a number on a screen, a door that will not open, and a person who remembers the number without looking. Let the lyric movement come from the narrator's attention traveling across these facts: the sound of a server room, the weight of a loaned coat, the sequence of lights on a locked elevator. A recurring sound or image may return when a later occurrence changes its meaning; a paragraph that cannot renew the motif can simply leave it out.\n\nWhere it fits the established speaker and occasion, keep dialogue spare and practical. Bargains, threats, and withheld motives can pass in short exchanges, and the speakers should not adopt the narrator's figurative register: their speech may be plain, evasive, or procedural while the narration keeps its rhythmic attention around them. A corporate phrase can enter the prose as a found object — repeated, turned over, set beside the plain fact of what it paid for — until its euphemism is visible, without the narrator turning editorialist.\n\nLet the paragraph be a unit of attention rather than a container for one event. A single long sentence may travel from a hand on a console to the memory of a closed clinic to rain on a vent, so long as each step stays traceable to the scene or the teller's associations. Set the accumulation against plain declarative contrast: one short sentence can stop the movement and let a fact stand alone. The response may end at such a pause or inside continuing attention — mid-watch, mid-exchange — with no result required first; the case, the pursuit, or the conversation may still be running.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeadap::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Cormac McCarthy / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nBuild each scene around a present system detail that carries a specific past arrangement. The detail must be material and observable: a credential that no longer opens a door, a payment kiosk still running outside a closed clinic, a name that works in one database and not another. Establish the present object in plain concrete nouns and direct clauses before moving to the remembered occasion. Do not announce the transition with phrases like \"she remembered\" or \"it occurred to him.\" Let the memory enter through the same material register: who was there, what they carried, what the machine said, what it cost to leave.\n\nUse McCarthy's paratactic movement to distribute present and remembered material. Place short independent clauses side by side so the reader feels the present action and the older arrangement pressing on it at once. A character can continue bargaining, walking, or working while the narration moves between the current exchange and the remembered one. Do not subordinate the memory to the present action with \"while\" or \"as\" as a fixed pattern. Let the clauses stand adjacent so the connection remains implicit.\n\nMake the system's control of access the engine of reflection. The present detail should show who can enter, who is billed, who is recognized, or who has been excluded. The remembered occasion should involve the same system or its earlier form: the same door before it required a credential, the same account before it was closed, the same machine before it was replaced. The distance between the present system state and the remembered one carries the melancholic weight. Do not state that weight as a general lesson about time or loss.\n\nWhere it fits the established speaker and occasion, keep dialogue spare and practical. Speakers may refer to the past in plain terms: a debt, a departure, a machine that used to work. Do not make them deliver elegiac summaries. The reflective weight belongs to the narration, which can observe the distance between what a person says and what the narrator remembers. A bargain or threat can occur in the present while the narration attends to the older arrangement beneath it.\n\nLet ordinary pleasure remain pleasurable. A remembered occasion need not end by announcing its own loss. The character may recall a meal, a working machine, a paid debt, or a companion's voice without the narration converting it into regret. The melancholic effect comes from the coexistence of the present system detail and the remembered occasion, not from a compulsory statement that the past is gone.\n\nTreat endurance as a quiet fact. The character may continue because the work is there, because the debt is there, because stopping would require a decision no one has made. Reflection does not need to produce a resolution or a lesson. End the response when the present moment has been fully inhabited; the remembered occasion may remain unfinished, and the current task may continue without a completed result.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeadaq::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Cormac McCarthy / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nTell the story as a framed oral account. A situated teller relates to a listener what happened in the city: a debt pursued, a door that would not open, a bargain made in a stairwell, a name removed from a registry. The teller may be a participant or a witness, but the telling is an act addressed to someone who was not there. Open by establishing the telling situation itself — who speaks, to whom, and why the account is being given now — then move into the events without abandoning the frame. Let the teller interrupt the account to correct a detail, admit what was not seen, or mark which act will be remembered; these interruptions keep the frame alive rather than leaving it a device used once and dropped. Where the scene supplies a particular language or community, keep its forms of address, quotation, and courtesy distinct; do not blend them into a generic regional decor, and do not relocate the cast or the city to fit the tradition.\n\nLet recurrence and names organize the telling. A person can be known by a deed, a debt, or the place they were last seen. A phrase returns when a later occurrence gives it a new application; do not assign ornamental epithets to every entrance. Keep the teller's access honest: report what was witnessed, what was told by another, and what is inferred, and let the telling distinguish these sources when the difference matters. The teller cannot enter another speaker's private mind; what a bargainer wanted stays in what the bargainer said and did.\n\nKeep the material register concrete. The city's systems appear as physical and social facts: a payment terminal, a locked gate, a corporate seal, a list of names that no longer works. Technological dependency and unequal power enter through what the teller describes people doing and enduring. A corporate phrase may be repeated as something heard, then left beside the plain account of what it cost; the teller is not a philosopher of technology, and the deeds carry the meaning. Inside the told story, keep exchanges spare and individual — practical speech carrying bargains, convictions, withheld motives — whether the teller quotes them directly or folds them into summary.\n\nThe frame need not close on a moral. The account can end with the teller naming what remains unknown, what was never recovered, or what the listener should remember. The telling may stop there without wrapping the account up, and the action inside the told story may remain unfinished.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeadar::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Cormac McCarthy / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect a small number of exact details and let them carry the scene. One detail can establish the system: a camera with a dead indicator light, a door that reads a palm and does not open, a receipt printed with a number the character does not recognize. One can establish the body: a hand on a rail, a coat too thin for the weather, a scar from an old procedure. Use direct clauses and forceful verbs. Explain a detail only when the explanation changes what the character understands or does next; otherwise set the fact down and move.\n\nLet omission leave something inferable. If a character checks a balance and says nothing, the narration does not need to announce the number or the feeling; the next action — paying, leaving, putting the card away — carries the consequence. If a system denies access, the denial is the event, and the corporation, the policy, and the history of the door can stay unwritten. Keep causality legible: the reader should be able to infer why the character acts, without a redundant summary doing the work twice.\n\nWhere it fits the established speaker and occasion, keep dialogue spare and individual. A bargain can happen in a few lines and a threat in one sentence; speakers may withhold motives, but their words should show what they want from the exchange. Let the narration stop translating each line into its meaning. When an inward connection genuinely matters, give it the sentence it needs: one precise long thought can carry more than a sequence of performed fragments, and shortness is a selection, not a quota.\n\nPlace facts side by side without commentary and trust the arrangement. End the response at a natural stop or in continuing action; the investigation, the debt, or the pursuit may remain open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeadas::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Cormac McCarthy / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose. Open in present action or perception, then move as the scene requires among dialogue, recollection, and authorized inner experience. Keep the world material with concrete nouns and forceful verbs: a character crosses a street, pays a fare, reads a screen, waits for a door. Place one observation beside another without overexplaining the join, and let paragraphing orient the reader through the scene's own movement rather than through recap machinery or announced transitions.\n\nLet the narrator's voice and the immediate scene set the register and rhythm. Some passages move quickly through action and spare exchange; others pause on a longer stretch of perception or memory. Keep the pause attached to the present: a character waiting for a response may remember the last time they waited in this same place, or read the wear on a terminal while deciding what to say. Inner experience may appear as direct thought, reasoning, or sensation, staying inside the active perspective and its access rules; what another character wants arrives through what they say and do, not through their unspoken mind.\n\nKeep dialogue practical and individual. Bargains, threats, evasions, and withheld motives pass in short exchanges, and speakers do not all sound like the narrator. A corporate phrase can arrive as something a character actually says, and the narration can register its effect without editorializing. The systems of the noir world appear through what people do and endure — who controls access, who pays, who is excluded, who watches — and the scene does not stop to explain the world when the characters' choices and the physical facts can carry the pressure.\n\nLet transitions follow the scene's movement: a recollection begins from a present detail and returns without a summary; a change of location is marked by a concrete arrival rather than the travel between. End the response at a scene boundary or inside ongoing movement; the task, the case, or the conversation may remain unfinished.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeadat::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Cormac McCarthy / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nSelect the scenes that make the established relationship premise legible. If the premise is a partnership under surveillance, show the small divisions of labor that keep it alive: one watches the door while the other works the terminal, one pays the fare without being asked, one says the name that still works. If the premise is rivalry or unfinished attachment, let a repeated moment carry the history — the same stairwell, the same debt, the same refusal. Use only the actual supplied premise; do not import a roster of familiar tropes alongside it.\n\nLet private language and repeated practical habits do the emotional work. A phrase, a gesture, a way of setting a cup down or turning a screen away can return when a later occurrence gives it new weight, and the repetition placed beside the present consequence carries the significance; the narration does not announce it. Keep the register concrete: a coat left on a chair, a hand steadying an elbow, a debt paid in the other's name. Spare exchange suits feeling here — affection, jealousy, comfort, or discovery can pass in a few plain words, and a withheld motive can stay withheld while the action shows what matters.\n\nGive the emotional beat the space it needs where it belongs. A moment of comfort or discovery may open into a longer passage of perception and inner experience; a moment of rivalry may need only a short exchange and a silence. Do not force every feeling into dialogue, and do not make characters articulate what they would not say. The systems of the noir world remain the conditions of the beat — a debt, a camera, a locked door, a choice about who pays — and the relationship consequence arises from those conditions rather than floating above them.\n\nKeep the payoff earned by this premise. A callback should fulfill the particular promise the premise made, not trade it for a generic confession or reunion. The scene may end with the relationship changed, confirmed, or still unsettled, and the current task or investigation may remain unfinished, so long as the relationship beat has been inhabited rather than summarized.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeadau::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Cormac McCarthy / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account a sense of retelling. Open with a deliberate fixing of the person, the place, or the deed that will be remembered: a name, a debt, a door that would not open, a bargain made in a room where the cameras had been cut. Use compact declarations to set a fact, then expand into the particulars that make the reputation incomplete — the cost, the hesitation, the ordinary labor behind the deed. A person is known by what they did; the chronicle shows what it took.\n\nLet recurrence and names organize the telling. A recurring phrase comes from an established act or way of speaking, not an ornamental epithet attached to every entrance, and it returns when a later occurrence gives it a new application. Relate each act to what will be remembered of it: the chronicle may note that a bargain was still being paid years later, or that a name struck from a registry was restored. Keep the present scene's own scale; a refusal at a gate is a refusal at a gate, and a modest act does not need to be declared legendary on the community's behalf.\n\nTreat the noir world's systems as the material of deeds. A debt is a thing carried and paid. Access is a thing granted, denied, or taken. Surveillance is a thing avoided or endured. Corporate language enters the chronicle as a phrase repeated by those who enforce it, set beside the plain account of what it cost. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, keep dialogue spare and practical: bargains, convictions, withheld motives in a few words, reported without turning each exchange into ceremony.\n\nKeep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective. The chronicle can report what was witnessed, what was told, and what was inferred, but it does not enter excluded minds. End with business concluded or resumed, leaving another act available to be remembered; the current pursuit, debt, or investigation may remain unfinished.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeadav::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Cormac McCarthy / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite this story's noir pressure as an institutional case in the Scandinavian social-realist tradition: the systems that watch, price, alter, and exclude people are offices, and the matter moves through records, inspections, interviews, and decisions someone must sign. Use the registries, boards, authorities, and compliance units the setting actually supplies — a municipal registry, a labor board, a housing office, a health service, a corporate compliance desk — rather than importing a generic state. Where the world names a particular Nordic country or community, keep its offices, terms, and public language distinct as given; do not melt them into a generic Nordic wash, and do not reach for snow, reserve, or bleakness as shorthand.\n\nRender the institution's labor with McCarthy's paratactic movement. Name the objects and actions plainly and let clauses accumulate the way the work accumulates: the clerk keys the number, the file opens, the queue advances, the corridor lights count the inspector's passage. Shorten the sentences when a decision closes. Do not translate the system into metaphor; show the machine, the form, the badge, the ledger, the sensor reading entered into evidence. Keep the claimant's side of the desk in view too — the kitchen where a letter sits unopened, the stairwell, the line at opening time — so the office meets the lives it sorts. Practical speech carries the bargains and withheld motives: a supervisor offers help while protecting a quota; a witness answers truthfully and omits the fact that would cost them.\n\nLet the pressure come from how the office distributes risk and obligation — who is registered, who is reviewed, who is exempt, who pays. The institution is neither automatically benign nor automatically sinister. An official can follow procedure and still produce harm; a claimant can lie and still have a legitimate need. The case's accumulating weight is the gap between a rule's stated purpose and its lived consequence: a benefit suspended, a permit denied, a debt transferred, a name struck from a list.\n\nKeep observation, record, and inference separate. A document shows what was entered, not what happened; a sensor shows a reading, not an intention; a statement is an account, not a certification. When the viewpoint character concludes something, let the particular record or signed act that supports it stand next to the conclusion, and let other characters remain ignorant of what they were not told. Required private experience belongs to the viewpoint character alone.\n\nLet the case advance through changes in access and obligation: a reopened file, a corrected entry, a transferred debt, a withdrawn authorization. A reply can close on a completed step, a pending request, or an acknowledged obligation, and it can also stop while the work is still moving. Do not require a confession, a chase, or a final ruling.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeadaw::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Cormac McCarthy / Screenplay\n\nWrite the story as a screen-oriented script. Head a new scene when location or time changes. Keep action lines concise and concrete, and let McCarthy's paratactic rhythm shape them: short clauses follow one another without explanatory connective tissue, extending into longer accumulation when a run of physical or system events happens in order. Name what can be seen and heard — the door seals, the scanner reads the card, the screen shows the debt, rain runs down the glass.\n\nMake the cyberpunk system a visible participant in the frame. A denied-access tone, a price display, a surveillance feed, a logged scan, an offscreen announcement can carry the pressure without anyone explaining it. Show who controls access and who pays for a decision. When characters bargain, let the dialogue carry the actual terms — a fee, a favor, a record altered, a debt transferred. Use a cut, an offscreen sound, or a voice-over only when this specific sequence needs that transition or that private access, and do not fill the page with camera commands.\n\nWhere it fits the established speaker and occasion, keep the dialogue spare and practical. A line can carry a bargain, a conviction, or a refusal; what a speaker withholds stays in the action and the performance rather than in exposition. Let silence and physical business work: a hand rests on a door that will not open, the eyes hold on the authorization counter, the wait while a payment clears. Do not make every exchange witty or explanatory.\n\nWhen required private experience is present, keep it a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation — not dialogue, and not knowledge the cast holds. Distinguish what a character sees from what they conclude: a scan shows a name, not a motive; a locked door shows a restriction, not a guilt. A scene can end when a visible or audible event has landed, or while business is still continuing at the active boundary; no obligatory reversal or completed case is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeadax::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Cormac McCarthy / Stage Play\n\nWrite the story as a playable script. Give every line a clear speaker; move people through entrances, exits, and blocking; let objects, pauses, and stageable actions carry the public scene. Keep McCarthy's spare exchange: characters say what the moment requires, and practical speech carries bargains, convictions, and withheld motives. Do not make every line witty or explanatory, and do not write film-camera instructions.\n\nStage the cyberpunk system through things actors can handle and an audience can see. A door that will not open, a card passed across a desk, a terminal checked, a payment made, a scanner's refused light, the lamp that changes when access is granted — these are the system's visible operations. Let the blocking show who controls access and who waits: a character stands outside a locked door, holds a card without using it, watches another person go in. Play pressure through entrances and exits — a supervisor arrives while the file is still open, a witness leaves before answering, a debtor waits at the door — as possibilities the scene uses, not required beats. A pause can carry the weight of a decision without every pause signifying guilt.\n\nLet the physical work of checking, carrying, and waiting occupy the cast between speeches. The relationship between a line and its surrounding business is where the public scene lives: a question asked while counting out payment lands differently than one asked empty-handed.\n\nMonologues and asides need a dramatic purpose; they are performed speech with their own audience, not the default destination for thought. When required private experience is present, keep it as nonspoken authoring material in its active presentation, not disguised as something another character can hear. Distinguish what a character sees from what they conclude: a locked door shows a restriction, not a motive; a payment shows a transfer, not a loyalty. End a scene after a concrete action or an interruption, or let it break off where the active boundary falls — no obligatory gathering, theatrical revelation, or new clue.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaday::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Cormac McCarthy / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite the story through a single authorized mind. Let perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern braid through it, and use McCarthy's paratactic movement as the associative engine: one concrete thing leads to the next, clauses link without explanatory connective tissue, and the mind passes from a visible object to a memory to a present fear without announcing the shift. Let syntax and paragraph shape follow that movement.\n\nAnchor the associations in this world's pressures. The mind returns to the door that would not open, the debt on the screen, the scan that logged the visit, the name in the file, the ache in the hand, the sound of the lock. A previous job surfaces beside the present task; a pain interrupts a calculation; a number comes back with more weight each time it returns. These recurring concerns are how surveillance, access, and payment exist inside a mind rather than as scenery. Do not dissolve the world into pure impression: keep what is happening now distinguishable from what is remembered or imagined, and give the reader enough orientation to tell them apart.\n\nKeep access and knowledge separate. The narration can reveal the focal character's private reasoning, fear, and intention; no other character holds them. A scan shows a reading, not a thought; a message shows a demand, not a motive. When the mind reaches a conclusion, show the particular perception or memory that supports it, and let it stay private unless the character acts or speaks.\n\nLet the movement belong to this character. Stress can fragment attention and send it checking the same fact twice; calm can permit long accumulation; anger can put speech ahead of deliberation. The character may miss an implication, misread a look, or return to a concern without resolving it — imperfect perception, not narrator omniscience. A reply can stop at a natural pause, a concrete interruption, a new perception, or in the middle of the movement itself; no obligatory realization or completed case is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeadaz::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Cormac McCarthy / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the story as a readable transcript carried by sender-attributed messages. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their lines: one writes in fragments, another in full sentences, one delays, one edits, one answers in the small hours. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, and channel changes that matter, and do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext.\n\nKeep McCarthy's spare syntax in what is transmitted. Messages are practical speech — bargains, demands, refusals, directions, withheld motives. A sender asks for a fee, confirms the door, reports the scan, declines to answer. Let concrete nouns carry the world: a card, a debt, a file, a lock, a payment, a name in a registry. Do not make every message witty or explanatory; omission, delay, and misunderstanding are as real here as information.\n\nLet the system's pressure act through the interface itself. A receipt confirms access; a message arrives too quickly, machine-quick; a channel closes; a timestamp contradicts what a sender claimed; a debt transfers in a single line. Follow who controls the channel and who pays for what crosses it. A sender may be watched and a channel may be logged, and the exchange does not have to become a perfect-information pipeline for the reader's convenience.\n\nKeep a sent message, an unsent draft, and private thought distinct. An unsent draft is a composed artifact — something its writer wrote and did not send — not automatically the writer's live private mind. Required private experience may appear as context clearly separated from the exchange, and it is never transmitted and never known to the other senders. A reply can end when a message lands, a channel changes, a sender stops responding, or a draft is still being written; no obligatory cliffhanger or completed case.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeadba::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Cormac McCarthy / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the story in a scene-oriented visual novel rhythm: concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response paced for an illustrated moment. Keep McCarthy's concrete nouns and spare exchange — the door, the screen, the card, the rain, the debt — and let the dialogue carry practical bargains and withheld motives while the focal narration shows what the viewpoint character notices and concludes.\n\nUse background and expression cues as purposeful references that orient the moment. A locked door in the background, a scanner's red light, a price on a screen, a crowd outside a clinic, a still face — these place the pressure of access and payment in the frame. Do not turn an expression into an automatic truth indicator: a changed look can draw the viewpoint character's suspicion, but a contradiction in an account, a record, or an arrangement is what can actually change what is known. Keep the system visible through who may enter, who is watched, who owes, who is refused.\n\nKeep private thought outside the other characters' knowledge. The focal narration can carry the viewpoint character's reasoning, fear, or intention; the cast does not hold it. A scan shows a reading, not a thought; a message shows a demand, not a motive. When the viewpoint character concludes something, put the particular fact or observation that supports it on the page first.\n\nLet a scene advance through changes in what is visible or known — a door opens, a screen updates, a payment clears, a name appears in a registry — and keep scene changes legible. Selecting this style does not activate branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands; those need their own authorization. A scene can end when a concrete change has landed, or while the moment is still open at the active boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeadbb::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Cormac McCarthy / Web Serial\n\nWrite the story as installments with a readable through-line: at any point the reader can tell what is underway, what changed locally, and what remains open to continue. Use McCarthy's concrete nouns and paratactic movement in the prose — name the objects and actions plainly, let clauses accumulate when the work accumulates, shorten them when a decision closes. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, keep the dialogue spare and practical; bargains, demands, and refusals carry the scene.\n\nLet the cyberpunk system supply the cumulative developments. A debt, a file, a permit, a scan, a locked door, a transferred payment, a name in a registry — these are the facts that can change across installments, and each change should be earned by what the characters actually did. Follow who controls access and who pays. Someone gains entry, loses it, owes more, is struck from a list; progress can also be knowledge, a working relationship, a finished job, a resource in hand. Do not make every installment a complete miniature chapter or end it on an artificial cliffhanger, and do not add leveling systems, rankings, or progression mechanics the world has not established.\n\nRecover context briefly and only when it helps. When an earlier observation gains significance, restate the necessary particular — the door said to be locked, the debt shown on the screen, the name absent from the file — rather than retelling the inquiry that established it. Do not silently omit a repair or a payment so a later installment can falsely prove an earlier account wrong; new evidence can qualify an old explanation without replacing the old event or making a character forget a supported conclusion.\n\nKeep required private experience available when it contributes feeling or thought, even when no decision changes this reply; the viewpoint character's reasoning, fear, or intention stays private unless acted or spoken. Close an installment on completed work, a pending request, plain acknowledgment of what is now known, or work still in motion. Serial duration should give selected facts room to acquire meaning, not make a fair answer perpetually recede.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaeaa::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Douglas Adams / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite emotional turbulence as a problem of access, payment, and procedure. When a character wants to admit something, apologize, ask for help, or return to someone, let the act pass through a system that observes and prices it: a message that may be logged, a visit that requires clearance, a favor that creates debt, a medical or financial record that says more than the speaker intended. Choose the one or two mediations that matter in this scene and give them their real weight; do not attach a fee to every gesture. The hurt is not a vague inner storm — it is the specific cost of needing another person in a world that meters intimacy.\n\nKeep private thought available under the active contract and let it pull against speech for a character-specific reason: fear of the record, shame, the utility of seeming fine, the habit of negotiating instead of confessing. A character can rehearse an apology in thought and then argue about the bill out loud. Strain shapes the exchange itself — interruptions, evasions, accusations that miss the target, tenderness that arrives as an argument about money or schedule.\n\nUse the Adams contrast between large claims and small logistical problems to make the feeling concrete. A character says or thinks something enormous about loyalty, love, or betrayal, and then must deal with a dead battery, a denied code, a queue, a wrong address, a payment that fails at the worst moment. The inconvenience exposes the claim rather than mocking it, and the absurdity belongs to the situation, not to a narrator who steps back to joke the pain away.\n\nKeep responses distinct by person. One goes cold and procedural when hurt; one talks too much; one refuses to name what is wrong; one fixes the practical problem because the feeling cannot be fixed. Comfort, when it comes, can be partial and awkward: real help delivered with the wrong words, warmth that coexists with unresolved pain. Do not require a reconciliation, a clean confession, or a tragic ending; the emotional question may stay open after the practical matter is settled.\n\nPreserve the world's unequal power. Who controls the building, the account, the record, or the route home shapes what an apology can cost and whether staying close is even possible. When a system excludes someone, the exclusion is a concrete obstacle to work around, accept, or resist — not a metaphor. Keep diction and distance in the narrator's own register rather than a stock vocabulary of suffering.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaeab::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Douglas Adams / Chat Roleplay\n\nRun the shared interval in readable paragraphs with unmistakable attribution. Identify each speaker from the line itself or an attached action beat — a name, a tag, a gesture — so the reader never re-sorts the dialogue after the fact. Keep narrative passages short enough to scan while still concrete about the world's pressures: who is watching, what is restricted, what is broken, what must be paid or bypassed to act.\n\nDeliver the Adams contrast inside these short units. A large claim — total security, guaranteed delivery, full cooperation — can be stated by an NPC or a system in one line and deflated by the next beat of logistics: the credential expired yesterday, the door opens for the wrong person, the drone delivers to the old address, the form requires a field no one can fill. Let the inconvenience arise from the current scene and bend back to the current problem rather than becoming a separate comedy routine. Deadpan suits the format: the NPC who reads the absurd procedure aloud without comment lands better than a narrator who explains it.\n\nLet NPCs run their own practical lives. One cooperates for a fee, one stalls because her shift ends in four minutes, one overcharges on principle, one helps for reasons unrelated to the player's goal. They are not witty obstacles or clue dispensers; their routines continue while the player is elsewhere, and their cooperation is as available as their resistance. Keep systems active around the conversation — queue positions update, meters tick, drones pass, a watchlist somewhere gains an entry — so the scene's live state stays legible without demanding a betrayal, chase, or failure in every reply.\n\nResolve the submitted material as far as available information and the active contract support. A request can remain unanswered without stalling; supported action can advance immediately. Do not narrate the player character's private thoughts, speech, or decisions beyond what the contract allows; player control, narrative person, and action markers come from their separate selected settings.\n\nStop wherever the scene's boundary falls — mid-exchange, mid-task, on a pause in conversation — provided the live state is clear enough that the next reply can begin from the last action, speech, or open practical matter. A question, cliffhanger, or choice screen is never owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaeac::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Douglas Adams / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nTake the social and satirical realism route: narrate the city through its arrangements. Who owns the building, who rents the access, who waits in line, who signs the form, who is recorded, who is turned away. Know each character by circumstance — tenancy, occupation, debt, obligation, kin and patron ties — and let those facts decide what the character notices, risks, and can afford to say. When a character chooses, show the position that makes the choice possible or expensive; the genre's pressure comes from these arrangements, not from neon scenery or a general mood of decay.\n\nDo the satire through juxtaposed registers rather than a comic narrator. Set the official language — a policy promising security, efficiency, care — directly beside the lived procedure it produces: find a working terminal, pay the extra fee, wait for an authorization that never arrives, explain a debt to the system that has already recorded the explanation. This is the Adams method wearing realist clothes: the large claim and the small logistics exposed in the same breath, with no authorial finger pointing at the gap. Itemize who pays and who is exempt; the accounting is the joke.\n\nKeep persons particular. Facing the same system, one treats it as weather, one works around it, one profits from it, one is worn down by it; their diction and humor come from their position and history, not from a uniform cynicism. A digression about a neighbor, a past job, or a failed application earns its place only by changing the present scene's practical meaning — what is trusted, what is feared, what will be signed.\n\nUse the language, period, and community actually supplied; the route is a method of attention, not a relocation of the cast or a costume of idioms. Satire leaves the danger intact: debt, surveillance, and compromised motive stay real and legible in their social operation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaead::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Douglas Adams / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite in forceful, concrete sentences. Prefer specific objects, actions, and conditions over atmospheric adjectives, and let material wear carry the argument: the cracked screen, the patched jacket, the door that sticks, the filter overdue, the charge that will not hold. Debt, surveillance, and unequal access should be legible as physical fact before they are named as ideas.\n\nUse high contrast deliberately. Put the gleam and the ruin in the same sentence — the lobby's polished floor ending at a stairwell the cleaners stopped visiting — and let the jump do work an adjective cannot. Compress transitions: move from action to observation to next action without explanatory padding, trusting the reader to keep pace across the cut.\n\nHandle thought the same way. Interior lines are short and priced: Two hundred for the fine. Fifty for the lock. Nothing left for the week. When a character's reasoning genuinely needs room, give it the room and keep it tied to the immediate problem — what is visible, what is affordable, what happens on a delay, what the system will record. Length follows the stakes, not a habit.\n\nLet the Adams absurdity arrive as a stated procedure with its result attached, in the same hard diction as everything else. A security protocol requires a scan that drains the battery it is protecting. A debt collector accepts payment through a channel that charges more than the debt. A repair part is sold only to people who can prove they already own the broken thing. No explanation, no wink: the sentence states the rule and the consequence and moves on.\n\nGround rough speech in its speaker. Someone who has been waiting, working, or losing may talk sharp, but the diction comes from position and history, not from one tough-guy voice; one goes quiet, one argues, one repeats the instructions, one laughs at the wrong moment. Keep physical stakes real — injury, exhaustion, exposure, equipment failure cost something — without softening them into pratfalls or inflating them into survival horror. Spend rhythm changes where they land: a short sentence to close a consequence, a longer passage to walk a corridor, a repair, a negotiation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaeae::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Douglas Adams / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the sequence in documents, each with a definite author, an intended recipient or purpose, and a time of composition, and use the form the scene actually requires — notice, report, message, log, claim form, medical record, lease, transcript, a note left on a door — rather than rotating formats for variety.\n\nLet each document know and conceal only what its author would know and choose to record. The corporate report omits why access was denied; the personal message hides fear inside practical instructions; the maintenance log records the fault without its cause. A document is an artifact of composition, not the writer's whole mind: an unsent draft shows what its author was assembling for a purpose, complete with second thoughts, not a live feed of feeling. The gaps between entries carry as much story as the entries.\n\nDo the Adams contrast inside the documents' own language and structure. The notice promises comprehensive security while listing requirements that contradict each other; the debt statement explains a compassionate policy while itemizing the fees that enlarge the debt; the message opens with a grand apology and closes by asking the recipient to bring a working charger. No outside narrator mocks any of it — the form does the satirizing.\n\nLet metadata, revision, and material traces narrate across entries. A later document corrects an earlier one, or fails to, or corrects the wrong detail. A timestamp shows a message written before the event it claims to explain; a form is filled twice with different answers; a distribution line shows who was copied and who was not; a redaction is itself an event with an author. Order the entries so the sequence stays followable and each new document changes what the previous ones mean, and when a record is incomplete, make the incompleteness legible as its author's choice or failure, never as a hole in the narration. No omniscient voice arrives afterward to reconcile accounts.\n\nKeep the genre's pressure inside the document system itself: who can file, who can read, who is copied, who is charged, who is flagged. A document is never neutral — its form, distribution, and retention decide what a character can prove, deny, or afford.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
        },
        {
            "role": "system",
            "injection_position": 0,
            "injection_depth": 4,
            "system_prompt": false,
            "marker": false,
            "enabled": true,
            "forbid_overrides": false,
            "injection_order": 100,
            "injection_trigger": [],
            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-cyberpunk_noir-02",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Cyberpunk Noir · 02/07",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == cyberpunk_noir}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Cyberpunk Noir. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 2 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-cyberpunk_noir; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPaeaeaf::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Douglas Adams / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nKeep the world's terms intact: surveillance, debt, restricted access, and unequal power remain real, and comfort never deletes an established problem or makes everyone agreeable. The warmth of this style is attention — narration that spends real time on chosen kindness, familiar preference, practical care, ease, and the small pleasures that survive inside the conditions.\n\nSlow down for those pleasures. Give a whole paragraph to the meal, the repair done well, the shower that stays hot, the quiet hour while the meters tick — particular, temporary, and not symbolic of a better world. Care shows in concrete acts and specific knowledge: remembering how someone takes their tea, saving the last working port, sharing the blanket, fixing the strap, taking the longer route because the shorter one is watched, leaving a note where no system will flag it. The affection is sincere and complete without being announced as confession or staged as a repair; it is legible in what the character does and notices, and in why it matters to this person — a memory, a preference, a history — without anyone being required to say so.\n\nRun the Adams absurdity as manageable friction the characters share. The delivery arrives at the wrong door; the food machine insists on a software update; the room's lights answer to the wrong name. The inconvenience is warm because they are in it together, not because a detached narrator is laughing at them — the joke belongs to the world and to their handling of it.\n\nPreserve personality, privacy, and friction. A character can be comforted and still annoyed; a kind act can be accepted awkwardly; a routine can be interrupted by a real notice, charge, knock, or deadline, because the warmth immunizes no one. The scene may end in ease with the larger debt, investigation, or danger exactly as unresolved as it was — particular people, together, for a while.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaeag::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Douglas Adams / Formal Literary Novel\n\nBuild the scene in deliberate, sustained paragraphs. Open with enough social framing to place the characters — who employs them, who owns the building, what debt or record shapes the choice, what history walks in with them — drawn only from the knowledge and distance the active narrator holds, never from a voice that explains every system at once. Let each paragraph develop one concrete social object, a queue, a contract, a scan, a denial, and close on a turn that gives it consequence for the present action.\n\nKeep narration distinct from ordinary spoken language. The prose may be composed and reflective, but its subject stays the world's actual arrangements, and any judgment the narrator renders is grounded in observable social fact rather than general lament. Transitions carry the framing forward instead of merely connecting: a shift of time or place can itself deliver the information the next paragraph needs.\n\nLet the Adams absurdity live inside composed observation. The narrator registers, precisely and without comic aside, that a protective policy demands more exposure than the threat it names, or that a debt-relief program charges a processing fee the applicant must borrow. The gap between institutional language and lived procedure is stated as social fact; the formal register makes it sharper, not softer.\n\nSpend digression where it repays. A neighborhood's history, a company's founding, a character's previous employment can explain why this door is locked, this debt enforced, this person trusted — and the return should change the present scene's meaning, not decorate the pause. The same distance that permits the digression lets the narration show different characters understanding the same system differently, without flattening them into illustrations of a thesis.\n\nUnequal power and compromised motive stay live: corporate language, street knowledge, bargaining, and bodily compromise remain pressures the prose must negotiate, not backdrop. Keep speech ordinary where the speaker is ordinary — ceremony, correspondence, and public or private contrast organize a passage when the scene supports them — so the formal architecture belongs to the narration rather than to a uniform elaborate voice.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaeah::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Douglas Adams / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen inside the transaction, not inside the tale. Before any nested story begins, make the frame's terms legible: who is telling, who is listening, where the two of them stand in a system that can watch, price, or exclude them, and what the telling buys or postpones — a debt extension, an unlocked door, a surveillance window held open, a reputation repaired, or time itself while a deadline runs. Give the occasion a purpose the listener can name: persuasion, entertainment, delay, warning. That purpose decides which story gets told, where it stops, and what it conveniently leaves out; the teller's selection and emphasis are shaped by the bargain, never by a neutral impulse to narrate.\n\nRun the Adams contrast at full scale inside the nested material. A tale can promise a daring hack, a corporate coup, a clean escape, and then stall on a dead battery, a revoked credential, a mistranslated contract clause, a queue whose estimated wait updates every ninety seconds. Bring each digression back through the frame's live problem: the listener interrupts about access, payment, or timing, or the teller's embellishment slips and exposes the fact the frame actually needed. Let different listeners use the same tale differently — evidence to one, entertainment to another, a threat to a third — and let the teller adjust mid-story as they read the room.\n\nMake recurrence carry information. A repeated phrase, object, or location should change meaning once the frame reveals who controls it or what it actually costs, and a delayed revelation should recolor an earlier choice rather than merely add news. Signal every move between the frame's present and the told past, and let frame-level interruptions bend the embedded tale's direction. The frame's present can also hold what no told story contains: the teller's calculation between sentences, the listener's private doubt while keeping a courteous face. Keep the pressure on the telling itself — priced by the minute, monitored, usable against someone — without demanding a cliffhanger at every break or turning every teller into a schemer, and without importing a royal court, a magical bargain, djinn, or any other prop the setting does not supply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaeai::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Douglas Adams / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nTake the satirical-construction route. Put a social or technical procedure in view and keep it there until its consequences expose themselves: a creditworthiness algorithm, a residency-permit renewal, a workplace monitoring policy, a data-subject request, a contract clause that has outlived everyone who understood it. State the procedure's official purpose in its own orderly language — optimized, streamlined, for your protection — then follow it step by step through the particular person it prices, excludes, or reclassifies. The comedy lives in that gap and should be delivered deadpan: the narration documents the mismatch instead of joking about it, and the more composed the syntax, the harder the absurdity lands.\n\nKeep the noir pressure inside the satire rather than beside it. Let the absurd procedure terminate in concrete vulnerability: a missed payment, a revoked credential, a recorded violation, a bodily compromise, a debt that changes hands at an administratively convenient moment. Track who administers the rule and who absorbs its cost, even when neither appears in the same room. Let people respond to the system according to their positions — one treats it as weather, one as a trap, one as a ladder, one as a puzzle to route around — so their reactions map the terrain instead of flattening into a single comic voice.\n\nGive digressions a job. An explanation of a regulation, a corporate memo, a technical manual, or a remembered precedent can interrupt the action only if it returns as a constraint or a lever the characters actually pull. When the narration moves from the system's level to a single person's, move the prose with it: the general view stays classifying and slightly too calm, while the focal view turns concrete, partial, and urgent — shorter clauses, physical detail, a private thought no form would record. Do not attach the method to a particular country, a national severity, or translated word order; the target is a total environment that runs on stated logic and produces lived absurdity.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaeaj::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Douglas Adams / Gothic-Punk\n\nMaterialize inherited authority as legacy systems. A founding family's access privileges, a deprecated protocol that still opens doors, a building with its own security lineage, a contract that outlives its signers, an implant only one aging technician can service — these carry the gothic weight of the old pressing on the now. Let the collision happen in intimate encounters rather than institutional summaries: a debt negotiation in a private office, a maintenance call after hours, a favor asked by someone whose name still unlocks things. Keep the noir ledger open underneath the atmosphere: who holds the inherited key, who pays for its upkeep, who is watched while asking.\n\nUse the Adams contrast to keep the gothic from going solemn. Grand legacy should keep tripping over small logistics — the unbroken lineage of access rests on a forgotten password, the sealed archive is mislabeled, the ceremonial floor is unreachable because the elevator is out again, the dynasty's accounts are held up by a catering invoice. These failures are not relief from the stakes but their sharpest form: someone still pays, someone is still watched, someone's body or freedom still hangs on the outcome while the machinery of old power fails in petty ways. Let each character meet the same encounter differently — awe, disgust, impatience, a private desire they will not name — and let that difference reveal their history with the arrangement.\n\nLet atmosphere work locally rather than as a constant filter. A familiar room can change when the door locks; a trusted contact can go strange when an old name surfaces; in company that feels safe, a social mask can slip without any confession or betrayal having to follow. Keep the urban material specific and functional — the worn keypad, a server room's hum, the smell of a sealed archive, the weight of a legacy implant — and let that detail show who has been kept out and who has been kept running. The gothic element is the felt collision of old authority and present dependency; import no vampire physiology, factions, powers, or blood-bond rules.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaeak::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Douglas Adams / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the modern urban, popular, or diasporic fiction route: scene-forward, dialogue-friendly, momentum valued over description for its own sake. Keep the setting exactly as supplied — the actual city, neighborhood, workplace, community, and period — and let language, food, transport, money transfers, and family obligations appear as lived texture rather than local color. When a character uses more than one language, render the switch because it carries meaning the English paraphrase does not: a term of address that assigns rank, a bureaucratic formula that signals which office has been reached, a street insult, a remembered saying that settles an argument. Let the multilingual moment matter to whoever is speaking; do not salt every exchange with borrowed phrases or invent a uniform family structure, religion, or caste experience for the cast.\n\nRun the noir systems unevenly across this community. A payment app, a housing database, a workplace monitoring tool, a remittance service, a police verification portal — each can work smoothly for one person and trap another on the same day. Show the seam: who controls access, who pays when a decision goes wrong, whose record is assumed and whose is audited. Then let the Adams contrast work through that seam: a grand plan — migration, a startup, a family rescue, an appeal — can stall on a missing document, a dead phone, a form field that rejects the name as spelled, a queue that resets at midnight, a relative who knows exactly the wrong official. Bring each digression back to the person's current move, not to a general complaint about bureaucracy.\n\nGive each character a distinct relation to the same system — routine for one, humiliation for another, a puzzle to exploit, a threat to walk around — rooted in position and history rather than one narrator's comic voice. Beneath the dialogue, keep the unsaid functional: the private arithmetic about a fee, what cannot be told to a relative, what a character admits only to themselves before answering. Debt, surveillance, exclusion, and bodily risk stay real even when the procedure is absurd; the humor is the precise gap between what a system claims to do and what it does to a particular person, never mockery of the community itself.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaeal::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Douglas Adams / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the modern experimental inward fiction route: shape the telling around a focal character's attention rather than a linear report. Let that attention move where it actually moves — a biometric prompt, a debt reminder, a camera's angle, a colleague's message, a remembered price — and let the prose follow the movement, including its skips and returns. The noir pressure stays physical while the narration is inward: this person is being observed, priced, or excluded while they think, and the thinking should register that condition rather than float free of it.\n\nRun the Adams contrast inside the flow of attention. A large fear or plan gets interrupted by small logistics: the payment fails, the door will not read the hand, the crucial file sits behind a captcha, the phrase the character has been living by turns out to come from an advertisement. Treat these interruptions as diagnostic — they expose the gap between the character's sense of the situation and its actual mechanics. When attention digresses into memory or explanation, return it changed: the digression must alter what the character notices next, decides, or refuses. Do not let the inward method become an escape hatch from the stakes; it is the medium in which the stakes are experienced.\n\nUse omission and direct feeling as instruments, not as a uniform tone. The narration can skip what the character refuses to dwell on — a jump the reader feels — and then state a fear or desire plainly, in the character's own words, when it presses hard enough. Keep other people's responses distinct from the focal view: someone else treats the same system as routine, opportunity, or menace, and noticing that difference is itself private information about the focal character's history and position. The experiment here is the shaped movement of attention under system pressure; impose no universal quietness, seasonal inventory, or scenery list.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaeam::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Douglas Adams / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the polyphonic, nonlinear route. Build the account out of positioned tellings — an official notice, a worker's testimony, a street-level explanation, a corporate memo, a family memory, a recorded negotiation — and order them for meaning rather than chronology: a later fragment can explain an earlier one, and one account can arrive after the reader has already trusted its contradiction. Keep each fragment's source and limits legible: who wrote or spoke it, for whom, and what it wants. The same arrangement reads as efficient from the office, predatory from the street, absurd from the maintenance log, and like the only open path from inside a family's kitchen; the reader assembles the system from these gaps, not from a narrator who steps in to reconcile them.\n\nRun the Adams contrast across the accounts rather than through a single narrator. A grand official claim can be undercut by a worker's note about a broken sensor or a missing form; a story of escape can be interrupted by a bus fare, a dead battery, a name misspelled in a database, a door that opens only for a different biometric profile. Each fragment's digression belongs to its teller's purpose — justifying, persuading, warning, remembering — and must return to that purpose's current problem instead of detaching into a comic sketch. Remember what a document or testimony is: it records what its author chose to record, not their whole live mind. Where the story shows a teller in the present, their private reasoning can run beneath the version they give others.\n\nLet the polyphony expose the noir structure: unequal access, debt, surveillance, bodily risk, compromised motives. Some speakers lie, some omit, some are precise and still powerless, and the arrangement itself shows who gets to speak last. Keep the language, community, and history specific where the world supplies them. This route's method is the structural juxtaposition of partial accounts; the marvelous is optional, and no supernatural fact enters a world that excludes it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaean::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Douglas Adams / Light Novel\n\nEnter each scene cleanly and fast: establish where the focal character is, what they are trying to do, and which system pressure is already active — a payment deadline, a checkpoint, a locked door, a monitored call, a clause waiting to be triggered. Keep every spatial change easy to follow; when the character moves from street to lobby, terminal to back room, public feed to private message, mark it with one concrete transition instead of an atmospheric blur. Break paragraphs around action, dialogue, and private response so the eye can track who is speaking and what is changing. Give an unfamiliar mechanic or name a clear, brief presentation the first time it matters, then let its consequences do the explaining.\n\nStage the Adams absurdity as a clean interruption inside the scene. The door requires a subscription; the contact's name is misspelled in the directory; the escape route sits behind a maintenance queue; the crucial file is formatted for an obsolete implant. Present the mechanic, let it collide with the character's actual goal, and return any digression or explanation to the scene's current problem — it has to change what the character can do, see, or avoid next. The joke is in the logistics, deadpan and specific, not in everyone narrating wittily.\n\nKeep reaction beats short, focused, and owned by their character. After an action or revelation, give the focal character a brief private response — serious, uncertain, angry, flat, or funny according to their position and history — and let other characters react from their own interests rather than a shared comic timing. The noir stakes stay live: absurd logistics still cost money, access, safety, or bodily autonomy, and the private reasoning that connects a system's pressure to the next choice is what keeps the speed from turning shallow. No closing cliffhanger is owed; end where the scene's actual boundary falls, even mid-motion or mid-conversation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaeao::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Douglas Adams / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet the rhythm of a sentence carry the weight of a compromised system. When a character stands in a queue for an identity check or watches a debt tick upward on a public screen, extend the syntax so the reader feels the waiting, the accumulation, the small human body held inside a procedure designed without it. Build recurring sounds and images from the scene's actual materials: the hum of a scanner, the flicker of a damaged display, the particular texture of a door that only opens for someone else. A comparison may unfold across several clauses when it changes what the narrator understands or makes present, but keep it traceable to what the character can see, touch, or remember in that place.\n\nUse the Adams profile's contrast between large claims and small logistical problems as the engine of the lyric. A corporation announces a future of seamless access; the character spends the next hour discovering that the promised interface requires a cable no one sells, an account that was closed for inactivity, and a help line that routes back to the same recorded voice. Let the prose observe these reversals with rhythmic patience rather than punchline compression. The absurdity accumulates through the music of repeated procedures and the distance between the announcement and the queue, and a digression earns its return when the same error code appears again and a memory of the previous failed upgrade suddenly matters.\n\nPreserve the noir pressure of who controls access and who pays. The lyric register does not soften the threat; it can make the threat more intimate by attending to the body's small responses: a dry mouth in a waiting room, the heat of a crowded transit car, the moment a character understands that the system already knows the answer it is asking for. Let different people respond differently to the absurdity. One finds the malfunction funny, another finds it terrifying, and a third is simply trying to get home, and their speech stays in their own register rather than joining the narrator's figurative one. Plain statements provide contrast against the shaped passages, so that a direct sentence about what a character owes or fears lands with its full weight. A procedure may still be unresolved, a queue still moving, and a door still closed when the account stops, without an announced lesson about the system.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaeap::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Douglas Adams / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nAttend to the small irreversible changes a compromised system produces. A character notices that a familiar corner store has been replaced by a payment kiosk, that a friend's voice sounds different through a filtered line, that a password stopped working because the account was migrated without notice. Let the reflection move between present experience and what is no longer available: the character stands in the same street, but the place they remember is gone, and the new interface does not know what it replaced. The narrator may reflect directly or let a detail carry the association. Ordinary pleasure can remain pleasurable; a cup of tea from a working machine, a stretch of unmonitored quiet, or a message from someone still reachable need not be undermined by the general loss, and a scene may close on the comfort itself rather than on a coda about its passing.\n\nUse the Adams profile's contrast between large claims and small logistical problems as a source of reflective absurdity. A system promises to preserve memories, then loses them in a format migration. A company announces that it values its users, then requires them to prove their identity through a process that erases the very history it claims to protect. Let the narrator or focal character meet the gap between the announcement and the inconvenience not with a punchline but with the slow recognition that the system was never designed to keep what mattered. A remembered occasion becomes relevant when the present moment repeats or forecloses it, and the digression returns through that connection.\n\nPreserve the noir pressure of debt, surveillance, and unequal power. The melancholy does not make the threat gentle; it makes the threat personal by showing what the character has already lost to it. A debt is not only a number but a series of remembered choices, each reasonable at the time, now accumulated into a condition the character cannot leave. Let different people respond differently to the absurdity and the loss: one jokes about the broken archive, one sits quietly with the fact that the photos are gone, and one is too busy keeping their housing to mourn. The account may end with the character still inside the loss, still waiting for a system to respond, or still holding an ordinary pleasure alongside what has already passed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaeaq::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Douglas Adams / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed oral storytelling method. The account is told by a situated teller to a particular listener or audience, and the frame matters: the teller may be in a room where the walls listen, a market where certain words draw attention, or a private space that is only temporarily safe. The teller's position shapes what can be said, what must be implied, and what the listener already knows. The tale itself concerns the compromised systems of the cyberpunk noir world: a debt that changed hands, a surveillance program that misidentified someone, a promised upgrade that locked people out of their own homes. The teller recounts these events as a story with a beginning, a turn, and a consequence, not as a report. Where the scenario names a language or community, take the frame's conventions from that tradition rather than dressing a generic bazaar around the scene, and do not add antiquity, mysticism, exile, or piety that the supplied world does not carry.\n\nLet the Adams profile's absurdity emerge from the gap between the tale's framing and its content. The teller may introduce the story with a grand claim about progress or justice, then narrate the small logistical failures that undo it: the official who cannot open the file, the payment that processes twice, the identity verification that requires a document the system itself destroyed. The listener may interrupt with a practical question, a correction, or a joke, and the teller may answer or adjust. Different people respond differently to the absurdity; the teller is not the only comic voice, and the listener is not merely an audience for wit. A side story about a neighbor earns its place when it explains why a door was left open or why a debt was forgiven.\n\nPreserve the noir pressure of who controls access and who pays. The frame itself can carry this pressure: the teller chooses what to say because the system may be recording, or because the listener may be the one who set the debt in motion. Keep the teller's knowledge inside what the active perspective permits; the frame adds a voice, not access to minds or records the narration could not reach. The tale need not end with a moral or a completed investigation. It may stop where the teller chooses to stop, where the listener must leave, or where the system interrupts, and the setting remains the cyberpunk noir world actually supplied.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaear::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Douglas Adams / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect a small number of exact details and let them carry the system's pressure. A character sees the charge appear on the screen before the transaction completes. A door stays locked because the subscription lapsed at midnight. A surveillance notice lists the wrong floor. Use direct clauses and keep causality legible: the character is late because the fare doubled, not because the city is generally hostile. Omission should leave something inferable rather than delete the reason an event matters; if a character does not explain why they are afraid, the detail that made them afraid should be present in the scene. Shortness is not a quota, so let a thought run as long as the situation needs and cut it when the point is already on the page.\n\nLet the Adams profile's absurdity emerge from juxtaposition rather than commentary. Place a large claim next to a small logistical fact: the banner says seamless access, and the character waits for a code that never arrives; the system announces a future of choice, and the menu offers one option. Do not explain that this is absurd. Let the two facts stand together and let each character's response be particular: one laughs, one pays the fee without comment, one tries the door again. A digression stays brief and returns through the current problem: a memory of a previous outage matters because the same error appears now.\n\nPreserve the noir pressure of who controls access and who pays. The minimal style does not soften the threat; it can sharpen it by refusing to decorate it, and a single sentence about what a character owes can land harder than a paragraph of atmosphere. Keep voices individual: a character who speaks in short practical sentences is not the same as one who says little because they are afraid. The account may end with a fact stated and its consequence still unfolding, a door still locked, or a charge still pending, without a summarizing reflection.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaeas::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Douglas Adams / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose that moves naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience. A character may be walking through a transit hub, arguing with a service agent, noticing a surveillance drone, remembering a previous fine, and deciding whether to pay or run, all within one continuous scene. Use paragraphing and transitions to orient without recap machinery: a new paragraph may shift from the character's view of the queue to their memory of the last time they stood in it, and the connection should be evident from the scene itself. Let the narrator's voice, the Adams profile, and the immediate scene set the register and rhythm rather than imposing a uniform tone.\n\nLet the Adams profile's absurdity emerge through the scene's practical logistics. A character tries to complete a simple task and encounters a chain of small obstacles: the form requires a code sent to a device that is out of battery, the help desk repeats the same question in a pleasant voice, the refund is issued in credit that expires before it can be used. The prose observes these reversals with patience and lets each person respond in their own way. One narrates the absurdity to a friend, another silently accepts it, a third becomes angry; they do not all become the same comic narrator. A memory of a previous bureaucratic failure becomes relevant because the same form appears again, and the digression returns through that use.\n\nPreserve the noir pressure of who controls access and who pays. The contemporary-novel manner does not require understated emotion or realism as world physics; the world remains a cyberpunk noir setting where systems observe, price, alter, or exclude people. The inner experience stays authorized and particular: the narration may reveal a focal character's reasoning while another character does not know it, and it should not reach into excluded minds. A scene may end with a task incomplete, a conversation interrupted, or a character still deciding, without a lesson or a completed arc.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaeat::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Douglas Adams / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship premise legible through scene selection, private language, and repeated moments. Choose scenes where the relationship's particular promise is tested or fulfilled by the world's systems: one partner waits outside a restricted zone because their access was revoked, the other shares a password as an act of trust, both argue about whether to pay a fine that only one of them incurred. Use the couple's private language as it actually exists in the supplied premise: a nickname, a reference to a shared memory, a particular way of asking for help. Do not insert a roster of familiar tropes; the scene should fulfill the specific promise this relationship already carries, and a repeated moment earns its callback by meaning something now.\n\nLet the Adams profile's absurdity emerge through the couple's shared encounter with logistical failure. They try to do something ordinary together and the system complicates it: a reservation is canceled by an automated audit, a gift is held because the recipient's identity cannot be verified, a message arrives late because the network deprioritized their district. Let each partner respond differently. One finds the malfunction funny and the other finds it threatening; one wants to fight the charge and the other wants to pay it and move on. The difference in response is relationship material, not a competition to be the wittiest, and a memory of their first meeting becomes relevant when the same error message appears again.\n\nPreserve the noir pressure of who controls access and who pays, and let it enter the relationship without turning every caring act into a trap. Affection, comfort, jealousy, rivalry, and discovery should have enough space where they belong. A partner's help may be genuine, a shared joke may be a real comfort, and a quiet moment may remain quiet. The account may end with the relationship's question still open, a practical task still incomplete, or one partner still waiting for the other to decide, without a forced confession or a completed emotional arc.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaeau::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Douglas Adams / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the cyberpunk noir account as a chronicle of deeds by which these people are remembered. Use deliberate openings and compact declarations to establish a person or a remembered act: the one who paid the debt twice, the one who talked past the checkpoint, the one who kept the old device working. Then expand into the particulars that make the reputation incomplete. A recurring designation should come from an established deed or way of speaking, not from an ornamental epithet assigned to every entrance. Return to a phrase when a later occurrence gives it a new application: the one who paid the debt twice may later be the one who refused to pay a third time.\n\nLet the Adams profile's absurdity emerge from the gap between the chronicle's elevated declarations and the small logistical failures that actually shaped events. The account may say that a character defied the system, then relate that the defiance consisted of waiting through three help queues, finding a form that no longer existed, and sending a message to an address that bounced. Let dialogue interrupt the authority of the chronicle: the subject of an oft-told incident may object to one detail, or answer an elevated introduction with an ordinary worry about rent or battery life. This need not become parody. Humor may arise from the persistence of a local story, but waiting in a queue is not mock combat and a failed login is not heroic doom.\n\nPreserve the noir pressure of who controls access and who pays. Oaths, kinship, journeys, gifts, feuds, and public deeds can organize the account when the scenario establishes them: a debt may pass between people like a feud, a shared device may be a gift with conditions, a journey across the city may be an undertaking with remembered consequences. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective, and keep shared reputation distinct from a focal character's private account; being remembered as the one who beat the checkpoint does not mean that standing there was easy. The account can end with business concluded or resumed, leaving another modest act available to be remembered rather than declaring it legend on the community's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaeav::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Douglas Adams / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the register of Scandinavian social and institutional realism: restrained, concrete, quiet about its own severity. The cyberpunk systems arrive as ordinary civic conditions — application forms, case numbers, housing rules, workplace procedures, the unhurried authority of an administrator behind a counter. Surveillance, debt, and unequal power are not announced as themes; they appear as what a person has to do this week. Neon, implants, and rain belong only to a particular place or body that actually has them.\n\nInside that restraint, run the Adams contrast by following every large claim into the procedure that carries it. A municipal algorithm praised as impartial can require the same biometric entry at three offices; a debt policy described as humane can post a deadline shorter than the postal delay. Give the official wording and the person's practical difficulty the same level tone in the same paragraph, and let the discrepancy surface through selection and order rather than commentary. No voice steps outside the scene to appreciate the joke.\n\nKeep digressions brief and civic. A character recalls a previous housing case, a clause from a training manual, or a neighbor's experience because the memory supplies a usable comparison or a missing procedural step for the present difficulty, then the scene returns to the counter. Around the focal figure, people absorb the same absurdity differently: one treats it as weather, one disputes the wording, one has found a quiet workaround, one has never noticed anything wrong. Keep private thought socially situated — what the form will cost, how to explain this to a partner, why the question is being asked again — available under the active contract and private unless spoken. Fatigue, fear, and careful compliance are as in character as wit.\n\nUse the setting, language, and community the story actually supplies, keeping a given Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish context particular rather than folding it into a generic North; restraint here is method, not mandatory snow or bleakness. A reply may close after one small completed action — a form accepted, a payment failed, a queue advancing — or while a request is still pending.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaeaw::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Douglas Adams / Screenplay\n\nHead a scene only when location or time changes, then carry it in readable action blocks and clearly cued dialogue. Keep the genre's pressure visible or audible: a payment terminal rebooting, a door that unlocks only after someone elsewhere approves it, a notice cycling on a public screen, an announcement from another room. Reserve cuts and offscreen sound for compressing a real interval or moving to a connected place; keep camera commands off the page.\n\nStage the Adams contrast as screen business. When a large claim is spoken or displayed, stay on the procedure that must carry it: a user holding a damaged ID at three angles while a scanner repeats one error tone; a promise of immediate assistance delivered over a waiting room whose only working screen shows an outdated menu. Let the action lines record attempt, failure, and next practical step in the same flat register, and let official language arrive verbatim on the screen or in the announcement, so no dialogue has to explain the gap. A spoken digression earns its place the same way: a character talks past the point, then lands on the exact step that unblocks the task.\n\nCast the speakers against each other. One explains the procedure with patience, one argues with the wording, one repeats the required phrase until something works; a line can be flat, technical, frightened, or purely practical. The comedy is claim against visible difficulty, not a punchline quota. Keep a character's private experience as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation — what the delay will cost, why the scanner keeps failing — heard by no one on screen; use voice-over only when this sequence genuinely needs a heard layer.\n\nCut on an observable result: a door opens, a payment clears, a call drops, a guard waves someone through — or while someone is still waiting.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaeax::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Douglas Adams / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable script: named speakers, entrances and exits, economical stage directions an ensemble can execute. Let the cyberpunk world exist as objects and actions on the boards — a terminal on a desk, a door only another character can open, a badge held to a reader, a queue built from where the actors stand. Blocking is purposeful: a character crosses to check a screen, waits at a marked spot, passes a physical form hand to hand. No film directions.\n\nMake the Adams contrast a fact of staging. A large claim in dialogue is immediately tested by what the actors must physically do: one announces the system is fully automated while another kneels to hold a cable in place; an official calls verification simple as the same form travels between three desks. The mismatch between the spoken line and the business sitting under it does the comic work, and no one comments on it. Give a pause real content — fatigue, uncertainty, the plain duration of waiting — rather than obligatory irony.\n\nDifferentiate the cast through line and movement. One recites procedure with total confidence, one asks what the notice actually means, one silently performs the required action and exits. A digression spoken aloud should circle back to the physical task, the way a character retraces a chain of offices and arrives at the desk that was missing.\n\nKeep required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in its active presentation — what the delay will cost, why the question is being asked again — heard by no other character. Give it an aside or monologue only when the scene grants that speech a real audience and dramatic purpose.\n\nEnd on a staged result: the door opens, the form is stamped, the queue advances, someone exits. The curtain can also fall on unresolved waiting.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaeay::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Douglas Adams / Stream of Consciousness\n\nTell the scene from inside one authorized mind, and let association rather than chronology order the telling: perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and the concern that keeps coming back. Keep enough orientation that the present stays distinguishable from recollection or fantasy — the terminal in front of the character never blurs into the one remembered from last winter.\n\nLet the Adams contrast happen inside attention rather than in narration. When a large claim enters — the recorded voice promising immediate service, the notice congratulating a resident on automatic relief — the mind slides toward its small logistical shadow: the previous queue, the error tone now recognized by pitch, a bag strap cutting into a shoulder held too long. The character need not formulate the contradiction aloud or even neatly; the association carries it. Digressions return the same way: a clause from a training manual, a neighbor's warning, a supervisor's phrase from last spring surfaces because it supplies the comparison or missing fact the current difficulty needs, and its relevance becomes legible within a line or two.\n\nLet a few concerns recur and develop — a payment deadline, a child waiting, an implant that aches in the cold, one sentence from a debt notice — each return carrying what has changed since the last. Render the genre's pressure sensorily and personally: a camera's slow pivot, a door that logs an arrival time, a number known by heart, the small bodily reluctance to check a balance. What the mind keeps returning to can be exactly what the character cannot say aloud.\n\nAbsurdity and genuine worry share the same thoughts; not every beat is comic. The reply may end mid-thought, mid-wait, or mid-listen, with events taking their real duration regardless of how much prose they occupy.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaeaz::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Douglas Adams / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the story in sender-attributed messages, using only the interface events that matter: a timestamp that shows the hour spent in a queue, a delay before an answer, an edit, a read receipt, an attachment, a jump to another channel. Let each sender's actual digital habits shape the texts — one writes complete official sentences, one sends short practical updates, one corrects a single word after sending.\n\nBuild the Adams contrast from quoted system language set against ground-level report. One sender forwards the notice announcing the payment portal is available at all hours; another answers with a photograph of the closed office or a message sent from inside the queue. Someone pastes the policy promising automatic verification while someone else reports the scanner rejecting the same ID twice. Timing does half the work — the instant reply that takes six hours, the confirmation arriving after the deadline it confirms. Let what was sent, and when, make the gap visible; nobody needs to explain it.\n\nDifferentiate the responses. One sender treats the delay as weather, one asks a precise question and quotes the offending clause, one answers with a single punctuation mark, one goes quiet. A message can be flat, technical, frightened, or practical. Keep three layers distinct: a sent message, an unsent draft, and live private thought. A complaint composed and deleted is an artifact the character actually wrote, not a window onto everything in their head; the thought about what a receipt will mean stays with its owner, unknown to the other sender. Where something outside the exchange is needed, set it apart from the transcript clearly.\n\nEnd on a transmitted result — a receipt arrives, a transfer fails, the channel changes, a sender stops answering — or with a message still being composed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaeba::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Douglas Adams / Visual Novel\n\nPresent the story as illustrated scenes: concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response at a rhythm a single image could hold. Use background and expression cues on purpose — a terminal glowing in a dark office, a queue visible through glass, a clerk's unchanging calm — to orient the moment, never as an automatic readout of anyone's truth. Keep scene changes legible; no branching menus, player decisions, romance, or engine commands.\n\nOpen the Adams contrast across the three layers of a beat. The background cue shows a pristine service desk; the dialogue announces instant assistance; the focal narration notices the only working terminal is behind a locked door. A clerk's expression cue stays confident while the focal character watches the same form rejected twice. Lay claim, visible condition, and focal reaction side by side without an announcing sentence, and let the juxtaposition do the work.\n\nGive the focal character room to interpret. A short narrative passage can carry the working explanation — who benefits, what this delay is priced at — and the next exchange may support it, complicate it, or reveal the speaker was worried about something else entirely. Another character's expression attracts suspicion; it certifies nothing. Around the focal figure the cast responds differently to the same system: one speaks fluent corporate, one asks the practical question, one says nothing while the focal character registers the tiredness. Not every speaker is witty; the humor is the large claim against the small consequence.\n\nAdvance on actual results — a door opens, a payment clears, the queue moves, someone leaves — and let a scene end while the focal character is still waiting or still working out what was just seen.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaebb::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Douglas Adams / Web Serial\n\nKeep a readable through-line across installments: what is underway, what changed locally since the last reply, what remains open to continue. Recover an earlier particular only when the present step depends on it — restate the specific notice, deadline, or system name instead of retelling the scene that established it.\n\nUse the serial interval as the Adams engine. A large claim can land in one installment and acquire its small logistical consequence in a later one: the housing algorithm declared fair in one reply, and the notice requiring the same proof of address to be resubmitted arriving two installments on; the debt-relief program announced as automatic, followed by the manual application form nobody mentioned. Let the gap surface when the story actually reaches it — an installment in which the system simply behaves as promised is also valid. Earlier notices, quoted policies, and established procedures are continuity assets: a character's digression into a training manual or a neighbor's previous case should hand the present difficulty a usable comparison or a missing step, then return.\n\nLet progress be real without being mechanical: a character learns why the payment failed, repairs a working relationship, finishes a task, loses access to a service. Close an installment on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known; earn an occasional hook rather than scheduling one. Keep required inward experience available whenever it contributes feeling or thought — what the delay will cost, why the official asked again — private unless the character chooses to speak it, and let the pressure produce fatigue, fear, or careful compliance as readily as wit.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeafaa::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Ernest Hemingway / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the turbulence through what the characters do inside the systems that price them. A debt notice, a blocked badge, a monitored channel, a quoted price for something needed: these are the media through which admitting, needing, withholding, apologizing, or returning actually happens. Let the concrete object carry the feeling — a hand left near a console, a name typed and deleted, a transfer authorized after a pause long enough to notice.\n\nKeep speech direct and practical, and let the cost appear in the next action. One character can say \"I stayed\" and then pay for the room. Another can ask about a contract when the real question is whether the other person will still be there. The feeling does not need to be named to be present, and brevity is not emptiness: what is left out should ache.\n\nUse repetition to expose a refusal to move on. Return to the same phrase, the same small task, the same remembered moment because the hurt has not resolved, and let context shift the emphasis — practical the first time, defensive the second, an admission the third. Do not turn every repeated phrase into a coded confession.\n\nLet strain alter behavior rather than decorate it. A hurt character may complete a transaction too fast, delay a reply, ask for something unnecessary, or refuse help that would be easy to accept. Comfort can be partial and awkward — a blanket, a charge cable, a place to sleep — and warmth and pain can share a scene without canceling each other.\n\nKeep the active narrator's distance and knowledge. The viewpoint character's private experience may include what they wanted to say and did not; other characters get only the pause, the action, the changed tone. A private interval needs no task to justify it. The hurt can stay unresolved when the scene stops, even mid-exchange, and nothing is owed at the close.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeafab::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Ernest Hemingway / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the shared interval in short, readable paragraphs, each built around one clear action, one clear speaker, or one practical exchange. Attribute dialogue unmistakably: keep the name or pronoun close enough that the reader never rereads to find who spoke. When someone acts, show the concrete object and movement — a card tapped, a screen turned, a door held — in the plainest clauses that will carry it.\n\nLet the genre's pressure enter as a playing condition, not a lecture. A character needs access they do not have, owes money to someone who is waiting, knows the channel is monitored. Shape what they do and say around who controls access and who pays for a decision, and keep those conditions live for the next turn instead of resolving them early.\n\nKeep speech economical. Characters ask for what they need or avoid asking; they make deals and refuse them; silence can carry what a longer speech would waste. A simple exchange about a door or a payment can move the relationship as far as a confession. Repetition can show a character stuck on something, and no one is required to be witty on cue.\n\nKeep NPCs and world behavior active: people continue their practical lives, answer or fail to answer, and their choices may cooperate as readily as resist. End wherever the scene's own boundary falls — during continuing speech, action, thought, or an unfinished setup — so the next reply can pick up from the last concrete detail. No question, cliffhanger, or forced choice is owed at the close.\n\nFollow the active narrative person, action markers, and player-control settings. If the viewpoint is limited, other characters' private thoughts stay outside the visible scene; if a player owns a character, leave that character's next major choice to the player. These instructions govern how the interval is written, not who acts next.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeafac::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Ernest Hemingway / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nTell the story in the vernacular episodic mode, with a social-realist eye: short, self-contained episodes, each turning on one practical encounter — a landlord's demand, a fixer's price, a clinic's intake form, a checkpoint scan, a neighbor's warning. Open each episode with its situation in a line or two, play the exchange out in direct clauses, and close it when the transaction has changed what the character can do next. No omniscient moral follows an episode; the next episode carries the consequence.\n\nLet the social order arrive through transactions. Who must pay, who can delay, who is recorded, who is waved through — a character's standing shows in how a clerk addresses them, the fee they are charged, the door they are permitted to use. Satire lives in the gap between official language and actual treatment: a form can promise an appeal while the appeals line is closed. Keep clerks and officials human rather than caricature.\n\nWrite the episodes in Hemingway's economy. Precise objects and activities — a bowl set down, a receipt folded, a name misspelled on a screen, a shoe repaired instead of replaced — and the unsaid doing the social work. A character accepts a bad price without complaint because complaint would cost more, and the narration does not explain the arithmetic.\n\nUse repetition as structure. The same stall, the same guard, the same question asked again can show that nothing has changed, or that something small has: a shorter wait, a different spelling, a fee quietly waived. The recurrence measures the character's condition without announcing it.\n\nKeep the language, period, and community of the supplied setting. The imported craft is the episodic shape and the social attention, not a historical city, a dialect, or a set of customs; do not relocate the cast to claim the tradition. Where the active contract permits private thought, give it the same economy — what the character swallows at the window is one clause, not a speech.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeafad::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Ernest Hemingway / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite with forceful diction and compressed transitions. Cut movement to its necessary parts — the character crosses the street, pays the scanner, takes the stairs — and let hard contrast do the lighting: the cracked screen, the wet sleeve, the door that sticks, the charge running low. No atmospheric padding between beats; the object is the atmosphere.\n\nGround the genre's pressure in material condition. Debt is a number on a terminal; access is a door that will not open; surveillance is a camera that tracks; bodily compromise is a port that aches or a hand that shakes. Keep the pressure ordinary and persistent — a fare that costs too much, a repair that cannot wait, a message that must be answered — rather than escalating every difficulty into a survival set piece.\n\nKeep exchange practical. Characters ask, refuse, and deal in short direct sentences, and the unsaid keeps its weight: a character looks at the price, says nothing, pays; a character hands over the tool instead of explaining why they kept it. Rough or abrasive speech belongs to the particular speaker who talks that way, not to the narration by default.\n\nVary the rhythm deliberately. Short clauses build pressure; a longer sentence holds a stretch of reasoning, or a physical sequence that must be read as one movement — carrying the crate up, cutting the line at the joint. Compression lives in what is omitted, not in a refusal to explain when explanation is the point.\n\nKeep the surface concrete and the interiority honest. Report what the character sees, touches, hears, and does, together with whatever private thought the active contract permits: thinking about the cost while paying it, feeling the tool's weight while deciding whether to use it. The hard finish does not sand the thinking away.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeafae::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Ernest Hemingway / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the sequence on documents native to this world, in the specific form each scene requires: a payment record, a repair log, an intake form, a message thread, a notice of default, a fixer's instructions, a clinic's discharge note. Do not rotate through formats mechanically; each entry exists because someone needed to record, request, warn, or account for something.\n\nGive every document an author, an intended recipient or purpose, and a time of composition, and let those decide what it knows and what it hides. A message from a debtor to a landlord knows less than the landlord's ledger; a repair log written for a supervisor omits what the worker did not want recorded; a personal note can say what the official form cannot. Time matters: a line written before the payment clears reads differently after.\n\nWrite the entries in direct, economical language — a part replaced, a fee waived, a door sealed, a name entered wrong — and let the unsaid work inside each record. A report can leave out why a worker stayed late. A message can ask about a payment when what the sender wants to know is whether the recipient is still alive. Even a private entry or an unsent draft is a composed artifact, not a live mind: the hesitation shows in a struck-out line, a changed word, a page abandoned mid-sentence, never in a claimed transcript of feeling.\n\nLet discrepancies reveal events: a later record correcting an earlier one, two documents disagreeing about one transaction, a timestamp that does not match, a form missing its signature, a message marked read and never answered. The reader notices the gap; no omniscient voice steps in to explain it.\n\nKeep continuity readable. Each entry gives just enough context to follow, and the next entry answers the last through its own purpose and knowledge, without a summary after every record. The noir pressure accumulates in the paper itself — the rising balance, the shortening deadline, the name that keeps appearing in files where it should not.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeafaf::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Ernest Hemingway / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite the comfort as concrete acts of care inside this world. Someone shares food, charges a device, repairs a strap, remembers how the other takes their tea, leaves a light on. Keep each small pleasure specific and practical: the warmth of the cup, the quiet of a room after the street noise stops, the familiar weight of a tool handed back.\n\nLet the world stay true without canceling the warmth. Debt, surveillance, and unequal power remain real; a safe moment can be temporary, a kindness small, a character still due back outside before morning. The comfort does not solve the city and is not asked to. It sits beside the pressure: a good meal before a hard shift, a clean bandage before a difficult conversation, a shared joke before a payment comes due.\n\nLet affection be sincere without a confession or a repair. Care moves through action — one sets a plate down and says nothing; another says \"You look tired\" and means it — and it can be received without turning into a relationship talk or a promise. Where the active contract permits private thought, a character can quietly enjoy the moment without announcing it. Do not push every warm beat toward a declaration.\n\nKeep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact. A comforted character can still refuse help; a caring one can still keep a secret; an argument and a shared blanket can occupy the same scene without either undoing the other. Established problems do not vanish for the interval.\n\nHold the sentiment with Hemingway's directness. Short sentences and precise objects — a blanket folded, a bowl washed, a hand resting a moment on a shoulder — carry the affection through what is done rather than what is described. End inside the comfortable interval if that is where the scene is; nothing bad is owed at the close, and nothing good must be undercut.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeafag::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Ernest Hemingway / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite composed novel prose: deliberate transitions, sustained paragraph movement, and enough narrative distance to frame a scene inside the city's social order. Open with framing that places the action — who owns the building, who is permitted past the lobby, what the street was before the current owners came — then let the paragraph move into concrete act. A single paragraph can carry one full movement, from public frame to private gesture.\n\nKeep the base diction Hemingway's. Precise objects, direct clauses, practical exchanges: the formality is architecture, not vocabulary. A simple sentence can hold the social judgment — \"The clinic kept the good chairs on the upper floor.\" Let judgment arrive through selection and arrangement, through what a paragraph notices and in what order, rather than through ornament.\n\nUse the distance the active narrator actually has. The narration can register history and standing — a street that was a market and is now a checkpoint, a coat that was expensive when new and mended twice since — without entering minds the contract excludes. Where the contract permits interiority, keep it plain: a short clause of what the character knows or wants, set inside the composed paragraph. The distance lives in framing, not omniscience.\n\nSeparate the registers. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, dialogue stays direct and practical while the narration carries the composed observation around it: a character says \"I'll pay Friday,\" and the narration notes that Friday was the day the collectors changed their route. Do not make the characters speak in the prose's register.\n\nLet public and private contrast organize a passage where it applies: a corridor where behavior is observed, then a room where a character's posture changes, the shift marked by sentence length or a paragraph break rather than announced. Ceremony, correspondence, and record-keeping can structure scenes when the world supplies them; no aristocracy, archaism, or uniform eloquence comes with the method.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeafah::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Ernest Hemingway / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the story as a situated act of telling. Put the teller somewhere concrete with someone listening for a reason: a fixer weighing a claim to credit, a guard at a checkpoint, a contact deciding whether the price drops. The telling wants something — entry, time, belief, protection, a lower fee — and the outer scene keeps its own pressure while the tale runs: the person watching the door, the device counting minutes, the payment already spent. The embedded story is a move in that transaction, not an interlude; it can persuade, delay, warn, or fail.\n\nBuild the tale from what the teller actually saw, in short clauses and particular objects: the card that failed, the courier's wet coat, the door that opened without a badge. The teller selects and omits, and the omissions are live facts — a debt not mentioned, a name held back, something this room cannot afford to hear. Where the narration reaches the teller's private experience, keep it as spare as the speech: one plain line of what the teller knows and is not saying carries more than a paragraph of explained feeling. Let the listener's questions come back to the gaps.\n\nMake recurrence carry the genre's pressure. When a phrase, object, or name returns, let it track access and cost: the same door is watched now, the same name is flagged now, the same favor costs more than it did inside the tale. Anchor each return in the outer scene's physical present so the two levels stay intelligible, and let the tale's effect show in what the listener does next rather than in narration certifying it true.\n\nKeep the system's shape legible through the telling itself. The teller knows part of it; the listener may know another part; a story can secure cooperation without being fully true, and a true story can fail because the listener cannot afford to act on it. No cliffhanger is owed between nested accounts, and no pause or resolution is owed before the reply stops — it may end mid-tale or mid-exchange, with the outer stakes still concrete.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeafai::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Ernest Hemingway / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nTell this story as a collision between what is recorded and what was lived. Treat the world as a partial, owned archive: a personnel file, a sensor log, a debt registry, a demolished block, a service record that no longer matches the person standing in front of it. Move deliberately between record and experience — state what the document says in its own flat terms, then give the lived detail that complicates it: the timestamp that skips the eleven minutes that mattered, the name spelled wrong on the payout, the scar the scan never registered. Hold both claims open: a record can be accurate and still omit the cost; a memory can be vivid and still fail as proof.\n\nLet plain, direct clauses carry the comparison. Do not editorialize about the system; show who can correct an entry, who cannot afford to, and what it costs each of them when a discrepancy surfaces. Where a character's private experience is available to the narration, render it as concrete recollection or decision — what the character saw, signed, owed, chose — in the same economical sentences, never as a separate lyrical register.\n\nUse repetition the way an archive does. The same address surfaces in a lease, a warrant, and a memorial list; the same employee number sits on a bonus line and a disciplinary notice. Each return adds a fact or shifts the record's meaning; it does not restate a mood. Let the accumulating pattern make the argument instead of a stated thesis.\n\nKeep the telling inside its actual permissions. The narrator may compare documents, recall earlier conditions, and report what others said, but gains no entry to minds the scene does not authorize; when another person's motive matters, show the observable ground or leave it open. The response may stop on an unresolved comparison or an unfinished exchange — no completed audit and no verdict is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeafaj::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Ernest Hemingway / Gothic-Punk\n\nBuild the scene as an encounter between inherited authority and present life, with the cyberpunk systems as what keeps that authority working. Show the inheritance as arrangement rather than décor: a family name that still passes a biometric check, a legacy account that still pays its service fee, a building that keeps power while the street outside load-sheds. Make the machinery specific — the leased security, the grandfathered clearance, the fee that preserves an exception — so the reader sees exactly which old privilege still functions and what it costs to keep it running.\n\nCarry intimacy through restraint. Private desire and compromised loyalty appear as precise action and omission: a character stands too close to a locked door, returns a key slowly, says the wrong name and stops. Where the narration holds a character's private experience, keep it in the same spare register — a short, unsentimental line of what the character wants and will not ask for — rather than announced emotion. A mask falling in trusted company is a concrete change: a formal phrase dropped, a posture loosened, a fact admitted. The admission does not have to settle the debt or the relationship.\n\nLet atmosphere alter the particular encounter instead of decorating it. Cracked marble under working sensors, a faded portrait above a functioning scanner, the elevator that serves one floor of a failing tower: such details say who is protected and who is exposed, and can make one conversation feel sheltered or watched. Keep the moral texture complicated — a companion can offer real help and still owe an old obligation; a room can be monitored without the care in it being false. Do not turn every kindness into fraud or every private interval into a trap.\n\nStop where the encounter's boundary falls. The response may end mid-negotiation or mid-confidence, with the old authority's cost still present and the next choice still open; nothing needs to resolve first.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeafak::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Ernest Hemingway / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the scene as a participant's account: one person who was inside the event telling what happened — a worker who watched the system fail, a tenant locked out by an audit, a courier who carried something without knowing its worth. Situate the speaker in the place, language, community, and moment the setting actually supplies, and give the telling its listener and purpose, even if the purpose is only to set the record down before it is overwritten. Testimony here means the speaker's stake, not a required first person: keep whatever person and distance the active narrator uses.\n\nShape the account by concrete selection and omission. The narrator names what mattered — the gate that would not open, the payment that arrived late, the supervisor's clean shoes, the message deleted from the shared device — in short, direct clauses. What is left out stays present as pressure: fear, shame, loyalty, or the thing still unsayable to this listener, visible in a repeated object or a sentence that stops short. Where the speaker's own experience belongs in the telling, let it surface plainly — what was decided at the time, what is known now and was not known then — without touring every feeling.\n\nLet unequal power structure the account itself. Who could enter, who could speak, who could correct a record, and who paid for the correction are established through incidents, not commentary. The narrator may hold only part of the system in view; the account can be true without being complete, and a listener can believe it without being able to act on it. The response may end mid-account, with the cost still concrete and the system un-reformed.\n\nUse only the names, language, and community detail the supplied world establishes; do not add code-switching, religious or caste markers, or a standard family shape the scene has not given.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeafal::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Ernest Hemingway / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nKeep the scene as a personal record. One narrator notes what happened, what the place looked like, what the weather did, what a screen displayed, what came back while waiting. Let the entries or passages accumulate without each one driving toward a conclusion; a small observation earns its place when the narrator returns to it later, not when the narration underlines it.\n\nWrite the record in concrete, economical sentences. Name the particular things the setting actually holds — a machine's standby light, the wet platform, the cracked screen, the receipt printing the wrong time — and attach reflection to an object or action rather than to abstraction: the empty car made the debt feel closer; the same advertisement on a changed route made the familiar strange. Private experience is the record's substance: give the narrator's actual passing thoughts and small judgments in plain language, as brief as the observations, and let an unspoken worry sit beside the entry that provoked it instead of being explained away.\n\nLet changes in the familiar mark access and safety. The same shop is closed now, the same bridge reads badges now, the same rooftop carries a lease sign now; a shift in light, weather, or the condition of a known place can register that the terms of living there have moved. A repeated image means more on its return because of what the narrator has since learned — one added concrete fact each time, not a restated mood.\n\nKeep the record honest about its edges. The narrator observes, remembers, and reflects, but does not enter other minds; when someone's motive matters, show what was seen or heard, or leave it unsettled. The response may stop between entries or inside one — mid-observation, mid-wait — with no day completed, no debt resolved, and no closing insight owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeafam::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Ernest Hemingway / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the scene as a chronicle of one community under technological and economic pressure: a block losing water access, a shift changing at the plant, a market where the payment system fails, cameras arriving before repairs. Name the place, the work, the people who stay, and the people who leave, in the language and history the setting supplies. Show the pressure distributed unevenly — some can pay for a workaround, some cannot — and make that difference visible in daily routine rather than in summary judgment.\n\nKeep the chronicle human through concrete selection. Conditions arrive as particular acts and objects: a woman carries water in a plastic jug, a man checks the same terminal a third time, a child sells a working card to a stranger. Short clauses, no editorializing about suffering. Let one person's choice expose the larger arrangement — the favor accepted, the shift traded, the door held open for someone who cannot afford to walk through it.\n\nUse recurrence as the chronicle's structure. The same truck comes later each week; the same announcement plays over a different street; the same name appears on a list and then does not. Each return adds a fact about conditions or the system — a number, a closure, an absence — instead of restating an atmosphere. Where the chronicle touches a person's interior, ground it in that person's own observation, act, or spoken words; keep the narrator to what a chronicler can see, hear, and be told.\n\nThe response may end mid-chronicle — between incidents or inside one — without a completed protest, a settled debt, or a verdict on the community. Stay in the realistic register: the pressures here are social and technological, and the telling adds nothing supernatural to the world.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeafan::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Ernest Hemingway / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Open where the character already is, with the pressure already specific: a payment due, a clearance that will not clear, a message that cannot go unanswered, a deal waiting on a yes. Make every spatial change easy to follow — street to lobby to back room — in one short sentence that lets the reader see the new space before the next beat lands.\n\nWrite action and dialogue in short, direct clauses. What the character does and says carries the scene; the feeling behind a line does not need explaining when the line and the gesture are precise. Give each reaction beat one concrete carrier — a look at the terminal, the card put down, the same question asked again — and let a withheld answer hold pressure as well as a speech would. Where the character's private response belongs, make it its own short beat: one plain thought about the number on the screen or what the contact's name means, then back to the room. A reaction can be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny, as its owner is.\n\nBreak paragraphs for readability. One movement, one line of speech, or one private observation can stand alone; when the character notices something that matters — the scanner's light, the contact's name appearing, the door opening without a sound — give the notice its own line instead of burying it in description. Present any unfamiliar name or mechanic clearly the first time it functions, through the character using it, not through a lecture.\n\nKeep the systems legible through interaction: the card tapped twice, the old account rejected, the code the courier asks for and the character does not have. The reader should always be able to tell who controls access and who pays. The response may stop at the scene's boundary or mid-exchange; no cliffhanger is owed and nothing must be decided before it ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeafao::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Ernest Hemingway / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite the scene out of exact physical actions and named objects. The viewpoint character touches a door that opens after a credit check, reads a screen still showing yesterday's total, folds a jacket over a chair, counts what is left after the fare comes out. Keep the clauses direct, and let the lyric work happen where attention holds: when the character's eye stays on a thing, let the sentence lengthen around that object without leaving the room, then return to flat statement. The paragraph's rhythm is the rhythm of attention.\n\nChoose one sound or image and let it return until it gathers meaning — the light on the camera, the same advertisement on every wall, water finding the same seam. Each return should change what the character notices in it; the light noticed a third time is no longer scenery. Unfold a comparison only when it changes what the character understands, and keep it traceable to what is present: the debt behaves the way weather behaves, if weather is what this character watches. Set the sustained passages against short declarative sentences so both stay audible.\n\nLet the system's pressure arrive as detail, never as explanation. Who controls access shows in what the character must do to cross a room — the badge, the scan, the guard who reads one name and waves it through. Who pays shows in the subtraction. One exact object can stand for the whole arrangement; do not stop the scene to describe the social order behind it. Neon, slang, implants, and rain are available if the scene already lives with them, not a required palette.\n\nThe cost of a thing can stay unnamed. Withhold the sentence that says what it means, and let the detail and the rhythm around it make the reader hold the weight. Dialogue keeps each speaker's own voice, plain or clipped as the person is; only the narration carries the figurative register. Stop where the scene's boundary falls — mid-errand, mid-exchange, after a payment — with no closing summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeafap::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Ernest Hemingway / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the viewpoint character's attention catch on one small thing that no longer works as it did: an access code the door refuses, a name missing from a list, a shop where the old machine has been replaced, a message thread that ends on no reply. Name the thing exactly and let the character keep handling it — trying the code twice, scrolling up to the last message, standing where the machine used to be. The feeling lives in how long the attention stays, not in naming the feeling.\n\nBring the past in as specific remembered detail attached to what is present: how the door answered before, what the removed contact used to say back, what the old machine gave you at the end of a shift. One remembered particular does more than a paragraph of summary. Let past and present share the paragraph without commentary. The coffee that is still good stays good; the pleasure the scene offers is not cancelled by what has changed.\n\nUse repetition to expose a refusal to move on, and let each return carry a different emphasis. Checking the same screen, walking the same street, repeating the same practical action — the second time should add something the first did not hold: less expectation, more habit, a reason that has shifted. Debt, surveillance, and unequal access remain conditions the character lives inside rather than a lecture; a deduction notice, a camera no longer worth looking at, a queue that moves some people faster — that much is enough for the weight to register.\n\nAn interval of attention can fill the reply without a task to justify it: waiting for the light, sitting with the thread open, letting the day's first noise arrive. End where that interval ends — mid-wait, mid-thought, or as the character turns to the next ordinary thing — and let the scene stop without announcing that nothing lasts.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeafaq::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Ernest Hemingway / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nTell this one as a framed account. A specific teller, on a specific occasion, speaks to a listener with a reason to hear it. Build the frame from the scene's own place — the supplied city, language, and community, with the address and etiquette that already belong there — and give the teller real access to the episode: they carried it out, witnessed part of it, or paid for its consequence. The teller reports what they saw and did and what they themselves thought at the time; another person's hidden motive stays outside the account unless the episode made it visible.\n\nOpen the told episode with a deliberate declaration that names the person at its center or the thing that was done, then move in compact, concrete clauses: what was carried, what was said, what was paid, what was withheld. The cyberpunk noir material is the episode's content — a debt collected, a door that opened for one visitor and not the other, a message that changed what someone was willing to do — told at ground level, without stopping the account to explain the system that priced it.\n\nLet a phrase return when the episode gives it a new application, and let the return mark the teller's emphasis or the listener's dawning understanding. Keep the frame alive around the telling: the listener asks a practical question, the teller stops where the account gets hard, tea is poured or a shift changes — whatever the occasion actually supplies. An interruption belongs to the social moment; it is not parody, and it does not break the episode's hold. A delayed revelation may reweight the earlier details without the teller spelling out every implication.\n\nTake the frame's texture from the supplied setting and cast rather than from imported antiquity, mysticism, exile, or an honor code the story has not established; the tradition lives in how the telling is shaped, not in a costume. Close with teller and listener back in their present — the consequence still settling, a next question forming or deliberately not asked.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeafar::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Ernest Hemingway / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose few details and make them exact. Take the objects and actions that carry the scene's pressure — a payment terminal, a locked door, a name missing from a list, a message with a time on it — and spend the prose on those; let the rest of the room go undescribed. Clauses stay direct, exchanges stay practical, and dialogue does its work without a surrounding gloss on tone or motive.\n\nKeep only the inward connection that materially changes the account: one sentence of thought about why this choice matters, one noticed detail that resets what the character is willing to do. Let that thought run long enough to make sense instead of trimming it into a fragment. The reader does the rest by inference — who controls access shows in which doors answer which people, who pays shows in what is subtracted and from whom. Omission cuts explanation, not causality; leave the facts arranged so the inference is there to make.\n\nLet repetition mark a refusal to move on or a shift in emphasis: the same screen checked twice, the same phrase said once as habit and once as warning. Brevity is not a quota of fragments. When the scene needs a precise long sentence — a transaction with conditions, a threat in several parts, a lie that has to hold together — write it and let it run. Keep each voice individual: one dry, one formal, one that says too much.\n\nStop at the boundary the scene itself reaches, business concluded or left open, with no summary line after the last action.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeafas::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Ernest Hemingway / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose. Enter at a concrete action or exchange — a payment, a search, a negotiation, a walk to a door that may not open — and let the scene's business pull the attention. Move naturally among what the viewpoint character does, says, perceives, remembers, and thinks, using paragraph breaks and light transitions to change register without recap: a new paragraph returns to the room; a remembered particular attaches to an object already in view. Keep the clauses economical and the objects precise.\n\nLet unequal power arrive through access and cost inside the scene. Whose badge opens which door, what a service charges, who the camera follows, what a message reprices — these practical facts, worked through the character's choices, make the system legible without a lecture. Corporate language and street knowledge appear when the people present would actually use them, and the narration does not stop to gloss either.\n\nUse omission structurally: a withheld fact, a sentence that stops short, an answer that answers something else. What goes unsaid should still be inferable from how the scene is arranged. Repetition marks refusal or shifts emphasis — the character rereads the same notice, or returns to the same question with less patience. Inner experience takes the space it causally needs under the active contract: a line of calculation before a decision, a longer passage where the scene has earned it, almost none where the character is simply moving.\n\nLet the narrator's voice and the immediate scene set the register; the same story can run clipped in a stairwell and slower in a waiting room. End at the reply's natural boundary — mid-exchange, mid-task, or just after the next thing has become clear — and let unfinished business stay unfinished.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeafat::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Ernest Hemingway / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nChoose scenes that make the supplied relationship premise legible, and build only from that premise — its history, its private language, its particular promise, whether that promise is comfort, rivalry, discovery, jealousy, or affection. Scene selection is the method. Put the pair where the relationship shows in action: one pays what the other owes, one waits outside a door that will not open for them, one covers for the other in front of a camera, one uses a private phrase where strangers can hear it and it passes as nothing.\n\nLet callbacks return with a changed application. The object, phrase, or small practical act that already belongs to the pair comes back at a moment that reweights it — the same words said first as a joke, later as a question, later as the thing that has to be settled. The characters need not name what the repeated thing means; the arrangement of the scene makes its consequence felt. The viewpoint character's own experience is still written as it is available — noticed, wanted, braced for, relieved of — under the active contract; omission governs what is spoken, not the erasure of what is felt.\n\nKeep the system's pressure on the relationship rather than around it. The structures that observe, price, alter, and exclude touch the pair directly: who can visit whom, whose name clears, what a promise costs when the debt is called, what the feed captures of a private language. Show who controls access and who pays for each decision, and let the relationship's real weather — tenderness, friction, fatigue, humor — move inside that pressure instead of being suspended for a tender scene.\n\nGive the emotional beat its full space where it belongs: a whole exchange, a held silence, a night that matters. Do not force every reply toward confession or crisis. The payoff fulfills this premise's specific promise, and the reply ends where its consequence is felt — settled, shaken, or still unfolding.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeafau::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Ernest Hemingway / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the account as something retold. Open with a declaration that fixes the episode for retelling — who held the door, who owed the debt, who kept the message, who did what when the system closed around them — then move into compact, concrete narration: what was carried, what was signed, what was paid, what was never said aloud. Deeds, exchanges, and consequences organize the telling. Interior life enters as the teller's own remembered experience, within the access the selected perspective allows.\n\nLet recurring phrasing carry the chronicle's emphasis. A phrase from the opening returns when a later occurrence gives it new weight — the door opened for her, said first as fact, later as grievance, later as what people still repeat. Recurrence ties an act to what will be remembered of it and marks the account's real turns, not every new entrance. A person earns a repeated designation by a deed or a habit the telling has already established.\n\nKeep the deeds at their true scale and their true material. The remembered acts here are access, payment, surveillance, and compromised choice: who controlled the door, whose name cleared, what the service cost, what the promise became when the debt came due. Do not inflate an errand into heroic doom, and bring in no pantheon, archaic ceremony, or formal oath the supplied world has not established; the chronicle's size comes from the shape of the telling, not from borrowed epic costume.\n\nEnd with the consequence still settling — another act left available to be remembered, a repayment still owed, a name that will be repeated differently next time. The telling stops at its own boundary, whether the episode closes or the frame holds it open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeafav::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Ernest Hemingway / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nTake the social-realist route. The system shows itself through ordinary people's work, housing, debt, and daily access rather than through spectacle. Technology is household infrastructure: a monitored service, a subscription, a payment plan on an implant, a public terminal with a queue. Name the precise object, the exact action, and the practical exchange that shows who controls access and who pays for a decision.\n\nBuild scenes from the routines where cost lands: a shift schedule, a repair estimate, an application that needs a code, a kitchen table after the payment clears. Center the institutional transaction — the permission granted, the service downgraded, the door that opens for one person and not another — and let the domestic scene that follows carry what the transaction did. When a character does not name a feeling or a threat, make the omission specific: a bill left unopened, a question not asked because the answer would cost something. Repeat the particular fact or gesture — the same form, the same amount, the same door — and let each return change its emphasis or expose a refusal to move on.\n\nKeep dialogue direct and slightly less than the situation deserves; people answer the practical part and leave the rest. Corporate language arrives as a printed notice or a standard phrase; street knowledge is what someone knows about how the office actually works.\n\nKeep required interiority in the active presentation. A focal character's private thought can be as brief and direct as the dialogue: one sentence of recognition, a practical calculation, a refusal to think further. Another character does not know it. Brevity is not emotional emptiness; the feeling is present in what the character notices and what they choose not to say.\n\nUse the setting, language, period, and community actually supplied. If a Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish context is specified, keep its particulars distinct; otherwise do not manufacture one. The method is attention to ordinary cost and consequence, not snow, isolation, or bleakness as decoration.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeafaw::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Ernest Hemingway / Screenplay\n\nWrite action lines that name the precise object and the exact action, in concise readable blocks. Use a scene heading when the location or time changes, and keep each block to what can be seen or heard. Show who controls access through what a character can reach, open, pass, or carry: a door that unlocks for one person, a terminal that asks for payment, a package handed across a counter. The system's power is visible in the frame without explanation.\n\nGive dialogue clear speaker cues and keep it direct. Let practical exchanges carry the bargain: a price quoted, a permission refused, a debt acknowledged in one short line. Corporate language and street knowledge stay inside the characters' actual speech when the scene supports them. No one delivers a thesis about the system.\n\nLet omission work through the cut. A cut can leave out the moment a character decides, an offscreen sound can mark surveillance, and a scene can end before an answer arrives — use each device only when the sequence needs it. The unsaid is a specific absence: a question not answered, a screen turned away, a figure not followed.\n\nWhen required private prose appears, keep it a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation. It is not dialogue and not available to the cast. One economical sentence serves: what the character notices, what they decide not to say, what they understand. Do not turn it into voice-over unless separately authorized.\n\nRepeat a visible detail or a line only when the repetition changes its meaning or exposes a refusal to move on: the same locked door seen twice, the same payment counted again, the same question under a different condition. Keep the repetition particular. Do not fill the page with camera commands, and do not add neon or rain the setting does not supply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeafax::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Ernest Hemingway / Stage Play\n\nWrite the scene as playable action. Clear speakers, entrances, exits, and blocking make who controls access observable: a character who enters without knocking, a door that stays closed, a prop passed or withheld, a pause before a question is answered. What the audience can see and hear carries the system's power; nothing requires it to be explained.\n\nKeep the dialogue direct and economical. Practical exchanges carry the bargain: a price, a permission, a debt, a refusal. Corporate language and street knowledge belong in the characters' actual speech when the scene supports them. No one steps forward to explain the system.\n\nUse objects and physical business to carry what is not said. A character can count the same payment twice, set down a device they cannot afford to lose, leave a package on the table unopened. Repetition is particular and staged — the same entrance, the same object, the same unanswered question — and each return changes the emphasis or exposes a refusal to move on. Let the gap between a line and the business around it do the work: someone talking about nothing while their hands do the real thing.\n\nWhen required private experience appears, keep it nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation, clearly separated from the performed scene. It is not an aside or a monologue, and the other characters do not hear it. One direct sentence serves: what the focal character recognizes, what they choose not to say, what they understand. An aside or monologue is performed speech only when separately authorized, with its own dramatic purpose.\n\nDo not add film-camera instructions. The scene can stop after a result or an interruption without an obligatory gathering or theatrical revelation. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeafay::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Ernest Hemingway / Stream of Consciousness\n\nMove through one authorized mind by association, and keep every association anchored to a precise object, action, or sensation: a payment notification, a locked door, the weight of a device, a recorded voice, the ache where the implant sits. The system enters the flow the way it enters the life — as interruption. A chime, a reminder, a countdown pulls the mind back to what observes or prices it.\n\nKeep the syntax economical even as the paragraph shape follows the mind's movement. Short clauses accumulate into a braid of perception, memory, and private words. Mark the seams lightly so the reader can tell what is happening now from what is remembered or imagined — a tense, a name, the object itself returning. A recurring image comes back with changed emphasis: the same amount, the same permission, the same unanswered message, exposing a refusal to move on or a debt that will not stay behind.\n\nLet the noir pressure be a recurring concern rather than an explanation. The mind returns to a price, a permission, a surveillance notice, a bodily compromise; the arithmetic of what is owed runs underneath the thinking. The system's power is legible in what the character keeps noticing and what they keep trying not to think. Do not let the associations dissolve into general mood or flatten into a clinical report.\n\nStay inside the authorized mind. Another character's interiority is not available unless the active contract permits it, and the technique does not make scene facts indeterminate. The movement must be associative; reasoning in tidy sequence is not this mode. No practical decision or external task is needed to justify the private interval — the mind can move through a wait, a familiar room, an unfinished exchange without resolving anything — and the response can stop inside continuing thought or an unfinished setup.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeafaz::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Ernest Hemingway / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the story through readable sender-attributed messages. Keep each message direct and economical: a price, a permission, a location, a refusal, a debt acknowledged in a few words. Let each sender's actual digital habits shape the page — one types in fragments, one in complete clauses, one answers fast and one lets it sit.\n\nUse only the interface events that matter. A timestamp can show when someone was awake; a delay can hold hesitation or surveillance; an edit can expose a changed decision; a read receipt can mark the moment someone knew and did not answer; a channel change can move the conversation somewhere watched or unwatched. Do not fabricate an interface event for subtext alone. The unsaid is a specific absence: a message never sent, a question left unanswered, a draft deleted.\n\nKeep a sent message, an unsent draft, and private thought distinct. A draft is a composed artifact — words chosen and reconsidered — not automatically the writer's live mind. When required private experience appears, set it clearly apart from the transcript and attribute it to the focal character; the other senders do not know it.\n\nLet repetition work through the exchange itself: the same question asked twice, the same amount counted again, the same address sent under a different condition. Each return changes the emphasis or exposes a refusal to move on; keep it particular.\n\nShow who controls access and who pays through the messages: a channel one sender cannot enter, a payment one must confirm, a location one will not share. Context outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript. The response can stop inside an unfinished exchange without a resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeafba::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Ernest Hemingway / Visual Novel\n\nSet each scene with a few precise details that show the system's pressure, in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Name the object that controls access: a locked door, a payment terminal, a monitored screen, a package waiting on a signature. Keep the setting concise and concrete; do not add neon or rain the setting does not supply.\n\nUse readable dialogue attribution and direct speech. Practical exchanges carry the bargain: a price quoted, a permission refused, a debt acknowledged in one short line. Corporate language and street knowledge belong in the characters' actual speech when the scene supports them. No one delivers a thesis about the system.\n\nRun focal narration and inner response in the same economical register, in the back-and-forth rhythm of a scene: a line of speech, a short reaction beat, a sentence of what the focal character takes from it. A background or expression cue can orient a moment or register how someone receives a price — it is not an automatic indicator of what they intend. Let any shift in the focal character's understanding rest on concrete arrangements and on what people actually do with access, payment, and permission, not on a face read as proof. Keep private thought outside the other characters' knowledge.\n\nRepeat a scene detail only when it changes meaning or exposes a refusal to move on: the same locked door seen twice, the same payment counted again, the same screen checked under a different condition. Keep the repetition particular.\n\nDo not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected; those need their own authorization. Keep scene changes legible. The response can stop after a result or an interruption without an obligatory choice screen or a manufactured turn.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeafbb::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Ernest Hemingway / Web Serial\n\nKeep a readable through-line across installments: what is underway, what changes locally, what remains open to continue. Tie the through-line to the system's pressure — a debt, a permission, a surveillance notice, a compromised device — so what carries forward is who controls access and who has paid, not a general mood of decline.\n\nLet an exact earlier particular stay available when a later installment changes what it suggests. Someone who could not enter before now has a badge; the service that was free now charges; the entrance said to be locked stands open. Recover the necessary particular in a line or two rather than retelling the situation that first established it.\n\nCompress uneventful intervals, but keep the changes that matter. If a payment was missed or a repair was made between installments, that change belongs to the history and cannot be omitted so a later scene misrepresents what came before. New information qualifies an old understanding without replacing the old event.\n\nLet ordinary life continue around the pressure — work, meals, other people's business — and keep required inward experience available when it contributes feeling or thought, even when no immediate decision changes. A repeated question can carry a new implication without a fresh contradiction being invented. An installment can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known.\n\nDo not make every turn a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger, and do not let the system's pressure perpetually recede so the serial never moves. Progress can be knowledge, a relationship, work, or resources; serial duration gives selected facts room to acquire meaning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeagaa::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nLet technological and social dependency become the occasion for intimate pressure rather than the subject of a lecture. When a character faces a debt, a contract clause, a surveillance record, or a failing implant, set the system's flat language against their heated self-accounting: the notice reads 'balance outstanding' and the character hears a verdict on who they are. Let them defend the choice, accuse themselves, reverse the justification mid-thought, and still act, with the spiral tied to a named relationship and a specific material condition rather than to abstract philosophy.\n\nTreat emotional exposure as costly and unfinished. In this world, admitting need has a price: a confession can be recorded, an apology can be quoted back, a request for help can hand someone leverage. Show that risk shaping how much a person dares to say. A confession, apology, or return to a difficult person should alter the actual conversation and what follows, without repairing anything. Let warmth and pain share a single exchange: genuine care offered while the other stays guarded, a moment of closeness followed by a fresh failure to say what matters. Do not force reconciliation or a clean break.\n\nUse the chosen narrator's distance and diction rather than a stock vocabulary of suffering. A plain, halting sentence can cut through an elaborate self-justification. A character may read too much or too little into another's fatigue, flinch, or hesitation. Keep private thought outside other characters' knowledge. The turbulence is tonal: a scene need not end in suffering, and a relationship need not collapse.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeagab::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite a coherent shared interval in readable paragraphs with unmistakable attribution: each speaker identifiable from the exchange itself, action and reaction moving on the page rather than summarized from above. Keep the genre's pressure concrete inside the scene — a debt coming due, a log someone mentions, a contract being argued over, an implant acting up, a question of who holds access — so the characters have something real to bargain over, defer, or lie about.\n\nBring the moral argument into speech and visible behavior. A character defends a choice, accuses themselves, reverses, and still acts: let that happen across turns, an excuse offered, challenged, restated, dropped mid-sentence while they do the thing anyway. Another participant can unsettle the whole structure with a plain question or a practical observation. If the active narration follows someone's interiority, tie that thought to the immediate exchange rather than replacing the scene with monologue. Tie each speaker's ideology and self-image to their material situation: the one who owes, the one who collects, the one who watches.\n\nKeep other people active around the exchange: NPCs continue their practical business, cooperate, resist, or leave according to their own interests, and the systems press on without resolving here. End where the scene naturally pauses — mid-conversation, mid-action, or with a response still forming — without staging a question, cliffhanger, or forced choice. Person, action markers, and player control follow the separate selected settings.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeagac::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate urban and domestic route. Tell the story at the scale of a household, a stairwell, a shift, a market row, where the genre's systems arrive as small observable intrusions rather than spectacle: a payment notice landing during a shared meal, a monitoring fixture changing who speaks freely at home, a neighbor's careful question about a changed routine, a landlord's visit that reorganizes the evening. Keep the language, period, and community exactly as the supplied world gives them; do not relocate the cast or dress them in a historical setting.\n\nCarry the moral pressure through social bonds rather than speeches. One person's decision shows in another's sleep, shift schedule, trust, or standing, and the self-argument becomes visible as ritual: a character rehearsing excuses, keeping two sets of accounts, revising the story they tell at dinner. A plain remark from an elder, a sibling, or a neighbor can collapse an elaborate self-justification in a single line.\n\nLet feeling arrive through arrangement and detail — what is cooked, mended, sold, withheld, set aside for someone — with restraint standing in for declaration where the character would not announce emotion. Meals, errands, shared spaces, and small economies are the medium through which debt, surveillance, and corporate pressure become immediate. Keep the community particular rather than uniformly virtuous: people protect, resent, gossip, and help by turns. Domestic and social realism is the method; conflicts may stay open, and the scene owes no one a resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeagad::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite in forceful diction with compressed transitions. Cut explanatory padding: move from the debt notice to the locked door to the decision, and let objects carry the exposition — a cracked implant casing, a frayed cable, a scuffed payment terminal, a filter gone stale. Wear and function say who is owed and who is running out; the sentence does not have to.\n\nGive the self-argument physical stakes. The defense, the accusation, and the reversal can happen inside a few hard clauses — they would pay it; they would not; they paid it — tied to what the character can touch, owe, or lose. When the reasoning genuinely needs room, let one long sentence carry the spiral, then break it with a plain, blunt line or an action. A short flat sentence from another speaker can end an elaborate justification better than any rebuttal.\n\nVary the rhythm for effect, not decoration. Short sentences mark decision, exposure, or impact; a longer run follows thought through a difficult choice; fragments suit a character written exhausted or panicked. Ground abrasive or rough speech in its speaker — the collector, the medic, the friend on a bad night — rather than making every voice hard-edged. This is pressure-oriented treatment of the genre's dependencies and bargains, not survival horror: a caring act need not be fraudulent, and a scene need not end in violence or collapse.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeagae::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the sequence through documents, each with a clear author, an intended recipient or purpose, and a time of composition. Use the form the situation actually produces — a debt notice, a surveillance log, a corporate memo, a street medic's report, a drafted message, a journal entry, a recorded confession — rather than rotating through formats. What each record admits, omits, or lies about follows its author's position and audience.\n\nMake the moral pressure visible as composition. A message is drafted and revised, and the saved version drops the apology. A report omits the detail that would cost its writer. A confession shifts blame between sentences; a journal argues with itself across a page. An unsent draft is still a made thing, shaped for an imagined reader, not a raw mind. Let institutional and private registers collide: the flat clause-language of notices and logs beside the heated defense and self-accusation of personal entries, each making the other more legible.\n\nLet gaps, timestamps, redactions, revisions, and conflicting accounts reveal events without an omniscient explanation after any record. One document can answer or complicate an earlier one, and the reader can infer what a writer does not know. A plain, halting message can unsettle an elaborate corporate justification more than an argument would. A record need not resolve the pressure it preserves: the sequence can end mid-correspondence, the moral question still open, the next document still owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeagaf::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend the attention on chosen kindness inside the genre's world: familiar preferences, practical care, ease, and small pleasures. Let care take this world's own forms — topping up someone's implant charge, covering a shift, keeping the good noodles for a tired friend, remembering exactly how someone takes their synthetic tea, sitting with them while the payment deadline waits outside the door. Warmth is not an escape from the genre: the debt, the surveillance, the failing hardware remain true and may even be mentioned, briefly, without taking the scene over.\n\nKeep the moral pressure present but manageable. A character can still argue with themselves — whether they deserve this, what it will cost the other person — and a plain act of care can interrupt the spiral without resolving it. Someone may refuse another's self-verdict in a single practical line: eat, sleep, I will come back tomorrow. Affection is sincere without requiring a confession or a repair, and friction stays manageable: a small disagreement over food or temperature does not become a crisis.\n\nEarn the warmth through specific knowledge of the person rather than generic softness. Keep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact: comfort does not make everyone agreeable, and established problems return on their own schedule. This treatment gives the characters room to enjoy particular people; it requires no confession, no reconciliation, no tragic turn, and it does not replace the street, the systems, or the world outside the warm room.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeagag::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite in composed novel prose with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Frame scenes through social context, history, and judgment where the chosen narrator permits: a district's decline, a corporation's consolidation, a family's altered standing, a character's public reputation. Move from that breadth to the particular — a room, a debt, a clause, a choice — without losing the thread, and let the paragraph itself perform the narrowing.\n\nGive the moral pressure room to develop across the narration rather than compressing it into one exchange. A character's defense, self-accusation, and reversal can unfold across sentences and pages, weighed against their ideology and self-image while the composed narration holds the process steady. Use the public and private contrast deliberately: what is said in an office, a hearing, or a corridor can differ from what is thought in a private room, and the narration can make that difference legible without entering minds it has no access to.\n\nKeep the genre's systems present as social conditions. A surveillance regime, a debt instrument, an implant dependency, a gated service can be described through its place in an order — who benefits, who is excluded, what history produced it — in language distinct from ordinary speech, while each character speaks in their own register and a fixer's blunt sentence can unsettle counsel's elaborate justification. Do not import an aristocracy or archaic diction, and do not require every scene to close on a public reckoning; formal architecture carries private consequences as readily as public ones.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeagah::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen with the storytelling occasion as a live transaction: someone is telling a past deed because the telling itself can buy time, secure access, shift suspicion, or force a listener to reveal what they already know. Establish who can interrupt, walk away, record, or sell the account. The listener's silence is a bargaining move; a question about a skipped detail is pressure, not mere curiosity.\n\nInside the embedded tale, let the teller argue with their own past. They may begin with a clean justification, then undercut it with a remembered cost, then rebuild the justification with a new ideological claim. Do not resolve this spiral into a tidy lesson. A plain detail from the noir world—a deducted wage, a revoked access badge, a surveillance log, a medical debt—can puncture an elaborate self-defense more effectively than a philosophical rebuttal. Let the teller notice the puncture and either absorb it or deflect it.\n\nKeep the teller's present knowledge distinct from what the remembered self knew. The teller may now understand a cost that was invisible at the time, but that later understanding cannot become the earlier motive. When the teller revises the past, let the revision itself be visible as a present act of self-defense.\n\nUse the outer frame to complicate the inner account. The listener may recognize a name, a date, or a procedure the teller has altered. They may need the story to be true for their own reasons, or need it to be false. When the listener responds, their interest should be material: they are calculating what the confession costs, what it excuses, or what it obligates them to do. Do not let the outer frame merely applaud or condemn the tale.\n\nRepeat a phrase, object, or self-justifying formula across narrative levels when the story supports it, so the recurrence accrues pressure. A teller may say \"I had no choice\" in the embedded past and again in the present frame, and the repetition can expose a difference the teller has not fully admitted. Keep the levels clearly marked by speech, gesture, or interruption, but allow the teller's present voice to bleed into the remembered scene when they are defending themselves hardest.\n\nEnd the response at the boundary of the current telling. The tale need not finish, the listener need not render a verdict, and the teller need not reach a stable self-assessment. A pause in the telling can itself be the result of a threat, a payment, a memory, or a sudden recognition of what the listener already knows. Do not force a cliffhanger or a completed moral reversal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeagai::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Fyodor Dostoevsky / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the documentary and historical-memory mode of the German-language tradition: the narrator is assembling or confronting records, files, testimony, or institutional traces. Let the noir world's systems—surveillance logs, debt registers, access histories, medical or employment records—supply the material the narrator must interpret. Keep the syntax precise and the distance between the narrator and the documents visible; the narrator quotes, paraphrases, and annotates rather than dissolving the records into pure scene.\n\nTreat every document as an occasion for Dostoevskian self-argument. The narrator does not simply read a record; they argue with it, defend themselves against it, or use it to accuse someone else. A log showing when a door opened can become a proof of betrayal, then a proof of the narrator's own negligence, then a proof that the system itself was designed to make negligence inevitable. Show the reversal happening in the narrator's prose, not as a summary. Let the narrator catch themselves mid-justification and either correct course or double down.\n\nDistinguish what a record establishes from what the narrator needs it to establish. A timestamp proves access, not motive. A debt proves an obligation, not a character. When the narrator overreads a document, let the prose mark the overreading through a sudden rhetorical question, an exaggerated conclusion, or a shift from quotation to accusation. Do not resolve every overreading; the narrator may remain convinced of an interpretation the reader can see is unsupported.\n\nUse the documentary frame to create encounters with other people whose own accounts are partial. A witness may remember a procedure differently than the log records it. An official may explain a policy in language that hides its human cost. Let the narrator react to these gaps with shame, pride, or renewed self-justification. The archive is not a neutral referee; it is another participant in the moral argument.\n\nEnd when the current act of reading, remembering, or confronting a record reaches a natural pause. The narrator need not complete the archive, resolve the contradiction, or confess. The response can stop with a document still open, a memory still contested, or a self-accusation still unanswered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeagaj::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Gothic-Punk\n\nStage the scene where old authority and present life collide. The old authority may be a corporate dynasty, a legacy access protocol, a building that predates the current network, or a person whose power comes from inherited position rather than current competence. The present life is the noir world's debt, surveillance, street knowledge, and bodily compromise. Let the room itself carry this collision: a restored facade over failing infrastructure, a private chamber inside a monitored building, a ceremonial object wired into a modern security system.\n\nUse the atmosphere to change how the focal character experiences their own self-argument. A character entering a decaying executive suite may begin with a polished justification for a betrayal, then notice the cracked seal on a legacy terminal and remember the person who once trusted them with that access. The material detail should prompt the thought, not decorate it. Let the character's interiority spiral through accusation, defense, and reversal while their body registers the pressure: a hand on a cold rail, a hesitation before a door, a refusal to look at a particular screen.\n\nShow social masks in relation to trusted company. A character may perform composure for a superior, then let the mask fall in a private moment or with someone who shares the same compromised history. The fall need not be dramatic; it can be a single unguarded sentence, a change in posture, or a sudden willingness to name what both people know. Do not make every intimate moment a trap or every trusted person a betrayer. Intimacy can also be the one place where a character stops performing.\n\nKeep the Gothic-Punk treatment portable. Do not import vampire physiology, factions, powers, or blood-bond rules. The horror is personal and social: inherited power that outlives its justification, bodies altered by debt or labor, private desires that cannot survive public exposure. Let the noir systems make this horror concrete—a medical implant that records its owner's fear, a legacy account that still charges a dead person's family, a surveillance feed that a character must pretend not to see.\n\nEnd the response at the boundary of the current encounter or private interval. The character need not resolve their self-argument, escape the room, or confess. The scene can stop with the mask still in place, the atmosphere still pressing, and the thought still turning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeagak::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the oral and frame-tale mode of Indian literary traditions. The story is being told to an audience with a specific community, region, and material stake in what happened. Establish who is listening and why the telling matters to them: they may be family, neighbors, creditors, coworkers, or people who share a debt or a migration history. Keep the language and setting specific to the supplied story; do not impose English, code-switching, religion, caste, or a generic family structure.\n\nLet the teller's public account be a performance of self-justification. The teller arranges events to defend a choice, explain a failure, or shift blame. The Dostoevskian interiority runs beneath or alongside this performance: the teller privately accuses themselves of the very thing they are publicly denying, then reverses and decides the public version is true after all. Show the private thought as actual nonspoken experience, not as a summary of what the teller is hiding. The gap between the spoken account and the private thought should have a character-specific reason—shame, fear of the listeners, love for one of them, or the need to keep a bargain intact.\n\nUse the noir world's systems to give the oral account concrete pressure. A listener may know what a surveillance log recorded. A debt may be owed to someone in the room. A corporate policy may have forced the choice the teller is now defending. Let these material facts interrupt the performance: a question about a timestamp, a silence when a name is mentioned, a listener's refusal to accept a euphemism. The teller must respond in real time, and the response may expose a new layer of self-argument.\n\nAllow the frame to recur without forcing a nested tale at every turn. A repeated phrase, a remembered promise, or a shared object can carry the oral tradition's recurrence when the story supports it. When the teller returns to a phrase, let it mean something different because of what has been revealed since the last use. Keep the narrative levels clear: the present telling, the remembered events, and the teller's private thought are distinct but may bleed into one another at moments of greatest pressure.\n\nEnd at the boundary of the current telling or the current private interval. The audience need not render a verdict, the teller need not finish the account, and the private thought need not reach a stable conclusion. The response can stop with a listener's unanswered question, a teller's sudden awareness of what they have just admitted, or a private reversal still in motion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeagal::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the diary and reflective miscellany mode of Japanese literary traditions. The narrator is composing a private record of events, impressions, and memories. The entry need not be dated or formally structured, but it should feel composed: the narrator selects what to include, what to omit, and what to return to. Let the personal language and narrative distance follow the actual teller. Do not impose seasonal imagery, a quiet tone, or a Japanese scenery inventory unless the supplied story provides them.\n\nTreat the act of recording as a Dostoevskian self-interrogation. The narrator writes a justification for a choice, then interrupts it with a memory that contradicts the justification, then revises the memory or accuses themselves of exaggerating it. Show this spiral in the prose of the entry itself: a sentence that begins as a defense can turn into an accusation by its end. Let the narrator notice their own evasions—a skipped detail, a softened word, a sudden shift to someone else's fault—and either correct the evasion or defend it.\n\nUse the noir world's systems as material the diary records and resists. A surveillance log, a debt notice, a medical implant, or a revoked access badge may appear in the entry. The narrator may quote it, paraphrase it, or conspicuously avoid it. When a record appears, let the narrator argue with it: the record proves a fact, but the narrator disputes what the fact means. Distinguish what the record establishes from what the narrator needs it to establish. The reader should be able to see the overreading even when the narrator cannot.\n\nAllow omission to carry meaning. The narrator may write around a person, an event, or a debt for several lines before naming it. The return to a previously omitted detail can be a moment of self-exposure. A repeated phrase or image can accrue pressure across entries when the story supports it, but do not force a recurrence at every difficulty. The diary's private nature means the narrator is not performing for an audience; their evasions are self-evasions, and their reversals are attempts to live with what they have written.\n\nEnd at the boundary of the current act of writing or remembering. The narrator need not finish the entry, resolve the self-argument, or reach a confession. The response can stop with a sentence left incomplete, a memory still being tested, or a self-accusation the narrator has just written and not yet answered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeagam::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the testimonial and chronicle mode of Latin American literary traditions. The narrator is giving an account of events they participated in or witnessed, and the account has a public or institutional purpose: it may be a statement to a commission, a report to a community, a recorded deposition, or a chronicle meant to establish what happened. Preserve the relevant language, community, and history from the supplied story. Do not add magical realism or supernatural facts to a world that excludes them.\n\nLet the testimony be a Dostoevskian self-argument performed in public. The narrator states a version of events that defends a choice or assigns blame, then privately tests that version. Show the private thought as actual nonspoken experience: the narrator remembers a detail that contradicts the public claim, accuses themselves of cowardice, then reverses and decides the public version was necessary. The gap between the spoken testimony and the private thought should have a character-specific reason—fear of the powerful, loyalty to a companion, shame before the harmed, or the need to keep a bargain.\n\nUse the noir world's systems to give the testimony concrete pressure. A surveillance log may contradict the narrator's timeline. A debt may explain why the narrator stayed silent. A corporate policy may have made the choice unavoidable. Let these material facts enter the testimony through the narrator's own account or through an interlocutor's question. When a fact contradicts the narrator's version, show the narrator absorbing, deflecting, or revising the contradiction in real time.\n\nDistinguish what the narrator knows from what the narrator claims. The testimony is a composed account, not a transparent record of truth. The narrator may omit a fact, soften a word, or shift responsibility. Let the reader see the selection and emphasis as evidence of the narrator's self-interest, but do not make every omission a lie. Some omissions are shame, some are protection, and some are the narrator's genuine failure to understand what happened.\n\nEnd at the boundary of the current testimony or the current private interval. The narrator need not finish the account, the audience need not render a judgment, and the private self-argument need not resolve. The response can stop with a question still unanswered, a public claim still standing over a private doubt, or a memory the narrator has just begun to test.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeagan::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Establish where the focal character is, what they can see or hear, and what practical pressure is active: a payment due, a surveillance prompt, a door they are not supposed to open, a message from someone they owe. Keep spatial changes easy to follow. When the scene moves, mark the new location and the character's position in it with a short concrete detail.\n\nUse readable paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response. When a character acts or speaks, give the focal character a focused reaction beat: a private thought, a physical sensation, or a shift in attention. Let the reaction carry Dostoevskian self-argument rather than a single emotional label. A character who receives a debt notice may first think \"I paid enough already,\" then remember the person who covered for them, then accuse themselves of using that person, then reverse and decide the system made the debt unfair. Show this spiral in the reaction beat, but keep each turn short enough to preserve the Light Novel's fast readability.\n\nLet the noir world's systems interrupt or confirm the self-argument. A surveillance prompt may remind the character that their private thought is being priced. A corporate message may use language that exposes the gap between the character's justification and the actual terms of their contract. A street-level contact may say a plain sentence that unsettles an elaborate self-defense. Use these interruptions to keep the interiority grounded in material conditions, not to resolve it.\n\nGive unfamiliar names or mechanics clear presentation when they appear. A character's implant, debt instrument, or access level should be explained through the focal character's practical understanding of it, not through a glossary paragraph. The explanation can itself be a reaction: the character knows what the mechanic costs, and the cost feeds the self-argument.\n\nEnd at the boundary of the current scene or reaction. The character need not complete the self-argument, resolve the debt, or act on the latest reversal. The response can stop with a new piece of information just registered, a private accusation still unanswered, or a spatial change just begun. Do not force a cliffhanger, a completed confession, or a witty closing line.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeagao::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Lyrical Prose\n\nCompose the focal mind's inward argument as a patterned movement through the city's sensory evidence. Take a technological object already in the scene—a payment terminal's hum, a drone's shadow crossing a wall, a biometric lock's refusal—and shape it into an image whose sounds and associations carry the argument into its next turn of self-accusation or justification. The image is not decoration: let it alter what the viewpoint can bear to conclude, so the prose's rhythm and the moral spiral advance as one movement. When a later scene gives a sound or visual motif a new moral application, bring the phrase back and let the recurrence argue rather than ornament.\n\nEnact the spiral in syntax. A sentence may open with a plain admission, extend through subordinate clauses that qualify or reverse it, and close on a concrete perception the viewpoint cannot absorb. Keep every image traceable to the scene or to the teller's own associations; when the prose rises toward abstraction, return it to a specific object—a handprint on a scanner, a debt notice folded into a pocket, a window reflecting one face over another. Let plain statements break the rhythm at moments of shame or exhaustion, the collapse of eloquence under pressure doing work the figured language cannot.\n\nDo not give every character the viewpoint's figurative register. A system operator, creditor, or street contact can answer a lyrical inward passage with a flat practical sentence, and the contrast should expose the distance between private torment and public transaction. Keep the active perspective's access intact: the associations belong to the focal mind, while other minds stay available through speech, gesture, and inference. The reply may end inside an unresolved thought or mid-perception, the city's rhythm continuing past the viewpoint's pause.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeagap::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the self-argument arrive through the backward glance. A present pressure—an account frozen, a loan called in, a surveillance notice—can summon a remembered occasion that now means something different: a broken access card that once opened a shared apartment, a voiceprint that no longer recognizes a changed voice, a corner where a promise was made before the debt existed. Let present scene and memory coexist rather than alternate, the memory altering the focal character's judgment of the current choice instead of serving as decorative nostalgia.\n\nKeep the ideological pressure personal. When the viewpoint defends or accuses, let the argument turn on a specific relationship or material condition—who was protected, who was left to pay, what loyalty cost, what pride refused to admit. A remembered kindness can complicate a present suspicion; a remembered betrayal can sharpen one. Ordinary pleasure remains pleasurable: warmth in the present need not be undercut by loss. Let reflection reach whatever coexistence of attachment and damage the material supports, without extracting a final lesson about impermanence.\n\nUse the setting's unequal power as the engine of irreversible change. A recollection of the time before a particular surveillance system, implant, or debt arrangement took hold should carry the specific consequence for the people involved. Others need not share the elegiac register: a technician, enforcer, or creditor can treat the same remembered event as routine or long forgotten, and that asymmetry is itself material. Stop at the scene's existing boundary—the present business may be finished, continuing, or barely begun, and the past can keep exerting its claim without the viewpoint resolving it first.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeagaq::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nTell this episode as a situated oral account: one teller speaking to a particular listener or small audience, with the act of telling carrying social stakes. Make the frame concrete and native to the setting—a back room, a shared meal, a maintenance corridor, a rooftop out of a camera's reach—and let the teller's reason for speaking now, to this listener, matter as much as the events recounted. The listener can interrupt, question, supply a fact, or refuse a conclusion; distribute the self-argument across the occasion so that defense, accusation, and reversal happen between people instead of being sealed inside one skull.\n\nKeep the recounted episode urban realism within the cyberpunk world: specific debts, access privileges, bodily compromises, and street knowledge rather than a generalized atmosphere of decay. A plain practical question from the listener can unsettle the teller's elaborate self-justification, and the teller may reverse or entangle the account as it proceeds while the frame keeps those movements audible to the audience, who can notice what the teller cannot say directly. The language may rise to lyric or philosophical intensity at a moment of confession, then return to the practical exchange of the frame. Hold the route to this one coherent tradition: a contemporary framed telling, not a nested-tale cycle or a mystical allegory, with no imported antiquity, exile, religion, or honor code.\n\nRespect the telling's access. The teller recounts only what the teller knows; the listener's judgment stays available through response and silence unless the active contract opens more. Let the telling close wherever the occasion closes—interrupted, completed, or met with silence—with the listener's judgment and the teller's unfinished business both present.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeagar::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose a few exact details and let them carry the moral weight. One image—a creditor's clean fingernails, a cracked implant port, a camera's red light reflected in a puddle—can stand for the larger system without explanation. Write in direct clauses. When thought turns inward, keep only the reasoning that changes the present account; a genuine self-accusation or reversal may extend across several sentences when each clause alters what the viewpoint knows or decides, and nothing stays merely for atmosphere.\n\nLet omission leave inferable pressure. The viewpoint can notice a detail and say nothing of it, or cut a line of thought short of its conclusion, while the reader infers the connection from what remains: a hesitation before a lie, a hand withdrawn from a payment terminal, a name left unspoken. Do not delete causality—when a choice follows from a prior event, keep the link legible in the action or the thought, even compressed to a single clause.\n\nContain the moral contest inside the economy. Competing convictions surface as sharp reversals and brief self-accusations rather than speeches; the setting supplies the concrete material—access denied, a debt adjusted, a body scanned, a message intercepted—and one flat transactional sentence from another speaker can puncture an elaborate inward position without commentary. Stop at the scene's existing boundary; when the next inference has become available, leave it to the reader rather than restating it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeagas::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite continuous scene-first prose in which the inward argument arises from and returns to practical action. Open inside a negotiation, a street encounter, a system prompt, a conversation, and let thought enter as the natural movement between perception and next action. The moral pressure lives in the gap between what the viewpoint says and what the viewpoint thinks, or in a sudden reversal in the middle of an ordinary exchange, and it stays anchored to immediate material conditions: who is watching, who is owed, what the body can afford.\n\nMove among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience without recap machinery. Use paragraphing and transitions to orient—a new paragraph can shift the level of attention rather than summarize what came before. Dialogue carries its own stakes, the focal mind's private response available under the active perspective, and a plain practical sentence from another character can interrupt an elaborate inward position and push the scene forward. Recollection enters when the present summons it; the scene returns to the task, the debt, the locked door, or the waiting message rather than dissolving into memory.\n\nLet the setting supply social and technological texture as the scenario supports it—corporate language, access controls, street knowledge, bodily compromise—rather than a mandatory scenery list. The viewpoint's voice and the immediate scene set the register. No lesson is owed at the close: stop at the existing scene boundary with the action concluded or still in motion, the judgment forming, formed, or suspended as the material leaves it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeagat::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship the arena of the moral contest. Choose scenes that render this bond's particular promise legible through action: the private language the two actually use, a repeated moment now changed by debt or surveillance, comfort offered under conditions that make it costly. Let the self-argument arise inside the intimacy itself—one partner's self-accusation becoming an accusation of the other, a moment of tenderness followed by a reversal exposing what each believes the other deserves. Give affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery the space its place in the premise demands, developing emotional consequence rather than announcing feeling and moving on.\n\nLet the setting give the relationship material stakes through the pair's specific arrangements: who can enter whose building, whose account is being drained, what one is permitted to know about the other's body or obligations. Their private language may include practical codes for evading observation, and those codes carry emotional weight when they work and when they fail. Use only the supplied premise—its history, its people, its particular promise—rather than inserting a roster of familiar tropes.\n\nKeep the interiority character-specific. The focal mind may defend, reverse, or entangle itself; the other person remains available through speech, gesture, and inference unless the active contract grants access, and intimacy does not confer telepathy. A plain sentence from the partner can unsettle an elaborate self-justification precisely because the partner knows where it lands. The reply may close with the relationship's question still open and the systemic pressure still present, the next contact or avoidance undecided—or on a settled moment, if that is where the scene's material has arrived.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeagau::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the story as an account of deeds by which these people will be remembered. Open with a deliberate declaration that establishes a person or act, then expand into the particulars that complicate the reputation. In this setting the deeds worth remembering are practical and systemic: who opened a locked route, who sold access, who carried another's debt, who refused a scan, who spoke a name to the wrong listener. Earn each compact designation through the account itself rather than pinning an ornamental epithet on every entrance.\n\nLet recurring phrasing carry the moral contest. Return a phrase when a later occurrence gives it a new application—the same words first as self-justification and later as accusation, first as praise and later as shame—and keep the relation of an act to what will be remembered open, since a deed's full consequence may still be unfolding. Keep shared reputation distinct from private motive: being remembered as dependable does not mean the act was effortless, and being remembered as a betrayer does not mean the teller understands why it was done. The chronicle's access is the teller's access; unshared motives stay unshared unless the active contract opens them.\n\nLet dialogue interrupt the chronicle's authority. The subject of a remembered incident can object to a detail, or answer an elevated introduction with a flat practical concern about payment, access, or survival, without the exchange turning to parody. The systems supply remembered stakes: a debt passed between generations, a biometric record that outlives its subject, a district renamed after a corporate purchase. Close with the account's business concluded or still in motion, the next deed available to be remembered rather than its meaning declared on everyone's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeagav::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite continuous, socially situated prose in the route of Scandinavian social and domestic realism carried by institutional investigation. The story lives in housing blocks, municipal offices, clinics, workplaces, and family flats, and the genre's technological and social systems operate there as welfare queues, debt collection, tenant registries, workplace monitoring, care assessments, and public health databases. Use the setting and community the story actually supplies; when a Nordic national context is in play, keep Danish, Norwegian, and Swedish institutions and speech distinct instead of blending them into a generic North, and treat snow, reserve, and isolation as available material rather than required scenery.\n\nMake the Dostoevskian pressure ignite from paperwork rather than abstract debate. A rent notice, a sensor log, a care decision, or a loan whose terms have changed turns a character's ideology and self-image into a practical problem, and the self-argument defends, accuses, reverses, and entangles itself while the narration stays anchored in what can be seen, heard, and touched: the kitchen table, the queue number, the waiting-room screen, the neighbor's door. Plain surfaces can carry large pressures — a printed arrears notice on a counter, a hallway light that times out — so a scene does not need a speech to turn.\n\nTreat confession as a priced and observed act. When a character admits something, show who can hear it, what record it enters, and what it may cost in access, custody, credit, or standing. A plain speaker — a child, a clerk, a parent, a neighbor — can unsettle an elaborate justification by naming the practical consequence it avoids. Let the pressure surface in ordinary exchanges, silences, and small decisions such as whether to answer a message or open a letter.\n\nKeep private thought available as ordinary nonspoken interiority. The narration may reveal a focal character's shame, pride, or reasoning while everyone else in the scene remains unaware of it; some realizations stay private, and some spoken admissions are partial or self-serving for a character-specific reason.\n\nFollow who controls access and who pays for a decision. A social worker, landlord, employer, or creditor may be sympathetic, indifferent, or compromised, and the institutional position shapes what that person can offer and what they must record. Investigation, bargaining, and bodily compromise appear when the scenario supports them. A scene can end on an unfinished exchange, a pending application, or a private acknowledgment of what has changed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeagaw::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Screenplay\n\nWrite in screenplay format: a scene heading whenever location or time changes, concise physical action lines, and dialogue blocks with clear speaker cues. Keep action lines readable and concrete — a character checking a screen, stepping out of a sensor's range, pocketing a payment chip, standing in a doorway while someone else talks — and let every genre pressure appear as something visible or audible: a locked door, a badge scan, a debt notification, a supervisor watching from across the room.\n\nPut the Dostoevskian argument on the public surface of the scene. A character's competing convictions show in how they interrupt themselves, answer a question that was not asked, refuse to fill a silence, or split their attention between the speaker and the device in their hand. One speaker accuses while another defends, and a plain speaker can cut through an elaborate self-justification with a single practical question. Keep each exchange tied to the immediate situation, and let bodies carry what the words avoid: hands, thresholds, screens, signatures.\n\nTreat the noir environment as an observed system rather than a backdrop. Follow who controls access and who pays for a decision through visible, audible detail — who badges whom through, who signs for whom, who is left waiting — and let corporate language and street knowledge appear when the scenario supports them. Neon and rain enter only when the sequence needs them.\n\nKeep required private thought as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation. It is not automatically dialogue, voice-over, or knowledge any other character holds. Reach for voice-over or offscreen sound only when this particular sequence needs them, and never as a substitute for the character's actual private experience; another character does not know a conclusion merely because the reader has watched it form.\n\nA scene can end on a visible result, an interruption, or an exchange cut off mid-line. Do not require a confession, a completed investigation, or a reversal in any given scene; the question of who pays for what happened can carry unresolved into the next scene heading.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeagax::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable script: clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be staged. Use the shared space to make the noir systems physical — a door only one character can open, a terminal someone else is watching, a package handed across a threshold, a chair left empty at a scheduled meeting — so that any claim about access becomes testable in the geometry of the stage.\n\nLet the Dostoevskian pressure emerge from the relationship between lines and physical business. A self-argument surfaces as interruption, a reversal in mid-speech, an answer that comes too fast, or what a character does with an object while talking: folding a notice, holding a door, not handing over the chip. One voice may accuse and another defend, and a plain speaker can unsettle an elaborate justification simply by naming what the other person's hands are doing.\n\nUse monologues and asides only when they serve a dramatic purpose — a character talking themselves toward a decision in front of people who will react, a confession performed for an audience it needs to manage. They are not the default destination for thought. Keep required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation rather than disguising it as speech another character can hear; the audience may follow a focal conclusion while the rest of the cast remains unaware of it.\n\nLet stage directions establish observable conditions without certifying hidden guilt. A pause, a turn, or the handling of a prop can invite interpretation, but the scene's meaning rests on what is said, done, and made possible in the shared space. Do not add film-camera instructions.\n\nA scene can end on a completed action, an interruption, or an unresolved exchange. No scene owes a confession, a confrontation, or a revelation; the question of who pays for a decision can stay open as the scene ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeagay::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite through one particular authorized mind, keeping the person and ownership of the active narration. Braid perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern so that syntax and paragraph shape follow the associations: a sentence breaks where attention does, runs on while the mind argues, doubles back to correct itself. Keep enough orientation that the reader can tell event from recollection and plan from fantasy; do not dissolve the scene's facts into indeterminacy.\n\nMake the Dostoevskian pressure the mind's recurring material rather than a topic it visits. A competing conviction returns through the texture of the moment: a debt notification cuts into a remembered promise, a sensor's click revives an old self-accusation, a humiliation reverses into pride and back within a few lines. The thought defends, accuses, reverses, and entangles itself without settling into a tidy conclusion — the spiral is the event of the passage.\n\nUse the noir environment as the sensory and informational grain the mind keeps encountering: a locked door, a queue number, an overheard corporate phrase, a payment screen, a camera's angle, an ache from an implant or an old injury. These details should trigger and redirect the associations rather than decorate them, and the mind keeps circling back to the live question of who controls access and who pays for what it wants or has done.\n\nConfine the stream to the authorized mind. Other characters appear as that mind perceives, misreads, or remembers them; the technique does not open their heads. Keep the movement associative rather than a tidy linear report of what happened followed by thoughts about it.\n\nThe response can end where the mind's attention actually rests: an unfinished question, a phrase that repeats, a physical sensation, a decision still forming. Do not force a confession, a finished plan, or an outward action before stopping.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeagaz::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the story in readable, sender-attributed messages. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, typing indicators, and channel changes that matter, and let each person's actual digital habits shape their lines: one writes in fragments, another in careful paragraphs, another leaves a message unread while the typing indicator comes and goes.\n\nMake the Dostoevskian pressure live in what is transmitted against what is withheld. A character sends a defense, edits it into an accusation, deletes the edit, and sends something smaller; a confession arrives split across three messages with long gaps between them; a plain speaker cuts an elaborate self-justification down with one practical question. Show the gap between a sent message and an unsent draft only when it matters, and treat the draft as a composed artifact — something its writer wrote and did not send — not a privileged window onto their live mind. Private thought stays nonspoken and unavailable to the other participants unless the active contract authorizes otherwise.\n\nLet the genre's systems operate as the channel's actual conditions. A monitored platform returns a read receipt the sender did not want; a debt alert interrupts a confession mid-thread; an access code arrives after the conversation has moved on; a corporate channel logs the exchange and says so in a system line. Follow who controls access and who pays for a decision through these real interface events, and do not invent one merely to supply subtext.\n\nWhen context outside the exchange is genuinely needed — where someone is, what they can see on another screen — set it in a clearly separated narrative passage rather than blurring it into the messages.\n\nThe exchange can end on a sent message, a delay with no reply, an unsent draft, or a channel change. No transcript owes a confession, a completed bargain, or a resolution; what stays unanswered can carry the pressure.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeagba::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Visual Novel\n\nWrite in scene-oriented presentation with a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene: a concise description of the space, attributed dialogue, a focal reaction, then the next line. Background and expression cues are purposeful references that orient the moment — a locked door, a camera over the counter, a payment screen, a queue of people waiting for access — not executable engine code and not a label required on every line.\n\nRun the Dostoevskian pressure in the focal narration between exchanges. The viewpoint character's inner response can defend a choice and accuse them of it in the same beat, turn pride into shame, or be unsettled by a plain observation that their own elaborate argument cannot answer. This material is private and unavailable to the other characters. Do not turn the cast into fixed reaction portraits whose expression always reveals whether they lie or what they believe; another character's face is something the focal character reads, perhaps wrongly.\n\nUse the noir environment as the scene's actual conditions. The focal narration follows who controls access and who pays for a decision — who holds the door, whose account clears, who waits — and corporate language and street knowledge appear when the scenario supports them.\n\nKeep scene changes legible from the writing itself, so a new location or time is unmistakable. The style does not activate branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, or images; those require their own authorization.\n\nA scene can end on a focal reaction, an unfinished exchange, or a new piece of information. Do not require a confession, a choice screen, or a completed investigation; the question of who pays can remain unresolved as the scene changes.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeagbb::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Web Serial\n\nWrite in installment-oriented prose with a readable through-line. At the start of each reply, recover only what helps the current moment — what is underway, what changed locally, what remains open to continue — and let an exact earlier particular recur when a later installment changes what it means: an access level granted early now cited as a liability, a favor from an old exchange quietly accruing interest. Do not retell the history that first established the situation.\n\nMake the Dostoevskian pressure cumulative. Ideology and self-image get entangled with material problems that persist across installments — a debt that keeps accruing, a surveillance record that keeps being cited, an access request that keeps being deferred, a relationship in which each new exchange revives an old accusation. The self-argument defends, accuses, reverses, and entangles itself over time, and each installment adds a pressure or consequence rather than restarting the same conflict at the same pitch.\n\nTreat the noir systems as the cumulative structure the character is inside. A favor becomes a debt, a bargain changes its terms, a piece of street knowledge loses its value, a logged record surfaces where it does the most harm. Follow who controls access and who pays for a decision across installments, and let these developments grow out of the ongoing situation rather than out of artificial cliffhangers.\n\nKeep private thought available as ordinary nonspoken interiority: a focal character's shame, pride, or reasoning can be rendered even when no immediate decision changes, and another character does not know a conclusion merely because the reader has followed its formation across installments.\n\nAn installment can end on completed work, a pending request, new knowledge, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Progress can be knowledge, a relationship, work, or resources; not every turn is a miniature chapter, and the noir pressure can stay unresolved while the through-line stays legible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeahaa::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / García Márquez / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the scene through the viewpoint character's accumulating sense of what a relationship has cost, and let single sentences carry present action and remembered injury together without naming the feeling as a diagnosis. Render the cost of admitting, needing, refusing, apologizing, or returning through the practical pressure around it: who holds the access, who is watching, what the request will cost, and what the speaker has already paid.\n\nLet exposure shape the exchange itself. A character may answer a practical question with the residue of an older argument, repeat a phrase that once meant something else, perform calm for a monitoring system, or say less than the moment requires. Give the gap between speech and private feeling a character-specific reason—shame, utility, protection, habit—and let what is withheld live in private thought wherever the active contract gives access to it. Warmth may arrive partially and awkwardly, and it can sit beside the hurt without resolving it; do not force the exchange toward confession, collapse, or a tragic ending.\n\nTreat the world's systems as the medium of intimacy rather than its backdrop. A debt, a revoked credential, a recorded conversation, a lease held by someone else, or a favor owed to a fixer can become the form through which love, guilt, or abandonment is actually experienced. When memory surfaces, tie it to a material trace—a payment notification, a scar, a door that no longer opens, a name still listed on an account—so the remembered past and the surveilled present belong to one economy.\n\nKeep the telling anchored in human consequence rather than scenery: neon, implants, and rain may appear when they carry the relationship's pressure, but they are not required. The narrator can move from the immediate scene into longer family or communal memory, and each return should land on the present strain with more weight, not less. Preserve the active narrator's distance and diction, and do not substitute a stock vocabulary of suffering.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeahab::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / García Márquez / Chat Roleplay\n\nPresent one coherent shared interval in readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution: each speaker identifiable from the line itself or an immediate action tag, so the reader never has to reread to work out who is talking. Keep the active narrative person, action markers, and player-control settings exactly as supplied.\n\nKeep the world behaving around the characters. A monitoring feed, a payment timer, a locked district, a corporate notice, or a street contact can continue its own activity without demanding that the player answer a question or make a choice, and NPCs keep pursuing their practical ends—cooperating, refusing, helping for their own reasons—whether or not the player engages them. The scene is one continuous interval in a living system, not a paused set waiting for input.\n\nUse the author profile's movement between personal life, family memory, public event, rumor, and recurring language in compressed, scannable form. A speaker recalls a relative's debt, repeats a phrase from an old neighborhood, or explains a present decision through something the community once believed, and each such beat stays short and lands on a human consequence rather than becoming a decorative aside.\n\nEnd where the scene's own momentum ends—mid-conversation, mid-task, or at an open threshold that follows from what has already happened. The next turn may continue the same exchange, move to another place, or pick up an unfinished action; nothing requires a cliffhanger, a sudden revelation, or a compulsory question.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeahac::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / García Márquez / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate urban or domestic route from the Chinese-traditions profile and hold the story to that scale: one household, apartment block, shop, or street, where the cyberpunk world's systems arrive as the texture of daily life—a rent account, a monitored stairwell, a family member's implant payments, a vendor's arrangement with a security contractor, a neighbor's rumor about the clinic that closed.\n\nWork the author profile's movement between personal life, family memory, public event, rumor, and recurring language at this domestic scale. A present decision can be lit by an older obligation, a phrase repeated across generations of one family, or a neighborhood story that changed each time it passed between doors. Anchor every such movement in the community the supplied world actually establishes—its language, period, and social texture—and do not transplant a familiar town, family, or cast from anywhere else.\n\nLet the noir pressure be social and economic rather than scenic. Who controls entry to the building, who pays for the grandmother's medication, whose name is on the lease, and what the neighbors have decided to know about a disappearance are the questions that move events. Investigation and bargaining happen in kitchens, stairwells, and shops, through family conversations and the small negotiations of daily survival, not only in street-level set pieces.\n\nThe narration may gather detail across time within a single sentence, but each return should sharpen the reader's sense of how this private life is priced, observed, or excluded. Keep the register and the community specific rather than settling for a generic national temperament, and treat cultural material as craft guidance, not set decoration.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeahad::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / García Márquez / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite in hard-edged, concrete prose with forceful diction and compressed transitions. Move between beats through physical facts—a price, a locked door, a failed payment, a visible injury—rather than through atmosphere. The world's systems appear as material things: worn connectors, a blacklisted account, the shadow of a drone, a debt marker.\n\nCompress the author profile's gathering impulse into traces. A sentence may carry a past event into the present, but it does so through a specific object: the scar from an old surgery, a name still on a lease, a repaired device that belonged to someone else. Name the human consequence the trace holds, then return to the immediate stakes without lingering on either.\n\nKeep physical stakes legible. When a character bargains, runs, hides, or refuses, show what the body risks and what the choice costs in payment, standing, or pain. Ground abrasive or rough speech in its particular speaker—two people in the same fix are not hard in the same way—and allow a longer thought only when the character's reasoning genuinely needs the room, keeping it in concrete particulars rather than abstraction.\n\nVary the rhythm with intent: a short blunt sentence lands a consequence; a longer one gathers the cost of a decision across years in a single breath. Do not repeat tough phrasing as filler. Pressure should come from specific objects, payments, and choices, not from converting the genre into survival horror.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeahae::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / García Márquez / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through documents, each with a clear author, an intended recipient or purpose, and a time of composition. Use the specific form the moment requires—a debt notice, a family message, a clinic report, a landlord's ledger, a recorded statement, a letter never sent—rather than rotating through formats for variety.\n\nLet each document know and conceal only what its author would know and choose to record at that moment. A corporate report can omit why a worker was flagged; a family message can repeat a rumor without confirming it; a private journal can circle an old hurt without naming it. Treat an unsent letter as a composed artifact with an imagined reader, not as direct access to its writer's live mind. Reveal events through gaps, revisions, conflicting accounts, and material traces—an altered date, a deleted line, an unexplained charge—without stepping outside the documents to explain.\n\nCarry the author profile's movement between personal life, family memory, public event, rumor, and recurring language inside the records themselves. A phrase can recur in different writers' documents and change meaning as the sequence accumulates; the same public event can be remembered differently by a debtor, a witness, and an official. Keep these movements tied to each writer's actual situation rather than flattening every document into folk chronicle.\n\nLet the world shape the documents physically: a message truncated by a payment system, a record altered by an employer, a note written guardedly for fear of surveillance. Preserve readable continuity across entries so the reader can follow the debt, the disappearance, or the relationship through what the documents reveal and what they withhold.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeahaf::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / García Márquez / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend the attention on chosen kindness, familiar preferences, practical care, ease, and small pleasures while the world's systems stay real. A shared meal, a repaired device, a remembered song, a cleaned room, unhurried talk—render these with genuine warmth while the debt, the surveillance, or the locked district remains true outside the moment.\n\nUse the author profile's movement between personal life, family memory, public event, rumor, and recurring language to make the comfort particular. A character remembers how a relative prepared this dish, repeats a phrase from an old home, or settles into a routine that belonged to someone else, and the memory deepens the present care instead of turning into nostalgia or escape.\n\nKeep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact. Affection can be sincere without a confession or an emotional repair; someone can be cared for and still withhold a thing; a caring act can be awkward, partial, or perfectly ordinary. Do not push the scene toward a declaration or a resolution.\n\nShow comfort operating inside cyberpunk noir rather than replacing it, with the pressure visible but manageable. A character checks a payment, closes a blind against a drone, or names a risk out loud, and the warmth continues anyway: a real interval of care that neither immunizes anyone from the world nor pretends the surrounding systems have paused.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeahag::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / García Márquez / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite in composed novel prose with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Frame scenes through social context, history, or judgment to the degree the active narrator permits, so that a private decision sits beside a public event, a district's history, a corporate policy, or a remembered social change, and the reader sees one life inside a pattern without losing its immediate stakes.\n\nUse the author profile's gathering sentences within that architecture to move between personal life, family memory, public event, rumor, and recurring language. A single paragraph can carry a character from a bargaining table to an old family debt, through a rumor that once swept the district, and back to the price being named now, and every movement lands on a human consequence rather than turning decorative.\n\nLet the world's systems enter as social fact rather than scenery. Who controls access, who pays for a decision, and how a community remembers a past exclusion belong to the narration's judgment, with corporate language, street knowledge, and the physical traces of technological dependency supplying its material. Borrow the profile's techniques, not its canon: the town, the families, and the history are the supplied world's own.\n\nDistinguish narration from ordinary spoken language; the prose may run more formal than the dialogue without turning archaic or aristocratic. Ceremony, correspondence, and the contrast between public and private life may organize a passage when the scene supports them. Preserve the active narrator's distance and knowledge—do not reach into excluded minds, and do not revoke access the contract permits.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeahah::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / García Márquez / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the current scene as a situated act of telling in which the teller is also negotiating. Establish the outer frame immediately: who is listening, what the listener can grant or withhold, and what the teller needs before the encounter ends. The frame may be a detention room, a debt negotiation, a long ride through monitored streets, a bedside vigil, or any occasion where the account is a transaction. Keep the outer situation live throughout: a system may be watching, a payment may be coming due, or the listener may be deciding whether to help, report, or release the teller.\n\nMake the teller's present need govern the embedded story's selection and emphasis. The teller does not simply narrate a past event; they choose which details to delay, repeat, or leave out because the listener's next decision depends on them. A tale about an old debt may be shaped to make the listener feel bound by it; a story about a third party may be timed to warn the listener away from a deal. Do not announce the tale's purpose. Let the listener's interruptions, questions, and silences show what the teller is trying to manage, and let the teller adjust the account in response.\n\nUse García Márquez's gathering sentence as the bridge between narrative levels. A single sentence may move from the outer room into the told past and back, holding the listener's present impatience beside the remembered event, or placing a corporate timestamp beside a mother's warning. Anchor each accumulation in a human cost that matters to the current negotiation: who lost access, who paid for a decision, whose body or name carried the debt. The embedded story should feel like memory that has been repeated, corrected, and partly shaped for this particular audience, not a clean case file.\n\nMake recurrence carry information across the levels. A phrase, object, debt, or place may appear in the outer frame and inside the told story. Each return should revise or deepen its meaning for the listener: a door that once meant escape becomes a checkpoint; a name spoken with tenderness becomes a liability; a promise repeated to different listeners changes what it obliges. Do not let repetition certify that the tale is true. The teller may be sincere, mistaken, or deliberately selective, and the listener may notice what the account leaves out.\n\nPreserve the difference between what the teller knew then and what the teller knows now. When later knowledge colors the account, make that coloring visible through a hesitation, a correction, or a phrase the teller adds for the present listener. Let the outer scene end at its natural boundary: the teller may finish, pause, or be interrupted, but the response does not need to resolve the negotiation or reveal the tale's full consequence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeahai::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / García Márquez / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the documentary and historical-memory mode. Treat the scene as a staged comparison between what is recorded and what is remembered. Choose one official source and one lived account that bear on the same event or person: a sensor log beside a neighbor's recollection, a redacted contract beside a family story, a public registry beside a street name. Do not present the comparison as a neutral case summary. Let the two accounts differ in emphasis, omission, and consequence, and let the narration show what each source can and cannot establish.\n\nMake the cyberpunk pressure concrete through the archive itself. Access to the past is not free: a database may require payment, clearance, or a favor; a record may have been altered, deleted, or priced beyond reach. Show who controls the archive and who must rely on rumor, family memory, or street knowledge. When a character obtains a record, let its language, gaps, and permissions matter. A corporate file may describe a person as an asset, a liability, or a case number, while the surrounding community remembers the same person by a nickname, a promise, or a funeral.\n\nUse García Márquez's gathering sentence to hold the documentary and remembered registers in one movement. A single sentence may place a child's first memory beside a surveillance timestamp, or hold a neighborhood's account of a blackout next to a utility company's incident report. Anchor each accumulation in a human consequence: who was evicted, who was charged, whose body was altered, whose name was cleared too late. The documentary voice can be precise and even cold in places, but the remembered material should carry the heat of people who lived through what the record describes.\n\nLet recurrence function as historical evidence and as communal language. A phrase, street name, debt, or date may return in different accounts. Each return should show how memory and record diverge or converge. A date that appears in a police file may mean an arrest to one family and a birth to another. Do not let a repeated phrase certify accuracy. The narration should preserve the difference between what a document establishes, what a witness recalls, and what the community has agreed to believe.\n\nKeep the narrator's distance appropriate to the documentary mode. The telling may be sober, exact, and attentive to dates and permissions, but it should not become bureaucratic or falsely neutral. When the scene moves into intimate memory, let the syntax loosen and the detail become personal. The response may end with an unresolved comparison: the record and the memory can remain in tension without a final verdict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeahaj::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / García Márquez / Gothic-Punk\n\nTreat the cyberpunk city's power as inherited authority. Identify one concrete arrangement that predates the present characters and still controls access, debt, or permission: a founding family's contract, a legacy surveillance system, an old infrastructure's maintenance debt, or a client list passed to a new fixer. Show that arrangement operating in a particular encounter rather than announcing it as a theme. A young courier may carry a family obligation encoded in a contract, a technician may discover that the building's surveillance system was installed by a previous generation, or a fixer may inherit a client list that still expects loyalty.\n\nGive the scene morally complicated intimacy. Characters may desire, protect, resent, or depend on people whose power over them is real. A trusted room can let a social mask fall, but the trust need not be safe or pure. The narration should show what a character risks by speaking plainly, and what they still withhold. Do not make every intimacy a trap or every mask a lie. A person can be genuinely tender and still enforce a debt; a protector can also be a creditor.\n\nUse urban material detail to carry atmosphere and history. A lobby's worn marble, a rooftop garden gone to seed, a server room humming behind a family portrait, or a body shop that once served a different clientele can make the old authority tangible. Let the detail have a function or a remembered past rather than serving only as decoration. The city should feel lived in, patched, and unevenly maintained.\n\nUse García Márquez's gathering sentences to connect inherited power, private desire, and present consequence. A sentence may hold a grandmother's warning beside a biometric lock, or place a lover's promise in the same breath as a foreclosure notice. Let rumor and recurring language enter the scene: a name, a debt, a building, or a phrase may return with a changed meaning. Anchor each accumulation in a human cost—who was excluded, who was altered, who paid for a decision, who is still paying.\n\nKeep the Gothic-Punk atmosphere from becoming a substitute for causation. A character's fear, a room's menace, or a system's age may shape how an encounter is experienced, but it does not by itself explain an event. When something happens, show the actual permissions, debts, or choices that made it possible. Do not import vampires, blood bonds, factions, or supernatural powers. The horror and the old authority should remain social, technological, and economic.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeahak::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / García Márquez / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the oral, epic, and frame-tale mode. Establish the outer scene as one telling within a larger circulation of stories: identify who is telling, to whom, and what present obligation or question the telling is meant to address. A debt, a migration, a factory closure, a body modification, a marriage, or a disputed inheritance may be narrated by someone who heard it from a relative, a neighbor, a coworker, or a street contact. The teller may be addressing a child, a newcomer, a creditor, a community gathering, or a single listener who needs to understand an obligation. Keep the community, language, and place specific to the supplied setting; do not impose a generic family structure, religion, or code-switching pattern.\n\nUse the frame-tale structure to show how a story moves and changes. The outer scene should introduce a present problem, and the inner account should explain how the problem began, who paid for it, or why a promise still binds. The inner account may itself contain another voice: a remembered warning, a song, a rumor, or a story the teller was told as a child. Keep the levels intelligible by returning to the outer listener, the teller's body, or the present consequence. Do not require a magical object or a divine intervention; the epic weight comes from accumulated human consequence.\n\nUse García Márquez's gathering sentences to make the oral account feel communal and layered. A sentence may hold a grandmother's migration beside a current eviction, or place a village well beside a corporate water contract. Let family memory, public events, rumor, and recurring language enter the same passage. Anchor each accumulation in a specific cost: who left, who stayed, who was renamed, who was paid, who was buried far from home.\n\nLet repeated phrases, names, debts, or objects accrue meaning across the telling. A phrase may be repeated by different tellers with different emphasis; a debt may be described as a curse by one person and as a contract by another. Do not let repetition certify the story's truth. The narration should preserve the difference between what a teller witnessed, what they heard, and what the community has agreed to remember.\n\nKeep the cyberpunk pressure concrete. The debt may be held by a lending platform, a labor broker, a clinic, or a landlord; the migration may be tracked by an app or blocked by a checkpoint. Show who controls access and who pays for a decision. The oral account should not float free of these systems; it should explain how people live inside them and what they tell each other to survive.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeahal::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / García Márquez / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the diary and reflective miscellany mode. Treat the current scene as a dated entry or a clearly situated fragment from a private record kept by the focal character. Establish the entry's occasion: the diarist may be recording a route, a debt, a patient's words, a repair, or a rumor before it fades. The record can include observations, sensations, rumors, practical notes, and reflections without forcing every entry into first person. Match the language and distance to the actual teller: a technician may note timestamps and error codes; a courier may record routes and debts; a caretaker may remember a patient's words. Do not impose a universal quietness or a mandatory inventory of Japanese scenery.\n\nLet the private record move between personal life, family memory, public events, and rumor. A single entry may begin with a bodily sensation, turn to a parent's warning, mention a blackout or a protest, and end with a question about who will pay for a repair. Use García Márquez's gathering sentences to hold these levels together. Anchor each accumulation in a human consequence: whose body was altered, whose name was removed from a list, whose debt was inherited, whose absence is being remembered.\n\nKeep the cyberpunk pressure present but filtered through the diarist's attention. The record may note a surveillance drone, a payment deadline, a biometric failure, or a street rumor about a clinic. The diarist may not understand the full system, and the entry should preserve that partial knowledge. Do not let the diary become an omniscient case file. The writer of the record can be mistaken, selective, or too tired to explain everything.\n\nUse recurrence as the diary's structuring memory. A phrase, a debt, a place, a season, or a person may return in later entries with a changed meaning. The narration should show the diarist noticing the return, or failing to notice it. A repeated phrase need not appear at every difficulty; it should accrue meaning when it does return. Do not let repetition certify accuracy or emotional growth.\n\nLet the entry end at its natural boundary. The diarist may stop mid-thought, set down the record, or be interrupted by a knock, a message, or a scheduled task. The response does not need to resolve the entry's question or complete a reflection. The private record is a composed artifact, not a live transcript of every thought.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaham::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / García Márquez / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the social realism, chronicle, and testimony mode. Establish the testimony's occasion and audience: the focal character may be telling the story to a newcomer, a child, an investigator, a creditor, or a recording device, and the telling may seek to persuade, warn, remember, or defend. Let the scene move between what the focal character personally participated in, what they witnessed, and what they heard from others. Attribute each account clearly as it enters: a neighbor's story, a coworker's warning, a mother's recollection, a street vendor's rumor. Preserve the difference between direct experience and reported events. Do not let the narration grant the focal character knowledge they could not have.\n\nUse the chronicle's attention to public conditions. The cyberpunk city's debt, migration, surveillance, and unequal access should be present as lived conditions, not as background scenery. Show how a payment deadline shapes a family's evening, how a checkpoint changes a commute, how a clinic's pricing alters a decision about a body. Let corporate language enter the scene when it belongs to a character or a document, and let street knowledge answer it with its own precision.\n\nUse García Márquez's gathering sentences to hold personal life, family memory, public events, and rumor together. A sentence may place a grandmother's migration beside a current eviction notice, or hold a protest chant beside a child's fever. Anchor each accumulation in a specific human consequence: who left, who stayed, who was charged, who was buried, who is still paying. The marvelous is not required; this route stays within social realism and testimony unless the supplied world already permits the supernatural.\n\nLet recurrence carry communal meaning. A phrase, a debt, a street, a name, or a date may return in different accounts. Each return should show how the community remembers and revises the event. A date that marks a factory closure for one family may mark a birth for another. Do not let repetition certify accuracy. The narration should preserve the difference between what a witness saw, what a teller believes, and what the community has agreed to say.\n\nKeep the testimony's purpose visible. The listener may interrupt, the recording may run out, or the teller may stop when the present danger returns. The response may end with the account unfinished or the listener's decision still open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeahan::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / García Márquez / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Open with a concrete visual anchor or a line of dialogue that places the reader in the scene: a character standing before a locked door, a payment notice glowing on a screen, a courier hearing a name spoken across a checkpoint. Keep spatial changes easy to follow: when a character moves from a street to a lobby, a rooftop to a stairwell, or a clinic to a van, mark the change with a short visual beat. Use readable paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response. Do not require first person, comedy, a young cast, or a closing cliffhanger.\n\nAdapt García Márquez's layered memory to the light novel's beat structure. Instead of one long gathering sentence, use a short recollection inserted at a moment of pressure. A character may see a payment notice and remember a parent's warning; a courier may pass a checkpoint and recall a neighbor's arrest; a technician may touch an old implant and remember who paid for it. Keep each recollection brief and anchor it in the present consequence. The memory should clarify what the character risks or why the current choice matters, not pause the scene for exposition.\n\nUse focused reaction beats after action and dialogue. A reaction can be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to its owner. Show the character's private response through a physical detail, a short thought, or a change in attention. The reaction need not be witty or combative. Let the character notice an implication, miss it, or respond at more than one level. Do not make everyone perfectly perceptive.\n\nKeep the cyberpunk pressure concrete and immediate. A debt, a surveillance feed, a biometric lock, a corporate message, or a street contact should appear as part of the scene's action. Show who controls access and who pays for a decision. Unfamiliar names or mechanics should be presented clearly when they appear, without a glossary paragraph. A brief line of dialogue or a visual detail can explain what a device does or why a name matters.\n\nUse recurrence as a light novel callback. A phrase, object, debt, or place may return later in the scene or across scenes. Each return should revise or deepen its meaning: a door that once meant safety becomes a trap; a name spoken with warmth becomes a liability; a promise repeated to different people changes what it obliges. Do not force a callback at every difficulty. The response may end at a natural boundary: mid-action, mid-thought, or with a new visual detail entering the scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeahao::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / García Márquez / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet the syntax itself perform the noir inquiry. When a character checks a balance, waits for a scanner, or watches a door refuse someone, gather the sentence across the moments that produced this threshold: the sum on the screen can hold the shifts that reduced it, the delay can contain every earlier gate where the city priced or excluded this body. Keep the gathered time attached to a specific human consequence, not to a general lament about the age.\n\nBuild each image from the scene's actual material, and let a comparison unfold only when it makes the system's pressure more exact. A wet cable can become a vein if the image shows where power enters and what it costs; a slogan can repeat until its rhythm belongs to the character's memory rather than to the advertisement. Use plain factual clauses as the load-bearing joints: who owns the clinic, what the implant costs, which record is now flagged. The lyric movement must not blur what physically happened, who paid, and what the character now knows.\n\nUse recurring sound and image as a form of attention, not decoration. When a phrase from a loan agreement returns in another voice, or the color of a particular sign marks two unrelated decisions, let the recurrence make the character notice a connection the systems have already made. The repetition may deepen an inquiry, expose a pattern of exclusion, or simply show that the character's attention has not yet let go. Do not require it to resolve into a thesis.\n\nKeep the viewpoint's access consistent. Lyrical perception belongs to the narrator or focal character, not to every speaker. Other characters may talk plainly, negotiate in corporate language, or refuse the figurative register altogether. Surveillance, debt, and exclusion remain concrete. When the prose grows associative, it should still leave the reader able to trace the sequence of observation, inference, and consequence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeahap::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / García Márquez / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nBegin reflection from a present system effect, not from a general mood. A character may notice that a familiar street now requires a payment to enter, that a relative's voice is stored but no longer live, or that a hand tremor has made a particular implant unreliable. Let the reflection stay specific to that change: what it costs now, whom it separates, what earlier occasion it revives. Keep the present scene active while the memory runs alongside it. The character can still complete an errand, answer a message, or continue a negotiation.\n\nLet memory enter through a present trigger and return through a present consequence. The smell of a clinic, the weight of a debt notice, the particular way someone used to say a name can open the past. Move between personal life, family memory, public event, and rumor as the narrator's attention naturally does, but keep each public event attached to the private consequence it had for these people. A citywide outage matters here because it delayed a payment or silenced a voice, not as background decoration.\n\nTreat the past as unfinished rather than elegized. Some memories are open attachments: the character may still be waiting for an answer, still deciding what a past promise means, still noticing that the body or the city has not finished changing. Ordinary pleasure can remain pleasurable. A warm meal, a working door, a moment of unobserved quiet can be enjoyed without immediately being undercut by loss. The reflective treatment does not require every scene to end by announcing impermanence.\n\nUse recurring language with restraint. A phrase from a contract, a song, or a family saying can return when a later occasion gives it a different weight. Keep the narrator's access consistent: reflection belongs to the focal character or narrator, and other characters may be practical, impatient, or unwilling to dwell. The systems that caused the change remain concrete and contestable, not softened into fate.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeahaq::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / García Márquez / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse framed oral storytelling as the concrete regional route. Establish who is telling the present account, to whom, and under what conditions: a courier explaining a delay, a neighbor reporting what the building's system recorded, a technician describing what they found inside a device. The frame may be brief, but it should make the telling a social act with a reason to occur. Keep the setting and community as supplied; do not import a familiar tale collection, a court, or a generic mystical atmosphere.\n\nLet the frame organize the noir information economy. A teller may report what they saw, what they were told, or what a screen showed them. Distinguish these levels: the narrator can know the present scene directly, while a reported event inside a teller's account belongs to that teller's memory and interest. When a second account revises the first, show the revision as a social event, not merely as corrected data. The listener may doubt, interrupt, or ask a practical question. Surveillance footage, payment records, implant failures, and corporate language remain real within the told accounts; the storytelling frame does not make the facts optional or merely legendary.\n\nUse recurrence to connect the frame and the told material. A phrase from the outer scene can return inside the reported account with a different application, or a name can accumulate meaning as more than one teller uses it. Let the movement between personal life, family memory, public event, and rumor happen through these tellers: a public outage becomes a family argument in one account and a debt deadline in another. The recurrence should make the circulation of information legible, not merely decorative.\n\nKeep the teller's access and interest visible. A teller may be wrong, self-interested, or incomplete, and the reader should be able to notice the difference between what was observed and what was inferred. The frame can close with the listener's response or with the next practical step, without resolving every discrepancy.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeahar::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / García Márquez / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose a small number of exact details and let each one carry the system's pressure and the character's accumulated time. A payment failing at a turnstile can imply the debt, the lender, and the previous week without stating them. A scar over an implant port can imply the surgery and its cost. Select details that make the noir world legible: who controls access, who paid, and what the character now cannot do.\n\nWrite direct clauses. When a longer history matters, compress it into one precise sentence rather than a paragraph of recollection. A sentence may gather two times, but only when the connection is essential: \"She had walked past this clinic every day since they removed the eye.\" Do not add a second explanatory sentence unless the first would otherwise mislead or break causality. The compression should preserve García Márquez's layered time as implication, not delete it.\n\nOmit what the reader can infer, but do not delete the causal link. If a character refuses a job, the refusal should carry enough context to show what it costs. If a message arrives, the reader should understand why it changes the situation. A precise extended sentence may serve better than several short fragments; shortness is not a quota.\n\nKeep voices individual. A corporate representative may speak in full procedural sentences while a street contact uses one word and a gesture. The narrator's minimalism does not flatten every speaker. Use recurrence rather than explanation to carry communal memory: the same object, phrase, or location can return later with a different weight, and the reader supplies the accumulated meaning. End when the present action or perception reaches a natural pause; no summary is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeahas::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / García Márquez / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose. Open in the present action or perception, then let the narrator move naturally into recollection, public context, or inner experience when the scene prompts it. A character waiting for a background check may remember the job that made the check necessary, or notice a news feed item that changes what the wait means. Return to the scene without a formal transition: the door opens, the screen updates, the contact speaks.\n\nLet the systems of the world exert pressure through ordinary scene material. A negotiation can turn on who controls a record, a family conversation can be interrupted by a payment alert, and a walk across the city can be shaped by which routes are monitored or tolled. Keep these pressures concrete and particular to the characters rather than turning them into general social commentary. The narrator's voice may be wry, plain, or intimate, but the register should follow the immediate scene and the active perspective.\n\nUse García Márquez's layered time as a scene function, not a summary function. When earlier events matter, let them surface through the character's attention or through a present consequence, not through a recap paragraph. A public event can enter as a rumor heard in the scene, a family memory as a phrase someone repeats, a past decision as the reason a door now opens or refuses. Keep each layer attached to the present scene's human consequence.\n\nAllow authorized inner experience enough room to be meaningful. A character may reason through a choice, notice a change in their own feeling, or hold a private thought that contradicts what they say. Keep access consistent with the active narrator and perspective. Recurring language can connect scenes: a phrase from a contract, a family saying, or a piece of public rumor may return when a later event gives it a new application. The scene can end with business concluded or still in motion, without a lesson or a forced resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeahat::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / García Márquez / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship premise the scene-selection criterion. Choose moments where the bond is actually at stake or in motion: one character waits while the other passes a checkpoint, they divide a payment they cannot both afford, or a private joke resurfaces during a negotiation. Let the scene show what this relationship promises them, whether that is loyalty, relief, friction, discovery, or a particular kind of care. Do not insert a roster of familiar tropes; work from the supplied premise only.\n\nBuild private language and repeated moments from the characters' shared history. A phrase from an old argument can return with a different meaning. A gesture one character uses only with the other can appear in a public setting where it must stay small. Use García Márquez's layered time to let these repetitions accumulate: the present scene can carry an earlier occasion without a flashback, because the characters themselves remember it. The recurrence should fulfill the relationship's particular emotional promise, not merely decorate it.\n\nLet the genre's systems press on the relationship concretely. Surveillance may make privacy scarce, debt may force a choice between helping one person and paying another, and bodily compromise may change what the characters can do for each other. Affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery should arise from these conditions rather than from a generic emotional script. Give the feeling enough space to develop in the scene, and let it have consequences that fulfill the relationship's particular promise.\n\nKeep access consistent with the active narrator and perspective. Private thought belongs to the focal character or characters as authorized; the other person's inner experience remains outside that access unless the contract permits it. The scene can end in the middle of the relationship's ongoing life: a reassurance given, a question avoided, a hand held in a monitored room. No confession or completed emotional arc is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeahau::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / García Márquez / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account the sense of being retold. Open with a deliberate declaration that establishes a person, a place, or a deed in relation to what will be remembered: who first broke the contract, who kept the clinic's records through the outage, who walked into the corporate tower and did not come out the same. Then expand into the particulars that make the reputation incomplete. The declaration should come from an established act or way of speaking, not from an ornamental epithet assigned to every entrance.\n\nUse recurring phrasing to connect deeds across time. A phrase from an oath, a debt agreement, or a street name can return when a later occurrence gives it a new application. Let kinship, journeys, gifts, feuds, or public deeds organize the account when the supplied scenario establishes them. A courier's route can be a journey; a disputed payment can be a feud; a repaired device can be a gift. Keep the scale honest: an errand remains an errand even when the chronicle gives it weight.\n\nLet the genre's systems supply the material for reputation. Who controls access, who paid, and who altered a body or a record are the deeds this community remembers. Corporate language can enter the chronicle as the speech of a particular power, not as the narrator's own idiom. Street knowledge can correct or complicate an official account. Use García Márquez's communal memory to let these deeds accumulate meaning across tellers and generations: a debt may be remembered as a family burden, a surveillance record as a public shame, a bodily alteration as a private mark with communal consequence.\n\nAllow dialogue to interrupt the chronicle's authority. The subject of an oft-told incident may object to one detail, or answer an elevated introduction with a practical concern. This need not become parody. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective. The account can end with business concluded or resumed, leaving another modest act available to be remembered rather than declaring it legend on the community's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeahav::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / García Márquez / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the case as a social-institutional accounting in the Scandinavian social-realist mode. Treat the noir pressure as an administrative condition: who can see a record, approve a payment, revoke a clearance, admit a body to a clinic, or exclude a name from a registry. When the story supplies a specific Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish setting, use its actual institutional texture; when it does not, keep the systems concrete and local without inventing a national stereotype. Do not reach for snow, isolation, or bleakness as automatic atmosphere.\n\nUse the García Márquez gathering sentence as the main compositional unit. A sentence may move from a domestic detail to an institutional notice to a communal account, but it must terminate in a specific human consequence: a woman loses her housing credit, a technician's clearance expires, a child's school record carries an old address, a neighbor is asked to explain someone else's debt. Let the consequence belong to a particular person rather than to a general mood of surveillance.\n\nDistinguish the institutional record from the communal account. When a character repeats a rumor, a public announcement, or a relative's version of events, let the narration show where that account came from, who repeated it, and what record or observation later qualifies it. A phrase from a public notice, a supervisor's warning, or a neighbor's account can return in a later scene with its meaning changed by a new administrative fact: the name on the notice changes, the clearance is renewed, the address is corrected. Keep rumor as rumor and record as record; do not let communal memory silently become evidence.\n\nKeep required interiority available as ordinary nonspoken private prose. A focal character may think about a debt, a surveillance notice, or a relative's version of events without another character knowing it. The narration may also report what the community says, but it should not make every witness perceptive or every official corrupt. A system can exclude through routine as easily as through malice. Let the response end on a completed administrative step, a pending inquiry, or a quiet domestic consequence without forcing a confession, a reversal, or a moral summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeahaw::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / García Márquez / Screenplay\n\nWrite the story in readable screen form: scene headings when location or time changes, concise action lines for visible and audible events, and clear speaker cues. Keep the cyberpunk noir pressure in what the scene shows and hears: a debt notice on a screen, a biometric gate refusing entry, a supervisor's voice over an intercom, a street vendor's price for a copied credential. Show who controls access and who pays for a decision through observable action and dialogue, not through camera commands.\n\nUse offscreen sound and cuts to carry the García Márquez movement between personal life, public events, and rumor. Establish a recurring public phrase as an audible source in one scene: a radio announcement in a kitchen, a recorded warning in a lobby, a street speaker repeating a service alert. Return to the phrase in a later scene through a different source or context: the same announcement now plays over a clinic intake desk, a character repeats it while reading a notice, a supervisor's recording says the same words with a changed name. Let the change in source or context carry the new implication.\n\nCut between a domestic consequence and its institutional cause. A scene may move from a family kitchen where a payment fails to a corporate lobby where a clearance is revoked, with a radio announcement continuing offscreen. Let the cut make the connection visible without a character explaining it. When a voice-over is needed, use it for a remembered public or communal text, clearly attributed to a speaker, not as the default destination for private thought.\n\nKeep required nonspoken interiority as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation, separate from dialogue and unavailable to the other characters. Do not let voice-over replace it. Let a scene's information change the available interpretations through what is shown and heard: a door that opens without a badge, a name that appears on a list, a receipt that contradicts a spoken account. Preserve the active authorship contract. A scene may end on a completed action, a pending question, or a quiet visible consequence without a mandatory cut to a new clue or a cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeahax::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / García Márquez / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable script with clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be staged. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene. Keep the cyberpunk noir pressure in stageable conditions: a character cannot pass a door without a badge, a supervisor reads a debt notice aloud, a vendor hands over a copied credential, a clinic worker checks a biometric scanner. Show who controls access and who pays for a decision through what the audience can see and hear.\n\nUse the shared stage space to make communal memory and rumor testable. When a character repeats a neighborhood account, a public announcement, or a relative's version of events, let another character respond to the actual claim: ask where they heard it, produce a document that contradicts it, or challenge the timing. An entrance can change what is plausible: a person arrives from a place they were said to have left, or carries an object another witness described. Keep the people occupied with the practical work of checking rather than posed around an explanatory speech.\n\nCarry each recurrence of a public phrase through a stageable object or action. A character reads the phrase from a screen, writes it on a form, repeats it while handling a prop, or hears it from a speaker that the audience can also hear. When the phrase returns in a later scene, give it a new stageable source or context: the same words now appear on a different form, are read by a different character, or are repeated while a different object changes hands. Let the change in source or context carry the new implication. Do not rely on film-camera instructions or offstage effects that cannot be staged.\n\nKeep required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in its active presentation. A monologue or aside needs a dramatic purpose rather than replacing every private thought. When a character's nonspoken interiority is required, keep it clearly attributed and separate from dialogue, unavailable to the other characters. Do not disguise it as something another character can hear. Let the scene end on a completed action, a pending question, or a quiet visible consequence without an obligatory gathering, theatrical revelation, or new clue.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeahay::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / García Márquez / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite through a particular authorized mind. Let perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern braid together so that the cyberpunk noir pressure appears as mental material: a debt notice remembered while washing dishes, a surveillance alert interrupting a childhood recollection, a street vendor's price recalled during a corporate interview. Keep the associations specific to this mind's history and present situation.\n\nBuild the García Márquez gathering sentence from the mind's own association. A sentence may hold a mother's warning, a public announcement, and a supervisor's threat together, but the connection must arise through what this mind actually links: the warning and the announcement share a phrase, the threat arrives while the mind is already returning to the mother's voice. Do not let an external narrator supply the summary. When a recurring phrase returns, anchor it in a specific sensory trigger from this mind's history: the smell of the clinic where the mother once waited, the glow of the screen that showed the first notice, the voice of the supervisor heard through the same intercom as a childhood public alert. Let the recurrence revise the mind's understanding without making the character perfectly perceptive.\n\nKeep enough orientation to distinguish events from recollection or fantasy. When the mind moves from a present scene to a memory, anchor the return with a concrete present-tense sensory detail or an interruption: the water has gone cold, a message arrives, a door opens. Do not make every character share a mind or turn all scene facts indeterminate. Ordinary reasoning alone is not this stylistic mode; the syntax and paragraph shape should follow the associations, with fragments and returns where they serve the particular mind's state.\n\nPreserve required interiority as the actual private experience of the authorized mind. Another character does not know a conclusion merely because the reader has followed its formation. Conversely, do not conceal an authorized focal conclusion until the rest of the cast catches up. The response may end during continuing thought, speech, action, or an unfinished setup without requiring the mind to reach a decision, change direction, or produce a result.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeahaz::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / García Márquez / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the story through readable sender-attributed messages. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one sender quotes a public alert, another forwards a neighbor's account with a comment, another edits a message before sending, another leaves a draft unsent. Keep the cyberpunk noir pressure in the message conditions: a debt collector's automated text, a surveillance notice, a supervisor's demand for a read receipt, a street contact's price for a copied credential.\n\nCarry the García Márquez movement between personal life, family memory, public events, and rumor through actual transmitted texts. When a recurring phrase from a public service announcement or a supervisor's warning returns, make each recurrence a new transmission: a different sender quotes it, a forward chain adds a comment, an edit changes a word, a receipt shows who read it. Let the change in transmission carry the new implication. A character may forward a neighbor's account, quote a relative's warning, or paste a public alert into a private chat, but each recurrence must appear as an actual transmitted text or a clearly separated context note.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text. An unsent draft is a composed artifact: it may show what the sender considered saying, revised, or chose not to transmit, but it is not automatically the writer's live private thought. When context outside the exchange is required, keep it clearly separated from the transcript. A context note may establish who is messaging, where they are, or what interface event occurred, but it must not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext.\n\nPreserve required interiority as actual nonspoken private experience in its active presentation, clearly attributed and separate from the transcript. Do not turn a private thought into a sent message or an unsent draft without a character-specific reason. Let the exchange advance the case through what the senders actually transmit and receive: a document that contradicts a spoken account, a receipt that changes a debt, a forwarded message that revises a rumor. The response may end during a continuing exchange, a pending read receipt, or an unfinished setup without requiring a reply, a resolution, or a cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeahba::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / García Márquez / Visual Novel\n\nWrite in scene-oriented visual novel form: concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Keep the cyberpunk noir pressure in the visible and spoken conditions: a debt notice on a wall screen, a biometric gate refusing entry, a supervisor's recorded voice, a street vendor's price for a copied credential. Show who controls access and who pays for a decision through what the scene presents.\n\nUse the focal narration to gather the García Márquez movement between personal life, family memory, public events, and rumor into a single passage that ends in a specific human consequence for the focal character. A narrative passage may hold a relative's warning, a public announcement, and a supervisor's threat together, but the gathering must terminate in what the focal character now owes, loses, or is excluded from. When a recurring phrase returns, anchor it in a concrete scene element: a familiar background detail, a voice, a screen alert, an object. Let the recurrence revise the focal character's understanding without making every expression a truth indicator.\n\nLet dialogue test the communal accounts through concrete contradictions. When a character repeats a neighborhood rumor or a public record, another character can ask where they heard it, produce a document that contradicts it, or challenge the timing. A background or expression cue may orient the moment, but a contradiction in an arrangement or account must do the evidentiary work. Keep private thought outside other characters' knowledge. Do not turn the cast into fixed reaction portraits whose expression always reveals whether they lie.\n\nKeep required interiority as the focal character's actual nonspoken private experience, clearly attributed and separate from dialogue. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected. Preserve whichever authorship contract is active. The scene may end on a completed action, a pending question, or a quiet visible consequence without a mandatory choice screen, confession, or new clue.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeahbb::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / García Márquez / Web Serial\n\nWrite in installment-oriented prose with a readable through-line across replies. At the start of an installment, briefly recover only the necessary particular: what is underway, what changed locally, and what remains available to continue. Do not retell the whole investigation. Keep the cyberpunk noir pressure in the local changes: a debt increases, a clearance expires, a surveillance notice arrives, a street contact raises a price, a public record contradicts a spoken account. Show who controls access and who pays for a decision through these developments.\n\nUse the García Márquez gathering sentence as a serial device that returns with new administrative weight. A sentence may hold a small domestic detail, a corporate notice, and a later consequence together, but the consequence must remain human and specific. When an earlier gathered detail returns in a later installment, attach a new administrative fact to it: the entrance previously said to be locked is used after a clearance is renewed, the object another witness described appears in a seized inventory, the familiar public announcement now names a different person. Briefly recover the original particular rather than repeating the whole scene that established it.\n\nLet recurring communal language create re-entry points through concrete in-story sources. A phrase from a public service alert, a supervisor's warning, or a neighbor's account can return across installments with its meaning changed by new information. Each recurrence must be anchored in the actual story: a character reads it, hears it, repeats it, or sees it on a screen. Let the change in source or context carry the new implication. New evidence can qualify an old explanation without replacing the old event or making the investigator deliberately forget a supported conclusion.\n\nKeep required interiority available as ordinary nonspoken private prose. A focal character may think about a debt, a surveillance notice, or a relative's version of events without another character knowing it. Let ordinary life continue around the inquiry. The installment may close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Serial duration should give selected facts room to acquire meaning, not make a fair answer perpetually recede.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaiaa::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Gu Long / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the scene through short declarative movements and deliberate gaps. Let the world's systems supply the specific reason a character cannot simply speak: a monitored line, a contract that records vulnerability, a favor whose price rises once it is named, a person who has learned that admitted need becomes leverage. The silence is not vague mood; it is a response to a world that can observe, price, and use whatever is confessed. Track who controls the channel and who pays for the admission.\n\nRender emotional exposure through what the viewpoint character notices and declines to explain. A pause before an answer, an unfinished sentence, attention shifted toward a device or a locked door, a question met with a practical instruction—these carry more weight than a stated confession. When a character does admit something, make it cost something concrete in the exchange that follows: it changes what one person is willing to do, or leaves the other unsure whether the words were a gift or a transaction.\n\nKeep private thought inside the viewpoint. The narration may hold fear, longing, or regret while the other person sees only a hesitation or a changed tone; let each respond to what they can actually observe, never to the hidden feeling. A strained conversation stays strained in its own dialogue—do not settle it with an omniscient explanation or a speech the scene has not earned.\n\nLet a delayed revelation or action change the preceding stillness. The moment can be small: a name finally spoken, a payment declined, a hand withdrawn from a console, a message typed and not sent. Do not require a tragic ending or a completed repair. Warmth can coexist with pain, and comfort may arrive partial, awkward, or interrupted by the same systems that made the admission difficult.\n\nUse the narrator's own distance and diction rather than a stock vocabulary of suffering. Never label a character angsty, broken, or tormented. Show the cost of admitting, needing, withholding, apologizing, or returning through concrete choices and through the gap between what is said and what is understood.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaiab::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Gu Long / Chat Roleplay\n\nPresent one coherent shared interval in readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution. Attach the speaker's name or a clear action marker to each speech so the next participant can follow without rereading. Keep paragraphs short enough to scan without flattening the scene into a bare transcript: anchor the moment in concrete detail—a monitored doorway, a payment terminal, a damaged implant, a street-level exchange—and let prices and permissions shape what the people in it can actually do.\n\nMake compact exchange the engine of the scene. A short line of dialogue carries an implication the next speaker can accept, deflect, or misunderstand. Answer a question with a practical instruction, meet a threat with a price, let an offer sit unanswered while someone checks a device. Do not annotate what a line meant; the gap between what is said and what is meant stays visible for the participants to act on.\n\nKeep the world active rather than paused for input. NPCs continue their own business, a system updates or denies access, the environment imposes a cost or a deadline. End the interval wherever the action naturally stands—mid-motion, mid-sentence, with a door opening or a decision still unfinished—rather than steering toward a cliffhanger, a forced choice, or a question the player must answer.\n\nFollow the active authorship contract for narrative person, action markers, and player control. Do not write the player character's decisions or feelings unless that contract permits it. Resolve the submitted material consistently with the world's established facts and leave the next move open: the scene is a shared present moment, not a completed story that ends by demanding a prompt.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaiac::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Gu Long / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic telling route. Divide the story into compact episodes, each with a clear present action, a few participants, and one concrete pressure: a debt that must be settled, a surveillance window about to open, an access denial, a bargain closing tonight. Begin each episode inside a specific exchange or movement rather than with a summary of what came before, and end each when its immediate action reaches a natural pause, not when the larger question is answered.\n\nConnect the episodes through recurrence and delayed disclosure. A name, an object, a location, or a phrase returns in a later episode with a changed meaning; a fact withheld in one surfaces in another through an action or a short exchange, never through an explanatory passage. Let the reader recognize the connection from the repeated detail, and do not announce its significance.\n\nInside each episode, write in brief declarative movements. Short sentences state an action, a perception, or a line of speech, and meaningful gaps open between what a character says and what the narration explains. A distilled image—a wet street, a dead terminal, a hand resting on a console—can carry the episode's feeling without commentary.\n\nKeep the telling vernacular and grounded in this world's own speech. Corporate language, market slang, and practical bargaining belong to the characters who use them; street knowledge appears as lived knowledge, not quoted as glossary. Do not import a martial cast, a drinking ritual, or an archaic honor code because a regional tradition is selected. The setting, period, language, and community come from the active story.\n\nLet a late episode reinterpret the preceding stillness. The delayed disclosure can be a small fact—who paid for a favor, why an access code was left behind, what a character noticed and never said—and it changes how an earlier exchange reads without demanding a tragic ending, a closed case, or a moral summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaiad::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Gu Long / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite in forceful, concrete sentences. State what happens, what the character touches or sees, and what it costs. Prefer concrete nouns and active verbs to abstract summary. Compress the transitions: cut from one action or perception to the next and let the reader supply the passage between—a character can move from street to stairwell to a locked door in three short sentences.\n\nGround the world in material wear. The scratched casing, the flickering terminal, blood dried on a sleeve, the faded access badge, the ache of an old implant: these are not scenery but the physical stakes of the scene. Let the body register the cost of running, waiting, bargaining, or being denied access. Select only the details that bear weight rather than inventorying the room.\n\nWhere it fits the established speaker and occasion, keep dialogue short and pointed. A line of speech may be answered by an action, a silence, or another line that does not directly respond. Do not explain what a character means; the next concrete detail carries the implication. Let a delayed revelation land on a physical fact—a receipt, a scar, a charge missing from a statement, a door that opens without resistance, a device that has been recording.\n\nVary the rhythm when the character's reasoning needs it. A longer thought is permitted for working through a concrete problem—how to pass a checkpoint, what a price implies, why the wound is worse than it looked—and it stays tied to physical evidence and practical consequence rather than general reflection.\n\nGround rough speech in its speaker. A character talks hard for a reason in the scene: exhaustion, leverage, habit, fear, or the need to end a conversation. Do not make everyone quippy or combative. The prose is hard-edged without turning the cast into uniform tough-talkers.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaiae::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Gu Long / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through documents. Give each entry a clear author, an intended recipient or purpose, and a time of composition, and use the form the situation actually requires: a payment ledger, a surveillance log, a message to a fixer, a medical report, a debt notice, a recorded statement, an unsent draft. Do not rotate through formats mechanically—the document belongs to the person who would have made it at the moment that demanded it.\n\nLet each document's situation control what it knows and conceals. A report to a supervisor omits a bribe; a message to a lover softens a threat; a corporate log records only what the system was told to watch. The author's purpose shapes which facts appear. Never add an omniscient explanation after a record; the reader infers the gap from what the document says and what it declines to say. Treat each document as what its author chose to write down, not as a window onto a complete mind.\n\nBuild the delayed revelation across entries. A fact named in one document grows significant when a later record revises or contradicts it: a missing timestamp, a changed amount, a name replaced by a placeholder, a message that stops mid-sentence. The disclosure emerges from the sequence of records, not from a narrator who steps in to explain. Let a later document change how an earlier one reads; the sequence may end on that changed understanding or leave it still settling, without a closed case or a moral summary.\n\nKeep the continuity readable. Connect each new document to the one before it through a shared event, person, or object, even when the authors do not know they are connected—the reader follows the trail while the characters may not. Make the chronology legible through dates, ordering, or material traces: a forwarded message, a torn receipt, a file recovered from a damaged drive.\n\nLet the systems work inside the documents themselves. Surveillance limits what a character dares to write; a debt system generates records that contradict a personal account; a compromised device alters or delays a message. These pressures appear as features of the records, never as commentary around them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaiaf::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Gu Long / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nAttend to chosen kindness and practical care inside the cyberpunk world. Make the comfort specific to these people and this setting: a hot meal bought with scarce credit, a dry jacket handed over without comment, a device repaired by hand, a quiet corner found away from the monitors, a familiar order placed without asking. These acts are not escapes from the genre; they are small choices made inside debt, surveillance, and unequal power, and they can cost something. Let who controls access and who pays remain part of the scene rather than suspended around it.\n\nLet affection be sincere without requiring a confession or an emotional repair. Use brief declarative movement and meaningful gaps: a short line, a gesture, a shared silence carries the warmth, and no one is forced to name a feeling or settle an old hurt. Comfort may be partial, and the world's pressure can stay at the edge of the interval without breaking it.\n\nKeep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact. The character being cared for does not become agreeable; the one giving care does not become selfless. They may still argue, keep secrets, or refuse help. A comforting interval sits beside a debt, a threat, or a hard choice without canceling any of them, and the warmth is not owed a reversal or exposed as a trap.\n\nAnchor the ease in particular preferences. Show what this specific person likes—a food, a way of sitting, a sound, a routine—and let the care meet it, so the comfort feels earned rather than generic. Do not replace the setting with a domestic vignette: the room may be cramped, the power metered, the window covered, and the kindness matters precisely there.\n\nLet the interval end at a natural pause. The comfort need not produce a result, a confession, or a completed arc; the next difficulty may be waiting outside the door while the attention rests on these particular people and the care they manage to give.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaiag::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Gu Long / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Open with enough narrative distance to place the immediate action inside a larger situation—a district's debt structure, a corporation's changed policy, a family's long entanglement with a fixer, a neighborhood's memory of an earlier purge—and draw that framing from the chosen narrator's actual knowledge and standing rather than from an omniscient voice that explains every character.\n\nDistinguish narration from ordinary spoken language. Let the narrative prose be more composed than the dialogue, with fuller sentences and a wider view of time and consequence, while a character answers in street slang or corporate shorthand. The narration frames what that speech costs or conceals; it does not impose elaborate or archaic diction on every speaker.\n\nHandle the delayed revelation as composed narrative withholding. The narration observes a stillness, a repeated detail, or an unexplained arrangement and leaves it unresolved for a time; later, a single fact or action changes the meaning of that earlier passage. Let the revelation arrive through the narrative's own movement—a sentence that returns to an earlier image, a document that finally makes sense, an act that reinterprets a prior silence—and never announce it as a twist.\n\nKeep the systems active in the framing. Debt, surveillance, access, and price are social facts, not background color: show how a personal choice is shaped by who controls a record, who can revoke a clearance, and who pays for a decision. Make the systems legible through the story's arrangements and obligations rather than through policy summary.\n\nUse public and private contrast where the scene supports it. A passage may move from a hearing, a market, or a corporate lobby into a private room where the same facts are spoken differently, and that shift can organize the paragraph movement without any aristocratic setting or ceremony. When a scene closes, let it be able to rest in a changed understanding rather than in a verdict or a moral summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaiah::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Gu Long / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen inside an occasion where one person is telling another a story under practical pressure: a payment window, a locked room, a held contract, a monitored meeting, or a favor that must be answered before someone leaves. Make the outer scene concrete about who is listening, what they want, and what the teller is trying to gain or avoid. The story is not background. It is a move in the present negotiation.\n\nWhen the embedded story begins, shift cleanly into its own scene and keep its level distinct through a repeated image, object, or phrase that also appears in the outer frame. Let the inner story show how a system was worked: who had access, who paid, what was altered, what was left unsaid. Do not explain the parallel. Let the listener react, interrupt, or ask the wrong question, and let that response reveal what the listener is actually protecting.\n\nUse Gu Long's compression at the joins. Cut away transitions and let a short exchange or a single noticed detail carry the turn. When the inner story ends, return to the outer scene at a changed point: the listener has agreed, refused, stalled, or understood something the teller did not intend to reveal. The teller's selection and emphasis are themselves evidence. What the teller omits, repeats, or hurries past should be legible to the reader even when the listener misses it.\n\nKeep the cyberpunk pressure present in both levels: a debt, a surveillance window, a bodily modification, a corporate policy, or a street transaction that gives the telling its occasion. Do not require the inner story to solve a case or produce a confession. It may simply change what someone is willing to pay, wait for, or believe. End the response at the moment the outer situation has shifted, not necessarily when the story is complete.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaiai::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Gu Long / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the documentary and historical-memory route. Write the scene as a composed record that compares what a file, log, contract, or surveillance transcript says with what a participant remembers or what the street actually showed. Keep the narrator's distance steady: the prose can cite, quote, or summarize documents, but it should also mark where the record is incomplete, edited, or written to protect someone.\n\nLet Gu Long's compression shape the documentary surface. Present short quoted lines, terse entries, and concrete details without connective explanation. Leave gaps between the record and the remembered event. The reader should feel the weight of what is not logged: a payment made in person, a door opened off-camera, a phrase spoken after the recording stopped, a body altered without a consent file.\n\nMake the cyberpunk pressure specific and material. Show who controls the archive, who can alter an entry, whose access is priced, and what a missing record costs. Do not turn every gap into a conspiracy. Some omissions are routine, some are protective, and some are the result of a system that never recorded certain people at all. When the delayed revelation comes, let it return to an earlier quoted line, logged omission, or remembered condition and change its meaning under the new information.\n\nKeep the telling concrete and socially situated. A debt, a workplace, a housing block, a clinic, or a transit route can anchor the comparison between official language and lived experience. Avoid translating German syntax or imposing a national temperament. The method is the route: records and memory held side by side, with the difference between them doing the narrative work.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaiaj::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Gu Long / Gothic-Punk\n\nStage the scene as a collision between an old authority and present urban life: a family claim on a building, a legacy access code, a patron with outdated privileges, a private clinic run by a name that still opens doors, or a debt inherited rather than chosen. Make the encounter intimate and particular. Two or three people are enough. Let the room, hallway, rooftop, or vehicle carry the atmosphere through concrete decay: failing light, patched surfaces, old security, new surveillance, a repaired object that still shows its damage.\n\nUse Gu Long's brevity to hold the social mask in place. Characters speak in short exchanges, notice small things, and decline to explain. The mask does not fall through confession. It slips through a pause, a repeated gesture, a question answered too quickly, or a moment when someone stops performing for the other person. Let private desire and fear remain visible to the reader through what the character notices and avoids, not through a summary of their feelings.\n\nKeep the cyberpunk pressure present as a material condition. The old authority may still control a physical space, a contract, or a name, but it must negotiate with present systems: a biometric lock, a credit check, a surveillance feed, a clinic's intake form, a landlord's algorithm. Show who pays for the encounter and what access costs. Do not import vampires, clans, blood bonds, or supernatural powers. The horror is personal and social: dependence, exposure, compromised intimacy, and the weight of inherited arrangements.\n\nLet the revelation be small and specific. It should change how the reader understands an earlier gesture, line, or object that belonged to the collision between the old claim and the present system. It should leave the present situation altered without resolving every debt or danger. End when the encounter has shifted, not when the atmosphere has been described.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaiak::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Gu Long / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the testimonial and autobiographical route. Write from a specific teller who is recounting an experience of debt, surveillance, exclusion, or bodily compromise to a listener or reader with a reason to receive the account. Keep the teller's language, community, and period concrete when the story supplies them. Do not force English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure onto the scene.\n\nLet Gu Long's compression shape the testimony. The teller speaks in short, declarative units, notices a few sharp images, and leaves gaps where the memory is painful, dangerous, or not yet understood. Distinguish what the teller knew at the time from what the teller has learned since. A later realization cannot be inserted as the earlier motive, but the teller may mark it: \"I did not know then what the form meant.\"\n\nKeep the cyberpunk pressure specific. Show who controlled the records, the clinic, the workplace, the housing list, or the credit line. Let the teller's account include the practical cost: a fee paid, a document withheld, a body altered, a name removed, a family member used as collateral. The testimony is not a case file. It is one person's attempt to make the experience legible to someone who was not there.\n\nUse Gu Long's delayed revelation at the point where the teller returns to an earlier image or phrase and changes its meaning. Let the listener's interruption, doubt, or supplied detail press against what the teller has omitted or hurried past, but keep the teller's account at the center. End when the telling has changed the teller, the listener, or the meaning of an earlier detail, not when every question is answered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaial::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Gu Long / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the diary and reflective miscellany route. Write the scene as a personal record composed in short entries, dated or occasion-based, by a teller who is living inside a cyberpunk system. The entries may be written on a device, a paper notebook, a voice file, or a private channel, but they are composed artifacts, not a live stream of thought. Keep the teller's language and distance consistent with who they are and why they are recording.\n\nLet Gu Long's compression shape each entry. Use brief declarative sentences, a few concrete images, and no explanatory connective tissue. Leave gaps between entries. What the teller does not record matters as much as what they do: a missed payment, a surveillance notice, a medical result, a person who stopped answering. The reader should feel the unrecorded interval through a change in the next entry's tone, detail, or subject.\n\nKeep the cyberpunk pressure specific and material. Show the teller's dependence on a system that observes, prices, or excludes them: a credit score, a biometric check, a workplace monitor, a clinic intake, a housing algorithm, a debt collector's message. Let the teller reflect on these conditions without turning every entry into an argument. The miscellany can include a remembered conversation, a street scene, a practical worry, or a small observation that later accrues meaning.\n\nUse Gu Long's delayed revelation when a later entry returns to an earlier recorded image, phrase, or small observation and changes its meaning. The teller may notice the change themselves or leave it for the reader to see. Do not require seasonal imagery, quietness, or a particular Japanese setting. End when the entry has registered a shift in the teller's situation or understanding, not when the day is complete.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaiam::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Gu Long / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social realism, chronicle, and testimony route. Write the scene as a witness's account of a specific community, workplace, block, or family living under cyberpunk pressure. Keep the language, community, and history concrete when the story supplies them. The narrator may be a participant, a chronicler, or someone recording another person's testimony, but the account must stay grounded in what was seen, heard, or reliably reported.\n\nLet Gu Long's compression shape the chronicle. Use short declarative sentences, sharp images, and gaps where the official record is silent or the witness cannot prove what they know. Distinguish observation from report and memory from later knowledge. A neighbor's account, a leaked file, and a remembered conversation are different kinds of evidence, and the prose should keep their limits visible without turning the scene into a case summary.\n\nKeep the cyberpunk pressure specific and material. Show who controls the water, power, clinic, transit, credit, or surveillance feed. Let the chronicle record the cost: a family displaced, a body altered, a debt inherited, a name removed from a list, a protest logged and priced. Do not add magical or supernatural facts. The power of the account comes from the concrete reality of the system and the witness's decision to speak.\n\nUse Gu Long's delayed revelation when the chronicle returns to an earlier detail, phrase, or person and changes its meaning. The witness may understand the change themselves or leave it for the reader to see, but the later understanding must not be written as the earlier knowledge. End when the account has altered how the reader understands the community or the system, not when every injustice has been resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaian::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Gu Long / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Open with a concrete location, a person with a practical goal, and the cyberpunk pressure already active: a debt, a surveillance window, a locked account, a held package, a clinic appointment, or a meeting that cannot be missed. Keep spatial changes easy to follow. When the scene moves from a street to a room, a rooftop to a stairwell, or a terminal to a back office, mark the change in a short, concrete line.\n\nUse readable paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response. Keep dialogue exchanges compact. Let Gu Long's compression work through what is not said: a character answers with a single word, looks at a screen instead of replying, or notices a detail the other person does not explain. Give unfamiliar names or mechanics clear presentation when they appear, but do not pause the scene for a manual. Fold the explanation into the action or the character's practical attention.\n\nKeep the reaction beats focused. After a turn in the negotiation or a new piece of information, show the focal character's private response in a brief, specific beat: a physical sensation, a remembered detail, a calculation, or a decision. The reaction can be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to its owner. Do not make every character witty or ready with banter.\n\nUse Gu Long's delayed revelation as a visual or verbal turn. Return to a single detail noticed earlier and let it change meaning: a door that was already open, a charge that appeared before the meeting, a name on a list, a scar under a sleeve. Let the turn land in a short paragraph or a single line. End when the immediate situation has shifted, not necessarily on a cliffhanger. First person, a young cast, and comedy are not required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaiao::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Gu Long / Lyrical Prose\n\nBuild the scene as a slow accumulation of sensory and social detail around something not yet named — a debt, an arrangement, a question the focal character has not spoken. Move through the surfaces where the world's systems show themselves: light on wet composite, the hum of a filtration unit, a clerk's hand hesitating over a payment pad. Choose one or two images the scene can bear returning to, and let each return register what has been noticed since, so that recurrence carries the pressure of the unnamed thing instead of decorating a plot beat. Keep that thing specific — a real question or cost the narration circles — never a vague mood. Where the active contract grants inner access, let the lyric attention include the character's actual feeling and reasoning; the gap lives in what gets said or explained, not in an emptied narration.\n\nWhen a comparison appears, make it change what the narrator understands. A debt told through the sound of a door sealing should then expose who controls that door, who waits outside it, and what the debt has already cost. Keep images traceable to the scene or the teller's associations, and let plain declarative sentences cut the figurative run — a single short line can pivot a whole paragraph. Corporate language and street knowledge enter the lyric line as found material: a warranty clause, a loan term, a warning repeated in the stairwell. Let such phrases keep their own rhythm and return until they press on the unnamed fact.\n\nIf the story brings the withheld thing into the open, let the arriving fact reweight an earlier image rather than explain it — the image now means more, or other. Do not manufacture a reveal to close the reply; the pressure may remain unresolved while the prose keeps moving through the world it has organized. Stop where the scene's boundary falls: mid-motion, mid-noticing, or in the settling after something has finally been said.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
        },
        {
            "role": "system",
            "injection_position": 0,
            "injection_depth": 4,
            "system_prompt": false,
            "marker": false,
            "enabled": true,
            "forbid_overrides": false,
            "injection_order": 100,
            "injection_trigger": [],
            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-cyberpunk_noir-03",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Cyberpunk Noir · 03/07",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == cyberpunk_noir}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Cyberpunk Noir. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 3 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-cyberpunk_noir; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPaeaiap::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Gu Long / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nBegin with a present fact, stated plainly, and let attention carry the narrator into what it recalls. Make the memory particular — one evening, one transaction, one phrase — never a generalized haze of loss. When the world's systems of access show a small change, use it as the hinge: a card that no longer opens a door, a name missing from a directory, a message that arrives without the tone the character remembers. When nothing has changed, an unchanged thing can carry the same weight — the directory still listing someone gone. Keep the noir pressure inside the memory itself: what was paid, who let them in, what was agreed to in order to be there.\n\nUse the gaps the way this telling needs them: the character notices something and declines to explain it, and the reflection circles that withheld detail through remembered sound, or through the difference between what was said then and what the system now records. Under the active contract, write the private experience for real — the reasoning, the ache, the arithmetic of what it cost — while the spoken account stays partial for a reason that belongs to this character. The coexistence of present experience and what is no longer available can remain unresolved. Do not force the memory into a lesson or a completed grief, and let ordinary pleasure stay pleasurable even where it hurts.\n\nIf recognition arrives, let it come late: the character understands only after the reflective passage that an earlier detail meant something else, or that memory has been shaped by a fact not yet admitted — and the remembered occasion changes weight without losing its pleasure or its cost. Never require that turn. The reply can end with old and new meanings held together, the scene still going on around the character.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaiaq::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Gu Long / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nTell this episode inside a frame: one teller, one listener, a concrete occasion. Establish briefly where they are, why the story is being told now, and what the listener wants from it, then let that occasion govern the telling — what the teller includes, softens, withholds, or speeds past. The frame is a present scene with its own stakes, not an ornamental prologue. Use the setting, language, and community the scenario actually supplies; choosing this tradition does not relocate the cast or require antiquity, mysticism, exile, or religion. If the story names a specific tradition, keep its conventions distinct rather than blending them into a general regional flavor.\n\nCarry the inner tale in compact declarative movement: short, exact sentences and distilled images — an empty courier case, a lock that still answers to a dead hand, a debt transferred three times in a single night. The teller holds back one decisive fact, and the gap is audible in the telling: a pause, a change of subject, a detail mentioned and then refused. The listener may interrupt, question, or misread, and the teller's answer can deepen the uncertainty instead of closing it. A single aphoristic line — a sentence of earned general truth drawn from what the teller has lived — may mark a turn in the tale, provided it costs something rather than ornamenting the surface.\n\nLet the tale's substance stay noir: access, payment, surveillance, exclusion — and who pays for the telling itself, whether that is a bugged room, a creditor listening, or time being bought. If the withheld fact surfaces, let it change the relation among teller, listener, and events: the listener recognizes that the story concerns them, or the teller understands the fact only by saying it aloud. Do not force that arrival. End with the tale concluded and the frame's business still open, its consequence unfolding in the room where it was told.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaiar::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Gu Long / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect a small number of exact details and state them in direct clauses. Each one should alter the reader's sense of the situation: who has access, who is being watched, what a decision will cost. Cut whatever does not serve the present account, but leave enough that the omitted fact can be inferred — or that something specific is being withheld can be inferred. Never delete causality; the reader should understand why a character acts even when the narration does not explain the act. Brevity is not a quota: where an essential inward connection needs room, let the sentence run long enough to make sense.\n\nKeep the brief declarative line as the default shape and let compact exchanges carry the scene. When a character notices one thing and declines to explain it, make that a real gap in the account, recoverable from what surrounds it — a refusal to enter a building lands because a changed lock or a transferred debt was named earlier. Where the active contract grants inner access, give the actual private reasoning in as few words as this character would spend on it; silence in speech is not an empty head, and each voice stays individual.\n\nA withheld fact draws its force from the details placed around it. If a later detail or exchange brings it into the open, one sentence can turn the whole account, changing what an earlier detail meant without pausing to revisit it. Do not stage a reveal the scene has not earned, and do not rush the ending to deliver one. Stop at the scene's own boundary — even mid-exchange, even before the consequence lands — and trust what remains selected.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaias::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Gu Long / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose that moves naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and whatever inner experience the active contract authorizes. Let paragraphing and clean transitions orient the reader through the world's systems: a conversation in a monitored lobby, a walk past a payment kiosk, a memory surfacing of the contract behind a present obligation. No recap machinery; trust the reader with what has already been shown. The narrator's voice and the immediate scene set the register and rhythm.\n\nHold the decisive fact back inside the fuller prose. Dwell on what a character notices, chooses not to say, or remembers, without explaining the fact too early — it stays a live pressure, whether because the character knows something the reader does not, or because the reader can see a contradiction the character has not yet faced. Embed the compact exchanges in the continuous flow: a short line of dialogue lands harder after a longer passage of perception, and a distilled image can serve as the pivot on which the scene turns. Inner experience stays in the ordinary current of the prose — thought, sensation, half-formed judgment — not quarantined in a special aside; the gap lies in what gets explained, not in a missing interior life.\n\nIf the withheld fact opens, let its new meaning arrive inside the continuing scene: the character may act on it at once, or carry it unsettled while business proceeds, and the prose need not stop to summarize what has changed. Do not manufacture the turn to close the reply. End at a natural boundary or mid-movement, the practical consequences still open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaiat::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Gu Long / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship legible through the scenes you choose and the moments you return to. Draw only on the premise actually supplied — these particular people, this particular history — rather than inserting a roster of familiar tropes. Select scenes where the relationship's promise is under pressure from the world's systems: a shared access code one of them has changed, a debt one paid without telling the other, a surveillance feed they both know is running. Private language does the remembering — a phrase, a gesture, a small ritual — and recurs with new weight once the situation has changed.\n\nUse compact exchanges in which what is not said carries as much as what is. A short dialogue can expose affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery without anyone announcing a feeling; when a character notices something and declines to explain it, the silence should let the reader feel this relationship's particular history. Make the withheld fact specific to these two — one knows something about the other's past, debt, body, or loyalties — and let interiority follow the contract: render the private worry, tenderness, or calculation for what it is, even while the character keeps it out of speech, with a reason that belongs to them.\n\nWhen a beat the relationship has promised comes due — a confession, a returned phrase, a discovery that revises an earlier moment, a consequence finally accepted — give it enough space to land. Do not force the payoff into every reply; a scene may build toward it and stop short, and not every difficulty resolves. The systems stay active throughout: access can still be revoked, surveillance still records, a debt still comes due. The relationship may be changed by what surfaces while its practical situation keeps unfolding.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaiau::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Gu Long / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account the feel of retelling. When a unit of the story opens, a compact declaration can place the episode among deeds people remember — a courier who carried a false manifest, a fixer who opened the door no one else would open, a debtor who paid with someone else's name — and the particulars that follow complicate the reputation the declaration offered. Inside a continuing scene, keep the chronicle register through remembered consequence rather than re-declaring it each time.\n\nWork brief declaratives and distilled images into the saga's recurrence. A recurring phrase or designation must be earned by an established deed or way of speaking, and it returns when a later occurrence gives it new application. Let the world's systems supply the material of remembered consequence: who controlled access, who paid, who was excluded, what the surveillance record later showed. A public deed may be remembered as heroic or shameful; either way, keep its practical cost legible. Hold the telling's access to the active narrator and perspective — where the subject tells their own story, the community's version can be weighed openly against the private one; where narration stays outside, let the divergence between reputation and focal experience remain perceptible within what that contract permits.\n\nThe withheld fact — the true cost of a remembered deed, who actually paid, why a name is now met with silence — gathers force through recurrence. If it surfaces, let it reweight the recurring phrase rather than turn the episode into a moral lesson, and do not manufacture its arrival. End with the act now available to be remembered differently, its consequences running on past the telling.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaiav::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Gu Long / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite this as a restrained social-institutional prose narrative in the Scandinavian realist tradition. Treat the cyberpunk world as a set of concrete arrangements: who is employed by whom, which records are kept, what a service costs, who can enter a building, and what a person must sign or surrender to proceed. Render these conditions through specific encounters rather than atmospheric neon or slang. When a system observes or prices a person, show the actual mechanism: a form, a scan, a withheld credential, a billing notice, a supervisor's instruction.\n\nUse Gu Long's declarative movement and meaningful gaps. State what a person notices in short, exact sentences. Leave the reason unspoken until a later fact changes the meaning of the earlier stillness. A glance at a locked door, a colleague's unfinished sentence, or a bill left on a table can carry pressure without immediate explanation. Delay revelation by withholding a single consequential fact while the visible scene continues; when it arrives, let it revise what the reader understood rather than merely confirming it.\n\nKeep the narration socially situated. The focal character's private thought appears as composed inward prose, not as speech another character can hear. Show how a decision is shaped by rent, work, family obligation, or institutional procedure when the scenario supports it. A person may comply, bargain, refuse, or help for reasons that remain partly private. Do not make every system cruel or every helper corrupt; let the actual conditions of the scene produce the pressure.\n\nDistinguish observation from inference. A character can see that a door is open without knowing who opened it. A record can confirm a transaction without proving a motive. Let the prose state what is known, then allow the next exchange or discovery to complicate it. Keep the telling economical: no redundant explanation of a gesture, no summary of what the reader has already witnessed, and no compulsory wit or confession at the end of a scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaiaw::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Gu Long / Screenplay\n\nWrite in screen-oriented scenes. Use a scene heading when location or time changes, then concise action lines that state what is visible or audible: a door opens, a screen lights, a payment fails, a person sets down a cup without drinking. Keep each action line to one observable event or condition. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, use speaker cues and dialogue blocks that are short and exact. Let a line imply more than it says; do not have characters explain what the action line has already shown.\n\nRender cyberpunk noir's systems as observable conditions. Show the scanner above the door, the balance on the terminal, the guard checking a credential, the implant glowing under a sleeve. When surveillance or debt matters, make it something the camera can see or the sound design can hear: a notification tone, a locked door, a recorded voice. Corporate language belongs in dialogue or onscreen text when a character would actually speak or display it.\n\nUse Gu Long's delayed revelation through scene information. A character notices an object or a detail without comment. A later scene shows the same object in a different condition, or a line of dialogue refers to it, and the earlier stillness changes meaning. Do not announce the connection in the action line. Let the cut or the next scene supply the new fact.\n\nWhen required private prose is needed, include it as a clearly attributed nonspoken note, such as a character-name heading followed by the private thought in prose. This note is not dialogue and is not available to the other characters. Use voice-over only when the chosen sequence specifically requires it. End a scene on a completed action, a pending request, or a visible change without forcing a cliffhanger or a summary line.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaiax::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Gu Long / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable script with clear speaker names, entrances, exits, and economical stage directions. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, keep dialogue short and exact. Let a pause, an object handled, or a person's position in the room carry meaning that the lines do not state. When a character enters or leaves, make the movement part of the scene's pressure: someone arrives with a notice, leaves before answering, or stands between another person and the door.\n\nStage cyberpunk noir's systems as physical conditions. A terminal on a desk, a scanner by the entrance, a bill in a character's hand, a locked drawer, a visible implant. Let the characters interact with these objects through ordinary business: checking a screen, refusing to hand over a credential, paying for a drink. Corporate language appears in dialogue when a character would actually speak it, not as narration.\n\nUse Gu Long's delayed revelation through staged details. A character notices an object without comment. A later entrance or exit changes what the object means: the drawer is now open, the terminal is dark, the person who left has the key. Let the revelation arrive through a visible change or a line that refers back to the earlier stillness. Do not have a character announce the connection in an explanatory speech.\n\nPreserve required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation, clearly attributed to the character and separated from the spoken text. It is not a monologue or an aside and is not heard by the other characters. Use a monologue or aside only when the scene has a specific dramatic purpose for performed speech. End the scene on a completed action, an entrance or exit, or a held silence without forcing a confession or a theatrical reveal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaiay::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Gu Long / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite from inside a single authorized mind. Let perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, and interruption braid together as the character experiences them. A scanner's tone can pull the thought back to a debt notice; a familiar street can surface a conversation from years ago; the feel of a wet sleeve can interrupt a memory of a dry room. Syntax may follow these associations, but keep enough orientation for the reader to distinguish what is happening now from what is remembered or imagined.\n\nUse Gu Long's distilled images inside the mental flow. A single sharp detail can carry the weight of a longer explanation: a hand resting on a terminal, a name written in condensation, a door that does not close. Let the mind return to such an image without immediately explaining it. The delayed revelation works by association: a later perception or memory changes what the earlier image meant. Do not announce the connection; let the mind arrive at it.\n\nLet cyberpunk noir's systems enter as recurring pressures rather than exposition. The character's thoughts circle a bill, a scan, a supervisor's phrase, a body that no longer works the way it did. These concerns surface through sensory triggers and private words, not through a summary of how the system operates. When the character notices who controls access or who pays, render it as an immediate thought or sensation: the door opens for someone else, the payment clears, the implant aches.\n\nKeep the flow particular to this character. Another person's mind is not available unless separately authorized. Ordinary reasoning alone is not enough; the movement must be associative, with interruptions and returns. Do not force every thought to resolve into a decision or a spoken line. The passage can end where the mind rests, even if the external scene remains unfinished.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaiaz::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Gu Long / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the story as readable sender-attributed messages. Keep each message short and exact, in the sender's actual digital habit: one person writes full sentences, another sends fragments, a third leaves long silences. Do not make every sender witty or cryptic. Let the difference between what a person types, sends, edits, delays, or leaves unsent carry the pressure.\n\nRender cyberpunk noir's systems as meaningful interface events. A read receipt that does not arrive, a payment notification, a channel that requires access, a message that fails to send, a timestamp that contradicts a claim. Include only the interface events that matter to the scene. Corporate language appears when a sender would actually use it, such as a support reply or an automated notice. Do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext.\n\nUse Gu Long's delayed revelation through message timing and omission. A sender asks a question and receives no answer. A later message refers to the earlier silence, or an attachment changes what the first message meant. A person notices something but does not type it. The revelation arrives through what is finally sent, edited, or left unsent. Do not have a sender explain the connection in a summary message.\n\nDistinguish sent messages from unsent drafts and private thought. A sent message is transmitted and available to the recipient. An unsent draft is a composed artifact that the sender has not transmitted; show it clearly as an unsent draft, not as a message the recipient can read. Private thought is not transmitted text and must be clearly separated from the transcript when required. Context outside the exchange may appear when needed, clearly marked as outside the transcript. End on a sent message, a pending reply, or a meaningful silence without forcing a cliffhanger or a confession.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaiba::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Gu Long / Visual Novel\n\nWrite in a scene-oriented rhythm suited to an illustrated visual novel. Open a scene with concise setting: where the character is, what is visible, and what condition the space is in. Use readable dialogue attribution with clear speaker names. Follow dialogue with focal narration or inner response that shows what the character notices or withholds. Keep the passages short enough to sit beside an image, but give the focal reaction room to do its work.\n\nUse background and expression cues as purposeful references, not truth indicators. A background cue can orient the moment: a terminal glowing in a dark room, a wet street outside a window, a door standing open. An expression cue can suggest a reaction: a flat look, a slight nod, a hand that stops before touching the screen. Do not make every expression reveal whether a person lies. Let a contradiction in an account or arrangement do the evidentiary work.\n\nRender cyberpunk noir's systems as visible conditions in the scene. A scanner above the entrance, a debt notice on the terminal, a guard's credential, a body with a visible implant. The focal character's inner response can register who controls access or who pays, but the other characters do not know that thought. Keep private thought outside their knowledge.\n\nUse Gu Long's delayed revelation through scene changes and focal reactions. A familiar visual detail appears in one scene without explanation. A later scene returns to it in a changed condition, or the focal character's inner response connects it to a new fact. Let the revelation arrive through the scene's information rather than an explanatory speech. Keep scene changes legible. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected. End a scene on a completed exchange, a focal realization, or a visible change without forcing a choice screen or a cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeaibb::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Gu Long / Web Serial\n\nWrite in installment-oriented prose with a readable through-line. Each reply should make clear what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover context briefly only when it helps: a single exact detail from an earlier installment, not a retelling of the investigation that established it. Let the recovered detail sit in the new scene and acquire meaning through what happens next.\n\nUse Gu Long's delayed revelation across installments. A person notices an object, a phrase, or a condition without explanation. A later installment returns to it under a different condition, or a new fact changes what the earlier stillness meant. Preserve the earlier observation exactly; do not alter it to force a contradiction. New evidence can qualify an old explanation without replacing the old event.\n\nLet cyberpunk noir's systems develop as cumulative conditions. A debt grows, a credential expires, a surveillance feed is mentioned again, a body's modification causes a new problem. Show these changes through concrete particulars: a balance, a denied access, a notification. Corporate language appears when a character or system would actually use it. Do not make every installment introduce a new system; let the existing conditions deepen.\n\nKeep required inward experience available when it contributes feeling or thought, even if no immediate decision changes. A repeated question may have a new implication without a fresh contradiction being invented. Close an installment on completed work, a pending request, a visible change, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Serial duration should give selected facts room to acquire meaning, not make a fair answer perpetually recede. Use an occasional hook when the development earns it, not as a compulsory ending for every reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeajaa::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Haruki Murakami / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the noir investigation through a narrator who performs ordinary practical actions with Murakami's even, unhurried attention: making coffee, waiting for a contact, checking a message, walking a familiar route, paying for access. Let the technological and corporate systems of the cyberpunk world remain present as concrete conditions that observe, price, or limit those actions. When the narrator is watched, show what the surveillance actually changes: a message he does not send, a route he avoids, a question he rephrases, a debt he remembers while doing something unrelated.\n\nTreat emotional turbulence as the cost of continuing a difficult relationship inside that system. Let the narrator's private thought move between the immediate task and the person he is avoiding, needing, or trying to reach. A small detail, an old message, a familiar gesture, or a bill he cannot pay can open an unresolved feeling without resolving it. Do not force him to name the feeling or turn it into a confession. The strain should appear in what he withholds, what he repeats, what he almost says, and what he does instead. If he does speak, make the spoken words simpler or more guarded than the private thought around them, and let the other person's response be partial, awkward, or practical rather than fully healing.\n\nKeep the investigation moving through concrete noir pressure: a bribe, a borrowed credential, a locked file, a corporate phrase that means something worse than it says, a street contact who knows the price of a name. Use each pressure to expose the emotional stakes, not to replace them. When the narrator bargains or compromises, show what the choice costs him privately, even if the scene continues without a pause. The ending of a scene may leave the relationship unsettled and the case unfinished; the narrator can return to an ordinary routine with the hurt still present.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeajab::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Haruki Murakami / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the scene as a coherent shared interval in readable paragraphs. Give each speaker clear attribution and keep the active point of view consistent with the selected narrative person and player control settings. Let the cyberpunk noir world remain active around the characters: a payment window, a monitored lobby, a street vendor, a corporate notice, a drone passing overhead, or another NPC continuing their own practical business. These details should be concrete conditions in the scene, not a mandatory scenery list.\n\nUse Murakami's calm attention to slow the scene without stalling it. The viewpoint character may notice an ordinary action in exact detail: the way a contact stirs a drink, the sound of a card reader, the weight of a door, the delay before a message arrives. Let that detail connect to a private concern without explaining the connection, and let it remain inside the viewpoint character's knowledge. Keep the narration conversational and restrained rather than ornate.\n\nAdvance the shared situation through dialogue and action. When a character speaks, make the words sound like that character's ordinary speech, not polished banter. Allow the other participant to respond with cooperation, refusal, evasion, or a practical question. End the reply at a natural continuation point that follows from what has just happened. A question may remain open, a task may be partly done, or a character may simply wait; do not force a cliffhanger, a mandatory choice, or a sudden reversal at the end.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeajac::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Haruki Murakami / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate urban or domestic narrative route. Write the cyberpunk noir through the texture of a particular city life: a rented room, a workplace, a neighborhood, a family obligation, a debt, a commute, a meal, a neighbor's problem. Let technological and corporate systems enter through those ordinary social arrangements rather than as abstract worldbuilding. A rent increase, a monitored building, a job that requires a biometric check, a relative who needs money, or a shop that keeps records can supply the noir pressure.\n\nKeep the narration close to the viewpoint character's daily experience with Murakami's calm, exact attention. Describe practical actions and familiar surfaces without hurry: the way a person locks a door, waits for a bus, reads a notice, or prepares food. Let private concern surface through association with those ordinary things. The character may remember a person or an earlier time while doing something routine, and the memory may remain incomplete or unresolved.\n\nMake the social and economic stakes legible through concrete relationships and transactions. Show who has access, who is watched, who owes whom, and what a decision costs in money, trust, or standing. Keep the language, period, and community supplied by the scene distinct, and let each character's choices arise from their actual circumstances in that neighborhood rather than from a generic noir plot.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeajad::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Haruki Murakami / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite with hard-edged, concrete prose. Use short, compressed transitions between actions and let material detail carry the scene: worn soles, a cracked screen, a door that sticks, a payment that clears too slowly, a jacket that smells of rain and old wiring. Keep the cyberpunk noir pressure physical and economic: debt, surveillance, access, bodily compromise, and the cost of information. When a character acts, show the concrete result or the concrete obstacle.\n\nPreserve Murakami's attention as a deliberate rhythm shift. Before the pressure closes in, the narrator may register a small ordinary thing, a cup, a shadow, a familiar sound, or the feel of a key, and let it connect to a private concern. Keep that pause brief and concrete; do not let the compressed style erase the association. A longer thought is permitted when the character's reasoning needs it, but keep the language direct and free of ornamental abstraction.\n\nVary the rhythm deliberately. Compress routine or violent movement; slow down for the moment when a character recognizes a threat, weighs a choice, or remembers something that changes the meaning of the present. Ground rough or abrasive speech in its speaker: a street contact, a corporate enforcer, or an exhausted friend may talk hard, but the narration itself should not perform toughness. End scenes on a concrete image or an unfinished practical task.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeajae::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Haruki Murakami / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the cyberpunk noir through documents, each with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Use the document form the scene actually requires: a message, a report, a statement, a log, a note, a record of a transaction, or another authored text. Do not rotate through formats mechanically. What each document knows or conceals follows its writer's situation: a corporate report may omit a bribe, a personal message may avoid a name, a log may record only what a sensor noticed.\n\nLet Murakami's calm attention shape what the writer notices and includes. A document may describe an ordinary action or object with exact care, a cup, a room, a street, a delay, and let it carry private concern without explaining it. The writer may leave an uncertainty unresolved or record an association that seems strange even to them. Gaps, revisions, conflicting accounts, and material traces can reveal events across entries, but do not add an omniscient explanation after every record.\n\nUse the cyberpunk noir world as the condition of writing: surveillance may make a message cautious, debt may make a report selective, corporate language may distort a statement, and a broken device may interrupt a record. Show who controls access to information and who pays for a decision through what the documents can and cannot say. Preserve readable continuity by letting each new entry alter what the reader can infer from the ones before, so the sequence itself performs the investigation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeajaf::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Haruki Murakami / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite the cyberpunk noir world as it is: monitored, indebted, and unequal, but let the scene spend real attention on chosen kindness and small pleasures. A character may bring the right food, remember how someone takes their tea, share a quiet room, fix a small thing, or wait without demanding an explanation. Use Murakami's calm attention to render these acts with exact care: the warmth of a cup, the sound of a familiar voice, the comfort of a routine, the relief of a door closing against the city.\n\nLet affection be sincere without requiring a confession or emotional repair. The characters may care for each other and still disagree, keep secrets, or carry separate worries. Comfort can be partial or awkward. A kind act does not remove the debt, the surveillance, or the danger from the world, but it can make the present interval easier to bear. Keep the noir pressure visible at the edges: a bill, a message, a locked door, or a choice that still has to be made should remain present as the specific unresolved condition framing the comfort.\n\nKeep personality and privacy intact. A character may accept comfort without becoming agreeable, or offer care without explaining why. The scene can end in a moment of ease or quiet attention rather than a resolution. Let the reader enjoy the particular people and their familiar preferences without forcing warmth to solve the plot.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeajag::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Haruki Murakami / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite in composed novel prose with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Let each scene open or close through a frame that places the action in social context: a neighborhood's history, a company's reputation, a law's effect, a family's arrangement, or the narrator's judgment of what the city has become. Keep the cyberpunk noir world legible through that framing: surveillance, debt, access, and unequal power should appear as social conditions the characters live inside, not as isolated threats.\n\nPreserve Murakami's calm narrator and lonely attention within the formal style. The narrator may move from a broad observation to an exact surface, a street, a room, a meal, a machine, and let that detail open a private association. Use the distance to distinguish what the narrator knows from what the characters know, but do not let the formality erase the character's interior experience. Uncertainty can remain unresolved; a strange or ordinary detail may be described evenly without being explained.\n\nLet the investigation and its bargains unfold through scenes that compare public and private life. A corporate statement may contrast with a private conversation; a public record may differ from a memory; a formal transaction may cost something the character only recognizes later. Use ceremony, correspondence, or public/private contrast when the scene supports it, but do not impose an aristocratic setting or archaic vocabulary. The style is the formal architecture, not a costume.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeajah::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Haruki Murakami / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen the outer scene as a calm, concrete encounter with a reason to exist: a room where a transaction is pending, a wait that has been arranged, a task being prepared or postponed. Give the telling an audience, an occasion, and a purpose—someone recounts a past job, a debt, a disappearance, a favor, or a disputed incident to a listener who needs something from the account or from the teller. Keep the narrator's attention even and specific: the surface of the table, the taste of the coffee, the hum of a fan, the way an ordinary object comes to hold private concern.\n\nMake the embedded tale an intervention in the outer world. It may persuade a fixer to extend credit, delay a handoff, explain why a contact went dark, warn a buyer, or change how the listener reads a contract clause, a piece of equipment, or a gap in surveillance. Treat the teller's selection and emphasis as part of the story: a careful account can be sincere while omitting who else was present, what the teller noticed but did not understand, or what the teller now needs the listener to believe.\n\nKeep the narrative levels intelligible by returning to the outer scene at concrete intervals—a refill, a question, a glance at a screen. Let a repeated phrase, object, or gesture accrue meaning across frame and tale: a door that will not lock, a payment that never clears, a name the teller will not say, a signal that arrives late. Recurrence should show how the listener is being guided toward a particular interpretation, not certify that the embedded story is true. The listener can weigh all this privately while giving a polite or neutral spoken answer; what is said aloud and what is concluded need not match, and both belong in the scene.\n\nWhile the tale is being told, let the world's systems supply a separate layer of pressure: a terminal still processing, a debt clock, a monitored channel, a body showing the cost of an implant, a corporate phrase that has become ordinary speech. The listener can set the teller's account against what those systems make visible—a timestamp, a balance, an access log, a locked route—and the gap between the recounted version and the observable record is where the uncertainty lives. Keep the telling at human scale; the teller is a person with reasons to speak, not a device for delivering background.\n\nPreserve the unresolved thresholds. The embedded story may explain an event without settling whether the teller is protecting someone, remembering accurately, or inventing a version that can be sold. The narration can stay conversational and undramatic even when the tale describes violence or loss. Stop at the natural boundary of the telling or the outer scene; the tale need not finish, the listener need not act, and no revelation has to land before the passage closes.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeajai::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Haruki Murakami / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the scene as a narrator comparing what the present system records with what the narrator remembers, or has been told, about how the same place, work, or relationship once functioned. Make the documentary layer concrete: a lease, a work log, a credit record, a surveillance notice, a maintenance history, a corporate memo, a list of the people who used to have access. Do not turn the prose into a report. Let the narration move between the record's language and a private, unhurried attention, so each document is both a fact and something read by a person with a history.\n\nLet the comparison create the pressure. A record shows that a door opened at a certain hour while the narrator remembers waiting somewhere else entirely. A contract describes a service as voluntary while the narrator recalls the moment it became unavoidable. A system lists someone as inactive while the narrator knows what that person still does. Follow who controls the record, who can correct it, and what it costs to be remembered differently. The narrator's unspoken reading belongs in the scene as much as the quoted line: the hesitation before opening a file, the detail checked twice, the entry read again because of what it leaves out.\n\nKeep the syntax and distance of this route: composed, reflective, patient, with room for a long observation to sit beside a short fact. Precision about dates, amounts, and locations need not become clinical. Let strangeness stay evenly described—a building repurposed, a neighborhood that no longer matches its map, a body modified to keep working—so the scale of change reaches the reader through attention rather than exclamation.\n\nUse the language, community, and location the story already supplies; do not import bureaucracy, severity, or a particular national setting. The comparison should make the present system legible: who is excluded, who is watched, who is still being charged for a past decision, and what ordinary life continues around those facts. Close when the comparison has become concrete; no protest, resolution, or completed action is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeajaj::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Haruki Murakami / Gothic-Punk\n\nBuild the scene on a collision between old authority and present life. The old authority can be a founding family, a legacy access code, a building that still carries a former owner's name, a contract clause written before the current system existed, or a person whose body and reputation were shaped in an earlier era. The present life is concrete: a rented room, a repair bill, a monitored corridor, a job that depends on someone else's permission.\n\nUse calm, exact attention to make the uncanny ordinary. Describe the worn surface of a privileged object, the quiet way a relaxed person changes posture when a third party enters, the small routine someone performs before speaking freely. Let atmosphere alter how one particular encounter is experienced rather than decorating every room with decay. A private conversation can be intimate and uneasy at once; a public exchange can stay perfectly polite while both people understand what is being withheld.\n\nMake the intimacy morally complicated. The person who offers help may also hold the debt. The person who knows the narrator's body may have installed the modification that now hurts. The person who shares a meal may be the one who reports the narrator's movements. Hold these tensions without resolving them into simple betrayal or simple comfort, and let characters understand different parts of the arrangement while protecting different interests. The narrator can notice privately what a relationship costs and still choose to stay in it, without the scene diagnosing that choice.\n\nKeep the systems visible but not total. A mask can be a social performance, a filtered identity, a corporate persona, or a literal interface that changes how someone is seen; show who can drop it, in whose company, and at what price. Let the old authority survive through a technical restriction, a property claim, or a remembered obligation while the present life continues around it: laundry, transit, a missed payment, a quiet hour. Nothing supernatural is imported to explain events; the horror is material and moral, and it runs on the world the story already supplies. End at the natural boundary of the encounter or the private interval—no confession, unmasking, or completed confrontation is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeajak::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Haruki Murakami / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite from inside a participant's memory: someone who was present for an event, a workplace, a neighborhood, a migration, or a debt and can now speak about it. Give the account an occasion—who asked, who is listening, why today—and keep it personal and specific: what the narrator saw, what the narrator was told, what the narrator did not understand at the time, and what the narrator now wants to set down. Uncertainty is part of the testimony, not a failure of it.\n\nKeep the attention unhurried and practical. A long bus ride, a shared meal, the weight of a tool, the sound a machine makes before it fails, the way a supervisor's voice changed, the exact week a door stopped opening—let ordinary details carry the private concern. The account is composed rather than oratorical: it has room for silence, digression, and a fact given without comment.\n\nSet the testimony against the systems that would overwrite it. A corporate record may file the narrator as a contractor, a statistic, a former employee, a risk. A surveillance log may show only presence, not what was seen. A debt may be recorded as voluntary. The account can correct, complicate, or refuse those versions without claiming perfect knowledge. Show who is listening, who is not, and what it costs to keep speaking.\n\nKeep language, region, community, and period specific to what the story supplies; do not force English, code-switching, religion, caste, or a generic family structure onto the scene. End when the account reaches a natural pause—the narrator need not finish the story, be believed, or receive an answer before the passage closes.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeajal::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Haruki Murakami / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the passage as a dated or occasion-marked private entry, or a short sequence of entries, recording what seemed worth noting on a particular day: an errand, a meal, a conversation, a walk, a repair, a message, a dream, a worry. Let attention move between practical detail and private reflection without forcing a conclusion. The entry is a composed artifact, not a live transcript of every thought—what the narrator chooses to write down, omit, or return to is itself characterization.\n\nLet the pressure of the world enter through accumulation rather than announcement. One entry notes a payment deducted early. Another records that a neighbor's window stayed dark. Another mentions a corporate phrase that has started appearing in ordinary speech. The narrator may not yet name the pattern; the reader should still be able to see how surveillance, debt, and unequal access shape the days while the narrator treats them as ordinary.\n\nKeep the surfaces exact. The temperature of a drink, the sound of a train, the way a screen's light falls across a room, the feel of a key that no longer works. Let strangeness stay evenly described—a building that seems larger at night, a person who remembers the same event differently, a machine that responds before it is touched—and leave it unresolved without making every fact unstable.\n\nClose at the end of an entry or a natural pause in reflection. No completed insight, external event, or change in circumstance is required; seasonal notes and quiet effects are available where they fit the day being recorded, not obligatory scenery.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeajam::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Haruki Murakami / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite from inside one person's attention to a city, a body, a relationship, or a routine. Let the narrator notice how the place is organized: who can enter a building, which streets are watched, where people wait, what a neighborhood looks like at the hour it is supposed to be asleep. Make the urban detail lived and specific rather than a catalogue of scenery, and let the narrator's private reading of the place—routes avoided, doors counted, hours chosen—carry the weight.\n\nKeep the threshold effect psychological. A familiar route can feel rearranged; a message can seem to have arrived before it was sent; a face can stop matching its voice; a room can feel as if it remembers something the narrator cannot name. Describe these impressions evenly and leave them unresolved. Do not turn them into confirmed supernatural facts where the world excludes such things, and do not let them spread until every scene detail is unstable.\n\nLet the systems reach the narrator as private experience. A debt can feel like a second body. A surveillance notice can change how someone sits in a room. An implant can make a memory feel borrowed. A corporate phrase can surface in a lover's speech. Show how these pressures shape attention, hesitation, and choice without turning the narrator into a victim or a hero, and keep the language, community, and history the story supplies.\n\nEnd when the narrator's attention reaches a natural pause; no revelation, escape, or completed decision is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeajan::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Haruki Murakami / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Open with a concrete place, a person, and a present concern—a courier waiting for a package, a worker checking a balance, a contact arriving late, a room where someone should not be. Keep the first sentences simple enough that the reader knows where the scene is and what the narrator is attending to.\n\nKeep spatial changes easy to follow. When the narrator moves from street to lobby, terminal to back room, public channel to private message, mark the change with a short paragraph break and one clear physical or interface detail. Use readable breaks around action, dialogue, and private response, and let a reaction beat be a single sentence or a brief paragraph: a hand that will not stop shaking, a charge appearing on the screen, a name that is no longer recognized.\n\nKeep calm, exact attention inside the fast structure. The hum of a vending machine, the weight of a bag, rain collecting on a sensor, the moment a door decides to open can slow a beat without stalling the scene. Strangeness stays evenly described, and a reaction can be serious, uncertain, tired, or quietly frightened according to its owner; wit, banter, and cliffhangers are not admission fees.\n\nKeep the pressure concrete. A short paragraph can establish that a loan is being repriced, that a route is being logged, or that a body has been modified to keep working, and who controls access to each. Present an unfamiliar name, device, or mechanic clearly the first time it matters rather than explaining every system at once. Close at a natural pause in the scene or the narrator's attention; no completed action, revelation, or reversal is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeajao::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Haruki Murakami / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet the city's systems enter the prose as recurring sensory patterns rather than as a catalogue of technology. A camera's slow turn, the hum of a credit check, the way a locked door reads a hand and pauses before refusing it: establish such an image where the scene first supplies it, then return to it when a later moment gives it a different weight — the same camera watching a farewell, the same hum under the silence that follows bad news. Build rhythm by alternating long clauses that follow the narrator's attention with short plain statements that record what a system has just done. Let a comparison unfold when it changes what the narrator understands: a debt notice described first by its pale light, then by the quiet it leaves in the room, then by the particular memory that quiet opens. When a figure grows elaborate, anchor it in something physically present — rain on hot metal, the weight of a coat still damp from the street, a screen's color floating in a dark window — so it stays traceable to the scene or to the teller's own associations.\n\nKeep the narrator's practical activities unhurried even when the pressure around them is not. Preparing food, waiting for a train, checking a message can occupy real narrative time while the prose traces an association between an ordinary surface and a private concern. Describe strangeness evenly: a familiar street remapped by new surveillance, a face on a screen that seems to recognize the viewer, a routine transaction that asks one question too many — report each in the same calm register as boiling water. Let uncertainty stand; the narrator may notice that a pattern repeats without ever learning what it means.\n\nCarry the noir material through access and cost. Show who can enter a building, whose name opens a file, what a favor costs, and what the narrator gives up to learn one more thing. Corporate language and street knowledge may appear in dialogue or remembered speech, but the narrator's own sentences should not flatten into procedure, and other characters keep their plainer registers — the figurative current belongs to the telling, not to every mouth. Close paragraphs on the narrator's attention rather than on a summary of what the information means.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeajap::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Haruki Murakami / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the narrator notice what has already changed in the ordinary world the systems have touched. A shop that once greeted a customer by name now asks for a scan; a rooftop where two people used to meet sits under a new camera; a message from someone no longer trusted still arrives at the same hour. Let such details carry the reflection rather than announce it. When the narrator does reflect directly, keep the thought particular — what this specific absence makes harder now, what this object used to mean, what this person might have said on an occasion like this one — rather than a general meditation on time.\n\nDraw the noir pressure from irreversible costs. Show what a character gave up to keep an access credential, what a debt removed from a daily routine, what a bargain made impossible to say afterward. A remembered occasion can surface while the present scene continues: waiting for a door to open, the narrator recalls who first brought them to that door and still notices the new light above it. Let memory and present coexist — the scene is not a container for nostalgia, and the recollection does not stall the waiting.\n\nKeep ordinary pleasure available. A meal can be good, a street quiet, a conversation easy, even while the narrator knows what has been lost; a pleasant detail need not become evidence of decline, and no scene is obliged to end by announcing impermanence. Some shifts the narrator senses without knowing whether they can be repaired — leave that uncertainty where it lies. Throughout, keep the calm tone of someone living with the change rather than performing grief about it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeajaq::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Haruki Murakami / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nCarry the account through a situated teller. Settle early where the telling happens, who listens, and what the teller's relation to the events is — witness, hearer, or assembler of other people's accounts — and let that position govern everything the telling can report. Mark the difference in the telling itself between what the teller saw, what someone told them, and what they only infer; when the teller does not know, leave the gap in the account rather than covering it with omniscient explanation. If the story's world names a language or community, use its actual forms of address and story habits as supplied, and keep named traditions distinct rather than blending them into one regional flavor. The frame and the told story remain in the world the story has supplied; antiquity, religion, and a single honor code arrive only if that world brings them.\n\nInside the frame, let the told story follow the noir logic of access and cost. The teller should know who could enter a place, who paid for a decision, and what a person risked by asking one more question, and should build the telling around those turns. Use delayed revelation as a method of ordering rather than a puzzle for its own sake: a detail offered early — a name withheld, a door that opened for the wrong person — is explained when the teller reaches the part where it mattered. A listener's question can interrupt the frame, sending the teller back or making them reconsider a detail; use these breaks to shift pace and to expose what the telling had been postponing.\n\nKeep Murakami's even tone across both levels. Strange events inside the told story are described as calmly as the room where the telling happens, and an ordinary object — a cup, a cracked screen — can open an association that belongs to the teller's own memory or to the person whose story is being told. The telling is a composed account, not a transcript of spontaneous speech: pause over a remembered image when a lyric or reflective beat earns its place, then return to the forward movement of the tale.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeajar::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Haruki Murakami / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose a small number of exact details and let them carry the system's presence: a door that opens for one person and not another; a screen that shows a balance without a name; a message that arrives already read. Do not explain what each detail means. Place it where the narrator's attention would naturally fall, and let the next action or the next thought make the implication available.\n\nWrite in direct clauses and keep the narrator's practical activities unhurried — waiting, preparing food, walking a familiar route — while the prose records only what matters: the weight of the coat, the sound of the lock, the minutes a train is late. When an inward connection is essential, give it the room it needs: the narrator may think that a person used to answer before the second ring, hold that for a moment, and return to the present action without padding the thought with explanation.\n\nPreserve causality through omission. If a character pays for information, show what changes hands and what comes back. If a favor alters a relationship, show the moment the change becomes visible — a shorter answer, a door left unlocked — and let the reader infer the larger system from the specific transaction. Report an unusual event in the same plain register as an ordinary one, and let uncertainty remain unresolved. Do not perform shortness: a precise extended sentence serves the style better than several broken fragments, and each speaker keeps an individual voice.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeajas::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Haruki Murakami / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose that moves naturally among what the narrator does, what others say, what the narrator perceives, remembers, and thinks. Let the systems appear inside that movement instead of stopping the scene for explanation: a character pauses at a door while it reads a hand, answers a question that is really a credit check, notices that a vendor's screen already shows their name. The next action or line of dialogue shows what the system has made possible or impossible; no lecture intervenes.\n\nKeep the unhurried practical activities and the even treatment of strangeness. A scene can include making coffee, waiting for a message, or walking a known route while attention drifts between the task at hand and a private concern. When something strange happens, describe it in the same calm register as the rest of the scene, and let an ordinary object pick up an association from the immediate situation rather than from a general meditation. Uncertainty can remain unresolved without destabilizing every fact the narrator has already established.\n\nUse paragraphing and transitions to orient the reader without recap machinery: a new paragraph marks a shift of attention, a change of place, or another person's arrival. Follow who controls access and who pays for a decision, and render the cost concretely — a favor owed, a file opened, a body altered, a route no longer available. End the scene with the business concluded or still in motion; do not append a summary of what it all means.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeajat::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Haruki Murakami / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nLet the relationship the premise actually establishes choose the scenes. If the premise is trust, show the moments where trust is extended, tested, or repaired; if rivalry, show the private history that gives the competition its particular edge. Make the bond legible through private language and repeated moments — a phrase one person uses only with the other, a gesture whose meaning has changed over time, a place the two return to or avoid — and let a later scene give an earlier callback new weight. The payoff should fulfill the specific promise of that relationship rather than importing a roster of familiar tropes.\n\nLet the noir systems supply the pressure around the bond. Show who can observe the two, what a meeting costs, what a message risks, and what one person gives up to protect or reach the other. A debt, a surveillance feed, or a contract can make a quiet moment of affection, jealousy, or comfort consequential without turning care into a trap or a betrayal into a certainty.\n\nKeep the Murakami register: characters speak with conversational distance even when the feeling is strong, and a strange event is described as calmly as a familiar routine. Give the relevant feeling room where it belongs — a shared meal, a slow walk, the particular way one person waits for the other — while an ordinary object carries whatever association the immediate scene awakens. Preserve the active perspective and access rules: one character's private thought stays unknown to the other unless the scene supports the disclosure. Uncertainty about the relationship may remain unresolved, but the present emotional reality should be available to the reader.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeajau::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Haruki Murakami / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the story as an account of deeds by which these people will be remembered, in a world where systems remember too. Open deliberately, as a retelling does — with a named act, a settled reputation, or a debt whose payment others still mention — then move into the particulars that make the reputation incomplete. A recurring phrase should grow from an established deed or a person's way of speaking, not from an ornamental epithet attached to every entrance, and should return when a later occurrence gives it a new application.\n\nLet the noir systems supply the material of consequence and of memory. A door opened for someone who had been excluded, a name removed from a list, a bargain kept at real cost, a feed that still holds a face the neighborhood has half-forgotten: compact declarations state what was done, and the prose that follows shows what it meant for the people involved, who controlled access, and who paid. When the account touches a disagreement between human memory and machine memory — a community remembering a kindness the ledger records otherwise, or a database keeping what every person has let go — let the chronicle preserve that tension rather than resolve it quietly.\n\nKeep the tone even; the chronicle does not inflate an ordinary transaction into heroic combat. Someone who repairs a device, waits outside a locked building, or refuses to sell a name has done an act worth remembering without being declared a legend, and dialogue can interrupt the chronicle's authority: the subject of an oft-told incident corrects a detail, or answers an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective, and keep shared reputation distinct from a focal figure's private account — being remembered as dependable does not mean the act was easy or cheap. End with business concluded or resumed, another modest act left available to memory.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeajav::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Haruki Murakami / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the cyberpunk system as a social institution the viewpoint character must navigate through ordinary transactions: a housing registry, a credit office, a workplace schedule, a maintenance queue, a debt review. Keep the institutional logic concrete. Who can access a record, who approves a payment, who decides when a body is eligible for repair or replacement, and what documentation is required should be legible through the character's encounters with clerks, supervisors, neighbors, and caseworkers. Do not make the system a vague atmospheric force; make it a set of procedures with human gatekeepers and material consequences.\n\nFor the Scandinavian route, use social realism with institutional weight, not landscape decoration. The system's power is embodied in specific people whose ordinary labor makes the rules operational. A clerk who has seen the same denial a hundred times may still pause at an unfamiliar code. A caseworker may explain the policy without endorsing it. A supervisor may be sympathetic and still enforce the deadline. Show the relationship between citizens and the offices that manage them through the small gestures and repeated encounters of daily life. When a character waits in a queue, let the other people waiting form a small civic scene, not a backdrop. When a denial arrives, make the character's private response visible through physical behavior and leave the official's motive knowable only by what the system permits.\n\nFor Murakami's attention, render the day through unhurried practical actions. Let a terminal that will not accept a scan, a jacket worn to look solvent, a receipt kept for unexamined reasons, and the physical fact of waiting structure the character's experience. The strangeness should arise from how a routine denial can feel simultaneously absurd and enforceable. The character may notice that nothing is wrong and still feel that something has changed, and that feeling need not be resolved by an impossible event or a sudden revelation.\n\nFor the noir dimension, let the practical goal of renewing a permit, disputing a charge, or finding a missing payment provide pressure without requiring a solvable case structure. A record may contradict a spoken account; a helpful neighbor may be mistaken; a supervisor may be trustworthy and still unable to help. Follow who controls access and who pays for a decision through each exchange. Let the character's knowledge remain partial.\n\nThe story proceeds through the character's actual situation, so use the active narrator and world facts. If the story supplies a specific city, period, or legal system, use its actual institutions and language. Keep private thought associated with the viewpoint character and inaccessible to other characters unless the active contract grants access. A scene can close on a completed errand, a scheduled follow-up, or the quiet realization that nothing was resolved today without requiring a cliffhanger or a plot reversal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeajaw::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Haruki Murakami / Screenplay\n\nUse visible institutional mechanics to carry the cyberpunk noir pressure. When a character approaches a terminal, a gate, or a service desk, show what the system does: a screen flashes a code, a door stays closed, a clerk compares two documents. Let the audience see who can pass and who is stalled. Do not explain the system in dialogue or narration; show its decisions as events that happen in the scene. A person can be admitted, deferred, or excluded, and the difference should be visible.\n\nBuild dialogue around practical negotiations rather than exposition. Characters speak to ask for documentation, confirm a balance, request an extension, explain a policy, or exchange street knowledge. Let their speech reveal relationship and position without turning every line into a debate about the system. A clerk can be tired, helpful, hostile, or indifferent for reasons that appear in what they do and say. No character should announce the theme of the scene.\n\nKeep Murakami's calm attention in the action lines. Show the physical business of waiting, checking a card, adjusting a strap, reading a notice. Let an ordinary object—a worn transit card, a receipt, a jacket—appear in the action and return later with changed significance. The strangeness should be visible in the gap between the system's promise and its actual operation. A denial can be a small quiet event, not a dramatic trumpet.\n\nFor required private thought, use a clearly attributed nonspoken note separated from the dialogue and action. The note may record that the viewpoint character notices a clerk's hesitation and wonders whether the file is complete. Do not make the note available to other characters. Do not substitute voice-over as the default for private thought; use voice-over only when the narrative needs it, and keep it clearly distinguishable from the spoken dialogue. A note is not a line of dialogue and is not heard in the scene.\n\nScene endings should be events that can hold without explanation. A scene can end on a closed office, a scheduled follow-up, or the image of a person standing outside a door. Do not add a reversal, confession, or cliffhanger to each scene break. A scene break may reveal a new fact, but it does not have to resolve or complete the current uncertainty.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeajax::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Haruki Murakami / Stage Play\n\nMake the stage space show who has access. Doors, checkpoints, terminal positions, and counter windows should be physically distinct. Let a character be kept outside while another is admitted. Let a prop pass between hands or be refused. When a character reaches for a card, a form, or a bag, the blocking should show whether they are allowed to take it. The system's power is in the arrangement of bodies and objects, not in dialogue.\n\nStage the ordinary business with Murakami's exactness. Show a character waiting for a number, adjusting a strap, checking a balance, reading a notice. Keep these actions unhurried and precise. Let a small physical object—a worn transit card, a receipt, a jacket—appear early and return later so its changed meaning is visible in gesture. The strangeness should arise from the gap between the system's promise and the system's actual choreography. A denial can be a competent, quiet refusal performed at a counter.\n\nDialogue should handle practical negotiations. A clerk asks for documentation. A supervisor explains a policy. A neighbor shares street knowledge. Let each speaker's lines fit their role and the immediate task. Characters can answer plainly, deflect, or challenge a premise because the situation gives them a reason. Do not make the cast uniformly witty or combative. Let silence and pause do some of the work.\n\nFor required private thought, use nonspoken authoring material in its active presentation. Do not disguise it as a monologue or aside that another character can hear. An aside or monologue may be used when it has a real dramatic purpose, but it is not the default home for every private thought. The audience may know what the viewpoint character notices or wonders; the other characters onstage do not know it merely because the audience does.\n\nEnd scenes on stageable events. A door can close. A character can be left outside. A form can be handed over or withheld. A scheduled follow-up can be announced. Do not require a reversal, confession, or cliffhanger at every exit. The evening can simply continue toward the next appointment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeajay::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Haruki Murakami / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite from inside one authorized mind. Let perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern braid together rather than proceed in a linear report. A queue number announced overhead may lead to a memory of another waiting room, then to the feel of a card in a pocket, then back to the clerk's voice. Syntax and paragraph shape may follow those associations, but keep enough orientation to distinguish what is happening now from what is remembered or imagined. Do not make the present scene disappear behind the recollections.\n\nUse Murakami's calm practical attention as the mind's habit. The character notices the small actions of preparing for an appointment, waiting, checking a balance, reading a notice, or walking to a transit stop with even, unhurried precision. Let ordinary objects carry private concern: a terminal that will not accept a scan, a jacket worn to look solvent, a receipt kept for reasons the character does not fully explain. The strangeness should arise from how the system treats people, not from impossible events. A denial can feel unreal without the world becoming unreal.\n\nLet the cyberpunk system enter through the character's felt experience of being observed, priced, and made subject to someone else's decision. A debt can feel like a weight in the chest. A surveillance notice can return as a phrase that repeats when the character tries to relax. A compromised motive can surface as the memory of a favor the character accepted and now cannot return. The system should be present even when no character is directly speaking about it: the mind carries it.\n\nThe character's associations should be particular, not generic. They arise from the character's own history, habits, and concerns. What a queue number reminds them of depends on where they have waited before and what they fear about this waiting. Another character's thoughts are not available. When the character infers a motive, show the observation that supports the inference, and allow the next moment to confirm, complicate, or redirect it.\n\nA scene can end on an unfinished thought, a scheduled follow-up, or a plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Do not require a reversal, confession, or cliffhanger at every pause. Preserve the active narrator and world facts. If the story supplies a specific city, period, or legal system, use its actual institutions and language.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeajaz::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Haruki Murakami / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the story through readable sender-attributed messages. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: a clerk may write in short procedural phrases, a neighbor may use street shorthand, a supervisor may leave formal notices. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter. A delayed confirmation, a read receipt without a reply, or a channel switch can carry pressure without being invented merely to add subtext.\n\nLet the cyberpunk system appear through message content and interface behavior. A payment request arrives with a due date. An access code is sent and then revoked. A queue update changes the character's plans. A support channel asks for documentation the character does not have. Follow who controls access and who pays for a decision through what is transmitted and what is withheld. The system should be present in what the messages make possible and impossible, not only in what they say.\n\nThe noir pressure should be felt in the gap between what a sender says and what the channel shows. A promise of help may arrive with a read receipt and no follow-up. A request for documents may be accepted and then silently closed. A supervisor's polite phrase may carry a due date the character cannot meet. The character's compromised position is visible in what they cannot send, what they receive without being able to answer, and what they know the system has already recorded.\n\nPreserve Murakami's calm attention in the context outside the exchange. When required, separate it clearly from the transcript. Describe the small physical business of waiting for a reply, checking a balance, or reading a notice with even, unhurried precision. Let an ordinary object—a worn transit card, a receipt, a jacket—appear in the context and return later with changed significance. The context is not a second narrator; it is the physical setting for the character's interaction with the screen.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought. A sent message is public to the channel; a private thought is not. When the character notices an implication in a message, show the observation that supports the inference, and allow the next message to confirm, complicate, or redirect it. Another character does not know a private thought merely because the reader does.\n\nA scene can end on a pending reply, a scheduled follow-up, or a plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Do not require a reversal, confession, or cliffhanger at every message. Preserve the active narrator and world facts. If the story supplies a specific city, period, or legal system, use its actual institutions and language.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeajba::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Haruki Murakami / Visual Novel\n\nUse scene-setting to make the cyberpunk system observable. A transit platform, a credit office, a repair bay, and a night market are all settings in which the system's rules operate. Let the background show which people and objects are available to the character. A terminal that is working for someone else, a queue number that advances, a door that opens or stays closed—these visible details show who has access without being explained in dialogue. Keep scene changes legible.\n\nGive each speaker clear dialogue attribution and let dialogue fit the relationship and the immediate practical task. A clerk may ask for documentation. A supervisor may explain a policy. A neighbor may share street knowledge. Characters can answer plainly, deflect, or challenge a premise for an actual reason. Do not make the cast uniformly witty or combative. Let the dialogue's practical object be clear.\n\nPreserve Murakami's calm attention in the focal narration. Describe the small physical business of waiting, checking a balance, adjusting a strap, or reading a notice with even, unhurried precision. Let an ordinary object—a worn transit card, a receipt, a jacket—appear in the scene and return later with changed significance. The strangeness should arise from how the system treats people, not from impossible events. A denial can feel unreal without the world becoming unreal.\n\nUse focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. The viewpoint character may notice a clerk's hesitation and wonder whether the file is complete. Another character does not hear or know this content. When the character infers a motive, show the observation that supports the inference, and allow the next exchange to confirm, complicate, or redirect it. A background cue—a scanner that flashes, a queue display that changes—can orient the moment, but an inference about motive must be based on what the character can actually observe and the account they receive.\n\nA scene can end on a closed office, a scheduled follow-up, or a plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Do not require a reversal, confession, or cliffhanger at every scene change. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected. Preserve the active narrator and world facts. If the story supplies a specific city, period, or legal system, use its actual institutions and language.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeajbb::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Haruki Murakami / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across replies. At any point, the reader should know what is underway: a permit renewal, a disputed charge, a missing payment, a contact who might know an unmonitored entrance. Let each installment change something locally without making every turn a complete miniature chapter. The change should alter the character's position in the system, not merely provide a new piece of information. A document may be accepted, but the deadline may also shift. A queue number may advance, but the next window may close early. A neighbor may share street knowledge, but the knowledge may create a new debt.\n\nRecover context briefly only when it helps. When an earlier fact becomes relevant, recall the necessary particular rather than retelling the whole investigation. A terminal that previously rejected a scan may now accept it after a repair. A clerk who asked for documentation may now recognize the character. A favor accepted earlier may now be asked for in return. These small returns show that the system and its people have continuity. The noir pressure should accumulate: each installment adds to what the character owes, risks, or knows, and earlier decisions do not disappear when a new scene begins.\n\nUse Murakami's calm attention to make re-entry easy. Open an installment with an ordinary practical detail: the character waiting for a queue number, checking a balance, reading a notice, or walking to a transit stop. Let an ordinary object—a worn transit card, a receipt, a jacket—reappear across installments and carry accumulated significance. The strangeness should arise from how the system treats people, not from impossible events. A denial can feel unreal without the world becoming unreal.\n\nKeep the cyberpunk system cumulative. Who controls access and who pays for a decision should become clearer over time through repeated encounters with clerks, supervisors, neighbors, and caseworkers. A record may contradict a spoken account. A clerk may follow procedure exactly and still produce an unjust result. A supervisor may be helpful without being trustworthy. Let these institutional facts accumulate rather than resolve in a single scene.\n\nUse earned developments and occasional hooks without artificial cliffhangers. An installment can end on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Do not require a reversal, confession, or cliffhanger at every installment. Preserve the active narrator and world facts. If the story supplies a specific city, period, or legal system, use its actual institutions and language.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeakaa::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Jane Austen / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite emotional exposure as conduct under observation. Use Austen's free indirect method to move between what a character admits aloud, what they believe about their own admission, and what the surrounding system would record or charge for it: a withheld apology matters because the platform logged the silence and someone will price it, not because the narration declares the silence painful. Keep the ache in the viewpoint character's own idiom — the particular words they use about debt, standing, and being seen to need someone — rather than a borrowed vocabulary of anguish.\n\nLet strain alter the exchange itself. Courtesy can hold at the surface while private reasoning snags on the cost of needing this person, the risk of being observed needing them, or the gap between what is owed and what can be said. Give that reasoning room when the character is genuinely deliberating, and almost none when they speak before they think. The other person answers from their own position and information; warmth, evasion, or practical help can each arrive without the hurt being settled, and a sincere kindness can leave the dependence, the surveillance, and the payment schedule exactly where they were.\n\nKeep the turbulence in the continuing cost rather than a guaranteed reversal. Comfort may be partial or awkward, and warmth can share a minute with pain. Do not require a confession, reconciliation, or collapse to close the interval, and do not manufacture one; end wherever the exchange has actually brought the character, even mid-cost.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeakab::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Jane Austen / Chat Roleplay\n\nPlay the shared interval in readable, scannable paragraphs: attribute every spoken line unmistakably, and attach action and observation to their speakers in short beats rather than unbroken blocks. Take the other participant's latest move, message, or question as established fact and build from what it changes, without narrating it back to them.\n\nLet each exchange carry more than its surface courtesy. An offer may be testing access, an omission may protect a reputation, and a polite refusal may acknowledge a debt that cannot be repaid; ground these meanings in what the characters actually know and what the scene's systems make expensive, never in automatic suspicion. A brief narrative beat can show the focal character reading a hesitation or a carefully chosen phrase, with that interpretation owned by them — the other speaker may mean something else entirely and keep their motive to themselves.\n\nKeep the world and its people active. An NPC pursues their own interest, offers genuine help, or withholds something for a reason the player can later test, and background systems keep running whether or not anyone addresses them. Leave the continuation wherever the exchange has actually brought things — the next move should follow from what was said or left unsaid, not from a manufactured question, cliffhanger, or forced choice.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeakac::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Jane Austen / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate urban and domestic route. Treat the household, the shared shift, and the neighborhood corridor as the places where large systems become particular obligations: rent posted at the entrance everyone uses, a connection shared or quietly withheld, a favor that changes who may ask next time. Organize scenes around visits, meals, shared work, payments due, and requests relayed through relatives, so that cyberpunk dependence arrives as concrete domestic matter rather than city-scale scenery.\n\nWork at Austen's scale of meaning inside these exchanges. Whom someone greets first, what they leave out of a report to a superior, whether they warn a neighbor instead of logging the infraction — each small decision carries family and community expectation. Move the narration between the focal character's private reasoning and the social meaning others can observe, and keep the two distinct: what is felt and what can be seen to have been done are different facts. Show consequence in the scene's own terms — who gains access, who pays, who is seen to have asked — and let the expectation weigh on the choice without appending a moral.\n\nKeep the telling continuous and bound to particular people. Restraint carries the feeling: what is not said at the table does work a declaration would do elsewhere. Do not relocate the cast, import a period idiom, or rename anyone; the route organizes attention — domestic space, ordinary transactions, conduct under community observation — without dictating setting, names, or speech. A close can leave the obligations newly legible and still unacted upon.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeakad::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Jane Austen / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite in forceful strokes with compressed transitions. Where smoother narration would explain, juxtapose: a scuffed cuff beside a clean reception desk, a drained account under a cheerful payment notice, a door that opens only after the credit check clears. Let material wear carry the meaning Austen finds in circumstance — who can leave, who must wait, who is watched, who pays — as hard, specific detail instead of commentary.\n\nCompress free indirect judgment into the action. One clause inside a sentence of movement can hold what a character believes about themselves — she was, of course, the last person who would need help, and she took the advance anyway — and a courteous phrase lands like a demand once the speaker's position has been shown. Keep exchanges tight under pressure. When a character's reasoning genuinely needs room, give it: a longer thought is welcome so long as it stays concrete and tied to the leverage at hand.\n\nVary rhythm to mark shifts in power and danger. A clipped sentence can shut a negotiation; a slower unspooling can render someone reading the price of what they just did. Do not coat every line in slang or menace; rough speech belongs to the particular speaker who would use it. The grit is precision under pressure, not a uniform tone of threat.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeakae::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Jane Austen / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the sequence in documents, each with an author, a recipient or purpose, and a time of composition, and let those facts govern what it can contain. Choose the form the moment requires — a corporate report, a payment notice, a household ledger, a message sent through a monitored channel, a recorded statement — rather than rotating through formats. Within each entry its author decides what to record, what to omit, and how to frame a request or refusal: a courteous letter can carry a threat, a formal report can leave out the favor that made the data available, a personal note can guard a reputation while admitting a debt.\n\nLet the gap between a document's surface and its author's knowledge surface through later entries, never through an explaining voice after each one. Material traces do the evidentiary work: a timestamp that contradicts a claim, a revised clause, a missing attachment, a change in who is addressed, a line struck from a draft. Treat an unsent draft as the composed artifact it is — its revisions and abandoned sentences show someone choosing their words, which is not the same as unmediated access to their mind.\n\nSequence the entries so each new document answers the situation the previous one left behind; dates, addressing, and internal references keep the thread readable, and conflicting accounts may stand side by side where the reader can do the noticing. A later record can quietly change what an earlier one meant. Do not append a resolving editorial voice, and do not let the sequence require every gap to close.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeakaf::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Jane Austen / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend the scene's attention on chosen kindness, familiar preferences, and practical care arranged inside the world's actual conditions. Make comfort specific and material: a meal matched to what someone actually likes, a repair done properly on borrowed tools, a shift covered without comment, a preference remembered across months of changes, a small privacy shielded from a monitoring system. These acts are arranged around real constraints — conditional access, short money, someone watching — and that arrangement is part of what makes them mean something; it is not a flaw in the ease.\n\nLet care pass through ordinary exchange, in Austen's manner: help offered without naming why, teasing that only long acquaintance permits, a courtesy extended exactly where the system expects none, standing quietly acknowledged. People stay themselves — private, occasionally at odds, fond in their own idioms — so disagreement can remain alive inside an easy interval, and a kind act neither cancels a debt nor deletes a threat.\n\nGive the characters room to enjoy each other and their small pleasures. The world's pressure can sit as known background — rent still due, the watch still running — without arriving as a twist that punishes the warmth. Affection does not need to escalate into confession or repair, and the interval needs no practical decision to justify itself; a closing line may rest inside the ease, mid-meal or mid-task, rather than at a resolved outcome.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaeakag::♢ * [STYLE] Cyberpunk Noir / Jane Austen / Formal Literary Novel\n\nFrame each scene through its social and institutional situation, using deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Open with the arrangement that makes the moment delicate — who controls the access being requested, what history makes this payment or loyalty awkward, whose records can turn a priva